/* =======================================================================
   vocab-notes.js — explanations and example sentences for Vocab Flip
   (Genki lessons 1–19, 946 words, 3546 explanations).
   Keyed by "kana|kanji" (kanji left empty for kana-only words), same as Vocab Flip.
     explain: the LONG explanations ("📖 Long why") — 2 to 5 per word, each from a
              different angle. One is picked at random each time the word comes up.
     short:   the SHORT version of each ("💡 Short why"), in the same order.
     ja / en: an example sentence (Story-time markup) and its translation.
   Japanese inside explanations is written 「かな」 or 「漢字|かな」: shown as the
   kanji, spoken as the kana by the Japanese voice.
   ======================================================================= */
window.VOCAB_NOTES = window.VOCAB_NOTES || {};
Object.assign(window.VOCAB_NOTES, {
  "せんせい|先生": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's take it apart. The first kanji, 「先|せん」, means ahead, or before. The second, 「生|せい」, means life, or to be born. Both use their Chinese-origin readings here. Put them together and you get someone born before you. In other words, a person who went down the road first and can now show you the way. You already know 「先|せん」 from 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week, the week that came before. And 「生|せい」 is everywhere in school words, like 「学生|がくせい」, student, and 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, international student. Here's something English speakers find surprising. 「せんせい」 isn't only for school teachers. Doctors, lawyers, politicians, and masters of an art are all called 「せんせい」 too. It's a title of respect for anyone with expert knowledge. You also attach it to a name, like 「やましたせんせい」, and you never use it about yourself. So to recap, a teacher is the one born ahead of you, who walks the path first." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a Japanese classroom, students simply call out 「せんせい」 to get the teacher's attention. No name is needed. It works the way you might say excuse me, or sir, in English. A typical moment sounds like this. 「せんせい、しつもんがあります」. Teacher, I have a question. When you add a name, the title replaces 「さん」. So it's 「やましたせんせい」, never 「やましたさんせんせい」. Japanese students almost never use a teacher's first name, even at university. The family name plus 「せんせい」 is the normal way, for years after you graduate. The same habit carries into hospitals. Patients call their doctor 「せんせい」, and nurses say it too. One common slip for learners is writing an email that starts with the teacher's first name. Start it with the family name and 「せんせい」 instead, and you will sound polite and natural. So whenever you speak to someone who teaches or treats you, reach for this title first." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each half of this word opens a door to many other words you will meet in Genki. The first kanji, 「先|せん」, carries the idea of going ahead. Besides last week, 「先週|せんしゅう」, you get last month, 「先月|せんげつ」. In both, the time that went ahead of now is the time that has passed. Read with its native reading, the same kanji becomes 「先に|さきに」, ahead or first, as in go on ahead. The second kanji, 「生|せい」, is all about life and birth. As a verb it becomes 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born. It also hides in 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, birthday, where it is read 「じょう」. And in school talk, a first-year student is 「一年生|いちねんせい」 and a high schooler is 「高校生|こうこうせい」. So one half of the word points forward in time, and the other half is about being alive. Learn these two kanji well and a whole cluster of school and time words becomes easy." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Teachers in Japan traditionally receive a lot of formal respect. In many schools, class starts with a short ritual. A student on duty calls out 「きりつ」, stand up, then 「れい」, bow. Everyone bows to the teacher, and the teacher bows back. Then the lesson begins. The same ritual often closes the class too. But Japanese people also have a sense of humor about the title. There is a well known old saying, 「せんせいといわれるほどのばかでなし」. Roughly, it means I am not such a fool that I need to be called teacher. It pokes fun at people who love being flattered with the title, since politicians and other important people get called 「せんせい」 so often. So the word carries real respect, but people also know it can be used as flattery. Use it sincerely with your own teachers, and you will always be on safe ground." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「先|せん」 means ahead and 「生|せい」 means born, so a teacher is someone born before you who walked the road first. It's also a title of respect for doctors, lawyers and other experts.",
      "Students just call out 「せんせい」 to get attention, and with a name it replaces 「さん」, as in 「やましたせんせい」. Doctors get called 「せんせい」 too, and you always use the family name, never the first name.",
      "「先|せん」 points ahead in time, as in 「先週|せんしゅう」 and 「先月|せんげつ」, while 「生|せい」 is about life, as in 「生まれる|うまれる」 and 「一年生|いちねんせい」. Learn these two kanji and a cluster of school and time words opens up.",
      "Classes often begin with a stand, bow ritual to the teacher, showing real respect. Yet an old saying mocks people who love being flattered as 「せんせい」, so use it sincerely with your own teachers."
    ],
    ja: "[先生|せんせい]は{日本人|にほんじん}です。",
    en: "The teacher is Japanese." },
  "がくせい|学生": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. 「学|がく」 means learning or study. 「生|せい」 means life, or a living person. So a student is a person whose life right now is about learning. Look at the bottom of 「学|がく」 and you'll find the kanji for child, 「子|こ」. A way to remember it: picture a child sitting under a roof, busy studying. 「学|がく」 is one of the most useful kanji you'll meet. It's in 「大学|だいがく」, college, in 「学校|がっこう」, school, where the sound shrinks a little to 「がっ」, and in 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, a student studying abroad. And 「生|せい」 shows up in 「先生|せんせい」, the teacher, so the teacher and the student share the same second kanji. In Japan, 「がくせい」 often points to college students in particular, while younger kids are more often called 「せいと」 or 「じどう」. To recap, learning plus person gives you a student." },
      { title: "School ladder", text: "Japanese has a neat set of words for students at each level of school, and they are built like steps on a ladder. An elementary school pupil is 「小学生|しょうがくせい」, a student of the small school. A junior high student is 「中学生|ちゅうがくせい」, a student of the middle school. A high school student is 「高校生|こうこうせい」. A college student is 「大学生|だいがくせい」, and a graduate student is 「大学院生|だいがくいんせい」. Notice that the size words grow as you climb. Small, middle, and then big for college. So if someone asks what you are, you can be more specific than just 「がくせい」. For example, 「わたしはだいがくせいです」 tells them right away that you are in college. And when you talk about your year, add a number in front of 「ねんせい」. A second-year is 「にねんせい」. Remember the ladder of small, middle, high, big, and you can place any student in one word." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji in this word are worth a closer look, because they have more readings than you might expect. 「学|がく」 is the modern simplified shape of an older, much busier kanji. The child at the bottom stayed, while the top was simplified into three short strokes over a cover. Its sound 「がく」 shrinks to 「がっ」 before a k sound, as in 「学校|がっこう」. As a verb with its native reading, it becomes 「学ぶ|まなぶ」, to learn, a slightly formal word you will meet later. Now 「生|せい」 is famous for having more readings than almost any other kanji. It's 「せい」 here, but it's 「う」 in 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born, and 「じょう」 in 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, birthday. It's even 「なま」, raw or fresh, in 「生ビール|なまビール」, draft beer. All of these readings circle around the idea of life, birth, and freshness. So when you see this kanji, don't panic. Think life, and let the word around it tell you the reading." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Being a student in Japan comes with some handy perks, and the words for them all start with this word. Your student ID card is 「学生証|がくせいしょう」. You show it at museums, cinemas, and train stations. Why? Because of the student discount, 「学生割引|がくせいわりびき」. Japanese people love to shorten long words, so everyone just says 「学割|がくわり」. At a ticket window you might hear, 「がくわりはありますか」, is there a student discount? In a self-introduction, the usual pattern is to name your school first, connected with 「の」. For example, 「わたしはとうきょうだいがくのがくせいです」, I'm a student at the University of Tokyo. The school comes first and the student comes last, the reverse of English word order. So remember your ID, ask about the discount, and put your school before the word student." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「学|がく」 means learning and 「生|せい」 means a living person, so a student is someone whose life is about learning. Spot the child 「子|こ」 under the roof of 「学|がく」, busy studying.",
      "Student words climb a ladder of size: 「小学生|しょうがくせい」, 「中学生|ちゅうがくせい」, 「高校生|こうこうせい」, 「大学生|だいがくせい」. For your year, put a number before 「ねんせい」, like 「にねんせい」.",
      "「学|がく」 shrinks to 「がっ」 in 「学校|がっこう」, and 「生|せい」 has many readings, like 「う」 in 「生まれる|うまれる」 and 「なま」 in 「生ビール|なまビール」. They all circle around life, so let the word around it decide the reading.",
      "Flash your 「学生証|がくせいしょう」 to get the student discount everyone calls 「学割|がくわり」. In introductions the school comes first, as in 「とうきょうだいがくのがくせいです」."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは[学生|がくせい]です。",
    en: "I am a student." },
  "だいがく|大学": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「大|だい」 means big, and 「学|がく」 means learning. A big place of learning. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings in this word. The shape of 「大|だい」 is a person standing with arms and legs stretched out wide, as if to say, this big! On its own as an adjective, it takes the native reading, 「大きい|おおきい」. But in compounds like this one it's usually 「だい」, as in 「大好き|だいすき」, to love, and 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, it's okay. 「学|がく」 is the same learning you see in 「学生|がくせい」 and 「学校|がっこう」. Add one more kanji, 「院|いん」, institution, and you get 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school. A note on meaning. Japanese doesn't split college and university the way English sometimes does. A four-year school of any size is a 「だいがく」. Students are 「だいがくせい」, and first-years say 「いちねんせい」. To recap, a big school of learning." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The big kanji, 「大|だい」, actually has three main readings, and you'll need all of them. The first is 「だい」, as in this word and in 「大学生|だいがくせい」, college student. The second is 「たい」. You'll meet it in 「大切|たいせつ」, important or precious, and 「大変|たいへん」, tough or terrible. The third is the native reading, 「おお」, in 「大きい|おおきい」, big. And there's one special word you just learn whole. 「大人|おとな」 means adult, literally a big person. Now compare this word with 「学校|がっこう」. 「がっこう」 is the general word for school of any kind. 「だいがく」 is specifically a university or college. So you can say your college is a 「がっこう」 in a loose way, but a high school is never a 「だいがく」. Keep the three readings of big in mind, and many new words will feel familiar." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When Japanese people talk about universities, they often clip long names into short nicknames. The University of Tokyo, 「東京大学|とうきょうだいがく」, is almost always called 「東大|とうだい」, taking the first kanji of each part. The same trick gives you many other nicknames, and students use them proudly. To ask someone where they go to college, you can say 「どこのだいがくですか」, which university is it? They might answer with the school name plus their year, like 「〜だいがくのさんねんせいです」. Talking about your major comes right after. 「せんこうはなんですか」. One difference from English is that Japanese doesn't usually say going to college as a general life stage. People simply say 「だいがくにいきます」, I go to college, or name the school. Next time you introduce yourself, try school name, then year, then major, and you'll sound like a local student." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "University life in Japan has its own rhythm, quite different from the American one. The school year starts in April, when the cherry blossoms are out, and ends in March. Getting in usually means passing tough entrance exams, and the stress of exam season is a big part of teenage life. Once students are in, many relax a little and throw themselves into clubs. Student clubs are often called 「サークル」, from the English word circle, and they range from tennis to manga to volunteering. Then, around the third year, students start job hunting, which is a serious, organized season in Japan. Many companies hire new graduates all at once each April, so timing really matters. So college in Japan is often described as a breathing space between exam pressure and working life. Keep that picture in mind when you hear Japanese friends talk about their 「だいがく」 days." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「大|だい」 is a person stretched out wide meaning big, and 「学|がく」 means learning, so a university is a big place of learning. Add 「院|いん」 and you get graduate school, 「大学院|だいがくいん」.",
      "The big kanji has three readings: 「だい」 here, 「たい」 in 「大切|たいせつ」, and 「おお」 in 「大きい|おおきい」. Unlike 「がっこう」, which is any school, 「だいがく」 is specifically a college.",
      "Long university names get clipped, so 「東京大学|とうきょうだいがく」 becomes 「東大|とうだい」. Ask 「どこのだいがくですか」 and introduce yourself with school, year, then major.",
      "The Japanese school year runs April to March, and college sits as a breathing space between tough entrance exams and job hunting. Many students spend it in clubs called 「サークル」."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは[大学|だいがく]の{一年生|いちねんせい}です。",
    en: "I'm a first-year student at college." },
  "ともだち|友だち": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first part, 「友|とも」, is the kanji for friend, read here with its native Japanese reading. The second part, 「だち」, is a sound-softened form of 「たち」, the ending that makes groups of people. You know it from 「わたしたち」, we. So the word originally meant friends, plural, a group of companions. Over time the plural feeling faded, and now 「ともだち」 works perfectly well for just one friend. That's why you can even say 「ともだちたち」, friends, without it sounding too odd, though most people just say 「ともだち」 for one or many. In textbooks you'll often see it written with the first part in kanji and the rest in kana, exactly like this word. A small culture note. Japanese makes a clear difference between a friend and a boyfriend or girlfriend, so 「ともだち」 never hints at romance. To recap, 「とも」 means friend, and 「だち」 is an old group ending that stuck." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the patterns you'll actually use with this word every day. To do something with a friend, use the particle 「と」. 「ともだちとえいがをみます」, I watch a movie with a friend. To become friends, use 「に」 with the verb 「なる」. 「ともだちになりましょう」, let's be friends. Notice that Japanese usually doesn't say my friend. Just 「ともだち」 is enough, because it's clear whose friend you mean. Adding 「わたしの」 every time sounds heavy, like saying my friend of mine. In casual speech among young men, you may also hear a rough slang form, 「ダチ」. It's very informal, so recognize it, but don't use it with your teacher. A tiny dialogue. 「あのひとはだれですか」. 「ともだちです」. Who is that person? A friend. So use 「と」 for with, 「に」 for becoming, and let 「ともだち」 stand on its own." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「友|とも」 also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「ゆう」, and it gives you a few close cousins of this word. 「友人|ゆうじん」 also means friend, but it sounds more formal and more written. You'd use it in a speech or a business setting. 「親友|しんゆう」 means a best friend, a close, trusted friend. The first kanji is the one for parent, and also for close or intimate. So the scale goes from 「ともだち」, everyday friend, to 「ゆうじん」, the polite version, to 「しんゆう」, your closest friend. About the shape. The kanji is traditionally explained as two hands, one over the other, working together. That's a lovely picture for friendship, two hands helping each other. Calling someone your 「しんゆう」 is a big compliment, so save it for someone special. Remember, friend in daily talk, formal friend in speeches, and best friend for the person you trust most." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「友|とも」 is friend, and 「だち」 is a softened form of the group ending 「たち」, so the word once meant a band of friends. The plural feel has faded, and now it works for one friend too.",
      "Use 「と」 to do things with a friend and 「に」 with 「なる」 to become friends, as in 「ともだちになりましょう」. Skip my, because plain 「ともだち」 already sounds natural.",
      "The Chinese reading 「ゆう」 gives 「友人|ゆうじん」, a formal friend, and 「親友|しんゆう」, a best friend. The kanji pictures two hands working together, a lovely image of friendship."
    ],
    ja: "メアリーさんは[友だち|ともだち]です。",
    en: "Mary is my friend." },
  "にほん|日本": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「日|に」 is the sun, and 「本|ほん」 means root or origin. So the name means the origin of the sun. Seen from China, Japan lies to the east, where the sun comes up, which is why it's called the land of the rising sun. The shapes help here. 「日|に」 began as a picture of the sun, a circle with a dot in the middle, later squared off. 「本|ほん」 is the tree kanji, 「木|き」, with an extra line marking its base, the root. Notice that 「日|に」 is a shortened reading. It's normally 「にち」, as in 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, and 「日曜日|にちようび」, Sunday. And 「本|ほん」 is the same kanji as book, 「本|ほん」, and bookstore, 「本屋|ほんや」. You may also hear the country called 「にっぽん」. Both are correct. 「にっぽん」 sounds a bit stronger and shows up on stamps, money, and at sports events. To recap, the root of the sun, the land where the sun rises." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The name of the country has an interesting history. In early Chinese records, the islands were called by a different name, read 「わ」 in Japanese. Around the late six hundreds and early seven hundreds, the Japanese court began using the new name, written with the sun and the origin. It was a proud choice, since it put Japan at the place where the sun comes from. For a long time, people read those kanji in more than one way, 「にほん」 and 「にっぽん」. Even today, the government has never fixed a single official reading. In two thousand nine, it formally said both are fine. The English word Japan also comes from these same two kanji. A southern Chinese pronunciation traveled through Malay and Portuguese traders before reaching Europe. So Japan, 「にほん」, and 「にっぽん」 are really three versions of one name." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a funny pair that trips up learners. The country, 「日本|にほん」, sounds exactly like 「二本|にほん」, two long thin things. That's because the kanji 「本|ほん」 doubles as a counter for things like pens, bottles, and umbrellas. And the sound of the counter changes with the number. One is 「いっぽん」, two is 「にほん」, and three is 「さんぼん」. So if you ask for two bottles at a shop, you'll say the same sounds as the name of Japan. Context always makes it clear. The first kanji is just as flexible. 「日|ひ」 alone means sun or day. It becomes 「び」 at the end of day names like 「月曜日|げつようび」, Monday. And it's 「か」 in dates, as in 「三日|みっか」, the third day of the month. So the two kanji in the country's name each have several voices." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The idea of the sun is right there in the national flag. The flag is a red circle on a white background, and its everyday name is 「日の丸|ひのまる」, the circle of the sun. It fits the country's name perfectly, the land at the origin of the sun. You will also see the country's name used as a label for anything traditionally Japanese. Japanese cuisine is 「日本料理|にほんりょうり」. Rice wine, which English speakers call sake, is usually 「日本酒|にほんしゅ」 in Japan, because 「さけ」 alone can mean any alcohol. To describe a thing as Japanese, connect it with 「の」, as in 「にほんのくるま」, a Japanese car. So whether it's the flag, the food, or the drink, the sun is never far from the name." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「日|に」 is the sun and 「本|ほん」 is root or origin, so the name means the origin of the sun, the land of the rising sun. Both 「にほん」 and the stronger 「にっぽん」 are correct.",
      "The Japanese court adopted the sun and origin name around the late six hundreds, and both readings are still officially fine. Even the English word Japan comes from these same two kanji via traders.",
      "「日本|にほん」 sounds exactly like 「二本|にほん」, two long thin things, because 「本|ほん」 is also a counter. Context always makes it clear which one you mean.",
      "The flag, 「日の丸|ひのまる」, is a circle of the sun that matches the name. Add the country to make Japanese things, like 「日本料理|にほんりょうり」 or 「にほんのくるま」."
    ],
    ja: "たけしさんは[日本|にほん]の{学生|がくせい}です。",
    en: "Takeshi is a student in Japan." },
  "りゅうがくせい|留学生": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one is built from a word you already know. 「留|りゅう」 means to stay, or to remain somewhere, and here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. Then comes 「学生|がくせい」, student. So it's a student who stays in another country in order to learn. Drop the last kanji and add the verb ending, and you get 「留学する|りゅうがくする」, to study abroad. So if you go to Japan for a year of school, you are doing 「りゅうがく」, and you are a 「りゅうがくせい」. Inside the word, the 「学|がく」 is the same learning as in 「大学|だいがく」 and 「学校|がっこう」, and the 「生|せい」 is the same person as in 「先生|せんせい」. Japanese universities have international student offices and dorms just for 「りゅうがくせい」. In Genki, Mary is one. When you introduce yourself in Japan, this might be the word that describes you. To recap, stay plus student, the student who stays abroad to study." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「留|りゅう」, is the only new piece in this word, so let's get to know it. At the bottom you'll find the field kanji, 「田|た」, the square divided into four. That makes it easy to spot. Its core idea is to stay, to stop, or to hold something in place. Besides 「りゅう」, it has a second Chinese-origin reading, 「る」. You'll meet that one in 「留守|るす」, being away from home, when nobody is in. An answering machine is 「留守番電話|るすばんでんわ」, the phone that keeps watch while you're out. So in one word the kanji means staying abroad, and in the other it means the house is staying empty while you're out. Both come back to the same idea, remaining in one place. Remember the field at the bottom, and the two readings 「りゅう」 and 「る」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you study in Japan, you'll use this word about yourself constantly, so here are the natural patterns. To say where you're from, use 「から」. 「アメリカからきたりゅうがくせいです」, I'm an international student who came from America. To say you're studying abroad right now, use the verb. 「にほんにりゅうがくしています」, I'm studying abroad in Japan. An exchange program is 「交換留学|こうかんりゅうがく」, where two schools swap students. Many 「りゅうがくせい」 live with a host family, a 「ホームステイ」, which is a great way to practice Japanese at the dinner table. Others live in dorms with students from many countries. A tiny dialogue. 「りゅうがくせいですか」. 「はい、いちねんかんりゅうがくしています」. Are you an international student? Yes, I'm studying here for a year. So whether you're introducing yourself or explaining your plans, this word and its verb will carry you a long way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「留|りゅう」 means to stay, added to 「学生|がくせい」, so it's a student who stays abroad to learn. Drop the last kanji and add 「する」 to get 「留学する|りゅうがくする」, to study abroad.",
      "Spot 「留|りゅう」 by the field 「田|た」 at its bottom, and remember its core idea of staying put. Its other reading 「る」 shows up in 「留守|るす」, when nobody is home.",
      "Say where you came from with 「から」, as in 「アメリカからきたりゅうがくせいです」, and use the verb for right now, 「にほんにりゅうがくしています」. Many students live with a host family or in dorms."
    ],
    ja: "メアリーさんはアメリカの[留学生|りゅうがくせい]です。",
    en: "Mary is an international student from America." },
  "とけい|時計": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「時|と」 is the kanji for time, and 「計|けい」 means to measure or count. So a clock or a watch is a time measurer. The reading is the unusual part. On its own, this kanji is normally read 「じ」, as in 「時間|じかん」, time, or 「とき」, as in 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes. It is never 「と」 except in this word. That's because the word 「とけい」 is older than this spelling. The kanji were picked later because their meanings fit, so treat the whole word as one special reading. 「計|けい」 itself has its normal Chinese-origin reading. It's the same idea as measuring or calculating. One more point. Japanese uses one word for both a clock on the wall and a watch on your wrist. If you need to be specific, a wristwatch is 「うでどけい」, an arm clock, where the sound softens to 「ど」. To recap, the time measurer, with a special reading you just have to learn." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "We know the word is older than its spelling, but where did it come from? The usual explanation traces it back to an ancient Chinese instrument for measuring shadows, used to track the sun and the seasons. Its name was written with kanji for earth and a kind of jade tablet, and it was read 「とけい」 in Japan. Over the centuries, as mechanical clocks arrived, the old sound stayed, but people tried out different kanji spellings for it. In the end, time plus measure won, because it described a clock so perfectly. Kanji chosen like this, to fit a word that already existed, are common in Japanese, and they explain many odd readings. So when a reading seems to break all the rules, it's often because the word came first and the kanji came later. Think of this word as a sound from the age of sundials, wearing a modern kanji outfit." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people actually talk about clocks and watches in daily life. To look at the clock, say 「とけいをみます」. If your watch stopped, it's 「とけいがとまりました」. If a clock is fast, Japanese says it's advancing, 「すすんでいます」. If it's slow, it's 「おくれています」, running late. An alarm clock is 「目覚まし時計|めざましどけい」, the clock that wakes your eyes. Notice the sound softens to 「ど」 again, just like in wristwatch. And here's a point learners miss. To ask the time, you don't mention the clock at all. Just say 「いま、なんじですか」. A tiny exchange at the station might go like this. 「あのとけい、すすんでいますよ」. 「えっ、ほんとうですか」. That clock is fast, you know. What, really? So remember advancing for fast, late for slow, and a softened 「ど」 whenever another word comes in front." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick that ties the sound to the meaning. Picture a giant, grumpy alarm clock standing at the end of your bed. Every morning at six, instead of ringing, it stomps over and steps right on your big toe. You leap up shouting, toe, okay, okay, I'm up! Toe, okay. 「とけい」. Now add the kanji. On the left of the first kanji is the sun, the original clock in the sky. On the left of the second kanji is the speech radical, the clock's loud voice yelling at you. So the sun tells the time, the voice shouts it out, and your poor toe takes the hit. Every time you see a clock from now on, wiggle your toes and say 「とけい」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「時|と」 means time and 「計|けい」 means measure, so it's a time measurer. The 「と」 reading appears only here, because the word is older than its kanji spelling.",
      "The sound traces back to an ancient Chinese shadow-measuring instrument used to track the sun. As clocks arrived, the old sound stayed, and time plus measure won out as the spelling.",
      "Say a clock is advancing, 「すすんでいます」, when fast and 「おくれています」 when slow. The sound softens to 「ど」 after another word, as in 「目覚まし時計|めざましどけい」.",
      "Picture a grumpy alarm clock stomping on your toe each morning while you yell toe, okay, which gives you 「とけい」. The sun on one kanji tells the time, and the speech radical on the other shouts it."
    ],
    ja: "わたしの[時計|とけい]はセイコーです。",
    en: "My watch is a Seiko." },
  "でんわ|電話": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「電|でん」 means electricity. The top of the kanji is the rain kanji, 「雨|あめ」, because 「電|でん」 started out meaning lightning, the electricity in a storm. 「話|わ」 means talk. Both are Chinese-origin readings here. So a telephone is electric talk. Very logical. 「電|でん」 leads a big family of modern words. 「電車|でんしゃ」 is a train, an electric car. 「電気|でんき」 is electricity, or the lights. 「電池|でんち」 is a battery, an electric pond. And 「話|わ」 is the same kanji as the verb 「話す|はなす」, to talk, where it takes its native reading. To make a phone call, add the verb ending to get 「電話する|でんわする」. Or say 「でんわをかけます」. Your phone number is 「でんわばんごう」. A note for today: people say 「けいたい」 or 「スマホ」 for a cell phone, but 「でんわ」 is still the general word for a phone call. To recap, electricity plus talk gives you the telephone." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Phone calls come with their own set phrases in Japanese. When you pick up, you say 「もしもし」, the classic hello used only on the phone. To call a person, mark them with 「に」. 「ともだちにでんわします」, I'll call my friend. A very common mistake is using 「を」 for the person. The 「を」 goes with the call itself, as in 「でんわをかけます」. To answer the phone, Japanese says you come out to it, 「でんわにでます」. Phone manners are a big deal in Japan. On trains, people set phones to silent mode, called 「マナーモード」, manner mode, and talking on the phone in a train car is frowned upon. A short call might go, 「もしもし、たけしさんですか」. 「はい、そうです」. Hello, is this Takeshi? Yes, it is. So say 「もしもし」 to start, use 「に」 for the person, and keep it quiet on the train." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the second kanji, 「話|わ」, because its parts tell a clear story. On the left is the speech radical, the same piece you'll find in 「語|ご」, language, and in 「読む|よむ」, to read. Whenever you see that piece on the left, expect a meaning about words or speaking. On the right is the kanji for tongue, 「舌|した」. So you have speech plus tongue. A very fitting picture for talking. The kanji has three readings to know. Here it's the Chinese-origin 「わ」. With its native readings, it's the verb 「話す|はなす」, to speak, and the noun 「話|はなし」, a talk or a story. And in the first kanji, the rain on top, as you know, points to lightning, the old meaning of electricity. So the full word is a storm's power carrying your tongue's speech. Look for the speech radical, and you'll start spotting talking words everywhere." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「電|でん」 means electricity, with rain on top because it first meant lightning, and 「話|わ」 means talk. So a telephone is simply electric talk.",
      "Answer with 「もしもし」 and mark the person you call with 「に」, as in 「ともだちにでんわします」. Keep it quiet on trains, with your phone in 「マナーモード」.",
      "「話|わ」 is the speech radical plus 「舌|した」, the tongue, a perfect picture of talking. Natively it reads 「話す|はなす」, to speak, and 「話|はなし」, a story."
    ],
    ja: "[電話|でんわ]{番号|ばんごう}はなんですか。",
    en: "What is your phone number?" },
  "ほん|本": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is a nice one to picture. Start with the tree kanji, 「木|き」. Add a short line across the bottom of the trunk, marking the base. That gives you 「本|ほん」, which first meant root or origin. From there it grew other meanings, including the real thing and book. The reading here is the Chinese-origin one, 「ほん」. You've already seen the root meaning in 「日本|にほん」, the origin of the sun. The real thing meaning shows up in 「本当|ほんとう」, truth, as in 「本当ですか|ほんとうですか」, really? A bookstore is 「本屋|ほんや」, a book shop. And later you'll use 「本|ほん」 as a counter for long thin things like pens, bottles, and umbrellas. That use goes back to the tree trunk shape. A way to remember it: books are made of paper, and paper comes from trees. To recap, a tree with its root marked, which became the word for book." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's a twist. This kanji is the word for book, but books are not counted with it. Books use a different counter, 「冊|さつ」. Two books are 「にさつ」. Instead, 「本|ほん」 counts long, thin things. Pens, bottles, umbrellas, trees, even bananas. Its sound changes with the number, so listen carefully. One is 「いっぽん」. Two is 「にほん」. Three is 「さんぼん」. Four is 「よんほん」, six is 「ろっぽん」, eight is 「はっぽん」, and ten is 「じゅっぽん」. To ask how many, say 「なんぼん」. The three sounds, 「ほん」, 「ぽん」 and 「ぼん」, follow the same pattern you'll see with other counters later. And some surprising things count as long and thin, too. Movies, phone calls, and even home runs use this counter. So count your pens with 「ほん」, but your books with 「さつ」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan is a nation of keen readers, and bookstores there have some charming habits. When you buy a book, the clerk will often ask if you'd like a cover, 「カバーをおかけしますか」. They then fold a paper cover around your book by hand, often printed with the store's logo. It keeps the book clean and hides what you're reading on the train. Many novels come out in a small pocket paperback size, called 「文庫本|ぶんこぼん」. Notice the sound softens to 「ぼん」 in that compound. These fit in a coat pocket, which is perfect for long commutes. Comics, or 「マンガ」, take up whole floors in big bookstores, and adults read them openly. Standing in a shop and reading without buying even has its own word, 「立ち読み|たちよみ」, standing reading. So if you buy a book in Japan, say yes to the paper cover and enjoy it on the train." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「本|ほん」 is the tree 「木|き」 with a line marking its root, so it first meant origin and later book. Books are made of paper, and paper comes from trees.",
      "Books are counted with 「冊|さつ」, while 「本|ほん」 counts long thin things like pens and bottles. Its sound shifts with the number, as in 「いっぽん」, 「にほん」, 「さんぼん」.",
      "Japanese bookstores often wrap your purchase in a paper cover, and pocket paperbacks called 「文庫本|ぶんこぼん」 suit long commutes. Reading in the shop without buying is 「立ち読み|たちよみ」."
    ],
    ja: "これはわたしの[本|ほん]です。",
    en: "This is my book." },
  "いま|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, written with the kanji 「今|いま」. It's one of the most useful time words you'll have. The same kanji appears in lots of words about the present. 「今日|きょう」 is today, 「今晩|こんばん」 is tonight, 「今週|こんしゅう」 is this week, and 「今年|ことし」 is this year. Notice how the reading changes from word to word, but the meaning of this, or now, stays. A common mistake for English speakers is to add a particle. With times like three o'clock you say 「さんじに」, but with 「いま」 you don't add 「に」. Just say 「いま、なんじですか」, what time is it now? 「いま」 can also mean just now or right away, as in 「いまいきます」, I'm coming now. A way to remember it: picture someone tapping a watch and saying, it's time, mate, the time is now. To recap, the plain word for now, used without a particle." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This little word combines with particles to make a handful of very useful phrases. 「いまから」 means from now on, or starting now. 「いまからべんきょうします」, I'm going to study starting now. 「いままで」 means until now, so far. 「いまでも」 means even now, still. And here's a lovely surprise. The greeting you say when you come home, 「ただいま」, contains this word too. It literally means just now, short for a longer phrase like I have just now returned. The family answers with 「おかえりなさい」, welcome back. You can also put the topic marker on it for contrast. 「いまはひまです」, right now, I'm free, hinting that later you might not be. So with a particle after it, this one short word can point to the future, the past, or the moment you walk in the door." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "One of the most polite and useful phrases with this word is 「いま、いいですか」. It means, is now a good time? You use it before you start talking to someone who might be busy, like a teacher in their office or a coworker at a desk. It's like knocking on a door with words. The other person might say 「はい、どうぞ」, yes, go ahead, or 「すみません、いまはちょっと」, sorry, now is a bit difficult. That trailing 「ちょっと」 is a soft way of saying no without saying it. When someone calls you from another room, the natural reply is 「いまいきます」, I'm coming now. And on the phone, 「いまどこですか」 means, where are you right now? So before you interrupt anyone in Japan, try 「いま、いいですか」 and watch how warmly people respond." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「今|いま」 shows up in present-time words like 「今日|きょう」 and 「今年|ことし」. Never add 「に」 after it, so just say 「いま、なんじですか」.",
      "Add particles for handy phrases: 「いまから」, from now, and 「いままで」, until now. It even hides in the greeting 「ただいま」, I've just now come home.",
      "Ask 「いま、いいですか」, is now a good time, before interrupting someone, like knocking with words. A reply of 「いまはちょっと」 is a soft no."
    ],
    ja: "[いま]、{何時|なんじ}ですか。",
    en: "What time is it now?" },
  "えいご|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「英語|えいご」. 「英|えい」 stands for England, and 「語|ご」 means language or word. Both use Chinese-origin readings. Why does 「英|えい」 stand for England? Long ago, foreign names were spelled with kanji chosen for their sounds, and the spelling for England began with 「英|えい」. That first kanji became the short name, so Britain is also called 「英国|えいこく」. The kanji itself means something like excellent or flower, which is a nice bonus. 「語|ご」 is the real workhorse here. Add it to a country name and you get that country's language. 「にほんご」 is Japanese, 「ちゅうごくご」 is Chinese, and 「がいこくご」 is a foreign language. A common mistake: 「えいご」 is only the language. An English person is 「イギリスじん」, and an American is 「アメリカじん」. Both of them speak 「えいご」, of course. To recap, England plus language gives you English." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "As an English speaker, you'll talk about English a lot in Japan, so here are the key patterns. To say you speak in English, use the particle 「で」, which marks the tool or means. 「えいごではなします」, I speak in English. To ask how to say something in English, try 「えいごでなんですか」, what is it in English? To say someone understands English, use 「が」 with the verb for understand. 「えいごがわかりますか」. Many learners say 「えいごをわかります」, but that sounds wrong to a Japanese ear. It's always 「が」 with that verb. People will also say 「えいごがじょうずですね」, your English is good, to Japanese friends who speak it. And sometimes a Japanese person will want to practice their English on you, so be patient and kind. So remember 「で」 for in English, and 「が」 when you understand it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "English holds a special place in Japanese life. Students study it for years, starting in elementary school. Since twenty twenty, it has been a formal subject in the fifth and sixth grades. Because the lessons focused for a long time on reading and grammar for exams, many Japanese people read English better than they speak it. English also floods into Japanese as katakana loanwords. You've already met 「コンピューター」 and 「ビジネス」. But be careful. Some katakana words look English and aren't. Japanese calls these made-in-Japan English words, 「和製英語|わせいえいご」. For example, 「サラリーマン」 means an office worker, not someone who earns a salary in general. So English is everywhere in Japan, but sometimes it has taken on a life of its own. When a katakana word seems familiar, double-check the meaning before you use it." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「語|ご」 doesn't only make language names. It shows up in other words about words and speech. 「外国語|がいこくご」 is a foreign language, from outside country plus language. 「敬語|けいご」 is honorific language, the respectful speech you'll study later in Genki. The first kanji means respect. 「物語|ものがたり」 means a tale or story, and here the kanji takes its native reading, 「かたり」, from the verb to tell. A famous example is the old classic, 「源氏物語|げんじものがたり」, The Tale of Genji. The left side of 「語|ご」 is the speech radical, which you'll recognize in 「話|わ」 and 「読む|よむ」. So the family is easy to spot by sight. Speech on the left, and a meaning about words. Once you know this kanji, everything from languages to polite speech to old stories becomes easier to read." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「英|えい」 was chosen for the sound of England, and 「語|ご」 means language, so England plus language gives English. Remember that an English person is 「イギリスじん」, not 「えいご」.",
      "Use 「で」 for in English, as in 「えいごではなします」, and always 「が」 with understand, as in 「えいごがわかりますか」. Saying 「えいごをわかります」 sounds wrong to a Japanese ear.",
      "English floods Japanese as katakana loanwords, but some are made in Japan, called 「和製英語|わせいえいご」. 「サラリーマン」, for example, means an office worker, so double-check familiar-looking words.",
      "「語|ご」 also builds 「外国語|がいこくご」, 「敬語|けいご」, and 「物語|ものがたり」, a tale. Look for the speech radical on its left, a sign of words and speaking."
    ],
    ja: "わたしの{専攻|せんこう}は[えいご]です。",
    en: "My major is English." },
  "にほんご|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is a perfect example of the country plus 「語|ご」 pattern. Start with 「日本|にほん」, Japan, the origin of the sun. Add 「語|ご」, language, read with its Chinese-origin reading. Japan's language. Once you know this pattern, you can make many languages yourself. 「えいご」 is English, 「ちゅうごくご」 is Chinese, 「かんこくご」 is Korean, and 「がいこくご」 is any foreign language. The same 「日本|にほん」 starts other words too. 「にほんじん」 is a Japanese person, using 「じん」, person, instead of 「ご」, language. So keep these two endings straight. 「ご」 is the language, 「じん」 is the people. Inside Japan, schools often call the subject 「こくご」, the national language, rather than 「にほんご」. 「にほんご」 is the name used when it's taught as a foreign language, which is exactly what you're doing. To recap, Japan plus language gives you Japanese." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the sentences you'll use most when people find out you're learning Japanese. To say you understand a little, use 「にほんごがすこしわかります」. To ask someone to speak Japanese with you, you can say 「にほんごでおねがいします」, in Japanese, please. Very soon, people will compliment you. 「にほんごがじょうずですね」, your Japanese is good! Even after a single 「こんにちは」, you may hear it. It's a kind encouragement. The classic modest reply is 「いいえ、まだまだです」, no, I still have a long way to go. Accepting the praise directly, with a simple thank you, can sound a little proud in Japanese, so this humble answer works well. To ask what a word means, say 「これはにほんごでなんですか」, what is this in Japanese? So learn the humble reply now, because you're going to need it often." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The Japanese language is famous for using three writing systems at once. Kanji came from China over a thousand years ago. Japanese people borrowed the characters and fitted them to their own words. Then, around the ninth century, two simpler scripts grew out of kanji. Hiragana came from flowing, cursive kanji shapes. Katakana came from small pieces of kanji, used to note down sounds. Today they each have a job. Kanji carry meaning, hiragana handle grammar endings and particles, and katakana spell loanwords and foreign names. A single everyday sentence often mixes all three scripts side by side. On top of that, Japanese has no spaces between words, and the scripts themselves help your eye find the word breaks. So when you write this word, you're stepping into a writing system shaped over more than a thousand years." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen to the rhythm of this word. It has four beats, not three. 「に」, 「ほ」, 「ん」, 「ご」. In Japanese, the 「ん」 sound counts as a full beat of its own, and it takes as much time as any other syllable. English speakers tend to swallow it, making the word sound like two quick parts. Try tapping it out on the table, one tap per beat. This beat counting matters everywhere. It's why 「にほんご」 has four beats, and 「にほんじん」 has five. It's also how Japanese poetry works. A haiku counts these beats, five, seven, and five. A good practice trick is to say the word slowly, like a robot, then speed it up while keeping every beat even. So give the 「ん」 its full time, and your Japanese will instantly sound more natural." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japan plus 「語|ご」, language, gives Japanese, and the same pattern makes 「えいご」 or 「かんこくご」. Keep 「ご」 for the language and 「じん」 for the people.",
      "People will praise 「にほんごがじょうずですね」 after even one word. Reply humbly with 「いいえ、まだまだです」, since a simple thank you can sound a little proud.",
      "Japanese mixes three scripts at once: kanji carry meaning, hiragana handle grammar, and katakana spell loanwords. With no spaces between words, the scripts help your eye find word breaks.",
      "The word has four beats, because 「ん」 counts as a full beat of its own. Give the 「ん」 its full time instead of swallowing it, and you'll sound more natural."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは[にほんご]の{学生|がくせい}です。",
    en: "I'm a student of Japanese." },
  "こうこう|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「高校|こうこう」. 「高|こう」 means high or tall, and 「校|こう」 means school. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings, and they happen to sound the same, which is why the word sounds like an echo. It really is high school, just as in English. The word is actually a short form of a longer official name, 「こうとうがっこう」, which means higher level school. People almost always use the short version. You know 「校|こう」 already from 「学校|がっこう」, school. And 「高|こう」 has a native reading, 「高い|たかい」, which means tall or expensive, as in 「背が高い|せがたかい」, to be tall. Add 「生|せい」, person, and you get 「こうこうせい」, a high school student. A culture note: Japanese high school takes three years and usually requires an entrance exam, unlike many American schools. To recap, high plus school, an easy one." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This word is all long vowels, and that's exactly where English speakers slip. It has four beats. 「こ」, 「う」, 「こ」, 「う」. Each 「う」 here just stretches the o sound. If you shorten it, you get 「ここ」, which means here. So, I teach at a high school could turn into, I teach at here. Hold each o sound for two beats, and the word stays a school. There's also a true sound-alike with a very different meaning, 「孝行|こうこう」, devotion to your parents. You'll usually hear it as 「親孝行|おやこうこう」, being a good son or daughter. A memory trick for keeping them apart is to imagine a high school student who pays for a family dinner. That's 「おやこうこう」 from a 「こうこうせい」. Context almost always tells you which one you're hearing. So stretch both vowels, and never let high school shrink down to here." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese high school has some iconic features you'll see in every anime and drama. Most students wear a school uniform, 「せいふく」. Boys often wear dark jackets, and girls wear blazers or sailor-style tops. After class, students throw themselves into club activities, 「ぶかつ」. Sports clubs may practice every day, even on weekends. The most famous high school event of all is the national baseball tournament, held at a stadium called 「甲子園|こうしえん」. Every summer, teams from across the country compete, and the whole nation watches on TV. Players who lose famously scoop up some of the stadium's dirt to take home as a keepsake. High school isn't required by law in Japan, but nearly everyone attends. So when you hear the word, picture uniforms, clubs, and summer baseball." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to other useful words. The school kanji, 「校|こう」, has the tree radical on its left. You'll find it in 「学校|がっこう」, school, and in 「校長|こうちょう」, the principal, literally the head of the school. Your old school, the one you graduated from, is 「母校|ぼこう」, your mother school, a nice match for the English alma mater, which means nourishing mother. The high kanji, 「高|こう」, gives you a word you'll hear constantly among young people, 「最高|さいこう」. It means the highest, and people shout it to say something is the best, or awesome. With its native reading, it's the adjective 「高い|たかい」, which covers both tall and expensive. Be careful, a 「たかいがっこう」 means an expensive school, not a high school. So high and school together make the school, but each half has its own lively family." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「高|こう」 means high and 「校|こう」 means school, so it's literally high school. It's a short form of 「こうとうがっこう」, and adding 「生|せい」 gives a high school student.",
      "Hold both long vowels for four beats, or high school shrinks to 「ここ」, here. A true sound-alike is 「親孝行|おやこうこう」, being devoted to your parents.",
      "Japanese high school means uniforms called 「せいふく」 and club activities called 「ぶかつ」. The big event is summer baseball at 「甲子園|こうしえん」, watched by the whole nation.",
      "「校|こう」 appears in 「校長|こうちょう」, principal, and 「母校|ぼこう」, alma mater, while 「高|こう」 gives 「最高|さいこう」, awesome. Careful, 「たかいがっこう」 means an expensive school."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[こうこう]の{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "My older brother is a high school teacher." },
  "ごご|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「午後|ごご」. The first kanji, 「午|ご」, refers to noon. The second, 「後|ご」, means after. Both use Chinese-origin readings. So the word is literally after noon, exactly the same idea as P.M. Why does 「午|ご」 mean noon? In the old system, the day was split into twelve two-hour blocks named after the twelve zodiac animals. The block of the horse, written with this kanji, was centered on midday. So noon became the hour of the horse. The second kanji, 「後|ご」, is the same one as in 「あとで」, later on. And you'll meet it as an ending that means later, as in 「三日後|みっかご」, three days later. The big grammar point is word order. In English, P.M. comes after the time. In Japanese, 「ごご」 comes first. So three P.M. is 「ごごさんじ」. Its partner is 「ごぜん」, A.M., before noon. To recap, after the hour of the horse, that is, after noon." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how this word fits into everyday Japanese time talk. It can stand alone as in the afternoon. 「ごごはじゅぎょうがあります」, I have class in the afternoon. Adding 「は」 makes it a contrast, as if to say, in the morning I'm free, but in the afternoon I have class. Japanese splits the day a little differently from English. Midday, around lunch, is 「ひる」. Late afternoon and early evening is 「ゆうがた」. And night is 「よる」. So 「ごご」 is the clock word, while those others describe the feel of the day. On train timetables and official schedules, Japan often uses the twenty-four-hour clock instead. So three P.M. may appear as 「じゅうごじ」, fifteen o'clock. In conversation, though, people say 「ごごさんじ」. So say 「ごご」 in speech, but be ready for fifteen o'clock on a timetable." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「後|ご」, the after kanji, is a busy one with several readings. Here it's the Chinese-origin 「ご」. You'll also see it in 「最後|さいご」, the very last, the most after of all. With a native reading, it's 「あと」, after or later, as in 「あとで」, later on. Another native reading is 「うしろ」, behind or at the back, as in 「いえのうしろ」, behind the house. And in weather forecasts you'll hear 「のち」, meaning and later. A forecast like sunny, later rain uses it. All of these share one idea, coming after, whether in time or in space. So afternoon is the time after the noon hour, and behind is the space after the front. Hold onto that one idea, and the four readings will start to feel like one family." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick that ties the sound to the meaning. Picture an office after lunch. Everyone is sleepy, slumped over their desks. Then the boss bursts in, clapping and shouting, go, go! Back to work! It's the afternoon! Go, go. 「ごご」. Now add the kanji. The first one, the noon kanji, looks a bit like a little signpost at midday, with a cross bar near the top. The second one, the after kanji, is busy and crowded, like the busy afternoon crowd heading back to work. So whenever you think of the afternoon, hear that boss shouting go, go. And remember to put it before the hour, 「ごごいちじ」, one P.M., just as the boss yells go before the work starts." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「午|ご」 means noon, the old hour of the horse, and 「後|ご」 means after, so it's literally after noon. Unlike English, it comes before the time, as in 「ごごさんじ」.",
      "「ごご」 is the clock word, while 「ひる」, 「ゆうがた」 and 「よる」 describe the feel of the day. Timetables often use the twenty-four-hour clock, like 「じゅうごじ」 for three P.M.",
      "「後|ご」 has several readings, like 「あと」, later, 「うしろ」, behind, and 「のち」 in forecasts. They all share one idea, coming after in time or space.",
      "Picture a boss bursting into a sleepy office after lunch shouting go, go, which gives you 「ごご」. He yells go before the work starts, just as 「ごご」 goes before the hour."
    ],
    ja: "いま[ごご]{三時|さんじ}です。",
    en: "It's three P.M. now." },
  "ごぜん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「午前|ごぜん」. The first kanji, 「午|ご」, refers to noon, and the second, 「前|ぜん」, means before. Both are Chinese-origin readings here. So it's literally before noon, the same idea as A.M. The noon kanji has a story. In the old Japanese way of telling time, the day was divided into twelve two-hour blocks, each named for one of the zodiac animals. The horse's block sat right around midday, and this is the horse's kanji. So 「ごぜん」 is before the hour of the horse. The second kanji, 「前|ぜん」, also has a native reading, 「まえ」, which means front or before. You'll see that one in 「なまえ」, name. As with 「ごご」, the word order flips compared to English. You put 「ごぜん」 first, then the time. So nine A.M. is 「ごぜんくじ」. Keep the pair together in your head: 「ごぜん」 is before noon, 「ごご」 is after noon. To recap, before the horse hour, that is, the morning." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「前|ぜん」, gives you several handy time words. Add the word for middle, and you get 「午前中|ごぜんちゅう」, during the morning, any time before noon. You'll hear it a lot for deliveries and appointments. On its own, with the native reading 「まえ」, it can come after a time to mean before. So 「さんじごふんまえ」 means five minutes to three. That's exactly how Japanese says five to three, using the before word after the time. You'll also hear 「まえ」 for location, as in 「えきのまえ」, in front of the station. And it's the second half of 「名前|なまえ」, name. So one kanji can mean before in time and in front in space. Next time you schedule something, try 「ごぜんちゅう」, sometime in the morning, and you'll sound very natural." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There's a difference between this word and the everyday word for morning, 「あさ」. 「あさ」 is a feeling of the day, the early hours when you wake up and have breakfast. 「ごぜん」 is a clock word. It covers everything from midnight up to noon. So two in the morning, when it's dark and you're asleep, is 「ごぜんにじ」. Nobody would call that 「あさ」. Midnight itself is 「ごぜんれいじ」, zero o'clock A.M. And noon exactly has its own word, 「正午|しょうご」, the true noon, which you'll hear in news reports. In schedules, you'll often see 「ごぜんちゅう」, meaning any time before noon. A delivery driver might ask, 「ごぜんちゅうはいかがですか」, how about sometime in the morning? So use 「あさ」 for the feeling, and 「ごぜん」 when you're reading the clock." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick that ties the sound to the meaning. Picture a monk at a quiet mountain temple, early in the day. Before noon, every single morning, he says to himself, time to go Zen. He sits down, closes his eyes, and meditates until the noon bell rings. Go Zen. 「ごぜん」. Before noon is the Zen time. After the bell, he jumps up, and it's time to go, go, back to work. That's the afternoon, 「ごご」. So the morning is calm and Zen, and the afternoon is busy and go, go. Keep this little monk in your mind, and the pair will never get swapped." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「午|ご」 is noon, the hour of the horse, and 「前|ぜん」 means before, so it's literally before noon. Like 「ごご」, it goes first, as in 「ごぜんくじ」.",
      "Add the middle kanji to get 「午前中|ごぜんちゅう」, any time in the morning, handy for deliveries. As 「まえ」 after a time it means before, as in 「さんじごふんまえ」, five to three.",
      "「あさ」 is the feeling of morning, while 「ごぜん」 is a clock word from midnight to noon. So two at night is 「ごぜんにじ」, never 「あさ」.",
      "Picture a monk who sits to go Zen every morning before the noon bell, giving 「ごぜん」. After the bell, it's go, go, the afternoon 「ごご」."
    ],
    ja: "いま[ごぜん]{九時|くじ}です。",
    en: "It's nine A.M. now." },
  "せんこう|専攻": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. 「専|せん」 means exclusive, or to specialize, to give yourself to one thing only. 「攻|こう」 means to attack, and by extension to work hard at something or to study it closely. Put them together and your major is the one subject you specialize in and go after with full force. A way to remember it: picture yourself charging at one subject on the battlefield, while all the other subjects watch from the side. This word is a key part of the Genki self-introduction. You'll ask 「せんこうはなんですか」, what's your major? And you'll answer with 「わたしのせんこうは」 plus a subject, like 「けいざい」, economics, 「れきし」, history, or 「にほんご」. Note that it's a noun, not a verb, so you say what your major is, using 「です」. In casual conversation, people may also ask what department or faculty you belong to. To recap, specialize plus attack, the subject you charge at." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how a real conversation about majors flows between students. 「せんこうはなんですか」. What's your major? 「けいざいです。〜さんは」. Economics. And you? 「れきしです」. History. Notice how the second speaker bounces the question back with just a name and 「は」. It's a very natural, short way to say, and you? In more formal speech, the word can also become a verb with 「する」. 「にほんごをせんこうしています」, I am majoring in Japanese. Japanese universities are organized into faculties, 「学部|がくぶ」, like the faculty of economics or the faculty of letters. People often name their faculty rather than their exact major. After a few months in Japan, you'll probably hear, 「なにをべんきょうしていますか」, what are you studying? So be ready with your subject, and remember to bounce the question back." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Each kanji in this word leads to other useful words. The first kanji, 「専|せん」, appears in 「専門|せんもん」, a specialty or field of expertise. A vocational school, where people train for one career, is 「専門学校|せんもんがっこう」, a specialty school. So your 「せんこう」 is what you study, and your 「せんもん」 is the field you become expert in. The second kanji, 「攻|こう」, has a right side called the striking hand radical. It appears in many kanji about action, and it gives this one its sense of attack. With its native reading, it becomes the verb 「攻める|せめる」, to attack, a word you'll hear in sports and games. And in sports commentary, you'll hear 「攻撃|こうげき」, attack, and its opposite, defense. So this word quietly borrows the language of strategy. You specialize and you attack your subject." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Japanese has many words that sound exactly alike, and this one has a famous twin. 「線香|せんこう」 means incense sticks, the thin sticks people burn at Buddhist altars and graves. They have a strong, smoky smell, and you'll see them in temples all over Japan. So, in theory, 「せんこうはなんですか」 could be a very strange question about incense! In practice, context makes everything clear. On campus, nobody is asking about incense. Here's a memory trick to keep them apart. Picture a student so devoted to their major that they burn incense to it every night, praying for good grades. Their 「せんこう」 is their 「せんこう」. This kind of sound-alike is common in Japanese, because so many words borrowed Chinese sounds. So when you hear a familiar sound, let the situation tell you which word it is." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「専|せん」 means to specialize and 「攻|こう」 means to attack, so your major is the one subject you go after with full force. Ask 「せんこうはなんですか」 and answer with 「です」.",
      "Answer the major question, then bounce it back with just a name and 「は」. More formally, use the verb, as in 「にほんごをせんこうしています」.",
      "「専|せん」 also gives 「専門|せんもん」, a specialty, and 「攻|こう」 has the striking hand radical for action. So the word borrows the language of strategy: you specialize and attack your subject.",
      "「せんこう」 has a famous twin, 「線香|せんこう」, incense sticks burned at temples. Picture a student burning incense to their major, and let context tell you which is meant."
    ],
    ja: "わたしの[専攻|せんこう]はれきしです。",
    en: "My major is history." },
  "なまえ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「名前|なまえ」. Both kanji use native Japanese readings here. 「名|な」 means name, and 「前|まえ」 means front or before. Rather than tell a history for why front was added, here's a hook. A way to remember it: your name is what you put out in front of you. It's the first thing people learn when you step forward. The kanji 「名|な」 has a Chinese-origin reading too, 「めい」, which you'll see in 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, literally having a name, and 「有名人|ゆうめいじん」, a celebrity. And 「前|まえ」 is the same front as in 「ごぜん」, before noon, where it's read 「ぜん」. When you ask someone else's name, it's polite to add 「お」 to the front, 「おなまえは」. You never add 「お」 to your own name. So you say 「わたしのなまえは」, my name is, and then your name. To recap, the name you carry out in front of you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Names in Japanese work a little differently from English, so here are the key points. Japanese names put the family name first and the given name second. The family name has its own word, 「名字|みょうじ」. The full name is often just called 「なまえ」, or more formally 「氏名|しめい」 on forms. On a form, you might see 「ここになまえをかいてください」, please write your name here. Since twenty twenty, the Japanese government has asked that Japanese names written in English also put the family name first in official documents. Among friends, people often use family names plus 「さん」, even after knowing each other for a while. A quick exchange might go, 「おなまえは」. 「スミスです。メアリー・スミスです」. So when Japanese people introduce themselves, listen for the family name first." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「名|な」 has three readings you'll keep meeting. Here it's the native reading 「な」. With 「めい」, it gives you 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous. And in restaurants, it's a formal counter for people. A server might say 「にめいさまですか」, a party of two? With 「みょう」, it's in 「名字|みょうじ」, the family name. And the second kanji, 「前|まえ」, is just the front you know from 「えきのまえ」, in front of the station. One more sibling. Writing your name is 「なまえをかく」, but signing with your name has its own word, 「署名|しょめい」, a signature. So whether you're famous, booking a table for two, or filling in your family name, the same kanji is there. Watch for its three readings, and you'll recognize it all over Japan." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Names in Japan carry a lot of thought. When parents choose a child's given name, they pick kanji for their meanings and their look, not just their sound. The same spoken name can be written with many different kanji. So when you meet someone, they may explain which kanji they use, like the one for flower or the one for bright. Because of this, even Japanese people sometimes can't read a new name correctly on the first try. Instead of signing with a handwritten signature, many Japanese people use a personal seal stamp, called 「はんこ」. It's carved with the family name, pressed in red ink onto documents. Bank accounts and official forms have long used them, though they're slowly giving way to digital signing. So a name in Japan is chosen with care, and sometimes stamped instead of signed." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「名|な」 means name and 「前|まえ」 means front, so your name is what you put out in front of you. Add 「お」 for someone else's name, but never for your own.",
      "Japanese names put the family name first, and the family name itself is 「名字|みょうじ」. On forms you'll see 「ここになまえをかいてください」.",
      "「名|な」 has three readings: 「な」 here, 「めい」 in 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, and 「みょう」 in 「名字|みょうじ」. Watch for all three and you'll spot it everywhere.",
      "Parents pick name kanji carefully for meaning and look, so even Japanese people sometimes misread names. Many people stamp a personal seal, a 「はんこ」, instead of signing."
    ],
    ja: "わたしの[なまえ]はメアリーです。",
    en: "My name is Mary." },
  "なん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a sound-changed form of 「なに」, what. Both are written with the kanji 「何|なに」. The question is when to use which. You use 「なん」 when the next sound is a d, t, or n sound, as in 「なんですか」, what is it? You also use it in front of counters, words that count things. 「なんじ」 is what time, 「なんさい」 is how old, and 「なんねんせい」 is what year in school. You use 「なに」 before most other sounds, especially particles like 「を」 and 「が」, as in 「なにをたべますか」, what will you eat? Later you'll meet 「なにか」, something, and 「なにも」, not anything. A common mistake is saying 「なにですか」. It sounds off to Japanese ears. The reason is just smoothness. 「なんです」 rolls off the tongue more easily. A way to remember it: when the next sound is d, t, or n, the word clips itself short to 「なん」. To recap, 「なん」 is 「なに」 shortened before those sounds and counters." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's look at a few tricky spots where the choice between 「なん」 and 「なに」 really matters. Before 「の」, it's 「なんの」, what kind of. 「なんのほんですか」, what kind of book is it? Before 「で」, both are possible, but they mean different things. 「なんで」 usually means why, in casual speech. 「なにで」 means by what means, as in 「なにでいきますか」, how will you get there? For languages, it's 「なにご」, what language. But for people, it's 「なんにん」, how many people. And for days of the week, it's 「なんようび」, what day. So the rule of thumb, before d, t, n, and before counters, still holds, but these extra cases are worth learning one by one. Try a little drill. What kind of book, 「なんのほん」. What language, 「なにご」. How many people, 「なんにん」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji for what, 「何|なに」, has a surprising background. On the left is the person radical, the slim standing figure you'll see in many kanji about people. The usual explanation is that the whole kanji first pictured a person carrying a load on their shoulder. Later, the character was borrowed for the sound of the question word what, and the carrying meaning moved to a new kanji. That new kanji added the grass radical on top, giving 「荷|に」, a load or baggage. You'll meet it in 「荷物|にもつ」, luggage. So luggage and what are close relatives, one with grass on top, one without. Keep the picture of someone carrying a mystery load and asking, what's in here? It makes the kanji easy to remember. And notice it's normally written in kana in casual text, so you'll see 「なん」 as often as the kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word pops up in lots of everyday phrases. When someone calls your name, the casual reply is 「なに」, what? Use 「はい」 with teachers, though. If something surprises you, you might say 「えっ、なに」, huh, what? If someone asks what's wrong and it's nothing, say 「なんでもないです」, it's nothing. At a restaurant, if a friend asks what you want and you don't mind, say 「なんでもいいです」, anything is fine. Both of those use 「なんでも」, which means anything at all. And to show you're impressed, you'll hear 「なんと」, which works like wow or what a. A tiny exchange. 「なにをたべますか」. 「なんでもいいです」. What will you eat? Anything is fine. So listen for these small phrases, because they'll help you sound relaxed in conversation." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「なん」 is 「なに」 clipped short before a d, t or n sound and before counters, as in 「なんですか」 and 「なんじ」. Before particles like 「を」, keep 「なに」, and never say 「なにですか」.",
      "Some cases are worth learning one by one: 「なんの」 for what kind, 「なにご」 for what language, 「なんにん」 for how many people. Casual 「なんで」 means why, while 「なにで」 means by what means.",
      "「何|なに」 first pictured a person carrying a load, but it was borrowed for the sound of what. The carrying meaning moved to 「荷|に」, with grass on top, as in 「荷物|にもつ」.",
      "Handy phrases include 「なんでもないです」, it's nothing, and 「なんでもいいです」, anything is fine. Both use 「なんでも」, anything at all, and help you sound relaxed."
    ],
    ja: "{専攻|せんこう}は[なん]ですか。",
    en: "What is your major?" },
  "にほんじん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「日本|にほん」, Japan, plus 「人|じん」, person. The kanji 「人|じん」 is a simple picture of a person walking, two legs striding apart. It has three readings you'll keep meeting, and the choice matters. The native reading, 「ひと」, is the word for person on its own. The reading 「じん」 attaches to a place to mean a person from there, like 「アメリカじん」 or 「がいこくじん」, a foreigner. And 「にん」 is used for counting people, as in 「さんにん」, three people. Watch out for the language versus people mix-up. 「にほんご」 is the Japanese language, and 「にほんじん」 is a Japanese person. A common slip is saying 「にほんじん」 when you mean the language, or the other way round. The 「じん」 ending also shows up in 「有名人|ゆうめいじん」, a celebrity, literally a famous person. To recap, Japan plus person, with 「じん」 as the person from a place." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "How do people actually ask where you're from? The blunt version is 「なにじんですか」, what nationality are you. It's grammatical, but it can sound a little abrupt, like asking what are you. A softer, more common question is 「おくにはどちらですか」, where is your country. You'll answer with the country, and the listener can work out the rest. Japanese people also use 「にほんじん」 a lot when talking about themselves as a group. You'll hear sentences like 「にほんじんはおちゃがすきです」, Japanese people like tea. These group statements are very common in casual talk, so don't be surprised by them. One more tip. When you say a friend is Japanese, use 「の」 to link the words. 「にほんじんのともだち」 means a Japanese friend. So ask gently, answer with your country, and link with 「の」." },
      { title: "Nippon or Nihon", text: "The country's name has two readings, and so does this word. Alongside 「にほん」 there's 「にっぽん」, with a small 「っ」 and a stronger p sound. Both are correct, and the government has never forced one over the other. 「にほん」 is what you'll hear in most everyday conversation. 「にっぽん」 feels punchier and more official. It's printed on Japanese stamps and banknotes, and fans chant it at sports matches. So you may also hear 「にっぽんじん」, a Japanese person, especially in speeches or on the news. It means exactly the same thing. When you speak, 「にほんじん」 is the safe, natural choice. When you hear 「にっぽんじん」, don't let the small 「っ」 throw you. Two spellings in sound, one meaning, so recognise both and use the softer one." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word is a noun, so it works with the basic sentence pattern from lesson one. 「たけしさんはにほんじんです」, Takeshi is Japanese. To ask, add 「か」. 「にほんじんですか」, are you Japanese? To say no, swap 「です」 for 「じゃないです」. 「いいえ、にほんじんじゃないです」, no, I'm not Japanese. To say someone is also Japanese, change 「は」 to 「も」. 「メアリーさんもにほんじんですか」, is Mary Japanese too? Notice that English uses an adjective here, Japanese, but Japanese uses a noun, a Japanese person. So you never need a special adjective form. You just say person from Japan, plus 「です」. And to describe a thing as Japanese, you don't use this word at all. A Japanese car is 「にほんのくるま」. So people take 「じん」, and things take 「の」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japan plus 「人|じん」, a walking person, gives a Japanese person. Keep 「じん」 for people from a place, 「ご」 for the language, 「ひと」 for person, and 「にん」 for counting.",
      "「なにじんですか」 can sound abrupt, so the softer 「おくにはどちらですか」 is more common. For a Japanese friend, link with 「の」, as in 「にほんじんのともだち」.",
      "You may also hear 「にっぽんじん」, with a stronger sound, in speeches and on the news. It means the same thing, but 「にほんじん」 is the safe everyday choice.",
      "It's a noun, so it fits the basic pattern 「たけしさんはにほんじんです」, with 「じゃないです」 for no and 「も」 for also. People take 「じん」, but things take 「の」, as in 「にほんのくるま」."
    ],
    ja: "たけしさんは[にほんじん]です。",
    en: "Takeshi is Japanese." },
  "はん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「半|はん」, meaning half. The Chinese-origin reading is used here. You put it straight after an hour to mean half past. 「さんじはん」 is half past three, or three thirty. 「ごごしちじはん」 is seven thirty P.M. That's really all there is to the grammar. Here are some things to watch out for. It only works for thirty minutes. For other minutes, you use 「ふん」 or 「ぷん」, minutes. Also, English has phrases like half to four, or quarter past. Japanese doesn't use those in everyday speech. Just state the hour and add 「はん」. And remember that 「はん」 comes after 「じ」, never before. The same kanji appears in other half words you'll meet later, like a half, or half price. A way to remember it: think of a clock face split in half by the minute hand pointing straight down. When the hand reaches the bottom, it's 「はん」. To recap, put 「はん」 after the hour for half past." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「半|はん」 means half wherever it goes, and it travels a lot. 「半分|はんぶん」 is a half, as in half of a cake. 「半年|はんとし」 is half a year, six months. 「半額|はんがく」 is half price, a word you'll love seeing on supermarket stickers in the evening. And with time, it can follow a length as well as a clock time. 「いちじかんはん」 means an hour and a half, a duration. Compare that with 「いちじはん」, which means half past one, a point on the clock. The only difference is 「かん」, but it changes a moment into a stretch of time. So the same little piece means half in every word, and you just need to notice what it's attached to." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "A few words sound close to this one, so let's keep them apart. First, 「ばん」, with a b sound. It can mean evening, as in 「ばんごはん」, dinner. It's also the 「ばん」 in 「ばんごう」, number. So half past and evening differ by just one consonant. Then there's 「ごはん」, a meal or cooked rice. It contains the sound 「はん」, but it's a completely different kanji and has nothing to do with half. Here's a memory trick for that one. Picture eating only half your rice bowl, a half 「ごはん」. Funny, but it helps you notice that the sounds match while the meanings don't. When you hear 「はん」 right after an hour with 「じ」, it's always half past. So listen for the 「じ」 before it, and you'll never mix them up." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's build full time sentences with it. To say what time it is now, use 「いま」 and 「です」. 「いま、くじはんです」, it's nine thirty now. To say when something happens, add the particle 「に」 after the time. 「くじはんにがっこうへいきます」, I go to school at nine thirty. For morning or afternoon, put 「ごぜん」 or 「ごご」 in front. 「ごごよじはん」 is four thirty P.M. To ask the time, use 「なんじ」. 「なんじですか」, what time is it? There is no question word for half. You ask the hour, and the answer brings the 「はん」. One more pattern. 「から」 and 「まで」 mean from and until. 「くじはんからじゅうじはんまで」, from nine thirty to ten thirty. So treat hour plus 「はん」 as one block, and attach your particles after it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「半|はん」 means half, so put it right after the hour for half past, as in 「さんじはん」. It works only for thirty minutes, and Japanese has no quarter past or half to.",
      "「半|はん」 means half in every word, like 「半分|はんぶん」 and 「半額|はんがく」, half price. Compare 「いちじかんはん」, an hour and a half, with 「いちじはん」, half past one.",
      "Don't confuse it with 「ばん」, evening, or the 「はん」 inside 「ごはん」, which is a different kanji. When 「はん」 comes right after an hour with 「じ」, it's always half past.",
      "Treat hour plus 「はん」 as one block and put particles after it, as in 「くじはんにがっこうへいきます」. There's no question word for half, so just ask 「なんじですか」."
    ],
    ja: "いま{三時|さんじ}[はん]です。",
    en: "It's three thirty now." },
  "ばんごう|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「番号|ばんごう」. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「番|ばん」 means a turn, or a place in order, like taking your turn in line. 「号|ごう」 means a number or a sign, like an issue number on a magazine or a train's name number. So a 「ばんごう」 is a number that marks something's place in an order. The kanji 「番|ばん」 shows up in 「一番|いちばん」, number one, or the best, which you'll learn later. In Genki's first lesson, the main use is 「でんわばんごう」, telephone number. You ask 「でんわばんごうはなんですか」, what is your phone number? When reading a phone number out loud, Japanese people often say the dash as 「の」. The number zero is 「ゼロ」 or 「れい」, and four and seven are usually 「よん」 and 「なな」 to avoid confusion. To recap, turn plus number, the number that gives something its place." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji here has its own family worth knowing. 「番|ばん」 is about a turn or place in a sequence. 「一番|いちばん」 is number one, and also the best. 「順番|じゅんばん」 means order, or whose turn it is. And 「交番|こうばん」 is a small neighborhood police box, where officers take turns keeping watch. You'll also hear 「ばん」 after a number, like platform three, 「さんばんせん」. The other kanji, 「号|ごう」, marks a numbered thing. On the bullet train, your car number is 「ごうしゃ」, as in 「ごごうしゃ」, car number five. Trains themselves have numbers ending in 「ごう」, like 「のぞみ」 with a number in front of 「ごう」. So 「ばん」 gives the place in line, and 「ごう」 gives the label. Together, they make a number that tells you where you stand." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese people love to remember numbers with puns. It's called 「ごろあわせ」. Each digit can be read several ways, so a string of numbers can be turned into words. The classic example is four, six, four, nine. Read as 「よ」, 「ろ」, 「し」, 「く」, it sounds like 「よろしく」, nice to meet you, or please treat me well. Shops and companies choose phone numbers that spell something catchy for exactly this reason. Numbers also carry luck. Four can be read 「し」, which sounds like death, and nine can be read 「く」, which sounds like suffering. So hospitals and hotels sometimes skip room numbers with four or nine. Next time you see a Japanese phone number on a sign, try reading it as a pun. Numbers in Japan are never just numbers." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond phone numbers, this word comes up constantly in daily errands. At a bank, a post office, or a clinic, you take a ticket with a number, then wait to be called. Staff will announce something like 「じゅうごばんのかた」, the person with number fifteen. Your room number at a hotel or dorm is 「へやばんごう」. On forms, you may be asked for your 「でんわばんごう」 or your student number. To ask what number something is, say 「なんばんですか」, what number is it? A tiny exchange at the clinic might go like this. 「なんばんですか」. 「にじゅうさんばんです」. What number are you? I'm number twenty-three. So keep your ears open for 「ばん」 when you're waiting in line, because that's your cue to stand up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「番|ばん」 means a turn or place in order and 「号|ごう」 means a number or sign, so it's a number marking something's place. The key use is 「でんわばんごう」, phone number.",
      "「番|ばん」 gives the place in line, as in 「一番|いちばん」 and 「順番|じゅんばん」, while 「号|ごう」 labels numbered things, like the train car in 「ごごうしゃ」. Together they tell you where you stand.",
      "Japanese people turn numbers into puns, called 「ごろあわせ」, so four, six, four, nine reads as 「よろしく」. Four and nine can sound like death and suffering, so some hotels skip them.",
      "At banks and clinics staff call out numbers like 「じゅうごばんのかた」, so listen for 「ばん」 while you wait. To ask what number, say 「なんばんですか」."
    ],
    ja: "{電話|でんわ}[ばんごう]はなんですか。",
    en: "What's your phone number?" },
  "わたし|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the standard, safe word for I. It works for everyone, man or woman, young or old, in almost any setting. That's why Genki teaches it first. The kanji is 「私|わたし」, which also carries the meaning private, as in a private school. There's an even more formal version, 「わたくし」, used in business or very polite speech. Men often use 「ぼく」 in casual talk, which feels softer and more boyish. And 「わたし」 plus 「たち」 makes 「わたしたち」, we. Here is the most common mistake English speakers make. They say 「わたしは」 at the start of every sentence. In Japanese, once it's clear you're talking about yourself, you usually drop it. Saying it too often sounds stiff, or as if you're stressing yourself on purpose. A way to remember it: think of saying, what about me, to point at yourself. To recap, the all-purpose polite I, used less often than you'd expect." },
      { title: "Pronoun family", text: "Japanese has a whole wardrobe of words for I, and each one dresses you differently. 「わたし」 is the neutral, all-purpose choice. 「わたくし」 is the extra-formal version, heard in business, speeches, and on the phone at work. 「あたし」 is a casual, softer form used mostly by women and girls. 「ぼく」 is used mostly by boys and men, and feels gentle and friendly. 「おれ」 is the rough, very casual masculine choice. It's fine with close friends, but rude in polite settings. The word for you, 「あなた」, is also tricky. Japanese people usually avoid it and use the person's name with 「さん」 instead. Saying 「あなた」 to someone's face can sound cold or distant. So stick with 「わたし」 for yourself and names for others, and you'll never sound out of place." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The word started life as the more formal 「わたくし」, and over time people clipped it down to 「わたし」. The kanji 「私|わたし」 and the old word 「わたくし」 originally meant private, or personal, as opposed to public. In older Japanese, 「わたくし」 was used for one's private affairs, the opposite of 「おおやけ」, public or official. Only later did it come to mean I, the private self speaking. You can still see the private meaning today. A private university is 「しりつだいがく」, using the reading 「し」 for the same kanji. And 「私立|しりつ」 is the word for privately run, as in a private school. So when you say 「わたし」, you're really naming your private self. From private matters to the word for me, it's a small piece of history in every introduction." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's see how this word combines with the particles you learn early on. With 「は」, it sets you up as the topic. 「わたしはがくせいです」, I am a student. With 「の」, it becomes my. 「わたしのほん」, my book. 「わたしのなまえ」, my name. With 「も」, it means me too, or I also. If a friend says 「わたしはがくせいです」, you can answer 「わたしもがくせいです」, I'm a student too. Add 「たち」 to make it plural. 「わたしたち」 means we. Notice that Japanese never changes the word itself. There's no separate form for me or mine, as in English. The particle after it does all the work. So keep the word the same, change the little particle, and you can say I, me, my, and we." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「わたし」 is the safe, neutral word for I that works for everyone, with 「わたくし」 as the extra-formal version. Drop it once it's clear you mean yourself, because repeating it sounds stiff.",
      "Japanese has a wardrobe of words for I, from formal 「わたくし」 to boyish 「ぼく」 and rough 「おれ」. Avoid 「あなた」 too, and use a name with 「さん」 instead.",
      "The word was clipped from 「わたくし」, which first meant private, the opposite of public. You still see that meaning in 「私立|しりつ」, privately run.",
      "The word itself never changes, and the particle does the work: 「わたしは」 for I, 「わたしの」 for my, 「わたしも」 for me too. Add 「たち」 for we."
    ],
    ja: "[わたし]はアメリカじんです。",
    en: "I am American." },
  "アメリカ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from America, so it's written in katakana. The sounds map over very neatly. English a becomes 「ア」, then 「メ」, then the r becomes 「リ」, and the final a becomes 「カ」. Unlike some country names, there's no extra vowel or long vowel to worry about. Notice that in Japanese, 「アメリカ」 usually means the United States, not the whole of the Americas. A fun extra fact. Long ago, foreign names were written with kanji chosen for their sound, and the spelling for America used the kanji for rice as its second character. That's why, in newspapers, you'll see the U.S. called 「米国|べいこく」, the rice country, even though it has nothing to do with rice. To make an American, add 「じん」, person, to get 「アメリカじん」. The English language is still 「えいご」, though, not a version of 「アメリカ」. To recap, America squeezed straight into katakana, meaning the U.S." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「米|こめ」 has become a quiet shorthand for America, and it builds a little family of its own. Its native reading, 「こめ」, means uncooked rice. But in news and politics, it's read 「べい」 and stands for the United States. 「日米|にちべい」 means Japan and the U.S., as in talks between the two countries. 「北米|ほくべい」 is North America, and 「南米|なんべい」 is South America. In those last two, 「べい」 has widened back out to mean the Americas as a whole. In everyday speech, people still say 「アメリカ」. The kanji forms are short and handy for headlines. So if you see the rice kanji in a news story about trade, think of the U.S., not a rice field. Same kanji, rice in the kitchen and America on the front page." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a habit of labelling things American, sometimes in surprising ways. Order an 「アメリカンコーヒー」 in a Japanese café and you'll get a lighter, weaker coffee, not a huge mug of drip. An 「アメリカンドッグ」 isn't a hot dog at all. It's a sausage on a stick coated in batter and fried, what Americans call a corn dog. You'll find them in convenience stores everywhere. American food chains, movies, and baseball are a big part of daily life in Japan too. Baseball in particular came to Japan from the U.S. in the eighteen seventies and became a national passion. So when you see 「アメリカン」 on a menu, expect something a little different from home. America in Japan is partly real and partly imagined, and that makes it fun to explore." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you introduce yourself in Japan, people will often follow up with a question about where in the U.S. you're from. You might hear 「アメリカのどこですか」, where in America? State names are katakana too. 「カリフォルニア」, 「テキサス」, 「ニューヨーク」. Some people may only know a few big places, so naming a nearby famous city can help. To say you're from somewhere, use 「から」, from. 「アメリカからきました」, I came from America. To describe things, link with 「の」. 「アメリカのだいがく」, an American college. And an American person is, of course, 「アメリカじん」. A tiny exchange. 「アメリカのどこですか」. 「コロラドです」. Where in America? Colorado. So be ready with your state or city in katakana, because the conversation usually goes there next." }
    ],
    short: [
      "America slides straight into katakana as 「アメリカ」, and it means the United States, not the whole Americas. Newspapers also call it 「米国|べいこく」, the rice country, from an old sound spelling.",
      "The rice kanji 「米|こめ」, read 「べい」, is news shorthand for the United States, as in 「日米|にちべい」. In 「北米|ほくべい」 and 「南米|なんべい」 it widens to the Americas.",
      "In Japan an 「アメリカンコーヒー」 is a light, weak coffee, and an 「アメリカンドッグ」 is a corn dog. Expect American things on menus to be a little different from home.",
      "People often ask 「アメリカのどこですか」, so be ready with your state or city in katakana. Say 「アメリカからきました」 to give where you're from."
    ],
    ja: "[アメリカ]の{大学|だいがく}の{学生|がくせい}です。",
    en: "I'm a student at an American college." },
  "イギリス|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one surprises people. It doesn't come from the word Britain at all. When Portuguese traders reached Japan in the fifteen hundreds, they brought their word for English, which sounds roughly like ing-lesh. Japanese squeezed those sounds into 「イギリス」. The n sound softened away, the l became an r sound, and an extra vowel was added at the end, as Japanese needs. That's why the word sounds a bit like English rather than Britain. In everyday use, 「イギリス」 covers the whole United Kingdom, even though it comes from a word for England. There's also a kanji name, 「英国|えいこく」, which you'll see in news writing, and it shares 「英|えい」 with 「えいご」, English. So a British person is 「イギリスじん」, and the language they speak is 「えいご」. A way to remember it: think of an English person introducing themself as English, with a Japanese accent. To recap, from the Portuguese word for English, used for all of Britain." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「英|えい」 is the key to English-related words. It originally means something like outstanding or brilliant, but it was picked for its sound to stand for England. 「英語|えいご」 is the English language, which you already know. 「英国|えいこく」 is the formal name for Britain, common in newspapers. 「英会話|えいかいわ」 means English conversation, and you'll see it on signs for language schools all over Japan. 「日英|にちえい」 means Japan and Britain, as in relations between the two. Notice something odd. The language is 「えいご」, but the country is usually 「イギリス」 in speech, not 「えいこく」. So the katakana word names the place, and the kanji 「英|えい」 handles the language and the formal name." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In everyday Japanese, 「イギリス」 covers the whole United Kingdom. But you'll sometimes hear the separate nations too, all in katakana. 「イングランド」 is England, 「スコットランド」 is Scotland, and 「ウェールズ」 is Wales. Northern Ireland is 「きたアイルランド」, using the native word for north. These names come up mostly in sports, like football and rugby, where the nations play as separate teams. To say someone is British, use 「イギリスじん」. To say a thing is British, link with 「の」, as in 「イギリスのおちゃ」, British tea. A small exchange. 「イギリスのどこですか」. 「ロンドンです」. Where in Britain? London. So use 「イギリス」 for the country, and save the nation names for when someone wants detail." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan and Britain have a long and close relationship. Both are island nations off the edge of a continent, and both drive on the left side of the road. When Japan opened up in the late eighteen hundreds, British engineers helped build its first railway, which opened in eighteen seventy-two between Tokyo and Yokohama. The connection continues in the imperial family. Both the current Emperor and Empress studied at Oxford University. British things are popular in Japan today too. Afternoon tea sets at hotels are a favorite treat, and British rock music has many devoted fans. So when you hear 「イギリス」, think of a country that Japan has learned from, traded with, and still loves to visit." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「イギリス」 comes not from Britain but from the Portuguese word for English, brought by traders in the fifteen hundreds. It now covers the whole United Kingdom.",
      "「英|えい」 handles English words like 「英語|えいご」, 「英会話|えいかいわ」 and the formal 「英国|えいこく」. In speech the country is usually 「イギリス」, while 「英|えい」 handles the language.",
      "「イギリス」 covers the whole United Kingdom, but sports bring out the separate nations, like 「イングランド」 and 「スコットランド」. A British thing links with 「の」, as in 「イギリスのおちゃ」.",
      "Japan and Britain are both island nations that drive on the left, and British engineers helped build Japan's first railway. Both the current Emperor and Empress studied at Oxford."
    ],
    ja: "ロバートさんは[イギリス]じんです。",
    en: "Robert is British." },
  "オーストラリア|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English Australia, so it's written in katakana. Watch how the sounds were fitted into Japanese. The opening sound, written au in English, becomes a long 「オー」, shown by the dash. Then s and t get their own syllables, 「ス」 and 「ト」, because Japanese can't stack consonants together. The l becomes an r sound, 「ラ」, since Japanese has no separate l. And the ending becomes 「リア」. The English name itself comes from Latin, meaning the southern land. So the whole name, read in Japanese, means the southern land too, just as it does in English. Japan and Australia have close ties. Many Japanese students go there for working holidays and language study, and lots of Australians ski in Hokkaido every winter. An Australian is 「オーストラリアじん」. To recap, Australia stretched into Japanese sounds, with a long 「オー」 at the start and an r sound instead of l." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There's a country name that sounds almost the same, and it trips up learners all the time. 「オーストリア」 is Austria, the country in Europe with Vienna and the Alps. 「オーストラリア」 is Australia, the country with kangaroos. The only difference is one syllable, 「ラ」, tucked into the middle. Count the beats. Austria has six, 「オ」, 「ー」, 「ス」, 「ト」, 「リ」, 「ア」. Australia has seven, with 「ラ」 before 「リ」. Here's a memory trick. Picture a kangaroo jumping into the middle of the word and shouting la. That extra 「ラ」 is the kangaroo. Japanese people mix these two up too, so you'll even hear jokes about it. When you say it, make sure the 「ラ」 is clear. So no kangaroo means Austria, and with the kangaroo, you're in Australia." },
      { title: "Kanji name", text: "Like America and Britain, Australia has a short kanji name used in newspapers. It's 「豪州|ごうしゅう」. The 「豪|ごう」 was chosen mainly for its sound, close to the start of the old way of writing the name. The 「州|しゅう」 means a large region, or a continent-sized land. You'll often see just the first kanji standing alone, especially in headlines. 「日豪|にちごう」 means Japan and Australia, as in a meeting between the two countries. It follows the same pattern as 「日米|にちべい」, Japan and the U.S. In conversation, people always say 「オーストラリア」. You only need the kanji name for reading news. So if a headline shows 「豪|ごう」 next to 「日|にち」, think of Australia." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Australia turns up in Japanese daily life in some unexpected places. In supermarkets, you'll see a lot of beef labelled 「オージービーフ」, Aussie beef. It's a familiar, affordable choice for Japanese families. The seasons are flipped, too. When Japan is in its cold winter, Australia is having summer, so a Christmas on the beach sounds exotic to Japanese ears. But the time difference is tiny. Eastern Australia is only about an hour ahead of Japan, which makes business and phone calls easy. Koalas are a big favorite, and several Japanese zoos keep them. So when you hear 「オーストラリア」, a Japanese listener might picture beaches, koalas, steaks, and sunshine at Christmas." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Australia becomes katakana with a long 「オー」 at the start, separate syllables for s and t, and an r sound in 「ラ」 instead of l. The name comes from Latin for the southern land.",
      "「オーストリア」 is Austria, while 「オーストラリア」, Australia, has one extra 「ラ」 in the middle. Picture a kangaroo jumping in shouting la, and you're in Australia.",
      "Newspapers use the short kanji name 「豪州|ごうしゅう」, often just 「豪|ごう」, as in 「日豪|にちごう」, Japan and Australia. In conversation people always say 「オーストラリア」.",
      "To Japanese listeners Australia means 「オージービーフ」 in supermarkets, koalas in zoos, and summer at Christmas. The time difference is only about an hour, which makes calls easy."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}は[オーストラリア]じんです。",
    en: "My friend is Australian." },
  "かんこく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「韓国|かんこく」. 「韓|かん」 is the Han in Korea's own name for itself, the same sound you hear in Korean words about the country. 「国|こく」 means country, and both use Chinese-origin readings. So the name is simply the Han country. The kanji 「国|こく」 is worth learning well. The outer box is a boundary, a border around the land, and the piece inside is the jewel kanji, so you can picture a precious land within borders. Its native reading is 「くに」, country, and it shows up in 「がいこく」, a foreign country, and 「がいこくじん」, a foreigner. You'll also find it in 「ちゅうごく」, China, where the sound softens to 「ごく」. The usual patterns apply. 「かんこくじん」 is a Korean person, and 「かんこくご」 is the Korean language. Korean food, dramas, and music are hugely popular in Japan, so you'll hear this word often. To recap, the Han country, with the same country kanji you'll see everywhere." },
      { title: "Language cousin", text: "Here's something that makes Korea special for Japanese learners. Korean and Japanese work in very similar ways. Both put the verb at the end of the sentence. Both use small particles after nouns, much like 「は」 and 「を」 in Japanese. Both also borrowed huge numbers of words from Chinese, so many words sound like cousins. The country's own name is a good example. In Korean it's said roughly like han-guk. In Japanese, it's 「かんこく」. Same Chinese characters, two local pronunciations. Many Japanese people study Korean for this reason, and many Koreans learn Japanese faster than English. So when you hear 「かんこく」, remember it's Japan's nearest neighbor in grammar as well as on the map." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Korean culture is hugely popular in Japan. It started with a wave of Korean TV dramas in the early two thousands, and the boom has a name, 「韓流|かんりゅう」, the Korean wave. The 「かん」 is the same as in 「かんこく」, and 「りゅう」 means a current or flow. Today, Korean pop groups fill stadiums in Japan, and Korean skincare and food are everywhere. In Tokyo, the neighborhood of 「新大久保|しんおおくぼ」 is famous for Korean restaurants and shops. Korean dishes are common at home too. 「キムチ」 is a supermarket staple, and 「ビビンバ」 is on many menus. So when someone says they love 「かんこく」, they might mean the dramas, the music, or the food." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Country names follow neat patterns, and this word is a perfect model. Add 「じん」 for a person. 「かんこくじん」, a Korean person. Add 「ご」 for the language. 「かんこくご」, the Korean language. Add 「の」 and a noun to describe a thing. 「かんこくのりょうり」, Korean food. To say someone comes from there, use 「から」. 「かんこくからきました」, I came from Korea. To say someone is a Korean student, link with 「の」. 「かんこくのりゅうがくせい」, an international student from Korea. These same patterns work for almost every country you learn. So master them once with 「かんこく」, and you can talk about people, languages, and things from anywhere." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「韓|かん」 is the Han from Korea's own name and 「国|こく」 means country, so it's the Han country. Add 「じん」 for a Korean person and 「ご」 for the language.",
      "Korean and Japanese both put the verb last, use particles after nouns, and borrowed many Chinese words. So Korea is Japan's nearest neighbor in grammar as well as on the map.",
      "Korean culture boomed in Japan with TV dramas, a wave called 「韓流|かんりゅう」. Today Korean pop, skincare and food like 「キムチ」 are everywhere.",
      "Use 「かんこく」 as a model for country patterns: 「かんこくじん」, 「かんこくご」, 「かんこくのりょうり」, and 「かんこくからきました」. These same patterns work for almost any country."
    ],
    ja: "スーさんは[かんこく]の{留学生|りゅうがくせい}です。",
    en: "Sue is an international student from Korea." },
  "ちゅうごく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「中国|ちゅうごく」. 「中|ちゅう」 means middle or center. Its shape is easy: a box with a line running straight through the middle. 「国|ごく」 means country, softened here from 「こく」 to 「ごく」. Both are Chinese-origin readings. So the name means the middle country, often translated as the Middle Kingdom. That's how China has long seen itself, as the center of the world. 「中|ちゅう」 also shows up in words like 「授業中に|じゅぎょうちゅうに」, during class, where it means in the middle of something. And it can be read 「じゅう」, as in 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day long. Watch out for one thing. In Japan, the same name can also refer to the 「ちゅうごく」 region in western Japan, around Hiroshima, so context matters. A Chinese person is 「ちゅうごくじん」, and the language is 「ちゅうごくご」. To recap, the middle country, with a line through the center of a box." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「中|なか」 has a native reading too, and it's one of the most useful words you'll know. On its own, 「なか」 means inside or in the middle. 「かばんのなか」 is inside the bag. With Chinese-origin readings, it appears in school words. 「中学生|ちゅうがくせい」 is a junior high school student, someone in the middle school. 「真ん中|まんなか」 means right in the center, the very middle. And 「中国語|ちゅうごくご」 is the Chinese language, built just like 「にほんご」. So in the native reading, you get inside, and in the Chinese-origin reading, you get middle in compound words. Notice how the meaning stays steady. It's always about the middle, the inside, or the center. One simple kanji, many doors, all leading to the middle." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Chinese food in Japan has its own name, 「中華|ちゅうか」, and its own style. 「中華料理|ちゅうかりょうり」 means Chinese cuisine, and small neighborhood Chinese restaurants are everywhere. Some favorites came from China and were reshaped in Japan. 「ラーメン」 was once commonly called 「中華そば|ちゅうかそば」, Chinese noodles, and you still see that name on old-fashioned shop curtains. 「ぎょうざ」, dumplings, came from China too. In Japan they're usually pan-fried and eaten with rice. Yokohama, Kobe, and Nagasaki each have a famous Chinatown, called 「中華街|ちゅうかがい」. So 「ちゅうごく」 is the country, and 「ちゅうか」 is the flavor you'll meet at the dinner table." },
      { title: "Kanji roots", text: "There's a deeper connection between this word and everything you're studying. The kanji themselves came to Japan from China, well over a thousand years ago. Japanese already had its own spoken language, so people used Chinese characters to write it. That's why most kanji have two kinds of readings. The native reading is the original Japanese word, like 「くに」 for country. The Chinese-origin reading imitates how the character was pronounced in China at the time, like 「こく」. When you read 「ちゅうごく」, both kanji use those Chinese-origin readings. Modern Chinese pronunciation has changed a lot since then, so the Japanese readings now sound quite different from today's Chinese. So every time you learn a reading, you're hearing a little echo of the past, carried over from China." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「中|ちゅう」 is a box with a line through the middle and 「国|ごく」 is country, so China is the middle country. Note that it can also mean a region of western Japan around Hiroshima.",
      "Natively 「中|なか」 means inside, as in 「かばんのなか」, and in compounds it means middle, as in 「中学生|ちゅうがくせい」 and 「真ん中|まんなか」. It's always about the middle or inside.",
      "Chinese food in Japan is 「中華|ちゅうか」, with dishes reshaped locally, like ramen, once called 「中華そば|ちゅうかそば」, and pan-fried 「ぎょうざ」. Several cities have a Chinatown called 「中華街|ちゅうかがい」.",
      "Kanji came from China, so most have a native reading like 「くに」 and a Chinese-origin reading like 「こく」. 「ちゅうごく」 uses the Chinese-origin readings, an echo of old Chinese pronunciation."
    ],
    ja: "リーさんは[ちゅうごく]じんです。",
    en: "Lee is Chinese." },
  "けいざい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「経済|けいざい」. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「経|けい」 means to manage or govern, and 「済|ざい」 means to rescue or help, or to settle something. The word is a shortened form of an old four-character phrase that meant governing the world and saving the people. That's a pretty grand idea, and it was the original sense of economics. In the late eighteen hundreds, when Japan was taking in Western learning, scholars chose this old phrase to translate the Western word economy, and it stuck. So the subject of money, markets, and trade carries the idea of running society well so people can live. You'll use it in self-introductions, as in 「せんこうはけいざいです」, my major is economics. You'll also hear 「にほんのけいざい」, the Japanese economy, on the news. A way to remember it: an economist's job is to keep the world working and help the people. To recap, govern plus rescue, managing society for its people." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word leads to other words you'll meet. 「経|けい」 appears in 「経験|けいけん」, experience, something you've gone through. The second kanji, 「済|ざい」, has a native verb, 「済む|すむ」, which means to be finished or settled. And here's a fun link. The everyday word 「すみません」 is usually explained as coming from this verb. It's the negative form, so it suggests something like, this isn't settled, I still owe you. That's why the same word can mean both sorry and thank you. Back to economics. 「経済学|けいざいがく」 is the academic study of economics, with 「がく」 for learning. A faculty of economics at a university is 「経済学部|けいざいがくぶ」. So one kanji gives you experience, and the other gives you excuse me." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a self-introduction, the word slots right into the pattern for majors. You can say 「せんこうはけいざいです」, my major is economics, or 「けいざいをべんきょうしています」, I'm studying economics. Outside school, you'll hear it on the news all the time. 「にほんのけいざい」 is the Japanese economy. To describe something as economical, Japanese adds 「てき」 to make 「経済的|けいざいてき」. A car that uses little gas is 「けいざいてき」. In daily talk, people also use it about money worries. Someone might joke that their wallet is in a tough situation, using 「けいざいてき」 to mean financially. A tiny exchange. 「せんこうはなんですか」. 「けいざいです」. What's your major? Economics. So it's a school subject, a news topic, and a word for being careful with money." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's economic story is one of the most famous in modern history. After the Second World War, the country rebuilt quickly. From the nineteen fifties to the early seventies, it grew so fast that people speak of an economic miracle. By the late nineteen eighties, prices for land and stocks had shot up. This period is called the bubble economy, or just 「バブル」. When the bubble burst in the early nineteen nineties, growth slowed for years. People often call this stretch the lost decades. You'll still hear older Japanese people talk about the bubble years with a mix of nostalgia and embarrassment. So when someone mentions 「けいざい」 in Japan, there's a lot of history behind it, from miracle to bubble and beyond." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「経|けい」 means govern and 「済|ざい」 means rescue, from an old phrase about governing the world and saving the people. Scholars picked that phrase in the late eighteen hundreds to translate economy.",
      "「経|けい」 gives 「経験|けいけん」, experience, while 「済|ざい」 gives the verb 「済む|すむ」, to be settled. 「すみません」 is usually traced to that verb, meaning this isn't settled yet.",
      "Slot it into your introduction with 「せんこうはけいざいです」, and you'll hear 「にほんのけいざい」 on the news. Add 「てき」 for 「経済的|けいざいてき」, economical or financial.",
      "Japan rebuilt after the war in a so-called economic miracle, then rode the 「バブル」 of the late eighties until it burst. Older people still recall the bubble with nostalgia and embarrassment."
    ],
    ja: "{専攻|せんこう}は[けいざい]です。",
    en: "My major is economics." },
  "コンピューター|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English computer, so it's in katakana. Look at how the sounds were squeezed in. Japanese can't end a syllable on a hard m or p, so com becomes 「コン」, using the only consonant that can close a syllable, the n sound. Then pu becomes 「ピュー」, with a small 「ュ」 to make the y glide and a long mark for the stretched vowel. The ending er becomes 「ター」, a long a sound, because Japanese turns the English er ending into a long vowel. You'll see that same pattern in lots of loanwords, like 「コーヒー」 and 「スーパー」. A small spelling note. In technical writing, you'll sometimes see it without the final long mark, 「コンピュータ」. Both are correct, and the long version is now more common. In daily life, people often just say 「パソコン」, short for personal computer. To recap, computer with its er turned into a long 「ター」." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The everyday word for a computer in Japan is 「パソコン」, and the way it was built is very typical of Japanese. It comes from 「パーソナルコンピューター」, personal computer. That's a mouthful, so Japanese clipped it. The first two beats of each word are kept. 「パソ」 from personal, and 「コン」 from computer. The same trick appears all over the language. 「リモコン」 is a remote control. 「エアコン」 is an air conditioner. 「スマホ」 is a smartphone. A laptop is 「ノートパソコン」, a notebook personal computer. So long loanwords tend to get chopped down to four snappy beats. Once you know the pattern, you can often guess what a mysterious short katakana word stands for." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Before the loanword took over, Japanese had a formal kanji term for a computer. It was 「電子計算機|でんしけいさんき」, an electronic calculating machine. 「電子|でんし」 means electronic, and it starts with the same kanji as 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, the electricity kanji. 「計算|けいさん」 means calculation. Its first kanji is the same one you see in 「時計|とけい」, a clock, the thing that measures time. 「機|き」 means machine. You'll rarely hear this long term today, except in very official or historical writing. But it shows how Japanese once built new words from kanji, and how loanwords later replaced many of them. So the old name was literally an electric counting machine, and the new one simply borrows the English." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the words you'll use around a computer every day. To use one is 「つかう」. 「コンピューターをつかいます」, I use a computer. Lots of computer vocabulary is katakana. 「メール」 is email, and 「インターネット」 is the internet. To type is 「うつ」, the same verb as hit, so you hit the keys. In Genki, the word appears as a major, meaning computer science. You can say 「せんこうはコンピューターです」, my major is computers. A tiny exchange. 「パソコン、ありますか」. 「はい、ノートパソコンがあります」. Do you have a computer? Yes, I have a laptop. So keep 「コンピューター」 for the subject, and use 「パソコン」 for the machine on your desk." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Computer becomes 「コンピューター」: com closes with 「ン」, pu becomes 「ピュー」, and the er ending turns into a long 「ター」. In daily life people mostly say 「パソコン」.",
      "「パソコン」 comes from 「パーソナルコンピューター」, clipped to the first two beats of each word. The same trick makes 「リモコン」, 「エアコン」 and 「スマホ」.",
      "The old formal term was 「電子計算機|でんしけいさんき」, an electronic calculating machine, built from kanji you know from 「電話|でんわ」 and 「時計|とけい」. Loanwords later replaced many such kanji words.",
      "Use 「コンピューター」 for the subject, as in 「せんこうはコンピューターです」, and 「パソコン」 for the machine on your desk. To type is 「うつ」, the same verb as hit."
    ],
    ja: "{専攻|せんこう}は[コンピューター]です。",
    en: "My major is computers." },
  "ビジネス|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English business, written in katakana. The English spelling is strange, but the sound is something like biz-nis, and Japanese follows the sound, not the spelling. So the first part becomes 「ビ」. The z sound needs a vowel after it, and Japanese uses 「ジ」 for that, giving 「ビジ」. The ending ness becomes 「ネス」, with a vowel tucked onto the final s. The result is 「ビジネス」. In Genki it appears as a major, business studies. But you'll also hear it in many compound words. A business hotel is a small, simple hotel for people on work trips, which is a very Japanese kind of place. Business class on a plane is the same idea as in English. A way to remember it: say business fast, then add a little vowel to every consonant. To recap, business, squeezed into four katakana by following its sound, not its spelling." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese business, the first moment of a meeting has its own ritual, the exchange of business cards. A business card is 「名刺|めいし」. You hold your card with both hands, turned so the other person can read it, and bow slightly as you offer it. When you receive one, you also take it with both hands and read it carefully. Writing on someone's card, or stuffing it into your back pocket, is seen as rude. During the meeting, the cards are often laid out neatly on the table in front of you. This ritual is part of what Japanese people call 「ビジネスマナー」, business manners, and new employees often get formal training in it. So in Japan, a little card carries a lot of respect." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This loanword combines with other words to make many compounds. 「ビジネスマン」 is a businessperson, usually picturing someone in a suit who works for a company. 「ビジネスクラス」 is business class on a plane. 「ビジネスマナー」 means business etiquette. But Japanese also has native words for work that you'll use more often. 「しごと」 is work or a job, the everyday word. 「しごとはなんですか」, what's your job? 「かいしゃ」 is a company, and 「かいしゃいん」 is a company employee. So what's the difference? 「ビジネス」 sounds modern and corporate, a bit like the business world. 「しごと」 is just your job, whatever it is. So use 「しごと」 for daily life, and save 「ビジネス」 for the world of suits and meetings." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese follows the sound of business, not its spelling, adding vowels to make 「ビジネス」. You'll meet it in compounds like a business hotel, a simple hotel for work trips.",
      "Business card exchange, using a 「名刺|めいし」, is a ritual: offer and receive cards with both hands and a slight bow. Writing on a card or pocketing it carelessly is rude.",
      "「ビジネス」 sounds modern and corporate, as in 「ビジネスマン」, while 「しごと」 is simply your job. Use 「しごと」 in daily life and save 「ビジネス」 for suits and meetings."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}の{専攻|せんこう}は[ビジネス]です。",
    en: "My older brother's major is business." },
  "れきし|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「歴史|れきし」. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「歴|れき」 means to pass through, one after another, like the long line of years going by. 「史|し」 means a record or chronicle, the written account of events. Put them together and history is the record of everything that has passed through time. A way to remember it: picture an old scroll unrolling, with year after year written along it. You'll use it in self-introductions, as in 「せんこうはれきしです」, my major is history. And you can talk about 「にほんのれきし」, Japanese history, which is a big topic that draws many students to Japan. Japanese history is often divided by periods named after cities or capitals, such as the Edo period, when the capital was Edo, now Tokyo. Those period names come up all the time in museums and travel. To recap, passing through plus record, the record of what has passed." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you know 「歴史|れきし」, a whole family of words opens up. The second kanji, 「史|し」, record, sticks onto place names to make the history of somewhere. 「日本史|にほんし」 is Japanese history, and 「世界史|せかいし」 is world history. In Japanese high schools, students often choose between those two as subjects, so you'll hear both a lot. The first kanji, 「歴|れき」, passing through, shows up in words about a person's own past. 「学歴|がくれき」 is your educational background, the schools you have passed through. And 「履歴書|りれきしょ」 is a résumé, the document listing the path of your life so far. If you ever apply for a part-time job in Japan, you'll fill one in, often by hand. Finally, add 「てき」 to get 「歴史的|れきしてき」, historic, as in a historic moment. So the pair splits neatly. One half is about the record, the other about the path you walked. Learn the family together and each word props up the others." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese people use 「れきし」 in a few set ways worth borrowing. The most common is to say a place has history. 「このまちはれきしがあります」 means this town has a lot of history, and you'll hear it from tour guides in Kyoto or Nara. You can also say the history is long, 「れきしがながい」, about a temple, a company, or a festival. When talking about likes, use it with 「すき」: 「れきしがすきです」, I like history. Notice the particle 「が」 there, not 「を」. That's a very common slip for English speakers, because English says I like history with history as the object. For your studies, 「れきしのクラス」 is a history class, and 「れきしのせんせい」 is a history teacher, like in the example sentence. Here's a mini exchange. One person asks 「せんこうはなんですか」, what's your major? The other answers 「れきしです。にほんのれきしがすきです」. Short, natural, and exactly what you'd say at a welcome party. Keep 「が」 with 「すき」 and you'll sound right." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan counts its own history in a special way, using era names. Alongside the Western calendar, each emperor's reign gets a name, and years are numbered from the start of that era. The current era is 「令和|れいわ」, which began in 2019. Before it came 「平成|へいせい」, and before that 「昭和|しょうわ」. So a Japanese person might say they were born in a certain year of 「平成|へいせい」 rather than giving the Western year. You'll see these era years on official forms, train passes, and even some receipts, which can be confusing at first. Further back, history is divided into periods such as the Edo period, when samurai ruled under the shoguns, and the 「明治|めいじ」 era, when Japan modernised very quickly after opening to the West. Many Japanese people know the main periods in order, the way some Americans know the presidents. Learning a few era names will make museums, forms, and conversations about history far less mysterious." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「歴|れき」 means passing through and 「史|し」 means a record, so history is the record of what has passed. Picture an old scroll unrolling year after year.",
      "「史|し」 makes 「日本史|にほんし」 and 「世界史|せかいし」, while 「歴|れき」 appears in your own past, like 「履歴書|りれきしょ」, a résumé. One half is the record, the other the path you walked.",
      "Say a place has history with 「このまちはれきしがあります」, and say you like it with 「れきしがすきです」. Keep 「が」 with 「すき」, not 「を」.",
      "Japan counts years by era names, like 「令和|れいわ」, 「平成|へいせい」 and 「昭和|しょうわ」, seen on forms and receipts. Knowing a few makes museums and forms far less mysterious."
    ],
    ja: "[れきし]の{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "She's a history teacher." },
  "しごと|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「仕事|しごと」. Both parts use native readings. 「仕|し」 means to serve or attend, and 「事|ごと」 means thing or matter. Notice the sound change. On its own, 「事|こと」 starts with a k sound, but after another word it softens into 「ごと」. This softening happens all the time in Japanese compounds. So a job is the matters you attend to in service. The kanji 「事|じ」 also has a Chinese-origin reading, which you'll see in 「用事|ようじ」, an errand, and 「返事|へんじ」, a reply. All of them are about things to do or deal with. When asking about someone else's job, add 「お」 for politeness, as in 「おしごとはなんですか」, what do you do? Note that 「しごと」 can mean both your occupation and the work you're doing today. So 「しごとにいきます」 means going to work. To recap, serve plus matter, the things you take care of." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Here's a surprise hiding in 「仕事|しごと」. The first part, 「し」, is generally explained as the stem of the verb 「する」, to do, the same 「し」 you hear in 「します」. The second part is 「こと」, thing, softened to 「ごと」. So at heart the word means a thing you do. The kanji 「仕|し」 was chosen to spell that 「し」 sound, and it fits nicely because it also carries the idea of serving. You can see the same spelling trick in 「仕方|しかた」, the way of doing something. That word lives inside the famous phrase 「しかたがない」, it can't be helped, literally there's no way of doing. So when you hear 「しごと」 and 「しかた」, think of 「する」 hiding at the front of both. It turns a dry vocabulary word into something you already know: the things you do, and the way you do them. Work, at its root, is simply doing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words around work, and they don't overlap perfectly. 「しごと」 is a noun, your job or the work in front of you. To make a verb, add 「する」: 「しごとをする」, to do work. The separate verb 「はたらく」 means to work, to labour, and it focuses on the activity of working. So 「どこではたらいていますか」 asks where you work. Then there's 「アルバイト」, a part-time job, borrowed from German, where the word just means work. Students in Japan almost always say 「アルバイト」 or the short 「バイト」 for their part-time job, not 「しごと」. And 「かいしゃいん」, office worker, describes the kind of job you have. Here's how they fit together in one breath: 「ちちはかいしゃいんです。まいにちはたらきます。しごとはたいへんです」. My father is an office worker. He works every day. His job is hard. Each word does its own job, so learn them as a team." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Some of the most useful phrases in daily Japanese are built on 「しごと」. Coworkers greet each other, and people leaving work, with 「おつかれさまです」, but you'll also hear people say 「しごと、がんばってください」, good luck with work. To say you're heading to the office, use the particle 「に」: 「しごとにいきます」. If someone can't come out tonight, they might say 「あしたしごとがあります」, I have work tomorrow, which is a gentle, very common way to decline. When your work is finished, it's 「しごとがおわりました」. And if you quit your job, it's 「しごとをやめます」. A mini dialogue. One friend asks 「あした、ひまですか」, are you free tomorrow? The other replies 「すみません、しごとがあります」. Notice there's no need to explain further. In Japan, work is accepted as a complete reason. Learn these few patterns and you can talk about your working life right away." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「仕|し」 means serve and 「事|こと」, softened to 「ごと」, means matter, so a job is the matters you attend to. Add 「お」 when asking about someone else's, as in 「おしごとはなんですか」.",
      "The 「し」 is usually explained as the stem of 「する」, to do, plus 「こと」, thing, so work is simply a thing you do. The same 「し」 hides in 「仕方|しかた」, as in 「しかたがない」.",
      "「しごと」 is the job itself, 「はたらく」 is the act of working, and students call a part-time job 「アルバイト」 or 「バイト」. Each word does its own job, so learn them as a team.",
      "Say 「しごとにいきます」 for heading to work, and decline plans with 「しごとがあります」. In Japan, work is accepted as a complete reason with no need to explain."
    ],
    ja: "お[しごと]はなんですか。",
    en: "What is your job?" },
  "かいしゃいん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「会社員|かいしゃいん」. All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「会|かい」 means to meet or gather, the same kanji as the verb 「会う|あう」, to meet. 「社|しゃ」 means a society, a group of people, and also a shrine. Together, 「会社|かいしゃ」 means a company, a group that gathers to do business. The last kanji, 「員|いん」, means a member. So an office worker is a company member. 「社|しゃ」 is in other words too. 「社長|しゃちょう」 is a company president, and 「神社|じんじゃ」 is a shrine, a place for the gods, where the sound changes to 「じゃ」. In Japan, 「かいしゃいん」 is how many people describe themselves at work, especially salaried office staff. Rather than giving a specific job title, people often say which company they work for and that they are a company member. To recap, gathering plus society plus member, a member of a company." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The last kanji, 「員|いん」, member, is a little factory for job words. Stick it on a place, and you get the person who works there. 「駅員|えきいん」 is a station attendant, a member of the station. 「店員|てんいん」 is a shop clerk, a member of the shop. 「銀行員|ぎんこういん」 is a bank employee. And 「会員|かいいん」 is a member of a club or a gym, the kind of word on a membership card. There's even 「全員|ぜんいん」, everyone, literally all members, which teachers use when they want the whole class to do something. Compare this with 「しゃ」 and 「じん」, which are also person endings but work differently. 「員|いん」 always means you belong to some group or organisation. So when you meet a new word ending in 「いん」, ask yourself which group the person belongs to. You'll usually guess the meaning before anyone tells you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, saying 「かいしゃいんです」 is a very normal way to describe your job, even though it's vague. People often don't name their exact role. Instead, they tell you which company they belong to. So if you ask a new acquaintance about work, the conversation often goes like this. 「おしごとは」. 「かいしゃいんです」. 「どちらのかいしゃですか」, which company? Then they name it. Later in Genki, you'll learn the phrase 「につとめています」, I am employed at, which is the polished way to say where you work. You'll also hear the casual 「サラリーマン」 for a male office worker. It looks like English but it's made in Japan, from salary plus man, and English speakers wouldn't use it. A common mistake is translating I'm an employee too literally. Just use 「かいしゃいん」, and if you want, add the company name. That's exactly how Japanese people do it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Company life shapes a lot of Japanese culture. Many new graduates join a company together in April, all on the same day, and there's often a welcome ceremony for them. Traditionally, people joined one firm and stayed for decades, and that idea of lifelong employment still colours how people think about work, though job changing is much more common now. When office workers meet someone new for business, they exchange business cards with both hands and a small bow, reading the card carefully before putting it away. Treating a card carelessly, or stuffing it in your back pocket, comes across as rude. Office workers also often go out drinking with colleagues after work, which is seen as a way to build relationships. On a crowded morning train in Tokyo, most of the people in dark suits are 「かいしゃいん」 heading in. So this simple word opens a window onto a whole way of working life." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「会社|かいしゃ」 is a company, a society that gathers, and 「員|いん」 means member, so an office worker is a company member. Many people describe their job this way instead of naming a role.",
      "Stick 「員|いん」 on a place and you get the person who belongs there, like 「駅員|えきいん」 and 「店員|てんいん」. When a new word ends in 「いん」, ask which group the person belongs to.",
      "Saying 「かいしゃいんです」 is normal even though it's vague, and the follow-up is usually 「どちらのかいしゃですか」. The casual 「サラリーマン」 is made-in-Japan English for a male office worker.",
      "Many new graduates join a company together in April, and lifelong employment still colours how people think about work. Card exchange and after-work drinks are part of office life."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は[かいしゃいん]です。",
    en: "My father is an office worker." },
  "こうこうせい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is three pieces you already know. 「高|こう」 means high, 「校|こう」 means school, and together 「こうこう」 is high school. Add 「生|せい」, a person, often a student, and you have a high school student. All three are Chinese-origin readings. The 「生|せい」 ending is the same one you saw in 「学生|がくせい」, student, 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, and 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, international student. Japanese builds school level words in this neat, regular way. A 「だいがくせい」 is a college student, made from 「だいがく」 plus 「せい」, and you'll also hear the shorter forms for younger students later on. A culture note: Japanese high school students usually wear uniforms, and many join a club after classes. To say what year someone is in, use 「いちねんせい」, first year, and so on. To recap, high school plus person, the high school student." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closely at the two kanji in 「高校|こうこう」. The first, 「高|たか」, high, began as a picture of a tall building, with a roof at the top and a gate or doorway below. Its native reading is in 「高い|たかい」, which means tall or high, and also expensive, as in 「このかばんはたかいです」. In 「高校|こうこう」 it takes the Chinese-origin reading 「こう」. The second kanji, 「校|こう」, school, has 「木|き」, tree, on its left side. On the right is a shape that means to cross or mix, and it supplies the sound 「こう」. You'll see that right side again in 「交番|こうばん」, a police box. So both kanji happen to be read 「こう」, which is why the word sounds like a little echo. The same 「校|こう」 ends 「学校|がっこう」, school. Next time you see 「高校|こうこう」, look for the tall building and the tree." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word is a long vowel workout, so it's worth slowing down. Both halves have a stretched vowel: 「こう」 and 「こう」 again. Each one is held for two beats, so the whole word has six beats: 「こ」, 「う」, 「こ」, 「う」, 「せ」, 「い」. If you squash them, you get something like 「ここせい」, which sounds strange, because 「ここ」 means here. Many English speakers do exactly that, because English doesn't care much about vowel length. A good trick is to tap the beats on the table as you say it. The 「せい」 at the end is also long, and in natural speech it sounds like a stretched e. Try saying 「こうこうせい」, then 「ちゅうがくせい」, junior high student, then 「だいがくせい」. All three end in that same two-beat 「せい」. Getting the long vowels right makes your Japanese clearer straight away. Tap it out, six beats, and you'll have it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "High school in Japan is three years, and it isn't compulsory, but almost everyone goes. To get in, junior high students usually take entrance exams, so the school you attend can say a lot about how well you did. Many students commute by train or bicycle, sometimes quite far, in their school uniform. Club activities are a huge part of life. Students may practise before school, after school, and on weekends. The most famous example is high school baseball. Every summer, teams from all over Japan compete in a national tournament at 「甲子園|こうしえん」 stadium near Osaka, and it's shown on TV across the country. Players who lose sometimes scoop up a little of the stadium's dirt to take home as a keepsake. School festivals, cultural days, and graduation are big events too, and they appear constantly in anime and dramas. So when you meet a 「こうこうせい」 in a story, you'll know the world they live in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「高校|こうこう」, high school, plus 「生|せい」, a student, gives a high school student. The same 「せい」 ending builds 「だいがくせい」 and other school level words.",
      "「高|こう」 began as a picture of a tall building, and natively it's 「高い|たかい」, tall or expensive. 「校|こう」 has a tree on the left and a sound part on the right that also reads 「こう」.",
      "The word has six beats, with both 「こう」 and the final 「せい」 held long. Squash them and you get 「ここせい」, so tap out all six beats.",
      "High school lasts three years and isn't compulsory, but nearly everyone goes after entrance exams. Clubs are huge, and summer baseball at 「甲子園|こうしえん」 is watched across the country."
    ],
    ja: "{妹|いもうと}は[こうこうせい]です。",
    en: "My younger sister is a high school student." },
  "しゅふ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「主婦|しゅふ」. Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「主|しゅ」 means main, or master, the person in charge. 「婦|ふ」 means woman or wife, and you can see the woman kanji, 「女|おんな」, on its left side. So the word means the woman in charge of the home. The kanji 「主|しゅ」 appears in 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」, someone's husband, literally the main person of the house. A cultural note. These words come from a time when roles in the home were very fixed. They're still common, but there's also a male form, 「しゅふ」 written with a different second kanji meaning husband, and many people now use more neutral wording. Genki uses it when describing family members, as in 「はははしゅふです」, my mother is a housewife. To recap, the main woman of the household, the housewife." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji in 「主婦|しゅふ」 have shapes that tell a story. 「主|しゅ」, main or master, is traditionally explained as a lamp stand, with the dot on top as the flame. The lamp sits at the centre of a room, so it came to mean the central, main thing. Its native reading is 「ぬし」, owner, which you'll meet in 「持ち主|もちぬし」, the owner of an object. The second kanji, 「婦|ふ」, has 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left. On the right is a shape that pictures a broom. So the kanji is literally a woman with a broom, which shows how household roles were seen in ancient China. Knowing that history helps you remember it, and also explains why some people today prefer different wording. Put the two together and you have the woman at the centre of the home. Lamp at the centre, woman with a broom: two pictures, one word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The two kanji in 「主婦|しゅふ」 each have useful relatives. 「主|しゅ」 shows up in 「主人|しゅじん」, which once meant master of the house and is still used for a husband, as in 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」, your husband. It's also in 「主人公|しゅじんこう」, the main character of a story, a word anime fans hear all the time. The second kanji, 「婦|ふ」, appears in 「夫婦|ふうふ」, a married couple, made from husband plus wife. Notice that 「ふう」 there is long. And in department stores you might see signs for 「婦人服|ふじんふく」, women's clothing. When people want to stress that someone stays home full time, they say 「専業主婦|せんぎょうしゅふ」, a full-time homemaker. Someone who also has a job outside is 「兼業主婦|けんぎょうしゅふ」. You won't need all of these right away, but they make the kanji feel familiar. Main person, couple, main character: the same small pieces again and again." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Genki this word appears in family descriptions, but it's worth knowing how it sounds today. Saying 「はははしゅふです」 is perfectly normal and polite when describing your own mother. If you talk about someone else's mother, you might ask 「おかあさんはおしごとをしていますか」, does your mother work? It's softer than asking directly whether she's a housewife. In modern Japan most married women also work outside the home, at least part time, so 「しゅふ」 is less of a default than it used to be. You'll hear people describe part-time jobs for homemakers with the word 「パート」, short for part time. And a stay-at-home dad can be called 「主夫|しゅふ」, which sounds exactly the same but uses the kanji for husband. In speech you can't tell them apart, so context does the work. Use 「しゅふ」 for describing family, and ask about work gently. That's how Japanese speakers handle it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「主|しゅ」 means main or master and 「婦|ふ」, with 「女|おんな」 on its left, means woman or wife, so it's the woman in charge of the home. Genki uses it as 「はははしゅふです」.",
      "「主|しゅ」 pictures a lamp stand at the centre of a room, while 「婦|ふ」 is a woman beside a broom. That old picture of household roles explains why some people now prefer other wording.",
      "「主|しゅ」 also gives 「主人公|しゅじんこう」, a story's main character, and 「婦|ふ」 gives 「夫婦|ふうふ」, a married couple. A full-time homemaker is a 「専業主婦|せんぎょうしゅふ」.",
      "Most married women in Japan now work, so ask gently, like 「おかあさんはおしごとをしていますか」. A stay-at-home dad is 「主夫|しゅふ」, which sounds exactly the same."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は[しゅふ]です。",
    en: "My mother is a housewife." },
  "だいがくいんせい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This long word is built from pieces you already have. Start with 「大学|だいがく」, college, a big place of learning. Add 「院|いん」, which means an institution or a building of a certain kind. That gives 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school. Then add 「生|せい」, a person or student, to get a graduate student. All readings are Chinese-origin. 「院|いん」 is worth noticing because it appears in other building words. 「病院|びょういん」 is a hospital, an institution for illness, and 「美容院|びよういん」 is a beauty salon, an institution for beauty. Be careful, those two sound almost the same, so the vowel length matters. Japanese graduate students are often in a master's course or a doctoral course. Many international students come to Japan as 「だいがくいんせい」 rather than as undergraduates. To recap, college plus institution plus person, the graduate student." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「院|いん」, institution, turns up in many places once you start looking. Besides 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school, you already know 「病院|びょういん」, hospital, and 「美容院|びよういん」, a hair salon. Build on hospital and you get two handy verbs. 「入院|にゅういん」 is going into hospital as an inpatient, literally entering the institution. 「退院|たいいん」 is leaving hospital, being discharged. In Kyoto you'll see 「院|いん」 in temple names too, because it can mean a building or compound within a temple. Grad students also have a nickname built from this kanji. Instead of the long 「だいがくいんせい」, people often just say 「院生|いんせい」, institution student. You'll hear that around university campuses all the time. So 「院|いん」 is basically a building with a purpose. Add what the purpose is, and you get the word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you tell someone in Japan you're a graduate student, the next question is usually what level. A master's course is 「修士|しゅうし」, and a doctorate is 「博士|はくし」. The doctorate kanji can also be read 「はかせ」, which is how kids and cartoons say professor or doctor, like a mad scientist character. So a conversation might go like this. 「だいがくいんせいです」. 「しゅうしですか」. 「はい、しゅうしのにねんせいです」, yes, second year of my master's. Notice that 「ねんせい」, year student, works for grad school too. To say what you research, you can use 「せんこう」 just like an undergraduate. Japanese grad students often spend long hours in a research lab with their professor and labmates, which can feel like a second family. It's also a common reason for international students to come to Japan, sometimes on a scholarship. Name your level, and you'll sound like you've been on campus for years." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「大学|だいがく」 plus 「院|いん」, institution, gives graduate school, and adding 「生|せい」 makes a graduate student. 「院|いん」 also appears in 「病院|びょういん」, hospital.",
      "「院|いん」 is a building with a purpose, as in 「病院|びょういん」 and 「入院|にゅういん」, entering hospital. Grad students are often just called 「院生|いんせい」.",
      "Name your level: a master's is 「修士|しゅうし」 and a doctorate is 「博士|はくし」, as in 「しゅうしのにねんせいです」. Grad students often spend long hours in a lab that feels like family."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[だいがくいんせい]です。",
    en: "My older brother is a graduate student." },
  "だいがくせい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「大学|だいがく」, college, plus 「生|せい」, student, all with Chinese-origin readings. It's the clearest example of a pattern Japanese loves. Take a school level and add 「せい」, and you get the student at that level. So 「こうこう」 becomes 「こうこうせい」, a high school student, and 「だいがくいん」 becomes 「だいがくいんせい」, a graduate student. You might wonder how this differs from 「がくせい」, student. They overlap a lot. 「がくせい」 is the general word, and in everyday speech it often means a college student anyway. 「だいがくせい」 is more precise, and it's what you use when you want to make clear you're at college. To say your year, add 「いちねんせい」 for first year, 「にねんせい」 for second, and so on. A culture note: Japanese college entrance exams are famously hard, but many students find the college years more relaxed. To recap, college plus person, the college student." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「大|だい」, big, is one of the oldest pictures in the writing system. It shows a person standing with arms and legs spread wide, making themselves as big as possible. Its native reading is in 「大きい|おおきい」, big. In 「大学|だいがく」 it's read 「だい」, but watch out, because in some words it becomes 「たい」, as in 「大変|たいへん」, tough or terrible. The second kanji, 「学|がく」, learning, has 「子|こ」, child, at the bottom, under a roof-like shape. Its older, more complex form also had hands at the top, and it's usually explained as children being taught under a roof. That's a lovely image for a school. Then 「生|せい」, life or person, finishes the word. Its native reading is in 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born. So in one word you have a big person, a child learning under a roof, and life itself. Three pictures stacked up give you a college student." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases real Japanese college students use. To give your year, say 「だいがくのにねんせいです」, I'm a second-year at college, or just 「にねんせいです」 if it's clear you're at college. To ask someone, it's 「なんねんせいですか」, what year are you? Students also talk about their faculty with 「がくぶ」, as in literature faculty or economics faculty, and many say which school they attend by name. Clubs are a big deal. Instead of the word club, students often say 「サークル」, from the English word circle, for relaxed hobby groups. And for part-time work they say 「バイト」. So a typical self-introduction at a party might be 「だいがくせいです。にねんせいです。バイトはカフェです」. In the third year, Japanese students start job hunting, often all wearing the same black suits to interviews, which is a famous sight in spring. Year, faculty, club, part-time job. Those four topics will carry you through any campus conversation." }
    ],
    short: [
      "College plus 「生|せい」 gives a college student, the clearest case of adding 「せい」 to a school level. 「がくせい」 is the general word, while 「だいがくせい」 makes clear you're at college.",
      "「大|だい」 is a person with limbs spread wide, 「学|がく」 is a child learning under a roof, and 「生|せい」 is life. Three pictures stacked up give you a college student.",
      "Give your year with 「にねんせいです」 and ask others with 「なんねんせいですか」. Faculty, 「サークル」 clubs and 「バイト」 will carry you through any campus chat."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[だいがくせい]です。",
    en: "My older sister is a college student." },
  "べんごし|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written 「弁護士|べんごし」. All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「弁|べん」 means to speak or argue, to make a case. 「護|ご」 means to protect or guard. Together, 「弁護|べんご」 means to defend someone by speaking for them, which is exactly what a defense lawyer does in court. The last kanji, 「士|し」, means a trained professional, someone with a qualification. So a lawyer is a qualified professional who protects people by arguing for them. The protect kanji also appears in 「看護師|かんごし」, a nurse, someone who watches over and protects patients. Notice that the endings differ, though. A nurse ends in a different kanji for professional, so the two words share 「護|ご」, protect, but not their last kanji. In Japan, passing the bar exam is famously difficult, and lawyers are often called 「せんせい」, like teachers and doctors. To recap, speak plus protect plus professional, the lawyer." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「士|し」 marks a qualified professional, and it builds several job words you'll meet. 「消防士|しょうぼうし」 is a firefighter, a professional who extinguishes and prevents fires. 「宇宙飛行士|うちゅうひこうし」 is an astronaut, a professional who flies through space. And of course 「弁護士|べんごし」, a lawyer. But Japanese has other professional endings that sound exactly the same. 「看護師|かんごし」, nurse, ends in 「師|し」, a kanji that suggests a skilled expert or teacher. 「医者|いしゃ」, doctor, ends in 「者|しゃ」, a general word for person. You can't hear the difference between 「士|し」 and 「師|し」, so the kanji is the only clue. A simple way to sort them is that 「士|し」 often goes with a licence or a trained role, like lawyer or firefighter, while 「師|し」 often goes with a skill you practise, like nursing. Learn each job word as a whole, and let the endings be a helpful hint." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「弁|べん」, has an unusual history. In the postwar spelling reforms, this simple shape replaced several older, more complex kanji that shared the same sound. One of them meant to speak or argue, which is the sense in 「弁護士|べんごし」. Others meant to distinguish, or a petal. That's why 「弁|べん」 pops up in words that don't seem related. It's in 「弁当|べんとう」, a boxed lunch, and in words for regional dialects, like 「大阪弁|おおさかべん」, Osaka dialect, where it's back to the speech meaning. The second kanji, 「護|ご」, protect, has the speech kanji 「言|げん」, words, on its left. So the lawyer word has speech in the first half and speech in the second, which suits a job that's all about talking for someone. When you see 「弁|べん」, don't expect one fixed meaning. It's a stand-in for several old kanji, so check the word it's in." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In daily Japanese, lawyers are treated with a lot of respect. People often address them as 「せんせい」, the same title used for teachers, doctors, and politicians. So a client might say 「せんせい、よろしくおねがいします」 at a first meeting. When talking about a lawyer in third person, you might hear 「べんごしさん」, which is polite but less formal than 「せんせい」. Becoming a lawyer in Japan has traditionally meant passing a very tough national exam, and there are far fewer lawyers per person than in the United States. People tend to settle disagreements without going to court when they can, so suing someone is less of an everyday idea. On TV, though, legal dramas are popular, with clever lawyers solving cases. If you want to say your dream job, it's 「べんごしになりたいです」, I want to become a lawyer. Notice the particle 「に」 with 「なる」. Call them 「せんせい」, and you'll be on the right side of the courtroom." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「弁|べん」 means to argue a case, 「護|ご」 means protect, and 「士|し」 marks a qualified professional. So a lawyer is a professional who protects people by speaking for them.",
      "The ending 「士|し」 marks licensed roles like 「消防士|しょうぼうし」 and 「弁護士|べんごし」, while the same-sounding 「師|し」 in 「看護師|かんごし」 suggests a practised skill. Only the kanji tells them apart.",
      "After postwar reforms, 「弁|べん」 stands in for several old kanji, so it appears in 「弁当|べんとう」 and 「大阪弁|おおさかべん」 too. 「護|ご」 has the speech kanji on its left, fitting a job of talking.",
      "Lawyers in Japan are often addressed as 「せんせい」, and the bar exam is famously tough. For a dream job, say 「べんごしになりたいです」, with 「に」 and 「なる」."
    ],
    ja: "メアリーさんのおとうさんは[べんごし]です。",
    en: "Mary's father is a lawyer." },
  "おかあさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Written 「お母さん|おかあさん」. The center is 「かあ」, from the mother kanji, 「母|かあ」. Around it you have a polite 「お」 on the front and the respectful 「さん」 on the end. So it's a respectful way to say mother. Here's the key rule, and English speakers trip over it all the time. When you talk about someone else's mother, you use 「おかあさん」. When you talk about your own mother to someone outside the family, you use the humble word 「はは」, written with the same kanji. Inside your own family, though, you can call your mother 「おかあさん」 to her face. This inside versus outside idea runs through all the family words. 「おとうさん」 and 「ちち」 work the same way for father, and 「おねえさん」 and 「あね」 for an older sister. A way to remember it: honor other people's family, and stay humble about your own. To recap, the respectful mother, for other people's moms." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「母|はは」 is traditionally explained as a picture of a woman with two dots added for the breasts, showing a mother who nurses her child. Compare it with 「女|おんな」, woman, and you can still see the kneeling figure inside, with the two dots tucked in the middle. The kanji has three readings to know. 「はは」 is the plain native reading, used for your own mother. 「かあ」 is a special reading that appears only inside 「お母さん|おかあさん」 and its casual cousins. And the Chinese-origin reading 「ぼ」 shows up in compounds. 「祖母|そぼ」 is your own grandmother, and 「母国|ぼこく」 is your home country, literally mother country, just like in English. There's also 「母の日|ははのひ」, Mother's Day. Careful with the stroke order: the two dots are written last. So one shape gives you mom, mother country, and grandmother. Look for the two dots, and you'll always spot the mother." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "It might surprise you that 「おかあさん」 hasn't always been the standard word. In the Edo period, people used many different words for mother depending on their class and region. Samurai families, merchants, and country people each had their own terms. The form 「おかあさん」 is thought to have existed in some circles, but it really spread nationwide in the early twentieth century, when it was used in the government's school reading books. Children all over Japan learned it in class, and it became the normal word. The same thing happened with 「おとうさん」. Today there are still lots of variations. Little kids say 「ママ」. Grown men sometimes call their mother 「おふくろ」 when talking to friends, which sounds rough and affectionate. And 「かあさん」 without the 「お」 is a little more casual. So the word you're learning is the polite, standard version that school helped make universal. When in doubt, 「おかあさん」 is always safe." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Here's a quirk of Japanese family life that surprises many learners. Once a couple has children, they often start calling each other 「おかあさん」 and 「おとうさん」, mom and dad, even when the kids aren't around. The family tends to use names from the point of view of the youngest child. So a husband might call his wife 「おかあさん」, and she might call him 「おとうさん」. Grandparents get called 「おばあちゃん」 and 「おじいちゃん」 by everyone, including their own grown children. It's not considered strange or unromantic. It just reflects each person's role in the family. On Mother's Day, the second Sunday in May, children give red carnations, as in many countries. And in conversations with strangers, a shopkeeper might even call a customer with a child 「おかあさん」. So this word is less a name and more a role. In a Japanese home, mom is often mom to everyone." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「おかあさん」 wraps the mother kanji in a polite 「お」 and respectful 「さん」, so use it for other people's mothers. For your own mother when talking to outsiders, use the humble 「はは」.",
      "「母|はは」 pictures a woman with two dots for nursing a child, and it reads 「はは」, 「かあ」, or 「ぼ」 as in 「母国|ぼこく」. Look for the two dots and you'll always spot the mother.",
      "「おかあさん」 spread nationwide in the early twentieth century through government school readers, replacing many class and regional words. When in doubt, it's always the safe choice.",
      "Once a couple has kids, they often call each other 「おかあさん」 and 「おとうさん」, naming roles from the youngest child's view. In a Japanese home, mom is often mom to everyone."
    ],
    ja: "[おかあさん]は{先生|せんせい}ですか。",
    en: "Is your mother a teacher?" },
  "おとうさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Written 「お父さん|おとうさん」. In the middle is 「とう」, from the father kanji, 「父|とう」. It's wrapped with the polite 「お」 in front and the respectful 「さん」 at the end. So this is the respectful word for father. It follows the same rule as 「おかあさん」. When you talk about someone else's father, say 「おとうさん」, as in 「たけしさんのおとうさん」, Takeshi's father. When you talk about your own father to people outside your family, switch to the humble word 「ちち」, written with the same kanji. At home, you can call your own father 「おとうさん」 directly. Kids often shorten it to 「とうさん」 or say the casual 「パパ」. A common mistake is introducing your own dad as 「おとうさん」 in a self-introduction. Japanese listeners will understand, but it sounds a little childish. To recap, the respectful father, with 「ちち」 for your own dad when speaking to others." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「父|ちち」 is traditionally explained as a hand holding a tool, perhaps a stick or a stone axe. It's an image of the person who works, or who leads and disciplines the family. You can see the crossed strokes at the bottom, like an arm and a tool held together. Like mother, it has several readings. 「ちち」 is the plain native reading for your own father. 「とう」 is a special reading used in 「お父さん|おとうさん」. The Chinese-origin reading 「ふ」 appears in compounds such as 「祖父|そふ」, your own grandfather. Pair it with 「母|ぼ」 and you get 「父母|ふぼ」, parents, a formal word you might see on school paperwork. There's also 「父の日|ちちのひ」, Father's Day, in June. A memory trick, not history: the top two strokes look like a pair of raised eyebrows, the face dad makes when you come home late. Hand with a tool, or raised eyebrows, whichever sticks better, that's father." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word lives right next to two lookalikes, so let's sort them out by sound. 「おとうさん」 has a long 「とう」, held for two beats, followed by 「さん」. The whole word has five beats: 「お」, 「と」, 「う」, 「さ」, 「ん」. Little brother, 「おとうと」, has the same opening but ends with a short 「と」 instead of 「さん」. And 「おっと」, husband, has a small 「っ」, a tiny pause, and no long vowel at all. Three words, three different rhythms. If you rush the long vowel in 「おとうさん」, you can end up sounding like you're saying something strange. A good practice is to say them in a row, slowly: 「おとうさん」, 「おとうと」, then the humble 「ちち」. Clap the beats if it helps. Kids sometimes drop the front and say 「とうさん」, or go casual with 「パパ」. Get the long vowel right, and dad, little brother, and husband will never get tangled." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Let's walk through how 「おとうさん」 actually comes up in conversation. When you meet a friend's family, you might be introduced to their dad and say 「はじめまして」 with a bow. Talking about him later, you'd say 「たけしさんのおとうさん」, Takeshi's father, adding the friend's name and 「の」. If you're asking about his job, 「おとうさんのおしごとはなんですか」 is polite and natural. The answer would use the humble word: 「ちちはかいしゃいんです」. In Japanese households, dads have traditionally been seen as the main earners who work long hours, though that's changing as more fathers take parental leave and share housework. On TV and in comics, you'll still see the classic dad who reads the newspaper at breakfast. There's even a familiar phrase, 「おとうさん、おかえりなさい」, welcome home, dad, when he gets back from work. So ask about other people's 「おとうさん」, and talk about your own 「ちち」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「おとうさん」 is the father kanji wrapped in 「お」 and 「さん」, used for someone else's father or your own at home. To outsiders, call your own dad 「ちち」.",
      "「父|ちち」 is traditionally a hand holding a tool, reading 「ちち」, 「とう」, or 「ふ」 as in 「祖父|そふ」. As a memory trick, the top strokes look like dad's raised eyebrows.",
      "「おとうさん」 has five beats with a long 「とう」, unlike short-ending 「おとうと」, little brother, and 「おっと」, husband. Get the long vowel right and they never tangle.",
      "Refer to a friend's dad as 「たけしさんのおとうさん」 and ask about his job politely. When others ask about yours, answer with the humble 「ちちはかいしゃいんです」."
    ],
    ja: "[おとうさん]は{会社員|かいしゃいん}ですか。",
    en: "Is your father an office worker?" },
  "おねえさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Written 「お姉さん|おねえさん」. The core is 「ねえ」, from the older sister kanji, 「姉|ねえ」, which has the woman kanji, 「女|おんな」, on its left. Around it are the polite 「お」 and the respectful 「さん」. Like the other family words, it has two sides. You use 「おねえさん」 for someone else's older sister, or to talk to your own older sister directly. When talking about your own older sister to outsiders, use the humble word 「あね」. The older brother pair works the same way, 「おにいさん」 and 「あに」. Younger siblings don't usually get the polite wrap. You just say 「いもうと」 or 「おとうと」. There's a fun extra use. People also call a young woman they don't know 「おねえさん」, for example a shop clerk or a waitress, as a friendly way to get her attention. To recap, the respectful older sister, with 「あね」 for your own when talking to others." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese families, how siblings address each other follows a clear rule. Younger siblings usually call older siblings by their role, not their name. So a little brother calls his big sister 「おねえちゃん」 or 「ねえちゃん」, and rarely uses her first name. Older siblings, on the other hand, call younger ones by name. A big sister would just call her little brother by his name. The same goes for older brothers, who get called 「おにいちゃん」. The 「ちゃん」 ending is the warm, family version, while 「さん」 is more polite. This reflects how much Japanese cares about who is older. Even twins sometimes keep track of who was born first. When younger kids grow up, some switch to their sister's name or a nickname, but many keep saying 「おねえちゃん」 all their lives. So if you hear a grown woman being called 「おねえちゃん」, she's almost certainly somebody's big sister. Role for the elder, name for the younger." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「姉|あね」 has 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left, telling you it's about a female person. The right side looks like the kanji for city or market, but in fact it comes from a different, older shape that mainly gave the sound. So don't try to find a market inside your sister. As a memory trick only, you can picture your big sister taking you shopping at the market, because she's old enough to go alone. The kanji has three readings to know. 「あね」 is the plain native reading for your own older sister. 「ねえ」 is the special reading in 「お姉さん|おねえさん」. And the Chinese-origin reading 「し」 shows up in 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters, which pairs older sister with younger sister. You'll also see 「姉妹|しまい」 in the phrase for sister cities between towns in different countries. So woman on the left, a sound hint on the right, and a market for your memory." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond family, 「おねえさん」 has a whole public life. Young children call any girl or young woman 「おねえさん」, like a teenager babysitting or a teacher's helper. Adults use it to call a waitress or a shop clerk who seems around their age or younger, as in 「おねえさん、すみません」. It's friendly, but some people find it a bit too casual, so 「すみません」 alone is the safest choice for a learner. There's a delicate side too. Women of a certain age may be pleased to be called 「おねえさん」 rather than 「おばさん」, auntie, which can make someone feel old. So a clever shopkeeper will often choose 「おねえさん」 as flattery. On TV, you'll hear 「おねえさん」 for the young woman who presents children's shows. In Genki, though, you'll mostly use it plainly, for someone else's older sister: 「おねえさんはだいがくせいですか」. Family word first, then a friendly public word, used with care." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「おねえさん」 wraps the older sister kanji in 「お」 and 「さん」 for someone else's sister or your own to her face. To outsiders, your own older sister is 「あね」.",
      "Younger siblings call older ones by role, like 「おねえちゃん」, while older siblings call younger ones by name. It's role for the elder and name for the younger.",
      "「姉|あね」 has 「女|おんな」 on the left and a sound part on the right that only looks like market. Its readings are 「あね」, 「ねえ」, and 「し」 in 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters.",
      "People also call young women like waitresses 「おねえさん」, often as flattery instead of 「おばさん」. For a learner, plain 「すみません」 is the safest way to get attention."
    ],
    ja: "[おねえさん]は{大学生|だいがくせい}ですか。",
    en: "Is your older sister a college student?" },
  "おにいさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「お兄さん|おにいさん」. The middle piece, 「兄|にい」, is the kanji for older brother, and in this word it has a special reading that only shows up here. Around it sit two polite wrappers: 「お」 at the front and 「さん」 at the back, the same 「さん」 you put after people's names. Because it's wrapped in politeness, this is the word for someone else's older brother, or for calling out to your own big brother at home. When you talk about your own older brother to other people, you drop the wrapping and use the humble word 「兄|あに」, the same kanji with its native reading. The same pattern runs through the whole family. 「おねえさん」 is someone's older sister, and 「姉|あね」 is your own. 「おかあさん」 and 「おとうさん」 work the same way for mother and father. You'll also hear 「おにいさん」 used for any young man, for example a shopkeeper calling out to a guy in his twenties. A way to remember the shape: 「兄|にい」 is a big mouth 「口|くち」 sitting on two legs, the big brother who does all the talking. That's a hook, not a history lesson. The kanji also hides in 「兄弟|きょうだい」, brothers and sisters. In short, polite wrapper plus brother: your friend's big brother, or yours when you call him." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a sound trap with a funny side. The long 「にい」 in 「おにいさん」 is held for two beats. If you cut it short, you get 「おにさん」, which sounds like it's built on 「おに」, a demon or ogre from Japanese folklore. So a rushed 「おにさん」 sounds a bit like Mr. Ogre. As a memory scene, picture a friend's big brother who is actually very gentle, but you keep accidentally calling him an ogre, and he gets grumpier every time, slowly growing horns. That's your reminder to stretch the vowel: 「お」, 「に」, 「い」, 「さ」, 「ん」, five beats. The same care matters in 「おねえさん」, where the long 「ねえ」 also needs two beats. Japanese families even have a holiday in February called 「節分|せつぶん」, when people throw beans at someone wearing an ogre mask to chase bad luck away, often a dad or big brother. Hold the long vowel, and your big brother stays human." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「兄|あに」 has more readings than most family words, so let's lay them out. 「あに」 is the plain native reading, for your own older brother when you talk to others. 「にい」 is the special reading inside 「お兄さん|おにいさん」. 「きょう」 is a Chinese-origin reading, heard in 「兄弟|きょうだい」, siblings. And another Chinese-origin reading, 「けい」, appears in formal words you'll meet much later. Notice that 「兄弟|きょうだい」 literally means older brother plus younger brother, but people use it for siblings in general, sisters included. So when someone asks 「きょうだいがいますか」, do you have siblings, you can answer with sisters too. Kids and close family use 「おにいちゃん」 or just 「にいちゃん」. Young men among friends sometimes say 「あにき」, big bro, which has a tough, gang-movie feel. So one shape gives you polite, humble, casual, and swaggering versions of big brother. Choose the one that matches the moment." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Out in the world, 「おにいさん」 doesn't only mean somebody's brother. Street vendors, market stall holders, and shop staff often call out to young men with 「おにいさん」, like a friendly hey you there. At a festival stand, you might hear 「おにいさん、どうですか」, how about it, young man? Little kids call any boy or young man 「おにいさん」 too, like the cool teenager next door. In children's TV shows, the young male presenter is often called the singing 「おにいさん」. In the opposite direction, a man getting called 「おじさん」, uncle, can feel like a sign of getting older, so a polite clerk may tactfully go with 「おにいさん」. For you as a learner, the main use is for someone else's brother: 「おにいさんはなんさいですか」, how old is your older brother? Use it for other people's brothers, and enjoy hearing it at festival stalls." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「おにいさん」 wraps 「兄|にい」 in polite 「お」 and 「さん」, for someone else's big brother or yours when you call him. Talking to others about your own, use humble 「あに」.",
      "Cut the long 「にい」 short and you get 「おにさん」, which sounds like it's built on 「おに」, an ogre. Stretch it to five beats so your big brother stays human.",
      "「兄|あに」 reads 「あに」, 「にい」, and 「きょう」 in 「兄弟|きょうだい」, which covers all siblings. Casual forms run from 「おにいちゃん」 to swaggering 「あにき」.",
      "Vendors and shop staff call out 「おにいさん」 to any young man, often as tact instead of 「おじさん」. Your main use is someone else's brother, as in 「おにいさんはなんさいですか」."
    ],
    ja: "[おにいさん]は{何歳|なんさい}ですか。",
    en: "How old is your older brother?" },
  "いもうと|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「妹|いもうと」, read with its native reading. On the left is 「女|おんな」, woman, the same piece you see in 「女の人|おんなのひと」 and in 「姉|あね」, older sister. On the right is a piece that on its own means not yet. A way to remember it: the girl in the family who isn't grown up yet. Treat that as a hook. Unlike 「おねえさん」 and 「おにいさん」, this word has no polite 「お」 and 「さん」 built in, because younger siblings sit lower in the family order. So plain 「いもうと」 is how you talk about your own younger sister. For someone else's younger sister, you add 「さん」 and say 「いもうとさん」. Its partner is 「おとうと」, younger brother, which works the same way. Together the four sibling words make a little set: older brother and older sister get the respectful forms, younger brother and younger sister stay plain. A common slip for English speakers is to just say sister. Japanese almost always tells you whether she's older or younger, so you have to choose. Quick recap: woman plus not yet, your little sister, plain for yours and 「さん」 for someone else's." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The word itself has a long history. Linguists trace 「いもうと」 back to an older form, 「いもひと」. In ancient Japanese, 「いも」 was a warm word a man used for a woman close to him, a sister or even a sweetheart, and you can find it in very old poetry. Add 「ひと」, person, and over centuries the sounds wore down until 「いもひと」 became 「いもうと」. That middle 「う」 is the leftover trace of 「ひ」 softening away, something that happened to many old words. Along the way the meaning narrowed too. Today it only means a younger sister, and it's used by anyone, not just by brothers. Its partner word, 「おとうと」, went through almost exactly the same change, which is why the two sound like a matching pair. So inside a simple family word sits a piece of ancient love poetry, worn smooth by time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how the word shows up when people talk about family. To ask if someone has siblings, you say 「きょうだいがいますか」. A natural answer is 「いもうとがひとりいます」, I have one younger sister. Notice the pattern: the person, then 「が」, then the number, then 「います」, because people use 「います」, not 「あります」. Two sisters would be 「ふたり」. Now a lovely cultural detail. Inside a Japanese family, older siblings are often called by a title, like 「おねえちゃん」 or 「おにいちゃん」, even by their own brothers and sisters. Younger siblings, though, are simply called by their name. Nobody calls their little sister by the word for younger sister. So you'll use 「いもうと」 when describing her to others, but at home she's just her name. Mini dialogue: 「いもうとさんはなんさいですか」, how old is your younger sister? 「じゅうにさいです」, she's twelve. That's the word in its natural setting, talking about family to someone outside it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「妹|いもうと」 is woman plus a piece meaning not yet, the girl who isn't grown up. It has no polite wrap, so use plain 「いもうと」 for yours and 「いもうとさん」 for someone else's.",
      "「いもうと」 wore down from ancient 「いもひと」, where 「いも」 was a warm word for a beloved woman. The middle 「う」 is the trace of 「ひ」 softening away.",
      "Answer 「いもうとがひとりいます」, using 「が」, the count, and 「います」 for people. At home, a little sister is called by her name, not by this word."
    ],
    ja: "[いもうと]は{十六歳|じゅうろくさい}です。",
    en: "My younger sister is sixteen." },
  "おとうと|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「弟|おとうと」, read with its native reading. Be careful with the sound, because it sits very close to 「おとうさん」, father. The difference is just the ending. 「おとうと」 stops short with 「と」, while father stretches out into 「おとうさん」. A way to remember it: the little brother wants to be just like dad, so his name is almost dad's name, only shorter and without the grown up 「さん」. Like 「いもうと」, younger sister, this word has no polite wrapping built in. You use plain 「おとうと」 for your own younger brother, and 「おとうとさん」 for someone else's. The same kanji also appears in 「兄弟|きょうだい」, siblings, where 「兄|きょう」 is older brother and 「弟|だい」 is younger brother, both with their Chinese-origin readings. So the word for brothers and sisters is literally older brother plus younger brother. Japanese cares a lot about who is older, so instead of just saying brother, you'll nearly always pick older or younger. Recap: 「弟|おとうと」, the little brother whose name is almost father's, plain for yours and with 「さん」 for someone else's." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has a real history worth knowing. It comes from an older form, 「おとひと」. In old Japanese, 「おと」 meant younger, the junior one, and you can still feel it in a few old words. Add 「ひと」, person, and you get the younger person. Over the centuries, the 「ひ」 in the middle softened and dropped out, leaving a long 「う」 sound behind, and 「おとひと」 became 「おとうと」. Its sister word went the same way, from 「いもひと」 to 「いもうと」. That shared history is why the two words rhyme so neatly. It also gives you a better way to tell it apart from 「おとうさん」, father. In little brother, the long 「う」 is the worn-down trace of 「ひと」, person, and the word ends right there with 「と」. Father carries its own respectful 「さん」 on the end, because he sits above you in the family. So the younger person stays short and plain, while the honored father gets the polite ending. Remember the history, and you'll never think little brother and dad are the same word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Let's put the word to work in a conversation about family. Someone asks, 「きょうだいがいますか」, do you have brothers or sisters? You can answer 「はい、おとうとがふたりいます」, yes, I have two younger brothers. People use 「います」 with living beings, and the number comes right before it. When you ask about someone else's brother, switch to 「おとうとさん」, as in 「おとうとさんはだいがくせいですか」, is your younger brother a college student? Here's a cultural detail. In many Japanese families, the older children get a title even at home, so a little boy might call his big sister 「おねえちゃん」. But the younger ones are just called by name. So 「おとうと」 is a word for describing him, not for calling him. Also notice that 「きょうだい」, written with older brother and younger brother, is used for siblings in general, even when sisters are included. A simple wrap-up: plain for yours, 「さん」 for theirs, and his name when you call him." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「弟|おとうと」 sounds close to 「おとうさん」 but stops short with 「と」, like a little brother who wants to be dad. Use plain 「おとうと」 for yours and 「おとうとさん」 for someone else's.",
      "「おとうと」 comes from 「おとひと」, the younger person, with the 「ひ」 softening into a long 「う」. Father keeps a respectful 「さん」, while the younger person stays short and plain.",
      "Answer 「おとうとがふたりいます」, and ask about someone else's with 「おとうとさん」. At home, he's called by his name, so the word is for describing him."
    ],
    ja: "[おとうと]は{高校生|こうこうせい}です。",
    en: "My younger brother is a high school student." },
  "かがく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「科学|かがく」, two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「科|か」 means a branch, a department or a course of study. You can find it in 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, a book for a school subject. 「学|がく」 means learning or study, the same piece you already know from 「学生|がくせい」, student, 「大学|だいがく」, college, and 「学校|がっこう」, school. Put them together and science is the branches of learning, all the organised fields of knowledge. Here's a fun trap. Japanese has another word pronounced exactly the same way, 「化学|かがく」, which means chemistry. The first kanji there is different, but the sound is identical. Because that gets confusing in speech, people sometimes call chemistry 「ばけがく」 to keep the two apart, using the native reading of its first kanji. You don't need that word yet, but it's a good reminder that Japanese has many sound-alike words that only kanji can tell apart. You'll meet 「かがく」 as a college major, alongside 「けいざい」, economics, and 「れきし」, history. Recap: branch plus learning, the branches of knowledge, science." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's look at the family around the first kanji, 「科|か」. It means a branch or a category, a way of dividing things into sections. Its left side is a grain plant, and its right side is a measuring scoop, so think of sorting grain into measured portions. You'll meet it in 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, a book for one school subject. You'll see it in 「科目|かもく」, a school subject or course. Hospitals use it for departments, like 「外科|げか」, surgery, the outside department. And at school, children don't study 「かがく」 by that name. Science class is called 「理科|りか」, the subject of natural principles. So if a Japanese child says their favorite subject is 「りか」, that's science class. The second kanji, 「学|がく」, is the one you already know from 「がくせい」 and 「だいがく」. Watch how it shortens to 「がっ」 in 「学校|がっこう」. So the family shows you two pieces: a category, and learning. Science is learning sorted into branches." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how the word lives in everyday Japanese. A person who does science is a 「科学者|かがくしゃ」, scientist, adding the ending that means a person who does something. Museums of science are popular weekend outings, and many cities have one with planetariums and hands-on exhibits for kids. On the news you'll often hear 「かがく」 paired with technology, because Japan has long seen science and engineering as central to its economy, from bullet trains to robots. When you talk about your studies, you can say 「かがくがすきです」, I like science, using 「が」 with 「すき」. Or you might ask a classmate, 「せんこうはかがくですか」, is your major science? Remember one practical tip. Because chemistry sounds identical, people usually let context sort it out, but if there's any doubt, they may add a word, like natural science, to be clear. A final takeaway: 「かがく」 is the big umbrella word, used for science as a field, in museums, in the news, and in your major." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「科|か」 means a branch of study and 「学|がく」 means learning, so science is the branches of knowledge. Watch out for 「化学|かがく」, chemistry, which sounds exactly the same.",
      "「科|か」 means a category, as in 「科目|かもく」 and the hospital department 「外科|げか」. At school, science class is actually called 「理科|りか」.",
      "A scientist is a 「科学者|かがくしゃ」, and you can say 「かがくがすきです」 with 「が」. 「かがく」 is the big umbrella word for science in museums, the news, and your major."
    ],
    ja: "[かがく]の{先生|せんせい}はアメリカじんです。",
    en: "The science teacher is American." },
  "せいじ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「政治|せいじ」, two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「政|せい」 means government or administration, the business of running the state. 「治|じ」 means to govern, to rule, or to set things in order. The same kanji is also used in words about calming things down or healing, so think of it as putting things right. Together they make governing and setting the country in order, which is politics. It's a fairly formal, serious word. You'll hear it on the news and use it to talk about a field of study, as in someone's major. Grouping it with the other majors in this lesson helps. 「けいざい」 is economics, 「れきし」 is history, 「ぶんがく」 is literature, and 「かがく」 is science. To say your major, you use 「せんこう」, so a student might say 「せんこうはせいじです」, my major is politics. A way to remember the sound: picture a politician saying, say, gee, I'll fix everything. That's just a sound hook. Recap: government plus setting in order, the work of running a country, politics." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's take the two kanji apart. 「政|せい」 has two sides. On the left is 「正|せい」, which means correct or proper, the same kanji as in 「正しい|ただしい」, correct. It also gives the kanji its sound, 「せい」. On the right is a component that shows a hand holding a stick. It appears in many action kanji, such as 「教|きょう」, teach, and 「数|すう」, number. So government is making things proper through action. Now 「治|じ」. Its left side is the three drops of water. That kanji was originally the name of a river in China. Many people like to link it to flood control, since taming rivers was a ruler's big job in ancient times, but treat that as a handy hook rather than proven history. The same kanji gives you 「治る|なおる」, to get better, and it's the second half of the era name 「明治|めいじ」, the period when Japan modernized. So the pair reads as correcting and setting in order. That's the job of politics." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you know 「政治|せいじ」, a whole set of words opens up. Add the ending for a professional and you get 「政治家|せいじか」, politician, a person whose house, or trade, is politics. The same ending makes 「作家|さっか」, writer. Replace the second kanji and you get 「政府|せいふ」, the government, the actual body that runs the country. A political party is 「政党|せいとう」. Now the second kanji, 「治|じ」. It appears in 「明治|めいじ」, the Meiji era, when it's read 「じ」, just as in politics. With its native reading, it becomes 「治る|なおる」, to heal or get better, and you'll hear that when someone recovers from a cold. Do you see the link? Healing a body and governing a country are both about putting things right. Japanese also has a related idea in the parliament, called 「国会|こっかい」, the national assembly, which you'll hear on the news along with 「せいじ」. So remember the core: correct, plus put in order, and the whole political family follows." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Here's a social point that will help you in real conversations. In Japan, many people avoid talking about politics with people they don't know well, much like religion or money. Bringing it up at a first meeting or a work dinner can feel a little awkward. That doesn't mean people don't care. It's just treated as a private topic, shared with close friends or family. So if you're a student and say 「せんこうはせいじです」, people will likely react with interest in your studies rather than asking your opinions. Japanese politics has its own rhythm too. Election campaigns are short, and you'll see vans driving through neighborhoods with candidates calling out their names through loudspeakers, which surprises many visitors. Posters of candidates line up on special boards near stations. The voting age was lowered to eighteen in recent years. So if you hear a loud van shouting a name over and over, that's politics in action. The takeaway: an important word, but handle it carefully in small talk." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「政|せい」 means government and 「治|じ」 means to govern or set in order, so politics is running the country and putting things right. Say your major with 「せんこうはせいじです」.",
      "「政|せい」 is 「正|せい」, proper, plus a hand holding a stick, so it's making things proper through action. 「治|じ」 has water on its left and also gives 「治る|なおる」, to get better.",
      "Add the professional ending for 「政治家|せいじか」, politician, or swap the second kanji for 「政府|せいふ」, government. Healing with 「治る|なおる」 and governing are both about putting things right.",
      "Many Japanese people avoid politics with people they don't know well, treating it as a private topic. Campaigns are short, with vans calling out candidates' names through loudspeakers."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}の{専攻|せんこう}は[せいじ]です。",
    en: "My older brother's major is politics." },
  "ぶんがく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「文学|ぶんがく」, two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「文|ぶん」 means writing, text or sentences. You'll see the same kanji in 「文法|ぶんぽう」, grammar, the rules of sentences, and in 「文化|ぶんか」, culture, the written and civilised side of life. 「学|がく」 means learning or study, the same kanji as in 「学生|がくせい」, student, 「大学|だいがく」, college, and 「科学|かがく」, science. So literature is the study of writing. Notice how English does something similar: literature comes from a Latin word for letters. Both languages built the word out of writing. Japan has a long literary tradition. One of the world's earliest novels, The Tale of Genji, was written by a woman at the Japanese court about a thousand years ago. If you study Japanese literature, that's often where the story starts. Like the other majors in this lesson, you can say 「せんこうはぶんがくです」, my major is literature. Recap: writing plus learning, the study of written works, literature." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's zoom in on 「文|ぶん」. It's a simple shape: a lid on top, and two crossing strokes below, like a person's legs. A widely held explanation is that it began as a picture of a person with a pattern or tattoo drawn on their chest. From pattern, the meaning grew to markings, then to writing and culture. That origin is what most scholars suggest, though like many ancient pictures it's not completely certain. This kanji has two readings you'll meet. Usually it's 「ぶん」, as in 「文法|ぶんぽう」, grammar, and 「作文|さくぶん」, composition, an essay you write for class. But in 「文字|もじ」, letters or characters, it's read 「も」. And in 「文化|ぶんか」, culture, it's back to 「ぶん」. The second kanji, 「学|がく」, is learning. In its old form, it showed a child under a roof with hands guiding, a picture of a school. So literature is the study of patterns made into writing. Keep that image of patterns becoming words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese literature goes far beyond one ancient novel. Around the same time as The Tale of Genji, another court woman wrote The Pillow Book, a collection of lists and sharp observations that still feels fresh. Centuries later, the poet Basho made the 「はいく」 famous, a tiny poem of five, seven and five sounds, often with a seasonal word in it. His poem about an old pond and a frog jumping in is known all over the world. In modern times, Natsume Soseki is a giant. His novel I Am a Cat is told from a cat's point of view, and his face used to be on the thousand yen note. Today, writers like Haruki Murakami are read in dozens of languages. If you study 「にほんのぶんがく」, you'll likely read some of these. A nice thing to say to a Japanese friend is that you like Japanese novels, and ask for a recommendation. People are often happy to share a favorite. The takeaway: a thousand years of stories, from court diaries to cats." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「文|ぶん」 means writing and 「学|がく」 means learning, so literature is the study of writing, just as the English word comes from letters. Japan's tradition reaches back to The Tale of Genji.",
      "「文|ぶん」 likely began as a person with a pattern on the chest, a meaning that grew into markings and writing. It reads 「ぶん」 in 「文法|ぶんぽう」 but 「も」 in 「文字|もじ」.",
      "Japanese literature runs from court writing like The Pillow Book to Basho's 「はいく」 and Soseki's novel about a cat. Asking a Japanese friend for a novel recommendation is a nice way to connect."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[ぶんがく]の{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "She's a teacher of Japanese literature." },
  "かさ|傘": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「傘|かさ」 uses its native reading here. This kanji is one of the easiest to picture. Look at its shape: a wide roof on top, like the canopy of an umbrella, and underneath it, little 「人|ひと」 shapes, people, huddled together. In the middle runs a straight line like the handle. So the kanji is drawn as an open umbrella with people sheltering under it. Some cultural notes help it stick. In Japan it rains a lot, especially in the rainy season in early summer, so umbrellas are everywhere. Convenience stores sell cheap clear plastic umbrellas, and when it suddenly pours, everyone buys one. Shops and offices often have an umbrella stand or plastic sleeves at the door so you don't drip on the floor. Sadly, those identical clear umbrellas also go missing a lot, because they're easy to mix up. Pair it with 「あめ」, rain, which you'll meet a little later. A typical question you can already ask: 「わたしのかさはどれですか」, which one is my umbrella? Recap: a roof with people under it, the umbrella." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases Japanese people actually use with an umbrella. To open it and hold it up, you don't say open. You say 「かさをさす」, a special verb for holding an umbrella over your head. To close it, you say 「かさをとじる」. Umbrellas are counted with 「ほん」, the counter for long thin things, so one umbrella is 「いっぽん」. When you arrive at a shop on a rainy day, look for the umbrella stand, called 「かさたて」, or the plastic sleeves at the entrance. A very common moment: you leave home without one, it starts pouring, and you dash into a convenience store to buy a cheap clear umbrella. A small mini dialogue. 「かさ、ありますか」, do you have an umbrella? 「いいえ、ありません」, no, I don't. 「じゃあ、いっしょにどうぞ」, then come under mine. Sharing an umbrella with someone is a classic romantic image in Japan. The takeaway: umbrellas are held up with 「さす」 and counted like pencils." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen closely to what happens when 「かさ」 joins other words. It often softens into 「がさ」. Rain umbrella is 「雨傘|あまがさ」. Notice two changes there. Rain, 「あめ」, turns into 「あま」, and umbrella turns into 「がさ」. A parasol for the sun is 「日傘|ひがさ」, sun umbrella, and you'll see many people using them in hot Japanese summers to protect their skin. A folding umbrella is 「折りたたみ傘|おりたたみがさ」, and it's the one people keep in their bag just in case. This softening of the first sound is very common in Japanese compounds, so you'll hear it again and again. One more sound note. A different word, also pronounced 「かさ」 and written with another kanji, means a traditional woven straw or bamboo hat, the wide cone shape you see in old pictures of travelers and rice farmers. Both keep rain off your head, so they're easy to connect. Wrap-up: 「かさ」 on its own, 「がさ」 at the end of a compound." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick to tie the sound to the meaning. Picture a sudden storm in Tokyo. Everyone runs into the same tiny convenience store. There's one clear plastic umbrella left on the rack. Two people reach for it at once, and the clerk shouts, hey, it's the last one, it's the last case! Case, case, 「かさ」. In the end they share it, standing squeezed together under the single umbrella as they walk out into the rain. Now add the kanji. Look at the shape: a big roof spread wide on top, and under it, several little person shapes packed tightly together. That's exactly the scene, a crowd of people huddled under one umbrella. So whenever you see the kanji, picture the last plastic umbrella in the store, with everyone crammed underneath it. That's just a memory trick, of course, but it locks the sound, the meaning and the shape together. Wrap-up: the last case in the store, and everyone squeezed underneath." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「傘|かさ」 is drawn as an open umbrella: a wide roof on top, little people huddled underneath, and a handle down the middle. With so much rain, cheap clear umbrellas are everywhere in Japan.",
      "You don't open an umbrella in Japanese, you hold it up with 「かさをさす」. Umbrellas are counted with 「ほん」, like pencils, so one is 「いっぽん」.",
      "In compounds 「かさ」 softens to 「がさ」, as in 「雨傘|あまがさ」 and 「日傘|ひがさ」, a parasol. A folding umbrella is 「折りたたみ傘|おりたたみがさ」.",
      "Picture two people grabbing the last umbrella in a store while the clerk shouts it's the last case, giving 「かさ」. They share it, just like the people huddled under the kanji's roof."
    ],
    ja: "[傘|かさ]はどこですか。",
    en: "Where is the umbrella?" },
  "くつ|靴": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「靴|くつ」 uses its native reading. The left side of the kanji is 「革|かわ」, which means leather or animal hide, and it shows up in many kanji for things made of leather. The right side mainly hints at the sound in Chinese, so you don't need to find a meaning there. Leather plus a sound hint gives you shoes, which for most of history were made of leather. The same leather piece appears in another word from this lesson, 「鞄|かばん」, bag. That's a nice pair: both things were made of leather. One very important cultural note goes with shoes. In Japan you take your shoes off when you enter a home, and often in some schools, temples and traditional restaurants too. There's a small entrance area just inside the door where shoes are left, and slippers are often waiting for you. Later you'll learn the verb 「はく」, to put on clothes below the waist, and that's the verb Japanese uses for putting on shoes too. A question you can already ask: 「これはだれのくつですか」, whose shoes are these? Recap: leather on the left, shoes, and take them off at the door." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Shoes have a big family, and listen for a sound change as you go. Socks are 「靴下|くつした」, literally under the shoes, which is exactly where they go. Now watch the first sound soften. Rain boots are 「長靴|ながぐつ」, long shoes, and the 「く」 turns into 「ぐ」. Leather dress shoes are 「革靴|かわぐつ」, leather shoes, again with 「ぐつ」. Sneakers for sports are 「運動靴|うんどうぐつ」, exercise shoes. This softening at the start of the second word is very common in Japanese compounds, so expect it whenever shoes come at the end. At school, children change into indoor shoes called 「上履き|うわばき」, and the cupboard at the entrance where shoes are stored is the 「下駄箱|げたばこ」, named after the old wooden sandals called 「げた」, even though nobody keeps sandals in it anymore. So one little word leads to socks, boots, sneakers and school shoes. Wrap-up: 「くつ」 on its own, 「ぐつ」 when it joins the end of a word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Shoes come with their own verbs and their own manners. To put shoes on, you say 「くつをはく」. To take them off, you say 「くつをぬぐ」. Those two verbs are the heart of the Japanese entrance ritual. When you visit a home, you step into the 「げんかん」, the entrance area, which sits a little lower than the rest of the house. You take off your shoes there and step up into the house without letting your socks touch the entrance floor. A polite extra touch is to turn your shoes so the toes point toward the door, ready for leaving. Your host might offer slippers. Here's a common mistake visitors make. Many homes keep separate slippers just for the toilet, so you switch into those and must remember to switch back. Walking out wearing the toilet slippers is a famous, slightly embarrassing slip. A tiny dialogue at the door: 「どうぞ、あがってください」, please come in, and you reply 「おじゃまします」. Wrap-up: 「はく」 to put on, 「ぬぐ」 to take off, and turn them toward the door." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「靴|くつ」 has 「革|かわ」, leather, on its left plus a sound hint, because shoes were made of leather. In Japan you take your shoes off when entering a home.",
      "「くつ」 softens to 「ぐつ」 at the end of a compound, as in 「長靴|ながぐつ」 and 「運動靴|うんどうぐつ」. Socks are 「靴下|くつした」, literally under the shoes.",
      "Put shoes on with 「はく」 and take them off with 「ぬぐ」 in the 「げんかん」, then turn them toward the door. Don't forget to switch out of the toilet slippers."
    ],
    ja: "これはだれの[靴|くつ]ですか。",
    en: "Whose shoes are these?" },
  "さかな|魚": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「魚|さかな」 uses its native reading here. This kanji started as a picture of a fish, set upright. At the top is the head, in the middle a square with a cross that looks like a scaly body, and the four dots along the bottom were once the tail fins. Those four dots look like the dots meaning fire in some other kanji, but here they come from the tail, so don't let them fool you. Once you see the fish standing on its nose, you won't forget it. The kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「ぎょ」, used in compound words, and it appears as a part in the kanji for many individual kinds of fish. Sushi restaurants sometimes have big cups covered in those fish kanji. A small extra: the word 「さかな」 is also used for snacks eaten with drinks, and in that sense it's traditionally explained as coming from words for sake plus side dish. Fish is central to Japanese food, from sushi and grilled fish at breakfast to fish markets in every city. You'll often hear 「さかな」 next to 「にく」, meat, when choosing a meal. Recap: a picture of a fish, head up, tail fins as four dots." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Here's a real piece of word history. For most of Japanese history, the everyday word for fish was not 「さかな」 at all. It was 「うお」, and you can still hear it today. Meanwhile, 「さかな」 meant something to eat alongside sake, a side dish for drinking. Fish was such a popular thing to eat with sake that, over time, the word for drinking snack slid over to mean fish itself. By around the Edo period, 「さかな」 had become the ordinary word for fish, and 「うお」 became more old-fashioned. But 「うお」 didn't disappear. It survives in compounds like 「魚市場|うおいちば」, fish market, and in some place names and shop names. So the same kanji can be read 「さかな」 or 「うお」, and the history explains why. The sense of drinking snack still exists too, usually written another way. Wrap-up: fish took over the name of the snack that went with sake." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「魚|ぎょ」 has a Chinese-origin reading that pops up in fun words. A goldfish is 「金魚|きんぎょ」, gold fish. A mermaid is 「人魚|にんぎょ」, person fish, which is a perfect description. Fishing as an industry uses it too, though you'll meet those words later. Now the fish shape also works as a part inside other kanji. Look on the left side of many kanji for kinds of fish and you'll find it. Tuna is 「鮪|まぐろ」. Salmon is 「鮭|さけ」. Sea bream, the fish eaten at celebrations, is 「鯛|たい」. Mackerel is 「鯖|さば」. You don't need to learn those kanji now, but once you know the fish shape, you can at least guess that a strange kanji on a menu is some kind of fish. And remember the soft sound change in compounds: grilled fish is 「焼き魚|やきざかな」, with 「さ」 turning into 「ざ」. Wrap-up: 「さかな」 alone, 「うお」 in old compounds, 「ぎょ」 in Chinese-style words, and the fish shape on the left of fish names." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Fish is everywhere in daily Japanese life, so here are some useful moments. A fish shop is 「さかなや」, and supermarkets have a big fresh fish section with whole fish and neatly cut slices. A traditional Japanese breakfast often includes a small piece of grilled fish with rice and miso soup. Raw sliced fish is 「さしみ」. At a restaurant, you might be asked to choose between meat and fish, and you can say 「さかなをおねがいします」, fish, please. If you don't like fish, you can say 「さかながちょっと」, a soft, polite way to say fish is a bit difficult for me, letting the sentence trail off. That trailing-off style is a very Japanese way of saying no without being blunt. One more thing to notice: Japanese doesn't separate one fish from many fish, so 「さかな」 covers both. Live fish in a tank are counted with 「ひき」, the counter for small animals. Wrap-up: 「さかなをおねがいします」 to order it, and 「さかながちょっと」 to gently turn it down." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「魚|さかな」 began as a picture of a fish standing on its nose, with the four dots at the bottom once its tail fins. Don't mistake those dots for fire.",
      "The old word for fish was 「うお」, while 「さかな」 meant a side dish for sake. Fish was such a popular snack that the name slid over, and 「うお」 survives in 「魚市場|うおいちば」.",
      "The reading 「ぎょ」 gives 「金魚|きんぎょ」 and 「人魚|にんぎょ」, and the fish shape sits on the left of fish names like 「鮭|さけ」. In compounds it softens, as in 「焼き魚|やきざかな」.",
      "Order it with 「さかなをおねがいします」, or turn it down softly with a trailing 「さかながちょっと」. Live fish in a tank are counted with 「ひき」."
    ],
    ja: "あれは{日本|にほん}の[魚|さかな]です。",
    en: "That is a Japanese fish." },
  "しんぶん|新聞": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「新聞|しんぶん」 is two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「新|しん」 means new. You'll recognise it from 「新しい|あたらしい」, new, where it takes its native reading, and from 「新幹線|しんかんせん」, the bullet train, the new trunk line. 「聞|ぶん」 means to hear. Look at its shape: it's a gate, 「門|もん」, with an ear, 「耳|みみ」, inside it, like someone listening at a gate. The same kanji is the verb 「聞く|きく」, to listen or to hear, with its native reading. So a newspaper is literally new things heard, the latest news that's going around. Back before mass printing, news really did travel by word of mouth, so the idea of hearing fits. Newspapers are still big in Japan. Some of the largest papers in the world by circulation are Japanese, and many homes get a morning paper delivered. A useful sentence: 「これはにほんのしんぶんです」, this is a Japanese newspaper. Recap: new plus hear, the new things you hear about, the newspaper." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has a surprising history. The two kanji came from Chinese, where the combination meant news, newly heard information. In fact, in modern Chinese, that word still means news, not newspaper. When printed papers spread in Japan in the eighteen hundreds, during the Meiji era, the word was used for the papers that carried that news. Over time, the Japanese meaning narrowed, and 「しんぶん」 came to mean the newspaper itself, the printed object. So for news on television or online, Japanese borrowed an English word instead: 「ニュース」. That gives you a neat pair. 「ニュース」 is the information, and 「しんぶん」 is the paper you hold in your hands. If you visit China and read the same kanji there, remember that it means news, not newspaper. Even within East Asia, words can drift apart. Wrap-up: once it meant news, and in Japan it settled into meaning the paper that brings it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how newspapers fit into daily life in Japan. To read the paper is 「しんぶんをよむ」. A newspaper company is a 「しんぶんしゃ」, adding the word for company. Many households subscribe, and big papers come in two editions a day: the morning edition, 「朝刊|ちょうかん」, and the evening edition, 「夕刊|ゆうかん」. Early in the morning, delivery workers on motorbikes or bicycles drop papers into mailboxes all over the neighborhood. Some students even do paper delivery as a part-time job, getting up before dawn. On trains, you used to see businessmen folding their newspapers lengthwise into long narrow strips so they could read without bumping their neighbors, a neat bit of crowded train etiquette. These days, more people read on their phones, but the paper is still a familiar sight. A useful sentence: 「まいあさしんぶんをよみます」, I read the newspaper every morning. Wrap-up: read it with 「よむ」, morning and evening editions, delivered to your door." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「新|しん」 means new and 「聞|ぶん」, an ear inside a gate, means hear, so a newspaper is new things heard. Many Japanese homes still get a morning paper delivered.",
      "In Chinese the word still means news, but in Japan it narrowed to the printed paper. For news on TV or online, Japanese uses 「ニュース」 instead.",
      "Read it with 「しんぶんをよむ」, and know that big papers come in morning and evening editions, 「朝刊|ちょうかん」 and 「夕刊|ゆうかん」. Delivery workers drop them in mailboxes before dawn."
    ],
    ja: "これは{日本|にほん}の[新聞|しんぶん]です。",
    en: "This is a Japanese newspaper." },
  "じてんしゃ|自転車": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「自転車|じてんしゃ」 is three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. 「自|じ」 means self, one's own. You've got it in 「自分で|じぶんで」, by oneself. 「転|てん」 means to turn or to roll. Notice it has 「車|くるま」, vehicle, on its left side, because wheels turn. You'll meet it again in 「運転する|うんてんする」, to drive, literally to move and turn. 「車|しゃ」 means vehicle or car. With its native reading it's 「車|くるま」, car, and with this Chinese-origin reading it's the end of 「電車|でんしゃ」, train, the electric vehicle. So a bicycle is a self turning vehicle, a vehicle whose wheels you turn with your own power. Each piece is doing honest work, which makes it easy to rebuild the word if you forget it. Bicycles are everywhere in Japan. People ride to the station, to school and to the supermarket, often with a basket on the front, and there are huge bicycle parking areas near train stations. In casual speech people also say 「チャリ」 for a bike. Recap: self plus turn plus vehicle, the bike you power yourself." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The last kanji, 「車|しゃ」, vehicle, builds a whole family, and the first kanji gives a fun twist. Compare 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, bicycle, with 「自動車|じどうしゃ」, automobile. Both start with self. A bicycle is a self-turning vehicle, and an automobile is a self-moving vehicle, one that runs without a horse pulling it. The English word automobile was built the same way, from words for self and moving. Now look at other 「しゃ」 words. 「電車|でんしゃ」, train, is the electric vehicle. A wheelchair is 「車いす|くるまいす」, wheel chair, where the kanji takes its native reading, 「くるま」. A parking lot is 「駐車場|ちゅうしゃじょう」, the place for stopping vehicles. And the first kanji, 「自|じ」, also gives you 「自分|じぶん」, oneself. So knowing these three kanji lets you read a lot of transport words on signs. Wrap-up: self-turning vehicle for the bike, self-moving vehicle for the car." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how to talk about bicycles naturally. To ride a bike, you say 「じてんしゃにのる」, and notice the particle 「に」. You ride on to the bike, not ride it directly. A very common mistake is using 「を」 here. To say you go somewhere by bike, use 「で」, as in 「じてんしゃでがっこうへいきます」, I go to school by bicycle. Bicycles are counted with 「だい」, the counter for machines, so one bike is 「いちだい」. The most common Japanese bike is nicknamed 「ママチャリ」, a sturdy city bike with a basket in front, often with a child seat, named for the moms who ride them to shops and daycare. There are also rules people take seriously. Riding two people on one bike is generally not allowed, nor is riding while holding an umbrella or looking at your phone. Bikes left in the wrong spot near stations can be towed away. Wrap-up: ride with 「に」, travel with 「で」, and count with 「だい」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick to lock in the sound. Picture a gentleman in a suit, very proud, riding a squeaky bicycle down a narrow Japanese street. He's trying to impress everyone, so he rings his little bell and shouts, gee, ten shy people are watching me! Gee, ten, shy, 「じてんしゃ」. As he says it, he wobbles, the basket on the front tips, and ten shy schoolchildren hide behind a vending machine, giggling. That's the sound hook. Now add the kanji meaning to the scene. He's pedaling by himself, that's self. The wheels spin round and round, that's turn. And the whole thing is a vehicle, that's the last kanji, which looks like a cart seen from above, with an axle and two wheels. Self, turn, vehicle. Of course, this scene is just a memory trick, but the sillier it is, the better it sticks. Wrap-up: gee, ten shy people watching a man on his self-turning vehicle." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「自|じ」 means self, 「転|てん」 means turn, and 「車|しゃ」 means vehicle, so a bicycle is a vehicle whose wheels you turn yourself. Bikes are everywhere in Japan, often with a front basket.",
      "Compare 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, the self-turning vehicle, with 「自動車|じどうしゃ」, the self-moving automobile. The 「車|しゃ」 also builds 「電車|でんしゃ」 and 「駐車場|ちゅうしゃじょう」.",
      "Ride with 「に」, as in 「じてんしゃにのる」, travel with 「で」, and count bikes with 「だい」. The everyday city bike with a basket is nicknamed 「ママチャリ」.",
      "Picture a proud man on a squeaky bike shouting gee, ten shy people are watching, giving 「じてんしゃ」. He pedals by himself, the wheels turn, and the whole thing is a vehicle."
    ],
    ja: "あれはだれの[自転車|じてんしゃ]ですか。",
    en: "Whose bicycle is that over there?" },
  "さいふ|財布": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「財布|さいふ」 is two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「財|さい」 means money, wealth or property. On its own this kanji is usually read 「ざい」, but in this word it softens to 「さい」. Look at the left side: it's 「貝|かい」, shell. In ancient China, cowrie shells were used as money, so many kanji about money, buying and value have a shell on the left. That's a real piece of history, and it makes the kanji easy to read: shell means money. 「布|ふ」 means cloth. Its native reading is 「ぬの」, but here it's the Chinese-origin reading. Put them together and a wallet is a money cloth, a cloth pouch for your wealth. Before leather wallets, people carried coins in cloth pouches, so the name fits. A cultural note: Japan was long known as a cash society, and coins are used a lot, so many wallets have a proper coin pocket. Lost wallets are also famously often handed in to the police box. A sentence you can already say: 「それはわたしのさいふです」, that's my wallet. Recap: shell for money plus cloth, a money pouch, your wallet." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The shell on the left of 「財|さい」 is your key to a whole family of money kanji, and the second kanji has a surprise relative too. Look at 「買う|かう」, to buy. Under the top part sits the same shell shape. Buying meant paying with shells. You'll meet it in 「貸す|かす」, to lend, with a shell at the bottom. 「費|ひ」, cost or expense, also has a shell below. And the first kanji itself appears in 「財産|ざいさん」, property or fortune, with its more usual reading 「ざい」. So whenever you see a shell shape in an unfamiliar kanji, there's a good chance money or value is involved. Now the second kanji, 「布|ふ」, cloth. It's the first half of 「布団|ふとん」, the futon, the cloth bedding Japanese people lay on the floor to sleep. A wallet and a futon share a kanji, because both are made of cloth. Wrap-up: shell means money, and cloth links your wallet to your futon." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Wallets carry some fun beliefs in Japan. These are folk customs, not facts, but many people know them. One popular saying is that a wallet bought in spring brings good luck. The joke is a pun: spring is 「はる」, and a similar-sounding verb means to swell up, so a spring wallet will swell with money. Another belief says yellow wallets attract money, while red ones are avoided by some people, because being in the red, 「赤字|あかじ」, means losing money. Some people also hold that long wallets let bills lie flat and comfortable, so money likes to stay. People often give wallets as gifts, and expensive brand wallets are a common present. On a more practical note, many Japanese wallets still have a roomy coin pocket, because coins up to five hundred yen are used a lot. A useful sentence if you lose one: 「さいふをおとしました」, I dropped my wallet. Wrap-up: buy it in spring, think twice about red, and keep coins handy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「財|さい」 has a shell on its left because shells were once money, and 「布|ふ」 means cloth. So a wallet is a cloth pouch for your money.",
      "A shell shape in a kanji often signals money, as in 「買う|かう」 and 「貸す|かす」. The cloth kanji 「布|ふ」 links your wallet to your 「布団|ふとん」.",
      "Folk beliefs say a wallet bought in spring swells with money, and some people avoid red because of 「赤字|あかじ」. If you lose one, say 「さいふをおとしました」."
    ],
    ja: "それはわたしの[財布|さいふ]です。",
    en: "That is my wallet." },
  "やさい|野菜": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「野菜|やさい」 is two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「野|や」 means field, open country, or wild. You'll find it in 「野球|やきゅう」, baseball, which is literally field ball. 「菜|さい」 means greens or vegetables. Look at the top: that's the grass shape, which appears in kanji for plants. Underneath is a piece showing a hand reaching over a tree, the idea of picking. So 「菜|さい」 is plants you pick to eat. Put them together and vegetables are greens from the field. That makes it easy to remember: baseball is played in a field, and vegetables are grown in one. A couple of uses help. 「やさい」 covers all kinds of vegetables, whether one or many, since Japanese nouns don't change for plural. Shops often sell vegetables individually or in small bags, and some prices can surprise visitors because good produce in Japan can be expensive. That matches the example sentence about vegetables being 「たかい」. Recap: field plus greens, plants from the field, vegetables." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word builds its own family. 「野|や」, field or wild, also gives you 「野原|のはら」, an open field or meadow, where it takes its native reading 「の」. It appears in place names, like 「長野|ながの」, Nagano, long field, the mountain area that hosted the Winter Olympics. And of course 「野球|やきゅう」, baseball, field ball. Now 「菜|さい」, greens. A common winter vegetable is 「白菜|はくさい」, Chinese cabbage, white greens, which goes into hot pot dishes. In spring, fields across Japan turn yellow with 「菜の花|なのはな」, rapeseed flowers, a classic spring scene, and here the kanji takes its native reading 「な」. People who don't eat meat follow a 「菜食|さいしょく」, a vegetable diet. So a field and some greens open up baseball, Nagano, cabbage and spring flowers. Wrap-up: 「や」 and 「の」 for fields, 「さい」 and 「な」 for greens." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Some everyday vegetable names in Japanese carry history in their sounds. Pumpkin, especially the green Japanese kind, is 「かぼちゃ」. It's widely accepted that the name comes from Cambodia. Portuguese traders brought the vegetable to Japan in the fifteen hundreds by way of Southeast Asia, and people named it after the place they believed it came from. Potato is 「じゃがいも」. The first part comes from an old Japanese name for Jakarta, in today's Indonesia, because the potato arrived through Dutch traders based there. The second part, 「いも」, means a root vegetable or tuber. Other vegetables are native or old words, like 「だいこん」, the long white radish, whose kanji literally means big root. And many newer ones are borrowed straight from English, like 「キャベツ」, cabbage, and 「レタス」, lettuce. So the vegetable aisle in a Japanese supermarket is a little map of world trade. Wrap-up: pumpkin from Cambodia, potato from Jakarta, and lettuce from English." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「野|や」 means field and 「菜|さい」, grass over a picking hand, means greens, so vegetables are greens from the field. Baseball, 「野球|やきゅう」, is played in a field too.",
      "「野|や」 reads 「の」 in 「野原|のはら」 and appears in 「長野|ながの」, while 「菜|さい」 gives 「白菜|はくさい」 and reads 「な」 in 「菜の花|なのはな」. Fields and greens open up a whole family.",
      "Vegetable names carry trade history: 「かぼちゃ」 is named after Cambodia and 「じゃがいも」 after Jakarta. Newer ones come from English, like 「キャベツ」 and 「レタス」."
    ],
    ja: "この[野菜|やさい]はたかいです。",
    en: "These vegetables are expensive." },
  "いす|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Usually written in kana, but the kanji is 「椅子|いす」. The first kanji has 「木|き」, tree or wood, on its left side, a hint that chairs were made of wood. The second kanji, 「子|す」, normally means child, but here it's used in an old, less common reading as a little suffix for objects. You'll see 「子|し」 doing that same job in 「帽子|ぼうし」, hat, also from this lesson, where it's read 「し」. It's a sort of thing-maker on the end of a word. Chairs pair naturally with 「机|つくえ」, desk, which also has the tree shape on its left. Desk and chair, both made of wood, both with a tree in their kanji. Later you'll learn the verb 「すわる」, to sit, and you'll say 「いすにすわります」, I sit on the chair. A cultural note: traditional Japanese rooms with straw mats were built for sitting on the floor, so chairs came into everyday homes more recently. A way to remember the sound: it sounds a bit like easy, as in take it easy and sit down. That's just a hook. Recap: wood plus a little object ending, the chair." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The odd reading at the end of 「椅子|いす」 has a real history. Normally the second kanji is read 「し」 or 「こ」. The reading 「す」 belongs to a later layer of Chinese readings that came into Japan in the medieval period, largely through Zen Buddhist monks who traveled to and from China. Along with tea, ink painting and temple customs, they brought new words, and chairs were part of that world, used in Zen temples long before ordinary homes had them. You can hear the same 「す」 reading in 「扇子|せんす」, a folding fan, and in 「様子|ようす」, the look or state of something. So when a word ends in that unexpected 「す」, it's often a sign of this later wave of borrowing. For centuries, most Japanese people sat on mats on the floor, and chairs were special. They only became normal in homes and schools after Western-style furniture spread from the late eighteen hundreds. Wrap-up: a reading carried in by Zen monks, for a seat that was once rare." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how chairs come up in everyday Japanese. To sit on a chair, you say 「いすにすわる」, and the particle is 「に」, because you sit onto something. A common mistake is using 「を」 there. To stand up from a chair, you'll later learn 「たつ」. Chairs are counted with 「きゃく」, a special counter for chairs and furniture with legs, though in casual speech people often just use the general counting words. Now a near-synonym to keep apart. 「いす」 is the physical object, the chair itself. A seat in the sense of your place, like a reserved seat on a train, a seat at a restaurant, or your spot in class, is 「せき」. So you'd ask a waiter for a seat by the window with 「せき」, not 「いす」. A wheelchair is 「車いす|くるまいす」, wheel chair. And the party game musical chairs is 「いすとりゲーム」, the chair-taking game. Mini dialogue: 「このいす、いいですか」, may I use this chair? 「はい、どうぞ」. Wrap-up: sit with 「に」, and remember 「いす」 is the object while 「せき」 is your place." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「椅子|いす」 has 「木|き」 on the left of its first kanji, since chairs were wooden, and 「子|す」 as a little object ending. It pairs with 「机|つくえ」, which also has a tree.",
      "The unusual 「す」 reading came in with Zen monks from China, as in 「扇子|せんす」 and 「様子|ようす」. Chairs were rare in homes until Western furniture spread from the late eighteen hundreds.",
      "Sit with 「に」, as in 「いすにすわる」, not 「を」. 「いす」 is the physical chair, while 「せき」 is your seat or place, like on a train."
    ],
    ja: "その[いす]は{先生|せんせい}のです。",
    en: "That chair is the teacher's." },
  "つくえ|机": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「机|つくえ」 uses its native reading. The kanji has two parts. On the left is 「木|き」, tree or wood, the same piece you see in 「木曜日|もくようび」, Thursday, where it's read 「もく」. On the right is a small shape that on its own means a little table or stand. It even looks like one, a flat top on two short legs. So wood plus little table gives you a desk. This kanji is one of the friendliest to break apart, because both halves point straight at the meaning. A desk naturally goes with 「いす」, chair, whose kanji also starts with the tree shape. Learn them together as the wooden pair in the classroom. In Japanese schools, students usually stay in one classroom and the teachers come to them, so each student has their own desk and chair. Students even clean the classroom themselves at the end of the day, moving the desks to sweep. Useful sentences: 「これはわたしのつくえです」, this is my desk, or with a location word, the pen is on the desk. Recap: wood plus a little table, the desk." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's sort out the furniture words that sit near 「つくえ」. A desk for working or studying is 「つくえ」. A table for eating, or any table in a Western-style room, is usually 「テーブル」, borrowed from English. A dining table is 「しょくたく」, a more formal word you'll see in writing. In traditional rooms, people sit on the floor around a low table, and in winter that low table might be a 「こたつ」, a table with a heater underneath and a quilt draped over it, one of the cosiest things about a Japanese winter. Office desks are sometimes called 「デスク」, and that word is also used for the editor at a newspaper desk. So the choice depends on purpose. If you study or work at it, it's a 「つくえ」. If you eat at it, it's a 「テーブル」. A common mistake is to call the dinner table 「つくえ」, which sounds like you're eating at your homework desk. Wrap-up: desk for work, table for meals, and the heated 「こたつ」 for winter." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Desks are perfect for practicing location words. To say something is on the desk, use 「つくえのうえ」, the top of the desk. Under the desk is 「つくえのした」, and inside the drawer is 「つくえのなか」. So you can say 「ほんはつくえのうえにあります」, the book is on the desk, using 「に」 and 「あります」 for things. There's also a lovely expression, to face your desk, which in Japanese means to sit down and study hard. A parent might proudly say their child faces the desk every evening. Where does the word come from? A commonly given explanation is that 「つくえ」 grew out of old words meaning a place to set down dishes, since early tables held food offerings. Treat that as a likely story rather than a certainty. Mini dialogue: 「わたしのペンはどこですか」, where's my pen? 「つくえのうえにありますよ」, it's on the desk. Wrap-up: on, under, inside, and the desk you face when you study." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「机|つくえ」 is 「木|き」, wood, plus a small shape meaning a little table, so wood plus table gives a desk. Learn it with 「いす」 as the wooden pair in the classroom.",
      "A 「つくえ」 is for studying or working, while you eat at a 「テーブル」. Calling the dinner table 「つくえ」 sounds like eating at your homework desk.",
      "Desks are perfect for location words like 「つくえのうえ」 and 「つくえのした」, as in 「ほんはつくえのうえにあります」. To face your desk means to study hard."
    ],
    ja: "これはわたしの[机|つくえ]です。",
    en: "This is my desk." },
  "ぼうし|帽子": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「帽子|ぼうし」 is two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. 「帽|ぼう」 means head covering or cap. On its left side is a piece that means cloth, which shows up in kanji for fabric things. That's a solid clue: a cloth thing for your head. 「子|し」 normally means child, but here it's just a little suffix that makes an everyday object. You see the same job in 「椅子|いす」, chair, where it's read 「す」. So 「帽子|ぼうし」 is basically head covering thing, and the 「子|し」 doesn't mean child at all. That's a common trap: don't try to find a child in the hat. A useful verb to go with it is 「かぶる」, to put on something on your head, which you'll learn later. Japanese uses different verbs for wearing depending on the body part, so a hat gets 「かぶる」 while shoes and jeans get 「はく」. In Japan you'll see a lot of hats in summer, and young school kids often wear brightly coloured caps so drivers can see them. A question you can already ask: 「あのぼうしはいくらですか」, how much is that hat? Recap: cloth head covering plus a little object ending, the hat." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "English uses one verb, wear, for almost everything. Japanese picks a verb by body part, and hats lead the way. For anything on your head, like a hat, cap or helmet, the verb is 「かぶる」, so 「ぼうしをかぶる」. For clothes on the upper body, like shirts and jackets, use 「きる」. For things below the waist, like trousers, skirts, socks and shoes, use 「はく」. For glasses, use 「かける」, as in 「めがねをかける」, to hang them on your face. For accessories like a watch or a tie, people often use 「する」. To describe what someone is wearing right now, add the ongoing form, as in 「ぼうしをかぶっています」, he's wearing a hat. Taking a hat off is commonly 「とる」, to remove, though 「ぬぐ」 is also heard. A typical mistake is saying 「ぼうしをきる」, which sounds like you're putting the hat on like a shirt. Wrap-up: 「かぶる」 for the head, 「きる」 for the top, 「はく」 for the bottom." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「子|し」 in 「帽子|ぼうし」 belongs to a family of everyday object words where it doesn't mean child at all. Snacks and sweets are 「お菓子|おかし」, with that same 「し」 ending. Condition or how things are going is 「調子|ちょうし」, as in asking how someone is feeling today. In 「椅子|いす」, chair, and 「様子|ようす」, appearance, the same kanji is read 「す」. So whenever this kanji sits at the end of a two-kanji word, think thing-maker, not child. The first kanji, 「帽|ぼう」, has its own small family. A baseball cap is 「野球帽|やきゅうぼう」, baseball hat. A straw hat is 「麦わら帽子|むぎわらぼうし」, a straw-of-wheat hat, the classic summer hat for kids at the beach. And a hat for school children might be a bright yellow one, which first graders wear so drivers can spot them easily on the way to school. Wrap-up: 「し」 or 「す」 at the end makes an object, and 「ぼう」 at the start means a head covering." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「帽|ぼう」 has a cloth piece on its left, a cloth head covering, and 「子|し」 is just an object ending, not child. You put a hat on with 「かぶる」.",
      "Japanese picks the verb for wearing by body part: 「かぶる」 for the head, 「きる」 for the top, 「はく」 for the bottom. Saying 「ぼうしをきる」 sounds like wearing a hat as a shirt.",
      "At the end of a word 「子|し」 makes an object, as in 「お菓子|おかし」 and 「調子|ちょうし」. 「帽|ぼう」 starts hat words like 「野球帽|やきゅうぼう」 and 「麦わら帽子|むぎわらぼうし」."
    ],
    ja: "あの[帽子|ぼうし]はいくらですか。",
    en: "How much is that hat?" },
  "これ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the first word of the famous 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 system. Japanese builds its pointing words from four starting sounds. Words starting with 「こ」 mean near me, the speaker. Words with 「そ」 mean near you, the listener. Words with 「あ」 mean away from both of us. And words with 「ど」 ask a question. Then the endings tell you what kind of word it is. 「れ」 makes a thing, so 「これ」, 「それ」, 「あれ」 and 「どれ」 are this one, that one, that one over there, and which one. 「こ」 plus 「こ」 makes a place, 「ここ」, here. And 「の」 makes a word that sits before a noun, so 「このペン」 is this pen. A common mistake for English speakers is putting a noun after 「これ」. You can't say this pen with 「これ」. Use 「これ」 alone, as in 「これはペンです」, this is a pen, or use 「この」 before a noun. Polite level: it's neutral and fine anywhere. A way to remember it: 「こ」 is close, both start with a k sound. Recap: near me plus thing, this one." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "「これ」 is a pronoun, so it can take any particle a noun can, and that opens up useful patterns. With 「も」, 「これも」 means this one too. With 「を」, 「これをください」, please give me this. Here's an important point. When someone asks which one with 「どれ」, the answer uses 「が」, not 「は」. So to 「どれがあなたのですか」, which one is yours, you answer 「これがわたしのです」, this one is mine. The question word and its answer both take 「が」. Two very common set phrases are built on it too. 「これから」 means from now on, or about to start, as in 「これからよろしくおねがいします」, said when you begin working with someone. 「これで」 means with this, and 「これでおわります」 is how a teacher or speaker says, that's all, we'll finish here. So this little word does a lot more than point. Wrap-up: 「これも」, 「これを」, 「これが」 for answers, and 「これから」 for what's ahead." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Shopping is where 「これ」 really shines, because you can point and get by. To ask a price: 「これはいくらですか」, how much is this? To buy it: 「これください」, this one, please, and in casual shop talk the particle often drops out. When you decide among options, say 「これにします」, I'll go with this one. Now a common mistake English speakers make. Don't use 「これ」 for people. In English you can say this is my friend, but in Japanese 「これ」 sounds like you're calling your friend a thing. For introductions, use the polite direction word 「こちら」, as in 「こちらはやまださんです」, this is Mr. Yamada. 「こちら」 is also the polite way to say this one in shops, so staff will often say 「こちらはいかがですか」, how about this one? Mini dialogue at a shop: 「これはいくらですか」. 「せんえんです」. 「じゃあ、これください」. Wrap-up: point with 「これ」 for things, and switch to 「こちら」 for people and polite service." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The 「こ」 words mean near me, 「そ」 near you, 「あ」 far from both, and 「ど」 ask, while 「れ」 makes a thing. Never put a noun after 「これ」, so use 「このペン」 for this pen.",
      "Answer a 「どれ」 question with 「が」, as in 「これがわたしのです」. Set phrases like 「これから」 and 「これでおわります」 build on it too.",
      "In shops, point with 「これはいくらですか」 and 「これください」. Never use 「これ」 for people, so introduce them with 「こちら」 instead."
    ],
    ja: "[これ]はわたしのペンです。",
    en: "This is my pen." },
  "それ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This belongs to the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 set of pointing words. The 「そ」 words mean near you, the person I'm talking to. So 「それ」 is the thing close to the listener: something in their hand, on their desk, or right next to them. English just says that, so it's easy to mix up 「それ」 and 「あれ」. Here's the test. If it's near the other person, use 「それ」. If it's far from both of you, use 「あれ」. The same 「そ」 starts the other words in the group. 「そこ」 means there, near you, and 「その」 goes before a noun, as in 「そのかばん」, that bag by you. In conversation, 「それ」 also points back to something the other person just said, a bit like English that, as in that's great. A common mistake: putting a noun right after it. You say 「それはなんですか」, what is that, but for that bag you need 「その」. The answer usually switches sides. If I ask about 「それ」, near you, you answer with 「これ」, because it's near you, the speaker, now. A way to remember it: 「そ」 is so close to you. Recap: near the listener plus thing, that one by you." },
      { title: "Conversation glue", text: "「それ」 is the base of several connecting words that keep a conversation flowing. 「それから」 means and then, or after that, perfect for listing things you did: I studied, and then I watched TV. 「それで」 means and so, or so then, used to move a story forward or to ask, and so what happened? 「それに」 means besides, on top of that, for adding another reason. 「それじゃ」 or the more polite 「それでは」 means well then, and it's how people wrap up or switch topics, as in 「それじゃ、また」, well then, see you. In all of these, 「それ」 points back to what was just said, and the ending adds the logic. Reactions use it too. When a friend tells you some news, 「それはいいですね」 means that's great, and 「それはたいへんですね」 means that sounds tough. Wrap-up: 「それから」, 「それで」, 「それに」 and 「それじゃ」, four handy ways to link ideas, all built on that." },
      { title: "Politeness ladder", text: "Each pointing word has a polite version and a casual version, and the 「そ」 set shows it clearly. The plain word for that one near you is 「それ」. The polite version is 「そちら」, which can mean that one, that direction, or even you and your side, as in your company. Staff and businesspeople use 「そちら」 a lot. The casual version, used with friends, is 「そっち」, with a small 「っ」 making a little catch in the sound. So in a shop, a clerk might say 「そちらはさんぜんえんです」, that one is three thousand yen, while a friend at home might say 「そっちのほうがいい」, that one's better. A useful polite phrase on the phone is 「そちらはやまださんのおたくですか」, is this the Yamada residence? Here 「そちら」 means the place you're calling. Choosing the right level makes you sound natural rather than stiff or rude. Wrap-up: 「それ」 for everyday, 「そちら」 for polite, 「そっち」 among friends." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The 「そ」 words point near the listener, so 「それ」 is the thing by you, while far from both is 「あれ」. Use 「その」 before a noun, and expect a 「これ」 in reply.",
      "「それ」 builds connecting words like 「それから」, and then, 「それで」, and so, 「それに」, besides, and 「それじゃ」, well then. Reactions use it too, as in 「それはいいですね」.",
      "The plain 「それ」 has a polite form, 「そちら」, used by staff and on the phone, and a casual 「そっち」 among friends. Pick the level that fits the situation."
    ],
    ja: "[それ]はなんですか。",
    en: "What is that?" },
  "あれ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the far member of the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 set of pointing words. The 「あ」 words mean far from both the speaker and the listener. So 「あれ」 is that thing over there, like a building across the street or a bag on a far shelf. The rest of the 「あ」 family follows the same idea. 「あそこ」 is over there, the place, and 「あの」 goes before a noun, as in 「あのひと」, that person over there. When both people are talking about something neither of them is near, 「あれ」 is the right choice. In conversation it has one more use. When you and a friend both know what you mean, 「あれ」 can point to a shared memory, like that thing we talked about. And on its own, said with surprise, 「あれ」 is also an exclamation like huh, what? when something isn't where you expected. A common mistake is using 「それ」 for something far away just because English says that for both. Ask yourself, is it near the listener or far from us both? A way to remember it: the 「あ」 sound is like away, so 「あれ」 is the thing way over there. Recap: far from us both plus thing, that one over there." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's sharpen two choices that trip learners up. First, the shape of the word. 「あれ」 stands alone as a thing, 「あの」 must come before a noun, and 「あそこ」 is a place. So that bag over there is 「あのかばん」, and the bag is over there is 「かばんはあそこです」. Using 「あれ」 for a place is a frequent slip. Second, the memory use. When you talk about something not in front of you, the choice between 「あれ」 and 「それ」 depends on who knows it. If both of you know it well, like a restaurant you went to together, use 「あれ」 or 「あの」. If only one of you knows it, like something you're mentioning for the first time, use 「それ」 or 「その」. So if a friend tells you about a new movie you haven't seen, you'd ask what's 「それ」, not 「あれ」. Wrap-up: 「あれ」 for things, 「あの」 before nouns, 「あそこ」 for places, and 「あれ」 only for shared memories." },
      { title: "Politeness ladder", text: "Like the other pointing words, 「あれ」 has a polite version and a casual version. The polite one is 「あちら」. It means that one over there, or that direction, and staff use it constantly when guiding you. Ask a station attendant where the exit is, and you might hear 「でぐちはあちらです」, the exit is that way, along with an open-hand gesture. Pointing with one finger can feel a bit rude, so staff often gesture with the whole hand. The casual version is 「あっち」, with a small 「っ」, used among friends and family, as in 「あっちにいこう」, let's go over there. The whole set lines up neatly: 「こっち」, 「そっち」, 「あっち」, and the question word 「どっち」, which one of two. A friend might ask 「どっちがいい」, which do you prefer? So you can match your level to the situation, polite with staff and strangers, relaxed with friends. Wrap-up: 「あれ」 for everyday, 「あちら」 when polite, 「あっち」 among friends, all meaning that way over there." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The 「あ」 words mean far from both speaker and listener, so 「あれ」 is that thing over there, while 「あの」 goes before nouns. It can also point to a shared memory or be a surprised exclamation.",
      "Use 「あれ」 for things, 「あの」 before nouns, and 「あそこ」 for places. For something not present, use 「あれ」 only when you both know it, otherwise 「それ」.",
      "The polite form 「あちら」 is what staff use when guiding you, and casual 「あっち」 is for friends. The set lines up as 「こっち」, 「そっち」, 「あっち」, 「どっち」."
    ],
    ja: "[あれ]はだれのかばんですか。",
    en: "Whose bag is that over there?" },
  "どれ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the question member of the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 pointing words. The 「ど」 words always ask. 「どれ」 is which one, 「どこ」 is where, and 「どの」 is which, used before a noun, as in 「どのかばん」, which bag. Use 「どれ」 when you're choosing one thing out of three or more. Pointing at a row of umbrellas, you'd ask 「あなたのかさはどれですか」, which one is your umbrella? When there are only two choices, Japanese prefers a different word, 「どちら」, which you'll learn later. One grammar point matters a lot. Question words like 「どれ」 can't take 「は」 as the topic. If 「どれ」 is the subject, use 「が」 instead, as in 「どれがいいですか」, which one is good? That's a common mistake for beginners, who are used to putting 「は」 everywhere. To answer, you just swap in one of the other pointing words, like 「これです」, it's this one. A way to remember it: the 「ど」 words are the doubtful ones, always asking. Recap: question plus thing, which one, and pair it with 「が」 not 「は」." },
      { title: "The whole family", text: "The pointing words come in four neat sets, and the question word sits at the end of each one. For things, there is 「これ」, 「それ」, 「あれ」 and 「どれ」. These stand on their own, with no noun after them. For things with a noun attached, there is 「この」, 「その」, 「あの」 and 「どの」, which must always be followed by a noun, as in 「どのペン」, which pen. For places, there is 「ここ」, 「そこ」, 「あそこ」 and 「どこ」. Later you will meet a polite set, 「こちら」, 「そちら」, 「あちら」 and 「どちら」. A classic beginner slip is to put a noun straight after 「どれ」. You can't say which-one pen. Either say 「どのペンですか」, or move the noun to the front and say 「ペンはどれですか」. Both work, and both are natural. Once you see the grid, every new pointing word drops into a slot you already know. So remember: 「どれ」 stands alone, 「どの」 always brings a noun." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a bakery counter with a dozen kinds of bread. The clerk smiles and asks 「どれにしますか」. That means which one will you go with, and it is one of the most common shop phrases you will hear. You can point and say 「これにします」, I'll take this one. If you really don't mind, say 「どれでもいいです」, any of them is fine. Notice that the answer copies the particle of the question. If a friend asks 「どれがいいですか」, which one is good, the natural answer is 「これがいいです」, using 「が」 again, not 「は」. That echo happens with every question word. Later you will also hear 「どれも」, which with a positive verb means every one, and with a negative verb means none of them. For example, at a buffet someone might sigh that every dish looks delicious. Keep the bakery scene in mind: which one, this one, and 「が」 in both." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on sound. The word sounds a bit like the English word door. Imagine a game show. You stand in front of three big doors, each with a prize behind it. The host, in a sparkly jacket, leans into the microphone and shouts the question at you, over and over, which one, which one? The audience starts chanting it too. Behind one door there is a car. Behind another there is a goat. Behind the last there is a giant bowl of 「とんかつ」. You have to pick. That's exactly the situation for this word: three or more choices, and you have to choose one. If there were only two doors, the host would use a different word, 「どちら」, but this show always has at least three. It's just a hook, but it ties the sound to the choosing. Next time you hesitate over a menu, hear the host shouting about doors." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The 「ど」 pointing words always ask, and this one asks which one when you are choosing among three or more things. As a subject it takes 「が」, never 「は」, as in 「どれがいいですか」.",
      "In the grid of pointing words, 「どれ」 stands alone while 「どの」 must bring a noun, so say 「どのペンですか」 or 「ペンはどれですか」, never a noun straight after 「どれ」.",
      "At a shop counter the clerk asks 「どれにしますか」, and you answer 「これにします」; the answer echoes the question's particle, so 「どれがいいですか」 gets 「これがいいです」.",
      "It sounds like door: picture a game show host shouting which one at three big doors, because this word is for picking one out of three or more choices."
    ],
    ja: "あなたのかさは[どれ]ですか。",
    en: "Which one is your umbrella?" },
  "あそこ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the far place word in the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 set. The place words are 「ここ」, here, near me, 「そこ」, there, near you, 「あそこ」, over there, away from both of us, and 「どこ」, where. Notice that 「あそこ」 is a little irregular. You might expect it to be just 「あこ」, following the pattern, but the real word has an extra 「そ」 in the middle. That's worth a moment of attention, because it's the one place word that breaks the rhythm. Use 「あそこ」 for a place you could point to across the room or down the street. A very useful sentence is 「おてあらいはあそこです」, the restroom is over there. In Japan, someone giving directions will often just point and say 「あそこです」, so it's a word you'll hear a lot when asking the way. You can also make it more specific by adding a noun, as in over there by the bank, later on. A way to remember the extra syllable: over there is further away, so the word stretches a little longer. That's just a hook. Recap: the far place word, a little longer than the pattern, over there." },
      { title: "Shared memory", text: "The 「あ」 words have a second job that English speakers rarely expect. Besides pointing at something far away you can see, they also point at something you and your listener both know, even if it is nowhere in sight. Imagine two friends talking about a ramen shop they visited last month. One says 「あそこのラーメン、おいしかったね」, that place's ramen was good, wasn't it. Nobody is pointing anywhere. The 「あ」 means that place we both remember. If only one of you knew the shop, Japanese would switch to a 「そ」 word instead, as in 「そこ」 or 「その」, because the 「そ」 words point at things the listener doesn't share. So 「あそこ」 can mean over there in space, or that place in our shared memory. The same is true of 「あれ」 and 「あの」. This is why 「あれ」 alone can mean that thing we both know about, which can be a little mysterious to overhear. So think of 「あ」 as far from both of us, or known to both of us." },
      { title: "Polite version", text: "In shops, hotels and stations, staff rarely say 「あそこ」 to customers. They use the polite partner, 「あちら」. A clerk will hold out an open hand, palm up, and say 「おてあらいはあちらです」, the restroom is that way. The whole set has polite twins. 「ここ」 becomes 「こちら」, 「そこ」 becomes 「そちら」, and 「どこ」 becomes 「どちら」. These polite words were originally about directions, this way, that way, so they feel softer and less pointed than naming the exact spot. Notice the gesture, too. Pointing with one finger can feel a bit rude, so staff gesture with the whole hand. As a learner, 「あそこ」 is perfectly fine for you to use with friends, classmates or a stranger in the street. You simply need to recognise 「あちら」 when you hear it, because in a department store you'll hear it far more often. So the casual word is 「あそこ」, and the customer service word is 「あちら」, with an open hand." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick using the sound. Picture yourself at a crowded festival, searching for a friend who wandered off. You scan the crowd, left, right, nothing. Then suddenly you spot her, far away across the square, waving from the top of a fountain. You gasp, ah, so, there she is. Ah, so. Now add the extra piece. You point and call out to the friend standing next to you, and the word stretches out because she is so far away. Notice that the scene is about a place far from both you and the person next to you. That's the exact space this word covers, not near me, not near you, but over there, away from both of us. The trick is only a hook, since the word has nothing to do with English, but the gasp of recognition helps the sound stick. So next time you spot something across a room, let the ah, so moment bring the word back." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the far place word, over there away from both of us, and it breaks the pattern with an extra 「そ」, so it is 「あそこ」 rather than 「あこ」.",
      "The 「あ」 words also point at something both speakers remember, so 「あそこのラーメン、おいしかったね」 means that place we both know, even with nothing in sight.",
      "Shop and station staff use the polite partner 「あちら」 with an open hand, as in 「おてあらいはあちらです」, while 「あそこ」 is fine for you with friends and strangers.",
      "Picture spotting a friend far across a festival square and gasping ah, so, there she is, a place far from both you and the person beside you."
    ],
    ja: "おてあらいは[あそこ]です。",
    en: "The restroom is over there." },
  "どこ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the question place word in the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 set. It sits with 「ここ」, here, 「そこ」, there near you, and 「あそこ」, over there. The 「ど」 at the start marks a question, just like in 「どれ」, which one, and 「どの」, which. The basic pattern is simple: put the thing you're looking for first, then 「は」, then 「どこですか」. So 「おてあらいはどこですか」 is where is the restroom, and 「ぎんこうはどこですか」 is where is the bank. Start with 「すみません」 to be polite, and you have a complete sentence for asking the way. The answer usually swaps 「どこ」 for one of the place words, like 「あそこです」, it's over there. 「どこ」 can also ask where someone is from: 「どこからきましたか」, where did you come from? A common mistake is putting 「どこ」 at the start like English does. In Japanese the question word sits where the answer would go, so the word order doesn't change. A way to remember the sound: picture a dog sniffing around asking where, where? That's just a hook. Recap: question plus place, where." },
      { title: "Particles with where", text: "This question word becomes much more useful once you add particles. With a verb of movement, add 「に」 or 「へ」. 「どこにいきますか」 means where are you going. With an action, add 「で」 for the place where it happens. 「どこでべんきょうしますか」 means where do you study. With 「の」 it asks where something comes from, or which place it belongs to. In a shop you might ask 「これはどこのとけいですか」, where is this watch from, and hear 「スイスのです」, it's a Swiss one. Notice how the answer simply swaps a place name into the same slot. Each particle changes the question in the same way it would change a normal sentence. The question word just holds the place open while you wait for the answer. A good habit is to practise the answers alongside the questions, so you hear the particle in both. So remember: 「に」 for going, 「で」 for doing, and 「の」 for where from." },
      { title: "Somewhere and nowhere", text: "Add a little piece to the end and this word changes meaning. Add 「か」 and you get 「どこか」, somewhere. 「どこかへいきましたか」 means did you go somewhere? Add 「も」 and use a negative verb, and you get nowhere. The answer to that question might be 「どこもいきませんでした」, or more often 「どこにもいきませんでした」, I didn't go anywhere. Notice the particle 「に」 slips in between. Add 「でも」 and you get anywhere at all, as in anywhere is fine. This pattern runs through all the question words. 「なにか」 is something, 「だれか」 is someone, and 「いつか」 is someday. It's one of the most powerful patterns in Japanese, because one rule unlocks a whole row of new words. A common mistake is using 「も」 with a positive verb for nowhere. The verb must be negative. So remember the trio: 「か」 for some, 「も」 with a negative for no, and 「でも」 for any." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Asking where something is can be tricky in Japan, because most streets have no names. Japanese addresses work by area and block numbers, not by street. So people give directions by landmarks instead: go past the convenience store, turn at the bank, it's next to the post office. When you ask 「すみません、えきはどこですか」, expect an answer full of buildings and turns. If you are truly lost, look for a small police box, called a 「交番|こうばん」. Officers there help people find addresses all day long, often with detailed local maps. You will also notice large area maps posted near stations. Many Japanese people will walk you part of the way rather than explain, especially if the directions are complicated. Learning a few landmark words, like 「ぎんこう」, bank, and 「ゆうびんきょく」, post office, makes the answers much easier to follow. So when you ask where, listen for landmarks, not street names." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The 「ど」 marks a question, and the question word sits where the answer would go, so ask 「おてあらいはどこですか」 and expect an answer like 「あそこです」.",
      "Particles shape the question just as in normal sentences: 「に」 for going in 「どこにいきますか」, 「で」 for doing, and 「の」 for where from, as in 「どこのとけいですか」.",
      "Add 「か」 for somewhere, 「も」 with a negative verb for nowhere, as in 「どこにもいきませんでした」, and 「でも」 for anywhere, a pattern shared by all question words.",
      "Japanese streets mostly have no names, so when you ask 「えきはどこですか」 listen for landmarks like the bank or post office, and try a 「交番|こうばん」 police box if lost."
    ],
    ja: "{銀行|ぎんこう}は[どこ]ですか。",
    en: "Where is the bank?" },
  "だれ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the question word for people. It has a kanji, 「誰|だれ」, but it's almost always written in kana. The basic pattern is the same as the other question words: put it where the answer would go and end with 「ですか」. So 「あのひとはだれですか」 is who is that person? Add 「の」 and it becomes whose. 「だれのかさですか」 is whose umbrella is it, and 「これはだれのですか」 is whose is this? That's useful in a classroom full of lost umbrellas and pencils. There's a politeness point to know. 「だれ」 is fine with friends, but if you're asking about someone in a polite setting, like a guest or a superior, Japanese uses the more respectful word 「どなた」. Another grammar point: as a subject, 「だれ」 takes 「が」, not 「は」, the same rule as other question words. For example, who came would use 「だれが」. A common mistake is using 「なん」, what, for people. Japanese keeps them separate: 「なん」 for things, 「だれ」 for people. A way to remember it: who dares? The word sounds a bit like dare. Recap: the who word, add 「の」 for whose." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「誰|だれ」 is rarely written, but it's worth a look because both halves are pieces you will meet again. On the left is the speech piece, the narrow form of 「言|げん」, to say. That fits a word you use to ask about people. On the right is a component that originally meant a bird, often called the short tailed bird. In this kanji it mainly gave the sound in old Chinese rather than meaning. You can spot that same bird piece on the right of 「雑|ざつ」 in 「雑誌|ざっし」, magazine, and on the right of 「難|むずか」 in 「難しい|むずかしい」, difficult. The kanji was only added to the official list of everyday kanji in two thousand ten, which tells you how long people have been writing the word in kana. In your own writing, kana is the safe and normal choice. Just recognise the kanji when it turns up in novels or song lyrics. So remember: speech on the left, a bird on the right." },
      { title: "Politeness ladder", text: "There are three steps for asking who. The plain word is fine among friends, as in 「あのひとはだれ」. One step up is 「どなた」, which you use when asking about someone respected. A receptionist might ask 「どなたですか」. The top step is 「どちらさま」, which literally means which direction, honoured. You hear it on the phone and at the door. When someone rings, the polite answer is 「どちらさまですか」, may I ask who is calling? Here is the trap. Asking 「だれですか」 straight to the face of the person you are talking to sounds blunt, almost suspicious, like demanding who are you? With strangers, the polite words are much safer. Also, Japanese generally avoids asking people directly who they are. It's more natural to ask their name politely, as in 「おなまえは」. So choose the step that matches the person: the plain word for friends, 「どなた」 for respect, and 「どちらさま」 at the door or phone." },
      { title: "Someone and no one", text: "Like the other question words, this one changes when you add a small piece. With 「か」 it becomes 「だれか」, someone. 「だれかいますか」 means is anybody there? With 「も」 and a negative verb, it becomes no one. 「だれもいません」 means nobody is here. With 「でも」 it becomes anyone at all, as in anyone can do it. Another handy combination comes from lesson four: 「と」, together with. 「だれといきましたか」 means who did you go with? And the answer swaps in a name, like 「ともだちといきました」, I went with a friend. You can also ask 「だれに」, to whom, when giving presents later on. A common mistake is using a positive verb with 「だれも」 to mean nobody. Japanese needs the negative verb. Think of the empty classroom: 「だれもいません」. So the pieces are 「か」 for someone, 「も」 with a negative for no one, and 「と」 for who with." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the who word, usually written in kana; add 「の」 for whose, as in 「だれのかさですか」, and as a subject it takes 「が」, not 「は」.",
      "The rare kanji 「誰|だれ」 has the speech piece on the left and a short tailed bird on the right, which mainly gave the sound, and kana remains the normal way to write it.",
      "Use the plain word with friends, 「どなた」 for respected people, and 「どちらさま」 at the door or on the phone, since asking someone 「だれですか」 to their face sounds blunt.",
      "Add 「か」 for someone, 「も」 with a negative verb for no one, as in 「だれもいません」, and 「と」 for who with, as in 「だれといきましたか」."
    ],
    ja: "あの{人|ひと}は[だれ]ですか。",
    en: "Who is that person?" },
  "とんかつ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is a fusion of Japanese and English. 「とん」 is the Chinese-origin reading of 「豚|ぶた」, pig or pork. You'll later see the native reading in 「豚肉|ぶたにく」, pork. 「かつ」 is short for 「カツレツ」, which comes from the English word cutlet. In that loanword, the sounds got squeezed: cutlet became 「カツレツ」, with the l turning into an r sound and extra vowels added. Then Japanese clipped it down to just 「かつ」. Put them together and you get pork cutlet. The dish itself is a thick pork cutlet, coated in breadcrumbs and deep fried, usually served sliced with shredded cabbage, rice, miso soup and a thick brown sauce. It started out as a Western style dish in the late eighteen hundreds and became thoroughly Japanese. There's a fun cultural note. 「かつ」 sounds the same as the verb 「勝つ|かつ」, to win. So students often eat 「とんかつ」 or 「かつどん」, a cutlet on rice, the night before a big exam for luck. At a restaurant you can say 「とんかつをください」, pork cutlet, please. Recap: pig plus cutlet, the deep fried pork cutlet that brings luck." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The 「とん」 part is the kanji 「豚|ぶた」, and its shape tells the story clearly. On the right is an old character that pictures a pig, with its body and legs. On the left is a narrow shape that looks like the moon, but here it is really the flesh piece, a squashed form of 「肉|にく」, meat. So the kanji is meat plus pig, a pig raised for meat. Its native reading, 「ぶた」, is the everyday word for pig, the animal. Its Chinese-origin reading, 「とん」, appears in food words. 「豚汁|とんじる」 is a hearty miso soup with pork and vegetables, though some people say 「ぶたじる」. Menus often write the dish as 「豚|とん」 plus katakana 「カツ」, mixing scripts. So when you see this kanji on a menu, expect pork. When you see it in a children's book, expect a cartoon pig. Remember: flesh beside a pig, read 「ぶた」 for the animal and 「とん」 on the menu." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you know 「かつ」, a whole family of dishes opens up. 「かつどん」 is a cutlet simmered with egg and onion and served over rice in a bowl. The 「どん」 means bowl, the same ending as 「ぎゅうどん」, beef bowl. 「カツカレー」 is a cutlet on top of Japanese curry rice, a hugely popular combination. 「チキンカツ」 is the chicken version. At a tonkatsu restaurant you choose a cut. 「ロース」 is the loin, with a strip of fat for flavour, and the word comes from English roast. 「ヒレ」 is the lean fillet, from the French word filet. So the menu itself is a mini lesson in loanwords. A set meal is usually called a 「定食|ていしょく」, which brings rice, soup, pickles and cabbage. So you might order a loin cutlet set. Remember: 「かつ」 is the cutlet, and the words around it tell you the meat, the cut, and how it's served." },
      { title: "At the restaurant", text: "Eating at a specialist tonkatsu restaurant has its own little rituals. Often you are given a small bowl with ridges inside and a wooden pestle, plus sesame seeds. You grind the seeds yourself, then pour in the thick sauce, so the sauce smells toasty and fresh. The shredded cabbage piled beside the cutlet is often free to refill, along with rice and miso soup at many places. Just catch a server's eye and say 「おかわりください」, a refill, please. Some people squeeze lemon over the cabbage, others use a sesame dressing. Mustard is usually on the table, too. Before you start, say 「いただきます」, and when you finish, 「ごちそうさまでした」. Tonkatsu is everyday food, so you'll also find it in convenience store sandwiches and lunch boxes. A thick cutlet sandwich is a popular train snack. Remember the ritual: grind the sesame, add the sauce, and ask for more cabbage." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It joins 「とん」, the Chinese-origin reading of 「豚|ぶた」, pork, with 「かつ」, clipped from 「カツレツ」, cutlet, and because 「かつ」 sounds like 「勝つ|かつ」, to win, students eat it before exams.",
      "The kanji 「豚|ぶた」 is the flesh piece beside a pictured pig, read 「ぶた」 for the animal and 「とん」 for pork on the menu.",
      "「かつ」 heads a family of dishes: 「かつどん」 on rice in a bowl, 「カツカレー」 on curry, and at restaurants you choose 「ロース」 loin or 「ヒレ」 fillet, often as a 「定食|ていしょく」 set meal.",
      "At a specialist restaurant you grind sesame seeds yourself before adding the thick sauce, and the shredded cabbage is often free to refill with 「おかわりください」."
    ],
    ja: "[とんかつ]をください。",
    en: "Pork cutlet, please." },
  "にく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「肉|にく」, and the shape is often described as a slab of meat with streaks of fat or ribs inside. Here's a surprise: 「にく」 is actually the Chinese-origin reading. It's been used for so long that it feels like a native word, and it's the reading you'll hear every day. The kanji goes on the end of words for different meats. Pork is 「豚肉|ぶたにく」, pig meat. Beef is 「牛肉|ぎゅうにく」, cow meat. And chicken is 「とりにく」, bird meat. The 「牛|ぎゅう」 in beef is the same one in 「牛乳|ぎゅうにゅう」, milk, cow milk. Once you know 「にく」, you can recognise all of them. A bit of history makes it more interesting. For over a thousand years, eating meat from animals like cows was officially discouraged in Japan, partly for Buddhist reasons, and fish was the main protein. Meat eating became popular in the late eighteen hundreds when Japan opened up to the West, which is when dishes like 「とんかつ」 and beef hot pots appeared. A useful question: 「これはにくですか」, is this meat? Recap: the meat kanji, added to animals to make pork, beef and chicken." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Here is a surprise hiding inside many kanji. When 「肉|にく」 becomes a component on the left or bottom of another kanji, it shrinks into a shape that looks exactly like 「月|つき」, the moon. But it has nothing to do with the moon. Kanji dictionaries call it the flesh radical, and it marks kanji about body parts and meat. You have already seen it in 「豚|ぶた」, pig, where it sits on the left. It's at the bottom of 「背|せ」, the back or height, in 「背が高い|せがたかい」, tall. It's on the left of 「脱|ぬ」 in 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, to take off clothes, which is about uncovering the body. Later you will meet many more body kanji with the same piece. So when you see a moon shape on the left of a kanji about the body, think flesh, not moon. It's one of the most useful tricks for guessing meanings. Remember: the moon on the left is often meat in disguise." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Meat shows up in some of the most loved Japanese dishes. 「焼き肉|やきにく」 is grilled meat, where you cook bite sized pieces yourself on a grill in the middle of the table. 「にくじゃが」 is a homely stew of meat and potatoes, often called a taste of mother's cooking. Here is an interesting regional twist. In eastern Japan, around Tokyo, people often picture pork when they hear plain 「にく」. In western Japan, around Osaka, it more often means beef. That's why a steamed meat bun is usually called 「にくまん」 in Tokyo but 「ぶたまん」 in Osaka, because there, meat alone would suggest beef. If you don't eat meat, a useful sentence is 「にくをたべません」, I don't eat meat. Be aware that soup stocks often contain fish, so vegetarians need to ask carefully. So remember: grilled at the table, stewed with potatoes, and pork or beef depending on where you are." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. The word sounds a bit like knee cook. Picture a chef in a tall white hat who has lost his frying pan. Desperate, he sits down, slaps a huge raw steak on his knee, and starts cooking it there with a blowtorch. The steak sizzles, smoke rises, and the customers watch in horror and fascination. He shouts proudly, knee cook, the best meat in town! Now add the kanji. Look at 「肉|にく」 and imagine the two little shapes inside the frame are the grill marks burned onto the steak. The outer frame is the slab itself. That picture of a slab with streaks is a common way to remember the shape. The scene is silly, but silly is what sticks. Next time you see meat on a menu, picture the chef cooking on his knee. So remember: knee cook, the meat word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「肉|にく」 goes on the end of animals to name meats, as in 「豚肉|ぶたにく」 pork and 「牛肉|ぎゅうにく」 beef, and 「にく」 is actually a Chinese-origin reading that feels native.",
      "As a component, 「肉|にく」 shrinks into a moon shape, the flesh radical, which marks body and meat kanji like 「豚|ぶた」 and 「背|せ」, so a moon on the left is often meat.",
      "Plain 「にく」 tends to mean pork around Tokyo but beef around Osaka, which is why the steamed bun is 「にくまん」 in Tokyo but 「ぶたまん」 in Osaka.",
      "It sounds like knee cook, so picture a chef grilling a steak on his knee, with the streaks inside 「肉|にく」 as grill marks on the slab."
    ],
    ja: "これは[にく]ですか。",
    en: "Is this meat?" },
  "メニュー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the French word menu, and it reached Japanese through English. Look at how the sounds were squeezed in. The first syllable stays 「メ」. Then the second part becomes 「ニュー」, using a small 「ュ」 to make a combined sound, and the long vowel mark at the end stretches it out, a bit like the English word new. Japanese doesn't have the exact French vowel, so it uses the closest thing. The meaning hasn't shifted: it's a list of food and drink at a restaurant. It's also used for menus in apps and on computers, just like in English. A typical way to ask for one is 「メニューをください」 or the slightly more polite 「メニューをおねがいします」, the menu, please. A cultural note helps here. Many Japanese restaurants display realistic plastic models of their dishes in the front window. These food models help you choose, and if you can't read the menu, you can just take the server outside and point. Many menus also have lots of photos for the same reason. Recap: a French word through English, squeezed into Japanese sounds, the restaurant menu." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how the menu fits into a typical restaurant visit. As you walk in, staff call out 「いらっしゃいませ」, welcome. Someone asks how many people, and you hold up fingers or say 「ふたりです」, two people. Once you're seated, there's often already a menu on the table, sometimes with photos of every dish. If not, ask for one. Many places will have an English version, so you can try 「えいごのメニューはありますか」, do you have an English menu? To call a server, it's normal to raise a hand and say 「すみません」 quite loudly. That isn't rude in Japan. Many casual restaurants even have a call button on the table. When you're ready, point at the menu and say 「これをください」, this one, please. A handy question is 「おすすめはなんですか」, what do you recommend? So the menu comes with a set of phrases: ask for it, point at it, and ask for the recommendation." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word is a good example of how small kana work. 「ニュ」 is written with a full size 「ニ」 and a small 「ュ」, and together they make one single beat, not two. Then the long vowel mark adds a second beat to that sound. So the word has three beats in total: 「メ」, 「ニュ」, and the stretched part. English speakers sometimes say it with the wrong rhythm, either too short, or splitting the middle into two separate syllables. Try tapping three times as you say it. The same combined sound appears in other words you know. 「ニュース」, news, starts the same way. And in 「ぎゅうにゅう」, milk, there are two of these combined sounds with long vowels. A common writing mistake is to use a full size 「ユ」, which would turn the combined sound into two beats and a different word altogether. So keep that 「ュ」 small. Remember: tap three times, and the small 「ュ」 glues onto the 「ニ」." },
      { title: "Beyond restaurants", text: "Japanese uses this loanword more widely than English does. Of course it means a restaurant menu, and you'll see 「ランチメニュー」, a lunch menu, outside cafés at midday. It's also used for the menus on a computer or a phone app, just like in English. But here's the interesting part. Japanese also uses it for any planned set of activities, especially exercise. A coach might give a team its practice plan for the day and call it the practice menu. At a gym, a personal trainer might write you a 「トレーニングメニュー」, a training menu, listing your exercises. Even a hair salon might show a menu of services, like cut, colour and perm, with prices. So if you hear the word in a sports club, it isn't about food. It simply means a list of options or a planned programme. Remember: in Japanese, a menu can feed you, or work you hard at the gym." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from French menu by way of English, with a small 「ュ」 making a combined sound and a long vowel mark, and you ask for one with 「メニューをください」.",
      "The menu brings a set of restaurant phrases: ask 「えいごのメニューはありますか」, point and say 「これをください」, or ask 「おすすめはなんですか」 for a recommendation.",
      "The small 「ュ」 glues onto 「ニ」 to make one beat, so the word has three beats in total, and a full size 「ユ」 would wrongly make it four.",
      "Japanese stretches this loanword to any planned list, so a coach's practice plan or a gym's 「トレーニングメニュー」 is a menu too, with no food involved."
    ],
    ja: "[メニュー]をおねがいします。",
    en: "The menu, please." },
  "えんぴつ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「鉛筆|えんぴつ」, two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「鉛|えん」 means lead, the metal. On its left side is 「金|かね」, metal or money, which marks lots of kanji for metals. 「筆|ぴつ」 means writing brush. Look at its top: it has the bamboo shape, because traditional brushes have bamboo handles. On its own the reading is 「ひつ」, but after 「ん」 it changes to 「ぴつ」. That sound change happens in many words, so it's worth noticing. Put them together and a pencil is a lead brush. Funnily enough, pencils don't contain lead at all. They use graphite, but people once thought graphite was a kind of lead, and English calls it pencil lead to this day. Japanese kept that same old idea in the kanji. Brushes were the normal writing tool in Japan for centuries, so a new writing tool got named after a brush. Japanese students still use pencils a lot, even at college, and many children practice kanji with pencils that are softer and darker than usual. It goes on the desk with 「けしゴム」, eraser, and 「ペン」, pen. Recap: lead plus brush, the pencil." },
      { title: "Counting pencils", text: "To count pencils, Japanese uses the counter 「ほん」, for long thin things like pens, bottles, umbrellas and trees. The tricky part is that its sound shifts with the number. One pencil is 「いっぽん」, with a small 「っ」 and a p sound. Two is 「にほん」, the plain form. Three is 「さんぼん」, with a b sound. Then four is 「よんほん」, six is 「ろっぽん」, eight is often 「はっぽん」, and ten is 「じゅっぽん」. So in a shop you might say 「えんぴつをいっぽんください」, one pencil, please. Notice the order: the thing, then 「を」, then the number and counter, then the verb. The counter doesn't go before the noun the way it does in English. Learning these sound changes once pays off, because the same pattern of p and b sounds appears with other counters too. So practise with your pencil case: one, two, three, with p, h and b." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「筆|ひつ」, the writing brush, has a native reading too: 「ふで」. It's the everyday word for a calligraphy brush. And a pencil case is a 「筆箱|ふでばこ」, literally a brush box, even though it now holds pencils and pens. A fountain pen is 「万年筆|まんねんひつ」, which means a ten thousand year brush, because it was meant to last forever. A writer or the author of a piece is a 「筆者|ひっしゃ」, the brush person. Back to pencils: coloured pencils are 「色鉛筆|いろえんぴつ」, colour pencils. A mechanical pencil is usually called 「シャーペン」, short for sharp pencil. That name comes from early mechanical pencils sold as the Eversharp and the Ever Ready Sharp, and the Japanese maker of the second one later grew into the electronics company Sharp. So remember the brush family: 「ふで」 for the brush, 「ふでばこ」 for the case, and 「ひつ」 hiding in pens and writers." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture an archaeologist kneeling at the edge of a deep pit in the desert. She's lost her only pencil, and it has rolled right into the pit. She peers down and moans, in pit, sue me, my pencil is in the pit! In pit. Now, to fix the kanji too, imagine the pencil at the bottom of the pit is glowing like heavy grey metal, because the first kanji, 「鉛|えん」, means lead, and it has the metal piece on its left. Lead is heavy, so it sank straight to the bottom. Meanwhile she pulls out a brush instead and keeps working, since the second kanji means brush. The scene is silly and the English doesn't match perfectly, but the image of a heavy metal pencil sinking into a pit makes the sound stick. So remember: a lead brush, lost in a pit." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「鉛|えん」, lead, plus 「筆|ぴつ」, writing brush, so a pencil is a lead brush, named after the old idea that graphite was lead, with 「ひつ」 shifting to 「ぴつ」 after 「ん」.",
      "Pencils are counted with 「ほん」, whose sound shifts: 「いっぽん」, 「にほん」, 「さんぼん」, and the number comes after the noun, as in 「えんぴつをいっぽんください」.",
      "The brush kanji 「筆|ふで」 has the native reading 「ふで」, giving 「筆箱|ふでばこ」, pencil case, while 「ひつ」 hides in 「万年筆|まんねんひつ」, fountain pen, and 「筆者|ひっしゃ」, writer.",
      "Picture an archaeologist moaning in pit as her heavy lead pencil sinks to the bottom, while she switches to a brush, linking the sound to lead plus brush."
    ],
    ja: "その[えんぴつ]はだれのですか。",
    en: "Whose pencil is that?" },
  "かばん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "It's usually written in kana, but there's a kanji, 「鞄|かばん」, and it's a great one to notice. On its left side is 「革|かわ」, leather, the same piece that's on the left of 「靴|くつ」, shoes. On its right side is a shape meaning to wrap. So the kanji is leather that wraps your things. Shoes and bags, both leather goods, share the same left side. That's a neat pair from the same lesson. The word itself covers almost any bag you carry: a school bag, a briefcase, a handbag or a backpack. Japanese elementary school children famously carry a sturdy leather backpack, and many families buy one as a special present when their child starts school. In daily life, Japanese also uses the loanword 「バッグ」, from English bag, especially for fashion bags. A simple sentence: 「あれはわたしのかばんです」, that over there is my bag. A way to remember the sound: picture tossing your bag onto the desk and it lands with a loud ka-ban! That's just a hook. Recap: leather plus wrap, any bag you carry." },
      { title: "Bag vocabulary", text: "There's a whole closet of bag words in Japanese, and several come from different languages. The general word you know covers almost everything. 「バッグ」, from English, leans towards fashion, like a handbag or shoulder bag. A backpack is a 「リュック」, short for a German word, rucksack. The famous stiff leather backpack that elementary school children carry is a 「ランドセル」, which comes from a Dutch word for a soldier's pack, borrowed back when the Dutch traded with Japan. A shopping bag or carrier bag from a store is often called a 「ふくろ」, a native word for bag or sack. At the checkout, a clerk may ask if you need one. And a wallet, which you might keep inside your bag, is a 「さいふ」. So one sentence could hold several languages: a German backpack, a Dutch school bag, and an English fashion bag. Remember: the native word covers everything you carry, and the loanwords narrow it down." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Bags come with useful verbs and particles. To carry or hold a bag, Japanese uses 「もつ」. To put something in it, you say 「かばんにいれる」, with 「に」 marking the inside. To take something out, it's 「かばんからだす」, with 「から」 for out of. If you lose it, the past tense is 「かばんをなくしました」, I lost my bag. Here's a very polite phrase you'll hear in offices: a junior colleague might offer 「かばんをもちましょうか」, shall I carry your bag? In Japan, bags are generally safe, and people often leave a bag on a café seat to save their place while ordering. Still, it's good manners on crowded trains to hold your backpack in front of you or put it on the rack above the seats, so it doesn't bump people. So the bag phrases are: 「もつ」 to carry, 「に」 to put in, 「から」 to take out, and hold it in front on the train." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「鞄|かばん」 is 「革|かわ」, leather, plus a shape meaning to wrap, so it is leather that wraps your things, sharing its left side with 「靴|くつ」, shoes.",
      "The native word covers any bag, while loanwords narrow it: 「バッグ」 for fashion, 「リュック」 from German for a backpack, and 「ランドセル」 from Dutch for the school backpack.",
      "Bag phrases use 「もつ」 to carry, 「かばんにいれる」 to put in, and 「かばんからだす」 to take out, and on crowded trains you hold a backpack in front."
    ],
    ja: "あれはわたしの[かばん]です。",
    en: "That over there is my bag." },
  "ジーンズ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word jeans. Look at how the sounds were squeezed into Japanese. The j sound becomes 「ジ」, and the long ee is shown with the long vowel mark, 「ジー」. Then the n becomes 「ン」. The final s in jeans actually sounds like a z in English, so Japanese uses 「ズ」, with the extra u vowel tacked on because Japanese syllables like to end in a vowel. That ending keeps the plural feel of the English word, since in English jeans are always plural. Japanese doesn't really do plurals, so here the 「ズ」 is just part of the word. There's also an older Japanese made word, 「ジーパン」, which squeezes jeans and pants together. You'll hear it from older people, while 「ジーンズ」 is the standard word now. Later you'll learn the verb 「はく」, which is used for putting on things below the waist, so you say 「ジーンズをはきます」, I put on jeans. That's a different verb from the one for shirts, and English speakers often mix them up. A useful shopping sentence: 「このジーンズはいくらですか」, how much are these jeans? Recap: English jeans with a 「ズ」 for the s, worn with 「はく」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Clothing verbs in Japanese depend on where the item goes on your body. Things for the upper body, like a shirt or sweater, use 「きる」. Things below the waist, like jeans, skirts, socks and even shoes, use 「はく」. Hats use 「かぶる」. Luckily, taking everything off uses one verb, 「ぬぐ」. Now here's an important grammar point. To say someone is wearing jeans right now, Japanese uses the ongoing form with 「いる」. 「ジーンズをはいています」 means she is wearing jeans. If you said the plain form 「はきます」, it would sound like you put them on as an action, or do it as a habit. So when you describe what someone looks like, use the ongoing form. For example, describing a friend at the station, you might say he is wearing jeans and a black shirt, using 「はいています」 and 「きています」. So remember: 「はく」 below the waist, and add 「います」 to describe what someone has on." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a famous place in the world of denim. The town of Kojima, in Kurashiki city in Okayama prefecture, is known as the home of Japanese jeans. The area had a long history of weaving cotton and making school uniforms, and in the nineteen sixties local makers began producing jeans. Today Japanese denim, especially the kind woven on old fashioned slow looms, is prized by fans around the world for its texture and the way it fades with wear. Some collectors pay very high prices for it. There's even a street in Kojima lined with jeans shops, with denim decorations everywhere. So if you are a fan of jeans, you might hear 「にほんのジーンズ」 spoken about with real respect. You can ask in a shop 「これはにほんのジーンズですか」, are these Japanese jeans? So remember: jeans may be American by birth, but Okayama made them a Japanese craft." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English jeans, the final s sounds like z so it becomes 「ズ」, and since jeans go below the waist you say 「ジーンズをはきます」.",
      "Clothes below the waist take 「はく」, tops take 「きる」, and to describe what someone has on you use the ongoing form, 「ジーンズをはいています」.",
      "Kojima in Okayama is known as the home of Japanese jeans, where denim woven on slow old looms is prized worldwide, so 「にほんのジーンズ」 is said with respect."
    ],
    ja: "この[ジーンズ]はいくらですか。",
    en: "How much are these jeans?" },
  "じしょ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「辞書|じしょ」, two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「辞|じ」 means words or expressions. In other words it can also mean to resign or step down, but here it's about words. 「書|しょ」 means writing or a book. That's a very busy kanji you'll see everywhere. With its native reading it's the verb 「書く|かく」, to write. With this reading it's in 「図書館|としょかん」, library, 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, and 「りれきしょ」, résumé. So a dictionary is a book of words. Notice how many book and writing words end in 「しょ」. Once you know 「書|しょ」 means written thing, you can guess the meaning of new words more easily. Most students today use electronic dictionaries or apps, and in Japan dedicated electronic dictionary devices were very popular with students for years. Paper dictionaries are still used in schools, though. A useful sentence: 「これはにほんごのじしょです」, this is a Japanese dictionary. To say an English to Japanese dictionary, you'd use the language names, which you'll learn later. Recap: words plus book, the dictionary." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at the first kanji, 「辞|じ」. Its left side is the kanji for tongue, and its right side is the same shape as in 「辛い|からい」, spicy. As a memory trick only, think of words that come off the tongue, some of them spicy. That's a hook, not the real history, because the modern shape is a simplified form of a much more complicated old kanji. The second kanji, 「書|しょ」, does have a well known origin. Its top part pictures a hand holding a brush upright, ready to write. So the heart of the kanji is a hand with a brush. You can see the same brush holding hand at the top of 「筆|ふで」 in 「えんぴつ」, pencil, under the bamboo. Once you see that hand, many writing kanji become easier to recognise. So remember: a spicy tongue for words, as a trick, and a real hand with a brush for writing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's a phrase that surprises learners. To look something up in a dictionary, Japanese says 「じしょをひく」, literally to pull the dictionary. It comes from the days of paper dictionaries, when you pulled through the pages to find a word. People still say it, even with apps. You can also say 「じしょでしらべる」, to check in the dictionary, with 「で」 marking the tool. In class, you might ask your teacher 「じしょをつかってもいいですか」, may I use a dictionary? Japanese learners face a special puzzle: how to look up a kanji you can't read. Old paper kanji dictionaries were organised by radical and stroke count. Today you can draw the kanji with your finger on a phone, which feels like magic after the old way. Many learners keep a dictionary app open all the time. So remember the strange verb: you pull a dictionary, and you look things up with 「で」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「辞|じ」, appears in some interesting places. With the native reading 「や」, it's in 「辞める|やめる」, to quit or resign, as in quitting a job. That's the word stepping down sense. It's also in 「お世辞|おせじ」, flattery, words you say to please someone. If someone praises your Japanese, you might laugh and say they are just flattering you. There is also a close cousin, 「辞典|じてん」, which also means dictionary. It's often used in titles and for specialised dictionaries, like a dictionary of idioms. In conversation, 「じしょ」 is the everyday word, while 「じてん」 sounds a bit more formal. The second kanji in that cousin, 「典|てん」, means a standard or model work. So you have a book of words and a standard work of words. So remember: 「じ」 is about words, 「やめる」 is the stepping down sense, and 「おせじ」 is words to please." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「辞|じ」, words, plus 「書|しょ」, writing or book, so a dictionary is a book of words, and many book words end in 「しょ」, like 「教科書|きょうかしょ」.",
      "As a memory trick only, 「辞|じ」 is a tongue beside a spicy shape, words off the tongue, while 「書|しょ」 genuinely pictures a hand holding a brush upright to write.",
      "To look something up you say 「じしょをひく」, literally pull the dictionary, from paper days, or 「じしょでしらべる」 with 「で」 marking the tool.",
      "「辞|じ」 also appears in 「辞める|やめる」, to quit, and 「お世辞|おせじ」, flattery, while 「辞典|じてん」 is a more formal cousin meaning dictionary."
    ],
    ja: "これは{日本語|にほんご}の[じしょ]です。",
    en: "This is a Japanese dictionary." },
  "テープ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word tape. The t becomes 「テ」, the long a sound is shown with the long vowel mark, 「テー」, and the final p becomes 「プ」, with an extra u vowel added because Japanese syllables end in vowels. So a one syllable English word turns into a three beat Japanese word. That vowel stretching and adding is the most common thing that happens to English loanwords. In older textbooks, 「テープ」 usually means a cassette tape for listening practice, which is why it appears in a lesson full of classroom items. Today it more often means sticky tape or tape for wrapping. Clear sticky tape has its own common name in Japan, a brand name that became the everyday word, a bit like how English speakers say Scotch tape. You'll also see it in words like video tape. If you need some tape in a shop or office, you can say 「テープはどこですか」, where is the tape? A way to remember the meaning shift: in the classroom of the past, tape meant sound, today it means sticking. Recap: English tape with the vowel stretched, cassette tape in older books, sticky tape today." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japan has a whole roll of tape words, and some were made in Japan. Clear sticky tape is usually called 「セロテープ」, which is actually a brand name from the company Nichiban that became the everyday word. Wide brown packing tape is 「ガムテープ」, gum tape, a Japanese made compound, from the older kind that had gummed glue you moistened. 「マスキングテープ」 started as painters' tape, but Japanese companies turned it into a colourful craft product, and decorated paper tape became a stationery craze around the world. There's also the measuring tape and the finish line tape at a race, which Japanese also calls 「テープ」. And in older textbooks, a cassette tape for listening practice. So the same loanword covers sticking, measuring, racing and recording. When you buy some at a convenience store, ask for the specific kind. So remember: brand names and Japanese made compounds tell you which tape you mean." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful with two loanwords that sound very similar. This word is 「テープ」, ending with the p sound 「プ」. But 「テーブル」, table, also starts with the same long 「テー」, then has a b sound and an extra 「ル」 at the end. When people speak quickly, learners sometimes mix them up and ask for a table when they wanted tape. The difference is the little circle versus the two little dashes. The circle on 「プ」 makes a p, and the dashes on 「ブ」 make a b. Also, tape has three beats and table has four. Try saying them side by side and tapping the beats: tape is three taps, table is four. So whenever a katakana word has a p or b sound, glance at the mark. So remember: a circle for tape, dashes and an extra beat for table." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English tape, the vowel stretches and a 「プ」 is added, giving three beats, and it meant cassette tape in older textbooks but usually means sticky tape today.",
      "Brand names and Japanese made compounds pick out the kind: 「セロテープ」 for clear tape, 「ガムテープ」 for packing tape, and 「マスキングテープ」 for decorative craft tape.",
      "Don't confuse 「テープ」 with 「テーブル」, table: the circle on 「プ」 makes a p, while 「ブ」 has dashes for b, and table has an extra beat."
    ],
    ja: "[テープ]はどこですか。",
    en: "Where is the tape?" },
  "トレーナー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word trainer, but in Japan it means a sweatshirt. Look at the sounds first. The tr becomes 「ト」 plus 「レ」, because Japanese can't put two consonants together, so it adds a vowel. The long ai sound becomes a stretched 「レー」. The n is 「ナ」, and the er at the end becomes another long vowel, 「ナー」, because the r sound is dropped. Now the meaning. In English, a trainer is a coach, or in Britain a sneaker. Japan took the word and used it for the cotton pullover top you'd wear while training. This is a word made in Japan from English parts, so don't use it that way in English, or people will think you mean a coach. Japanese still also uses 「トレーナー」 for a fitness trainer or coach, depending on context. In fashion, younger people often say 「スウェット」, from sweat, for the same kind of top. A good shopping sentence: 「そのトレーナーはいくらですか」, how much is that sweatshirt? Recap: English trainer, the top you train in, the sweatshirt." },
      { title: "Made-in-Japan English", text: "This word belongs to a big, fun group that Japanese calls 「わせいえいご」, which means English made in Japan. These words are built from English pieces, but an English speaker would never use them that way. 「マンション」, from mansion, is just an ordinary apartment building, not a palace. 「サラリーマン」, salary man, is an office worker. 「コンセント」, from consent, is an electrical outlet in the wall. 「ノートパソコン」 is a laptop. And 「トレーナー」, the sweatshirt, fits right in. The trick with these words is to treat them as Japanese words that happen to sound English. If you try to guess the meaning from English, you'll often guess wrong, and if you use them abroad, people will look puzzled. When you meet a new katakana word, it's worth checking whether it's a true loan or a made-in-Japan one. So remember: the sweatshirt is part of a whole family of homemade English." },
      { title: "Wearing it", text: "Here's something English speakers trip over. Japanese has several different verbs for putting on clothes, and the right one depends on where the clothing goes. For anything on the upper body, like a shirt, a jacket or a sweatshirt, the verb is 「きる」. So you say 「トレーナーをきる」, to put on a sweatshirt. For things you step into, like jeans, skirts, socks and shoes, the verb is 「はく」, as in 「ジーンズをはく」. For hats, the verb is 「かぶる」, which means to cover your head. Using 「きる」 for jeans is a classic beginner mistake, and Japanese people will understand you, but it sounds odd. By the way, if your sweatshirt has a hood, most people call it a 「パーカー」, from the English parka. At a shop, a simple request works well: 「このトレーナーをください」, I'll take this sweatshirt. The rule to keep: tops are 「きる」, legs and feet are 「はく」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English trainer, Japan used it for the cotton pullover you train in, so it means a sweatshirt, though it can still mean a coach depending on context.",
      "It belongs to 「わせいえいご」, English made in Japan, alongside 「マンション」 and 「コンセント」, so treat it as a Japanese word and don't guess from English.",
      "Tops take 「きる」, so you say 「トレーナーをきる」, while jeans and shoes take 「はく」 and hats take 「かぶる」."
    ],
    ja: "その[トレーナー]はいくらですか。",
    en: "How much is that sweatshirt?" },
  "ノート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is short for the English word notebook. Japanese often clips long loanwords, keeping just the first part. Here only note made it in. The sounds change a little: the n plus long o becomes 「ノー」, with the long vowel mark, and the final t becomes 「ト」, with an extra o vowel because Japanese syllables end in vowels. Here's the key point for English speakers. 「ノート」 means the notebook itself, the thing you write in. It doesn't mean a short written message. For a quick note or memo, Japanese uses a different loanword, 「メモ」. Mixing these up is a very common mistake. The word also shows up in 「ノートパソコン」, a laptop, literally a notebook personal computer. Japan loves stationery, and shops are full of beautiful notebooks, often with grid lines to help you write kanji neatly. Students usually have one notebook for each subject. A useful question: 「これはだれのノートですか」, whose notebook is this? Recap: notebook clipped to note, always the book, never the message." },
      { title: "Clipped loanwords", text: "Japanese loves to chop long loanwords down to a few comfortable beats, and this word is one of many. Once you notice the habit, a lot of katakana suddenly makes sense. 「パソコン」 is personal computer, cut to the first two beats of each half. 「コンビニ」 is convenience store. 「エアコン」 is air conditioner. 「リモコン」 is remote control. 「スマホ」 is smartphone. The most common pattern takes the first two beats of each English word and glues them together, so you get four tidy beats. Sometimes, as with this word, only the first part survives and the rest is simply dropped. So when you hear a short katakana word that seems strange, try imagining the longer English words it might have been cut from. Often the full version will pop right out. In short, Japanese clips loanwords like a hedge, and this notebook word is one neat trim." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Notebooks are counted with 「さつ」, the counter for bound things like books and magazines. One notebook is 「いっさつ」, two is 「にさつ」, and three is 「さんさつ」. So at a shop you might say 「ノートをにさつください」, two notebooks, please. In class you'll hear a handy phrase, 「ノートをとる」, which means to take notes, writing down what the teacher says in your notebook. If you missed a class, you could ask a friend 「ノートをみせてください」, please show me your notes. That's a very common request among Japanese students, and it's polite to say thank you with a small snack afterwards. A notebook for a diary has its own word, 「にっき」, and a notebook for vocabulary might be called a 「たんごちょう」, a word book. So the key phrases are: count with 「さつ」, and 「ノートをとる」 to take notes in class." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Clipped from English notebook, it always means the notebook itself, never a short written message, which is 「メモ」 instead.",
      "Japanese chops long loanwords into a few beats, as in 「パソコン」 and 「コンビニ」, usually two beats of each word, but here only the first part, note, survived.",
      "Notebooks are counted with 「さつ」, as in 「ノートをにさつください」, and in class 「ノートをとる」 means to take notes."
    ],
    ja: "これはだれの[ノート]ですか。",
    en: "Whose notebook is this?" },
  "ペン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word pen. It's one of the simplest loanwords. The p and e stay as 「ペ」, and the final n becomes 「ン」, the one Japanese sound that can end a syllable without a vowel. So it's a nearly perfect copy of the English word, just two beats long. Notice the little circle on 「ペ」. That small circle turns the h sounds into p sounds in Japanese. It's how you know this is a p and not a b. The word builds into other everyday words. A ballpoint pen is 「ボールペン」, ball pen. A felt tip marker is often called a sign pen, a Japanese made compound. In a classroom it sits on your desk next to 「えんぴつ」, pencil, and 「けしゴム」, eraser. In Japan, official documents and forms are usually filled in with pen, often in black ink, and many pens have erasable ink that students love. Useful sentences: 「これはわたしのペンです」, this is my pen, or 「ペンをかしてください」, please lend me a pen, which you'll be able to say later. Recap: English pen, almost unchanged, the little circle makes it a p." },
      { title: "Pen family", text: "Several writing tools in Japanese are built around this little word. The mechanical pencil is 「シャープペンシル」, usually shortened to 「シャーペン」. One of the early Japanese versions was sold as the Ever-Ready Sharp Pencil, and its inventor later named his electronics company Sharp after it. A marker pen is a 「マーカー」, and a highlighter is often called a 「けいこうペン」, a glowing pen. The case you keep them in is a 「ペンケース」, or the older native word 「ふでばこ」, which literally means brush box, from the days when people wrote with brushes. The brush itself is a 「ふで」, and calligraphy with a brush is still taught in Japanese schools. So the old brush and the modern pen sit side by side in the same pencil case. Wrap-up: once you know this word, a whole drawer of stationery opens up." },
      { title: "Counting pens", text: "Pens are long and thin, so Japanese counts them with 「ほん」, the counter for long, slender things like pencils, umbrellas, bottles and even bananas. The sound of this counter shifts a little with the number, which takes some practice. One pen is 「いっぽん」, with a small pause and a p sound. Two is 「にほん」. Three is 「さんぼん」, with a b sound. Four is 「よんほん」, and five is 「ごほん」. Six is 「ろっぽん」, eight is often 「はっぽん」, and ten is 「じゅっぽん」. The pattern is that a small pause before it turns the sound into p, and the 「ん」 of three turns it into b. Try it at a shop: 「ペンをにほんください」, two pens, please. Notice that two pens and the word for Japan sound exactly the same, so context does the work. Wrap-up: pens are counted with 「ほん」, and its sound shifts with the number." },
      { title: "Spot the twin", text: "Here's a little quirk of writing that this word shows off. The katakana 「ヘ」 and the hiragana 「へ」 are almost identical. Even native speakers can't always tell them apart in print. That means the first letter of 「ペン」 looks exactly like the hiragana you'd see in a word like 「ぺこぺこ」, which means starving. So how do you know you're reading katakana? Look at the neighbours. The second letter, 「ン」, is clearly katakana, so the whole word must be too. Be careful with that one as well, because 「ン」 and 「ソ」 are another famous look-alike pair. In 「ン」 the long stroke sweeps up from the bottom left, while in 「ソ」 it falls down from the top. Writing them slowly a few times helps a lot. So the tip is: when one letter could be either script, let the letters around it decide." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It copies English pen almost unchanged in two beats, and the little circle on 「ペ」 is what turns the sound into a p rather than a b.",
      "It anchors a drawer of stationery words, like 「シャーペン」, mechanical pencil, 「けいこうペン」, highlighter, and 「ペンケース」 beside the older 「ふでばこ」.",
      "Pens are counted with 「ほん」, whose sound shifts to 「いっぽん」 and 「さんぼん」, and two pens, 「にほん」, sounds exactly like the word for Japan.",
      "The katakana 「ヘ」 and hiragana 「へ」 look almost identical, so let the clearly katakana 「ン」 next to it tell you the whole word is katakana."
    ],
    ja: "この[ペン]は{千円|せんえん}です。",
    en: "This pen is a thousand yen." },
  "おてあらい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「お手洗い|おてあらい」. It's made of three pieces. The front 「お」 is the polite prefix that softens a word. Then 「手|て」, hand, with its native reading. You know it from 「手紙|てがみ」, letter, and 「手伝う|てつだう」, to help, lending a hand. Then 「洗い|あらい」, washing, from the verb 「洗う|あらう」, to wash. So literally it's the honourable hand washing, the place where you wash your hands. That's a polite, indirect way to name the room, just like English washroom or restroom. Japanese has a few words for it. 「トイレ」, from English toilet, is the most common everyday word and is fine almost anywhere. 「おてあらい」 is softer and more polite, and you'll see it on signs in stores and stations. A cultural note: Japanese toilets are famous for high tech seats with heated seats and built in washing, and many have a button that plays a flushing sound for privacy. Asking the way is easy: 「すみません、おてあらいはどこですか」, excuse me, where is the restroom? Recap: polite plus hand plus washing, the restroom." },
      { title: "Polite word ladder", text: "Japanese has a whole ladder of words for this room, from blunt to very refined, and choosing the right one says something about you. At the bottom is 「便所|べんじょ」, an old, blunt word. You'll still hear it from older men or see it in old buildings, but it sounds rough. In the middle is 「トイレ」, the everyday word that nearly everyone uses. Then comes 「おてあらい」, softer and politer, perfect when asking a stranger. At the top is 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」, literally a makeup room, like the English powder room. You'll see it on signs in department stores, hotels and on airplanes. Notice how each politer word avoids naming what actually happens in there, talking about hand washing or makeup instead. English does the same with restroom and bathroom. So to sum up: the higher you climb the ladder, the more politely indirect the word becomes." },
      { title: "Borrowing the restroom", text: "Here's a phrase that surprises English speakers. In Japanese you borrow a restroom. To ask if you can use one in a shop or at someone's house, you say 「おてあらいをかりてもいいですか」, may I borrow the restroom? The verb is 「かりる」, to borrow, and you'll learn the may I pattern in a later lesson. Convenience stores in Japan often let customers use their restrooms, and it's polite to buy something small afterwards. On the doors, look for the kanji 「男|おとこ」, man, and 「女|おんな」, woman. Men's signs are often blue and women's red, but the kanji are the safest guide. At a friend's house, the host might simply point and say 「どうぞ」, go ahead. In stations, signs show the way with the same word or with 「トイレ」. To wrap up: in Japanese you don't use the restroom, you borrow it." },
      { title: "Slipper manners", text: "In many Japanese homes, and in some traditional restaurants and inns, the restroom has its own special pair of slippers waiting at the door. You take off the house slippers you've been wearing, step into the restroom slippers, and then switch back when you leave. The idea is to keep the restroom floor separate from the rest of the house, which is all about cleanliness. The most famous foreigner mistake is forgetting to switch back and walking into the living room or onto the straw mats still wearing the restroom slippers. People will notice, and it's a little embarrassing, though most hosts will just smile. These slippers often say 「トイレ」 on them, or have a cute pattern, so they're easy to spot. A good habit is to glance down at your feet every time you come out. The lesson: in a Japanese home, the restroom has its own shoes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the polite 「お」 plus 「手|て」, hand, plus 「洗い|あらい」, washing, so literally the honourable hand washing, a softer word than the everyday 「トイレ」.",
      "The words climb from blunt 「便所|べんじょ」 through 「トイレ」 and 「おてあらい」 to refined 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」, each politer one more indirect about what happens there.",
      "In Japanese you borrow a restroom, asking 「おてあらいをかりてもいいですか」, and on doors the kanji 「男|おとこ」 and 「女|おんな」 are the safest guide.",
      "In many homes the restroom has its own slippers, and the classic mistake is forgetting to switch back and walking out still wearing them."
    ],
    ja: "すみません、[おてあらい]はどこですか。",
    en: "Excuse me, where is the restroom?" },
  "たかい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「高い|たかい」, with its native reading. The main meaning of this word is high or tall, and expensive comes from that: a high price. English does the same thing when it says prices are high. The kanji is often described as a picture of a tall building, a tower with a roof on top. You already know this kanji with its Chinese-origin reading, 「こう」, in 「高校|こうこう」, high school, and 「高校生|こうこうせい」, high school student. Later you'll also learn 「背が高い|せがたかい」, which means tall, literally one's height is high. So one word covers both things. Context tells you which one is meant. A building that's 「たかい」 is tall, and a watch that's 「たかい」 is expensive. Its opposites split apart, though. Cheap is 「やすい」, and low is 「ひくい」, so be careful to choose the right one. This is an i adjective, so you can put it straight before a noun, as in 「たかいとけい」, an expensive watch, or end a sentence with it, as in 「このとけいはたかいです」. Recap: the high kanji, a high price, expensive." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an i adjective, and it changes its own ending to make negative and past forms. To say it's not expensive, drop the final 「い」 and add 「くない」, giving 「たかくないです」. Another polite version is 「たかくありません」. For the past, drop the 「い」 and add 「かった」, giving 「たかかったです」, it was expensive. That one has three k sounds in a row, so say it slowly at first. Put both together for the past negative: 「たかくなかったです」, it wasn't expensive. A very common beginner mistake is to add 「でした」 after the adjective, as in 「たかいでした」. That's wrong. The past lives inside the adjective itself, and 「です」 just stays at the end for politeness. Try a short exchange. A friend asks 「そのとけいはたかかったですか」, was that watch expensive? You answer 「いいえ、たかくなかったです」. So keep in mind: the adjective carries its own tense, and 「です」 just adds politeness." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Shopping in Japan, you'll hear and use this word a lot, often softened. If something costs too much, people rarely say a flat no. Instead they say 「ちょっとたかいですね」, it's a little expensive, isn't it, which politely signals they won't buy it. Haggling isn't normal in most Japanese shops, so a better follow-up is 「もうすこしやすいのはありますか」, do you have a slightly cheaper one? The word also stretches well beyond prices. A high voice is 「こえがたかい」, a high fever is 「ねつがたかい」, and a high temperature outside can be described the same way. Here's a quick dialogue. A shopper picks up a bag and says 「これはいくらですか」. The clerk answers 「ごまんえんです」, fifty thousand yen. The shopper says 「ちょっとたかいですね」 and gently puts it down. Wrap-up: 「ちょっとたかい」 is the polite way to say no thanks." },
      { title: "Beyond price", text: "The kanji 「高|たか」 shows up in some very handy words beyond the price tag. Add 「さ」 to the stem and you get 「高さ|たかさ」, the height of something, like a building or a mountain. One of the most useful words in casual Japanese is 「最高|さいこう」, the highest, which people use to mean the best or awesome. After a great meal or a fun trip, you'll hear 「さいこう」 with a big smile. The opposite kind of feeling is 「最低|さいてい」, the lowest, which means terrible. This kanji also appears in many family names. 「高橋|たかはし」, high bridge, is one of the most common surnames in Japan, so you'll probably meet someone with it. And remember from your lessons that it's in 「高校|こうこう」, high school, with the Chinese-origin reading. So the high kanji covers prices, heights, school and even the best of everything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「高い|たかい」 pictures a tall building, and its core meaning is high, so expensive is just a high price, though cheap is 「やすい」 while low is 「ひくい」.",
      "As an i adjective it carries its own tense: 「たかくないです」, 「たかかったです」 and 「たかくなかったです」, never 「たかいでした」, with 「です」 only adding politeness.",
      "In shops, 「ちょっとたかいですね」 is the polite way to decline, since haggling isn't normal, and the word also covers a high voice or a high fever.",
      "The kanji 「高|たか」 also gives 「高さ|たかさ」, height, and 「最高|さいこう」, the highest, which people use to mean awesome, plus the common surname 「高橋|たかはし」."
    ],
    ja: "この{時計|とけい}は[たかい]です。",
    en: "This watch is expensive." },
  "こくばん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In kanji it's 「黒板|こくばん」, two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「黒|こく」 means black. With its native reading it's the adjective 「黒い|くろい」, black, which you'll learn soon. 「板|ばん」 means board or plank. On its left side is 「木|き」, tree or wood, because boards are made of wood, the same piece you see in 「机|つくえ」, desk. On its own the reading is often 「はん」, but here it becomes 「ばん」. So the word is literally black board, exactly the same idea as the English. Some Japanese words are like that, built the same way as English, which makes them easy. A funny detail: Japanese blackboards are usually dark green, but they're still called black boards, just like in many English speaking schools. In Japanese classrooms the blackboard is still very common, and students take turns cleaning it at the end of the day. The person on duty wipes the board and cleans the erasers. A useful sentence: 「あれはこくばんです」, that over there is the blackboard. Recap: black plus board, exactly like the English, the blackboard." },
      { title: "Classroom phrases", text: "A few phrases come up again and again around the blackboard. A teacher might say 「こくばんをみてください」, please look at the board. When a student is called up to write an answer, they write it on the board, using 「チョーク」, chalk, from the English word. The board eraser is called a 「こくばんけし」, literally a blackboard eraser, built just like 「けしゴム」, with the erasing verb in front. The student on duty for the day is the 「日直|にっちょく」, and part of the job is wiping the board between classes and at the end of the day. Many schools and offices now use whiteboards, which are usually called 「ホワイトボード」, and you write on those with a 「マーカー」. A quick classroom scene: the teacher writes a kanji and asks 「よめますか」, can you read it? So to sum up: look at the board, write with chalk, and the duty student wipes it clean." },
      { title: "Black and board", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to useful families. The black kanji, 「黒|くろ」, gives you 「黒い|くろい」, black. Its partner is 「白|しろ」, white, with 「白い|しろい」. Here's a neat pair: a whiteboard can also be called 「白板|はくばん」, white board, built exactly like the blackboard word, though the katakana version is more common in daily life. The board kanji, 「板|ばん」, appears in 「看板|かんばん」, a shop sign or signboard, the kind that hangs outside a restaurant. With its native reading, 「板|いた」, it means a plank or a board on its own. Sushi chefs are sometimes called 「いたまえ」, literally in front of the board, meaning the cutting board. The tree piece on the left of the board kanji shows that boards were wooden. So in short: black and white pair up, and the board kanji takes you from classroom to shop sign to sushi counter." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「黒|こく」, black, plus 「板|ばん」, board, with the wood piece on its left, so it is literally blackboard, just like English, even though the boards are usually green.",
      "Around the board you hear 「こくばんをみてください」, students write with 「チョーク」, and the duty student, the 「日直|にっちょく」, wipes it with a 「こくばんけし」.",
      "「黒|くろ」 pairs with 「白|しろ」, white, while 「板|いた」 alone means a plank and shows up in 「看板|かんばん」, signboard, and 「いたまえ」, a sushi chef."
    ],
    ja: "あれは[こくばん]です。",
    en: "That over there is the blackboard." },
  "けしゴム|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word mixes a native Japanese verb with a Dutch loanword. 「けし」 comes from 「消す|けす」, to erase or to turn off, in the form that joins onto other words. The same kanji, read with its Chinese-origin reading 「しょう」, shows up in 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, firefighter, someone who puts out fires. 「ゴム」 means rubber, and it came from the Dutch word gom. The Dutch were the main Europeans trading with Japan for about two centuries, so several Dutch words came in that way. Notice it's written in katakana, while 「けし」 is in hiragana, because one half is Japanese and the other half is foreign. Put them together and you get erasing rubber. British English does almost the same thing, calling an eraser a rubber. Japanese also uses 「ゴム」 alone for a rubber band. Japanese erasers are a big deal among students. There are collectable novelty erasers shaped like food and animals, and serious ones that erase very cleanly. Asking for one is easy: 「けしゴムをください」, an eraser, please. Recap: erase plus rubber, half Japanese, half Dutch, the eraser." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The first half of this word shows a building trick you'll use constantly. Take a verb, use its stem, the part that comes before 「ます」, and stick a noun after it. 「けします」 gives the stem 「けし」, so erasing plus rubber gives the eraser. The same trick makes 「たべもの」, food, from eating plus thing, 「のみもの」, a drink, 「よみもの」, reading material, and 「かいもの」, shopping, from buying plus thing. Now the verb itself. 「けす」 means to erase, but also to turn off, so 「でんきをけす」 is to turn off the lights and 「テレビをけす」 is to turn off the TV. It's a u-verb, so the polite form is 「けします」. It has an intransitive partner, 「きえる」, which means to go out or disappear by itself, like a light going out or a word fading away. So the pattern is: verb stem plus noun builds new words, and the eraser is one of them." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a silly scene to lock in the word, purely as a memory trick. Picture a magician at a children's party. He waves his wand at a messy drawing and shouts a spell that sounds a bit like the start of the word, and parts of the picture vanish, as if erased. That's the verb for erasing and switching off hiding in the first half. Then he reaches into his mouth, pulls out a huge pink wad of chewing gum, and rubs it across the page to wipe away the rest. The second half of the word does sound a little like gum, and it really does mean rubber, which is what gum and erasers are both stretchy like. The kids cheer, the paper is clean, and the magician bows. Wrap-up: a vanishing spell plus a wad of gum, and you have the eraser." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It joins 「けし」 from 「消す|けす」, to erase, with 「ゴム」, rubber from Dutch, so it is erasing rubber, half Japanese in hiragana and half Dutch in katakana.",
      "A verb stem plus a noun builds new words, so erasing plus rubber gives the eraser, just as 「たべもの」 and 「のみもの」 are built, and 「けす」 also means to turn off.",
      "Picture a magician whose spell makes a drawing vanish, then rubbing gum across the page, since the first half is the erasing verb and the second sounds like gum and means rubber."
    ],
    ja: "[けしゴム]をください。",
    en: "An eraser, please." },
  "ビデオ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word video. Look at how the sounds were squeezed in. Japanese traditionally has no v sound, so v becomes b, giving 「ビ」. The middle d and e become 「デ」, and the final o stays as 「オ」. That v to b swap is one of the most common changes in loanwords, so it's worth remembering whenever you see a 「バ」, 「ビ」, 「ブ」, 「ベ」 or 「ボ」 in katakana. There is a special katakana spelling with a small vowel for v, but everyday words like this one use b. In this textbook, 「ビデオ」 means the video player, the machine, as well as a video tape, because the book was written when every classroom had one. Today people mostly use 「ビデオ」 for a video recording or a clip, like one you watch online. Japan was the home of the big video cassette formats, and Japanese companies made most of the world's video players for years. A simple sentence: 「これはにほんのビデオです」. Recap: English video, v turned into b, the video player." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The way Japanese talks about videos has shifted over the years. For clips online, like the ones you watch on your phone, the most common word today is 「どうが」, which literally means moving picture. You'll see it on websites and hear it from young people all the time. 「ビデオ」 still lives on in many compounds, though. A camcorder is a 「ビデオカメラ」, and a video call is often called a 「ビデオつうわ」. To record or film a video, you use the verb 「とる」, as in 「ビデオをとる」. To watch one, use 「みる」, as in 「ビデオをみる」. In the past, every town had rental shops called 「レンタルビデオ」 stores, where families borrowed tapes and later discs for the weekend. A few still survive. Quick dialogue: one friend asks 「そのどうが、みましたか」, did you watch that clip? So keep in mind: 「ビデオ」 for the older sense and compounds, 「どうが」 for clips online." },
      { title: "The missing v", text: "Once you know Japanese turns v into b, a whole set of katakana words becomes easy to decode. 「テレビ」 is television, with v becoming b and the end clipped off. 「バイオリン」 is violin. 「ビタミン」 is vitamin. 「バレーボール」 is volleyball. And 「ベスト」 can mean either best or vest, since both come out the same way. There is a special spelling for a true v sound, made from 「ウ」 with two small marks, giving 「ヴ」. You'll sometimes see it in names or stylish writing, like 「ヴァイオリン」, but most people still pronounce it as b, and dictionaries usually list the b spelling. When you hear a Japanese b in a loanword, try swapping in a v, and the English word often jumps out. So here's the rule: v becomes b, and the video word is your model for the whole group." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English video, Japanese turned the v into a b, giving 「ビ」, and in this textbook it means the video player or tape.",
      "For online clips people now mostly say 「どうが」, moving picture, while 「ビデオ」 lives on in compounds like 「ビデオカメラ」 and 「ビデオつうわ」.",
      "Since v becomes b, swapping a v back in decodes words like 「テレビ」, 「バイオリン」 and 「ビタミン」, even though a special 「ヴ」 spelling exists."
    ],
    ja: "これは{日本|にほん}の[ビデオ]です。",
    en: "This is a Japanese video player." },
  "ドア|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word door. Notice the ending. Japanese doesn't keep the r sound at the end, so the word ends on an open a sound, 「ア」, much like a British pronunciation of door without the final r. The d plus o becomes 「ド」. So you get a short, two beat word. This dropped r is very common in English loanwords: curtain becomes 「カーテン」, and many words ending in er end in a long vowel instead. Now the meaning. 「ドア」 is used for Western style doors, the kind on hinges that swing open, plus car doors and automatic doors in shops and trains. Traditional Japanese rooms have sliding doors and paper screens, and those have their own native words, often using 「戸|と」, door. So the loanword helps keep the Western style door separate. A cultural note: taxi doors in Japan open and close automatically, controlled by the driver, so don't pull them yourself. In the classroom, the door sits with the 「まど」, window, which you'll meet later. A simple sentence: 「あのドアはおてあらいです」, that door is the restroom. Recap: English door with the r dropped, the swinging Western style door." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Doors come with two important verb pairs, and they teach a key point of Japanese grammar. When you open the door yourself, you use 「あける」, with 「を」: 「ドアをあける」. When the door opens on its own, or you just describe it opening, you use 「あく」, with 「が」: 「ドアがあく」. Closing works the same way. 「ドアをしめる」 means I close the door, while 「ドアがしまる」 means the door closes. The first verb in each pair needs someone doing it. The second just reports what happens. You'll hear this on every Japanese train. The announcement says 「ドアがしまります」, the doors are closing, because the doors close by themselves. Shop doors that slide open automatically are 「じどうドア」, automatic doors. A common mistake is saying 「ドアがあける」, which mixes the two up. Remember: 「を」 with 「あける」 when you do it, 「が」 with 「あく」 when it happens." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A traditional Japanese house has very few swinging doors. Instead, rooms are divided by sliding panels. 「ふすま」 are opaque panels covered in thick paper, often with painted designs, used between rooms and on closets. 「しょうじ」 are lighter wooden lattices covered with thin white paper that lets soft light through, usually facing the outside. Sliding panels save space, because they don't need room to swing, and they can be removed to turn two small rooms into one big one for a party. Even modern apartments often have one Japanese style room with sliding panels. The front door is different. It opens onto the 「げんかん」, the entrance area, where you take off your shoes before stepping up into the house. In many Japanese homes the front door swings outward, so shoes left in the entrance don't block it. So the big idea is: Western rooms swing, traditional rooms slide." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English door with the final r dropped, it names Western style swinging doors, car doors and automatic doors, while traditional sliding doors have native words.",
      "Use 「ドアをあける」 when you open it yourself and 「ドアがあく」 when it opens on its own, which is why trains announce 「ドアがしまります」.",
      "Traditional rooms slide rather than swing, using paper-covered 「ふすま」 and light 「しょうじ」, while the front door opens onto the 「げんかん」 where shoes come off."
    ],
    ja: "あの[ドア]はおてあらいです。",
    en: "That door is the restroom." },
  "カーテン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word curtain. Look at how the sounds were squeezed into Japanese. The c becomes 「カ」. Then the ur sound in the middle of curtain, which is a sort of r coloured vowel, becomes a long a, written with the long vowel mark, 「カー」. The r itself disappears, the same thing that happens in 「ドア」, door. Then the t and the ending become 「テン」, with the final n as 「ン」. So curtain turns into four beats in Japanese. That pattern, an English ur or er turning into a long vowel, shows up again and again. You'll see it in 「コンピューター」, computer, and 「トレーナー」, trainer. The meaning is the same as in English: the cloth hanging over a window. Japanese homes with traditional rooms might use paper screens instead, but most homes today have curtains. Many Japanese families buy blackout curtains, since the sun rises very early in summer. In the classroom, curtains go with the 「まど」, window, and the 「ドア」, door. A useful sentence: 「あのカーテンはたかいです」, that curtain is expensive. Recap: English curtain, the ur stretched into 「カー」, the curtain." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has its own traditional cousins of the curtain. The most famous is the 「のれん」, a short cloth curtain split into panels that hangs over the entrance of a shop or restaurant. It usually shows the shop's name or logo. When the 「のれん」 is hanging out, the shop is open. When it's taken inside, the shop is closed for the day. To enter, you duck slightly and part the panels with your hand. The word also carries the idea of a shop's reputation, so an old established business is proud of its 「のれん」. In summer, many homes hang 「すだれ」, blinds made of thin strips of bamboo or reed. They block the hot sun but let the breeze through, and they look cool and seasonal. So beyond the Western curtain, you'll see shop curtains that signal open or closed, and bamboo blinds for summer." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Look at a typical Japanese window and you'll often see two layers. There's a thick curtain for darkness and warmth, and behind it a thin 「レースのカーテン」, a lace curtain. The lace one stays closed during the day, so people outside can't see in, which matters in crowded neighbourhoods where houses stand close together. To open curtains, use 「あける」, as in 「カーテンをあける」. To close them, use 「しめる」, as in 「カーテンをしめる」. A morning scene might go like this. A parent knocks and says 「もうしちじですよ」, it's already seven, then pulls the curtains open, and the sleepy kid groans 「まぶしい」, it's too bright. Moving into a new apartment usually means buying curtains, because Japanese rentals often come with bare windows. Window sizes vary, so people measure carefully first. Wrap-up: two layers, open with 「あける」, close with 「しめる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English curtain, the ur sound becomes a long 「カー」 and the r disappears, the same pattern seen in 「コンピューター」 and 「トレーナー」.",
      "Its traditional cousin is the 「のれん」, a split shop curtain hung out when open and taken in when closed, while summer homes hang bamboo 「すだれ」 blinds.",
      "Japanese windows often have a thick curtain plus a 「レースのカーテン」 for privacy, opened with 「あける」 and closed with 「しめる」."
    ],
    ja: "あの[カーテン]はたかいです。",
    en: "That curtain is expensive." },
  "たべる|食べる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「食|しょく」 is built around a food dish with a lid on top. Picture lifting that lid at mealtime to see what is inside. In this verb it uses its native Japanese reading, 「た」, and the hiragana 「べる」 after it carries the grammar. When the kanji appears in compound words, it usually switches to its Chinese-origin reading, 「しょく」. You can see that in 「食堂|しょくどう」, the cafeteria, a hall for eating. In 「食べ物|たべもの」, food, it keeps the native reading: an eating thing. Watch for this kanji squeezed onto the left side of other kanji, too. It sits inside 「飲む|のむ」, to drink, and inside the 「飯|はん」 of 「ご飯|ごはん」, a meal. Grammar note: this is a ru-verb, so you drop the 「る」 and add 「ます」 to get 「食べます|たべます」. Recap: a lidded dish of food, read 「た」 here, and the anchor for a whole family of eating words." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a ru-verb, and it's the perfect model for the whole group, because every form is built by dropping the final 「る」. The polite present is 「食べます|たべます」. The negative is 「食べません|たべません」. The past is 「食べました|たべました」. The te-form, for requests and linking actions, is 「食べて|たべて」, as in 「食べてください|たべてください」, please eat. The plain negative is 「食べない|たべない」. Nothing changes in the stem, so these are the easy ones. The particles are worth practising. What you eat takes 「を」, and where you eat takes 「で」. So 「どこでひるごはんを食べますか|どこでひるごはんをたべますか」 asks where you eat lunch. To invite someone, use 「食べましょう|たべましょう」, let's eat, or 「食べませんか|たべませんか」, won't you eat with me, which sounds softer. Recap: drop 「る」, add the ending, and nothing else changes." },
      { title: "At the table", text: "Eating in Japan starts and ends with set phrases. Before the first bite, everyone says 「いただきます」, which comes from a humble verb meaning to receive. It thanks the cook, the farmer and even the food itself. After the meal, you say 「ごちそうさまでした」, thank you for the feast. Saying these is expected, even when eating alone at a restaurant, quietly. There are also different verbs for eating depending on who's doing it. When you speak respectfully about a teacher or a customer eating, you use 「めしあがる」, as in 「どうぞめしあがってください」, please help yourself. When you speak humbly about yourself, you use 「いただく」. Among close male friends, you might hear the rough verb 「くう」, which is fine with buddies but rude in polite company. So the plain verb sits in the middle. Wrap-up: 「いただきます」 before, 「ごちそうさま」 after, and match the verb to the person." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick built on sound. Picture a big striped tabby cat sitting at a tiny dining table with a napkin tucked under its chin. In front of it is a little silver bell. Every evening, the tabby rings the bell, ding ding, and waits to be served dinner. Tabby plus bell sounds a lot like the Japanese verb, so when you think of eating, see that cat ringing its bell for food. Now add the kanji. The cat's dinner arrives under a domed lid, the kind you lift off with a flourish, and the top of the eating kanji looks like that lid, as your main lesson explained. The cat lifts the lid, sees fish, and gobbles it up, leaving only a clean plate. It then rings the bell again for seconds. Wrap-up: the tabby with the bell, lifting the lid on dinner, means to eat." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「食|しょく」 is a lidded food dish, read 「た」 in this verb and 「しょく」 in compounds like 「食堂|しょくどう」, and it hides inside 「飲む|のむ」 and 「ご飯|ごはん」.",
      "As the model ru-verb, every form just drops 「る」: 「食べます|たべます」, 「食べて|たべて」, 「食べない|たべない」, with 「を」 for what you eat and 「で」 for where.",
      "Meals open with 「いただきます」 and close with 「ごちそうさまでした」, and the verb shifts with the person: 「めしあがる」 for respect, 「いただく」 for humility, rough 「くう」 among buddies.",
      "Tabby plus bell sounds like the verb, so picture a tabby ringing its bell for dinner and lifting a domed lid, which matches the top of the eating kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}{朝|あさ}ごはんを[食べます|たべます|たべる]。",
    en: "I eat breakfast every day." },
  "のむ|飲む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the left side of the kanji. That is the food kanji from 「食べる|たべる」, squeezed narrow. The right side is a person with a wide open mouth, the same piece that often means yawning or gaping. Food plus an open mouth gives you drinking. The reading here is the native Japanese one, 「の」, with 「む」 in hiragana. It is a u-verb, so the polite form is 「飲みます|のみます」. Its family is easy to spot. 「飲み物|のみもの」 is a drinking thing, a drink, just as 「食べ物|たべもの」 is an eating thing. Here is a twist English speakers do not expect. In Japanese you drink medicine: 「薬を飲む|くすりをのむ」 means to take medicine, even if it is a pill. You can also use it with 「お酒|おさけ」 to mean drinking alcohol in general. Recap: food side plus open mouth, read 「の」, used for anything you swallow, from water to pills." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a u-verb ending in 「む」, and that ending has its own pattern for the te-form and the past. The 「む」 becomes 「ん」, and then you add 「で」 or 「だ」 with a voiced sound. So the te-form is 「飲んで|のんで」, and the plain past is 「飲んだ|のんだ」. The same thing happens with 「よむ」, to read, which becomes 「よんで」, and with verbs ending in 「ぶ」 and 「ぬ」, like 「あそぶ」, to play, becoming 「あそんで」. Many learners remember these three endings with a little chant, 「む」, 「ぶ」, 「ぬ」, all go to 「んで」. In polite speech, the stem is 「飲み|のみ」, giving 「飲みます|のみます」 and 「飲みましょう|のみましょう」, let's drink. To ask what someone wants to drink, say 「なにを飲みますか|なにをのみますか」. Wrap-up: 「む」 turns into 「んで」, so drink becomes 「飲んで|のんで」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Drinking together is a big part of Japanese social life. After work, colleagues often go to a 「飲み会|のみかい」, a drinking party, at a casual pub. It usually starts with everyone raising a glass and saying 「かんぱい」, cheers, which literally means dry the cup. A key manner is that you don't pour your own drink. Instead, you keep an eye on the people around you and top up their glasses, and they'll do the same for you. If you don't want more, leave your glass full. Many places offer 「飲み放題|のみほうだい」, all you can drink for a set time and price, which is popular for groups. Not drinking alcohol is perfectly fine. Just order oolong tea or juice and join the toast anyway. The point of the evening is the company, not the alcohol. Recap: say 「かんぱい」, pour for others, and you're part of the group." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Try this memory trick, which plays on sound. Picture a little garden gnome with a red pointy hat, sitting on a toadstool. He's terribly thirsty, so he picks up a mug that's bigger than his whole body and starts gulping. Glug, glug, glug. Gnome plus a long satisfied moo-like sigh at the end sounds close to the Japanese verb, so the thirsty gnome is your hook for drinking. Now give him the kanji. Next to him sits his lunch tray, the food piece from the eating kanji, and he opens his mouth wide as a yawn to drain the mug, which matches the open mouth on the right side of the drinking kanji. Then he takes his medicine with the last sip, because in Japanese you drink pills too. Wrap-up: a thirsty gnome with a giant mug and a wide-open mouth means to drink." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is the food piece beside a person with a wide open mouth, read 「の」, and you use it for anything you swallow, even medicine, as in 「薬を飲む|くすりをのむ」.",
      "As a u-verb ending in 「む」, its te-form is 「飲んで|のんで」 and plain past 「飲んだ|のんだ」, following the chant that 「む」, 「ぶ」 and 「ぬ」 all go to 「んで」.",
      "At a 「飲み会|のみかい」 everyone toasts with 「かんぱい」 and you pour for others rather than yourself, while choosing a soft drink is perfectly fine.",
      "Picture a thirsty garden gnome gulping from a giant mug with a moo-like sigh, his lunch tray beside him and his mouth wide open, matching both halves of the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}コーヒーを[飲みます|のみます|のむ]。",
    en: "I drink coffee in the morning." },
  "ねる|寝る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The top of the kanji is the roof piece you also see on 「家|いえ」, house. Squeezed into the lower left is a narrow shape that comes from an old drawing of a bed. So you have a bed under your roof, which is where you sleep. The reading here is the native Japanese one, 「ね」, with 「る」 in hiragana. It is a ru-verb, so the polite form is 「寝ます|ねます」. Be careful with the meaning. It covers both going to bed and being asleep, and it can even mean simply lying down. To say you feel sleepy, Japanese uses a different word, 「眠い|ねむい」, with a different kanji. The same 「寝|ね」 shows up in 「朝寝坊する|あさねぼうする」, to oversleep, which is literally sleeping into the morning. A common pattern is a time plus 「に」, as in going to bed at eleven. Recap: a bed under a roof, read 「ね」, for going to bed and sleeping." },
      { title: "Sleep words", text: "Japanese has a small family of sleep words, and each has its own job. This verb is the broad one, covering going to bed, lying down and sleeping. A close partner is 「眠る|ねむる」, which focuses on actually being asleep, deeply and quietly, like a baby sound asleep. It's more literary and sounds a little gentle. The opposite of going to bed is 「起きる|おきる」, to get up or wake up. And to wake someone else up, you use 「起こす|おこす」. The bed kanji also appears in 「昼寝|ひるね」, a nap, literally noon sleep, and in 「寝坊|ねぼう」, oversleeping. A common phrase in the morning is 「よくねましたか」, did you sleep well? And to say someone is asleep right now, use the te-form with 「いる」: 「ねています」. So the family is: going to bed and sleeping with this verb, deep sleep with 「ねむる」, and waking with 「おきる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a true ru-verb, so every form simply drops 「る」. That gives you some of the shortest verb forms in Japanese. The polite form is 「寝ます|ねます」, the past is 「寝ました|ねました」, the te-form is 「寝て|ねて」, and the plain negative is 「寝ない|ねない」. The stem is just one kana, 「ね」. Be careful, though, because not every verb ending in 「る」 works this way. A few look like ru-verbs but are actually u-verbs, such as 「かえる」, to go home, 「はいる」, to enter, and 「しる」, to know. Those become 「かえります」 and 「はいります」, keeping the 「り」. This one is the real thing. A useful pattern from your lessons is the te-form for requests: a parent might say 「はやくねてください」, please go to bed early. Recap: a short, clean ru-verb, 「ねます」 and 「ねて」, with nothing tricky inside." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Before bed in Japan, people say 「おやすみなさい」, good night, or just 「おやすみ」 to family and friends. If you stayed up too late, you might complain of 「寝不足|ねぶそく」, not enough sleep, a word you'll hear from tired students and office workers all the time. Japanese commuters are famous for dozing on trains, and somehow waking up just in time for their stop. Napping in public, even briefly in a meeting, is often seen as a sign that someone is working hard, not as laziness. Traditionally, people slept on a 「ふとん」, a padded mattress laid on the straw mat floor at night and folded away into a closet in the morning. Many families still use them. Here's a quick exchange. A friend asks 「なんじにねますか」, what time do you go to bed? You answer 「じゅうにじごろねます」, around twelve. Wrap-up: 「おやすみなさい」, then off to your 「ふとん」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows a bed under a roof, read 「ね」, and it covers going to bed, sleeping and lying down, while feeling sleepy is a different word, 「眠い|ねむい」.",
      "This is the broad sleep verb, while 「眠る|ねむる」 means being deeply asleep and 「起きる|おきる」 is getting up, and the kanji also appears in 「昼寝|ひるね」, a nap.",
      "It is a true ru-verb with a one-kana stem, giving 「寝ます|ねます」 and 「寝て|ねて」, unlike look-alikes such as 「かえる」 and 「はいる」 that keep 「り」.",
      "People say 「おやすみなさい」 before bed, complain of 「寝不足|ねぶそく」 after staying up late, and traditionally sleep on a 「ふとん」 folded away each morning."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}じゅういちじに[寝ます|ねます|ねる]。",
    en: "I go to bed at eleven every night." },
  "よむ|読む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The left side of this kanji is the speech piece, which comes from 「言|げん」, to say. Whenever you see it squeezed onto the left of a kanji, the meaning has something to do with words or language. It appears in 「話す|はなす」, to talk, in the 「語|ご」 of 「日本語|にほんご」 and 「外国語|がいこくご」, and in the 「説|せつ」 of 「説明する|せつめいする」, to explain. Reading is taking in words, so it belongs in this family. The right side happens to look like the kanji for selling. A way to remember it, as a hook only: the words on a page are selling you a story. In this verb the kanji has its native reading, 「よ」, with 「む」 in hiragana. It is a u-verb, so the polite form is 「読みます|よみます」. You read books, magazines like 「雑誌|ざっし」, and newspapers like 「新聞|しんぶん」. Recap: the words radical on the left, read 「よ」, for taking in written words." },
      { title: "Readings of kanji", text: "This verb gives Japanese its name for the readings of a kanji. The noun form, 「読み|よみ」, means a reading, and you've been meeting two kinds all along. 「音読み|おんよみ」 is the sound reading, the one that came from Chinese, like 「しょく」 for the eating kanji. 「訓読み|くんよみ」 is the meaning reading, the native Japanese word attached to the kanji, like 「た」 in the eating verb. When you look up a kanji, the dictionary lists both kinds. A handy rule of thumb is that kanji standing alone or with hiragana after them usually take the native reading, while kanji joined to other kanji usually take the Chinese-origin one. And the way a word is read is its 「読み方|よみかた」, which you'll ask about constantly: 「このかんじのよみかたはなんですか」. Wrap-up: this verb names the very readings you're learning." },
      { title: "Reading the air", text: "Japanese stretches this verb far beyond books. The famous phrase 「空気を読む|くうきをよむ」 means to read the air, picking up on the mood of a room and what people want without them saying it out loud. It's a highly valued social skill in Japan, where people often hint instead of speaking directly. Someone who can't do it is said to be unable to read the air, and that's a mild criticism. You can also read a person's heart or feelings, 「きもちを読む|きもちをよむ」, and in games like shogi, you read your opponent's next move. The verb even covers reading aloud. A teacher might say 「こえにだしてよんでください」, please read it out loud. And in poetry, composing a short poem is described with a verb pronounced the same way. Wrap-up: in Japanese you can read a book, a face, a move, and even the air." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a u-verb ending in 「む」, which gives it a special te-form. The 「む」 turns into 「ん」 and you add 「で」, so you get 「読んで|よんで」, and the plain past is 「読んだ|よんだ」. That's exactly the same pattern as 「のむ」, which becomes 「のんで」. A very handy request is 「読んでください|よんでください」, please read it. The polite forms use the stem 「読み|よみ」, giving 「読みます|よみます」 and 「読みましょう|よみましょう」. Adding 「方|かた」 to the stem gives you 「読み方|よみかた」, a way of reading, just as 「たべかた」 is a way of eating. Later you'll meet the can-do form, 「読める|よめる」, as in being able to read kanji, a sentence you'll be proud to say. Recap: 「む」 goes to 「んで」, and the stem builds useful new words." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The left side is the speech piece from 「言|げん」, marking words and language as in 「話す|はなす」 and 「日本語|にほんご」, and reading means taking in words, read 「よ」 here.",
      "Its noun 「読み|よみ」 names kanji readings: 「音読み|おんよみ」, the Chinese-origin sound reading, and 「訓読み|くんよみ」, the native meaning reading.",
      "Japanese reads far beyond books, and the famous 「空気を読む|くうきをよむ」, reading the air, means sensing a room's mood without anyone saying it.",
      "As a 「む」 verb its te-form is 「読んで|よんで」, like 「のんで」, and its stem builds 「読み方|よみかた」, a way of reading."
    ],
    ja: "{図書館|としょかん}で{本|ほん}を[読みます|よみます|よむ]。",
    en: "I read books at the library." },
  "えいが|映画": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「映|えい」, means to reflect or to project, the way an image shows up on a mirror or on water. It has the sun on its left side, which fits a word about light and images. The second, 「画|が」, means a picture or a drawing. Put them together and you get a projected picture, which is exactly what a movie is: light thrown onto a big screen. The 「画|が」 also shows up in 「漫画|まんが」, comics, which are drawn pictures, and in 「漫画家|まんがか」, a cartoonist. Add the building kanji 「館|かん」 from 「図書館|としょかん」 and you get 「映画館|えいがかん」, a movie theater, a building for movies. To say you watch a movie, use 「見る|みる」, as in watching on the weekend. Recap: a projected picture, 「えい」 plus 「が」, and add 「館|かん」 for the theater." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a pair that trips up almost every beginner. This word for a movie sounds very close to 「えいご」, the English language. They both start with the same long 「えい」 sound, and only the last beat changes, 「が」 for the movie and 「ご」 for the language. Say them side by side a few times: 「えいが」, 「えいご」. The kanji make the difference clear. The language word uses 「英|えい」, from the old name for England, plus 「語|ご」, language, the same piece you see in 「にほんご」. The movie word uses the projecting kanji and the picture kanji instead. So the shared sound is just a coincidence. A sentence can even hold both: 「えいごのえいがをみます」, I watch English language movies. Listen carefully for that last beat. In short: 「が」 at the end is a movie, 「ご」 at the end is the language." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To invite someone to a movie, you'll use a pattern from your lessons: 「えいがをみにいきませんか」, would you like to go see a movie? The 「に」 after 「み」 means to do something, so you go in order to see. Ask about tastes with 「どんなえいががすきですか」, what kind of movies do you like? Japanese animation is called 「アニメ」, and Japanese live action films are often called 「ほうが」, while foreign films are 「ようが」, Western pictures. At the theater, foreign films are shown either with subtitles, 「じまく」, or dubbed, 「ふきかえ」, and people often choose by preference. Seats are reserved in advance, so you pick yours when buying a ticket. Audiences are very quiet, and many people sit through all the credits until the lights come up. Wrap-up: invite with 「みにいきませんか」, then choose subtitles or dubbing." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has one of the world's great film traditions. In the middle of the last century, director 「黒澤明|くろさわあきら」 made samurai classics that inspired Western filmmakers, and some of his stories were later remade as westerns. Animation is just as famous. Studio Ghibli, led by 「宮崎駿|みやざきはやお」, made films loved around the world, and one of them won an Academy Award. Japanese films often have a gentler, slower pace than Hollywood films, spending time on everyday moments, family meals and changing seasons. Going to the movies on the first day of each month is popular, because many theaters offer a discount that day. Many big anime films open in Japan months before they reach other countries, so fans abroad wait eagerly. Recap: from samurai epics to hand-drawn animation, Japanese movies have left a big mark on the world." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「映|えい」, to project or reflect, with the sun on its left, plus 「画|が」, picture, so a movie is a projected picture, and adding 「館|かん」 gives 「映画館|えいがかん」, a theater.",
      "It sounds nearly like 「えいご」, English, differing only in the last beat, so listen for 「が」 for the movie and 「ご」 for the language.",
      "Invite someone with 「えいがをみにいきませんか」, and in theaters foreign films come with subtitles, 「じまく」, or dubbed, 「ふきかえ」.",
      "Japan's film tradition runs from the samurai classics of 「黒澤明|くろさわあきら」 to the Studio Ghibli animation of 「宮崎駿|みやざきはやお」, loved worldwide."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}に[映画|えいが]を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch a movie on the weekend." },
  "おんがく|音楽": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings here. The first, 「音|おん」, means sound. On its own it has a native reading, 「おと」, for a sound or a noise. The second, 「楽|がく」, means music, and it also carries the idea of enjoyment and ease. So sound plus music, or enjoyable sound, gives you music. The 「楽|がく」 is the part to watch, because it changes its reading depending on the word. In 「楽器|がっき」, a musical instrument, it shrinks to 「がっ」, and the word means a music tool. In 「楽しみです|たのしみです」, I am looking forward to it, it switches to the native reading 「たの」, the fun side of the kanji. Same shape, same core idea of pleasure, different sounds. To listen to music, use 「聞く|きく」, and to play it on an instrument you would use other verbs later. Recap: sound plus enjoyment equals music, and 「楽|がく」 connects music, instruments and fun." },
      { title: "Sound family", text: "The sound kanji, 「音|おん」, has a surprising number of readings and relatives. With its native reading, 「おと」, it means a sound or noise, and it's in 「足音|あしおと」, footsteps, literally foot sounds. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「おん」, it's in 「発音|はつおん」, pronunciation, a word your teacher will use a lot. It also appears in 「音読み|おんよみ」, the sound reading of a kanji. Then there's a third reading, 「ね」, which shows up in a few special words. 「音色|ねいろ」 is the tone colour of an instrument, and 「本音|ほんね」, literally true sound, means your real feelings, as opposed to what you say to be polite. Add 「家|か」 to the music word and you get 「音楽家|おんがくか」, a musician. So the sound kanji takes you from footsteps to pronunciation to someone's true feelings. Wrap-up: one sound kanji, three readings, lots of words." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To ask about someone's taste, say 「どんなおんがくがすきですか」, what kind of music do you like? Many genre names are borrowed from English, like 「クラシック」, classical, 「ジャズ」, jazz, and 「ロック」, rock. Japanese pop music is hugely popular, and so are anime theme songs. Listening is 「おんがくをきく」, and to say you often listen, 「よくおんがくをききます」. Then there's karaoke, which Japan gave the world. The word 「カラオケ」 is made from 「から」, empty, plus a clipped form of the English word orchestra. So it means an empty orchestra, the band without a singer. People rent small private rooms with friends or coworkers and take turns singing for an hour or two. Choosing a song everyone knows is a nice move. Here's a quick exchange: 「カラオケにいきませんか」, shall we go to karaoke? Wrap-up: ask about tastes, then head to the empty orchestra." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Music is a regular subject in Japanese schools, and the class is simply called 「おんがく」. Elementary school children learn to play the 「けんばんハーモニカ」, a keyboard harmonica that you blow into through a tube, and later the 「リコーダー」, the recorder. Almost every adult in Japan remembers carrying one in their school bag. In middle school, many schools hold a 「合唱|がっしょう」 contest, where each class practises a choir song for weeks and performs in front of the whole school. It's a big event, full of emotion, and classmates often bond over it. After school, many students join brass band or light music clubs, where they form rock bands. Traditional instruments, like the 「こと」, a long zither, and the 「しゃみせん」, a three-stringed lute, are also introduced. So in short: music in Japan is a shared school experience, from recorders to choir contests." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「音|おん」, sound, plus 「楽|がく」, music and enjoyment, and 「楽|がく」 shifts to 「がっ」 in 「楽器|がっき」 and to 「たの」 in 「楽しみです|たのしみです」.",
      "The sound kanji has three readings: 「おと」 in 「足音|あしおと」, 「おん」 in 「発音|はつおん」, and 「ね」 in 「本音|ほんね」, your true feelings.",
      "Ask 「どんなおんがくがすきですか」, and remember that 「カラオケ」 is 「から」, empty, plus clipped orchestra, an orchestra without a singer.",
      "Music is a shared school experience in Japan, from the 「けんばんハーモニカ」 and 「リコーダー」 to middle school 「合唱|がっしょう」 choir contests."
    ],
    ja: "よく[音楽|おんがく]を{聞きます|ききます}。",
    en: "I often listen to music." },
  "おちゃ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「ちゃ」, tea, with the polite 「お」 in front. The 「お」 is so standard here that saying 「ちゃ」 alone sounds rough, so treat 「おちゃ」 as the normal word. It can be written with the kanji 「茶|ちゃ」, which has the grass piece on top, since tea is a plant. In Japan, plain 「おちゃ」 means green tea, the everyday drink served free in many restaurants. If you want the reddish brown kind most English speakers drink, that is 「紅茶|こうちゃ」, literally crimson tea, and it shares the same 「茶|ちゃ」. The tea kanji also hides in 「喫茶店|きっさてん」, an old style coffee shop, where it is read 「さ」. Here is a fun bit of history. The English word tea and the Japanese 「ちゃ」 come from the same Chinese word, said in different Chinese dialects. Words that traveled by sea became tea, and words that traveled overland became cha. Recap: polite 「お」 plus 「ちゃ」, and in Japan it means green tea unless you say otherwise." },
      { title: "Tea types", text: "Japanese tea comes in many kinds, and each has its own name built on 「ちゃ」. 「まっちゃ」 is powdered green tea, whisked into a bright green froth. It's the tea of the tea ceremony and a popular flavour for sweets and ice cream. 「せんちゃ」 is the most common everyday green tea, made from steamed leaves. 「ほうじちゃ」 is roasted green tea, brown and toasty with little bitterness, often served in the evening. 「むぎちゃ」, barley tea, is a cold drink that families keep in the fridge all summer, and it actually has no tea leaves at all. 「げんまいちゃ」 mixes green tea with toasted brown rice and tastes nutty. The tea ceremony itself is called 「さどう」 or 「ちゃどう」, the way of tea, and it's a slow, careful art of preparing and serving. Recap: add a word in front of 「ちゃ」 and you get a whole menu of Japanese teas." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Tea is everywhere in daily life. When you visit someone's home or office, you'll usually be served tea without asking, often with a small sweet, and the host will say 「おちゃをどうぞ」, please have some tea. To suggest a break, people say 「おちゃにしましょう」, let's have tea. Young people use a casual verb, 「おちゃする」, which means going out together for a drink at a café, even if they order coffee. Vending machines and convenience stores sell bottled green tea in huge variety, and here's the surprise for many visitors: it's unsweetened. If you expect sweet iced tea, you'll get a bitter shock. Tea is also free and refillable at many restaurants, so you can just ask for more with 「おちゃ、おねがいします」. Quick dialogue: a host asks 「おちゃ、いかがですか」, how about some tea? You answer 「いただきます」. Wrap-up: tea comes free, unsweetened and often." },
      { title: "Tea in words", text: "Tea is so central to Japanese life that it shows up in everyday words and sayings. The colour brown is 「ちゃいろ」, literally tea colour, after the brownish colour of tea leaves and roasted tea. A brown cat might be called a 「ちゃとら」, a tea tiger, meaning a ginger tabby. If a tea stalk floats upright in your cup, people say 「ちゃばしらがたつ」, a tea pillar is standing, and it's believed to be a sign of good luck. There's also a playful expression, 「おちゃのこさいさい」, which means it's a piece of cake, something very easy. The 「おちゃのこ」 part refers to a little snack served with tea, which is easy to eat. Kids love saying it after an easy test. Wrap-up: in Japanese, tea gives you a colour, a lucky sign and a way to say something is easy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The polite 「お」 is standard on 「ちゃ」, and plain 「おちゃ」 means green tea in Japan, while black tea is 「紅茶|こうちゃ」, crimson tea.",
      "Adding a word in front of 「ちゃ」 names each kind: powdered 「まっちゃ」, everyday 「せんちゃ」, roasted 「ほうじちゃ」, and barley 「むぎちゃ」, which has no tea leaves at all.",
      "Tea is served to guests without asking, often free at restaurants, and bottled green tea is unsweetened, which surprises visitors expecting sweet iced tea.",
      "Tea gives Japanese the colour brown, 「ちゃいろ」, a lucky floating stalk, and 「おちゃのこさいさい」, meaning something is a piece of cake."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[おちゃ]を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink green tea in the morning." },
  "がっこう|学校": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「学|がく」, means learning or study. You already know it from 「学生|がくせい」, a student, and 「大学|だいがく」, a college. In this word its last sound squashes into a small 「っ」, so 「がく」 plus 「こう」 becomes 「がっこう」. Japanese does this a lot when a 「く」 runs into a k sound. The second kanji, 「校|こう」, means school. It has the tree piece on its left. A way to remember it, as a hook only: an old schoolhouse built of wood. You know 「校|こう」 from 「高校|こうこう」, high school, which is a high school in the literal sense, high plus school. The learning kanji also shows up in 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, an international student, and 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester, where it squashes into 「がっ」 again. Recap: learning plus school building, with 「がく」 squashing to 「がっ」 in front of 「こう」." },
      { title: "School ladder", text: "The Japanese school system has a word for each step, and most of them are built from this word. Before school comes 「ようちえん」, kindergarten. Then children spend six years at 「小学校|しょうがっこう」, small school, which is elementary school. Next come three years at 「中学校|ちゅうがっこう」, middle school, and these first nine years are compulsory. Then most go on to 「高校|こうこう」, high school, which is short for a longer word built the same way. After that comes 「大学|だいがく」, college or university. The students get matching names: 「小学生|しょうがくせい」, an elementary school student, 「中学生|ちゅうがくせい」, a middle school student, and 「高校生|こうこうせい」 and 「大学生|だいがくせい」. Notice that the student words drop the school kanji and keep only the learning one. In short: small, middle, high and big, each step up the ladder has its name." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Talking about school means choosing the right particle. Going to school takes 「に」, as in 「がっこうにいきます」. Doing something at school takes 「で」, as in 「がっこうでべんきょうします」, I study at school. When school finishes for the day, you say 「がっこうがおわります」, and when it starts, 「がっこうがはじまります」. If you're sick and stay home, that's 「がっこうをやすみます」, I'll be absent from school. The verb 「やすむ」 means to rest, so you're literally resting from school. A common mistake is using 「で」 with go, but movement towards a place always takes 「に」 or 「へ」. Here's a quick morning dialogue. A parent calls 「はやく、がっこうにおくれますよ」, hurry, you'll be late for school! The kid grabs a bag and shouts 「いってきます」, I'm off. The parent replies 「いってらっしゃい」. Wrap-up: go with 「に」, study with 「で」, and say 「いってきます」 on the way out." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese school life has some customs that surprise visitors. The school year starts in April, when the cherry blossoms bloom, so entrance ceremonies and pink petals go together. Students change into indoor shoes called 「うわばき」 at the entrance, keeping the building clean. There are usually no janitors for classrooms. Instead, students clean their own classrooms, halls and even restrooms during 「そうじ」 time every day, which is meant to teach responsibility. Lunch is often 「きゅうしょく」, a school lunch that students serve to each other wearing white aprons and caps, and everyone eats together in the classroom. Most middle and high schools have uniforms, 「せいふく」, and after classes, students throw themselves into club activities, called 「ぶかつ」, from sports to music to calligraphy. Recap: cherry blossoms in April, indoor shoes, student cleaning, shared lunch and clubs after school." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「学|がく」, learning, plus 「校|こう」, school, with 「がく」 squashing into 「がっ」 before the k sound of 「こう」.",
      "Each school step is built from it: 「小学校|しょうがっこう」, 「中学校|ちゅうがっこう」, then 「高校|こうこう」 and 「大学|だいがく」, while the student words keep only the learning kanji.",
      "Go to school with 「に」, as in 「がっこうにいきます」, and do things there with 「で」, as in 「がっこうでべんきょうします」.",
      "Japanese school starts in April with cherry blossoms, and students wear indoor shoes, clean their own classrooms during 「そうじ」, and serve each other 「きゅうしょく」 lunch."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}[学校|がっこう]に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I will go to school tomorrow." },
  "としょかん|図書館": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, each read with its Chinese-origin reading. The first, 「図|と」, means a drawing, chart or plan. In most words it is read 「ず」, as in 「地図|ちず」, a map, so 「と」 is a special reading you mostly meet in this word. The second, 「書|しょ」, means writing or a written document. It is the same kanji as the verb 「書く|かく」, to write, and you see it in 「辞書|じしょ」, a dictionary, 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, a textbook, and 「履歴書|りれきしょ」, a résumé. The third, 「館|かん」, means a large building or hall. Look at its left side and you will find the food piece from 「食べる|たべる」. It also ends 「映画館|えいがかん」, a movie theater, 「美術館|びじゅつかん」, an art museum, and 「旅館|りょかん」, a Japanese inn. Add it up: a building for drawings and writings, which is a library. Recap: 「と」, 「しょ」, 「かん」, pictures and writing in a big building." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Public libraries in Japan are free, and you sign up for a card so you can borrow books. The verb for borrowing is 「借りる|かりる」, and the verb for giving them back is 「返す|かえす」. So a classic library sentence is 「本を借ります|ほんをかります」, I borrow a book. Students also treat the library as a quiet study space, especially before exams, and the good seats fill up early. Now watch the particles, because this word shows the difference perfectly. Where you go takes 「に」, as in 「図書館に行きます|としょかんにいきます」. Where an action happens takes 「で」, as in 「図書館で勉強します|としょかんでべんきょうします」. Mixing those two up is one of the most common beginner slips. Inside a school, a small library room is often called 「図書室|としょしつ」, using the kanji for a room, while a big standalone building gets the full word. And a shop where you pay for books is a different word again, 「本屋|ほんや」. So the library lends, and the bookstore sells." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Say this word in four even beats. The first is 「と」, the second is 「しょ」, the third is 「か」, and the last is 「ん」. The second beat is a single sound. The small 「ょ」 melts into the 「し」, so it is one quick sho, never she followed by yo. Keep the first beat short, too. Learners sometimes stretch it into 「とう」, and that makes the word sound wrong to a Japanese ear. The final 「ん」 counts as a full beat of its own, so give it its time instead of swallowing it. Small 「ょ」 can change meaning completely. 「びよういん」, with a full-size 「よ」, is a beauty salon. 「びょういん」, with a small 「ょ」, is a hospital. Same letters, very different visit. You can practice the same quick sound at the start of 「食堂|しょくどう」, a cafeteria. That is a different kanji, but your mouth makes the same move. Keep it to four tidy beats and the word will sound natural." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a hushed library late at night. On a reading table sits an empty tin can. Out of it climbs a big toe wearing a tiny bow tie, and it begins putting on a show for the books. Toe, show, can. The books lean off the shelves to watch. Then the librarian peers over her glasses and whispers that this is a library, not a theater. The embarrassed toe bows and slides back into its can. Now connect the scene to the kanji. The first kanji means a drawing, so the toe's show is a slideshow of little sketches. The second means writing, so the audience is made of written books. The third means a big hall, so the whole thing takes place in a grand building with high ceilings. The sillier the picture, the better it sticks. Next time you see the word, see the toe, the show and the can." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「図|と」, drawing, plus 「書|しょ」, writing, plus 「館|かん」, large building, so a library is a building for drawings and writings, with 「と」 a special reading of 「図|ず」.",
      "Libraries lend with 「借りる|かりる」 and take returns with 「返す|かえす」, and you go there with 「に」 but study there with 「で」.",
      "Say it in four even beats, with the small 「ょ」 melting into 「し」 for one quick sound, the first beat kept short, and the final 「ん」 given its full time.",
      "Picture a toe in a bow tie climbing out of a tin can to put on a show in a grand library hall: toe, show, can."
    ],
    ja: "[図書館|としょかん]で{本|ほん}を{読みます|よみます}。",
    en: "I read books at the library." },
  "テレビ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English television. The full Japanese form is 「テレビジョン」, but almost nobody says that. Japanese loves to clip long loanwords and keep only the front, so it became 「テレビ」. Notice two sound changes. Japanese traditionally has no v sound, so v turns into a b sound, giving 「ビ」. You see the same swap in 「ビデオ」, video. And the vowels are kept short and clear, with no long stretched sounds. Clipping is everywhere in Japanese loanwords. 「パソコン」 comes from personal computer, and 「エアコン」 comes from air conditioner. When you watch TV, you use 「見る|みる」, so you watch the TV rather than looking at a screen. The same word also means the TV set itself, the physical box in your living room. Recap: television, clipped to its first half, with v becoming b." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases you will actually need with this word. To watch it, use 「見る|みる」: 「テレビを見ます|テレビをみます」. To turn it on, use 「つける」, and to turn it off, use 「けす」. So before bed you might say 「テレビをけします」. When you saw something on TV, the screen is the means, so it takes 「で」: 「テレビで見ました|テレビでみました」, I saw it on TV. If a person appears on TV, the verb is 「出る|でる」, to come out: 「テレビに出ます|テレビにでます」. A single show is a 「番組|ばんぐみ」, and a TV station is a 「テレビ局|テレビきょく」. Here is a tiny dialogue. One person asks 「昨日の夜、何をしましたか|きのうのよる、なにをしましたか」, what did you do last night. The other answers 「テレビを見ました|テレビをみました」, I watched TV. A common mistake is saying you look at TV with a different verb or particle. Just remember 「を」 plus 「見る|みる」 for watching, and 「で」 for things seen on it. Those few pieces cover almost every TV sentence." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese television has some traditions worth knowing. The public broadcaster is called 「エヌエイチケー」, and households with a TV are expected to pay a reception fee to support it. Every New Year's Eve it airs a huge singing contest, 「紅白歌合戦|こうはくうたがっせん」, the red and white song battle, where female and male singers compete as two teams. Many families have it on in the background while they eat on the last night of the year. Each weekday morning the same broadcaster runs a short serial drama that many people watch with breakfast, nicknamed 「朝ドラ|あさドラ」, the morning drama. Commercial channels are famous for variety shows, where a panel of celebrities reacts to clips and games. Watch one and you will notice colorful captions popping up on screen to repeat the jokes and key lines. Those captions are actually great for learners, because you can read along with what people say. Japanese TV is a fun window into everyday language." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is television clipped from 「テレビジョン」 to its front half, with v turning into b as in 「ビデオ」, and you watch it with 「見る|みる」.",
      "You watch with 「テレビを見ます|テレビをみます」, turn it on with 「つける」 and off with 「けす」, and things seen on it take 「で」, as in 「テレビで見ました|テレビでみました」.",
      "The public broadcaster 「エヌエイチケー」 airs the New Year's Eve 「紅白歌合戦|こうはくうたがっせん」, while variety shows' on-screen captions make great reading practice for learners."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[テレビ]を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch TV every night." },
  "ざっし|雑誌": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「雑|ざつ」, means mixed, assorted or miscellaneous. In this word its last sound squashes into a small 「っ」 before the 「し」, so 「ざつ」 plus 「し」 becomes 「ざっし」. The second, 「誌|し」, means a record or a periodical publication. Look at its left side and you will see the speech piece, the same one on 「読む|よむ」, to read, and 「話す|はなす」, to talk. That piece always points to words and language. So a magazine is a mixed record, a bundle of assorted articles, photos and ads. That fits real magazines well, which mix many short pieces on different topics. Compare 「新聞|しんぶん」, a newspaper, which is literally new things heard, news. A typical sentence is reading a magazine at a cafe with 「読む|よむ」. Recap: mixed plus written record, with 「ざつ」 squashing to 「ざっ」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closer at the first kanji, the one meaning mixed. Its right side is a component often called the old bird, a short-tailed bird shape. You can find the same bird tucked into the lower right of 「曜|よう」 in 「曜日|ようび」, the days of the week. The meaning mixed shows up in many useful words. 「雑音|ざつおん」 is noise, mixed sounds that you do not want. 「雑談|ざつだん」 is small talk, chatting about this and that. 「複雑|ふくざつ」 means complicated, things tangled together. And there is a surprise reading in 「雑巾|ぞうきん」, a cleaning rag, where the kanji is read 「ぞう」. The second kanji, the magazine one, combines the speech piece on the left with 「志|こころざし」 on the right, a kanji meaning will or aim. You will mostly meet the second kanji at the end of words for publications. So the heart of this word is a mixed bag, a jumble of articles in one place. Learn the first kanji well, because it keeps turning up." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji in this word works like a label for printed publications, and it attaches to the end of other words. 「週刊誌|しゅうかんし」 is a weekly magazine, using the week kanji you know from 「毎週|まいしゅう」. 「月刊誌|げっかんし」 is a monthly magazine. Japanese also likes to clip the front word, so a fashion magazine is often just 「ファッション誌|ファッションし」. Another member is 「日誌|にっし」, a log or daily record, like a class log that the student on duty fills in. Notice the same small 「っ」 squeeze happens there, just like in this word. Be careful of a sound alike. 「紙|かみ」, paper, has a Chinese-origin reading 「し」 too, but it is a different kanji with the thread piece on the left. In 「新聞紙|しんぶんし」, it means newspaper as paper, the stuff you wrap things in. So when you hear 「し」 at the end of a publication word, think of the speech piece and printed pages. This little label opens up a whole shelf of words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Magazines have a special place in Japanese life, especially manga magazines. Many famous manga series first appear a chapter at a time in thick weekly magazines printed on cheap, colored paper. Only later are the chapters collected into small books. A weekly manga magazine can be as thick as a phone book and is read quickly and often left behind. Convenience stores traditionally have a magazine rack right by the front window. Standing there and reading for free even has its own word, 「立ち読み|たちよみ」, literally stand reading. It was so common that people joked the window was full of readers. These days some stores tape magazines shut to discourage it. Train stations used to have small kiosks selling magazines for the commute, though phones have replaced much of that. If you want easy reading practice, magazines are a good choice, because the articles are short and full of photos. Pick one on a hobby you love." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「雑|ざつ」, mixed, plus 「誌|し」, a periodical record with the speech piece on its left, so a magazine is a mixed record, with 「ざつ」 squashing to 「ざっ」.",
      "The mixed kanji 「雑|ざつ」 has an old bird on its right and appears in 「雑音|ざつおん」, noise, 「雑談|ざつだん」, small talk, and 「複雑|ふくざつ」, complicated.",
      "「誌|し」 works as a label for publications, as in 「週刊誌|しゅうかんし」 and 「月刊誌|げっかんし」, not to be confused with 「紙|かみ」, paper, which has the thread piece.",
      "Manga first appear chapter by chapter in thick weekly magazines, and reading for free at a convenience store rack even has its own word, 「立ち読み|たちよみ」."
    ],
    ja: "カフェで[雑誌|ざっし]を{読みます|よみます}。",
    en: "I read a magazine at a cafe." },
  "スポーツ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English sports, plural, and that plural s survives in the Japanese word. Watch how the sounds get squeezed into Japanese syllables. Japanese syllables almost always end in a vowel, so the s at the start gets a little u vowel and becomes 「ス」. The p joins with o to make 「ポ」. The or sound is borrowed the British way, without a strong r, so it turns into a long o, written with the long bar 「ー」. The final ts lands neatly on 「ツ」, a sound Japanese already has. To say you play sports, use 「する」: 「スポーツをする」. That pattern of a loanword plus 「する」 is everywhere, so it is worth getting used to. Specific sports work the same way, like 「テニス」 and 「サッカー」. One more note: Japanese uses this one word for both sport and sports, so there is no singular or plural to worry about. Recap: English sports, with extra vowels, a long o in place of the r, and paired with 「する」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The classic small talk question is 「好きなスポーツは何ですか|すきなスポーツはなんですか」, what sport do you like. You can answer with any sport name plus 「です」. To say you play, use 「する」, and to say you watch, use 「見る|みる」. So someone might say they do not play much, but they love watching on TV. A near synonym to know is 「運動|うんどう」. That word means physical exercise in general, like walking or stretching, while 「スポーツ」 means games and sports with rules. So a doctor tells you to get more 「運動|うんどう」, not more sports. A person who plays professionally is a 「スポーツ選手|スポーツせんしゅ」, an athlete. The place where you work out is often a 「スポーツジム」, or just 「ジム」. Here is a mini dialogue. One person asks 「週末、何をしますか|しゅうまつ、なにをしますか」. The other says 「スポーツをします」. Easy and natural. With 「する」 for playing and 「見る|みる」 for watching, you can talk about any sport you like." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Sports are woven into Japanese school life. Most middle and high school students join an after-school club called 「部活|ぶかつ」, short for club activities. Sports clubs practice almost every day, often on weekends too, and the bond between older and younger members is a big part of school memories. Baseball is hugely popular. The national high school baseball tournament, known by the name of its stadium, 「甲子園|こうしえん」, is broadcast across the country, and the whole nation cheers for teams from their home region. Schools also hold a sports day called 「運動会|うんどうかい」, where classes compete in relay races and tug of war while parents picnic and film. Sumo is the traditional national sport, with big tournaments several times a year. Soccer has grown hugely since the professional league began in the nineties. So if you want to make friends in Japan, asking about someone's school club is a great conversation starter." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English sports, with vowels added to 「ス」, a long o replacing the r, and a final 「ツ」, and you play it with 「する」, as in 「スポーツをする」.",
      "Use 「する」 for playing and 「見る|みる」 for watching, and keep it apart from 「運動|うんどう」, which means general physical exercise rather than games with rules.",
      "Most students join an after-school 「部活|ぶかつ」 club, the nation follows the 「甲子園|こうしえん」 high school baseball tournament, and schools hold a 「運動会|うんどうかい」 sports day."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}に[スポーツ]をします。",
    en: "I play sports on the weekend." },
  "デート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English date, but only in the romantic sense. It never means a calendar date. For the day of the month, Japanese uses completely different words, such as 「日付|ひづけ」. So the meaning got narrower when it was borrowed. Now the sounds. The English vowel in date glides from e toward i. Japanese flattens that glide into one long e, written with the long bar 「ー」, so you get 「デー」. Then the final t cannot end a syllable on its own, so it picks up an o vowel and becomes 「ト」. Most English words ending in t or d get an o like this. Like many loanwords, it pairs with 「する」, so 「デートをする」 means to go on a date. You can also say you have a date with someone using 「と」, with. A common mistake is using it for an appointment with a friend or a doctor. That would sound romantic, which is probably not what you mean. Recap: romantic date only, long e plus an o at the end, and used with 「する」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To go on a date with someone, use 「と」 for the partner: 「彼女とデートします|かのじょとデートします」 means I have a date with my girlfriend. To ask someone out, the verb is 「誘う|さそう」, to invite, so you invite them on a date. A first date is 「初デート|はつデート」, using a kanji meaning first. A popular place for dates is called a 「デートスポット」. Here is a near synonym to keep apart. 「デート」 is one outing. Being in a relationship, going out steadily, is a different verb, 「付き合う|つきあう」. So you can go on a date without dating someone in the steady sense. A mini dialogue. One friend teases, 「土曜日はデートですか|どようびはデートですか」, is Saturday a date? The other answers shyly, 「はい、映画を見ます|はい、えいがをみます」, yes, we are watching a movie. Japanese friends love to ask about this, so expect some teasing. Keep this word for romance, and you will use it just right." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has its own calendar of date days. Christmas Eve is the big one. While families celebrate New Year together, Christmas Eve is seen as a romantic night for couples, with fancy dinners, lights and cake. Restaurants book up weeks ahead. Valentine's Day works differently from many Western countries. Traditionally women give chocolate to men. Chocolate for someone you truly like is 「本命チョコ|ほんめいチョコ」, true favorite chocolate, while chocolate given out of social duty to coworkers is 「義理チョコ|ぎりチョコ」, obligation chocolate. Then, one month later on March fourteenth, comes White Day, when men are expected to return the favor with gifts. Fireworks festivals in summer are another classic date, often with people wearing light cotton robes called 「ゆかた」. Knowing these customs helps you understand Japanese dramas and songs, which are full of them. Love has a schedule in Japan." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English date but only the romantic kind, never a calendar date, with a long 「デー」 and an o added to make 「ト」, used as 「デートをする」.",
      "Your partner takes 「と」, as in 「彼女とデートします|かのじょとデートします」, and one outing differs from dating steadily, which is 「付き合う|つきあう」.",
      "Christmas Eve is Japan's big couples' night, and on Valentine's Day women give 「本命チョコ|ほんめいチョコ」 or 「義理チョコ|ぎりチョコ」, returned by men on White Day."
    ],
    ja: "{土曜日|どようび}に[デート]をします。",
    en: "I have a date on Saturday." },
  "テニス|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English tennis. The sound changes are simple. English writes a double n, but it is really just one n sound, so Japanese uses a single 「ニ」. The final s cannot end a syllable, so it picks up a quiet u vowel and becomes 「ス」. In normal speech that final u is barely voiced, so it sounds close to the English. To say you play tennis, use 「する」: 「テニスをする」. This is the standard pattern for most sports in Japanese, like 「サッカー」 and 「スポーツ」 itself. English speakers sometimes reach for a separate verb meaning to play, but for sports 「する」 is what you want. Tennis is popular in Japan, especially soft tennis, a version played with a soft rubber ball that many students take up in school clubs. Recap: tennis with a single n and an extra u, and you play it with 「する」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the words that travel with this sport. You play on a 「テニスコート」, swing a 「ラケット」, and hit a 「ボール」. A school tennis club is a 「テニス部|テニスぶ」. To say someone is good at it, use 「上手|じょうず」 with 「が」: 「テニスが上手です|テニスがじょうずです」. Watch out when talking about yourself, though. Calling your own skill 「上手|じょうず」 sounds like bragging, so people say 「好き|すき」 instead, or say they are bad at it to be modest. Now the time particles. Days of the week take 「に」, so you say 「日曜日にテニスをします|にちようびにテニスをします」. But words like 「毎日|まいにち」 and 「明日|あした」 do not take 「に」. A tiny dialogue: one person asks 「テニスをしますか」, do you play tennis? The other replies 「はい、時々します|はい、ときどきします」, yes, sometimes. That small set of words lets you talk about tennis with anyone." },
      { title: "Sports cousins", text: "Many sports in Japanese are loanwords, and they follow the same patterns as this one. You play almost all of them with 「する」. 「ゴルフ」 is golf, where the l turns into an r-like sound and lonely consonants get a u. 「サッカー」 is soccer, with a small 「っ」 for the double c. Japanese also clips long sports names. Basketball becomes 「バスケ」, and volleyball becomes 「バレー」. Here is a fun trap. 「バレー」 with a long bar is volleyball, but 「バレエ」 with a small extra 「エ」 is ballet, the dance. They sound almost the same, and only the spelling shows which one you mean. Some sports kept older native words too, like 「野球|やきゅう」 for baseball, literally field ball, which is far more common than the loanword. So when you learn a new sport, check whether Japanese borrowed it, clipped it, or made its own word. Each one tells a little story of how it reached Japan." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English tennis, with the double n reduced to one 「ニ」 and a quiet u added for 「ス」, and like most sports you play it with 「する」.",
      "Say someone is good with 「テニスが上手です|テニスがじょうずです」, but for yourself use 「好き|すき」 instead, since calling your own skill 「上手|じょうず」 sounds like bragging.",
      "Sports loanwords share patterns, like clipped 「バスケ」 and 「バレー」, but watch out because 「バレー」 is volleyball while 「バレエ」 is ballet."
    ],
    ja: "{日曜日|にちようび}に[テニス]をします。",
    en: "I play tennis on Sunday." },
  "アイスクリーム|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English ice cream, and it is a great example of how Japanese adds vowels. English ice cream is just two syllables. Japanese syllables mostly end in vowels, so every lonely consonant gets one. The i sound in ice is a glide from a to i, so it becomes 「アイ」, and the final s gets a u vowel to make 「ス」. In cream, the c and r are crowded together, so each one needs its own vowel. The c becomes 「ク」, and the r becomes 「リ」 with a long vowel 「ー」. The final m becomes 「ム」. Two syllables in English grow into seven beats in Japanese. In everyday speech people often shorten it to just 「アイス」, which also means ice cream, not plain ice. If you want ice cubes, that is 「こおり」. That shortcut trips up English speakers who order ice and get dessert. Recap: ice cream stretched out with extra vowels, and often clipped to 「アイス」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how this word shows up when you actually buy some. At a shop you choose a flavor, which is 「味|あじ」, taste. Flavors attach straight to it, so vanilla is 「バニラ味|バニラあじ」. A clerk or friend might ask 「何味にしますか|なにあじにしますか」, which flavor will you have. You answer with the flavor plus 「にします」, like 「チョコにします」, I'll have chocolate. That pattern of choosing with 「にします」 works for ordering anything. Next, cone or cup. A cone is 「コーン」 and a cup is 「カップ」. At a convenience store you will mostly find packaged treats in the freezer, all called by the short form. The verb is 「食べる|たべる」, so you eat it, just like in English. And a time word such as 「今日|きょう」 needs no particle, so 「今日アイスを食べます|きょうアイスをたべます」 is perfect. With flavor, 「にします」, and 「食べる|たべる」, you can handle any ice cream stand in Japan." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan takes frozen desserts seriously, and the flavors can surprise you. Green tea, 「抹茶|まっちゃ」, is a classic, slightly bitter and very popular. You will also find black sesame, sweet potato, and red bean. Soft serve has its own Japanese-made name, 「ソフトクリーム」, soft cream, and tourist spots love to sell local flavors of it, like lavender in fields of flowers or rich milk in dairy regions of the north. Stores constantly release flavors labeled 「期間限定|きかんげんてい」, limited time only, so there is always something new to try. People also enjoy eating ice cream in winter. A favorite cozy scene is sitting under a heated table called a 「こたつ」, warm legs, eating something icy. Another favorite is ice cream wrapped in soft rice cake, a fusion of old and new sweets. So when you visit, skip the plain vanilla now and then. Japan rewards the adventurous eater." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Ice cream grows from two English syllables to seven Japanese beats as every lonely consonant gets a vowel, and it is often clipped to 「アイス」, which means ice cream, not ice.",
      "Flavors attach to 「味|あじ」, as in 「バニラ味|バニラあじ」, and you choose with 「にします」, as in 「チョコにします」.",
      "Japan loves 「抹茶|まっちゃ」 and other surprising flavors, soft serve called 「ソフトクリーム」, and constant 「期間限定|きかんげんてい」 limited editions."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}[アイスクリーム]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I will eat ice cream today." },
  "ハンバーガー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English hamburger, which itself is named after the German city of Hamburg. Look at the sound changes. The m in ham turns into 「ン」, which naturally sounds like m before a b. The er sounds in burger have weak r sounds, so Japanese turns them into long vowels with the long bar 「ー」, giving 「バー」 and 「ガー」. That r becoming a long vowel happens in many loanwords, like 「コンピューター」. Here is a cultural note that surprises visitors. Japan also has a dish called 「ハンバーグ」, a hamburg steak. It is a juicy patty, usually served on a plate with sauce and rice, with no bun at all. So 「ハンバーガー」 is the sandwich in a bun, and 「ハンバーグ」 is the plate dinner. Mixing them up at a restaurant can get you the wrong meal. Recap: hamburger with r sounds turned into long vowels, and not to be confused with the bunless 「ハンバーグ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Ordering one in Japan uses phrases you can learn right now. Point and say 「ハンバーガーを一つください|ハンバーガーをひとつください」, one hamburger, please. A meal with fries and a drink is called a 「セット」. The clerk will then ask whether you are eating there or taking it out. Eating in is 「店内|てんない」, inside the store, and the polite question is 「店内でお召し上がりですか|てんないでおめしあがりですか」, will you be eating in? Taking it out is 「持ち帰り|もちかえり」, carrying home. You can simply answer with either word. That polite question uses a very honorific verb for eating that you will study much later, so just recognize the sound for now. Clerks speak very politely to customers, and you can answer simply and politely with 「です」 or 「ください」. One more thing. Japanese people often eat burgers neatly using the paper wrapper, so their hands stay clean. With these few phrases, you can order a burger with confidence." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「バーガー」 has become a building block of its own. Interestingly, the same thing happened in English. People misheard hamburger as ham plus burger, so burger became a word, even though the original name came from the city of Hamburg and had nothing to do with ham. Japanese happily uses the piece too. 「チーズバーガー」 is a cheeseburger. 「てりやきバーガー」, a teriyaki burger, has a sweet soy glaze and is a Japanese favorite you rarely see abroad. 「エビバーガー」 has a fried shrimp patty. There is even the 「ライスバーガー」, where the bun is replaced by two patties of pressed rice, invented by a Japanese chain in the eighties. Fish burgers, chicken burgers and seasonal specials fill the menus too. So once you know the ending, you can read nearly any burger menu, because the front part tells you what is inside. Burger is a word that grew from a mistake, and Japan ran with it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From hamburger, the weak r sounds become long vowels in 「バー」 and 「ガー」, and it is the sandwich in a bun, not 「ハンバーグ」, a bunless patty served on a plate.",
      "Order with 「ハンバーガーを一つください|ハンバーガーをひとつください」, and when asked, answer 「店内|てんない」 to eat in or 「持ち帰り|もちかえり」 to take out.",
      "Burger became its own building block from a mishearing, so Japan has 「てりやきバーガー」, 「エビバーガー」, and the 「ライスバーガー」 with rice patties as the bun."
    ],
    ja: "{昼|ひる}ごはんに[ハンバーガー]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat a hamburger for lunch." },
  "おさけ|お酒": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the polite 「お」 plus 「酒|さけ」. The 「お」 is almost always used, so treat 「お酒|おさけ」 as the normal word. Look at the kanji. On the left are three drops, the water piece, which is a squeezed form of 「水|みず」, water. On the right is a shape that shows a jar for fermenting and storing liquor. Liquid in a wine jar gives you alcohol. The reading here is the native Japanese one, 「さけ」. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「しゅ」, which you hear in 「日本酒|にほんしゅ」, Japanese rice wine. That is an important cultural point. In English, sake means rice wine, but in Japanese 「お酒|おさけ」 means any alcoholic drink at all, including 「ビール」 and wine. If you want the rice wine specifically, say 「日本酒|にほんしゅ」. To drink alcohol, use 「飲む|のむ」. The legal drinking age in Japan is twenty. Recap: water drops plus a liquor jar, read 「さけ」, and it means alcohol in general." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The right side of this kanji is a component shaped like a jar, and it has a big family. Wherever it appears, fermentation or alcohol is usually close by. 「酔う|よう」 means to get drunk, and it has the jar on the left. 「酢|す」, vinegar, has it too, and vinegar is made by fermenting. The 「醤|しょう」 in 「醤油|しょうゆ」, soy sauce, has the jar at the bottom, and soy sauce is fermented as well. So the jar piece marks a whole kitchen of fermented things. Now the readings of the alcohol kanji itself. Besides 「さけ」 and 「しゅ」, there is a third reading, 「さか」, used when it comes first in a word. 「酒屋|さかや」 is a liquor shop. 「居酒屋|いざかや」 is a Japanese pub, literally a place where you stay and drink, and there the sound softens to 「ざか」. Spot the jar and you can guess the theme of a kanji before you know it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the expressions that come up around drinks. Before the first sip, everyone raises a glass and says 「乾杯|かんぱい」, cheers. To say someone handles alcohol well, Japanese says they are strong, 「お酒が強い|おさけがつよい」. Someone who gets tipsy fast is weak, 「お酒が弱い|おさけがよわい」. These are very common ways to describe people. If you do not drink, the clear polite answer is 「お酒は飲みません|おさけはのみません」. Notice the 「は」 there. It frames alcohol as the topic, which softens the sentence. Japanese people also refuse gently by trailing off, saying 「お酒はちょっと|おさけはちょっと」, alcohol is a bit, and letting the sentence fade. That trailing off is understood as a polite no. A work party or any drinking get-together is called a 「飲み会|のみかい」, drinking meeting. Not drinking is perfectly fine there. Just order a soft drink and join the toast." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Drinking in Japan comes with gentle manners. At a group meal, you usually do not pour your own drink. Instead, you pour for the people around you and they pour for you. Younger people often keep an eye on the glasses of their seniors and top them up before they run empty. When someone pours for you, it is polite to lift your glass slightly with both hands. Rice wine breweries have their own traditions too. Many hang a large ball of cedar leaves, a 「杉玉|すぎだま」, over the entrance. When it is freshly hung and green, it signals that new sake has been made, and as it slowly turns brown, the sake matures. Brewing traditionally happens in the cold months. Rice wine is also offered at shrines, and you may see stacks of decorated barrels on display there as offerings. So this word reaches from the pub to the shrine. Knowing the pouring custom alone will win you friends." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is water drops beside a liquor jar, read 「さけ」, and 「お酒|おさけ」 means any alcohol at all, while the rice wine itself is 「日本酒|にほんしゅ」.",
      "The jar piece marks fermented things, like 「酢|す」, vinegar, and 「醤油|しょうゆ」, while the alcohol kanji reads 「さか」 up front, as in 「居酒屋|いざかや」, a pub.",
      "People are described as strong or weak with alcohol, 「お酒が強い|おさけがつよい」 or 「お酒が弱い|おさけがよわい」, and trailing off with 「お酒はちょっと|おさけはちょっと」 is a polite no.",
      "At group meals you pour for others rather than yourself, and breweries hang a cedar 「杉玉|すぎだま」 that is green with new sake and browns as it matures."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは[お酒|おさけ]を{飲みません|のみません}。",
    en: "I don't drink alcohol." },
  "コーヒー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word did not come from English. It came from Dutch, where the word is spelled koffie. In the Edo period, when Japan closed itself off to most of the world, Dutch traders were among the very few Europeans allowed to trade there, at a small island in Nagasaki. Coffee arrived with them, along with many other Dutch words. Look at the sounds. The first o is stretched into a long vowel with the bar 「ー」. The f sound did not exist in older Japanese, so the f plus i became 「ヒ」. Then the end is stretched long too, giving 「ヒー」. That is why it sounds like a stretched out version of koffie. There are even old kanji used just for their sounds to write it, but katakana is what you will see on menus. To drink coffee, use 「飲む|のむ」. You can find it in cans from vending machines everywhere, hot in winter and cold in summer. Recap: from Dutch koffie, with long vowels and f turned into an h sound." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Ordering coffee in Japan uses a few loanword shortcuts. Hot coffee is 「ホットコーヒー」, and iced coffee is 「アイスコーヒー」. In cafes people often just say 「ホット」 or 「アイス」 on its own, and the staff understand. Black coffee is 「ブラック」, so you might hear someone say they drink it black. Milk is 「ミルク」, and sugar is 「さとう」. Iced coffee is very popular, and Japanese coffee shops have served it for a very long time. A nice habit to notice is the word 「一杯|いっぱい」, one cup. People invite each other with 「コーヒーを一杯どうですか|コーヒーをいっぱいどうですか」, how about a cup of coffee? It is a gentle, friendly invitation. Remember the verb is 「飲む|のむ」, to drink. And morning time words go at the front, like 「朝、コーヒーを飲みます|あさ、コーヒーをのみます」. With these, you can order exactly what you like." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "When coffee first arrived, scholars who studied Dutch books needed a way to write it in kanji. Several spellings were tried, and the one that stuck is 「珈琲|コーヒー」. A scholar of Dutch learning in the early eighteen hundreds is usually credited with choosing it, though details vary by source. The kanji were picked for their sounds, not their meaning, much like spelling a name. Both kanji have the jewel piece on the left. A popular story says they were chosen because coffee berries on the branch looked like the red beads on a traditional hairpin. It is a lovely story, but treat it as a story. Today you will still see that kanji spelling on the signs of old fashioned coffee shops, where it gives a classic, retro feeling. In everyday writing, katakana is standard. So the word carries a little piece of history, from Dutch traders, to scholars with brushes, to vending machines on every corner." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word has four beats, and two of them are long vowels. Say 「コ」 and hold it for an extra beat, then 「ヒ」 and hold it again. Skipping the long vowels is a common learner habit, and it makes the word hard to catch. In Japanese, vowel length can change meaning entirely. The famous example is 「おばさん」, aunt, versus 「おばあさん」, grandmother. Make the middle long, and you add a generation. Watch out for a close sound alike too. 「コピー」, with a p sound and a long vowel only at the end, means a photocopy. So if you ask an office worker for one when you mean the other, you might get paper instead of a drink. Another nearby word is 「紅茶|こうちゃ」, black tea, which also begins with a long o. To practice, clap four times while saying the word. Each long vowel gets its own clap. Hold those long sounds and people will understand you at the first try." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It came from Dutch koffie via Dutch traders in the Edo period, with long vowels added and the f sound turned into 「ヒ」.",
      "Cafes understand just 「ホット」 or 「アイス」, and a friendly invitation is 「コーヒーを一杯どうですか|コーヒーをいっぱいどうですか」.",
      "Scholars of Dutch picked the kanji 「珈琲|コーヒー」 purely for sound, and today it gives old-fashioned coffee shop signs a retro feel.",
      "It has four beats with two held long vowels, and skipping them blurs it, or worse, turns it toward 「コピー」, a photocopy."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[コーヒー]を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink coffee in the morning." },
  "みず|水": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is one of the oldest pictures in the writing system. It shows flowing water: a central stream down the middle, with splashes or ripples on both sides. Here it uses its native Japanese reading, 「みず」. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「すい」, which you know from 「水曜日|すいようび」, Wednesday, literally water day. The weekdays use the old elements, so Monday is moon day, Tuesday fire day, and Wednesday water day. When water squeezes onto the left side of another kanji, it becomes three little drops. You can spot them in 「お酒|おさけ」, alcohol, and in the 「漢|かん」 of 「漢字|かんじ」, kanji. Whenever you see those three drops, think liquid. One more note: 「水|みず」 means cold or room temperature water. Hot water is a different word, 「おゆ」, so you would not ask for 「水|みず」 to make tea. Recap: a picture of a stream, read 「みず」 alone and 「すい」 in Wednesday, and it becomes three drops on other kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In most Japanese restaurants, a glass of cold water is brought to you as soon as you sit down, and it is free. Refills are free too. Restaurants often call it 「お冷|おひや」, the polite cold one, and you may hear staff use that word. To ask for more, you can say 「お水ください|おみずください」. The polite 「お」 is common when ordering. The verb is 「飲む|のむ」, to drink, just like for coffee or tea. Tap water across Japan is safe to drink, and the formal word for it is 「水道水|すいどうすい」, water from the water supply. Many people drink bottled water anyway, often labeled with the loanword 「ミネラルウォーター」. Here is a tiny dialogue in a restaurant. You raise your hand and say 「すみません、お水ください|すみません、おみずください」. The server answers 「はい」 and brings a refill. That one sentence will serve you every single day." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Many words you will meet use the Chinese-origin reading 「すい」. 「水道|すいどう」 is the water supply, literally the water road. 「水泳|すいえい」 is swimming, the water plus a kanji for swimming. 「香水|こうすい」 is perfume, fragrant water. And 「水族館|すいぞくかん」 is an aquarium. It ends with the same big building kanji as 「図書館|としょかん」, so an aquarium is a building for the water tribes. The native reading 「みず」 also forms words. 「水着|みずぎ」 is a swimsuit, water clothes, using the kanji from wearing clothes. Notice the second sound softens there. In general, when the kanji is part of a compound made of Chinese-origin readings, expect 「すい」. When it joins native Japanese words, expect 「みず」. That rule has exceptions, but it is a helpful guide. So one simple picture of a stream opens the door to swimming, perfume and fish tanks." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Imagine you are at the zoo on a blazing summer day, desperately thirsty. Every fountain is broken. You stagger up to the elephant enclosure and croak, me, zoo, water please. The elephant looks at you, fills its trunk from its pool, and sprays you with a huge, cold blast of water. Me, zoo. Now you are soaked, but at least you are not thirsty. To tie in the kanji, picture the elephant's spray. A strong stream shoots down the middle, and splashes fly out to both sides, exactly like the shape of the character. Every time you see that kanji, see the spray, feel the cold, and hear yourself shouting at the zoo. The funnier the picture, the faster it comes back. Me, zoo, splash." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「水|みず」 pictures a flowing stream with splashes, read 「すい」 in 「水曜日|すいようび」, and it means cold water, since hot water is 「おゆ」.",
      "Restaurants bring free cold water, often called 「お冷|おひや」, and you ask for a refill with 「お水ください|おみずください」.",
      "Chinese-origin compounds use 「すい」, as in 「水泳|すいえい」 and 「水族館|すいぞくかん」, while native words use 「みず」, as in 「水着|みずぎ」.",
      "Croak me, zoo at the elephant and get sprayed, with the stream down the middle and splashes to both sides matching the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[水|みず]を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink water every day." },
  "あさごはん|朝ご飯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is two words put together. 「朝|あさ」 means morning, read with its native Japanese reading. 「ご飯|ごはん」 means cooked rice, and by extension a meal, since rice was the center of a traditional Japanese meal. The 「ご」 is a polite prefix, and 「飯|はん」 is the Chinese-origin reading of the rice kanji. Look at the left side of 「飯|はん」 and you will find the food piece from 「食べる|たべる」. So breakfast is literally the morning rice. This word is one of a set of three. Swap the time word and you get 「昼ご飯|ひるごはん」, lunch, and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」, dinner. Learn the pattern once and you have all three meals. The morning kanji also appears in 「今朝|けさ」, this morning, which has a special reading, and in 「朝寝坊する|あさねぼうする」, to oversleep. A traditional Japanese breakfast really does include rice, along with miso soup and fish, though toast is common now too. Recap: morning plus rice, the first of the three meal words." },
      { title: "Formal and casual", text: "Japanese has three common ways to say breakfast, and each fits a different setting. This word is the standard, polite everyday one. Use it with anyone. A rougher, casual version is 「朝飯|あさめし」, using 「めし」, the native reading of the rice kanji. It sounds blunt, and you mostly hear it from men among close friends. The formal, written version is 「朝食|ちょうしょく」, with Chinese-origin readings for both kanji. The second kanji there is the eating kanji from 「食べる|たべる」. You will see this formal word at hotels, where a room plan with breakfast included is labeled 「朝食付き|ちょうしょくつき」. It also appears on signs announcing breakfast times. The same three levels exist for lunch and dinner too. For example, the formal word for lunch is 「昼食|ちゅうしょく」. So listen for the level. Polite everyday, rough and casual, or formal and written. Using the right one for the setting makes your Japanese sound natural." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at the rice kanji, 「飯|はん」. The left side is the food piece, but focus on the right side. It is 「反|はん」, a kanji that means against or opposite. Here it mostly gives the sound, not the meaning. This is how many kanji work. One part hints at meaning, and the other part hints at pronunciation. You can test it. 「販|はん」, to sell, as in a vending machine, 「自動販売機|じどうはんばいき」, has the same right side and the same sound. 「版|はん」, a printing edition, has it too. And 「坂|さか」, a slope, has it too. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「はん」, though in everyday words you mostly hear the native 「さか」. So when you see that right side, guess 「はん」, and you will often be right. The rice kanji itself has a native reading 「めし」, which you hear in casual speech. Spotting the sound part is one of the best tricks for reading new kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A Japanese breakfast has some famous favorites beyond the rice itself. One is 「納豆|なっとう」, fermented soybeans that are sticky and stringy, stirred with soy sauce and mustard and poured over hot rice. People either love it or really do not, and Japanese friends will enjoy asking whether you can eat it. Another is 「卵かけご飯|たまごかけごはん」, a raw egg cracked over hot rice with a splash of soy sauce, quick and comforting. It works because eggs in Japan are produced with strict safety standards for eating raw. Before eating, people put their hands together and say 「いただきます」, and afterward 「ごちそうさまでした」. These phrases thank the food and the people who prepared it. Busy families often grab toast, yogurt, or a rice ball from a convenience store instead. So breakfast can be very traditional or very quick. Either way, it starts with the same thankful phrase." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「朝|あさ」, morning, plus 「ご飯|ごはん」, cooked rice and a meal, so breakfast is morning rice, and swapping the time word gives lunch and dinner.",
      "This is the standard polite word, while 「朝飯|あさめし」 is rough and casual and 「朝食|ちょうしょく」 is the formal word seen in hotels.",
      "The right side of 「飯|はん」 is 「反|はん」, which gives the sound, so the same piece in 「販|はん」 and 「版|はん」 also suggests 「はん」.",
      "Breakfast favorites include sticky 「納豆|なっとう」 and 「卵かけご飯|たまごかけごはん」, raw egg over hot rice, starting with 「いただきます」."
    ],
    ja: "{七時|しちじ}に[朝ご飯|あさごはん]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat breakfast at seven." },
  "ひるごはん|昼ご飯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「昼|ひる」, noon or daytime, plus 「ご飯|ごはん」, a meal. Both pieces use their usual readings: 「ひる」 is the native Japanese reading of the noon kanji, and 「ご飯|ごはん」 is rice with a polite 「ご」. Look at the bottom of 「昼|ひる」 and you can find the sun, 「日|ひ」, which fits a word for the bright middle of the day. So lunch is literally the noon rice. It belongs to the set of three meal words. 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」 is breakfast and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」 is dinner. Only the time word changes. In casual speech people also say 「お昼|おひる」 to mean lunch, as in asking someone if they have eaten lunch yet. A cultural note: many Japanese workers and students bring a boxed lunch called 「おべんとう」, packed with rice and small side dishes. Convenience stores sell them too. Recap: noon plus rice, the middle of the three meal words, often shortened to 「お昼|おひる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The noon kanji appears in a handful of everyday words, with two main readings. The native reading 「ひる」 is the one you hear most. 「昼休み|ひるやすみ」 is the lunch break, noon plus rest, the precious hour at school or work. 「昼寝|ひるね」 is a nap, noon plus sleep, using the same kanji as 「寝る|ねる」. 「昼間|ひるま」 means the daytime, the stretch of hours while it is light. The Chinese-origin reading is 「ちゅう」, and you meet it in formal words. 「昼食|ちゅうしょく」 is the formal word for lunch, the kind you see on a schedule or a hotel sign. Notice how the formal word swaps both readings to Chinese-origin ones. That is a common pattern. Everyday words often use native readings, while formal written words use Chinese ones. So when you see this kanji at the start of a casual word, try 「ひる」 first. In a stiff, formal compound, try 「ちゅう」. The noon kanji is small, but it fills your whole midday." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Lunch is one of the easiest ways to make friends, so these phrases are worth knowing. To invite someone, use the 「ませんか」 pattern: 「一緒に昼ご飯を食べませんか|いっしょにひるごはんをたべませんか」, won't you eat lunch with me? It is polite and gentle, because it asks rather than tells. To ask if someone has eaten, use 「もう」, already: 「もう昼ご飯を食べましたか|もうひるごはんをたべましたか」. Among friends this shrinks to 「お昼、食べた|おひる、たべた」, with no particles at all. On campus, many students eat in the cafeteria, the 「食堂|しょくどう」, which literally means eating hall. For where you eat, use 「で」, as in 「学校で昼ご飯を食べます|がっこうでひるごはんをたべます」. Here is a quick dialogue. One student says 「お昼、行きませんか|おひる、いきませんか」, shall we go to lunch? The other answers 「いいですね」, sounds good. With just these lines, lunchtime becomes a chance to practice every day." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese elementary and middle schools, lunch is a lesson in itself. Most schools serve a shared school lunch called 「給食|きゅうしょく」. There is no cafeteria line. Instead, students on lunch duty put on white coats and caps, carry the food from the kitchen, and serve their classmates right in the classroom. Everyone eats the same balanced meal together at their desks, often with the teacher, and then the students clean up. The idea is to teach nutrition, teamwork and gratitude. Many adults remember favorite lunch dishes for the rest of their lives. For office workers, the noon hour means a rush. Restaurants near business districts fill up fast, and many offer a cheap, quick lunch set. People who are short on time might grab standing noodles at a counter near the station, or a meal from a convenience store. So lunch in Japan ranges from a classroom ritual to a five minute bowl of noodles." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「昼|ひる」, noon, with the sun at its bottom, plus 「ご飯|ごはん」, so lunch is noon rice, often shortened to 「お昼|おひる」.",
      "The noon kanji reads 「ひる」 in everyday words like 「昼休み|ひるやすみ」 and 「昼寝|ひるね」, but 「ちゅう」 in formal 「昼食|ちゅうしょく」.",
      "Invite with 「一緒に昼ご飯を食べませんか|いっしょにひるごはんをたべませんか」, or ask whether someone has eaten with 「もう」.",
      "School lunch, 「給食|きゅうしょく」, is served by students in white coats and eaten together in the classroom, teaching nutrition, teamwork and gratitude."
    ],
    ja: "{学校|がっこう}で[昼ご飯|ひるごはん]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat lunch at school." },
  "ばんごはん|晩ご飯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「晩|ばん」, evening, plus 「ご飯|ごはん」, a meal. The 「晩|ばん」 uses its Chinese-origin reading, and it has the sun piece on its left side, as many time words do. So dinner is literally the evening rice. It completes the set of three meal words with 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast, and 「昼ご飯|ひるごはん」, lunch. The 「晩|ばん」 is worth knowing well, because it is in other words you have. 「今晩|こんばん」 means this evening, tonight, and 「毎晩|まいばん」 means every evening. It is even hiding in a greeting you learned on day one. 「こんばんは」, good evening, is really 「今晩|こんばん」 plus the topic particle 「は」. That is why that greeting is written with 「は」 at the end, even though it is pronounced like 「わ」. You may also hear another word for dinner, 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」, using the kanji for early evening. Both are fine. Recap: evening plus rice, and the same 「晩|ばん」 as in good evening." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese splits the end of the day into several words, and it helps to know where each one sits. 「夕方|ゆうがた」 is late afternoon into dusk, when the sky starts to turn. 「晩|ばん」 is the evening, the time for dinner and relaxing. 「夜|よる」 is night, and it can stretch into the late hours. The two meal words for dinner follow this split. 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」 and 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」 mean the same thing, though some speakers feel the second sounds a touch softer. The formal written word, though, is 「夕食|ゆうしょく」, built from the dusk kanji, not the evening one. You will see it on hotel signs. The evening kanji also has a sense of late. 「一晩|ひとばん」 means one night, like staying over for one night. 「晩年|ばんねん」 means the last years of someone's life, the evening of a life. So the evening kanji carries the feeling of the day winding down. Learn the order, dusk, evening, night, and your time words fall into place." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Dinner is the meal most families still try to share, so these phrases are very homey. A parent calls the family to the table with 「晩ご飯ですよ|ばんごはんですよ」, dinner's ready. Kids often ask 「晩ご飯、何|ばんごはん、なに」, what's for dinner? That casual question drops every particle. To say you eat at home, use 「で」 for the place: 「うちで晩ご飯を食べます|うちでばんごはんをたべます」. Eating out has its own word, 「外食|がいしょく」, literally outside eating. So you might say a family eats out on weekends. To invite a friend, use the 「ませんか」 pattern again: 「一緒に晩ご飯を食べませんか|いっしょにばんごはんをたべませんか」. Here is a mini dialogue between roommates. One asks 「今日の晩ご飯、どうしますか|きょうのばんごはん、どうしますか」, what should we do for dinner today? The other says 「カレーはどうですか」, how about curry? These little exchanges happen in every Japanese home each night." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「晩|ばん」, evening, plus 「ご飯|ごはん」, so dinner is evening rice, and the same 「晩|ばん」 hides in 「こんばんは」, which is 「今晩|こんばん」 plus 「は」.",
      "The day winds down from 「夕方|ゆうがた」 to 「晩|ばん」 to 「夜|よる」, and while 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」 also means dinner, the formal word is 「夕食|ゆうしょく」.",
      "Families call 「晩ご飯ですよ|ばんごはんですよ」, you eat at home with 「で」, and eating out is 「外食|がいしょく」."
    ],
    ja: "{家|うち}で[晩ご飯|ばんごはん]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat dinner at home." },
  "いえ|家": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji. The top is a roof, the same roof piece that sits on 「寝る|ねる」, to sleep. Underneath is an old character for a pig. So the parts really are a roof and a pig. Scholars disagree about exactly why, so take this as a hook only: picture an old farmhouse where the family and the livestock live under the same roof. In this word the kanji uses its native Japanese reading, 「いえ」. In compound words it often uses the Chinese-origin reading 「か」. You can hear that in 「家族|かぞく」, family, the people of one house, and in 「家具|かぐ」, furniture, the tools of a house. It also attaches to the end of words for professions, like 「作家|さっか」, a writer, and 「漫画家|まんがか」, a cartoonist, meaning a person who has made that craft their trade. And in 「大家さん|おおやさん」, the landlord, it is read 「や」. Compared with 「うち」, 「家|いえ」 leans toward the physical building. Recap: a roof with a pig under it, read 「いえ」, and 「か」 in family and furniture." },
      { title: "More readings", text: "This kanji is famous for having many readings, and each one belongs to a different kind of word. You already have 「いえ」 and 「か」. Another is 「け」, used after a family name to mean that household. So 「田中家|たなかけ」 means the Tanaka family, the way a wedding sign or a funeral notice might say it. You will often see this on formal signs. The reading 「や」 shows up in money matters. 「家賃|やちん」 is rent, the fee for a house. And this same kanji can even be read 「うち」, the warm word for home, though writers often use kana for that word. That is a lot of readings for one shape, but there is a rough rule. When the kanji stands alone, it is usually 「いえ」. When it joins other Chinese-origin kanji, it is usually 「か」. After a surname, it is 「け」. Treat each word as its own little package, and the readings will sort themselves out over time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Because this word points to the building, it shines in sentences about houses as property. To buy a house is 「家を買う|いえをかう」. To build one is 「家を建てる|いえをたてる」. A big house is 「大きい家|おおきいいえ」, and here you would not usually use the warm word for home. To ask where someone lives, you can say 「家はどこですか|いえはどこですか」, where is your house? For studying at home, both words work: 「家で勉強します|いえでべんきょうします」. Now a politeness point. When you talk about someone else's home respectfully, use 「お宅|おたく」, a polite word for your house or your household. Using the plain word for a teacher's home can sound a little blunt. A small dialogue. One person asks 「家はどこですか|いえはどこですか」. The other answers 「駅の近くです|えきのちかくです」, it's near the station. So think building and property for this word, and you will choose it at the right moments." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Entering a Japanese house follows a clear ritual. Just inside the front door is a small area called the 「玄関|げんかん」, the entryway. It sits a step lower than the rest of the floor. Here you take off your shoes and step up into the house, often into slippers waiting for guests. Shoes are turned to face the door, ready for leaving. Wearing outdoor shoes inside would be a real shock. Rooms with straw mats, 「たたみ」, are entered in socks or bare feet, not even slippers. As you step inside someone else's home, the polite phrase is 「お邪魔します|おじゃまします」, literally I am going to disturb you, a humble way of saying thank you for having me. As you leave, you say 「お邪魔しました|おじゃましました」, sorry for disturbing you. Guests often bring a small gift too. Learn the step, the shoes and the phrase, and you will be a welcome visitor." }
    ],
    short: [
      "As a hook, the kanji is a roof with a pig under it, read 「いえ」 alone and 「か」 in 「家族|かぞく」, and it leans toward the physical building more than 「うち」.",
      "Its readings sort by word type: 「いえ」 alone, 「か」 in compounds, 「け」 after a surname as in 「田中家|たなかけ」, and 「や」 in 「家賃|やちん」, rent.",
      "It fits houses as property, as in 「家を買う|いえをかう」, and for someone else's home respectfully you use 「お宅|おたく」.",
      "Take off your shoes in the 「玄関|げんかん」, step up into the house, and say 「お邪魔します|おじゃまします」 as you enter."
    ],
    ja: "[家|いえ]で{勉強します|べんきょうします}。",
    en: "I study at home." },
  "うち|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, and it can also be written with the same kanji as 「家|いえ」. The key difference is the feeling. 「いえ」 leans toward the building itself, the structure. 「うち」 leans toward home, your own household, the place you belong. That makes sense because 「うち」 also means inside, as opposed to outside. Your home is the inside of your world. So when you go home, the natural phrase is 「うちに帰る|うちにかえる」. Saying it with 「いえ」 is not wrong, but it sounds more like returning to a building. 「うち」 can also mean my family or our group, as in our company or our school, the inside group you belong to. This inside versus outside idea runs deep in Japanese culture and language, and you will see it again in politeness levels later. A way to remember it, as a hook: home is where you go in from the outside world. Recap: home in the warm sense, the inside of your life, and the natural word for going home." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The warm word for home comes with two of the most heartfelt phrases in Japanese. When you walk in the door, you say 「ただいま」, I'm home. Whoever is there answers 「おかえりなさい」, welcome back, or casually 「おかえり」. Families say this every single day, and even people living alone sometimes say it to an empty room. To invite a friend over, use 「うちに来ませんか|うちにきませんか」, won't you come to my place? Notice that 「うち」 here quietly means my home, with no word for my needed. For going home, use 「に」 or 「へ」 with 「帰る|かえる」. For things done at home, use 「で」, as in 「うちで晩ご飯を食べます|うちでばんごはんをたべます」. Here is a tiny scene. A child opens the door and calls out 「ただいま」. A parent in the kitchen calls back 「おかえり」. It is a simple exchange, but it carries a lot of warmth. Learn both phrases with this word." },
      { title: "Inside and outside", text: "The inside meaning of this word shapes how Japanese people talk. Your inside group might be your family, your company or your school. Everyone else is outside. When you talk to outsiders, you lower your own group slightly to be humble. That is why someone says 「うちの母|うちのはは」, my mother, using the humble word for mother, but calls a friend's mother 「お母さん|おかあさん」, with respect. The same rule reaches the office. When a receptionist speaks to a client, they refer to their own boss without the polite title 「さん」, because the boss is inside and the client is outside. That surprises many learners. People also say 「うちの子|うちのこ」 for my child, and some call their pets that too. In the Kansai region, some speakers, especially women, even use 「うち」 to mean I. So this little word is a doorway into a whole way of thinking. Inside means family, and family is talked about humbly." },
      { title: "Other meanings", text: "The same sound has a few more jobs, all connected to the idea of inside. When it means inside rather than home, it is often written with the kanji 「内|うち」. You will see that kanji read 「ない」 in compounds like 「案内|あんない」, guidance, which is leading someone inside a place. Another use is about a period of time. 「今日のうちに|きょうのうちに」 means within today, before the day is over, as if the day were a box and you stay inside it. Later in your studies you will meet the pattern of doing something while a situation lasts, also built on this word. For example, doing something while you are young. And in counting, 「三つのうち|みっつのうち」 means out of the three, among them. So when you hear this word without anything about houses, ask yourself what it is inside of. A home, a group, a stretch of time, or a set. That one question unlocks every meaning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means home in the warm sense, the inside of your world, while 「いえ」 leans toward the building, so going home is naturally 「うちに帰る|うちにかえる」.",
      "Walking in you say 「ただいま」 and family answer 「おかえりなさい」, and 「うちに来ませんか|うちにきませんか」 invites a friend to my place.",
      "It marks your inside group, so you talk humbly about it, as in 「うちの母|うちのはは」, while calling a friend's mother 「お母さん|おかあさん」.",
      "Every meaning is about inside something, written 「内|うち」, as in 「今日のうちに|きょうのうちに」, within today, or 「三つのうち|みっつのうち」, out of three."
    ],
    ja: "{六時|ろくじ}に[うち]に{帰ります|かえります}。",
    en: "I go home at six." },
  "カフェ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from French café, which reached Japanese as the name for a modern coffee shop. Look at the sounds. Older Japanese had no fe sound. To write one, Japanese takes 「フ」, a soft f-like sound, and adds a small 「ェ」 after it. The small vowel tells you to blend them into a single beat. This trick of adding a small vowel is how Japanese spells many new foreign sounds. You see it in 「ファイル」, file, with a small 「ァ」, and in 「サーフィン」, surfing, with a small 「ィ」. Note there is no accent mark and no long vowel at the end, so it stays short and crisp. Japan also has an older word for a coffee shop, 「喫茶店|きっさてん」. That word suggests a classic, slightly old fashioned place with a counter and hand-poured coffee. 「カフェ」 sounds more modern and trendy, like a chain or a stylish spot. Recap: French café, with a small 「ェ」 to build the fe sound, and the modern cousin of 「喫茶店|きっさてん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word also lives inside many drink names, because in French and Italian it simply means coffee. 「カフェオレ」 comes from French café au lait, coffee with milk. 「カフェラテ」 comes from Italian, milk coffee made with espresso. 「カフェモカ」 adds chocolate. Even 「カフェイン」, caffeine, shares the same root, since it is the substance in coffee. And the trendy 「デカフェ」 means decaf, coffee with the caffeine removed. So the word has two lives in Japanese. On its own, it means the place, a coffee shop. At the front of a drink name, it means the coffee in your cup. The difference between the two milk drinks is a common question. Many Japanese cafes use 「カフェオレ」 for drip coffee with milk, and 「カフェラテ」 for espresso with milk, though not every shop is strict. Next time you look at a menu, spot this word at the front of each drink, and you will know coffee is inside." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has turned the cafe into an art form, with themed versions you will not find everywhere. The most famous is the 「猫カフェ|ねこカフェ」, the cat cafe, where you pay by the hour to sip a drink while cats wander around. They became popular partly because many apartments do not allow pets. There are also cafes with owls, rabbits and even hedgehogs. Another kind is the 「メイドカフェ」, the maid cafe, especially in Tokyo's electronics district, where staff dress in costumes and greet customers with playful lines. A third is the internet and manga cafe, often shortened to 「ネカフェ」. There, you rent a small booth with a computer, shelves of comics, free drinks, and sometimes a shower, and some people even stay overnight. Students also use ordinary cafes as study rooms, sitting for hours over one drink. So when a friend suggests a cafe, it might be worth asking which kind." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From French café, it builds the fe sound with 「フ」 plus a small 「ェ」, and it sounds more modern than the older 「喫茶店|きっさてん」.",
      "At the front of drink names it means coffee, as in 「カフェオレ」, 「カフェラテ」 and 「カフェモカ」, while on its own it means the shop.",
      "Japan has themed versions like the 「猫カフェ|ねこカフェ」, the 「メイドカフェ」, and the 「ネカフェ」, where people rent booths and sometimes stay overnight."
    ],
    ja: "[カフェ]でコーヒーを{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink coffee at a cafe." },
  "あした|明日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the first kanji, 「明|めい」. It is made of the sun on the left and the moon on the right, and it means bright. The second is 「日|ひ」, day. So the kanji say the next bright day. Here is the important part. The reading 「あした」 does not come from the separate readings of these two kanji. It is a native Japanese word for tomorrow, and the kanji were matched to it by meaning. That is called a special reading, and you learn the whole word as one piece. Several time words work this way, including 「今日|きょう」, today, and 「昨日|きのう」, yesterday. You can see the normal reading of 「明|めい」 in 「説明する|せつめいする」, to explain, which is making something bright and clear. In formal speech, tomorrow can also be 「あす」 or 「みょうにち」, but 「あした」 is the everyday word. Time words like this usually take no particle 「に」. Recap: sun plus moon is bright, the next bright day, read 「あした」 as one whole word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word sits in a neat row of days, and most of them have special readings too. Two days ago is 「おととい」. Yesterday is 「昨日|きのう」. Today is 「今日|きょう」. Tomorrow is this word. The day after tomorrow is 「あさって」, which can be written 「明後日|あさって」, using the same bright kanji plus the kanji for after. Learn them as a line, from left to right, and practice saying them in order like a little song. Now the formal side. In news and weather reports you will hear 「あす」, as in tomorrow's weather. In business, people say 「みょうにち」 when speaking very politely, for example when confirming a visit with a client. Yesterday has a formal twin too, 「さくじつ」. For everyday life, though, the first set is all you need. Remember that none of these take the particle 「に」. Just place the word at the start and keep going. One row of five words covers almost every plan you make." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The bright kanji, sun beside moon, has several readings that are worth recognizing. Its native reading 「あか」 appears in 「明るい|あかるい」, bright or cheerful. That word describes both a sunny room and a sunny personality. The verb 「明ける|あける」 means for the night or a period to end and the light to come. You know it from the New Year greeting 「明けましておめでとうございます|あけましておめでとうございます」, congratulations on the year opening. The Chinese-origin reading 「めい」 is common in formal words. 「明治|めいじ」 is the name of the era when Japan modernized, bright rule. 「文明|ぶんめい」 is civilization. There is also a rarer reading 「みょう」, which is exactly the one in 「みょうにち」, the formal word for tomorrow. So this one kanji connects brightness, a cheerful person, the start of a new year, and tomorrow. All of them are about light coming." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "In classical Japanese, this word did not mean tomorrow. It meant morning, especially the morning that follows a night. You find it in old poems, where lovers part at dawn. Its partner in those poems was 「ゆうべ」, the evening. Over the centuries, the idea of the coming morning slid into the idea of the coming day, and the word settled on tomorrow. Meanwhile, the old word for evening shifted too. Today 「ゆうべ」 means last night, and it can be written 「夕べ|ゆうべ」 or 「昨夜|ゆうべ」. So the pair that once meant morning and evening now point to the next day and the night before. The shorter form 「あす」 is related, and is the older word for the next day. That is why it sounds more formal and literary today. When you say this word, you are carrying a little piece of dawn from a thousand years ago. Tomorrow began life as a sunrise." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「明|めい」, sun plus moon, means bright, so 「明日|あした」 is the next bright day, but 「あした」 is a special reading learned as one whole word.",
      "It sits in a row with 「おととい」, 「昨日|きのう」, 「今日|きょう」 and 「あさって」, while 「あす」 and 「みょうにち」 are the formal versions, and none take 「に」.",
      "The bright kanji also gives 「明るい|あかるい」, cheerful, 「明ける|あける」 from the New Year greeting, and 「みょう」 in 「みょうにち」, all about light coming.",
      "In classical Japanese it meant the morning after a night, paired with 「ゆうべ」, evening, and the coming morning slowly became the coming day."
    ],
    ja: "[明日|あした]テニスをします。",
    en: "I will play tennis tomorrow." },
  "きょう|今日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first kanji, 「今|いま」, means now. The second, 「日|ひ」, means day. So the kanji say this day, the now day. But the reading 「きょう」 does not come from adding up the kanji readings. It is a special reading that belongs to the whole word, just like 「明日|あした」 and 「昨日|きのう」. Learn it as one piece. The first kanji is very useful elsewhere. On its own it is 「今|いま」, now. In compound words it is often read 「こん」. You know it from 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, 「今月|こんげつ」, this month, and 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester. In each one it means this current one. And here is a surprise: the greeting 「こんにちは」, hello, is really these same two kanji read 「こんにち」, plus the particle 「は」. So hello is literally as for today. Another special reading uses it too: 「今朝|けさ」, this morning. Recap: now plus day, read 「きょう」 as one word, and the same 「今|こん」 that means this." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a neat set of words for the days around now, and most of them have special readings that you learn as whole words. Yesterday is 「昨日|きのう」. Tomorrow is 「明日|あした」. Push one step further and you get 「おととい」, the day before yesterday, and 「あさって」, the day after tomorrow. Every one of these is a time word that usually goes without the particle 「に」, so you simply say today, then the rest of the sentence. Each of the kanji pairs also has a more formal, Chinese-style reading. In a shop sign or a station announcement you will see 「本日|ほんじつ」 for today, and you may hear 「昨日|さくじつ」 for yesterday and 「明日|みょうにち」 for tomorrow in very polite speech. So the everyday word is the soft native one, and the stiff one is for business. Learn the five-day ladder from the day before yesterday to the day after tomorrow, and you can place almost any plan in time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is constantly used as the topic of a sentence with 「は」. 「今日は暑いですね|きょうはあついですね」 means it is hot today, isn't it, and it is a classic small-talk opener. Using 「は」 here sets today apart, often in contrast with other days. So 「今日は忙しいです|きょうはいそがしいです」 hints that today, at least, I am busy. Notice that you never say 「に」 after it when you mean on this day. Another handy phrase is 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」, by the end of today, which you hear at work when something has a deadline. In shops, a sign saying 「本日休業|ほんじつきゅうぎょう」 means closed today, using the formal word for today. And at the end of a class or meeting, people often say 「今日はありがとうございました|きょうはありがとうございました」, thank you for today. That one phrase covers thanks for your time, your effort and your company. Today is the easiest topic in any conversation." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The reading of this word has a real history. In older Japanese, before modern spelling rules, it was written with the kana 「けふ」. Over many centuries, Japanese pronunciation changed. The sound written with 「ふ」 in the middle of words softened and eventually disappeared, and the vowels that were left blended together into a long sound. Through those regular changes, the old spelling ended up pronounced 「きょう」. The same kind of drift happened to many words. For example, the old spelling 「てふ」 for butterfly became the modern 「ちょう」. This is why the reading does not match the kanji piece by piece. The kanji were attached to an old native word for today, and the sound of that word kept changing on its own. Modern spelling, reformed after the Second World War, simply writes it the way people say it. So today is a very old word wearing new spelling." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「今|いま」, now, plus 「日|ひ」, day, with the special reading 「きょう」, and the same kanji read 「こんにち」 hide in 「こんにちは」.",
      "It sits in a five-day ladder from 「おととい」 to 「あさって」, takes no 「に」, and has the formal twin 「本日|ほんじつ」 on shop signs.",
      "It often takes 「は」 as a topic, as in 「今日は暑いですね|きょうはあついですね」, and 「今日はありがとうございました|きょうはありがとうございました」 thanks people at the end of a meeting.",
      "It was once spelled 「けふ」, and regular sound changes turned it into 「きょう」, just as 「てふ」 became 「ちょう」, so the reading doesn't match the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "[今日|きょう]は{土曜日|どようび}です。",
    en: "Today is Saturday." },
  "あさ|朝": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "In the modern kanji, you can find the sun, 「日|ひ」, tucked in the left side, and a shape on the right that looks like the moon, 「月|つき」. Historians argue about where that right side really came from, so treat this as a hook only: picture the sun rising while the moon is still in the sky. That is early morning. Here the kanji uses its native Japanese reading, 「あさ」. You will see it again in 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast, the morning meal, and in 「朝寝坊する|あさねぼうする」, to oversleep, to sleep into the morning. Watch out for 「今朝|けさ」, this morning. It uses the same kanji, but the whole word has a special reading, so it is not read with 「あさ」 at all. Morning time words often appear at the very start of a sentence, as in getting up at seven in the morning. You may also hear 「毎朝|まいあさ」, every morning, on the same pattern as 「毎日|まいにち」. Recap: sun and moon together in the early sky, read 「あさ」, the morning in breakfast." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Morning sets up a whole family of time words and meals. The three meals in Japanese follow the time of day. 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」 is breakfast, 「昼ご飯|ひるごはん」 is lunch, and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」 is dinner. Literally, they are morning rice, noon rice and evening rice, because 「ご飯|ごはん」 means both cooked rice and a meal. You also have 「毎朝|まいあさ」, every morning, and 「早起き|はやおき」, getting up early. This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading too, 「ちょう」. You will see it in 「朝食|ちょうしょく」, a more formal word for breakfast that hotels love to use on their signs and booking pages. Another word worth knowing is 「朝日|あさひ」, the morning sun, which is also the name of a famous newspaper and a beer company. So at home you eat 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, but at a hotel the menu says 「朝食|ちょうしょく」. Same morning, different level of formality." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A common mix-up for English speakers is the greeting. Good morning is not built from this word at all. It is 「おはようございます」, which comes from a word meaning early. So you use the greeting to say hello, and you use this noun to talk about the time. Another trap is clock times. For seven in the morning, you can say 「朝七時|あさしちじ」, which sounds natural and casual. For a more precise or official time, Japanese uses 「午前|ごぜん」, the a.m. word, as in 「午前七時|ごぜんしちじ」. A traditional Japanese breakfast is rice, miso soup, grilled fish and pickles, though toast and coffee are very common now too. Many people also know the morning radio exercise routine, 「ラジオ体操|らじおたいそう」, which has been broadcast for generations and is done by children at summer camps and by older people in parks. Mornings in Japan start early and on schedule." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture yourself in a dark bedroom. Someone throws open the curtains, and the morning sun hits your face. You squint, stretch, and let out a big sigh: ah, sun. Ah, sa. Each morning that sound escapes you as the light floods in. Now attach the kanji. Remember the hook from before, the sun on the left and a moon shape on the right. In your scene, the moon is still hanging pale in the sky while the sun climbs up, and you are standing at the window saying ah, sun, ah, sa. Make it a bit silly: you are wearing pajamas with little suns on them, and your cat is annoyed by the light. The sillier the picture, the better it sticks. So every time you hear 「あさ」, see the curtains fly open and hear yourself sighing at the sun." }
    ],
    short: [
      "As a hook, the kanji shows the sun rising while the moon is still in the sky, read 「あさ」, though 「今朝|けさ」 has its own special reading.",
      "The three meals follow the time of day, 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, 「昼ご飯|ひるごはん」, 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」, while hotels use the formal 「朝食|ちょうしょく」 with the reading 「ちょう」.",
      "Good morning is 「おはようございます」, from a word meaning early, not this noun, and precise clock times use 「午前|ごぜん」 rather than 「朝|あさ」.",
      "Picture curtains flying open and the sun hitting your face as you sigh ah, sun, with the pale moon still in the sky."
    ],
    ja: "[朝|あさ]{七時|しちじ}に{起きます|おきます}。",
    en: "I get up at seven in the morning." },
  "こんばん|今晩": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「今|こん」, means now or this current one. The second, 「晩|ばん」, means evening. This evening is tonight. The pattern of 「今|こん」 meaning this one is very productive. 「今週|こんしゅう」 is this week, 「今月|こんげつ」 is this month, and 「今学期|こんがっき」 is this semester. The 「晩|ばん」 is shared with 「毎晩|まいばん」, every evening, and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」, dinner. Here is the fun part. The greeting 「こんばんは」, good evening, is exactly this word plus the topic particle 「は」. Literally it means as for this evening, and the rest of the polite sentence has dropped away over time. That is why the greeting ends with 「は」 even though it sounds like 「わ」. 「こんにちは」 works the same way with today. You may also hear 「今夜|こんや」, which means tonight too, using the kanji for night from 「夜|よる」. Recap: this plus evening, and the same word that hides in good evening." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at the second kanji, 「晩|ばん」. On its left side is the sun, 「日|ひ」. Many kanji about time and light carry this sun piece. You know it from 「時|じ」, time, 「明るい|あかるい」, bright, 「曜日|ようび」, day of the week, and 「昨日|きのう」, yesterday. In this kanji the sun signals a time of day, the part when the sun is going down. The right side mostly helps with the sound, so it is not worth hunting for a picture there. This kanji almost always has the reading 「ばん」, which makes it easy. The first kanji, 「今|こん」, has a roof-like top and a small mark underneath. In this word it uses its Chinese-origin reading. On its own it is 「今|いま」, now. So this word is a neat package: the now kanji and the sunset kanji, both with their Chinese-style readings. Remember the sun on the left, and you know the time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is perfect for making plans. Combine it with the invitation pattern from your lessons and you get 「今晩、一緒に晩ご飯を食べませんか|こんばん、いっしょにばんごはんをたべませんか」, would you like to have dinner together tonight. A friend might answer 「いいですね」, sounds good. Like other time words based on now, it usually does not take 「に」. You just put it at the start and carry on. There are other ways to say tonight. 「今夜|こんや」 uses the kanji for night and can stretch later into the night. In casual chat, many people simply say 「今日の夜|きょうのよる」, tonight, literally today's night. This word sits comfortably in polite everyday speech, so it is a safe choice with a teacher, a coworker or a new friend. You will also hear it in weather forecasts, talking about rain or cold tonight. Use it whenever tonight's plans come up." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick to connect the sound to tonight. Picture a concert hall this evening. The lights dim, the conductor walks out, and the very first thing that happens is a huge drum: con, BANG. The whole audience jumps. That is tonight's show, con bang. Picture the drummer as a bit too enthusiastic, hitting the drum so hard the music stand falls over, and the conductor glaring at him. Now add the kanji. The sun on the left of the second kanji is just slipping below the horizon outside the concert hall as the drum goes off. So the scene is a sunset, a concert, and con, BANG. Whenever you want to say tonight, remember that drum making everybody jump. And when someone greets you in the evening with the greeting that grows from this word, you can hear that same drum in the background. Tonight always starts with a bang." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「今|こん」, this, plus 「晩|ばん」, evening, so tonight, and the greeting 「こんばんは」 is exactly this word plus the topic particle 「は」.",
      "The evening kanji 「晩|ばん」 has the sun on its left, like many time kanji, signalling the time when the sun goes down, and it is almost always read 「ばん」.",
      "It suits polite plans like 「今晩、一緒に晩ご飯を食べませんか|こんばん、いっしょにばんごはんをたべませんか」, needs no 「に」, and has casual cousins like 「今日の夜|きょうのよる」.",
      "Picture tonight's concert opening with a huge drum, con, BANG, while the sun slips below the horizon outside."
    ],
    ja: "[今晩|こんばん]{映画|えいが}を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I will watch a movie tonight." },
  "まいにち|毎日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「毎|まい」, means every or each. The second, 「日|にち」, means day. Every day. The 「毎|まい」 is a little machine for making words. Put it in front of a time word and you get every one of them. 「毎晩|まいばん」 is every evening, 「毎週|まいしゅう」 is every week, and 「毎朝|まいあさ」 is every morning. The 「日|にち」 reading of the day kanji also shows up in 「日曜日|にちようび」, Sunday, and in 「一日|いちにち」, one day. A grammar tip that English speakers often miss: words with 「毎|まい」 do not take the particle 「に」. You just say every day and then the rest of the sentence. Adding 「に」 is a common beginner mistake. The same goes for 「今日|きょう」 and 「明日|あした」. Habits are a great use for this word, with the plain 「ます」 form, like studying Japanese every day. Recap: every plus day, and 「毎|まい」 makes every week and every night too, with no 「に」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「毎|まい」, is worth a close look because it hides inside other kanji you know. Add the water radical, the three drops on the left, and you get 「海|うみ」, the sea. Add the tree radical and you get 「梅|うめ」, the Japanese plum. In those, this piece mostly helps with the sound in the original Chinese, so do not look for a story about every in the sea. Spotting it helps you remember how to write them. The second kanji, 「日|ひ」, is the sun and day, and it has many readings. Here it is 「にち」. In 「本日|ほんじつ」, today in formal speech, it is 「じつ」. In 「日曜日|にちようび」 the last one is 「び」, a softened 「ひ」. And in dates like 「二日|ふつか」, the second of the month, it is read 「か」. So it is one of the most flexible kanji there is. Remember 「毎|まい」 inside the sea, and 「日|にち」 as the day." },
      { title: "Frequency words", text: "This word lives in a group of words that answer how often. Arranged from most to least often, you have 「いつも」, always, then this word, every day, then 「よく」, often, 「ときどき」, sometimes, and later 「あまり」 and 「ぜんぜん」, not much and not at all, which go with a negative verb. There is also 「たいてい」, usually, for what normally happens. Every day is a specific schedule, while always is a general habit. You could say you eat breakfast every day, but you always drink coffee. A nice thing about this group is that they all sit before the verb without extra particles. Another useful pattern is 「一日に|いちにちに」 plus a number of times, for how many times a day. So once a day is 「一日に一回|いちにちにいっかい」. With this family you can describe any routine in your life, from strict daily habits to rare treats." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word can also act like an adjective when you add 「の」. 「毎日の生活|まいにちのせいかつ」 means daily life, and 「毎日の練習|まいにちのれんしゅう」 means daily practice. So it can describe a noun, not just a verb. You will also see it as part of a name. 「毎日新聞|まいにちしんぶん」 is one of Japan's major national newspapers, literally the every day newspaper. A fun expression is 「毎日が日曜日|まいにちがにちようび」, every day is Sunday. People say it, half joking, about retired people who no longer have to work, or about someone on a long break. Also notice how the word is used to complain. 「毎日、雨ですね|まいにち、あめですね」 means it rains every single day, doesn't it, a typical sigh during the rainy season. With 「の」 it means daily, and on its own it means every day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「毎|まい」, every, plus 「日|にち」, day, and 「毎|まい」 makes 「毎晩|まいばん」 and 「毎週|まいしゅう」 too, all without the particle 「に」.",
      "The 「毎|まい」 piece hides in 「海|うみ」 and 「梅|うめ」 as a sound part, while 「日|ひ」 is very flexible, read 「にち」, 「じつ」, 「び」 or 「か」.",
      "It sits among frequency words from 「いつも」 to 「ときどき」, and every day is a specific schedule while always is a general habit.",
      "With 「の」 it becomes daily, as in 「毎日の生活|まいにちのせいかつ」, and the joke 「毎日が日曜日|まいにちがにちようび」 describes retired people."
    ],
    ja: "[毎日|まいにち]{日本語|にほんご}を{勉強します|べんきょうします}。",
    en: "I study Japanese every day." },
  "まいばん|毎晩": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「毎|まい」, means every or each. The second, 「晩|ばん」, means evening. Every evening, every night. It is built exactly like 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, and 「毎週|まいしゅう」, every week. Learn 「毎|まい」 once and you can make a whole set. The 「晩|ばん」 links this word to two others you know. 「今晩|こんばん」 is this evening, tonight, and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」 is the evening meal, dinner. So if you know the meal, you know the time. A grammar tip: like other 「毎|まい」 words, this one does not need the particle 「に」. Just say every night, then the rest of the sentence. Adding 「に」 is a very common beginner mistake. This word is perfect for describing routines with the 「ます」 form, like listening to music every night or watching TV every night. Recap: every plus evening, built like 「毎日|まいにち」, and no 「に」 needed." },
      { title: "Evening versus night", text: "Japanese splits the end of the day with two kanji, and this word uses the evening one. 「晩|ばん」 covers evening, from around sunset into the night. 「夜|よる」 means night more broadly, including the late, dark hours. In practice they overlap a lot, and every night as a routine almost always uses this word. There is a word built with the other kanji, but it sounds old-fashioned and literary, so stick with this one. The meal is the clearest example of the evening kanji. 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」 is dinner, and many people also say 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」, using the kanji for early evening, from 「夕方|ゆうがた」. For late night, people say 「夜遅く|よるおそく」, late at night. So for dinner time and evening habits, think 「晩|ばん」. For the late dark hours, think 「夜|よる」. Choose this word for every evening's routine, and you will sound natural." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "One of the most common every night habits in Japan is the bath. Many Japanese people take a bath every evening rather than a shower in the morning. The phrase is 「お風呂に入る|おふろにはいる」, literally to enter the bath. You wash and rinse outside the tub first, then soak in clean hot water to relax. Often the whole family uses the same tub water one after another, which is why you never wash inside the tub. So a very Japanese sentence would be 「毎晩お風呂に入ります|まいばんおふろにはいります」, I take a bath every night. Other typical routines are watching the evening news, having dinner with family, or studying late for exams. If you stay with a Japanese host family, someone will probably ask you when you want to take your bath. Answering that question politely is a small but real piece of everyday life." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Imagine you live in an apartment with a very loud neighbor. Every single night, at exactly the same time, he comes home and slams his door: BANG. Your clock could be set by it. You lie in bed and think, there it is, my bang, my nightly bang. My, bang. Picture it in detail. The light from the hallway flashes under his door, you hear his keys jingle, and then the whole wall shakes. Your cat jumps off the bed every time. It happens every evening as the sun goes down, which matches the sun piece on the left of the second kanji. So whenever you want to say every night, think of that slamming door that never misses a night. My bang. And if the neighbor is going out instead, think about the door slamming the other way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「毎|まい」, every, plus 「晩|ばん」, evening, built like 「毎日|まいにち」 and linked to 「今晩|こんばん」 and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」, and it takes no 「に」.",
      "「晩|ばん」 covers evening from sunset while 「夜|よる」 is night more broadly, and a nightly routine almost always uses this word.",
      "A classic nightly habit is the bath, 「毎晩お風呂に入ります|まいばんおふろにはいります」, washing outside the tub before soaking.",
      "Picture a loud neighbor slamming his door every night at sunset: my bang, my nightly bang."
    ],
    ja: "[毎晩|まいばん]{音楽|おんがく}を{聞きます|ききます}。",
    en: "I listen to music every night." },
  "しゅうまつ|週末": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「週|しゅう」, means week. It has the movement piece wrapping around the bottom left, and inside is a part meaning around or a full circuit. A week is a cycle that comes around again and again. You know it from 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week, 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week, and 「毎週|まいしゅう」, every week. The second kanji, 「末|まつ」, means end or tip. It is the tree shape with an extra long stroke across the top, marking the tip of the branches, the far end of the tree. Compare 「本|ほん」, which has a mark at the bottom, near the roots, the origin. The same 「末|まつ」 appears in 「期末試験|きまつしけん」, a final exam, the test at the end of a term. Put them together and you get the end of the week, the weekend, a perfect match with English. Use 「に」 after it to say on the weekend. Recap: week plus end, built like English, with 「末|まつ」 marking the tip of the tree." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is the star of Friday conversations. 「週末に何をしますか|しゅうまつになにをしますか」 means what are you doing on the weekend, and it is one of the most common questions between classmates and coworkers. On Monday, the question turns into the past: 「週末に何をしましたか|しゅうまつになにをしましたか」, what did you do on the weekend. You can add the usual time markers in front. 「今週末|こんしゅうまつ」 is this weekend, 「来週末|らいしゅうまつ」 is next weekend, and 「先週末|せんしゅうまつ」 is last weekend. In casual speech, many people say 「土日|どにち」 instead, short for Saturday and Sunday. The opposite of the weekend is 「平日|へいじつ」, the weekdays, a word you see on shop hours and train timetables. Prices at hotels and theme parks are often different on 「平日|へいじつ」 and 「土日|どにち」. So learn this question, and you have small talk ready for every Friday and Monday." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word build big families. The end kanji, 「末|まつ」, attaches to other time words. 「年末|ねんまつ」 is the end of the year, a busy season of cleaning and parties. 「月末|げつまつ」 is the end of the month, when bills and reports are due. The week kanji, 「週|しゅう」, builds words for weeks. Add the counter and you get 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」, one week, the length of time. Put it with 「に」 and a number of times, and you can say how often per week, as in 「週に二回|しゅうににかい」, twice a week. 「週刊誌|しゅうかんし」 is a weekly magazine, the kind sold at every train station kiosk. So with one end kanji you can talk about the end of a week, a month or a year, and with one week kanji you can count weeks and say how often. Two small kanji, a lot of useful words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The two-day weekend is newer in Japan than you might think. For a long time, many companies worked half a day or a full day on Saturday. The system of two days off each week, called 「週休二日制|しゅうきゅうふつかせい」, spread gradually from the late twentieth century. Public schools also used to hold classes on Saturday mornings. They moved step by step to a full five-day week, which was completed in 2002. Some private schools and cram schools still hold Saturday classes today. Even now, many shops, restaurants and service jobs are busiest on the weekend, so for many workers their days off fall on weekdays instead. Popular weekend activities include shopping, visiting parks, and going out to eat with family. Long weekends with a national holiday attached are called 「三連休|さんれんきゅう」, three days off in a row. So the weekend in Japan is precious and fairly recent." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「週|しゅう」, week, a cycle that comes around, plus 「末|まつ」, end, a tree with a long stroke marking the tips, so the end of the week, just like English.",
      "Friday's question is 「週末に何をしますか|しゅうまつになにをしますか」, and the opposite of the weekend is 「平日|へいじつ」, weekdays, while many say 「土日|どにち」 casually.",
      "「末|まつ」 also makes 「年末|ねんまつ」 and 「月末|げつまつ」, while 「週|しゅう」 gives 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」 and 「週に二回|しゅうににかい」.",
      "The two-day weekend, 「週休二日制|しゅうきゅうふつかせい」, spread only gradually, and public schools didn't fully drop Saturday classes until 2002."
    ],
    ja: "[週末|しゅうまつ]に{友だち|ともだち}と{映画|えいが}を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch a movie with friends on the weekend." },
  "いつ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the question word for time. It belongs to a family of question words you already know. 「なん」 asks what, 「どこ」 asks where, 「だれ」 asks who, and 「いつ」 asks when. In a sentence it usually sits right before the verb, and the sentence ends with 「か」. For example, when will you go to Japan. A key grammar point: 「いつ」 does not take the particle 「に」. English speakers often add it because time words like at three o'clock use 「に」, but with 「いつ」 you leave it out. You can also use it as a noun with 「です」, as in asking when someone's birthday is: 「誕生日はいつですか|たんじょうびはいつですか」. It is related to 「いつも」, always, which is literally when plus also, any time at all. A way to remember it, as a hook: it sounds a bit like English it's, so imagine asking, it's when? Recap: the when word, no 「に」, and the root of 「いつも」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Add a small word to the end of this question word and you get a whole set of new meanings. Add 「か」 and you get 「いつか」, someday, some time in the future. 「いつか日本に行きたいです|いつかにほんにいきたいです」 means I want to go to Japan someday. Add 「も」 and you get 「いつも」, always, which you already know. Add 「でも」 and you get 「いつでも」, anytime at all, as in feel free to come anytime. You can also add particles that mark a starting or ending point. 「いつから」 asks since when or from when, and 「いつまで」 asks until when. 「いつまでに」 asks by when, which is the deadline question you hear at work and school. And 「いつごろ」 asks around when, for a softer, rougher answer. The same pattern works with other question words, like 「だれか」, someone, and 「どこか」, somewhere. So one small question word opens a door to someday, always, anytime and until when." },
      { title: "When versus what time", text: "Japanese has several ways to ask about time, and choosing the right one makes you sound natural. This word is the broadest. It asks when, and the answer could be a date, a season, next year or after class. If you want a clock time, use 「何時|なんじ」, what time. If you want a day of the week, use 「何曜日|なんようび」, what day. For a date, use 「何月何日|なんがつなんにち」, what month and what day. A big difference is the particle. The words built with 「何|なん」 take 「に」 when you ask about the time of an action, as in 「何時に起きますか|なんじにおきますか」, what time do you get up. This word never does. When you answer, though, a clock time does take 「に」, as in 「七時に起きます|しちじにおきます」. So ask broadly with this word, and ask precisely with the 「何|なん」 words." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is essential for making plans. A very common and polite scheduling question is 「いつがいいですか」, when is good for you. The other person might answer 「金曜日がいいです|きんようびがいいです」, Friday is good. Between friends, people often drop the polite ending and just say 「いつ」 with a rising tone, especially when reacting to news. If a friend says they are going to Japan, a quick 「いつ」 asks when. Another set phrase is 「いつでもどうぞ」, please come anytime, a warm and generous thing to say to a guest. You will also hear 「いつの間に|いつのまに」, when did that happen, said in surprise when you notice something changed without you seeing it. With birthdays and holidays, the pattern with 「です」 at the end is the natural way to ask. So this little word helps you set dates, react to news and invite people over." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the question word for time, joining 「なん」, 「どこ」 and 「だれ」, and unlike clock times it never takes 「に」.",
      "Add 「か」 for someday, 「も」 for always, and 「でも」 for anytime, while 「いつから」, 「いつまで」 and 「いつまでに」 ask from, until and by when.",
      "This word asks broadly, while 「何時|なんじ」 and 「何曜日|なんようび」 ask precisely and take 「に」, which 「いつ」 never does.",
      "Schedule with 「いつがいいですか」, welcome a guest with 「いつでもどうぞ」, and say 「いつの間に|いつのまに」 in surprise at an unnoticed change."
    ],
    ja: "[いつ]{日本|にほん}に{行きますか|いきますか}。",
    en: "When will you go to Japan?" },
  "〜ごろ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This little ending makes a point in time fuzzy. You stick it right onto a clock time, a day, or a date. 「三時|さんじ」 is three o'clock sharp. 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」 is three-ish, around three. It also works with bigger units, like 「土曜日ごろ|どようびごろ」, around Saturday. You can add 「に」 after it or leave it off; both sound natural. Here is the common mistake. 「ごろ」 is only for a moment on the timeline, a when. For an amount, like about two hours or about ten dollars, Japanese uses a different word, 「ぐらい」. So around three o'clock takes 「ごろ」, but about three hours does not. You will meet the same sound in 「このごろ」, these days, which is a fuzzy stretch of recent time. A way to remember it: 「ごろ」 sounds like a ball rolling around, 「ごろごろ」, never landing on exactly one spot. To recap, put it after a time to say roughly when." },
      { title: "Hidden kanji", text: "This ending is usually written in kana, but it has a kanji, 「頃|ころ」. On its own the reading is 「ころ」, and it means a period of time, roughly when. When it attaches to a time word, the first sound softens into 「ご」, a common sound change in Japanese compounds. That is why you hear 「ごろ」 after a time but 「ころ」 when it stands alone. The standalone form is very common. 「子どもの頃|こどものころ」 means when I was a child, and 「その頃|そのころ」 means around that time, back then. The kanji also shows up in other words. 「近頃|ちかごろ」 means lately, recently. 「日頃|ひごろ」 means usually, in daily life. And 「年頃|としごろ」 refers to a certain age, such as being at the age when something is typical. In all of them, the kanji adds the idea of a rough period rather than an exact point. So behind the little ending is a whole word for a time in life." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In daily life, this ending makes plans sound relaxed and polite. Asking 「何時ごろ帰りますか|なんじごろかえりますか」, around what time will you be home, sounds softer than demanding an exact time. The other person can answer 「七時ごろ|しちじごろ」, around seven, without promising anything. You can also ask 「いつごろ」, around when, for longer stretches, and answer with things like 「来月ごろ|らいげつごろ」, around next month. It is common in seasons too. People say 「桜は四月ごろ咲きます|さくらはしがつごろさきます」, the cherry blossoms bloom around April. The opposite idea, exactly, is 「ちょうど」. So 「ちょうど三時|ちょうどさんじ」 is three o'clock on the dot, while three-ish uses this ending. Japanese often prefers a little vagueness when talking about other people's time, so this ending is a polite and friendly tool. Use it whenever an exact time would sound too strict." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Stuck onto a point in time it means around, as in 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」, but for amounts like about three hours Japanese uses 「ぐらい」 instead.",
      "Its kanji 「頃|ころ」 means a rough period, read 「ころ」 alone as in 「子どもの頃|こどものころ」, softening to 「ごろ」 after a time word.",
      "It makes plans sound relaxed and polite, as in 「何時ごろ帰りますか|なんじごろかえりますか」, while 「ちょうど」 is its opposite, exactly."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[十一時ごろ|じゅういちじごろ|〜ごろ]{寝ます|ねます}。",
    en: "I go to bed around eleven every night." },
  "いく|行く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「行|い」 began as a picture of a crossroads, two streets crossing, the place where you set off in some direction. Here it takes its native Japanese reading, 「い」. In other words it uses the Chinese-origin reading 「こう」. You know it from 「旅行|りょこう」, travel, a journey you set out on, and from 「飛行機|ひこうき」, an airplane, a machine that flies and goes. Mark where you are going with 「に」 or 「へ」, like 「学校に行きます|がっこうにいきます」, I go to school. It is a u-verb, so the polite form is 「行きます|いきます」. Watch out for one quirk you will meet later: its te-form is 「行って|いって」, not the pattern you would expect. One more thing English speakers trip on. Japanese chooses between going and coming based on the speaker. If you move away from where you are now, it is always this verb, even when English might say I'm coming. To recap, a crossroads kanji for heading off somewhere." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a building block for many patterns. With a verb stem plus 「に」, you can say you go to do something. 「食べに行きます|たべにいきます」 means I go to eat, and 「遊びに行きます|あそびにいきます」 means I go out to have fun. Swap in a noun for an activity and it still works: 「買い物に行きます|かいものにいきます」, I go shopping. For invitations, use 「行きませんか|いきませんか」, won't you go, and 「行きましょう|いきましょう」, let's go. In plain speech the negative is 「行かない|いかない」, and the past is 「行った|いった」, following the same odd pattern as the te-form. Later you will meet 「行ったことがある|いったことがある」, I have been somewhere before. And remember the particles. The destination takes 「に」 or 「へ」, but the route you move along takes 「を」, as in walking along a road. So this one verb carries a lot of grammar." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Every Japanese home has a little ritual when someone leaves. The person going out says 「行ってきます|いってきます」. Literally, it means I will go and come back. The people staying at home answer 「行ってらっしゃい|いってらっしゃい」, please go and come back. It is said by children leaving for school, parents going to work, and even roommates popping out to the store. When they return, the pair of greetings is 「ただいま」, I'm home, and 「お帰りなさい|おかえりなさい」, welcome back. Both phrases are built on the te-form 「行って|いって」, which is why that quirky form is so important. English has no exact match, and learners often find it heartwarming. The phrase promises that you will return, and the reply wishes you a safe trip. You will also hear it said at workplaces, when someone heads out to meet a client. It is a small daily promise to come back." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has more readings than most. As well as 「い」 and 「こう」, it can be read 「ぎょう」, as in 「行事|ぎょうじ」, an event or annual festival, and 「一行|いちぎょう」, one line of text. It also forms the verb 「行う|おこなう」, to carry out or hold, a formal word you see in notices about events and exams. There is also an older form of this verb itself, 「ゆく」. It sounds more literary and is common in songs, poems and some set words, such as 「行方|ゆくえ」, someone's whereabouts. Here is a nice surprise. In 「銀行|ぎんこう」, bank, the kanji is not about going. In Chinese it also meant a trading company or guild, so a bank is literally a silver firm. And 「急行|きゅうこう」 is an express train, one that goes in a hurry. So one kanji covers going, doing, lines and even banks." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「行|い」 began as a crossroads, read 「い」 here and 「こう」 in 「旅行|りょこう」, and you use it whenever you move away from where you are.",
      "A stem plus 「に」 says you go to do something, as in 「食べに行きます|たべにいきます」, and the plain past 「行った|いった」 follows its odd te-form.",
      "Leaving home you say 「行ってきます|いってきます」, I'll go and come back, and those staying answer 「行ってらっしゃい|いってらっしゃい」.",
      "The kanji also reads 「ぎょう」 in 「行事|ぎょうじ」, forms 「行う|おこなう」, to carry out, and in 「銀行|ぎんこう」 it once meant a trading firm."
    ],
    ja: "{図書館|としょかん}に[行きます|いきます|いく]。",
    en: "I go to the library." },
  "かえる|帰る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is about going back to your home base. That usually means your house, but it can be your hometown or your own country. Coming back to the office after lunch is not quite it; it is the return to where you belong. The most common phrase is 「うちに帰ります|うちにかえります」, I go home. Here the kanji takes its native Japanese reading, 「かえ」. Grammar alert: even though it ends in 「える」, it is a u-verb, not a ru-verb. So the polite form is 「帰ります|かえります」, and later the negative is 「帰らない|かえらない」. It belongs to a small club of tricky ones that look like ru-verbs but are not. A fun cultural note. The word for frog is also 「かえる」, and many Japanese people carry little frog charms when they travel. The pun is that the frog helps you come home safely. A way to remember it: picture a frog hopping back home. To recap, going back to where you belong, and it conjugates as a u-verb." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji is a simplified form of an older, more complex character. The right side is the same shape found in the kanji for broom, and it also appears in 「婦|ふ」, as in 「主婦|しゅふ」, a homemaker. In the old Chinese dictionary tradition, the original character was explained as a woman going to her husband's home when she married. Scholars debate the details, so take that as background, not a certain picture. Today the meaning is simply returning to where you belong. In compounds this kanji reads 「き」. 「帰国|きこく」 means returning to your own country, which you will hear from exchange students and travelers. 「帰宅|きたく」 is a formal word for going home, used in news and at work, as in 「帰宅時間|きたくじかん」, the time you get home. So the native reading 「かえ」 is the everyday verb, and 「き」 is the formal compound reading. One kanji, two levels of speech." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb has some extremely common relatives in daily life. The noun 「帰り|かえり」 means the return trip or the way home. 「帰りに|かえりに」 means on the way home, as in stopping at a store on the way home. At the door, when someone comes home, the family says 「お帰りなさい|おかえりなさい」, welcome home, answering 「ただいま」, I'm home. In casual speech, that greeting is often shortened to 「お帰り|おかえり」. At work, leaving before others is delicate. People say 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」, excuse me for leaving before you, rather than announcing that they are going home. A common English speaker mistake is using this verb for returning an object. Giving back a book uses a different verb with the same sound, 「返す|かえす」. This verb is only for people going back. So use it for yourself, and use the other one for the library book." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Several words share the sound 「かえる」, and context or pitch keeps them apart. This verb, to go back home, is one. The frog is another, which is why the travel charm pun works. A third is 「変える|かえる」, to change something, written with a completely different kanji. In standard Tokyo speech, the pitch helps a little. The going home verb starts high and drops after the first syllable. The verbs to change and the frog start low and stay level. Do not worry too much about pitch at first, because context almost always makes it clear. There is also a grammar difference. The going home verb is a u-verb, so its polite form is 「帰ります|かえります」. The change verb is a ru-verb, so its polite form is 「変えます|かえます」. Hearing 「かえります」 versus 「かえます」 tells you which one it is. So listen for that extra syllable to know who is going home." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means going back to where you belong, as in 「うちに帰ります|うちにかえります」, and despite ending in 「える」 it is a u-verb, so 「帰ります|かえります」.",
      "The kanji was traditionally explained as a woman going to her husband's home, a debated picture, and reads 「き」 in compounds like 「帰国|きこく」 and 「帰宅|きたく」.",
      "Its noun gives 「帰りに|かえりに」, on the way home, but returning an object uses a different verb, 「返す|かえす」.",
      "It shares its sound with frog and with 「変える|かえる」, to change, but the u-verb 「かえります」 versus the ru-verb 「かえます」 tells them apart."
    ],
    ja: "{六時|ろくじ}にうちに[帰ります|かえります|かえる]。",
    en: "I go home at six." },
  "はなす|話す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji 「話|はな」. On the left is the speech radical, the same piece you will see in 「言う|いう」, to say. On the right is a shape that looks like 「舌|した」, tongue. A way to remember it: words plus a tongue make talking. In this verb the kanji uses its native reading, 「はな」. In compounds it uses the Chinese-origin reading 「わ」. That is the 「わ」 in 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, literally electric talk, since 「電|でん」 is electricity. It also appears in 「電話する|でんわする」, to make a call. With this verb, the person you talk with takes 「と」, and the language takes 「で」. So 「友だちと日本語で話します|ともだちとにほんごではなします」 means I talk with my friend in Japanese. It is a u-verb, so the polite form is 「話します|はなします」. Compare it with 「言う|いう」, which is about the words someone says, while this one is about having a conversation. To recap, speech plus tongue, the talking kanji." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The talking kanji builds a useful family. Take the stem of this verb as a noun and you get 「話|はなし」, a story, a talk, or what someone said. 「話をする|はなしをする」 means to have a talk, and 「面白い話|おもしろいはなし」 is an interesting story. With the Chinese-origin reading 「わ」, you get 「会話|かいわ」, conversation, literally meeting and talking. That is the word on your textbook's dialogue pages. You know 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, and 「電話する|でんわする」, to call. And there is the very common phrase 「お世話になります|おせわになります」, which thanks someone for taking care of you or helping you. People use it constantly in business emails and when staying with a host family. Its past form, thank you for everything you have done for me, is a classic goodbye. So the same kanji covers stories, conversations, phone calls and a key phrase of gratitude." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a u-verb ending in 「す」, and those have one simple rule for the te-form and the past. The 「す」 turns into 「し」. So the te-form is 「話して|はなして」 and the plain past is 「話した|はなした」. That makes it one of the easiest u-verbs. The plain negative is 「話さない|はなさない」. Later you will meet the potential form, can speak. For this verb it is 「話せる|はなせる」, and with that form, the language usually takes 「が」 instead of 「を」. So 「日本語が話せます|にほんごがはなせます」 means I can speak Japanese. With the te-form you can make 「話しています|はなしています」, someone is talking right now, and 「話してください|はなしてください」, please speak, often said with slowly, as in please speak slowly. Learn the 「す」 to 「し」 swap once and you can use it with every verb that ends the same way." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Particles change the meaning of this verb in small but important ways. 「日本語を話します|にほんごをはなします」 means I speak Japanese, a statement about the language as a skill. 「日本語で話します|にほんごではなします」 means I talk in Japanese, about the language used in a particular conversation. Both are correct, but they fit different situations. The person you talk to can take 「と」 or 「に」. With 「と」, you talk together, a two-way conversation. With 「に」, you speak to someone, more one-way, like reporting to your boss. Another handy verb is 「話しかける|はなしかける」, to start talking to someone, such as a stranger on a train. And if someone talks too long, people may say their story is long. A common learner request is 「もう少しゆっくり話してください|もうすこしゆっくりはなしてください」, please speak a little more slowly. Get the particles right and your talking will sound natural." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「話|はな」 is the speech radical beside a tongue shape, read 「わ」 in 「電話|でんわ」, and the person takes 「と」 while the language takes 「で」.",
      "Its family includes 「話|はなし」, a story, 「会話|かいわ」, conversation, and the grateful phrase 「お世話になります|おせわになります」.",
      "As a 「す」 verb, 「す」 turns into 「し」, giving 「話して|はなして」 and 「話した|はなした」, while the potential 「日本語が話せます|にほんごがはなせます」 uses 「が」.",
      "「日本語を話します|にほんごをはなします」 states a skill while 「日本語で話します|にほんごではなします」 names the language used, and 「と」 is two-way while 「に」 is one-way."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と{日本語|にほんご}で[話します|はなします|はなす]。",
    en: "I talk with my friend in Japanese." },
  "おきる|起きる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「起|お」 has the run radical on its left side, the same shape that stands alone as 「走|はし」 in 「走る|はしる」, to run. That piece is about movement, getting your feet going. In this word the kanji takes its native reading, 「お」. A way to remember it: picture yourself springing up out of bed, ready to run. That is getting up. It is a ru-verb, so you drop the 「る」 and add 「ます」 to get 「起きます|おきます」. It pairs naturally with its opposite, 「寝る|ねる」, to go to bed. Notice that it is about you getting up yourself. When you wake someone else up, Japanese uses a related verb with the same kanji, 「起こす|おこす」. One small cultural note: a classic Japanese worry is 「朝寝坊|あさねぼう」, oversleeping, which is exactly what happens when this verb fails. To recap, the run radical for jumping out of bed." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a trap that catches many learners. This verb describes the moment of getting up or waking up. Put it in the te-form with 「いる」 and you get 「起きています|おきています」. That does not mean I am getting up right now. It means I am awake, the state that continues after you got up. So if someone calls late at night and asks if you are awake, you answer 「起きています|おきています」. To say what time you get up, use the plain polite form, 「七時に起きます|しちじにおきます」. The verb has a second meaning, too: to happen or occur. 「事故が起きました|じこがおきました」 means an accident happened, and 「地震が起きました|じしんがおきました」 means an earthquake happened. Things getting up and starting is the shared idea. As a ru-verb, the te-form is 「起きて|おきて」, the negative is 「起きない|おきない」, and 「起きて|おきて」 alone is a casual get up." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji appears in a few words you will meet soon. 「早起き|はやおき」 means getting up early, and also a person who does it. It comes from 「早い|はやい」, early, plus the stem of this verb. There is a famous proverb, 「早起きは三文の徳|はやおきはさんもんのとく」, getting up early earns you three coins. A 「文|もん」 was a small old coin, so it is a modest reward, but the point is that early risers gain something. It is roughly like the early bird catches the worm. In compounds this kanji takes the Chinese-origin reading 「き」. 「起床|きしょう」 is a formal word for getting up, seen on schedules at camps, dorms and hospitals, as in 「起床時間|きしょうじかん」, wake-up time. So at home you get up with this verb, but the dormitory schedule says 「起床|きしょう」. Same idea, different level." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture your alarm going off. You sit straight up in bed, eyes wide, and shout, Oh, key! You have just remembered you left your house key in the front door all night. You leap out of bed and run to the door in your pajamas. Oh, key, oh, key. That frantic jump out of bed is exactly what this verb means. Now tie in the kanji. Remember the run piece on the left side. In your scene, you are literally running from your bed to the door, chasing that key. Make it vivid: the alarm is still ringing, your hair is a mess, and the neighbor is watching you through the window. Whenever you hear 「おきる」, picture yourself bolting up and shouting about the key. Getting up has never been so dramatic." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「起|お」 has the run radical on its left, like springing out of bed to run, and it is a ru-verb, so 「起きます|おきます」.",
      "「起きています|おきています」 means you are awake, not getting up right now, and the verb also means to happen, as in 「地震が起きました|じしんがおきました」.",
      "It forms 「早起き|はやおき」, getting up early, as in the proverb 「早起きは三文の徳|はやおきはさんもんのとく」, while schedules use the formal 「起床|きしょう」.",
      "Picture bolting up when the alarm rings, shouting oh, key, and running from bed to the door, matching the run piece in the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}{六時|ろくじ}に[起きます|おきます|おきる]。",
    en: "I get up at six in the morning." },
  "みる|見る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「見|み」 is one of the clearest pictures in Japanese. On top is 「目|め」, the eye, and underneath are two little legs. So it is an eye walking around, looking at things. In this verb it takes its native reading, 「み」. In compounds it often uses the Chinese-origin reading 「けん」. It covers seeing, looking, and watching all at once, so use it for movies, TV, and sports, where English switches to watch. That means 「テレビを見ます|テレビをみます」 and 「映画を見ます|えいがをみます」. It is a ru-verb with a very short stem, so the polite form is just 「見ます|みます」. You will meet a whole family built on the same kanji. 「見せる|みせる」 is to show, making someone else see. 「見える|みえる」 is to be visible, something that can be seen. 「見つかる|みつかる」 is to be found. To recap, an eye on legs, the verb for seeing and watching." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Because this is a ru-verb, every form keeps the tiny stem 「み」 and only the ending changes. The te-form is 「見て|みて」, the negative is 「見ない|みない」, and the past is 「見た|みた」. That te-form unlocks some very useful patterns. Add 「いる」 and you get 「見ている|みている」, to be watching right now. Add this same verb again, usually written in kana, and you get a pattern meaning try doing something and see how it goes. 「食べてみる|たべてみる」 is to try eating it, to taste and see. You can also put the stem in front of 「に行く|にいく」 to say you go somewhere in order to see something. 「映画を見に行きます|えいがをみにいきます」 means I am going to go see a movie. One listening tip. The stem is so short that the polite forms fly by in speech, so pay attention to whether you hear the positive ending or the negative one. To wrap up, one tiny stem that powers watching, trying, and going to see." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A few uses of this verb surprise English speakers. In Japanese you do not have a dream, you see one. 「夢を見る|ゆめをみる」 is to dream. The bigger trap is people. In English you say you saw your friend yesterday when you mean you met up. In Japanese, meeting someone takes 「会う|あう」 with the particle 「に」, so 「友だちに会いました|ともだちにあいました」 is I saw my friend. Using the looking verb for a friend sounds like you watched them from across the street. In daily life you will hear the quick command 「見て|みて」, look!, from kids tugging at a parent's sleeve. And in a shop, 「見ているだけです|みているだけです」 means I am just looking, a lifesaver when a clerk comes over to help. In spring, the whole country goes out for 「花見|はなみ」, flower viewing, which is literally flower looking. To wrap up, you see your dreams, you meet your friends, and you can always say you are just looking." },
      { title: "Look versus visible", text: "Your vocabulary pairs this verb with 「見える|みえる」, and the difference is worth getting exactly right. This verb is something you do on purpose. You choose to look, so the thing you look at takes 「を」. 「山を見ます|やまをみます」 is I look at the mountain. The partner verb describes something that simply comes into view, whether you try or not, and the thing that can be seen takes 「が」. 「山が見えます|やまがみえます」 means the mountain is visible, or I can see the mountain. Picture a hotel room near Mount Fuji. When you pull back the curtain and there it is, you use the partner verb, because the view just appeared. When you sit down with a coffee and gaze at it for ten minutes, you use this one. Hearing works the same way, with 「聞く|きく」 for listening on purpose and 「聞こえる|きこえる」 for sounds that reach your ears on their own. To wrap up, looking is a choice with 「を」, while being visible just happens, with 「が」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「見|み」 is an eye, 「目|め」, walking on two little legs, looking around. It covers seeing, looking and watching, so you use it for TV and movies too, as in 「テレビを見ます|テレビをみます」.",
      "As a ru-verb it keeps the tiny stem 「み」, giving 「見て|みて」, 「見ない|みない」 and 「見た|みた」. Its te-form powers 「見ている|みている」 for watching now, the try-and-see pattern like 「食べてみる|たべてみる」, and 「見に行く|みにいく」 for going to see something.",
      "In Japanese you see a dream, 「夢を見る|ゆめをみる」, but you meet a friend with 「会う|あう」, never this verb. In a shop, 「見ているだけです|みているだけです」 means you are just looking.",
      "This verb is looking on purpose, so the thing takes 「を」, as in 「山を見ます|やまをみます」. Its partner 「見える|みえる」 is for things that simply come into view, with 「が」, as in 「山が見えます|やまがみえます」."
    ],
    ja: "{今晩|こんばん}テレビを[見ます|みます|みる]。",
    en: "I will watch TV tonight." },
  "くる|来る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the opposite of 「行く|いく」. It means moving toward where the speaker is, or toward the place being talked about. The kanji 「来|く」 is traditionally said to have started as a picture of a wheat plant, and the meaning come was borrowed onto it. In this verb it uses the native reading. In time words it uses the Chinese-origin reading 「らい」, meaning the one that is coming. So 「来週|らいしゅう」 is next week, 「来月|らいげつ」 is next month, and 「来年|らいねん」 is next year, the week, month, and year that are on their way to you. Grammar alert: this is one of only two irregular verbs, along with 「する」. Its vowel changes. The dictionary form is 「くる」, but the polite form is 「来ます|きます」, and later the negative is 「来ない|こない」. The kanji stays the same while the sound shifts, so read carefully. To recap, the coming verb, irregular, and the 「らい」 in next week and next year." },
      { title: "Whose point of view", text: "Here is a mistake almost every English speaker makes. In English, when your friend calls and says come over, you answer I'm coming. In Japanese that sounds wrong. This verb only means moving toward where the speaker is now, or toward a place the speaker treats as home base. When you head to the other person, you are leaving your own spot, so you use 「行く|いく」. On the phone you say 「今行きます|いまいきます」, I'm on my way, literally I'm going now. The person waiting for you would say 「来てください|きてください」, please come. Think of it as a camera that always stays with the speaker. Things moving toward the camera come. Things moving away from the camera go. So a friend at your house might say 「また来てね|またきてね」, come again, as you leave, while you answer that you will go again. To wrap up, coming always points toward the speaker, so when you head to someone else, you go." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is irregular, so it is worth memorizing as a little chant. The dictionary form starts with 「く」, the polite form with 「き」, and the negative with 「こ」. Then the te-form is 「来て|きて」 and the plain past is 「来た|きた」. The kanji never changes, only the sound, so you have to read the ending to know how to say it. The te-form is where it gets really useful, because it attaches to other verbs as a direction helper. 「持ってくる|もってくる」 is to bring a thing, literally hold it and come. 「連れてくる|つれてくる」 is to bring a person or an animal along. And 「迎えに来る|むかえにくる」 means to come to pick someone up. Notice the difference between bringing things and bringing people. You never use the holding verb for a friend. To wrap up, an irregular verb that changes its first sound, and a helper that brings things and people toward you." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "You already know that the Chinese-origin reading 「らい」 gives you next week, next month, and next year. That reading carries the idea of what is on its way. The same piece shows up in more words from your lessons. 「来学期|らいがっき」 is next semester, the school term that is coming. 「将来|しょうらい」 means the future, especially your own future plans, like what job you want. The first kanji there carries a sense of about to, so the word is roughly what is about to come. Compare that with 「来年|らいねん」, which is just the calendar year ahead. Japanese people ask 「将来、何になりたいですか|しょうらい、なにになりたいですか」, what do you want to be in the future, to students all the time. On the native side, you have the verb itself and its helpers like 「連れてくる|つれてくる」. So you can tell at a glance: 「らい」 at the front of a time word means the coming one. To wrap up, 「らい」 is the coming one, from next semester all the way to your future." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This verb is irregular, so the sound shifts while the kanji stays: 「くる」, 「来ます|きます」, 「来ない|こない」. Its Chinese-origin reading 「らい」 means the one that is coming, as in 「来週|らいしゅう」 and 「来年|らいねん」.",
      "Coming always points toward the speaker, like a camera that stays with them. So when you head over to someone, you say 「今行きます|いまいきます」, not this verb.",
      "Memorize the irregular chant of 「く」, 「き」, 「こ」, then 「来て|きて」 and 「来た|きた」. The te-form becomes a direction helper, with 「持ってくる|もってくる」 for bringing things and 「連れてくる|つれてくる」 for bringing people.",
      "The reading 「らい」 at the front of a time word means the coming one. It gives 「来学期|らいがっき」, next semester, and 「将来|しょうらい」, your future, roughly what is about to come."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}がうちに[来ます|きます|くる]。",
    en: "My friend is coming to my house." },
  "する|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the all-purpose verb of Japanese. On its own it means to do, but its real power is turning nouns into actions. Put a noun with 「を」 before it, like 「テニスをする」, to play tennis, or 「宿題をする|しゅくだいをする」, to do homework. Many nouns can even attach directly. 「勉強|べんきょう」 is study, the noun, and 「勉強する|べんきょうする」 is to study. 「電話|でんわ」 is a phone, and 「電話する|でんわする」 is to call. 「旅行|りょこう」 is travel, and 「旅行する|りょこうする」 is to travel. It is one of only two irregular verbs, together with 「来る|くる」. The polite form is 「します」, and later the negative is 「しない」. A common mistake for English speakers is reaching for 「あそぶ」 for playing sports. With sports and games, Japanese simply does them, so use this verb. A way to remember it: think of it as the glue that sticks onto a noun and makes it move. To recap, to do, the verb that turns nouns into actions." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Because this verb is irregular, its forms are worth drilling as a set. The te-form is 「して」, the plain past is 「した」, and the negative is 「しない」. Every noun-plus-verb combination inherits those same forms, so once you know them, you can conjugate hundreds of verbs for free. 「勉強した|べんきょうした」 is studied, and 「電話して|でんわして」 is the te-form of calling. Another big surprise is that this verb has no regular potential form. To say can do, Japanese switches to a completely different verb, 「できる」. So I can play tennis is 「テニスができます」. There is one more handy pattern. A noun plus 「にする」 means to decide on that thing. At a café, 「コーヒーにします」 means I will have coffee, I have decided on coffee. To wrap up, learn the forms once, and they work for every noun you turn into a verb." },
      { title: "Beyond doing", text: "This verb has some uses that have nothing to do with doing. One is wearing small accessories. A tie, a watch, or a necklace is something you do in Japanese, so 「ネクタイをしています」 means he is wearing a tie. Clothes for the body and hats have their own verbs, so this one is for the extras. Another use is noticing a sound, smell, or taste. 「いいにおいがする」 means something smells good, literally a good smell is doing. 「音がする|おとがする」 means you hear a sound. Notice the particle there is 「が」, not 「を」, because the smell or sound is the one doing it. You will also hear 「どうしますか」 all the time, meaning what will you do, or what would you like to do. A waiter might say it, or a friend making plans. To wrap up, besides doing things, this verb wears accessories and senses smells and sounds." },
      { title: "Loanword verbs", text: "This verb is the reason Japanese can borrow English verbs so easily. Take almost any English action, write it in katakana, and stick this verb on the end. 「コピーする」 is to make a copy. 「デートする」 is to go on a date. 「ジョギングする」 is to go jogging. 「メールする」 is to send a message. Japanese doesn't try to conjugate the English word itself. It leaves the borrowed noun frozen and lets this verb do all the grammar work. So the past is 「コピーした」 and the negative is 「デートしない」. This is the same trick Japanese used centuries ago with words borrowed from Chinese, like the noun for study. The only difference is the source language. A warning: not every English word works, and some combinations mean something unexpected, so check before you invent one. Still, it is a great way to grow your vocabulary fast. To wrap up, a borrowed noun plus this verb gives you a brand new action." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This all-purpose doing verb turns nouns into actions, like 「勉強|べんきょう」 into 「勉強する|べんきょうする」. Use it for sports and games too, as in 「テニスをする」, rather than 「あそぶ」.",
      "Learn the irregular forms 「して」, 「した」 and 「しない」 once, and every noun-plus-verb combo inherits them. For can do, switch to 「できる」, and a noun plus 「にする」 means deciding on it, like 「コーヒーにします」.",
      "This verb also wears small accessories, as in 「ネクタイをしています」. With 「が」 it senses smells and sounds, like 「いいにおいがする」 and 「音がする|おとがする」.",
      "Write almost any English action in katakana and add this verb, as in 「コピーする」 or 「デートする」. The borrowed noun stays frozen while this verb does all the grammar work."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}テニスを[します|します|する]。",
    en: "I play tennis on the weekend." },
  "べんきょうする|勉強する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「勉|べん」 means to make an effort, to push yourself. 「強|きょう」 means strong, the same kanji as in 「強い|つよい」, strong. Both use their Chinese-origin readings here. Put them together and you get pushing hard with effort, which is how Japanese thinks of studying. Then add 「する」 to turn the noun into a verb. Here is a fun anecdote. In Chinese, the same two characters mean doing something reluctantly, forcing yourself. And in Japanese shops, especially in the old merchant city of Osaka, a shopkeeper might say they will make an effort for you with this word, meaning they will give you a discount. So the core idea really is straining yourself. Grammar note: the subject you study takes 「を」, like 「日本語を勉強します|にほんごをべんきょうします」. You can also say it as 「日本語の勉強をします|にほんごのべんきょうをします」. It is different from 「練習する|れんしゅうする」, to practice, which is drilling a skill. To recap, effort plus strong, hard work at your books." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "English has one word for learning, but Japanese splits it into several, and each has its own flavor. This verb is studying on your own with effort, like reviewing notes at the library. 「習う|ならう」 is learning from someone, a teacher or a coach. You learn piano or calligraphy from a teacher with that verb, and the teacher takes 「に」. 「練習する|れんしゅうする」 is practicing a skill over and over, like drilling kanji or running the same song. On the other side of the desk, 「教える|おしえる」 is to teach. And the second kanji of this word, 「強|きょう」, is the same one in 「強い|つよい」, strong. So if you studied hard and got strong at something, the kanji connects the effort to the result. A quick test: if there is a teacher in the sentence, use the learning-from verb. If you are alone with a textbook, use this one. To wrap up, study alone, learn from someone, practice a skill, and teach others." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some phrases built on this word that Japanese people use every day. 「勉強になりました|べんきょうになりました」 means that was educational, or I learned a lot. People say it after a talk, a meeting, or even a friend's advice, as a polite way of saying thank you for teaching me. You can also add a little ending meaning in the middle of. 「日本語を勉強中です|にほんごをべんきょうちゅうです」 is I am currently studying Japanese. It is a great humble line when someone praises your Japanese. Parents tell kids 「勉強しなさい|べんきょうしなさい」, go study, in that slightly bossy tone every child knows. And students often talk about 「試験勉強|しけんべんきょう」, studying for an exam. A cultural note: Japanese high school students often go to cram school in the evening, and many study at cafés or the library until late. To wrap up, a word you will hear for learning, for thanking, and from every nagging parent." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Imagine a guy named Ben. Ben is at the gym, but instead of a barbell, he is lifting an enormous dictionary over his head. He is red in the face and shouting, today! Today I get strong! So you have Ben, plus today. Ben pushes hard today, and that sounds like the word. The scene matches the real kanji too. The first kanji is about pushing yourself with effort, and the second one is strong, so Ben is straining and getting stronger, just like the characters say. Every time you open your textbook, picture Ben in the corner doing reps with the dictionary and cheering you on. Then remember to add the doing verb at the end to make it an action. To wrap up, Ben lifting books today, effort plus strong, is how you study." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「勉|べん」 means pushing yourself with effort and 「強|きょう」 means strong, so studying is straining hard at your books. The subject you study takes 「を」, as in 「日本語を勉強します|にほんごをべんきょうします」.",
      "This verb is studying on your own with effort, while 「習う|ならう」 is learning from a teacher and 「練習する|れんしゅうする」 is drilling a skill. If there is a teacher in the sentence, use the learning-from verb instead.",
      "People say 「勉強になりました|べんきょうになりました」 to mean I learned a lot, as a polite thank you. 「日本語を勉強中です|にほんごをべんきょうちゅうです」 is a nice humble reply when someone praises your Japanese.",
      "Picture Ben at the gym lifting a huge dictionary and shouting that today he gets strong, Ben plus today. It matches the kanji, effort plus strong."
    ],
    ja: "{図書館|としょかん}で[勉強します|べんきょうします|べんきょうする]。",
    en: "I study at the library." },
  "いい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the everyday, friendly form of an older adjective, 「よい」. Both mean good, but in conversation people almost always say 「いい」. Here is the catch that trips up every learner. Only the plain present form uses the 「いい」 shape. As soon as you conjugate it, it goes back to 「よ」. So not good is 「よくない」, not 「いくない」. Was good is 「よかった」. You already know that one inside 「よかったら」, if you like, which literally means if it is good. The adverb 「よく」, often or well, comes from the same root. You will also hear it a lot in 「頭がいい|あたまがいい」, smart, which literally says the head is good. It can also mean no thanks, depending on the tone, when someone says 「いいです」 to decline an offer, so listen for context. A way to remember it: think of a happy squeal, eee, that's good. To recap, 「いい」 when you say it plain, but 「よ」 whenever you change its shape." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adjective powers a lot of everyday phrases, and each one has its own job. 「いいですよ」 means sure, go ahead, when someone asks a favor. 「いいですね」 means that sounds great, when someone suggests a plan. Combined with the te-form, 「〜てもいいですか」 asks permission, so 「写真を撮ってもいいですか|しゃしんをとってもいいですか」 is may I take a photo? Later you will meet 「〜ほうがいい」 for advice, as in you had better do something. And 「どちらでもいいです」 means either one is fine. Here is a tricky one. When a clerk asks if you want a bag and you say 「いいです」, it usually means no thanks. To be clear, people add 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」 or a polite no thank you. Tone and context do the rest. To wrap up, this one little word says sure, sounds great, may I, and no thanks." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The most common mistake with this word is the past polite form. English speakers want to say 「いいでした」, but that is wrong. Adjectives that end in 「い」 carry the past tense themselves, so you say 「よかったです」, it was good. Remember that every conjugated form goes back to the older 「よ」 shape. The te-form for linking is 「よくて」, as in good and cheap. Becoming good is 「よくなる」, so 「風邪がよくなりました|かぜがよくなりました」 means my cold got better. The same rule follows this word into compounds. 「かっこいい」, cool or good-looking, ends with this word, so its negative is 「かっこよくない」, and its past is 「かっこよかった」. The same happens with 「頭がいい|あたまがいい」. Once you spot the word at the end, switch to 「よ」 whenever you conjugate. To wrap up, never say 「いいでした」, and let the older shape do the conjugating, even inside cool." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Three short words built from the same vowel can cause real trouble, so listen carefully. This word is two 「い」 sounds, held for one beat each. 「いいえ」, no, starts the same way but adds 「え」 at the end. If you drop that last sound, you turn no into good, which flips your answer. 「ええ」, a relaxed yes, is two 「え」 sounds. So the three words go good, no, and yes. You can practice them as a tiny chain, saying each one slowly. Pitch matters too. Said flat with a falling end, this word sounds like a calm good. Said with a quick rise, it can sound like a question, is it okay? And stretched with a sigh, it can mean never mind. A useful habit is to answer questions with a full phrase, like 「いいですね」, so your meaning is clear even if your vowels are shaky. To wrap up, two 「い」 sounds for good, add 「え」 for no, and two 「え」 sounds for yes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the everyday form of the older 「よい」, and only the plain present uses the 「いい」 shape. Every conjugated form goes back to 「よ」, as in 「よくない」 and 「よかった」.",
      "This word says sure in 「いいですよ」, sounds great in 「いいですね」, and asks permission in 「〜てもいいですか」. But when a clerk offers a bag, 「いいです」 usually means no thanks.",
      "Never say 「いいでした」, because the past polite form is 「よかったです」. The 「よ」 rule follows the word into compounds too, so cool becomes 「かっこよくない」 and 「かっこよかった」.",
      "This word is two 「い」 beats, 「いいえ」 adds 「え」 to make no, and 「ええ」 is two 「え」 sounds for yes. Drop that last 「え」 from no and you flip your answer to good."
    ],
    ja: "それは[いい]ですね。",
    en: "That's good." },
  "あまり|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This adverb softens a negative. Pair it with a negative verb or adjective and it means not very much, not very often. So 「テレビはあまり見ません|テレビはあまりみません」 means I don't watch TV much. It is not a flat no, just a little. The key rule for English speakers is that it needs a negative ending in this meaning. You cannot say 「あまり見ます|あまりみます」 to mean I watch a little. On the frequency scale it sits near the bottom. From most to least, you have 「いつも」, always, then 「たいてい」, usually, then 「よく」, often, then 「ときどき」, sometimes, then this one, not much, and finally 「ぜんぜん」, not at all. In casual speech you will also hear it squeezed into 「あんまり」. A way to remember it: picture a dial turned almost all the way down, but not quite to zero. To recap, a soft not much that always goes with a negative." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adverb works with more than verbs. It also softens negative adjectives and nouns. With an adjective that ends in 「い」, you say 「あまり高くないです|あまりたかくないです」, it is not very expensive. With a na-adjective, you say 「あまり好きじゃないです|あまりすきじゃないです」, I don't like it very much. With a noun, 「あまり学生じゃない|あまりがくせいじゃない」 sounds odd, because you either are a student or you are not, so save it for things that come in degrees, like 「あまり上手じゃないです|あまりじょうずじゃないです」, I am not very good at it. Notice where the adverb sits. It comes before the part that gets negated, usually right in front of the adjective or the verb phrase. You can also add 「は」 to the topic to set up the contrast, like 「肉はあまり食べません|にくはあまりたべません」, meat I don't eat much. To wrap up, this softener fits in front of verbs, adjectives, and nouns, as long as a negative ending follows." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese people use this word as a polite shield. Saying you hate something with 「きらい」 can sound blunt, so instead people say 「あまり好きじゃない|あまりすきじゃない」, I don't like it much. Even when the truth is I can't stand it, this softer version is the normal choice. You will also hear it on its own as a trailing answer. Someone asks if you drink coffee, and you say 「うーん、あまり」 and let it fade. The listener fills in the negative for you, and nobody has to be direct. Here is a small dialogue. 「スポーツをよくしますか」, do you play sports often? 「いいえ、あまりしません」, no, not much. A mistake to avoid is using it for a big quantity. If you mean you watch a lot of TV, use 「よく」 or 「たくさん」 instead. To wrap up, a soft and polite way to say not really, sometimes with the rest left unsaid." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This adverb comes from a noun meaning the remainder, the leftover part. It can be written 「余り|あまり」, from the verb 「余る|あまる」, to be left over, to be more than needed. In math class, the remainder after division is still called this word. So the basic idea is too much, beyond what is needed. That explains a second use you will meet in reading. In a phrase like 「あまりに高い|あまりにたかい」, it means excessively, too expensive, because the price goes beyond reason. Over time, the negative use took over in everyday speech. Not beyond the normal amount turned into not very much. So when you say you don't watch TV much, you are really saying your TV watching does not go over the line. In daily life, the adverb is almost always written in kana. To wrap up, from leftovers and excess to the soft not very much." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This adverb softens a negative to mean not very much, so it always needs a negative ending, as in 「テレビはあまり見ません|テレビはあまりみません」. On the frequency ladder it sits just above 「ぜんぜん」.",
      "This softener also goes right before negative adjectives, as in 「あまり高くないです|あまりたかくないです」 and 「あまり好きじゃないです|あまりすきじゃないです」. Save it for things that come in degrees.",
      "Japanese people use 「あまり好きじゃない|あまりすきじゃない」 as a polite shield instead of the blunt 「きらい」. It can even stand alone, as in 「うーん、あまり」, letting the negative fade unsaid.",
      "It comes from 「余り|あまり」, the leftover or remainder, from 「余る|あまる」, to be more than needed. Not beyond the normal amount turned into not very much, while 「あまりに高い|あまりにたかい」 keeps the excessive sense."
    ],
    ja: "テレビは[あまり]{見ません|みません}。",
    en: "I don't watch TV much." },
  "ぜんぜん|全然": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「全|ぜん」 means whole or complete. You know it from 「全部|ぜんぶ」, all of it, every part. 「然|ぜん」 is a kanji that describes a state, the way something is. Both use their Chinese-origin readings. Together they mean completely, wholly. Paired with a negative, that gives you not at all, a no all the way through. So 「お酒は全然飲みません|おさけはぜんぜんのみません」 means I don't drink alcohol at all. It is the strongest step on the scale below 「あまり」, not much. Keep that order in mind: 「あまり」 is just a little no, this one is a total no. A cultural note. Younger Japanese people often use it with positive words, as in 「全然いい|ぜんぜんいい」, totally fine. Some older people find that sloppy, but you will hear it everywhere. In your class, stick with negatives. To recap, wholly and completely, which with a negative means not at all." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at the second kanji, 「然|ぜん」. At the bottom are four little dots. That is a squashed form of the fire radical, the same four dots you see under many kanji to do with heat. Scholars generally agree this kanji first meant to burn. Later it was borrowed to mean so, or in that state, and a new kanji with an extra fire on the side was made for burning. Today it shows up at the end of words that describe how something is. 「自然|しぜん」 is nature, things as they are by themselves. 「当然|とうぜん」 means naturally, of course. The first kanji, 「全|ぜん」, means whole, and you know it from 「全部|ぜんぶ」, all of it. It also appears in 「安全|あんぜん」, safety, and 「全国|ぜんこく」, the whole country. So this word is the whole state of things, completely. To wrap up, a whole kanji plus a state kanji with hidden fire underneath." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Here is a surprise about the rule your textbook teaches. Today, careful speakers say this word must go with a negative. But language historians point out that around a hundred years ago, famous writers like Natsume Soseki used it with positive sentences too, simply meaning completely. The strict rule that it needs a negative became popular later, in the twentieth century, and many people came to see the positive use as slangy. Then younger speakers brought the positive use back, as in totally fine. So the modern argument about whether it is sloppy is a bit ironic. Both uses have history. For your class and for polite speech, though, stick with the negative pairing, since that is what teachers and most adults expect. To wrap up, the not at all rule is newer than it looks, but it is still the safe choice for you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In conversation this word is often used on its own as a quick answer. Someone asks 「疲れましたか|つかれましたか」, are you tired? You can just say 「全然|ぜんぜん」, not at all, and the negative is understood. It is also very common with understanding. 「全然わかりません|ぜんぜんわかりません」 means I don't understand at all, a phrase every language learner needs. Another one is 「全然大丈夫です|ぜんぜんだいじょうぶです」, it is totally fine, which people say to reassure someone who apologizes. That one sounds natural even to many older speakers, because fine here means no problem at all. Be careful with tone, though. Saying this word too strongly about someone's cooking or gift can sound harsh. In those moments, the softer 「あまり」 is kinder. To wrap up, a one-word answer for not at all, perfect for being clear, but use it gently." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「全|ぜん」 means whole, as in 「全部|ぜんぶ」, and 「然|ぜん」 describes a state, so together they mean completely. With a negative that becomes not at all, a stronger no than 「あまり」.",
      "The four dots under 「然|ぜん」 are squashed fire, since it first meant to burn before being borrowed to mean in that state. It ends words like 「自然|しぜん」 and 「当然|とうぜん」, while 「全|ぜん」 means whole.",
      "About a hundred years ago writers like Natsume Soseki used this word in positive sentences, and the strict negative-only rule came later. Still, the negative pairing is the safe choice for class and polite speech.",
      "On its own this word answers a question with not at all, and 「全然わかりません|ぜんぜんわかりません」 is a phrase every learner needs. About someone's cooking or gift it can sound harsh, so the softer 「あまり」 is kinder."
    ],
    ja: "お{酒|さけ}は[全然|ぜんぜん]{飲みません|のみません}。",
    en: "I don't drink alcohol at all." },
  "たいてい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This adverb tells you how often something happens, and it sits high on the scale, just under 「いつも」, always. It means usually, most of the time. So 「たいてい七時に起きます|たいていしちじにおきます」 is I usually get up at seven. It goes near the start of the sentence, before the verb, like the other frequency words. Let's place it on the full ladder. From most to least, you have 「いつも」, always, then this word, usually, then 「よく」, often, then 「ときどき」, sometimes, then 「あまり」, not much, and 「ぜんぜん」, not at all, with a negative. Unlike the last two, this one goes with a positive verb. It can be written with kanji, but in everyday writing and in your textbook it is usually just kana. A way to remember it: it sounds a bit like tight-tay, so picture a tight daily routine you nearly always stick to. To recap, usually, the second rung from the top of the frequency ladder." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word has a second job that your textbook may not show right away. Put 「の」 after it and it works before a noun, meaning most. 「たいていの人|たいていのひと」 means most people. 「たいていの日本人は|たいていのにほんじんは」 means most Japanese people. That pattern is very common in conversation and in writing. As an adverb, it sits early in the sentence, often right after the topic or the time word. 「週末はたいてい家にいます|しゅうまつはたいていいえにいます」 means on weekends I am usually at home. When written in kanji, it is 「大抵|たいてい」. The first character is big, the same one in 「大学|だいがく」, but you don't need the second character, which is rare. Most people simply write the word in kana. To wrap up, use it alone for usually, or with 「の」 for most of something." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is the word you need for small talk about routines. A classic question is 「週末はたいてい何をしますか|しゅうまつはたいていなにをしますか」, what do you usually do on weekends? A natural answer is 「たいてい友だちと遊びます|たいていともだちとあそびます」, I usually hang out with friends. It is softer than 「いつも」, always, which can sound too strong. If you say you always get up at six, people may tease you about that one morning you didn't. So Japanese speakers often pick this word to leave a little room. Here is a mini dialogue. 「朝ご飯を食べますか|あさごはんをたべますか」, do you eat breakfast? 「ええ、たいてい食べます|ええ、たいていたべます」, yes, I usually do. A common mistake is pairing it with a negative to mean usually not. That is possible, but beginners should stick with positive sentences. To wrap up, a friendly word for describing your normal routine without sounding absolute." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This adverb means usually, the second rung of the frequency ladder just under 「いつも」, and it takes a positive verb. 「たいてい七時に起きます|たいていしちじにおきます」 means I usually get up at seven.",
      "Add 「の」 and it works before a noun to mean most, as in 「たいていの人|たいていのひと」, most people. Alone it means usually, often right after the topic or time word.",
      "It is the go-to word for small talk about routines, like 「週末はたいてい何をしますか|しゅうまつはたいていなにをしますか」. It is softer than 「いつも」, leaving a little room for exceptions."
    ],
    ja: "[たいてい]{七時|しちじ}に{起きます|おきます}。",
    en: "I usually get up at seven." },
  "ちょっと|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This little word means a small amount, a bit. 「日本語をちょっと話します|にほんごをちょっとはなします」 is I speak a little Japanese. It is the casual cousin of 「少し|すこし」, which means the same thing but sounds a bit more careful. You will also hear it on its own in 「ちょっと待って|ちょっとまって」, wait a sec. Here is the big cultural point. Japanese people often avoid a direct no. Instead, they say 「ちょっと」 and let the voice trail off, often with a little intake of breath. That unfinished sentence means that's a bit difficult, which really means no. If you invite someone and hear 「土曜日はちょっと|どようびはちょっと」, they are politely declining Saturday. Don't push for a reason. A way to remember it: think of a tiny pinch between your thumb and finger, just a smidge. To recap, a little bit, and when it trails off, a gentle no." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond meaning a little, this word is a handy tool for getting someone's attention politely. 「ちょっとすみません」 means excuse me a moment, to stop someone on the street or call over a shop clerk. 「ちょっといいですか」 means do you have a minute? You will hear it at work before someone asks a question. It also works as an understatement, the way English says a bit when it really means quite. 「ちょっと高いですね|ちょっとたかいですね」 literally says it's a little expensive, but it often means it's too expensive for me. Japanese people use this softening constantly. 「ちょっと寒い|ちょっとさむい」 might mean it is freezing. On the other hand, a parent calling 「ちょっと!」 sharply means hey, stop that! So the tone carries a lot. To wrap up, a tiny word for grabbing attention, softening complaints, and, said sharply, for hey!" },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The small 「っ」 in the middle matters. It is a tiny pause, a held breath, before the 「と」. If you drop it, you get a non-word, so make sure you feel that little stop. Try clapping the beats. There are three, 「ちょ」, the pause, and 「と」. A whole family of common adverbs shares this same ending, with a small 「っ」 and then 「と」. 「もっと」 means more. 「きっと」 means surely. 「ずっと」 means all along or by far. Practicing them together trains your ear for the pause. A cute variation is 「ちょっぴり」, just a tiny bit, which you will hear in ads and songs. The rhythm of these words is a big part of sounding natural, so it is worth saying them out loud until the pause feels automatic. To wrap up, three beats with a held breath in the middle, just like more, surely, and all along." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word is usually written in kana, but there is an old way to write it with kanji: 「一寸|ちょっと」. The kanji are one, and an old unit of length that was about three centimeters, roughly an inch. So the spelling means one inch, a tiny amount. The kanji were chosen to fit the meaning, not the sound. The same two kanji, read with their regular sound, show up in a famous Japanese folk tale, 「一寸法師|いっすんぼうし」, the one-inch boy. He is a tiny hero who sails down a river in a rice bowl, using a chopstick as a paddle and a needle as a sword, and he defeats an ogre. It is a bit like Tom Thumb in English stories. You don't need to write this word in kanji, but if you see those two characters in an old book or a sign, you will know. To wrap up, a little bit, once written as one inch, like the tiny hero of the folk tale." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the casual cousin of 「少し|すこし」, meaning a little bit. When it trails off, as in 「土曜日はちょっと|どようびはちょっと」, it is a polite no, so don't push for a reason.",
      "「ちょっとすみません」 politely grabs someone's attention. It also softens complaints, so 「ちょっと高いですね|ちょっとたかいですね」 often means too expensive, though a sharp 「ちょっと!」 means hey, stop that.",
      "The small 「っ」 is a held breath, so the word has three beats. It shares that rhythm with 「もっと」, 「きっと」 and 「ずっと」.",
      "Its old kanji spelling, 「一寸|ちょっと」, means one inch, about three centimeters, a tiny amount. The same characters name the tiny folk hero 「一寸法師|いっすんぼうし」, the one-inch boy."
    ],
    ja: "{日本語|にほんご}を[ちょっと]{話します|はなします}。",
    en: "I speak a little Japanese." },
  "ときどき|時々": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「時|とき」 means time. It takes its native reading here. The second character is not really a kanji at all. It is a repeat mark that means write the last one again. So the word is literally time, time. Something that happens at times and times, now and then. When the word doubles, the second 「と」 softens to 「ど」, which is very common in Japanese doubling. You can see the same kanji with the same native reading in 「時|とき」, the word for when or at the time of. With its Chinese-origin reading 「じ」, it shows up in 「時間|じかん」, time or hours, and in clock times like 「三時|さんじ」. It is also the first part of 「時計|とけい」, a clock. On the frequency ladder it sits in the middle. It is less than 「よく」, often, and more than 「あまり」, not much, and it takes a positive verb. To recap, time doubled, so it happens from time to time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The repeat mark in this word is used in many other doubled words, and they follow a pattern. In native Japanese words, the second half often softens its first sound. 「人々|ひとびと」, people in general, turns the second 「ひ」 into 「び」. 「色々|いろいろ」, various, keeps its sound because 「い」 cannot soften. Doubling in Japanese often adds a sense of many, or here and there. Many people, many colors, many times. With Chinese-origin readings, the sound usually does not soften. 「少々|しょうしょう」 means a little, and you will hear it in a polite phrase at every shop and on every phone call, 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, please wait a moment. So the repeat mark is just a shortcut, and how the second half sounds depends on the word. To wrap up, the repeat mark means write it again, and doubling often means many, here and there." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Watch a Japanese weather forecast and you will hear this word in a set phrase: 「晴れ時々曇り|はれときどきくもり」. That means sunny, sometimes cloudy, or sunny with occasional clouds. Forecasters use it to show that the second kind of weather will come and go. You might also see rain mixed in the same way. In conversation, the word fits in the middle of the frequency range, so it is a safe, neutral answer to how often questions. 「ときどき料理をします|ときどきりょうりをします」, I sometimes cook. If you want to say rarely or once in a while, a different word is better, one you will meet later. A small tip: in writing, many people just use kana for this word, because it is simple and friendly. To wrap up, from weather reports to daily chat, this word means now and then." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「時|とき」 means time and the second mark just repeats it, so it is literally time, time, and the second 「と」 softens to 「ど」. It sits in the middle of the frequency ladder with a positive verb.",
      "The repeat mark makes doubled words, which often mean many or here and there, like 「人々|ひとびと」 and 「色々|いろいろ」. Native words often soften the second half, while Chinese-origin ones like 「少々|しょうしょう」 usually don't.",
      "Weather forecasts use 「晴れ時々曇り|はれときどきくもり」, sunny with occasional clouds. In chat it is a safe, neutral middle answer, as in 「ときどき料理をします|ときどきりょうりをします」."
    ],
    ja: "[時々|ときどき]{図書館|としょかん}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I sometimes go to the library." },
  "よく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This adverb comes from 「よい」, good, the same root as 「いい」. Adding 「く」 turns the adjective into an adverb, the way English adds ly. So its first meaning is well, as in doing something well. You hear that in 「よくできました」, well done, the phrase teachers write on homework. Its second meaning is often, a lot. The idea is that doing something well and doing something often go together. So 「よくカフェに行きます|よくカフェにいきます」 is I often go to cafes. On the frequency ladder it sits below 「たいてい」, usually, and above 「ときどき」, sometimes. Context tells you which meaning you have. With a verb about how often, like going or eating, it is usually often. With a verb of ability or understanding, like 「わかる」, it means well. 「よくわかります」 is I understand well. A way to remember it: good things happen often. To recap, from good, meaning well or often." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adverb hides a few everyday expressions. With a negative, it softens what you don't know. 「よくわかりません」 means I don't really understand, or I'm not quite sure. It sounds gentler than a flat I don't know, and Japanese people use it constantly to avoid sounding blunt. It also expresses surprise or admiration about how much someone does something. 「よく食べますね|よくたべますね」 can mean wow, you eat a lot! And when a guest arrives after a long trip, a host might say 「よく来ましたね|よくきましたね」, how nice of you to come all this way. In that phrase it praises the effort. Teachers' 「よくできました」 works the same way. So listen to the whole sentence, because this word can mean often, well, a lot, or great job. To wrap up, often and well, plus a soft not really and a warm well done." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word shows you exactly how Japanese turns adjectives into adverbs. Take an adjective ending in 「い」, change that last 「い」 to 「く」, and it now describes a verb. 「早い|はやい」, early, becomes 「早く|はやく」, as in getting up early. 「高い|たかい」 becomes 「高く|たかく」, high or expensively. This word is the same change, made from the older form 「よい」. That is why it is not spelled with an 「い」 at the start. The same adverb form joins the verb 「なる」, to become. 「よくなる」 means to get better, like a cold improving or your Japanese getting stronger. And 「早くなる|はやくなる」 means to become early. So once you know the rule, one adjective gives you an adverb and a becoming phrase for free. To wrap up, swap the final 「い」 for 「く」, and good becomes well, often, and getting better." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on English. The sound of this word is a bit like yolk, the yellow part of an egg. Picture a diner where you eat breakfast often, almost every day. The cook knows you so well that he cracks your eggs perfectly every time, and the yolks come out round and golden. You eat there often, and he cooks them well. So the egg yolk ties together both meanings of the word, often and well. When you say 「よくカフェに行きます|よくカフェにいきます」, imagine that same golden yolk on your plate at your favorite café. And when the teacher writes 「よくできました」 on your homework, picture a little gold star shaped like a yolk. To wrap up, a perfect yolk, served often and cooked well." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「よい」, good, with 「く」 making an adverb, so it means well or often. With a verb of how often it means often, but with 「わかる」 it means well.",
      "With a negative it softens, so 「よくわかりません」 means I'm not quite sure. It also shows admiration, as in 「よく食べますね|よくたべますね」, and praises effort, as in 「よくできました」.",
      "Change the final 「い」 of an adjective to 「く」 and it becomes an adverb, like 「早い|はやい」 to 「早く|はやく」. This word is that change made from 「よい」, and with 「なる」 it gives 「よくなる」, to get better.",
      "It sounds like yolk, so picture a diner you visit often, where the cook makes your yolks perfectly every time. The yolk ties together both meanings, often and well."
    ],
    ja: "[よく]カフェに{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I often go to cafes." },
  "そうですね|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it into pieces. 「そう」 means so, that way, like that. 「です」 is the polite is. And 「ね」 is the little particle that invites agreement, like right? or isn't it? at the end of an English sentence. Put together, it literally says it is so, isn't it. The magic is that the same phrase does two different jobs, and your tone tells people which one. Said brightly and quickly, it means that's right, I agree. If a friend says it is hot today, you answer 「そうですね」, it sure is. Said slowly and thoughtfully, with the 「ね」 stretched out, it becomes a filler that buys you time, like well, let me see. You will hear Japanese speakers use it constantly while they think of an answer. Don't confuse it with 「そうですか」, with 「か」 at the end, which means oh, is that so, when you just learned something new. A way to remember it: 「ね」 nods along with the other person. To recap, so plus a nod, meaning I agree, or let me think." },
      { title: "Casual versions", text: "This phrase changes shape depending on who you are talking to. With friends, drop 「です」 and say 「そうだね」, yeah, right. Some speakers shorten it even more to 「そうね」, which sounds soft and a little old-fashioned, or 「そっか」 when they just learned something. You can also add 「よ」 to make 「そうですよね」, which means right? I totally agree. It shows stronger agreement, like you were already thinking the same thing. When someone shares a feeling, like this exam was so hard, that version makes you sound warm and understanding. The polite version is fine with teachers, coworkers, and strangers. A small tip: avoid using the casual form with your boss or teacher, because it can sound too familiar. To wrap up, polite with 「です」, casual without it, and extra agreeable with 「よ」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese conversation, listeners talk too. They make small sounds while the other person is speaking to show they are paying attention. This is called 「あいづち」, and this phrase is one of the most common pieces. Others are 「はい」, 「ええ」, and 「へえ」, which means oh really. On the phone, if you stay silent while someone talks, they may ask if you are still there, because silence can feel like you are not listening. English speakers sometimes think all those yes sounds mean agreement, but they often just mean I'm following you. Japanese speakers also use fillers while thinking, like 「えーと」 and 「あのう」, before an answer. Mixing in these small sounds will make your Japanese feel much more natural. To wrap up, keep the conversation going with small agreeing sounds, and this phrase is the classic one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is so plus 「です」 plus the agreeing particle 「ね」. Said brightly it means I agree, but with the 「ね」 stretched slowly it becomes a let-me-think filler, unlike 「そうですか」, oh, is that so.",
      "With friends drop 「です」 and say 「そうだね」, but avoid that with your teacher or boss. Adding 「よ」 to make 「そうですよね」 shows warmer, stronger agreement.",
      "Listeners in Japanese keep making small sounds called 「あいづち」, and this phrase is the classic one. Those sounds often just mean I'm following you, not that the listener agrees."
    ],
    ja: "[そうですね]、テニスはどうですか。",
    en: "Let me see, how about tennis?" },
  "でも|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word starts a new sentence and flips what came before it. The first sentence says one thing, and then 「でも」 says, however, here is the other side. For example, 「日本語はむずかしいです。でも、おもしろいです|にほんごはむずかしいです。でも、おもしろいです」 means Japanese is hard. But it is interesting. Notice that it sits at the very start of the second sentence, usually with a little pause or comma after it. That is the main difference from English, where but often joins two parts in one sentence. To connect two clauses inside a single sentence, Japanese uses a different tool, which you will meet later as 「けど」 or 「が」 tacked onto the end of the first clause. 「でも」 is fine in both polite and casual speech. Later you will also see it used after a noun to mean even or something like, as in coffee or something, so listen for where it sits. A way to remember it: picture a demo that looks great, but then something breaks. To recap, a sentence opener that means but, flipping the story." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "After a noun, this little word changes job completely. Glued to a question word, it means any at all. 「いつでも」 is anytime. 「なんでも」 is anything. 「だれでも」 is anybody, and 「どこでも」 is anywhere. So 「なんでも食べます|なんでもたべます」 means I eat anything. Glued to an ordinary noun, it softens an invitation. 「コーヒーでも飲みませんか|コーヒーでものみませんか」 means shall we have coffee or something? The or something takes the pressure off, so the other person can easily suggest tea instead. It can also mean even. 「子どもでもわかります|こどもでもわかります」 means even a child understands. The trick is position. At the very start of a sentence, followed by a pause, it means but. Stuck right onto a word, it means any, even, or or something. To wrap up, listen for where it sits, and the meaning follows." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In conversation this word often starts a reply, and it can sound a little like pushing back. Say a friend compliments your Japanese. A very typical modest answer is 「でも、まだまだです」, but I still have a long way to go. It is also how people gently disagree without saying no. If someone suggests a picnic, you might hear 「でも、雨ですよ|でも、あめですよ」, but it is raining. There is even a teasing phrase, 「でもでもだって」, for someone who answers every suggestion with but, but, because. Parents say it to whining kids. One tip on tone. This word is casual to neutral. In writing, speeches, or formal reports, people switch to 「しかし」 or 「ですが」, which feel more businesslike. In a polite chat with your teacher, though, this one is completely fine. To wrap up, a soft but for replies, best saved for speech." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Where did this word come from? It is usually explained as a shortened 「それでも」, which means even so. Take that apart and you find three pieces. 「それ」 is that. 「で」 is a form of the linking verb is. And 「も」 is the particle meaning also or even. So the full phrase is even being that way. Over time people dropped the front, and the short form became an everyday conjunction. You can still use the long version, and it feels a bit stronger, like nevertheless. 「それでも、行きます|それでも、いきます」 means even so, I'm going. You will meet the same 「で」 plus 「も」 later in the pattern for even if, as in 「雨でも行きます|あめでもいきます」, I'll go even if it rains. Once you see the pieces, the word feels less like a random sound. To wrap up, even being that, worn down to a quick but." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This word opens a new sentence and flips the one before it, as in 「日本語はむずかしいです。でも、おもしろいです|にほんごはむずかしいです。でも、おもしろいです」. To join two clauses inside one sentence, Japanese uses 「けど」 or 「が」 instead.",
      "Glued to a question word it means any, as in 「なんでも」 and 「いつでも」, and after a noun it softens an invitation, as in 「コーヒーでも飲みませんか|コーヒーでものみませんか」. At the start of a sentence with a pause, it means but.",
      "Replies often start with it, as in the modest 「でも、まだまだです」 after a compliment. It is casual to neutral, so formal writing switches to 「しかし」 or 「ですが」.",
      "It is usually explained as a shortened 「それでも」, even so, made of that, the linking 「で」 and 「も」. The same pieces return later in 「雨でも行きます|あめでもいきます」, I'll go even if it rains."
    ],
    ja: "[でも]、{今日|きょう}は{行きません|いきません}。",
    en: "But I won't go today." },
  "どうですか|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「どう」 is the question word how, in what way. You will see it again in 「どうして」, why, literally how come, and in 「どうやって」, how, by what means. Add 「ですか」, the polite is plus the question marker, and you get how is it? This phrase does two jobs. First, it asks for someone's opinion or how something went. 「日本の生活はどうですか|にほんのせいかつはどうですか」 means how is life in Japan? Second, it makes a suggestion or an offer. 「コーヒーはどうですか」 means how about some coffee? and 「明日はどうですか|あしたはどうですか」 means how about tomorrow? That second use is super handy for making plans. When you want to be extra polite, for example with a customer or a guest, swap in the fancy version 「いかがですか」, which means the same thing. In casual speech with friends, just say 「どう」 with rising intonation. To recap, how plus is it, for asking opinions and making suggestions." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The question word inside this phrase belongs to a neat set of four. 「こう」 means like this, in this way. 「そう」 means like that, the way you said. 「ああ」 means like that over there, the way everyone knows. And 「どう」 asks in which way, or how. It is the same this, that, that over there, which pattern you know from 「これ、それ、あれ、どれ」 and 「ここ、そこ、あそこ、どこ」. So you already know the answer to this phrase in its family. When someone asks how is it, a very natural reply is 「そうですね」, well, let me see, built on the same that way word. And 「ああ、そうですか」, oh, is that so, uses the that way word too. Learning them as a set means one pattern unlocks four useful words. To wrap up, one member of the 「こそあど」 family, the one that asks how." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Change the tense and the phrase gets even more useful. 「どうでしたか」 means how was it? It is perfect after a trip, a test, or a weekend. 「旅行はどうでしたか|りょこうはどうでしたか」, how was your trip? 「テストはどうでしたか」, how was the test? Notice the topic comes first with 「は」, then the question. Later in your book you will meet a pattern for giving advice that ends with this same phrase. You take the past short form of a verb, add 「ら」, and then ask how about it. 「薬を飲んだらどうですか|くすりをのんだらどうですか」 means why don't you take some medicine? It sounds like friendly advice rather than an order. With friends, you can chop it down to 「飲んだら|のんだら」 with a rising tone. To wrap up, past tense for how was it, and a pattern for why don't you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "How do you answer this phrase? If it asks your opinion, use an adjective. 「おいしいです」, it's delicious. 「楽しいです|たのしいです」, it's fun. 「ちょっとむずかしいです」, it's a bit hard. If it is an invitation, like how about Saturday, saying yes is easy: 「いいですね」, sounds good. Saying no is where Japanese gets subtle. People rarely say a flat no. Instead you say 「土曜日はちょっと|どようびはちょっと」, Saturday is a little, and trail off. Everyone understands that means it does not work. Here is a mini scene. A host offers tea. 「お茶はどうですか|おちゃはどうですか」. You answer 「ありがとうございます。いただきます」, thank you, I'll have some. To wrap up, answer with an adjective, accept with sounds good, and decline by trailing off." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「どう」 means how, and adding 「ですか」 gives how is it, for asking opinions. It also makes suggestions, as in 「コーヒーはどうですか」, and 「いかがですか」 is the extra polite version.",
      "「どう」 belongs to the set 「こう」, 「そう」, 「ああ」, 「どう」, like 「これ、それ、あれ、どれ」. So a natural reply, 「そうですね」, uses the same family's that-way word.",
      "The past 「どうでしたか」 asks how was it, as in 「旅行はどうでしたか|りょこうはどうでしたか」. Later, a past form plus 「ら」 plus this phrase gives friendly advice, as in 「薬を飲んだらどうですか|くすりをのんだらどうですか」.",
      "Answer an opinion question with an adjective and accept an invitation with 「いいですね」. To decline, trail off as in 「土曜日はちょっと|どようびはちょっと」 rather than saying a flat no."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[どうですか]。",
    en: "How about tomorrow?" },
  "ええ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a yes, but a softer and more relaxed one than 「はい」. Think of the difference between yes and yeah, though 「ええ」 is still perfectly polite in everyday conversation. You will hear it a lot when someone is chatting comfortably, agreeing with what was just said, or answering a simple question. 「行きますか|いきますか」, are you going? 「ええ、行きます|ええ、いきます」, yeah, I'm going. In formal situations, like answering a teacher in class, a job interview, or responding when your name is called, stick with 「はい」. That one is crisp and clear. Also, Japanese people often make an 「ええ」 sound while listening, to show they are following along, so it is not always a firm agreement. It can just mean I hear you. Be careful with the tone. A long, rising 「えええ」 is a surprised what?! And never mix it up with 「いいえ」, which is no. A way to remember it: 「ええ」 sounds like a relaxed yeah. To recap, a soft, conversational yes." },
      { title: "The yes ladder", text: "Japanese has a whole ladder of yes words, and each has a matching no. At the top, crisp and formal, is 「はい」, with 「いいえ」 for no. In the middle is this word, relaxed but still polite, paired with a softer no, 「いや」. At the bottom, for friends and family, is 「うん」, yeah, with 「ううん」 for nope. Be careful with those last two, because they differ mostly in tone and length. The yes is short and drops. The no is two beats with a little bump. Picking the right rung is like choosing between yes, yeah, and uh huh in English. With a teacher in class, stay at the top. Chatting politely with a neighbor, the middle rung fits nicely. With your best friend, the bottom rung is natural, and the top one can sound stiff. To wrap up, three rungs of yes, and this one sits comfortably in the middle." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Length and tone change this sound a lot, so let us tune your ear. Two beats with a calm, falling tone is the yes you are learning. A short, sharp 「え」 with a rising tone means huh? or pardon? 「えっ」 with a tiny stop at the end is a gasp, what, really? A long, wavering 「えー」 while someone looks at the ceiling is just thinking, like um. And a long, stretched 「えーっ」 that rises is a complaint, aww, no way. Kids say it when told to clean their room. So a single vowel carries five different messages. The key for you is to hold the yes for two clear beats and let it fall gently. Do not confuse it with 「いい」, good, which uses a different vowel. To wrap up, two calm, falling beats mean yes, and the other versions are reactions." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a soft, relaxed yes, more like yeah, but still polite in everyday chat. In class, interviews or when your name is called, stick with the crisp 「はい」.",
      "Japanese has a yes ladder, with 「はい」 at the top, this word in the middle paired with 「いや」, and 「うん」 for friends. This one fits polite chat with a neighbor.",
      "Two calm beats with a falling tone mean yes, while a short rising 「え」 means huh and 「えっ」 is a gasp. A long 「えー」 is thinking, and a rising 「えーっ」 is a complaint."
    ],
    ja: "[ええ]、{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "Yes, I'll go." },
  "きく|聞く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji 「聞|き」. The outside is 「門|もん」, a gate, and inside the gate is 「耳|みみ」, an ear. Picture an ear pressed up against a gate, listening to what is happening on the other side. In this verb the kanji takes its native reading, 「き」. With its Chinese-origin reading 「ぶん」, it appears in 「新聞|しんぶん」, newspaper. 「新|しん」 is new, the same kanji as in 「新しい|あたらしい」, so a newspaper is literally new things you hear about. This verb has two meanings. The first is to listen or to hear, like 「音楽を聞きます|おんがくをききます」, I listen to music. The second is to ask, like asking a teacher a question. In that case the person you ask takes 「に」. Asking is just how you go and hear an answer, so the two meanings fit together. It is a u-verb, so the polite form is 「聞きます|ききます」. To recap, an ear at the gate, for listening and for asking." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb has a close partner, 「聞こえる|きこえる」, which means to be audible, to be able to hear. It works just like the seeing pair 「見る|みる」 and 「見える|みえる」. The first verb is what you choose to do. The partner is what reaches you whether you try or not. So 「音楽を聞きます|おんがくをききます」 is I listen to music, but 「音が聞こえます|おとがきこえます」 is I can hear a sound. On the phone, 「聞こえますか|きこえますか」 means can you hear me? You will also see a fancier kanji, 「聴く|きく」, with the same reading. It is used for listening carefully and on purpose, like to a concert or a lecture. Your textbook's spelling is the everyday one and is always safe. To wrap up, you listen with this verb, and sounds reach you with its partner." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a u-verb ending in 「く」, so its te-form follows the rule for 「く」. The 「く」 turns into 「い」 and you add 「て」, giving 「聞いて|きいて」. The past is 「聞いた|きいた」. Compare 「書く|かく」, which becomes 「書いて|かいて」. The one famous exception in this group is 「行く|いく」, which becomes 「行って|いって」 instead, so don't let that one confuse you. With the te-form you can make polite requests. 「聞いてください|きいてください」 means please listen. When the verb means ask, the person you ask is marked with 「に」, and the thing you ask about with 「を」. 「先生に名前を聞きました|せんせいになまえをききました」 means I asked the teacher's name. Later, 「聞いてもいいですか|きいてもいいですか」 means may I ask? To wrap up, a regular 「く」 verb, with 「に」 for the person asked." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this verb everywhere. A lost tourist says 「道を聞きましょう|みちをききましょう」, let's ask for directions, literally ask the road. Before a question at work, people say 「ちょっと聞いてもいいですか|ちょっときいてもいいですか」, can I ask you something quickly? Among friends, an excited 「聞いて聞いて|きいてきいて」 means listen, listen, I have news! And a frustrated 「聞いてない|きいてない」 means nobody told me, I never heard about this. That last one is a common complaint when plans change. One mistake English speakers make is using this verb for ask a favor or ask someone to do something. That needs a different verb, 「頼む|たのむ」. This one is for questions and information. To wrap up, listen for news, ask for information, and complain when nobody told you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「聞|き」 is an ear, 「耳|みみ」, pressed against a gate, 「門|もん」, listening. The verb means both to listen and to ask, with the person you ask taking 「に」.",
      "Its partner 「聞こえる|きこえる」 is for sounds that reach you on their own, just like 「見る|みる」 and 「見える|みえる」. So 「音楽を聞きます|おんがくをききます」 is listening on purpose, while 「音が聞こえます|おとがきこえます」 is a sound you can hear.",
      "As a regular 「く」 verb its te-form is 「聞いて|きいて」, unlike the exception 「行って|いって」. When it means ask, the person takes 「に」 and the thing takes 「を」, as in 「先生に名前を聞きました|せんせいになまえをききました」.",
      "You hear it in 「道を聞きましょう|みちをききましょう」, the excited 「聞いて聞いて|きいてきいて」, and the complaint 「聞いてない|きいてない」, nobody told me. Asking someone to do a favor takes 「頼む|たのむ」 instead."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}{音楽|おんがく}を[聞きます|ききます|きく]。",
    en: "I listen to music every night." },
  "はやい|早い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「早|はや」 is small and simple. On top is 「日|ひ」, the sun, and underneath is a shape that looks like a cross or a post. A way to remember it: picture the sun just peeking over a post at dawn, very early in the day. That image is a hook, not the official history of the kanji. In this word it uses its native reading, 「はや」. It means early in time, like getting up early or arriving early. Here is a common learner trap. Japanese has another word with exactly the same sound that means fast in speed, and it is written with a different kanji, 「速い|はやい」. Spoken aloud they sound identical, so context does the work. Early morning uses this one; a fast car uses the other. Your vocab list even lists 「早く|はやく」 as early or fast, because the adverb form gets both spellings. The adverb shows up in sentences like 「早く起きます|はやくおきます」, I get up early. It also appears in 「おはようございます」, good morning, which is built on this same word, meaning it is early. To recap, sunrise kanji for early, not fast." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an i-adjective, so it follows the regular pattern. The negative is 「早くない|はやくない」, not early. The past is 「早かった|はやかった」, it was early. Joining it to another phrase uses 「早くて|はやくて」. Swap the final 「い」 for 「く」 and you get the adverb, which describes how an action happens. 「早く寝ます|はやくねます」 means I go to bed early. On its own, the adverb is a common command. A parent at the door shouts 「早く|はやく」, which means hurry up! A softer request is 「早く来てください|はやくきてください」, please come quickly. With 「なる」, the adverb shows change. 「早くなりました|はやくなりました」 means it got earlier. To wrap up, regular i-adjective forms, and the adverb doubles as hurry up." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The opposite is 「遅い|おそい」, and it is handy because one word covers both late and slow. Japanese splits early and fast into two spellings, but the opposite stays as one. 「遅く起きます|おそくおきます」 is I get up late. The same kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「そう」 appears in 「早朝|そうちょう」, early morning, a word you will see in news and on signs. Two everyday native words also use it. 「早口|はやくち」 is fast talking, literally an early mouth, and 「早口言葉|はやくちことば」 are tongue twisters. 「素早い|すばやい」 means nimble, quick on your feet. In those last two, the meaning leans toward speed, which shows how close early and fast are. To wrap up, one opposite for late and slow, and a small family of quick words." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When a friend shows up before the meeting time, people often say 「早いですね|はやいですね」, you're early! It is friendly, not a complaint. If something feels premature, you say 「まだ早い|まだはやい」, it's still too early, maybe about going home from a party or getting married. A very warm phrase for someone who is sick is 「早く元気になってください|はやくげんきになってください」, please get well soon, literally become healthy quickly. Here is a mini scene. Your host mother sees you up at six. 「早いですね|はやいですね」. You answer 「ええ、今日はテストがあります|ええ、きょうはテストがあります」, yeah, I have a test today. Remember the kanji choice. Early in time takes this spelling, and moving quickly takes the other one. To wrap up, early arrivals, too early, and get well soon." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Picture the sun, 「日|ひ」, peeking over a post at dawn, so this spelling means early in time. Fast in speed sounds identical but is written 「速い|はやい」.",
      "As a regular i-adjective it gives 「早くない|はやくない」 and 「早かった|はやかった」. Its adverb 「早く|はやく」 describes actions, and shouted alone it means hurry up.",
      "The opposite 「遅い|おそい」 covers both late and slow in one word. The kanji also gives 「早朝|そうちょう」, early morning, and 「早口|はやくち」, fast talking.",
      "「早いですね|はやいですね」 is a friendly you're early, and 「まだ早い|まだはやい」 means it's still too early. 「早く元気になってください|はやくげんきになってください」 means get well soon."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[早く|はやく|はやい]{起きます|おきます}。",
    en: "Today I will get up early." },
  "どようび|土曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「土|ど」 means earth or soil, here with its Chinese-origin reading. 「曜|よう」 is a kanji you will mostly see in days of the week. And 「日|び」 means day, with a sound change from 「ひ」 to 「び」. The days of the week in Japanese come from an old system that named the seven days after the sun, the moon, and five planets. Each planet has a name built on one of the classical elements. The planet Saturn is called 「土星|どせい」, the earth star. So Saturday is earth day, and it lines up with Saturn day in English too, which is nice. Notice how the whole week fits this pattern. 「月曜日|げつようび」 is moon day, Monday. 「火曜日|かようび」 is fire day, Tuesday. 「水曜日|すいようび」 is water day, Wednesday. 「木曜日|もくようび」 is wood or tree day, Thursday. A way to remember it: Saturday is for working in your garden, digging in the earth. To recap, earth day, Saturn day, Saturday." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "For a long time, Saturday was not a day off in Japan. Many offices and schools worked half a day on Saturday morning, then everyone went home for lunch. Public schools held Saturday classes for decades. In the early nineteen nineties, schools started giving some Saturdays off, and by two thousand two every Saturday was free. Adults who grew up before that still remember racing home on Saturday noon to watch TV. Today, though, many kids go to 「塾|じゅく」, cram school, or club practice on Saturday anyway, so the day is still busy. Sports clubs at school, called 「部活|ぶかつ」, often meet on weekends too. To wrap up, Saturday once meant half a school day, and for many students it is still not completely free." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There is a summer word with exactly the same sound, written 「土用|どよう」. It refers to periods of about eighteen days before each change of season, in the old calendar. The famous one is in midsummer. On a day in that period called 「土用の丑の日|どようのうしのひ」, the day of the ox, people across Japan eat grilled eel, 「うなぎ」, for energy in the heat. Shops put up big banners with the word for eel. It is not connected to Saturday at all. It just shares the earth kanji and the sound. So if you hear people talking about eel and this sound in July, they don't mean the weekend. To wrap up, same sound, but one is Saturday and the other is eel season." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In everyday speech people often drop the last part and just say 「土曜|どよう」. On calendars and schedules it shrinks to the single kanji 「土|ど」 in a little box. Saturday and Sunday together are 「土日|どにち」, and that is the most common way to say the weekend in casual talk. The more formal word is 「週末|しゅうまつ」, the end of the week. When you say something happens on Saturday, use 「に」. 「土曜日に映画を見ます|どようびにえいがをみます」 means I'll see a movie on Saturday. To make it the topic, use 「は」, as in 「土曜日は暇です|どようびはひまです」, I'm free on Saturday. Here is a mini dialogue. 「土日は何をしますか|どにちはなにをしますか」. 「土曜日にアルバイトをします|どようびにアルバイトをします」. To wrap up, short forms for chat, and 「に」 for when." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「土|ど」 is earth, so this is earth day, matching Saturn, 「土星|どせい」, just like Saturn day in English. Every weekday follows the same pattern of the sun, moon and five planets.",
      "For decades Japanese schools and many offices worked Saturday mornings, and only by two thousand two was every Saturday off. Even now many kids spend it at 「塾|じゅく」 or club practice.",
      "The sound twin 「土用|どよう」 is an old seasonal period, famous for 「土用の丑の日|どようのうしのひ」, when people eat eel in midsummer. It just shares the earth kanji and sound, not Saturday.",
      "People shorten it to 「土曜|どよう」, and 「土日|どにち」 is the casual word for the weekend. Use 「に」 for when, as in 「土曜日に映画を見ます|どようびにえいがをみます」."
    ],
    ja: "[土曜日|どようび]に{映画|えいが}を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch a movie on Saturday." },
  "にちようび|日曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「日|にち」 is the sun, here with its Chinese-origin reading. 「曜|よう」 is the kanji you will mostly see in days of the week. And the last 「日|び」 is day, with its native reading softened from 「ひ」 to 「び」. So this word uses the same kanji twice, with two different readings. The first one is the sun, and the second one is the day. Sun day: a perfect match with English Sunday. This is no accident, because both Japanese and English day names come from the same ancient idea of naming the days after the sun, the moon, and the five visible planets. The partner day is 「月曜日|げつようび」, moon day, which matches Monday. The same sun kanji with the reading 「に」 also starts 「日本|にほん」, Japan, which means the origin of the sun, the land of the rising sun. And with the reading 「にち」 again, it shows up in 「毎日|まいにち」, every day. To recap, sun plus day of the week, Sunday, the easiest day name to remember." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Every day of the week shares the middle kanji, 「曜|よう」, so it is worth a close look. It is a big, busy character. On the left is 「日|ひ」, the sun, which tells you it has to do with light. On the right is a crowded piece with 「羽|はね」, feathers, sitting on top of a shape that often marks birds. That right side mainly supplies the sound. The kanji means to shine, and in the old Chinese system it referred to the seven shining bodies in the sky, the sun, the moon, and the five planets you can see with the naked eye. Each day of the week is named after one of them. You will almost never see this kanji outside day names, so you can think of it as the week kanji. To wrap up, sun on the left, and a meaning of shining bodies that name the week." },
      { title: "Many readings of sun", text: "The sun kanji, 「日|ひ」, has more readings than almost any other kanji you will learn, and this word shows two at once. Here is a tour. With the native reading 「ひ」, it means sun or day, as in 「この日|このひ」, this day. Softened to 「び」, it ends every day of the week. Read 「にち」, it begins this word and ends 「毎日|まいにち」, every day. Shortened to 「に」, it starts 「日本|にほん」, Japan. In 「休日|きゅうじつ」, a day off, it is 「じつ」. And in dates it becomes 「か」, as in 「三日|みっか」, the third. The first of the month is a special one, 「一日|ついたち」. Don't try to memorize these as rules. Learn each word as a whole, and the readings will settle in. To wrap up, one kanji, many readings, and this word shows off two of them." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan even has a name for the Sunday evening blues. A long-running family cartoon called 「サザエさん」 has aired on Sunday evenings since nineteen sixty nine. For many people, its theme song means the weekend is over and work or school starts tomorrow. So the gloomy feeling on Sunday night is jokingly called 「サザエさん症候群|サザエさんしょうこうぐん」, the Sazae-san syndrome. Another fun word is 「日曜大工|にちようだいく」, a Sunday carpenter. It means do it yourself home projects, and big home improvement stores use it in their ads. Sunday is also a popular day for family outings, shopping, and visiting grandparents. To wrap up, Sunday is for family and fixing shelves, until the cartoon theme song reminds you Monday is coming." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The sun kanji appears twice with two readings, first 「にち」 for sun and then 「び」 for day, so it is literally sun day. It matches Sunday because both come from naming days after the sun, moon and planets.",
      "Every day name shares 「曜|よう」, with the sun on its left, meaning to shine. It refers to the seven shining bodies in the sky that name the week, so think of it as the week kanji.",
      "The sun kanji has many readings, from 「ひ」 and 「び」 to 「にち」, 「に」, 「じつ」 and 「か」, and this word shows two. Learn each word as a whole rather than memorizing rules.",
      "The Sunday-night blues are jokingly called 「サザエさん症候群|サザエさんしょうこうぐん」, after a cartoon whose theme song signals the weekend's end. 「日曜大工|にちようだいく」, a Sunday carpenter, means DIY home projects."
    ],
    ja: "[日曜日|にちようび]に{家|いえ}で{勉強します|べんきょうします}。",
    en: "I study at home on Sunday." },
  "おどる|踊る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji 「踊|おど」. On the left side is the foot radical, the same piece you see at the bottom left of 「足|あし」, foot or leg. Kanji with that piece usually have something to do with feet or stepping. In this verb the kanji uses its native reading, 「おど」. A way to remember it: feet moving to music, that is dancing. It is a u-verb, so the polite form is 「踊ります|おどります」. Drop the 「る」 and you get the noun 「踊り|おどり」, a dance. A great cultural note. Every summer, Japanese towns hold festivals for 「お盆|おぼん」, the time when families honor their ancestors. At these festivals people dance 「盆踊り|ぼんおどり」, slow circle dances around a tall stage with drummers, often in light summer kimono. It is a big part of 「お祭り|おまつり」, festival season, and anyone can join in, even beginners. Don't confuse it with the loanword 「ダンス」, which you'd use for modern styles. To recap, a foot radical for moving your feet, the native verb for dancing." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a handy rule for spotting verb groups. A verb can only be a ru-verb if the sound before 「る」 is an i or e sound, like 「見る|みる」 or 「食べる|たべる」. This verb has an o sound before the 「る」, so it must be a u-verb. That means the te-form doubles a little 「っ」, giving 「踊って|おどって」, and the past is 「踊った|おどった」. The negative changes the 「る」 to 「ら」, giving 「踊らない|おどらない」. Some useful patterns follow. 「踊りましょう|おどりましょう」, let's dance. 「踊りに行きます|おどりにいきます」, I'm going dancing. And later, 「踊っている|おどっている」, someone is dancing right now. To wrap up, an o sound before 「る」 means u-verb, so the little 「っ」 appears." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has two native verbs for dancing. This one suggests stepping and jumping, feet moving to a beat. The other, 「舞う|まう」, suggests graceful turning and floating, like a classical dancer, or leaves and snow drifting in the air. Its kanji, 「舞|ぶ」, is the middle of 「歌舞伎|かぶき」, so you already know it. The noun from this verb leads to some surprises. A 「踊り子|おどりこ」 is a dancer, often in old stories. And a 「踊り場|おどりば」, literally a dancing place, is the landing in the middle of a staircase. When your heart dances with excitement, the sound is the same but the kanji changes to 「躍る|おどる」, which means to leap. To wrap up, stepping versus floating, and one surprising word for a staircase landing." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "One of Japan's biggest dance festivals is 「阿波踊り|あわおどり」 in Tokushima, on the island of Shikoku. Every August, huge groups dance through the streets to drums, flutes, and the twang of the 「三味線|しゃみせん」. The dancers chant a famous song that goes 「踊る阿呆に見る阿呆|おどるあほうにみるあほう」, fools who dance and fools who watch. The next line says that if you are a fool either way, you might as well dance. It is a perfect invitation for shy people to join in. Many towns have their own local dance style, and people practice for weeks. If you visit Japan in summer, look for a festival near you. To wrap up, if you are going to be a fool anyway, you might as well dance." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「踊|おど」 has the foot radical on its left, so think of feet moving to music. Its noun 「踊り|おどり」 gives 「盆踊り|ぼんおどり」, the summer festival circle dance.",
      "An o sound before 「る」 means it must be a u-verb. So the te-form doubles a little 「っ」, 「踊って|おどって」, and the negative is 「踊らない|おどらない」.",
      "This verb is stepping to a beat, while 「舞う|まう」 is graceful floating, like the 「舞|ぶ」 in 「歌舞伎|かぶき」. Surprisingly, a 「踊り場|おどりば」 is a staircase landing.",
      "At 「阿波踊り|あわおどり」 in Tokushima, dancers chant 「踊る阿呆に見る阿呆|おどるあほうにみるあほう」, fools who dance and fools who watch. Since you are a fool either way, you might as well dance."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[踊りました|おどりました|おどる]。",
    en: "I danced with my friends." },
  "ピアノをひく|ピアノを弾く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces here. 「ピアノ」 comes from Italian pianoforte, shortened just like piano in English. The sounds fit into Japanese easily. The verb is 「弾く|ひく」. The kanji 「弾|ひ」 has 「弓|ゆみ」, a bow, on its left side, the kind of bow used to shoot arrows. Plucking a bowstring is a good image to hold onto, and this verb covers playing instruments where you pluck, strike, or press: piano, guitar, violin. Here it takes its native reading. With its Chinese-origin reading 「だん」, the same kanji means a bullet, something shot out, which fits the bow image too. The big mistake for English speakers is using 「する」 or 「あそぶ」 for play. For instruments you pluck or press, Japanese uses this specific verb. Wind instruments, like the flute, use 「吹く|ふく」, to blow, instead. Be careful too, because another 「ひく」, written differently, means to pull, which is the one in 「かぜをひく」, to catch a cold. They sound the same but are unrelated. A way to remember it: a pianist pressing keys like plucking strings. To recap, the plucking verb for playing the piano." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a u-verb ending in 「く」, so the te-form is 「弾いて|ひいて」 and the past is 「弾いた|ひいた」. Asking someone to play is 「弾いてください|ひいてください」. Later you will learn the potential form, can do. For this verb it is 「弾ける|ひける」, and the particle usually switches from 「を」 to 「が」. So 「ピアノが弾けます|ピアノがひけます」 means I can play the piano. That particle change is a common stumbling point, so notice it early. There is also a nice compound, 「弾き語り|ひきがたり」, which means singing while accompanying yourself on an instrument, like a singer at a piano. To ask about someone's hobbies, try 「ピアノを弾きますか|ピアノをひきますか」. To wrap up, a regular 「く」 verb, and with can play, the particle becomes 「が」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Piano lessons are one of the most popular after-school activities for Japanese children. These lessons and classes are called 「習い事|ならいごと」, and piano, swimming, and calligraphy are classic choices. Japan is also home to two of the world's best-known piano makers, Yamaha and Kawai. Both started in the city of Hamamatsu, which calls itself a city of music. Many schools have a piano in every classroom, and a student often plays for the class chorus contests that schools hold every year. You will also find street pianos in some train stations, where anyone can sit down and play. To wrap up, piano is a big part of growing up in Japan, from lessons to school choirs." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the verb. Picture a giant piano with keys as big as steps. Every time you press a key, a little hiccup pops out of the piano. Hic! Hic! Each note is a hiccup, so to play the piano is to make it go hic. That sound should remind you of 「ひく」. Now add the kanji. Imagine the pianist pulling back an archer's bow, the 「弓|ゆみ」 on the left side of 「弾|ひ」, and twanging the string between notes. Hiccup, twang, hiccup. Silly, but it ties the sound, the meaning, and the bow shape together. Just remember that this is a trick, not the history of the word. To wrap up, a hiccuping piano and a twanging bow help you remember the verb for playing." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「弾|ひ」 has a bow, 「弓|ゆみ」, on its left, so the verb is for plucking, striking or pressing instruments like piano and guitar. Don't use 「する」 or 「あそぶ」, and wind instruments take 「吹く|ふく」.",
      "It is a regular 「く」 verb, so you get 「弾いて|ひいて」 and 「弾いた|ひいた」. In the potential form the particle switches to 「が」, as in 「ピアノが弾けます|ピアノがひけます」.",
      "Piano is a classic 「習い事|ならいごと」 for Japanese kids, and Yamaha and Kawai both started in Hamamatsu. Students often play piano for the yearly class chorus contests.",
      "Picture a giant piano that hiccups on every key, hic, to recall 「ひく」. Add a pianist twanging the archer's bow on the left of 「弾|ひ」 between notes."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[ピアノを弾きます|ピアノをひきます|ピアノをひく]。",
    en: "I play the piano every day." },
  "ギター|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This loanword comes from English guitar, which itself came from Spanish guitarra. Look at how the sound was squeezed into Japanese. The g and the i become 「ギ」. The t sound becomes 「タ」. And the er at the end, which many English speakers hardly pronounce, turns into a long a sound, written with the long line, 「ー」. That is a very common pattern. Lots of English words ending in er do the same thing, like 「コンピューター」, computer. An electric guitar is 「エレキギター」, from electric, shortened. For playing it, Japanese doesn't use 「する」. You use 「弾く|ひく」, to pluck, the same verb as for the piano. So I play the guitar is 「ギターを弾きます|ギターをひきます」. A guitar is a 「楽器|がっき」, musical instrument. That word shares the kanji 「楽|がく」 with 「音楽|おんがく」, music. 「楽|がく」 is music or fun, and 「器|き」 is a tool or vessel, so an instrument is a music tool. To recap, English guitar with a long vowel at the end, played with 「弾く|ひく」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how people actually talk about the guitar. To say you play, use 「弾く|ひく」. To say you are taking lessons, 「ギターを習っています|ギターをならっています」, I'm learning the guitar. To say you can play, 「ギターが弾けます|ギターがひけます」. A guitarist is 「ギタリスト」. An acoustic guitar is 「アコースティックギター」, which is long, so people shorten it to 「アコギ」. Japanese loves to chop long loanwords down to a few beats. A bass guitar is just 「ベース」. Japanese school music clubs and teen bands are hugely popular, and many anime feature high school bands. Here is a mini dialogue. 「趣味は何ですか|しゅみはなんですか」, what are your hobbies? 「ギターです。毎日弾きます|ギターです。まいにちひきます」, the guitar, I play every day. To wrap up, use the plucking verb, and enjoy the short forms." },
      { title: "Instrument cousins", text: "The guitar has plenty of cousins in Japanese, and most Western ones are loanwords. 「バイオリン」 is violin, and 「ドラム」 is drums. 「トランペット」 is trumpet. But Japan also has its own traditional string instrument, the 「三味線|しゃみせん」, with three strings and a long neck. Its name means three flavor strings. It is plucked with a big flat pick called a 「ばち」, so it takes the same plucking verb as the guitar. Another is the 「琴|こと」, a long wooden zither laid on the floor. For drums, Japanese uses a different verb, 「たたく」, to hit. The traditional drum is the 「太鼓|たいこ」. So you pluck strings and hit drums. To wrap up, Western instruments come as loanwords, and Japan's own string instruments get plucked just like the guitar." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English guitar, with the final er turning into a long vowel, 「ー」, like 「コンピューター」. You play it with the plucking verb, 「ギターを弾きます|ギターをひきます」, not 「する」.",
      "Use 「弾く|ひく」 to play, 「習っています|ならっています」 for lessons, and 「ギターが弾けます|ギターがひけます」 for can play. Japanese chops long loanwords down, so an acoustic guitar becomes 「アコギ」.",
      "Western instruments come as loanwords, while Japan's 「三味線|しゃみせん」 and 「琴|こと」 get plucked with the same verb as the guitar. Drums like the 「太鼓|たいこ」 take 「たたく」, to hit."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[ギター]を{弾きます|ひきます}。",
    en: "My older brother plays the guitar." },
  "くすりをのむ|薬を飲む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「薬|くすり」 is medicine, with its native reading. Look at its shape. On top is the grass radical, the little pair of crosses that marks plants, like in 「花|はな」, flower. Under it is 「楽|らく」, the same kanji that means music in 「音楽|おんがく」 and fun in 「楽しみ|たのしみ」. A way to remember it: plants that make you feel at ease. In the old days, most medicine really did come from herbs and plants. The second part is 「飲む|のむ」, to drink, which you know from 「飲み物|のみもの」, drinks. Here is the twist for English speakers. In English you take medicine. In Japanese you always drink it, even if it is a pill or a powder, because you swallow it with water. So never use 「とる」 for medicine. Say 「薬を飲みます|くすりをのみます」. You will also see 「薬局|やっきょく」, pharmacy, where the kanji uses its Chinese-origin reading 「やっ」. To recap, a plant kanji for medicine, and you drink it rather than take it." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the second kanji, 「飲|の」. On the left is the eating radical, a squeezed form of 「食|しょく」, the kanji in 「食べる|たべる」. That tells you the kanji has to do with food or mouths. On the right is 「欠|けつ」, which is often explained as a person with a wide open mouth. So the shape suggests eating and an open mouth, which fits drinking. Here it uses its native reading. With its Chinese-origin reading 「いん」, it shows up in 「飲食店|いんしょくてん」, places to eat and drink, a word you'll see on signs. Other everyday words are 「飲み物|のみもの」, drinks, and 「飲み会|のみかい」, a drinking party with coworkers or classmates. To wrap up, an eating radical and an open mouth make the drinking kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a Japanese pharmacy, a few words go a long way. Cold medicine is 「風邪薬|かぜぐすり」. Notice the sound change, from 「く」 to 「ぐ」, when it is the second part of a compound. Eye drops are 「目薬|めぐすり」. But you don't drink those. You use the verb 「さす」, to put in. Cream or ointment is something you 「塗る|ぬる」, spread on. Swallowed medicine is always drunk, though. The label will often say 「食後|しょくご」, after meals, and how many times a day. Here is a mini dialogue at the counter. 「一日三回飲んでください|いちにちさんかいのんでください」, take it three times a day. 「食後ですか|しょくごですか」, after meals? 「はい、食後です|はい、しょくごです」. To wrap up, drink pills, put in eye drops, spread on cream." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb 「飲む|のむ」 is a u-verb ending in 「む」. In the te-form, 「む」 becomes 「んで」, so it is 「飲んで|のんで」, and the past is 「飲んだ|のんだ」. A doctor might say 「この薬を飲んでください|このくすりをのんでください」, please take this medicine. Later in your book you will meet a pattern for advice, built on the past short form plus 「ほうがいいです」. 「薬を飲んだほうがいいですよ|くすりをのんだほうがいいですよ」 means you should take some medicine. The opposite uses the negative, 「飲まないほうがいいです|のまないほうがいいです」, you'd better not drink it. To say you took it already, 「もう飲みました|もうのみました」. To wrap up, 「む」 turns into 「んで」, and medicine fits the you had better pattern." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「薬|くすり」 is the grass radical over 「楽|らく」, plants that put you at ease. In Japanese you always drink medicine, even pills, so say 「薬を飲みます|くすりをのみます」, never 「とる」.",
      "「飲|の」 is the eating radical from 「食|しょく」 plus 「欠|けつ」, a person with a wide-open mouth, which fits drinking. It also appears in 「飲み物|のみもの」 and 「飲み会|のみかい」.",
      "Swallowed medicine is always drunk, but eye drops, 「目薬|めぐすり」, take 「さす」 and cream takes 「塗る|ぬる」. Labels often say 「食後|しょくご」, after meals.",
      "Its 「む」 becomes 「んで」 in the te-form, giving 「飲んで|のんで」. It fits the advice pattern 「薬を飲んだほうがいいですよ|くすりをのんだほうがいいですよ」, you should take some medicine."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[薬を飲みます|くすりをのみます|くすりをのむ]。",
    en: "I take medicine in the morning." },
  "かぜをひく|風邪をひく": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「風邪|かぜ」 is written with two kanji. The first, 「風|かぜ」, means wind, and on its own it is read 「かぜ」 too. You see it with its Chinese-origin reading 「ふう」 in 「台風|たいふう」, typhoon. The second kanji, 「邪|じゃ」, means evil or wicked. So a cold is literally an evil wind. This comes from traditional Chinese medicine, which taught that harmful winds could blow into the body and make you sick. The whole pair is simply read as 「かぜ」, the same sound as wind, so context tells you which one is meant. Now the verb. You don't get or catch a cold in Japanese. You 「ひく」 one, which in this sense means to pull or draw in. Picture yourself drawing that evil wind into your body. So I caught a cold is 「風邪をひきました|かぜをひきました」. Don't mix this 「ひく」 up with 「弾く|ひく」, to play the piano. Same sound, different word. To recap, an evil wind, and you pull it in." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Pay attention to tense here, because English and Japanese line up differently. If you caught a cold and you are sick right now, Japanese usually uses the te-iru form, 「風邪をひいています|かぜをひいています」, I have a cold, I am in the state of having caught one. The past, 「風邪をひきました|かぜをひきました」, focuses on the moment you caught it. When explaining why you missed class, you will often add the explaining ending 「んです」. 「風邪をひいたんです|かぜをひいたんです」 means you see, I caught a cold. That ending sounds natural when giving a reason. The verb is a u-verb ending in 「く」, so the te-form is 「ひいて」. To wrap up, use the te-iru form for being sick now, and 「んです」 when giving a reason." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you have a cold, these phrases help. 「熱があります|ねつがあります」, I have a fever. 「のどが痛いです|のどがいたいです」, my throat hurts. 「せきが出ます|せきがでます」, I have a cough. In Japan, people with a cold usually wear a mask in public, out of courtesy so they don't spread it. Calling in sick for school or work is common, and people often apologize for the trouble. When someone tells you they are sick, the standard kind reply is 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care of yourself. Here is a mini dialogue. 「どうしたんですか」, what's wrong? 「風邪をひいたんです|かぜをひいたんです」. 「お大事に|おだいじに」. To wrap up, describe your symptoms, wear a mask, and answer with take care." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese home remedies for a cold are cozy. The classic sick food is 「おかゆ」, soft rice porridge, often with a pickled plum on top. Some families make 「卵酒|たまござけ」, warm sake with a beaten egg, for adults to drink before bed. There is also an old folk remedy of wrapping a green onion around your neck, though people mostly mention it as a funny family tradition now. And there is a joking proverb, 「バカは風邪をひかない|バカはかぜをひかない」, which means fools don't catch colds. People say it to tease a friend who never gets sick, or to laugh at themselves. To wrap up, porridge, warm egg sake, and a teasing proverb for those who never get sick." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「風邪|かぜ」 is wind plus evil, from the old idea that harmful winds blow into the body. You 「ひく」 a cold, pulling that evil wind in, which is unrelated to 「弾く|ひく」.",
      "If you are sick right now, use 「風邪をひいています|かぜをひいています」, the state of having caught one. To give it as a reason, add 「んです」, as in 「風邪をひいたんです|かぜをひいたんです」.",
      "Describe symptoms with 「熱があります|ねつがあります」 or 「のどが痛いです|のどがいたいです」, and wear a mask in public out of courtesy. The kind reply to someone sick is 「お大事に|おだいじに」.",
      "Classic cold remedies are 「おかゆ」 and the adult 「卵酒|たまござけ」, warm sake with egg. The teasing proverb 「バカは風邪をひかない|バカはかぜをひかない」 says fools don't catch colds."
    ],
    ja: "{昨日|きのう}[風邪をひきました|かぜをひきました|かぜをひく]。",
    en: "I caught a cold yesterday." },
  "とり|鳥": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「鳥|とり」 is a real picture of a bird, and you can still see it. At the top is the head, with a little stroke for the eye. In the middle is the body and folded wings. And the four dots at the bottom are the tail feathers and feet. Here it uses its native reading, 「とり」. With its Chinese-origin reading 「ちょう」 it appears in more advanced words. A neat detail is that the four dots also show up in other kanji, like the bottom of the kanji for horse, so you may start to recognize them as legs. This word means bird in general, but in Japanese food it often means chicken, too. 「焼き鳥|やきとり」, grilled chicken on skewers, is a classic snack at bars and summer festivals. So if you see this kanji on a menu, think chicken. When there is a bird in a tree, use 「いる」 for it, since it is alive and moves on its own, not 「ある」. To recap, a picture of a bird, and on menus, chicken." },
      { title: "Kanji family", text: "Once you know the bird kanji, you will spot it hiding in other characters. Put a mouth, 「口|くち」, on its left and you get 「鳴|な」, as in 「鳴く|なく」, the verb for animals making their calls. A bird plus a mouth is a bird singing. The kanji for island, 「島|しま」, is traditionally explained as a bird sitting on a mountain, a rocky place in the sea where birds rest. The bird has lost its tail feathers there, and a mountain sits underneath. In compounds, the bird kanji takes the reading 「ちょう」, as in 「白鳥|はくちょう」, swan, the white bird. A small bird is 「小鳥|ことり」. And the farmyard chicken is 「鶏|にわとり」, whose native name means garden bird. To wrap up, look for the bird in singing and in islands." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up in everyday phrases that have nothing to do with birdwatching. When you get goosebumps, Japanese calls it 「鳥肌|とりはだ」, bird skin. People even say 「鳥肌が立ちました|とりはだがたちました」 after a moving song, meaning it gave me chills. At the supermarket, chicken meat is labeled 「鶏肉|とりにく」, though people often write it with the simpler bird kanji. And to kill two birds with one stone is 「一石二鳥|いっせきにちょう」, one stone, two birds. That four kanji phrase is generally thought to be a translation of the English proverb. You will also hear 「焼き鳥屋|やきとりや」, a grilled chicken shop, where coworkers go after work. To wrap up, goosebumps, chicken meat, and two birds with one stone." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The Chinese zodiac has a bird year too, but it uses a completely different kanji. The rooster year is written 「酉|とり」, with the same sound. People say 「酉年|とりどし」, the year of the rooster. On New Year's cards, you will see cute roosters drawn for that year. There is also a November festival at shrines called 「酉の市|とりのいち」, held on rooster days, where people buy decorated bamboo rakes for good luck in business. That kanji doesn't look like a bird at all. It actually started as a picture of a wine jar. So when you hear this sound in a zodiac or festival context, picture the jar kanji. In everyday talk about real birds, it is the picture of the bird. To wrap up, same sound, but real birds use the bird picture and the zodiac uses the jar." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「鳥|とり」 is a picture of a bird, with head, body and four tail-and-feet dots. On menus it often means chicken, as in 「焼き鳥|やきとり」.",
      "Add a mouth to get 「鳴|な」, a bird singing, and the island kanji 「島|しま」 is a bird resting on a mountain. In compounds it reads 「ちょう」, as in 「白鳥|はくちょう」, swan.",
      "Goosebumps are 「鳥肌|とりはだ」, bird skin, used for chills from a moving song. Two birds with one stone is 「一石二鳥|いっせきにちょう」.",
      "The zodiac rooster uses a different kanji with the same sound, 「酉|とり」, which began as a picture of a wine jar. Real birds use the bird picture, while 「酉年|とりどし」 uses the jar."
    ],
    ja: "{木|き}の{上|うえ}に[鳥|とり]がいます。",
    en: "There is a bird in the tree." },
  "おぼえる|覚える": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji 「覚|おぼ」. The bottom part is 「見|み」, to see, the eye on legs from 「見る|みる」. The top part is a crown-like shape with three little strokes. A way to remember it: picture seeing something again and again until it sticks in your head, like drilling flashcards. In this verb the kanji uses its native reading, 「おぼ」. It is a ru-verb, so the polite form is 「覚えます|おぼえます」. One subtle point for English speakers. This verb is about the act of memorizing, committing something to memory. To say I remember right now, Japanese uses the te-iru form, 「覚えています|おぼえています」, meaning I have it memorized and still hold it. And to say I forgot, you'd use a different verb altogether. The same kanji also appears in the phrase 「目が覚める|めがさめる」, to wake up, literally the eyes become aware. Seeing clearly, waking up, and remembering are all about the mind becoming alert. To recap, a seeing kanji for locking things into memory." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The same kanji powers a little family about waking up. 「覚める|さめる」 is to wake up, and 「覚ます|さます」 is to wake someone, or to wake yourself. Put them together with eyes and you get 「目覚まし時計|めざましどけい」, an alarm clock, an eye waking clock. With its Chinese-origin reading 「かく」, the kanji appears in 「感覚|かんかく」, a sense or feeling. Now for near-synonyms. To recall something is 「思い出す|おもいだす」, to pull it out of memory. To forget is 「忘れる|わすれる」. To memorize by rote, like a list of words, is 「暗記する|あんきする」. And just knowing a fact is 「知っている|しっている」. This verb sits between them, about storing something so you have it. To wrap up, waking up and remembering share a kanji, and each memory verb has its own job." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a ru-verb, so every form is simple. Drop 「る」 and add the ending. Te-form 「覚えて|おぼえて」, past 「覚えた|おぼえた」, negative 「覚えない|おぼえない」. The thing you memorize takes 「を」. The most important pattern is the te-iru form for remembering. 「覚えていますか|おぼえていますか」 means do you remember? A natural no is 「覚えていません|おぼえていません」, I don't remember. Don't say 「覚えません|おぼえません」 for that, because it means I won't memorize it. Later you will meet 「覚えておく|おぼえておく」, to memorize for later use, as in 「覚えておいてください|おぼえておいてください」, please keep this in mind. To wrap up, the te-iru form means remember, and the plain negative means won't memorize." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb goes beyond flashcards. When you start a new job, you have to 「仕事を覚える|しごとをおぼえる」, learn the job, pick up the ropes. A new part-time worker hears it all the time. Teachers say 「この言葉を覚えてください|このことばをおぼえてください」, please memorize this word. Among friends, 「覚えてる|おぼえてる」 with a rising tone means remember? before telling an old story. And when you meet someone you met once before, 「私のこと、覚えていますか|わたしのこと、おぼえていますか」 means do you remember me? A common complaint is 「漢字がなかなか覚えられません|かんじがなかなかおぼえられません」, I just can't memorize kanji. Many learners will agree. To wrap up, you learn words, learn jobs, and ask if people remember you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「覚|おぼ」 has 「見|み」, to see, at the bottom, like seeing something until it sticks. The verb is the act of memorizing, so I remember is 「覚えています|おぼえています」.",
      "The same kanji powers waking up, 「覚める|さめる」, as in 「目覚まし時計|めざましどけい」, alarm clock. This verb is about storing something, unlike 「思い出す|おもいだす」, recalling, or 「忘れる|わすれる」, forgetting.",
      "「覚えていますか|おぼえていますか」 asks do you remember, and the no is 「覚えていません|おぼえていません」. The plain 「覚えません|おぼえません」 means I won't memorize it.",
      "Beyond flashcards it means learning a job, as in 「仕事を覚える|しごとをおぼえる」. 「覚えてる|おぼえてる」 with a rising tone starts an old story, remember."
    ],
    ja: "{漢字|かんじ}を[覚えます|おぼえます|おぼえる]。",
    en: "I memorize kanji." },
  "かぶき|歌舞伎": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it apart. 「歌|か」 is song, the same kanji as in 「歌|うた」, song, 「歌う|うたう」, to sing, and 「歌手|かしゅ」, singer. 「舞|ぶ」 is dance, especially graceful stage dance. 「伎|き」 is skill or art. All three use Chinese-origin readings here. Song, dance, skill: a perfect description of Japan's classic stage theater. But here is the anecdote. The name actually came first, from an old verb 「かぶく」, which meant to lean, and then to act wild, flashy, and outrageous. The kanji were chosen later to fit the sound, and they happened to describe the art beautifully. Kabuki began around the year sixteen hundred, started by a woman performer named Okuni in Kyoto. Later, women were banned from the stage, so today all the roles, even female ones, are played by men. Shows are famous for bold red and white makeup, dramatic poses, and audience members shouting actors' names at key moments. To recap, song plus dance plus skill, written over an older word for wild." },
      { title: "Words from the stage", text: "Kabuki has given everyday Japanese some colorful words. The 「花道|はなみち」, flower path, is a raised walkway running through the audience, where actors make grand entrances and exits. Today, a grand final exit from a career is also called a flower path. A dramatic pose that an actor freezes in is a 「見得|みえ」, and 「見得を切る|みえをきる」 now means to show off or strike a pose in daily life. Stagehands dressed all in black are 「黒子|くろご」, and the audience pretends they are invisible. People now use that word for someone working behind the scenes. Finally, one great acting family had a famous set of eighteen plays, and that is why your specialty or party trick is called 「十八番|おはこ」. To wrap up, flower paths, poses, and people in black all live on in everyday speech." },
      { title: "Visiting today", text: "The most famous theater is the 「歌舞伎座|かぶきざ」 in Ginza, Tokyo, with a traditional palace-like front. A full program can last four hours or more, split into acts with breaks. Many people eat a boxed lunch at their seats during the intermission. One popular explanation says the 「幕の内弁当|まくのうちべんとう」, a classic bento, gets its name from this between-acts meal, though other explanations exist. If you are short on time or money, you can buy a ticket for a single act and sit up in the top gallery. English audio guides and subtitle devices help visitors follow the story. Listen for regulars shouting actors' stage names at big moments. To wrap up, a long show, a bento in the break, and single-act tickets for beginners." },
      { title: "Female roles", text: "Since the seventeenth century, every role in kabuki, including the women, has been played by men. Actors who specialize in female roles are called 「女形|おんながた」, or sometimes 「おやま」. They do not try to copy real women exactly. Instead, they train for years to create an idealized feminine style, with small steps, knees together, shoulders sloped, and a high, soft voice. Many actors come from long family lines, and sons begin training as small children. Some of these actors are among the most famous stars in Japan, and a few have been named Living National Treasures. Their skill is so admired that women sometimes study their graceful movement. To wrap up, men in female roles, creating an elegant style through years of training." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji spell song, dance and skill, but they were chosen later to fit the older verb 「かぶく」, to act wild and flashy. Okuni started it in Kyoto around sixteen hundred, and today men play every role.",
      "Kabuki gave everyday words like 「花道|はなみち」, a grand exit, and 「見得を切る|みえをきる」, striking a pose. Black-clad stagehands, 「黒子|くろご」, now mean people behind the scenes, and a specialty is 「十八番|おはこ」.",
      "At the 「歌舞伎座|かぶきざ」 in Ginza, a full program runs four hours or more, with bento eaten in the breaks. Beginners can buy a cheap single-act ticket for the top gallery.",
      "Men who specialize in female roles, 「女形|おんながた」, train for years to create an idealized feminine style rather than copy real women. Some are famous stars and even Living National Treasures."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[歌舞伎|かぶき]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw kabuki with a friend." },
  "いぬ|犬": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here the kanji takes its native Japanese reading. In compound words it switches to the Chinese-origin reading, 「けん」. Look at the shape. It is almost exactly 「大|だい」, the kanji for big that you know from 「大学|だいがく」 and 「大きい|おおきい」, with one extra dot up in the top right. A way to remember it: picture a big dog with one ear perked up. That dot is the ear. Be careful with a close cousin. 「太る|ふとる」, to gain weight, also starts from 「大|だい」, but its dot sits low, inside the legs. Dot up high, dog. Dot down low, getting fat. The dog kanji also lives on inside other kanji. Squeezed down onto the left side, it becomes a narrow animal mark, and you can see it in 「猫|ねこ」, cat. For counting dogs, use the small animal counter you will meet later, 「〜ひき」. A cultural note: Japan's most famous dog is 「ハチ公|ハチこう」, who waited at 「渋谷|しぶや」 station for his owner every day for years. There is a statue of him there, and it is a classic meeting spot. So the recap: big plus a perked-up ear makes a dog." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let us build some dog vocabulary. A puppy is 「子犬|こいぬ」, child dog. A doghouse is 「犬小屋|いぬごや」, where 「こや」 softens to 「ごや」. Japan's native breeds keep this word in their names. The small, fox-like 「柴犬|しばいぬ」 is famous worldwide on the internet. The large 「秋田犬|あきたいぬ」 comes from Akita in the north, and Hachiko was one of them. With the Chinese-origin reading 「けん」, you get 「番犬|ばんけん」, a guard dog, and 「愛犬|あいけん」, a beloved pet dog. Dogs in Japanese say 「ワンワン」, not woof, and little kids sometimes call a dog 「ワンちゃん」. To wrap up, puppies, breeds, guard dogs, and a dog that says wan wan." },
      { title: "Idioms", text: "Dogs show up in some well-known sayings. When two people can't stand each other, English says they fight like cats and dogs. Japanese says they are like dogs and monkeys, 「犬猿の仲|けんえんのなか」. Note the Chinese-origin reading of the dog kanji there. Another proverb is 「犬も歩けば棒に当たる|いぬもあるけばぼうにあたる」, even a dog, if it walks around, will run into a stick. It is famous as the first card in a traditional New Year's card game. Its meaning is debated. Some say it warns that if you go poking around, you will meet trouble. Others take it to mean that if you get out there, you will run into good luck. Both readings are common today. To wrap up, dogs and monkeys fight, and a walking dog meets a stick." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Dogs play a part in a Japanese custom for expecting mothers. In the old calendar, days cycle through the twelve zodiac animals, and one of them is the dog. In the fifth month of pregnancy, on a day of the dog, many women visit a shrine to pray for a safe birth. They often receive a special sash to wrap around the belly. The custom exists because dogs are seen as giving birth easily and having many healthy puppies. The zodiac dog uses a different kanji, 「戌|いぬ」, with the same sound. Some shrines even have statues of a mother dog with puppies that visitors pat for luck. To wrap up, a day of the dog, a shrine visit, and a prayer for an easy birth." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is 「大|だい」, big, with a dot up top like a perked ear, while 「太|ふと」 has its dot low inside the legs. Squeezed to the left side, it becomes the animal mark in 「猫|ねこ」.",
      "A puppy is 「子犬|こいぬ」, and native breeds like 「柴犬|しばいぬ」 and 「秋田犬|あきたいぬ」 keep the word in their names. With the reading 「けん」 you get 「番犬|ばんけん」 and 「愛犬|あいけん」.",
      "Enemies are like dogs and monkeys, 「犬猿の仲|けんえんのなか」. 「犬も歩けば棒に当たる|いぬもあるけばぼうにあたる」 says a walking dog hits a stick, read as either trouble or luck.",
      "Because dogs give birth easily, pregnant women visit a shrine on a day of the dog to pray for a safe birth. The zodiac dog is written with a different kanji, 「戌|いぬ」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[犬|いぬ]と{公園|こうえん}にいきます。",
    en: "I go to the park with my dog every day." },
  "ねこ|猫": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. The Chinese-origin reading, 「びょう」, shows up only in rarer compound words. Break the kanji in two. The left side is the animal mark, a squeezed-down form of 「犬|いぬ」, dog. Lots of animal kanji carry it. The right side is 「苗|びょう」, which means a seedling. It isn't there for its meaning. It is there for its sound, and that is where the Chinese-origin reading comes from. A way to remember it: the Chinese word for cat sounds a lot like a meow, so think of the right side as the meow, and the left side as the label that says this is an animal. A cultural note: in many Japanese shops you will see the 「招き猫|まねきねこ」, the beckoning cat statue with one paw raised. It is supposed to wave in customers and good luck. Like dogs, cats are counted with 「〜ひき」, the counter for small animals. Recap: animal mark on the left, meow sound on the right, and you have a cat." },
      { title: "Idioms", text: "Cats sneak into many Japanese expressions. If you are so busy you'd even accept help from a cat, you say 「猫の手も借りたい|ねこのてもかりたい」, I'd even borrow a cat's paws. Someone who can't handle hot food or drinks has a 「猫舌|ねこじた」, a cat tongue, since cats famously avoid hot food. Hunched posture is 「猫背|ねこぜ」, cat back. Giving something precious to someone who can't appreciate it is 「猫に小判|ねこにこばん」, gold coins to a cat, like pearls before swine. And when someone acts sweet and innocent to hide their real self, they are 「猫をかぶる|ねこをかぶる」, wearing a cat. To wrap up, cat paws, cat tongues, and cat backs, all in daily speech." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Japanese cats say 「ニャー」 or 「ニャン」, not meow. That sound is so tied to cats that it has become a pun on the calendar. February twenty second is 「猫の日|ねこのひ」, cat day. Why? Because two, two, two can be read 「ニャン、ニャン、ニャン」, a play on the Japanese word for two, 「に」. Cute characters often end their sentences with 「にゃ」, and fans copy it as a playful way of talking. A kitten is 「子猫|こねこ」, and a stray cat is a 「野良猫|のらねこ」. As for where the word itself comes from, a popular story links it to 「寝る|ねる」, to sleep, since cats sleep so much. But that is a folk explanation, and the real origin is uncertain. To wrap up, the cat says nyan, and two two two is cat day." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan is famous for its cat culture. Cat cafés, 「猫カフェ|ねこカフェ」, became hugely popular in the two thousands, offering people in small apartments a chance to relax with cats. There are also cat islands, small fishing islands where cats outnumber people. One of the best known is 「田代島|たしろじま」 in Miyagi, which even has a little cat shrine. Fishermen there traditionally treated cats with affection, partly because they kept mice away. Some train stations have had cat stationmasters too. The most famous was Tama, a cat who wore a little hat at a rural station in Wakayama and helped save the line by attracting tourists. To wrap up, cafés, islands, and even a cat stationmaster." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The left side is the animal mark from 「犬|いぬ」, and the right side, 「苗|びょう」, supplies the sound, like a meow. Shops display the 「招き猫|まねきねこ」 to beckon customers and luck.",
      "Busy people would borrow a cat's paws, 「猫の手も借りたい|ねこのてもかりたい」, and someone who can't handle hot food has a 「猫舌|ねこじた」. Acting sweet to hide your real self is 「猫をかぶる|ねこをかぶる」.",
      "Japanese cats say 「ニャン」, so February twenty-second, two two two, is 「猫の日|ねこのひ」. The link to 「寝る|ねる」 is just a folk story.",
      "Japan's cat culture includes 「猫カフェ|ねこカフェ」 and cat islands like 「田代島|たしろじま」. The most famous cat stationmaster, Tama, helped save a rural line in Wakayama."
    ],
    ja: "[猫|ねこ]はいすのうえにいます。",
    en: "The cat is on the chair." },
  "はな|花": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. The Chinese-origin reading is 「か」. The kanji has two parts. On top are the little marks for grass. That top piece shows up on a huge number of plant kanji, including the 「茶|ちゃ」 of 「お茶|おちゃ」 and the 「菜|さい」 of 「野菜|やさい」, vegetables. Underneath is 「化|か」, which means to change or transform. It gives the kanji its Chinese-origin sound. You will see it again in 「化粧品|けしょうひん」, cosmetics, things that change how you look. A way to remember it: a plant that changes, as a tight bud turns into a bloom. Watch out for a sound twin. 「鼻|はな」, nose, is also said this way. Context almost always makes it clear. A cultural note: in spring, people gather under cherry trees for 「花見|はなみ」, flower viewing, with picnics and drinks. Recap: grass on top, change underneath. A plant that blooms is a flower." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word blooms into a big family. 「花火|はなび」, flower fire, means fireworks, a summer highlight. A single petal is a 「花びら|はなびら」. A flower shop is a 「花屋|はなや」. The traditional art of flower arranging is 「生け花|いけばな」, bringing flowers to life. With the Chinese-origin reading 「か」, you get 「花瓶|かびん」, a vase, and 「花粉症|かふんしょう」, pollen allergy, which hits millions of people every spring when cedar pollen fills the air. You may also hear 「お花見|おはなみ」 with the polite 「お」 in front. Notice that native words use 「はな」 and more technical words use 「か」. To wrap up, fireworks, petals, vases, and hay fever all grow from this one kanji." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word and nose, 「鼻|はな」, sound the same on paper, but in standard Tokyo pitch they behave differently. Listen to what happens to a particle after them. With flower, the voice rises on the second beat and then drops on the particle. 「花が|はなが」 goes low, high, low. With nose, the voice rises and stays up, so 「鼻が|はなが」 goes low, high, high. On their own, they sound almost identical, so the difference really shows up only when something follows. Don't worry too much about this, because context almost always tells you which one is meant. But if you want to sound natural, try saying a flower drops its particle, and a nose holds it up. To wrap up, flower falls after it, nose stays flat." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A famous saying pokes fun at flower viewing. 「花より団子|はなよりだんご」 means dumplings rather than flowers. It describes people at a cherry blossom picnic who care more about the food than the blossoms, and more generally anyone who prefers practical things to beauty. There are some flower manners too. When visiting someone in the hospital, avoid potted plants, because a plant putting down roots suggests the patient will stay stuck in bed. Cut flowers are better. White chrysanthemums are linked with funerals and graves, so they are not a cheerful gift. Shops will help you choose if you say what the occasion is. To wrap up, dumplings over flowers, and pick cut flowers for a hospital visit." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The grass marks on top sit over 「化|か」, to change, like a bud transforming into a bloom. Watch out for the sound twin 「鼻|はな」, nose.",
      "The word blooms into 「花火|はなび」, fireworks, 「花屋|はなや」, and 「生け花|いけばな」. With the reading 「か」 you get 「花瓶|かびん」 and 「花粉症|かふんしょう」, pollen allergy.",
      "In Tokyo pitch, the voice drops on a particle after flower, 「花が|はなが」, but stays up after nose, 「鼻が|はなが」. Context usually tells you which one is meant anyway.",
      "「花より団子|はなよりだんご」, dumplings over flowers, teases people who prefer food to blossoms. Bring cut flowers, not potted plants, to a hospital, and avoid white chrysanthemums as gifts."
    ],
    ja: "{公園|こうえん}に[花|はな]がたくさんあります。",
    en: "There are lots of flowers in the park." },
  "こうえん|公園": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji here use their Chinese-origin readings, which is typical for two-kanji words. 「公|こう」 means public or official, belonging to everyone rather than to one person. 「園|えん」 means a garden or a fenced-in plot of grounds. Notice the big box around the outside of the kanji. That box is a real piece that often marks an enclosure. Put them together and a public garden, open to everybody, is a park. The garden kanji has a sibling you will meet in Lesson 10. 「動物園|どうぶつえん」 is a zoo, literally a garden of moving things, animals. Same 「園|えん」, same idea of an enclosed space. A cultural note: a Japanese park can be a huge green space like 「上野公園|うえのこうえん」 in 「東京|とうきょう」, but it can also be a tiny neighborhood lot with a swing, a slide and a sandbox. Both are called this. Recap: public plus garden equals park." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's zoom in on the shapes. The public kanji, 「公|こう」, has two pieces stacked up. On top is a little pair of strokes spreading apart, the same shape as the number eight, 「八|はち」. Underneath is a small closed hook. An old Chinese dictionary explained it as opening up what is private and sharing it, though modern scholars debate that story. Its native reading, 「公|おおやけ」, means official or public, and you will mostly see the kanji in compounds. The garden kanji, 「園|えん」, is a box with a piece inside. The inside part is mainly there for sound. You can spot the same inner piece in 「遠い|とおい」, far, whose Chinese-origin reading is also 「えん」. The garden kanji has a native reading too, 「園|その」, but it is rare and poetic, found in a few names. In everyday words you will almost always hear 「えん」. So, a shared top, a sound piece in a box." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The garden kanji, 「園|えん」, turns up in many places where people gather inside a set space. A kindergarten is 「幼稚園|ようちえん」, a garden for little children. A botanical garden is 「植物園|しょくぶつえん」, a garden of plants. An amusement park is 「遊園地|ゆうえんち」, literally play garden ground, with the 「遊|ゆう」 of 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, to play. You can see the pattern: a fenced-in area with a special purpose. The public kanji has a family too. A public school is 「公立|こうりつ」, set up by the public, and a public hall or community center is 「公民館|こうみんかん」. When you meet a new word with 「公|こう」 in it, think government or open to everyone. When you meet 「園|えん」, think of a gated space full of something, whether it is plants, animals, rides or small children. Two kanji, two families, and the park sits right where they meet." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Parks are a big part of daily life in Japan. Small children play there after kindergarten while parents chat on the benches. You will hear the playground words 「ブランコ」, swings, 「すべり台|すべりだい」, the slide, and 「砂場|すなば」, the sandbox. Older people may gather in the early morning for group radio exercises, set to a cheerful piano tune broadcast on the radio. Watch the particles. You go to the park with 「に」 or 「へ」, as in 「公園に行きます|こうえんにいきます」. But you do things in the park with 「で」, as in 「公園で遊びます|こうえんであそびます」. Here is a tiny dialogue. One friend asks 「どこで会いましょうか|どこであいましょうか」, where shall we meet. The other answers 「公園で会いましょう|こうえんであいましょう」, let's meet at the park. In spring, people spread blue tarps under the cherry trees for flower viewing, and someone may arrive hours early to save a spot. Remember: go to the park with 「に」, play there with 「で」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「公|こう」 means public, open to everyone, and 「園|えん」 is a fenced-in garden, so a public garden is a park. The same 「園|えん」 appears in 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, zoo.",
      "「公|こう」 stacks an eight-like shape over a small hook, read 「おおやけ」 on its own. 「園|えん」 is a box around a sound piece also found in 「遠い|とおい」, far.",
      "「園|えん」 marks a gated space with a purpose, as in 「幼稚園|ようちえん」, 「植物園|しょくぶつえん」 and 「遊園地|ゆうえんち」. 「公|こう」 means government or open to all, as in 「公立|こうりつ」 and 「公民館|こうみんかん」.",
      "You go to the park with 「に」, as in 「公園に行きます|こうえんにいきます」, but do things there with 「で」, as in 「公園で遊びます|こうえんであそびます」. Playgrounds have 「ブランコ」, 「すべり台|すべりだい」 and 「砂場|すなば」."
    ],
    ja: "{日曜日|にちようび}に[公園|こうえん]で{本|ほん}をよみました。",
    en: "I read a book in the park on Sunday." },
  "おてら|お寺": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「寺|てら」 is a Buddhist temple, using its native reading. The 「お」 in front is the polite prefix, which people almost always add when talking about temples in everyday speech. In names of famous temples you will hear the Chinese-origin reading, 「じ」, instead. The Golden Pavilion in 「京都|きょうと」 is 「金閣寺|きんかくじ」. Don't mix this up with 「神社|じんじゃ」, a Shinto shrine, which you will meet in Lesson 11. A handy rule: temples are Buddhist, with monks, incense and statues of the Buddha. Shrines are Shinto, with the big red gates. The temple kanji is also a building block inside other kanji you know, usually on the right side. In 「時|とき」, time, it sits next to the sun. In 「待つ|まつ」, to wait, and 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, it sits beside another part. In those kanji it mainly helps with the sound. Recap: polite prefix plus temple, and if you spot this shape on the right of a kanji, you are seeing the temple piece." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Here is what a visit to a temple usually looks like. You walk through a big wooden main gate, often guarded by two fierce statues. Near the main hall there may be a large incense burner. People light incense sticks and wave the smoke over themselves, especially over any part of the body they want to heal or strengthen. Then you pray quietly with your palms pressed together. A key difference from a Shinto shrine: at a temple you normally don't clap. At a shrine, people usually bow and clap twice. Temples are also tied to family memory. Many families have their graves on temple grounds, and they visit during the summer festival for the ancestors, 「お盆|おぼん」. On New Year's Eve, temples ring their huge bells one hundred and eight times, a sound called 「除夜の鐘|じょやのかね」, to clear away the troubles of the old year. So temples mean quiet prayer, incense smoke, and the bells at midnight." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the shape of the temple kanji. On top is 「土|つち」, earth or soil. Underneath is a small piece, 「寸|すん」, which is an old unit of length and often hints at a hand. The original meaning of this character in China was probably not temple at all. It was used for a government office. The traditional story goes that when Buddhist monks first came to China from India, they were housed in one of these official buildings. Over time, the word for that office came to mean a place where monks lived and worshipped, and so a temple. Treat that as the well-known traditional account rather than proven fact. In Japanese you meet two readings. The native 「てら」 is the everyday word, and the Chinese-origin 「じ」 is used in names and compounds. So when you see earth over a hand, think of an old office turned into a home for monks." },
      { title: "Temple names", text: "Temple names follow a handy pattern. Most famous temples have names built from Chinese-origin readings, and they end in 「じ」. For example, 「東大寺|とうだいじ」 in 「奈良|なら」 has the giant bronze Buddha. 「浅草寺|せんそうじ」 is the big temple in 「浅草|あさくさ」 in Tokyo. But when the name in front is a native Japanese word, the temple kanji often takes its native reading, and the first sound gets voiced, turning 「てら」 into 「でら」. The famous one is 「清水寺|きよみずでら」 in 「京都|きょうと」, the pure water temple, with its wooden stage high on a hillside. A mountain temple is 「山寺|やまでら」. So the ending gives you a clue about the whole name. A 「じ」 ending usually means a Chinese-style name. A 「でら」 ending usually means a native name in front. It is a tendency, not an iron law, so learn famous names one by one. Listen to the ending, and you can guess the style of the name." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is a Buddhist temple with the polite 「お」, and famous names use the reading 「じ」, as in 「金閣寺|きんかくじ」. Don't mix it up with 「神社|じんじゃ」, a Shinto shrine with red gates.",
      "At a temple you wave incense smoke over yourself and pray with palms together, but you normally don't clap as at a shrine. On New Year's Eve temples ring their bells a hundred and eight times, 「除夜の鐘|じょやのかね」.",
      "The kanji is earth, 「土|つち」, over 「寸|すん」, a hand. Traditionally it first meant a government office, which came to mean temple after visiting Buddhist monks were housed in one.",
      "Famous temples with Chinese-style names end in 「じ」, like 「東大寺|とうだいじ」, while a native name in front often gives 「でら」, like 「清水寺|きよみずでら」. It is a tendency, so learn famous names one by one."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}で[お寺|おてら]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw a temple in Kyoto." },
  "ぎんこう|銀行": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「銀|ぎん」 means silver. Its left side is the metal mark, which comes from 「金|きん」, gold or metal. That is the same kanji as in 「お金|おかね」, money, and 「金曜日|きんようび」, Friday. 「行|こう」 is the kanji you know from 「行く|いく」, to go. But here it has an older meaning. In Chinese, this character also meant a trading firm or a merchant guild. So a silver firm, a business that deals in money, is a bank. The word was put together in the 1800s, when Japan needed a term for Western-style banks. The same reading 「行|こう」 appears in 「旅行|りょこう」, travel, though there it keeps its everyday meaning of going. A tip for life in Japan: banks often close mid-afternoon on weekdays, but cash machines at convenience stores and post offices run much longer. Recap: silver plus firm. A money business is a bank." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the second kanji, 「行|こう」, more closely. Its oldest forms look like a crossroads seen from above, two roads crossing. That is why it is about going and moving along a road. This kanji has several readings you will meet. As a verb it is 「行く|いく」, to go, and later 「行う|おこなう」, to carry out or hold an event. In compounds it is often 「こう」, as here and in 「旅行|りょこう」. It can also be 「ぎょう」. In the kana chart, the row of sounds starting with a is called 「あ行|あぎょう」, and a line of text is 「行|ぎょう」 too. Now the first kanji, 「銀|ぎん」. The right side is a piece that gives the sound. The same piece appears in 「根|ね」, root, where its Chinese-origin reading is 「こん」, a close cousin of that sound. So, a crossroads, many readings, and a sound part next to metal." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Silver has a famous place in 「東京|とうきょう」. The shopping district 「銀座|ぎんざ」 got its name from silver. In the early 1600s, the government set up a silver mint there, an official workshop that made silver coins. The second kanji, 「座|ざ」, means a seat, and back then it also meant a guild or an official workshop. So the name means the silver guild. The mint later moved away, but the name stayed, and today the area is known for luxury shops and department stores. Silver was the money metal of western Japan for centuries, which fits nicely with your word for bank. Silver also shows up in medals. Gold, silver and bronze medals are 「金メダル|きんメダル」, 「銀メダル|ぎんメダル」 and 「銅メダル|どうメダル」. And the color silver is 「銀色|ぎんいろ」. So silver means money and shine, from coins to medals to a famous street." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are words you need at the bank. A bank account is 「口座|こうざ」, and to open one you say 「口座を開きます|こうざをひらきます」. To take money out of an account, people say 「お金をおろします|おかねをおろします」, literally to bring money down. To put money in is 「お金を預けます|おかねをあずけます」. A bank employee is 「銀行員|ぎんこういん」, with the same ending as 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, office worker. Traditionally, Japanese banks asked for a personal name stamp, 「はんこ」, instead of a signature, and many still use one, though this is slowly changing. Watch the particles. You go to the bank with 「に」, as in 「銀行に行きます|ぎんこうにいきます」. You work at the bank with 「で」, as in 「銀行で働いています|ぎんこうではたらいています」. So at the bank, you open an account, bring money down, and maybe stamp your little seal." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「銀|ぎん」 is silver, with the metal mark from 「金|きん」, and 「行|こう」 here keeps an old meaning of trading firm. A silver firm, a money business, is a bank, a word coined in the 1800s.",
      "The kanji 「行|こう」 began as a crossroads seen from above, so it is about going, with readings like 「いく」, 「こう」 and 「ぎょう」. In 「銀|ぎん」, the right side gives the sound, as in 「根|ね」.",
      "Tokyo's 「銀座|ぎんざ」 is named after a silver mint set up there in the early 1600s, the silver guild. Silver also shines in 「銀メダル|ぎんメダル」 and 「銀色|ぎんいろ」.",
      "At the bank you open a 「口座|こうざ」, take money out with 「おろします」 and deposit with 「預けます|あずけます」. You go to the bank with 「に」 but work there with 「で」."
    ],
    ja: "[銀行|ぎんこう]は{九時|くじ}からです。",
    en: "The bank opens at nine." },
  "ゆうびんきょく|郵便局": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「郵|ゆう」 and 「便|びん」 together make 「郵便|ゆうびん」, mail. 「郵|ゆう」 carries the idea of sending messages. 「便|びん」 carries the idea of a delivery or a service. You already know 「便|べん」 from 「便利|べんり」, convenient, in Lesson 7. Same kanji, a slightly different reading. The shared idea is something that makes life easier, which is what a postal service does. 「局|きょく」 means a bureau or an office, a place that handles one kind of public business. So the mail bureau is the post office. A cultural note: look for the red symbol that looks like a capital T with an extra bar on top. That is the postal mark, and you will see it on post offices, mailboxes and even before postal codes on envelopes. Japan's post offices also offer banking, so people go there to pay bills and save money too. Recap: mail plus bureau equals post office." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The post office has a starring role every New Year. Japanese people send New Year greeting cards, 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, to friends, family, coworkers and teachers. People mail them in late December, and the post office holds them back and then delivers them all on the morning of January first. Families sit down after breakfast and read through the stack. The cards often show the animal of the coming year from the Chinese zodiac. Many cards from the post office even carry a lottery number at the bottom, with small prizes. You will also see bright red mailboxes on street corners, called 「ポスト」. Some have two slots, one for regular letters and postcards and one for larger or express items. On envelopes, the seven-digit postal code is 「郵便番号|ゆうびんばんごう」, with 「番号|ばんごう」 meaning number. So think of the post office as the place that makes the New Year morning mail arrive on time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji here has a family. 「便|びん」, with this reading, means a delivery or a scheduled service. Airmail is 「航空便|こうくうびん」, and sea mail is 「船便|ふなびん」, using the native reading of 「船|ふね」, ship. The same kanji is used for a flight. Airline staff might say the next flight is 「次の便|つぎのびん」. With the other reading, 「べん」, it means handy, as in 「便利|べんり」, convenient, and its opposite 「不便|ふべん」, inconvenient. The last kanji, 「局|きょく」, means a bureau that handles one kind of business. A TV station is 「テレビ局|テレビきょく」. A pharmacy is 「薬局|やっきょく」, the medicine bureau, with 「薬|くすり」 for medicine. So when you see 「局|きょく」 at the end of a word, picture an office or a station with one special job. Delivery with one reading, handy with another, and a bureau at the end." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture yourself at the post office counter. You want stamps, so you ask for 「切手|きって」. The first kanji means cut, and the second means hand, so it is a little cut ticket. A postcard is 「はがき」. To send a letter is 「手紙を出します|てがみをだします」, literally to put out a letter. To send a package is 「荷物を送ります|にもつをおくります」. At the counter you might say 「これをアメリカまでお願いします|これをアメリカまでおねがいします」, this one to America, please. The clerk may ask whether you want it by air or by sea. Notice 「まで」 here. It marks how far the letter goes, all the way to America. Post offices also have a rule English speakers find surprising: many open on weekdays only, while a few big central branches stay open longer. So the key words are stamps, postcards, and 「まで」 for where it goes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「郵便|ゆうびん」 is mail, sending plus delivery service, and 「局|きょく」 is a bureau, so this is the mail bureau. 「便|べん」 is also in 「便利|べんり」, things that make life easier.",
      "People send 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」 in late December, and the post office delivers them all on the morning of January first. Red street mailboxes are called 「ポスト」.",
      "「便|びん」 means a delivery or scheduled service, as in 「航空便|こうくうびん」 and even a flight, while 「べん」 means handy. 「局|きょく」 is an office with one job, as in 「テレビ局|テレビきょく」 and 「薬局|やっきょく」.",
      "At the counter ask for 「切手|きって」 or 「はがき」, and say 「これをアメリカまでお願いします|これをアメリカまでおねがいします」. 「まで」 marks how far the letter goes."
    ],
    ja: "[郵便局|ゆうびんきょく]で{手紙|てがみ}をだしました。",
    en: "I mailed a letter at the post office." },
  "きっさてん|喫茶店": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three Chinese-origin readings, one of them unusual. 「喫|きっ」 means to take in, to drink, or to smoke. The mouth mark on its left side fits that idea. 「茶|さ」 is tea. Normally this kanji is read 「ちゃ」, as in 「お茶|おちゃ」 and 「紅茶|こうちゃ」. Here it uses an older reading, 「さ」, which survives in a few set words. 「店|てん」 is shop. You will meet it on its own in Lesson 13 with its native reading, 「店|みせ」. So a tea-drinking shop is a cafe. A cultural note: this word has a nostalgic, old-school feel. Picture a dim room with wood paneling, a coffee master pouring slow drip coffee, jazz playing, and a morning set of thick toast and a boiled egg. For a bright modern place, people say 「カフェ」 instead, a loanword from French. Recap: drink plus tea plus shop. The classic Japanese coffee house." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the first two kanji. 「喫|きつ」 has the mouth mark on its left, and the right side mainly gives the sound. You will meet it in signs about smoking. A smoking area is 「喫煙所|きつえんじょ」, and the word for smoking is 「喫煙|きつえん」. No smoking is 「禁煙|きんえん」, which is on stickers everywhere. Old cafes were often smoky, and many still allow it, which fits the drinking and smoking meaning of this kanji. The tea kanji, 「茶|ちゃ」, has the grass mark on top, two little crosses that appear on many kanji for plants and herbs. Tea leaves come from a plant, so that fits. Its rare reading 「さ」 shows up in 「茶道|さどう」, the tea ceremony, which you may also hear as 「ちゃどう」. So in this word you have a mouth taking things in, a plant with its leaves, and an old reading kept alive in tea culture." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's coffee houses have a real history. One of the first well-known coffee shops opened in Tokyo in 1888, in the 「上野|うえの」 area. Coffee houses really took off in the twentieth century, and in the decades after the war, many neighborhoods had several. Some had themes. A jazz coffee house, 「ジャズ喫茶|ジャズきっさ」, had huge speakers and shelves of records, and customers sat in silence just to listen. There is also a term you may see on old signs, 「純喫茶|じゅんきっさ」, a pure cafe. It meant a place that simply served coffee and light food, with no alcohol and no hostesses. Today many of these places are run by the same owner for decades, with the same chairs, cups and menu. Young people now visit them for the retro mood and photos. So an old sign with this word can be a door into Showa-era Japan." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen to the little pause at the start of this word. The first kanji is really 「きつ」. But when it meets the 「さ」 of tea, the 「つ」 shrinks into a small 「っ」, a tiny silent beat. So you get 「きっさ」, not 「きつさ」. This sound change is common when a kanji ending in 「つ」 or 「く」 meets a sound like k, s or t. You know it already from 「学校|がっこう」, where 「がく」 plus 「こう」 squeezes into 「がっこう」. You also hear it in 「一回|いっかい」, once. That small 「っ」 counts as a full beat, so the word has five beats: 「き」, the pause, 「さ」, 「て」, 「ん」. English speakers often rush it and say 「きさてん」, which sounds wrong to Japanese ears. Try clapping it out, with a silent clap on the pause. Keep the little hiccup, and the word will sound natural." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is drink plus tea plus shop, with tea in its old reading 「さ」 instead of 「ちゃ」. The word feels nostalgic and old-school, while a bright modern place is a 「カフェ」.",
      "「喫|きつ」 has the mouth mark and appears in smoking words like 「喫煙|きつえん」, which fits smoky old cafes. 「茶|ちゃ」 has the grass mark, and its rare reading 「さ」 survives in 「茶道|さどう」.",
      "Coffee houses boomed after the war, including 「ジャズ喫茶|ジャズきっさ」 where people sat silently listening to records. A 「純喫茶|じゅんきっさ」 sign marks a pure cafe with no alcohol, a door into Showa-era Japan.",
      "The 「つ」 of 「きつ」 shrinks into a small 「っ」 before 「さ」, just like 「学校|がっこう」. That pause is a full beat, so don't rush it into 「きさてん」."
    ],
    ja: "[喫茶店|きっさてん]でコーヒーをのみました。",
    en: "I drank coffee at a cafe." },
  "スーパー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word supermarket, and then Japanese chopped it short. Japanese loves to clip long loanwords down to their first few sounds. Listen to how the sounds were squeezed. The long vowel in super became a stretched sound, and the er at the end, with its r sound, simply turned into a long vowel too. English r sounds at the end of a syllable usually vanish like that in Japanese. So in Japanese, this word on its own means a supermarket, not super as in great. Super as a prefix still exists, as in Superman, but if you say you are going to the 「スーパー」, everyone knows you mean the grocery store. Its neighbor 「デパート」, department store, was clipped the same way. So was the convenience store, 「コンビニ」. A tip: many Japanese supermarkets mark down bento boxes and prepared food in the evening, with discount stickers, so the hour before closing is popular. Recap: supermarket, trimmed down to its first half." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how a trip to the supermarket goes. You pick up a basket, 「カゴ」, and fill it. Then you go to the checkout, called the 「レジ」, short for register. Since 2020, plastic bags in Japan cost a few yen, so the cashier will often ask 「袋はいりますか|ふくろはいりますか」, do you need a bag. If you brought your own, you can say 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」, I'm fine, which here politely means no thanks. Many people carry a folding shopping bag called an 「エコバッグ」. The cashier may also ask if you have a points card, 「ポイントカード」. Watch the particles. You go to the supermarket with 「に」, and you buy things there with 「で」, as in 「スーパーで肉を買いました|スーパーでにくをかいました」. In many stores you bag your own groceries at a separate counter after paying. So at the register, listen for the bag question and the points card question." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word belongs to a big family of clipped loanwords that Japanese uses every day. A personal computer is 「パソコン」, clipped from personal computer. An air conditioner is 「エアコン」. A remote control is 「リモコン」. A smartphone is 「スマホ」. In each case Japanese keeps the first few beats of each part and throws away the rest. Our word kept just the first part of supermarket. The full form 「スーパーマーケット」 does exist, but it sounds stiff and is mostly for signs and formal writing. Be careful: as the first part of a longer word, the same sound can still mean super in the English sense, like 「スーパーマン」. On its own, it is the store. A good habit when you hear a strange short katakana word is to ask what two longer English words might be hiding inside it. So listen for clipped words, and try to rebuild the full English behind them." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese clipped supermarket down to its first half, with the final er becoming a long vowel. On its own it means the grocery store, like the clipped 「デパート」 and 「コンビニ」.",
      "At the 「レジ」, cashiers often ask 「袋はいりますか|ふくろはいりますか」, since bags cost a few yen, and 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」 politely means no thanks. You buy things there with 「で」.",
      "It belongs to the clipped-loanword family with 「パソコン」, 「エアコン」 and 「スマホ」. As the first part of a longer word like 「スーパーマン」, it can still mean super."
    ],
    ja: "[スーパー]で{肉|にく}をかいました。",
    en: "I bought meat at the supermarket." },
  "デパート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English department store, chopped down to just the first part, department, and then trimmed again. Here is how the sounds got squeezed. Each consonant gets a vowel after it, so de and pa come out clearly. The ar sound, with its r, becomes a long vowel. And the final t picks up an extra o sound, which is what Japanese usually does with a t at the end. A fun thing for English speakers: in English, depart means to leave. In Japanese, this word only ever means the big store. A cultural note: Japanese department stores are famous for their basement food halls, called 「デパ地下|デパちか」. The second half, 「地下|ちか」, means underground. You will see the same kanji in 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, the subway, an underground iron road. Down there you will find beautiful sweets, bento, and free samples. Gift-wrapping upstairs is an art form too. Recap: department store, clipped to department, and then squeezed into Japanese sounds." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Japan's department stores grew out of old kimono shops. The best-known example is 「三越|みつこし」. It began in 1673 as a kimono fabric shop in 「江戸|えど」, the old name for Tokyo. That shop became famous for a new idea: fixed prices and payment in cash, instead of haggling and buying on credit. In 1904 the company announced it would become a department store on the American model, selling all kinds of goods under one roof. Other kimono shops followed. Later, railway companies built their own department stores right on top of their big stations, so shoppers could step off the train and go straight in. That is why many of these stores today carry the name of a train line. So the big store above the station often has roots in a kimono shop or a railway, not in a supermarket. Old kimono and railways built this word's world." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Department stores are organized by floors, so you need the floor counter, 「〜階|〜かい」. The first floor is 「一階|いっかい」. Watch the sound changes: the third floor is 「三階|さんがい」, and you ask which floor with 「何階|なんがい」. Basement floors use 「地下|ちか」, underground, so the first basement level is 「地下一階|ちかいっかい」. Each section is a 「売り場|うりば」, a selling place. If you want watches, you can ask a staff member 「時計売り場は何階ですか|とけいうりばはなんがいですか」, which floor is the watch section on. Staff speak very politely, and you will hear 「いらっしゃいませ」, welcome, everywhere. At opening time, staff often stand by the doors and bow as the first customers walk in. Many stores also have a rooftop, 「屋上|おくじょう」, which used to hold small amusement rides and in summer may host a beer garden. So learn the floor counter, and the whole store opens up." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Department stores are the center of Japan's gift-giving seasons. In summer, people send a midsummer gift, 「お中元|おちゅうげん」, and at the end of the year a year-end gift, 「お歳暮|おせいぼ」. These go to people who have helped you, such as a boss, a teacher, relatives or business partners. Popular gifts are things that get used up: fancy fruit, coffee, beer, cooking oil, sweets or good soap. The department store wrapping paper itself says something. A gift in the paper of a famous store tells the receiver that care and money went into it. Stores set up whole floors for these seasons, with catalogs and delivery service, so you can order a gift and have it sent straight to someone's home. A small gift when you visit someone's house, a souvenir, also often comes from the basement food hall. So for many Japanese people, the department store is where gifts begin." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is department store clipped to department, with the r becoming a long vowel and an o added after the final t. Department stores are famous for their basement food halls, 「デパ地下|デパちか」.",
      "Department stores grew from kimono shops like 「三越|みつこし」, founded in 1673, and from railway companies building stores atop stations. That is why many still carry a train line's name.",
      "Learn the floor counter 「〜階|〜かい」, with sound changes like 「三階|さんがい」 and 「何階|なんがい」, and 「地下|ちか」 for basements. Each section is a 「売り場|うりば」.",
      "Department stores are where the gift seasons happen, with 「お中元|おちゅうげん」 in summer and 「お歳暮|おせいぼ」 at year's end. A famous store's wrapping paper itself tells the receiver care went in."
    ],
    ja: "[デパート]で{時計|とけい}をかいました。",
    en: "I bought a watch at the department store." },
  "ゲーム|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word game. Look at how the sounds changed. The English vowel in game, a sliding ay sound, became a long e sound in Japanese. And the final m picked up a u sound, because Japanese syllables like to end on a vowel. In Japanese, this word covers video games most of all, plus board games and card games. Ask a Japanese friend if they like these, and they will probably think of a game console first. A common mistake: in English we say we watched a baseball game. In Japanese, a sports match is usually 「試合|しあい」, which you will learn in Lesson 12. This loanword is more for things you play for fun. A cultural note: an arcade is a 「ゲームセンター」, a game center, often clipped to 「ゲーセン」. Big ones in 「秋葉原|あきはばら」 have floors of claw machines, rhythm games and photo booths. To say you play, use 「する」 or 「やる」 after the word. Recap: English game, with a long vowel and a u on the end." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This loanword combines easily with other words. A game console is 「ゲーム機|ゲームき」, using the kanji for machine. Phone games are 「スマホゲーム」, and older people may still call video games 「テレビゲーム」, TV games. Board games are 「ボードゲーム」 and card games are 「カードゲーム」. When a game ends badly, you hear 「ゲームオーバー」, borrowed straight from the screen. Besides 「する」, you can also say you play with a game using 「で遊ぶ|であそぶ」, as in 「ゲームで遊びました|ゲームであそびました」. To say you played with a friend, put 「と」 after the friend: 「友だちとゲームをしました|ともだちとゲームをしました」. Here is a short exchange. One person asks 「週末何をしましたか|しゅうまつなにをしましたか」, what did you do on the weekend. The other answers 「友だちとゲームをしました|ともだちとゲームをしました」, I played games with a friend. Learn the friend with 「と」, and you can tell that whole story." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a long history with games. The company 「任天堂|にんてんどう」, Nintendo, was founded in 1889 in 「京都|きょうと」 to make playing cards, especially a traditional flower card game called 「花札|はなふだ」. Decades later it moved into toys and then video games. In 1978 the arcade game Space Invaders became such a hit in Japan that some arcades had rows of nothing else. In 1983 Nintendo released a home console that Japanese people still remember fondly by its clipped name, 「ファミコン」, short for family computer. Since then, Japanese games, characters and music have spread all over the world. On a crowded train today you will see people of every age playing games on their phones. So the word may be borrowed from English, but Japan has written a big part of the story of games." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English game, it mostly means video games, and you play with 「する」 or 「やる」. A sports match is 「試合|しあい」 instead.",
      "It combines into 「ゲーム機|ゲームき」, 「スマホゲーム」 and 「ボードゲーム」. Mark who you played with using 「と」, as in 「友だちとゲームをしました|ともだちとゲームをしました」.",
      "Nintendo, 「任天堂|にんてんどう」, began in 1889 in Kyoto making 「花札|はなふだ」 playing cards. Later came Space Invaders and the 「ファミコン」, and Japan wrote much of gaming's story."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[ゲーム]をしました。",
    en: "I played games with my friend." },
  "アルバイト|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This loanword comes from German, not English. The German word Arbeit simply means work. The sounds were squeezed into Japanese in a neat way. The ar became two syllables, with the r sound turning into a 「ル」. The bei part stayed close to the German. And the final t picked up an o sound, which is typical for a t at the end of a word. The meaning also shifted. In German it means any work. In Japanese it narrowed to a part-time job, especially the kind students do, like working at a convenience store or a restaurant. In casual speech it gets clipped to 「バイト」. You will hear students say they have 「バイト」 tonight. Compare 「仕事|しごと」, from Lesson 1, which is a general word for a job or work. A full-time career is 「仕事|しごと」. Shifts at the cafe to pay rent are this word. To say you work part-time, add 「する」 after it. Recap: German for work, narrowed in Japanese to a part-time job." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how students talk about part-time work. To say where you work, use 「で」: 「コンビニでアルバイトをしています」, I work part-time at a convenience store. The hourly wage is 「時給|じきゅう」, from 「時|じ」, hour, and a kanji meaning pay. Getting a job usually starts with a short interview, 「面接|めんせつ」. Your workplace is often called 「バイト先|バイトさき」, and your pay is 「バイト代|バイトだい」. A friend might ask 「どこでバイトしているの」, where do you work. There is also a related word, 「パート」, from the English part-time. It means much the same thing, but it is used more for adults, such as parents working shorter hours, while students usually say they have a 「バイト」. Common student jobs are restaurant work, convenience stores, cram school tutoring and event staff. So use 「で」 for the place, and listen for 「時給|じきゅう」 when the pay comes up." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word is part of a whole group of German loanwords in Japanese. In the late 1800s, Japan modernized quickly and looked to different countries for different fields. Medicine and science were strongly influenced by Germany, so many medical words came from German. A patient's medical chart is 「カルテ」, from the German word for card. An allergy is 「アレルギー」, and energy is 「エネルギー」, both pronounced the German way. Mountaineering and skiing borrowed German too. A backpack is a 「リュックサック」, often clipped to 「リュック」, and a ski slope is a 「ゲレンデ」. The word for a theme or topic, 「テーマ」, also comes from German. Part-time work joined this list through students, who used the German word for work in their own slang. A newer word, 「フリーター」, was made in Japan by gluing English free to the end of the German Arbeiter, worker. It means someone who lives on part-time jobs. So listen for the German behind some katakana words." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from German Arbeit, work, but in Japanese it narrowed to a part-time job like students do, often clipped to 「バイト」. A general job or career is 「仕事|しごと」.",
      "Use 「で」 for the workplace, as in 「コンビニでアルバイトをしています」, and listen for 「時給|じきゅう」, hourly wage. Adults working shorter hours usually say 「パート」.",
      "It is one of many German loanwords from Japan's modernization, like 「カルテ」, 「アレルギー」 and 「リュック」. 「フリーター」, someone living on part-time jobs, was made in Japan from English free plus German Arbeiter."
    ],
    ja: "{土曜日|どようび}に[アルバイト]をします。",
    en: "I work my part-time job on Saturdays." },
  "かいもの|買い物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native Japanese readings. 「買|か」 is the start of 「買う|かう」, to buy. Look at the bottom of the kanji. That piece is 「貝|かい」, a shell. In ancient China, shells were used as money, so the shell mark often appears in kanji about money and value. It also sits on the left of 「財|さい」 in 「財布|さいふ」, wallet. 「物|もの」 means thing. You know it from 「食べ物|たべもの」, food, things to eat, and 「飲み物|のみもの」, drinks, things to drink. In some other words it switches to its Chinese-origin reading, like the 「物|ぶつ」 of 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, zoo. Put them together: buying things. That is shopping. To use it, say you go shopping, with 「に行く|にいく」 after the word, or say you do shopping with 「する」 after it. Notice the pattern of verb stem plus thing. Buying things, eating things, drinking things. Once you see it, you can guess these words on sight. Recap: buy plus thing equals shopping." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This noun gives you two handy patterns. First, you can do shopping: 「買い物をします|かいものをします」. Second, you can go shopping: 「買い物に行きます|かいものにいきます」. In the second one, 「に」 marks the purpose of going. Compare it with the verb 「買う|かう」. The verb needs an object, the thing you buy, with 「を」. So 「本を買います|ほんをかいます」 means I buy a book. The noun is more general. It just means doing your shopping, without naming any item. If you want both, you can say 「デパートで買い物をしました|デパートでかいものをしました」, I did some shopping at the department store, and then add what you bought in the next sentence. Later in Genki you will learn another purpose pattern, the verb stem plus 「に行く|にいく」, as in 「買いに行く|かいにいく」, go to buy. Notice that 「買い|かい」 is the same stem as in this word. So the stem plus 「物|もの」 is a noun, and the stem plus 「に行く|にいく」 is a trip with a purpose." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the top of the buy kanji, 「買|か」. It is a flat box with lines inside, a shape that usually stands for a net. Below it is the shell, 「貝|かい」, the old symbol of money. A traditional explanation reads it as a net scooping up valuables, getting things in exchange for money. Treat that as the classic textbook story. Now meet its partner, 「売る|うる」, to sell. The modern kanji is simplified, but its older full form had the buy kanji sitting inside it, under a part related to going out. So selling was pictured as putting out goods that had been bought. You may see that older form in historical signs. The two verbs are a natural pair. A shop sells, and a customer buys. A sales floor is 「売り場|うりば」, and a buyer is 「買い手|かいて」. So buy is a net over a shell, and sell once carried buy inside it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Two shopping traditions are worth knowing. The first is the lucky bag, 「福袋|ふくぶくろ」. In the first days of January, shops sell sealed bags at a fixed price, and the goods inside are worth more than you pay. You don't know exactly what is in them, which is part of the fun. People line up early in the cold, and popular stores sell out fast. The first kanji means good fortune, and the second is the 「袋|ふくろ」 you hear at the supermarket checkout. The second tradition is the local shopping street, 「商店街|しょうてんがい」. It is a covered or open street lined with small family shops: a butcher, a fruit seller, a tofu maker, a bakery. Many have struggled as big supermarkets spread, but lively ones still feel like the heart of a neighborhood. So shopping in Japan can mean a gamble on a lucky bag, or a slow walk down the local street." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「買|か」 has the shell, 「貝|かい」, at its bottom, since shells were once money, and 「物|もの」 means thing. Buying things is shopping, in the same pattern as 「食べ物|たべもの」 and 「飲み物|のみもの」.",
      "You can do it, 「買い物をします|かいものをします」, or go for it, 「買い物に行きます|かいものにいきます」, with 「に」 marking purpose. Unlike the verb 「買う|かう」, it names no item.",
      "The buy kanji is traditionally read as a net scooping up a shell of money. Its partner 「売る|うる」, to sell, once had the buy kanji inside its older full form.",
      "In early January shops sell sealed lucky bags, 「福袋|ふくぶくろ」, worth more than their price. The local 「商店街|しょうてんがい」, a street of small family shops, still feels like a neighborhood's heart."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}と[買い物|かいもの]にいきました。",
    en: "I went shopping with my mother." },
  "クラス|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word class. Here is how the sounds were squeezed. English cl starts with two consonants together, and Japanese can't do that, so a little u sound slips in after the k. The l becomes a Japanese r sound, as always. And the final s picks up a u sound too. The meaning is mostly the same as English: a group of students who study together, or a level, as in a beginner class. It can also mean a rank or grade, as in top class. A common mistake: when you mean the actual lesson or lecture period, Japanese often uses 「授業|じゅぎょう」, which you will meet in Lesson 11. So your class, meaning your classmates, can be fun, but the lesson that starts at nine is better called 「授業|じゅぎょう」. A cultural note: in Japanese schools, students in a 「クラス」 often stay together all year in the same homeroom, and the teachers come to them. Recap: English class, with extra vowels tucked in." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some ways this word gets used. A classmate is 「クラスメート」, but people also say 「同じクラスの人|おなじクラスのひと」, someone in the same class. At a language school, levels are classes: a beginner class is 「初級クラス|しょきゅうクラス」, and an advanced class is 「上級クラス|じょうきゅうクラス」. To count people in a class, use 「います」, because people are living things: 「クラスに学生が十人います|クラスにがくせいがじゅうにんいます」, there are ten students in the class. Notice 「に」 marking the place where they are. In Japanese schools, homerooms are often named with a native counter, 「組|くみ」. So a student might say they are in second year, group three, 「二年三組|にねんさんくみ」. The formal kanji word for a school class is 「学級|がっきゅう」, which you will see in official notices. So use 「います」 for the people, and listen for 「組|くみ」 in school talk." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese schools, a class is like a small team that runs its own room. Students take turns on daily duty, 「日直|にっちょく」. The duty students may lead the greeting at the start of lessons, write the date on the board, and keep the class diary. One student calls out stand, bow, sit, and the whole class follows. At the end of the day, students clean their classroom, hallways and even toilets themselves, a time called 「掃除|そうじ」. Schools usually have few or no cleaning staff for this, and the idea is that you take care of the space you use. In elementary school, students often serve school lunch to each other too, wearing white caps and aprons. Each class also chooses a class representative. Sports days and school festivals are often class against class. So when a Japanese person remembers their class, they remember a team that cleaned, ate and competed together." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English class, with extra u sounds slipped in, it means a group of students or a level. For the actual lesson period, Japanese often uses 「授業|じゅぎょう」.",
      "Levels are classes, like 「初級クラス|しょきゅうクラス」, and you count people in one with 「います」. Japanese homerooms use 「組|くみ」, as in 「二年三組|にねんさんくみ」.",
      "A Japanese class runs its own room, with 「日直|にっちょく」 duty, students cleaning in 「掃除|そうじ」 time, and serving lunch to each other. So a class is remembered as a team."
    ],
    ja: "[クラス]に{学生|がくせい}が{十人|じゅうにん}います。",
    en: "There are ten students in the class." },
  "ひと|人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native reading. The Chinese-origin readings are 「じん」 and 「にん」, and you already use both. The kanji began as a picture of a person seen from the side, standing or walking on two legs. It is one of the simplest kanji to recognize. Here is the family. In 「日本人|にほんじん」, a Japanese person, it is 「じん」, used for nationality. For counting people, it is 「にん」, as in three people, 「三人|さんにん」. But one person and two people are special native readings, 「一人|ひとり」 and 「二人|ふたり」. You also have 「男の人|おとこのひと」 and 「女の人|おんなのひと」, man and woman, with the native reading. When this kanji is squeezed onto the left side of another kanji, it becomes a thin standing stroke called the person mark. In 「休み|やすみ」, rest or a day off, you see the person leaning against 「木|き」, a tree. A person resting against a tree. Recap: a stick-figure person, with three readings depending on the job." },
      { title: "Politeness ladder", text: "When you point out a person, this word sits in the middle of a politeness ladder. 「あの人|あのひと」, that person, is neutral and fine among friends. But for someone you should respect, like a customer or your teacher's guest, the polite word is 「方|かた」. So 「あの方|あのかた」 means that person in a respectful way. Shop staff will ask 「何名様ですか|なんめいさまですか」 instead of using the plain counter, which is even more polite. On the other side, there are rougher words that you don't need yet. Another everyday use: this word can mean other people in general. 「人の物|ひとのもの」 means someone else's things. And 「いい人|いいひと」 means a nice person, often said about someone kind. Compare 「日本人|にほんじん」 and 「日本の人|にほんのひと」. Both mean a Japanese person, but the second one sounds a bit softer and more conversational. So pick your rung: neutral 「人|ひと」, respectful 「方|かた」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The person kanji hides in many words, with different readings. 「大人|おとな」, adult, is a special reading of the whole word, big person. 「人気|にんき」 means popularity, the feeling people have about something. 「人口|じんこう」 is population, people mouths, since every mouth needs feeding. A famous person is 「有名人|ゆうめいじん」. When the native reading comes second in a compound, it often gets voiced to 「びと」. A sweetheart is 「恋人|こいびと」, love person. And people in general, repeated with the doubling mark, is 「人々|ひとびと」. There is also 「人間|にんげん」, a human being, which is used when you talk about humans as a species or about human nature. Use 「人|ひと」 for everyday talk about people, and 「人間|にんげん」 when you are comparing humans with animals or robots. So one simple kanji gives you adults, popularity, population, sweethearts and humanity." },
      { title: "Lookalike", text: "This kanji has a twin that trips up many learners: 「入|にゅう」, the kanji in 「入る|はいる」, to enter, and 「入口|いりぐち」, entrance. They look almost the same, two strokes leaning on each other. The difference is which stroke sits on top. In the person kanji, the left stroke is the main one, and the right stroke leans against it from partway down. In the enter kanji, it is the other way around: the right stroke rises higher, and the left one leans in. Here is a memory trick, just a trick, not history. The person stands proudly with the left leg forward, like someone walking along. The enter kanji looks like a tent flap opening, with the right side lifted so you can step inside. Look for this at stations, where the entrance sign uses the enter kanji. So check which stroke is on top: a person walking, or a door opening." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a person seen from the side, read 「ひと」 natively, 「じん」 for nationality and 「にん」 for counting. One and two people are special, 「一人|ひとり」 and 「二人|ふたり」.",
      "「あの人|あのひと」 is neutral, but for someone you respect say 「あの方|あのかた」. On its own the word can also mean other people, as in 「人の物|ひとのもの」.",
      "The kanji gives 「大人|おとな」, 「人気|にんき」 and 「人口|じんこう」, and turns into 「びと」 in 「恋人|こいびと」. Use 「人間|にんげん」 when comparing humans with animals or robots.",
      "Its twin 「入|にゅう」, enter, flips which stroke is on top. In the person kanji the left stroke leads like a walker, while enter lifts the right side like a tent flap."
    ],
    ja: "あの[人|ひと]は{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "That person is a teacher." },
  "あなた|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It is the standard textbook word for you, but here is the big surprise for English speakers: Japanese people avoid saying it to someone's face most of the time. Instead, they use the person's name plus 「さん」. So rather than asking are you a student, you would ask if 「田中さん|たなかさん」 is a student, even while talking to that very person. Why? Using this word to someone older or higher in rank can sound distant, a bit cold, or even rude, as if you didn't bother to learn their name. Teachers are called 「先生|せんせい」, not this. There is a twist. Between married couples, a wife traditionally calls her husband this word, and then it means something like dear or honey. You will still see it on forms, ads and song lyrics, where it speaks to everybody at once. A way to remember it: think of it as a word you know but keep in your pocket. Use names first. Recap: it means you, but a name plus 「さん」 is almost always the better choice." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word began as a word for a direction, not a person. In older Japanese it meant over there, or that far side, and it is related to 「かなた」, the far distance, which you may still hear in songs and poems. Speaking about someone by pointing vaguely in their direction was a polite, indirect way to refer to them, because naming a respected person straight out felt too bold. Over the centuries it settled into meaning you, and for a while it was actually respectful. Words of respect tend to wear out with use, though, and this one slowly became plain, and then a bit distant. You may sometimes see it written with kanji, 「貴方|あなた」, using a kanji that means precious or noble, a reminder of its politer past. So the word for you started as over there, a polite little point into the distance." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a whole set of words for you, and each one carries a mood. 「君|きみ」 is used by some men toward friends or people younger than them, and it is common in song lyrics. 「あんた」 is a squeezed, casual form of our word. It can sound warm in some regions, but often it sounds rough or annoyed. 「お前|おまえ」 is very blunt, used between close male friends or in anger, and you should avoid it. Our word is the most neutral of the set, which is why textbooks teach it. The same variety exists for I. 「私|わたし」 is neutral, 「僕|ぼく」 is a softer word used mostly by boys and men, and 「俺|おれ」 is a rougher masculine word. To talk to more than one person, you can add 「たち」, as in 「あなたたち」. So Japanese has many yous, and most of the time the best one is the person's name." },
      { title: "Just drop it", text: "Here is the simplest way to avoid this word: leave it out entirely. Japanese happily drops the subject when it is clear from context. If you are looking right at someone and ask 「学生ですか|がくせいですか」, are you a student, everyone knows you mean them. Adding this word in front can make the question feel pointed, as if you are singling them out. The same goes for answers. You don't need I either: 「はい、学生です|はい、がくせいです」 is perfect. So where does the word feel natural? On surveys and forms, which speak to anyone who fills them in, like 「あなたの名前|あなたのなまえ」, your name. In advertising and pop songs, which speak to every listener at once. And when you truly don't know someone's name, though even then, people usually just drop the subject. So the most natural you in Japanese is often no word at all." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese people usually avoid saying it to someone's face and use their name plus 「さん」 instead, since it can sound cold. Between spouses, though, a wife may use it to mean dear.",
      "It began as a direction word meaning over there, related to 「かなた」, a polite way to point at someone indirectly. Once respectful, it wore down over the centuries to plain and even distant.",
      "Japanese has many yous, from 「君|きみ」 to the rough 「あんた」 and blunt 「お前|おまえ」, and this one is the most neutral. Most of the time the best you is the person's name.",
      "The simplest fix is to drop it, since 「学生ですか|がくせいですか」 clearly means you. It feels natural mainly on forms, in ads and in pop songs."
    ],
    ja: "[あなた]は{学生|がくせい}ですか。",
    en: "Are you a student?" },
  "レポート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word report. The sounds were squeezed like this. The re stays as a simple syllable. The port part loses its r sound, which becomes a long o instead. And the final t picks up an extra o, which Japanese usually adds after a t at the end. The big meaning shift: in Japanese, this word most often means a student's written assignment, like a term paper or a lab report. If a Japanese university student says they have one due tomorrow, they mean an essay to hand in. A news report on TV is usually 「ニュース」, not this word. Typical verbs: you write one with 「書く|かく」, and you hand one in with 「出す|だす」, which you will learn in Lesson 16. A way to remember it: it is the report you have to give your professor, not the one you watch on the evening news. Recap: English report, squeezed into Japanese sounds and narrowed to a school paper." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how this word lives in a Japanese university. A professor announces the topic and the deadline, 「締め切り|しめきり」. A student might say 「レポートは月曜日までです|レポートはげつようびまでです」, the paper is due by Monday. Notice 「まで」, by or until, marking the deadline. Handing it in is formally 「提出|ていしゅつ」, which you will see on course pages. Length is usually given in characters, not words. A professor might ask for 「二千字|にせんじ」, two thousand characters. That sounds like a lot, but each character is short, so it is a few pages. For handwritten work, students may use special grid paper, 「原稿用紙|げんこうようし」, with one square for each character, and rules about where to start paragraphs and put punctuation. Students often groan 「レポートが多い|レポートがおおい」, I have a lot of papers. So remember the deadline with 「まで」, and count in characters, not words." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The same English word report reached Japanese more than once, and the spellings drifted apart. In schools it settled as our word. But on television, a live report from the scene is often spelled 「リポート」, and the person holding the microphone is a 「リポーター」. You will also hear 「レポーター」, so both spellings exist, with TV leaning toward the first one. For a report at work, like a written summary for your boss, Japanese often uses a native kanji word instead: 「報告|ほうこく」, to report or a report. A written report document is 「報告書|ほうこくしょ」, with 「書|しょ」 meaning writing. Office workers are drilled in reporting, contacting and consulting, a famous phrase in Japanese business training. So the loanword is mostly for school papers, the TV spelling is for news on the spot, and the kanji word is for the office. Three versions of report, each with its own home." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English report, it mostly means a student's written assignment, not a news report, which is 「ニュース」. You write one with 「書く|かく」 and hand it in with 「出す|だす」.",
      "Mark the deadline with 「まで」, as in 「レポートは月曜日までです|レポートはげつようびまでです」. Length is counted in characters, like 「二千字|にせんじ」, not words.",
      "School papers use this spelling, while TV news on the spot leans toward 「リポート」. A report at work is the kanji word 「報告|ほうこく」."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}[レポート]を{書きます|かきます}。",
    en: "I will write a paper today." },
  "ごはん|ご飯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「飯|はん」 is the main kanji, and here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. Look at the left side of the kanji. It is the food mark, a squeezed version of 「食|しょく」, the kanji in 「食べる|たべる」, to eat. The same food mark sits on the left of 「飲む|のむ」, to drink. The 「ご」 in front is a polite prefix. As a rule of thumb, Chinese-origin words take 「ご」, while native words take 「お」. Rice is so central to Japanese eating that the word for cooked rice also became the word for a meal. That is why you have 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast, morning rice, 「昼ご飯|ひるごはん」, lunch, and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」, dinner. Even if breakfast is toast, it is still called morning rice. A related word: uncooked rice grains, the kind you buy in a bag, are 「米|こめ」, a different word. Recap: food mark plus the polite prefix. Cooked rice, and by extension, any meal." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the right side of the rice kanji, 「飯|はん」. That piece is 「反|はん」, which gives the sound. You will meet it later in 「反対|はんたい」, opposite or against. So food mark on the left for the meaning, and a sound piece on the right. This kanji also has a native reading, 「めし」. On its own, 「めし」 is a blunt, casual word for a meal, used mostly by men among friends, like asking whether you've eaten yet in a rough, friendly way. It also appears in set words, like a rice ball called 「握り飯|にぎりめし」, the older word for a rice ball. Then there is a third option, the loanword 「ライス」. In Western-style restaurants, rice served flat on a plate next to a hamburger steak is called 「ライス」, while rice in a bowl at home is this word. So one food, three words: polite, rough, and restaurant style." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word shows up in everyday phrases far beyond the rice itself. A parent calling from the kitchen says 「ご飯できたよ|ごはんできたよ」, food's ready. Friends invite each other with 「ご飯食べに行こう|ごはんたべにいこう」, let's go get something to eat, even if they end up eating noodles. Before eating, everyone says 「いただきます」, and afterward 「ごちそうさまでした」, thanks for the meal. If you want a second bowl, you ask for 「おかわり」, a refill. Some restaurants even give free rice refills. A common mistake is to think the word only means a bowl of white rice. In conversation, asking someone if they have eaten 「ご飯|ごはん」 just means have they eaten a meal. Here is a tiny exchange. 「もう晩ご飯を食べましたか|もうばんごはんをたべましたか」, have you eaten dinner yet. 「はい、食べました|はい、たべました」, yes, I have. So listen for it as an invitation, a call to the table, and a meal of any kind." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A traditional Japanese meal is built around the rice bowl. The classic pattern is rice, a bowl of soup, usually miso soup, and a few side dishes, plus pickles. The rice bowl is 「茶碗|ちゃわん」. Its name literally contains the tea kanji, even though it is used for rice. Each family member often has their own personal rice bowl and chopsticks. Table manners matter here. Never stand your chopsticks straight up in a bowl of rice. That is how rice is offered to the dead at funerals and family altars, so it feels unlucky at the table. Also don't pass food from one pair of chopsticks to another, which echoes a funeral rite too. You may lift the rice bowl up close to your mouth, which is normal and polite. And people try to finish every grain, since wasting rice is frowned on. So the rice bowl is the center of the table, with a few rules around it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「飯|はん」 has the food mark from 「食|しょく」, and 「ご」 is the polite prefix for Chinese-origin words. Rice is so central that it also means a meal, as in 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, even with toast.",
      "The right side 「反|はん」 gives the sound. The same food is the blunt 「めし」, the polite 「ご飯|ごはん」, or restaurant-style 「ライス」 on a plate.",
      "In everyday talk it means a meal of any kind, as in 「ご飯食べに行こう|ごはんたべにいこう」, even if you eat noodles. Ask for a refill with 「おかわり」.",
      "A traditional meal centers on the rice bowl, 「茶碗|ちゃわん」, with soup and side dishes. Never stand chopsticks upright in rice, because that is how rice is offered to the dead."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[ご飯|ごはん]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat a meal in the morning." },
  "パン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is one of the oldest loanwords in Japanese, and it does not come from English. It comes from Portuguese, from the word pão, meaning bread. Portuguese traders and missionaries arrived in Japan in the 1500s, and they brought bread with them. The word stuck long before English loanwords arrived. That is why it is written in katakana but doesn't sound like the English word bread at all. Spanish speakers will also recognize it, since Spanish uses pan too. A fun trap: the frying pan you cook with is 「フライパン」, which comes from English frying pan. Same sound, completely different source, so it has nothing to do with bread. A cultural note: Japanese bakeries are amazing, with sweet buns, curry bread, and melon bread, a sweet bun with a crispy top. A bakery is a 「パン屋|パンや」, using the same shop ending as 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore. Recap: Portuguese bread, arriving in the 1500s and never leaving." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Bread arrived with a group of other Portuguese words in the 1500s and 1600s. A buttery sponge cake from 「長崎|ながさき」 is 「カステラ」, named after a Spanish region in the Portuguese way of saying it. A button is 「ボタン」, from the Portuguese word for button. Tobacco is 「タバコ」. A traditional New Year card game is 「カルタ」, from the Portuguese word for a card. Some are so old that people forget they are foreign at all. Then there are bread words built in Japan. A sliced loaf is 「食パン|しょくパン」, eating bread. A bun filled with sweet bean paste is 「あんパン」. A bun with curry inside, deep fried, is 「カレーパン」. And a bakery is a 「パン屋|パンや」. So bread leads you to cakes, buttons and cards, all carried across the sea by Portuguese ships." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The sweet bean bun, 「あんパン」, is a famous Japanese invention. In 1874, a bakery in Tokyo called 「木村屋|きむらや」 had a clever idea. Western bread was still unfamiliar and not very popular, so they filled a soft bun with sweet red bean paste, a flavor everyone knew from Japanese sweets. They even used a yeast made from rice, like the starter for sake. The story goes that in 1875 the emperor tried a version topped with a salted cherry blossom, and it became a hit. That shop still exists in 「銀座|ぎんざ」 today. From there, Japanese bakeries kept inventing: curry bread, bread with sweet cream inside, and even a superhero for children, 「アンパンマン」, whose head is a bean bun he shares with hungry people. So Japanese bread culture grew by mixing a Western food with a Japanese filling." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the words you need for bread at breakfast. A sliced loaf is 「食パン|しょくパン」, and slices are counted with 「枚|まい」, the counter for flat things like paper and shirts. So one slice is 「一枚|いちまい」 and two slices are 「二枚|にまい」. To toast bread, you say 「パンを焼きます|パンをやきます」, using the verb for grilling or baking. Japanese sliced bread is often very thick, sold in packs of four, five or six slices, and people pick their favorite thickness. Here is a breakfast exchange. Someone asks 「朝ご飯はパンですか、ご飯ですか|あさごはんはパンですか、ごはんですか」, is breakfast bread or rice. You answer 「パンです」, it's bread. Notice the particle in 「パンを食べます|パンをたべます」, with 「を」 for the thing you eat. So count slices with 「枚|まい」, and toast them with 「焼きます|やきます」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from Portuguese pão, brought by traders and missionaries in the 1500s, not from English. The 「フライパン」 you cook with is a separate English loan, nothing to do with bread.",
      "Bread came with other Portuguese words like 「カステラ」, 「ボタン」, 「タバコ」 and 「カルタ」. Japan then built its own bread words, like 「食パン|しょくパン」, 「あんパン」 and 「カレーパン」.",
      "In 1874 the Tokyo bakery 「木村屋|きむらや」 filled a soft bun with sweet red bean paste, creating 「あんパン」. Japanese bread culture grew by mixing a Western food with a Japanese filling.",
      "Count slices of 「食パン|しょくパン」 with 「枚|まい」, as in 「一枚|いちまい」. You toast bread with 「パンを焼きます|パンをやきます」."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[パン]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat bread in the morning." },
  "バスてい|バス停": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a mixed word: a loanword glued to a kanji. The first half is 「バス」, from the English word bus, which you met in Lesson 5. The vowel in bus becomes a short a sound, and a little u is added after the final s. The second half is 「停|てい」, with its Chinese-origin reading. It means to stop or to halt. On its left side is the person mark, the squeezed form of 「人|ひと」. A way to remember it: picture a person standing still under a little roofed shelter, waiting for the bus to halt. Put them together and a bus halt is a bus stop. Compare 「駅|えき」, station, from Lesson 10. Trains stop at a station. Buses stop at this. A cultural note: Japanese bus stops usually have a detailed timetable posted, and buses tend to stick closely to it. On many city buses you get on at the back, take a numbered ticket, and pay at the front when you get off. Recap: bus plus halt equals bus stop." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word is actually a clipped version of a longer, more formal word. The official term for a bus stop is 「停留所|ていりゅうじょ」, a stopping and staying place. It is built from 「停|てい」, stop, a kanji meaning to stay, and 「所|じょ」, place, a version of the 「所|ところ」 in 「台所|だいどころ」, kitchen. So a bus stop was once 「バス停留所|バスていりゅうじょ」. That was a mouthful, so everyday speech chopped it down to the first beat of the kanji part, giving our word. You will still see the full word on some signs and announcements. This is the same clipping habit Japanese uses with loanwords, but applied to a kanji word. Streetcars also use the long word for their stops. So the bus stop you wait at is a shortened stopping and staying place." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how to talk about bus stops. To wait at one, use 「で」: 「バス停で友だちを待ちました|バスていでともだちをまちました」, I waited for my friend at the bus stop. Notice that the person you wait for takes 「を」. A common mistake is to use 「に」 there. To walk to the stop, use 「まで」, as far as: 「バス停まで歩きます|バスていまであるきます」. On the bus, you can check your route by asking the driver 「このバスは駅に行きますか|このバスはえきにいきますか」, does this bus go to the station. To get off, press the button, and a recorded voice says 「次、止まります|つぎ、とまります」, stopping next. The kanji in this word, 「停|てい」, also appears in 「停電|ていでん」, a power cut, where the electricity stops. So wait at the stop with 「で」, wait for your friend with 「を」, and walk there with 「まで」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It glues 「バス」 to 「停|てい」, to halt, which has the person mark on its left, so a bus halt is a bus stop. Trains stop at a 「駅|えき」, while buses stop here.",
      "It is clipped from the formal 「停留所|ていりゅうじょ」, a stopping and staying place, once 「バス停留所|バスていりゅうじょ」. Everyday speech chopped the kanji part down to its first beat.",
      "Wait at the stop with 「で」 and for your friend with 「を」, as in 「バス停で友だちを待ちました|バスていでともだちをまちました」. Walk there with 「まで」."
    ],
    ja: "[バス停|バスてい]で{友だち|ともだち}をまちました。",
    en: "I waited for my friend at the bus stop." },
  "びょういん|病院": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「病|びょう」 means illness. Look at the frame around its top and left. That frame is the sickness mark, and you will find it on many kanji about disease and pain. You will meet the same kanji in 「病気|びょうき」, being sick, in Lesson 12. 「院|いん」 means an institution, a large building with a special purpose. You will see it again in 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school, in Lesson 16. So an illness institution is a hospital. Now the famous trap. In Lesson 10 you will learn 「美容院|びよういん」, a hair salon. It shares the same 「院|いん」. The only difference in sound is that this word has a squished 「びょう」, while the salon has two separate beats, 「び」 and 「よう」. Say the wrong one, and you may tell a taxi driver you need a haircut when you need a doctor. A way to remember it: the sick word is squeezed, like someone doubled over. Recap: illness plus institution equals hospital." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, this word officially means a medical facility with at least twenty beds for patients. A small local doctor's office is often called a 「医院|いいん」 or a 「クリニック」. But in conversation, people say they are going to the hospital, 「病院に行きます|びょういんにいきます」, even when they mean the small clinic down the street. At reception, the 「受付|うけつけ」, you show your health insurance card, 「保険証|ほけんしょう」. Then you fill in a short form about your symptoms and wait for your name to be called. A useful line is 「頭が痛いです|あたまがいたいです」, my head hurts. Watch the particles: you go to the hospital with 「に」, and something happens at the hospital with 「で」. So in everyday talk, this word covers almost any trip to the doctor, big or small." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The institution kanji, 「院|いん」, has a mound mark on its left side, a shape that looks like a big ear. It shows up in kanji about hills, steps and walled grounds. With it, you can talk about a hospital stay. To be admitted to the hospital is 「入院|にゅういん」, entering the institution. To leave it is 「退院|たいいん」, withdrawing from the institution. So a friend might say they were 「入院|にゅういん」 for a week. Buddhist temples can be called 「寺院|じいん」 in formal writing, temple institution, and many temple names end in 「院|いん」 too. On the illness side, a sick person is 「病人|びょうにん」, and a sudden illness is 「急病|きゅうびょう」, using the 「急|きゅう」 of 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry. So 「院|いん」 means an important building, and coming in and going out of one are 「入院|にゅういん」 and 「退院|たいいん」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Visiting someone in the hospital has its own word and customs. The visit is 「お見舞い|おみまい」, and so is the gift you bring. Fruit, sweets or a magazine are common choices. There are a few traditional things to avoid. Potted plants are a classic no, because a plant with roots suggests the patient will take root and stay in bed a long time. Flowers that drop their heads, or plants with a strong smell, are avoided too. The number four can be read 「し」, which sounds like the word for death, so some hospitals skip room numbers with a four in them. Nine can be read 「く」, which sounds like suffering, so that number is sometimes avoided as well. When you leave, people often say 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care of yourself. So bring fruit, skip the potted plant, and say 「お大事に|おだいじに」 on the way out." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「病|びょう」 has the sickness frame and 「院|いん」 is an institution, so an illness institution is a hospital. Don't confuse its squeezed 「びょう」 with 「美容院|びよういん」, a hair salon.",
      "Officially a hospital has at least twenty beds, but people say 「病院に行きます|びょういんにいきます」 even for a small clinic. At the 「受付|うけつけ」 you show your 「保険証|ほけんしょう」.",
      "「院|いん」 has the mound mark and means an important building. Entering the hospital is 「入院|にゅういん」 and leaving is 「退院|たいいん」.",
      "A hospital visit and its gift are both 「お見舞い|おみまい」, and potted plants are avoided since roots suggest staying in bed. On leaving, people say 「お大事に|おだいじに」."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}[病院|びょういん]にいきます。",
    en: "I will go to the hospital tomorrow." },
  "ホテル|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word hotel. The sounds were squeezed in a simple way. Each consonant gets its own vowel, so ho and te come out cleanly. The final l becomes a Japanese r sound with a u after it, because Japanese has no l and likes to end on a vowel. Notice that the stress pattern changes. English leans on the second half of the word, while Japanese gives each beat an even weight. In Japan this word means a Western-style hotel with beds. The traditional Japanese inn, with tatami mats, futons and often hot spring baths, is a 「旅館|りょかん」, which you will learn in Lesson 15. Its first kanji is the 「旅|りょ」 of 「旅行|りょこう」, travel. A cultural note: Japan has some unique styles of this word. A business hotel is small, cheap and efficient, made for people traveling for work. A capsule hotel gives you just a sleeping pod. Recap: English hotel, squeezed into Japanese sounds, and the Western cousin of the 「旅館|りょかん」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Checking in at a Japanese hotel follows a set script. At the front desk you say 「予約しました|よやくしました」, I made a reservation, then give your name. Staff speak in very polite language, so expect long, formal sentences. Check-in is often fixed at three in the afternoon, and check-out is often ten or eleven in the morning, and staff stick to those times closely. To say you will stay at a hotel, use the verb 「泊まる|とまる」 with the particle 「に」: 「ホテルに泊まります|ホテルにとまります」. A common English-speaker mistake is to use 「住む|すむ」, to live, for a hotel stay, which sounds like you moved in for good. In the room, you usually find slippers, a thin cotton robe and a kettle with green tea bags, even in cheap places. So remember the verb for staying the night, and expect polite service on a strict schedule." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a whole range of places to sleep, and each one has its own word. A business hotel, 「ビジネスホテル」, has tiny rooms, a tiny bathroom unit, and a good price, and it is perfect for one night near a station. A capsule hotel, 「カプセルホテル」, began in Osaka in 1979. You sleep in a plastic pod stacked two high, and share baths and lockers. A love hotel, 「ラブホテル」, is rented by the hour by couples who want privacy, and these often have wild themes like castles or spaceships. Then there is the 「旅館|りょかん」, the traditional inn, and the 「民宿|みんしゅく」, a cheaper family-run guesthouse in the countryside. Each kind tells you something about Japanese life, from crowded cities to quiet hot spring towns. So when you book a room in Japan, the word in front tells you what kind of night you will have." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English hotel, it means a Western-style hotel with beds. The traditional tatami inn is a 「旅館|りょかん」 instead.",
      "Say you stay at a hotel with 「ホテルに泊まります|ホテルにとまります」, never 「住む|すむ」, which sounds like moving in. Expect polite service on strict check-in and check-out times.",
      "Japan has 「ビジネスホテル」 for cheap stays and the 「カプセルホテル」 with sleeping pods, first opened in Osaka in 1979. The word in front tells you what kind of night you will have."
    ],
    ja: "[ホテル]は{駅|えき}のちかくです。",
    en: "The hotel is near the station." },
  "ほんや|本屋": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji. The first uses its Chinese-origin reading, the second its native reading. 「本|ほん」 is book, from Lesson 1. Its shape is a tree, 「木|き」, with an extra short line across the bottom. That line marks the root, so the kanji originally meant root or origin. Later it came to mean book too. You also see the origin meaning in 「日本|にほん」, the origin of the sun, the land where the sun rises. 「屋|や」 means a shop, and also the person who runs one. It attaches to many things to make shop names. A bakery is a 「パン屋|パンや」. The same kanji, with the same reading, is in 「部屋|へや」, a room, from Lesson 5. So a book shop is a bookstore. A cultural note: big Japanese bookstores have whole floors of manga, and many people stand and browse for ages. Also, staff will often offer to wrap your book in a paper cover, so nobody on the train can see what you are reading. Recap: book plus shop equals bookstore." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The shop kanji 「屋|や」 builds a huge family of shop names. A bakery is 「パン屋|パンや」. A flower shop is 「花屋|はなや」. A fish shop is 「魚屋|さかなや」. A shop selling vegetables is 「八百屋|やおや」, written with eight hundred, meaning a shop with countless items, and it has a special reading. The same ending also describes people by their personality. Someone very shy is a 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」, which you will meet later in Genki. A person who cries easily is a 「泣き虫|なきむし」, but someone who gets angry easily is an 「怒りっぽい人|おこりっぽいひと」, so not every trait uses this ending. Now compare 「店|みせ」, the general word for a shop. You say 「店|みせ」 for any store, while 「屋|や」 attaches to what is sold. A bookstore can also be called a 「書店|しょてん」, a more formal word you see on signs. So the shop kanji tells you what is sold, and it can even describe a person." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Using a bookstore in Japan comes with a few set phrases. At the register, the clerk often asks 「カバーをおかけしますか」, shall I put a cover on it. You can say 「はい、お願いします|はい、おねがいします」, yes please, or 「いいえ、けっこうです」, no thank you. Then comes 「袋はいりますか|ふくろはいりますか」, do you need a bag. Many people now say no to save plastic. To ask for a section, say 「マンガはどこですか」, where is the manga. Remember that books in Japan open from the right, because traditional Japanese text runs from top to bottom and right to left. Manga follows the same rule, so you read the last page first if you open it the English way. Also, when talking about a person who runs a bookstore, 「本屋さん|ほんやさん」 with 「さん」 means the bookseller, or the shop in a friendly way. So be ready for the cover question, and remember the right-to-left page order." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Imagine a giant open book standing up in the street, so big it has a roof on it and a door in the spine. You walk in and a cheerful clerk shouts 「や！」 like a friendly hello. That 「や」 is the shop ending. Book, plus a shout of yah at the door, gives you the bookstore. Now picture the shape. The first kanji is a tree with a line across its root, so imagine the tree's roots growing into the floor of the book building. The second kanji, the shop one, has a kind of roof or overhang at the top left, so picture that roof sitting over the giant book. Every time you see a shop name ending in this sound, hear the clerk shouting yah. So book plus a friendly yah means the bookstore." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「本|ほん」 is a tree with a line marking its root, origin, later meaning book, and 「屋|や」 is a shop. Book plus shop is a bookstore.",
      "「屋|や」 attaches to what is sold, as in 「パン屋|パンや」 and 「花屋|はなや」, and can describe a person, like 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」. 「店|みせ」 is the general word for any shop.",
      "Clerks often ask 「カバーをおかけしますか」, offering a paper cover. Japanese books and manga open from the right, since traditional text runs right to left.",
      "Picture a giant book building with a roof, where a clerk shouts a cheerful 「や」 at the door. Book plus a friendly yah gives you the bookstore."
    ],
    ja: "[本屋|ほんや]で{日本語|にほんご}の{本|ほん}をかいました。",
    en: "I bought a Japanese book at the bookstore." },
  "まち|町": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native reading. The Chinese-origin reading is 「ちょう」, which you will see in many addresses and place names. The kanji has two parts. On the left is 「田|た」, a rice field, which really does look like a field divided into four plots. On the right is a simple piece that gives the kanji its Chinese-origin sound. A way to remember it: picture rice fields with a straight road running beside them, and houses popping up along that road. That is how a town grows. In meaning, this word covers both a small town and a part of a city where people live and shop. You can say you go out into town, meaning downtown. Another kanji with the same native reading is used for busy shopping streets, but this one is the everyday choice for a town. A tip on addresses: in Japan, the address often names a district ending in 「〜町|〜ちょう」, so if you see that ending on a map, you know it is a neighborhood name. Recap: a field with a road beside it, growing into a town." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This town word has a sibling with the same native reading but a different kanji, 「街|まち」. That one has the walking or going element on both sides, with earth stacked in the middle, and it suggests busy streets, shops and city crowds. So 「町|まち」 is a town or neighborhood as a place where people live, while 「街|まち」 leans towards lively shopping streets. Both are said the same way, so only writing shows the difference. Compare the size words too. A village is 「村|むら」, with the tree element on the left. A city is 「市|し」, used in names like 「横浜市|よこはまし」. A big city in general is 「都会|とかい」. The Chinese-origin reading of the town kanji, 「ちょう」, appears in 「町長|ちょうちょう」, the mayor of a town. So village, town, city and big city form a ladder from small to large, and our word sits in the middle." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how this word shows up in conversation. To say you live in a town, use 「に」 with 「住んでいます|すんでいます」: 「小さい町に住んでいます|ちいさいまちにすんでいます」, I live in a small town. To describe a town, adjectives go in front: 「静かな町|しずかなまち」, a quiet town, and 「にぎやかな町|にぎやかなまち」, a lively town. People also say 「町に行きます|まちにいきます」, I go into town, meaning the busy center for shopping. A very common phrase is 「いい町ですね|いいまちですね」, what a nice town, a friendly thing to say when you visit someone's home area. A mini dialogue: someone asks 「どんな町ですか|どんなまちですか」, what kind of town is it. You answer 「古くて、静かな町です|ふるくて、しずかなまちです」, it's an old, quiet town. So learn it with a describing word in front and 「に」 when you live there." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has a rice field, 「田|た」, on the left, like fields with a road beside them where houses grow into a town. In addresses it reads 「ちょう」 for district names.",
      "Its sibling 「街|まち」 leans toward lively shopping streets, while this one is a place where people live. On the size ladder it sits between 「村|むら」 and 「市|し」.",
      "Say where you live with 「に」, as in 「小さい町に住んでいます|ちいさいまちにすんでいます」. Describe it with adjectives in front, like 「静かな町|しずかなまち」."
    ],
    ja: "この[町|まち]はしずかです。",
    en: "This town is quiet." },
  "レストラン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English restaurant, which English itself borrowed from French. Here is how the sounds were squeezed. The re stays simple. The st in the middle is two consonants in a row, which Japanese can't do, so each gets its own vowel: a 「ス」 and then a 「ト」. The r in the middle becomes a Japanese r sound, the vowel turns into a plain a, and the word ends with the 「ン」 sound. The final t of the English word disappears entirely, much like in the French pronunciation. In Japan, this word tends to suggest a sit-down place with a menu, often Western-style food. Other eating places have their own words. A cafeteria at a school or company is a 「食堂|しょくどう」, which you will learn in Lesson 7. A general word for a shop, including small eateries, is 「店|みせ」. A cultural note: a very common type is the 「ファミレス」, short for family restaurant, a casual chain with big picture menus and a drink bar where you refill your own drinks. Many restaurants also show plastic food models in the window, so you can point at what you want. Recap: English restaurant, with vowels added and the final t dropped." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Eating out in Japan has its own little script. As you enter, staff call out 「いらっしゃいませ」, welcome. Then they ask 「何名様ですか|なんめいさまですか」, how many people. You can hold up fingers or say 「二人です|ふたりです」, two people. To call a server, you can press a call button on the table if there is one, or say 「すみません」 politely. To order, point at the menu and say 「これをお願いします|これをおねがいします」. When you finish, say 「ごちそうさまでした」 on the way out. A common surprise for English speakers is that there is no tipping, and leaving money on the table may even get you chased down the street by a worried server. You often pay at the register by the door, not at the table. So learn welcome, how many people, excuse me, and the thank you at the end." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has many words for places to eat, and each one paints a different picture. Our word suggests a sit-down place, often Western food. A 「食堂|しょくどう」 is a simple canteen or cafeteria. A 「喫茶店|きっさてん」 is an old-style coffee shop, often with light meals. A 「カフェ」 is a modern cafe. An 「居酒屋|いざかや」 is a Japanese pub, where people share small plates with drinks after work. A 「ファミレス」 is a family restaurant chain, built from the English words family and restaurant chopped short. Japanese loves this chopping. A convenience store becomes 「コンビニ」, and a personal computer becomes 「パソコン」. Notice how our word itself is never shortened, maybe because it is easy enough already. So choose the word that fits the place: canteen, coffee shop, pub, family chain or sit-down restaurant." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English restaurant, it gets extra vowels and drops the final t, and suggests a sit-down place, often Western-style. A school cafeteria is a 「食堂|しょくどう」, and a casual chain is a 「ファミレス」.",
      "Staff greet you with 「いらっしゃいませ」 and ask 「何名様ですか|なんめいさまですか」. There is no tipping, and you usually pay at the register by the door.",
      "Each eating place has its own word, from 「食堂|しょくどう」 and 「喫茶店|きっさてん」 to 「居酒屋|いざかや」 and 「ファミレス」. This word means a sit-down restaurant, often Western food."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}のちかくの[レストラン]で{食べました|たべました}。",
    en: "I ate at a restaurant near the station." },
  "きのう|昨日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has a special reading. The kanji are chosen for meaning, but the whole word is read as one native unit, not piece by piece. 「昨|さく」 means previous or last, and 「日|にち」 is the sun or day. So previous day, yesterday. Notice that 「昨|さく」 has its own little sun on the left side too. In formal speech, like news or business letters, people use the Chinese-origin reading, 「昨日|さくじつ」, with the same two kanji. This word has two famous siblings with the same trick. 「今日|きょう」, today, and 「明日|あした」, tomorrow, are also special readings that you can't build from the pieces. So yesterday, today and tomorrow are a little trio of exceptions. Learn them as whole words. Usage tip: like other time words such as today and tomorrow, this word usually doesn't take the particle 「に」. You just say yesterday I went, with no particle after it. Recap: previous plus day, read as a single special word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for previous, 「昨|さく」, appears in other time words with its Chinese-origin reading. Last year in formal speech is 「昨年|さくねん」, and last night is 「昨夜|さくや」. In everyday talk, people say 「去年|きょねん」 for last year and 「昨日の夜|きのうのよる」 or 「ゆうべ」 for last night. Now go back one more day. The day before yesterday is 「おととい」, and it has a formal partner, 「一昨日|いっさくじつ」, built with one plus previous plus day. The day after tomorrow is 「あさって」. So the full line runs 「おととい」, then 「昨日|きのう」, then 「今日|きょう」, then 「明日|あした」, then 「あさって」. Notice that the everyday words are native and irregular, while the formal versions use regular Chinese-origin readings. So learn the everyday chain for talking with friends, and recognize the formal ones in news and business." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Yesterday pulls every verb into the past. With polite verbs, the ending is 「ました」: 「昨日、映画を見ました|きのう、えいがをみました」. For negatives, it is 「ませんでした」: 「昨日は勉強しませんでした|きのうはべんきょうしませんでした」, I didn't study yesterday. Notice the 「は」 in that second sentence. Adding 「は」 after a time word sets it up as the topic, often for contrast, as if to say, yesterday, at least, I didn't study. With adjectives, yesterday also needs past forms. 「昨日は寒かったです|きのうはさむかったです」, it was cold yesterday, uses the past of an i-adjective. 「昨日は暇でした|きのうはひまでした」, I was free yesterday, uses the past of a na-adjective. A frequent mistake is saying 「です」 with an i-adjective in the past, so watch out for that. So yesterday is a signal: switch everything in the sentence into the past." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a sound trick, clearly just a trick. The word sounds a bit like key no, as in no key. Picture yourself yesterday, standing outside your front door, patting all your pockets. No key. You spent the whole of yesterday locked out, sitting on the step eating snacks and waving at the neighbors. Every time you think of the day before today, remember that locked door. Now tie it to the kanji. The first one has a small sun on the left side, so imagine that sun slowly setting while you were still stuck outside. The second kanji is a plain sun, today's sun rising. Yesterday's sun set, and you still had no key. So the day before today is the day you had no key." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「昨|さく」 means previous and 「日|にち」 day, but the whole word has a special reading, like 「今日|きょう」 and 「明日|あした」. Like those, it usually takes no 「に」.",
      "「昨|さく」 appears in formal time words like 「昨年|さくねん」 and 「昨夜|さくや」. The everyday chain runs 「おととい」, 「昨日|きのう」, 「今日|きょう」, 「明日|あした」, 「あさって」.",
      "Yesterday switches everything into the past, like 「昨日、映画を見ました|きのう、えいがをみました」 and 「昨日は寒かったです|きのうはさむかったです」. Adding 「は」 after it sets it up as a contrasting topic.",
      "It sounds like key no, so picture yourself locked out all yesterday with no key. The small sun in the first kanji is yesterday's sun setting while you sat outside."
    ],
    ja: "[昨日|きのう]{映画|えいが}を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw a movie yesterday." },
  "〜じかん|〜時間": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「時|じ」 is time or hour. On its left side is 「日|ひ」, the sun, and on its right is 「寺|じ」, temple, which supplies the sound. You know this kanji from 「〜時|〜じ」, o'clock, and from 「時計|とけい」, watch, where it takes a different reading. 「間|かん」 means an interval, a space between. Look at its shape: 「門|もん」, a gate, with 「日|ひ」, the sun, inside. A way to remember it: sunlight shining through the gap in a gate. You will see the native reading of this kanji in 「この間|このあいだ」, the other day, in Lesson 16. So a time interval, a span of time, is a number of hours. Here is the most common mistake. 「三時|さんじ」 is three o'clock, a point on the clock. Add this ending and 「三時間|さんじかん」 is three hours, a length of time. Point versus length. Watch the number readings: four hours is 「四時間|よじかん」, and nine hours is 「九時間|くじかん」. Recap: hour plus interval equals hours, a length of time." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is how lengths of time fit into a sentence. Usually the length comes right before the verb with no particle at all: 「毎日三時間勉強します|まいにちさんじかんべんきょうします」, I study three hours every day. To say about, add 「ぐらい」 after it: 「二時間ぐらい|にじかんぐらい」, about two hours. Compare 「ごろ」, which goes with clock times, not lengths: 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」, around three o'clock. So 「ぐらい」 is for how long, and 「ごろ」 is for what time. To ask how long, say 「何時間|なんじかん」, how many hours, or 「どのぐらい」, how long. Half an hour more is 「半|はん」 after the counter: 「二時間半|にじかんはん」, two and a half hours. And lengths can also take 「も」 to stress a lot: 「五時間も寝ました|ごじかんもねました」, I slept a whole five hours. So put the length before the verb, and use 「ぐらい」 for about." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「間|かん」, interval, builds a whole set of length counters. Hours are 「〜時間|〜じかん」. Weeks are 「〜週間|〜しゅうかん」, so two weeks is 「二週間|にしゅうかん」. Days use a different pattern, with native numbers for the first ten: 「二日|ふつか」 can mean either the second of the month or two days, and context decides. Months use 「か月|かげつ」, so three months is 「三か月|さんかげつ」. Years use 「年|ねん」, often with 「間|かん」 added for clarity: 「三年間|さんねんかん」, three years. Minutes are simply 「〜分|〜ふん」, and the same word can mean a time on the clock or a length, again decided by context. This gap kanji also gives you 「時間|じかん」 alone, meaning time in general, as in 「時間がありません|じかんがありません」, I don't have time. So the interval ending turns points into lengths for hours, weeks and years." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「時|じ」 is hour and 「間|かん」 is an interval, sunlight through a gate, so this counts lengths of time. 「三時|さんじ」 is three o'clock, but 「三時間|さんじかん」 is three hours.",
      "The length goes right before the verb with no particle, as in 「毎日三時間勉強します|まいにちさんじかんべんきょうします」. Use 「ぐらい」 for about with lengths, but 「ごろ」 for clock times.",
      "The 「間|かん」 ending turns points into lengths, as in 「二週間|にしゅうかん」 and 「三年間|さんねんかん」. On its own, 「時間|じかん」 means time in general."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[三時間|さんじかん|〜じかん]{勉強|べんきょう}します。",
    en: "I study for three hours every day." },
  "いちじかん|一時間": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the hours counter with the number one in front. 「一|いち」 is the Chinese-origin reading of one, and the kanji is just one stroke. Easy. Then comes 「時間|じかん」. 「時|じ」 means hour, and 「間|かん」 means an interval, a space between. Together they mean a span of time. So one span of an hour is one hour. The big trap is with 「一時|いちじ」, without the ending. That is one o'clock, a point on the clock. If you say you will wait for 「一時|いちじ」, you mean until one o'clock. If you say you will wait for this word, you mean for sixty minutes. Handy add-ons. An hour and a half is 「一時間半|いちじかんはん」, using 「半|はん」, half, from Lesson 1. About an hour is 「一時間ぐらい|いちじかんぐらい」, using the word for about that you learn in this same lesson. You also have the same pattern with weeks later on: 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」 is one week. Recap: one plus hours, a length of time, not the time on the clock." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen closely to the beats in this word. It has five even beats: 「い」, 「ち」, 「じ」, 「か」, 「ん」. The final 「ん」 counts as a full beat, so don't swallow it. Compare it with one week, 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」. There the one changes shape, and the 「ち」 turns into a small 「っ」, a tiny pause before the 「しゅ」. That happens because one often shrinks before sounds like s, k, t and p. You hear it in 「一回|いっかい」, once, and 「一分|いっぷん」, one minute, too. But before 「じ」, the one stays whole, so one hour keeps a clean 「いち」. Now compare 「一時|いちじ」, one o'clock, with only three beats. If you drop the final two beats, you have changed a length into a clock time. So count your beats: five for one hour, three for one o'clock." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "One hour is a handy unit in daily life, and you will hear it everywhere. A train ride: 「東京まで一時間かかります|とうきょうまでいちじかんかかります」, it takes one hour to Tokyo. The verb 「かかる」 means to take time, and the length goes right before it with no particle. A wait: 「一時間待ちました|いちじかんまちました」, I waited an hour, often said with a sigh. At a karaoke box, rooms are rented by time, so a clerk may ask how long you want, and you can answer 「一時間お願いします|いちじかんおねがいします」. Parking signs and all-you-can-eat menus also list prices per hour or per time limit. To stress that an hour is long, add 「も」: 「一時間も待ちました|いちじかんもまちました」, I waited a whole hour. So use it with 「かかる」 for travel time, and with 「も」 when you want sympathy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is one plus the hours counter, a length of sixty minutes. Don't confuse it with 「一時|いちじ」, one o'clock, a point on the clock.",
      "It has five even beats, and the one stays whole before 「じ」, unlike 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」. Drop the last two beats and you get 「一時|いちじ」, one o'clock.",
      "Use it with 「かかる」 for travel time, as in 「東京まで一時間かかります|とうきょうまでいちじかんかかります」. Add 「も」 to stress how long it felt, as in 「一時間も待ちました|いちじかんもまちました」."
    ],
    ja: "[一時間|いちじかん]テレビを{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I watched TV for one hour." },
  "せんしゅう|先週": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「先|せん」 means ahead, or before, earlier. You know it from 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, literally someone born before you, someone who has gone ahead. 「週|しゅう」 means week. On its left and bottom is the movement mark, a little road, which shows up in kanji about moving and going round. A week goes round and round. So the week before is last week. This kanji has a whole family. 「今週|こんしゅう」 is this week and 「来週|らいしゅう」 is next week, both from Lesson 6. 「毎週|まいしゅう」 is every week, and 「週末|しゅうまつ」 is the weekend, the week's end, from Lesson 3. The same pattern partly works with months: 「今月|こんげつ」 is this month and 「来月|らいげつ」 is next month. But be careful with years. Last year is a different word, 「去年|きょねん」, not built this way. Like most time words, this one usually takes no 「に」 after it. Recap: before plus week equals last week." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This word has some tricky neighbors. 「選手|せんしゅ」 means an athlete or a player, as in a baseball player. It has the same first part but a short vowel at the end, and it is only three beats long. Our word has four beats, because the final 「しゅう」 is a long vowel, held for two beats. So hold that last sound, or you might say last athlete instead of last week. Another neighbor is 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, which shares the same first kanji and sound. Then there is 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week, and 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, which share the long 「しゅう」 at the end. A good habit is to say the trio out loud together, keeping the long ending each time. So hold the long vowel, and last week will never turn into last athlete." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Last week needs the past tense, and it combines nicely with other words. To name a day in last week, link them with 「の」: 「先週の土曜日|せんしゅうのどようび」, last Saturday, literally Saturday of last week. Then you can add 「に」 to that day, because days of the week take 「に」: 「先週の土曜日に京都に行きました|せんしゅうのどようびにきょうとにいきました」. But last week alone takes no 「に」. You can also say 「先週末|せんしゅうまつ」, last weekend, joining last week and weekend into one word. To contrast, add 「は」: 「先週は忙しかったです|せんしゅうはいそがしかったです」, last week, I was busy. The same first kanji gives you 「先月|せんげつ」, last month. So link days with 「の」, skip 「に」 after last week alone, and keep everything in the past." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at the week kanji. Its outer part, curving along the left and bottom, is the movement element, often called the road radical. You also see it in 「近い|ちかい」, near, 「遠い|とおい」, far, and 「道|みち」, road, all about distance and going places. Inside sits a shape that carries the sound and has a sense of going all the way around. So the week is a cycle that goes round. Now the first kanji, ahead or before. It has a native reading too, 「さき」, meaning the tip or what lies ahead. You will hear 「お先に|おさきに」, a polite phrase for going before someone, as in leaving work first. Notice how one kanji can point both to the past, as in last week, and to the future, as in what lies ahead, depending on the word. So the road around gives the week, and before gives the last one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「先|せん」 means before, as in 「先生|せんせい」, and 「週|しゅう」 is week, so the week before is last week. It joins 「今週|こんしゅう」 and 「来週|らいしゅう」, but last year is 「去年|きょねん」.",
      "Hold the long final 「しゅう」 for four beats, or it turns into 「選手|せんしゅ」, athlete. Practice it with 「今週|こんしゅう」 and 「来週|らいしゅう」, which share that long ending.",
      "Link it to a day with 「の」, as in 「先週の土曜日|せんしゅうのどようび」, which then takes 「に」. Last week alone takes no 「に」, and everything stays in the past.",
      "The week kanji has the road radical curving around a piece meaning going all the way round, so a week is a cycle. 「先|さき」 natively means what lies ahead, as in 「お先に|おさきに」."
    ],
    ja: "[先週|せんしゅう]{京都|きょうと}にいきました。",
    en: "I went to Kyoto last week." },
  "とき|時": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native reading of the time kanji. In compound words it uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「じ」. The shape: 「日|ひ」, the sun, on the left, and 「寺|じ」, temple, on the right. The sun tells you it is about time, and the temple part gives the Chinese-origin sound. As a word on its own, it means time, but its biggest job is as a grammar word meaning when. You put a description in front of it. For example, 「子供の時|こどものとき」 means when I was a child, literally the time of being a child. Later in Genki you will use it after verbs and adjectives, like when I am busy, or when I went to Japan. A common mistake: English speakers try to use it like the question word when. But to ask when, you use 「いつ」, from Lesson 3. This word only works in the middle of a sentence, explaining the time of something. Its family: 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes, is this same native reading doubled. And 「時間|じかん」 and 「時計|とけい」 use other readings of the same kanji. Recap: sun plus temple, the time when something happens." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word joins with every kind of describing piece, and each has its own glue. After a noun, use 「の」: 「学生の時|がくせいのとき」, when I was a student. After an i-adjective, no glue at all: 「寒い時|さむいとき」, when it is cold. After a na-adjective, use 「な」: 「暇な時|ひまなとき」, when I am free. After a verb, use the plain form: 「日本に行く時|にほんにいくとき」, when I go to Japan, which you will meet in Lesson 16. Notice that the glue is exactly the same as when these words describe any noun, like 「静かな町|しずかなまち」. That is because this word is a noun. Then add 「に」 if you like, or 「は」 for contrast: 「暇な時は本を読みます|ひまなときはほんをよみます」, when I'm free, I read books. So treat it like any noun, and use the usual linking piece before it." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji appears in many words you know, with different readings. With the native reading, 「時々|ときどき」 means sometimes, made by doubling the word with the repeat mark. 「その時|そのとき」 means at that time, and 「時々|ときどき」 and 「その時|そのとき」 are both very common. With the Chinese-origin reading 「じ」, you get 「何時|なんじ」, what time, and 「時間|じかん」, time or hours. Later you will meet 「時代|じだい」, an era or period, as in the samurai era. Then there is a special one, 「時計|とけい」, a clock or watch, where the two kanji are read together in a way you can't build from parts. Compare our word with 「時間|じかん」. Both relate to time, but 「時間|じかん」 is time as an amount, as in I have no time. Our word is a moment or occasion. So the same kanji gives you sometimes, what time, hours, and clock." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is everywhere in storytelling and memories. People start stories with 「子供の時|こどものとき」, when I was a child, or 「高校生の時|こうこうせいのとき」, when I was a high school student. Asking someone about their past is a classic conversation topic. A mini dialogue: someone asks 「子供の時、何をしましたか|こどものとき、なにをしましたか」, what did you do as a child. You answer 「よく川で泳ぎました|よくかわでおよぎました」, I often swam in the river. It also gives helpful everyday phrases. 「困った時は言ってください|こまったときはいってください」, please tell me when you are in trouble, is something a kind host might say. Signs in buildings say 「地震の時|じしんのとき」, in case of an earthquake, before giving instructions. So this little word opens up your past, and helps you talk about what to do in a situation." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is the sun beside 「寺|じ」, and its big job is meaning when, after a description, as in 「子供の時|こどものとき」. To ask when, use 「いつ」 instead.",
      "As a noun it takes the usual linking piece, like 「学生の時|がくせいのとき」, 「寒い時|さむいとき」 and 「暇な時|ひまなとき」. After a verb use the plain form, as in 「日本に行く時|にほんにいくとき」.",
      "The kanji gives 「時々|ときどき」, 「何時|なんじ」 and the special 「時計|とけい」. Unlike 「時間|じかん」, an amount of time, this word is a moment or occasion.",
      "Stories start with 「子供の時|こどものとき」 or 「高校生の時|こうこうせいのとき」. It also gives phrases like 「困った時は言ってください|こまったときはいってください」."
    ],
    ja: "{子供|こども}の[時|とき]、{犬|いぬ}がいました。",
    en: "When I was a child, I had a dog." },
  "げつようび|月曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「月|げつ」 is the moon. The kanji began as a picture of a crescent moon. On its own it is read 「月|つき」 natively. 「曜|よう」 is the kanji for days of the week. It has 「日|ひ」, the sun, on its left side and carries the idea of shining. The days of the week were named after shining things in the sky. 「日|び」 is day. The sound softens to 「び」 here. So moon day is Monday, exactly like English, where Monday also comes from moon day. Here is the system. The seven days are named after the sun, the moon, and the five planets visible to the naked eye, whose Chinese names come from the five elements: fire, water, wood, metal and earth. You already know 「日曜日|にちようび」, sun day, and 「土曜日|どようび」, earth day, which is Saturn. The moon reading 「げつ」 also appears in 「今月|こんげつ」 this month and 「来月|らいげつ」 next month, since months follow the moon. Recap: moon plus shining plus day equals Monday." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a famous name for the Sunday evening blues before Monday. It is called the 「サザエさん」 syndrome. 「サザエさん」 is a gentle family cartoon that has been on television since 1969, airing on Sunday evenings at half past six. Its cheerful ending song means the weekend is nearly over, so for many people, hearing it brings a sinking feeling about Monday, work and school. The seven-day week itself became official in Japan in the 1870s, when the government adopted the Western calendar and made Sunday a day of rest for officials. The planet names for the days were already known earlier from Chinese and Buddhist astrology, so they were ready to use. Today, many national holidays are fixed on Mondays to create three-day weekends, a system called Happy Monday. So Monday in Japan brings both a cartoon-sized sigh and some lovely long weekends." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The moon kanji has three common readings, and each one belongs to a family. Natively, it is 「つき」, the moon in the sky, as in a beautiful moon. With 「げつ」, it means a month as a unit or Monday: 「今月|こんげつ」, this month, 「先月|せんげつ」, last month, and 「一か月|いっかげつ」, one month as a length. With 「がつ」, it names the months of the year: 「一月|いちがつ」, January, 「四月|しがつ」, April. A useful rule: 「がつ」 is for the name of a month, and 「げつ」 is for months as a span or in compounds. In everyday speech, people often drop the last kanji and just say 「月曜|げつよう」, Monday, and calendars may show only 「月|げつ」 in a small box. So the moon gives you the night sky, the months, and the start of the week." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Days of the week take the particle 「に」 when something happens on them: 「月曜日に試験があります|げつようびにしけんがあります」, there is an exam on Monday. To say every Monday, use 「毎週月曜日|まいしゅうげつようび」, every week's Monday. To say Monday morning, link with 「の」: 「月曜日の朝|げつようびのあさ」. To ask which day, the question is 「何曜日|なんようび」, what day of the week. A mini dialogue: someone asks 「今日は何曜日ですか|きょうはなんようびですか」. You answer 「月曜日です|げつようびです」. Notice that 「今日|きょう」, today, takes no 「に」, but a day name does. Also note that shops, museums and many restaurants in Japan close one weekday each week, and Monday is a very common choice for museums. So use 「に」 with the day name, and check opening days before visiting a museum on Monday." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「月|げつ」 is the moon, so this is moon day, just like English Monday. The week is named after the sun, moon and five visible planets.",
      "The Sunday-evening dread before Monday is called the 「サザエさん」 syndrome, after a cartoon airing since 1969. Many holidays are fixed on Mondays for three-day weekends, called Happy Monday.",
      "The moon kanji reads 「つき」 for the moon, 「げつ」 for months as spans and Monday, and 「がつ」 for month names like 「四月|しがつ」. People often shorten Monday to 「月曜|げつよう」.",
      "Day names take 「に」, as in 「月曜日に試験があります|げつようびにしけんがあります」, unlike 「今日|きょう」. Many Japanese museums close on Mondays, so check before you go."
    ],
    ja: "[月曜日|げつようび]にテストがあります。",
    en: "There is a test on Monday." },
  "かようび|火曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「火|か」 means fire. The kanji began as a picture of flames rising up, and it still looks like a little campfire with sparks flying out of each side. On its own, it is read natively as 「火|ひ」. 「曜|よう」 is the day-of-the-week kanji, about shining things in the sky. 「日|び」 is day. So fire day is Tuesday. Why fire? The planet Mars has the Chinese name fire star, 「火星|かせい」, and its red color fits that name nicely. Tuesday is the day of Mars, and in languages like French and Spanish it is still named after Mars. English Tuesday comes from a Norse god of war, which matches Mars, the Roman god of war. So the whole week lines up: Monday is the moon, Tuesday is fire, which is Mars, and so on through the planets. A way to remember it: Tuesday is the fiery red planet day. Recap: fire plus shining plus day equals Tuesday." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a trap. The verb 「通う|かよう」 means to commute or to go regularly, as in going to school every day. It sounds exactly like the start of Tuesday. So when you hear 「かよう」, listen for what comes next. If you hear 「び」, it is Tuesday. If you hear 「学校に通う|がっこうにかよう」, it is going to school regularly. You can even make a sentence with both: 「火曜日に学校に通います|かようびにがっこうにかよいます」. Another mix-up is with Wednesday, 「水曜日|すいようび」. The two kanji, fire and water, are both simple pictures, and beginners often swap them. Remember that fire comes first in the week, then water puts it out. Also, the fire kanji has the native reading 「ひ」, but in Tuesday it is always the short 「か」. So listen for the 「び」 at the end, and let fire come before water." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The fire kanji builds many words with both its readings. Natively, 「火|ひ」 is fire, as in to light a fire. Fireworks are 「花火|はなび」, fire flowers, with the sound softening to 「び」. With the Chinese-origin reading 「か」, a house fire or blaze is 「火事|かじ」, which you will meet later in Genki. A volcano is 「火山|かざん」, a fire mountain. Mars is 「火星|かせい」, the fire star. So the fire kanji appears in the sky, in the mountains, and in summer festivals. Here is the reading tip. In compounds with other Chinese-origin pieces, use 「か」. In native words and on its own, use 「ひ」. Fireworks are a native compound, so they use 「び」, the softened 「ひ」. So fire gives you blazes, volcanoes, fireworks and Tuesday." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick to fix the sound and the picture together. Picture a crow, 「カー」 it caws, sitting on top of a campfire on Tuesday morning. Its feathers start smoking, and it yells caw, then flies off with its tail on fire. So 「か」, like a caw, is the fire sound in Tuesday. Now look at the kanji shape. It has a central stroke like a person standing in the flames, with two little sparks flying out on each side. Imagine those sparks are the crow's burning tail feathers. Then add the shining kanji and the day kanji, and you have a fire day. Every Tuesday, picture that poor crow shooting off into the sky like a little firework. So caw plus campfire means Tuesday." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「火|か」 is a picture of flames, so this is fire day, from Mars, 「火星|かせい」, the red fire star. French and Spanish name Tuesday after Mars too.",
      "「通う|かよう」, to commute, sounds just like the start of Tuesday, so listen for the 「び」. Don't swap it with 「水曜日|すいようび」, since fire comes first and water puts it out.",
      "The fire kanji reads 「か」 in compounds like 「火事|かじ」 and 「火山|かざん」, and 「ひ」 in native words. 「花火|はなび」, fireworks, softens it to 「び」.",
      "Picture a crow cawing 「カー」 on a campfire one Tuesday and flying off with its tail on fire. The kanji's sparks on each side are its burning feathers."
    ],
    ja: "[火曜日|かようび]に{日本語|にほんご}のクラスがあります。",
    en: "I have Japanese class on Tuesday." },
  "すいようび|水曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「水|すい」 means water. The kanji began as a picture of flowing water, with a main stream in the middle and splashes on either side. On its own, natively, it is 「水|みず」, which you learned in Lesson 3. 「曜|よう」 is the day-of-the-week kanji, and 「日|び」 is day. So water day is Wednesday. The planet behind it is Mercury, whose Chinese name is water star, 「水星|すいせい」. In French and Spanish, Wednesday is still named after Mercury. The water kanji has a squeezed form too. On the left side of a kanji, it becomes three little drops. You can see those drops in 「海|うみ」, sea, 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, to swim, and 「洗う|あらう」, to wash. Everything wet. A way to remember it: Wednesday is the middle of the week, and you need a drink of water to get through it. Recap: water plus shining plus day equals Wednesday." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The water kanji has two readings, and each one builds a family. Natively, 「水|みず」 is water to drink. Swimwear is 「水着|みずぎ」, water clothes. With the Chinese-origin reading 「すい」, swimming as a sport is 「水泳|すいえい」, water plus swim. Tap water and plumbing is 「水道|すいどう」, a water road. Mercury, the planet, is 「水星|すいせい」. A useful rule: when it pairs with another Chinese-origin kanji, you get 「すい」. In native words, you get 「みず」. Notice that the example sentence for this word is about swimming in a pool on Wednesday, and swimming is 「水泳|すいえい」, so Wednesday and swimming share their first sound. Also notice a related word for cold drinking water at a restaurant, 「お冷|おひや」, a polite word that does not use this kanji at all. So the water kanji gives you swimming, swimsuits, plumbing and the middle of the week." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. The start sounds a bit like sweet. Picture yourself on Wednesday, the middle of the week, so tired that someone hands you a giant glass of sweet water with a straw. You take a long sip and go 「すいー」, as you slide down the slide into a swimming pool. Now the shape. The water kanji has a long hooked stroke down the middle like a river, with splashes on each side. Imagine those splashes flying off you as you hit the pool. Then the shining kanji and the day kanji follow. So every Wednesday, you are sliding into the water with a sweet sigh. Remember the sweet sigh and the splash, and the middle of the week is water day." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how Wednesday works in real conversations. Plans: 「水曜日に会いましょう|すいようびにあいましょう」, let's meet on Wednesday, using 「に」 for the day. To say every Wednesday, people often say 「毎週水曜日|まいしゅうすいようび」. In schedules, the shortened form 「水曜|すいよう」 is very common in speech, and calendars often show just 「水|すい」. In Japan, Wednesday is a famous day for movie discounts. Many cinemas once offered 「レディースデー」, ladies' day, with cheaper tickets on Wednesdays, though many have since changed to a discount for everyone. Some companies also have a no overtime day on Wednesdays to help staff go home early. A mini dialogue: someone asks 「水曜日は暇ですか|すいようびはひまですか」, are you free on Wednesday. You answer 「はい、暇です|はい、ひまです」. So make plans with 「に」, and look out for midweek bargains." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「水|すい」 is a picture of flowing water, so this is water day, from Mercury, 「水星|すいせい」. On the left of a kanji it becomes three drops, as in 「海|うみ」 and 「泳ぐ|およぐ」.",
      "Natively it is 「みず」, as in 「水着|みずぎ」, while with other Chinese-origin kanji it is 「すい」, as in 「水泳|すいえい」 and 「水道|すいどう」. Wednesday and swimming share that first sound.",
      "Picture yourself midweek sipping sweet water, sighing 「すいー」 as you slide into a pool. The kanji's splashes fly off you as you hit the water.",
      "Make plans with 「に」, as in 「水曜日に会いましょう|すいようびにあいましょう」, and 「水曜|すいよう」 is the common short form. Wednesday is famous for movie discounts in Japan."
    ],
    ja: "[水曜日|すいようび]にプールでおよぎます。",
    en: "I swim in the pool on Wednesday." },
  "もくようび|木曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「木|もく」 means tree or wood. The kanji began as a picture of a tree, with a trunk, branches spreading at the top, and roots at the bottom. On its own, natively, it is read 「木|き」. 「曜|よう」 is the day-of-the-week kanji, and 「日|び」 is day. So wood day is Thursday. The planet behind it is Jupiter, whose Chinese name is wood star, 「木星|もくせい」. In French and Spanish, Thursday is still named after Jupiter. English Thursday comes from Thor, the Norse god of thunder, who was matched with Jupiter. The tree kanji is a building block you already know. 「本|ほん」, book, is a tree with a line marking the root. 「休み|やすみ」, rest, is a person leaning on a tree. Be careful not to mix up 「木|もく」 Thursday and 「金|きん」 Friday, since both are near the weekend. Wood comes first, then gold. Recap: tree plus shining plus day equals Thursday." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's spend more time with the tree kanji, because it is a champion builder. Stack two trees side by side and you get 「林|はやし」, a grove or small woods. Stack three and you get 「森|もり」, a forest. That is a real pattern, more trees, more woods. The tree kanji also has more readings than you might expect. Besides 「もく」 here and native 「き」, it is read 「ぼく」 in a few words, like 「大木|たいぼく」, a huge tree. And in some native compounds it shrinks to 「こ」, as in 「木の葉|このは」, tree leaves. Now look at the middle kanji, 「曜|よう」. On its left is the sun mark, 「日|ひ」. That fits, because this kanji is about shining and the heavenly lights. The right side is complicated and mostly carries the sound, so don't worry about memorizing its pieces yet. The good news is that you will only ever need it for the days of the week. So the whole word is tree, shining light, day. Learn the tree well, and three, four and five trees' worth of kanji open up." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Why does a Japanese week have planet names at all? The seven-day week with its seven heavenly bodies came from West Asia into India and China, and from there to Japan. In the early ninth century, the monk Kūkai brought back Buddhist astrology texts from China that used the seven luminaries, the sun, the moon, and the five visible planets. Each of those five planets was linked to one of the five Chinese elements, wood, fire, earth, metal and water. That is why Jupiter became the wood star. Heian court nobles noted the day of the week in their diaries, mostly for fortune-telling. But ordinary life did not run on a seven-day week for a long time. Only after Japan adopted the Western calendar in 1873, and made Sunday a day off a few years later, did the week become part of daily life. So this ancient name became an everyday word only about a hundred and fifty years ago. The idea traveled a long road to reach Thursday." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In daily life, people often drop the last kanji and just say 「木曜|もくよう」. On calendars, timetables and shop signs, Thursday is usually shrunk to a single kanji, the tree, often inside a little circle or brackets. So a sign showing the tree kanji next to closed tells you the shop takes Thursdays off. When you do something on a specific day, add 「に」. 「木曜日に|もくようびに」 means on Thursday. But if it is every Thursday, use 「毎週|まいしゅう」 in front, every week, and you can drop the 「に」. To ask what day it is, say 「何曜日ですか|なんようびですか」. A tiny exchange: someone asks when the test is. You answer 「木曜日です|もくようびです」. And if you want to sound like a local, try 「木曜の夜|もくようのよる」, Thursday night. Keep an eye out for that single tree on signs, and you will read Thursday everywhere." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「木|もく」 is tree or wood, 「曜|よう」 marks the days of the week, and 「日|び」 is day, so wood day is Thursday, named for Jupiter, the wood star 「木星|もくせい」. Tree plus shining plus day equals Thursday.",
      "The tree kanji is a champion builder: two trees make 「林|はやし」, a grove, and three make 「森|もり」, a forest. The middle kanji 「曜|よう」 has the sun mark on its left because it is about shining heavenly lights.",
      "The planet-named week came from West Asia through India and China, linking each planet to an element, which made Jupiter the wood star. It only became part of daily Japanese life after the Western calendar arrived in 1873.",
      "People often shorten it to 「木曜|もくよう」, and signs and calendars squeeze it to just the tree kanji, so a tree next to closed means the shop takes Thursdays off. Say 「木曜日に|もくようびに」 for on Thursday."
    ],
    ja: "[木曜日|もくようび]は{忙しい|いそがしい}です。",
    en: "I'm busy on Thursday." },
  "きんようび|金曜日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「金|きん」 means gold, and more broadly metal. On its own, natively, it is 「金|かね」, the money word you know from 「お金|おかね」. It also shows up squeezed onto the left side of metal kanji, like 「銀|ぎん」, silver, in 「銀行|ぎんこう」, bank. 「曜|よう」 is the day-of-the-week kanji, and 「日|び」 is day. So gold day is Friday. The planet behind it is Venus, whose Chinese name is metal star, 「金星|きんせい」. In French and Spanish, Friday is still named after Venus. A fun bit of slang: people sometimes call Friday night 「花金|はなきん」, short for flower Friday, the night the week blooms into the weekend. It is like saying thank goodness it's Friday. A way to remember it: Friday is payday in many places. Gold, money, Friday. Recap: gold plus shining plus day equals Friday." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The gold kanji is one you will use constantly, mostly about money. In 「お金|おかね」 it is native 「かね」. A rich person is 「お金持ち|おかねもち」, someone holding money. With the Chinese-origin reading 「きん」, it builds lots of practical words. 「料金|りょうきん」 is a fee or charge, like a taxi fare. 「現金|げんきん」 is cash, literally present money. 「貯金|ちょきん」 means savings, money stored up. And 「税金|ぜいきん」 is tax. In each one, the gold kanji simply means money. As a side piece on the left, it marks metal. You know 「銀|ぎん」, silver, and you will see 「鉄|てつ」, iron, in 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, the subway, literally the underground iron road. So the gold kanji on its own is money and metal, and on the side it signals something made of metal. Friday sits right in the middle of a very useful family." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Friday night has a special feel on Japanese TV. For decades, one channel has run a famous Friday night movie slot, and it is especially known for showing Studio Ghibli films. When a Ghibli classic airs, people post about it online all evening, and some fans even shout along with famous lines. There was also a government campaign in 2017 called Premium Friday. It encouraged companies to let workers leave at three in the afternoon on the last Friday of each month, to go shopping or eat out. It got a lot of media attention, but very few workplaces actually adopted it, and it is now mostly remembered as a joke about long working hours. So Friday in Japan means a well-earned dinner out, maybe a drink with coworkers, and a Ghibli film at home. Friday is the night Japan finally relaxes." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the reading. Picture a king on Friday afternoon. He is the king of a shiny metal kingdom, and the sound of the gold kanji here is 「きん」, which sounds a bit like king. Every Friday, this king opens his golden treasure chest and hands out gold coins to everyone, because it is payday. Coins spill across the floor, clinking and glittering. The whole kingdom cheers, and they all head out for a big Friday night feast. Now, the next morning is Saturday, and the treasure chest is empty. So remember: Friday is the gold king's payday party. Wood day, Thursday, comes just before, when everyone is still chopping wood and working hard. Then the gold arrives. Gold king, payday, Friday." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「金|きん」 is gold or metal, 「曜|よう」 marks the weekdays, and 「日|び」 is day, so gold day is Friday, named for Venus, the metal star 「金星|きんせい」. Think of Friday as payday: gold, money, Friday.",
      "The gold kanji usually means money, as in 「お金|おかね」, 「料金|りょうきん」 for a fee, 「現金|げんきん」 for cash and 「貯金|ちょきん」 for savings. Squeezed onto the left side, as in 「銀|ぎん」, it marks something made of metal.",
      "Friday night means relaxing in Japan, with a famous TV movie slot known for Studio Ghibli films. The 2017 Premium Friday campaign to let workers leave early mostly flopped and is remembered as a joke about long hours.",
      "Picture a gold king, since 「きん」 sounds like king, who opens his treasure chest every Friday and hands out gold coins for payday. Thursday, wood day, comes first while everyone is still chopping wood."
    ],
    ja: "[金曜日|きんようび]に{友だち|ともだち}と{出かけます|でかけます}。",
    en: "I go out with friends on Friday." },
  "あう|会う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading of 「会|かい」. In compounds it takes the Chinese-origin reading, 「かい」. The kanji means to meet or to gather. You already know the Chinese-origin reading from 「会社|かいしゃ」, a company, literally a gathering of people working together, and 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, office worker. Now the most important grammar point. You meet a person with the particle 「に」, not 「を」. So you say you meet 「友だちに|ともだちに」, to your friend. English speakers often use 「を」 because meet takes a direct object in English. In Japanese, think of it as going toward someone for a meeting. It is an u-verb, so the polite form is 「会います|あいます」, and the te-form is 「会って|あって」. Also note: this verb is for meeting or seeing people. For seeing a movie or a thing, use 「見る|みる」. If you say you will see a friend tomorrow, use this verb. A useful phrase: 「また会いましょう|またあいましょう」 means let's meet again, a friendly way to say goodbye. Recap: gather and meet, with 「に」 for the person you meet." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The meet kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「かい」 builds a lot of words about people coming together. 「会話|かいわ」 is conversation, meeting plus talk, the name of the dialogue sections in your textbook. 「会議|かいぎ」 is a meeting at work. 「社会|しゃかい」 is society, where people gather into groups. And 「教会|きょうかい」 is a church, a gathering for teaching. Watch the order: 「会社|かいしゃ」, company, and 「社会|しゃかい」, society, use the same two kanji flipped around. Now one native relative. Add 「出|で」, to go out, and you get 「出会う|であう」, to come across someone or meet by chance, often used for meeting the love of your life. In every one of these, the shared piece is people gathering. Flip, add and combine, and this one kanji keeps giving." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a sound trap. 「合う|あう」 sounds exactly the same, but it means to fit or to match. A shirt fits you, or your opinions match. That verb takes 「に」 too, which makes it even easier to mix up. So the color suits you is 「色が合う|いろがあう」. Only context and kanji tell you which one you hear. A quick test: if a person is coming to see you, it is the meeting verb. If two things fit together, it is the matching one. Also, there is a related particle choice. You can meet 「友だちに|ともだちに」 or 「友だちと|ともだちと」. With 「と」, it sounds like you both planned it together, a mutual meeting. With 「に」, it can be one-sided, like you went to see someone, or bumped into them. Both are natural in many sentences. Remember: people meet, things match." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "There is a beautiful saying from the world of the tea ceremony: 「一期一会|いちごいちえ」. Notice the meet kanji is read 「え」 here, a rare old reading. The saying means one time, one meeting. The idea is that every gathering happens only once in a lifetime. Even if you meet the same friend again, this exact moment will never come back, so you should treat it with care and full attention. Tea masters used it to remind hosts to make each guest's visit special. Today, people use it on all sorts of occasions, from wedding speeches to travel blogs, and it is a popular phrase for calligraphy. Japanese also has very polite ways to talk about meeting. When you are introduced to someone important, you might hear 「お会いできてうれしいです|おあいできてうれしいです」, I'm happy to meet you. Every meeting is precious, so meet people well." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means to meet or gather, as in 「会社|かいしゃ」, a gathering of people working together. The key grammar point is that you meet a person with 「に」, not 「を」, so you meet 「友だちに|ともだちに」.",
      "With the reading 「かい」 the kanji builds words about people gathering, like 「会話|かいわ」 for conversation and 「会議|かいぎ」 for a work meeting. 「会社|かいしゃ」, company, and 「社会|しゃかい」, society, are the same two kanji flipped.",
      "「合う|あう」 sounds identical but means to fit or match, as in 「色が合う|いろがあう」, so remember that people meet and things match. Meeting 「友だちと|ともだちと」 sounds mutual, while 「に」 can be one-sided.",
      "The tea ceremony saying 「一期一会|いちごいちえ」, one time, one meeting, teaches that each gathering happens only once in a lifetime. So treat every meeting with care and full attention."
    ],
    ja: "{昨日|きのう}{友だち|ともだち}に[会いました|あいました|あう]。",
    en: "I met a friend yesterday." },
  "ある|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the verb for the existence of things that don't move by themselves: objects, buildings, plants and events. It is usually written in kana. The pattern is: place, then 「に」, then the thing, then 「が」, then this verb. For example, there is a book on the desk. Its partner, 「いる」, which you learn in this same lesson, is for people and animals. A handy test: can it walk away on its own? Then use 「いる」. If not, use this one. A tree, a car, or a building takes this word. It also covers having things. If you have time, you say time exists. If you have a fever, 「熱がある|ねつがある」, from Lesson 12, a fever exists. And events too: there is a party on Saturday, using this verb. Conjugation: the polite form is 「あります」. The negative is irregular. It is just 「ない」, with nothing in front. A way to remember it: things that just are, sitting still, take this word. Recap: there is, for objects, plants and events, and for having things." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's work through the forms. The polite set is 「あります」, 「ありません」, 「ありました」, and 「ありませんでした」. In casual speech, the negative is the odd one, just 「ない」. So its past negative is 「なかった」, and its casual past is 「あった」. Next, a word-order tip. When you are introducing something new, use 「が」: there is a cafe near the station. But when you already know about the thing and want to say where it is, flip it around with 「は」. 「トイレはあそこにあります」 means the toilet is over there. Ask where something is with 「どこにありますか」. One more pattern. For events, the place takes 「で」, not 「に」. So a party at the university is 「大学でパーティーがあります|だいがくでパーティーがあります」. Things sit at a place with 「に」, events happen at a place with 「で」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This verb is almost always written in kana, but it does have kanji, and you will spot them in compounds. One is 「有る|ある」, meaning to have or possess. You already know its Chinese-origin reading 「ゆう」 from 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, literally having a name. The other is 「在る|ある」, meaning to exist at a place. You will see that one in words like 「現在|げんざい」, the present time, the moment that exists now. So the two kanji split the two main meanings of this verb. Having things, and being located somewhere. In normal writing, Japanese people just use kana for the verb itself, so you don't need to write these. But when you meet 「有名|ゆうめい」, remember it is having a name, built from this very verb. Two hidden kanji, one simple kana verb." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This little verb pops up in many everyday patterns. In a shop, you can ask 「〜がありますか」, do you have this? The clerk might say 「ありません」, or more politely, 「ございません」, a very respectful version you hear in stores. For your schedule, 「明日、テストがあります|あした、テストがあります」 means I have a test tomorrow. For possessions, 「時間がありません|じかんがありません」 means I don't have time. In Lesson 11, you will use it to talk about experience. After a past verb, 「ことがある」 means I have done something before. So I have been to Japan uses this verb at the end. A common mistake is using it for pets or people, like there is a dog. A dog moves, so it needs 「いる」. One small verb covers objects, plans, possessions and experiences." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This verb is for things that can't move on their own, like objects, plants, buildings and events, while 「いる」 is for people and animals. It also covers having things, and its casual negative is just 「ない」.",
      "The casual negative is the odd one, 「ない」, giving 「なかった」 and 「あった」 for the past. Things sit at a place with 「に」, but events happen at a place with 「で」, as in 「大学でパーティーがあります|だいがくでパーティーがあります」.",
      "It is usually written in kana, but it hides two kanji: 「有る|ある」 for having, seen in 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous or having a name, and 「在る|ある」 for existing at a place. They split the verb's two main meanings.",
      "Ask 「〜がありますか」 in a shop to say do you have this, and use it for plans and possessions like 「時間がありません|じかんがありません」. Never use it for pets or people, since a dog moves and needs 「いる」."
    ],
    ja: "{机|つくえ}のうえに{本|ほん}が[あります|あります|ある]。",
    en: "There is a book on the desk." },
  "かう|買う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading of the kanji. In compounds, the Chinese-origin reading is 「ばい」. Look at the bottom of the kanji. That piece is 「貝|かい」, a shell. In ancient China, shells were used as money, so the shell mark appears in many kanji about money and value. It also sits on the left of 「財|さい」 in 「財布|さいふ」, wallet, where you keep your money. The family: the noun 「買い物|かいもの」, shopping, literally buying things, comes straight from this verb's stem. It is an u-verb. The polite form is 「買います|かいます」, and the te-form is 「買って|かって」, with a small pause. You buy something with 「を」, and where you buy it takes 「で」. So at the supermarket, I buy bread. A sound trap: in Lesson 11 you will learn 「飼う|かう」, to keep a pet. Same sound, different kanji. You buy a dog with this verb, and then you keep it with the other one. Recap: shell money underneath. To buy." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb ends in 「う」, and that gives it one quirk that catches everyone. When you make the casual negative, the 「う」 does not become 「あ」. It becomes 「わ」. So the negative is 「買わない|かわない」, I don't buy. The same thing happens with 「会う|あう」, which becomes 「会わない|あわない」. Remember: verbs ending in 「う」 change to 「わ」 in the negative. The te-form follows the 「う」, 「つ」, 「る」 rule, so you get the doubled pause in 「買って|かって」. Next, a handy pattern from Lesson 7. Take the stem and add 「に行く|にいく」 to show the purpose of going. 「パンを買いに行きます|パンをかいにいきます」 means I'm going to buy bread. And 「買ってきます|かってきます」 means I'll go buy it and come back. Watch that hidden 「わ」, and your shopping sentences will sound right." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The shell at the bottom carries the money idea, but look at the top piece too. It is a flattened shape that looks like a net, the same piece you find at the top of a few other kanji. The traditional explanation is a net gathering up valuables, so getting goods with money. Treat that as the classic explanation rather than certain history. The Chinese-origin reading is 「ばい」. Its most common word is 「売買|ばいばい」, buying and selling, a word you will see in business and real estate news. The first kanji there is 「売る|うる」, to sell, which you will learn later. And here is a fun bit. Sell and buy, 「売|ばい」 and 「買|ばい」, have the same Chinese-origin reading. So 「売買|ばいばい」 is the same sound twice, almost like saying bye-bye to your money. Net on top, shell below, buying with money." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. The verb sounds a bit like cow, said quickly. Picture yourself walking into a supermarket and seeing, right next to the bread, a real live cow with a price tag around its neck. You think, why not? You hand the cashier a big pile of shiny seashells, because in this scene shells are still money, just like the bottom of the kanji. The cashier counts the shells, nods, and bags the cow. Well, the cow sticks out of the bag. You lead it home through the streets. Now every time you hear this verb, picture you, a shopping bag, and a very confused cow. Buy a cow with shells." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The bottom of the kanji is 「貝|かい」, a shell, because shells were once money in ancient China, so the kanji is shell money underneath. You buy something with 「を」 at a place marked with 「で」.",
      "Verbs ending in 「う」 change it to 「わ」 in the negative, so the negative is 「買わない|かわない」, never with 「あ」. Use the stem plus 「に行く|にいく」 for going to buy, as in 「パンを買いに行きます|パンをかいにいきます」.",
      "The classic explanation is a net on top gathering valuables over the shell money below, so getting goods with money. Sell and buy share the reading 「ばい」, so 「売買|ばいばい」 sounds like saying bye-bye to your money.",
      "The verb sounds a bit like cow, so picture buying a live cow at the supermarket and paying with a big pile of seashells, just like the shell at the bottom of the kanji. Buy a cow with shells."
    ],
    ja: "スーパーで{肉|にく}を[買いました|かいました|かう]。",
    en: "I bought meat at the supermarket." },
  "かく|書く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading of the kanji. In compounds, the Chinese-origin reading is 「しょ」, and you already know several of those words. Look at the top of the kanji. That piece is a hand holding a brush, a real part that appears in kanji about writing. A way to remember the whole thing: picture that hand and brush hovering over a sheet of paper, mid-stroke. Now the family, all with 「しょ」. 「図書館|としょかん」, library, a building of drawings and writing. 「辞書|じしょ」, dictionary, a book of words. 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, from Lesson 6. And later, 「履歴書|りれきしょ」, a résumé, in Lesson 14. Whenever you spot this kanji, think of writing. It is an u-verb. The polite form is 「書きます|かきます」, and the te-form is 「書いて|かいて」. The thing you write takes 「を」, like a letter, and the person you write to takes 「に」. A sound trap: in Lesson 15 you will meet 「描く|かく」, to draw or paint. Same sound, different kanji. Words take this one, pictures take the other. Recap: a hand with a brush. To write." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a great model for all verbs ending in 「く」. In the te-form, 「く」 becomes 「いて」. So write becomes 「書いて|かいて」, and listen, 「聞く|きく」, becomes 「聞いて|きいて」. The past is the same shape: 「書いた|かいた」. The only exception you need to know is 「行く|いく」, which becomes 「行って|いって」. The casual negative is 「書かない|かかない」. Now the particles. The thing you write takes 「を」, the person takes 「に」, and the tool takes 「で」. So I write a letter to my mother with a pen uses all three. With the stem plus 「方|かた」, you get 「書き方|かきかた」, the way to write, as in how to write this kanji. And a very useful request: 「ここに名前を書いてください|ここになまえをかいてください」, please write your name here. One verb, one clean pattern for every 「く」 verb." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Writing by hand still has a special place in Japan. Children study 「書道|しょどう」, calligraphy, at school, using a brush, black ink and thin paper. Notice that word starts with the writing kanji, read 「しょ」, plus the way. So it is the way of writing, like a martial art for the brush. Every January, there is a custom called 「書き初め|かきぞめ」, the first writing of the year. People write a lucky word or a goal for the year with a brush, usually on January 2nd. Schools often give it as a winter break assignment, and the best works are displayed. Even TV news covers big calligraphy events. And handwriting matters in daily life. Some companies still ask for handwritten résumés. So when you practice writing kanji neatly, you are joining a long tradition. Write carefully, and it shows." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. The verb sounds like a crow's call, caw, with a 「く」 at the end. Picture a very clever crow sitting at a school desk. It grips a calligraphy brush in its claw, the hand-and-brush piece at the top of the kanji. The crow dips the brush in ink and starts writing a letter, and every time it finishes a word it shouts caw! Ink splatters everywhere. The teacher walks by and says, nice handwriting. The crow puffs out its chest and keeps writing, caw, caw, caw. Now when you see this verb, picture the crow at the desk with the brush. A crow that writes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The top of the kanji is a hand holding a brush, so it means writing, and with the reading 「しょ」 it appears in 「図書館|としょかん」, 「辞書|じしょ」 and 「教科書|きょうかしょ」. Don't confuse it with 「描く|かく」, which is for pictures.",
      "This is the model for verbs ending in 「く」, which change to 「いて」 in the te-form, as in 「書いて|かいて」, with 「行く|いく」 as the only exception. The thing takes 「を」, the person 「に」, and the tool 「で」.",
      "Children study 「書道|しょどう」, the way of writing, with brush and ink, and each January people do 「書き初め|かきぞめ」, writing a lucky word or goal for the year. Handwriting still matters in Japan.",
      "The verb sounds like a crow's caw, so picture a clever crow at a school desk gripping a calligraphy brush in its claw and shouting caw after every word it writes. A crow that writes."
    ],
    ja: "{手紙|てがみ}を[書きました|かきました|かく]。",
    en: "I wrote a letter." },
  "とる|撮る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading. The Chinese-origin reading, 「さつ」, appears in words about filming and photography. On the left side is the hand mark, a squeezed form of 「手|て」, hand. It appears in lots of action kanji, like 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, 「払う|はらう」, to pay, and 「探す|さがす」, to look for. On the right is 「最|さい」, the kanji for most. Here is the key idea. The native verb for take has several kanji, and each one picks a different kind of taking. This one is only for photos and video. In Lesson 11 you will meet 「取る|とる」, which is for taking or getting things in general, like taking a class or picking something up. So the standard phrase is 「写真を撮る|しゃしんをとる」, to take a picture. 「写真|しゃしん」 means photo, literally a copy of the truth. It is an u-verb. The polite form is 「撮ります|とります」, and the te-form is 「撮って|とって」. A way to remember it: your hand reaches for the camera button. Recap: hand mark plus a sound part. Take, but only for pictures." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a trap. This verb ends in 「る」, but it is an u-verb, not a ru-verb. You can tell from the sound before the 「る」. It is an o sound, not an i or e sound, so it is an u-verb. That means the te-form is 「撮って|とって」, with the little pause, never a short form without it. The casual negative is 「撮らない|とらない」, and the stem for polite speech is 「撮り|とり」. Now some patterns from your lessons. To ask permission, use the te-form plus 「もいいですか」. 「写真を撮ってもいいですか|しゃしんをとってもいいですか」, may I take a picture? To say photos are not allowed, use 「てはいけません」. And in Lesson 7, 「写真を撮っています|しゃしんをとっています」 means someone is taking pictures right now. Remember, it ends in 「る」, but it acts like an u-verb, with that doubled pause." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Tourists in Japan need this verb a lot. To ask a stranger to take your photo, say 「すみません、写真を撮ってくださいませんか|すみません、しゃしんをとってくださいませんか」. That is a polite way to say could you take our picture please. Hand over your phone and point to the button. When the photographer is ready, they will usually say 「はい、チーズ」, from the English say cheese. Some people count down instead, like one, two, three, in Japanese. You will also see signs about photography. The formal word is 「撮影|さつえい」, filming or shooting, using the Chinese-origin reading of this kanji. So a sign reading 「撮影禁止|さつえいきんし」 means photography prohibited. You will often see that in museums, temples and shops. And 「撮影中|さつえいちゅう」 means filming in progress. Ask politely before you shoot, and watch for those signs." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan loves taking pictures. One famous example is the photo sticker booth. Since the mid-nineties, groups of friends, especially teenagers, have squeezed into these booths in game centers. The booth takes photos, then you decorate them on a screen with stamps, hearts and writing, and print them as tiny stickers. The machines can make your eyes bigger and your skin smoother, sometimes to a funny degree. Another habit: when posing, many people flash a peace sign with two fingers. Nobody agrees on exactly where it came from, but it has been a standard pose since around the 1970s. Food photos are also big. At cafes, it is very common to photograph your dessert before eating it. So when you hear this verb, think of smiling faces, peace signs and pretty plates. In Japan, taking pictures is part of having fun." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The hand mark on the left plus a sound part on the right make a kanji of taking used only for photos and video, as in 「写真を撮る|しゃしんをとる」. General taking uses a different kanji, 「取る|とる」.",
      "Even though it ends in 「る」, the o sound before it makes it an u-verb, so the te-form is 「撮って|とって」 with the little pause. Ask permission with 「写真を撮ってもいいですか|しゃしんをとってもいいですか」.",
      "Ask a stranger to take your photo with 「すみません、写真を撮ってくださいませんか|すみません、しゃしんをとってくださいませんか」. Watch for 「撮影禁止|さつえいきんし」 signs, meaning photography prohibited, in museums, temples and shops.",
      "Japan loves taking pictures, from photo sticker booths that friends decorate with stamps and hearts to the peace-sign pose and photographing desserts before eating. Taking pictures is part of having fun."
    ],
    ja: "{海|うみ}で{写真|しゃしん}を[撮りました|とりました|とる]。",
    en: "I took pictures at the sea." },
  "まつ|待つ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading. The Chinese-origin reading is 「たい」. The kanji has two parts. On the left is a mark that looks like a doubled person mark. It is the going mark, often linked to walking and roads. On the right is 「寺|てら」, temple. It mostly helps with the sound here. But it makes a nice hook. A way to remember it: picture yourself walking to the temple gate and waiting for your friend there. The temple piece shows up in kanji you know. 「時|とき」, time, has it on the right, and so does 「持つ|もつ」, to hold. Time, hold and wait all share it. Now the grammar. You wait for someone with 「を」. English uses for, so learners sometimes reach for another particle, but in Japanese the person or thing you wait for is the direct object. So you wait 「友だちを|ともだちを」. It is an u-verb ending in 「つ」. The polite form is 「待ちます|まちます」, and the te-form is 「待って|まって」. A phrase you will hear constantly: 「ちょっと待ってください|ちょっとまってください」, please wait a moment. Recap: walking plus temple, waiting at the gate." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb shows up in some of the most common phrases you will hear in Japan. In restaurants and shops, staff say 「お待たせしました|おまたせしました」 when they bring your food or come back to you. It means sorry to have kept you waiting, and it is said even if you only waited a few seconds. On the phone or at a counter, you will hear 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, a very polite please wait a moment. Among friends, it is just 「ちょっと待って|ちょっとまって」. And for meeting up, there is the noun 「待ち合わせ|まちあわせ」, a planned meeting place and time. Friends decide where to meet, like in front of a station exit. If you arrive late, say 「お待たせ|おまたせ」, sorry to keep you waiting. Say it when you arrive late, and you will sound very natural." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is your model verb for the 「つ」 ending, and there are not many of them, so it is worth learning well. The polite form is 「待ちます|まちます」, with 「ち」, not 「つ」. The casual negative is 「待たない|またない」, with 「た」. The te-form is 「待って|まって」, and the casual past is 「待った|まった」, both with the little pause. 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, follows exactly the same path. In Lesson 7, you can say 「待っています|まっています」, I am waiting. And in Lesson 9, with the casual past, you can say 「長く待った|ながくまった」, I waited a long time. One more tip. When asking someone to wait for you, add 「ください」 to the te-form, but when telling a friend, 「待って|まって」 alone is fine, often stretched out with feeling. Learn the 「た, ち, つ, て」 chain, and every 「つ」 verb falls into place." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's most famous story about waiting is about a dog. 「ハチ公|はちこう」 was an Akita dog who walked to Shibuya Station every day to meet his owner, a university professor, on his way home. In 1925, the professor died suddenly at work and never came back. But the dog kept going to the station and waiting every evening, for about nine more years. His loyalty became famous across the country, and a bronze statue of him was put up at the station in 1934. Today, the statue is one of the most popular meeting spots in Tokyo. When friends make plans in Shibuya, they often say let's meet at the statue. So the most famous waiting dog became the place where everyone waits for each other. Next time you wait at a station, think of that loyal dog." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The going mark on the left plus temple, 「寺|てら」, on the right give a hook: waiting for your friend at the temple gate. The person you wait for takes 「を」, as in 「友だちを|ともだちを」.",
      "Staff say 「お待たせしました|おまたせしました」, sorry to keep you waiting, even after a few seconds, and polite counters use 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」. Say 「お待たせ|おまたせ」 to friends when you arrive late.",
      "This is the model verb for the 「つ」 ending, running through the chain 「待たない|またない」, 「待ちます|まちます」, 「待つ|まつ」, 「待って|まって」. 「持つ|もつ」 follows exactly the same path.",
      "The Akita dog 「ハチ公|はちこう」 waited at Shibuya Station every evening for about nine years after his owner died. His statue is now one of Tokyo's most popular spots for waiting for friends."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}で{友だち|ともだち}を[待ちました|まちました|まつ]。",
    en: "I waited for my friend at the station." },
  "わかる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is often written with kana alone at your level, but its kanji is 「分かる|わかる」. 「分|ぶん」 means to divide or a part. The native verb 「分ける|わける」 means to divide or sort things. The idea behind understanding is that things have been sorted out and separated clearly in your mind. You know the same kanji from 「自分|じぶん」, oneself, a portion that is you, in Lesson 16, and from minutes, which divide an hour. Now the most important grammar point. The thing you understand takes 「が」, not 「を」. So you say 「日本語がわかります|にほんごがわかります」. English speakers often use 「を」, because understand takes a direct object in English. Think of it more like Japanese is clear to me. It is an u-verb. The polite form is 「わかります」. The past form, 「わかりました」, is extremely common. It means understood, or got it, and in a workplace it often means I'll do that. The negative, 「わかりません」, is the polite way to say I don't know or I don't understand. Recap: things divided out clearly in your mind, with 「が」 for what you understand." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji for this verb is 「分|ぶん」, and it is worth meeting properly. The bottom is a curved knife, and the top is two strokes spreading apart, the idea of cutting something into parts. It changes its reading a lot. For minutes, it is 「ふん」 or 「ぷん」, as in 「五分|ごふん」, five minutes, and 「十分|じゅっぷん」, ten minutes. With the reading 「ぶん」, it means a part or a portion, as in 「半分|はんぶん」, half. And here is a fun twist: the same two kanji as ten minutes can also be read 「十分|じゅうぶん」, meaning enough, ten parts out of ten. Context tells you which. The native verbs are this one, to understand, and 「分ける|わける」, to divide. So minutes divide the hour, half divides a whole, and understanding divides things clearly. One cutting kanji, many useful words." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a mistake that can sound rude. When someone asks you something and you don't have the answer, say 「わかりません」, I'm not sure. Avoid 「知りません|しりません」, I don't know, in that situation. That one says the information is simply not in your head, and it can sound cold, almost like it's none of my business. This verb sounds softer, like I can't work it out. Next, the past form. 「わかりました」 is the normal okay, got it. But at work, to a boss or customer, people switch to something more formal, like 「かしこまりました」, certainly. Also, asking a teacher 「わかりましたか」, did you understand, sounds a bit like you're testing them. Teachers ask that to students, not the other way around. And 「わかった」 is the casual got it among friends. Choose softly, and people will feel the respect." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has some quirks. First, it already includes the idea of being able, so it has no separate can form. You simply say 「わかります」, I can understand. Second, it describes a state that changes, so in Lesson 7 you will meet 「わかっています」. That doesn't mean I am understanding right now. It means I already know, I'm aware. Said firmly, it can sound like I know, I know, stop telling me. Third, it pairs with a transitive partner, 「分ける|わける」, to divide or to sort something. One thing becomes clear by itself, the other is something you do to things. And in Lesson 9 casual speech, the past 「わかった」 is an everyday got it, while 「わからない」 is I don't get it. Let this verb describe a state, not an action, and it will feel natural." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Its kanji 「分|ぶん」 means to divide, so understanding is things sorted out clearly in your mind. What you understand takes 「が」, not 「を」, as in 「日本語がわかります|にほんごがわかります」.",
      "The kanji is a knife cutting something into parts, read 「ふん」 for minutes, 「ぶん」 for a part as in 「半分|はんぶん」, half, and even 「十分|じゅうぶん」 for enough. Minutes, halves and understanding all divide things.",
      "When you lack an answer, say 「わかりません」, which sounds soft, rather than 「知りません|しりません」, which can sound cold. At work, people upgrade a plain got it to 「かしこまりました」.",
      "It already includes the idea of being able, so it has no separate can form, and it describes a state, not an action. 「わかっています」 means I already know, and said firmly it sounds like I know, I know."
    ],
    ja: "{日本語|にほんご}が[わかります|わかります|わかる]。",
    en: "I understand Japanese." },
  "いる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the verb for the existence of people and animals, things that can move around on their own. It is usually written in kana. The pattern is: place, then 「に」, then the person or animal, then 「が」, then this verb. For example, there is a cat under the chair. Its partner, 「ある」, from this same lesson, is for objects, plants and events. A handy test: can it walk away by itself? Then use this word. People, dogs, fish and birds take it. A tree or a car takes the other one. It also covers having people in your life. If you have a younger sister, you say a younger sister exists, using this verb. It is a ru-verb. The polite form is 「います」, and the negative is 「いません」. Its te-form is 「いて」. A heads-up: in Lesson 7, this verb becomes a helper after the te-form, to say someone is doing something right now, or is in a state. So you will be using it a lot. And a sound trap: in Lesson 8 you will learn another verb with this same sound meaning to need. That one is an u-verb with different endings. Recap: there is, for living things that move on their own." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This verb is normally written in kana, but it has a kanji, 「居|い」, and you will see that kanji in some fun words. 「居間|いま」 is the living room, the room where people stay. And 「居酒屋|いざかや」 is a Japanese pub. Break that down: stay, sake, shop. Originally it was a sake shop where you could stay and drink, instead of just buying and leaving. Today it is the classic place for coworkers and friends to share small plates and drinks. The kanji itself has a shape like a person sitting, with the kanji for old tucked inside. Don't worry about writing it yet. In ordinary sentences, people use kana for this verb, so you mainly need to recognize it inside words. Next time you see a sign for a pub, remember it is the place where you stay." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The rule says living, moving things take this verb. But real life has some interesting edge cases. A taxi waiting outside takes this verb, because there is a driver in it. People will say there is a taxi using 「いる」. A bus coming toward you is also often treated as alive. But fish at the supermarket take 「ある」, because they are food now, not living creatures. Ghosts and monsters take this verb, since they move by themselves. And robots can go either way, depending on whether the speaker thinks of them as almost alive. For family, 「兄弟がいますか|きょうだいがいますか」, do you have brothers and sisters, is a very common question. For pets, 「犬がいます|いぬがいます」, I have a dog. When in doubt, ask yourself whether the speaker sees it as moving on its own." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This one is a ru-verb, so its forms are simple. Casual negative 「いない」, casual past 「いた」, and past negative 「いなかった」. Compare its partner 「ある」, whose negative is the odd 「ない」. With this verb, nothing strange happens. In Lesson 19, you will learn the honorific version, 「いらっしゃる」, used for teachers, bosses and customers. So the teacher is in the classroom becomes very respectful with that verb. Shop staff use it constantly in their greeting at the door. A word order point: when asking where a person is, say 「先生はどこにいますか|せんせいはどこにいますか」, where is the teacher, using 「は」 for the person you already know about. And for how many, 「何人いますか|なんにんいますか」, how many people are there. Simple ru-verb forms, with a fancy honorific waiting later." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This verb is for people and animals, things that can walk away on their own, while 「ある」 is for objects. It is a ru-verb, polite 「います」, and it also covers having family or pets.",
      "Its rare kanji 「居|い」 shows up in 「居間|いま」, the living room where people stay, and 「居酒屋|いざかや」, a pub that started as a sake shop where you could stay and drink.",
      "The real test is whether the speaker sees it as moving on its own: a taxi with a driver and ghosts take 「いる」, but fish at the supermarket take 「ある」. Robots can go either way.",
      "As a ru-verb its forms are simple, with 「いない」, 「いた」 and 「いなかった」, unlike the odd negative of 「ある」. Later you will learn the honorific 「いらっしゃる」 for teachers and customers."
    ],
    ja: "{教室|きょうしつ}に{先生|せんせい}が[います|います|いる]。",
    en: "The teacher is in the classroom." },
  "〜ぐらい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a little ending you attach to an amount to mean about or roughly. You put it right after the number and counter. For example, 「一時間ぐらい|いちじかんぐらい」 means about an hour, and you can say about thirty minutes or about ten people the same way. You will also hear it said 「くらい」, with a softer first sound. Both are correct, and they mean the same thing. Now the classic mistake. In Lesson 3 you learned 「〜ごろ」, which also means about. The difference: 「〜ごろ」 is about a point in time, like around three o'clock. This ending is about an amount or a length, like about three hours. So you go to bed around eleven with 「ごろ」, and you sleep about seven hours with this one. A way to remember it: when you measure how much or how long, use this. When you point at the clock, use 「ごろ」. Family: 「どのぐらい」, how long or how much, comes in Lesson 10, and 「このぐらい」, about this much, comes in Lesson 16. Recap: about, for amounts and lengths." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This ending comes from a noun, 「位|くらい」, which means rank or level. You can still see that meaning in words like 「一位|いちい」, first place in a contest, using the Chinese-origin reading. The native noun meant a position on a scale. Over time, people started attaching it after amounts to say at about this level, and from there it became about or roughly. When it is attached to a word, the first sound often softens to 「ぐ」, which is why you hear both versions. The kanji for rank has the person mark on the left and stand on the right, a person standing in their position. You will not write the ending with that kanji, though. It is always kana. So when you say about two hours, you are really saying at the level of two hours. A rank on a scale became roughly." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This ending is everywhere in everyday questions about how much and how long. 「いくらぐらいですか」 asks about how much does it cost. 「どのぐらいかかりますか」 asks about how long does it take, using 「かかる」 from Lesson 10. The answer might be 「三十分ぐらいです|さんじゅっぷんぐらいです」, about thirty minutes. Japanese speakers use it even when they know the exact number, because it sounds softer and less pushy. There is a second use you will hear. After a noun, it can mean at least, or something as small as. 「電話ぐらいしてください|でんわぐらいしてください」 means at least give me a call. It suggests that this is the minimum you could do. A parent might say that to a teenager who came home late. Use it for amounts, and listen for that at least feeling too." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Attach it after a number and counter to mean about an amount or length, as in 「一時間ぐらい|いちじかんぐらい」. Use 「〜ごろ」 instead for about a point in time, like around three o'clock.",
      "It comes from the noun 「位|くらい」, meaning rank or level, so about two hours is really at the level of two hours. Attached to a word, the first sound often softens to 「ぐ」.",
      "It fills everyday questions like 「いくらぐらいですか」 and 「どのぐらいかかりますか」, and sounds softer even when you know the exact number. After a noun it can mean at least, as in 「電話ぐらいしてください|でんわぐらいしてください」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}{二時間|にじかん}[ぐらい|ぐらい|〜ぐらい]{勉強|べんきょう}します。",
    en: "I study about two hours every day." },
  "ごめんなさい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This apology has a clear makeup. 「ごめん」 can be written 「御免|ごめん」, a polite prefix plus a kanji meaning pardon or permission. Then 「なさい」 is a soft way of telling someone to do something. So the whole phrase is roughly please grant me pardon. Now the important part: when to use it. This is a warm, personal apology. It is common between friends and family, and it is the word children say to their parents. Shorter and more casual still is just 「ごめん」, which you will meet in Lesson 16. For strangers, shops, teachers or work, people usually say 「すみません」 instead. That word also works as excuse me and even thank you for your trouble. So if you bump into someone on the train, 「すみません」 is the safe choice. In very formal situations, like a company apologizing to customers, you will hear much longer, stiffer phrases. A way to remember it: this one is for people close to your heart. Recap: please pardon me, for friends and family." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This apology belongs to a little family of phrases built on 「ごめん」. The shortest, 「ごめん」, is a quick casual sorry between friends. Add a soft 「ね」 and you get 「ごめんね」, a gentle sorry, a little sweeter. Then there is the full version, this one. But the surprise family member is 「ごめんください」. That one is not an apology at all. It is what you call out when you arrive at someone's house or a small shop and nobody is at the door. Something like excuse me, is anyone home? You are asking pardon for intruding. So the same root word covers both sorry and excuse me for coming in. The pardon kanji, 「免|めん」, also appears in 「免許|めんきょ」, a license, like a driver's license, official permission. So the whole family is really about asking permission." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Apologizing in Japan is often physical, not just verbal. People bow while saying sorry, and the deeper the bow, the more serious the apology. A light nod is enough for a small mistake with a friend. A company president apologizing on TV might bow very deeply and hold it for several seconds. Apologies are also used to smooth things over, not only to admit fault. People say sorry for small inconveniences, like asking for a favor or being slightly late. In business, you will hear 「申し訳ありません|もうしわけありません」, a much more formal apology. It literally means there is no excuse. Children grow up saying the warm, personal apology at home. Then as adults, they learn the stiffer business phrases too. A good apology in Japan is in the words, the bow and the timing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Knowing how to answer an apology is just as useful as giving one. If a friend apologizes to you, you can say 「いいよ」, it's fine, or 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, no problem. With a kind tone, that closes the matter. You can also say 「気にしないで|きにしないで」, don't worry about it, a phrase you will be able to build after Lesson 8. Here is a small dialogue. A friend arrives late and says sorry, I'm late. You reply, it's okay, I just got here too. Another tip: tone matters. Said quickly and lightly, this word is a casual oops. Said slowly with a bow, it is a sincere apology. Kids say it in a stretched-out, sing-song way when they have done something naughty. Learn to give and to accept apologies, and conversations stay warm." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is built from 「御免|ごめん」, pardon, plus 「なさい」, a soft command, so roughly please grant me pardon. It is a warm apology for friends and family, while strangers, shops and work usually get 「すみません」.",
      "The 「ごめん」 family runs from a quick 「ごめん」 to a gentle 「ごめんね」 to this full version. The surprise member is 「ごめんください」, which you call out when arriving at a house or shop, asking pardon for intruding.",
      "Apologies in Japan come with a bow, and the deeper the bow, the more serious the apology. Business uses the much more formal 「申し訳ありません|もうしわけありません」, literally there is no excuse.",
      "To accept an apology, say 「いいよ」 or 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」 with a kind tone, or 「気にしないで|きにしないで」, don't worry about it. Tone matters too, since said lightly it is a casual oops, but slowly with a bow it is sincere."
    ],
    ja: "[ごめんなさい]、{宿題|しゅくだい}をわすれました。",
    en: "I'm sorry, I forgot my homework." },
  "それから|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built from two pieces you already know. 「それ」 means that, from Lesson 2. 「から」 means from, or after. So it is literally from that, or after that. It is used to connect events in order, like and then. You usually put it at the start of a new sentence. For example: I ate breakfast. And then I went to school. It can also add one more item to a list, meaning and also, or in addition. A customer ordering food might say coffee, and then, a cake too. Compare some neighbors. 「でも」, from Lesson 3, means but. 「だから」, from this lesson, means so or therefore. And 「そして」, from Lesson 11, also means and then, but feels a little more like written storytelling. A common mistake is to use it to join two nouns, like dogs and cats. For that you need the particle 「と」. This word links sentences and events, not single nouns. A way to remember it: start from that point, and move on to the next thing. Recap: that plus from. After that, and then." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Remember the 「こ, そ, あ, ど」 pattern from Lesson 2, the this, that, that over there, and which words? Add 「から」 to them and you get a small family about time. 「これから」 means from now on, or starting now. You hear it at the start of meetings and in speeches: from now on, let's work hard. 「それから」, this word, is after that, moving forward from a point just mentioned. And 「あれから」 means since then, looking back at a moment you and the listener both remember, often from long ago. So after we graduated, I haven't seen him since then, would use 「あれから」. The shared piece in all three is 「から」, from, and the first part picks the point in time. Now, here, or back then. One pattern, three ways to move through time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Besides linking events, this word has two everyday uses worth knowing. First, it can mean since then, especially with a negative. 「それから会っていません|それからあっていません」 means I haven't seen them since then. Second, in conversation, a listener can say it with a rising tone to mean and then? What happened next? It shows you are interested and keeps a story going. Imagine a friend telling you about a date. They say we went to dinner. You lean in and say, and then? They continue, then we went to karaoke. In ordering or listing, it adds one more item, like I'd like coffee, and also a sandwich. A waiter might also ask, anything else? using a related phrase. Use it to keep stories flowing, and as a friendly prompt for more." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word joins two separate sentences, but Japanese also has ways to join actions inside one sentence. In Lesson 6, you learned the te-form for listing actions in order. 「起きて、食べて、出かけました|おきて、たべて、でかけました」, I got up, ate, and went out. That is smooth and compact. This word does the same job across a sentence break, so it gives a bit more of a pause and emphasis. You might use the te-form for small steps, then this word for a bigger next stage. Another option you will meet is the te-form plus 「から」, which means after doing something, stressing the order strongly. So after I eat, I brush my teeth. Notice how all of these use 「から」 or the te-form for sequence. Short steps with the te-form, a fresh sentence with this word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「それ」, that, plus 「から」, from, so after that or and then, placed at the start of a new sentence to connect events or add an item. It links sentences, not nouns, which need 「と」.",
      "Add 「から」 to the 「こ, そ, あ, ど」 words for a time family: 「これから」 is from now on, 「それから」 is after that, and 「あれから」 is since then, looking back at a shared memory.",
      "With a negative it can mean since then, as in 「それからあっていません」, and a listener can say it with a rising tone to mean and then, what happened next? It keeps stories flowing.",
      "The te-form links small steps inside one sentence, while this word joins across a sentence break with more pause and emphasis. Use the te-form for short steps and a fresh sentence with this word for a bigger next stage."
    ],
    ja: "{宿題|しゅくだい}をしました。[それから]、ねました。",
    en: "I did my homework. And then I went to bed." },
  "だから|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built from two pieces. 「だ」 is the casual form of 「です」, it is. 「から」 means because. So it is literally it is so, because of that. In English, that becomes so, or therefore. You put it at the start of a sentence to show a result. The first sentence gives the reason, and the next one starts with this word. For example: Tomorrow there is a test. So I will study tonight. The polite version is 「ですから」. That sounds more formal, and you will hear it from teachers and in announcements. Compare 「から」 on its own. Inside one sentence, 「から」 goes at the end of the reason. This word starts a new sentence instead. Same idea, different place. A caution: said with a sharp tone, this word can sound like I told you so, or that's why. So in polite speech, keep it gentle, or use 「ですから」. A way to remember it: it is the hinge between the reason and the result. Recap: it is plus because. So, therefore." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a very common emotional use. Said with strong stress, 「だから言ったでしょ|だからいったでしょ」 means see, I told you so. A parent might say it when a kid forgets an umbrella and gets soaked. You will also hear it on its own, with a sigh, in an argument. It sounds like, that's what I keep saying. And 「だから何|だからなに」 means so what? That one is quite rude, so save it for listening, not for speaking. On the friendly side, it's useful in explanations. You can give a reason, then wrap up with this word. I have a part-time job tomorrow. So I can't go. In polite situations, switch to 「ですから」, or keep the reason and result in one sentence with 「から」. Watch the tone, because this little word can sound logical or grumpy." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "There are several so and because words in Japanese, and each has its own feeling. 「から」, inside one sentence, gives a clear reason. In Lesson 12 you will meet 「ので」, which also means because, but sounds softer and more polite, good for explaining to a teacher or boss. 「それで」 means and so, or that's why, and is often used when telling a story about what happened next. And 「だって」 is a casual, slightly childish but, because, used to give an excuse. A child might say but I was tired, starting with that one. This word sits in the middle. It starts a new sentence with a firm result. Firm, soft, storytelling or excuse, choose the one that fits your mood." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「だ」, the casual form of 「です」, plus 「から」, because, so it starts a new sentence with the result, meaning so or therefore. Said sharply it can sound like I told you so, so use 「ですから」 politely.",
      "Said with strong stress, 「だから言ったでしょ|だからいったでしょ」 means see, I told you so, and 「だから何|だからなに」 means a rude so what. It can sound logical or grumpy, so watch the tone.",
      "Among the because words, 「から」 gives a reason in one sentence, 「ので」 is softer, 「それで」 tells what happened next, and 「だって」 gives a childish excuse. This word starts a new sentence with a firm result."
    ],
    ja: "あしたはテストです。[だから]、{勉強|べんきょう}します。",
    en: "There's a test tomorrow. So I'll study." },
  "たくさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It means a lot, and it works for both things you can count, like books, and things you can't, like water or homework. There are two main ways to use it. As an adverb, it comes right before the verb: eat a lot, or study a lot. As a describer, it goes before a noun with 「の」, as in 「たくさんの人|たくさんのひと」, a lot of people. A tip for natural Japanese: the adverb pattern is more common. Instead of saying I have many books, Japanese often says books, a lot, I have. Compare its opposite, 「ちょっと」, from Lesson 3, which means a little. A related word is 「多い|おおい」, many, from Lesson 12. That one is an adjective, so it usually comes at the end, as in people are many. This word comes before the verb or the noun. A way to remember it: picture a big pile of things stacking up higher and higher. Recap: many, much, a lot, for counted things and uncounted things alike." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word is usually written in kana, but you may see it in kanji as 「沢山|たくさん」. The first kanji means a marsh or a stream, with the water mark on the left, and the second kanji is mountain. Marsh plus mountain. It is tempting to imagine piles of things as big as mountains, but most dictionaries treat these kanji as just a spelling chosen for the sound. Where the word itself really came from isn't certain, so don't build too much on the kanji. What is clear is that it has been used to mean plenty for centuries. The mountain kanji does have a reading you'll meet, 「さん」, as in 「富士山|ふじさん」, Mount Fuji. And the first kanji is read 「たく」 here. Written in kana or kanji, it is the same everyday word for plenty." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several ways to say a lot, and they fit different spots. This word is the all-rounder. 「いっぱい」 is more casual and has a feeling of full, like a full stomach or a room packed with people. 「多い|おおい」, from Lesson 12, is an adjective, so it usually comes at the end. Tokyo has many people becomes Tokyo, people are many. For crowds of people, there is a special word, 「大勢|おおぜい」, many people, a big force. On the small side, 「少し|すこし」 and 「ちょっと」 both mean a little, with 「ちょっと」 a bit more casual. And 「全然|ぜんぜん」 with a negative means not at all. So you could say I ate a lot, I ate a little, or I didn't eat at all. Match the word to the spot in the sentence, and the feeling you want." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a fun use. 「もうたくさん」 means I've had enough, or that's enough already. It can be said kindly, when someone keeps piling food on your plate, but it can also be a frustrated that's enough, when you're fed up. Another everyday phrase: 「たくさん食べてね|たくさんたべてね」, eat a lot, okay? Grandparents and hosts say it to guests, especially to young people. You can reply 「いただきます」 and start eating. To make it stronger, people add 「本当に|ほんとうに」, really, in front, as in really a lot. And when you thank someone for a big pile of gifts, you might say thank you for so many things, using this word before the noun with 「の」. Eat a lot, and know how to say when you've had enough." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means a lot for both countable and uncountable things, and the most natural pattern puts it right before the verb, though 「たくさんの人|たくさんのひと」 also works before a noun. Unlike 「多い|おおい」, it doesn't go at the end.",
      "It can be written 「沢山|たくさん」, marsh plus mountain, but dictionaries treat those kanji as a spelling chosen for the sound. The word's true origin is uncertain, but it has meant plenty for centuries.",
      "This word is the all-rounder, while 「いっぱい」 feels full, 「多い|おおい」 is an adjective that comes at the end, and 「大勢|おおぜい」 is for crowds of people. Match the word to its spot and feeling.",
      "「もうたくさん」 means I've had enough, said kindly to more food or in frustration. Hosts and grandparents tell guests 「たくさん食べてね|たくさんたべてね」, eat a lot, okay?"
    ],
    ja: "{公園|こうえん}に{人|ひと}が[たくさん]います。",
    en: "There are a lot of people in the park." },
  "〜と|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This little particle has two jobs, and both are about putting things together. First job: and, between nouns. 「犬と猫|いぬとねこ」 means dogs and cats. It gives a complete list, meaning exactly these, nothing more. Second job: together with, for people. You put it after a person to show who you do something with. So you go to the movies with a friend by saying 「友だちと|ともだちと」, then the verb. You can make the together idea stronger by adding 「一緒に|いっしょに」, together, from Lesson 5. So with my friend, together, I go. Now the big mistake for English speakers. In English, and joins anything: nouns, verbs, whole sentences. This particle only joins nouns. You can't use it to say I ate and slept, or to join two adjectives. For linking sentences, use words like 「それから」, and then. Verbs and adjectives get their own te-forms later. Compare 「一人で|ひとりで」, alone, from this lesson. Alone, versus with a friend. Recap: and between nouns, and together with for people." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Compare this particle with its neighbor 「や」, which you will meet in Lesson 11. Both join nouns, but they paint different pictures. This one gives a complete list. 「パンと卵を買いました|パンとたまごをかいました」 means I bought bread and eggs, and that's everything. 「や」 gives just a few examples, implying there's more. Bread, eggs, and so on. People often add 「など」 at the end to make that clear. So when someone asks what's in your bag, this particle means here is the full list, while 「や」 means here are some highlights. In Lesson 10, this particle also helps with comparisons. 「コーヒーと紅茶とどちらが|コーヒーとこうちゃとどちらが」, which of coffee and tea, is the classic pattern for asking which is better. And remember, it only goes between nouns, not after the last one. Exactly these, or just some examples? Choose this particle or 「や」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This little sound has more than one job, and you will meet the others soon. Don't be surprised when it appears after a whole sentence. In Lesson 8, it marks a quotation. 「雨が降ると思います|あめがふるとおもいます」 means I think it will rain. Here it works like quotation marks, closing off what you think or say. You will also see it in 「と言っていました|といっていました」, someone said that. Later still, after a verb, it can mean whenever or if, as in when you turn right, you will see the station. So how do you tell them apart? Look at what comes before it. After a person or a noun, it is and or with. After a sentence, before verbs like think or say, it is quoting. Same sound, different jobs, and the word before it tells you which." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases you will use most with this particle. To ask who someone is going with, say 「だれと行きますか|だれといきますか」, who are you going with? The answer might be 「家族と|かぞくと」, with my family, or 「一人で|ひとりで」, alone. If you want to stress together, say 「友だちと一緒に|ともだちといっしょに」, together with friends. Here is a small dialogue. Someone asks what you did on the weekend. You say I went to the sea with my older sister, putting this particle after sister. And for comparing, a friend might ask which you like better, cats or dogs, using it between the two. A common mistake is to drop it, saying just friend went. In Japanese, the particle is needed. Always mark who you were with, and the sentence will make sense." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This particle means and between nouns, giving a complete list, and together with after a person, as in 「友だちと|ともだちと」. Unlike English and, it only joins nouns, never verbs, adjectives or sentences.",
      "This particle gives a complete list, as in 「パンと卵を買いました|パンとたまごをかいました」, while 「や」 gives just some examples with more implied. It also builds comparisons like 「コーヒーと紅茶とどちらが|コーヒーとこうちゃとどちらが」.",
      "After a sentence, before verbs like think or say, the same sound marks a quotation, as in 「雨が降ると思います|あめがふるとおもいます」. After a person or noun it means and or with, so the word before it tells you which.",
      "Ask 「だれと行きますか|だれといきますか」 to find out who someone is going with, and answer 「家族と|かぞくと」 or 「一人で|ひとりで」. Don't drop it, because Japanese needs the particle to mark who you were with."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}[と|と|〜と]{映画|えいが}を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw a movie with my friend." },
  "どうして|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This question word is built from two pieces. 「どう」 means how, from Lesson 8 style questions like how was it. 「して」 is the te-form of 「する」, to do. So it is roughly having done how, which turned into why. It is used to ask the reason for something. You usually put it at the start of the question. For example: why aren't you eating? The answer usually ends with 「から」, because. Why didn't you come? Because I was sick, with 「から」 at the end. There are a few other ways to ask why. 「なぜ」 is more formal and common in writing. 「なんで」 is casual and very common among friends. This word sits nicely in the middle, polite enough for most situations. A caution: asking why bluntly can feel pushy in any language. Softening it with a gentle tone helps a lot. Its relative 「どうやって」, coming in Lesson 10, means how, by what means. Same 「どう」 at the start, different question. Recap: how plus doing. Why." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Why-questions and their answers pair beautifully with a pattern from Lesson 12, the explanation ending 「んです」. When you ask for a reason, adding it sounds more natural and caring. 「どうして来なかったんですか|どうしてこなかったんですか」, why didn't you come? It shows you want to understand the situation. The answer often uses it too. 「頭が痛かったんです|あたまがいたかったんです」, I had a headache, you see. Even without that pattern, a simple answer can end in 「から」. Here is another tip. The answer doesn't repeat the whole question. Japanese often just gives the reason. Why are you studying Japanese? Because I like anime. That is enough. Also, in casual speech, you might hear 「どうして」 on its own, said with feeling, like but why? Pair why with the explanation ending, and you will sound like a native." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The 「どう」 at the start of this word means how, and it builds a whole family. 「どう」 alone asks how is it, or how about. 「どうやって」, from Lesson 10, asks how, by what method. 「どうぞ」 is please, go ahead. 「どうも」 is a handy thanks or hello. Then come two very useful relatives of this exact word. 「どうしたんですか」 means what happened, or what's wrong, said when someone looks upset or hurt. And 「どうしよう」 means what should I do, often said in a panic. Then there is 「どうしても」, which means no matter what or by any means. I want to go to Japan no matter what uses it. So the shared piece is how, and the endings decide whether you ask why, what happened, or what to do. One small sound, a big family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Choosing a why word is about tone. With friends and family, 「なんで」 is the everyday choice, and it can sound playful or annoyed, depending on your voice. In writing, news and formal speeches, 「なぜ」 is common. It can feel stiff in conversation. This word is the safe middle choice, fine for most situations. But be careful asking a boss or teacher why directly. It can sound like you are challenging them. Instead, people often soften it, by saying something like may I ask the reason? or adding a gentle explanation pattern. A natural mini dialogue between friends goes like this. Why did you come to Japan? Because I love the food. That's it, short and simple. Pick the right why for the person, and your question won't sound like a challenge." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「どう」, how, plus 「して」, the te-form of 「する」, which turned into why, and answers usually end with 「から」. It sits between formal 「なぜ」 and casual 「なんで」, polite enough for most situations.",
      "Why questions pair nicely with the explanation ending 「んです」, as in 「どうして来なかったんですか|どうしてこなかったんですか」, which sounds more caring. Answers just give the reason, often with 「から」 or 「んです」.",
      "The 「どう」 at the start means how and builds a family, including 「どうしたんですか」, what happened, 「どうしよう」, what should I do, and 「どうしても」, no matter what. The endings decide the question.",
      "Choosing a why word is about tone: 「なんで」 with friends, 「なぜ」 in writing and speeches, and this word as the safe middle. Asking a boss or teacher why directly can sound like a challenge, so soften it."
    ],
    ja: "[どうして]{日本語|にほんご}を{勉強|べんきょう}しますか。",
    en: "Why do you study Japanese?" },
  "ひとりで|一人で": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This has two parts. 「一人|ひとり」 means one person, and 「で」 is a particle that shows the way something is done. 「一人|ひとり」 is a special native reading. The kanji are 「一|いち」, one, and 「人|ひと」, person, but you don't read them separately. Two people is also special, 「二人|ふたり」. From three on, counting people switches to the Chinese-origin pattern, like 「三人|さんにん」. So with one person, on your own, is alone. You put it before the verb. For example, I went to 「東京|とうきょう」 alone, or I live alone. It doesn't mean lonely. It just means by yourself, and Japanese people often say it with pride, like I did it all by myself. For feeling lonely there is a different word. Compare 「友だちと|ともだちと」, with a friend, using the particle 「と」 from this lesson. Alone or with somebody. A cultural note: 「一人暮らし|ひとりぐらし」, living alone, is a big life step for Japanese students who move to a city for university. Recap: one person, plus the particle of manner. On your own." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Counting people is full of surprises. One person and two people have special native readings, 「一人|ひとり」 and 「二人|ふたり」. Then from three, you use the regular pattern with 「にん」. But watch out for four. It is 「四人|よにん」, not with the other readings of four. Seven can be 「七人|しちにん」 or 「ななにん」, and nine is 「九人|きゅうにん」. To ask how many people, say 「何人|なんにん」. At a restaurant, staff ask how many in your party, and you can answer with this counter, or just hold up fingers. If you are alone, you answer 「一人です|ひとりです」. Be careful not to mix up 「何人|なんにん」, how many people, with 「何人|なにじん」, what nationality. Same kanji, different reading and meaning. Remember the special one and two, and the tricky four." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's compare a few close neighbors. This word means alone, without company. 「自分で|じぶんで」 means by yourself, using your own effort, without help. They can overlap, but the focus is different. I made this cake by myself, with no help, uses 「自分で|じぶんで」. I went to the movies alone, without friends, uses this word. Next, 「一人っ子|ひとりっこ」 is an only child, one child in the family. And 「一人暮らし|ひとりぐらし」 is living alone. If you want to say lonely, the adjective is 「さびしい」. There is also 「一人ぼっち|ひとりぼっち」, all alone, which does have that sad, lonely feeling. So be careful. This word on its own is neutral, but the one with the extra ending is sad. Alone and proud, or alone and lonely, the ending makes the difference." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Doing things alone is very normal and even trendy in Japan. Many restaurants have counter seats designed for solo diners. Some ramen shops even have little booths with dividers, so you can focus on your noodles without anyone watching. There are karaoke places that welcome solo singers, and hotels and travel packages built for one guest. Shops and restaurants often use the polite phrase 「お一人様|おひとりさま」, one honored guest. That phrase has also become a word for people who happily do activities on their own, like eating out or traveling solo. So if you want to eat dinner alone at a restaurant in Japan, nobody will think it is strange. You just tell the staff 「一人です|ひとりです」 and take a seat. In Japan, alone can be a choice to enjoy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「一人|ひとり」, one person, uses a special native reading, and 「で」 shows the way something is done, so it means on your own. It means by yourself, not lonely, and is often said with pride.",
      "One and two people have special readings, 「一人|ひとり」 and 「二人|ふたり」, and from three on you use 「にん」, with four as the tricky 「四人|よにん」. Don't confuse 「何人|なんにん」, how many people, with 「何人|なにじん」, what nationality.",
      "This word means without company, while 「自分で|じぶんで」 means by your own effort without help. 「一人ぼっち|ひとりぼっち」 is the one with a sad, lonely feeling, so the ending makes the difference.",
      "Doing things alone is normal and even trendy in Japan, with counter seats, ramen booths and solo karaoke, and 「お一人様|おひとりさま」 has become a word for people who enjoy solo activities. Just tell the staff 「一人です|ひとりです」."
    ],
    ja: "[一人で|ひとりで]{映画|えいが}を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw a movie alone." },
  "もしもし|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phone greeting has a real origin. It comes from 「申す|もうす」, a humble verb meaning to say. In the past, people answering a call said I'm speaking, I'm speaking, by repeating that verb, and over time it shrank to the doubled sound you use today. You will see the same humble verb in Lesson 13 in 「〜と申します|〜ともうします」, a humble way to say my name is. Usage: say it when you answer the phone, or when you can't hear the other person and want to check they are still there. It is mostly for phone calls. Face to face, people say hello differently. The one exception: you might use it to get the attention of a stranger who dropped something, but that is less common. In business calls, people often skip it and say their company name instead. A fun bit of folklore, just a story and not history: people used to say that ghosts and foxes in disguise could only say this word once, so saying it twice proved you were human. Recap: a doubled, shortened I'm speaking. Hello on the phone." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how a typical phone call goes. When you answer at home, many people say this greeting, or 「はい」 plus their family name. For example, yes, this is the Tanaka home. When you call someone, you say this greeting and then give your name. 「もしもし、スミスです」, hello, it's Smith. If you are calling a home, you can ask is this the Tanaka residence? using the polite word 「お宅|おたく」, someone's home. If you can't hear, say this greeting again with a rising tone, are you there? Business calls are different. Office workers answer with the company name, and say thank you for your continued support before anything else. So save this greeting for personal calls. At the end, people say 「失礼します|しつれいします」, a polite goodbye on the phone. Answer, give your name, and close with that polite goodbye." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This greeting is a doubled word, and Japanese loves doubling. Repeating a word often makes it plural, frequent or more intense. You already know 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes, which is time doubled. 「人々|ひとびと」 means people, many persons. 「色々|いろいろ」 means various, many colors or kinds. Notice the little repeat mark in the second half when they are written in kanji. It means repeat the kanji before me, so you don't have to write it twice. Also notice how the second half often changes its first sound, like 「とき」 becoming 「どき」 and 「ひと」 becoming 「びと」. Doubling also creates lots of sound words, like 「どきどき」 for a pounding heart. This greeting is a kana word, so there is no repeat mark, but it follows the same rhythm. Two beats, then the same two beats again." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Phone manners in Japan are quite strict in public. On trains and buses, people are expected not to talk on the phone at all. You will hear announcements asking passengers to switch their phones to silent mode, which in Japanese is called manner mode, written in katakana as 「マナーモード」. If a call comes in, people often whisper a quick reply, like I'm on the train, I'll call you back, and hang up. Near priority seats, some trains used to ask people to turn their phones off completely. Even in restaurants and cafes, loud phone calls get looks. So if you hear this greeting on a train, it's usually followed by a whisper and a quick goodbye. Be quiet on the train, and save your calls for the platform." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from the humble verb 「申す|もうす」, to say, repeated as I'm speaking, I'm speaking until it shrank to today's doubled sound. Use it to answer the phone or check that the other person is still there.",
      "On personal calls, say this greeting and then your name, as in 「もしもし、スミスです」, and close with 「失礼します|しつれいします」. Business calls skip it and open with the company name instead.",
      "Japanese loves doubling words for plural, frequent or intense meanings, like 「時々|ときどき」, 「人々|ひとびと」 and 「色々|いろいろ」, often softening the second half's first sound. This greeting follows the same two-beats-twice rhythm.",
      "On Japanese trains and buses people are expected not to talk on the phone, and announcements ask for 「マナーモード」, silent mode. If a call comes in, people whisper a quick reply and call back later."
    ],
    ja: "[もしもし]、たけしさんですか。",
    en: "Hello? Is this Takeshi?" },
  "こども|子供": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings. 「子|こ」 means child. The kanji began as a picture of a baby, with a big head and arms reaching out, and its legs wrapped up. In other words it often takes the Chinese-origin reading 「し」. You see that in 「帽子|ぼうし」, hat, and 「お菓子|おかし」, sweets, from Lesson 11, where it works like a little ending for things. The second kanji, 「供|とも」, means a companion or to attend, and it has the person mark on its left. In this word it is borrowed to write 「ども」, an old ending that made plurals. So the word first meant children, a group of kids. Over time it came to mean just a child, one or more. You will often see this word written with kana for the second half, as 「子ども|こども」. Many schools and newspapers prefer that spelling. The opposite is 「大人|おとな」, adult, in Lesson 13. That is another special reading, made of big and person. A cultural note: Japan has a national holiday for children, 「こどもの日|こどものひ」, on May 5th. Families fly colorful carp-shaped streamers outside their homes. Recap: a baby picture plus a companion, a child." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The child kanji appears in lots of everyday words, and its reading shifts. With 「こ」, you get 「女の子|おんなのこ」, a girl, 「男の子|おとこのこ」, a boy, and 「子犬|こいぬ」, a puppy, a child dog. A son is 「息子|むすこ」, where it becomes 「こ」 at the end. With the Chinese-origin reading 「し」, it often works as a little ending for objects, like 「帽子|ぼうし」, hat. And in 「調子|ちょうし」, condition, as in how are you feeling, it's also 「し」. Then a surprise: in 「椅子|いす」, chair, it is read 「す」. That's a rare reading, but chair is an everyday word, so you will see it often. So the same little baby kanji means child in some words, and is just a handy ending in others. Watch for 「こ」, 「し」 and even 「す」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a grammar puzzle. The second half of this word was once a plural ending, so the word originally meant children. But today, Japanese speakers often add 「たち」 too, saying 「子供たち|こどもたち」, the children. That is a plural on top of an old plural. Over time, the old ending lost its plural feeling, so people added a new one. The old ending lives on elsewhere. In very humble business speech, 「私ども|わたくしども」 means we, our company. And in rough speech, it can be added to people you look down on. Normally, Japanese nouns don't need any plural. One child or ten children are both this word, and the context tells you how many. Use 「たち」 only when you want to stress a group. One word, one child or many, and the context decides." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Every November, families celebrate 「七五三|しちごさん」, seven, five, three. Children of those ages are dressed up, often in kimono, and taken to a shrine to pray for healthy growth. Traditionally, three is for both boys and girls, five is for boys, and seven is for girls, though families vary. The official day is November 15th, but many families go on any convenient weekend around then. Kids receive a long stick of red and white candy, called thousand-year candy, in a bag decorated with cranes and turtles, symbols of long life. In the past, many children didn't survive early childhood, so reaching these ages was a real reason to celebrate. Today, the day is also a big photo occasion, with studios offering costume packages. A day for children to dress up, and for families to give thanks." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「子|こ」 began as a picture of a baby, and 「供|ども」 was borrowed to write an old plural ending, so the word first meant children. It is often written 「子ども|こども」, and its opposite is 「大人|おとな」.",
      "The child kanji shifts readings: 「こ」 in 「女の子|おんなのこ」 and 「子犬|こいぬ」, 「し」 as an ending in 「帽子|ぼうし」, and even 「す」 in 「椅子|いす」, chair. Sometimes it means child, sometimes it is just a handy ending.",
      "The second half was once a plural ending, but it faded, so people now add another plural, 「子供たち|こどもたち」. Normally Japanese nouns need no plural, and context tells you one child or many.",
      "Each November, children aged three, five and seven dress up for 「七五三|しちごさん」 and visit a shrine to pray for healthy growth, receiving long red and white thousand-year candy. It celebrated surviving early childhood."
    ],
    ja: "[子供|こども]が{公園|こうえん}であそんでいます。",
    en: "A child is playing in the park." },
  "しゃしん|写真": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「写|しゃ」, means to copy or to depict. As a verb it shows up with its native reading in 「写す|うつす」, to copy or to capture an image. The second, 「真|しん」, means truth, reality, the genuine thing. Put them together and you get a copy of the truth, an image that captures what is really there. That is a nice fit for a photograph, which records the world exactly as it looks. The word was around before cameras, used for lifelike pictures, and it was naturally picked up for photography. When you take a photo, the verb is 「撮る|とる」, so you say 「写真を撮る|しゃしんをとる」. Notice that 「撮る|とる」 has the hand mark on the left, your hand on the camera. It sounds the same as the ordinary 「取る|とる」, to take, but for photos you use the camera one. One more tip. A selfie is often just called a 「自撮り|じどり」, a self shot, using that same camera kanji. To recap: copy plus truth, a true copy of the world, a photograph." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The truth kanji, 「真|しん」, has a native reading that turns up in many words, 「ま」. As a prefix, it means truly, exactly, or completely. 「真ん中|まんなか」 is the exact middle. 「真っ白|まっしろ」 is pure white, and 「真っ黒|まっくろ」 is pitch black. Notice the small pause that appears in those color words. 「真夏|まなつ」 is the height of summer, and 「真冬|まふゆ」 is the dead of winter. With the Chinese-origin reading 「しん」, it means serious or genuine, as in 「真面目|まじめ」, serious, a special reading worth knowing. Now the first kanji, 「写|しゃ」. Its top is a cover shape, like a lid. Its native verb is 「写す|うつす」, to copy. So this pair is copy plus truth. Truly, exactly, the very middle, a true copy, all from one kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Photos are counted with 「枚|まい」, the counter for flat things like paper, shirts and tickets. So you took three photos is 「写真を三枚撮りました|しゃしんをさんまいとりました」. To show someone your photos, say 「写真を見せてください|しゃしんをみせてください」, please show me the photos, or offer with shall I show you? A photographer is 「写真家|しゃしんか」, with the expert ending. To ask for a group photo, people say let's take a picture together, adding 「一緒に|いっしょに」. On forms like visa applications or job résumés, you are asked to attach a 「写真|しゃしん」 of a set size. A mini dialogue: someone asks, did you take lots of pictures on your trip? You answer, yes, about two hundred. Count them with 「枚|まい」, and show them off." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "When photography arrived in Japan in the mid-nineteenth century, it caused excitement and some fear. Early studios opened in port cities like Nagasaki and Yokohama in the 1860s. A popular rumor of the time said that a photograph could steal part of your soul or shorten your life. A related superstition said that if three people posed together, the one in the middle would die first. Some older people still mention it as a joke today. In the samurai era's last years, there were even famous portraits of figures like Sakamoto Ryōma, which are now historical treasures. So this word was once a little scary. Now everyone carries a camera in their pocket. From soul-stealer to selfie, the photo has come a long way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「写|しゃ」 means to copy and 「真|しん」 means truth, so a photograph is a true copy of the world. You take one with the camera kanji, 「写真を撮る|しゃしんをとる」.",
      "The truth kanji's native reading 「ま」 means truly or exactly, as in 「真ん中|まんなか」, the exact middle, 「真っ白|まっしろ」, pure white, and 「真夏|まなつ」, the height of summer.",
      "Count photos with 「枚|まい」, the counter for flat things, as in 「写真を三枚撮りました|しゃしんをさんまいとりました」. Ask to see them with 「写真を見せてください|しゃしんをみせてください」.",
      "When photography reached Japan in the 1860s, rumors said a photo could steal part of your soul, and the person in the middle of three would die first. From soul-stealer to selfie, it has come a long way."
    ],
    ja: "{海|うみ}の[写真|しゃしん]を{撮りました|とりました}。",
    en: "I took a picture of the sea." },
  "うみ|海": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word uses the native Japanese reading. The kanji is a great one to take apart. On the left are three little strokes, the water mark. You will see it on lots of water words you already know: 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, to swim, 「湖|みずうみ」, lake, 「洗う|あらう」, to wash, and 「温泉|おんせん」, hot spring. Now look at the right side. It is 「毎|まい」, the same kanji as in 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, and 「毎晩|まいばん」, every night. A way to remember it: every drop of water eventually ends up in the sea. Water plus every equals sea. That is just a hook, but the two pieces really are water and every. Japan is an island country, so the sea is everywhere in daily life, from seafood to summer beach trips. There is even a national holiday in July called 「海の日|うみのひ」, Marine Day, to give thanks for the ocean. When you go to the beach in Japanese, you usually just say 「海に行く|うみにいく」, go to the sea. To recap: water mark plus every, and every drop flows to the sea." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The sea kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「かい」. You'll see it in 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, sea outside, as in a trip abroad. 「日本海|にほんかい」 is the Sea of Japan, and 「海岸|かいがん」 is the seashore. The native sea word also hides in 「湖|みずうみ」, lake. Its sound is literally water plus sea, a freshwater sea, though it is written with its own kanji. And there are fun special readings. 「海老|えび」, shrimp, is written sea plus old, maybe because a shrimp's bent back and long whiskers look like an old man's. That reading doesn't come from the kanji sounds at all. And 「海苔|のり」, the seaweed wrapped around sushi and rice balls, is also a special reading. So this one kanji gives you overseas, the seashore, shrimp and seaweed. The sea is full of words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Summer by the sea is a big part of Japanese life. Each summer, beaches hold an opening day called 「海開き|うみびらき」, the official start of the swimming season, often in July. Before that, swimming may be allowed, but no lifeguards are on duty. Along popular beaches, temporary huts called sea houses, 「海の家|うみのいえ」, pop up for the season. They sell classic beach food like fried noodles, shaved ice with syrup, and ramen, and offer showers and lockers. Another custom is watermelon splitting. A blindfolded person tries to whack a watermelon with a stick while friends shout directions. The season usually winds down in mid-August, around the Obon holiday, when jellyfish appear and people say it's time to stop swimming. Opening day, sea houses and watermelon, that is summer at the sea in Japan." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the reading. The word sounds a bit like oo-me, as in, ooh, me! Picture yourself running across hot sand toward the sea. You dive in, and every single wave lifts you up, again and again, every day. That is the right side of the kanji, every. And the three drops on the left are the water splashing in your face. Each time a wave rises you shout, ooh, me, me! Your friends on the beach laugh, but you don't care. You are riding the waves every day of summer. So, water plus every, and a happy ooh, me! That is the sea. Picture the waves lifting you, and the word comes right back." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The left side is the water mark and the right side is 「毎|まい」, every, so a handy hook is that every drop of water ends up in the sea. To go to the beach, just say 「海に行く|うみにいく」.",
      "With the reading 「かい」 it builds 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, and 「海岸|かいがん」, seashore. Special readings give 「海老|えび」, shrimp, and 「海苔|のり」, the seaweed around sushi and rice balls.",
      "Japanese beach summers start with 「海開き|うみびらき」, opening day, and seasonal huts called 「海の家|うみのいえ」 sell fried noodles and shaved ice. The season winds down around Obon, when jellyfish appear.",
      "The word sounds like ooh, me, so picture riding the waves every day of summer, the every on the right of the kanji, with water splashing your face from the three drops on the left."
    ],
    ja: "{夏|なつ}に[海|うみ]へいきます。",
    en: "I go to the sea in summer." },
  "サーフィン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a loanword from English, surfing. Watch how the sounds got squeezed into Japanese. The sur part becomes 「サー」, with the long bar standing in for that drawn-out vowel. Then the f sound. Old Japanese had no real f followed by i, so a small 「ィ」 was added after 「フ」 to make 「フィ」. You will see that same trick in other loanwords with fi sounds. Finally, the ing ending. Usually it becomes 「イング」 in Japanese, but here the final g dropped away, leaving just 「イン」. To say you do it, use 「サーフィンをする」, to do surfing, just like other sports. Japan has a real surf culture. The Shonan coast near Tokyo is famous for it, and surfing made its Olympic debut at the Tokyo Games, held on a beach in Chiba. So the word goes naturally with 「海|うみ」, the sea, which you just learned. To recap: English surfing, with a long 「サー」, the small 「ィ」 trick for f, and the g dropped at the end." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word shows a pattern you'll meet in other loanwords: the English ing ending can come into Japanese in two shapes. Usually it keeps the g sound, as 「イング」. You know 「ショッピング」, shopping, and 「ジョギング」, jogging. 「ハイキング」, hiking, works the same way. But a few older or very common words lost the final sound and became just 「イン」, like this one. A famous food example is 「プリン」, the much-loved Japanese custard pudding. It comes from English pudding, with the ending squeezed and the d sound changed too. Notice also the small 「ィ」 here. Newer loanwords use it to make sounds Japanese didn't have, like 「フィ」 and 「ティ」. So when you hear a loanword ending in 「イン」, it may be hiding an English ing. Shopping keeps its tail, but surfing and pudding lost theirs." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To say you are going surfing, use the purpose pattern from Lesson 7. A noun of activity plus 「に行く|にいく」. 「サーフィンに行きます|サーフィンにいきます」 means I'm going surfing. You can do the same with 「買い物|かいもの」, shopping, or 「スキー」, skiing. The person who surfs is a 「サーファー」. The board is just 「ボード」. And the most important word for surfers is 「波|なみ」, wave. You might hear today the waves are good, or big waves. To say you can surf, you'll use a pattern from Lesson 13. Japanese surfers often head out at dawn, before work. Here is a quick exchange. Someone asks what you do on weekends. You say I go surfing at the sea. Go surfing, ride the waves, and talk about them in Japanese." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Many sports in Japanese are loanwords, and most of them take 「する」, to do. 「テニスをする」, play tennis. 「ゴルフをする」, play golf. 「スキーをする」, to ski. This word works the same way. Notice that English uses different verbs, like play golf, go skiing, or surf. Japanese just uses do for all of them. Compare native verbs. Swimming has its own verb, 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, so you don't say do swimming in everyday speech. Soccer and baseball work with 「する」 too, as in 「野球をする|やきゅうをする」. For some activities you can also say 「やる」, a more casual do. So if you learn a new sport in katakana, just add 「をする」 and you're ready. Loanword sports plus do, the easiest grammar in Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This loanword from English surfing uses a long bar for 「サー」, the small 「ィ」 trick to make 「フィ」, and drops the final g. You do it with 「サーフィンをする」.",
      "English ing usually becomes 「イング」, as in 「ショッピング」 and 「ジョギング」, but a few common words shrank to 「イン」, like this one and 「プリン」, pudding. The small 「ィ」 makes sounds Japanese lacked.",
      "Use the purpose pattern, 「サーフィンに行きます|サーフィンにいきます」, to say you are going surfing. The key surfer word is 「波|なみ」, wave, and a surfer is a 「サーファー」.",
      "Most loanword sports simply take 「する」, like 「テニスをする」 and 「ゴルフをする」, where English uses play, go or a separate verb. Swimming is the exception, with its own verb 「泳ぐ|およぐ」."
    ],
    ja: "{海|うみ}で[サーフィン]をしました。",
    en: "I went surfing in the sea." },
  "たんじょうび|誕生日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji. The first two make the word for birth, and the last one is day. 「誕|たん」 means birth. You will rarely see it outside this word, so think of it as the birthday kanji. 「生|じょう」 means life or to be born. This is a less common reading of a very common kanji. You already know it as 「せい」 in 「学生|がくせい」, student, 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, and 「生活|せいかつ」, daily life. Same kanji, same idea of life, just a different Chinese-origin reading here. Then 「日|び」, day. It is normally 「ひ」, but the h sound softens to a b sound when it is joined on, a common sound change. So birth plus day, birthday, exactly like English. To wish someone a happy birthday, say 「お誕生日おめでとう|おたんじょうびおめでとう」, adding 「ございます」 to be polite. Here is a cultural note. Japan used to count everyone's age by the New Year, so everyone got a year older together. Personal birthday celebrations became really common only in the twentieth century. To recap: birth, life, day, the day you came to life." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The life kanji, 「生|せい」, has more readings than almost any other kanji, so this word is a good time to meet them. Here it is 「じょう」. With 「しょう」, it means a whole life, as in 「一生|いっしょう」, a lifetime. With 「せい」, you know 「学生|がくせい」, student. Its native readings include 「い」 in 「生きる|いきる」, to live, and 「う」 in 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born, which you'll meet with birth dates. And 「なま」 means raw or fresh, as in 「生ビール|なまビール」, draft beer, and 「生卵|なまたまご」, a raw egg. The kanji is often explained as a plant sprouting from the ground, though that's the traditional reading of its shape. Now the first kanji, 「誕|たん」. It has the speech mark on the left, and it originally meant big or boastful talk. How it came to mean birth is unclear. Born, alive, raw, and a lifetime, all in one kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To ask about someone's birthday, say 「誕生日はいつですか|たんじょうびはいつですか」, when is your birthday? The answer uses months and dates, like 「三月三日です|さんがつみっかです」, March 3rd. Watch for the special date readings from Lesson 4, like 「みっか」, 「とおか」, and 「はつか」 for the twentieth. A birthday gift is 「誕生日プレゼント|たんじょうびプレゼント」. In Lesson 14, you can say what you got with 「もらう」, and what you gave with 「あげる」. People often sing the English birthday song, and many Japanese families have a cake with candles, often a strawberry shortcake. To wish someone well casually, say 「誕生日おめでとう|たんじょうびおめでとう」, and with friends, even just 「おめでとう」. Ask the date, give the gift, and say congratulations." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "One birthday is especially important in Japan: the sixtieth, called 「還暦|かんれき」. The name means returning to the calendar. In the traditional East Asian calendar, combining the twelve zodiac animals with ten other signs makes a cycle of sixty years. At sixty, you have lived through the whole cycle and returned to the year you were born, like a fresh start. To celebrate, family members traditionally give the person something red, often a red sleeveless jacket and a red cap. Red is linked with babies and with warding off bad luck, so it symbolizes being reborn. Today, many people find the red jacket a bit embarrassing, so families give red sweaters, red wine, or red flowers instead. Turning sixty in Japan means starting your life over, in red." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「誕|たん」 is birth, 「生|じょう」 is life with a less common reading, and 「日|び」 is day softened from 「ひ」, so birth plus day, exactly like English. Say 「お誕生日おめでとう|おたんじょうびおめでとう」 to celebrate.",
      "The life kanji has many readings: 「せい」 in 「学生|がくせい」, 「い」 in 「生きる|いきる」, 「う」 in 「生まれる|うまれる」, and 「なま」 for raw, as in 「生卵|なまたまご」. It is born, alive, raw and a lifetime in one kanji.",
      "Ask 「誕生日はいつですか|たんじょうびはいつですか」 and answer with a date like 「三月三日です|さんがつみっかです」, watching the special date readings. Friends can simply say 「おめでとう」.",
      "The sixtieth birthday, 「還暦|かんれき」, marks returning to your birth year after a full sixty-year calendar cycle. Families traditionally give something red, like a red jacket and cap, to symbolize being reborn."
    ],
    ja: "あしたは{私|わたし}の[誕生日|たんじょうび]です。",
    en: "Tomorrow is my birthday." },
  "てがみ|手紙": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native Japanese readings. 「手|て」 means hand. You know it from 「手伝う|てつだう」, to help, lending a hand, and 「手袋|てぶくろ」, gloves, hand bags. It is also in 「上手|じょうず」 and 「下手|へた」, good at and bad at, skilled or clumsy hands. 「紙|かみ」 means paper. The left side is the thread mark, because paper was made from fibers. In this word 「かみ」 softens to 「がみ」 when it is joined to 「て」. Paper by hand, a handwritten letter. That is the image. Be careful with the sound 「かみ」. It also means hair, 「髪|かみ」, with a different kanji. Here is a fun fact. In Chinese, the very same two characters mean toilet paper, so a Chinese reader may smile at this word. To say you write one, use 「手紙を書く|てがみをかく」, and for sending, 「送る|おくる」. Japan still has a strong letter culture, like New Year postcards sent to everyone you know. To recap: hand plus paper, a letter written by hand." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji have Chinese-origin readings you'll meet in other words. The hand kanji is read 「しゅ」 in words for people with a skill, as if hands stand for the person doing the work. 「歌手|かしゅ」 is a singer. 「選手|せんしゅ」 is an athlete or player. 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」 is a driver. The paper kanji is read 「し」 in compounds. 「用紙|ようし」 is a form or sheet of paper for some purpose, like an answer sheet. And 「和紙|わし」 is traditional Japanese paper, made from plant fibers, famous for being strong and soft. Look at the paper kanji's left side again: the thread mark. The right side mainly gives the sound. So when you see the thread mark, think fibers, string and cloth, and now paper. Hands become skilled people, and thread becomes paper." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Formal Japanese letters follow set rules. They often open with a fixed greeting word, 「拝啓|はいけい」, something like dear sir or madam, and close with a matching word, 「敬具|けいぐ」, respectfully yours. Right after the opening, writers add a line about the season, like the cherry blossoms are blooming, or it is getting colder. Then they ask about the reader's health before getting to the point. On the envelope, the name goes in the middle, often written vertically, followed by 「様|さま」, a polite title like Mr. or Ms. To a friend, though, you can write a casual letter without all that. A short handwritten note is also common with gifts. You can write it with 「手紙を書く|てがみをかく」 and send it with 「出す|だす」, to mail. Formal letters follow a script, but a note to a friend can be free." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The biggest letter tradition in Japan is the New Year card, 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」. People send postcards to friends, relatives, coworkers and teachers, with greetings and often that year's zodiac animal. If you post them by a deadline in late December, the post office holds them and delivers them all on the morning of January 1st. Families enjoy sorting through the stack together. Post office cards even include lottery numbers, with small prizes. There is also an important rule. If someone in your family died that year, you don't send greetings. Instead, in November or early December, you send a mourning postcard to let people know not to send you one. In recent years, more people send greetings by phone apps, but millions of cards still go out. A card for every relationship, delivered on New Year's morning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「手|て」 is hand and 「紙|かみ」 is paper, softened to 「がみ」, so a letter is paper by hand. Write one with 「手紙を書く|てがみをかく」, and fun fact, in Chinese the same characters mean toilet paper.",
      "The hand kanji reads 「しゅ」 for skilled people, as in 「歌手|かしゅ」, singer, and 「選手|せんしゅ」, athlete. The paper kanji reads 「し」, as in 「和紙|わし」, and its thread mark points to fibers.",
      "Formal letters open with 「拝啓|はいけい」, add a seasonal line and ask about the reader's health, then close with 「敬具|けいぐ」. A note to a friend can skip all that.",
      "People send New Year cards, 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, to everyone they know, and cards posted by the deadline all arrive on January 1st. If your family had a death that year, you send a mourning card instead."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}に[手紙|てがみ]を{書きました|かきました}。",
    en: "I wrote a letter to my mother." },
  "すき|好き": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is made of two pieces you may already recognize: 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left, and 「子|こ」, child, on the right. It is commonly explained as a mother with her child, the picture of affection. A lovely way to remember liking something. Here it takes its native reading, 「す」. Now the grammar, which trips up English speakers. In English you like something, and that thing is the object. In Japanese, the thing you like is marked with 「が」, not 「を」. So you say 「ねこが好きです|ねこがすきです」, I like cats. A way to think about it: the cat is likable to me. Also, even though this word looks like it ends in an i sound, it is a na-adjective. So before a noun you say 「好きな食べ物|すきなたべもの」, favorite food, and the negative is 「好きじゃない|すきじゃない」. Add 「大|だい」, big, in front and you get 「大好き|だいすき」, to love something. One more cultural note. 「好きです|すきです」 said directly to a person is a classic love confession in Japan. To recap: woman plus child, affection, with 「が」 marking what you like." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here are patterns that let you say much more with this word. To like doing something, turn the verb into a noun with 「の」, from Lesson 8. 「本を読むのが好きです|ほんをよむのがすきです」 means I like reading books. To talk about your favorite thing, put it before a noun with 「な」. 「好きな色は青です|すきないろはあおです」, my favorite color is blue. Now the negative. The opposite word is 「嫌い|きらい」, dislike, another na-adjective, even though it ends in 「い」. But Japanese people often avoid saying it directly, because it sounds strong. Instead they say 「あまり好きじゃないです|あまりすきじゃないです」, I don't really like it. That softens things a lot, especially about food someone made for you. Like doing things with 「の」, and soften your dislikes with 「あまり」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The liking kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「こう」, and a second native reading, 「この」. With 「こう」, you get 「好物|こうぶつ」, a favorite food, liked thing, as in my favorite food is curry. And 「友好|ゆうこう」, friendship, often between countries or cities, like sister-city ties. With 「この」, you get 「好み|このみ」, taste or preference, the things someone likes. And here's a delicious one: 「お好み焼き|おこのみやき」, the famous savory pancake. The name literally means grilled as you like it, because you choose what goes in, like cabbage, pork, seafood or cheese. Osaka and Hiroshima each have their own famous style. So the liking kanji gives you favorite food, friendship, personal taste, and a pancake. Next time you eat one, you're eating it as you like it." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful with the long vowel. This word is two short beats. But 「スキー」, with the long bar, is skiing. So if you say you like skiing, it's 「スキーが好きです|スキーがすきです」, and the two words sit right next to each other. Say the long vowel clearly, or it sounds like I like liking. There is also another native word with the same short sound, meaning a gap or an opening, like a gap in someone's guard. You'll see that in martial arts or sports commentary. It's rare in everyday talk, so context usually makes it clear. And watch the pitch. In standard Japanese, this word has its pitch rise after the first beat. So the short word is liking, the long word is skiing. Hold the vowel, and you won't say you like liking." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is woman plus child, commonly explained as a mother's affection. The thing you like takes 「が」, as in 「ねこが好きです|ねこがすきです」, and it is a na-adjective despite its look.",
      "Like doing something by adding 「の」, as in 「本を読むのが好きです|ほんをよむのがすきです」, and name favorites with 「な」. Soften dislikes with 「あまり好きじゃないです|あまりすきじゃないです」 rather than the strong 「嫌い|きらい」.",
      "With 「こう」 the kanji gives 「好物|こうぶつ」, a favorite food, and with 「この」 it gives 「好み|このみ」, taste. 「お好み焼き|おこのみやき」 literally means grilled as you like it.",
      "This word is two short beats, while 「スキー」 with the long bar is skiing. Hold the vowel in 「スキーが好きです|スキーがすきです」, or you'll say you like liking."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}は{猫|ねこ}が[好き|すき]です。",
    en: "I like cats." },
  "おいしい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana, but you may see it with kanji as 「美味しい|おいしい」. Those kanji mean beautiful and taste. You know the first from 「美術館|びじゅつかん」, art museum, and the second from 「意味|いみ」, meaning. So it is literally beautiful taste. Where does the sound come from? There was an old word, 「いしい」, meaning good or tasty. The polite 「お」 was added in front, and it was used as refined language by women at the imperial court before spreading to everyone. Today it is the standard, polite way to say something tastes good. Men often say 「うまい」 in casual speech, which means the same thing. If something tastes bad, the word is 「まずい」. Be careful, it is quite blunt. Here is a habit worth copying. In Japan, people say 「おいしい」 out loud the moment they taste something, at home, on TV, everywhere. After a meal, say 「おいしかったです」, it was delicious, using the past form. To recap: polite 「お」 plus an old word for good, beautiful taste." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you can say delicious, you'll want to describe tastes. Here are the basic flavor words, all i-adjectives. 「甘い|あまい」 means sweet. It can also describe someone who is too soft or lenient, like a parent who spoils a child. 「辛い|からい」 means spicy hot, like curry. Careful, the same kanji is read 「つらい」 when it means painful or hard. 「しょっぱい」 means salty. 「すっぱい」 means sour, like a pickled plum. 「苦い|にがい」 means bitter, like black coffee. And for flavorful but not specific, 「おいしい」 covers it all. You can combine them, like sweet and delicious. Japanese food fans also talk about rich versus light flavors. So at a meal, you can say this is sweet, that's spicy, and it's all delicious. Five flavors, and one word to praise them all." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular i-adjective, so its forms are a good model. The negative is 「おいしくない」, not delicious. The past is 「おいしかった」, it was delicious. The past negative is 「おいしくなかった」. Now a useful new pattern from Lesson 13. Drop the final 「い」 and add 「そう」, and you get 「おいしそう」, it looks delicious. Japanese people say this the moment food is placed in front of them, before tasting. So the classic order at a meal is: food arrives, you say it looks delicious, you say the greeting before eating, you taste, then you say it's delicious. In Lesson 7, you can also link it with the te-form: 「安くておいしい|やすくておいしい」, cheap and delicious, a favorite phrase for good restaurants. Looks delicious, tastes delicious, and cheap and delicious, too." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Its casual cousin, 「うまい」, gave the world a famous food word. In 1908, a Japanese chemist named Kikunae Ikeda wondered why kelp broth tasted so good. He found that the key was glutamate, and he named this savory taste 「うま味|うまみ」, literally the delicious taste, from 「うまい」. Today, umami is recognized worldwide as the fifth basic taste, alongside sweet, sour, salty and bitter. It's the deep, savory flavor in soup stock, soy sauce, mushrooms, tomatoes and aged cheese. Ikeda's discovery also led to a well-known seasoning product. So when you say delicious with this word, remember that its casual partner gave a name to a whole taste. Next time you sip miso soup, you're tasting a word from Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "An old word for good, 「いしい」, got the polite 「お」 at the imperial court, and the optional kanji 「美味しい|おいしい」 mean beautiful taste. Say it the moment you taste something, and 「おいしかったです」 after the meal.",
      "The basic flavor words are all i-adjectives: 「甘い|あまい」 sweet, 「辛い|からい」 spicy, 「しょっぱい」 salty, 「すっぱい」 sour and 「苦い|にがい」 bitter. This word praises them all.",
      "As a regular i-adjective it gives 「おいしくない」 and 「おいしかった」, and dropping the 「い」 for 「おいしそう」 means it looks delicious, said as food arrives. 「安くておいしい|やすくておいしい」 praises a good, cheap restaurant.",
      "In 1908 chemist Kikunae Ikeda found glutamate made kelp broth tasty and named the taste 「うま味|うまみ」, from the casual 「うまい」. Umami is now known worldwide as the fifth basic taste."
    ],
    ja: "このケーキはとても[おいしい]です。",
    en: "This cake is very delicious." },
  "おおきい|大きい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「大|おお」 is a picture of a person standing with arms and legs spread wide, making themselves as big as possible. Here it takes its native reading 「おお」. But the same kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「だい」, and you know many words with it. 「大学|だいがく」, college, is the big school. 「大好き|だいすき」 is big liking, to love. 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, it's okay, also starts with it. There is also 「たい」 in 「大変|たいへん」, tough, a big change or big trouble. And a fun irregular one: 「大人|おとな」, adult, a big person. The word itself is an i-adjective. The past is 「大きかった|おおきかった」, and the negative is 「大きくない|おおきくない」. You may also see 「大きな|おおきな」 before a noun, which means the same thing. The opposite is 「小さい|ちいさい」, small. To recap: a person stretching out wide, big, read 「おお」 here and 「だい」 in many other words." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch the spelling of the long vowel. Most long o sounds are written with 「う」, like 「おとうさん」 or 「どうぞ」. But this word writes it with 「お」, 「おお」. It belongs to a small group of exceptions. Others you'll meet are 「多い|おおい」, many, 「遠い|とおい」, far, and 「十|とお」, ten things. There's even a sentence Japanese kids use to remember them. Now another trap. Big, and many, 「多い|おおい」, start with the same sound. Big is about size, many is about number. A big dog is one large animal. Many dogs is a crowd of them. They also use different kanji. So the sound 「おお」 at the start can be big or many, and you need to listen for the rest. Long o with 「お」, and keep big and many apart." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Big is used in some ways English doesn't use it. A loud voice is a big voice. 「声が大きい|こえがおおきい」 means someone talks loudly. Turn up the volume is make the sound bigger. And to ask someone to speak up, you can say please say it in a bigger voice, using the adverb form 「大きく|おおきく」. With 「なる」 from Lesson 10, 「大きくなる|おおきくなる」 means to grow bigger, or to grow up. People often tell children you've gotten so big! And 「大きくなったら|おおきくなったら」 means when I grow up, as in when I grow up I want to be a pilot. For clothes, though, stores usually use letter sizes, like L or M, in English. So big covers loud voices, growing children and dreams for the future." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Imagine you're walking down the street and see a giant key, taller than a building, lying across the road. You stop and shout, oh, key! Oh, so big! Everyone around you looks up too. It's a key the size of a train. Now look at the kanji again. It's a person spreading their arms and legs as wide as possible, trying to hold that giant key. The person strains and stretches, but the key is still bigger. So whenever you hear this word, think of someone shouting oh, key! at a key too big to carry. Big person, giant key, oh, key!" }
    ],
    short: [
      "「大|おお」 is a person spreading arms and legs wide to look big, read 「だい」 in words like 「大学|だいがく」 and 「大好き|だいすき」. It is an i-adjective, the opposite of 「小さい|ちいさい」.",
      "Its long vowel is written 「おお」, one of a few exceptions like 「多い|おおい」 and 「遠い|とおい」. Keep big apart from 「多い|おおい」, many, since big is size and many is number.",
      "A loud voice is a big voice, 「声が大きい|こえがおおきい」, and 「大きくなる|おおきくなる」 means to grow up. 「大きくなったら|おおきくなったら」 starts a when I grow up dream.",
      "Picture a giant key lying across the road and shout oh, key, oh, so big, while the spread-out person of the kanji strains to hold it. Big person, giant key, oh, key."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}の{家|いえ}は[大きい|おおきい]です。",
    en: "My house is big." },
  "ちいさい|小さい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「小|ちい」 is a small, simple shape: a short vertical stroke with a tiny mark on each side. A way to remember it: something tiny being split into even tinier bits. That is a hook, not an origin story. Here it takes its native reading, 「ちい」. It is the partner of 「大きい|おおきい」, big. Learn them as a pair. Just like big, it is an i-adjective. The past is 「小さかった|ちいさかった」, and the negative is 「小さくない|ちいさくない」. And just like big, there is also a form 「小さな|ちいさな」 that goes before a noun, as in 「小さな町|ちいさなまち」, a small town. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「しょう」, as in 「小学校|しょうがっこう」, elementary school, the small school. That pairs nicely with 「大学|だいがく」, the big school. Kids and casual speakers often say 「ちっちゃい」, a cute, squeezed version of the same word. To recap: a tiny stroke split by little marks, small, the partner of big." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Compare this kanji with a close look-alike, 「少|すこ」, as in 「少し|すこし」, a little. That one is the small kanji with an extra slanted stroke at the bottom. The meanings are close but different. This kanji is about size, small in size. The look-alike is about amount, a little or few. Its other word is 「少ない|すくない」, few, the opposite of many. So a small cat uses this kanji, and a few cats uses the look-alike. This kanji also has short native readings. It becomes 「こ」 in 「小鳥|ことり」, a little bird, and 「小指|こゆび」, the little finger. And it becomes 「お」 in 「小川|おがわ」, a small stream, a common family name too. So small can be 「ちい」, 「しょう」, 「こ」 or even 「お」. Size uses this one, amount uses the one with the extra stroke." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some everyday ways to use small. A quiet voice is a small voice, 「声が小さい|こえがちいさい」, so a teacher might say speak up, your voice is small. The phrase 「小さい時|ちいさいとき」 means when I was little, a very common way to talk about your childhood. When I was little, I lived in Osaka. In the kitchen, 「小さく切る|ちいさくきる」 means cut it into small pieces. And with 「なる」, things become small, like a sweater that shrank in the wash. People also say 「小さい|ちいさい」 about small worries, like it's a small matter, don't worry. Kids use the cute 「ちっちゃい」 all the time, for tiny bugs or baby shoes. So small describes voices, childhood, cooking and worries, not only size." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. The word sounds a bit like cheese size. Picture a tiny mouse at a cheese shop. It points at the biggest wheel of cheese and says that one. The shopkeeper laughs and hands over a crumb, a little cheese-sized piece no bigger than a grain of rice. The mouse holds it up proudly. Now look at the kanji: a short line in the middle with a tiny mark on each side. That's the mouse in the middle holding a crumb in each hand. So when you hear the word, picture the mouse with its cheese-sized crumbs. Tiny mouse, tiny cheese, cheese size, small." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「小|ちい」 is a short stroke with tiny marks on each side, the partner of 「大きい|おおきい」, and it reads 「しょう」 in 「小学校|しょうがっこう」, the small school. Kids say the cute 「ちっちゃい」.",
      "This kanji is about size, while the look-alike 「少|すこ」 with an extra stroke is about amount, as in 「少し|すこし」 and 「少ない|すくない」. It also reads 「こ」 in 「小鳥|ことり」 and 「お」 in 「小川|おがわ」.",
      "A quiet voice is a small voice, 「声が小さい|こえがちいさい」, and 「小さい時|ちいさいとき」 means when I was little. In the kitchen, 「小さく切る|ちいさくきる」 means cut into small pieces.",
      "The word sounds like cheese size, so picture a tiny mouse proudly holding a cheese-sized crumb in each hand, just like the kanji's short middle line with a tiny mark on each side."
    ],
    ja: "この[小さい|ちいさい]{猫|ねこ}はかわいいです。",
    en: "This small cat is cute." },
  "あたらしい|新しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「新|あたら」 has two sides. On the left are the pieces for stand and tree, and on the right is a shape that means axe. It is often explained as taking an axe to a tree to get fresh wood. Fresh cut wood is new. In this word it has its native reading, 「あたら」. The Chinese-origin reading is 「しん」, and you know it from two words already. 「新聞|しんぶん」, newspaper, is new things heard, the news. And 「新幹線|しんかんせん」, the bullet train, is the new trunk line. There is also the famous Tokyo district Shinjuku, written with this same kanji at the front. It is an i-adjective, so the past is 「新しかった|あたらしかった」 and the negative is 「新しくない|あたらしくない」. Its opposite is 「古い|ふるい」, old. At New Year in Japan, people buy new things and start fresh, which fits the feeling of this word. To recap: an axe cutting fresh wood, new, read 「しん」 in newspaper and bullet train." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has a strange history, one that Japanese language historians often mention. In older Japanese, new was 「あらたし」, with the sounds in a different order. You can still see that in the word 「新た|あらた」, meaning new or fresh, as in a fresh start. Meanwhile, there was a separate old word, 「あたらし」, meaning precious, or what a shame to waste. Over time, the two words got confused, and the form with 「あたら」 took over the meaning new. The original meaning of precious faded away. So today's word is built on a mix-up of two old words. That's why the reading in this word is 「あたら」, while the related word 「新た|あらた」 keeps the older order. Old words get mixed up, and new ones are born from the mistake." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The new kanji builds lots of useful words with its Chinese-origin reading 「しん」. 「新年|しんねん」 is the new year. 「新鮮|しんせん」 means fresh, like fresh fish or vegetables. 「新人|しんじん」 is a newcomer, a rookie at work or a new singer. And 「新入生|しんにゅうせい」 is a new student, one who just entered school. There's also a rare native reading, 「にい」, in the place name 「新潟|にいがた」, a prefecture famous for rice and sake. Compare the opposite family: 「古い|ふるい」 means old for things, but for people, you say older or elderly with other words. Don't call a person 「古い|ふるい」. And note that this word is for things, while for a person being young, you use 「若い|わかい」. New things, fresh food, new people, and a rice-growing region, all from one kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, the big new start of the year is actually April. The school year and most company business years begin on April 1st. That's when new students enter schools, with entrance ceremonies, and new employees start work, often all on the same day. Fresh graduates in dark suits fill trains and offices. It all happens right around cherry blossom season, so pink petals and new beginnings go together in people's minds. You'll hear words like new school term and new employees everywhere. Companies hold welcome parties, often cherry blossom viewing picnics in the park. And spring is a busy time for buying new things, like school bags, suits and furniture for first apartments. In Japan, new means spring and April as much as New Year." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is often explained as an axe taken to a tree for fresh-cut wood, and its reading 「しん」 appears in 「新聞|しんぶん」 and 「新幹線|しんかんせん」. Its opposite is 「古い|ふるい」.",
      "Old Japanese said 「あらたし」 for new, still seen in 「新た|あらた」, while a separate 「あたらし」 meant precious. The two got confused, and the 「あたら」 form took over the meaning new.",
      "With 「しん」 it builds 「新年|しんねん」, new year, 「新鮮|しんせん」, fresh, and 「新人|しんじん」, a rookie. This word is for things, while young people are 「若い|わかい」.",
      "Japan's big fresh start is April, when the school year and business year begin and new students and employees start together during cherry blossom season. New means spring as much as New Year."
    ],
    ja: "[新しい|あたらしい]{時計|とけい}を{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought a new watch." },
  "ふるい|古い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「古|ふる」 is made of 「十|じゅう」, ten, on top of 「口|くち」, mouth. A way to remember it: a story passed down through ten mouths, generation after generation, must be very old. That is a hook, not a proven origin. Here it takes its native reading, 「ふる」. It is the opposite of 「新しい|あたらしい」, new. Now an important usage note. Use this word for things, ideas and buildings, like 「古いお寺|ふるいおてら」, an old temple. Do not use it to say a person is old. Calling someone 「古い|ふるい」 sounds like they are outdated. For people, talk about age, or say someone is 「年上|としうえ」, older than you. Here is another trap. 「古い友だち|ふるいともだち」 means a friend you have known for a long time, not an elderly friend. It is an i-adjective, so the past is 「古かった|ふるかった」 and the negative is 「古くない|ふるくない」. Japan has a lot of very old things, from temples in Kyoto to shops running for hundreds of years. To recap: ten mouths passing down a story, old, for things and not for people." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for old has two readings you will keep meeting. The native reading, 「ふる」, lives in everyday words. 「古本|ふるほん」 is an old book, a used book, and a 「古本屋|ふるほんや」 is a secondhand bookshop. 「古着|ふるぎ」 is old clothes, meaning secondhand clothing, which is very popular with young people in Tokyo. The Chinese-origin reading is 「こ」. You hear it in 「中古|ちゅうこ」, used or secondhand, as in 「中古車|ちゅうこしゃ」, a used car. You also hear it in 「古典|こてん」, the classics, old literature studied at school. So the native reading tends to sit in homely words for old stuff, and the Chinese reading in more technical or bookish ones. Here is a bonus. This kanji hides inside another one you know. The counter 「個|こ」, for small objects, has a person on the left, and on the right a box with this old kanji inside it. That is why the counter is also read 「こ」. So when you see this shape, think old, and expect either 「ふる」 or 「こ」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful with a few look-alike sounds. You also know the verb 「降る|ふる」, to fall, as in 「雨が降る|あめがふる」, rain falls. That verb has no 「い」 on the end, and it uses a completely different kanji. If you hear 「ふるい」 with that final 「い」, think old. If you hear 「ふる」 or 「ふります」, think rain or snow. Here is a lovely related word: 「ふるさと」, your hometown, the place you come from. It is often written with the old kanji plus the kanji for village, so it literally feels like your old village. There is a famous children's song simply called 「ふるさと」, about the mountains and rivers of home, and many Japanese people can sing it by heart. In conversation, you can turn this adjective into a change of state too. 「古くなる|ふるくなる」 means to get old, like a phone that is getting old and slow. So the 「い」 on the end matters: with it you get old, without it you get falling rain." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adjective is not just about age in years. It often means out of date. 「考えが古い|かんがえがふるい」 means your way of thinking is old-fashioned. A teenager might say it to a parent, and it stings a little. 「この情報は古いです|このじょうほうはふるいです」 means this information is out of date. With food, it suggests something is no longer fresh. 「古いパン|ふるいパン」 is old bread, past its best. Be careful with time words. If you want to say long ago, use the noun 「昔|むかし」, not this adjective. 「昔の写真|むかしのしゃしん」 is a photo from long ago. 「古い写真|ふるいしゃしん」 is an old photo, maybe faded and curled at the edges. Here is a mini dialogue. One friend says 「そのかばん、古いね|そのかばん、ふるいね」, that bag is old, isn't it. The other answers 「うん、でも好きなんだ|うん、でもすきなんだ」, yeah, but I like it. So this word covers old things, stale things, and outdated ideas, while long ago belongs to a different word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is ten over mouth, a handy hook for a story passed down through ten mouths. Use it for things, never to call a person old, and 「古い友だち|ふるいともだち」 is a long-time friend.",
      "The native reading 「ふる」 sits in homely words like 「古本|ふるほん」 and 「古着|ふるぎ」, while 「こ」 appears in 「中古|ちゅうこ」, used, and 「古典|こてん」. It even hides inside the counter 「個|こ」.",
      "Don't confuse it with 「降る|ふる」, to fall, as in rain, which has no 「い」 on the end. A lovely relative is 「ふるさと」, your hometown, often written as old village.",
      "It often means out of date, as in 「考えが古い|かんがえがふるい」, old-fashioned thinking, or stale food. For long ago use 「昔|むかし」, since 「昔の写真|むかしのしゃしん」 differs from a faded 「古い写真|ふるいしゃしん」."
    ],
    ja: "この[古い|ふるい]{お寺|おてら}はきれいです。",
    en: "This old temple is beautiful." },
  "たべもの|食べ物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is a verb plus a noun, a very handy pattern in Japanese. The first part, 「食べ|たべ」, is the stem of 「食べる|たべる」, to eat. The second part, 「物|もの」, means thing, a physical object. Eat plus thing, things to eat, food. The same kanji has a Chinese-origin reading in 「食堂|しょくどう」, cafeteria, the eating hall. And 「物|もの」 shows up all over your vocabulary. Its partner is 「飲み物|のみもの」, things to drink. 「買い物|かいもの」 is buying things, shopping. 「着物|きもの」 is a thing to wear, a kimono. 「果物|くだもの」 is fruit, and 「建物|たてもの」 is a building. Once you see the pattern, you can often guess a new word: verb stem plus 「物|もの」. A natural question to ask a new friend is 「どんな食べ物が好きですか|どんなたべものがすきですか」, what kind of food do you like. To recap: eat plus thing, food, partner of drink, one of a whole family of thing words." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the kanji for eat. Most dictionaries describe it as a picture of a dish of food with a lid on top. The roof-like strokes at the top are the lid, and the part below is the vessel full of food. On its own its native reading is 「た」, as in 「食べる|たべる」. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「しょく」. You hear that in 「食事|しょくじ」, a meal, 「和食|わしょく」, Japanese food, and 「洋食|ようしょく」, Western food. When this kanji is squeezed onto the left side of another kanji, it becomes the food radical. You have seen it in the drink kanji. You have also seen it in 「館|かん」, the building kanji in 「図書館|としょかん」, library, 「旅館|りょかん」, inn, and 「映画館|えいがかん」, movie theater. That building kanji is usually explained as a hall where guests were housed and fed, so the food radical fits. So one lidded dish of food shows up in eating, drinking, and even big public buildings." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "English speakers often reach for this word when another one would sound more natural. Here is how three words split the job. 「食べ物|たべもの」 is food as a category, anything you can eat. It is perfect for questions like what food do you like, or what food is Kyoto famous for. 「料理|りょうり」 is cooking or a dish, something prepared, as in Italian cooking or my mother's cooking. 「ご飯|ごはん」 is cooked rice, and also a meal in general, as in let's go get a meal. So you would say 「イタリア料理|イタリアりょうり」 for Italian food at a restaurant, rather than using this word. And if you invite a friend to eat, you say 「ご飯を食べに行こう|ごはんをたべにいこう」, let's go eat. A common mini dialogue: 「好きな食べ物は何ですか|すきなたべものはなんですか」, what is your favorite food. 「すしです」, sushi. So this word is best for food as a category, while dishes and meals have their own words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Food in Japan comes wrapped in a couple of set phrases that everyone says. Before eating, people put their hands together and say 「いただきます」. It comes from a humble verb meaning to receive, so it is like saying I humbly receive this. After eating, they say 「ごちそうさまでした」, a thank you for the meal and the effort that went into it. You say these even when eating alone, and even when you paid for the food yourself. Seasonal food matters a lot too. People look forward to bamboo shoots in spring, eel in high summer, and chestnuts and sweet potatoes in autumn. Many regions are proud of a local specialty, and travelers often plan trips around eating it. Picky eating is fine to admit politely. You might say a certain food is a little hard for you, rather than saying you hate it. So when food is on the table, remember to start with 「いただきます」 and finish with 「ごちそうさまでした」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the stem of 「食べる|たべる」 plus 「物|もの」, thing, so things to eat. The same pattern gives 「飲み物|のみもの」, 「買い物|かいもの」 and 「着物|きもの」, so you can often guess new thing words.",
      "The eat kanji is a lidded dish of food, read 「しょく」 in 「食事|しょくじ」 and 「和食|わしょく」. Squeezed onto the left it becomes the food radical, seen in drink and in 「館|かん」, a hall where guests were fed.",
      "This word means food as a category, while 「料理|りょうり」 is a prepared dish and 「ご飯|ごはん」 is rice or a meal. So say 「イタリア料理|イタリアりょうり」 at a restaurant and 「ご飯を食べに行こう|ごはんをたべにいこう」 to invite a friend.",
      "Before eating, people say 「いただきます」, I humbly receive, and after, 「ごちそうさまでした」, even when eating alone or paying themselves. Seasonal foods and local specialties matter a lot too."
    ],
    ja: "どんな[食べ物|たべもの]が{好き|すき}ですか。",
    en: "What kind of food do you like?" },
  "のみもの|飲み物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「食べ物|たべもの」, food, and it is built the same way: a verb stem plus thing. 「飲み|のみ」 is the stem of 「飲む|のむ」, to drink. 「物|もの」 means thing. Drink plus thing, things to drink. Look closely at the kanji for drink. The left side is the eating kanji, squeezed narrow. The right side is a shape that shows a person with an open mouth. Put them together and you have consuming with your mouth open, drinking. In Japanese, 「飲む|のむ」 covers more than drinks. You also use it for medicine, 「薬を飲む|くすりをのむ」, to take medicine, because you swallow it. At a restaurant or someone's home, you will often be asked 「飲み物は何がいいですか|のみものはなにがいいですか」, what would you like to drink. Also, 「飲み会|のみかい」 is a drinking party, a big part of Japanese work and school social life. To recap: drink plus thing, things to drink, with eat on the left side of the kanji." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb inside this word is 「飲む|のむ」, a u-verb ending in 「む」. That ending has a pattern worth locking in. The polite form is 「飲みます|のみます」. For the te-form and the past, the 「む」 turns into 「んで」 and 「んだ」. So you get 「飲んで|のんで」 and 「飲んだ|のんだ」. The verb 「読む|よむ」, to read, works exactly the same way: 「読んで|よんで」 and 「読んだ|よんだ」. So does 「休む|やすむ」, to rest. The stem 「飲み|のみ」 is the piece that sits inside this word. That same stem appears in the going-to-do pattern. 「飲みに行く|のみにいく」 means to go out drinking, usually with alcohol implied among adults. If a coworker says 「今晩、飲みに行きませんか|こんばん、のみにいきませんか」, they are inviting you out for drinks tonight. So one verb stem gives you the noun for drinks and the invitation to go drinking." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a Japanese restaurant the drinks question usually comes first, before food. Staff will politely ask 「お飲み物はいかがですか|おのみものはいかがですか」, how about something to drink. The 「お」 in front makes it polite. At an izakaya, a casual Japanese pub, groups often say 「とりあえずビール」, beer for now, just to get things started fast. Many family restaurants have a 「ドリンクバー」, an all-you-can-drink self-service drink counter. To order, you can simply name the drink and add 「をください」, please give me. For example 「お茶をください|おちゃをください」. If someone asks what you want and you have no preference, 「何でもいいです|なんでもいいです」 means anything is fine. And everywhere in Japan you will find vending machines selling hot and cold drinks, sometimes on quiet country roads far from any shop. So expect the drinks question first, and answer with the drink plus 「をください」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Drinking together in Japan follows a few gentle customs. Nobody drinks until the group has raised glasses and said 「乾杯|かんぱい」, cheers. Taking a sip before that looks a bit rude. At a group meal, people pour drinks for each other rather than for themselves. If you notice a neighbor's glass is getting low, you offer to top it up. Then they will usually return the favor. When someone pours for you, it is polite to lift your glass a little toward the bottle. If you do not drink alcohol, that is perfectly fine. You can toast with tea or juice, and say you cannot drink, 「飲めません|のめません」. At work parties, juniors often take care of pouring for seniors, as a small show of respect. So at a Japanese table, wait for 「乾杯|かんぱい」, and keep an eye on your neighbor's glass." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the stem of 「飲む|のむ」 plus 「物|もの」, things to drink, and the drink kanji is the eat kanji beside a person with an open mouth. 「飲む|のむ」 also covers medicine, as in 「薬を飲む|くすりをのむ」.",
      "The verb 「飲む|のむ」 models the 「む」 ending, which becomes 「んで」 and 「んだ」, just like 「読む|よむ」. Its stem gives 「飲みに行く|のみにいく」, to go out drinking.",
      "At restaurants the drinks question comes first, 「お飲み物はいかがですか|おのみものはいかがですか」. Answer with the drink plus 「をください」, as in 「お茶をください|おちゃをください」.",
      "Nobody drinks before the group says 「乾杯|かんぱい」, and people pour for each other rather than themselves, watching their neighbor's glass. Not drinking alcohol is fine, so toast with tea or juice."
    ],
    ja: "[飲み物|のみもの]はなにがいいですか。",
    en: "What would you like to drink?" },
  "くだもの|果物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one has a special reading. The two kanji are read together as one unit, 「くだもの」, rather than piece by piece. The first kanji means fruit, and also result, the fruit of your efforts. Its shape has a tree at the bottom with something round growing on top. Fruit on a tree. The second kanji is 「物|もの」, thing, as in 「食べ物|たべもの」 and 「飲み物|のみもの」. Where does the sound come from? Old Japanese had 「く」, an old word for tree, and 「だ」, an old linking sound like today's 「の」. So 「くだもの」 was originally a thing of the tree. Here is a cultural note. Fruit in Japan can be very expensive, and perfect melons or boxes of fruit are given as fancy gifts. In winter, families eat 「みかん」, mandarin oranges, sitting around a heated table. Also, a tomato counts as a vegetable, 「野菜|やさい」, in Japanese, not a fruit. To recap: fruit on a tree, plus thing, an old phrase meaning thing of the tree." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji of this word is a tree with a field-like shape on top, the fruit growing on the branches. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「か」. You will meet it in 「結果|けっか」, result, literally the fruit that forms at the end. So the idea of fruit stretches to mean the outcome of something. Now here is a fun find. Put the grass radical on top of this fruit kanji, and you get the kanji in 「お菓子|おかし」, sweets and snacks. Long ago, before sugar was common, fruits and nuts were the treats people snacked on, so the snack kanji was built from the fruit kanji. That snack kanji keeps the same 「か」 sound. So when you see a tree with a round thing on top, think fruit, and look for it hiding under grass in the word for sweets." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In everyday speech you will hear both this native word and the loanword 「フルーツ」. 「フルーツ」 has a trendier, dessert-like feel. You see it on cafe menus, as in 「フルーツパフェ」, a fruit parfait, or 「フルーツサンド」, a sandwich filled with cream and fruit. 「果物|くだもの」 is the plain everyday word, the one you would use at the supermarket or in a health talk. A shop that sells fruit is a 「果物屋|くだものや」, using the same shop ending as bookstore. You already know several fruits: 「りんご」, apple, 「みかん」, mandarin, and 「バナナ」, banana. A natural question is 「どんな果物が好きですか|どんなくだものがすきですか」, what kind of fruit do you like. In Japan, fruit is often peeled and sliced and served on a plate after dinner, shared by the whole family. So use the native word for everyday talk, and the loanword when it sounds like dessert." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just for fun. Picture a hungry monkey at a fruit stall. He holds up an empty bowl and begs, could I have more, but he talks so fast it comes out as 「くだもの」. The shopkeeper laughs and piles the bowl high with apples and mandarins. Now connect it to the kanji. The monkey climbs a tree to grab more, and you see the fruit sitting right on top of the tree, just like the shape of the first kanji. Then he stuffs it all into a box marked thing, the 「物|もの」 kanji. So every time you hear 「くだもの」, see the fast-talking monkey begging for more fruit from the top of the tree. Remember, this is a sound trick only, not where the word comes from." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a special reading of two kanji, fruit on a tree plus 「物|もの」, from old 「く」, tree, and 「だ」, a linking sound, so a thing of the tree. In Japan, perfect fruit is a fancy gift and tomatoes count as vegetables.",
      "The fruit kanji, a tree with something round on top, reads 「か」 in 「結果|けっか」, result. Add the grass radical on top and you get the kanji in 「お菓子|おかし」, sweets, since fruit and nuts were the old treats.",
      "「果物|くだもの」 is the plain everyday word, while 「フルーツ」 feels trendy and dessert-like, as in 「フルーツパフェ」. In Japan, fruit is often sliced and shared on a plate after dinner.",
      "As a sound trick only, picture a fast-talking monkey begging for more fruit, grabbing it from the top of a tree like the first kanji and stuffing it into a box marked thing, 「物|もの」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[果物|くだもの]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat fruit every morning." },
  "りょこう|旅行": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. 「旅|りょ」 means a journey. You will see it again in 「旅館|りょかん」, a traditional Japanese inn, a journey building. On its own, with the native reading, it is 「旅|たび」, a trip, a slightly more poetic word. 「行|こう」 means to go. It started as a picture of a crossroads, a place where people go. You know it with the native reading in 「行く|いく」, to go. And you know 「こう」 from 「銀行|ぎんこう」, bank, and 「飛行機|ひこうき」, airplane, a flying going machine. Journey plus go, going on a journey, travel. Add 「する」 and it becomes a verb, 「旅行する|りょこうする」, to travel. Here is a cultural note. When Japanese people travel, they are expected to bring back 「お土産|おみやげ」, souvenirs, for family and coworkers. To recap: journey plus go, travel, sharing the go kanji with bank and airplane." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, journey, has an interesting past. Its right side is generally explained as a flag with people gathered under it. In ancient China the kanji referred to a band of soldiers marching under a banner, and from there it came to mean people on the move, a journey. That old army meaning still survives in a military word for a brigade, 「旅団|りょだん」. On the left side you can see the shape of 「方|ほう」, direction, though here it is really part of the flag picture. The second kanji, go, began as a drawing of a crossroads. So the whole word pictures people setting off under a banner and heading down the road. Its readings change too. On its own the journey kanji is 「たび」, and the go kanji is 「い」 in 「行く|いく」. So picture a group marching under a flag through a crossroads, and you have travel." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word is a great base for building others. Put words in front of it to say what kind of trip it is. 「海外旅行|かいがいりょこう」 is overseas travel, literally beyond the sea. 「家族旅行|かぞくりょこう」 is a family trip. 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」 is a school trip, which we will come back to. 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」 is a honeymoon, literally a newlywed trip. Put words after it, and you get 「旅行会社|りょこうがいしゃ」, a travel agency, a word you already have. Close relatives are 「観光|かんこう」, sightseeing, and 「ツアー」, a package tour. The difference is that this word means the whole trip, while sightseeing is what you do once you are there. So add a word in front to describe the trip, and you can talk about almost any kind of travel." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the particle patterns. To say where you travel, use 「に」 or 「へ」 with go: 「京都に旅行に行きます|きょうとにりょこうにいきます」, I will go on a trip to Kyoto. Notice the double 「に」. The first marks the place, the second marks the purpose, going for a trip. You can also use the verb form: 「京都を旅行します|きょうとをりょこうします」, I travel around Kyoto. With 「を」, it feels like moving through the place. To ask a friend about a recent trip, say 「旅行はどうでしたか|りょこうはどうでしたか」, how was your trip. Typical answers are 「楽しかったです|たのしかったです」, it was fun, or 「ちょっと疲れました|ちょっとつかれました」, I am a little tired. A common mistake is saying I traveled to work. For daily commuting, this word is wrong. It is only for trips away from home. So use it for real trips, and watch the double 「に」 when you say you are going on one." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Almost every Japanese student goes on a 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」, a school trip, often in junior high or high school. For generations, the classic destinations have been the old capitals, Kyoto and Nara, full of temples and shrines. Students travel as a whole grade, stay together in inns, and explore in small groups with a planned route. Many adults say it is one of their strongest school memories, with late night chats and pillow fights in big tatami rooms. The ancient deer in Nara Park, which bow for special crackers, are a favorite part of the trip. Some schools now go further, to Okinawa or even abroad. And of course everyone comes home with souvenirs for their family. So when Japanese friends talk about Kyoto, there is a good chance their first visit was on a school trip." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「旅|りょ」 is a journey and 「行|こう」 is to go, the same go as in 「銀行|ぎんこう」 and 「飛行機|ひこうき」, so journey plus go is travel. Add 「する」 to make it a verb, and bring back 「お土産|おみやげ」.",
      "The journey kanji is explained as a flag with people gathered under it, once a band of soldiers marching under a banner. Picture a group marching under a flag through the crossroads of the go kanji.",
      "Put a word in front to name the trip, as in 「海外旅行|かいがいりょこう」, overseas travel, 「家族旅行|かぞくりょこう」, a family trip, and 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」, a honeymoon. This word is the whole trip, while 「観光|かんこう」 is sightseeing.",
      "Say 「京都に旅行に行きます|きょうとにりょこうにいきます」 with a double 「に」, one for the place and one for the purpose. It is only for trips away from home, never for commuting.",
      "Almost every Japanese student goes on a 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」, a school trip, classically to Kyoto and Nara with their temples and bowing deer. Many adults count it among their strongest school memories."
    ],
    ja: "{夏|なつ}に[旅行|りょこう]をします。",
    en: "I'll travel in the summer." },
  "バス|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a loanword from English, bus. It is one of the easiest: the b and the s sounds come across almost directly, with an extra a vowel in the middle and a quiet u sound at the end, 「バス」. Here is a fun trap. The same sound, 「バス」, is also the Japanese for bath, from English bath, because the th sound becomes s. So a 「バスタオル」 is a bath towel, not a bus towel. Context makes it clear. For a real Japanese bath, people usually say 「お風呂|おふろ」. You already know 「バス停|バスてい」, bus stop, which adds a kanji meaning to stop. To ride the bus, say 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」, with 「に」, and to get off, 「バスを降りる|バスをおりる」. A cultural note. On many Japanese city buses, you take a little ticket as you board and pay when you get off, so watch what the locals do. To recap: English bus, with an extra vowel squeezed in, and the same sound can mean bath." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch out for a near twin. 「パス」, with a small circle mark, is pass, as in a bus pass, passing a ball, or saying I will pass on something. 「バス」, with the two little dash marks, is the bus. The marks change b and p. So 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」 is ride the bus, while 「パスする」 means to pass, or to skip. There is also a third meaning of 「バス」, the bass voice in a choir, from the musical word. For the bass guitar, people say 「ベース」 instead, which helps keep things clear. When you say how you travel, use 「で」: 「バスで行きます|バスでいきます」, I go by bus. To say how long it takes, 「バスで十分です|バスでじゅっぷんです」, it is ten minutes by bus. So listen for the dash marks, b for the bus and p for pass." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japan has some handy kinds of buses for travelers. A 「高速バス|こうそくバス」 is a highway bus that connects cities. It is usually much cheaper than the bullet train. A 「夜行バス|やこうバス」 is a night bus. You sleep in a reclining seat and wake up in another city, saving a hotel night too. Students and budget travelers love them. In cities, you will hear the price called 「バス代|バスだい」, the bus fare, using the same ending as other fees. Many people tap an IC card when they get on or off, so they do not need coins. To check you are on the right bus, ask the driver 「このバスは駅に行きますか|このバスはえきにいきますか」, does this bus go to the station. So for cheap travel between cities, a highway bus or night bus is a classic choice." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English bus with an extra vowel squeezed in, and the same sound also means bath, as in 「バスタオル」. Ride with 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」 and get off with 「バスを降りる|バスをおりる」.",
      "With the circle mark, 「パス」 is pass, while the dash marks make 「バス」 the bus. Say how you travel with 「で」, as in 「バスで行きます|バスでいきます」.",
      "A 「高速バス|こうそくバス」 links cities more cheaply than the bullet train, and a 「夜行バス|やこうバス」 lets you sleep on the way and save a hotel night. Ask 「このバスは駅に行きますか|このバスはえきにいきますか」 to check your bus."
    ],
    ja: "[バス]で{学校|がっこう}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to school by bus." },
  "てんき|天気": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. 「天|てん」 means heaven or sky. Its shape is a person, 「大|だい」, with an extra line on top, the sky above their head. 「気|き」 is a rich kanji meaning spirit, air, or energy. It is like the invisible mood of something. Sky plus energy, the mood of the sky, the weather. That 「気|き」 is one of the most useful kanji you will meet. You already know several words with it. 「元気|げんき」 is source energy, being healthy and lively. 「病気|びょうき」 is sick energy, illness. 「電気|でんき」 is electric energy, electricity or the lights. And 「気温|きおん」 is air warmth, temperature. There is also 「天気予報|てんきよほう」, the weather forecast. The most common small talk in Japan is about the weather. 「いい天気ですね|いいてんきですね」, nice weather, isn't it, is a classic greeting. To recap: sky plus energy, the mood of the sky, weather." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The sky kanji, 「天|てん」, appears in some wonderful words. 「天国|てんごく」 is heaven, the sky country. 「天才|てんさい」 is a genius, someone whose talent seems to come from the sky. You also know 「天ぷら|てんぷら」. Be careful there, though. Most people think that name came from Portuguese, and the kanji were added for their sound, so do not read sky into it. The sky kanji also has a native reading, 「あま」. You hear it in 「天の川|あまのがわ」, the river of heaven, the Milky Way. That is the river separating two star lovers in the summer star festival, 「七夕|たなばた」. Back to weather words. 「天気予報|てんきよほう」 is the forecast, and 「気温|きおん」 is the temperature. So the sky kanji gives you heaven, genius, and the Milky Way, as well as the weather." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Weather talk in Japan has its own little set of words. You already know 「晴れ|はれ」, sunny, 「曇り|くもり」, cloudy, 「雨|あめ」, rain, and 「雪|ゆき」, snow. To describe the weather, use 「が」 with an adjective: 「天気がいい|てんきがいい」, the weather is good, and 「天気が悪い|てんきがわるい」, the weather is bad. To give the forecast, use the noun with 「です」: 「明日は雨です|あしたはあめです」, tomorrow it will rain. A friendly question is 「明日の天気はどうですか|あしたのてんきはどうですか」, what is tomorrow's weather like. Polite speakers and TV presenters often add 「お」 in front, 「お天気|おてんき」, which sounds softer and friendlier. A common English speaker mistake is to use this word for climate, the weather a place has all year. For that, Japanese uses a different word, 「気候|きこう」. So this word is today's or tomorrow's sky, not the climate of a whole country." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "When Japanese children want good weather for a school trip or sports day, they make a little doll called a 「てるてる坊主|てるてるぼうず」. It is a simple figure made of white cloth or tissue, with a round head, often with a face drawn on. Children hang it by the window, hoping for sunshine the next day. There is even a well known children's song asking it to make tomorrow sunny. The doll gets a lot of work in early summer, during the rainy season, 「梅雨|つゆ」, when it can rain for weeks on end. Another children's game is kicking off a shoe and seeing how it lands. If it lands right side up, tomorrow will be fine. So if you see a small white doll hanging in a Japanese window, someone is hoping for good weather." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「天|てん」 is sky, a person with a line above their head, and 「気|き」 is spirit or energy, so weather is the mood of the sky. 「気|き」 also appears in 「元気|げんき」, 「病気|びょうき」 and 「電気|でんき」.",
      "The sky kanji gives 「天国|てんごく」, heaven, and 「天才|てんさい」, genius, and its native reading 「あま」 appears in 「天の川|あまのがわ」, the Milky Way. 「天ぷら|てんぷら」 likely just borrowed the kanji for sound.",
      "Describe weather with 「が」, as in 「天気がいい|てんきがいい」, and forecasts with 「です」, as in 「明日は雨です|あしたはあめです」. This word is today's sky, while climate is 「気候|きこう」.",
      "Children hang a little white cloth doll called a 「てるてる坊主|てるてるぼうず」 by the window to wish for sunshine, especially during the rainy season, 「梅雨|つゆ」. Kicking off a shoe to see how it lands is another weather game."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[天気|てんき]がいいです。",
    en: "The weather is nice today." },
  "しゅくだい|宿題": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. 「宿|しゅく」 has a roof on top, and it means lodging, a place where you stay the night. It also carries the idea of something kept or held over until later. You can see it in the name of the Tokyo district Shinjuku, which was once a new post town where travelers stayed. 「題|だい」 means a topic or a title, as in a problem you are set. Put them together and you have a problem that is held over to be worked on later, at the place you stay. Homework. For the verb, people say 「宿題をする|しゅくだいをする」, to do homework. In casual speech, 「宿題をやる|しゅくだいをやる」 is also very common. A cultural note. Japanese schoolkids are famous for summer vacation homework, including keeping a diary and doing a free research project. Lots of them rush to finish it in the last days of August. To recap: lodging plus topic, a problem held over and taken home, homework." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to useful relatives. The second kanji, topic or title, appears in 「問題|もんだい」, a problem or a question on a test. It is one of the most common words in Japanese. It also appears in 「話題|わだい」, a topic of conversation, literally talk topic. The first kanji, lodging, has a native reading, 「やど」, an old word for an inn. You will also meet 「民宿|みんしゅく」, a family-run guesthouse, often in the countryside, where you sleep on futons and eat home cooking. It connects in meaning to 「泊まる|とまる」, to stay the night, which you already know, though that verb uses a different kanji. So in this word, the lodging kanji gives the idea of something kept overnight, and the topic kanji gives the problem. Look for the topic kanji in problem and conversation topic, and the lodging kanji in inns." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Homework comes with its own set of verbs. The teacher gives homework with 「出す|だす」: 「先生が宿題を出しました|せんせいがしゅくだいをだしました」, the teacher gave homework. Students hand it in with the same verb, from the other side: 「宿題を出してください|しゅくだいをだしてください」, please hand in your homework. The most common confession in any classroom is 「宿題を忘れました|しゅくだいをわすれました」, I forgot my homework. Notice that in Japanese you forget the homework itself, even when you mean you left it at home. Here is a mini dialogue. Mom asks 「宿題、終わった|しゅくだい、おわった」, did you finish your homework. The child answers 「まだ」, not yet. At university, longer written assignments are usually called 「レポート」, a report, rather than this word. So the teacher gives it and you hand it in with the same verb, 「出す|だす」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just for fun. Picture a student staring at a giant pile of homework. A gust of wind shakes the desk, and the pile topples over. The student wails, it shook, I'll die, which comes out sounding like 「しゅくだい」. Now tie it to the kanji. The first kanji has a roof on top, so picture the pile of homework under the roof of your house, waiting for you at night. The second kanji means a topic, so each sheet in the pile has a big problem written across the top. So every time you hear 「しゅくだい」, see that wobbling pile under your roof, shaking until you cry out. Remember, this trick is about the sound only, not where the word comes from." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「宿|しゅく」 is lodging under a roof, with the idea of something held over, and 「題|だい」 is a topic or problem, so homework is a problem taken home to work on later. You do it with 「宿題をする|しゅくだいをする」.",
      "The topic kanji appears in 「問題|もんだい」, a problem, and 「話題|わだい」, a conversation topic. The lodging kanji is read 「やど」 for an inn and appears in 「民宿|みんしゅく」, a family-run guesthouse.",
      "Teachers give homework and students hand it in with the same verb, 「出す|だす」. The classic classroom confession is 「宿題を忘れました|しゅくだいをわすれました」, and university assignments are usually 「レポート」.",
      "As a sound trick only, picture a towering pile of homework under your roof shaking until you wail it shook, I'll die, each sheet topped with a big problem like the topic kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[宿題|しゅくだい]がたくさんあります。",
    en: "I have a lot of homework today." },
  "テスト|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a loanword from English, test. Watch how the sounds got squeezed in. Japanese syllables almost always end in a vowel, so English consonant clusters get vowels added. The st at the end of test becomes two syllables, 「ス」 and 「ト」, with a quiet u and a full o sound added. So one English syllable turns into three Japanese ones, 「テ」, 「ス」, 「ト」. You will see the same thing with lots of loanwords that end in t, which usually gain an o. For taking a test, people say 「テストを受ける|テストをうける」, to receive a test, or simply 「テストがある」, there is a test. Japanese also has a native-feeling word, 「試験|しけん」, which you will meet later. That one sounds more formal, for big exams like entrance exams or finals. A 「テスト」 is more everyday, like a quiz in class. Japanese schools are famous for tough high school and university entrance exams, so students hear both words a lot. To recap: English test, with extra vowels added after the s and the t, an everyday quiz or test." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Tests come with scores, and scores have their own words. The counter 「点|てん」 means points. 「百点|ひゃくてん」 is a hundred points, a perfect score, and every Japanese child dreams of it. To get a score, use 「取る|とる」, to take: 「テストでいい点を取りました|テストでいいてんをとりました」, I got a good score on the test. Notice 「で」, marking the test as the place where it happened. Students talk about 「テスト勉強|テストべんきょう」, studying for a test. A short quiz in class is often a 「小テスト|しょうテスト」, a small test. To ask how it went, say 「テストはどうでしたか」, how was the test. A typical groan is 「難しかったです|むずかしかったです」, it was hard. Since a test is an event, you say there is one with 「ある」: 「明日テストがあります|あしたテストがあります」. So scores are counted in 「点|てん」, and you take them with 「取る|とる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's entrance exam season, in winter, is a big deal for students and families. The whole process is called 「受験|じゅけん」. Many students go to cram school, 「塾|じゅく」, in the evenings after regular school, sometimes for years. And there is a famous good luck snack. Kit Kat, in Japanese 「キットカット」, sounds a lot like 「きっと勝つ|きっとかつ」, which means you will surely win. So people give Kit Kats to students before big exams as a cheerful charm. Students also visit shrines to pray for success and buy small good luck charms, 「お守り|おまもり」, for passing. There are even foods avoided before exams, like anything slippery, because the word for slip is also slang for failing an exam. So if you see a student holding a chocolate bar before an exam, it might be a lucky charm, not just a snack." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word is not only for school. In Japanese, as in English, it also means trying something out. 「マイクテスト」 is a mic check before a speech or concert. A 「テスト中|テストちゅう」 sign means testing in progress, maybe on a new machine or website. Now look at other loanwords that end in the same 「スト」 shape, where English st gets an extra vowel. 「リスト」 is a list. 「トースト」 is toast. 「ポスト」 is a mailbox, or a post in a company. They all turn a short English ending into two Japanese beats, 「ス」 and 「ト」. So once you can say this word, you can say a whole group of loanwords with the same ending." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This loanword adds vowels to English test, turning one syllable into three, 「テ」, 「ス」, 「ト」. It is an everyday quiz, while the more formal 「試験|しけん」 is for big exams.",
      "Scores are counted in 「点|てん」, with 「百点|ひゃくてん」 the perfect score, and you get one with 「取る|とる」, as in 「テストでいい点を取りました|テストでいいてんをとりました」. A test is an event, so it takes 「ある」.",
      "Entrance exam season, 「受験|じゅけん」, means cram school and shrine visits, and students get Kit Kats because 「キットカット」 sounds like 「きっと勝つ|きっとかつ」, you will surely win.",
      "It also means trying something out, as in 「マイクテスト」, a mic check. Other loanwords share the 「スト」 ending, like 「リスト」, 「トースト」 and 「ポスト」."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}[テスト]があります。",
    en: "There is a test tomorrow." },
  "へや|部屋": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has a special reading. The two kanji are read together as 「へや」, rather than with their usual separate readings. The first kanji, read 「ぶ」 elsewhere, means a part or a section. You will meet it later in words like club activities at school. The second kanji, 「屋|や」, means a roof, a house, or a shop. You already know it at the end of 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore, a book shop. Put them together and a room is a section of the house. That makes good sense. In Japan, rooms are often measured in tatami mats rather than square meters. A six mat room is a common size for a small bedroom. Apartment ads even describe layouts with codes like one room plus a kitchen. A cultural note. Before you step into a Japanese room with tatami, you take your slippers off too, not just your shoes. To say my room, just add 「わたしの」, giving 「わたしの部屋|わたしのへや」. To recap: a section of the house, a room, with the shop and house kanji you know from bookstore." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to big families. The second kanji, 「屋|や」, is used for shops and for kinds of people. You know 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore. There is also 「花屋|はなや」, flower shop, and 「パン屋|パンや」, bakery. It can also describe a type of person. You have the word 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」, a shy person, someone who is always embarrassed. The first kanji, read 「ぶ」 elsewhere, means a part or section. It appears in 「全部|ぜんぶ」, all, literally all the parts. It also appears in 「部長|ぶちょう」, the head of a department, and in 「部活|ぶかつ」, school club activities, which almost every Japanese teen does. So one kanji gives you shops and personalities, and the other gives you parts, departments and clubs." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some everyday ways to talk about your room. To tidy it up, say 「部屋を片付ける|へやをかたづける」. To clean it, say 「部屋を掃除する|へやをそうじする」. To complain about size, use 「狭い|せまい」, cramped, or 「広い|ひろい」, spacious. 「狭い部屋|せまいへや」 is a cramped room, and Japanese city apartments often are. There is also the ending 「室|しつ」, which means room in more formal or technical words. You know 「教室|きょうしつ」, classroom. 「和室|わしつ」 is a Japanese-style room with tatami, and 「洋室|ようしつ」 is a Western-style room. Here is a mini dialogue. A parent says 「部屋を片付けなさい|へやをかたづけなさい」, tidy your room. The teen sighs 「はーい」, okay. So you tidy and clean a room, and describe it as cramped or spacious." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just for fun. Picture yourself bursting through the door of your room after a long day, throwing your bag down and shouting hey ya, which sounds like 「へや」. Your cat looks up from the bed, unimpressed. Now tie it to the kanji. The second kanji is the shop and house kanji, so think of your room as your own little shop inside the house. The first kanji means a part, so your room is your part of the house, the one section that is all yours. Every time you come home, you shout 「へや」 at the one part of the house that belongs to you. Remember, this trick is about the sound only." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The two kanji take a special reading together, part plus house or shop, so a room is a section of the house. The second kanji is the one you know from 「本屋|ほんや」, and rooms are often measured in tatami mats.",
      "The house kanji 「屋|や」 names shops like 「花屋|はなや」 and types of people like 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」. The part kanji appears in 「全部|ぜんぶ」, 「部長|ぶちょう」 and 「部活|ぶかつ」.",
      "Tidy a room with 「部屋を片付ける|へやをかたづける」 and describe it as 「狭い|せまい」 or 「広い|ひろい」. The formal ending 「室|しつ」 gives 「教室|きょうしつ」, 「和室|わしつ」 and 「洋室|ようしつ」.",
      "As a sound trick, picture bursting into your room shouting hey ya at the one part of the house, like the first kanji, that is your own little shop inside the house."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}の[部屋|へや]は{小さい|ちいさい}です。",
    en: "My room is small." },
  "ぼく|僕": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji has the person mark on the left, the same one you see in 「休み|やすみ」, rest, a person by a tree. Here it takes its Chinese-origin reading, 「ぼく」. The kanji originally meant a servant, so calling yourself this was a humble way to say I. Today it is the word boys and men often use for I in casual and friendly situations. It sounds softer and gentler than 「おれ」, which is rough and very casual, and more relaxed than 「わたし」, which is neutral and polite. In a job interview or with your boss, a man would usually switch to 「わたし」 or the even more formal 「わたくし」. A common pattern is a boy using 「ぼく」 as a child and adding 「おれ」 with friends as he grows up. You will also hear it in lots of song lyrics, sometimes even sung by women. A way to remember it: picture a polite young man bowing, saying 「ぼく」 with a little smile. That is just a hook. To recap: a person mark, an old humble word for servant, now a soft and casual I for men." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a surprising use. Adults often speak to a little boy they do not know by calling him 「ぼく」. A shopkeeper might lean down to a small child and ask 「ぼく、何歳|ぼく、なんさい」, how old are you, little man. Here the word is not I at all. It works like a friendly name for the boy, taken from the word he would use about himself. For plural, boys and men say 「僕たち|ぼくたち」 or the more casual 「僕ら|ぼくら」, we. Small children, by the way, often use their own name instead of any word for I. Grown men switch between pronouns depending on the scene. The same person may say 「ぼく」 with a gentle friend, 「おれ」 with old classmates, and 「わたし」 at a business meeting. So listen for who is speaking and to whom, because this word can even mean you when an adult talks to a little boy." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Pronouns often come in pairs. The classic partner of 「ぼく」 is 「君|きみ」, you. You know this kanji as the name ending 「くん」, used for boys and juniors. Read 「きみ」, it means you, said by someone to an equal or a junior. Together they appear in countless love songs, like I and you, 「ぼく」 and 「きみ」. In real conversation, though, using you too much sounds pushy, so Japanese people often use the other person's name instead. Compare the main words for I. 「わたくし」 is very formal. 「わたし」 is neutral, used by everyone. 「ぼく」 is a softer male word. 「おれ」 is a rough male word. Choosing between them is like choosing what clothes to wear for an occasion. So remember the song pair, 「ぼく」 and 「きみ」, and save 「わたし」 for formal moments." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This little pronoun carries a lot of image in Japanese culture. Many of novelist Haruki Murakami's narrators use 「ぼく」, which helps give his stories their quiet, thoughtful, slightly detached feel. In anime and manga, a character's pronoun is part of their personality. A gentle, polite boy says 「ぼく」, while a tough rival says 「おれ」. There is even a character type, a girl who calls herself 「ぼく」, which gives her a tomboyish, unusual flavor. Pop songs often use it too, because it sounds young and sincere. Translators find this tricky, because English has only one word for I, and all this flavor gets lost. So when you hear someone say 「ぼく」, notice the personality it paints, not just the meaning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the person mark and once meant servant, so it began as a humble I. Today it is a soft, casual I for boys and men, gentler than 「おれ」 and more relaxed than 「わたし」.",
      "Adults often call a little boy 「ぼく」, as in 「ぼく、何歳|ぼく、なんさい」, so here it means you. Men switch pronouns by scene, and the plural is 「僕たち|ぼくたち」 or 「僕ら|ぼくら」.",
      "Its classic partner is 「君|きみ」, you, the pair found in countless love songs. Choosing among 「わたくし」, 「わたし」, 「ぼく」 and 「おれ」 is like choosing clothes for an occasion.",
      "A character's pronoun paints personality, so Murakami narrators use 「ぼく」 for a quiet, thoughtful feel, gentle anime boys say it, and a girl using it seems tomboyish. English translations lose all that flavor."
    ],
    ja: "[僕|ぼく]は{大学|だいがく}の{学生|がくせい}です。",
    en: "I am a college student." },
  "エルサイズ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is made from English, the letter L plus the word size. The letter L is read 「エル」 in Japanese. Since Japanese has no separate l sound, it uses its r-like sound, and an extra u sound is added at the end. Then size becomes 「サイズ」, with the z sound followed by a quiet u. So you get large size, but said with the letter, just like on a clothing tag. The same pattern works for other sizes: 「エム」 for M and 「エス」 for S. Here is a practical tip for shopping in Japan. Japanese clothing sizes tend to run smaller than American ones. A Japanese L may fit like an American M, so try things on. You may also see 「エルエル」 for extra large on some tags. And the same word works for drinks at a coffee shop or fast food place. To recap: the letter L read 「エル」, plus size, large size, just like a tag." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "English letters have set Japanese names, and two of them are easy to mix up. The letter L is 「エル」. The letter R is 「アール」, with a long a sound. Both use the same Japanese r-like sound, because Japanese has only one sound covering both. So the vowels are what tell them apart. Here are a few more. A is 「エー」, B is 「ビー」, C is 「シー」, M is 「エム」, S is 「エス」, and X is 「エックス」. So extra large may be written XL and read 「エックスエル」, or written LL and read 「エルエル」, depending on the shop. You will also hear these letter names for things like 「ディーブイディー」, DVD. So remember, L is 「エル」 and R is 「アール」, and the vowel at the front keeps them apart." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some phrases for shopping in a Japanese clothes store. To ask for a bigger size, say 「もっと大きいサイズはありますか|もっとおおきいサイズはありますか」, do you have a bigger size. To ask to try something on, say 「試着してもいいですか|しちゃくしてもいいですか」, may I try it on. Staff will show you to a small fitting room. Take your shoes off before stepping inside. If it does not fit, say 「サイズが合いません|サイズがあいません」, the size does not fit. Some items are labeled 「フリーサイズ」, free size, meaning one size for everyone. At a fast food place, drinks come in 「エス」, 「エム」, and 「エル」. So with a few phrases, you can ask for a bigger size and try it on." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The letter L is read 「エル」 and size becomes 「サイズ」, just like a clothing tag, with 「エム」 and 「エス」 for the others. Japanese sizes run small, so a Japanese L may fit like an American M.",
      "English letters have set Japanese names, and L, 「エル」, and R, 「アール」, share one r-like sound, so the vowel keeps them apart. Extra large may be 「エックスエル」 or 「エルエル」 depending on the shop.",
      "Ask 「もっと大きいサイズはありますか|もっとおおきいサイズはありますか」 for a bigger size and 「試着してもいいですか|しちゃくしてもいいですか」 to try something on. 「フリーサイズ」 means one size for everyone."
    ],
    ja: "[エルサイズ]のシャツをください。",
    en: "A size L shirt, please." },
  "あつい|暑い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the hot for weather and air, like a hot summer day. The kanji has 「日|ひ」, sun, sitting on top. A way to remember it: the sun beating down on you from above. The sun on top tells you this is weather heat. It uses its native reading, 「あつ」. Here is the important part. Japanese has more than one 「あつい」, written with different kanji. This one, 「暑い|あつい」, is for the weather. The other one, 「熱い|あつい」, is for things that are hot to touch, like coffee or a bath. The opposite of this one is 「寒い|さむい」, cold weather. The opposite of the other one is 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch. Japanese summers are famously hot and humid. People greet each other with 「暑いですね|あついですね」, it's hot, isn't it. That is one of the most common summer small talk lines you will hear. To recap: sun on top, hot weather, and the partner of cold weather." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "You know the sun sits on top of this kanji. Now look underneath. The bottom part is 「者|しゃ」, the kanji for person, as in a person who does something. That bottom part also hints at the sound, because the Chinese-origin reading of the whole kanji is 「しょ」. You will meet that reading in 「残暑|ざんしょ」, the heat that lingers after summer should be over, a word you hear a lot in September. You will also meet it in 「暑中見舞い|しょちゅうみまい」, a card people send in midsummer asking how you are coping with the heat. Its partner word for weather, 「寒い|さむい」, has its own Chinese reading, 「かん」. So the full picture is the sun on top and a person below, the person roasting under the summer sun." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular i-adjective, so it is a good one for practicing adjective forms. 「暑くなる|あつくなる」 means to get hot. In June you will hear 「暑くなりましたね|あつくなりましたね」, it has gotten hot, hasn't it. The te-form, 「暑くて|あつくて」, links a reason. 「暑くて寝られません|あつくてねられません」, it is so hot I cannot sleep. Add 「すぎる」 to the stem for too much: 「暑すぎる|あつすぎる」, too hot. For looks hot, add 「そう」: 「暑そう|あつそう」, it looks hot, like when you see someone in a heavy coat in August. There is also a very useful compound, 「蒸し暑い|むしあつい」, steamy hot, muggy. That is the true feeling of a Japanese summer. So with one adjective you can say it got hot, too hot, looks hot, and muggy." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has many ways of coping with summer heat. In midsummer people send greeting cards called 「暑中見舞い|しょちゅうみまい」, asking how the other person is holding up in the heat. They fan themselves with flat round fans, 「うちわ」, often handed out free at festivals and stations. Many people suffer from 「夏バテ|なつバテ」, summer fatigue, when the heat leaves you tired with no appetite. A classic cure is eating grilled eel on a special midsummer day, believed to give you stamina. Cold noodles are another favorite, like chilled soba or somen dipped in a cool sauce. On TV you will hear warnings about 「熱中症|ねっちゅうしょう」, heatstroke. Notice that word uses the fire kanji, not the sun one, because it is about your body overheating. So Japanese summer comes with cards, fans, eel, and cold noodles." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The sun 「日|ひ」 sits on top of the kanji, so this is hot weather, while 「熱い|あつい」 is hot to the touch. Its opposite is 「寒い|さむい」, and 「暑いですね|あついですね」 is classic summer small talk.",
      "Under the sun sits 「者|しゃ」, person, which also hints at the reading 「しょ」, so picture a person roasting under the summer sun. That reading appears in 「残暑|ざんしょ」, lingering late-summer heat.",
      "As a regular i-adjective it gives 「暑くなる|あつくなる」, to get hot, 「暑すぎる|あつすぎる」, too hot, and 「暑そう|あつそう」, looks hot. 「蒸し暑い|むしあつい」, muggy, is the true feel of Japanese summer.",
      "Japanese summer means 「暑中見舞い|しょちゅうみまい」 cards, 「うちわ」 fans, grilled eel against 「夏バテ|なつバテ」, and cold noodles. Heatstroke, 「熱中症|ねっちゅうしょう」, uses the fire kanji because the body overheats."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}はとても[暑い|あつい]です。",
    en: "It's very hot today." },
  "さむい|寒い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is cold for weather and air, the cold you feel all over your body on a winter day. The kanji has a roof on top, and at the very bottom there are two little strokes. Those two strokes are a form of the ice mark. You can see the ice mark on the left of 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch. And the same two little strokes sit at the bottom of 「冬|ふゆ」, winter. So think of it as ice creeping in under the roof of the house. Cold. It uses its native reading, 「さむ」. Its opposite is 「暑い|あつい」, hot weather. Be careful not to mix it up with 「冷たい|つめたい」. That one is for things, like cold water, or for cold-hearted people. A cultural note. Many Japanese houses are not well insulated, so winter indoors can be chilly. That is why people love the heated table called a 「こたつ」, often with mandarin oranges on top. To recap: ice under the roof, cold weather, the partner of hot weather." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word belongs to a neat set of four temperature words, one for each season. In spring it is 「暖かい|あたたかい」, warm. In summer it is 「暑い|あつい」, hot. In autumn it is 「涼しい|すずしい」, cool and pleasant. In winter it is 「寒い|さむい」, cold. These four are all about the air around you, not things you touch. There are some useful relatives too. 「寒気|さむけ」 is a chill, the shivery feeling you get when coming down with a cold. 「肌寒い|はださむい」 means skin cold, a little chilly, like a cool autumn evening. The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「かん」. You will see it in 「寒波|かんぱ」, a cold wave, on the weather forecast. So learn the four seasons as a set, warm, hot, cool, and cold." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a fun slang meaning. When someone tells a joke that falls flat, people say it is 「寒い|さむい」. The joke makes the room go cold, like a chilly silence. This is especially used for bad puns, the kind of joke a middle-aged dad tells. That kind of pun even has a name, 「おやじギャグ」, old man gag. So if you make a pun and your friends shiver and say 「さむっ」, they are teasing you. That short 「さむっ」 is a common casual exclamation, the adjective cut short with a small 「っ」. For real weather, a common greeting is 「寒くなりましたね|さむくなりましたね」, it has gotten cold, hasn't it. And to tell someone they look cold, say 「寒そうですね|さむそうですね」. So this word can describe a cold day, and also a joke that leaves everyone cold." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Since many Japanese homes have no central heating, people have clever ways to stay warm. The most famous is the 「こたつ」, a low table with a heater underneath and a thick blanket around the edge. Once you are in, you never want to leave. Another winter must is the 「カイロ」, a disposable heat pack. You shake it, it warms up, and you slip it into a pocket or stick it inside your clothes. At dinner, families gather around a 「鍋|なべ」, a hot pot simmering in the middle of the table. Hot springs, 「温泉|おんせん」, are especially loved in winter, sometimes with snow falling around an outdoor bath. And convenience stores sell steaming oden and hot canned drinks from heated shelves. So Japanese winters are cold indoors, but full of cozy ways to warm up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is ice creeping in under a roof, with the two-stroke ice mark at the bottom, also seen in 「冬|ふゆ」. This is cold weather, while 「冷たい|つめたい」 is for cold things.",
      "Learn the four seasonal air words as a set: 「暖かい|あたたかい」 warm, 「暑い|あつい」 hot, 「涼しい|すずしい」 cool, and 「寒い|さむい」 cold. Relatives include 「寒気|さむけ」, a chill, and 「肌寒い|はださむい」, chilly.",
      "A joke that falls flat is called 「寒い|さむい」, especially a dad's pun, an 「おやじギャグ」, and friends tease you with 「さむっ」. For real weather, say 「寒くなりましたね|さむくなりましたね」.",
      "With little central heating, Japanese homes stay warm with the 「こたつ」 heated table, 「カイロ」 heat packs and 「鍋|なべ」 hot pots. Winter is cold indoors, but full of cozy ways to warm up."
    ],
    ja: "{冬|ふゆ}は[寒い|さむい]です。",
    en: "It's cold in winter." },
  "あつい|熱い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is hot for things you touch or drink, like hot coffee, a hot bath, or a hot pan. Look at the bottom of the kanji. Those four dots are a form of the fire mark. Fire underneath, so it is hot to touch. It uses its native reading here, 「あつ」. The Chinese-origin reading is 「ねつ」, and it means fever, as in 「熱がある|ねつがある」, to have a fever. That is your body getting hot. Now compare it with 「暑い|あつい」, which has the sun on top and is for hot weather. Same sound, different kanji, and the kanji tells you what kind of heat it is. Sun on top, weather. Fire underneath, things. The opposite of this one is 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch. You can also use it for feelings, like a hot, passionate heart. Japanese baths can be very hot, so you might hear 「熱い|あつい」 as someone steps in. To recap: fire underneath, hot things, the same kanji as fever." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The fire kanji in this word builds some strong compounds, all with its Chinese-origin reading, 「ねつ」, which often shortens to 「ねっ」. You know 「熱がある|ねつがある」, to have a fever. 「熱心|ねっしん」 is a hot heart, meaning eager and enthusiastic, like a keen student. 「情熱|じょうねつ」 is passion, burning emotion. 「熱中症|ねっちゅうしょう」 is heatstroke, your body overheating. You hear it in weather warnings every summer. 「熱湯|ねっとう」 is boiling water, the kind on the instructions for cup noodles. So whenever this kanji appears, something is burning, whether it is water, your body, or your heart. Fire underneath means heat, in things and in feelings." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you touch something hot, Japanese people often cry out a short 「あつっ」, with a small 「っ」 cutting it off. It is like saying ouch, hot. There is also a lovely word for people who cannot handle hot food or drinks: 「猫舌|ねこじた」, cat tongue. Cats famously wait for their food to cool down. If you are cat-tongued, you might say 「私は猫舌です|わたしはねこじたです」, I have a cat tongue. Slurping noodles is normal in Japan. One reason people give is that sucking in air cools the hot noodles on the way in. To warn someone, say 「熱いから気をつけて|あついからきをつけて」, it is hot, so be careful. So remember 「あつっ」 for the moment you touch something hot, and cat tongue for people who wait." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There is a third 「あつい」 to know, with its own kanji. 「厚い|あつい」 means thick, like a thick book or a thick slice of bread. Its kanji has a cliff shape over the top, and nothing to do with heat. 「分厚い|ぶあつい」 is very thick, like a huge dictionary. So now you have three. The sun kanji is hot weather. The fire kanji is hot to touch. The cliff kanji is thick. In speech, context almost always makes it obvious. A thick coffee is rare, but a hot one is common. Their opposites help too. Hot weather pairs with cold weather. Hot things pair with cold to the touch. And thick pairs with 「薄い|うすい」, thin. So when you hear 「あつい」, think about whether you would feel it on the air, on your fingers, or by measuring it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Four dots at the bottom are the fire mark, so this is hot to the touch, like coffee or a bath, while sun-topped 「暑い|あつい」 is weather. The reading 「ねつ」 means fever, as in 「熱がある|ねつがある」.",
      "With 「ねつ」, often shortened to 「ねっ」, the fire kanji builds 「熱心|ねっしん」, eager, 「情熱|じょうねつ」, passion, and 「熱湯|ねっとう」, boiling water. Something is always burning, water, body or heart.",
      "Touch something hot and people cry 「あつっ」, while someone who can't handle hot food has 「猫舌|ねこじた」, a cat tongue. Warn someone with 「熱いから気をつけて|あついからきをつけて」.",
      "A third 「厚い|あつい」, with a cliff shape, means thick, as in 「分厚い|ぶあつい」, very thick. Sun means hot weather, fire means hot to touch, and cliff means thick."
    ],
    ja: "このおちゃは[熱い|あつい]です。",
    en: "This tea is hot." },
  "おもしろい|面白い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings. 「面|おも」 means face or surface. 「白|しろ」 means white, and by extension bright. So the word is literally something like bright face. A way to remember it: picture people sitting around a fire listening to a great story, their faces lit up white by the flames. That is a popular folk story, not a proven origin, so treat it as a hook. The word covers two ideas that English splits. It can mean interesting, like an interesting book. It can also mean funny, like a funny comedian. Context tells you which. The opposite is 「つまらない」, boring. It is an i-adjective, so the past is 「面白かった|おもしろかった」, and the negative is 「面白くない|おもしろくない」. After a movie, 「面白かったです|おもしろかったです」 is a natural thing to say whether it was gripping or hilarious. If you specifically mean full of laughs, you might add that it made you laugh. To recap: face plus white, faces lighting up, interesting or funny." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, face, is often explained as an outline of a face with an eye in the middle, and you can see something like the eye shape inside it. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「めん」. You will meet it in 「画面|がめん」, a screen, picture face. It also appears in 「地面|じめん」, the ground, the earth's surface, and 「お面|おめん」, a mask, like the character masks sold at festival stalls. The second kanji, white, has the native reading 「しろ」, as in 「白い|しろい」, white. In this word, both kanji use native readings. So when you see the face kanji, think of faces, surfaces and screens, and in this word, a face lit up white." },
      { title: "Fun versus funny", text: "English speakers often mix this word up with 「楽しい|たのしい」, fun. Here is the difference. 「楽しい|たのしい」 is about your own good time. A party, a trip, playing with friends, these are fun to experience. 「面白い|おもしろい」 is about the thing itself being interesting or funny. A book, a movie, a person, an idea. So after a party, say 「楽しかったです|たのしかったです」, it was fun. Saying it was interesting can sound odd, like you were studying the guests. Here is another trap. If you want to say you are interested in something, do not use this word about yourself. 「私は面白いです|わたしはおもしろいです」 means I am funny or I am an interesting person. For I am interested, use 「興味があります|きょうみがあります」, I have an interest. So fun for experiences, this word for things and people, and a different phrase for being interested." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In western Japan, especially Osaka, being funny is a prized quality. Calling someone 「面白い人|おもしろいひと」 is high praise. Osaka is the home of 「漫才|まんざい」, a style of stand-up comedy for two. One performer, the 「ボケ」, says silly or mistaken things. The other, the 「ツッコミ」, corrects them sharply, often with a quick slap on the head or chest. People in Osaka play these roles in everyday chat too, and a friend who says something silly expects you to call it out. Many famous comedians come from the region, and comedy shows fill Japanese TV. In Osaka dialect, you might also hear the word 「おもろい」, a shortened local version. So in Osaka especially, a person who makes everyone laugh is truly 「面白い|おもしろい」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「面|おも」 is face and 「白|しろ」 is white, and a popular hook is listeners' faces lit white by a fire during a great story. It covers both interesting and funny, the opposite of 「つまらない」.",
      "The face kanji, often explained as a face outline with an eye inside, reads 「めん」 in 「画面|がめん」, screen, 「地面|じめん」, ground, and 「お面|おめん」, mask. Think faces, surfaces and screens.",
      "「楽しい|たのしい」 is about your own good time, while this word says a thing or person is interesting or funny. 「私は面白いです|わたしはおもしろいです」 means I am funny, so say 「興味があります|きょうみがあります」 for interested.",
      "In Osaka, being funny is high praise, and 「漫才|まんざい」 comedy pairs a silly 「ボケ」 with a sharp 「ツッコミ」. Locals even shorten the word to 「おもろい」."
    ],
    ja: "この{本|ほん}は[面白い|おもしろい]です。",
    en: "This book is interesting." },
  "つまらない|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It means boring, dull, not worth much. A way to remember it: think of something with nothing packed inside, an empty box of a movie. That is a hook. It is the opposite of 「面白い|おもしろい」, interesting or funny. Even though it ends in 「ない」, it is a regular i-adjective on its own. The past is 「つまらなかった」. In casual speech you will often hear it squeezed to 「つまんない」. Now a great cultural note. When Japanese people give a gift, they traditionally say 「つまらないものですが」. It means this is a trifling thing, but please take it. It is not an insult to the gift. It is a humble way of offering something, playing down your own effort. These days some people find it a bit old-fashioned, and might say something like this is a little something instead. But you will still hear it, so do not be confused when someone hands you a nice present and calls it boring. To recap: boring, the opposite of interesting, and a humble phrase for giving gifts." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Most dictionaries link this word to the verb 「詰まる|つまる」, to be packed full or to come together. In older Japanese, that verb could also mean to make sense or to feel satisfied, as if everything fits neatly into place. Add the negative 「ない」, and you get something that does not come together, does not satisfy, and is not worth much. From there it grew into the meanings boring and trivial. You can still see this in phrases like 「つまらないこと」, a trivial matter, and the humble gift phrase you learned already. Today the verb 「詰まる|つまる」 is mostly used for things getting blocked or packed, like a clogged pipe or a stuffy nose, 「鼻が詰まる|はながつまる」. So the word that started as not adding up became today's word for boring." },
      { title: "Bored versus boring", text: "English splits bored and boring, and Japanese has its own way of handling that. This word describes the thing that is boring: a movie, a class, a job. It can also be said as an exclamation when you are bored, as in, ugh, this is so dull. But for the feeling of being bored, Japanese often uses 「退屈|たいくつ」, a na-adjective meaning tedious, or 「暇|ひま」, having nothing to do. 「退屈です|たいくつです」 means I am bored. Be careful with people. Calling someone 「つまらない人|つまらないひと」 means a dull, boring person. That is quite harsh. For an opinion that is gentler, Japanese people often say something was 「あまり面白くなかった|あまりおもしろくなかった」, it was not very interesting. So use this word for boring things, and soften it when it is about people or someone else's favorite movie." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adjective ends in 「ない」, which can make its forms feel strange. Just treat the whole thing as one i-adjective. The negative is 「つまらなくない」, it is not boring. Yes, two negative-looking pieces in a row. The te-form is 「つまらなくて」, as in 「つまらなくて寝ました|つまらなくてねました」, it was so boring I fell asleep. For looks boring, drop the final 「い」 and add 「そう」: 「つまらなそう」. 「つまらなそうな顔|つまらなそうなかお」 is a bored-looking face. Notice that the 「ない」 here is part of the word itself, not a negative you can remove. There is no positive form meaning fun. So treat it as one solid i-adjective, and conjugate the last 「い」 like any other." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Despite ending in 「ない」, it is one regular i-adjective meaning boring, often squeezed to 「つまんない」. The gift phrase 「つまらないものですが」 humbly calls your present a trifling thing.",
      "It comes from 「詰まる|つまる」, once meaning to come together or satisfy, plus 「ない」, so something that doesn't add up. Today that verb mostly means clogged, as in 「鼻が詰まる|はながつまる」.",
      "This word describes the boring thing, while being bored is 「退屈|たいくつ」 or 「暇|ひま」. Calling someone 「つまらない人|つまらないひと」 is harsh, so soften with 「あまり面白くなかった|あまりおもしろくなかった」.",
      "Treat the whole word as one i-adjective, so the negative is 「つまらなくない」 and looks boring is 「つまらなそう」. The 「ない」 is part of the word, so there is no positive form to remove it to."
    ],
    ja: "その{映画|えいが}は[つまらない]です。",
    en: "That movie is boring." },
  "やさしい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana, and it has two meanings. Each meaning has its own kanji. For easy, as in an easy problem, it is written 「易しい|やさしい」. For kind or gentle, as in a kind person, it is written 「優しい|やさしい」. That second kanji may look familiar. It is the same one you see at the end of 「俳優|はいゆう」, actor. Both meanings share the same feeling: something soft, gentle, not harsh on you. An easy test is gentle on your brain. A kind person is gentle on your heart. The opposite of the easy meaning is 「難しい|むずかしい」, difficult. Another word for easy is 「簡単|かんたん」, simple, which is a na-adjective. Use 「やさしい」 for problems or questions, and also for people, like 「やさしい人|やさしいひと」, a kind person. A way to remember it: picture a gentle teacher giving you an easy quiz. That is a hook. To recap: gentle on you, easy for problems and kind for people." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kind kanji, 「優|やさ」, has the person mark on the left. On the right is a kanji meaning worry or sorrow. A popular way to remember it is a person who feels other people's sorrow, and so is kind. That is a hook, not a proven origin. This kanji also means excellent. With the Chinese-origin reading 「ゆう」, you get 「優勝|ゆうしょう」, winning a championship, and you know it in 「俳優|はいゆう」, actor. The easy kanji, 「易|やさ」, has the sun on top. Its Chinese-origin readings are 「い」 and 「えき」. You will meet them in 「容易|ようい」, simple, a formal word, and in 「貿易|ぼうえき」, trade between countries, where the kanji carries its other meaning, to exchange. So one kanji gives you kindness and excellence, and the other gives you ease and exchange." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The kind meaning takes 「に」 for who or what you are kind to. 「子供に優しい|こどもにやさしい」 means kind to children. This pattern gets used in modern advertising. 「環境に優しい|かんきょうにやさしい」 means kind to the environment, eco-friendly. 「肌に優しい|はだにやさしい」 means gentle on the skin, like a soap. To say be kind, use 「優しくする|やさしくする」: 「友だちに優しくしてください|ともだちにやさしくしてください」, please be kind to your friends. Now for the easy meaning, here is a tip. In daily conversation, people often prefer 「簡単|かんたん」 for an easy task, like an easy recipe. The easy meaning of this word is used more for the difficulty level of questions, tests and reading, like an easy textbook. So say kind to someone with 「に」, and for everyday easy tasks, reach for 「簡単|かんたん」." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has traveled a long way. Many scholars connect it to an old verb, 「痩す|やす」, to grow thin. In the oldest Japanese poetry, it described feeling so ashamed or self-conscious that you seemed to shrink away. From feeling shy and modest, the meaning moved to graceful and refined, someone with gentle, elegant manners. Then it moved again, to kind and considerate, the main meaning today. The easy meaning grew alongside it, from the idea of something gentle and not demanding. You can still sense that softness in the modern word. It never sounds sharp. The modern verb 「痩せる|やせる」, to lose weight, which you know, comes from that same old root. So a word that once meant shrinking with shyness grew into one of the warmest words in Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means easy, written 「易しい|やさしい」, and kind, written 「優しい|やさしい」, both sharing a gentle feeling. An easy test is gentle on your brain, and a kind person is gentle on your heart.",
      "The kind kanji 「優|やさ」 also means excellent, as in 「優勝|ゆうしょう」 and 「俳優|はいゆう」, and is remembered as a person feeling others' sorrow. The easy kanji 「易|やさ」 also means exchange, as in 「貿易|ぼうえき」.",
      "Kind to someone takes 「に」, as in 「子供に優しい|こどもにやさしい」, and ads use 「環境に優しい|かんきょうにやさしい」 for eco-friendly. For everyday easy tasks, people prefer 「簡単|かんたん」.",
      "Many scholars link it to 「痩す|やす」, to grow thin, once meaning shrinking with shame, then graceful, then kind. The modern 「痩せる|やせる」, to lose weight, shares that root."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}は[やさしい]です。",
    en: "The teacher is kind." },
  "むずかしい|難しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji means difficult or trouble. It uses its native reading here, 「むずか」. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「なん」, as in a word meaning a problem or hardship, which you will meet later. It is the opposite of 「やさしい」, easy, and of 「簡単|かんたん」, simple. It is an i-adjective, so the past is 「難しかった|むずかしかった」 and the negative is 「難しくない|むずかしくない」. Young people often shorten it to 「むずい」 in casual chat. Now an important cultural note. When you ask a Japanese person for something, they may answer 「ちょっと難しいです|ちょっとむずかしいです」. Literally that is, it is a little difficult. But it very often means no, that will not be possible. It is a polite, soft way of refusing without saying no directly. So if your request gets this answer, do not push. Thank them and move on. To recap: difficult, the opposite of easy, and a soft, polite way of saying no." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the right side of this kanji. That is a classic component that means a short-tailed bird. It shows up in quite a few kanji, and in this one it does not carry the meaning in any obvious way, so don't try to force a story out of it. What really helps is the family of readings. The native reading here is 「むずか」. The Chinese-origin reading is 「なん」, as in 「難問|なんもん」, a tough question, and 「困難|こんなん」, hardship. There is a third reading, 「がた」, used when the kanji is added to other words. And here is the best surprise. The everyday word 「ありがとう」 comes from 「有り難い|ありがたい」. It literally meant hard to have, so rare. Something rare is precious, and from precious it drifted to grateful. So every time you say thank you, this difficult kanji is hiding inside." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's sort out the neighbors. 「難しい|むずかしい」 is about something being hard to do or understand, like a test or a puzzle. 「大変|たいへん」 is about something being tough to go through, a lot of work or trouble, like moving house or a busy week. A math problem is difficult. Cleaning the whole house is tough. The opposites are 「やさしい」, easy, and 「簡単|かんたん」, simple. 「簡単|かんたん」 leans toward quick and uncomplicated, like a simple recipe. Soon you will also meet the ending 「にくい」, added to a verb stem to say hard to do, as in 「読みにくい|よみにくい」, hard to read. And older speakers in some regions say 「むつかしい」, a softer variant of the same word. Pick the word by asking whether it is hard to figure out, or hard to get through." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese people use this word to talk about more than tests. They use it for situations. A friend asks if you can come to a party on Friday. You can't. A gentle answer is 「金曜日はちょっと…|きんようびはちょっと」, trailing off. If you want to be clearer but still kind, you add 「難しいです|むずかしいです」. In business, you will hear 「それは難しいですね|それはむずかしいですね」. Literally, that is difficult, isn't it. In practice, it usually means no. Learners also use it about Japanese itself. Japanese people love to hear you say 「日本語は難しいです|にほんごはむずかしいです」, and they will often answer 「でも、上手ですね|でも、じょうずですね」, but you are good at it. The polite reply to that compliment is 「いいえ、まだまだです」, no, I still have a long way to go. Now you have a whole little conversation ready." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means difficult or trouble, read 「なん」 in compounds, and young people shorten the word to 「むずい」. 「ちょっと難しいです|ちょっとむずかしいです」 usually means a polite no, so don't push.",
      "The kanji reads 「むずか」, 「なん」 as in 「困難|こんなん」, and 「がた」 when added to words. 「ありがとう」 comes from 「有り難い|ありがたい」, hard to have, so rare, precious and then grateful.",
      "This word is hard to do or understand, like a puzzle, while 「大変|たいへん」 is tough to go through, like moving house. The ending 「にくい」 makes hard to do, as in 「読みにくい|よみにくい」.",
      "In business, 「それは難しいですね|それはむずかしいですね」 usually means no. Say 「日本語は難しいです|にほんごはむずかしいです」 and you may hear 「でも、上手ですね|でも、じょうずですね」, answered with 「いいえ、まだまだです」."
    ],
    ja: "{漢字|かんじ}は[難しい|むずかしい]です。",
    en: "Kanji are difficult." },
  "かっこいい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is a squeezed version of two pieces. The first is 「かっこう」, meaning appearance, shape, or how you look. The second is 「いい」, good. So it is literally good looking appearance, looks good, or cool. It can describe people, but also things, like a cool car, a cool jacket, or a cool move in sports. Here is the common mistake. Because it ends in 「いい」, it conjugates just like 「いい」 does. And 「いい」 is irregular. Its forms come from an older word, 「よい」. So the negative is 「かっこよくない」, not good-looking, and the past is 「かっこよかった」, it was cool. Saying 「かっこいくない」 is a classic learner mistake. Its opposite is 「かっこわるい」, uncool or embarrassing. You will hear 「かっこいい」 all the time from fans talking about actors and singers. To recap: appearance plus good, cool or good-looking, with the same tricky forms as 「いい」." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Say it slowly. There is a small pause in the middle, a small 「っ」, and then a long 「い」 sound at the end. That pause matters. Without it, you would be saying something closer to 「かこ」, which means the past. So hold the beat before the 「こ」. The source word, 「格好|かっこう」, also ends with a long 「う」. When it joins with 「いい」, that long sound gets squeezed short. You will hear the same squeezed piece in 「かっこ悪い|かっこわるい」, uncool, and in the casual verb 「かっこつける」, to try to look cool, to show off. In very casual speech, people even cut it down to 「かっこい」 or stretch it out as 「かっこいーい」 when they are really impressed. So keep the little stop in the middle, and let the ending ring." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is one of the most useful compliments in Japanese, and it is not only for handsome men. A woman with a strong, stylish presence can be 「かっこいい」. So can a smart phone case, a guitar solo, a sports car, or a skilled goal in soccer. Compare it with 「かわいい」, cute. A puppy is cute. A motorcycle is cool. A friend in a new jacket might be either one, depending on the style. For a good-looking man, you will also hear the slang 「イケメン」. It is commonly explained as a blend of the casual phrase for being cool and the English word men. A mini dialogue. A friend shows you a new bike. You say 「わあ、かっこいいですね」. They smile and say 「ありがとう」. If someone tries too hard, though, people tease them for 「かっこつけている」, putting on airs. Use it freely, and it will make people happy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It squeezes 「かっこう」, appearance, plus 「いい」, good, so cool or good-looking. Like 「いい」, it conjugates from 「よい」, giving 「かっこよくない」 and 「かっこよかった」, never 「かっこいくない」.",
      "Keep the small 「っ」 pause in the middle, or it sounds closer to 「かこ」, the past. The squeezed piece also appears in 「かっこ悪い|かっこわるい」, uncool, and 「かっこつける」, to show off.",
      "It is a compliment for anyone or anything stylish, from a strong woman to a guitar solo, while 「かわいい」 is cute. A handsome man is also an 「イケメン」, and trying too hard is 「かっこつけている」."
    ],
    ja: "あの{人|ひと}は[かっこいい]です。",
    en: "That person is good-looking." },
  "こわい|怖い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the left side of the kanji. That narrow, upright shape is the heart mark, a squeezed version of the heart kanji. It is used for words about feelings. You will see the same heart mark on 「忙しい|いそがしい」, busy, which you are learning in this lesson too. So scary is a feeling in your heart. The word uses its native reading, 「こわ」. English splits this idea into scary and scared. Japanese often uses the same word for both. 「この映画は怖い|このえいがはこわい」 means this movie is scary. But 「怖い|こわい」 on its own, said about yourself, can mean I am scared. You can also use it for people, like a strict, frightening teacher, 「怖い先生|こわいせんせい」. A cultural note. Japan has a big tradition of ghost stories, told especially in summer, because a good chill is supposed to cool you down. To recap: the heart mark on the left, a feeling, scary or scared." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "You already know the heart mark on the left. Now look at the right side. It is 「布|ぬの」, the kanji for cloth. Here, the cloth piece is mostly there for its sound. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「ふ」, and that is also the Chinese reading of the whole kanji. You will meet that reading in 「恐怖|きょうふ」, fear or terror, a word you see on horror movie posters. A memory trick, not an origin: picture a ghost made of a white cloth sheet, floating toward you, and your heart on the left starting to pound. The everyday word, though, always uses the native reading, 「こわ」. So the heart for the feeling, and the cloth for the sound, with a sheet ghost to help you remember." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a subtle rule about feeling words. In Japanese, you can say directly that you are scared, but you cannot see inside other people's hearts. So for yourself, 「怖い|こわい」 is fine. For someone else, Japanese adds 「がる」 to the stem and makes a verb: 「怖がる|こわがる」, to act scared, to be afraid. 「弟は犬を怖がっています|おとうとはいぬをこわがっています」, my little brother is afraid of dogs. Notice it takes 「を」. Another useful form is 「怖そう|こわそう」, it looks scary, for a movie poster or a big dog. And watch out for a sound-alike verb, 「壊れる|こわれる」, to break. Different kanji, completely different meaning. A broken phone is not a scary phone. So scared for me, 「怖がる|こわがる」 for others, and don't mix it up with breaking." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "There is a famous comic story in traditional Japanese storytelling called 「まんじゅうこわい」, I'm scared of sweet buns. A group of young men are bragging about what they fear: snakes, spiders, and so on. One man says the thing he fears most is 「まんじゅう」, sweet bean buns. His friends decide to prank him. They pile his room full of buns and wait to hear him scream. Instead, they peek in and find him happily eating them all while pretending to be terrified. When they ask what he is really afraid of, he says, right now, a nice hot cup of tea would scare me. The story is still told today, and people jokingly say the title when they secretly want something. So now you know a Japanese joke built on this very word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The heart mark on the left shows it is a feeling, like in 「忙しい|いそがしい」. It covers both scary and scared, as in 「この映画は怖い|このえいがはこわい」, and also a strict teacher, 「怖い先生|こわいせんせい」.",
      "The right side is 「布|ぬの」, cloth, which gives the reading 「ふ」, as in 「恐怖|きょうふ」, terror. As a memory trick, picture a white sheet ghost floating toward you while the heart mark pounds.",
      "You can say you are scared yourself, but for others add 「がる」 to make 「怖がる|こわがる」, which takes 「を」. Don't confuse it with 「壊れる|こわれる」, to break.",
      "In the comic story 「まんじゅうこわい」, a man claims to fear sweet buns, then happily eats the pile his friends leave to prank him. People jokingly quote the title when they secretly want something."
    ],
    ja: "この{映画|えいが}は[怖い|こわい]です。",
    en: "This movie is scary." },
  "きらい|嫌い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji has 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left, the same piece that appears in 「好き|すき」, to like. That makes a handy pair to remember, two feeling words that both start with the woman piece. It uses its native reading, 「きら」. Now the grammar, which works just like 「好き|すき」. The thing you dislike is marked with 「が」, not 「を」. 「やさいが嫌いです|やさいがきらいです」 means I dislike vegetables. And here is the big trap. Even though it ends in an i sound, it is a na-adjective, not an i-adjective. So the negative is 「嫌いじゃない|きらいじゃない」, I do not dislike it. Never 「きらくない」. Before a noun you say 「嫌いな食べ物|きらいなたべもの」, a food you dislike. A cultural note. In Japanese, saying you dislike something directly can sound strong. People often soften it by saying they are not very fond of it, using 「あまり好きじゃない|あまりすきじゃない」. To recap: the partner of like, marked with 「が」, and a na-adjective despite its ending." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "You know the woman piece on the left. Now look at the right side. It is a kanji meaning to combine or hold two things at once, and it gives the sound. The Chinese-origin reading of the whole kanji is 「けん」. You will see it in 「嫌悪|けんお」, strong disgust, a serious word. The same kanji has another native reading you will hear every day: 「嫌|いや」. Saying 「嫌だ|いやだ」 or 「いや」 means no way, I don't want to. And a third reading, 「げん」, appears in 「機嫌|きげん」, mood. 「ご機嫌|ごきげん」 means in a great mood. So one kanji gives you dislike, no way, and someone's mood. Here, for this word, it is the native reading, 「きら」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Three close neighbors, each with its own job. 「嫌い|きらい」 is a steady dislike, a taste. I dislike carrots, I dislike winter. 「嫌|いや」 is a reaction right now, I don't want this. A child refusing to go to bed shouts 「いやだ」. You might dislike nothing about your job, but still say 「いやだ」 on a rainy Monday morning. 「嫌がる|いやがる」 is how you describe someone else showing they don't want something. My cat hates baths, it struggles and shows it. Then there is the pair 「好き嫌い|すききらい」, likes and dislikes. 「好き嫌いはありますか|すききらいはありますか」 is a common question before a meal, are there foods you don't eat. Parents tell picky children 「好き嫌いしないで|すききらいしないで」, don't be picky. So a steady taste, a right now refusal, and the pair for picky eating." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a trap that catches a lot of learners. 「きらい」 and 「きれい」 sound very close. One means dislike. The other means pretty or clean. Mixing them up can be a disaster. Imagine telling your host mother her cooking is 「きらい」 when you meant to say her kitchen is 「きれい」. The difference is the middle vowel. 「きらい」 has an open 「ら」, like a wide mouth saying ah. 「きれい」 has 「れ」, and the ending sounds like a long eh. A memory trick: open your mouth wide for the ah in dislike, like you are saying yuck. Smile for the eh in pretty. Both are na-adjectives, so the grammar won't save you. Only the vowel will. Say them side by side a few times, and the difference will stick." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Like 「好き|すき」, the kanji starts with the woman piece, and what you dislike takes 「が」. It is a na-adjective, so the negative is 「嫌いじゃない|きらいじゃない」, and people often soften it with 「あまり好きじゃない|あまりすきじゃない」.",
      "The kanji reads 「きら」 here, 「いや」 in 「嫌だ|いやだ」, no way, and 「げん」 in 「機嫌|きげん」, mood, as in 「ご機嫌|ごきげん」. One kanji gives dislike, refusal and mood.",
      "「嫌い|きらい」 is a steady dislike, 「嫌|いや」 is a refusal right now, and 「嫌がる|いやがる」 describes someone else not wanting something. 「好き嫌い|すききらい」 means likes and dislikes, as in picky eating.",
      "「きらい」 and 「きれい」 sound close but mean dislike and pretty, so only the middle vowel saves you. Open wide for the ah in dislike, like saying yuck, and smile for the eh in pretty."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}は{肉|にく}が[嫌い|きらい]です。",
    en: "I dislike meat." },
  "だいすき|大好き": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is just 「好き|すき」, to like, with 「大|だい」, big, put in front. Big liking, to love. The kanji 「大|だい」 is a person stretching out arms and legs as wide as possible. Here it has its Chinese-origin reading. You know it from 「大学|だいがく」, college, and 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, it's okay. The kanji 「好|す」 is woman plus child, a picture of affection. The grammar is the same as for 「好き|すき」. The thing you love takes 「が」. 「すしが大好きです|すしがだいすきです」 means I love sushi. It is a na-adjective, so before a noun you say 「大好きな|だいすきな」, as in my very favorite thing. Here is a usage tip. For food, hobbies, music and so on, this is the natural way to say you love something. For romantic love, Japanese people rarely say the heavier word for love. They are much more likely to say 「大好き|だいすき」. Its opposite is 「大嫌い|だいきらい」, to hate. To recap: big plus like, to love, with 「が」 marking what you love." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji has several readings, and it is worth sorting them out. Here it is 「だい」, a Chinese-origin reading, which is the usual one when it acts as a prefix meaning very or great. It can also be 「たい」, as in 「大変|たいへん」, tough, and 「大切|たいせつ」, precious. Its native reading is 「おお」, as in 「大きい|おおきい」, big. And there is a special one you must just memorize: 「大人|おとな」, an adult. The second kanji, 「好|す」, also has a Chinese reading, 「こう」, which shows up in 「大好物|だいこうぶつ」, a favorite food, literally a greatly liked thing. So 「大好物|だいこうぶつ」 is a fun bonus word. Tell someone ramen is your favorite, and they will be impressed. Both kanji, many readings, but here it is simply 「だい」 and 「す」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, saying I love you with the heavy word for romantic love is rare and very dramatic. So how do people confess their feelings? Usually with 「好きです|すきです」, I like you, which in a confession clearly means I have feelings for you. A bit stronger is 「大好きです|だいすきです」. Families use it warmly too. A child might hug a grandparent and say 「おばあちゃん、大好き|おばあちゃん、だいすき」. Fans use it for idols, teams and games. Friends say it about food: 「この店のラーメン、大好き|このみせのラーメン、だいすき」. Notice the casual versions drop 「です」. One caution: telling a coworker you love them, even jokingly, can be awkward. Stick to food and hobbies at work. So it is warm, versatile, and the natural way Japanese people say love." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The prefix 「大|だい」 works like the English word super. Once you notice it, you will see it everywhere. 「大好き|だいすき」, super liking, to love. 「大嫌い|だいきらい」, super disliking, to hate. 「大ファン|だいファン」, a big fan. 「大人気|だいにんき」, hugely popular. 「大成功|だいせいこう」, a huge success. 「大好物|だいこうぶつ」, a favorite food. But be careful. Not every word starting with this kanji is an intensifier. 「大学|だいがく」 is a college, not a big study, and 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」 means okay. For a shopping example, a store sign might say 「大セール|だいセール」, a big sale. So when you hear 「だい」 in front of a word you know, think super, and you will often guess the meaning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「好き|すき」 with 「大|だい」, big, in front, so big liking, to love, with 「が」 marking what you love. Japanese people say it far more than the heavier word for romantic love.",
      "The big kanji reads 「だい」 as a prefix, 「たい」 in 「大変|たいへん」, 「おお」 in 「大きい|おおきい」, and specially in 「大人|おとな」. Bonus word: 「大好物|だいこうぶつ」, a favorite food.",
      "Confessions usually use 「好きです|すきです」 or the stronger 「大好きです|だいすきです」, and families and fans use it warmly. At work, stick to food and hobbies to avoid awkwardness.",
      "The prefix 「大|だい」 works like super, as in 「大ファン|だいファン」, 「大人気|だいにんき」 and 「大成功|だいせいこう」. Not every word with it is an intensifier, though, like 「大学|だいがく」."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}はサーフィンが[大好き|だいすき]です。",
    en: "I love surfing." },
  "だいきらい|大嫌い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is 「嫌い|きらい」, to dislike, with 「大|だい」, big, in front. Big dislike, to hate. It follows the same pattern as 「大好き|だいすき」, big liking, to love. Learn them as a pair. The kanji 「大|だい」 is a person stretching out wide, read with its Chinese-origin reading here, like in 「大学|だいがく」. The grammar works like 「嫌い|きらい」. The thing you hate takes 「が」. 「虫が大嫌いです|むしがだいきらいです」 means I hate bugs. It is a na-adjective, even though it ends in an i sound, so before a noun you say 「大嫌いな|だいきらいな」. Here is a strong caution. This word is very direct. Said about a person, it can really hurt. In daily conversation, people often soften things with 「あまり好きじゃない|あまりすきじゃない」, not very fond of it. That said, in anime and dramas you will hear it shouted between friends or lovers, sometimes not quite meaning it. To recap: big plus dislike, to hate, the strong partner of love." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "When people say this word with real feeling, they often add a little stop in the middle: 「大っ嫌い|だいっきらい」. That small pause makes it punchier, like slamming a door. You will hear it in dramas, anime and arguments. The same kind of emphatic small pause shows up in 「すっごい」, instead of 「すごい」, for super amazing. Notice the word has three beats before the last part, 「だ」, 「い」, then 「き」. Keep the 「だい」 clear so it doesn't sound like plain 「きらい」. And don't confuse the end with 「きれい」, pretty. Hating something and finding it pretty are very different messages. So the plain form for normal speech, and the version with the pause when emotions are high." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is much stronger in Japanese conversation than hate often is in casual English. In English, people say I hate traffic or I hate Mondays without much thought. In Japanese, 「大嫌い|だいきらい」 can sound intense, especially said to someone's face. It is safe for things: 「ゴキブリが大嫌い|ゴキブリがだいきらい」, I hate cockroaches. 「テストが大嫌い|テストがだいきらい」, I hate tests. Everyone will nod. About a person, though, it is serious. Saying it to someone can end a friendship. There is also a classic scene in anime and manga. A character shouts 「大嫌い|だいきらい」 at someone they secretly like, then runs away blushing. Viewers know it really means the opposite. If you just want to say you are not a fan, use 「苦手|にがて」, not my thing, which is softer. So use it for bugs and tests, and save it carefully for people." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「嫌い|きらい」 with 「大|だい」 in front, so big dislike, to hate, the strong partner of 「大好き|だいすき」. It is very direct, so said about a person it can really hurt.",
      "With real feeling, people add a small pause, 「大っ嫌い|だいっきらい」, like slamming a door. Keep the 「だい」 clear so it doesn't sound like plain 「きらい」 or 「きれい」.",
      "It is much stronger than casual English hate, fine for bugs and tests but serious about a person. For not my thing, the softer word is 「苦手|にがて」."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}はテストが[大嫌い|だいきらい]です。",
    en: "I hate tests." },
  "きれい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. There is a kanji spelling, but it is complicated and rarely used. It has two meanings. One is pretty or beautiful, like a beautiful flower, 「きれいな花|きれいなはな」. The other is clean, like a clean room. The idea that joins them is something pure and neat, pleasing to look at. Its opposite for the clean meaning is 「汚い|きたない」, dirty, which you will meet later. Now the big trap. Even though it ends in an i sound, it is a na-adjective, not an i-adjective. So the negative is 「きれいじゃない」, not 「きれくない」. Before a noun, you say 「きれいな」, and the past is 「きれいでした」. Two other words that look like i-adjectives but are actually na-adjectives are 「好き|すき」 and 「嫌い|きらい」. A way to remember it: picture a clean, beautiful room. One image covers both meanings. That is a hook. To recap: pretty and clean, with the na-adjective trap." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for beautiful and clean, and each has its own flavor. 「きれい」 is the everyday all-rounder. Pretty, neat, clean, tidy. 「美しい|うつくしい」 is more poetic and serious. You would use it for a mountain view, a piece of music, or in writing. It sounds a little formal in casual chat. 「かわいい」 is cute, small and sweet, like a kitten or a baby's tiny shoes. For clean in the hygienic sense, there is 「清潔|せいけつ」, sanitary, used for kitchens and hospitals. A handy verb phrase is 「きれいにする」, to make clean, to tidy up. 「部屋をきれいにしてください|へやをきれいにしてください」, please tidy your room. So for daily life, 「きれい」 is your go-to, and the others add a special shade." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a surprising extra use as an adverb. 「きれいに」 can mean neatly, but also completely, totally. If a child eats everything on the plate, a parent says 「きれいに食べたね|きれいにたべたね」, you ate it all up nicely. People also say 「きれいに忘れた|きれいにわすれた」, I completely forgot, as if the memory was wiped clean. Japanese people also use it for handwriting and voices: 「字がきれいですね|じがきれいですね」, your handwriting is neat, is a common compliment for learners. And it describes a clear sky or clear water, like a beautiful river you can see the bottom of. One caution: when complimenting a person's looks, it is fine among friends, but at work it can feel too personal, just like in English. So beyond pretty and clean, think neat, clear, and wiped clean." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Cleanliness is a big part of daily life in Japan, and it starts early. In most Japanese schools, there are no janitors cleaning the classrooms. Students do it themselves every day. The cleaning time is called 「そうじ」. Children sweep floors, wipe desks and clean the hallways together. At sporting events, Japanese fans have become famous worldwide for picking up their trash in the stands after the game. At home, people take off their shoes at the entrance to keep the floors clean. And many streets have few public trash cans, so people carry their trash home. The idea behind it is simple. A shared space should be left clean for the next person. So when you hear this word in Japan, you are hearing a value, not just an adjective." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means both pretty and clean, joined by the idea of something pure and neat. Despite its ending it is a na-adjective, so the negative is 「きれいじゃない」, never 「きれくない」.",
      "「きれい」 is the everyday all-rounder, while 「美しい|うつくしい」 is poetic, 「かわいい」 is cute, and 「清潔|せいけつ」 is hygienic. 「きれいにする」 means to tidy up.",
      "As an adverb, 「きれいに」 can mean completely, as in 「きれいに食べたね|きれいにたべたね」 or 「きれいに忘れた|きれいにわすれた」. 「字がきれいですね|じがきれいですね」 is a common compliment for neat handwriting.",
      "Japanese students clean their own classrooms daily in 「そうじ」 time, fans pick up trash at games, and people carry trash home. The value is leaving shared spaces clean for the next person."
    ],
    ja: "この{部屋|へや}は[きれい]です。",
    en: "This room is clean." },
  "げんき|元気": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. 「元|げん」 means origin, source, or the base of something. 「気|き」 means spirit, air, or energy, the invisible force inside things. Put them together and you get your source energy, the basic life force that keeps you going. Healthy and lively. That 「気|き」 is a superstar kanji. You know it from 「天気|てんき」, the mood of the sky, weather, 「病気|びょうき」, sick energy, illness, and 「電気|でんき」, electric energy. The opposite feeling is 「元気がない|げんきがない」, to have no energy, to look down. It is a na-adjective, so you say 「元気な子供|げんきなこども」, an energetic child. You will use it in greetings constantly. 「お元気ですか|おげんきですか」 is how are you, for someone you have not seen for a while. And 「元気でね|げんきでね」 is a warm way to say take care when parting for a long time. To recap: source plus energy, your basic life force, healthy and lively." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji is often explained as a picture of a person with a big head on top of two legs. The head became the idea of the top, the beginning, the source. Its main Chinese-origin reading is 「げん」, as here. But it also has a second one, 「がん」, in 「元日|がんじつ」, New Year's Day, the very first day of the year. Its native reading is 「もと」, meaning origin or the former state, as in 「元の場所|もとのばしょ」, the original place. You will also meet 「地元|じもと」, your hometown, literally the ground you come from. So this one kanji says beginning in several ways: the first day, the origin, and home. In 「元気|げんき」, it is the source of your energy." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases you will actually hear with this word. 「元気になる|げんきになる」 means to get better, to recover. After a cold, a friend might say 「元気になってよかった|げんきになってよかった」, I'm glad you're better. 「元気を出して|げんきをだして」 means cheer up, literally put out your energy. Say it to a friend who is feeling down. When someone asks 「お元気ですか|おげんきですか」, the classic polite reply is 「おかげさまで、元気です|おかげさまで、げんきです」, thanks to you, I'm well. It doesn't mean they actually helped. It's a warm thank you to the world. A common mistake: English speakers use it like a daily hello. But this question is for people you haven't seen for a while. For a coworker you saw yesterday, just say good morning. So recover, cheer up, and thanks to you, all built on this word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「気|き」, shows up in dozens of words about feelings and energy. Here are some you will use soon. 「気持ち|きもち」, a feeling. 「気持ちいい|きもちいい」, it feels good, like a hot bath. 「気分|きぶん」, your mood right now. 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」, I feel sick. 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, be careful, literally attach your spirit to it. 「人気|にんき」, popularity, the energy of people around something. Note the special reading 「にん」 here. 「気になる|きになる」, to be on your mind, to bother you or make you curious. In each one, the kanji brings that invisible inner energy or attention. So when you see it, think spirit or mind, and the meaning usually falls into place." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「元|げん」 is source and 「気|き」 is energy, so it is your basic life force, healthy and lively. Ask 「お元気ですか|おげんきですか」 after a long time, and say 「元気でね|げんきでね」 for a long parting.",
      "The source kanji, a person with a big head, reads 「げん」 here, 「がん」 in 「元日|がんじつ」, New Year's Day, and 「もと」 for origin, as in 「地元|じもと」, hometown. It says beginning in several ways.",
      "「元気になる|げんきになる」 means to recover and 「元気を出して|げんきをだして」 means cheer up. Reply to how are you with 「おかげさまで、元気です|おかげさまで、げんきです」, and don't use it as a daily hello.",
      "The energy kanji 「気|き」 fills feeling words like 「気持ち|きもち」, 「気分|きぶん」, 「気をつけて|きをつけて」 and 「人気|にんき」. When you see it, think spirit or mind."
    ],
    ja: "{子供|こども}たちはとても[元気|げんき]です。",
    en: "The children are very energetic." },
  "しずか|静か": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji has two parts. On the left is the kanji for blue or green, and on the right is a shape meaning to compete or struggle. A way to remember it: calm blue water where all the struggle has settled down. That is a hook, not a proven origin. It uses its native reading, 「しず」. It is a na-adjective, so you say 「静かな部屋|しずかなへや」, a quiet room, and the negative is 「静かじゃない|しずかじゃない」. Its opposite is 「にぎやか」, lively and bustling. Learn them as a pair. There is also 「うるさい」, noisy or annoying, which is negative, while 「にぎやか」 is positive. A very useful phrase is 「静かにしてください|しずかにしてください」, please be quiet. Teachers say it to students all the time. A cultural note. Japanese trains are famously quiet. People avoid phone calls and keep their voices low. To recap: calm blue with the struggle settled, quiet, the opposite of lively." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「せい」, that you will see in more serious words. 「冷静|れいせい」 means calm and composed, keeping a cool head. 「安静|あんせい」 is rest, what a doctor tells you to do when you are sick in bed. Both words keep the core idea, stillness. The kanji also appears in a place name: 「静岡|しずおか」, a prefecture southwest of Tokyo, famous for green tea fields and great views of Mount Fuji. There it uses the native reading again, 「しず」, plus 「おか」, hill. So here are two ways to read the same shape, 「しず」 for everyday quiet, and 「せい」 for calm in more formal words." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Because this is a na-adjective, you can turn it into an adverb by adding 「に」. 「静かに歩いてください|しずかにあるいてください」, please walk quietly. 「静かに食べる|しずかにたべる」, to eat quietly. Add 「なる」 for to become: 「静かになりました|しずかになりました」, it got quiet. That is what you say when the kids finally fall asleep. Combine with the te-form to link ideas: 「静かで、きれいな町|しずかで、きれいなまち」, a quiet and pretty town. For na-adjectives, the linking form is 「で」. In casual speech, people just say 「静かにして|しずかにして」, be quiet, or even a soft 「しーっ」. So quiet as an adjective, quietly as an adverb, and the linking form for describing places." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a deep love of quiet moments, and one of the most famous poems in the language shows it. The poet Basho wrote a haiku at a mountain temple in northern Japan: 「閑さや岩にしみ入る蝉の声|しずかさや いわにしみいる せみのこえ」. It means, roughly, such stillness, the voice of the cicadas soaks into the rocks. The kanji he used for stillness is a different one from the one in your word, but the reading is the same. The surprise is that cicadas are very loud. Yet Basho felt the silence of the mountain so deeply that even their buzzing seemed to sink into the stone. Today, many people climb the long stairway at that temple to feel the same quiet. So in Japan, quiet is not just the absence of noise, it is something to appreciate." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is blue or green beside a struggle shape, a hook for calm water where the struggle has settled. It is a na-adjective, the opposite of 「にぎやか」, and 「静かにしてください|しずかにしてください」 means please be quiet.",
      "The reading 「せい」 appears in formal words for stillness, like 「冷静|れいせい」, calm and composed, and 「安静|あんせい」, bed rest. The place name 「静岡|しずおか」 uses the native 「しず」.",
      "Add 「に」 for an adverb, as in 「静かに歩いてください|しずかにあるいてください」, and 「なる」 for became quiet. Link it with 「で」, as in 「静かで、きれいな町|しずかで、きれいなまち」.",
      "Basho's famous haiku at a mountain temple says the stillness was so deep that even the loud cicadas' voices seemed to soak into the rocks. In Japan, quiet is something to appreciate."
    ],
    ja: "{図書館|としょかん}は[静か|しずか]です。",
    en: "The library is quiet." },
  "にぎやか|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It describes a place or event that is lively, bustling, full of people and fun noise. Think of a busy shopping street, a festival, or a family dinner with lots of chatter. It is a positive word. That is the key difference from 「うるさい」, which means noisy in an annoying way. A busy market is 「にぎやか」. Your neighbor's loud music at midnight is 「うるさい」. It is a na-adjective, so you say 「にぎやかな町|にぎやかなまち」, a lively town. Its opposite is 「静か|しずか」, quiet. A cultural note. Japanese 「お祭り|おまつり」, festivals, with their drums, food stalls and crowds, are the perfect picture of this word. At New Year, shrines are very 「にぎやか」 with visitors, and shopping streets get the same way before holidays. To recap: lively and bustling in a good way, the opposite of quiet." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This word is usually written in kana, but it does have a kanji, and the kanji tells a nice story. On the left is the shell radical, 「貝|かい」. In ancient China, shells were used as money, so this radical appears in many kanji about money and trade, like 「買う|かう」, to buy. So a lively, bustling place, in the kanji's eyes, is full of buying and selling. The same kanji gives the verb 「にぎわう」, to be crowded and lively. A travel show might say a shopping street is crowded with tourists, using that verb. You don't need to write the kanji yet. But the shell for money makes a market scene easy to picture. So think busy market stalls whenever you hear this word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word describes places, events and also people. For places: 「にぎやかな通り|にぎやかなとおり」, a busy street, or 「にぎやかな店|にぎやかなみせ」, a lively shop. For people, 「にぎやかな人|にぎやかなひと」 is someone talkative and fun, the life of the party. For a family, 「にぎやかな家族|にぎやかなかぞく」 is a big, cheerful family. It is also a polite, gentle word. If a guest's children are running around noisily, a host might smile and say 「にぎやかですね」. It sounds kind, though it may hint that things are a bit loud. With 「なる」, 「にぎやかになりました」 means it got lively, like a quiet town during a festival. So it is the friendly, warm way to talk about noise and crowds." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means lively and bustling in a positive way, like a festival or a busy shopping street, unlike annoying 「うるさい」. It is a na-adjective, the opposite of 「静か|しずか」.",
      "Its rarely used kanji has the shell radical for money, so a bustling place is full of buying and selling, a busy market scene. The same kanji gives the verb 「にぎわう」, to be crowded and lively.",
      "It works for places, events and people, like 「にぎやかな人|にぎやかなひと」, the life of the party. A host might say 「にぎやかですね」 about noisy young guests, kindly hinting that things are loud."
    ],
    ja: "この{町|まち}は[にぎやか]です。",
    en: "This town is lively." },
  "ひま|暇": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji has 「日|ひ」, sun or day, on the left, a fitting piece for a word about time. It uses its native reading, 「ひま」. It means having free time, not being busy. Its opposite is 「忙しい|いそがしい」, busy. It is a na-adjective, so you say 「暇な日|ひまなひ」, a free day, and the negative is 「暇じゃない|ひまじゃない」. Here is an important politeness note. Asking someone 「暇ですか|ひまですか」 can sound a bit too direct, almost like are you idle. With friends it is fine. But with a teacher or boss, it is better to ask if they have some time. The word can also mean bored when you have nothing to do. A teenager lying around might groan that they are so bored, using this word. So it covers both free time and boring empty time, depending on context. A way to remember it: picture a free afternoon, with the sun on the left of the kanji, and nothing on your schedule. That is a hook. To recap: sun on the left, free time, the opposite of busy." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "You know the sun piece on the left. The right side gives the sound, and it is the key to the Chinese-origin reading, 「か」. You will see that reading in 「休暇|きゅうか」, a vacation or time off work, and 「余暇|よか」, leisure time. Both keep the idea of free time, but they sound more formal than the everyday word. The same kanji has one more native reading, 「いとま」, an old word for free time or for leaving. A polite guest getting up to leave someone's house might say 「そろそろおいとまします」, I should take my leave now. It is old-fashioned but still heard. Here, though, it is simply 「ひま」. So one kanji gives you free time, vacation, and a polite way to say goodbye." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A very common question when making plans is 「今週末、暇?|こんしゅうまつ、ひま?」, are you free this weekend? It is casual, for friends. Another is 「暇なとき、何をしますか|ひまなとき、なにをしますか」, what do you do in your free time. You will be asked this in class and when meeting people. Then there is 「暇つぶし|ひまつぶし」, literally crushing free time, killing time. Playing a phone game on the train is a classic way. You can also say 「暇がない|ひまがない」, I have no time. At work, a shop clerk might say 「今日は暇ですね|きょうはひまですね」, it's slow today, meaning few customers. But be careful. Asking your boss if they are 「暇|ひま」 can sound like you think they are not working. So it's for friends, free time, and slow days, not for asking your boss." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just for fun, using the sound. The word sounds a bit like the start of Himalaya. Picture a man with a whole free month on his calendar, nothing scheduled. What does he do with all that free time? He climbs the Himalayas, in his pajamas, carrying a giant cup of tea. At the top, he sits down, looks out over the clouds, and sighs, I'm still free. Meanwhile, his busy friend is down at the bottom, running around with three phones. Now look at the kanji. The sun on the left is the sun rising over those mountains on his long, empty day. So when you hear 「ひま」, picture the man with all the time in the world on top of the Himalayas." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The sun or day 「日|ひ」 on the left fits a word about time, meaning free time, the opposite of 「忙しい|いそがしい」. 「暇ですか|ひまですか」 is fine with friends but too direct for a teacher or boss.",
      "The right side gives the reading 「か」, as in 「休暇|きゅうか」, vacation, and 「余暇|よか」, leisure. An old reading 「いとま」 survives in the polite leaving phrase 「そろそろおいとまします」.",
      "Ask friends 「暇なとき、何をしますか|ひまなとき、なにをしますか」, and killing time is 「暇つぶし|ひまつぶし」. A clerk's 「今日は暇ですね|きょうはひまですね」 means a slow day, but never ask your boss if they are free this way.",
      "The word sounds like the start of Himalaya, so picture a man with a free month climbing the Himalayas in pajamas, with the kanji's sun rising over the mountains on his long, empty day."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[暇|ひま]です。",
    en: "I'm free tomorrow." },
  "およぐ|泳ぐ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji has the water mark on the left, the three little drops you saw in 「海|うみ」, sea. On the right is the kanji for eternal or long-lasting. A way to remember it: moving through water on and on, stroke after stroke. That is a hook, but the pieces really are water and long. It uses its native reading, 「およ」. This is a u-verb. In its polite form it becomes 「泳ぎます|およぎます」. For the te-form, the 「ぐ」 ending turns into 「いで」, giving 「泳いで|およいで」. You know another verb with the same ending: 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry, which becomes 「急いで|いそいで」. When you say where you swim, use 「で」, the place of action: 「海で泳ぐ|うみでおよぐ」, to swim in the sea. Or 「プールで泳ぐ|プールでおよぐ」, to swim in a pool. A cultural note. Most Japanese elementary schools have an outdoor pool, and swimming lessons in summer are a standard part of school life. To recap: water plus long-lasting, swimming, with the 「ぐ」 to 「いで」 te-form." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "You know the right side is the kanji for eternal or long-lasting. It is not only there for the meaning. It also gives the sound. That piece is read 「えい」, and so is the whole kanji in its Chinese-origin reading. You will hear it in 「水泳|すいえい」, swimming as a sport, literally water swimming. The same right side appears in 「永遠|えいえん」, forever. Now for the swimming styles, built on the native reading. 「平泳ぎ|ひらおよぎ」, breaststroke, literally flat swimming. 「背泳ぎ|せおよぎ」, backstroke, literally back swimming. The crawl and butterfly use borrowed English words: 「クロール」 and 「バタフライ」. So everyday swimming is 「およぐ」, the sport is 「すいえい」, and the strokes are built on the verb." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "A few patterns you will use a lot with this verb. To say you can swim, use the potential form. Change the final 「ぐ」 to 「げる」: 「泳げる|およげる」, can swim. Politely, 「泳げます|およげます」. The negative is 「泳げません|およげません」, I can't swim. To say you are going swimming, use the stem plus 「に行く|にいく」, go to do: 「泳ぎに行きます|およぎにいきます」, I'm going swimming. For an ongoing action, use the te-form plus 「いる」: 「子供がプールで泳いでいる|こどもがプールでおよいでいる」, the kids are swimming in the pool. And to ask a friend, 「一緒に泳ぎませんか|いっしょにおよぎませんか」, won't you swim with me. So can swim, go swimming, and is swimming, all from one verb." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Summer in Japan is beach season, and there is even a national holiday for the sea, 「海の日|うみのひ」, Marine Day, in July. Beaches officially open for swimming in early summer with a ceremony, and lifeguards watch over marked swimming areas. Many Japanese people believe you shouldn't swim in the sea after 「お盆|おぼん」, the mid-August holiday for honoring ancestors. One practical reason often given is that jellyfish appear in large numbers by then, and the waves get rougher. Some older people also link it to the spirits of the dead, who are said to visit during that time. So beach trips tend to happen in July and early August. If you visit in late August, you may find the beaches emptier, and now you know why." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The water mark plus the kanji for long-lasting make a hook of moving through water stroke after stroke. The 「ぐ」 ending becomes 「いで」 in the te-form, 「泳いで|およいで」, and the place takes 「で」.",
      "The long-lasting part gives the reading 「えい」, as in 「水泳|すいえい」, swimming as a sport, and 「永遠|えいえん」. Strokes build on the verb, like 「平泳ぎ|ひらおよぎ」 and 「背泳ぎ|せおよぎ」.",
      "Change 「ぐ」 to 「げる」 for can swim, 「泳げる|およげる」, and use 「泳ぎに行きます|およぎにいきます」 for going swimming. 「泳いでいる|およいでいる」 means is swimming.",
      "Japan celebrates 「海の日|うみのひ」 in July, but many believe you shouldn't swim in the sea after 「お盆|おぼん」 in mid-August. Jellyfish and rough waves are the practical reasons, so late-August beaches empty out."
    ],
    ja: "{海|うみ}で[泳ぎました|およぎました|およぐ]。",
    en: "I swam in the sea." },
  "のる|乗る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is often described as a person standing on top of a tree. That is a hook to remember it, picture someone riding high up. It uses its native reading, 「の」. Now the key grammar point, which English speakers often get wrong. The thing you ride takes 「に」, not 「を」. So you say 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」, to ride the bus, and 「電車に乗る|でんしゃにのる」, to take the train. Think of it as getting on to the bus, moving onto it. The opposite, to get off, is 「降りる|おりる」, and that one takes 「を」, getting off from the bus. You will meet 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」 later, which means to miss a train or bus, literally to be late to board. This is a u-verb, so the te-form is 「乗って|のって」. You can also use it for riding a bicycle, a horse, or a ride at an amusement park. To recap: someone up high, riding, with 「に」 for what you get on." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you know this verb, a whole set of travel words opens up. Add 「物|もの」, thing, and you get 「乗り物|のりもの」, a vehicle, literally a thing you ride. A station platform or taxi stand is a 「乗り場|のりば」, a riding place. When you change trains, you 「乗り換える|のりかえる」, ride and switch, and station signs everywhere say 「乗り換え|のりかえ」 for transfers. There is also a partner verb, 「乗せる|のせる」, to give someone a ride or to put something on board. 「駅まで乗せてください|えきまでのせてください」 means please give me a ride to the station. In formal words the same kanji is read 「じょう」, its Chinese-origin reading. A passenger is a 「乗客|じょうきゃく」, and a train ticket is sometimes called a 「乗車券|じょうしゃけん」. Two readings, one idea: getting on board. Learn the native family first, and the formal ones will make sense later." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb is everywhere in daily travel. A friend might ask 「何に乗って来たの|なににのってきたの」, what did you ride here on. You could answer 「自転車に乗って来た|じてんしゃにのってきた」, I came by bike. Here is a common mix-up. If you want to say you go somewhere by car, the natural way is 「車で行く|くるまでいく」, using 「で」 for the means. But if you want to talk about the act of riding itself, like I rode a horse for the first time, use this verb with 「に」. Japanese also uses it in some fun idioms. 「相談に乗る|そうだんにのる」 means to listen and give advice, as if you climb aboard someone's problem with them. And 「調子に乗る|ちょうしにのる」 means to get carried away, riding a wave of good mood too far. A parent might tell a showing-off kid 「調子に乗らないで|ちょうしにのらないで」, do not get carried away. So the verb rides buses, horses, and even moods." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a crowded rush-hour train in Tokyo. The doors open, and a tiny grandmother marches up to board. A station worker in white gloves calls out no room, no room. She ignores him, steps on anyway, and says, no rule says I can't. No rule. That sound, close to 「のる」, is her riding the train no matter what. Now add the kanji. The explanation often given is a person up on a tree, so imagine the same grandmother later, perched high in a tree, still refusing to get off. Every time you say 「乗ります|のります」, picture her firmly on board, gripping the strap. And remember she got on to the train, so the vehicle takes 「に」. One stubborn grandmother, one firm grip, one verb for getting on." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Picture the kanji as a person riding high on a tree, and remember the key grammar: the vehicle takes 「に」, as in 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」, while getting off with 「降りる|おりる」 takes 「を」.",
      "Add 「物|もの」 for 「乗り物|のりもの」, a vehicle, and you also get 「乗り場|のりば」, a boarding place, and 「乗り換える|のりかえる」, to transfer trains. The formal reading 「じょう」 shows up in 「乗客|じょうきゃく」, a passenger.",
      "For going by car, say 「車で行く|くるまでいく」 with 「で」, but use this verb with 「に」 for the act of riding. It also appears in idioms like 「調子に乗る|ちょうしにのる」, to get carried away.",
      "Picture a stubborn grandmother pushing onto a packed Tokyo train, saying no rule, close to 「のる」. She gets on to the train, so the vehicle takes 「に」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}バスに[乗ります|のります|のる]。",
    en: "I ride the bus every day." },
  "やる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It is a casual word for to do, much like 「する」. People say 「宿題をやる|しゅくだいをやる」, to do homework, or 「テニスをやる」, to play tennis. It often feels a bit more active and hands-on than 「する」, like really getting into something. It is a u-verb. The polite form is 「やります」 and the te-form is 「やって」. Here is a politeness note. 「やる」 is casual. In formal settings, 「する」 is safer, and for your boss's actions you would use more respectful words. It has a second meaning too, to give to someone below you, like giving food to a pet or water to plants. 「犬にえさをやる|いぬにえさをやる」 means to feed the dog. You will hear 「やった」 a lot as a happy shout, meaning I did it, or yes. And 「やってみる」 means to give it a try. A way to remember it: think of a coach clapping and shouting let's do it. That is a hook. To recap: a casual, energetic to do, plus a joyful 「やった」 when you succeed." },
      { title: "Giving downward", text: "The second meaning of this verb, to give, fits into the giving system you meet in Genki. There are verbs for giving that depend on who is above whom. 「あげる」 is the neutral way to say I give to someone. 「くれる」 is when someone gives to me or my group. And this verb is the downward version, giving to someone clearly below you, such as a pet, a plant, or sometimes your own young child. So you water flowers with 「花に水をやる|はなにみずをやる」, and you feed the cat with 「猫にえさをやる|ねこにえさをやる」. Many people today say 「あげる」 even for pets, because it sounds gentler, so you will hear both. Be careful with people, though. Saying you gave something to a friend with this verb can sound rude or bossy. Stick to 「あげる」 for people. So, a giving verb that points downward, best saved for animals and plants." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb builds lots of handy words. Add 「気|き」, spirit or energy, and you get 「やる気|やるき」, motivation, the spirit of doing. 「やる気がない|やるきがない」 means I have no motivation, a phrase every student uses on Monday morning. Add 「方|かた」, way, and 「やり方|やりかた」 is the way of doing something. 「やり方を教えてください|やりかたをおしえてください」 means please show me how to do it. Combine it with 「直す|なおす」, to fix, and 「やり直す|やりなおす」 means to redo or start over. Then there is the past form used as a cheer, 「やった」, and its stretched version 「やったー」 when you pass a test. Compare all of these with 「する」. You could not swap 「する」 into most of them. They are fixed expressions, so learn them as chunks. So, one casual verb, a whole toolbox of everyday phrases." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a pirate captain on the deck of a ship. His crew is lazy, lying around in the sun. He stomps his boot, raises his sword, and roars yar, let's do it. Ya, ru. The sailors jump up and get to work, hauling ropes and scrubbing the deck. That pirate cry is your hook for 「やる」, to do, with all its casual, energetic flavor. Pirates are not polite, and neither is this verb, so it reminds you to save it for friends and family, not your boss. Later, the crew finds the treasure chest, and the captain throws his hat in the air shouting 「やった」, we did it. And when the pirates feed their parrot, they toss it a cracker, which is the giving-downward meaning. One loud pirate, one rough, lively verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A casual, energetic way to say to do, like 「宿題をやる|しゅくだいをやる」, best kept for friends rather than formal settings. You will hear 「やった」 as a happy shout meaning I did it.",
      "It is the downward giving verb, for pets and plants, as in 「花に水をやる|はなにみずをやる」. With people, stick to 「あげる」, because this verb can sound rude or bossy.",
      "It builds fixed chunks like 「やる気|やるき」, motivation, 「やり方|やりかた」, the way of doing something, and 「やり直す|やりなおす」, to redo, none of which work with 「する」.",
      "Picture a pirate captain roaring yar, let's do it, ya, ru, at his lazy crew. Pirates are not polite, and neither is this verb, so save it for friends and family."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}とゲームを[やります|やります|やる]。",
    en: "I play games with my friend." },
  "でかける|出かける": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first kanji, 「出|で」, means to go out or exit. Here it uses its native reading. You know it from 「出す|だす」, to take something out, and 「せきが出る|せきがでる」, a cough comes out. With its Chinese-origin reading it appears in 「出身|しゅっしん」, coming from a place, where you came out from. The rest, 「かける」, adds a sense of setting off. So the whole word means to step out and set off somewhere. This is a ru-verb, so it becomes 「出かけます|でかけます」 and 「出かけて|でかけて」. Here is how it differs from 「行く|いく」, to go. With 「行く|いく」, you usually say where you are going. With 「出かける|でかける」, the focus is on leaving the house. You do not need a destination. A very common sentence is 「母は今出かけています|はははいまでかけています」, my mother is out right now. On the phone, you will hear this often when someone is not home. To recap: exit plus set off, to go out, with the focus on leaving home." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Watch how this verb behaves in the te-iru form. 「出かけています|でかけています」 does not mean someone is in the middle of leaving. It means they went out and are still out. The action happened, and the result continues. Compare 「家に帰っています|いえにかえっています」, which likewise means someone has gone home and is there now. This is a key pattern from Genki. It also pairs nicely with purpose. 「買い物に出かける|かいものにでかける」 means to go out shopping, with 「に」 marking the purpose. And it differs from its cousin 「出る|でる」, which means to exit a specific place, like 「部屋を出る|へやをでる」, leave the room. That one takes 「を」 for the place you leave. Our word is about the whole outing, not stepping through a door. So remember: going out for an outing, and in te-iru form, someone who is still away." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Leaving the house in Japan comes with set phrases. As you head out the door, you say 「行ってきます|いってきます」, I will go and come back. The person staying home answers 「行ってらっしゃい|いってらっしゃい」, go and come back safely. These are said every single morning in Japanese homes. The noun 「お出かけ|おでかけ」 means an outing, and families talk about weekend outings with it. A mother might tell her child 「お出かけするよ|おでかけするよ」, we're going out. You will also see the phrase 「出かける前に|でかけるまえに」, before going out, as in check the gas before going out. On the phone, if the person you want is out, you may hear 「ただいま出かけております|ただいまでかけております」, a very polite version used in offices. So this verb lives in the doorway, along with the little greetings Japanese people trade every time they leave." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built from English sounds. Break the word into deck, car, keys. Picture yourself on a Sunday morning, stepping out onto your back deck in your pajamas. You are holding your car keys, jingling them, ready to go out. But your neighbor on the next deck is also holding car keys, and you both shout at the same time, I'm going out. Deck, car, keys, 「でかける」. Now link it to the kanji. The first kanji, 「出|で」, looks a bit like a little mountain shape with two levels, and you can imagine climbing up and out of the house over it. That is just a hook, not the real origin. Every time you leave, jingle those imaginary keys and hear the word. So, deck, car, keys, and out you go." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The first kanji 「出|で」 means to exit, and 「かける」 adds setting off. Unlike 「行く|いく」, the focus is on leaving home, so no destination is needed, as in 「母は今出かけています|はははいまでかけています」.",
      "In te-iru form, 「出かけています|でかけています」 means someone went out and is still out, not that they are leaving. Compare 「出る|でる」, which means exiting a specific place with 「を」.",
      "Leaving home comes with set phrases: you say 「行ってきます|いってきます」 at the door and hear 「行ってらっしゃい|いってらっしゃい」 back. The noun 「お出かけ|おでかけ」 means an outing.",
      "Break the sound into deck, car, keys: picture yourself on your back deck jingling car keys, ready to go out, 「でかける」."
    ],
    ja: "{日曜日|にちようび}に[出かけました|でかけました|でかける]。",
    en: "I went out on Sunday." },
  "いっしょに|一緒に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, and then the particle 「に」 making it an adverb. The first is 「一|いち」, one. Here it shrinks to 「いっ」, with a small pause, before the next sound. You know it from 「一人|ひとり」, one person, 「一番|いちばん」, number one, and 「一日|いちにち」, one day. The second kanji, 「緒|しょ」, has the thread mark on the left and means a cord or string. A way to remember it: everyone tied together on one string. That is a hook, but the pieces really are one and cord. So doing something together is like being on the same thread. The most useful pattern is inviting someone. 「一緒に行きませんか|いっしょにいきませんか」 means won't you go with me. Asking with a negative like this sounds softer and friendlier than a direct question. You can also say who you are with by adding 「と」: 「友だちと一緒に|ともだちといっしょに」, together with a friend. To recap: one plus cord, tied together, doing things together." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond inviting someone, this word shows up in some useful set phrases. Without 「に」, 「一緒|いっしょ」 can mean the same. 「私も一緒です|わたしもいっしょです」 means me too, I am the same, for example when a friend says they are from Ohio and so are you. With 「する」, 「一緒にする|いっしょにする」 means to lump things together, and 「一緒にしないで|いっしょにしないで」 means do not lump me in with them. At a shop checkout, the clerk may ask 「ご一緒でよろしいですか|ごいっしょでよろしいですか」, is it okay to put these together, meaning in one bag or on one bill. At a restaurant, staff might ask if you are paying together. Here is a mini dialogue. 「一緒に昼ご飯を食べない|いっしょにひるごはんをたべない」, want to have lunch together. 「いいね、食べよう|いいね、たべよう」, sounds good, let's eat. So, together, the same, and one bill, all from this one word." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word has a small 「っ」, a tiny pause, after the first sound. Say it with a beat of silence: 「いっ」, pause, 「しょ」. If you drop the pause, it starts to sound like a different word. Now meet its tricky neighbor, 「一生|いっしょう」, a whole lifetime. The only difference is the long vowel at the end. So 「一緒|いっしょ」 is together, and 「一生|いっしょう」 is for your entire life. Both even start with the same kanji for one. You will hear the second in the famous phrase 「一生懸命|いっしょうけんめい」, with all your might, as in study as hard as you can. Mix them up and a friendly invitation could sound oddly dramatic. Practice by clapping: 「い、っ、しょ」 has three beats, while 「い、っ、しょ、う」 has four. So, three beats for together, four for a lifetime." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Picture two kids on a playground who refuse to let go of each other. They are holding hands, running everywhere as a pair. Their teacher yells, each shall go home separately. The kids shout back, no, we go, each shall, together. Each shall, 「いっしょ」. Now think about the kanji, which really is one plus cord. Imagine the teacher sighing and tying a single string around their wrists, so wherever one goes, the other must follow. They go down the slide together, eat lunch together, even sneeze together. When the bell rings, they walk home side by side, still tied by one thread. So whenever you hear 「一緒に|いっしょに」, see the two stubborn kids tied to one string, doing everything as a pair." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji are one and cord, so doing things together is like being tied on one thread. The key pattern is inviting someone softly with 「一緒に行きませんか|いっしょにいきませんか」.",
      "Without 「に」, 「一緒|いっしょ」 can mean the same, as in 「私も一緒です|わたしもいっしょです」, me too. At a checkout, 「ご一緒でよろしいですか|ごいっしょでよろしいですか」 asks about putting things together.",
      "Keep the small pause in 「いっしょ」, and do not stretch the end, or it becomes 「一生|いっしょう」, a lifetime. Three beats for together, four for a lifetime.",
      "Picture two stubborn kids shouting each shall, 「いっしょ」, while a teacher ties one string around their wrists, the real kanji pieces of one plus cord."
    ],
    ja: "[一緒に|いっしょに]{帰りましょう|かえりましょう}。",
    en: "Let's go home together." },
  "すごく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It comes from the adjective 「すごい」, amazing or incredible, which you will meet later. When an i-adjective changes its final 「い」 to 「く」, it becomes an adverb that can describe other words. So 「すごい」 becomes 「すごく」, and it means extremely or really. 「すごくおいしい」 means really delicious, and 「すごく難しい|すごくむずかしい」 means extremely difficult. It is casual and very common in conversation. In more formal speech or writing, 「とても」 is a safer choice for very. Here is something you will hear a lot. Young people often say 「すごいおいしい」, using the adjective form directly. That is everyday slang, but the standard form is 「すごく」. A fun fact. The adjective 「すごい」 originally meant eerie or dreadful, something that gives you chills. Over time it shifted to mean amazing, a bit like how English awesome went from fearsome to great. To recap: 「すごい」 turned into an adverb with 「く」, extremely, casual and common." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a whole set of very words, and each has its own feel. 「とても」 is the safe, neutral one, fine in class and in writing. This word is more casual and emotional, the one you use with friends when something really impresses you. 「本当に|ほんとうに」 means truly or really, and it works with feelings too, like really thank you. 「大変|たいへん」 can mean very in formal speech, and also means tough or awful on its own. Then there are two that need a negative: 「あまり」, not very, and 「全然|ぜんぜん」, not at all. 「あまり高くない|あまりたかくない」 is not very expensive, and 「全然高くない|ぜんぜんたかくない」 is not expensive at all. A good rule is to match the word to the setting. Write 「とても」 in a report, but tell your friend the ramen was amazing with this word. So, many ways to say very, with this one as the lively, casual choice." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word teaches a pattern you can use with every i-adjective. Drop the final 「い」 and add 「く」, and you get an adverb. 「早い|はやい」, early, becomes 「早く|はやく」, as in get up early. 「遅い|おそい」, late, becomes 「遅く|おそく」. The one odd case is 「いい」, good, which turns into 「よく」, because its older form was 「よい」. That gives you 「よくできました|よくできました」, well done. For na-adjectives, you use 「に」 instead, so 「上手|じょうず」 becomes 「上手に|じょうずに」, skillfully. This word can describe verbs too, not only adjectives. 「すごく疲れた|すごくつかれた」 means I'm really tired, and 「すごく笑った|すごくわらった」 means I laughed a lot. So it is not just a fixed word. It is a living example of a rule you can apply anywhere: swap the 「い」 for 「く」, and an adjective becomes an adverb." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The adjective behind this word is one of the most common reactions in Japanese conversation. When a friend shows you a great photo, you say 「すごい」, wow, amazing. Add 「ね」 and it becomes friendly agreement, 「すごいね」. Young people often clip it into a short burst, 「すごっ」, like a sharp gasp. It can also be sarcastic, just like amazing in English, so tone matters. In polite speech, praise to someone older usually sounds better as something like 「すばらしいですね」, wonderful. Here is a mini dialogue. 「このケーキ、私が作ったの|このケーキ、わたしがつくったの」, I made this cake. 「え、すごい。すごくおいしい」, what, amazing, it's really delicious. Notice the two forms right next to each other: the adjective as a reaction, then the adverb describing how delicious. So, one root, two jobs: a wow on its own, and a really in front of other words." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「すごい」, amazing, with the final 「い」 changed to 「く」 to make an adverb meaning extremely, as in 「すごくおいしい」. It is casual, so 「とても」 is safer in formal speech.",
      "Among the very words, 「とても」 is the safe neutral one, while this is the casual, emotional choice for friends. Note that 「あまり」 and 「全然|ぜんぜん」 need a negative.",
      "It shows a rule for every i-adjective: swap 「い」 for 「く」 to get an adverb, like 「早く|はやく」, with 「いい」 becoming 「よく」. It can describe verbs too, as in 「すごく疲れた|すごくつかれた」.",
      "The root 「すごい」 works alone as a wow reaction, while this form says really in front of other words, as in 「すごい。すごくおいしい」."
    ],
    ja: "このケーキは[すごく]おいしいです。",
    en: "This cake is extremely delicious." },
  "だいじょうぶ|大丈夫": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. 「大|だい」 means big, the person stretching out wide, as in 「大学|だいがく」, college. 「丈|じょう」 is an old measure of length, linked to a tall height. 「夫|ぶ」 means man. With its native reading 「おっと」, it means husband. The pair 「丈夫|じょうぶ」 on its own means sturdy, strong, or healthy. In Chinese, these three characters were used for a great, brave man, someone strong you could rely on. In Japanese it came to mean safe, sound, nothing to worry about. Like a strong man saying, I can handle it. Now the tricky part. 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」 can mean I am fine. It can also be a polite no thanks. If a shop clerk offers a bag and you say 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」, you mean you do not need one. Use tone and context to tell which. To recap: big, strong man, sturdy and safe, it's okay, and sometimes no thanks." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word answers many everyday questions. If someone trips on the stairs, people ask 「大丈夫ですか|だいじょうぶですか」, are you okay. The usual answer is 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」, or casually 「大丈夫、大丈夫|だいじょうぶ、だいじょうぶ」, said twice to wave off worry. It also asks whether something is all right to do. 「この水、飲んでも大丈夫|このみず、のんでもだいじょうぶ」 means is it okay to drink this water. And it works for schedules. 「明日の三時、大丈夫ですか|あしたのさんじ、だいじょうぶですか」 means does three tomorrow work for you. If you are not okay, say 「大丈夫じゃない|だいじょうぶじゃない」, I'm not all right. Because it is a na-adjective, it can even sit before nouns, so you might hear about an okay plan or a safe place. So, one word for are you hurt, is this allowed, and does this time work." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese often turns down offers indirectly, and this word is the modern favorite. There are three common ways to say no thanks. 「いいです」 is short and can be confusing, because it might mean yes, that is good. 「結構です|けっこうです」 is formal and quite firm. It can sound a little cold if said flatly. This word is the softest of the three, which is why younger people use it so much. To be clear and kind, add thanks first: 「ありがとうございます、大丈夫です|ありがとうございます、だいじょうぶです」. Many people also wave a hand slightly in front of their face, a gesture that means no, not for me. Some older people find this use of the word a bit strange, since originally it meant safe or sturdy, not no. But it has become standard in shops everywhere. So when declining, a thank you plus this word is gentle and clear." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a sound-alike memory trick. Break the word into die, Joe, boo. Picture your friend Joe, a big, tough guy, skateboarding down a steep hill. He wipes out spectacularly and lands in a pile of leaves. Everyone gasps and asks if he is hurt. Joe pops up, brushes himself off, and shouts, I didn't die, Joe's fine, boo to you all. Die, Joe, boo, 「だいじょうぶ」. That is the whole word: a big, sturdy guy who bounces back and says he is fine. It even fits the real kanji meaning, a great strong man. Next time someone asks you 「大丈夫ですか|だいじょうぶですか」, picture Joe in the leaves, grinning and waving. So, die, Joe, boo, and nobody needs to worry." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Big plus 「丈夫|じょうぶ」, sturdy, originally described a great strong man you could rely on, so it means safe and fine. It can also be a polite no thanks, like declining a bag at a shop.",
      "It covers are you okay with 「大丈夫ですか|だいじょうぶですか」, is this all right to do, and does this time work, as in 「明日の三時、大丈夫ですか|あしたのさんじ、だいじょうぶですか」.",
      "It is the softest way to decline, gentler than 「いいです」 or the firm 「結構です|けっこうです」. Add thanks first for a clear, kind no.",
      "Break it into die, Joe, boo: big tough Joe wipes out on his skateboard, pops up and shouts I didn't die, matching the kanji meaning of a great strong man."
    ],
    ja: "[大丈夫|だいじょうぶ]ですか。",
    en: "Are you okay?" },
  "とても|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It means very, and it is the standard, polite choice. It goes in front of adjectives: 「とてもおいしい」, very delicious, 「とても静か|とてもしずか」, very quiet. It works well in both writing and polite speech. In casual talk, people often swap in 「すごく」. Here is something useful. With a negative, it has a special meaning, cannot possibly. 「とても食べられない|とてもたべられない」 means I cannot possibly eat it all. But for not very, Japanese uses a different word. You say 「あまり」 with a negative. So not very interesting is 「あまり面白くない|あまりおもしろくない」, not 「とても」 with a negative. That is a common mistake for English speakers who just translate very word for word. A way to remember it: think of a toe that is very, very sore, toe-te-mo. That is just a sound-alike hook. To recap: the standard very, with 「あまり」 for not very." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has a surprising history. For centuries it was used mainly with negatives, meaning no matter how hard you try, it just won't happen. That is the sense still alive in 「とても無理|とてもむり」, absolutely impossible, and 「とても間に合わない|とてもまにあわない」, there is no way I will make it in time. Then, in the early twentieth century, people in Tokyo began using it in positive sentences to mean very. Some writers of the time complained that this was sloppy, new-fashioned Japanese. Today the positive very is completely standard, and it is the safe, textbook choice. So the word you use for very delicious once belonged only to sentences about hopeless situations. Language drifts, and yesterday's slang becomes tomorrow's textbook word. Remember both sides: very in positive sentences, and there's no way in negative ones." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word loves adjectives, but it is picky about verbs. 「とても寒い|とてもさむい」, very cold, and 「とても好き|とてもすき」, like very much, both sound natural, because 「好き|すき」 is a na-adjective. Feeling verbs also work, like 「とても困っています|とてもこまっています」, I'm really in trouble, or the thank-you phrase 「とても助かりました|とてもたすかりました」, you really helped me out. But with action verbs, English speakers often go wrong. If you want to say I study a lot, do not put this word in front of the verb. Use 「たくさん」 for a large amount, as in 「たくさん勉強する|たくさんべんきょうする」, or 「よく」 for often, as in 「よく食べる|よくたべる」. A quick test: if English says very much after an action, Japanese usually wants 「たくさん」 or 「よく」. So this word is for how strong a quality or feeling is, not how much you do something." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is the very of polite messages, letters, and speeches. In a thank-you note to a host family, you might write 「とても楽しかったです|とてもたのしかったです」, it was really fun. Or to a teacher, 「とても勉強になりました|とてもべんきょうになりました」, I learned a great deal. These phrases are used again and again in Japanese thank-you notes, and teachers love seeing them. At a job interview or in front of a class, this word keeps your tone polished. With friends, it can sound a little stiff, so you will hear a friend gush about a movie with 「すごく」 or 「めっちゃ」, which is Kansai-flavored slang. A good habit: use this word whenever you write anything polite, and switch to 「すごく」 when you are chatting. So, the safe and polished very, perfect for cards, emails, and speeches." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the standard, polite very for writing and polite speech. For not very, use 「あまり」 with a negative, as in 「あまり面白くない|あまりおもしろくない」, not this word.",
      "For centuries it went only with negatives, meaning no way, as still heard in 「とても無理|とてもむり」. The positive very began in early twentieth-century Tokyo and is now completely standard.",
      "It goes with adjectives and feeling verbs like 「とても助かりました|とてもたすかりました」, but not action verbs. For doing a lot, use 「たくさん」 or 「よく」 instead.",
      "It is the very of polite thank-you notes, like 「とても楽しかったです|とてもたのしかったです」. With friends, switch to 「すごく」, because this word can sound a little stiff."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[とても]{暑い|あつい}です。",
    en: "It's very hot today." },
  "どんな|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in kana. It belongs to a family of words built on four starting sounds: 「こ」 for near me, 「そ」 for near you, 「あ」 for over there, and 「ど」 for the question. You already know the family from 「これ」, 「それ」, 「あれ」, 「どれ」, this, that, that over there, which one. In the same way, 「こんな」 is this kind of, 「そんな」 is that kind of, 「あんな」 is that kind of over there, and 「どんな」 is what kind of. It always comes right before a noun. 「どんな食べ物|どんなたべもの」, what kind of food. 「どんな人|どんなひと」, what kind of person. To answer, you usually describe with an adjective: 「やさしい人です|やさしいひとです」, a kind person. A common mistake is using 「なに」, what, when you really mean what kind. 「なに」 asks for the thing itself. 「どんな」 asks what it is like. To recap: the 「ど」 question member of the 「こそあど」 family, what kind of, always before a noun." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Three question words can look alike in English, but they do different jobs. This word asks about the type or nature of something: what kind of. 「どの」 asks you to pick one from a group: which. So 「どの本|どのほん」 is which book, out of these on the table, while 「どんな本|どんなほん」 is what kind of book, like mystery or romance. A third, 「どういう」, is close to this word but sounds a little more precise, asking what sort, or what exactly. There is also a handy pattern with 「でも」. 「どんな人でも|どんなひとでも」 means any kind of person at all, and 「どんな時でも|どんなときでも」 means at any time, no matter what. That pattern shows up in songs and in promises. So, pick a book with 「どの」, ask about its type with this word, and add 「でも」 to say any kind at all." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is perfect for getting to know people. Picture someone telling you about their new boyfriend. You ask 「どんな人|どんなひと」, what is he like. Your friend answers 「やさしくて、面白い人|やさしくて、おもしろいひと」, a kind and funny person. Notice the te-form joining two adjectives, which you learn in Genki. They might add 「めがねをかけている」, he wears glasses, using the te-iru form for appearance. The phrase 「どんな感じ|どんなかんじ」 is also very common. It means what is it like, or how is it going. You can ask about a new job, a new city, or a dish you have never tried. At a restaurant, you might ask the server about a menu item with 「どんな料理ですか|どんなりょうりですか」, what kind of dish is it. So, whenever you want a description rather than a name, this is the word to reach for." },
      { title: "That kind of thing", text: "The sister words 「こんな」, 「そんな」, and 「あんな」 appear in very common phrases, and knowing them helps this word stick. The most useful one is 「そんなことない」, that is not true, which is the standard modest reply to a compliment. If someone says your Japanese is great, you answer 「そんなことないです」, oh, not at all. Another is 「こんなに」, this much or to this degree, as in 「こんなに寒いとは思わなかった|こんなにさむいとはおもわなかった」, I did not think it would be this cold. And 「あんな人|あんなひと」, a person like that, can sound a little critical, as if pointing at someone you disapprove of. So the whole family is not just neutral pointing words. They often carry a feeling. Learn 「そんなことない」 first, because you will need it the moment people praise your Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the 「ど」 question member of the 「こそあど」 family, alongside 「こんな」 and 「そんな」, and it always comes before a noun. Unlike 「なに」, it asks what something is like.",
      "Use 「どの」 to pick one, which book, and this word to ask about type, what kind of book. Add 「でも」 for any kind at all, as in 「どんな人でも|どんなひとでも」.",
      "It asks for a description rather than a name, as in 「どんな人|どんなひと」, what is he like. The phrase 「どんな感じ|どんなかんじ」 asks what something is like or how it is going.",
      "Its sister words carry feeling, and the one to learn first is 「そんなことない」, the modest reply when someone praises your Japanese."
    ],
    ja: "[どんな]{音楽|おんがく}が{好き|すき}ですか。",
    en: "What kind of music do you like?" },
  "〜まい|〜枚": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a counter, a word you attach to numbers when counting flat, thin things. Paper, tickets, photos, shirts, plates, stamps, slices of bread, all of these are counted with it. The kanji has 「木|き」, tree, on the left, so think of thin sheets cut from wood. That is a hook to remember it. It uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「まい」. The good news is that it is very regular. One sheet is 「一枚|いちまい」, two is 「二枚|にまい」, three is 「三枚|さんまい」. No tricky sound changes. To ask how many, say 「何枚|なんまい」. In Japanese, the counter usually comes after the thing and its particle: 「写真を三枚撮りました|しゃしんをさんまいとりました」, I took three photos. You will use this with 「写真|しゃしん」, photos, 「手紙|てがみ」, letters, and 「切符|きっぷ」, train tickets. English speakers often forget counters and just say the number. Japanese almost always needs one. To recap: tree on the left, thin sheets, the counter for flat things." },
      { title: "Counter family", text: "Japanese counters sort things by shape, and this one is for flat things. Its neighbors handle other shapes. Long, thin things like pencils, bottles, and umbrellas take 「本|ほん」, so 「ペン一本|ペンいっぽん」 is one pen. Bound things like books and magazines take 「冊|さつ」. Machines and cars take 「台|だい」. Small animals take 「匹|ひき」, and people take 「人|にん」. When in doubt, the native counters 「ひとつ」, 「ふたつ」, 「みっつ」 work for many everyday objects. Notice how the shape decides. A sheet of paper is flat, so this counter. A notebook made of paper is bound, so 「冊|さつ」. A rolled-up poster could even be counted as long and thin. The good news is that this counter has no sound changes, unlike 「本|ほん」, which turns into 「いっぽん」 and 「さんぼん」. So, flat things get this easy, regular counter, and shapes decide the rest." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will use this counter every time you buy tickets, stamps, or paper. At a post office, you might say 「この切手を三枚ください|このきってをさんまいください」, three of these stamps, please. At a ticket window, 「大人二枚|おとなにまい」 means two adult tickets. Clothing that lies flat, like shirts and T-shirts, is counted this way too, and so are plates, CDs, and slices of pizza or toast. Photos are flat, so when a friend asks you to take their picture, they might say 「もう一枚お願いします|もういちまいおねがいします」, one more, please. That phrase is extremely common at tourist spots. Here is a mini dialogue at a convenience store copier. 「何枚コピーしますか|なんまいコピーしますか」, how many copies. 「十枚です|じゅうまいです」, ten. So, whenever something is thin and flat, from a ticket to a slice of bread, reach for this counter." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji has two parts. On the left is 「木|き」, tree, the wood radical, which you see in many kanji for trees and wooden things. On the right is a component often called the striking hand, a hand holding a stick. You have already met that right side in other kanji, such as 「教|きょう」, teach, in 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, and in 「数|すう」, number. So when you see that right side again, recognise it as an old friend rather than a new shape. This kanji is almost always read with its Chinese-origin reading, 「まい」, and you will rarely meet any other reading at your level. That makes it one of the friendliest kanji to learn. Practice by writing 「一枚|いちまい」, 「二枚|にまい」, and 「何枚|なんまい」 a few times. So, a tree on the left, a hand with a stick on the right, read simply as 「まい」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This counter for flat, thin things has 「木|き」, tree, on the left, so think of sheets cut from wood. It is regular, with no sound changes, so you say 「一枚|いちまい」 and 「三枚|さんまい」.",
      "Counters sort by shape: flat things take this one, long things take 「本|ほん」, and bound things take 「冊|さつ」. Unlike 「本|ほん」, this counter has no sound changes.",
      "Use it for tickets, stamps, shirts, plates and photos, as in 「大人二枚|おとなにまい」, two adult tickets. At tourist spots, 「もう一枚お願いします|もういちまいおねがいします」 asks for one more photo.",
      "The kanji is a tree on the left and a hand holding a stick on the right, the same side you know from 「教|きょう」, and it is read simply as 「まい」."
    ],
    ja: "{写真|しゃしん}を[二枚|にまい|〜まい]{撮りました|とりました}。",
    en: "I took two pictures." },
  "やすみ|休み": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is one of the best pictures in Japanese. On the left is the person mark, and on the right is 「木|き」, tree. A person leaning against a tree, taking a break. Rest. It uses its native reading, 「やす」. This noun comes from the verb 「休む|やすむ」, to rest, or to be absent from school or work. So the word covers several ideas: a holiday, a day off, a break, and an absence. 「明日は休みです|あしたはやすみです」 means tomorrow is a day off. And if a classmate is absent, you might hear that they are off today, using this same word. You can attach it to seasons: 「夏休み|なつやすみ」, summer vacation, and 「冬休み|ふゆやすみ」, winter vacation. At lunch, workers and students have a 「昼休み|ひるやすみ」, the lunch break. A cultural note. Japan has many national holidays, and a cluster of them in spring, called Golden Week, when the whole country travels. To recap: a person resting against a tree, a break, a day off, or a holiday." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This noun is made from the verb 「休む|やすむ」 by taking its stem, the part before 「ます」. 「休みます|やすみます」 becomes 「休み|やすみ」. Japanese makes many nouns this way. 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, to play, gives 「遊び|あそび」, play or fun. 「帰る|かえる」, to return, gives 「帰り|かえり」, the way home. The verb itself has a useful pattern. To skip school or work, you use 「を」 for the place you are absent from: 「学校を休む|がっこうをやすむ」, to miss school. And to rest, you might hear 「ゆっくり休んでください|ゆっくりやすんでください」, please get some good rest, which people say to someone who is sick or tired. The te-form is 「休んで|やすんで」, because verbs ending in 「む」 change to 「んで」. So, the verb gives you rest and absence, and its stem gives you the noun for a break or a holiday." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「きゅう」, used in more formal words. A holiday or day off on a calendar is a 「休日|きゅうじつ」. A short break during work or a long drive is a 「休憩|きゅうけい」, and you will see that word on signs at highway rest stops. A run of holidays in a row is a 「連休|れんきゅう」, connected rests, which is what people call long weekends and Golden Week. A shop that is closed on a regular day each week posts 「定休日|ていきゅうび」, regular closing day, on its door. Even a leave of absence from school, 「休学|きゅうがく」, uses the same kanji. So the native word is the everyday break or day off, and the 「きゅう」 words are the official, calendar versions. Spot the kanji, and you can guess the meaning of each sign: some kind of rest." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Every night in Japan, families say 「お休みなさい|おやすみなさい」, good night, literally please rest. Between friends it shortens to 「おやすみ」. At school, the short break between classes is 「休み時間|やすみじかん」, break time, when students rush to the hallway. If a shop is closed, a handwritten sign might read 「本日休み|ほんじつやすみ」, closed today. Coworkers ask each other 「休みの日は何をしていますか|やすみのひはなにをしていますか」, what do you do on your days off. That is a classic small-talk question. A sick employee might call in and say 「今日は休みます|きょうはやすみます」, I'll take today off. Here is a common mix-up. English speakers sometimes use this word for a long trip abroad, but for a vacation with travel, people also say 「旅行|りょこう」, a trip. So, this word covers good night, break time, closed today, and the day off you've been waiting for." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows a person leaning against a tree, resting. The noun comes from 「休む|やすむ」 and covers a break, day off, holiday or absence, as in 「夏休み|なつやすみ」.",
      "It is the stem of 「休む|やすむ」, the part before 「ます」, a common way to make nouns. The verb uses 「を」 for what you miss, as in 「学校を休む|がっこうをやすむ」.",
      "The formal reading 「きゅう」 gives the calendar words, like 「休日|きゅうじつ」, a holiday, 「休憩|きゅうけい」, a break, and 「連休|れんきゅう」, a run of holidays.",
      "Families say 「お休みなさい|おやすみなさい」, good night, literally please rest, and students get 「休み時間|やすみじかん」 between classes."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[休み|やすみ]です。",
    en: "Tomorrow is a day off." },
  "おみやげ|お土産": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has a special reading. The two kanji are read together as 「みやげ」, with polite 「お」 in front. The first kanji is 「土|ど」, earth or land. You know it from 「土曜日|どようび」, Saturday, the earth day. The second kanji means to produce or give birth. Put them together and you get the products of the land, things that a place is known for. That is exactly what a good souvenir is in Japan, a local specialty. Here is a big cultural note. Bringing back souvenirs is almost an obligation in Japan. When you travel, you bring back a box of local sweets for your coworkers or classmates. Train stations and airports have huge shops full of individually wrapped treats, made just for sharing. Every region has its famous gift, and people love comparing them. The souvenir is less about you, and more about sharing the trip with the people you left behind. To recap: products of the land, local specialties, the gifts you bring home to share." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The two kanji in this word are read together as a single unit, a special reading where the kanji express the meaning and the sound is a native Japanese word. Separately, they would be read 「ど」 and 「さん」, and 「土産|どさん」 was originally a Chinese word for local products. Japanese borrowed the spelling but kept its own word for the sound. Where the native word itself comes from is not settled. One popular theory links it to gifts brought back from shrines, and another says it comes from words about looking at things and choosing them. Scholars still debate it, so treat any story you hear as a guess. What is clear is the long tradition behind it. For centuries, pilgrims who visited famous shrines brought back charms and goods for the people at home, who often helped pay for the trip. So, Chinese spelling, native sound, and a custom with very old roots." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how giving a souvenir actually sounds. You walk into the office after a trip and say 「これ、京都のお土産です。どうぞ|これ、きょうとのおみやげです。どうぞ」, here's a souvenir from Kyoto, please help yourself. Notice 「の」: a souvenir of Kyoto. Coworkers answer 「わあ、ありがとうございます」 and take one sweet each. When someone is heading off on a trip, friends tease them with 「お土産、よろしくね|おみやげ、よろしくね」, don't forget my souvenir. When visiting someone's home, a gift you bring in your hand is called 「手土産|てみやげ」. Older people may present it with the humble phrase 「つまらないものですが」, it's just a small thing. Here is a common mix-up. A birthday present is not this word. It is a 「プレゼント」. This word is for gifts connected to a trip or a visit. So, bring it back, hand it out, and say where it came from." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Every region in Japan has its 「名物|めいぶつ」, its famous product, and souvenir shops are built around them. Hiroshima sells maple-leaf shaped cakes. Kyoto is known for soft cinnamon sweets. Hokkaido is famous for chocolate and dairy treats. Many products are labeled 「限定|げんてい」, limited, meaning you can only buy them in that region, which makes them perfect proof you really went there. Even global brands join in, with local flavors of snacks sold only in certain prefectures. The gift often matters less than the gesture. If you take time off work, your coworkers covered for you, and a box of sweets is a small thank-you. That is why people sometimes feel pressure to buy enough for everyone. And there is a matching custom of returning the kindness, called 「お返し|おかえし」. So, regional specialties, limited editions, and a quiet thank-you to the people who stayed behind." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The two kanji, read together as 「みやげ」, mean products of the land, a local specialty. Bringing back sweets to share with coworkers is almost an obligation in Japan.",
      "It is a special reading: the Chinese spelling for local products, with a native Japanese sound whose origin scholars still debate. Pilgrims bringing back goods from shrines show how old the custom is.",
      "Hand it out with 「これ、京都のお土産です|これ、きょうとのおみやげです」, using 「の」 for where it came from. A birthday present is a 「プレゼント」, not this word.",
      "Shops sell each region's 「名物|めいぶつ」, often 「限定|げんてい」, limited to that area, and the box of sweets is a quiet thank-you to coworkers who covered for you."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}で[お土産|おみやげ]を{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought souvenirs in Kyoto." },
  "いそがしい|忙しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the left side of the kanji. It is the narrow heart mark, used for feeling words. You will see it on 「怖い|こわい」, frightening, which you are learning in this lesson too. On the right is a kanji that means to lose or to perish. It is commonly remembered as losing your heart, being so rushed that you have no room for your feelings. Treat that as a hook. The word uses its native reading, 「いそが」. It is related to the verb 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry. You can hear the shared sound at the start. Busy and hurrying go together. It is an i-adjective. The past is 「忙しかった|いそがしかった」, and the negative is 「忙しくない|いそがしくない」. Its opposite is 「暇|ひま」, free and not busy. Use it for people and for days, like 「忙しい日|いそがしいひ」, a busy day. A cultural note. Saying you are busy is very common in Japan, and 「お忙しいところ|おいそがしいところ」 is a polite phrase used when thanking someone for their time, meaning while you are so busy. To recap: the heart mark plus to lose, busy, related to hurry." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Here is a neat discovery. The kanji for busy, 「忙|いそが」, is built from two parts: the heart on the left, and on the right the part meaning to lose or to perish. Now look at the kanji in 「忘れる|わすれる」, to forget. It uses exactly the same two parts, just stacked differently. The lose part sits on top, and the full heart sits underneath. So busy and forget are made of the same pieces. People love to joke that when you are too busy, you forget things, which is a nice memory hook, though not the real origin. When the heart is on the side, it is squeezed into a narrow shape, the version you see on feeling words. When it is at the bottom, it keeps its full form, 「心|こころ」. The Chinese-origin reading of the busy kanji is 「ぼう」, as in 「多忙|たぼう」, extremely busy. So, same pieces, two words: heart beside means busy, heart below means forget." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adjective is the most common polite excuse in Japanese. To turn down an invitation, people use the te-form to give a reason: 「ちょっと忙しくて|ちょっといそがしくて」, I'm a bit busy, and then trail off. That trailing off is softer than saying no directly. The te-form of an i-adjective swaps 「い」 for 「くて」, which is exactly the pattern you learn in Genki. Another useful form is 「忙しそう|いそがしそう」, looks busy, from the lesson on seeming. You might say 「忙しそうですね|いそがしそうですね」 to a coworker buried in paperwork. Here is a mini dialogue. 「最近どう|さいきんどう」, how have you been. 「すごく忙しい|すごくいそがしい」, super busy. 「大変だね|たいへんだね」, that sounds tough. That last reply, showing sympathy, is the natural response. So, a reason to decline, a way to sympathize, and the answer to how's it going." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built from English sounds. Picture a frantic tourist in Kyoto, sprinting from temple to temple with a giant map. His wife begs him to slow down and sit for a cup of tea. He shakes his head, sweating, and shouts, he so gotta see everything. He so gotta see. Say it fast and it sounds like 「いそがしい」. The tourist is so busy that he forgets his hat at one temple, his camera at another, and his wife at a third. That links nicely to the heart-losing shape of the kanji. Imagine him finally collapsing on a bench at sunset, saying 「忙しかった|いそがしかった」, that was busy. So, he so gotta see it all, and he's busy, busy, busy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is the heart mark plus to lose, a hook for being too rushed for feelings. It is related to 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry, and its opposite is 「暇|ひま」.",
      "The busy kanji uses the same two parts as 「忘れる|わすれる」, to forget, just arranged differently: heart beside means busy, heart below means forget.",
      "It is the classic soft excuse, 「ちょっと忙しくて|ちょっといそがしくて」, trailing off instead of saying no. You can also sympathize with 「忙しそうですね|いそがしそうですね」.",
      "Picture a frantic tourist in Kyoto shouting he so gotta see everything, which sounds like 「いそがしい」, so busy he loses things at every temple."
    ],
    ja: "{月曜日|げつようび}はとても[忙しい|いそがしい]です。",
    en: "Mondays are very busy." },
  "あける|開ける": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Start with the kanji 「開|あ」. The outer frame is 「門|もん」, a gate, the two swinging doors of an old gateway. Inside sits a shape usually explained as two hands lifting away the wooden bar that holds the doors shut. Hands lift the bar, the gate swings open. In this verb the kanji takes its native Japanese reading, 「あ」, and the 「ける」 is just written in hiragana. That gate frame is a great family marker. 「閉める|しめる」, to close, is the same gate with a bar stuck inside it. 「聞く|きく」, to listen, is a gate with an ear inside, like an ear pressed to the door. And 「時間|じかん」 has a gate with the sun inside, sunlight slipping through the gap. You open things with 「を」: 「まどをあける」, open the window, 「ドアをあける」, open the door. It is a verb where you do the opening to something. A way to remember it: when you open something, you often go ah, so 「あ」 starts the word. So, a gate with the bar lifted: 「あける」, and its partner with the bar locked in, 「しめる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has a partner, and mixing them up is one of the most common mistakes in Japanese. This one is transitive. Someone opens something, and the thing takes 「を」: 「ドアを開ける|ドアをあける」, I open the door. Its partner, 「開く|あく」, is intransitive. The thing opens by itself, or we do not care who did it, and it takes 「が」: 「ドアが開く|ドアがあく」, the door opens. They even belong to different verb groups. This one is a ru-verb, while the partner is a u-verb, so its polite form is 「開きます|あきます」. The closing pair works the same way: 「閉める|しめる」, I close it, and 「閉まる|しまる」, it closes. On a train, the announcement says the doors will open using the intransitive one, because nobody is personally opening them. A good test: if you can say who did it, use this verb with 「を」. So, someone opens it with 「を」, it opens with 「が」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The gate kanji has more than one reading, and each one opens a different door. In this verb it reads 「あ」. But the same kanji also reads 「ひら」 in 「開く|ひらく」, which means to open something wide, like opening a book or opening up a flower, and also to hold an event, like a party or a meeting. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「かい」, used in formal words. A shop opening for the day, or a brand-new store, is 「開店|かいてん」. The start of an event is 「開始|かいし」, and a movie being released to the public is 「公開|こうかい」. You will see 「開店|かいてん」 and its opposite, 「閉店|へいてん」, closing, on store signs all over Japan. Notice that the closing word uses the gate with a bar inside. So, one gate kanji, three readings: 「あ」 in this everyday verb, 「ひら」 for opening wide or holding events, and 「かい」 on signs." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Japanese has several verbs pronounced 「あける」, each with its own kanji. This one, with the gate, is to open a door, a box, or a window. But on New Year's Day, everyone says 「明けましておめでとうございます|あけましておめでとうございます」. That uses 「明ける|あける」, with the bright kanji, meaning dawn breaks or a new year begins. The night opens into day. There is also 「空ける|あける」, with the sky or empty kanji, meaning to empty something out or to make space, like clearing a seat for someone or keeping your schedule free. Many learners write the New Year greeting with the gate kanji by mistake, so watch out for it on cards. The idea behind all three is opening something up, which is why they share a sound. So, gate for doors, bright for dawn and the new year, empty for clearing space." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a gate, 「門|もん」, with hands lifting away the bar, so the gate swings open. You open things with 「を」, and its partner 「しめる」 has the bar locked inside.",
      "This is the transitive verb, someone opens something with 「を」, while its partner 「開く|あく」 means it opens by itself with 「が」. If you can say who did it, use this verb.",
      "The gate kanji has three readings: 「あ」 in this verb, 「ひら」 in 「開く|ひらく」 for opening wide or holding events, and 「かい」 on signs like 「開店|かいてん」.",
      "Several verbs sound like 「あける」: this gate one for doors, 「明ける|あける」 for dawn and the New Year greeting, and 「空ける|あける」 for clearing space."
    ],
    ja: "{窓|まど}を[開けます|あけます|あける]。",
    en: "I'll open the window." },
  "でんき|電気": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「電|でん」, has the rain kanji on top, and underneath is a shape that stands for a flash of lightning. Rain plus lightning: electricity. The second, 「気|き」, means spirit, energy, or something in the air. You already know it from 「天気|てんき」, weather, and 「元気|げんき」, healthy and energetic. So 「でんき」 is lightning energy, electricity. In everyday speech, though, it very often just means the lights in a room. 「でんきをつけてください」 is please turn on the light, and 「でんきをけしてください」 is please turn it off. The 「電|でん」 part is a family on its own. 「電話|でんわ」 is electric talk, a telephone. 「電車|でんしゃ」 is an electric vehicle, a train. 「電池|でんち」 is an electric pond, a battery. Any time you hear 「でん」 at the start of a modern gadget word, think lightning. So, lightning plus energy, which in daily life means flipping the light switch." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a Japanese home, this word mostly means the lights. You turn them on with 「つける」 and off with 「消す|けす」. English speakers sometimes say open the light, translating from other languages, but Japanese never uses 「開ける|あける」 for lights. A parent might shout 「電気、つけっぱなしだよ|でんき、つけっぱなしだよ」, you left the light on. The ending 「っぱなし」 means left in that state. Your monthly electricity bill is 「電気代|でんきだい」, electricity charge. A big electronics store is often called a 「電気屋|でんきや」, though the giant chains are known by their own names. If the light will not come on, you say 「電気がつかない|でんきがつかない」. Here is a mini dialogue. 「暗いね|くらいね」, it's dark. 「電気つけようか|でんきつけようか」, shall I turn on the light. So, it's the lights, the electric bill, and the store where you buy a new fan." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「気|き」, is one of the busiest in Japanese. It means spirit, air, energy, or mood, and it hides in many words you will meet. 「病気|びょうき」 is sickness, energy gone bad. 「空気|くうき」 is the air, sky energy. 「人気|にんき」 is popularity, people's energy, and 「人気がある|にんきがある」 means something is popular. 「気分|きぶん」 is your mood. And 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care, literally means attach your attention. With 「電気|でんき」, the kanji suggests an invisible force flowing through the wires, the same mysterious energy as in weather or health. In each word, 「気|き」 adds the sense of something you cannot see but can definitely feel. So, lightning energy for electricity, and a whole family of invisible forces: air, mood, sickness, popularity, and care." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a famous electrical quirk. The country runs on two different frequencies. Eastern Japan, including Tokyo, uses fifty hertz, and western Japan, including Osaka, uses sixty. This goes back to the late eighteen hundreds, when Tokyo bought generators from Germany and Osaka bought them from the United States, and the split was never fixed. Older appliances sometimes only worked on one side of the country, and moving house could mean buying a new washing machine. The divide also makes it harder to share power between regions during emergencies. The plugs themselves look almost like American ones, with two flat prongs, and the voltage is about one hundred volts. So, a country of lightning energy split down the middle, a little piece of history hiding behind every light switch." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The first kanji is rain plus lightning, and the second means energy, so it is lightning energy, electricity. In daily life it usually means the lights, as in 「でんきをつけてください」.",
      "Turn the lights on with 「つける」 and off with 「消す|けす」, never 「開ける|あける」. A parent might complain 「電気、つけっぱなしだよ|でんき、つけっぱなしだよ」, you left the light on.",
      "The second kanji 「気|き」 means an invisible energy you can feel, found in 「病気|びょうき」, 「空気|くうき」, 「人気|にんき」 and 「気をつけて|きをつけて」.",
      "Japan runs on two frequencies, fifty hertz in the east and sixty in the west, because Tokyo and Osaka bought generators from different countries in the late eighteen hundreds."
    ],
    ja: "[電気|でんき]をつけてください。",
    en: "Please turn on the light." },
  "まど|窓": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one is a native Japanese word, written with a single kanji and no hiragana tail. Look at the top of the kanji. That roof with two little marks under it is the hole radical, the same top you find on the kanji for hole or cave. And a window, really, is just a hole cut into a wall to let in light and air. At the bottom you will see the heart shape, 「心|こころ」, which you also find in 「心配する|しんぱいする」, to worry. You do not need a story for the whole kanji. Just hole on top is enough to recognise it. You open and close windows with the verbs from this lesson: 「まどをあける」 and 「まどをしめる」. A classic classroom line is 「まどをあけてもいいですか」, may I open the window. A way to remember it: it sounds a bit like mad oh, so picture a mad face peeking through a hole in the wall. So, a hole in the wall that lets the light in: 「まど」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word builds some very useful compounds. A service counter at a bank, post office, or city hall is a 「窓口|まどぐち」, window mouth, the little window where you talk to the clerk. Signs saying which counter handles what are everywhere. On a train or plane, the window seat is 「窓側|まどがわ」, window side, and the aisle seat is 「通路側|つうろがわ」. Glass for a window is 「窓ガラス|まどガラス」. The kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「そう」, and you meet it in a lovely word: 「同窓会|どうそうかい」, a class reunion, literally a gathering of the same window. People who studied together looked out the same school window. So, a window seat, a service counter, and a reunion of old classmates, all built from a hole in the wall." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese office culture has a slightly sad phrase built on this word: 「窓際族|まどぎわぞく」, the window-side tribe. It describes older employees who have been quietly sidelined. Instead of being fired, they are given a desk by the window with little real work, and they spend the day gazing outside. The term became popular in the late nineteen seventies, when lifetime employment made layoffs rare. On a lighter note, Japanese homes usually have sliding windows rather than ones that swing open, and most have a screen called 「網戸|あみど」, a net door, to keep summer insects out. On hot days you will hear people ask 「窓を開けてもいいですか|まどをあけてもいいですか」 before opening one on a train or bus. So, sliding glass, bug screens, and a lonely desk by the window." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The top of the kanji is the hole radical, and a window is just a hole in the wall to let in light. You open and close it with 「まどをあける」 and 「まどをしめる」.",
      "It builds 「窓口|まどぐち」, a service counter, and 「窓側|まどがわ」, a window seat. The reading 「そう」 gives 「同窓会|どうそうかい」, a class reunion, people who looked out the same window.",
      "The phrase 「窓際族|まどぎわぞく」, the window-side tribe, describes sidelined older employees given a desk by the window with little real work."
    ],
    ja: "[窓|まど]を{閉めて|しめて}ください。",
    en: "Please close the window." },
  "すわる|座る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「座|すわ」 is read with its native Japanese reading here. Look at it in layers. Across the top and down the left is a roof with a wall, a building shape. Under that roof is the ground, 「土|つち」, the same piece you see in 「土曜日|どようび」, Saturday. And sitting on that ground are two little people, 「人|ひと」, one on each side. Two people sitting on the ground under a roof: sitting. It is one of the easiest kanji pictures to see once someone points it out. The verb is a regular u-verb, so it becomes 「すわります」 and 「すわって」. In class you will hear 「すわってください」, please sit down, and you mark the seat with 「に」: 「いすにすわる」, sit on a chair. A cultural note: the formal way of kneeling on your heels on a tatami floor is called 「正座|せいざ」, proper sitting, and it uses this very kanji. Its opposite is 「たつ」, to stand. So, two people on the ground under a roof, settled down: 「すわる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「座|ざ」 has a Chinese-origin reading that shows up in some surprising places. A seat on a train or in a theater is a 「座席|ざせき」. The flat floor cushion you sit on in a tatami room is a 「座布団|ざぶとん」, a sitting futon. A constellation is a 「星座|せいざ」, a star seat, and in Japan people often ask your star sign with this word. And here is the oddest one: a bank account is a 「口座|こうざ」. As a memory aid, think of it as your money's own seat in the bank. The kanji always carries the idea of a fixed spot where something sits. So when you see this kanji, think of a place where something settles down, whether it is a person on a cushion, a star in the sky, or your money in the bank." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There are several ways to sit on a Japanese floor. Formal kneeling, which you met already, is hard on the legs. A relaxed cross-legged position is called 「あぐら」, traditionally more common among men. Many women sit with their legs folded to one side. When a host says 「どうぞ、楽にしてください|どうぞ、らくにしてください」, please make yourself comfortable, it is your cue to stop kneeling. After kneeling too long, your feet go numb, and you say 「足がしびれた|あしがしびれた」, my legs fell asleep. For chairs, polite speakers often use another verb, 「かける」. A receptionist will say 「おかけください」, please have a seat, which is more polite than asking you to sit with this verb. So, kneeling, cross-legged, and asking to sit politely: three useful ways to settle down." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The te-iru form of this verb is a great example of a pattern from Genki. 「座っています|すわっています」 does not usually mean someone is in the middle of the motion of sitting down. It means they sat down and are now seated. The action finished, and the result continues. So 「田中さんは座っています|たなかさんはすわっています」 means Mr. Tanaka is sitting, seated in his chair. The same is true of its partner: 「立っています|たっています」 means is standing. Another useful pattern is asking permission. 「ここに座ってもいいですか|ここにすわってもいいですか」 means may I sit here, perfect on a crowded train or in a café. The answer might be 「どうぞ」, go ahead. Because this verb ends in 「る」 but is a u-verb, the negative is 「座らない|すわらない」, not with a dropped 「る」. So, te-iru for being seated, te-mo-ii for asking, and a u-verb hiding behind its 「る」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows two people sitting on the ground under a roof. You mark the seat with 「に」, as in 「いすにすわる」, and the opposite is 「たつ」.",
      "The reading 「ざ」 carries the idea of a fixed spot where something sits, as in 「座席|ざせき」, a seat, 「座布団|ざぶとん」, a floor cushion, and 「星座|せいざ」, a constellation.",
      "Floor sitting ranges from formal kneeling to cross-legged 「あぐら」. For chairs, a polite host says 「おかけください」, please have a seat, rather than this verb.",
      "In te-iru form, 「座っています|すわっています」 means someone is already seated. It is a u-verb despite its 「る」, so the negative is 「座らない|すわらない」."
    ],
    ja: "ここに[座って|すわって|すわる]ください。",
    en: "Please sit here." },
  "たつ|立つ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「立|た」 is a picture of a person standing tall. At the top is the head, the middle strokes are arms spread out, and the long line at the bottom is the ground the person stands on. In this verb it takes its native reading, 「た」. The verb ends in 「つ」, so it conjugates like 「待つ|まつ」, to wait. You get 「たちます」 in polite form and 「たって」 in the te-form. That little doubled sound in 「たって」 is a common stumbling point, so say it out loud a few times. In class you will hear 「たってください」, please stand up, and it pairs naturally with 「すわってください」, please sit down. Japanese school days often begin with a student calling out for everyone to stand, bow, and sit. You will also see this kanji on the left side of other kanji you will meet later, always with that standing person shape. A way to remember it: the kanji even looks like someone standing on a little platform, ready to give a speech. So, a person standing on the ground with arms out: 「たつ」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has a transitive partner, 「立てる|たてる」, which means to stand something up, like an umbrella against a wall. It also has a common figurative use: 「計画を立てる|けいかくをたてる」, to make a plan, as if standing it up. Our verb itself, 「立つ|たつ」, appears in an extremely useful phrase from Genki: 「役に立つ|やくにたつ」, to be useful, literally to stand in a role. 「この辞書はとても役に立ちます|このじしょはとてもやくにたちます」 means this dictionary is really useful. Another pattern is the te-iru form. 「立っている|たっている」 means is standing, a continuing state, not in the act of rising. Watch the 「つ」 ending carefully. It becomes 「ち」 before 「ます」, 「た」 in the negative 「立たない|たたない」, and the doubled sound in 「立って|たって」. So, stand up, stand it up, be useful, and stay standing, all from one picture of a person." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「りつ」, and it is common in formal words. Japanese school days begin with three commands. 「起立|きりつ」, rise and stand. 「礼|れい」, bow. 「着席|ちゃくせき」, take your seat. A student leader calls them out, and the whole class moves together. You also see 「りつ」 in the names of schools and institutions. 「国立|こくりつ」 means national, set up by the state, as in a national university. 「私立|しりつ」 means private, set up by individuals. 「県立|けんりつ」 is prefectural. And there is 「立派|りっぱ」, splendid or impressive, where the reading is shortened before the next sound. So, 「た」 when a person stands, and 「りつ」 when a school is set up, or a class rises to greet the teacher." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Several verbs are pronounced 「たつ」, and you will meet them as your Japanese grows. This one, with the standing person, means to stand. 「経つ|たつ」, with a different kanji, means time passes: 「一年経った|いちねんたった」, a year has passed. 「建つ|たつ」, with the building kanji, means a building stands or gets built. There is even 「発つ|たつ」, to depart on a journey. They all conjugate the same way, so the context is your guide. Now another sound trap. The te-forms of 「立つ|たつ」, 「待つ|まつ」, to wait, and 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, are 「立って|たって」, 「待って|まって」, and 「持って|もって」. They differ only in their first sound. Say them slowly: stand, wait, hold. So, many verbs share this sound, but only this kanji shows a person standing tall." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a person standing tall, head on top, arms out, feet on the ground line. It conjugates like 「待つ|まつ」, so watch the doubled sound in 「たって」.",
      "Its partner 「立てる|たてる」 means to stand something up or make a plan, while this verb gives 「役に立つ|やくにたつ」, to be useful. The 「つ」 ending shifts to 「ち」, 「た」 and 「って」.",
      "The formal reading 「りつ」 appears in the classroom command 「起立|きりつ」, rise, and in school types like 「国立|こくりつ」, national, and 「私立|しりつ」, private.",
      "Other verbs sound like 「たつ」, such as 「経つ|たつ」 for time passing and 「建つ|たつ」 for buildings. Also keep 「立って|たって」, 「待って|まって」 and 「持って|もって」 apart."
    ],
    ja: "みなさん、[立って|たって|たつ]ください。",
    en: "Everyone, please stand up." },
  "にもつ|荷物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with readings worth noticing. The first, 「荷|に」, means a load or cargo, something you carry on your back or on a cart. On top it has the grass radical, and underneath is 「何|なに」, what, which mostly gives the kanji its sound. Think of it as bundles of straw being hauled around. The second kanji, 「物|もつ」, means thing. You know it well from 「食べ物|たべもの」, food, 「飲み物|のみもの」, drink, and 「買い物|かいもの」, shopping, where it is read with the native reading 「もの」. In 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, the zoo, it gets the Chinese-origin reading 「ぶつ」. Here it takes a rarer Chinese-origin reading, 「もつ」. Same thing kanji, new sound. Put them together and you get load things: bags, luggage, the stuff you are lugging around. A kind person might ask 「にもつをもちましょうか」, shall I carry your bags. And notice the lucky coincidence that the verb to carry is 「もつ」, the same sound as the end of this word. So, cargo plus things: the bags you carry, 「にもつ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「荷|に」, means a load, and it builds several travel words. 「手荷物|てにもつ」 is hand luggage, the carry-on bag you take on a plane. 「荷造り|にづくり」 is packing, literally making the load. Before a trip, you might complain that packing is a pain. 「重荷|おもに」 is a heavy load, used figuratively for a burden on your mind. And here is a surprising use. Calling a person 「お荷物|おにもつ」 means they are a burden, someone the team has to carry. That is not something you want to hear about yourself. Compare the word with 「かばん」, a bag. A bag is the container. This word is everything you are lugging around, whatever form it takes, from suitcases to shopping bags to boxes. So, hand luggage, packing, and burdens, all from the kanji for a load." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japan has clever ways to deal with luggage. At stations, you can leave bags in a 「コインロッカー」, a coin locker. At hotels or airports, you 「預ける|あずける」 your bags, which means to leave them in someone's care. 「荷物を預けてもいいですか|にもつをあずけてもいいですか」 means may I leave my bags here. Many travelers also use luggage forwarding, sending suitcases to their next hotel so they can ride trains empty-handed. On crowded trains, there are racks overhead for bags, and putting your backpack on the rack or wearing it on your front is good manners. Here is a mini dialogue. 「荷物、多いですね|にもつ、おおいですね」, you have a lot of bags. 「はい、お土産です|はい、おみやげです」, yes, they're souvenirs. So, lockers, leaving bags, and keeping your load out of other people's way." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick from English sounds. Picture an airport porter who has stacked so much luggage that the pile is balanced on his knee. A traveler runs up with two more suitcases. The porter groans, knee, more, two? Knee, mo, tsu. He wobbles on one leg as the tower of bags sways, then everything crashes down around him. Hear the word in his groan: 「にもつ」. As a bonus, the verb to carry is 「持つ|もつ」, the same sound as the end of the word. So the porter is literally carrying the luggage on his knee. When someone offers 「荷物を持ちましょうか|にもつをもちましょうか」, shall I carry your bags, picture him smiling with relief. So, knee, more, two, and a mountain of luggage." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「荷|に」 means a load and 「物|もつ」 means thing, here with its rare reading, so it is load things, your bags. Conveniently, the verb to carry is also 「もつ」.",
      "The load kanji builds 「手荷物|てにもつ」, hand luggage, and 「荷造り|にづくり」, packing. Unlike 「かばん」, the container, this word is everything you are lugging.",
      "To leave your bags with someone, use 「預ける|あずける」, as in 「荷物を預けてもいいですか|にもつをあずけてもいいですか」. On trains, keep your load on the rack or in front of you.",
      "Picture an airport porter balancing a tower of luggage on his knee, groaning knee, more, two, 「にもつ」, as more suitcases arrive."
    ],
    ja: "[荷物|にもつ]を{持ちましょうか|もちましょうか}。",
    en: "Shall I carry your bags?" },
  "おふろ|お風呂": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The 「お」 at the front is the polite prefix, the same one in 「お金|おかね」 and 「おちゃ」. People almost always say the bath with it. The first kanji, 「風|ふ」, means wind. You know it from 「風邪|かぜ」, a cold, and 「台風|たいふう」, a typhoon. The second kanji, 「呂|ろ」, is mostly there for its sound. So in this word the kanji are best treated as spelling the sound rather than adding up to a meaning. Do not try to find wind in your bath. The cultural side is the real lesson here. A Japanese bath is for soaking, not for washing. You wash and rinse yourself outside the tub first, then climb into very hot, clean water to relax. Because everyone is already clean, the family often shares the same bathwater, one after another, and you never pull the plug when you get out. To take a bath, you enter it: 「おふろにはいる」. Many people bathe at night to unwind, and use a quick shower in the morning. So, polite 「お」 plus two sound-spelling kanji, for a deep, hot soak at the end of the day." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word builds several fun compounds. The room with the tub is the 「風呂場|ふろば」, the bath place, usually separate from the toilet in Japanese homes. An open-air bath at a hot spring is a 「露天風呂|ろてんぶろ」, where the sound softens to 「ぶろ」 after another word. A neighborhood public bathhouse is a 「銭湯|せんとう」, and a natural hot spring is an 「温泉|おんせん」. Then there is the 「風呂敷|ふろしき」, a square cloth for wrapping and carrying things. The usual explanation is that people once wrapped their clothes in such cloths at the bathhouse, or spread them out to sit on while changing. Today it is used for wrapping gifts and lunch boxes. The water itself is 「お湯|おゆ」, hot water, a separate word from cold 「水|みず」. So, bath room, outdoor bath, wrapping cloth, all carrying the sound of this little word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Modern Japanese bathtubs often fill and heat themselves at the touch of a button. When the bath is ready, a cheerful recorded voice announces 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」, the bath has heated up. Millions of people hear that every evening. Families take turns in the same water, and the very first turn, in clean fresh water, is called 「一番風呂|いちばんぶろ」, the number one bath. It was traditionally given to the head of the household or to a guest, as an honor. If you are a guest, your host might say 「お先にどうぞ|おさきにどうぞ」, please go first. Here is a mini dialogue. 「お風呂、どうぞ|おふろ、どうぞ」, the bath is ready for you. 「ありがとうございます。お先に入ります|ありがとうございます。おさきにはいります」, thank you, I'll go first. So, a talking bathtub, a first-bath honor, and a polite thank you." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Where does the word itself come from? Nobody knows for certain, and you should treat every explanation as a theory. One common idea links it to an old word, 「むろ」, a small enclosed room, because early Japanese baths were often steam baths in closed rooms rather than tubs of hot water. Steam bathing was common at Buddhist temples, which offered baths to the public as an act of charity. Over the centuries, soaking in hot water became the norm, especially once public bathhouses spread in cities like Edo, old Tokyo. The kanji were chosen later, mostly for their sounds, which is why you cannot find a sensible meaning in wind plus spine. So the history is a story of steam rooms turning into hot tubs, while the name stayed fuzzy. Enjoy the bath, and let scholars argue about the name." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji just spell the sound, so do not look for wind in your bath. The real lesson is that you wash outside the tub and then soak, so you enter it with 「おふろにはいる」.",
      "It builds 「風呂場|ふろば」, the bath room, and 「露天風呂|ろてんぶろ」, an open-air bath, where the sound softens to 「ぶろ」. It also gives 「風呂敷|ふろしき」, a wrapping cloth.",
      "Many tubs announce 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」 when the bath is hot, and the first soak in fresh water, 「一番風呂|いちばんぶろ」, traditionally goes to the household head or a guest.",
      "Nobody knows the origin for certain, but one theory links it to 「むろ」, a small enclosed room, since early baths were often steam rooms. The kanji were chosen later for sound."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[お風呂|おふろ]に{入ります|はいります}。",
    en: "I take a bath every night." },
  "シャワー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes straight from the English word shower. Watch how the sounds were squeezed into Japanese. The sh sound becomes 「シャ」, using a small 「ャ」 to make the blended sound. The ow in the middle turns into 「ワ」. And the er at the end, which Japanese does not have, simply becomes a long vowel, that dash 「ー」. You see the same long dash ending in 「コンピューター」, computer, and 「スーパー」, supermarket. English er endings very often turn into a stretched vowel like this. The meaning has not shifted at all, it is just the shower. The tricky part is the verb. In English you take a shower, but in Japanese you say 「シャワーをあびる」, which means to be poured over by a shower. Do not translate take with this word. A common daily rhythm in Japan is a quick 「シャワー」 in the morning and a proper 「おふろ」 at night. So, English shower with the er turned into a long vowel: 「シャワー」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb that goes with this word, 「浴びる|あびる」, deserves its own spotlight. It means to have something poured over you, or to bathe in it. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「浴びます|あびます」 and 「浴びて|あびて」. A Genki pattern fits perfectly here: 「シャワーを浴びてから、寝ます|シャワーをあびてから、ねます」, after taking a shower, I go to bed. That 「てから」 means after doing. You can use the same verb for other things washing over you, like 「日光を浴びる|にっこうをあびる」, to soak up sunlight. In casual speech, some people simply say 「シャワーする」, using 「する」, and you will hear it, but 「浴びる|あびる」 is the standard choice. Be careful not to say you enter a shower with 「入る|はいる」, which belongs to the bath. So, you pour a shower over yourself, and you enter a bath." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Learning this word unlocks a whole group of katakana words that end the same way. English words ending in an ow plus er sound become 「ワー」 in Japanese. A tower is 「タワー」, as in the famous red Tokyo Tower. Power is 「パワー」, and a flower is 「フラワー」, though for real flowers people usually say 「花|はな」. A power spot, a place said to have spiritual energy, is a popular phrase, 「パワースポット」. Notice how each one squeezes the English diphthong into the single sound 「ワ」, then stretches the ending into a long vowel. The pattern is so regular you can often guess the katakana for new words. If you hear 「シャワー」 and 「タワー」 side by side, you can feel the rhyme. So, shower, tower, power, flower: one katakana ending, a whole family of borrowed words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese bathrooms are set up differently from Western ones. The bath area is a fully waterproof wet room. You shower outside the tub, often sitting on a small plastic stool, using a handheld shower head and a basin. The water drains straight into the floor. At hot springs and public baths, there are rows of these shower stations, and you must wash thoroughly before stepping into the shared water. In the late nineteen eighties, a buzzword spread among young people: 「朝シャン|あさシャン」, a morning shampoo, as it became trendy to wash your hair in the morning. Today a quick morning shower is common, especially in hot, sticky summers, but many families still treat the evening bath as the main event. So, sit on a stool, shower first, and only then relax in the tub." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English shower, with the er ending turned into a long vowel. The verb is not take but 「シャワーをあびる」, to have a shower poured over you.",
      "The verb 「浴びる|あびる」 is a ru-verb meaning to be poured over, as in 「シャワーを浴びてから、寝ます|シャワーをあびてから、ねます」. You enter a bath with 「入る|はいる」, but not a shower.",
      "English words ending in an ow plus er sound become 「ワー」, so shower rhymes with 「タワー」, tower, and 「パワー」, power.",
      "In a Japanese wet room you shower outside the tub, often sitting on a small stool, and you must wash fully before entering shared bath water."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[シャワー]をあびます。",
    en: "I take a shower in the morning." },
  "きょうかしょ|教科書": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「教|きょう」, means teach. It is the same kanji as in 「教える|おしえる」, to teach, where it has its native reading. You also find it in 「教室|きょうしつ」, a classroom, and 「教師|きょうし」, a schoolteacher. The second, 「科|か」, means a subject or field of study. On the left is the grain radical, and on the right is a measuring scoop, so you can picture sorting grain into separate piles, the way school sorts knowledge into subjects. You will find it in 「科学|かがく」, science. The third, 「書|しょ」, means writing or a book. Its native reading is in 「書く|かく」, to write. It also shows up in 「辞書|じしょ」, dictionary, 「図書館|としょかん」, library, and 「履歴書|りれきしょ」, a résumé. So the pieces add up to teaching, subject, book: a book that teaches a subject. Notice how many school words are hiding in here. Learning these three kanji gives you a whole cluster of classroom vocabulary. So, teach plus subject plus book: your 「きょうかしょ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word comes with a set of classroom phrases you will hear every day. The teacher says 「教科書を開いてください|きょうかしょをひらいてください」, please open your textbook. Notice the verb 「開く|ひらく」, the one for opening wide, used for books. At the end of class, 「教科書を閉じてください|きょうかしょをとじてください」, please close your textbook. If you forget yours, you might ask a classmate 「教科書を見せてくれない|きょうかしょをみせてくれない」, could you show me your textbook. There is also a handy figurative phrase. 「教科書通り|きょうかしょどおり」 means by the book, done exactly as the textbook says. A coach might praise a 「教科書通り|きょうかしょどおり」 play, or someone might complain that an answer is too textbook. So, open it, close it, borrow it, and do things by the book." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Textbooks in Japanese schools go through a government process. Publishers write the books, but the Ministry of Education reviews and approves them before they can be used in schools. This approval process is called 「教科書検定|きょうかしょけんてい」, textbook screening. It sometimes makes the news, especially when history textbooks are debated, both inside Japan and with neighboring countries. Another fact many visitors do not know: children in compulsory education, elementary and junior high school, receive their textbooks free. Students write their names in them and keep them. Japanese textbooks also tend to be slim and colorful compared with heavy American ones. So, every 「教科書|きょうかしょ」 a Japanese child carries has been checked by the government and handed over free of charge." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built from English sounds. Picture a girl named Kyoko who is obsessed with cars. Her school holds a big car show, and she sneaks her huge textbook in under her arm. Kyoko's car show, 「きょうかしょ」. She opens the book on the hood of a shiny red sports car and starts studying, while engines roar all around her. Teachers walk by and shake their heads, but Kyoko just turns the page. Now link it to the kanji meaning: teach, subject, book. At Kyoko's car show, every car comes with a little textbook explaining how it works. Every time you see your own 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, picture Kyoko studying on that car hood. So, Kyoko's car show, the place where studying meets horsepower." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The three kanji are teach, subject and book, so it is a book that teaches a subject. Each piece unlocks classroom words like 「教室|きょうしつ」, 「科学|かがく」 and 「辞書|じしょ」.",
      "In class you hear 「教科書を開いてください|きょうかしょをひらいてください」, using 「開く|ひらく」 for books. The phrase 「教科書通り|きょうかしょどおり」 means by the book.",
      "The government screens and approves school textbooks, a process called 「教科書検定|きょうかしょけんてい」, and children in compulsory education get them free.",
      "Picture Kyoko studying her textbook on the hood of a sports car at her school car show: Kyoko's car show, 「きょうかしょ」."
    ],
    ja: "[教科書|きょうかしょ]の{二十|にじゅう}ページを{見て|みて}ください。",
    en: "Please look at page twenty of the textbook." },
  "てつだう|手伝う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings. The first is 「手|て」, hand. You know it from 「手紙|てがみ」, a letter, 「上手|じょうず」, skillful, and 「お手洗い|おてあらい」, the restroom, the place to wash your hands. The second, 「伝|つた」, means to pass along or to convey, like passing a message down a line. It has the person radical on the left. Here its sound softens from 「つた」 to 「つだ」 because it follows another word. That kind of softening happens a lot in compounds. So the word is literally to pass along your hands, which is exactly the English idea of lending a hand. You help someone with 「を」: 「ともだちをてつだう」, help a friend. A very useful phrase is 「てつだいましょうか」, shall I help. One thing to watch: this is for helping with a task, physically pitching in. It is a regular u-verb, so you get 「てつだいます」 and 「てつだって」. So, hands passed along to someone else: 「てつだう」, to lend a hand." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb can take either the person or the task as its object, both with 「を」. 「母を手伝う|ははをてつだう」 means help my mother. 「宿題を手伝う|しゅくだいをてつだう」 means help with the homework. To say both at once, link them with 「の」: 「友だちの引っ越しを手伝う|ともだちのひっこしをてつだう」, help a friend with their move. It also combines with the giving verbs from Genki. 「手伝ってあげる|てつだってあげる」 is I help someone, as a favor. 「手伝ってくれる|てつだってくれる」 is someone helps me. 「手伝ってもらう|てつだってもらう」 is I get someone to help. A grateful line is 「手伝ってくれてありがとう|てつだってくれてありがとう」, thanks for helping me. Watch the te-form. Verbs ending in 「う」 become 「って」, so 「手伝って|てつだって」. So, help a person or a task, add a giving verb, and say thanks." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「伝|でん」, appears in other words about passing things along. 「伝える|つたえる」 means to convey or pass on a message. A very polite phrase is 「よろしくお伝えください|よろしくおつたえください」, please give them my regards. With the Chinese-origin reading 「でん」, it gives 「伝統|でんとう」, tradition, customs passed down. Now compare this verb with a near-synonym, 「助ける|たすける」. That one means to save or rescue someone in trouble, like pulling a person out of the water. Shouting 「助けて|たすけて」 means help me, I'm in danger. Our verb is lending a hand with a job, like washing dishes or moving boxes. If you shout this verb in an emergency, it sounds like you want someone to help with chores. So, pass along a hand for tasks, and save 「助ける|たすける」 for rescues." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Offering help is an everyday courtesy in Japan. When you see someone struggling, you can say 「何か手伝いましょうか|なにかてつだいましょうか」, is there anything I can help with. A common polite refusal is 「大丈夫です、ありがとう|だいじょうぶです、ありがとう」. The noun 「手伝い|てつだい」 means help, and with the polite 「お」 it becomes 「お手伝い|おてつだい」, household chores children do. Parents ask kids to do 「お手伝い|おてつだい」, like setting the table, and some give a little pocket money for it. A person hired to help at home is sometimes called 「お手伝いさん|おてつだいさん」, a housekeeper. Here is a mini dialogue. 「手伝おうか|てつだおうか」, want me to help. 「ありがとう、助かる|ありがとう、たすかる」, thanks, that's a big help. So, offering help, kids' chores, and the grateful 「助かる|たすかる」 that follows." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It combines 「手|て」, hand, with 「伝|つた」, pass along, softened to 「つだ」, so it means to lend a hand with a task. A handy offer is 「てつだいましょうか」, shall I help.",
      "Either the person or the task takes 「を」, and you link both with 「の」, as in 「友だちの引っ越しを手伝う|ともだちのひっこしをてつだう」. It pairs with giving verbs like 「手伝ってくれる|てつだってくれる」.",
      "It means helping with chores, while 「助ける|たすける」 means rescuing someone in trouble, so shout 「助けて|たすけて」 in an emergency, not this verb.",
      "Offer help with 「何か手伝いましょうか|なにかてつだいましょうか」, and children do 「お手伝い|おてつだい」, household chores. The grateful reply is 「助かる|たすかる」."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}を[手伝いました|てつだいました|てつだう]。",
    en: "I helped my friend." },
  "かんじ|漢字": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with Chinese-origin readings, and this word is literally the name of the writing system. The first, 「漢|かん」, refers to the Han, the great Chinese dynasty of about two thousand years ago. It has the water radical on the left because the name originally belonged to the Han River, and the dynasty took its name from that region. Even today the Chinese call themselves the Han people. The second, 「字|じ」, means a written character or letter. On top is a roof, and underneath is 「子|こ」, child. A way to remember it: picture children in a schoolhouse practicing their characters. So the word adds up to Han characters, the characters of China. Japan borrowed this script about fifteen hundred years ago, then built 「ひらがな」 and 「カタカナ」 out of simplified kanji to write sounds that kanji alone handled clumsily. That is why one kanji can have both a Chinese-origin reading and a native Japanese reading. You will meet 「字|じ」 again whenever people talk about handwriting or letters. So, Han plus character: 「かんじ」, the characters Japan borrowed from China." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Kanji are not all pictures, which is a relief once you know it. Scholars sort them into a few types. Some really are simplified pictures, like 「山|やま」, mountain, and 「川|かわ」, river. Some show an idea with marks, like 「上|うえ」 and 「下|した」, a line above or below a base. Some combine meanings, like 「休|やす」, a person by a tree. But the largest group by far combines a meaning part with a sound part. Take 「晴|は」 in 「晴れ|はれ」, clear weather. The sun on the left gives the meaning, and 「青|あお」 on the right gives the Chinese-origin sound, 「せい」. The same sound part appears in 「清|せい」, pure, with water on the left. Once you start spotting sound parts, you can often guess how an unfamiliar kanji is read. So, not just pictures, but a clever system of meaning plus sound." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "How many kanji do Japanese people actually use? The government publishes a list of common-use kanji, 「常用漢字|じょうようかんじ」, which has 2,136 characters. Children learn just over a thousand of these in elementary school, and the rest by the end of high school. Adults can read many more, especially in names. There is even a popular national kanji test, often called 「漢検|かんけん」, with levels from beginner to fiendishly hard. People take it for fun, and some list it on their résumés. A common worry today is forgetting how to write kanji by hand, since phones and computers convert typing automatically. People complain that they can read a kanji but cannot write it. So, about two thousand everyday characters, a test to prove your level, and a shared worry about handwriting." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a sound trap. 「感じ|かんじ」, with completely different kanji, means a feeling or an impression. It is one of the most common words in casual Japanese. 「いい感じ|いいかんじ」 means nice, good vibe, and 「どんな感じ|どんなかんじ」 means what's it like. So when someone says 「かんじ」, you need context to know if they mean characters or a feeling. There is also 「幹事|かんじ」, the organizer of a party or a work dinner, the person who books the restaurant and collects the money. Being picked as the organizer for a year-end party is a classic office chore. All three sound almost the same. In writing, the kanji make it clear. In speech, listen for clues: writing, homework, and textbooks mean characters, while vibes and impressions mean a feeling. So, one sound, three words: characters, a feeling, and the party organizer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「漢|かん」 refers to the Han dynasty, and 「字|じ」 means character, so it is Han characters, the script Japan borrowed from China and later simplified into kana.",
      "Most kanji are not pictures but combine a meaning part with a sound part, like 「晴|は」, where the sun gives the meaning and 「青|あお」 gives the sound 「せい」.",
      "The official common-use list, 「常用漢字|じょうようかんじ」, has 2,136 characters, and people even take a national kanji test, 「漢検|かんけん」, for fun.",
      "Watch for 「感じ|かんじ」, a feeling, as in 「いい感じ|いいかんじ」, and 「幹事|かんじ」, a party organizer, which sound almost the same."
    ],
    ja: "[漢字|かんじ]はむずかしいです。",
    en: "Kanji are difficult." },
  "ページ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word page. The vowel in page is a long a sound, which Japanese writes as 「ペ」 plus a long dash, 「ー」, stretching it out. Then the soft g at the end becomes 「ジ」, because Japanese words cannot end in a bare consonant like that, so a vowel gets added. That is the same reason English words like bridge or orange get extra vowels when they come into Japanese. The meaning is exactly the same as English. To say a page number, just put the number in front: 「じゅうページ」, page ten, and 「なんページですか」, what page is it. Teachers say this constantly in class, so it is worth hearing it as a chunk. A small trap: page ten and ten pages sound the same in Japanese. The context tells you which. Pair it with 「きょうかしょ」 for the classic classroom request: 「きょうかしょのじゅうページをみてください」. So, English page, stretched and given a final vowel: 「ページ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond page numbers, this word appears in a few handy phrases. To turn a page, you say 「ページをめくる」, flip the page. A teacher might say 「次のページを見てください|つぎのページをみてください」, please look at the next page. The counter works the same for amounts: 「三ページ読みました|さんページよみました」, I read three pages. There is no sound change with numbers, which makes it easy. Here is a fun twist. In Japanese, 「ホームページ」, home page, usually means an entire website, not just its front page. So a shop might say check our home page, meaning our website. English speakers are sometimes confused when asked for a company's home page. Here is a mini dialogue. 「今、何ページ|いま、なんページ」, what page are we on. 「四十五ページだよ|よんじゅうごページだよ」, page forty-five. So, turning pages, counting pages, and a home page that is really a whole website." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese books are often built backwards from an English reader's point of view. Novels, manga, and many textbooks are printed in vertical lines that run top to bottom, read from right to left. That means the cover is on what you would think of as the back, and you turn the pages toward the right. Page numbers count up as you move leftward through the book. Manga panels follow the same direction, starting at the top right of each page. Translated manga sold abroad often keep this order, with a warning on the last page telling English readers they started at the wrong end. Science and math books, and many language textbooks, use horizontal lines and open the Western way. So, when you pick up a Japanese book, check which way the pages run before you start." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English page, with a long vowel and a final vowel added. Put the number in front, as in 「なんページですか」, what page is it.",
      "Turn the page with 「ページをめくる」, and count pages with no sound change, as in 「三ページ|さんページ」. Note that 「ホームページ」 usually means a whole website.",
      "Many Japanese books run vertically from right to left, so the cover is on what English readers see as the back and the pages turn the other way."
    ],
    ja: "{十|じゅう}[ページ]を{読んで|よんで}ください。",
    en: "Please read page ten." },
  "つぎ|次": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, read with the kanji's native reading. The kanji itself has a debated origin, so rather than tell a story, here is a hook. A way to remember it: the right half is the same piece you see on the right of 「歌|うた」, song, often explained as a person with an open mouth. Picture someone with their mouth open, calling out next. The grammar is the most useful part. 「つぎ」 is a noun, so to say next something, you link it with 「の」: 「つぎのでんしゃ」, the next train, 「つぎのページ」, the next page. It can also stand alone as a topic: 「つぎは、だれですか」, who is next. If you ride trains in Japan, you will hear it all day. The announcement 「つぎは」 followed by a station name tells you the next stop. One mistake to avoid: 「つぎ」 means next in a sequence, the one that follows. For next week or next year you use 「来|らい」 instead, as in 「来週|らいしゅう」 and 「来年|らいねん」. So, the one that comes after in line, joined with 「の」: 「つぎ」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「じ」, which appears in some useful words. 「次回|じかい」 means next time, used for the next episode, meeting, or lesson. You will see it at the end of TV shows. The table of contents in a book is 「目次|もくじ」, where the first kanji means items, so it is the list of items in order. A family's second son is 「次男|じなん」, and the second daughter is 「次女|じじょ」. So the kanji carries the idea of coming second or following in order. There is also a fun doubled form: 「次々|つぎつぎ」, one after another, where the little repeat mark means say it again. 「次々に人が来た|つぎつぎにひとがきた」 means people kept arriving one after another. So, the native reading for the next one, and 「じ」 for second sons, next episodes, and tables of contents." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is a workhorse in daily life. At a bank or a clinic, staff call out 「次の方、どうぞ|つぎのかた、どうぞ」, next person, please, using the polite word for person. When giving directions or instructions, 「次に|つぎに」 means next, as in first do this, next do that. Recipes and manuals are full of it. When telling a story about the past, 「次の日|つぎのひ」 means the next day, the day after. Do not use 「明日|あした」 for that, because tomorrow only means the day after today. If someone makes a mistake, you might hear 「次から気をつけてね|つぎからきをつけてね」, be careful from next time on. Here is a mini dialogue. 「次、どこに行く|つぎ、どこにいく」, where to next. 「次は、お寺に行こう|つぎは、おてらにいこう」, next, let's go to the temple. So, next in line, next step, and next day, all with one little word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means next in a sequence and links to nouns with 「の」, as in 「つぎのでんしゃ」, the next train. For next week or year, use 「来|らい」 instead, as in 「来週|らいしゅう」.",
      "The reading 「じ」 means following or second, as in 「次回|じかい」, next time, 「目次|もくじ」, a table of contents, and 「次男|じなん」, a second son.",
      "For telling past stories, 「次の日|つぎのひ」 means the next day, not 「明日|あした」. At clinics staff call 「次の方、どうぞ|つぎのかた、どうぞ」, next person, please."
    ],
    ja: "[次|つぎ]の{電車|でんしゃ}は{何時|なんじ}ですか。",
    en: "What time is the next train?" },
  "おかね|お金": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The 「お」 is the polite prefix, and people nearly always use it with money. Saying just 「かね」 sounds rough. The kanji 「金|かね」 means gold, or metal in general. In this word it has its native reading, 「かね」. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「きん」, which you know from 「金曜日|きんようび」, Friday, the day of metal or gold. A way to remember the shape: picture a little roof over the ground, with two nuggets of gold sparkling in the soil. So money is polite metal, a nice reminder that money started out as coins. The family is big. 「お金持ち|おかねもち」 is a money holder, a rich person. 「税金|ぜいきん」 is tax, and 「奨学金|しょうがくきん」 is a scholarship, both using the Chinese-origin reading. A very useful phrase is 「おかねがありません」, I have no money. You can also hear 「金|きん」 alone for gold, like a gold medal. So, gold or metal, made polite: 「おかね」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the verbs that go with money. You pay with 「払う|はらう」: 「お金を払う|おかねをはらう」. You take cash out of an ATM with 「下ろす|おろす」, literally to lower it, so 「お金を下ろす|おかねをおろす」 means to withdraw money. You save it with 「貯める|ためる」. And when something costs money, you say 「お金がかかる|おかねがかかる」, it takes money, a phrase people use when complaining about rent or a hobby. Japan has long been a cash-loving country, and while cards and phone payments are spreading fast, small restaurants and shrines may still want cash. Coins are called 「小銭|こぜに」, small change, and people carry a coin purse for them. Here is a mini dialogue. 「お金、ある|おかね、ある」, do you have money. 「ちょっとしかない|ちょっとしかない」, just a little. So, pay it, withdraw it, save it, and watch it disappear." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Money is part of several Japanese customs. At New Year, children receive 「お年玉|おとしだま」, gifts of money from parents, grandparents, and relatives, in small decorated envelopes. Kids can collect quite a lot. At weddings, guests give cash, not presents, in fancy envelopes tied with special cords. The bills should be crisp and new, because new bills show you prepared for the happy day. At funerals, the opposite is true: used bills are preferred, so it does not look like you were ready for a death. Money is also rarely handed over bare. Even in shops, you place it on a small tray by the register rather than in the clerk's hand. So in Japan, how you give money, new or used, wrapped or on a tray, matters almost as much as how much." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「金|きん」 builds many everyday money words with its Chinese-origin reading. 「料金|りょうきん」 is a fee or charge, like a bus fare or a phone bill. 「現金|げんきん」 is cash, present money, and shops sometimes post signs saying cash only with this word. 「貯金|ちょきん」 is savings, stored money, and a piggy bank is a 「貯金箱|ちょきんばこ」. 「お金持ち|おかねもち」, the money holder, uses the native reading. Without the polite prefix, 「金|かね」 alone sounds blunt, the way a gangster in a movie demands money. You will hear it in rough speech, but stick with the polite form yourself. So, fees, cash, and savings with 「きん」, and money in your pocket with the polite 「おかね」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means gold or metal, read 「かね」 here and 「きん」 in 「金曜日|きんようび」. The polite 「お」 is nearly always used, because plain 「かね」 sounds rough.",
      "You pay with 「払う|はらう」, withdraw with 「下ろす|おろす」, and save with 「貯める|ためる」. When something is costly, say 「お金がかかる|おかねがかかる」.",
      "How you give money matters: kids get 「お年玉|おとしだま」 at New Year, crisp new bills go to weddings, used bills to funerals, and in shops you use a tray.",
      "With the reading 「きん」, the kanji gives 「料金|りょうきん」, a fee, 「現金|げんきん」, cash, and 「貯金|ちょきん」, savings. Plain 「金|かね」 sounds blunt."
    ],
    ja: "[お金|おかね]がありません。",
    en: "I don't have any money." },
  "パソコン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a loanword from English, but one that has been chopped. It started as personal computer, which in full Japanese sounds would be long and clumsy. So Japanese took the first two sounds of personal, 「パソ」, and the first two of computer, 「コン」, and glued them together. You lose the r and the long vowels in the process. Japanese loves this four-sound chopping trick. 「エアコン」, air conditioner, is built the same way, and you will meet many more like it. The full word 「コンピューター」 also exists, but in daily life a laptop or desktop at home is a 「パソコン」. This word was made in Japan. An English speaker would not understand it, so do not use it abroad. You use it with the particle 「で」 when it is your tool: 「パソコンでしゅくだいをします」, I do my homework on the computer. So, the front of personal plus the front of computer: 「パソコン」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese loves to chop long loanwords into short, catchy ones, often two sounds plus two sounds. Once you see the pattern, many words make sense. A smartphone is 「スマホ」, from smart phone. A remote control is 「リモコン」, from remote control. A digital camera is 「デジカメ」. A family restaurant, the casual chain kind, is 「ファミレス」. And a convenience store is 「コンビニ」, from convenience. Many of these are made in Japan, so English speakers would not understand them abroad. The trick for guessing them is to think of the English phrase, take the first few sounds of each part, and glue them together. Try it when you hear a new katakana word. So, chopped and glued: the same recipe that turned personal computer into this word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A laptop is usually called a 「ノートパソコン」, a notebook computer, because Japanese uses notebook for something thin and portable. A desktop is a 「デスクトップ」. People say 「パソコンを使う|パソコンをつかう」, to use a computer, and when one breaks, 「パソコンが壊れた|パソコンがこわれた」. If it freezes, you might hear 「動かない|うごかない」, it won't move, it won't respond. Many offices still rely heavily on email and spreadsheets, and older workers sometimes ask younger ones for help with the computer. Here is a mini dialogue. 「レポート、手で書いたの|レポート、てでかいたの」, did you write the report by hand. 「ううん、パソコンで書いた|ううん、パソコンでかいた」, no, I wrote it on the computer. Notice 「で」 for the tool. So, laptops are notebooks, broken ones won't move, and you write with 「で」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "How do Japanese people type thousands of kanji on an ordinary keyboard? Most type in romaji, letters of the alphabet, and software turns the sounds into kana as they go. Then they press the space bar to convert to kanji. This step is called 「変換|へんかん」, conversion. Because many words share the same sound, a list pops up, and you pick the right kanji from it. This is why picking the wrong kanji is a common typo in Japanese, like choosing 「感じ|かんじ」, feeling, when you meant characters. On smartphones, most young people use flick input instead, sliding a finger in different directions from each kana key. It is extremely fast. So, type the sound, convert it, pick the kanji, and you have written Japanese on a computer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It glues the start of personal, 「パソ」, to the start of computer, 「コン」, a made-in-Japan word English speakers would not understand. Use 「で」 for it as a tool.",
      "Japanese chops long loanwords into catchy pieces, like 「スマホ」, smartphone, 「リモコン」, remote control, and 「コンビニ」, convenience store.",
      "A laptop is a 「ノートパソコン」, a notebook computer, and when one breaks you say 「パソコンが壊れた|パソコンがこわれた」. Mark it as your tool with 「で」.",
      "People type sounds in letters, and the software converts them to kana and then kanji in a step called 「変換|へんかん」, picking from a list of same-sound words."
    ],
    ja: "[パソコン]で{日本語|にほんご}をべんきょうします。",
    en: "I study Japanese on my computer." },
  "エアコン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Another chopped English loanword. It comes from air conditioner. The air part became 「エア」. English air ends in an r sound that Japanese does not have, so it is just a vowel. Then conditioner was cut down to its first bit, 「コン」. Put them together and you get a short, punchy four-sound word, exactly the same trick as 「パソコン」, from personal computer. Once you spot the pattern, a lot of katakana words suddenly make sense. In Japan one unit often does both cooling and heating, so the same word covers your heater in winter too. You turn it on with 「つける」 and off with 「けす」, the same verbs as for lights: 「エアコンをつけてください」. Japanese summers are hot and humid, so expect to hear this word a lot from June to September. A way to remember it: air, plus the con from conditioner. So, English air conditioner, trimmed down to its first pieces: 「エアコン」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the words you need to work one. The cooling setting is 「冷房|れいぼう」, and the heating setting is 「暖房|だんぼう」. You set the temperature, 「温度|おんど」, with a remote control, 「リモコン」, another chopped loanword. When the room feels nice and cool, people say 「エアコンが効いている|エアコンがきいている」, the air conditioner is working well. When it is too cold, someone will ask you to raise the temperature. In summer, you will hear 「エアコンをつけてもいいですか|エアコンをつけてもいいですか」, may I turn on the air conditioner. Here is a mini dialogue. 「暑いね|あついね」, it's hot. 「エアコン、つけようか」, shall I turn on the air conditioner. 「うん、お願い|うん、おねがい」, yes, please. So, cooling, heating, temperature, and a remote: everything for a comfortable room." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In 2005, the Japanese government started a campaign called 「クールビズ」, Cool Biz. Offices were asked to set their air conditioners to around twenty-eight degrees Celsius in summer, which is fairly warm, to save energy. To make that bearable, workers were encouraged to leave their ties and jackets at home. Before this, many salarymen wore full suits even on the hottest days. The campaign changed office fashion across the country, and every summer TV news still shows politicians in open-collar shirts. There is a winter version too, encouraging lower heating and warmer clothes. Japanese summers can be brutal, and heatstroke warnings are common, so officials also remind older people not to avoid using the air conditioner just to save money. So, a warmer office, no necktie, and a nation dressing for the weather." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It chops air conditioner into 「エア」 plus 「コン」, just like 「パソコン」. One unit usually cools and heats, and you use 「つける」 and 「けす」 like lights.",
      "Cooling is 「冷房|れいぼう」 and heating is 「暖房|だんぼう」, set with a 「リモコン」. When it works well, people say 「エアコンが効いている|エアコンがきいている」.",
      "The 2005 「クールビズ」 campaign set office air conditioners to about twenty-eight degrees in summer and let workers drop ties and jackets to save energy."
    ],
    ja: "あついですから、[エアコン]をつけます。",
    en: "It's hot, so I'll turn on the air conditioner." },
  "でんしゃ|電車": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「電|でん」, is electricity, rain on top with a lightning flash below. It is the same as in 「電気|でんき」, electricity or lights, and 「電話|でんわ」, the telephone. The second, 「車|しゃ」, means vehicle. The kanji is a picture of a cart seen from above: the box in the middle is the body, and the line through it is the axle with wheels at each end. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「車|くるま」, a car. With the Chinese-origin reading 「しゃ」 it appears in 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, a bicycle, a vehicle that turns by itself. So this word is electric vehicle, which in Japan means a train running on electric rails. Japan's commuter trains are famous for being on time to the minute. To say by train, use 「で」: 「でんしゃでいきます」. And for subways there is 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」. So, electricity plus vehicle: the 「でんしゃ」 you ride to work." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Riding trains comes with a set of everyday phrases. When a train is late, you say 「電車が遅れた|でんしゃがおくれた」, the train was delayed, which is also a common excuse at work. A packed rush-hour train is a 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」, a full-of-people train. The last train of the night is the 「終電|しゅうでん」, short for final electric train, and missing it after a party is a classic problem. People check the time and say 「終電、何時|しゅうでん、なんじ」, what time is the last train. Changing lines is 「乗り換え|のりかえ」. Here is a mini dialogue. 「どうやって来たの|どうやってきたの」, how did you get here. 「電車で来たよ|でんしゃできたよ」, I came by train. So, delayed trains, packed trains, and the last train home." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for train, and they differ in a useful way. This word focuses on trains powered by electricity, which is almost all trains in Japanese cities. 「列車|れっしゃ」, lined-up vehicles, is a more general and formal word, used for long-distance trains or in announcements. 「汽車|きしゃ」, steam vehicle, means an old steam train, and some older people still use it loosely for any train. The bullet train is 「新幹線|しんかんせん」, new trunk line. The subway is 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, underground iron. And the 「車|しゃ」 reading appears in 「駐車場|ちゅうしゃじょう」, a parking lot, and 「車内|しゃない」, inside the car, which you hear in train announcements. So, electric trains for daily rides, and a whole family of vehicle words around them." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Riding a Japanese train has its own etiquette. Talking on the phone is frowned upon, and announcements ask you to set your phone to silent mode. People keep conversations quiet. Many lines have women-only cars during rush hour, marked with pink signs, to protect passengers from harassment. Priority seats near the doors are for elderly people, pregnant women, and people with disabilities. Many stations play short departure melodies instead of a harsh bell, and some stations have their own special tunes linked to local history or famous songs. Train staff bow when entering and leaving a car. And on busy platforms, people line up neatly in marked spots, letting passengers off first. So, quiet phones, polite lines, and a little song before the doors close." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「電|でん」, electricity, plus 「車|しゃ」, vehicle, a picture of a cart with an axle, so an electric vehicle, a train. To say by train, use 「でんしゃでいきます」.",
      "Common phrases include 「電車が遅れた|でんしゃがおくれた」, the train was late, 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」, a packed train, and 「終電|しゅうでん」, the last train.",
      "This is the everyday electric train, while 「列車|れっしゃ」 is more formal, 「汽車|きしゃ」 is a steam train, and 「新幹線|しんかんせん」 is the bullet train.",
      "Train etiquette means silent phones, quiet talk, priority seats, and neat lines on the platform, letting passengers off first."
    ],
    ja: "[電車|でんしゃ]で{大学|だいがく}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to college by train." },
  "くに|国": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word uses the kanji's native reading, 「くに」. Look at the shape. The outer box is an enclosure, like a border or a city wall drawn around a territory. Inside is 「玉|たま」, a jewel. So you can see it as a treasure guarded inside borders: a country. The older form of this kanji had a different piece inside, and the jewel version is the modern simplified one, but the picture works well for today's kanji. The Chinese-origin reading is 「こく」, which often softens to 「ごく」 at the end of a word. You already know it from country names: 「中国|ちゅうごく」, China, and 「韓国|かんこく」, Korea. And from 「外国|がいこく」, a foreign country, an outside country, plus 「外国人|がいこくじん」, a foreigner, and 「外国語|がいこくご」, a foreign language. On its own, 「くに」 can mean country, but also your home region, where you are from. A polite way to ask someone's home country is 「おくにはどちらですか」. So, a jewel inside a border: 「くに」, your country or homeland." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「こく」, this kanji builds many words about nations. 「国際|こくさい」 means international, as in an international airport. 「国内|こくない」 is domestic, inside the country, and you see it on airport signs for domestic flights. 「全国|ぜんこく」 is nationwide, the whole country. 「帰国|きこく」 is returning to your home country, used by travelers and exchange students. And in Japanese schools, the subject called 「国語|こくご」, national language, is Japanese itself, the way English class works for American students. Notice how these words are formal and official. Everyday speech often uses the native reading instead, like 「国に帰る|くににかえる」, go back to my country. So, the native word for home and country, and 「こく」 for the official, international vocabulary." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word can mean more than a nation. It often means your home region, the place you grew up, even within Japan. Older people from the countryside may talk about going back to their 「国|くに」 when they mean their hometown. Local dialect is sometimes called 「お国言葉|おくにことば」, home-region words. A famous novel by Kawabata is titled 「雪国|ゆきぐに」, Snow Country, about the snowy region on the Sea of Japan side. Notice the sound softens to 「ぐに」 after another word. Japan itself is often called the land of the rising sun. Here is a mini dialogue. 「夏休みは何をしますか|なつやすみはなにをしますか」, what will you do over summer break. 「国に帰ります|くににかえります」, I'll go back to my country. So, a nation, a homeland, or the village you came from." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Before 1946, this kanji was written in an older, more complex form, 「國|くに」. Inside the border box sat a different component, one that included the shape of a halberd, an old weapon. Many scholars explain it as land being guarded by arms inside its borders. After World War Two, Japan simplified many kanji, and the jewel version became the standard. You can still see the old form in some company names, temple signs, and in Taiwan and Hong Kong, which use traditional characters. Mainland China uses the same jewel version as Japan for this kanji. So the modern shape is a jewel inside the border, but the older one showed a weapon guarding the land, a reminder that every simple kanji may hide a longer history." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a jewel, 「玉|たま」, inside a border, a treasure guarded as a country. The reading 「こく」 appears in 「外国|がいこく」, and the native word can also mean your homeland.",
      "The reading 「こく」 builds formal words like 「国際|こくさい」, international, 「国内|こくない」, domestic, and 「帰国|きこく」, returning home. Everyday speech says 「国に帰る|くににかえる」.",
      "It can mean your home region, even within Japan, as in 「お国言葉|おくにことば」, local dialect. After another word it softens to 「ぐに」, as in 「雪国|ゆきぐに」.",
      "Before 1946 it was written 「國|くに」, with a weapon shape inside the border, often explained as land guarded by arms, before the jewel form became standard."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}の[国|くに]はアメリカです。",
    en: "My country is America." },
  "こんしゅう|今週": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「今|こん」, means now or this present one. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「今|いま」, now. It also appears in 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, 「今月|こんげつ」, this month, and 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester. Watch out, because 「今日|きょう」 and 「今年|ことし」 use special readings, so not every word with this kanji starts with 「こん」. The second, 「週|しゅう」, means week. It has the movement radical on the left and bottom, and inside is a piece meaning to go around in a circuit. A week is a cycle that keeps coming around. So this word is simply this week. The real gift here is the pattern. 「先週|せんしゅう」 is last week, the week before. 「今週|こんしゅう」 is this week. 「来週|らいしゅう」 is next week, the coming week. Swap 「週|しゅう」 for 「月|げつ」 and you get last month, this month, and next month with the same front pieces. You also have 「毎週|まいしゅう」, every week, and 「週末|しゅうまつ」, the weekend, the week's end. So, now plus week: 「こんしゅう」, the middle of the last, this, next set." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how the word lives in everyday plans. Japanese people often add 「中|じゅう」 to make 「今週中に|こんしゅうじゅうに」, sometime before this week is over. At work you will hear 「こんしゅうじゅうにおねがいします」, please get it done by the end of this week. It is a polite way to set a deadline. You can also point to a specific day with 「の」: 「こんしゅうのどようび」 is this Saturday. And when you want to turn down an invitation gently, people often say 「こんしゅうはちょっと」, this week is a little, and simply trail off. The listener understands it means no. Adding 「は」 to the time word, like that, sets up a contrast: this week is bad, but maybe another week is fine. So the word is not just a date. It is a soft tool for planning and for politely saying not now." },
      { title: "Week or a week", text: "There are two different ideas English mixes together, and Japanese keeps them apart. 「こんしゅう」 is a slot on the calendar, the current week from start to end. But if you want to talk about a length of time, a span of seven days, you use the counter 「〜週間|しゅうかん」. 「いっしゅうかん」 is one week, 「にしゅうかん」 is two weeks, and 「さんしゅうかん」 is three weeks. Notice the small 「っ」 in 「いっしゅうかん」. That doubling happens a lot when one meets a sound like 「し」. So, 「こんしゅうはいそがしいです」 means I am busy this week. But 「いっしゅうかんにほんにいました」 means I was in Japan for a week. Same week kanji, but one is a place on the calendar and the other is a stretch of time. Keep the calendar slot and the stretch separate, and both words fall into place." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a silly memory trick built on the sound. Picture a bright orange traffic cone that you wear as a shoe. It is a cone shoe, and it sounds a lot like 「こんしゅう」. Imagine your whole office has agreed that this week, and only this week, everyone must clomp around in cone shoes. On Monday you put them on. By Wednesday everyone is tired of the noise. On Friday you finally kick them off for the weekend. Whenever you need to say this week, picture that clunky orange cone shoe on your foot right now. And remember the kanji too. The first one is the now kanji, so the cone shoe is happening now. The second has the circling movement shape, the week that comes around again. So, cone shoes for one week only: 「こんしゅう」, this week." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「今|こん」, now, plus 「週|しゅう」, week, a cycle that comes around. It sits in the set 「先週|せんしゅう」, 「今週|こんしゅう」, 「来週|らいしゅう」, last, this and next week.",
      "Add 「中|じゅう」 for a polite deadline, 「こんしゅうじゅうに」, by the end of this week. To decline softly, say 「こんしゅうはちょっと」 and trail off.",
      "This word is a calendar slot, while a span of time uses 「〜週間|しゅうかん」, as in 「いっしゅうかん」, one week.",
      "Picture yourself clomping around in orange traffic cone shoes for this week only: cone shoe, 「こんしゅう」."
    ],
    ja: "[今週|こんしゅう]はいそがしいです。",
    en: "I'm busy this week." },
  "らいしゅう|来週": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「来|らい」, means to come. With its native reading it is the verb 「来る|くる」, to come. In time words it means the coming one, the one that is on its way. The second, 「週|しゅう」, means week, with the movement radical wrapped around a piece meaning to go around: a week cycles around. So this is the coming week, next week. The 「来|らい」 family is very regular. 「来月|らいげつ」 is next month, 「来年|らいねん」 is next year, and 「来学期|らいがっき」 is next semester. Even 「将来|しょうらい」, the future, uses it, the time that is about to come. Line it up with its partners: 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week, 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, and 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week. Like other time words, it usually does not need a particle: 「らいしゅう、きょうとにいきます」 works fine. A common slip is using 「つぎのしゅう」 for next week. People understand it, but 「らいしゅう」 is the natural word. So, the coming week: 「らいしゅう」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let us look harder at the first kanji, 「来|らい」. A common traditional explanation is that it began as a picture of a stalk of wheat, and the word for come was borrowed onto it because the two words sounded alike in old Chinese. That kind of sound borrowing happened often. Today, though, it simply means to come. What makes this kanji tricky is its many readings. In time words it is 「らい」. In the dictionary form of the verb it is 「く」, as in 「来る|くる」. In the polite form it changes to 「き」, as in 「来ます|きます」. And in the negative it becomes 「こ」, as in 「来ない|こない」. That is one kanji wearing four different sounds, all because 「くる」 is irregular. So when you see it in a time word, relax: it is almost always 「らい」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is everywhere in school and work life. A teacher might say 「しゅくだいはらいしゅうのげつようびまでです」, the homework is due by next Monday. Notice 「の」 linking next week to the day, and 「まで」, until or by. At the end of a weekly class, people say 「また、らいしゅう」, see you next week. It is friendly and warm, a shortened version of see you again next week. When making plans, you might ask 「らいしゅうはどうですか」, how about next week. And if you want to say sometime during next week, you can say 「らいしゅうじゅうに」, the same pattern as this week. Remember that, as a time word, it normally does not take 「に」. You just put it near the start of the sentence. So, homework, plans, and goodbyes: 「らいしゅう」 keeps the week ahead organized." },
      { title: "The week after", text: "Once you have next week, you can stretch one step further. Put 「さ」 in front and you get 「さらいしゅう」, the week after next. The 「さ」 comes from a kanji meaning again, so it is the coming week, again. The same trick works for years: 「さらいねん」 is the year after next. For months, you can say 「さらいげつ」, the month after next. Looking backward is different. The week before last is 「せんせんしゅう」, simply doubling the front sound of last week. So the full line of weeks runs: 「せんせんしゅう」, 「せんしゅう」, 「こんしゅう」, 「らいしゅう」, and 「さらいしゅう」. These are very handy when you are scheduling a trip or a test two weeks out. So, add 「さ」 for one more step forward: 「さらいしゅう」, the week after next." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「来|らい」 means coming, so this is the coming week, lined up with 「先週|せんしゅう」 and 「今週|こんしゅう」. Use it rather than 「つぎのしゅう」, and it usually needs no particle.",
      "The first kanji may have started as a picture of wheat, borrowed for come by sound. It wears four readings, but in time words it is almost always 「らい」.",
      "Link it to a day with 「の」, as in 「らいしゅうのげつようび」, next Monday, and say goodbye after a weekly class with 「また、らいしゅう」.",
      "Put 「さ」 in front for 「さらいしゅう」, the week after next, while looking back two weeks is 「せんせんしゅう」."
    ],
    ja: "[来週|らいしゅう]、{京都|きょうと}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'll go to Kyoto next week." },
  "らいねん|来年": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「来|らい」, means coming, the same kanji as the verb 「来る|くる」, to come. In time words it marks the next one. The second, 「年|ねん」, means year. You know it from counting years with 「〜年|ねん」, and its native reading appears in 「年上|としうえ」, someone older, someone above you in years. Put them together and it is the coming year, next year. It slots straight into the same pattern as 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week, and 「来月|らいげつ」, next month. One small twist: for this year, Japanese does not say 「こんねん」. It uses a special reading, 「今年|ことし」. So the set you want to remember is 「ことし」, this year, and 「らいねん」, next year. A cultural note: around New Year, people say 「らいねんもよろしくおねがいします」 at the end of December, asking for continued good relations in the coming year. Like other time words, it usually works without a particle. So, the coming year: 「らいねん」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「年|ねん」, has a nice history. Its older form is usually explained as a person carrying a bundle of ripe grain. The grain harvest came once a year, so the harvest became the word for a year. Today it also means age, how many years you have lived. It has two main readings. 「ねん」 is the Chinese-origin reading, used for counting years and in compounds like 「らいねん」. 「とし」 is the native reading, used on its own for year or age, as in 「としをとる」, to grow older. A few words even allow both readings. 「毎年|まいとし」 and 「まいねん」 both mean every year, and both are correct. So whenever you see this kanji, picture someone hauling in the yearly harvest. That is the root of the year, and of next year: 「らいねん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here is the full family of year words, and it has two surprises. Next year is regular, 「らいねん」. The year after next is 「さらいねん」. But last year is not 「せんねん」, the way last week is 「せんしゅう」. Instead it is 「去年|きょねん」. The first kanji means gone or departed, so it is the year that has left. And this year is the special reading 「今年|ことし」. Every year is 「毎年|まいとし」. You will also meet 「〜年生|ねんせい」, for school year, as in 「いちねんせい」, a first year student. So the line of years runs 「きょねん」, 「ことし」, 「らいねん」, 「さらいねん」. Practice saying it as a little chant, because the first two do not follow the pattern you would guess. So, two irregulars and then a regular step forward: 「らいねん」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "There is a fun Japanese saying about this word. 「らいねんのことをいうとおにがわらう」 means if you talk about next year, the demon laughs. The idea is that the future is impossible to predict, so making grand plans for next year is a bit silly. Even a demon would find it funny. People quote it with a smile when someone is planning too far ahead. Even so, next year is a big deal in December. People send New Year cards called 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, timed to arrive on New Year's Day. They also have year-end parties called 「忘年会|ぼうねんかい」, forget-the-year parties, to put the old year behind them before the new one begins. So, make your plans, but stay humble: 「らいねん」 might laugh at you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is coming plus year, matching 「来週|らいしゅう」 and 「来月|らいげつ」. This year is not 「こんねん」 but the special reading 「今年|ことし」.",
      "The year kanji is usually explained as a person carrying ripe grain, since the harvest came once a year. It reads 「ねん」 for counting and 「とし」 on its own.",
      "Last year is the irregular 「去年|きょねん」, the year that has left, and this year is 「今年|ことし」, so the line runs 「きょねん」, 「ことし」, 「らいねん」, 「さらいねん」.",
      "A saying warns that if you talk about next year, the demon laughs, 「らいねんのことをいうとおにがわらう」, because the future cannot be predicted."
    ],
    ja: "[来年|らいねん]、{日本|にほん}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'll go to Japan next year." },
  "よる|夜": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, using the kanji's native reading. If you look closely at the kanji, tucked inside on the right is a shape like 「夕|ゆう」, the evening kanji, often described as a crescent moon. So there is a moon hiding in the night. That is a good thing to look for when you are trying to recognise it. The Chinese-origin reading is 「や」, which you will meet in words like 「今夜|こんや」, tonight. Learn it with the other times of day: 「あさ」, morning, 「ひる」, midday, and 「よる」, night. You know two of them already in meals: 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast, and 「昼ご飯|ひるごはん」, lunch. Now for a useful contrast. 「晩|ばん」 also means evening or night, as in 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, 「毎晩|まいばん」, every night, and 「晩ご飯|ばんごはん」, dinner. 「ばん」 leans toward evening, the time you eat dinner, while 「よる」 covers the dark hours, including late at night. To say at night, add 「は」 or 「に」 depending on the sentence: 「よるはテレビをみます」. So, a moon tucked inside the kanji: 「よる」, the night." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The native reading of this kanji sometimes shortens from 「よる」 to just 「よ」. You hear it in 「夜中|よなか」, the middle of the night, those late hours after midnight. And 「夜空|よぞら」 is the night sky, full of stars. The Chinese-origin reading 「や」 also shows up. 「夜食|やしょく」 is a late-night snack, something students eat while studying. And 「深夜|しんや」 is late at night, the deep night, a word you will see on convenience store signs and late TV schedules. Compare those with 「よる」 itself, which is the broad, plain word for nighttime. So one kanji gives you a whole night out: the night sky, the dead of night, and the snack you sneak at two in the morning. They all share the dark hours after the sun goes down. And the plain everyday word for that time is 「よる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A few practical points make this word easy to use. For a clock time at night, use 「に」: 「よるじゅうじにねます」, I go to bed at ten at night. For late at night, say 「よるおそく」, as in 「よるおそくまでべんきょうしました」, I studied until late at night. Here is a common slip. English speakers want to say tonight as this night, but Japanese does not say 「このよる」 for that. Use 「こんばん」 in everyday talk, or 「こんや」, which feels a bit more like writing or announcements. Also notice the greetings around nighttime. In the evening you greet people with 「こんばんは」. When you part late at night, or go to bed, you say 「おやすみなさい」. So, ten at night, late at night, and goodnight: 「よる」 is the backdrop for all of them." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a DJ who only works after dark. Every night at midnight he grabs the microphone and shouts, yo, rule the night. Yo, rule. That sounds like 「よる」. Picture the crowd under the stars, the moon hanging above the club, and that DJ yelling it again and again while everyone dances until morning. Then the sun comes up, the DJ goes quiet, and he will not say a word until it is dark again. And remember the moon tucked inside the kanji. That is the moon hanging over his club. So, a DJ shouting yo, rule the night under the moon: 「よる」, the night." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A moon shape like 「夕|ゆう」 hides inside the kanji. While 「晩|ばん」 leans toward dinnertime evening, this word covers all the dark hours, including late night.",
      "The reading shortens to 「よ」 in 「夜中|よなか」 and 「夜空|よぞら」, and becomes 「や」 in 「夜食|やしょく」, a late-night snack, and 「深夜|しんや」.",
      "Say 「よるおそく」 for late at night, but for tonight use 「こんばん」 or 「こんや」, never 「このよる」.",
      "Picture a DJ who only works after dark, shouting yo, rule the night under the moon, which sounds like 「よる」."
    ],
    ja: "[夜|よる]はテレビを{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch TV at night." },
  "たいへん|大変": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「大|たい」, means big. It is a picture of a person with arms and legs stretched out wide. It has several readings: 「だい」 in 「大学|だいがく」, college, 「おお」 in 「大きい|おおきい」, big, and here 「たい」. The second, 「変|へん」, means change, or strange, something out of the ordinary. So the word is a big change, a big upheaval, which is how it comes to mean a tough or terrible situation. The most useful phrase is 「たいへんですね」. You say it when someone tells you something hard, like working every day or having three tests. It means that sounds rough, and it is a key way to show sympathy in Japanese conversation. You can also say 「たいへんでした」 about something tough you went through. In formal speech it can also mean very, as in 「たいへんありがとうございます」, but for now focus on tough. It is a na-adjective, so before a noun it takes 「な」: 「たいへんなしごと」, a hard job. So, a big change in your life: 「たいへん」, and when someone is struggling, 「たいへんですね」." },
      { title: "Formal very", text: "You will hear this word in polite, formal Japanese as a strong very, and it is worth recognising. In shops and restaurants, staff say 「たいへんおまたせしました」, I am very sorry to have kept you waiting. On trains you may hear an announcement that says 「たいへんごめいわくをおかけしております」, we are causing you great inconvenience. In business letters and emails, 「たいへんもうしわけございません」 is a deep apology, we are terribly sorry. In all of these, it simply makes the feeling stronger and more respectful. In casual talk with friends you would use 「とても」 or 「すごく」 instead. So, the same word that means a tough situation also becomes a dignified, formal very. When the speaker is bowing, that is probably the meaning: 「たいへん」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let us dig into the second kanji, 「変|へん」. On its own it is a na-adjective meaning strange or odd. 「へんなひと」 is a strange person, and 「へんですね」 is that is weird. It has native readings too, and they are two verbs you will soon meet. 「変える|かえる」 is to change something, and 「変わる|かわる」 is for something to change on its own. Careful, because 「かえる」 written this way is not the same as going home, which has a different kanji. So the core idea of this kanji is that something is different from the usual, something has shifted. That is how a big shift turned into a big problem, and how a tough situation became 「たいへん」. Look for that same shift in 「へん」, 「かえる」, and 「かわる」. So, big plus out of the ordinary: 「たいへん」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a farm hen that has gotten herself completely tied up in string. She is a tie hen, which sounds like 「たいへん」. She squawks, she flaps, and she knocks over the feed bucket. The farmer runs out, sees the mess, and shouts that this is a disaster. Then he sees the tiny hen tangled up and says, oh, you poor thing, that must be rough. That second reaction is exactly how Japanese people use the word: to say wow, that sounds hard, when someone tells them their troubles. So whenever a friend tells you about a long week, picture that poor tied-up hen and say 「たいへんですね」. A tied-up hen in a big mess: 「たいへん」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Big plus change or strange, a big upheaval, so it means tough or terrible. The key phrase is 「たいへんですね」, that sounds rough, to show sympathy.",
      "In polite speech it is a strong, respectful very, as in 「たいへんおまたせしました」, sorry to keep you waiting. With friends, use 「とても」 or 「すごく」 instead.",
      "「変|へん」 means strange or different from usual, as in 「へんなひと」, and gives 「変える|かえる」 and 「変わる|かわる」. A big shift became a big problem.",
      "Picture a hen tied up in string, a tie hen, and the farmer saying you poor thing, which is how you use 「たいへんですね」 when friends share troubles."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}アルバイトです。[大変|たいへん]です。",
    en: "I work my part-time job every day. It's tough." },
  "あそぶ|遊ぶ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is read with its native reading, 「あそ」. Look at the bottom left. That swooping line is the movement radical, the same one in 「週|しゅう」, week, and 「返す|かえす」, to return. It is often used for going places, so you can picture wandering around for fun. The verb is a u-verb ending in 「ぶ」, so you get 「あそびます」 and, in the te-form, 「あそんで」. Now the most important part, a mistake English speakers make all the time. In English, play covers games, sports, and music. In Japanese, 「あそぶ」 does not. For sports you use 「する」, as in 「テニスをする」. For instruments you use a different verb, 「ひく」, as in 「ピアノをひく」. 「あそぶ」 is for children playing, and for adults hanging out and having a good time with friends: 「ともだちとあそびます」. A very friendly invitation is 「あそびにきてください」, please come visit and hang out. So, wandering around for fun with friends: 「あそぶ」, not sports and not music." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The right side of 「遊|ゆう」 is usually explained as a banner or streamer from a flag, with a child under it. Flags flutter around freely in the wind, and the movement radical adds roaming. So the kanji suggests drifting about freely, without a fixed task. That is the spirit of play. The Chinese-origin reading is 「ゆう」, and you will meet it in 「遊園地|ゆうえんち」, an amusement park, literally a garden for play. You may also see 「遊具|ゆうぐ」, play equipment like swings and slides in a park. The native reading, 「あそ」, is the one in this verb. Notice that the movement radical here is the same one you know from 「週|しゅう」, week, and 「近い|ちかい」, near. So, a fluttering banner roaming freely: 「あそぶ」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a great model for the 「ぶ」 row. The dictionary form is 「あそぶ」. The polite form is 「あそびます」. The te-form is 「あそんで」, and the plain past is 「あそんだ」. The plain negative is 「あそばない」. Now a very useful pattern. Take the stem, 「あそび」, add 「に」, and then a movement verb. 「あそびにいく」 is to go somewhere to have fun. 「あそびにくる」 is to come over for fun. The 「に」 here marks your purpose. You can say 「ともだちのうちにあそびにいきました」, I went over to my friend's house to hang out. The stem also works as a noun: 「あそび」 is play or fun. So, a regular 「ぶ」 verb with a handy purpose pattern: 「あそぶ」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a kindergarten playground. A little kid hides behind a slide, jumps out at his friend, and shouts boo. His friend gasps and says, ah, so, boo. Ah so boo, which sounds like 「あそぶ」. Then they both scream with laughter, run off to the swings, and do it all over again for the rest of the afternoon. Nobody is keeping score. There is no sport and no instrument. It is just free, silly fun. That is exactly the feeling of this verb. So, a kid on a playground saying ah, so, boo: 「あそぶ」, to play and have fun." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Unlike English play, it is not for sports or instruments, which use 「する」 and 「ひく」. It means kids playing or adults hanging out, as in 「ともだちとあそびます」.",
      "The kanji is usually explained as a fluttering banner plus the movement radical, drifting about freely. The reading 「ゆう」 gives 「遊園地|ゆうえんち」, an amusement park.",
      "It models the 「ぶ」 row, with te-form 「あそんで」. Stem plus 「に」 plus a movement verb gives purpose, as in 「あそびにいく」, to go somewhere for fun.",
      "Picture a kid jumping out from behind a slide and his friend gasping ah, so, boo, 「あそぶ」, just free, silly fun with no sport or instrument."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[遊びます|あそびます|あそぶ]。",
    en: "I hang out with my friends." },
  "いそぐ|急ぐ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「急|いそ」 is read here with its native reading. At the bottom of the kanji sits 「心|こころ」, heart, the same piece you see in 「心配する|しんぱいする」, to worry. A way to remember it: picture your heart racing when you are running late. The Chinese-origin reading is 「きゅう」, and you already know it from 「急に|きゅうに」, suddenly. Something sudden and something hurried share the same feeling of a heart that will not slow down. The verb is a u-verb ending in 「ぐ」, and those have a special te-form: 「いそいで」, not with a 「て」 but with a 「で」. The polite form is 「いそぎます」. You will hear 「いそいでください」, please hurry, and the opposite, very useful phrase 「いそがなくてもいいです」, you do not have to hurry. Its natural opposite in this lesson is 「ゆっくり」, slowly and unhurriedly. So, a racing heart: 「いそぐ」, to hurry." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji, 「きゅう」, is all over Japanese train stations, so it is worth knowing. A local train that stops everywhere is 「普通|ふつう」. A faster train that skips some stations is 「急行|きゅうこう」, a hurrying train. Faster still is the limited express, 「特急|とっきゅう」, a special hurry. Notice how 「とく」 and 「きゅう」 squeeze together with a small 「っ」. You will also see 「至急|しきゅう」 on office emails, meaning urgent, please deal with it right away. And of course 「急に|きゅうに」, suddenly. So when you stand on a platform and see this kanji on the display, you know that train is in a hurry. The same rushing heart is behind all of them. So, from express trains to urgent emails: the family of 「いそぐ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some everyday ways to use this verb. When you are rushing and someone stops you, say 「すみません、いそいでいます」, sorry, I am in a hurry. The te-form also works like an adverb meaning in a hurry. 「いそいでたべました」 is I ate in a rush, and 「いそいでかえりました」 is I hurried home. There is also a famous proverb: 「いそがばまわれ」. It literally means if you are in a hurry, go the long way around. The idea is that the safe, steady route is often faster than a risky shortcut, a bit like the English saying haste makes waste. People quote it when someone is rushing and making mistakes. So, you can be in a hurry, do things in a hurry, and remember that sometimes the long way is the fast way: 「いそぐ」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture yourself late for the train, sprinting down the street. Suddenly you step in a giant puddle of sticky green slime. Your shoe makes a horrible squelch, and you yell, eew, so goo. Eew so goo, which sounds like 「いそぐ」. You cannot stop to clean it. You have to keep running, slime and all, your heart pounding. And remember that pounding heart sits right at the bottom of the kanji. So whenever you need to hurry, picture yourself racing for the train with one gooey shoe, shouting eew, so goo. A rushed run through slime: 「いそぐ」, to hurry." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The heart 「心|こころ」 sits at the bottom, like a heart racing when you are late, and the reading 「きゅう」 gives 「急に|きゅうに」, suddenly. Its te-form is 「いそいで」, with 「で」.",
      "The reading 「きゅう」 marks faster trains, 「急行|きゅうこう」 and the limited express 「特急|とっきゅう」, and urgent emails say 「至急|しきゅう」.",
      "Say 「すみません、いそいでいます」 when rushing, and use the te-form for in a hurry. The proverb 「いそがばまわれ」 means the long way can be faster.",
      "Picture sprinting for the train, stepping in sticky slime and yelling eew, so goo, 「いそぐ」, with your heart pounding like the bottom of the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "[急いで|いそいで|いそぐ]ください。",
    en: "Please hurry." },
  "かえす|返す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「返|かえ」 is read with its native reading. On the bottom left is the movement radical, and inside is a piece meaning to turn back or to flip over. So it is a movement that turns back around: returning. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「へん」, which you know from 「返事|へんじ」, a reply, words sent back. And 「お返し|おかえし」 is a return gift, given back to say thanks. Now the key contrast. 「帰る|かえる」, to go home, sounds almost the same and also means going back. The difference is who or what goes back. With 「かえる」, you yourself go back home. With 「かえす」, you send a thing back to someone. So you 「かえる」 to your house, but you 「かえす」 a book to the library. You mark what you return with 「を」 and the person with 「に」: 「ともだちにほんをかえす」. It pairs naturally with 「借りる|かりる」, to borrow. First you borrow, then you return. So, a movement that turns back, applied to a thing: 「かえす」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has an intransitive partner, and the pair follows a common pattern. 「かえす」, ending in 「す」, is when you return something. It takes 「を」 for the object. Its partner, 「返る|かえる」, is when something comes back by itself. You will often see it as 「かえってくる」, to come back. 「てがみのへんじがかえってきた」 means the reply to my letter came back. Japanese has many pairs like this, where the 「す」 verb is the one you do to something. Think of 「出す|だす」 and 「出る|でる」, to put out and to come out. Now the forms. Since it ends in 「す」, the te-form is 「かえして」, so you get 「かえしてください」, please give it back. That is exactly what you say when a friend has kept your manga too long. So, you return it, and it comes back: 「かえす」 and 「かえる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Returning things in Japan goes well beyond library books. Gift-giving has a strong sense of balance. When you receive a gift, you often give something back, called 「お返し|おかえし」. There is a customary guideline for big occasions like weddings or condolences, called 「半返し|はんがえし」, half return, meaning a return gift worth about half of what you received. Notice the sound change, 「かえし」 becoming 「がえし」 inside the compound. Another well-known example is White Day on March fourteenth. On Valentine's Day, women traditionally give chocolate. A month later, men are expected to give something back, and the White Day gift is the 「おかえし」. So in Japan, returning is not only about borrowed items. It is about keeping relationships in balance: 「かえす」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a cheeky crow that steals your shiny spoon from a café table. The next day, the crow feels guilty. It flies back, lands on your table, drops the spoon, and squawks kah, then hisses ssss as if saying sorry. Kah, ssss: 「かえす」. The crow has returned what it took. Notice that the crow did not go home. It brought a thing back to you. That is exactly the difference from 「帰る|かえる」, where you yourself go home. So whenever you return a book or pay back money, picture that guilty crow dropping the spoon with a kah and a ssss. A crow bringing back what it stole: 「かえす」, to return something." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a movement that turns back, applied to a thing you send back, unlike 「帰る|かえる」, where you yourself go home. Mark it 「ともだちにほんをかえす」.",
      "Its intransitive partner 「返る|かえる」 means something comes back, often as 「かえってくる」. The te-form gives 「かえしてください」, please give it back.",
      "Gift-giving keeps balance with an 「お返し|おかえし」, like the half-value 「半返し|はんがえし」 or the White Day gift men give back after Valentine's Day.",
      "Picture a guilty crow returning your stolen spoon with a kah and a ssss, 「かえす」, bringing a thing back rather than going home."
    ],
    ja: "{本|ほん}を[返します|かえします|かえす]。",
    en: "I'll return the book." },
  "けす|消す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「消|け」 is read here with its native reading. On the left are three drops of water, the water radical. On the right is a piece that means small or diminishing. So you can picture water making something smaller and smaller until it is gone, the way water puts out a fire. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「しょう」, and you know it from 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, a firefighter, someone who fights fires. That is a perfect sibling, since putting out fires is exactly the image of this kanji. In daily life, the verb covers anything you make disappear. You turn off the lights, 「でんきをけす」, the TV, 「テレビをけす」, and the air conditioner, 「エアコンをけす」. You also erase writing from a board or a page with it. The opposite, to turn things on, is 「つける」. It is a u-verb, so you get 「けします」 and 「けして」. So, water putting out a fire: 「けす」, to make something go off or go away." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has an intransitive partner too. 「消える|きえる」 is when something goes out or disappears by itself. 「でんきがきえた」 means the light went out, maybe a power cut. Compare 「でんきをけした」, I turned off the light. Notice the reading of the kanji changes from 「け」 to 「き」. The same pattern shows up with turning things on: 「つける」 is when you switch something on, and 「つく」 is when it comes on by itself. One more handy sibling sits in every pencil case. An eraser is 「消しゴム|けしゴム」, an erasing rubber, mixing a native verb stem with a loanword from Dutch. So, you erase with a 「けしゴム」, you switch off with 「けす」, and things vanish on their own with 「きえる」. All three share the same water putting out a fire." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb covers more than switches. You put out a fire or a candle with it: 「ひをけす」. On a phone or computer, it means to delete: 「しゃしんをけす」, to delete a photo. On a whiteboard it means to wipe the writing off. But there are a few traps. For a running tap, you do not use it. You say 「みずをとめる」, stop the water. For a computer or phone power switch, 「けす」 is fine in casual talk, but you will also hear 「でんげんをきる」, cut the power. And one mistake English speakers make: you do not close a TV. Use 「けす」, not 「しめる」. So, flames, lights, screens, and writing all go away with 「けす」. Water you stop instead." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a birthday cake covered in candles. Instead of blowing them out, the birthday girl blows a big, dramatic kiss across the table. Mwah. The kiss flies through the air and every candle goes out at once. Kiss, which sounds a lot like 「けす」. Then she blows another kiss at the lamp, and the light goes off. One more at the TV, and the screen goes black. Everyone cheers. So whenever you want to turn something off or make something disappear, picture that magical kiss putting it out. And remember the water drops on the left of the kanji, doing the same job. A kiss that switches things off: 「けす」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Water on the left plus small on the right pictures water shrinking a fire until it is gone, as in 「消防士|しょうぼうし」. It turns off lights and TVs, the opposite of 「つける」.",
      "Its intransitive partner is 「消える|きえる」, to go out by itself, as in 「でんきがきえた」. An eraser is a 「消しゴム|けしゴム」.",
      "It works for fires, screens, deleting photos and wiping boards, but a running tap uses 「みずをとめる」, and you never close a TV with 「しめる」.",
      "Picture a birthday girl blowing a magical kiss that puts out every candle, then the lamp and the TV: kiss, 「けす」."
    ],
    ja: "{電気|でんき}を[消して|けして|けす]ください。",
    en: "Please turn off the light." },
  "しぬ|死ぬ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「死|し」 is read 「し」 here. On the left is a radical that stands for bones or remains. On the right is a small shape for a person, often described as someone kneeling or fallen. Bones beside a person: death. It is a heavy kanji, but a clear one. The grammar point is why the textbook includes this word so early. This is the only common modern verb that ends in 「ぬ」. So it is the model for that whole row. Its polite form is 「しにます」, and its te-form is 「しんで」, following the same pattern as 「よむ」 becoming 「よんで」 and 「あそぶ」 becoming 「あそんで」. If you can conjugate 「しぬ」, you have mastered the 「ぬ」 row in one go. A cultural note on politeness: when talking about a person's death, Japanese speakers usually prefer the softer 「なくなる」, to pass away. 「しぬ」 is fine for pets, characters in stories, and grammar practice, but it can sound blunt about people. You will also hear it in jokes, like saying you are dying of hunger. So, bones beside a person, and the one verb ending in 「ぬ」: 「しぬ」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This verb explains a superstition you will notice in Japan. One Chinese-origin reading of the number four is 「し」, which sounds exactly like this kanji. Because of that, many people avoid the number four. Hospitals and hotels sometimes skip room numbers with four, and gifts are rarely given in sets of four. When counting, people usually say 「よん」 for four instead of 「し」, partly to avoid that gloomy sound. Nine has a similar problem: its reading 「く」 sounds like suffering. So the number you will hear most is 「きゅう」 for nine. This is why, in Genki, you learn 「よん」 and 「きゅう」 as the everyday readings. It is not just a random rule. It quietly keeps death and suffering out of daily counting. So, the sound of four and the sound of 「しぬ」 are a little too close for comfort." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a trap with the te-iru form. In English, is dying describes something in progress. But 「しんでいます」 does not mean is dying. It means is dead, a state that resulted from dying. This is because 「しぬ」 is a change that happens in an instant, like 「けっこんする」, to get married. With verbs like these, the te-iru form describes the result that is still true now. 「けっこんしています」 means is married, not is getting married. So 「このむしはしんでいます」 means this bug is dead. If you really want to say about to die, you would use something different, like 「しにそうです」, looks like it is going to die. So when you see 「しんでいます」, think is dead. It is the lasting result of 「しぬ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Despite its heavy meaning, this verb pops up a lot in casual exaggeration, just like in English. 「しにそう」 means I am about to die, and young people use it all the time. 「おなかがすいてしにそう」 is I am starving to death, and 「あつくてしにそう」 is it is so hot I could die. There is also 「しぬほど」, to the point of death, used as a very strong very. 「しぬほどおいしい」 means insanely delicious. These are fine with friends, but they sound childish or rude in polite settings, so keep them out of conversations with teachers or bosses. And remember, when talking about someone who has actually passed away, choose the gentler words instead. So, a dramatic exaggeration with friends, and a word to handle carefully elsewhere: 「しぬ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the only common modern verb ending in 「ぬ」, so it models that row with 「しにます」 and 「しんで」. For people, the softer 「なくなる」 is preferred.",
      "One reading of four is 「し」, the same sound as this verb, so many people avoid four and count with 「よん」 instead.",
      "Because dying happens in an instant, 「しんでいます」 means is dead, not is dying. For about to die, use 「しにそうです」.",
      "Young people exaggerate with 「しにそう」, as in 「おなかがすいてしにそう」, and 「しぬほど」 as a strong very, but keep these away from teachers and bosses."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}の{犬|いぬ}が[死にました|しにました|しぬ]。",
    en: "My dog died." },
  "たばこをすう|たばこを吸う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a set phrase: 「たばこ」, tobacco or cigarettes, plus 「を」, plus the verb 「吸う|すう」, to inhale. First, 「たばこ」. It is a loanword, but an old one from Portuguese, tabaco, brought by Portuguese traders in the fifteen hundreds. Because it arrived so long ago, it is usually written in hiragana, like a native word. Now the kanji 「吸|す」, read with its native reading. On the left is the mouth radical, 「口|くち」, mouth. So it is something you do with your mouth: suck in or breathe in. The verb also works for breathing in air and for sucking through a straw. So Japanese does not have a single word for smoking. You literally breathe in tobacco. It is a u-verb, so you get 「すいます」 and 「すって」. A very common question is 「たばこをすってもいいですか」, may I smoke. And a sign or rule might say 「ここでたばこをすってはいけません」. In Japan, smoking is often limited to special smoking areas, so look for those. So, an old Portuguese loanword plus a mouth verb: 「たばこをすう」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb here is the everyday word for breathing in. At the doctor, you will hear 「いきをすってください」, please breathe in. And the next instruction is 「はいてください」, please breathe out, using the verb 「吐く|はく」. So 「すう」 and 「はく」 are a natural pair. You also use 「すう」 for drinking through a straw, and a vacuum cleaner sucks up dust with a related word, 「すいこむ」, to suck in. The Chinese-origin reading of the kanji is 「きゅう」. It appears in 「呼吸|こきゅう」, breathing, combining a kanji for breathing out with one for breathing in. So one mouth kanji covers air, juice through a straw, and smoke. That is why smoking is simply 「たばこをすう」: breathing in the tobacco." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's relationship with smoking has changed a lot. For a long time, smoking was common in restaurants, trains, and offices. Today it is much more restricted. A national law that took full effect in twenty twenty bans smoking inside most restaurants and public buildings, with exceptions for special smoking rooms. Many city areas also ban smoking while walking on the street. So smokers look for a designated smoking spot, called 「喫煙所|きつえんじょ」. The opposite sign, which you will see everywhere, is 「禁煙|きんえん」, no smoking. Notice that both use the same smoke kanji, 「えん」. In formal signs Japanese often uses these Chinese-origin words, while in conversation people just say 「たばこをすう」. So if you see 「きんえん」, put the cigarette away." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the verb. Picture a woman named Sue sitting at a café. She has the biggest milkshake you have ever seen, with a long, bendy straw. She takes one enormous breath in and sucks the whole thing down in one go. Her friends chant her name: Sue, Sue, Sue. That sound matches 「すう」. Then she does the same with a deep breath of fresh mountain air. That is the core of the verb: breathing or sucking something in through your mouth. And remember the mouth radical on the left of the kanji. So when someone breathes in smoke, picture Sue with that giant straw. Breathing in through the mouth: 「すう」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「たばこ」 is an old loanword from Portuguese, and 「吸う|すう」 has the mouth radical and means to breathe in, so smoking is literally breathing in tobacco.",
      "At the doctor you hear 「いきをすってください」, breathe in, then 「はいてください」, breathe out. The reading 「きゅう」 appears in 「呼吸|こきゅう」, breathing.",
      "Smoking is now banned in most restaurants and public buildings, so smokers look for a 「喫煙所|きつえんじょ」, while 「禁煙|きんえん」 means no smoking.",
      "Picture Sue sucking down a giant milkshake through a straw while friends chant Sue, Sue, matching 「すう」, breathing in through the mouth."
    ],
    ja: "ここで[たばこを吸っては|たばこをすっては|たばこをすう]いけません。",
    en: "You must not smoke here." },
  "つかう|使う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「使|つか」 is read with its native reading. On the left is the person radical, a person standing sideways. The right side is a piece connected with officials and duties. Historically the kanji had to do with a person you send on an errand, someone you put to use. From there the meaning widened to using anything at all. So 「つかう」 is to put something or someone to work for you. You mark the thing you use with 「を」: 「パソコンをつかう」, use a computer, 「はしをつかう」, use chopsticks. A very handy phrase is 「つかってもいいですか」, may I use it, perfect for borrowing a pen or a phone. It is a u-verb ending in 「う」, so you get 「つかいます」 and the te-form 「つかって」, with that little doubled sound. A way to remember it: you often need help catching a tool you use, so think of catching and using. So, a person put to work on a task: 「つかう」, to use." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb shows a quirk that every verb ending in 「う」 shares. In the polite form, it is 「つかいます」. But in the plain negative, a 「わ」 sound suddenly appears: 「つかわない」, not 「つかあない」. The same thing happens with 「かう」, to buy, which becomes 「かわない」, and 「あう」, to meet, which becomes 「あわない」. The reason is historical. In older Japanese these verbs ended in a different consonant, and the 「わ」 is a leftover of it in the negative. In the te-form, they all get the small 「っ」: 「つかって」, 「かって」, 「あって」. So whenever you meet a verb ending in a plain 「う」, remember the hidden 「わ」 in the negative. 「このペンはつかわないでください」 means please do not use this pen. So, a hidden 「わ」 in the negative: 「つかう」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese uses this verb in some places English would not. You spend money with it: 「おかねをつかう」, to use money. You spend time with it as well: 「じかんをつかう」. And there is a lovely expression, 「気を使う|きをつかう」, literally to use your spirit, meaning to be considerate and attentive to others' feelings. It is a very Japanese value. Someone who always notices if your cup is empty is good at it. If someone is going to too much trouble for you, you can say 「きをつかわないでください」, please do not worry about me. You will also see 「使い方|つかいかた」, how to use something, made by adding 「かた」 to the stem. So you can use tools, money, time, and even your attention: 「つかう」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「し」, and it shows up in some useful places. On a restroom door in Japan, you may see 「使用中|しようちゅう」, in use, occupied. The middle kanji means use as well. And 「大使|たいし」 is an ambassador, literally a great messenger. So 「大使館|たいしかん」 is an embassy, the ambassador's building. This connects back to the old meaning of someone sent on an errand. An ambassador is a person sent on the biggest errand of all, to represent a country. So the kanji carries both ideas: putting things to use, and sending people on missions. Spot the 「し」 reading on signs, and the native reading, 「つか」, in the verb. A person sent out to serve: 「つかう」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The person radical hints at its old sense of someone sent on an errand, put to use, which widened to using anything. A handy phrase is 「つかってもいいですか」.",
      "Like all verbs ending in a plain 「う」, a hidden 「わ」 appears in the negative, 「つかわない」, a leftover of an older consonant.",
      "It also means spending money and time, and 「気を使う|きをつかう」 means being considerate. Say 「きをつかわないでください」, please do not worry about me.",
      "The reading 「し」 appears in 「使用中|しようちゅう」, occupied, and 「大使|たいし」, an ambassador, a person sent on the biggest errand."
    ],
    ja: "パソコンを[使っても|つかっても|つかう]いいですか。",
    en: "May I use the computer?" },
  "はいる|入る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「入|はい」 is read with its native reading here. It is only two strokes, and it looks like an arrow pointing in or a wedge pushing into an opening. Be careful not to mix it up with 「人|ひと」, person. The person kanji leans the other way. The same kanji has another native reading, 「い」, in 「入れる|いれる」, to put something in. You enter, or you put something inside. Now for the grammar. Mark the place you go into with 「に」: 「へやにはいる」, enter the room. Japanese also uses it in some surprising set phrases. 「おふろにはいる」 is to take a bath, literally to enter the bath. And 「保険に入る|ほけんにはいる」 is to buy insurance, entering an insurance plan. Here is a big trap. It ends in 「いる」, so it looks like a ru-verb, but it is actually a u-verb. So the forms are 「はいります」 and 「はいって」, with that doubled sound. So, an arrow pointing in: 「はいる」, to enter." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This little kanji turns up in lots of signs and life events. At the door of a building you will see 「入口|いりぐち」, entrance, the entering mouth. Notice the reading 「いり」 here. Its partner is 「出口|でぐち」, exit, the coming-out mouth. Those two signs alone will get you in and out of every train station. The Chinese-origin reading is 「にゅう」. 「入学|にゅうがく」 is entering a school, and a school entrance ceremony is a big spring event in Japan. 「入院|にゅういん」 is being admitted to hospital, entering the hospital. And 「入れる|いれる」 is putting something in, the transitive partner. So you have 「いりぐち」 at the door, 「にゅうがく」 at school, 「にゅういん」 at the hospital, and 「はいる」 for stepping in. The arrow keeps pointing inward." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese stretches this verb well beyond walking through a door. You join a club or a team with it: 「テニスサークルにはいる」, join the tennis club. You get into a university with it: 「だいがくにはいる」. It also describes what is inside something: 「かばんにほんがはいっています」, there are books in the bag. And there is a very useful phrase, 「気に入る|きにいる」, to take a liking to something. Careful, the reading there is 「いる」, not 「はいる」. In a shop you might say 「このかばん、きにいりました」, I really like this bag. So with 「に」 for the place you go into, the verb covers rooms, baths, clubs, schools, and even your heart. Step in, sign up, or fall for something: 「はいる」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture yourself knocking on a friend's door. Their roommate, Rue, swings it open with a huge grin. You wave and say, hi, Rue. Hi, Rue, which sounds like 「はいる」. Rue steps aside and pulls you in, and you walk into a warm room full of friends. Every time you visit, it is the same: you say hi, Rue, and you step inside. And remember the shape of the kanji, two strokes like an arrow pointing in through the doorway. So whenever you need the verb for entering, picture greeting Rue at the door and walking straight in. Hi, Rue, and in you go: 「はいる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji looks like an arrow pointing in, and the place takes 「に」, as in 「おふろにはいる」. It looks like a ru-verb but is a u-verb, so the te-form is 「はいって」.",
      "It gives 「入口|いりぐち」, entrance, opposite 「出口|でぐち」, and with the reading 「にゅう」, 「入学|にゅうがく」, entering school, and 「入院|にゅういん」.",
      "It also means joining a club or school and being inside something, as in 「かばんにほんがはいっています」. Note 「気に入る|きにいる」 reads 「いる」.",
      "Picture greeting your friend's roommate Rue at the door with hi, Rue, 「はいる」, and walking straight in."
    ],
    ja: "{部屋|へや}に[入って|はいって|はいる]ください。",
    en: "Please come into the room." },
  "もつ|持つ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「持|も」 is read with its native reading. On the left is the hand radical, a squished form of the hand kanji. The right side mostly gives the sound. So the meaning lives in that hand: holding something in your hand. The verb ends in 「つ」, so its forms are 「もちます」 and 「もって」. You carry or hold things with 「を」: 「にもつをもつ」, carry the bags. Notice that 「荷物|にもつ」 ends with the same sound, a happy coincidence. Now the important twist. 「もっています」 often means to own or to have, not just holding right now. 「くるまをもっています」 is I own a car. The family is very useful. 「持ってくる|もってくる」 is to bring a thing, hold it and come. 「持っていく|もっていく」 is to take a thing along, hold it and go. And 「お金持ち|おかねもち」 is a money holder, a rich person. So, a hand gripping something: 「もつ」, to hold, to carry, and to own." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a model for the 「つ」 row, so learning it unlocks others. The polite form is 「もちます」, with 「ち」. The te-form is 「もって」, with a small 「っ」. The plain past is 「もった」, and the negative is 「もたない」, with 「た」. The same pattern works for 「待つ|まつ」, to wait: 「まちます」, 「まって」, 「またない」. And for 「立つ|たつ」, to stand. Now a practical point. When someone asks 「かさをもっていますか」, it can mean do you own an umbrella, or do you have one on you right now. Context decides. If you do not, say 「もっていません」. Do not answer with 「もちません」, which sounds like a general habit, as in I never carry. So, the 「つ」 row in one verb, and 「もっています」 for have: 「もつ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is where you will hear this verb in daily life. At a fast-food counter in Japan, staff ask whether you will eat in or take it out. Takeout is 「お持ち帰り|おもちかえり」, literally holding it and going home. Your belongings are 「持ち物|もちもの」, held things. A school trip handout lists the 「もちもの」 you need to bring. And if someone is carrying heavy bags, a polite offer is 「おもちしましょうか」, shall I carry that for you. That humble form uses 「お」 plus the stem plus 「する」, a pattern you will learn later. It is very common in shops, when staff offer to carry your bag to the door. So, takeout, belongings, and a kind offer to help: they all grow from 「もつ」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The right side of this kanji is 「寺|てら」, which on its own means a Buddhist temple. Here, though, it is not about temples. It mostly gives the sound. You can see the same piece in 「時|じ」, time, with the sun on the left, and 「持|じ」 shares that reading 「じ」 in its Chinese-origin words. You can also spot it in 「待つ|まつ」, to wait, with the walking radical on the left. So when you see the temple piece on the right of a kanji, you have a good hint that it belongs to this family. One more lovely word uses this kanji: 「気持ち|きもち」, a feeling, literally how you hold your spirit. 「きもちいい」 means it feels good. So, a hand holding things, and even holding a feeling: 「もつ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The hand radical on the left means holding in your hand. In te-iru form, 「もっています」 often means to own, as in 「くるまをもっています」.",
      "It models the 「つ」 row like 「待つ|まつ」. To say you do not have something, answer 「もっていません」, not 「もちません」, which sounds like a habit.",
      "Takeout is 「お持ち帰り|おもちかえり」, your belongings are 「持ち物|もちもの」, and a polite offer to carry bags is 「おもちしましょうか」.",
      "The right side is 「寺|てら」, a sound part shared with 「時|じ」 and 「待つ|まつ」. It also appears in 「気持ち|きもち」, a feeling."
    ],
    ja: "{荷物|にもつ}を[持ちます|もちます|もつ]。",
    en: "I'll carry the bags." },
  "やすむ|休む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is one of the classic kanji pictures. The kanji 「休|やす」 is a person, the person radical on the left, leaning against a tree, 「木|き」, on the right. Someone resting against a tree on a hot day: taking a break. In this verb it has its native reading, 「やす」. The noun form, 「休み|やすみ」, means a holiday, a day off, a break, or an absence. Think of summer holidays and lunch breaks. Now the meaning that surprises English speakers. 「やすむ」 does not only mean to rest. It also means to be absent, to skip or take a day off. You mark what you skip with 「を」: 「がっこうをやすむ」, to miss school, 「アルバイトをやすむ」, to take a day off from your job. If you are sick, you might say 「きょうはやすみます」, I am taking today off. It is a u-verb ending in 「む」, so you get 「やすみます」 and 「やすんで」. So, a person leaning on a tree: 「やすむ」, to rest, or to take a day away." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji gives you a whole calendar of breaks. 「夏休み|なつやすみ」 is summer vacation, and 「冬休み|ふゆやすみ」 is winter break. 「昼休み|ひるやすみ」 is the lunch break at school or work. And the greeting you say at bedtime, 「おやすみなさい」, is really a polite command: please rest. With friends, just 「おやすみ」. The Chinese-origin reading is 「きゅう」. 「休日|きゅうじつ」 is a day off, and 「連休|れんきゅう」 is a run of holidays in a row. The most famous one in Japan is Golden Week, a string of public holidays from late April into early May. So, from lunch breaks to Golden Week to goodnight, the person leaning on a tree is everywhere: 「やすむ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how people use this verb at work and school. To call in sick, you might say 「かぜでやすみます」, I am taking the day off because of a cold. The 「で」 gives the reason. If a friend looks exhausted, you can say 「すこしやすんでください」, please rest a little. In shops and restaurants, watch for the sign 「定休日|ていきゅうび」, regular closing day, the day each week they are always shut. Many places close on a set weekday, so this sign saves a wasted trip. And when you need a pause in a meeting or a class, someone may say 「ちょっとやすみましょう」, let us take a short break. So you can skip, rest, and pause, all with one verb: 「やすむ」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a huge sumo wrestler after a long, sweaty training session. He waddles over to a big shady tree, leans his back against the trunk, and slides down to the ground with a sigh. His friend cheers, yeah, sumo, take a rest. Yeah, sumo, which sounds like 「やすむ」. Now look at the kanji in your mind: the person on the left, leaning on the tree on the right. That is our sumo wrestler, finally taking a break. And if he does not show up to practice tomorrow, that is also this verb. So, a sumo wrestler resting against a tree: 「やすむ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a person leaning on a tree, resting, but the verb also means to be absent, with 「を」 for what you skip, as in 「がっこうをやすむ」.",
      "It gives 「夏休み|なつやすみ」 and 「昼休み|ひるやすみ」, and bedtime 「おやすみなさい」 is literally please rest. The reading 「きゅう」 appears in 「連休|れんきゅう」.",
      "Call in sick with 「かぜでやすみます」, using 「で」 for the reason, and watch for 「定休日|ていきゅうび」, a shop's regular closing day.",
      "Picture a sumo wrestler sliding down against a shady tree while a friend cheers yeah, sumo, 「やすむ」, just like the person beside the tree in the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は{学校|がっこう}を[休みます|やすみます|やすむ]。",
    en: "I'm skipping school today." },
  "しめる|閉める": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「閉|し」 is read with its native reading. The outer frame is 「門|もん」, a gate. Inside sits a piece that looks like a bar or a bolt jammed across the doors. So it is a gate with the bar in place: closed. Its partner is 「開ける|あける」, to open, the same gate with the bar being lifted out. Learning them as a pair makes both easy, since they share the gate and only the inside differs. Other gate kanji you know include 「聞く|きく」, to listen, with an ear at the gate, and 「時間|じかん」, with the sun shining through. You close things with 「を」: 「まどをしめる」, close the window, and 「ドアをしめる」, close the door. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「しめます」 and 「しめて」. 「しめてください」 is please close it. A small heads up: there is a similar verb, 「しまる」, where the door closes on its own. 「しめる」 is when you close it. So, a gate with the bar locked in: 「しめる」, to close." },
      { title: "Shop signs", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「へい」, and you will see it on shop doors. 「閉店|へいてん」 means the shop is closed, or closing time. Its opposite uses the open kanji: 「開店|かいてん」, the shop opens for business. You may see a sign showing the opening and closing times together. In conversation, though, people use the native verbs. For a shop, the intransitive one is natural: 「みせはなんじにしまりますか」, what time does the shop close. The shop closes, so the door-closes-by-itself verb fits. But if you are the shop owner, you say 「みせをしめます」, I close up the shop. So 「へいてん」 on signs, 「しまる」 when the shop closes, and 「しめる」 when you are the one shutting the door." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful, because the sound 「しめる」 belongs to several different verbs with different kanji. Our word, with the gate kanji, is closing doors and windows. But 「締める|しめる」, with the thread radical, is tightening or fastening: 「ネクタイをしめる」, to put on a tie, and 「シートベルトをしめる」, to fasten your seatbelt. On a plane or bus you will hear 「シートベルトをおしめください」, please fasten your seatbelt. There is also 「湿る|しめる」, with the water radical, meaning to get damp. Luckily, context makes them easy to tell apart. Doors and windows mean closing. Ties and belts mean fastening. Laundry on a rainy day means damp. So, the gate kanji for doors: 「閉める|しめる」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture your friend leaving the window wide open as a huge storm rolls in. You warn her, if you do not close it now, she may rue the day. She may rue, which sounds like 「しめる」. She laughs and ignores you. Then the rain blows in, soaks her desk, and her homework turns to mush. She definitely rues the day. Next time, she runs across the room and slams the window shut before the first drop falls. So whenever you need the verb for closing, picture her rushing to shut it, so she does not rue the day. She may rue it: 「しめる」, to close." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a gate, 「門|もん」, with a bar jammed across it, the pair of 「開ける|あける」 with its bar lifted. You close things with 「を」, while 「しまる」 means it closes by itself.",
      "Signs say 「閉店|へいてん」, closed, opposite 「開店|かいてん」. A shop closes by itself with 「しまる」, but the owner closes up with 「しめる」.",
      "Watch for 「締める|しめる」, to fasten a tie or seatbelt, and 「湿る|しめる」, to get damp. Doors and windows use the gate kanji.",
      "Picture a friend who ignores a storm and leaves the window open, so she may rue the day, 「しめる」, until she rushes to shut it."
    ],
    ja: "ドアを[閉めて|しめて|しめる]ください。",
    en: "Please close the door." },
  "おしえる|教える": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「教|おし」 is read with its native reading here. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「きょう」, and that opens up a whole family of school words. 「教科書|きょうかしょ」 is a textbook, a book that teaches a subject. 「教室|きょうしつ」 is a classroom, a teaching room. And 「教師|きょうし」 is a schoolteacher. So whenever you see this kanji, think teaching. Now the grammar. The person you teach takes 「に」, and the thing you teach takes 「を」. 「ともだちにえいごをおしえる」 is to teach English to a friend. It is a ru-verb, so the forms are 「おしえます」 and 「おしえて」. Here is a meaning English speakers often miss. 「おしえる」 also means to tell or to let someone know, especially information. 「でんわばんごうをおしえてください」 is please tell me your phone number. In English you would never say teach me your number, but in Japanese it is completely natural. So, the teaching kanji: 「おしえる」, to teach, and also to tell someone what they want to know." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let us look at the shapes in this kanji. On the right is a radical often called the strike or tap radical, traditionally explained as a hand holding a stick. It appears in kanji about doing something actively. On the lower left is 「子|こ」, child. Above the child is a piece usually explained as crossed marks, often linked to learning or imitation. So the traditional picture is a teacher with a stick, guiding a child as they learn. It is a strict, old-fashioned classroom. You can see the same right side in 「数|かず」, number, and 「放す|はなす」, to let go. So, a hand with a pointer and a child learning below: 「おしえる」, the teaching kanji." },
      { title: "Teach or learn", text: "Japanese has several verbs around teaching and learning, and they work from different sides. 「おしえる」 is the teacher's side, giving knowledge. From the student's side, the most common verb is 「習う|ならう」, to learn from someone, often a skill or lessons: 「ピアノをならう」. There is also 「学ぶ|まなぶ」, a more formal or bookish word for studying and learning. And 「べんきょうする」 is to study, the effort you put in yourself. Here is a neat one: 「教わる|おそわる」, to be taught, uses the same kanji with a different reading. 「せんせいにおそわりました」 means I was taught by my teacher. So the teacher 「おしえる」, the student 「ならう」 or 「おそわる」, and everyone 「べんきょうする」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "One of the most useful sentences for a traveler uses this verb. When you are lost, you can say 「すみません、えきへのいきかたをおしえてください」, excuse me, please tell me how to get to the station. Here is a mini exchange. You ask, 「メールアドレスをおしえてもらえますか」, could you tell me your email address. Your friend replies, 「いいですよ」, sure. Notice that in English you would say tell me, but Japanese uses the teaching verb. One more point about politeness. You can ask your teacher 「おしえてください」, but do not tell your teacher 「おしえてあげます」, I will teach you, about something they asked. It can sound like you are talking down. So, directions, contact details, and lessons: all things you ask someone to 「おしえる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The person taught takes 「に」 and the subject takes 「を」. It also means to tell someone information, as in 「でんわばんごうをおしえてください」, please tell me your phone number.",
      "The traditional picture is a hand holding a stick on the right and a child learning on the lower left, a teacher guiding a pupil.",
      "This is the teacher's side, while students 「習う|ならう」 or are taught with 「教わる|おそわる」, and everyone 「べんきょうする」.",
      "Ask directions with 「えきへのいきかたをおしえてください」, but never offer your teacher 「おしえてあげます」, which can sound like talking down."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}は{日本語|にほんご}を[教えます|おしえます|おしえる]。",
    en: "The teacher teaches Japanese." },
  "わすれる|忘れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「忘|わす」 is read here with its native reading. It is built from two pieces stacked on top of each other. On top is 「亡|ぼう」, which means lost, gone, or perished. On the bottom is 「心|こころ」, heart or mind, the same piece you see in 「心配する|しんぱいする」, to worry, and 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry. Gone from the mind: forgetting. It is one of those kanji where the parts tell the story very honestly. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「わすれます」 and 「わすれて」. Now the second meaning. In Japanese, 「わすれる」 also covers leaving something behind by accident. 「かさをわすれました」 can mean I forgot my umbrella, as in I left it at home or on the train. The classic school excuse is 「しゅくだいをわすれました」, I forgot my homework. A friendly reminder is 「わすれないでください」, please do not forget. So, something lost from the heart: 「わすれる」, to forget, or to leave behind." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a famous reputation for returning lost things, and this verb is at the center of it. A forgotten item is 「忘れ物|わすれもの」, a forgotten thing. Train stations have lost property offices that collect huge numbers of umbrellas, phones, and wallets each year, and a surprising amount gets back to the owners. If you leave your umbrella on a train, you go to the station and say 「でんしゃにかさをわすれました」. Notice the 「に」 marking the place you left it. Before you get off, announcements often remind passengers, 「おわすれもののないように」, please make sure you have not left anything behind. Teachers say 「わすれものはありませんか」 at the end of a trip. So, the forgotten item has its own word, and a whole system to bring it home: 「わすれる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "A few patterns help this verb do more work. To forget to do something, put 「の」 after the dictionary form and then 「をわすれる」. 「でんわするのをわすれました」 means I forgot to call. The 「の」 turns the action into a thing you can forget. Next, there is the te-form plus 「しまう」, which you will meet later: 「わすれてしまいました」, I went and forgot, with a feeling of regret. And to say I have forgotten, as a current state, use 「わすれています」. Finally, here is a tip from the noun side. You can stick the stem onto another word, as in 「わすれもの」, a forgotten thing. So forget a thing with 「を」, forget to do with 「のを」, and leave it somewhere with 「に」: 「わすれる」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a party where you meet a nice man, and ten minutes later his name is gone from your head. You point at him and stammer, was it Ray? Ray? Ruh? Was Ray, ruh: it sounds like 「わすれる」. He laughs and says no, it is Steve. Everyone at the party now calls him was-it-Ray for the rest of the night. And remember the kanji: lost on top, heart below. His name is lost from your mind. So whenever something slips away, picture yourself pointing and stammering, was it Ray. Lost from the mind: 「わすれる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is lost, 「亡|ぼう」, on top of heart, 「心|こころ」, gone from the mind. It also means leaving something behind, as in 「かさをわすれました」.",
      "A forgotten item is a 「忘れ物|わすれもの」, and you report the place with 「に」, as in 「でんしゃにかさをわすれました」. Japan's lost property system often gets things back.",
      "To forget to do something, use the dictionary form plus 「のをわすれる」, as in 「でんわするのをわすれました」, I forgot to call.",
      "Picture stammering was it Ray at a man whose name slipped your mind, which sounds like 「わすれる」, lost from the mind."
    ],
    ja: "{宿題|しゅくだい}を[忘れました|わすれました|わすれる]。",
    en: "I forgot my homework." },
  "おりる|降りる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「降|お」 is read with its native reading here. On the left is the hill radical, a shape that stands for a mound or a slope. On the right is a piece usually explained as two feet stepping downward. So you can picture feet going down a hill: descending, coming down. The same kanji has another native reading in 「降る|ふる」, which is used for rain or snow falling: 「あめがふる」. Rain comes down from the sky, and you come down off a train. Same kanji, same downward movement. Now the grammar, which is where people trip. You get off something with 「を」: 「でんしゃをおりる」, get off the train. Its opposite, 「乗る|のる」, to get on, uses 「に」: 「でんしゃにのる」. So the pair is 「にのる」 and 「をおりる」. Learn them as a set. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「おります」 and 「おりて」. So, feet stepping down a hill: 「おりる」, to get off." },
      { title: "Down two ways", text: "This verb can be written with two different kanji, and they split the meaning nicely. With the hill-and-feet kanji, 「降りる|おりる」, it usually means getting off a vehicle: a train, a bus, a taxi, or a plane. With the simple down kanji, 「下りる|おりる」, it usually means going down something, like stairs or a mountain: 「かいだんをおりる」, to go down the stairs. Both are read the same and both are ru-verbs. In real life many people mix them a little, and you will often see the verb written in hiragana. But it is a useful guide. Getting off transport: the hill kanji. Moving downward: the down kanji. Notice both still take 「を」 for what you leave or go down. So, off the train or down the stairs, the sound stays 「おりる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Crowded Japanese trains are where this verb really earns its keep. If you are stuck at the back and your stop arrives, say 「すみません、おります」, excuse me, I am getting off. People will squeeze aside for you. Announcements say things like 「おおりのかたは」, those who are getting off, before the doors open. On a bus, you press the button when you want to get off, and the display lights up with a word meaning stopping next. If someone asks where you get off, they say 「どこでおりますか」. Notice 「で」 here marks the station where it happens. You can answer 「つぎでおります」, I get off at the next one. Getting on and off together is 「乗り降り|のりおり」. So, squeezing out of a packed train: 「おります」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a sleepy commuter dozing on the train. Suddenly the conductor announces his station. He jolts awake and gasps, oh, really, already? Oh really. Say it fast with a Japanese accent and it sounds like 「おりる」. He grabs his bag and leaps off the train just as the doors close, tumbling onto the platform. Every morning it happens again: he falls asleep, hears his stop, says oh really, and jumps off. And remember the kanji shape: feet stepping down a hill, just like his feet stepping down off the train. So, a startled oh really, and off you go: 「おりる」, to get off." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a hill with feet stepping down, the same kanji as rain falling in 「降る|ふる」. Learn the pair 「でんしゃにのる」 and 「でんしゃをおりる」.",
      "With the hill kanji, 「降りる|おりる」 means getting off transport, while 「下りる|おりる」 means going down stairs or a mountain, both taking 「を」.",
      "On a packed train say 「すみません、おります」, and mark the stop with 「で」, as in 「つぎでおります」. Getting on and off is 「乗り降り|のりおり」.",
      "Picture a sleepy commuter jolting awake at his stop, gasping oh really, which sounds like 「おりる」, as he leaps off the train."
    ],
    ja: "{電車|でんしゃ}を[降ります|おります|おりる]。",
    en: "I'll get off the train." },
  "かりる|借りる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「借|か」 is read with its native reading. On the left is the person radical. On the right is a piece meaning long ago, which mostly gives the kanji its sound. A way to remember it: you borrow from a person, so a person stands at the side. Here is the big trap. 「かりる」 is to borrow, to receive something for a while. Its opposite, 「貸す|かす」, is to lend, to give something for a while. They even sound alike, so mixing them up is the most common mistake. A way to remember it: in 「かりる」 the item comes to me. In 「かす」 it goes away from me. The person you borrow from takes 「に」 or 「から」: 「ともだちにほんをかりる」, borrow a book from a friend. It pairs naturally with 「返す|かえす」, to return. First you 「かりる」, then you 「かえす」. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「かります」 and 「かりて」. A useful request is 「かりてもいいですか」, may I borrow it. So, something that comes to you for a while: 「かりる」, to borrow." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese uses this verb in a few places that surprise English speakers. In a café or a shop, if you need the restroom, the polite way to ask is 「トイレをかりてもいいですか」, may I borrow the toilet. In English that sounds strange, but in Japanese it is perfectly normal and polite. You are using something that belongs to someone else for a short time. The same goes for a phone: 「でんわをかりてもいいですか」. You can also rent with it. 「くるまをかりる」 is to rent a car, and 「アパートをかりる」 is to rent an apartment. So it covers borrowing from a friend, renting from a company, and even using a shop's restroom. Anything you use for a while and do not keep: 「かりる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「しゃく」, and its most important word is 「借金|しゃっきん」, debt, literally borrowed money. Notice how 「しゃく」 shrinks to 「しゃっ」 before 「きん」. Now the family of lending and borrowing. 「貸す|かす」 is to lend. Put the stems together and you get 「貸し借り|かしかり」, lending and borrowing, the back and forth of favors between people. In Japanese culture, people often feel a bit uneasy about owing someone. Saying 「かりがある」 means I owe you one, I have a debt of a favor. So 「かり」 by itself can mean a favor you owe. That is a nice way to remember the core idea: when you borrow, you now owe. And 「返す|かえす」 settles it. Borrow, owe, and pay back: 「かりる」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture your friend Lee standing in your driveway, staring at your shiny new car. You toss him the keys and say, car, Lee, take it, just bring it back. Car, Lee, which sounds close to 「かりる」. Lee drives off grinning. He is borrowing your car. Now picture the important part: the car is coming toward Lee, to him, for a while. That is the direction of borrowing. When he brings it back on Sunday with a full tank, he 「かえす」. So whenever you borrow something, picture tossing the car keys to Lee. Car, Lee: 「かりる」, to borrow." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means to borrow, with the item coming to you, while 「貸す|かす」 means to lend. Borrow from someone with 「に」, then give it back with 「返す|かえす」.",
      "You also politely borrow a shop's toilet with 「トイレをかりてもいいですか」, and rent cars or apartments with this same verb.",
      "The reading 「しゃく」 gives 「借金|しゃっきん」, debt, and 「貸し借り|かしかり」 is lending and borrowing. Saying 「かりがある」 means I owe you one.",
      "Picture tossing your car keys to your friend Lee, car, Lee, 「かりる」, with the car coming to him for a while."
    ],
    ja: "{図書館|としょかん}で{本|ほん}を[借ります|かります|かりる]。",
    en: "I borrow books at the library." },
  "シャワーをあびる|シャワーを浴びる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is 「シャワー」, from English shower, plus 「を」, plus the verb 「浴びる|あびる」. The kanji 「浴|あ」 is read with its native reading. On the left are three drops of water, the water radical. The verb means to be poured over, to bathe in, to be showered with something. So in Japanese you do not take a shower. You pour the shower over yourself, or soak it up. That is why 「シャワーをとる」 is wrong, a very common mistake for English speakers. Always use 「あびる」. The same verb shows up with other things that pour over you, like soaking up sunlight. A sibling worth knowing: the light cotton summer kimono, 「浴衣|ゆかた」, uses this kanji. It began as a robe worn after bathing. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「シャワーをあびます」 and 「シャワーをあびて」. Compare the bath: for 「おふろ」 you use 「はいる」, to enter. For 「シャワー」 you use 「あびる」, to be poured over. So, water poured over you: 「シャワーをあびる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese bathing has its own order, and the shower plays a special role. At home, people usually wash and rinse their bodies outside the tub, using a shower or a bucket, while sitting on a small stool. Only when they are completely clean do they climb into the hot bath to soak. Because everyone gets in clean, the bath water is often shared by the whole family, one after another. At hot springs and public baths, the same rule is strict. You wash at the shower stations first, and then you soak. Getting into the bath unwashed is a big etiquette mistake. Many people shower quickly in the morning and take a long soak at night. So in Japan, 「シャワーをあびる」 is for getting clean, and the bath is for relaxing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「あびる」 means to have something pour over you, and it goes far beyond water. 「日光をあびる|にっこうをあびる」 is to bask in sunlight. 「ちゅうもくをあびる」 is to be in the spotlight, showered with attention. You will also hear about a performer who is showered with applause. The Chinese-origin reading of the kanji is 「よく」. 「浴室|よくしつ」 is a bathroom, the room for bathing, and 「入浴|にゅうよく」 is a formal word for taking a bath, entering the bathing. You will see it on hotel signs and hot spring rules. So whether it is water, sunshine, or a crowd's attention, it all pours over you with 「あびる」. And the formal bathing words carry the same kanji as 「よく」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a beekeeper who reaches up to grab a honeycomb from a shelf. The hive tips over and hundreds of bees pour down all over him like water from a shower head. He shrieks, ah, bees, ah, bees, roo. Ah bee roo, which sounds like 「あびる」. Luckily, they are friendly bees, and they just buzz around him like warm water. Every morning he says it is better than coffee. So whenever you take a shower, picture that cascade of bees pouring down over you. Something poured over you from above: 「シャワーをあびる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「浴びる|あびる」 has the water radical and means to be poured over, so you never say 「シャワーをとる」. A bath uses 「はいる」, but a shower uses 「あびる」.",
      "In Japan you wash under the shower outside the tub before soaking, since the clean bath water is shared, so the shower is for getting clean and the bath for relaxing.",
      "The verb covers anything pouring over you, like sunlight or attention, and the reading 「よく」 gives 「浴室|よくしつ」, a bathroom, and 「入浴|にゅうよく」.",
      "Picture a beekeeper whose hive tips over, showering him with bees as he shrieks ah, bee, roo, 「あびる」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[シャワーを浴びます|シャワーをあびます|シャワーをあびる]。",
    en: "I take a shower every morning." },
  "つける|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is usually written in hiragana, which is how you will see it most often. It is for switching something on: lights, the TV, the air conditioner, a heater. 「でんきをつける」 is to turn on the light, and 「テレビをつける」 is to turn on the TV. Its opposite is 「消す|けす」, to turn off, so learn them as a pair: 「つける」 and 「けす」. Here is the grammar point. It is a ru-verb, so you get 「つけます」 and 「つけて」. 「つけてください」 is please turn it on. Be careful not to confuse it with 「つく」, where the light comes on by itself. 「つける」 is when you do it. A useful fact: the same sound 「つける」 also means to attach or to stick something on, written with different kanji. That is a good memory hook. A way to remember it: picture attaching power to a lamp, and on it goes. So the light comes on when you 「つける」, and goes off when you 「けす」." },
      { title: "One sound, many jobs", text: "The sound 「つける」 does a lot of work in Japanese, and different meanings can be written with different kanji. Turning things on is usually written in hiragana. But attaching, sticking, or putting something on is often written 「付ける|つける」. From that attaching meaning comes one of the most common phrases in the language: 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care, be careful. Literally, it means attach your attention. People say it when you leave on a trip, or when you are crossing a busy road. Another everyday use is 「なまえをつける」, to give something a name, attaching a name to it. So the same sound turns on the TV, sticks a label on a box, names a cat, and sends you off safely. Just remember switching on is the hiragana one: 「つける」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is what you can switch on with this verb, and where Japanese prefers something else. Lights, the TV, the radio, the air conditioner, and heaters all go with it: 「エアコンをつけてください」. You also light a fire or a match with it: 「ひをつける」. But for some switches, people often say 「いれる」, to put in, instead. 「スイッチをいれる」 is to flip a switch on, and 「でんげんをいれる」 is to power on a machine. The opposite there is 「きる」, to cut. So you might 「つける」 the TV, or 「でんげんをいれる」 on a computer. A common question in a shared room is 「でんきをつけてもいいですか」, may I turn on the light. So, lights, screens, and flames come on with 「つける」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has an intransitive partner, 「つく」, and the pair matters when you describe what is on. 「でんきをつけます」 means I switch on the light. That is the action. But to say the light is on, as a state, you use the partner verb in its te-iru form: 「でんきがついています」. Notice the particle changes from 「を」 to 「が」, since nobody is doing anything now. The light just is on. So if you notice the TV is on in an empty room, you say 「テレビがついています」. And if you want to tell someone to switch it off, you say 「けしてください」. Its partner for off is 「きえる」: 「でんきがきえています」, the light is off. So, 「つける」 to do it, and 「ついています」 to say it is on." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means switching things on, like 「でんきをつける」, and pairs with 「消す|けす」 for off. Do not confuse it with 「つく」, where the light comes on by itself.",
      "The same sound written 「付ける|つける」 means to attach, which gives 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care, literally attach your attention.",
      "Lights, TVs and flames come on with this verb, but for switches and machines people also say 「スイッチをいれる」 and 「でんげんをいれる」.",
      "To say something is on as a state, use the partner 「つく」, as in 「でんきがついています」, with 「が」 instead of 「を」."
    ],
    ja: "テレビを[つけて|つけて|つける]ください。",
    en: "Please turn on the TV." },
  "でんわする|電話する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the noun 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, plus 「する」, to do. 「電話|でんわ」 has two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. 「電|でん」 is electricity, rain on top with a flash of lightning below. You know it from 「電気|でんき」 and 「電車|でんしゃ」. 「話|わ」 means talk. On the left is the speech radical, words coming out of a mouth, and on the right is 「舌|した」, tongue. Its native reading is in the verb 「話す|はなす」, to speak. So a telephone is electric talk, and doing electric talk is calling someone. Adding 「する」 to a noun to make a verb is a huge pattern in Japanese. You will see it again with 「しんぱいする」 and many more. Here is the grammar. The person you call takes 「に」: 「ともだちにでんわする」, call a friend. Do not use 「を」 for the person. That is a common mistake. A very useful line is 「あとででんわします」, I will call you later. So, electric talk plus do: 「でんわする」, to call." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phone phrases that go with this verb. When you pick up the phone, you say 「もしもし」, hello. Answering the phone is 「でんわにでる」, to come out to the phone. A very common alternative to this verb is 「でんわをかける」, to place a call. Both mean the same thing, and you will hear both. To talk on the phone, use 「で」 for the means: 「でんわではなす」. If someone is unavailable, you might hear 「あとでおでんわします」, I will call you back later, with polite 「お」. And here is a mini exchange. 「もしもし、たなかです」. 「あ、たなかさん、どうしたんですか」. So, pick up with 「もしもし」, answer with 「でる」, and make the call with 「でんわする」." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Both parts of this word have nice histories. The telephone reached Japan in the late eighteen hundreds, and the word 「でんわ」 was created in Japan from two Chinese characters, electric plus talk. Many modern words were built this way in that era, and some, like this one, later spread to Chinese too. Then there is 「もしもし」. It comes from 「申す|もうす」, a humble verb meaning to say. Early callers said 「もうし、もうし」, I say, I say, to get the other person's attention, and it was later shortened. So when you say 「もしもし」, you are politely saying I am speaking, I am speaking. So, a Japanese-made word for electric talk, opened with a humble I say: 「でんわする」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb shows how the noun plus 「する」 pattern works. You can say 「でんわする」 or 「でんわをする」, both are fine. The same goes for 「べんきょうする」 and 「べんきょうをする」. But there is one thing to watch. A sentence should not have two objects marked with 「を」 on the same verb. So 「にほんごをべんきょうをする」 sounds wrong. Use 「にほんごをべんきょうする」 instead. With this verb, the person takes 「に」, so you can freely say 「ははにでんわをする」. In the past, it is 「でんわしました」. And in the te-form, 「でんわして」, as in 「あとででんわしてください」, please call me later. So one simple 「する」 conjugation covers all the forms: 「でんわする」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「電話|でんわ」 is electric plus talk, and adding 「する」 makes it a verb. The person you call takes 「に」, not 「を」, as in 「ともだちにでんわする」.",
      "Pick up with 「もしもし」 and answer with 「でんわにでる」. 「でんわをかける」 is a common alternative to this verb.",
      "The word 「でんわ」 was coined in Japan, and 「もしもし」 comes from the humble verb 「申す|もうす」, early callers saying I say, I say.",
      "You can say 「でんわする」 or 「でんわをする」, but avoid two 「を」 on one verb, as in 「にほんごをべんきょうをする」."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}に[電話します|でんわします|でんわする]。",
    en: "I'll call my friend." },
  "つれてくる|連れてくる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the te-form of 「連れる|つれる」, to take along, plus 「くる」, to come. The kanji 「連|つ」 is read with its native reading. Inside is 「車|くるま」, a cart or vehicle, and wrapped around it is the movement radical. A way to see it: carts rolling along one after another, things moving in a line together. So 「つれる」 is to go along with someone, to take them with you. Then 「くる」 adds the direction, toward here. Together it means to come with someone, to bring a person here. The big rule is the split between people and things. For people and animals, use 「つれてくる」: 「ともだちをつれてくる」, bring a friend. For objects, use 「持ってくる|もってくる」 instead. It also flips direction with 「いく」: 「連れていく|つれていく」 is to take someone there. The 「くる」 part conjugates irregularly, so you get 「つれてきます」 and 「つれてきました」. So, moving along together and coming here: 「つれてくる」, to bring a person." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「れん」, and it brings a family of connected things. 「連休|れんきゅう」 is a run of holidays, days linked together. 「連絡|れんらく」 is getting in touch, keeping people connected, as in 「れんらくしてください」, please contact me. 「国連|こくれん」 is the United Nations, short for a longer name meaning nations joined together. So the core idea is things linked one after another, carts in a row. That is why the native verb 「つれる」 means to have someone go along with you, linked to you as you move. Your companion can even be called your 「つれ」. So, carts in a line, holidays in a row, and a friend walking along beside you: 「つれてくる」, to bring someone along." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how this phrase works in real life. Before a party, you might ask the host, 「ともだちをつれてきてもいいですか」, may I bring a friend. It also works for pets: 「いぬをつれてきてもいいですか」, can I bring my dog. Parents say they will bring the kids with 「こどもをつれてきます」. In restaurants you will hear the companion word in polite form. If your friend has already arrived, staff may say 「おつれさまがおまちです」, your companion is waiting for you. A common mistake is using this for objects. You cannot bring a cake with this verb. Use 「もってくる」 for the cake. So, friends, kids, and dogs come along with 「つれてくる」." },
      { title: "Bring or pick up", text: "Here is a neighbor of this phrase that is easy to confuse. 「迎えに来る|むかえにくる」 means to come to pick someone up, to come and meet them. 「えきにむかえにきてください」 is please come pick me up at the station. The person coming is the driver or helper. With 「つれてくる」, you are already together, and you come here with the person. So 「あにがともだちをつれてきました」 means my brother came here with his friend. Also remember the direction words. Come here is 「くる」, and go there is 「いく」, so 「つれていく」 is to take someone over there. You might say 「こどもをこうえんにつれていきます」, I will take my child to the park. So, pick up with 「むかえにくる」, and bring along with 「つれてくる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows carts moving in a line, so 「つれる」 means taking someone along, and 「くる」 adds toward here. Use it for people and animals, and 「持ってくる|もってくる」 for things.",
      "The reading 「れん」 means linked, as in 「連休|れんきゅう」 and 「連絡|れんらく」, and a companion can be called your 「つれ」.",
      "Ask a host 「ともだちをつれてきてもいいですか」, may I bring a friend, but you cannot bring a cake with this verb.",
      "「迎えに来る|むかえにくる」 means coming to pick someone up, while this verb means arriving already together with them."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}を[連れてきます|つれてきます|つれてくる]。",
    en: "I'll bring a friend." },
  "もってくる|持ってくる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is two verbs joined: 「持つ|もつ」, to hold or carry, in its te-form, plus 「くる」, to come. The kanji 「持|も」 has the hand radical on the left, a hand holding something. So the phrase literally means hold it and come: bring a thing here. The te-form here works like and then. You pick it up, and then you come. Now the key rule. This is for things only. For people and animals you use 「連れてくる|つれてくる」. So you 「もってくる」 an umbrella, but you 「つれてくる」 a friend. The direction also matters. 「もってくる」 is toward where the speaker is. 「持っていく|もっていく」 is taking it away from here, over there. If a friend asks you to a party, you might ask 「なにをもっていきましょうか」, what shall I take. The host might answer 「のみものをもってきてください」, please bring drinks. The 「くる」 part is irregular, so you get 「もってきます」 and 「もってきました」. So, hold it and come: 「もってくる」, to bring a thing." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The te-form plus 「くる」 pattern goes beyond bringing things, and it is very handy. Take 「かう」, to buy. 「ジュースをかってきます」 means I will go buy some juice and come back. You leave, do the action, and return. Japanese people say this all the time when stepping out briefly. 「ちょっとトイレにいってきます」 is I will just pop to the restroom. And the famous phrase 「いってきます」, said when leaving home in the morning, is exactly this: I will go and come back. So 「もってくる」 is one member of a big family where you do something and come back here. If the speaker stays put and the item arrives, it is 「もってくる」. If you head out with it, it is 「もっていく」. So, hold it and come back: 「もってくる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, bringing something when you visit is a strong custom. When you go to someone's home, you usually bring a small gift, often sweets, called 「手土産|てみやげ」, a gift in hand. When you hand it over, a traditional humble phrase is 「つまらないものですが」, it is just a little something. Younger people often say something warmer, like 「よかったらどうぞ」, please enjoy it if you like. After a trip, people bring back local snacks for coworkers, called 「おみやげ」. Each person gets one small individually wrapped treat. So if a friend invites you over, it is a good idea to ask yourself 「なにをもっていこうかな」, what should I take. Bringing something shows thanks before you even walk in. So, never arrive empty-handed: 「もってくる」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch the small 「っ」 in this phrase. 「もって」, with the little pause, is the te-form of to hold. Without the pause, 「もて」 belongs to a completely different word. 「もてる」 means to be popular, especially with the opposite sex. 「かれはもてます」 is he is really popular with girls. It comes from the potential form of 「もつ」, can hold, which drifted to mean able to hold people's interest. So if you rush and drop the pause, 「もってきて」 can start to sound like a different word. Give that small 「っ」 its full beat, like a tiny hiccup: 「もっ、てきて」. Practise it slowly and then speed up. So, keep the pause and bring the umbrella: 「もってくる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is hold it and come, to bring a thing here, only for objects, while people use 「連れてくる|つれてくる」. Taking it there is 「持っていく|もっていく」.",
      "Te-form plus 「くる」 means do something and come back, as in 「ジュースをかってきます」, and 「いってきます」 uses the same pattern.",
      "When visiting a home, bring a small gift, 「手土産|てみやげ」, perhaps with the humble 「つまらないものですが」.",
      "Keep the small 「っ」 in 「もって」, because 「もてる」 means to be popular, so give the pause a full beat."
    ],
    ja: "{傘|かさ}を[持ってきて|もってきて|もってくる]ください。",
    en: "Please bring an umbrella." },
  "あとで|後で": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「後|あと」, after, plus the particle 「で」. The kanji 「後|あと」 is read with its native reading here. On the left is a radical connected with walking along a road. A way to remember it: picture someone walking behind, coming after the others. The same kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「ご」. You know it from 「ごご」, afternoon, which is written 「午後|ごご」, after noon. You also have 「〜後|ご」 for in a certain amount of time, and 「後|あと」 on its own for after an event. Its other native reading is 「うしろ」, behind, so after in time and behind in space share a kanji. Now the usage. 「あとで」 means later, at some unspecified time after now. 「あとででんわします」 is I will call you later, and 「じゃあ、またあとで」 is see you later. A common slip is to say 「あと」 alone for later. You need the 「で」 to make it later on. So, after plus 「で」: 「あとで」, later on." },
      { title: "Polite later", text: "This kanji has one more native reading worth knowing, 「のち」, and it gives you a polite version of later. In business and shops, people say 「後ほど|のちほど」 instead of 「あとで」. 「のちほどごれんらくします」 means I will contact you later, in a polished, respectful tone. Store staff and receptionists use it constantly. With friends, 「のちほど」 would sound stiff, so stick to 「あとで」. You will also hear 「またあとで」 among friends who will see each other the same day. If you will see someone on another day, people tend to say 「またこんど」, next time, or 「またらいしゅう」. So 「あとで」 is the everyday later. 「のちほど」 is the polished office later. Same kanji, different reading, and a different level of politeness." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is how to say after something happens. With a noun, add 「の」 and then 「あとで」. 「じゅぎょうのあとで」 means after class. 「ばんごはんのあとで」 means after dinner. With a verb, use the plain past form, the ta-form, then 「あとで」. 「たべたあとで、はをみがきます」 means after eating, I brush my teeth. Notice the verb is always in the past form, even when the whole sentence is about the future. That is because the first action is already finished when the second one happens. Without anything in front, 「あとで」 simply means later. So 「あとで」 can stand alone as later, or follow a noun with 「の」, or follow a ta-form verb. It always means after." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture yourself running out the door, late for work, when you slam your big toe into the doorframe. You yell, ah, my toe. But there is no time. You decide to deal with it later. Ah, toe, deal with it later. Ah, toe, de: 「あとで」. All day long you limp around, telling everyone, I will fix my toe later. And remember the kanji: a person walking along a road behind the others. You are limping behind everyone else because of that toe. So whenever you put something off, picture yourself holding your sore toe and promising to deal with it later: 「あとで」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「後|あと」 means after, and the 「で」 makes it later on, as in 「じゃあ、またあとで」. Do not drop the 「で」 when you mean later.",
      "In business, the polite reading 「後ほど|のちほど」 replaces this word, as in 「のちほどごれんらくします」, but it sounds stiff with friends.",
      "After a noun add 「の」, as in 「じゅぎょうのあとで」, and after a verb use the past form, as in 「たべたあとで」, even for future plans.",
      "Picture stubbing your toe while late for work and crying ah, toe, deal with it later, 「あとで」."
    ],
    ja: "[後で|あとで]{電話します|でんわします}。",
    en: "I'll call you later." },
  "すぐ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This little word is usually written in hiragana and sits right before the verb it describes. 「すぐいきます」 is I will go right away, and 「すぐきてください」 is please come right away. You will also often hear it with 「に」 added, 「すぐに」, with the same meaning. It can also describe distance, meaning very close by: 「えきはすぐそこです」, the station is right over there. And it has a very useful relative, 「もうすぐ」, very soon or in a few moments. 「もうすぐなつやすみです」 is summer vacation is just around the corner. In a shop or restaurant, staff often say 「すぐおもちします」, I will bring it right away, so it is worth recognising. A way to remember it: it sounds like Sue goes, and Sue always goes immediately. It is quick to say, which suits its meaning. So, no waiting at all: 「すぐ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a meaning that is less obvious than right away. Before a verb, it can also mean easily, at the drop of a hat. 「かれはすぐおこります」 means he gets angry easily, he flies off the handle. 「わたしはすぐわすれます」 means I forget things easily. 「このこはすぐなきます」 is this child cries at the slightest thing. So it describes how fast something happens once a trigger appears. There is also 「いますぐ」, right this minute, which is stronger and more urgent. A parent might say 「いますぐきなさい」, come here this instant. So 「すぐいきます」 is a promise to go soon, but 「すぐわすれる」 is a cheerful confession that your memory is short. Speedy, or easily: both are 「すぐ」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This little word can attach to more than verbs. Before 「あと」, it means right after: 「じゅぎょうのすぐあと」 is right after class. Before words of place, it means right there, very close: 「すぐちかく」 is very nearby, and 「すぐそば」 is right beside. You will often hear 「えきのすぐまえ」, right in front of the station, when someone gives directions. As for 「すぐに」, the 「に」 is optional before a verb. Both 「すぐいきます」 and 「すぐにいきます」 are fine, and 「すぐに」 can sound slightly more careful. So, right after, right near, right now: 「すぐ」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a different memory scene. Picture a superhero named Sue whose only power is speed. She is so fast that if you ask her for a coffee, she is back before you finish the sentence. Her catchphrase, shouted as she zooms off, is Sue, go. Sue, go: 「すぐ」. One day she zooms off so fast she knocks over a whole table of snacks, and she is back to clean it up before anyone notices. Imagine her blurring past every time you hear the word. So when someone says something will happen right away, picture Sue the speed hero already gone. Faster than a sentence: 「すぐ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means right away and sits before the verb, as in 「すぐいきます」, and it can also mean very close, as in 「えきはすぐそこです」. 「もうすぐ」 means very soon.",
      "Before a verb it can also mean easily, at the slightest trigger, as in 「わたしはすぐわすれます」. 「いますぐ」 is more urgent, right this minute.",
      "It can mean right after, 「じゅぎょうのすぐあと」, or right near, 「えきのすぐまえ」. The 「に」 in 「すぐに」 is optional before a verb.",
      "Picture Sue, a speed superhero who zooms off shouting Sue, go, 「すぐ」, back before you finish your sentence."
    ],
    ja: "[すぐ]{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'll go right away." },
  "ゆっくり|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in hiragana, and it describes doing something without rushing. Listen to the small 「っ」 in the middle. It creates a tiny pause, almost as if the word itself is slowing down. A way to remember it: say it slowly and let that pause hang in the air. It has two main uses. The first is slowly. 「ゆっくりはなしてください」, please speak slowly, is a lifesaver for learners. Use it often. The second is leisurely, relaxed, taking your time. A host might say 「ゆっくりしてください」, please make yourself at home. And on a day off you might say 「きょうはゆっくりやすみます」, I will take it easy and rest today. So it is not only about speed. It is also about a calm, unhurried feeling, which is why it sounds warm and kind. It is the natural opposite of 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry, and of 「すぐ」, right away. So, the word with a built-in pause: 「ゆっくり」, slowly and with time to breathe." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word belongs to a big family of adverbs with the same rhythm: a sound, a small 「っ」, another sound, and 「り」 at the end. Learn the shape and you will spot them everywhere. 「はっきり」 means clearly, distinctly: 「はっきりいってください」, please say it clearly. 「しっかり」 means firmly or properly: 「しっかりべんきょうする」, to study properly. 「すっかり」 means completely: 「すっかりわすれました」, I completely forgot. And 「びっくりする」 is to be startled or surprised. They all have that bouncy pause in the middle, which makes them feel vivid and expressive. So when you hear a word shaped like this, guess that it is an adverb describing how something happens. And the slow, gentle member of the family is 「ゆっくり」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this word as a kindness in shops, hotels, and restaurants. When staff bring your food or show you to your room, they often say 「どうぞごゆっくり」, please take your time, please relax and enjoy. The 「ご」 makes it polite. When someone is sick or tired, a warm thing to say is 「ゆっくりやすんでください」, please rest well and take it easy. When you are visiting someone and they want you to stay longer, they might say 「ゆっくりしていってください」, stay and relax a while. And for learners, a small extra helps a lot: 「もうすこしゆっくりおねがいします」, a little slower, please. So, a waiter's welcome, a get-well wish, and a learner's rescue line: 「ゆっくり」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick that uses a real Japanese word inside it. 「くり」 means chestnut. Now picture a tiny snail trying to carry a huge chestnut across a garden path. It moves so slowly. Inch by inch, the chestnut wobbles on the snail's back. A child watching says, you, chestnut, slow down, you will drop it. But the snail is already going as slowly as possible. You, kuri. 「ゆっくり」. The snail takes all afternoon, but it never hurries and never drops the chestnut. So whenever you want to say slowly or take your time, picture that patient snail and its chestnut. A snail hauling a chestnut: 「ゆっくり」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The small 「っ」 makes the word itself slow down. It means slowly, as in 「ゆっくりはなしてください」, and also relaxed, as in 「ゆっくりしてください」.",
      "It shares a bouncy rhythm with adverbs like 「はっきり」, clearly, 「しっかり」, firmly, and 「すっかり」, completely, so this shape usually signals an adverb.",
      "Staff say 「どうぞごゆっくり」, please take your time, and you can tell a sick friend 「ゆっくりやすんでください」.",
      "Picture a snail slowly carrying a big chestnut, 「くり」, while a child says you, chestnut, 「ゆっくり」."
    ],
    ja: "[ゆっくり]{話して|はなして}ください。",
    en: "Please speak slowly." },
  "けっこうです|結構です": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「です」. 「結|けつ」 means to tie or to join, and its sound shortens to 「けっ」 here. 「構|こう」 means to build or to put together. The original sense of the compound was the way something is put together, its structure. From well put together, it came to mean fine, splendid, or quite enough. Now the tricky part. In daily life 「けっこうです」 is very often a polite no thank you. If someone offers more tea and you say 「けっこうです」, you mean I am fine, I have enough. But it can also mean that is fine, that is acceptable, as in 「それでけっこうです」, that will do. So tone, gesture, and context tell you which. A small wave of the hand usually means no thanks. If you want to make a no thank you clear, add 「いいえ」 first. Learners sometimes accept something by saying 「けっこうです」 and then get nothing, so be careful. So, well put together, enough: 「けっこうです」, I am fine as I am." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a use of this word that learners hear constantly but rarely notice. As an adverb, without 「です」, 「けっこう」 means fairly, quite, or more than I expected. 「このラーメン、けっこうおいしいね」 means this ramen is pretty good, better than I thought. 「にほんごのテストはけっこうむずかしかった」 means the Japanese test was quite hard. It carries a little surprise, as if you expected less. It is different from 「とても」, which just means very. With 「けっこう」, there is that sense of more than I assumed. You can even answer a question with it. If someone asks if you go to the gym often, you can say 「けっこういきます」, I go quite a lot actually. So, a polite no thank you, and a surprised pretty good: 「けっこう」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「結|けつ」, means to tie or bind, and its family is a happy one. Its native reading is in the verb 「結ぶ|むすぶ」, to tie, as in tying a ribbon or a knot. And with the Chinese-origin reading you get 「結婚|けっこん」, marriage. That is the same shortening to 「けっ」 that you see in this word. 「けっこんする」 is to get married, to tie the knot, and 「結婚式|けっこんしき」 is a wedding ceremony. You will also meet 「結果|けっか」, a result, the way things are tied up in the end. So this one kanji ties ribbons, ties couples together, and ties up results. Notice how 「けっこう」 and 「けっこん」 sound almost the same at the start. So, a well-tied, well-built thing: 「けっこうです」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Tone matters a lot with this phrase. When you say 「けっこうです」 to a pushy salesperson or someone handing out flyers, it is a firm no, and can sound quite cold. That is useful when you need to be clear. With a host offering more food, it is gentler to say 「いいえ、もうけっこうです。ありがとうございます」, no, I have had plenty, thank you. Adding thanks keeps it warm. Many young people prefer 「だいじょうぶです」, I am okay, as a softer refusal. The positive meaning comes up too, often from someone in charge. A doctor finishing a check-up might say 「はい、もうけっこうですよ」, okay, you are all done. So, firm to a stranger, gentle with thanks to a host, and all done from the doctor: 「けっこうです」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Tie plus build originally meant well put together, so fine or quite enough. It is often a polite no thank you, but can also mean that is fine, so context decides.",
      "Without 「です」, 「けっこう」 means fairly or more than expected, as in 「このラーメン、けっこうおいしいね」, with a hint of surprise that 「とても」 lacks.",
      "The first kanji means to tie, as in 「結ぶ|むすぶ」 and 「結婚|けっこん」, marriage, shortened to 「けっ」 just like in this word.",
      "To a pushy salesperson it is a firm no, so with a host add thanks to keep it warm. A doctor saying 「もうけっこうですよ」 means you are done."
    ],
    ja: "おちゃは[結構です|けっこうです]。",
    en: "No tea for me, thank you." },
  "ほんとうですか|本当ですか": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「本当|ほんとう」, truth, plus 「ですか」, is it. 「本|ほん」 is a tree, 「木|き」, with an extra mark near its base, pointing to the root. So its core meaning is root, origin, or the real thing. You know it from 「日本|にほん」, the sun's origin, and of course 「本|ほん」, book. 「当|とう」 means to hit the mark, to be on target or correct. Together, 「ほんとう」 is the real, on-target thing: the truth. Asking 「ほんとうですか」 is asking is that the truth, which is how Japanese says really. Use it when you are surprised by news: 「あしたテストですか。ほんとうですか」. With friends, it gets shortened to 「ほんと」 or 「ほんとに」, often with rising intonation. It also works as an adverb, 「ほんとうに」, meaning truly or really, as in 「ほんとうにありがとう」, thank you so much. So, the root of the matter, hitting the mark: 「ほんとうですか」, really." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word has some handy relatives. 「本当は|ほんとうは」 means actually, or the truth is, used when you reveal something different from what people assumed. 「ほんとうはさしみがきらいです」 means actually, I do not like sashimi. Its opposite is 「うそ」, a lie. 「うそをつく」 is to tell a lie. Among friends, 「うそ」 by itself is a common reaction meaning no way, you are kidding. It does not accuse anyone of lying. Then there is 「本物|ほんもの」, the real thing, a genuine article, as opposed to a fake. Notice it uses the same root kanji. So the truth, the actual truth, a genuine item, and its opposite, a lie, all sit in one little family. And the question that checks the truth is 「ほんとうですか」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese has several ways to react to news, and choosing the right one matters. 「そうですか」 is calm and mild, I see, often with a falling tone. 「ほんとうですか」 shows real surprise, really. 「えっ、ほんとうですか」 is even stronger. Here is a small trap. If you react to everything with 「ほんとうですか」, it can start to sound like you doubt the other person. So save it for news that genuinely surprises you. In casual talk, it shrinks to 「ほんと」 with a rising tone, and in polite talk you keep the long vowel, 「ほんとう」. A mini exchange: 「らいげつ、にほんにいきます」. 「ほんとうですか。いいですね」. Really? That is great. So, mild 「そうですか」 for ordinary news, and 「ほんとうですか」 when it really surprises you." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a friend telling you a wild story at a café. He swears he saw a cat drive a car down the street. You squint at him and say, honest? Toe? Because while he talks, he keeps wiggling his big toe, which he only does when he is making things up. Honest, toe, sounds like 「ほんとう」. He laughs, stops wiggling, and admits it was a toy car. So whenever you hear unbelievable news, picture squinting at your friend's wiggling toe and asking, honest? Asking whether it is the truth: 「ほんとうですか」, really?" }
    ],
    short: [
      "「本|ほん」 means root or the real thing and 「当|とう」 means to hit the mark, so it asks is that the truth. Friends shorten it to 「ほんと」.",
      "「本当は|ほんとうは」 means actually, its opposite is 「うそ」, a lie, often said as no way, and 「本物|ほんもの」 is the real thing.",
      "Use it for real surprise, while 「そうですか」 is a mild I see. Reacting to everything with it can sound like you doubt the speaker.",
      "Picture a friend telling a wild story while wiggling his big toe, and you squint and ask honest, toe, 「ほんとう」."
    ],
    ja: "あしたテストですか。[本当ですか|ほんとうですか]。",
    en: "There's a test tomorrow? Really?" },
  "めがね|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is usually written in hiragana, though it does have kanji, 「眼鏡|めがね」. The kanji are eye and mirror, so you can picture a mirror or lens for your eyes. They have a special set reading for the whole word rather than reading each kanji on its own. You already know the eye part in its plain form, 「目|め」, eye. Now the important verb. You do not wear glasses with 「きる」 like clothes. You use 「かける」, to hang. Glasses hang on your ears and nose, so 「めがねをかける」 is to put on glasses. To describe someone who wears glasses, you say 「めがねをかけています」. Japanese has different wearing verbs for different parts of the body, so this is a good one to remember as a set phrase. A fun extra: sunglasses are 「サングラス」, a loanword, and they also take 「かける」. So, eye mirrors that hang on your face: 「めがね」, with 「かける」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The two kanji in 「眼鏡|めがね」 lead separate lives, and you will meet both. The first one, 「眼|がん」, has 「目|め」, eye, as its left side, so you know at a glance it is about seeing. On its own it is a more technical word for eyeball, and doctors use it. 「眼科|がんか」 is the eye clinic you would visit for an eye exam. The second kanji means mirror. Read with its native reading it is 「鏡|かがみ」, the mirror on your bathroom wall. Its left side is the metal radical, because the oldest mirrors were polished bronze, not glass. Read the Chinese way, it becomes 「きょう」, and it turns up in lens gadgets. 「望遠鏡|ぼうえんきょう」 is a telescope, a far looking lens. 「双眼鏡|そうがんきょう」 is binoculars, a pair of eye lenses. And reading glasses are 「老眼鏡|ろうがんきょう」, old age eye lenses. In those words each kanji keeps its own reading. Only everyday glasses get the special whole word reading. So when you see these two kanji inside a longer word, think eye and lens, and read them the Chinese way." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Putting glasses on uses 「かける」, but taking them off has its own verb too. You say 「めがねをはずす」, to remove or unhook them. You can also just say 「とる」, to take off, and people will understand. Now listen to how Japanese describes a person by their glasses. You can make the glasses part of the description in front of the noun: 「めがねをかけているひと」, the person who is wearing glasses. You will also hear the shorter 「めがねをかけたひと」, using the past form, which describes the state the same way. Both mean the person in glasses. This is very handy when you point someone out at a party. If someone wears contacts instead, that is 「コンタクト」, short for contact lenses, and you put them in with 「いれる」. A tiny dialogue. 「めがねはどこ」, where are my glasses? 「あたまのうえだよ」, they are on your head. That one happens in every country. So: 「かける」 to put them on, 「はずす」 to take them off." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "If you buy a pair of glasses frames made in Japan, there is a very good chance they came from one small city. Sabae, in Fukui prefecture on the Sea of Japan coast, makes the vast majority of the country's frames. The industry started a little over a century ago, when local farmers looked for winter work during the snowy months and began crafting frames by hand. Today the city is famous for light titanium frames, and it even has a glasses museum. There is also a lovely old idiom built on this word. 「おめがねにかなう」 means to win the approval of someone important, as if you passed through their critical lens. A boss or a strict parent might look you over, and if you pass, you have met their 「めがね」. Here the glasses stand for someone's judgment. You will not need it in class, but it shows how naturally this everyday object grew a deeper meaning. So: a snowy town of frame makers, and glasses that stand for judging eyes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「眼鏡|めがね」 are eye and mirror, a lens for your eyes, and you put glasses on with 「かける」, to hang, because they hang on your ears and nose.",
      "Inside longer words these kanji take Chinese readings, eye and lens: 「眼科|がんか」 is the eye clinic, 「望遠鏡|ぼうえんきょう」 a telescope, and 「双眼鏡|そうがんきょう」 binoculars.",
      "Glasses go on with 「かける」 and come off with 「はずす」, and to point someone out you say 「めがねをかけているひと」, the person wearing glasses.",
      "Most Japanese frames come from the snowy city of Sabae, and the idiom 「おめがねにかなう」 means winning an important person's approval, glasses standing for judgment."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は[めがね]をかけています。",
    en: "My father wears glasses." },
  "うたう|歌う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「歌|うた」 is read here with its native reading, and it is the same kanji as the noun 「歌|うた」, song. Look at its right side. That shape is usually explained as a person with a wide open mouth, the kind you see in kanji about breathing or singing out. The left side gives the sound. So the kanji is someone opening their mouth wide to let a song out. The Chinese-origin reading is 「か」. You know it from 「歌手|かしゅ」, a singer, a song person, and 「歌舞伎|かぶき」, kabuki, which uses song, dance, and skill. The verb is a u-verb, so the forms are 「うたいます」 and 「うたって」. You sing a song with 「を」: 「うたをうたう」. Yes, the word repeats, and that is totally natural. And a cultural note you will love: karaoke is a Japanese invention, and its name, 「カラオケ」, means empty orchestra, music with no singer. So a night out often ends with everyone taking turns to 「うたう」. So, an open mouth letting a song out: 「うたう」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb ends in the vowel 「う」, and that group has a sneaky habit. When you make the plain negative, a 「わ」 appears out of nowhere. It is not 「うたあない」, it is 「うたわない」, I don't sing. The same thing happens with 「かう」, to buy, which becomes 「かわない」, with 「あう」, to meet, which becomes 「あわない」, and with 「てつだう」, which becomes 「てつだわない」. In older Japanese these verbs had an extra consonant in that spot, and it survives today only in the negative. Everything else is regular. The te-form doubles the consonant: 「うたって」. The past is 「うたった」. Want to sing is 「うたいたい」, and let's sing is 「うたいましょう」. Try it in a sentence: 「カラオケでうたいたいです」, I want to sing at karaoke, and its opposite, 「わたしはうたわない」, I don't sing. So: watch for the hidden 「わ」 in the negative of every verb ending in 「う」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a trap for English speakers. In English, birds sing. In Japanese, birds usually don't use this verb at all. A bird's song is described with 「なく」, to cry or call, so you say 「とりがないている」, the bird is singing. This verb is mainly for people singing songs. Now some phrases you will hear on a night out. Someone hands you the microphone and asks, 「なにをうたいますか」, what will you sing? To say you love singing, use 「の」 to turn the verb into a noun: 「うたうのがすきです」, I like singing. If a friend sings well, you can say 「うたがじょうずですね」, you're good at singing. The classic humble reply is 「いいえ、ぜんぜん」, not at all, even if they were great. And if you want to join in, 「いっしょにうたいましょう」, let's sing together. So: people sing with this verb, birds call with 「なく」, and singing well is 「じょうず」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, nothing more. The word sounds a bit like the American state Utah. Picture a huge choir standing on the red rocks of Utah, hundreds of people in matching robes, belting out a song so loud that it echoes off the canyon walls. A tourist in the parking lot covers his ears. Every echo bounces back: Utah, Utah, Utah. Now make the picture match the kanji. Each singer has a mouth opened as wide as possible, just like the open mouthed person on the right side of 「歌|うた」. And if you want the polite form, imagine the conductor bowing at the end and saying 「うたいました」, we sang. Next time you need the verb for singing, just walk back into that canyon and listen. So: a giant choir in Utah, mouths wide open, singing." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「歌|うた」 shows a person with a wide open mouth letting a song out, and you sing a song with 「を」, as in 「うたをうたう」.",
      "Like every verb ending in 「う」, its plain negative gains a hidden 「わ」, so it becomes 「うたわない」, never 「うたあない」.",
      "This verb is for people singing songs, while birds call with 「なく」, and a good singer is praised with 「うたがじょうずですね」.",
      "It sounds like Utah, so picture a giant choir on Utah's red rocks, every singer's mouth wide open like the right side of 「歌|うた」."
    ],
    ja: "カラオケで[歌います|うたいます|うたう]。",
    en: "I sing at karaoke." },
  "かみ|髪": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is built from two parts. The whole top half is a radical that means long hair. If you look closely at its upper left, you can see the same shape as 「長い|ながい」, long, with three slanting strokes beside it, like strands of hair streaming out. The reading here is the native Japanese one. Now, a warning and a gift at the same time. The word sounds exactly like 「紙|かみ」, paper, the same 「紙|がみ」 you know from 「手紙|てがみ」, a letter. Only the kanji and the context tell them apart. A way to remember it: picture someone writing a letter on paper with a single long hair as the pen. To describe someone's hair, use 「が」 with an adjective: 「かみがながい」, has long hair, or 「かみがみじかい」, has short hair. So: long-hair radical on top, sounds like paper, and you describe it with 「が」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has a second, Chinese-origin reading, 「はつ」, and it shows up in some very practical words. Blond hair is 「金髪|きんぱつ」, golden hair. A haircut at an old-fashioned barber is 「散髪|さんぱつ」. In those words the reading changes to a 「ぱ」 sound after the 「ん」, which is a normal sound shift. The native word also changes shape in compounds. Bangs are 「前髪|まえがみ」, the front hair, and here 「かみ」 softens to 「がみ」. A related everyday word is 「髪の毛|かみのけ」. 「毛|け」 by itself is hair or fur in general, like body hair or a dog's coat. Adding it makes the head hair meaning clear, and it can also mean a single strand, as in finding a hair in your soup. There is also one famous irregular word. Gray or white hair is 「白髪|しらが」, read as a single special word. So: 「はつ」 in blond and barber words, 「がみ」 in bangs, and 「しらが」 for white hair." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is something that surprises English speakers. When you get a haircut, Japanese usually says 「かみをきりました」, I cut my hair, even though a stylist did the cutting. Nobody imagines you held the scissors. It is just how the sentence is said. You can also say where it happened: 「びよういんでかみをきりました」, I had my hair cut at the beauty salon. If you want short hair, the adverb form of the adjective comes in handy: 「みじかくきってください」, please cut it short. Other everyday phrases are 「かみをあらう」, to wash your hair, and 「かみをかわかす」, to dry it. A friend who notices your new look might say 「かみ、きった？」, did you cut your hair? And the polite compliment is 「かみがきれいですね」, your hair is beautiful. So: you always cut your own hair in Japanese, even at the salon." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There are at least three common words pronounced like this. Hair, 「髪|かみ」. Paper, 「紙|かみ」. And god, 「神|かみ」, the spirits honored at a 「神社|じんじゃ」, a shrine. In standard Tokyo speech, the pitch gives you a small hint. God starts high and drops, so it sounds like KA mi. Hair and paper both start low and rise, and the fall only shows on the particle that follows. That means hair and paper sound identical, and context does the work. That makes them a favorite for puns. Do not worry about pitch too much while speaking. In real conversation the topic tells everyone which one you mean, since you rarely write a letter on your hair or comb a sheet of paper. So: three words, one sound, and context sorts them out." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The top of the kanji is a long-hair radical with streaming strands, the word sounds exactly like 「紙|かみ」, paper, and you describe hair with 「が」, as in 「かみがながい」.",
      "In compounds the kanji reads 「はつ」, as in 「金髪|きんぱつ」, blond hair, softens to 「がみ」 in 「前髪|まえがみ」, bangs, and white hair is the special 「白髪|しらが」.",
      "Even when a stylist cuts it, Japanese says 「かみをきりました」, I cut my hair, as if you did it yourself.",
      "Hair, paper and god, 「神|かみ」, all share this sound, and since hair and paper even share the same pitch, context is what sorts them out."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[髪|かみ]が{長い|ながい}です。",
    en: "My older sister has long hair." },
  "め|目": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is a real picture of an eye. Imagine an eye drawn sideways, with the pupil in the middle, and then stood up on end to save space. That is why it is a tall box with two lines inside. The reading here is the native one. Two family words help it stick. First, look at the kanji 「見る|みる」, to see. Its top half is this very eye, sitting on a pair of legs: an eye walking around, looking at things. Second, the counter 「一日目|いちにちめ」, the first day, uses the same kanji for the idea of number so-and-so in a row. And you can hear the eye sound at the start of 「めがね」, glasses, things for your eyes. When describing people, use 「が」: 「めがおおきい」, has big eyes. So: a picture of an eye, the top of seeing, and the start of glasses." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To talk about eyesight, Japanese uses this word with simple adjectives. 「めがいい」 means you have good eyesight. 「めがわるい」 means bad eyesight. So a natural explanation for glasses is 「めがわるいので、めがねをかけています」, my eyes are bad, so I wear glasses. Notice it is not about the beauty of your eyes, only how well they see. When you describe looks, you use size or shape: big eyes, round eyes. Some other useful everyday phrases: 「めがさめる」 is to wake up, literally the eyes become clear. And there is a famous expression of delight, 「めがない」, to have no eyes for something, which actually means to be crazy about it, as in 「あまいものにめがない」, I can't resist sweets. It sounds like the opposite, so it is a fun one to remember. So: good eyes see well, bad eyes need glasses, and having no eyes means you love it." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When this kanji is read the Chinese way, it becomes 「もく」. You will meet it in words like 「科目|かもく」, a school subject, and 「目的|もくてき」, a purpose or goal, literally the eye's target. Careful, because 「もく」 is also the sound of a completely different kanji, 「木|き」, tree, which gives you 「木曜日|もくようび」, Thursday. Same sound, different shape. One has a box with lines inside, the other has branches. This eye shape also works as a building block in other kanji. You see it on the left of 「眼|がん」, the eyeball kanji from glasses. You see it in the bottom of 「着る|きる」, to wear. And you see it in the top of 「見る|みる」. When you notice it inside a bigger kanji, the word often has something to do with looking, though not always. So: 「め」 alone, 「もく」 in subject and purpose, and not to be confused with Thursday's tree." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "This is a memory trick, nothing more. The word sounds just like the English shrug word, meh. Picture a teenager on a sofa. Their parents are showing slide after slide of vacation photos. A waterfall. Meh. A museum. Meh. Each time, the teenager rolls their eyes all the way up into their head, so far you can only see the whites. The eyes are doing all the talking, and the only word that comes out is meh. Now add the kanji. The teenager's eyeballs, turned sideways and stacked up tall, look like the box with two lines inside. Whenever you need the word for eye, picture those rolling eyes and that bored little sound. It works for 「めがね」 too: meh, but with glasses on. So: rolling eyes and a bored meh, and you have the eye." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a picture of an eye turned on end, and it also sits on top of 「見る|みる」, to see, like an eye walking on legs.",
      "Eyesight is 「めがいい」 or 「めがわるい」, and the playful 「めがない」, having no eyes for something, actually means you can't resist it.",
      "Read the Chinese way it becomes 「もく」, as in 「目的|もくてき」, purpose, but don't confuse it with 「木|き」, tree, which gives Thursday the same sound.",
      "It sounds like a bored meh, so picture a teenager rolling their eyes at vacation photos, eyeballs stacked tall like the kanji's box."
    ],
    ja: "{妹|いもうと}は[目|め]が{大きい|おおきい}です。",
    en: "My younger sister has big eyes." },
  "くち|口": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is one of the easiest kanji in the language. It is simply a picture of an open mouth, drawn as a square. The reading here is the native one. The fun part is how often this little box hides inside other kanji, nearly always connected to speaking, eating or making sound. Look at 「歌|うた」, song. On its left side you can find little mouths stacked up, singing away. Look at 「兄|あに」, older brother. It is a big mouth sitting on top of legs. And 「知る|しる」, to know, has an arrow on the left and a mouth on the right. Whenever you spot this square inside a bigger kanji, think about the mouth first. To describe people, use 「が」, as in 「くちがおおきい」, has a big mouth, which is a physical description, not an insult like in English. So: a square for a mouth, hiding inside songs, brothers and knowing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word means much more than the mouth on your face. It means any opening, a way in or out, and it is everywhere at train stations. In compounds it usually softens to 「ぐち」. 「出口|でぐち」 is an exit, the going out mouth. 「入口|いりぐち」 is an entrance, the going in mouth. 「改札口|かいさつぐち」 is the ticket gate. And 「窓口|まどぐち」, window mouth, is a service counter, like at a bank or post office. Signs in Japanese stations often say north exit or east exit, with the direction followed by this kanji. When the kanji is read the Chinese way, it becomes 「こう」. The best example is 「人口|じんこう」, population. It means people mouths, the number of mouths a country has to feed. So the kanji covers eating, speaking, and openings of all kinds. So: mouths on faces, mouths of buildings, and mouths to feed." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese has some lovely expressions with this word. When you serve food to a guest, a host may humbly ask 「おくちにあいますか」, does it suit your mouth, meaning does it suit your taste. Saying someone has a hard mouth, 「くちがかたい」, is a compliment. It means they can keep a secret. The opposite, 「くちがかるい」, a light mouth, means they gossip. And 「ひとくち」 means one mouthful, a single bite or sip. Friends say 「ひとくちちょうだい」, give me a bite, when they want to taste your food. You might also hear a parent tell a kid 「くちをあけて」, open your mouth, at the dentist. These phrases show the mouth stands for taste, secrets and bites. So: a hard mouth keeps secrets, and one mouthful is a taste." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, purely for fun. The word sounds a little like the fashion brand Gucci, with a k sound. Picture a very fancy designer handbag. You unzip it, and instead of a zipper there is a giant mouth with lips and teeth. It chomps down on your wallet. It talks back when you try to open it. It burps after eating your phone. That is the Gucci mouth bag. Now tie it to the kanji. Look at the bag from the front, and it is a simple square, just like the shape of 「口|くち」, a square mouth waiting to open. Every time you spot this square in a sign, like at a station exit, picture the hungry handbag swallowing passengers on their way in and out. So: a talking, snapping designer bag, the square mouth." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is simply a square open mouth, and it hides inside many kanji about speaking or sound, like 「歌|うた」 and 「兄|あに」.",
      "It also means any opening, softening to 「ぐち」 in station words like 「出口|でぐち」, exit, and reading 「こう」 in 「人口|じんこう」, population, the mouths to feed.",
      "A hard mouth, 「くちがかたい」, means someone keeps secrets, a light mouth means they gossip, and 「ひとくち」 is a single mouthful.",
      "It sounds a bit like Gucci, so picture a designer handbag whose opening is a square, snapping mouth that swallows your wallet."
    ],
    ja: "{弟|おとうと}は[口|くち]が{大きい|おおきい}です。",
    en: "My younger brother has a big mouth." },
  "せがたかい|背が高い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is really a little sentence: height is high. 「背|せ」 is a native reading meaning back, and also your height, the length of your back standing up. The kanji has the shape for north on top, which shows two people standing back to back, and the body radical underneath. So it is literally the body part on your back side. 「高い|たかい」 is the same word you already know as expensive. Its kanji is a picture of a tall building with a roof and a tower. High prices, high buildings, high height. A common mistake for English speakers is to say a person is 「ながい」, long. Japanese never does that. For people, always use this phrase, and the opposite is 「背が低い|せがひくい」. Use it like this: 「あにはせがたかいです」, my older brother is tall. Before a noun it works too: 「せがたかいひと」, a tall person. So: your back is high, and that means tall." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This phrase works smoothly with the comparison patterns you meet in later lessons. To say someone is taller than someone else, add 「より」 after the shorter person: 「あにはわたしよりせがたかいです」, my older brother is taller than me. To say who is tallest, use 「いちばん」: 「かぞくでいちばんせがたかいのはちちです」, the tallest in my family is my father. To talk about growing taller, combine it with 「なる」: 「せがたかくなりました」, I got taller, with the 「い」 changing to 「く」. Here is one more neat trick. When the phrase describes a noun, the 「が」 can switch to 「の」. So you can say 「せがたかいひと」 or 「せのたかいひと」, a tall person, and both are natural. That swap happens in many descriptive phrases with 「が」. So: 「より」 to compare, 「なる」 to grow, and 「の」 can stand in for 「が」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The kanji 「背|せ」 really means back, the back of your body. When you mean the back itself, people usually say 「背中|せなか」, the middle of the back, as in scrubbing someone's back at the bath. So height is the measure of your back standing up straight. In casual speech you will also hear it stretched to 「せい」, as in 「せいがたかい」, and that is completely normal. For official numbers, like at the doctor or on a form, height is 「身長|しんちょう」, body length, and Japan uses centimeters. A friend might ask 「せはなんセンチですか」, how many centimeters tall are you? A height of six feet is about 183 centimeters, so it helps to know your number in metric before you go. So: the back is 「せなか」, height on a form is 「しんちょう」, and casually it can be 「せい」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「高|こう」 shows up with its Chinese-origin reading, 「こう」, in some words you know well. 「高校|こうこう」 is high school, a high level school, and a student there is 「高校生|こうこうせい」. Young people love the word 「最高|さいこう」, the highest, which means the best, awesome. After a great concert, you might hear 「さいこう」 shouted over and over. There is also 「高速|こうそく」, high speed, as in an expressway. The native reading 「たかい」 covers height and price, and it can also mean a high voice or a high position. Notice what all these share: being high on some scale, whether it is a building, a price, a level of school, or a person. That is why one adjective can mean both tall and expensive. So: 「こう」 in high school and awesome, 「たかい」 for anything up high." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It literally says your back is high, since 「背|せ」 is your back and height, and people are never 「ながい」, long; the opposite is 「背が低い|せがひくい」.",
      "Compare with 「より」, grow taller with 「せがたかくなりました」, and when it describes a noun the 「が」 can become 「の」, as in 「せのたかいひと」.",
      "The back itself is usually 「背中|せなか」, official height on a form is 「身長|しんちょう」 in centimeters, and casual speech often stretches it to 「せい」.",
      "The kanji 「高|こう」 gives 「高校|こうこう」, high school, and 「最高|さいこう」, awesome, all sharing the idea of being high on some scale, tall or expensive."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[背が高い|せがたかい]です。",
    en: "My older brother is tall." },
  "かぶる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the verb for putting something on your head, like a hat, a cap or a helmet. It is usually written in kana. Japanese is picky here. Instead of one verb for wearing, it chooses the verb by where the item goes. 「きる」 is for tops and whole outfits, 「はく」 is for anything below the waist, 「かける」 is for glasses, and this one is for the head. A classic mistake is to say 「ぼうしをきる」. That sounds like you are trying to wear your hat as a shirt. Say 「ぼうしをかぶる」 instead. It is a regular verb: 「かぶります」, 「かぶって」. To say someone is wearing a hat right now, use the 「ている」 form: 「ぼうしをかぶっています」. A way to remember it: it sounds a little like cover, so you cover your head. So: the head verb, and hats are never 「きる」." },
      { title: "Taking it off", text: "Japanese has several verbs for putting things on, but taking things off is simpler. For a hat, you use 「ぬぐ」, the same verb you use for taking off clothes and shoes. So 「ぼうしをぬぐ」 means to take off your hat. 「ぬぐ」 is also the verb for taking off your shoes at the entrance of a home, 「くつをぬぐ」, which you will do a lot in Japan. Glasses are the odd one out, since you unhook them with 「はずす」. Now a tiny scene. You walk into a temple hall wearing a cap. A friend whispers, 「ぼうしをぬいでください」, please take off your hat. You say 「あ、すみません」 and take it off. So the pair to remember is simple. On goes 「かぶる」, and off comes 「ぬぐ」. So: hats go on with 「かぶる」 and come off with 「ぬぐ」." },
      { title: "Beyond hats", text: "This verb is really about covering something from above, and that idea stretches nicely. It is sometimes written with the kanji 「被る|かぶる」, which has the clothing radical on the left. Kids pull the futon over their heads, and that is 「ふとんをかぶる」. If a wave splashes you or you get drenched, you can say 「みずをかぶる」, to get covered in water. In casual modern Japanese, there is another fun use. It can mean to overlap or clash. If two friends show up in the same outfit, someone says 「ふく、かぶったね」, our clothes overlap. If two meetings are at the same time, the schedule 「かぶる」. The image is one thing landing on top of another. That casual sense is very common among young people, so it is worth knowing when you hear it outside a hat context. So: anything that covers you from above, and in slang, things that clash." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, nothing more. This verb sounds a lot like 「かぶき」, kabuki theater, but the two words are not related. Use the sound anyway. Picture a kabuki actor backstage, a few minutes before the curtain opens. His assistant lifts an enormous wig, a tower of black hair with combs and ribbons, and lowers it onto his head. It is so heavy his knees wobble. The assistant says 「かぶって」, put it on, and the actor groans. Then a second wig arrives, and a helmet, and a straw hat on top of that. The stack grows taller and taller. Whenever you need the verb for putting something on your head, picture that wobbling kabuki actor buried under hats and wigs. So: kabuki head pieces, piled on top, are what you 「かぶる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the wearing verb for anything on your head, like hats and helmets, so say 「ぼうしをかぶる」, never 「ぼうしをきる」.",
      "Hats come off with 「ぬぐ」, the same verb as taking off clothes and shoes, so the pair is 「かぶる」 on and 「ぬぐ」 off.",
      "The core idea is covering from above, like 「ふとんをかぶる」 or 「みずをかぶる」, and in casual slang it means two things clash or overlap.",
      "It sounds like 「かぶき」, so picture a kabuki actor backstage, wobbling under a growing stack of wigs, helmets and hats."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}はぼうしを[かぶっています|かぶっています|かぶる]。",
    en: "My father is wearing a hat." },
  "おじいさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built like a gift wrap. The polite 「お」 goes on the front and the friendly 「さん」 goes on the end, with the core 「じい」 in the middle. The big trap is the long vowel. Hold the 「い」 for two beats and it means grandfather. Cut it short to 「おじさん」 and you have said uncle, or just a middle-aged man. The same trick works with women: 「おばあさん」 is grandmother, and 「おばさん」 is aunt. A way to remember it: grandpa is older, so he gets the longer sound. Like many family words, this one stretches beyond family. Children and adults call any elderly man by this word, a bit like saying the old gentleman. When you talk about your own grandfather to others in a formal setting, adults switch to a humbler word, 「祖父|そふ」, but in everyday talk this word is fine. So: wrapped in 「お」 and 「さん」, and the long vowel makes him older." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Inside the family, grandchildren rarely use the full polite form. They call their grandfather 「おじいちゃん」, swapping 「さん」 for the warm, childlike 「ちゃん」. Even adults keep calling their grandpa this way, because it feels affectionate. Very small children may say 「じいじ」. When talking about your own grandfather to friends, you can say 「うちのおじいちゃん」, my grandpa, in casual speech. In polite talk with outsiders, adults switch to the humble 「そふ」. And when talking to a stranger who is an elderly man, many people avoid this word altogether, because not everyone enjoys being called old. Shop staff may prefer 「おきゃくさま」, honored customer. A typical sentence: 「おじいちゃんはまいあささんぽします」, Grandpa takes a walk every morning. So: 「ちゃん」 for your own grandpa, 「そふ」 for formal talk, and care with strangers." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This word is usually written in kana, but it has two kanji spellings with different meanings. When it means your grandfather, it is written 「お祖父さん|おじいさん」. The first kanji means ancestor, and the second is 「父|ちち」, father. So grandfather is the ancestor father. When it means any old man, it can be written 「お爺さん|おじいさん」, and that kanji also contains 「父|ちち」, sitting on its top. In both, the whole word gets a special reading. Look at the ancestor kanji. Its left side is the altar radical, which also appears in 「社|しゃ」 from 「会社|かいしゃ」 and 「神社|じんじゃ」. It points to worship and spirits, a natural fit for ancestors. Read normally, the two kanji give 「祖父|そふ」, grandfather, and with mother instead, 「祖母|そぼ」, grandmother. Together, grandparents are 「祖父母|そふぼ」. So: the ancestor kanji with the altar radical, and father below it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has one of the oldest populations in the world, and it takes elders seriously. The third Monday in September is a national holiday called 「敬老の日|けいろうのひ」, Respect for the Aged Day. Families visit grandparents, and towns give gifts to their oldest residents. Turning sixty is a milestone called 「還暦|かんれき」. In the traditional Chinese calendar, sixty years completes a full cycle, so it is like being reborn. Families traditionally give the person something red, such as a red vest, a color tied to babies and new beginnings. Grandfathers also star in old folk tales, which often begin with 「むかしむかし、あるところにおじいさんとおばあさんがいました」, once upon a time, in a certain place, there lived an old man and an old woman. So: a holiday for elders, a red vest at sixty, and the grandpa of every folk tale." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The core 「じい」 is wrapped in polite 「お」 and 「さん」, and its long vowel matters, because the short 「おじさん」 means uncle instead.",
      "Grandchildren call their own grandpa 「おじいちゃん」, adults use the humble 「そふ」 in formal talk, and many avoid the word with elderly strangers.",
      "For grandfather it is written 「お祖父さん|おじいさん」, the ancestor kanji with the altar radical plus 「父|ちち」, father, the ancestor father.",
      "Japan honors elders with 「敬老の日|けいろうのひ」 in September, gives a red vest at sixty, and starts folk tales with an old man and an old woman."
    ],
    ja: "[おじいさん]は{八十|はちじゅう}さいです。",
    en: "My grandfather is eighty." },
  "きる|着る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is the sheep, 「羊|ひつじ」, on top of the eye, 「目|め」. A way to remember it, purely as a hook: wool from a sheep pulled up over your face as you put on a sweater. The reading here is the native one. The same kanji has a second life. In 「着く|つく」, it means to arrive. And in 「着物|きもの」, kimono, it is literally a thing you wear. Use this verb for clothes on your upper body or your whole body, like shirts, sweaters, coats, dresses and kimono. Pants and shoes take 「はく」 instead. Watch out for a sound twin, 「切る|きる」, to cut. They behave differently. This one is a ru-verb, so it becomes 「きます」 and 「きて」. The cutting one is an u-verb, 「きります」 and 「きって」. That difference is how you tell them apart when you hear them. To say wearing right now, use 「きています」. So: sheep's wool on the body, the kimono verb, and not the cutting one." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a busy family. With its native reading softened to 「ぎ」, it names many clothes. 「水着|みずぎ」 is a swimsuit, water clothes. 「下着|したぎ」 is underwear, the clothes underneath. 「上着|うわぎ」 is a jacket or outer layer, with a special reading for the top part. Read the Chinese way, it becomes 「ちゃく」, and that reading usually carries the arrival meaning. 「到着|とうちゃく」 is arrival, and you see it on airport boards. It is also a counter for suits and outfits, so one suit is 「いっちゃく」. And in a race, first place is also 「いっちゃく」, the first to arrive. That is a neat way to link the two meanings: clothes settle onto your body, and runners settle into the finish line. Both are about something reaching its place. So: 「ぎ」 in swimsuits and jackets, 「ちゃく」 for arriving and first place." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The 「ている」 form of this verb has a specific meaning worth knowing. 「きています」 usually does not mean you are in the middle of pulling on a sweater. It means the sweater is already on, you are wearing it. That is because putting on clothes is a change that leaves a result, and 「ている」 describes the result. The same thing happens with 「かぶっています」 and 「かけています」. Next, this form is perfect for describing people. Put the verb phrase before the noun: 「あかいセーターをきているひと」, the person wearing a red sweater. You can point someone out at a party like this. For past events, use 「きました」: 「きのう、きものをきました」, I wore a kimono yesterday. The opposite verb, to take off, is 「ぬぐ」. So: 「きています」 means it's on, and it makes great descriptions of people." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A kimono is called 「着物|きもの」, simply a thing you wear, because once it was just clothing. Today people wear them mostly for ceremonies, like coming of age day, weddings and graduations. The lighter cotton version is the 「浴衣|ゆかた」, worn to summer festivals and at hot spring inns. There is one rule everyone should know. When you put one on, the left side always goes over the right side, as seen by the wearer. The other way, right over left, is used only for dressing the dead at funerals. Putting on a formal kimono properly is hard, so there is a word, 「着付け|きつけ」, for the skill of dressing someone in one, and people pay professionals or take classes. So: left over right, always, and getting dressed can be an art." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a sheep over an eye, so picture wool pulled over your face, and it is the verb for clothes on your upper or whole body, unlike pants with 「はく」.",
      "Softened to 「ぎ」 it names clothes like 「水着|みずぎ」, swimsuit, while read 「ちゃく」 it means arriving, as in 「到着|とうちゃく」, arrival.",
      "Because putting on clothes leaves a result, 「きています」 means it is already on, which makes it perfect for describing people, like 「あかいセーターをきているひと」.",
      "A kimono is 「着物|きもの」, simply a thing you wear, and it always closes left over right, since right over left is only for dressing the dead."
    ],
    ja: "{新しい|あたらしい}セーターを[着ています|きています|きる]。",
    en: "I'm wearing a new sweater." },
  "かわいい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native word, normally written in kana. It comes from an older form, 「かわゆい」, which you still hear now and then in old songs and stories. It is an 「い」 adjective, so it bends like 「たかい」: not cute is 「かわいくない」, and was cute is 「かわいかった」. In Japan this word is everywhere. It is a whole aesthetic. Towns, police departments and even prisons have round, smiling mascot characters, and people say this word about animals, babies, clothes, stationery, and even a nicely wrapped bento box. Grown men say it too, and nobody blinks. That is very different from English cute, which can sound childish. It works for people as well, though for a stylish adult you may hear 「かっこいい」, cool, for men, or 「きれい」, pretty. A way to remember it: picture a fluffy kitten that makes you go wah. So: an 「い」 adjective that Japan has turned into a way of life." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has a surprising history. Dictionaries trace it back through 「かわゆい」 to an older classical word that described a face glowing red with embarrassment, a feeling of being hard to look at. Over centuries the meaning drifted. It moved to pitiful, the feeling of watching someone weak or small, and from there to lovable and dear. That pitiful meaning still lives in a cousin word, 「かわいそう」, poor thing. If a puppy is left out in the rain, you say 「かわいそう」. Be careful not to mix them up, because one means cute and the other means pitiful. You may also see the word written 「可愛い|かわいい」, with kanji meaning can and love. Those kanji were picked later to fit the sound and the meaning, so they don't explain its origin. So: from a blushing face, to pity, to cute, and its cousin still means poor thing." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "English speakers often mix this word up with 「こわい」, scary. They have a similar shape, and saying a baby is scary is not the compliment you meant. Here is how to keep them apart. The cute one has four beats, and the last two are both 「い」, so hold that vowel for two full beats. The scary one has only three beats, starts with an o sound, and has a single 「い」 at the end. If you count beats on your fingers, four means cute and three means scary. You will also hear people stretch the end when excited, 「かわいいー」, with a long, rising sound, especially when a puppy walks by. And the opposite feeling, poor thing, is 「かわいそう」, one extra piece added on. So: four beats for cute, an o sound for scary, and 「そう」 for pity." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Add 「がる」 to this adjective and you get a verb, 「かわいがる」, to dote on, to treat with love. You 「かわいがる」 a pet, a grandchild or a younger coworker you look after. 「おばあちゃんはねこをとてもかわいがっています」, Grandma dotes on her cat. It is a very warm verb. Another form is 「かわいらしい」, which means charming or sweet, a little softer and more elegant, often used about small things or children. And to say something is not quite cute but cool, people switch to 「かっこいい」. Many young people pile on emphasis with casual words like 「すごくかわいい」, really cute, or 「めっちゃかわいい」, super cute, though 「めっちゃ」 is very casual, so keep it with friends. So: 「かわいがる」 to cherish, 「かわいらしい」 for charming, and lots of emphasis for extra cute." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This 「い」 adjective comes from older 「かわゆい」, and in Japan it is a whole aesthetic that even grown men use freely for mascots, pets and bento boxes.",
      "It traces back to a word for a face blushing red, then shifted to pitiful and finally to cute, and its cousin 「かわいそう」 still means poor thing.",
      "Don't mix it up with 「こわい」, scary: the cute word has four beats ending in two 「い」, while the scary one has three and starts with an o sound.",
      "Add 「がる」 to get 「かわいがる」, to dote on a pet or grandchild, while 「かわいらしい」 is a softer, more elegant charming."
    ],
    ja: "この{猫|ねこ}は[かわいい]です。",
    en: "This cat is cute." },
  "かぞく|家族": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two Chinese-origin readings combine here. 「家|か」 is the house kanji. Look at it: a roof on top, and under the roof, a pig. In ancient China, a home was where your livestock lived with you. The same kanji with its native reading is 「家|いえ」, home, which you already know. It also appears in 「作家|さっか」, writer, and 「家具|かぐ」, furniture. 「族|ぞく」 means clan or tribe. It shows a flag with arrows under it, people gathering under one banner. Put them together: the clan of one house. Japanese people often count the family when introducing it, including themselves. 「よにんかぞくです」 means we are a family of four, me included. You will also hear the question 「なんにんかぞくですか」, how many people are in your family. So: a pig under a roof, plus a clan under a flag, makes the family." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「族|ぞく」, means a group of people tied together, and it pops up in some fun words. 「民族|みんぞく」 is an ethnic group, a people. 「貴族|きぞく」 is the nobility, the noble clan. One of the best is 「水族館|すいぞくかん」, an aquarium. Break it down: water, tribe, building. The building for the water tribe, the fish. Japan has some famous aquariums, so it is a useful word. In more colorful words, it can mean a group of people with a shared style. Motorcycle gangs are called 「暴走族|ぼうそうぞく」, the reckless running tribe. And the first kanji, 「家|か」, appears in many words for a person with a skill or profession, like 「作家|さっか」, writer. So: 「ぞく」 means a tribe, whether it is your family, the nobles or the fish." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you ask about someone else's family, add the polite 「ご」 in front: 「ごかぞく」. A very common friendly question is 「ごかぞくはおげんきですか」, is your family well? You would never use 「ご」 for your own family, because Japanese is humble about its own side. For yours, you just say 「かぞく」, or casually, 「うちのかぞく」, my family. You will also hear many compound words. 「かぞくりょこう」 is a family trip. 「かぞくしゃしん」 is a family photo. And people often say a pet is family: 「いぬもかぞくです」, the dog is family too. When asked how many in your family, you can answer 「ちちとははとあにとわたしです」, listing the members. So: 「ごかぞく」 for theirs, plain 「かぞく」 for yours." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "This is a memory trick, not a real word origin. There is a verb, 「かぞえる」, which means to count. The family word starts with the very same sounds, 「かぞ」. Picture a mother standing beside the family minivan. She is counting her children out loud as they pile in: one, two, three, four. Then she counts the dog, the grandmother and the umbrella. Every time she counts, she is counting the 「かぞく」. Then she gets to the end and realizes she counted herself too, just like Japanese people do when they say how many are in the family. The two words are not historically connected, but the picture holds them together nicely. So: count the family, and you can't forget the word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「家|か」 is a pig under a roof, the house, and 「族|ぞく」 is a clan gathered under a flag, so together it is the clan of one house.",
      "「族|ぞく」 means a tribe of people, as in 「貴族|きぞく」, nobility, and 「水族館|すいぞくかん」, an aquarium, the building for the water tribe.",
      "Say 「ごかぞく」 with the polite 「ご」 for someone else's family, but plain 「かぞく」 for your own, since Japanese stays humble about its own side.",
      "It starts like 「かぞえる」, to count, so picture a mother counting everyone into the minivan and finally counting herself too."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}の[家族|かぞく]は{四人|よにん}です。",
    en: "There are four people in my family." },
  "おばあさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the mirror image of 「おじいさん」. The polite 「お」 goes on the front, the friendly 「さん」 on the end, and the core 「ばあ」 in the middle. Again, the long vowel matters. Stretch the 「あ」 and you are talking about grandmother. Cut it short to 「おばさん」 and you get aunt, or a middle-aged woman. A way to remember it: grandma has lived longer, so she gets the longer sound. Like grandfather, this word goes beyond family, and it is used for any elderly woman. A bit of social advice: be careful with these words for strangers. Calling a woman in her forties 「おばさん」 can sting, and calling someone 「おばあさん」 when she doesn't feel old can sting even more. When talking formally about your own grandmother, adults use 「祖母|そぼ」, but this word is fine in casual talk. So: long 「あ」, grandmother, short 「あ」, aunt." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Just like with grandfather, the family version is usually 「おばあちゃん」, with the soft 「ちゃん」. Adults keep using it for their own grandmother because it sounds loving. Very small children may say 「ばあば」. For formal talk about your own grandmother, it is 「そぼ」. When written in kanji with the grandmother meaning, the word is 「お祖母さん|おばあさん」, the ancestor kanji plus mother. For an old woman in general, you might see 「お婆さん|おばあさん」, and that kanji has 「女|おんな」, woman, on the bottom. Grandmothers often have a big role in Japanese families. Many people remember their grandma's cooking. And grandchildren are 「まご」, so a grandmother might proudly say 「まごがさんにんいます」, I have three grandchildren. So: 「おばあちゃん」 at home, 「そぼ」 in formal talk, and 「まご」 for the grandkids." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Grandmothers have their own famous neighborhood in Tokyo. Sugamo, near Ikebukuro, is jokingly called 「おばあちゃんのはらじゅく」, the Harajuku of grandmas. Harajuku is the trendy street for teens, and Sugamo is the shopping street older women love. It has a popular temple, shops selling comfortable clothes, traditional sweets and, famously, red underwear, which is thought to bring good health and luck. Grandmothers also star in Japanese folk tales. In the famous story of Momotaro, the Peach Boy, an old woman is washing clothes in the river when a giant peach floats by. She brings it home, and a baby boy pops out. The story starts with the classic line 「おばあさんはかわへせんたくに」, the old woman went to the river to do laundry. So: a grandma's shopping paradise, and the old woman who found the peach." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Like 「おじいさん」, the core 「ばあ」 is wrapped in 「お」 and 「さん」, and its long 「あ」 means grandmother, while the short 「おばさん」 means aunt.",
      "At home she is 「おばあちゃん」, in formal talk about your own grandmother she is 「そぼ」, and her grandchildren are 「まご」.",
      "Sugamo in Tokyo is nicknamed 「おばあちゃんのはらじゅく」, the grandmas' Harajuku, and in Momotaro it is the old woman who finds the giant peach."
    ],
    ja: "[おばあさん]はやさしいです。",
    en: "My grandmother is kind." },
  "ちち|父": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji's origin is debated, so here is a hook instead. A way to remember it: the two dots at the top and the big cross at the bottom look like a stern dad with his arms folded across his chest. The important thing is how this word is used. Japanese splits family words into two sets. This plain set is for your own family when talking to outsiders. So if your teacher asks about your dad, you say 「ちちはかいしゃいんです」, my father is an office worker. When you talk about someone else's father, or call your own dad at home, you use 「お父さん|おとうさん」. Notice it is the very same kanji, just with a special reading. The plain one is like being modest about your own side. It pairs with 「母|はは」, mother. In June, Japan also celebrates 「父の日|ちちのひ」, Father's Day. So: your own dad, said humbly to others." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「ふ」, and you will meet it in family words. 「祖父|そふ」 is grandfather, the ancestor father. 「父母|ふぼ」 means father and mother, parents, and you see it on school forms. In 「父兄|ふけい」, father and older brother, it used to mean the guardians of a student, though that word now sounds old-fashioned. Then there is the special reading in 「お父さん|おとうさん」. There the kanji is read 「とう」, a reading used basically only in this word, so just learn it as a set. It also sits on top of the kanji for old man in 「お爺さん|おじいさん」, so when you spot this shape inside a bigger character, think of a father figure. So: 「ちち」 for your own dad, 「ふ」 in grandfather and parents, and 「とう」 in the polite word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is something charming about Japanese families. People are often called by their role, seen from the youngest child's point of view. So once a couple has kids, the wife often calls her husband 「おとうさん」, dad, and he may call her 「おかあさん」, mom. It sounds odd in English, but it is completely normal in Japan. Kids may call their father 「パパ」 when they are little, then switch to 「おとうさん」 as they grow up. Adult men sometimes call their own father 「おやじ」, my old man, which is rough and casual, not something to say to his face in a formal setting. And when you are talking about your father to a teacher or boss, you use the humble 「ちち」. A quick example: 「ちちはりょうりがじょうずです」, my father is good at cooking. So: 「ちち」 to outsiders, 「おとうさん」 at home, even from his wife." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be aware that this word has a homophone. 「乳|ちち」 means milk, especially breast milk, and also the breast itself. That kanji, read 「にゅう」, gives us 「牛乳|ぎゅうにゅう」, cow's milk, the milk you buy at the store. In everyday life the context is almost always obvious, because you are talking about family. Still, it's good to know why this word sometimes draws a giggle. There are also a couple of tips for clear pronunciation. The two syllables are the same, so make both crisp and equal, without stretching either one. It's not a long vowel word. And remember the pair: 「ちち」 and 「はは」 both repeat their sound, which makes them easy to remember together. So: one father, one milk, and your context keeps them apart." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the plain, modest word for your own father when talking to outsiders, while someone else's father or your dad at home is 「お父さん|おとうさん」.",
      "The kanji reads 「ふ」 in 「祖父|そふ」, grandfather, and 「父母|ふぼ」, parents, and takes the special reading 「とう」 only in the polite 「お父さん|おとうさん」.",
      "Families name people by role from the youngest child's view, so even a wife may call her husband 「おとうさん」, while you use 「ちち」 with a teacher or boss.",
      "It sounds the same as 「乳|ちち」, milk or breast, which explains the occasional giggle, but talking about family makes the meaning obvious."
    ],
    ja: "[父|ちち]は{会社員|かいしゃいん}です。",
    en: "My father is an office worker." },
  "はは|母": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji is a picture of a woman, with two dots added for breasts, a nursing mother. That shape hides inside another word you know: 「毎日|まいにち」, every day. The top of 「毎|まい」 contains this same mother shape. A way to remember it: mothers do things every day. Just like 「父|ちち」, this word is the plain, modest one, used for your own mother when talking to people outside the family. 「はははせんせいです」, my mother is a teacher. Notice how the sound repeats: that is the word, followed by the topic marker. For someone else's mother, or when calling your own mom, use 「お母さん|おかあさん」, the same kanji with a special reading. Japan celebrates 「母の日|ははのひ」, Mother's Day, in May, with red carnations, just like in many other countries. So: a nursing mother, and your own mom said modestly." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「ぼ」, and it appears in some important words. 「祖母|そぼ」 is grandmother. 「父母|ふぼ」 is parents. Then it grows beyond family. 「母国|ぼこく」 is your mother country, your homeland. 「母語|ぼご」 is your mother tongue, your native language. So English and Japanese share the same metaphor here. In the polite word 「お母さん|おかあさん」, the kanji takes a special reading, 「かあ」, used basically only in that word. Remember the pattern from father. Plain for your own, special reading with 「お」 and 「さん」 for someone else's. One small caution with the kanji: it looks a lot like 「毎|まい」, every, which adds a little hat on top. Read carefully: no hat means mother. So: 「ぼ」 in grandmother, homeland and mother tongue, and the hatless shape is mother." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Little kids often call their mother 「ママ」, and later switch to 「おかあさん」 or the affectionate 「おかあちゃん」. Adult men sometimes call their own mother 「おふくろ」, a warm but casual word, used when talking to friends. Very polite speech, like when you address a friend's mother, may use 「おかあさま」. As with dad, once a couple has children, the husband often calls his wife 「おかあさん」. It just means the kids' mom. The home cooking people miss most is called 「おふくろのあじ」, mom's flavor, and restaurants even advertise it. When you mention your own mother to a teacher, stick with 「はは」: 「はははりょうりがすきです」, my mother likes cooking. So: 「はは」 to outsiders, 「おかあさん」 at home, and 「おふくろのあじ」 for that taste of home." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just for fun. This word sounds exactly like laughter, ha ha. Picture a mom who laughs at every single one of your jokes, even the terrible ones. You tell her a pun about a cat, and she laughs, ha ha. You trip over the dog, and she laughs, ha ha. Your father tells the same joke, and she just rolls her eyes. Only her own children get the laugh. Now make it stick with the kanji. Picture the two dots inside the kanji as her eyes squeezed shut while she laughs. Next time you need the word for your mother, just hear that warm laugh: ha ha. And remember that the plain word is the humble one, for talking about her to other people. So: a mom who always laughs, ha ha." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a woman with two dots for breasts, a nursing mother, and the plain word is for your own mother when talking to outsiders.",
      "Read 「ぼ」, it gives 「祖母|そぼ」, grandmother, 「母国|ぼこく」, homeland, and 「母語|ぼご」, mother tongue, and the kanji without a hat on top is mother, not 「毎|まい」.",
      "Use 「はは」 with outsiders and 「おかあさん」 at home, while men may casually say 「おふくろ」, as in 「おふくろのあじ」, the taste of mom's cooking.",
      "It sounds like laughter, so picture a mom who laughs ha ha at all your jokes, eyes squeezed shut like the two dots in the kanji."
    ],
    ja: "[母|はは]は{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "My mother is a teacher." },
  "あに|兄": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji is simple to build. On top sits 「口|くち」, mouth. Under it, two strokes that are legs, the same legs you see under the eye in 「見る|みる」. A way to remember it: a big mouth on legs, the older brother bossing the younger kids around. This plain word is for your own older brother when talking to others: 「あにはだいがくせいです」, my older brother is a college student. For someone else's older brother, or when calling your own, use the polite 「お兄さん|おにいさん」, with the special reading 「にい」. In 「兄弟|きょうだい」, siblings, the same kanji takes its Chinese-origin reading, 「きょう」. Japanese also always marks older and younger. There is no plain word for brother that ignores age, so you have to choose. So: a mouth on legs, the bossy older brother, said modestly about your own." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The two legs at the bottom of this kanji appear in several characters you already know. They sit under 「元|げん」, as in 「元気|げんき」. They sit under 「先|せん」, as in 「先生|せんせい」. And they hold up the eye in 「見る|みる」. The shape is a person's legs, so in each of these the top part is carried by a person. The kanji has two Chinese-origin readings. One is 「きょう」, from 「兄弟|きょうだい」. The other is 「けい」, in formal words like 「父兄|ふけい」, an old word for a student's guardians, or 「義兄|ぎけい」, brother-in-law. In the polite 「お兄さん|おにいさん」, it takes a special reading, 「にい」. So one little kanji has four readings you may meet. So: legs like in healthy and teacher, and readings 「あに」, 「にい」, 「きょう」 and 「けい」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Inside a family, younger siblings usually call their big brother 「おにいちゃん」, using 「ちゃん」 for warmth. They hardly ever use his name. In rough, casual male speech, you may hear 「あにき」, big bro. It is also used in gangster movies for a senior gang member, so it has a tough-guy feel. Outside the family, 「おにいさん」 is a friendly way to address a young man you don't know. A street vendor might call out to a passing young man with it, a bit like hey there, young man. Shop clerks and bartenders get called this way too. But when you talk about your own brother to others, the plain humble word is correct: 「あにはびょういんではたらいています」, my older brother works at a hospital. So: 「おにいちゃん」 at home, 「あにき」 for tough guys, 「あに」 to outsiders." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "When you say how many brothers you have, Japanese uses 「いる」, because people are living things, not 「ある」. Then you add the counter for people. So: 「あにがひとりいます」, I have one older brother. Two is 「ふたり」, so 「あにがふたりいます」, I have two older brothers. Notice that one person and two people have special counts, 「ひとり」 and 「ふたり」, and from three on, the counter is 「にん」, like 「さんにん」. The number usually comes after the family word and the particle, right before the verb. Another useful form is the question 「おにいさんがいますか」, do you have an older brother. Notice you use the polite word for the other person's brother. Answer with 「はい、あにがいます」, using the humble word for your own. So: 「いる」 for people, 「ひとり」 and 「ふたり」, and switch words when the brother changes owners." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a big 「口|くち」, mouth, on two legs, the bossy older brother, and the plain word is for your own older brother when talking to others.",
      "The same legs hold up 「元|げん」, 「先|せん」 and 「見る|みる」, and this kanji has four readings: 「あに」, 「にい」, 「きょう」 and 「けい」.",
      "Younger siblings call him 「おにいちゃん」, tough guys say 「あにき」, and 「おにいさん」 is also a friendly way to address a young man you don't know.",
      "Brothers are counted with 「いる」 and 「ひとり」 or 「ふたり」, and you ask 「おにいさんがいますか」 but answer with the humble 「あにがいます」."
    ],
    ja: "[兄|あに]は{大学生|だいがくせい}です。",
    en: "My older brother is a college student." },
  "あね|姉": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. On the left of the kanji is 「女|おんな」, woman. That same woman side shows up in 「妹|いもうと」, younger sister, so both sisters share it. The right side is a part that mostly gives the sound in Chinese, and it looks like the kanji for market. A way to remember it: the older sister is the woman sent to the market. This plain word is for your own older sister when talking to others: 「あねはとうきょうにすんでいます」, my older sister lives in Tokyo. For someone else's sister, or when calling your own sister at home, use 「お姉さん|おねえさん」, with the special reading 「ねえ」. A small cultural note: in Japan, younger siblings usually call older ones by this title instead of by name, while older siblings call younger ones by name. So: woman plus market, your own big sister, said modestly." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「し」, and it lives in the word 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters. Older sister plus younger sister makes all sisters, just like brothers make siblings. This word gives Japan a very common phrase. 「姉妹都市|しまいとし」 means sister cities, and Japanese towns have hundreds of them around the world. If you live in a city in another country, there is a decent chance it has a Japanese sister city. The word also works for companies or schools that are paired up. In the polite word 「お姉さん|おねえさん」, the kanji takes a special reading, 「ねえ」. And the whole kanji is built with the woman radical on the left, shared with 「妹|いもうと」, which makes the two sisters easy to spot as a pair. So: 「し」 in sisters and sister cities, 「ねえ」 in the polite word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Younger siblings call their big sister 「おねえちゃん」, and often keep that habit even as adults. Outside the family, 「おねえさん」 is a friendly way to address a young woman you don't know, like a shop clerk or a waitress, though in a restaurant most people just say 「すみません」 to call staff. There is an old expression, 「あねさんにょうぼう」, which describes a wife who is older than her husband, an older sister wife. It is said with affection, often implying she takes charge. And there is a fun habit in anime and manga. Cool, confident older women characters are often nicknamed 「あねご」 or 「ねえさん」 by the younger characters. When talking about your own sister politely, stick to the plain word: 「あねはぎんこうにつとめています」, my older sister works at a bank. So: 「おねえちゃん」 at home, 「おねえさん」 to strangers, 「あね」 to outsiders." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left, shared with 「妹|いもうと」, and a market-like part on the right, so picture the older sister sent to the market.",
      "Read 「し」 it forms 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters, which gives the common phrase 「姉妹都市|しまいとし」, sister cities.",
      "At home she is 「おねえちゃん」, 「おねえさん」 politely addresses a young woman you don't know, and you use plain 「あね」 with outsiders."
    ],
    ja: "[姉|あね]は{東京|とうきょう}に{住んでいます|すんでいます}。",
    en: "My older sister lives in Tokyo." },
  "きょうだい|兄弟": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji here use their Chinese-origin readings. 「兄|きょう」 is the older brother kanji you know from 「兄|あに」. 「弟|だい」 is the younger brother kanji from 「弟|おとうと」. Older brother plus younger brother. But the word covers all siblings, sisters included. That surprises English speakers. If you want to be fully precise in writing, you may see the longer 「兄弟姉妹|きょうだいしまい」, brothers and sisters. A useful question is 「きょうだいがいますか」, do you have any siblings? Another is 「なんにんきょうだいですか」. Here is the trap: the answer counts you too. If you say 「さんにんきょうだいです」, it means there are three kids in total, including yourself, so you have two siblings. That is different from English, where you would count only the others. So: older plus younger brother, all siblings, and you count yourself." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「弟|おとうと」, younger brother, has an interesting traditional explanation. It is usually described as a cord wound around a stick in neat, ordered loops. That idea of order and sequence came to mean the one who comes after, the younger brother. The kanji also has several readings. 「だい」 is used here. 「てい」 appears in the formal 「兄弟|けいてい」, a rare literary way to read the same two kanji. And 「で」 shows up in 「弟子|でし」, a disciple or apprentice, the junior who learns under a master, like in martial arts or traditional crafts. In 「兄弟|きょうだい」, the first kanji also uses an unusual reading, 「きょう」, instead of the more common 「けい」. That's why this word can feel odd to read at first. So: a cord wound in order for the younger one, and 「で」 for an apprentice." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The word 「きょうだい」 can be written with different kanji depending on who the siblings are, and the sound stays the same. 「兄弟|きょうだい」 is the standard spelling. But if you have an older brother and a younger sister, writers sometimes use 「兄妹|きょうだい」, older brother plus younger sister. An older sister and a younger brother can be 「姉弟|きょうだい」. Only sisters have their own word, 「姉妹|しまい」. You will see these special spellings in novels, manga and song lyrics, where the writer wants to show exactly who the siblings are. In speech, though, you always just hear 「きょうだい」. A related word is 「ひとりっこ」, an only child, a child who is one alone. So if someone asks if you have siblings and you don't, answer 「いいえ、ひとりっこです」. So: one sound, several spellings, and 「ひとりっこ」 for none at all." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you ask about someone else's siblings politely, add 「ご」: 「ごきょうだいはいますか」, do you have siblings? To describe your place in the family, Japanese uses 「なんばんめ」, which number. You might say 「わたしはさんにんきょうだいのまんなかです」, I'm the middle of three kids. The oldest is 「いちばんうえ」, the top, and the youngest is 「いちばんした」, the bottom. In Japan, birth order still carries a feeling. The oldest is often expected to be responsible, and the youngest to be a bit spoiled. Among close male friends, this word can also mean buddy or bro, a bit like calling a friend brother in English, and you hear it in movies about tough guys. So: 「ごきょうだい」 for others, 「うえ」 and 「した」 for oldest and youngest, and a bro in tough-guy talk." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Older brother plus younger brother, yet it covers all siblings, and when you say 「さんにんきょうだいです」 the count includes yourself.",
      "The younger brother kanji is traditionally a cord wound in ordered loops, the one who comes after, and it reads 「で」 in 「弟子|でし」, apprentice.",
      "The sound stays 「きょうだい」 but writers may spell it 「兄妹|きょうだい」 or 「姉弟|きょうだい」 to show who the siblings are, and an only child is 「ひとりっこ」.",
      "Ask politely with 「ごきょうだい」, describe the oldest and youngest as 「いちばんうえ」 and 「いちばんした」, and among tough guys it can mean bro."
    ],
    ja: "[兄弟|きょうだい]がいますか。",
    en: "Do you have any brothers or sisters?" },
  "おとこのひと|男の人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「男|おとこ」 has two parts you can see clearly. On top is 「田|た」, a rice field. Underneath is 「力|ちから」, strength or power. Strength in the rice field. That is the man who did the heavy farm work, in the old picture of things. The reading here is the native one. In other words you may meet the Chinese-origin readings, like 「だん」 in 「男性|だんせい」, male, a more formal word. Then 「の」 links it to 「人|ひと」, person. Man's person, a man. A tip for English speakers: saying just 「おとこ」 on its own can sound blunt or rough, like saying that guy. Adding 「のひと」 makes it neutral and polite. The same pattern gives you 「男の子|おとこのこ」, boy, a male child. And the pair to this word is 「女の人|おんなのひと」, woman. So: field plus strength, the man, made polite with 「のひと」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has two Chinese-origin readings, and both turn up often. 「だん」 is the more common one. 「男性|だんせい」 is male, a polite and neutral word you see on forms and hear in announcements. 「男子|だんし」 means boys or men, as in a men's team in sports or the boys in a class. The other reading is 「なん」. You meet it in birth order words. 「長男|ちょうなん」 is the eldest son, the senior son. 「次男|じなん」 is the second son, the next son. Japanese family registers and introductions often use these, so you might hear 「わたしはちょうなんです」, I am the eldest son. Notice that 「長|ちょう」, from 「ながい」, appears here with its senior meaning. So: 「おとこ」 native, 「だん」 for male and boys, and 「なん」 for sons by birth order." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This kanji is one you'll want to recognize quickly in Japan, because it marks men-only spaces. At a hot spring, 「おんせん」, the baths are separated, and the entrance has a curtain called a 「のれん」 with this kanji on it. The men's side is usually blue, and the women's is usually red. Many places swap the sides of the baths on certain days, so read the curtain every time, not just the first time. Public restrooms use this kanji too, often next to a blue figure. Getting it wrong at a hot spring is one of the classic travel nightmares, so learning to recognize it is a real life skill. In polite announcements, staff say 「だんせい」 instead, like the men's bath. So: look for this kanji and the blue curtain before you walk in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「男|おとこ」 is a rice field over strength, the man doing heavy farm work, and adding 「のひと」 turns blunt 「おとこ」 into a neutral, polite word.",
      "The kanji reads 「だん」 in 「男性|だんせい」, male, and 「男子|だんし」, boys or men, and 「なん」 in birth-order words like 「長男|ちょうなん」, eldest son.",
      "Learn to spot this kanji on hot spring curtains and restrooms, usually blue for men, and check every time because baths sometimes swap sides."
    ],
    ja: "あの[男の人|おとこのひと]は{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "That man is a teacher." },
  "おんなのひと|女の人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「女|おんな」 is commonly explained as a picture of a woman kneeling, with her arms crossed in front of her. The reading here is the native one. This kanji is a great anchor, because it shows up as the left side of many others. It is in 「姉|あね」, older sister, and 「妹|いもうと」, younger sister. It is in 「好き|すき」, to like, where a woman sits beside a child. It even appears in 「彼女|かのじょ」, she or girlfriend, where it uses the Chinese-origin reading 「じょ」. Add 「の」 and 「人|ひと」, person, and you get a woman. Just like with the man word, saying only 「おんな」 can sound rough, so adding 「のひと」 keeps it polite. A girl is 「女の子|おんなのこ」. So: a kneeling figure, the woman side in sisters and liking, made polite with 「のひと」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has three readings you'll meet. The native one is 「おんな」, used here. The most common Chinese-origin reading is 「じょ」. 「女性|じょせい」 is female, the polite neutral word on forms and in announcements. 「女子|じょし」 means girls or women, as in a women's team or the girls in a class. 「長女|ちょうじょ」 is the eldest daughter. And 「彼女|かのじょ」, she or girlfriend, uses it too. The third reading, 「にょ」, is older and rarer. It shows up in words like 「女房|にょうぼう」, an old-fashioned word for wife. There is also a special case in 「女神|めがみ」, goddess, where the kanji is read just 「め」. Most of the time, though, think 「おんな」 for the plain word and 「じょ」 in compounds. So: 「おんな」 native, 「じょ」 for female and daughter, and 「にょ」 in an old word for wife." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese has several ways to say woman, and they differ in tone. 「おんなのひと」 is the everyday, neutral choice when you point someone out. 「じょせい」 is more formal, used in news, on signs and in business. 「おんな」 alone can sound blunt. And here is something that surprises learners. 「おんなのこ」, literally girl, is often used for young adult women too, especially among friends, a bit like saying girls in English for a group of women. Japan also has 「じょせいせんようしゃりょう」, women-only train cars, during rush hour on many city lines, with pink signs on the platform. They were introduced to protect women from harassment on crowded trains. A man who wanders in by mistake will get some looks, so watch for the signs. So: 「おんなのひと」 for everyday, 「じょせい」 for formal, and pink signs on the train platform." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「女|おんな」 is a kneeling woman, and it shows up as the woman side of 「姉|あね」, 「妹|いもうと」 and 「好き|すき」, while adding 「のひと」 keeps it polite.",
      "The common compound reading is 「じょ」, as in 「女性|じょせい」, female, and 「長女|ちょうじょ」, eldest daughter, while the rarer 「にょ」 appears in 「女房|にょうぼう」.",
      "「おんなのひと」 is the everyday neutral choice and 「じょせい」 the formal one, and the pink signs for 「じょせいせんようしゃりょう」 mark women-only train cars."
    ],
    ja: "あの[女の人|おんなのひと]はだれですか。",
    en: "Who is that woman?" },
  "かいしゃ|会社": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「会|かい」 is the meeting kanji from 「会う|あう」, to meet. 「社|しゃ」 has the altar radical on the left and earth on the right. It first meant a shrine to the earth god, where villagers gathered, and grew into the idea of a group or association. You can still see the shrine meaning in 「神社|じんじゃ」, a shrine, where the sound shifts slightly. So a company is a meeting association. Family words with this kanji: 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, office worker, a member of the company, and 「社長|しゃちょう」, company president, the head of the association. Here is a fun twist. Flip the two kanji around and you get 「社会|しゃかい」, society. Same pieces, bigger group. So: people meeting in an association, and flipped, it becomes society." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese business talk has special words for your company and theirs. When you speak to someone from another company, you call your own company 「へいしゃ」, our humble company, and theirs 「おんしゃ」, your honored company. In writing, like on a resume or email, theirs becomes 「きしゃ」. Job applicants practice these words carefully. In casual talk, people simply say 「うちのかいしゃ」, my company. To say where someone works, you can use 「かいしゃにつとめる」, to be employed at a company, with 「に」. Or 「かいしゃではたらく」, to work at a company, with 「で」. Notice the particle changes with the verb. And to go to work, people often say 「かいしゃにいく」 even if they don't work in an office. So: 「へいしゃ」 for us, 「おんしゃ」 for you, and watch the particle with each verb." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Starting a job in Japan is a ceremony. Most new graduates join companies at the same time, at the beginning of April, when the school year also begins. Companies hold a formal welcome event called 「にゅうしゃしき」, the entering the company ceremony, where everyone in dark suits listens to speeches from the president. These new employees are called 「しんにゅうしゃいん」. For years, many people stayed with one company for their whole career, and colleagues became like a second family. After-work drinking parties, 「のみかい」, are a classic part of office life, though many younger workers now skip them. You'll also hear the polite thanks after a long day, 「おつかれさまです」, said again and again in every office. So: April ceremonies, dark suits, and thank you for your hard work." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「会|かい」, means to meet or gather, and it builds a lot of words. 「会話|かいわ」, meeting talk, is conversation, and you see it in every chapter of your textbook. 「会議|かいぎ」 is a business meeting. 「教会|きょうかい」 is a church, a teaching gathering. 「会員|かいいん」 is a member of a club. The second kanji, 「社|しゃ」, gives 「社員|しゃいん」, a company employee, and 「本社|ほんしゃ」, the head office, the main company. When it means company, you can drop it after a name too, like a newspaper company is 「しんぶんしゃ」. Notice the pattern: this kanji turns many nouns into an organization. So: 「かい」 is where people gather, and 「しゃ」 is the group they form." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「会|かい」 is meeting and 「社|しゃ」 began as an earth shrine where villagers gathered, so a company is a meeting association, and flipped it becomes 「社会|しゃかい」, society.",
      "With another company you call yours 「へいしゃ」 and theirs 「おんしゃ」, and the particle shifts with the verb: 「かいしゃにつとめる」 but 「かいしゃではたらく」.",
      "New graduates join companies together in April at a ceremony called 「にゅうしゃしき」, and 「おつかれさまです」 echoes through every office.",
      "「会|かい」 is where people gather, as in 「会話|かいわ」 and 「会議|かいぎ」, and 「社|しゃ」 is the group they form, as in 「社員|しゃいん」 and 「本社|ほんしゃ」."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は[会社|かいしゃ]で{働いています|はたらいています}。",
    en: "My father works at a company." },
  "しょくどう|食堂": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「食|しょく」 is the eating kanji you know from 「食べる|たべる」 and 「食べ物|たべもの」, food, where it takes the native reading. 「堂|どう」 means a hall, a large room for people to gather. You can see earth at the bottom, a solid building. An eating hall. In Japan, this word has two flavors. On campus or at a company, it is the cafeteria, where you line up and get cheap set meals. Students often shorten the university one to 「がくしょく」, student cafeteria. In towns, small old-fashioned family diners also use the name, serving simple set meals with rice, miso soup and pickles. They often have a cloth curtain hanging over the door. So: eating plus hall, from the school cafeteria to the cozy neighborhood diner." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「食|しょく」, shows up in a lot of words about meals, and knowing them helps you read menus. 「食事|しょくじ」 is a meal, the polite word. 「朝食|ちょうしょく」 is breakfast, the word you see on hotel signs. 「和食|わしょく」 is Japanese food. 「洋食|ようしょく」 is Western-style food, like curry rice or hamburger steak. And at a cafeteria, the star is 「定食|ていしょく」, a set meal. A set meal usually means a main dish with rice, miso soup and pickles, all on one tray. Ask for 「とんかつていしょく」 and you get the whole tray, not just the pork. You'll also see 「食券|しょっけん」, a meal ticket, where the reading squeezes to 「しょっ」 before the next sound. So: 「しょく」 is eating, from breakfast to the set meal on your tray." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「堂|どう」, means a hall, a big room or building for people, and it brings a certain grandness. In a school, 「講堂|こうどう」 is the auditorium, the lecture hall. At a temple, 「本堂|ほんどう」 is the main hall with the statue of the Buddha. On long distance trains, the dining car used to be 「食堂車|しょくどうしゃ」, an eating hall on wheels. The kanji also appears in a great phrase, 「正々堂々|せいせいどうどう」, which means fair and square, openly and honorably, like a sports team promising to play fairly. The bottom of the kanji is 「土|つち」, earth, a solid building standing on the ground. So: 「どう」 is a hall, from a temple to an auditorium to a dining room." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Many cafeterias and cheap diners use a ticket machine. You walk in, find the machine near the door, pay, and press the button for your meal. A ticket comes out. You hand it to the staff and wait for your tray. The machines can have dozens of buttons, sometimes with pictures, sometimes just kanji, so knowing the food words really helps. A typical exchange at the counter: the staff says 「おまちどうさまでした」, sorry to keep you waiting, and puts your tray down. You say 「いただきます」 before eating and 「ごちそうさまでした」 at the end, often to the kitchen as you leave. Many people also return their own tray to a return counter. So: buy a ticket, take your tray, and say thanks on the way out." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「食|しょく」 is eating and 「堂|どう」 is a hall, so it is an eating hall, from a school cafeteria, shortened to 「がくしょく」, to a cozy neighborhood diner.",
      "「食|しょく」 helps you read menus, especially 「定食|ていしょく」, a set meal with rice, miso soup and pickles on one tray.",
      "「堂|どう」 is a grand hall standing on earth, as in 「講堂|こうどう」, auditorium, and 「本堂|ほんどう」, a temple's main hall.",
      "Many cafeterias have a ticket machine by the door, so buy a ticket, hand it over for your tray, and say 「ごちそうさまでした」 as you leave."
    ],
    ja: "[食堂|しょくどう]で{昼ごはん|ひるごはん}を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat lunch in the cafeteria." },
  "うた|歌": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one, and it is the same kanji as the verb 「歌う|うたう」, to sing. Look at the left side and you can find little mouths, 「口|くち」, stacked up. The right side is a radical that shows a person with their mouth wide open, which is used in kanji about breathing and voice. So it is a person singing with an open mouth. The kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「か」. You hear it in 「歌手|かしゅ」, singer, a person whose skill is song, and in 「歌舞伎|かぶき」, kabuki theater, where the first kanji is this one, song, followed by dance and skill. Japan also gave the world karaoke, and singing together after work or with friends is a big social habit. So: mouths and an open-mouthed person, the song in kabuki and karaoke." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In older Japanese, this word also meant a poem. Classical poems were meant to be chanted aloud, so song and poem were the same thing. The classic form is 「和歌|わか」, Japanese song, and the most common type is 「短歌|たんか」, short song, a poem of thirty one syllables in five lines, arranged five, seven, five, seven, seven. Haiku came later and is shorter. There's a famous New Year card game called 「百人一首|ひゃくにんいっしゅ」, one hundred poets, one poem each. A reader chants the start of a poem, and players race to slap the card with its ending. Competitive players are lightning fast. Many Japanese kids memorize some of these poems in school. So: the song word is also the poem word, and poems are still read aloud in a fast card game." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji builds a whole family of music words. 「国歌|こっか」 is a national anthem, the country song. 「歌詞|かし」 is song lyrics, the words of a song. 「演歌|えんか」 is a traditional style of Japanese ballad, full of heartbreak, harbors and rain, loved by older generations. When the native word comes second in a compound, it keeps its sound, as in 「子守歌|こもりうた」, a lullaby, a child minding song. And 「はなうた」 is humming, a song through your nose. The singer you already know is 「歌手|かしゅ」. If you like karaoke, you'll see the 「かし」 scrolling on the screen. So: 「か」 in anthem, lyrics and enka, and 「うた」 for lullabies and humming." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To talk about singing ability in Japanese, you use 「じょうず」 and 「へた」 with 「が」: 「うたがじょうずですね」, you're good at singing, or 「うたがへたです」, I'm bad at singing. To ask about music taste, try 「どんなうたがすきですか」, what kind of songs do you like? Notice that 「すき」 also takes 「が」 for the thing you like. If you want to sing a song, the verb is the same kanji, so 「うたをうたう」. People also say 「このうた、しってる？」, do you know this song? When someone sings well, a common compliment is 「いいうただね」, it's a nice song, and the classic modest reply to praise is 「いいえ、まだまだです」, not at all, I still have a long way to go. So: 「じょうず」 and 「へた」 with 「が」, and stay humble when praised." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows stacked mouths and a person with a wide open mouth, a person singing, and its reading 「か」 appears in 「歌手|かしゅ」, singer, and 「歌舞伎|かぶき」.",
      "In older Japanese this word also meant a poem, like the 「短歌|たんか」 of thirty one syllables, still chanted aloud in the card game 「百人一首|ひゃくにんいっしゅ」.",
      "Read 「か」 it gives 「国歌|こっか」, anthem, and 「歌詞|かし」, lyrics, while as a second part it stays 「うた」, as in 「子守歌|こもりうた」, lullaby.",
      "Singing skill takes 「じょうず」 or 「へた」 with 「が」, as in 「うたがじょうずですね」, and the modest reply to praise is 「いいえ、まだまだです」."
    ],
    ja: "この[歌|うた]が{好き|すき}です。",
    en: "I like this song." },
  "サークル|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This loanword comes from English circle, as in a circle of friends. Watch how the sounds got squeezed in. The c becomes 「さ」. The ir sound turns into a long 「あー」, because Japanese doesn't have that English vowel. Then the cl gets split apart with extra vowels, becoming 「くる」, and the l turns into an r sound. In meaning it shifted, too. At Japanese universities, this word means a casual student club for a hobby, like tennis, music, travel or anime. It is different from the more serious 「部活|ぶかつ」, a club or team with strict practice, which you hear more about at high schools. Joining a circle is a big part of college social life in Japan, and it is where many people make their closest friends. To say you're in one: 「テニスのサークルにはいっています」. So: an English circle, turned into the relaxed college club." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In April, at the start of the school year, Japanese universities go wild with recruiting. Older students set up booths and hand out flyers to new students, inviting them to join their circle. This season is called 「しんかん」, short for welcoming new students, and there are often free welcome parties. A common question among students is 「なんのサークルにはいっていますか」, what circle are you in? Notice the verb 「はいる」, to enter, with 「に」. You join a circle by entering it. Circles go on trips, hold parties, and many spend more time socializing than doing the actual hobby. Some are serious, some are just an excuse to hang out. A typical answer: 「テニスのサークルです。でも、あまりテニスはしません」, it's a tennis circle, but we don't play much tennis. So: join in April, enter it with 「はいる」, and expect lots of socializing." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word shows a common pattern in loanwords from English. English vowels with an r after them, like ir, er and ar, usually become a long vowel in katakana, written with the long bar. You see it in many words you know. 「コンピューター」, computer. 「ハンバーガー」, hamburger. 「パーティー」, party. 「スーパー」, supermarket. In each, the long bar holds a vowel for two beats. Count the beats here: 「サ」, the long bar, 「ク」, 「ル」. Four beats. Say it with even timing, not like the two quick English syllables. The ending is also typical. English words ending in an l sound get an extra 「る」 in Japanese, like 「ボール」, ball, and 「ホテル」, hotel. Note that this word means a club, not the shape. The geometric circle is 「まる」 or 「えん」. So: long bar for the vowel, 「る」 for the l, and four even beats." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English circle, it means a casual university hobby club, more relaxed than the strict 「部活|ぶかつ」, and you join with 「はいる」.",
      "Clubs recruit wildly in April during 「しんかん」, students ask 「なんのサークルにはいっていますか」, and many circles socialize more than they do the hobby.",
      "The English ir sound becomes a long bar and the final l gains 「る」, giving four even beats, and remember it means a club, not the shape 「まる」."
    ],
    ja: "テニスの[サークル]に{入っています|はいっています}。",
    en: "I'm in a tennis club." },
  "くるま|車": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is a picture of a cart seen from above. The center box is the cart, and the long line through the middle is the axle, with wheels at each end. The reading here is the native one. The same kanji with its Chinese-origin reading, 「しゃ」, shows up in many words you know. 「電車|でんしゃ」, train, is an electric vehicle. 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, bicycle, is a self-turning vehicle. In those words, the kanji means any vehicle with wheels. On its own, though, this native word almost always means a car. A way to remember it: 「くるくる」 is the Japanese sound for spinning around, and a car's wheels go round and round. So: a cart with an axle seen from above, the native word for car, and 「しゃ」 in train and bicycle." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「しゃ」, this kanji turns up in all sorts of vehicle words. 「自動車|じどうしゃ」 is an automobile, a self-moving vehicle, the formal word you see on official signs. 「駐車場|ちゅうしゃじょう」 is a parking lot, a place for stopping vehicles, and you'll spot it on blue signs everywhere. 「救急車|きゅうきゅうしゃ」 is an ambulance. A single car of a train is 「車両|しゃりょう」. The native reading stays in a few useful compounds. 「くるまいす」 is a wheelchair, a chair with wheels. And 「はぐるま」, tooth wheels, are gears, where the sound softens to 「ぐるま」. So: 「しゃ」 for vehicles and parking, and 「くるま」 for cars and wheelchairs." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The particles with this word trip up English speakers. To get in a car, you use 「に」, because you ride into it: 「くるまにのる」. Saying 「くるまをのる」 is a common mistake. To get out, it's 「くるまをおりる」, with 「を」, since you leave it. To go somewhere by car, the means of travel takes 「で」: 「くるまでいきます」, I'll go by car. To drive, use 「うんてんする」: 「くるまをうんてんします」. And to park, the verb is 「とめる」, to stop: 「ここにくるまをとめてもいいですか」, may I park my car here? A nice little dialogue: 「なんでいきますか」, how are you going? 「くるまでいきます」, by car. So: 「に」 to get in, 「を」 to get out, and 「で」 to travel by it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan drives on the left, like the United Kingdom, so the steering wheel is on the right. Car culture has some special features. Tiny cars called 「けいじどうしゃ」, light cars, are hugely popular. They have small engines and lower taxes, and they're recognizable by their yellow license plates if they are private cars. New drivers in their first year must display a green and yellow sticker, a little leaf called 「わかばマーク」, young leaf mark, so others know to give them space. Elderly drivers have their own mark too. Parking is hard to find in cities, and in many areas you need proof that you have a parking space before you can register a car. That's why many city people rely on trains. So: drive on the left, watch for yellow plates, and give the leaf mark plenty of space." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a cart seen from above with an axle through the middle, and read 「しゃ」 it means any wheeled vehicle, as in 「電車|でんしゃ」 and 「自転車|じてんしゃ」.",
      "Read 「しゃ」 it gives 「自動車|じどうしゃ」, automobile, and 「駐車場|ちゅうしゃじょう」, parking lot, while 「くるまいす」, wheelchair, keeps the native reading.",
      "You get in with 「に」, as in 「くるまにのる」, get out with 「を」, as in 「くるまをおりる」, and travel by car with 「で」.",
      "Japan drives on the left, tiny 「けいじどうしゃ」 have yellow plates, and new drivers show the green and yellow 「わかばマーク」."
    ],
    ja: "[車|くるま]で{会社|かいしゃ}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to work by car." },
  "ながい|長い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji is usually explained as a picture of an old person with long flowing hair, which gives the meanings long and also senior. That same shape sits in the top left of 「髪|かみ」, hair. The senior meaning comes out in the Chinese-origin reading, 「ちょう」, in 「社長|しゃちょう」, company president, the head person of the company. Use this adjective for length in space and in time. Long hair, a long road, a long movie, a long vacation. The opposite is 「みじかい」, short. One trap: don't use it for tall people. Japanese says 「背が高い|せがたかい」 for height. Like other 「い」 adjectives, it changes to 「ながくない」, not long, and 「ながかった」, was long. So: long-haired elder, long in space and time, and the head of the company." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「ちょう」, this kanji often means chief or head of something. 「校長|こうちょう」 is a school principal, the head of the school. 「部長|ぶちょう」 is a department manager at a company, or the captain of a school club. 「市長|しちょう」 is a mayor, the head of a city. It also means the senior one in a family: 「長男|ちょうなん」 is the eldest son. And it keeps the long meaning in words like 「長所|ちょうしょ」, a strong point, literally a long place, where you measure up well. Its opposite is 「短所|たんしょ」, a weak point, using the short kanji. Job interviewers in Japan love to ask about both. So: 「ちょう」 for the head of anything, and 「ちょうしょ」 for your strong points." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adjective joins with other words to make handy compounds. 「ながぐつ」 is rubber boots, long shoes, the ones kids wear in the rain. 「ながそで」 is long sleeves. 「ながでんわ」 is a long phone call, the kind that annoys everyone at home. 「ながいき」 means a long life, and 「ながいきしてね」 is a warm wish to an elderly relative. You can also use the adverb form, 「ながく」, with verbs: 「ながくすんでいます」, I've lived here a long time. And when a friend tells a long story, someone might tease, 「はなしがながい」, your story is long. And when people leave a job, they often say 「ながいあいだ、おせわになりました」, thank you for looking after me all this time. So: rubber boots, long sleeves, long calls and a long life." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, nothing more. The word sounds a bit like the English word nag, followed by an eye sound. Picture a neighbor who nags. Every morning she leans over the fence and starts a speech about your lawn, your recycling and your parking. The speech gets longer and longer. You check your watch. An hour. Two hours. Her hair keeps growing as she talks, flowing down past her shoulders, past her knees, across the lawn, just like the long haired figure the kanji is often said to show. By the end of the speech, her hair has wrapped around the whole house. Long hair, long talk, a long, long nag. So: the nagging neighbor with endless hair, and you have long." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is an old person with long flowing hair, giving long and senior, and it covers length in space and time but never tall people.",
      "Read 「ちょう」 it means the head of something, as in 「校長|こうちょう」, principal, and 「部長|ぶちょう」, manager, and 「長所|ちょうしょ」 is a strong point.",
      "It forms handy compounds like 「ながぐつ」, rubber boots, and 「ながそで」, long sleeves, and 「ながいきしてね」 wishes an elder a long life.",
      "It sounds like nag, so picture a nagging neighbor whose speech grows longer while her hair grows down past her knees and wraps the house."
    ],
    ja: "{妹|いもうと}は{髪|かみ}が[長い|ながい]です。",
    en: "My younger sister has long hair." },
  "みじかい|短い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. On the left of the kanji is 「矢|や」, arrow. On the right is a shape that means bean. The real history of the combination is debated, so here is a hook. A way to remember it: in old times you could measure short things with an arrow, so anything shorter than an arrow is short. That same arrow appears in 「知る|しる」, to know, so the two kanji are cousins. Use this adjective for short things in length or time: short hair, a short story, a short vacation. 「あにはかみがみじかいです」, my older brother has short hair. But here is a common mistake. It is never used for people's height. For a short person you say 「背が低い|せがひくい」, low height. The opposite is 「ながい」, long. So: an arrow and a bean, short things and short time, but never short people." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading, 「たん」, this kanji makes many short words. 「短所|たんしょ」 is a weak point, the partner of 「長所|ちょうしょ」, a strong point. 「短期|たんき」 means short term, like a short-term study abroad program. 「短大|たんだい」 is a junior college, short for a short-term university. 「短歌|たんか」 is the classical short poem with thirty one syllables. And 「短気|たんき」, a short spirit, means short-tempered, someone who gets angry fast. Notice the two 「たんき」 words sound the same but are written differently, so the kanji really matter. The long kanji usually sits on the other side of a pair, as in strong point and weak point. So: 「たん」 for short term, short tempers and short poems." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The adverb form of this adjective, 「みじかく」, is very useful with verbs. At the hair salon you might say 「みじかくしてください」, please make it short, or 「すこしみじかくきってください」, please cut it a little shorter. In a meeting, someone may ask you to keep it brief: 「みじかくはなしてください」. There is also a common idiom. 「きがみじかい」, the spirit is short, means impatient or quick tempered. If a friend snaps easily, you could say 「かれはきがみじかい」. For short sleeves, Japanese doesn't actually use this word. Short sleeves are 「はんそで」, half sleeves, while long sleeves are 「ながそで」. That catches a lot of learners. So: 「みじかく」 at the salon, a short spirit for a short temper, and half sleeves for short sleeves." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has 「矢|や」, arrow, beside a bean, so picture measuring with an arrow, and it is for short length or time but never short people, who are 「背が低い|せがひくい」.",
      "Read 「たん」 it makes 「短所|たんしょ」, weak point, 「短期|たんき」, short term, and 「短気|たんき」, short-tempered, so the kanji tell the twin sounds apart.",
      "At the salon say 「みじかくしてください」, a short spirit, 「きがみじかい」, means impatient, and short sleeves are actually 「はんそで」, half sleeves."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は{髪|かみ}が[短い|みじかい]です。",
    en: "My older brother has short hair." },
  "はやい|速い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji has the moving radical along its bottom left, the same walking road you see in kanji about travel. So this kanji is about speed, things in motion. It has a sound twin you already know: 「早い|はやい」, early. That one has 「日|ひ」, the sun, on top, because it is about time, like the early morning sun. So the trick is to ask: is it about speed or about time? A fast train uses this kanji. An early train uses the sun one. A runner who is quick, a car that is fast, a person who talks fast: all this one. Waking up early, coming home early: the sun one. When spoken there is no difference, so context decides. The adverb form 「はやく」 also comes in both kanji. So: moving radical for speed, sun kanji for early." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「そく」, and it appears in speed words. 「速度|そくど」 is speed, the word on speed limit signs. 「時速|じそく」 means per hour, so 「じそくひゃっキロ」 is one hundred kilometers an hour. 「高速道路|こうそくどうろ」 is an expressway, a high speed road, often just shortened to 「こうそく」. And 「速達|そくたつ」 is express mail, a fast delivery at the post office. Notice that the time kanji in 「早い|はやい」 has its own Chinese reading, 「そう」, as in 「早朝|そうちょう」, early morning. So the two kanji for the same sound split even in their compounds. If you see 「そく」, think speed. If you see 「そう」 in a time word, think early. So: 「そく」 for speed, highways and express mail." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese often says what part is fast. A fast runner has fast feet: 「あしがはやい」. To say someone does something fast, use 「の」 to turn the verb into a noun: 「たべるのがはやい」, eats fast, or 「はなすのがはやい」, talks fast. Now the opposite. English splits it into slow and late, but Japanese uses one word, 「おそい」, for both, with one kanji. So 「でんしゃがおそい」 can mean the train is slow, or the train is late, and context tells you which. Isn't it funny? Japanese uses two kanji for fast and early, but just one for slow and late. A tiny dialogue: 「もっとはやくはしって」, run faster. 「むりだよ。あしがおそいんだ」, I can't, I'm slow. So: fast feet are 「あしがはやい」, and 「おそい」 covers both slow and late." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adjective is perfect for practicing comparisons. To compare two things, use 「より」: 「しんかんせんはバスよりはやいです」, the bullet train is faster than the bus. To ask which is faster, say 「どちらがはやいですか」. To name the fastest, use 「いちばん」: 「ひこうきがいちばんはやいです」, the plane is the fastest. The adverb form, 「はやく」, also shows up in requests: 「はやくきてください」, please come quickly. In writing, hurry up and come soon usually take the time kanji, 「早く|はやく」, while moving at high speed, like running fast, takes the speed kanji, 「速く|はやく」. Out loud they sound identical. And for getting faster, add 「なる」: 「はやくなりました」, it got faster. So: 「より」 to compare, 「いちばん」 for fastest, and 「はやく」 when you're in a hurry." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This kanji has the moving radical and means fast speed, while its sound twin 「早い|はやい」 has the sun on top and means early.",
      "Read 「そく」 it gives speed words like 「速度|そくど」 and 「高速道路|こうそくどうろ」, while the early kanji reads 「そう」, as in 「早朝|そうちょう」.",
      "Japanese names the fast part, like 「あしがはやい」, fast feet, and its opposite 「おそい」 covers both slow and late.",
      "Compare with 「より」, name the fastest with 「いちばん」, and in writing hurry up takes 「早く|はやく」 while moving at speed takes 「速く|はやく」."
    ],
    ja: "{電車|でんしゃ}は[速い|はやい]です。",
    en: "Trains are fast." },
  "せがひくい|背が低い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a small sentence: height is low. 「背|せ」 is the same native word as in 「背が高い|せがたかい」, meaning your back, and your height. 「低い|ひくい」 means low, with the native reading. Its kanji has the person radical on the left, 「人|ひと」, and a right side that carries the sense of bottom or base. A person near the bottom. The same adjective works for a low ceiling, a low voice, or low temperatures. The big mistake English speakers make is translating short directly as 「みじかい」. In Japanese, 「みじかい」 is only for length, like hair or pencils, never for people. So a short person is always this phrase. Use it the same way as tall: 「おとうとはせがひくいです」, my younger brother is short. It can feel a little personal, so be kind when you use it. So: your back is low, and that means short." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The adjective 「ひくい」 means low in many settings, not just height. A low ceiling, a low mountain, a low voice, 「こえがひくい」, and a low temperature are all described with it. Here's a neat point. 「たかい」 has two different opposites. When it means high or tall, its opposite is 「ひくい」. When it means expensive, its opposite is 「やすい」, cheap. So a tall, expensive building has two possible opposites, depending on what you mean. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「てい」, this kanji gives 「最低|さいてい」, the lowest, which also means the worst or terrible. It is the opposite of 「さいこう」, the best. You might hear someone say 「さいていだ」, that's the worst. So: 「ひくい」 for anything low, and two opposites for 「たかい」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Describing someone as short can sound blunt, so Japanese speakers often soften it. One way is to say not very tall: 「あまりせがたかくないです」. That is gentler than saying short directly. Another is to add 「ちょっと」, a little: 「ちょっとせがひくいです」. You may also hear the word 「こがら」, small framed or petite, which is a kinder, more neutral description, especially for women. It's best to use the direct phrase about yourself or close family. For example, 「わたしはせがひくいので、たかいたなにてがとどきません」, I'm short, so I can't reach high shelves. In comparisons, 「より」 works here too: 「おとうとはわたしよりせがひくいです」. So: soften it with 「あまりたかくない」, and use it freely about yourself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It literally says your back is low, using 「低い|ひくい」 with the person radical, and short people are never 「みじかい」, which is only for length.",
      "「たかい」 has two opposites, 「ひくい」 for high and 「やすい」 for expensive, and read 「てい」 the kanji gives 「最低|さいてい」, the worst.",
      "Calling someone short can sound blunt, so soften it with 「あまりせがたかくないです」 or the kinder 「こがら」, and use the direct phrase freely about yourself."
    ],
    ja: "{弟|おとうと}は[背が低い|せがひくい]です。",
    en: "My younger brother is short." },
  "あたまがいい|頭がいい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a little sentence: the head is good. 「頭|あたま」 uses its native reading. Look at the kanji. The right side is a radical that means head. You can see the very same radical on the right of 「顔|かお」, face. That is a nice anchor: face and head share the head piece. Then 「いい」, good. So someone with a good head is smart. English has a similar idea, a good head on your shoulders. Use it like this: 「あねはあたまがいいです」, my older sister is smart. Remember that 「いい」 changes to 「よ」 when it bends, so the past is 「あたまがよかった」 and the negative is 「あたまがよくない」. The opposite, 「あたまがわるい」, bad head, means not smart, and can be quite rude, so use it carefully. So: a good head, and the head radical shared with face." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The head shows up in lots of useful Japanese expressions. 「あたまがいたい」 means I have a headache, your head hurts. 「あたまにくる」, it comes to the head, means to get angry, like when something makes your blood boil. 「あたまがかたい」, a hard head, means stubborn or inflexible, someone who can't think outside the box. Its opposite, 「あたまがやわらかい」, a soft head, means flexible thinking, which is a compliment. Here's a little dialogue. 「せんせいはあたまがかたいね」, the teacher is so rigid. 「うん。でも、あたまはいいよ」, yeah, but he is smart. Notice how the head can be good, hard, soft or painful, and each one means something different. So: a good head is smart, a hard head is stubborn, and a hurting head needs medicine." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has a few readings beyond the native one. 「とう」 shows up in 「先頭|せんとう」, the front of a line, the head position. It is also a counter for large animals, like 「いっとう」, one horse or cow. Another reading, 「ず」, appears in 「頭痛|ずつう」, a headache, the formal word you'll see on medicine boxes. On the left side of the kanji, there is 「豆|まめ」, bean, and it gives the sound in Chinese. On the right, as you know, is the head radical, shared with 「顔|かお」, face. The same radical sits on the right of 「題|だい」, a title or topic, as in 「しゅくだい」, homework. So: 「あたま」 native, 「とう」 at the front and for big animals, and 「ず」 in headache." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several ways to say smart, with different flavors. 「あたまがいい」 is the most common, everyday phrase. 「かしこい」 means clever or wise. People use it for children and pets a lot, as in 「かしこいいぬですね」, what a clever dog. 「てんさい」 is a genius, used with excitement or as a joke when a friend has a good idea. When you put this phrase before a noun, the 「が」 often changes to 「の」: 「あたまのいいひと」, a smart person. And in the past tense, remember 「いい」 bends to 「よ」: 「あのひとはむかしからあたまがよかった」, that person was always smart. So: 「あたまがいい」 for everyday, 「かしこい」 for clever kids and dogs, and 「てんさい」 for a genius." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A good head means smart, and the kanji's head radical on the right is shared with 「顔|かお」, face; remember 「いい」 bends to 「よかった」 and 「よくない」.",
      "The head can be good, hard or soft: 「あたまがかたい」 means stubborn, 「あたまがやわらかい」 flexible, and 「あたまにくる」 means getting angry.",
      "Beyond 「あたま」, the kanji reads 「とう」 in 「先頭|せんとう」, the front of a line, and 「ず」 in 「頭痛|ずつう」, headache.",
      "「あたまがいい」 is the everyday smart, 「かしこい」 suits clever kids and dogs, 「てんさい」 is a genius, and before a noun it often becomes 「あたまのいいひと」."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[頭がいい|あたまがいい]です。",
    en: "My older sister is smart." },
  "しんせつ|親切": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「親|しん」 means parent, and from that, close or intimate. You know it from 「親|おや」, parent, with the native reading, and 「両親|りょうしん」, parents. A popular hook, not real history: the kanji looks like standing on a tree, watching, a parent watching over a child. 「切|せつ」 is the cutting kanji, 「切る|きる」, to cut, also in 「切符|きっぷ」, ticket. In this word it carries the sense of keen or heartfelt, feelings that cut deep. So the idea is close and heartfelt, the warm care a parent would show. It is a 「な」 adjective: 「しんせつなひと」, a kind person. It is used mostly for kind actions toward others, like helping someone find the station. Another word, 「やさしい」, describes a gentle personality. So: parent-like closeness that cuts deep, which is kindness in action." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When someone helps you, the classic polite thanks is 「ごしんせつにありがとうございます」, thank you for your kindness. It's a lovely phrase to use with strangers who give you directions. To describe a kind action, use the adverb form, 「しんせつに」: 「みちをしんせつにおしえてくれました」, they kindly showed me the way. The opposite is 「ふしんせつ」, unkind or unhelpful, used for bad service or confusing instructions, like 「ふしんせつなせつめい」, an unhelpful explanation. Notice this word is often about how helpful someone is to you, while 「やさしい」 is about someone's gentle character. A shop clerk who helps you find something is 「しんせつ」. A grandmother is 「やさしい」. So: thank people for their kindness, and use 「ふしんせつ」 for unhelpful." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji belong to useful families. The first, 「親|しん」, means parent and also close. 「親友|しんゆう」 is a best friend, a close friend. 「親しい|したしい」 means close or intimate, as in close friends. And 「親子|おやこ」, parent and child, uses the native reading. There's even a dish called 「おやこどん」, a rice bowl with chicken and egg, the parent and the child together. Kind of dark when you think about it. The second kanji, 「切|せつ」, appears in another word you'll want: 「大切|たいせつ」, important or precious. Here 「せつ」 carries a sense of earnest, heartfelt feeling, the same as in kindness. So kindness and preciousness share a piece. So: close like a best friend, and heartfelt like something precious." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「な」 adjective, so it follows the same pattern as 「しずか」 and 「げんき」. Before a noun, use 「な」: 「しんせつなひと」, a kind person. To say not kind, use 「じゃない」: 「あのひとはしんせつじゃないです」. Past tense is 「しんせつでした」, was kind. To join it to another adjective, use 「で」: 「しんせつでおもしろいひと」, a kind and funny person. And when you describe how someone does something, use 「に」, as in 「しんせつにしてくれました」, they treated me kindly. A common mistake is to add 「い」, as if it were an 「い」 adjective. 「しんせつい」 is not a word. So: 「な」 before nouns, 「で」 to join, and 「に」 for doing it kindly." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「親|しん」 is parent, close, and 「切|せつ」 here means heartfelt, so it is parent-like caring shown in kind actions, while 「やさしい」 describes a gentle personality.",
      "Thank a helpful stranger with 「ごしんせつにありがとうございます」, and use 「ふしんせつ」 for unhelpful service or explanations.",
      "「親|しん」 gives 「親友|しんゆう」, best friend, and 「切|せつ」 appears in 「大切|たいせつ」, precious, so kindness and preciousness share a heartfelt piece.",
      "As a 「な」 adjective it is 「しんせつなひと」 before nouns, 「で」 to join, 「に」 for doing something kindly, and never 「しんせつい」."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}はとても[親切|しんせつ]です。",
    en: "The teacher is very kind." },
  "べんり|便利": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「便|べん」 has the person radical on the left, and means convenience, ease, and also mail or delivery. That other meaning comes out as 「びん」 in 「郵便局|ゆうびんきょく」, post office. 「利|り」 has grain on the left and a knife on the right. Cutting the harvest brings profit, so it means advantage, benefit and sharpness. Ease plus benefit: convenient. It is a 「な」 adjective, so you say 「べんりなみせ」, a convenient store. Put 「不|ふ」, meaning not, in front and you get 「不便|ふべん」, inconvenient, using the same first kanji. Japan's convenience stores are the perfect example. They are open all night, and you can pay bills, send packages and print documents there. 「コンビニはべんりです」 is a sentence everyone agrees with. So: ease plus a sharp benefit, the word for every convenience store." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's follow the two kanji into other words. The first one, 「便|べん」, shows up in 「郵便局|ゆうびんきょく」, the post office, where it is read 「びん」 and carries the delivery meaning. It also hides in an old word for toilet, 「便所|べんじょ」, which you may still see on signs at old train stations. There the kanji takes on a more earthy, bathroom sense. The second kanji, 「利|り」, gives you 「利用|りよう」, to make use of something, as in using the library or a service. Now a near-synonym puzzle. 「かんたん」 means easy or simple, like an easy test. 「らく」 means comfortable or effortless, like a relaxing job. This word is different from both. It means handy, something that makes life smoother. A smartphone is handy, a quiz can be easy, and a big sofa is comfortable. So: delivery, use, and handy all live in one small family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The most natural way to praise a place in Japanese is to say why it is handy. 「えきにちかくて、べんりです」 means it's close to the station, so it's convenient. You'll hear that about apartments all the time, because being near a station is the top concern for renters in Japanese cities. To say handy for something, use 「に」: 「かいものにべんりです」, convenient for shopping. Here is a little dialogue. One friend says 「このアプリ、べんりですよ」, this app is really handy. The other answers 「へえ、つかってみます」, oh, I'll try using it. One warning. Be careful calling a person 「べんりなひと」. It can sound like that person is useful to you, someone you can use, which is not a compliment. For a helpful person, praise their kindness instead. So: use it for places, tools and apps, and add 「に」 for what it's handy for." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Imagine a friend named Benny who is the handiest guy you know. Benny always has a charger, a snack, an umbrella and a pen in his pockets. Whenever you need anything, you shout, Benny, really? You have that too? Benny really is convenient. Now attach the kanji. Benny is a person, which matches the person radical on the left of 「便|べん」. And he carries a pocket knife, which matches the knife on the right side of 「利|り」. Picture Benny pulling out that tiny knife to open your package from the post office. Every time you hear 「べんり」, see Benny the walking convenience store. So: Benny, really, the handiest person you'll ever meet." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「便|べん」 means ease and 「利|り」, grain cut with a knife, means benefit, so ease plus benefit is convenient, and its opposite is 「不便|ふべん」.",
      "「便|びん」 carries delivery in 「郵便局|ゆうびんきょく」 and 「利|り」 gives 「利用|りよう」, and this word means handy, unlike 「かんたん」, easy, or 「らく」, comfortable.",
      "Praise a place by why it is handy, like 「えきにちかくて、べんりです」, but avoid calling a person 「べんりなひと」, which sounds like someone you use.",
      "Picture Benny, the handiest guy you know, a person like the radical in 「便|べん」, pulling out a pocket knife like the one in 「利|り」."
    ],
    ja: "コンビニは[便利|べんり]です。",
    en: "Convenience stores are convenient." },
  "はく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the verb for putting on anything below your waist. Pants, jeans, skirts, socks, shoes and slippers all take it. It is usually written in kana. Japanese chooses the verb by body part, so you need to switch. 「きる」 is for shirts and coats, 「かぶる」 is for hats, and 「かける」 is for glasses. A very common English speaker mistake is 「ジーンズをきる」. That sounds odd. Say 「ジーンズをはく」 instead, and for shoes, 「くつをはく」. It is an u-verb: 「はきます」, 「はいて」. To say someone is wearing something right now, use 「はいています」. In Japan, you will do this a lot, because you take shoes off at the entrance of homes and put on slippers. A way to remember it: picture yourself hiking up your pants. So: the waist-down verb, for pants, skirts, socks and shoes." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an u-verb ending in 「く」, so it follows the same pattern as 「かく」, to write. The 「て」 form is 「はいて」, and the past is 「はいた」. Its opposite is 「ぬぐ」, to take off, and that one works for everything, tops, shoes, and pants alike. So you put things on with four different verbs, but you take them all off with one. Now the 「ている」 form. 「スカートをはいています」 can mean she is wearing a skirt right now, describing how someone looks. That is the most common use, especially when describing people in a picture or explaining who is who. 「あかいくつをはいているひと」 means the person wearing red shoes. This verb can also be written with a kanji, which you may see on shoe shop signs, but kana is fine everywhere. So: a 「く」 verb like 「かく」, with 「ぬぐ」 as its one partner for taking off." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Japanese has several different verbs pronounced exactly the same way, and context tells them apart. Besides putting on shoes, the same sound means to sweep, as in sweeping the floor with a broom. You may hear it when people talk about cleaning: 「にわをはく」, sweep the garden. The same sound also means to throw up, which is not a word you want to mix up at a dinner table, and in a softer sense, to breathe out. These are all different kanji, and you will rarely confuse them, because the object makes it clear. Shoes, socks and pants mean wearing. A floor or a garden means sweeping. There is also a counter with this sound for nights at a hotel, as in 「いっぱく」, one night's stay. So: one sound, many verbs, and the object always gives the game away." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, putting on and taking off things below the waist is a daily ritual. At the entrance of a home, called the 「げんかん」, you step out of your shoes and onto a raised floor. Then you usually put on slippers. Many homes keep separate toilet slippers just inside the bathroom door. You switch into them, and switch back when you leave. Forgetting and walking into the living room in toilet slippers is a classic foreigner mistake that gets a laugh. On tatami mats, you take slippers off too and walk in socks. That is why Japanese people often wear neat socks and shoes that slip on easily. Some restaurants, temples and schools work the same way, with shoe lockers at the door. So: in Japan you'll use this verb many times a day, at every doorway." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the wearing verb for anything below the waist, like pants, skirts, socks and shoes, so say 「ジーンズをはく」, not 「ジーンズをきる」.",
      "It conjugates like 「かく」, giving 「はいて」, its 「ている」 form describes what someone has on, and everything comes off with the single verb 「ぬぐ」.",
      "The same sound also means to sweep, as in 「にわをはく」, and to throw up, but the object always makes clear which verb you mean.",
      "In Japan you swap footwear at every doorway, from shoes to slippers at the 「げんかん」 and into separate toilet slippers, so you'll use this verb many times a day."
    ],
    ja: "くつを[はきます|はきます|はく]。",
    en: "I put on my shoes." },
  "しる|知る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji is 「矢|や」, arrow, on the left, and 「口|くち」, mouth, on the right. A way to remember it, as a hook: knowledge comes out of your mouth as fast and straight as an arrow. That same arrow is in 「短い|みじかい」, short. At its heart, this verb means to come to know, to find out. That is why you need the 「ている」 form for the state of knowing: 「しっています」, I know. But here is the trap that catches everyone. The negative is NOT 「しっていません」. It is simply 「しりません」, I don't know. Also, even though it ends in 「る」, it is an u-verb, so it becomes 「しって」 with a small 「っ」. A typical question: 「でんわばんごうをしっていますか」, do you know the phone number? So: an arrow from the mouth, know is 「しっています」, and don't know is 「しりません」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's compare this verb with 「わかる」, because English uses I know and I understand in overlapping ways. This verb is about having information: a fact, a person, a place. 「あのひとをしっていますか」, do you know that person? 「わかる」 is about grasping or figuring something out. If someone explains directions and you get it, say 「わかりました」, not this verb. Answering questions is where learners slip. To yes, you say 「はい、しっています」. To no, you say 「いいえ、しりません」. When talking to a customer or a boss, people use a humble or honorific version. The honorific question is 「ごぞんじですか」, do you happen to know? and the humble answer is 「ぞんじません」. You will hear those in shops and offices. So: information you have, not something you just understood." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji spreads into many useful words. Add the noun ending and you get 「お知らせ|おしらせ」, a notice or announcement. You'll see it on website banners and posters at the station. The verb 「知らせる|しらせる」 means to let someone know, to inform. Two people coming to know each other gives 「知り合う|しりあう」, to get acquainted, and the noun 「知り合い|しりあい」, an acquaintance, someone you know but who isn't quite a friend. With the Chinese-origin reading 「ち」, the kanji gives 「知識|ちしき」, knowledge. And a person you do not know at all is a 「しらないひと」, a stranger. Japanese kids are taught not to follow one. So: notices, informing, acquaintances and knowledge all grow from one arrow and a mouth." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick using the sound. The word sounds a bit like she rules. Picture the cleverest girl in your class. Every time the teacher asks a question, her hand shoots up. She knows the answer, she knows the capital of every country, she knows everyone's phone number. The whole class sighs: she rules, she knows everything. Now the twist. One day the teacher asks something she doesn't know. She stands up, face red, and says, 「しりません」. The class gasps. Remember that dramatic moment, because the negative is the part people get wrong. She doesn't say she is not knowing. She just says, plainly, I don't know. So: she rules because she knows, and 「しりません」 when she doesn't." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is 「矢|や」, arrow, beside 「口|くち」, mouth, and since the verb means come to know, I know is 「しっています」 but I don't know is simply 「しりません」.",
      "This verb is about having information, like a fact or a person, while 「わかる」 is grasping something, so answer 「はい、しっています」 or 「いいえ、しりません」.",
      "The kanji grows into 「お知らせ|おしらせ」, notice, 「知り合い|しりあい」, acquaintance, and with the reading 「ち」, 「知識|ちしき」, knowledge.",
      "It sounds like she rules, so picture the class know-it-all who, stumped for once, stands up red-faced and says plainly 「しりません」."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}の{電話番号|でんわばんごう}を[知っています|しっています|しる]か。",
    en: "Do you know the teacher's phone number?" },
  "すむ|住む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji has the person radical on the left, and on the right, 「主|しゅ」, which means master or main. You see that piece in 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」, someone's husband, the master of the house in old thinking. So: a person who is master of a place, living there. Grammar matters with this verb. You mark the place with 「に」, not 「で」: 「とうきょうにすんでいます」, I live in Tokyo. And use the 「ている」 form for where you live now. A common English speaker mistake is to say 「とうきょうにすみます」, which sounds like I will live in Tokyo, a future plan. It is an u-verb: 「すみます」, 「すんで」. The Chinese-origin reading 「じゅう」 shows up in 「住所|じゅうしょ」, address, the place you live. So: a person who is master of a place, with 「に」 and the 「ている」 form." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There are other verbs with exactly this sound, and one of them hides inside a phrase you say every day. 「すむ」 written with a different kanji means to be finished or settled, as in 「しごとがすんだ」, the work is done. Now think about 「すみません」. It comes from that verb in the negative. The idea is something like this isn't settled, I still owe you, which became the all-purpose excuse me, sorry and thank you. So whenever you apologize in Japanese, you are using a cousin of this word, not the living verb. A third verb with the same sound means to become clear, like clear water or a clear sky. Context makes them easy to tell apart. A place with 「に」 means living. So: one sound, three verbs, and 「すみません」 belongs to the finished one." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji's Chinese-origin reading is 「じゅう」. You know it from 「住所|じゅうしょ」, address. It also appears in 「住宅|じゅうたく」, housing, a word you'll see in real estate ads, and 「住民|じゅうみん」, residents of a town. The noun 「すまい」 means a home or residence. Now some near-synonyms. 「くらす」 means to live in the sense of getting by day to day. 「ひとりぐらし」, living alone, is a very common phrase for students and young workers. 「いきる」 means to be alive, as opposed to dead. English uses one word, live, for all three, but Japanese splits them. Where you live is this verb. How you live your daily life is 「くらす」. Being alive is 「いきる」. So: an address, a home, residents, and three different ways to live." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The basic question is 「どこにすんでいますか」, where do you live? Between friends, it shortens to 「どこにすんでるの」. In polite situations, like talking to an older person or a client, you'll hear 「おすまいはどちらですか」, where is your residence? Japanese people often answer by naming the nearest station, not a street. 「しぶやのちかくにすんでいます」, I live near Shibuya. That's because most streets in Japan don't have names, and addresses work by numbered blocks instead. You can also say how long: 「ごねんかんすんでいます」, I've lived here for five years. Notice the 「ている」 form again, even with a time length, where English uses have lived. So: answer with a station name, and keep using 「すんでいます」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a person beside 「主|しゅ」, master, someone who is master of a place, and you say where you live with 「に」 and the 「ている」 form, as in 「とうきょうにすんでいます」.",
      "A different verb with this sound means to be finished or settled, and 「すみません」 comes from its negative, the idea that something is still not settled.",
      "Read 「じゅう」 it gives 「住所|じゅうしょ」, address, and Japanese splits live into this verb for where, 「くらす」 for daily life, and 「いきる」 for being alive.",
      "Ask 「どこにすんでいますか」, expect an answer naming the nearest station since most streets have no names, and keep 「すんでいます」 even with a length of time."
    ],
    ja: "{東京|とうきょう}に[住んでいます|すんでいます|すむ]。",
    en: "I live in Tokyo." },
  "はたらく|働く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. This kanji is special: it was invented in Japan, not in China. The Japanese made it by putting the person radical next to 「動|どう」, to move. A person who keeps moving is working. The moving piece 「動|どう」 is in words you know, like 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, zoo, the garden of moving creatures, and 「活動|かつどう」, activity. It is an u-verb: 「はたらきます」, 「はたらいて」. Mark the workplace with 「で」, not 「に」: 「ぎんこうではたらいています」, works at a bank. That is the opposite of 「住む|すむ」, which takes 「に」. Also note the difference from 「しごと」, which is the noun for a job. This verb is the action of working. So: a person in motion, a kanji made in Japan, and the workplace takes 「で」." },
      { title: "Made in Japan", text: "Kanji made in Japan are called 「国字|こくじ」, national characters. They were created for things or ideas the Japanese wanted to write but couldn't find a good Chinese character for. Some you'll meet: 「畑|はたけ」, a dry field for vegetables, combining fire and rice paddy, probably from burning fields to clear them. 「峠|とうげ」, a mountain pass, is mountain plus up plus down. And 「込む|こむ」, to be crowded, as in a crowded train. Most of these only have a native reading. This kanji is unusual because it also got a Chinese-style reading, 「どう」, borrowed from the moving part inside it. That is how it appears in 「労働|ろうどう」, labor. So: a home-grown kanji that even earned a Chinese-style reading." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "There are a few ways to talk about work in Japanese, and they are not the same. This verb is the physical or general act of working. It takes 「で」 for the place. A close relative is 「つとめる」, to be employed by an organization. It takes 「に」: 「かいしゃにつとめています」, I work for a company. That one sounds more formal, and it's about which company employs you. Then there's 「しごとをする」, to do work, often used for the task in front of you right now. 「いま、しごとをしています」, I'm working right now. Then 「アルバイトをする」, to do a part-time job, which students use constantly. So: 「で」 with this verb for the place of work, 「に」 with 「つとめる」 for your employer." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan is famous for long working hours, and some vocabulary around work reflects that. 「ざんぎょう」 is overtime, staying late after the official end of the day. For decades, many office workers stayed late because leaving before the boss felt rude. There's even a word, 「かろうし」, death from overwork, which became internationally known. In recent years, the government passed work style reform laws to cap overtime, and many companies now push people to go home earlier and take their paid holidays. Another tradition is the after-work drinking party, where coworkers relax together. When someone finishes for the day, people say 「おつかれさまです」, thank you for your hard work. So: a culture of hard work, slowly learning to rest." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This kanji was invented in Japan from a person beside 「動|どう」, to move, a person who keeps moving, and the workplace takes 「で」, as in 「ぎんこうではたらいています」.",
      "It is a 「国字|こくじ」, a kanji made in Japan like 「畑|はたけ」 and 「峠|とうげ」, but unusually it gained a Chinese-style reading, 「どう」, as in 「労働|ろうどう」, labor.",
      "This verb takes 「で」 for the place of work, while 「つとめる」 takes 「に」 for your employer, and 「しごとをする」 is for the task in front of you.",
      "Japan is known for long hours and 「ざんぎょう」, overtime, even giving the world 「かろうし」, though work reforms now push people to rest more."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は{銀行|ぎんこう}で[働いています|はたらいています|はたらく]。",
    en: "My mother works at a bank." },
  "ふとる|太る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. Look at the kanji. It is 「大|だい」, big, with one extra dot. A way to remember it: big plus a little extra. The same kanji also gives the adjective 「太い|ふとい」, thick, and with its Chinese-origin reading, 「たい」, it is in 「太陽|たいよう」, the sun, a big bright thing in the sky. The grammar is the important part. This verb means the change, gaining weight. So 「ふとりました」 means I gained weight. To describe someone who is heavy, use the 「ている」 form, 「ふとっています」. It is a common mistake to make an adjective out of it for people. Even though it ends in 「る」, it is an u-verb, so it becomes 「ふとって」. A cultural note: this topic is discussed quite openly in Japan, so don't be shocked if someone mentions it. So: big plus a dot, gaining weight, and 「ふとっています」 for heavy." },
      { title: "Thick and thin", text: "This kanji family is all about thickness. The adjective 「太い|ふとい」 means thick or fat, for things like a thick pen line, a thick rope, or a thick tree trunk. Its opposite is 「ほそい」, thin or slender, for a thin noodle or a narrow road. 「太字|ふとじ」 is bold text, literally thick characters, which you'll see in word processing menus. 「太もも|ふともも」 is your thigh, the thick part of the leg. And with the Chinese-origin reading, this kanji is part of 「太平洋|たいへいよう」, the Pacific Ocean. Notice the difference between people and objects. For a thick object, you use the adjective. For a person who is heavy, you use this verb in the 「ている」 form. So: thick ropes, bold text and thighs all share this kanji with gaining weight." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb belongs to a group of change verbs that you'll keep meeting in Genki. The plain form describes the moment of change, and the 「ている」 form describes the result you can see now. You've seen the same pattern with getting married and with knowing. You can add how much: 「さんキロふとりました」, I gained three kilos. To describe a person with it, put the verb before the noun: 「ふとっているねこ」, a fat cat. You can also say 「ふとったねこ」 with the past form, and it means the same thing. Watch out: to say someone is chubby kindly, people often avoid this verb and say something softer. Also remember the u-verb trap. The 「て」 form is 「ふとって」, not 「ふとて」. So: the change, the state, and a small 「っ」 in the middle." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a man stepping onto a bathroom scale. His big toe hits the metal first, and the foot says, ooh! Foot, ooh, roo. Then he looks down at the number and shrieks. He gained weight. Now the kanji. It's big, with one extra dot. Imagine that dot is the extra slice of cake he ate last night, sitting right between his legs. Every time he eats another slice, the dot gets bigger, and so does he. Next time you see the kanji, picture big plus one more slice of cake, and hear his foot saying ooh on the scale. So: foot, ooh, and the extra dot is cake." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is 「大|だい」, big, plus one extra dot, and the verb means the change of gaining weight, so heavy is 「ふとっています」; it is an u-verb, 「ふとって」.",
      "The kanji is about thickness, as in 「太い|ふとい」, thick, and 「太字|ふとじ」, bold text, but for a heavy person you use this verb in the 「ている」 form.",
      "As a change verb, the plain form is the moment and 「ている」 the visible result, so 「さんキロふとりました」 is gaining three kilos and 「ふとっているねこ」 a fat cat.",
      "Picture a man's foot saying ooh on the bathroom scale, and the kanji's extra dot is the slice of cake he ate last night."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}ケーキを{食べます|たべます}から、[太りました|ふとりました|ふとる]。",
    en: "I eat cake every day, so I gained weight." },
  "かける|かける": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The core meaning of this verb is to hang something on something. You hang glasses on your nose and ears, so 「めがねをかける」 means to put on glasses. It can be written with a kanji, but for glasses it is usually in kana. This one verb does lots of jobs, all tied to the hanging idea. 「でんわをかける」 means to make a phone call, as if hanging a line out to someone. 「アイロンをかける」 means to iron clothes, laying the iron over them. Japanese chooses the wearing verb by body part, so remember 「きる」 is for tops, 「はく」 below the waist, and 「かぶる」 for hats. It is a ru-verb: 「かけます」, 「かけて」. To describe someone who wears glasses, use the 「ている」 form: 「ちちはめがねをかけています」, my father wears glasses. So: to hang, used for glasses, phone calls and ironing." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has an intransitive partner, 「かかる」, and the pair works like 「あける」 and 「あく」. With this verb, someone does the hanging, and the thing takes 「を」. With 「かかる」, the thing simply hangs or happens, and takes 「が」. So 「でんわをかける」 is I make a call, but 「でんわがかかってきた」 is a call came in. Locks work the same way. 「かぎをかける」 means to lock something, and 「かぎがかかっています」 means it is locked. And the partner gives you one of the most useful phrases in Japanese: 「じかんがかかる」, it takes time. 「えきまでにじゅっぷんかかります」, it takes twenty minutes to the station. So: you hang it with 「を」, it hangs by itself with 「かかる」 and 「が」." },
      { title: "Many hats", text: "This verb wears many hats, and you'll hear lots of them. 「いすにかける」 means to sit down on a chair, as if hanging yourself on it. Staff in offices and clinics say 「おかけください」, please have a seat. 「おんがくをかける」 means to put on music. 「こえをかける」, to hang your voice on someone, means to call out to them or strike up a conversation. 「しんぱいをかける」 means to cause someone worry. Add 「で」 at the front and you get 「でかける」, to go out, one of your Genki verbs. In writing, the kanji for most of these has the hand radical, fitting the idea of hanging by hand. The common thread is always placing something onto something else. So: glasses, chairs, music, voices and worries all get hung with one verb." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick from the sound. The word sounds a little like cake. Picture your grandfather at the breakfast table, unable to find his glasses. So instead, he takes two round little cakes and hangs them over his ears like spectacles. Then he hangs a phone receiver on the cake as well, to make a call. And then he grabs the iron and starts ironing his shirt, with a cake hanging from the handle. Everything he touches, he hangs a cake on. Ridiculous, but memorable. Glasses, phone calls and ironing, all hanging with cake. So: hang a cake on it, and you've got the verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The core meaning is to hang one thing on another, so you hang glasses on your face with 「めがねをかける」, and also make calls and iron clothes with it.",
      "Its intransitive partner is 「かかる」: you lock something with 「かぎをかける」, it is locked with 「かぎがかかっています」, and 「じかんがかかる」 means it takes time.",
      "It wears many hats, from 「おかけください」, please have a seat, to 「おんがくをかける」, put on music, all about placing one thing onto another.",
      "It sounds like cake, so picture grandpa hanging two little cakes over his ears as glasses, then hanging cakes on the phone and the iron too."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}はめがねを[かけています|かけています|かける]。",
    en: "My father wears glasses." },
  "やせる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native verb, usually written in kana. It is the opposite of 「太る|ふとる」, gaining weight. It works the same way. The plain form is the change: 「やせました」, I lost weight. To describe someone who is thin, use the 「ている」 form, 「やせています」, my brother is thin. Unlike 「ふとる」, this one is a ru-verb, so it becomes 「やせます」 and 「やせて」. A cultural note: in Japan, saying 「やせましたね」, you've lost weight, is generally taken as a compliment, and people talk about weight more openly than many English speakers expect. Still, it is personal, so read the room. A way to remember it, as a sound hook: imagine someone stepping off the scale and shouting yes, sir, I lost weight! So: the change of getting thinner, and 「やせています」 for thin." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This verb is usually written in kana, but it does have a kanji, and the kanji tells a small story. It sits under the sickness radical, the shape that looks like a little hospital bed with a roof. You'll find that same radical on top of 「病気|びょうき」, illness. In older times, getting thin was often a sign of being sick or hungry, not a goal. The verb can also describe land. 「やせたとち」 means poor, barren soil, land that is thin on nutrients. Its opposite, 「こえた」 land, is rich and fertile. Today, of course, the word usually comes with a cheerful tone, as in a successful diet. But the kanji remembers the older, harder meaning. So: a word that started near sickness and now often comes with a smile." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In everyday Japanese, you'll hear this verb a lot around the word 「ダイエット」. In Japanese, that loanword doesn't just mean the food you eat. It means a weight-loss effort of any kind. 「ダイエットしています」 means I'm trying to lose weight, even if it's by jogging. To say you want to lose weight, use the 「たい」 form: 「やせたいです」. Since this is a ru-verb, just drop the 「る」 and add 「たい」. You can give an amount: 「にキロやせました」, I lost two kilos. And for objects, use a different word. A thin book or a thin noodle is 「ほそい」 or 「うすい」, not this verb. This verb is only for living bodies, people and animals. So: 「ダイエット」 to try, 「やせたい」 to wish, and 「やせました」 to celebrate." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the opposite of 「太る|ふとる」, so 「やせました」 means you lost weight and 「やせています」 means thin, but unlike that verb it is a ru-verb.",
      "Its kanji sits under the sickness radical, as in 「病気|びょうき」, because getting thin once signaled illness or hunger, and 「やせたとち」 is barren soil.",
      "「ダイエット」 means any weight-loss effort, 「やせたいです」 says you want to lose weight, and thin objects use 「ほそい」 or 「うすい」, not this verb."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[やせています|やせています|やせる]。",
    en: "My older brother is thin." },
  "けっこんする|結婚する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. 「結|けつ」 means to tie. It has the thread radical on the left, and on the right, the kanji for good fortune. Tying threads for good luck. Before the 「こ」 sound, its ending squeezes into a small 「っ」, giving 「けっ」. You know the same squeezed sound from 「結構です|けっこうです」, that's fine. 「婚|こん」 means marriage, with 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left. The right side is the kanji for dusk, and an old Chinese dictionary explains that weddings were held at dusk. So: tying the knot, just like the English phrase. Then add 「する」, to do. The grammar is key. 「けっこんしました」 means got married, an event. 「けっこんしています」 means is married, a state. You marry someone with 「と」. A wedding is 「結婚式|けっこんしき」. So: tie plus marriage, and the 「ている」 form for married." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, marriage, builds a whole family of life-event words. 「婚約|こんやく」 is an engagement, literally a marriage promise. 「新婚|しんこん」 means newly married, and 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」 is a honeymoon trip. 「離婚|りこん」 is divorce, with a kanji that means to separate. 「未婚|みこん」 means unmarried, with a kanji meaning not yet. And the first kanji, to tie, appears in 「結果|けっか」, a result, the way things tie up in the end. Notice how these words follow the same idea as the main one. Each is two Chinese-origin kanji, and most of them can take 「する」 to become verbs, like 「りこんする」 and 「こんやくする」. So: engagement, newlyweds, honeymoon, and divorce, all built on one kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The partner in marriage is marked with 「と」, never 「を」. English says I married her, so learners try 「かのじょをけっこんした」, which is wrong. Say 「かのじょとけっこんしました」. Asking someone if they're married is fairly common in Japan, but it's personal, so a polite form helps: 「ごけっこんされていますか」. The answer might be 「はい、しています」 or 「いいえ、まだです」, no, not yet. That word for not yet is very natural here. When someone announces a wedding, the set response is 「ごけっこん、おめでとうございます」, congratulations on your marriage. You'll also hear 「けっこんしき」 for the ceremony, and people say they will 「あげる」 a ceremony, like raising it up. So: 「と」 for the partner, 「おめでとう」 for the news." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese weddings mix traditions in a way that surprises many visitors. Many couples choose a chapel-style ceremony, with a white dress and often a foreign minister leading it, even though only a small part of Japan is Christian. Others choose a Shinto ceremony at a shrine, with a white kimono and sips of sake from shared cups. After the ceremony comes the 「ひろうえん」, the reception, with speeches, outfit changes, and a tearful letter from the bride to her parents. Guests bring money as a gift, in a special decorated envelope. The usual amount for a friend is thirty thousand yen, and people avoid even numbers like twenty thousand, because an even number can be split in two, which feels unlucky for a couple. So: chapels, shrines, and odd numbers of crisp new bills." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「結|けつ」 means tie, squeezing to 「けっ」, and 「婚|こん」 means marriage, so it is tying the knot, with 「けっこんしています」 for being married and 「と」 for the partner.",
      "The marriage kanji builds life-event words like 「婚約|こんやく」, engagement, 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」, honeymoon, and 「離婚|りこん」, divorce.",
      "You marry someone with 「と」, never 「を」, so say 「かのじょとけっこんしました」, and congratulate with 「ごけっこん、おめでとうございます」.",
      "Couples choose chapel or Shinto ceremonies, then a 「ひろうえん」 reception, where guests give odd amounts of money like thirty thousand yen, since even numbers can split."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[結婚しています|けっこんしています|けっこんする]。",
    en: "My older sister is married." },
  "〜が|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This 「が」 is a connector, not the subject marker you already know. It joins two sentences that contrast, and it means but. Put it on the end of the first sentence, then a pause, then the second: 「むずかしいですが、おもしろいです」, it's difficult, but it's interesting. It sits after the verb or adjective, so you can tell it apart from the subject 「が」, which comes after a noun. How does it compare to other buts? 「でも」 starts a brand new sentence. 「けど」 is more casual and very common in conversation. This one is neutral to polite, and fits well with 「です」 and 「ます」. It also has a soft, cushioning use. 「すみませんが」 means excuse me, but, as a gentle lead in to a request. A way to remember it: think of it as a gate between two opposite ideas. So: after the first sentence, it means but, polite and smooth." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's make sure you never mix up the two kinds of this particle. The subject marker sits right after a noun: 「ねこがすきです」. The connector sits after a whole sentence, usually after 「です」 or a 「ます」 verb, and it's followed by a pause and a new idea. 「ねこはすきですが、いぬはすきじゃないです」, I like cats, but I don't like dogs. Notice something nice in that example: the contrast often pairs with 「は」 on both sides, because you're comparing two things. The connector can also follow plain forms, as in 「たかいが、おいしい」, though in casual speech most people would use 「けど」 there instead. In formal writing, like newspapers, you'll see it after plain 「だ」. So: after a noun it marks the subject, after a sentence it means but." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In real conversations, this connector often does a quiet job: softening. Japanese people start many requests with a cushion phrase that ends in it. 「しつれいですが」 means excuse me for asking, but, and it comes before a personal question, like asking someone's age. 「もうしわけありませんが」, I'm very sorry, but, comes before bad news in shops and offices. Even more interesting, the second half is often left unsaid. 「ちょっとききたいことがあるんですが」, I have something I'd like to ask, but. Then the speaker just stops, and the listener knows to say 「はい、なんですか」. Ending on this word sounds humble and polite, because you are not pushing your request. So: a cushion before requests, and sometimes the whole sentence trails off right after it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This 「が」 comes after a whole sentence, not a noun, and means a polite but, as in 「むずかしいですが、おもしろいです」, more polite than casual 「けど」.",
      "After a noun 「が」 marks the subject, but after a sentence it means but, and the contrast often pairs with 「は」 on both sides, as in 「ねこはすきですが、いぬはすきじゃないです」.",
      "It often cushions requests, as in 「しつれいですが」, and a sentence can even trail off right after it, which sounds humble because it doesn't push."
    ],
    ja: "{日本語|にほんご}はむずかしいです[が]、おもしろいです。",
    en: "Japanese is difficult, but it's interesting." },
  "なにも|何も": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「何|なに」, what, plus 「も」, which means even or also. Even what. The reading of the kanji is the native one, and the kanji has the person radical on the left. The key rule: it goes with a negative verb, and together they mean not anything, nothing. 「なにもたべませんでした」, I didn't eat anything. A common mistake for English speakers is to use it with a positive verb, as in I eat anything. That doesn't work. For something, use the sibling word 「何か|なにか」 instead: 「なにかたべましたか」, did you eat something? The same pattern works with other question words. 「だれも」 with a negative is nobody, and 「どこも」 is nowhere. You'll also hear it as a quick reply, often with 「べつに」: 「べつになにも」, nothing in particular. So: what plus even, with a negative, meaning nothing at all." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word quietly swallows certain particles. Normally you'd say 「なにをたべましたか」, but in the negative, the 「を」 disappears: 「なにもたべませんでした」. The same happens with 「が」: 「なにもありません」, there's nothing. But other particles stay, and they go before the 「も」. So with 「に」 you get 「だれにもいいません」, I won't tell anyone, and with 「へ」, 「どこへもいきません」, I won't go anywhere. Now here's how to say the anything that English speakers want. Use 「なんでも」 with a positive verb: 「なんでもたべます」, I'll eat anything. And for the opposite extreme, there's 「なにもかも」, absolutely everything. So: 「も」 plus negative is nothing, 「でも」 plus positive is anything." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word appears in some very Japanese polite phrases. When a host offers food to a guest, they might say 「なにもありませんが、どうぞ」, we have nothing, but please help yourself, even if the table is full. It's a humble way to offer, playing down your own effort. Similarly, someone describing their hometown might say 「なにもないところですが、いいところですよ」, it's a place with nothing, but it's a nice place. You'll also hear it in the very natural reply 「なにも」 when someone asks what you did over the weekend and you did nothing worth mentioning. In casual speech, people sometimes say 「なんにも」, which adds a bit of emphasis, as in nothing at all. So: a humble phrase for hosts, and a shrug for lazy weekends." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「何|なに」, what, plus 「も」, even, and it pairs with a negative verb to mean nothing, while something with a positive verb is 「何か|なにか」.",
      "It swallows 「を」 and 「が」 but keeps other particles before 「も」, as in 「だれにも」, and anything with a positive verb is 「なんでも」 instead.",
      "Hosts humbly say 「なにもありませんが、どうぞ」 even at a full table, and 「なんにも」 adds emphasis, nothing at all."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[何も|なにも]{食べませんでした|たべませんでした}。",
    en: "I didn't eat anything today." },
  "〜にん|〜人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This counter uses the Chinese-origin reading of 「人|ひと」, person. The kanji is a picture of a person walking, seen from the side, two legs apart. Put a number in front to count people: 「さんにん」, three people, 「ごにん」, five people. How many people is 「なんにん」. Now the irregulars, which you must memorize. One person is 「一人|ひとり」 and two people is 「二人|ふたり」, both using old native words. Four people is 「よにん」, not 「しにん」, because 「し」 sounds like death. Seven can be 「しちにん」 or 「ななにん」. The same kanji with the other Chinese-origin reading, 「じん」, means a person from somewhere, like 「にほんじん」, a Japanese person, or 「外国人|がいこくじん」, foreigner. Grammar tip: put the number after the noun and particle, right before the verb: 「がくせいがさんにんいます」. So: a walking person, used for counting people, with one, two and four as exceptions." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This one simple kanji has more readings than almost any other, so let's sort them out. 「ひと」 is the native word, a person. 「じん」 marks nationality or belonging, like 「アメリカじん」. 「にん」 is for counting, and also appears in a few words like 「にんき」, popularity. The old native counter 「り」 survives in one and two people. And there's 「大人|おとな」, adult, a special reading that you just memorize as a whole. Here's a pair that trips people up. 「なんにん」 means how many people. But 「なにじん」 means what nationality. Mix them up, and you'll ask a group how many they are when you meant where they're from. So: 「にん」 counts, 「じん」 tells where from, and 「なんにん」 is not 「なにじん」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The place you'll use this counter most is the entrance of a restaurant. The staff will greet you and ask 「なんめいさまですか」. That uses a different, very polite counter, 「めい」, which you'll mostly hear from staff. You answer with your normal counter: 「さんにんです」, three people. Or you just hold up three fingers, which works perfectly too. For one or two, it's 「ひとりです」 or 「ふたりです」. You'll also use it for families. 「よにんかぞくです」 means we're a family of four, a very common way to introduce your family. Notice that four uses 「よ」 here too. So: answer restaurant staff with this counter, and describe your family with it." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There are two common places to put a number of people in a sentence. The most natural for beginners is after the noun and its particle, right before the verb: 「ともだちがごにんきました」, five friends came. Here, the counter works like an adverb, telling how many. The other way uses 「の」 before the noun: 「ごにんのともだち」, five friends, which is a bit more like describing a set group. You can also use 「で」 with the counter to say how many people do something together: 「さんにんでいきました」, three of us went. That works for any number, not only for the two of us. So: number before the verb is most natural, 「の」 to describe, and 「で」 for a group doing something together." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This counts people with the Chinese reading of 「人|ひと」, a walking person, but one and two are 「一人|ひとり」 and 「二人|ふたり」, and four is 「よにん」 to avoid death's 「し」.",
      "The kanji reads 「ひと」, 「じん」, 「にん」 and more, so don't confuse 「なんにん」, how many people, with 「なにじん」, what nationality.",
      "At a restaurant staff ask 「なんめいさまですか」 with a polite counter, but you answer with this one, like 「さんにんです」, and 「よにんかぞくです」 describes your family.",
      "The most natural spot is right before the verb, as in 「ともだちがごにんきました」, with 「の」 to describe a group and 「で」 for a group doing something together."
    ],
    ja: "{学生|がくせい}が[三人|さんにん|〜にん]います。",
    en: "There are three students." },
  "ひとり|一人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word doesn't follow the regular counter. 「一|ひと」 uses its native reading, the same 「ひと」 as in 「ひとつ」, one thing. And 「人|り」 uses an old native counter for people that only survives in one and two people. From three on, you switch to the regular counter, 「さんにん」. The word has two jobs. One is counting: one person. The other is alone. Add 「で」 and you get 「一人で|ひとりで」, by myself: 「ひとりでたべます」, I eat alone. In Japan, doing things solo has become a whole trend. You will see booths for one person at ramen shops and karaoke for one. Another useful phrase is 「ひとりっこ」, an only child. Don't say 「いちにん」, which is a common beginner slip. So: the native one plus an old people counter, meaning one person or all by yourself." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word builds some very expressive phrases. 「ひとりぐらし」 means living alone, and it's what students and young workers say when they move out. 「ひとりごと」 is talking to yourself, literally one-person words. If you mutter while cooking, a friend might laugh and say that's a 「ひとりごと」. 「ひとりぼっち」 means all alone, with a lonely feeling, the way a child might feel at a new school. And doubling the word gives 「ひとりひとり」, each and every person, one by one. A teacher might say everyone is different, 「ひとりひとりちがいます」. Finally, a polite version, 「おひとりさま」, is how a restaurant greets a solo customer, and it's also a trendy word for enjoying things on your own. So: living alone, talking to yourself, lonely, and each person, all from one person." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "English uses by myself for two different ideas, and Japanese splits them. 「ひとりで」 means alone, with nobody else around. 「じぶんで」 means on your own, without anyone's help. So 「ひとりでりょうりします」 means I cook alone, maybe in an empty kitchen. But 「じぶんでりょうりします」 means I cook it myself, I don't buy ready-made food. A child might proudly say they did their homework 「じぶんで」, meaning without mom's help, even if mom was in the room. You can also put the word before a noun with 「の」: 「ひとりのじかん」, time on your own, alone time, which many busy people say they want. So: 「ひとりで」 for no company, 「じぶんで」 for no help." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses native 「ひと」 plus an old people counter 「り」 that survives only in one and two, and with 「で」 it means alone, as in 「ひとりでたべます」.",
      "It builds expressive phrases like 「ひとりぐらし」, living alone, 「ひとりごと」, talking to yourself, and 「ひとりひとり」, each and every person.",
      "Japanese splits by myself into 「ひとりで」, with no company, and 「じぶんで」, with no help, as in doing homework yourself."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[一人|ひとり]で{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat alone every day." },
  "ふたり|二人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Just like 「一人|ひとり」, this word uses old native readings instead of the regular counter. 「二|ふた」 is the native reading of two, the same 「ふた」 as in 「ふたつ」, two things. And 「人|り」 is the same old people counter as in one person. These two words, one person and two people, are the only ones built this way. Three people and up use the regular 「にん」 counter. Add 「で」 and you get 「ふたりで」, the two of us, together: 「ふたりでえいがをみました」, the two of us watched a movie. The word also carries a romantic feeling, the two of them, a couple. Japanese song titles and movies use it a lot. Don't say 「にじん」 or 「ににん」, which is a common beginner slip. A way to remember it: one and two people are special, because they are the people closest to you. So: native two plus the old people counter, two people, or a pair." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The native word for two, 「ふた」, shows up in a few other places. 「ふたつ」 is two things. 「ふたご」 means twins, literally two children. 「ふたたび」 means once again, a slightly formal word you'll see in writing. The number word for the second day of the month, 「二日|ふつか」, comes from the same old root, though the sound has changed. And then there is a famous oddity: twenty years old is 「はたち」, a completely special word, because turning twenty was traditionally the age of adulthood. Now back to people. 「ふたりとも」 means both of them: 「ふたりともがくせいです」, both of them are students. So: twins, the second, and both of them, all from the old native two." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you're out with one friend, this word comes up constantly. At a restaurant, 「ふたりです」 gets you a table for two. When someone speaks politely about two guests, they say 「おふたり」, the two of you: 「おふたりはどうやってしりあいましたか」, how did the two of you meet? That question is very typical for couples. There's also 「ふたりきり」, just the two of us, with no one else around, often with a romantic or awkward feeling. A movie scene where two characters are left alone in an elevator is a 「ふたりきり」 moment. Finally, notice the counting rhythm you should have ready: 「ひとり」, 「ふたり」, 「さんにん」, 「よにん」. The first two are special, then the pattern changes. So: a table for two, the polite pair, and the romantic just the two of us." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Like 「一人|ひとり」, it uses native 「ふた」 plus the old counter 「り」, and with 「で」 it means the two of us together, often with a romantic feel.",
      "The old native two also gives 「ふたつ」, two things, and 「ふたご」, twins, and 「ふたりとも」 means both of them.",
      "Get a table for two with 「ふたりです」, politely ask a couple about 「おふたり」, and 「ふたりきり」 means just the two of you alone."
    ],
    ja: "[二人|ふたり]で{映画|えいが}を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "The two of us watched a movie." },
  "べつに|別に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「別|べつ」 uses its Chinese-origin reading, and means separate or different. It has the knife radical on the right side, which fits the idea of cutting things apart. The same kanji with its native reading is in 「別れる|わかれる」, to separate or break up. Add 「に」 and it becomes an adverb: separately, especially. With a negative, it means not especially, nothing in particular. 「べつになにもありません」, there's nothing in particular. Careful, though. On its own as a short reply, it can sound cold, like a bored teenager saying whatever. A famous example: in 2007, an actress answered reporters at her movie premiere with just this word, and the curt reply turned into a national scandal. So use it with a soft tone, or add more words. So: cut apart and separate, and with a negative, nothing in particular." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji means separate or different, and you'll find it in practical words. 「べつべつ」 means separately, each on its own. At a restaurant, when friends want to pay their own bills, they say 「べつべつにおねがいします」, separate checks, please. 「とくべつ」 means special, set apart from the ordinary, as in 「とくべつなひ」, a special day. 「くべつ」 means distinguishing between things, telling them apart. And before a noun with 「の」, you get 「べつの」, another or a different one: 「べつのみせにいきましょう」, let's go to a different shop. The native reading gives 「わかれる」, to part ways, and 「さようなら」 is the classic farewell for that moment. So: separate checks, a special day and a different shop all come from this kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adverb has two lives. With a negative, it's not especially, the one most learners know. But without a negative, it means separately or in addition. 「これはべつにはらいます」 means I'll pay for this separately. 「べつにいいです」 is a tricky one. It can mean it's fine, never mind, no need, which is perfectly polite in the right tone. Now picture a mini dialogue. A friend asks 「どうしたの」, what's wrong? You answer 「べつに」. If you say it with a smile, it means oh, nothing. If you say it flatly, eyes on your phone, it sounds like none of your business. That's why many people add a little more: 「べつになんでもないよ」, it's nothing, really. So: separately in a positive sentence, nothing much in a negative one, and always watch your tone." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「別|べつ」 means separate, with the knife radical for cutting apart, and with a negative it means nothing in particular, though alone it can sound cold, so watch your tone.",
      "The kanji gives 「べつべつ」, separate checks, 「とくべつ」, special, and 「べつの」, a different one, as in going to a different shop.",
      "Without a negative it means separately, as in 「これはべつにはらいます」, and a flat 「べつに」 sounds like none of your business, so soften it with more words."
    ],
    ja: "[別に|べつに]{何も|なにも}ありません。",
    en: "Nothing in particular." },
  "もちろん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word does have a Chinese-origin kanji spelling, but it is almost always written in kana. The original meaning is roughly no need to argue it. That makes sense for of course: it is so obvious it doesn't need discussing. Use it to agree with energy: 「もちろんいきます」, of course I'll go. It is friendly and fine in polite conversation. It can stand alone as a full answer: someone asks if you're coming, and you just say it. It also works with sentences like 「もちろんです」, of course, which sounds a bit more polite. A tip for English speakers: it can sound a little strong if someone asks you a real question, since it implies the answer was obvious. A way to remember it, as a sound hook: picture someone yelling mochi run! Would you run for mochi? Of course! So: no need to argue, a warm and energetic of course." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several ways to say of course or definitely, and each has its own flavor. This word is the friendly, all-purpose one. 「とうぜん」 means naturally or as a matter of course, but it sounds stronger, even a little arrogant, as if anyone should know. 「ぜひ」 means by all means, and it shows eagerness about something you want to do: 「ぜひいきたいです」, I'd love to go. 「たしかに」 means that's true, used to agree with someone's point. And 「いいですよ」 is the easy, relaxed yes. So if a friend invites you to a party, 「もちろん」 or 「ぜひ」 both sound warm. If a coworker says the trains are crowded today, 「たしかに」 fits. So: of course, naturally, by all means, and that's true are four different flavors of yes." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "One of the most natural uses is answering a request for permission. Someone asks 「ここにすわってもいいですか」, may I sit here? You answer 「もちろんです。どうぞ」, of course, please go ahead. It sounds welcoming. It also works in front of a negative to say of course not: 「もちろんいきません」, of course I won't go, or 「もちろん、だいじょうぶですよ」 to reassure someone. Inside a longer sentence, it can mean needless to say: 「にほんごはもちろん、えいごもはなせます」, she speaks English, and Japanese of course. That pattern, noun plus this word, then a bigger point, is very common. So: a warm yes to requests, a firm of course not, and needless to say in the middle of a sentence." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Its original sense is no need to argue it, so it is a warm, energetic of course, as in 「もちろんいきます」, though it can sound strong for a real question.",
      "It is the friendly all-purpose of course, while 「とうぜん」 sounds stronger, 「ぜひ」 shows eagerness, and 「たしかに」 agrees with a point.",
      "Answer permission requests with 「もちろんです。どうぞ」, and mid-sentence it means needless to say, as in 「にほんごはもちろん、えいごもはなせます」."
    ],
    ja: "[もちろん]{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "Of course I'll go." },
  "よかったら|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This little phrase comes from 「いい」, good. When 「いい」 bends, it uses its older form 「よい」, so the past tense is 「よかった」, was good. Add 「ら」, which makes a condition, if. So literally, if it is good for you. You will study this 「たら」 if pattern later, but this phrase is so useful it gets learned early as a set expression. Use it to offer or invite gently, often with a 「ませんか」 question: 「よかったら、いっしょにいきませんか」, if you like, would you like to go together? It softens the request, so the other person can easily say no. That fits the Japanese habit of leaving room for others to decline politely. In more formal situations, you'll hear 「よろしかったら」, which is even more polite. So: if it was good, which means if you like, the soft key to invitations." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This phrase gives you a sneak peek at a pattern you'll study later. Take the past form of a word and add 「ら」, and you get if or when. It works with other words too. 「ひまだったら」 means if you're free. 「あめだったら」 means if it rains. 「たかかったら」 means if it's expensive. The past form here doesn't mean past time, it just makes the condition. Notice that 「いい」 always switches to its older form 「よ」 when it bends, so you get 「よかった」, never 「いかった」. On its own, 「よかった」 is also a useful word: I'm glad, or what a relief, said when good news arrives. There's another way to say the same phrase, 「よければ」, which uses a different if pattern but means exactly the same. So: past form plus 「ら」 makes if, and 「いい」 always bends as 「よ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase is the perfect way to offer something without pressure. Handing a coworker a snack from your trip, you say 「よかったら、どうぞ」, if you like, please have one. Japanese people often bring small souvenir sweets back for the office, and this is exactly the phrase that goes with them. You can attach it to a request too: 「よかったら、メールアドレスをおしえてください」, if you don't mind, please tell me your email. And when someone offers you something with it, you can accept with 「ありがとうございます、いただきます」. If you want to decline, just say 「あ、だいじょうぶです」, oh, I'm fine, with a smile. The phrase gave you that easy way out. So: hand over a gift, ask a favor, and always leave a door open." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「いい」 bent to past 「よかった」 plus conditional 「ら」, if it is good for you, softening invitations like 「よかったら、いっしょにいきませんか」.",
      "Past form plus 「ら」 makes if, as in 「ひまだったら」, and 「いい」 always bends to 「よ」, so 「よかった」 on its own means I'm glad.",
      "Hand over souvenir sweets with 「よかったら、どうぞ」, which leaves room to decline with a smiling 「あ、だいじょうぶです」."
    ],
    ja: "[よかったら]、いっしょに{行きませんか|いきませんか}。",
    en: "If you like, would you like to go together?" },
  "おにいさん|お兄さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the polite version of 「兄|あに」, older brother. It is the same kanji, but with a special reading, 「にい」, wrapped in a polite 「お」 at the front and 「さん」 at the end. Japanese family words come in two sets. The plain ones, like 「あに」, are for your own family when talking to outsiders. The polite ones, like this, are for other people's families: 「おにいさんはなにをしていますか」, what does your older brother do? But you also use this word to call out to your own older brother at home, since younger siblings call older ones by title, not by name. It even stretches beyond family. A shopkeeper might call a young man this word, a bit like calling out to a young guy. So: the polite older brother, used for other people's, for calling your own, and for young men." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji is a mouth sitting on top of a pair of legs, the same legs you see at the bottom of 「先|せん」 in 「先生|せんせい」. Scholars give different explanations for what the picture first meant, so let's not pretend to know. As a memory hook, you can think of it as a person who's all mouth, the older brother who's always telling you what to do. Now the readings. On its own, the plain word is 「あに」. In this polite word, it takes the special reading 「にい」. With the Chinese-origin reading 「きょう」, it starts 「兄弟|きょうだい」, siblings. And with another Chinese-origin reading, 「けい」, it appears in more formal words. So: one simple shape with four readings, and 「にい」 for the polite word." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Family titles in Japanese can turn into each other with a single long vowel, so listen closely. This word has a long 「いい」 sound, stretched out. Now compare 「おじさん」 and 「おじいさん」. With a short sound, it's uncle or a middle-aged man. With a long 「いい」, it's grandfather or an old man. The same happens with 「おばさん」, aunt, and 「おばあさん」, grandmother. Call a forty-year-old woman 「おばあさん」 by accident, and you've just called her granny. So practice holding the long vowels. And just like with 「おねえさん」, the stretched sound is part of the word. If you say 「おにさん」 with a short sound, it starts to sound like 「おに」, a demon from Japanese folklore. So: stretch the long vowels, or your big brother becomes a demon and your aunt becomes a grandma." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a whole set of variations depending on how close you are. At home, little kids say 「おにいちゃん」, the cute, affectionate version. Many adults keep calling their brother that their whole lives. Very small children might even say 「にいに」. On the other side, there's 「あにき」, a rough, masculine version used by some men, and in movies it's also how gang members address a senior member. When a shop clerk at a market wants to get a young man's attention, they might call out 「おにいさん、やすいよ」, hey young man, it's cheap! It's flattering, since it makes the person sound young. Notice that Japanese often uses family words for strangers, while English would just say sir or hey. So: 「おにいちゃん」 at home, 「あにき」 in movies, and this word at the market." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the polite form of 「兄|あに」, read 「にい」 inside 「お」 and 「さん」, used for someone else's brother, for calling your own, and even for young men.",
      "The kanji is a mouth on legs, picture a brother who's all mouth, and it reads 「あに」, 「にい」 here, 「きょう」 in 「兄弟|きょうだい」, and 「けい」 in formal words.",
      "Hold the long 「いい」, because a short 「おにさん」 starts to sound like 「おに」, a demon, just as long vowels separate 「おじさん」 from 「おじいさん」.",
      "At home it becomes 「おにいちゃん」, rough men and movie gangsters say 「あにき」, and market sellers flatter young men with 「おにいさん、やすいよ」."
    ],
    ja: "[お兄さん|おにいさん]は{何|なに}をしていますか。",
    en: "What does your older brother do?" },
  "おねえさん|お姉さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the polite version of 「姉|あね」, older sister. It is the same kanji, but with a special reading, 「ねえ」, wrapped in 「お」 and 「さん」. The rule follows the brother word. Use the plain 「あね」 for your own sister when you talk to others, and this polite word for someone else's sister: 「おねえさんはどこにすんでいますか」, where does your older sister live? You also use it to call your own older sister at home, because in Japan younger siblings call older ones by this title, not by name. It goes beyond family too. Children call any young woman this, and customers sometimes use it for young women working in shops or restaurants. Calling a woman this instead of 「おばさん」 is the flattering choice. So: the polite older sister, for others' sisters, for calling your own, and for young women." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji has the woman radical on the left, just like younger sister and mother. The right side looks exactly like the modern kanji for city, 「市|し」. Historically it was a different element that only came to look like city, so don't build a real meaning on it. But as a memory hook, you can picture the older sister as the one who already goes to the city on her own, while the little ones stay home. Now the readings. On its own, the plain word is 「あね」. In this polite word, it becomes 「ねえ」. And with its Chinese-origin reading, 「し」, it starts the word 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters. You'll meet the same pair in 「姉妹都市|しまいとし」, sister cities, which many Japanese and American towns have. So: a woman beside a city-like shape, read 「あね」, 「ねえ」, or 「し」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese families have an interesting habit. People often address each other from the point of view of the youngest child. So in a family with two daughters, even the parents may call the older daughter 「おねえちゃん」, big sister, because that's what she is to the youngest. A mother might say, 「おねえちゃん、てつだって」, big sis, help me. The same thing happens with mom and dad. Spouses often call each other 「おかあさん」 and 「おとうさん」 once they have kids. In casual speech, the cute form 「おねえちゃん」 is the most common way to call your own sister, and a rougher version, 「あねき」, is sometimes used by adult men. So: in a Japanese family, titles follow the youngest child's view of the world." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the polite form of 「姉|あね」, read 「ねえ」, used for someone else's sister, for calling your own, and flatteringly for young women instead of 「おばさん」.",
      "The kanji is the woman radical beside a shape like 「市|し」, city, picture the big sister who goes to the city alone, and it reads 「あね」, 「ねえ」 or 「し」.",
      "Families use titles from the youngest child's view, so even parents may call the older daughter 「おねえちゃん」, as in 「おねえちゃん、てつだって」."
    ],
    ja: "[お姉さん|おねえさん]はどこに{住んでいますか|すんでいますか}。",
    en: "Where does your older sister live?" },
  "いもうと|妹": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The kanji has 「女|おんな」, woman, on the left, the same side you see in 「姉|あね」, older sister. On the right is a part that means not yet. A way to remember it, as a hook: a girl who is not yet grown up, the little sister. In Chinese-origin words, this kanji reads 「まい」, as in 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters. This plain word is for your own younger sister. For someone else's younger sister, add 「さん」: 「いもうとさん」. Unlike older siblings, younger ones don't get a special title when you call them. In Japanese families, you just call your younger sister by her name. Notice also that in Japanese you must say whether a sister is older or younger. There is no common everyday word for just sister. So: a woman not yet grown, your little sister, called by her name." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The right side of this kanji is 「未|み」, not yet, which also stands alone in useful words. 「未来|みらい」 means the future, the time that has not yet come. 「未婚|みこん」 means not yet married. So the little sister really does carry the not yet idea inside her. Now the sibling words. 「姉妹|しまい」 means sisters, built from older sister plus younger sister. Its partner, 「兄弟|きょうだい」, older brother plus younger brother, is the everyday word for siblings in general, and people use it even when the siblings include sisters. So the question 「ごきょうだいは」, do you have brothers or sisters, works for everyone. And 「姉妹都市|しまいとし」 means sister cities, pairs of towns in different countries that keep friendly ties. So: the future, sisters, and siblings all connect to this kanji's family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When someone asks about your family, here's how to talk about your sister naturally. Use 「が」 and a counter: 「いもうとがひとりいます」, I have one younger sister. To list several siblings, join them with 「と」: 「あにがひとりと、いもうとがふたりいます」, one older brother and two younger sisters. If you're the youngest, you can say 「すえっこです」, I'm the baby of the family. When asking someone else, switch to the polite form: 「いもうとさんはおいくつですか」, how old is your younger sister? Notice one more trick. The word for younger sister needs no special call name. At home, older siblings just use her name, often with 「ちゃん」 added. So: 「が」 and a counter to describe, 「さん」 for someone else's, and her name when you call her." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is 「女|おんな」, woman, beside a part meaning not yet, a girl not yet grown, and you call your little sister by name, not a title.",
      "The right side is 「未|み」, not yet, as in 「未来|みらい」, future, and the kanji joins older sister in 「姉妹|しまい」, sisters.",
      "Say 「いもうとがひとりいます」 for your own, use 「いもうとさん」 for someone else's, and call her by name, often with 「ちゃん」."
    ],
    ja: "[妹|いもうと]は{高校生|こうこうせい}です。",
    en: "My younger sister is a high school student." },
  "おとうと|弟": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native one. The shape of this kanji has long been connected to the idea of order, coming next in line. You can see that sense in 「第|だい」, the kanji used for numbering like number one or the first. It is this same shape with bamboo on top. So the younger brother is the one who comes next in order. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「だい」, it is the second half of 「兄弟|きょうだい」, siblings. This plain word is for your own younger brother. For someone else's, add 「さん」: 「おとうとさん」. As with younger sisters, you call your younger brother by his name at home, not by a title. A way to remember it: the word starts just like 「おとうさん」, father, but ends in a short 「と」. Be careful saying it, because that tiny ending changes your dad into your little brother. So: next in order, your younger brother, and not your father." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Family words in Japanese can sound dangerously close, so let's line them up. This word has a long 「とう」 in the middle and ends in 「と」. 「おとうさん」, father, has the same long 「とう」, but ends in 「さん」. Then there's 「おっと」, husband, which has a small 「っ」 and no long vowel at all. So: 「おとうと」, 「おとうさん」, 「おっと」. Younger brother, father, husband. Say them slowly a few times and feel where the long vowel and the small pause sit. Mixing up 「おっと」 and 「おとうと」 could give a very confusing family story. 「おっと」 is also a little exclamation, like oops, so if you drop something, you might hear yourself say it. So: hold the long 「とう」 for brother and father, but snap it short for husband." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading, this kanji appears in a few interesting words. 「兄弟|きょうだい」, siblings, you already know. 「弟子|でし」 means an apprentice or disciple, someone who learns from a master. Here the kanji is read 「で」. In Japan, many traditional crafts, like sushi making, pottery, or comedy, were passed on through a master and apprentice relationship. A 「でし」 would work for years, cleaning and watching, before being taught directly. You can see how that fits the idea of order: the apprentice comes after the master, like a younger brother. The word 「きょうだい」 is also used jokingly between close male friends, a bit like calling someone bro. So: younger brother, siblings, and apprentices all share the idea of coming next in line." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji carries the idea of coming next in order, also seen in 「第|だい」, and the word starts like 「おとうさん」, father, but ends in a short 「と」.",
      "Line them up: 「おとうと」 is younger brother, 「おとうさん」 father, and 「おっと」, with a small 「っ」 and no long vowel, husband.",
      "Read 「で」 it gives 「弟子|でし」, an apprentice who comes after the master, fitting the kanji's idea of coming next in line."
    ],
    ja: "[弟|おとうと]は{十|じゅう}さいです。",
    en: "My younger brother is ten years old." },
  "そうじする|掃除する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's take it apart. The first kanji, 「掃|そう」, means to sweep. Look at its left side and you'll see the hand shape that shows up in lots of action kanji, so a hand is doing the sweeping. The second kanji, 「除|じ」, means to remove or get rid of. Both are read with their Chinese-origin readings here. Put them together and you get sweep and remove: you sweep the dirt up and take it away. In Japanese, 「掃除|そうじ」 on its own is a noun, cleaning. Add 「する」 and it becomes a verb, the same trick you've seen with 「べんきょうする」, 「洗濯する|せんたくする」 and 「料理する|りょうりする」. These three chores sit together in the same lesson, so they're easy to learn as a set. Notice that you clean a place, so the room or the house takes 「を」: 「へやを掃除する|へやをそうじする」. A cultural note: in Japanese schools, students clean their own classrooms. There is a daily cleaning time, so most Japanese people grew up doing 「そうじ」 with a broom in hand. To recap: sweep plus remove, plus 「する」, gives you to clean." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word show up elsewhere. The first, to sweep, gives you 「掃除機|そうじき」, a vacuum cleaner, literally a cleaning machine. Its native reading is the verb 「はく」, to sweep, the same sound as the verb for putting on shoes. With the word for big in front, you get 「おおそうじ」, a big cleaning. The second kanji, to remove, appears in 「削除|さくじょ」, delete, which you'll see on computer and phone menus. Now some near-synonyms. 「かたづける」 means to tidy up, putting things back where they belong. 「きれいにする」 means to make something clean or pretty in general. So you might 「かたづける」 your desk, then do 「そうじ」 to wipe the dust off it. So: a vacuum, a delete button, and tidying are all close relatives." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "At the end of every year, Japanese households do the 「おおそうじ」, the big cleaning. In late December, families scrub windows, wipe walls, clean the kitchen fan, and throw out things they don't need. Offices and schools often do it too, with everyone pitching in on the last working day. The idea is to start the new year fresh, welcoming it into a clean home. Many people say it's connected to old customs of preparing the house to welcome the god of the new year. After the cleaning, people decorate the entrance with pine branches and enjoy the New Year holidays. It's a bit like spring cleaning, but tied to a specific date. So: the year ends with a huge cleaning, and the new one begins spotless." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「掃|そう」, with a hand radical, means sweep and 「除|じ」 means remove, so you sweep the dirt and take it away, and the place you clean takes 「を」.",
      "The sweep kanji gives 「掃除機|そうじき」, vacuum cleaner, the remove kanji gives 「削除|さくじょ」, delete, and 「かたづける」 means tidying things away rather than cleaning.",
      "At the end of December households do the 「おおそうじ」, a big cleaning to welcome the new year into a spotless home."
    ],
    ja: "{毎週|まいしゅう}{土曜日|どようび}にへやを[掃除します|そうじします|そうじする]。",
    en: "I clean my room every Saturday." },
  "あめ|雨": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is one of the clearest pictures in Japanese. The line across the top is the sky, the frame below it is like a cloud or a window onto the sky, and the four little dots inside are drops of water falling. On its own it's read 「あめ」, the native Japanese reading. The best part is that 「雨|あめ」 works as a weather badge on top of other kanji. Look at 「雪|ゆき」, snow. It has the rain kanji squashed on top, because snow falls from the sky too. 「曇り|くもり」, cloudy, contains the cloud kanji, and that cloud kanji also wears the rain shape on top. And 「電気|でんき」, electricity, starts with a kanji that has rain on top as well. That kanji first meant lightning, the flash that comes with a storm, and that's how it ended up meaning electricity. So whenever you see that flattened rain shape on top of a kanji, think sky and weather. Rain falls with the verb 「降る|ふる」: 「雨が降る|あめがふる」. A cultural note: in early summer Japan has a rainy season of several weeks, and people carry umbrellas everywhere. Recap: sky, cloud and drops make rain, and that shape tops many weather kanji." },
      { title: "Sound changes", text: "When this word joins other words, its sound often shifts, and spotting the pattern helps a lot. In front of another word, it frequently becomes 「あま」. An umbrella for rain is 「あまがさ」, a rain cloud is 「あまぐも」, and storm shutters are 「あまど」, rain doors. When it comes second, it often turns into 「さめ」. Light rain is 「こさめ」, and spring rain is 「はるさめ」, which is also the name of glass noodles, because they look like fine, shiny rain. Then there's a special one: the rainy season is 「梅雨|つゆ」, plum rain, because it falls when plums ripen, read as a whole word. And heavy rain is simply 「おおあめ」. So: 「あま」 in front, 「さめ」 behind, and a plum rain in June." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There is another word with exactly the same letters: candy. The difference is pitch, how the voice rises and falls. In standard Tokyo Japanese, rain starts high and drops on the second sound. Candy starts low and rises, staying level after that. Think of rain falling down from the sky, high to low. Candy is the opposite, so think of a sweet that lifts your mood. Don't worry too much, though. Pitch varies by region, and in Osaka people even use a different pattern. Context almost always makes it clear. If it's coming down from the sky, it's rain. If it's in your mouth, it's candy. Osaka people are famous for carrying candy in their bags and giving it out, and they call it 「あめちゃん」. So: rain falls high to low, candy rises." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the verbs that go with rain. It falls with 「ふる」 and stops with 「やむ」: 「あめがやみました」, the rain stopped. If you get caught in the rain, Japanese uses a special passive: 「あめにふられました」, I was rained on, which sounds a bit like complaining, and it is. Convenience stores sell cheap clear plastic umbrellas, and they're everywhere in Japan. Shops often have umbrella stands or plastic sleeves at the door so you don't drip on the floor. In a weather forecast, you'll hear 「あめのちはれ」, rain, then sunny later. And the chance of rain is given as a percentage. So: 「ふる」 to fall, 「やむ」 to stop, and always have a clear umbrella nearby." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is sky, cloud and falling drops, and its flattened shape tops weather kanji like 「雪|ゆき」 and 「雲|くも」, while rain falls with 「降る|ふる」.",
      "In compounds it becomes 「あま」 in front, as in 「あまがさ」, umbrella, and 「さめ」 behind, as in 「こさめ」, light rain, and the rainy season is 「梅雨|つゆ」.",
      "Candy shares the same sound, but in Tokyo speech rain falls from high to low while candy rises, and context usually makes it clear.",
      "Rain falls with 「ふる」 and stops with 「やむ」, and getting caught in it is the complaining passive 「あめにふられました」."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[雨|あめ]がふっています。",
    en: "It is raining today." },
  "せんたくする|洗濯する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji in this word have three little drops of water on their left side. That water radical tells you right away that this word is about water. The first kanji, 「洗|せん」, means to wash. Its right side is 「先|せん」, the same kanji as in 「先生|せんせい」, and it lends its sound, so 「洗|せん」 is read just like it. That's a real hint you can use: water plus the sound of 「先|せん」. It's the same kanji you'll see in 「洗う|あらう」, to wash, but there it uses the native reading. The second kanji, 「濯|たく」, means to rinse. Both readings here are the Chinese-origin ones. Wash and rinse your clothes, and that's laundry. Add 「する」 to make it a verb, just like 「掃除する|そうじする」. A cultural note: many Japanese homes don't use a dryer. People hang their laundry outside on the balcony, so weather forecasts often tell you whether it's a good day for drying clothes. Recap: water, wash and rinse together make laundry." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a trap for listeners. There's another word with exactly the same sound that means choice or selection. You'll see it in test questions as a choice among answers, and on screens asking you to select an option. Different kanji, same sound. Context usually saves you: laundry goes with clothes and washing machines, choice goes with options and decisions. Now the rhythm of a related word. The washing machine is 「せんたくき」, but in natural speech it usually turns into 「せんたっき」, with a small 「っ」 pause where the 「く」 used to be. That kind of squeeze happens a lot when a 「く」 sound meets a 「き」 sound. Laundry items, the pile of clothes itself, are 「せんたくもの」. So: one sound for laundry and choice, and a small 「っ」 hiding in washing machine." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Doing laundry in Japanese uses a little chain of verbs. First, 「せんたくする」, wash the clothes. Then 「ほす」, hang them out to dry, as in 「せんたくものをほします」. When they're dry, you 「とりこむ」, bring them in, and then 「たたむ」, fold them. Notice that you wouldn't normally say you 「あらう」 your clothes in daily life, though it's not wrong. 「あらう」 is for hands, dishes, your face, or a car. Laundry as a whole chore uses this word. A typical Sunday plan might be: 「あさ、せんたくして、そうじしました」, in the morning I did laundry and cleaned. Use the 「て」 form, 「せんたくして」, to chain chores together. So: wash, hang, bring in, fold, each with its own verb." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This word appears in the opening lines of Japan's most famous folk tale, Momotaro, the Peach Boy. The story begins: once upon a time, an old man and an old woman lived together. The old man went to the mountains to gather firewood, and the old woman went to the river to do laundry. While she was washing clothes in the river, a giant peach came floating down, and inside it was a baby boy. Almost every Japanese child knows that opening by heart. In those days, laundry meant scrubbing clothes on a board in cold river water. Today washing machines do the work, but the old image lives on in the story. So: Momotaro's adventure began with grandma doing the laundry." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Both kanji carry the water radical, 「洗|せん」 wash, borrowing its sound from 「先|せん」, and 「濯|たく」 rinse, so wash and rinse is laundry.",
      "The same sound also means choice, and the washing machine 「せんたくき」 is usually squeezed to 「せんたっき」 in speech.",
      "Laundry chains its own verbs: 「せんたくする」 to wash, 「ほす」 to hang, 「とりこむ」 to bring in, and 「たたむ」 to fold, while 「あらう」 is for hands and dishes.",
      "Momotaro begins with the old woman doing laundry in the river when a giant peach floats by with a baby boy inside."
    ],
    ja: "{日曜日|にちようび}に[洗濯します|せんたくします|せんたくする]。",
    en: "I do laundry on Sundays." },
  "りょうりする|料理する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's break it down. The first kanji, 「料|りょう」, has rice, 「米|こめ」, on its left and a measuring ladle on its right. So it's about measuring out materials. It came to mean ingredients or materials, and also fees. The second kanji, 「理|り」, means reason, logic, or putting things in order. Both use their Chinese-origin readings here. Together they suggest taking your ingredients and handling them in a sensible, orderly way. Measured materials, put in order: that's a cooked dish. On its own, 「料理|りょうり」 is a noun meaning cooking or cuisine, so you can say 「日本料理|にほんりょうり」 for Japanese food. Add 「する」 and it becomes the verb to cook, the same pattern as 「掃除する|そうじする」 and 「洗濯する|せんたくする」. You can also use 「作る|つくる」 for making a particular dish, like a cake, but 「料理する|りょうりする」 is the general word for cooking as an activity. Recap: measured ingredients, arranged with reason, give you cooking." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji here leads to everyday words. The first, materials and fees, gives 「材料|ざいりょう」, ingredients, the things you need for a recipe. It also gives 「料金|りょうきん」, a fee or charge, and 「無料|むりょう」, free of charge, which you'll see on signs all over Japan. The second kanji, reason and order, gives 「理由|りゆう」, a reason, and 「無理|むり」, impossible, literally without reason. When a friend asks you to do something you can't, 「むりです」 is the honest answer. Notice the pattern with 「無|む」, meaning without: no fee is free, no reason is impossible. So: ingredients, free, reasons, and impossible all share a kanji with cooking." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The noun form is everywhere in daily life. Types of cuisine are named with it: 「ちゅうかりょうり」, Chinese food, and 「イタリアりょうり」, Italian food. To praise someone's cooking skill, say 「りょうりがじょうずですね」, you're good at cooking. Home-cooked food made by someone you know is 「てりょうり」, hand cooking, and it has a warm, loving feeling. Then there's 「じすい」, cooking for yourself, which students living alone talk about a lot because it saves money. To say what dish you're making, switch to 「つくる」: 「カレーをつくります」. You wouldn't say you 「りょうりする」 a curry. That verb is the general activity. So: name cuisines with it, praise skills with it, and use 「つくる」 for a particular dish." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Traditional Japanese food, called 「和食|わしょく」, was added to UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage in 2013. One of its classic ideas is a meal of rice, a bowl of soup, and three side dishes, called 「いちじゅうさんさい」, one soup, three dishes. That gives balance: a main protein, like grilled fish, and two vegetable sides, with pickles on the side too. Japanese cooking also cares a lot about the seasons. Menus change for spring bamboo shoots, summer eel, autumn mushrooms, and winter hot pots. Presentation matters too, with small dishes of different shapes and colors arranged to look beautiful. Before eating, everyone says 「いただきます」. So: rice, soup, three dishes, and the seasons on your plate." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「料|りょう」 is rice with a measuring ladle, ingredients, and 「理|り」 is reason or order, so ingredients handled in order make cooking.",
      "The first kanji gives 「材料|ざいりょう」, ingredients, and 「無料|むりょう」, free, while the second gives 「理由|りゆう」, reason, and 「無理|むり」, impossible.",
      "Use it to name cuisines and praise skill, as in 「りょうりがじょうずですね」, but switch to 「つくる」 for a particular dish, like 「カレーをつくります」.",
      "「和食|わしょく」 is UNESCO-listed heritage, built on rice, soup and three side dishes, called 「いちじゅうさんさい」, with menus that follow the seasons."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}はよく[料理します|りょうりします|りょうりする]。",
    en: "My mother often cooks." },
  "あらう|洗う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the left side of this kanji. Those three little drops are the water radical, the same one in many water words. So the kanji is clearly about water. The right side is 「先|せん」, from 「先生|せんせい」. It's there mostly for its sound. In this verb the kanji uses its native reading, 「あら」, with 「う」 after it. In 「洗濯|せんたく」, laundry, the very same kanji uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「せん」, which matches the sound of 「先|せん」. Same kanji, same idea of washing, two readings. There's one more sibling you already know: 「お手洗い|おてあらい」, the restroom. It literally means the place where you wash your hands. That's the polite word for the toilet in Japan. You wash something, so the thing takes 「を」: 「手を洗う|てをあらう」, to wash your hands. It's a regular u-verb: 「洗います|あらいます」, 「洗って|あらって」. Recap: water on the left means washing, read 「あら」 here, and you'll see it again in laundry and in the restroom." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji does a lot of cleaning around the house. 「せんめんじょ」, the washroom, is the area with the sink, where you wash your face and brush your teeth. In Japanese homes, it's usually separate from the toilet and from the bath. 「せんざい」 is detergent, the soap you use for laundry or dishes. With the native reading, you get 「さらあらい」, dishwashing, a common first part-time job. And 「かおをあらう」 means to wash your face, the first thing many people do in the morning. All of these share the water radical and that same idea of washing. So: the washroom, detergent, and dishwashing all come from one watery kanji." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an u-verb ending in 「う」, so its 「て」 form uses a small 「っ」: 「あらって」, like 「かう」 becomes 「かって」. The negative is 「あらわない」, because 「う」 verbs switch to 「わ」 in the negative, not 「あ」. That trips up many learners. A very common pattern is 「てから」, after doing something: 「てをあらってから、たべます」, I eat after washing my hands. Parents say this to children all the time. You can also use it with the 「ましょう」 form for suggestions: 「さらをあらいましょう」, let's wash the dishes. And a polite request: 「てをあらってください」, please wash your hands, which you'll see on signs in restaurants and hospitals. So: small 「っ」 in the 「て」 form, 「わ」 in the negative." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, you wash before you get into the bath, not in it. The bathroom has a separate washing area with a stool, a bucket, and a shower. You sit down, wash your body and hair thoroughly, and rinse off all the soap. Only then do you step into the hot tub to soak and relax. The water in the tub stays clean, so the whole family can share the same bathwater one after another. At public baths and hot springs, this rule is strict, and people watch to see that you follow it. Signs often say 「からだをあらってから、はいってください」, please wash your body before entering. So: wash first, then soak, and never bring soap into the tub." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is the water radical plus 「先|せん」 for sound, read 「あら」 here but 「せん」 in 「洗濯|せんたく」, and it also appears in 「お手洗い|おてあらい」, restroom.",
      "The washing kanji gives 「せんめんじょ」, the washroom with the sink, 「せんざい」, detergent, and with the native reading 「さらあらい」, dishwashing.",
      "As a 「う」 verb its 「て」 form is 「あらって」 and its negative takes 「わ」, 「あらわない」, and 「てをあらってから」 means after washing your hands.",
      "In Japan you wash thoroughly in a separate area before soaking, so the tub water stays clean, and signs say 「からだをあらってから、はいってください」."
    ],
    ja: "{手|て}を[洗います|あらいます|あらう]。",
    en: "I wash my hands." },
  "はれ|晴れ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is a lovely little picture. On the left is the sun, 「日|ひ」. On the right is the kanji for blue, 「青|あお」. Sun plus blue gives you a bright, clear, sunny sky. The blue part also gives the kanji its Chinese-origin sound in other words, but here we use the native reading, 「は」, followed by 「れ」. 「晴れ|はれ」 is a noun, sunny weather, the kind of word you hear in a forecast: 「あしたは晴れです|あしたははれです」. It comes from the verb 「晴れる|はれる」, to clear up. You'll hear it when the rain stops and the sky opens up. Put it next to its weather friends: 「雨|あめ」, rain, 「曇り|くもり」, cloudy, and 「雪|ゆき」, snow. Notice that cloudy also has the sun, 「日|ひ」, but buried under a cloud. A cultural note: Japanese children hang a little white cloth doll in the window before a school trip or sports day. It's a wish for a sunny day. Recap: sun and blue sky make sunny." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji brightens up several words. The verb 「はれる」 means to clear up: 「あしたははれるでしょう」, it will probably clear up tomorrow. Here's a surprising relative: 「すばらしい」, wonderful, is written with this same kanji in its middle. A bright, clear feeling. 「はれぎ」 means your best clothes, like a formal kimono worn for a special day such as a coming-of-age ceremony. 「きばらし」 means a change of pace, something to clear your mind, like going for a walk when you're stressed. In that word, the sound changes to 「ばら」. So: clearing up, wonderful, your best clothes, and clearing your head all share this sunny kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese has a fun way to talk about luck with the weather. A person who always seems to bring sunshine is called a 「はれおとこ」, sunny man, or 「はれおんな」, sunny woman. If every trip you plan has clear skies, your friends will call you one. The opposite is 「あめおとこ」 or 「あめおんな」, the rain-bringers, and people joke about not inviting them to picnics. There's also a deeper idea. Japanese folklore studies talk about 「はれ」 and 「け」, special days and ordinary days. Festivals, weddings and New Year are 「はれ」 days, when you wear your best clothes. Everyday life is 「け」. So: be a sunny person, and save your best clothes for the special days." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is the sun 「日|ひ」 beside 「青|あお」, blue, so sun plus blue sky means sunny, a noun from the verb 「晴れる|はれる」, to clear up.",
      "The kanji brightens 「すばらしい」, wonderful, 「はれぎ」, your best clothes, and 「きばらし」, a change of pace that clears your mind.",
      "Someone who always brings sunshine is a 「はれおとこ」 or 「はれおんな」, and folklore splits days into special 「はれ」 days and ordinary 「け」 days."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[晴れ|はれ]です。",
    en: "It will be sunny tomorrow." },
  "くもり|曇り": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here's a kanji you can almost see. At the top is the sun, 「日|ひ」. Under it is the kanji for cloud, 「雲|くも」. So the sun is stuck behind a cloud. That's cloudy weather. The cloud kanji itself wears the rain kanji, 「雨|あめ」, on top, so this one kanji stacks sun, rain and cloud together. It's a little weather report in one character. Here it's read with the native reading, 「くも」, followed by 「り」. The word 「くも」 for a cloud and 「くもり」 for cloudy weather share the same sound, which is a nice anchor. 「曇り|くもり」 is the noun you hear in a weather forecast. It comes from the verb 「くもる」, to get cloudy. Compare it with 「晴れ|はれ」, sunny, where the sun sits happily next to blue sky. In cloudy, the sun is covered up. You'll often hear the combination 「曇りのち雨|くもりのちあめ」 in forecasts, cloudy, later rain. Recap: the sun hidden under a cloud gives you cloudy, read like the word for a cloud." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「くもる」, to get cloudy, has some lovely uses beyond the sky. When you walk into a warm room on a cold day, your glasses fog up: 「めがねがくもりました」. A mirror fogging up after a hot shower uses the same verb. Even a person's face can cloud over when they're worried: 「かおがくもる」. 「くもりぞら」 is a cloudy sky, gray and dull. And 「くもりガラス」 is frosted glass, the kind used in bathroom windows that lets light in but blocks the view. All of these share the idea of something clear becoming hazy. So: foggy glasses, a misty mirror, a worried face and frosted glass, all cloudy in Japanese." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the kanji. Picture the sun, round and happy, trying to come out for the day. But a huge rain cloud floats right over it and sits on its head, like a big fluffy hat. The poor sun is squashed underneath. Now, the cloud has a spider's web caught in it, because the word for spider also sounds just like 「くも」. A spider is dangling from the cloud, waving at the sun. The sun sighs. It's going to be a gray day. When you see the kanji, picture the sun at the top, and the cloud with its rain hat underneath, hiding everything. So: the sun buried under a cloud, with a spider swinging from it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji puts the sun 「日|ひ」 above the cloud 「雲|くも」, a sun hidden by cloud, and it is read just like the word for cloud plus 「り」.",
      "The verb 「くもる」 means something clear going hazy, like glasses fogging, 「めがねがくもりました」, a worried face, or 「くもりガラス」, frosted glass.",
      "Picture the sun squashed under a fluffy rain cloud, with a spider, also 「くも」, dangling from it and waving."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[曇り|くもり]です。",
    en: "It's cloudy today." },
  "ゆうめい|有名": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's take it apart. The first kanji, 「有|ゆう」, means to have or to exist. The second kanji, 「名|めい」, means name. It's the same kanji used to write 「なまえ」, your name, where it has its native reading. Here both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. So the word literally says having a name. Not just any name, but a name that people know. If your name is out there in the world, you're famous. You've got a sibling for it too: 「有名人|ゆうめいじん」, a celebrity. Add 「人|じん」, person, and you get a famous person. Grammar note: 「有名|ゆうめい」 is a na-adjective. So you say 「有名な店|ゆうめいなみせ」, a famous shop, with 「な」 in front of a noun. To say what something is famous for, use 「で」: 「京都はお寺で有名です|きょうとはおてらでゆうめいです」, Kyoto is famous for its temples. A common mistake is to drop the 「な」 before a noun. Keep it in. Recap: to have a name that people know means famous." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji have useful families. The first, to have or exist, gives 「有料|ゆうりょう」, requiring a fee, the opposite of 「無料|むりょう」, free. You'll see those two words on parking lots and toilets in Japan. The second, name, gives 「名刺|めいし」, a business card, literally a name card. Exchanging them is a ritual in Japanese business. It also gives 「名物|めいぶつ」, a famous local product, like a town's special snack, and 「名所|めいしょ」, a famous sight or spot. And with its native reading, it's in 「名前|なまえ」, your name. So: paid parking, business cards, local specialties and famous sights all grow from having a name." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is often confused with 「にんき」, popular. They overlap, but they're different. Famous means many people know it. Popular means many people like it. A shop can be famous for a scandal, but not popular. A small local cafe can be popular with students without being famous. In Japan, nearly every town is famous for something, and people love to ask: 「なにでゆうめいですか」, what is it famous for? Travel shows and souvenir shops are built around a town's 「めいぶつ」, its famous local dish or product. When you visit somewhere, bringing back those specialties for coworkers is expected. So: famous is known, 「にんき」 is liked, and every town has its claim to fame." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「有|ゆう」 is to have and 「名|めい」 is name, so having a name people know is famous; as a 「な」 adjective you say 「ゆうめいなみせ」 and famous for takes 「で」.",
      "「有|ゆう」 gives 「有料|ゆうりょう」, requiring a fee, while 「名|めい」 gives 「名刺|めいし」, business card, 「名物|めいぶつ」, local specialty, and 「名所|めいしょ」, famous sight.",
      "Famous means widely known while 「にんき」 means widely liked, and every Japanese town is famous for something, often its 「めいぶつ」."
    ],
    ja: "この{店|みせ}は[有名|ゆうめい]です。",
    en: "This shop is famous." },
  "ゆき|雪": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the top of this kanji. It's the rain kanji, 「雨|あめ」, flattened into a lid. That tells you it's something that falls from the sky. The bottom part looks like a sideways letter E. In older forms of the kanji, that bottom part was a hand holding a broom. A way to remember it: snow is the weather you have to sweep up afterwards. Here it's read with the native reading, 「ゆき」. Snow falls with the same verb as rain, 「降る|ふる」: 「雪が降る|ゆきがふる」, it snows. Put it next to the other rain-topped kanji you know. 「曇り|くもり」, cloudy, contains the cloud kanji with rain on top, and 「電気|でんき」, electricity, starts with a kanji that was first about lightning. All of them share that sky-water shape. A cultural note: northern Japan gets huge amounts of snow. Sapporo holds a famous snow festival every winter, with giant sculptures made of snow and ice. And kids everywhere build a snowman, which in Japanese is 「雪だるま|ゆきだるま」, a snow doll made of two balls instead of three. Recap: rain on top, a broom below, and you get snow." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Snow builds lots of seasonal words. 「はつゆき」 is the first snow of the year, and people often mention it as news. 「おおゆき」 is heavy snow, the kind that stops trains. 「ゆきがっせん」 is a snowball fight, literally a snow battle. 「ゆきぐに」 means snow country, the regions near the Sea of Japan that get buried every winter. And 「ゆきだるま」, the snowman, comes from a round doll called a 「だるま」, which has no arms or legs. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「せつ」, the kanji shows up in weather reports, as in 「積雪|せきせつ」, the depth of fallen snow. So: first snow, heavy snow, snowball fights and snow country, all built from 「ゆき」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There's another very common word with exactly the same sound, and you'll see it at every train station. It means bound for, coming from the verb 「いく」, to go. A train sign that says 「とうきょうゆき」 means bound for Tokyo, not Tokyo snow. Announcements will say the train is bound for a certain city, using that same sound. It's written with the kanji for go, not the kanji for snow, so the written signs are easy to tell apart. Listening is harder, but context is your friend. If it comes after a place name on a train platform, it's the direction. If it's falling from the sky, it's snow. So: on a platform it means bound for, and in winter it means snow." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Some parts of Japan get more snow than almost anywhere else people live. Cold winds from Siberia pick up moisture over the Sea of Japan, then dump it as snow when they hit the mountains. Towns in Aomori, Niigata, and Akita can have several meters of snow. A famous novel by Kawabata Yasunari, called 「雪国|ゆきぐに」, opens with a train coming out of a long tunnel into the snow country. In Akita, children build 「かまくら」, igloo-like snow huts, and sit inside drinking warm drinks during a winter festival. Meanwhile in the south, like Okinawa, snow almost never falls. So: Siberian winds, deep snow country, and cozy snow huts." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is rain flattened on top with a hand holding a broom below, snow you sweep up afterward, and it falls with 「降る|ふる」 like rain.",
      "It builds seasonal words like 「はつゆき」, first snow, 「ゆきがっせん」, snowball fight, and 「ゆきぐに」, snow country, and reads 「せつ」 in 「積雪|せきせつ」.",
      "At train stations the same sound means bound for, as in 「とうきょうゆき」, written with the kanji for go instead of snow.",
      "Siberian winds over the Sea of Japan bury northern towns in meters of snow, where Akita children build 「かまくら」, snow huts."
    ],
    ja: "{冬|ふゆ}は[雪|ゆき]がたくさんふります。",
    en: "It snows a lot in winter." },
  "てんきよほう|天気予報": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a four-kanji compound, but it's really two words you can handle. The first half, 「天気|てんき」, is weather, a word you already know. 「天|てん」 means heaven or sky, the same 「天|てん」 you see in 「天ぷら|てんぷら」. 「気|き」 means spirit or air, as in 「元気|げんき」 and 「電気|でんき」. So the weather is the air of the sky. The second half is 「予報|よほう」. 「予|よ」 means beforehand, in advance. You know it from 「予約|よやく」, a reservation, and 「予定|よてい」, a plan. Both are things set up in advance. 「報|ほう」 means a report or news. So a forecast is a report made in advance, and this is a report on the sky's weather before it happens. All four kanji use their Chinese-origin readings here, which is normal for long compounds like this. A cultural note: Japanese weather forecasts on TV are very detailed. Besides rain and temperature, they often tell you whether it's a good day to dry laundry, and in spring they even report how the cherry blossoms are coming along. Recap: sky weather plus an advance report gives you the weather forecast." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Most people in Japan check the forecast every morning, on the TV news before work or on a phone app. The verb is usually 「見る|みる」, to look at it, or 「聞く|きく」 if it comes over the radio. A pattern from Lesson 17 pairs perfectly with this word: 「天気予報によると、あしたは雨が降るそうです|てんきよほうによると、あしたはあめがふるそうです」. According to the forecast, it's supposed to rain tomorrow. The phrase 「によると」 names your source, and 「そうです」 at the end marks the news as something you heard, not something you know yourself. Forecasts come with their own little vocabulary. 「晴れ|はれ」 is sunny, 「曇り|くもり」 is cloudy, and 「雨|あめ」 is rain. When the forecast turns out right, people say it hit the mark, 「当たった|あたった」. When it's wrong, they say it missed, 「外れた|はずれた」. So a grumpy commuter caught in a shower might mutter that the forecast missed again. Wrap-up: check the forecast, then pass it on with 「によると」 and 「そうです」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan the forecast does much more than say sun or rain, because the seasons bring their own special reports. In early summer, the weather agency announces the start of the rainy season, 「梅雨入り|つゆいり」, region by region, moving up the country from Okinawa. Weeks later it announces the end, and everyone cheers because real summer has arrived. From late summer into autumn, forecasts track each 「台風|たいふう」, typhoon, with maps showing its predicted path, and schools and trains may shut down when a big one hits. In late winter and spring, many forecasts add a pollen report, because a huge number of people suffer from hay fever caused by cedar trees. You'll see people in masks on high pollen days. And in winter, forecasts on the Sea of Japan side warn of heavy snow. Wrap-up: in Japan, the forecast follows the seasons, from rainy season to typhoons to pollen." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the second half of the word. Picture an old pirate ship tossing on the waves. High up in the crow's nest, a scruffy pirate squints at the clouds. He's the ship's weatherman. Every morning he cups his hands and bellows his prediction down to the deck: storm by noon, yo ho! Clear skies tonight, yo ho! The crew trusts him completely, even though he's wrong half the time. That cry, yo ho, is your hook for 「予報|よほう」, a forecast. Just remember that the second beat is long, 「ほう」, so stretch it out like a pirate would. Now put the pirate together with the sky. Sky weather, 「天気|てんき」, plus the pirate's yo ho, gives you the whole word, 「天気予報|てんきよほう」. Wrap-up: the pirate's yo ho is the forecast, so listen to him before you leave port." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's two words joined: 「天気|てんき」, the air of the sky, plus 「予報|よほう」, where 「予|よ」 means in advance and 「報|ほう」 means report. So it's a report on the sky's weather made before it happens.",
      "Pass the forecast on with 「天気予報によると|てんきよほうによると」 to name your source and 「そうです」 to mark it as hearsay. When it's right it 「当たった|あたった」, and when it's wrong it 「外れた|はずれた」.",
      "Japanese forecasts follow the seasons, announcing the start of the rainy season 「梅雨入り|つゆいり」, tracking each 「台風|たいふう」, and adding pollen reports in spring for the many hay fever sufferers.",
      "Picture a pirate in the crow's nest bellowing his weather prediction with a yo ho, your hook for 「予報|よほう」. Add sky weather, 「天気|てんき」, and you get 「天気予報|てんきよほう」."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}の[天気予報|てんきよほう]を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I'll check tomorrow's weather forecast." },
  "きおん|気温": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「気|き」, means air or spirit. You know it from 「天気|てんき」, weather, and from 「元気|げんき」, energetic. In this word it's the air around you. The second, 「温|おん」, means warm. Notice the three little drops of water on its left side. Think of warm water. In fact, you know it from 「温泉|おんせん」, a hot spring, which is literally a warm spring of water. Put them together: the warmth of the air. That's the air temperature, the number you hear in the weather report. It's specifically about the air outside. For the temperature of water, or a person's fever, Japanese uses other words. You give the number with the counter 「度|ど」, degrees: 「気温は三十度です|きおんはさんじゅうどです」. Japan uses Celsius, so thirty degrees is a hot summer day, and zero is freezing. Recap: the air's warmth is the temperature, and it shares its warm kanji with hot springs." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The warm kanji, 「温|おん」, opens up a small family of temperature words. You already know 「温泉|おんせん」, a hot spring. When you take your temperature at the doctor, that's 「体温|たいおん」, body temperature, using the kanji for body. For water in a pool or the sea, it's 「水温|すいおん」, water temperature. So the pattern is simple: put the thing in front, and add 「温|おん」. The air version, 「気温|きおん」, is the one you'll hear daily. Now a near-synonym to keep separate. 「暖かい|あたたかい」 means warm, for the weather or a room. There's a second spelling of the same sound, 「温かい|あたたかい」, using our warm kanji, and it's used for warm things you touch or eat, like soup, or even a warm heart. The sound is identical, only the kanji changes. Wrap-up: put a word in front of the warm kanji and you name whose temperature it is." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's a mistake English speakers make all the time. In English we say the temperature is hot or cold. Japanese doesn't do that. The temperature itself is high or low: 「気温が高い|きおんがたかい」, and 「気温が低い|きおんがひくい」. You save 「暑い|あつい」 and 「寒い|さむい」 for how the day feels. In the same way, the temperature goes up, 「上がる|あがる」, and goes down, 「下がる|さがる」. In weather reports you'll hear 「最高気温|さいこうきおん」, the high for the day, and 「最低気温|さいていきおん」, the low. A tiny exchange you could have with a coworker: 「きょうは何度ですか|きょうはなんどですか」, what's the temperature today? 「三十五度だそうです|さんじゅうごどだそうです」, apparently thirty-five degrees. Then you both sigh and say 「暑いですね|あついですね」. Wrap-up: the temperature is high or low, and the day is hot or cold." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. Picture a giant old radiator in a freezing apartment. It has a big brass key sticking out of its side. You walk over, shivering, and turn the key. Click. The key is on. Slowly, the air in the room starts to warm up, and the thermometer on the wall climbs one degree, two degrees, three. Key, on. That's 「きおん」, the air temperature. And it even matches the kanji. The first kanji, 「気|き」, is the air, and the second, 「温|おん」, is the warmth, so turning the key on warms the air. Next time you hear the temperature on the weather report, picture that brass key clicking on and the room heating up. Wrap-up: turn the key on, and the air temperature rises." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「気|き」 is the air, as in 「天気|てんき」, and 「温|おん」 is warm, as in 「温泉|おんせん」, so it's the warmth of the air outside, counted in degrees with 「度|ど」.",
      "Put a word in front of the warm kanji 「温|おん」 to say whose temperature it is: 「体温|たいおん」 for the body, 「水温|すいおん」 for water, and 「気温|きおん」 for the air.",
      "The temperature itself is high or low, 「気温が高い|きおんがたかい」 or 「気温が低い|きおんがひくい」, while 「暑い|あつい」 and 「寒い|さむい」 describe how the day feels.",
      "Picture turning the brass key on an old radiator until the air warms up: key, on, 「きおん」. It even matches the kanji, air plus warmth."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}の[気温|きおん]は{三十度|さんじゅうど}です。",
    en: "Today's temperature is thirty degrees." },
  "なつ|夏": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word uses the native Japanese reading, 「なつ」. The kanji's shape doesn't have a simple, reliable story, so let's anchor it with a hook instead. A way to remember it: it sounds a bit like nuts, and Japanese summer is nuts. It's hot, it's very humid, and it's full of festivals and fireworks. It belongs to the set of four seasons you know: 「春|はる」, spring, 「夏|なつ」, summer, 「秋|あき」, fall, and 「冬|ふゆ」, winter. Learning them as a set helps, and they all come up together when people talk about 「季節|きせつ」, the seasons. A common phrase: 「夏休み|なつやすみ」 is summer vacation, using 「休み|やすみ」, a holiday, which you already know. A cultural note: summer is the season of fireworks shows by the river, people in light cotton robes, cold noodles, and shaved ice with syrup. In mid-August many people travel to their hometowns to honor their ancestors, so trains and highways get very crowded. Words you'll use with it: 「暑い|あつい」, hot, and 「気温|きおん」, temperature, which often climbs past thirty degrees. Recap: summer is the hot, crazy season, and it sounds like nuts." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the very bottom of this kanji. That little shape, which looks like a person walking with a slight limp, is a component that also appears in the kanji for winter, 「冬|ふゆ」, only there it sits on top. So summer and winter, the two extreme seasons, share a building block. That's a nice way to remember that they're a pair. This kanji has more than one reading. Here it uses the native reading, 「なつ」, which you'll use for everyday talk. In more formal compounds it's read 「か」. For example, 「初夏|しょか」 is early summer, a word you'll see in poetry and in letters. A rarer reading, 「げ」, shows up in 「夏至|げし」, the summer solstice, the longest day of the year. And 「真夏|まなつ」 means midsummer, the very height of the heat. Wrap-up: the native reading 「なつ」 is your everyday word, and the kanji shares a piece with winter." },
      { title: "Summer small talk", text: "In Japan, the weather is the safest small talk there is, and in summer it's practically required. When you meet a neighbor or a coworker, the opener is 「暑いですね|あついですね」, it's hot, isn't it? The answer is a sympathetic 「本当に暑いですね|ほんとうにあついですね」, really hot. Because Japanese summers are so humid, you'll also hear 「蒸し暑い|むしあつい」, muggy, literally steamy hot. A very common summer complaint is 「夏バテ|なつばて」. It means summer fatigue, that worn out feeling when the heat makes you tired and kills your appetite. People fight it by eating eel, which is believed to give you stamina. In the hottest part of summer, people also send short greeting postcards asking how friends are bearing the heat. Wrap-up: in summer, start every conversation with how hot it is, and expect a sigh in return." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese summer has some charming traditions beyond fireworks. On a special midsummer day called 「土用の丑の日|どようのうしのひ」, people line up to eat grilled eel, because it's said to give you strength for the hot weather. At the beach, kids play 「すいか割り|すいかわり」. One player is blindfolded, spun around, and tries to smash a watermelon with a stick while friends shout directions. Summer is also the season for ghost stories. The idea is that a good scare sends a chill down your spine, which helps you forget the heat. Haunted houses pop up at summer festivals for the same reason. And in the evening, you'll hear the tinkling of glass wind chimes hung by windows, a sound that people say makes them feel cooler. Wrap-up: eel for strength, watermelon for fun, and ghost stories to give you chills in the heat." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Remember 「なつ」 because it sounds like nuts, and Japanese summer is nuts: hot, humid and full of festivals. It's one of the four seasons, and 「夏休み|なつやすみ」 is summer vacation.",
      "The bottom of this kanji is a component that sits on top in 「冬|ふゆ」, so summer and winter share a piece. Everyday talk uses 「なつ」, while formal compounds use 「か」, as in 「初夏|しょか」.",
      "Summer small talk opens with 「暑いですね|あついですね」, and you'll hear 「蒸し暑い|むしあつい」 for muggy and 「夏バテ|なつばて」 for that tired, appetite-killing summer fatigue.",
      "Summer traditions include eating eel for strength, smashing watermelons blindfolded at the beach, and telling ghost stories, because a good scare sends a chill that helps you forget the heat."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[夏|なつ]はあついです。",
    en: "Summer in Japan is hot." },
  "ふゆ|冬": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word uses the native Japanese reading, 「ふゆ」. Look at the bottom of the kanji. Those two little dots come from the kanji element for ice, the same two strokes you see on the left of some cold-related kanji. So ice sits right at the bottom of winter. A way to remember the sound: picture blowing on your cold hands, going foo, and then thinking, you, it's freezing. Foo, you. It's one of the four seasons: 「春|はる」, 「夏|なつ」, 「秋|あき」 and 「冬|ふゆ」. The adjective that goes with it is 「寒い|さむい」, cold, for weather. And snow, 「雪|ゆき」, falls in winter with the verb 「降る|ふる」. A cultural note: many Japanese homes don't have central heating. Instead, families gather around a low table with a heater underneath and a blanket draped over it. Everyone tucks their legs under and eats mandarin oranges. It's one of the classic images of a Japanese winter. Winter also holds the biggest holiday of the year, the New Year, when families get together and visit shrines. Recap: ice at the bottom of the kanji, foo you for the sound, and that's winter." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The top of this kanji is a shape that also appears at the bottom of 「夏|なつ」, summer. So the hottest and coldest seasons share a building block, which makes them easy to remember as a pair. The native reading, 「ふゆ」, is what you use in everyday speech, as in 「冬休み|ふゆやすみ」, winter vacation. But the kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「とう」. You'll meet it in 「冬眠|とうみん」, hibernation, the long winter sleep of bears, and in 「冬至|とうじ」, the winter solstice, the shortest day of the year. Those icy little marks at the bottom are related to the left side of 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch. Kanji with that ice shape tend to be about cold or freezing. Wrap-up: the everyday reading is 「ふゆ」, the formal one is 「とう」, and it's the mirror of summer." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Two words for cold get mixed up all the time, and winter is when you need them most. 「寒い|さむい」 is for the air and the weather, how cold it feels around you. 「冷たい|つめたい」 is for things you touch, like cold water, a cold window, or icy hands. So on a winter morning you might say 「きょうは寒いですね|きょうはさむいですね」, but when you grab a metal railing, it's 「冷たい|つめたい」. Winter small talk often starts with 「寒くなりましたね|さむくなりましたね」, it's gotten cold, hasn't it? And people part with a caring line: 「風邪をひかないでください|かぜをひかないでください」, don't catch a cold. You'll see lots of people wearing masks in winter, partly to avoid catching colds and partly to be polite when they have one. And out come the 「手袋|てぶくろ」, gloves. Wrap-up: the weather is 「寒い|さむい」, the railing is 「冷たい|つめたい」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A few winter traditions are worth knowing. On the winter solstice, many families take a bath with whole citrus fruits called yuzu floating in the water. It smells wonderful, and people say it keeps you from catching colds. On New Year's Eve, families eat long buckwheat noodles called 「年越しそば|としこしそば」. The long noodles are a wish for a long life, and some say biting through them cuts off the troubles of the old year. Christmas in Japan is not a family religious holiday. It's more of a romantic evening for couples, and a surprising number of families eat fried chicken and a strawberry sponge cake with whipped cream. And then, in the coldest weeks, people head to hot springs and soak outside while snow falls around them. Wrap-up: citrus baths, long noodles and fried chicken make a Japanese winter." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The two dots at the bottom of the kanji come from the ice element, so ice sits at the bottom of winter. For the sound, blow on your cold hands, foo, and think, you, it's freezing.",
      "The top of this kanji appears at the bottom of 「夏|なつ」, making summer and winter a pair. Everyday speech uses 「ふゆ」, while 「とう」 appears in words like 「冬眠|とうみん」, hibernation.",
      "Use 「寒い|さむい」 for cold weather and air, but 「冷たい|つめたい」 for cold things you touch, like a metal railing on a winter morning.",
      "Japanese winter means citrus baths on the solstice, long 「年越しそば|としこしそば」 noodles for a long life on New Year's Eve, and fried chicken at Christmas."
    ],
    ja: "[冬|ふゆ]はさむいです。",
    en: "Winter is cold." },
  "けさ|今朝": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's look at the two kanji. 「今|いま」 means now, and 「朝|あさ」 means morning, as in 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast. Now plus morning: this morning. But here's the twist. When these two kanji come together, they don't use either of their usual readings. The whole word gets a special reading, 「けさ」. You can't build it from the pieces, so learn it as one sound. You've already met several words like this, and they're all time words with 「今|いま」 in them. 「今日|きょう」, today, is one. 「今年|ことし」, this year, is another. Both have special readings that don't come from the individual kanji. Compare them with 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, and 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, which use the regular reading 「こん」. So the rule of thumb is: today, this morning, and this year are special. Most of the others use 「こん」. A common mistake is to say 「こんあさ」. That's not a word. Like other time words, you don't need 「に」 after it: 「けさ七時に起きました|けさしちじにおきました」. Recap: now plus morning, but read as one special word, 「けさ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's build a little family of morning words around this one. The plain word is 「朝|あさ」, morning. Add 「ご飯|ごはん」 and you get 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast. Put 「毎|まい」 in front and you get 「毎朝|まいあさ」, every morning, where the morning kanji keeps its native reading. And there's 「朝寝坊する|あさねぼうする」, to oversleep, a very handy excuse. Notice that only this one word, 「今朝|けさ」, breaks the pattern with a special reading. Now for the evening side. 「今晩|こんばん」 is tonight, and you know it from the greeting 「こんばんは」. Another word for tonight is 「今夜|こんや」. A time-saving tip: 「けさ」 almost always talks about something that already happened this morning, so it usually goes with past tense verbs. Wrap-up: morning words share the kanji 「朝|あさ」, but this morning gets its own special sound." },
      { title: "Special readings", text: "This word belongs to a club of words with special readings, where the sound belongs to the whole word, not to each kanji. Japanese has a name for them, 「熟字訓|じゅくじくん」. You've met several already. 「今日|きょう」, today, and 「今年|ことし」, this year, are in it. So is 「明日|あした」, tomorrow. 「大人|おとな」, an adult, is big plus person, but read as one word. 「一人|ひとり」, one person, is another. And the pair 「上手|じょうず」 and 「下手|へた」, good at and bad at, also have readings you can't build from the parts. The good news is that these words tend to be common, so you hear them constantly and they stick. The trick is to stop trying to sound out the kanji and treat the pair of characters like a single picture with a single name. Wrap-up: when two kanji have a reading that doesn't fit, it's probably one of these special club members." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick built on the sound. Picture yourself this morning. The alarm didn't go off. You wake up, look at the clock, and it's already nine. You should panic, but instead you shrug, pull on yesterday's clothes, and start singing that old song, que sera, sera. Whatever will be, will be. You stroll to the station still humming it, ten minutes late and totally calm. Kesa, que sera. That's 「けさ」, this morning. Each time you need to talk about this morning, picture yourself strolling late and singing. It works especially well because the word usually goes with past events, and your calm stroll already happened. You might even use it in a real excuse: 「けさ、朝寝坊しました|けさ、あさねぼうしました」, I overslept this morning. Wrap-up: this morning, que sera, and you're late but happy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's now plus morning, but the pair takes a special whole-word reading, 「けさ」, like 「今日|きょう」 and 「今年|ことし」. Never say 「こんあさ」.",
      "Morning words share 「朝|あさ」, as in 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」 and 「毎朝|まいあさ」, but this morning breaks the pattern with its own sound and usually pairs with past tense verbs.",
      "It belongs to the club of special readings, 「熟字訓|じゅくじくん」, along with 「明日|あした」 and 「大人|おとな」, so treat the two kanji as one picture with one name.",
      "Picture yourself oversleeping this morning and strolling to the station singing que sera, sera. Kesa, que sera, that's 「けさ」, and it fits past events."
    ],
    ja: "[今朝|けさ]{七時|しちじ}に{起きました|おきました}。",
    en: "I got up at seven this morning." },
  "あさって|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, usually written in kana. It fits into a chain of days that's worth learning together. 「きのう」 is yesterday, 「きょう」 is today, 「あした」 is tomorrow, and 「あさって」 is the day after that. A way to remember it: 「あした」 and 「あさって」 both start with the same sound, so think of A for After. Tomorrow, and then After tomorrow. And if you want to go one more step, the day after the day after tomorrow is 「しあさって」, which is just 「あさって」 with an extra sound in front. Going backwards, the day before yesterday is 「おととい」. Like other time words relative to today, you don't put 「に」 after it. Say 「あさって行きます|あさっていきます」, with no 「に」. It's perfectly fine in polite speech, and it's very common when making plans: 「あさってはひまですか」, are you free the day after tomorrow? Recap: tomorrow is 「あした」, and one step further is 「あさって」, the A for After day." },
      { title: "Hidden kanji", text: "This word is usually written in kana, but it does have a kanji spelling you'll see on calendars and in more formal writing: 「明後日|あさって」. Look at the three pieces. The first is the bright kanji from 「明るい|あかるい」, which also starts 「明日|あした」, tomorrow. 「後|ご」 means after, as in 「午後|ごご」, afternoon. And 「日|ひ」 is day. So the kanji spell out the day after tomorrow piece by piece. Like 「明日|あした」, it's read as a whole word, with a special reading that doesn't come from the parts. In very formal speech, such as a business announcement, you may hear the same kanji read 「明後日|みょうごにち」. Tomorrow has the same kind of formality ladder: 「あした」 is everyday, 「明日|あす」 is a bit more formal, and 「明日|みょうにち」 is very formal. Wrap-up: the kanji spelling means bright, after, day, but everyday Japanese just says 「あさって」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word lives in plans and deadlines. A typical exchange at school: 「レポートはいつまでですか」, when is the report due? 「あさってまでです」, until the day after tomorrow. If you want to say by the day after tomorrow, use 「までに」: 「あさってまでに出してください|あさってまでにだしてください」, please hand it in by then. Since it's in the future, use it with the non-past form of the verb, like 「行きます|いきます」, not the past form. Between friends, you'll hear it when juggling schedules: 「あしたはだめだけど、あさってならいいよ」, tomorrow's no good, but the day after is fine. A fun bit of trivia: the word for three days from now is 「しあさって」, but in some regions of Japan, people use a different word for that day, and even swap the order of the days beyond it. So locals sometimes double check. Wrap-up: the day after tomorrow is the go-to word for plans and deadlines." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Learn it in the chain 「きのう」, 「きょう」, 「あした」, 「あさって」, thinking of A for After tomorrow. Like other relative time words, it takes no 「に」.",
      "Its kanji spelling, 「明後日|あさって」, spells out bright, after, day, read as one special word, though everyday Japanese just writes it in kana.",
      "It's the go-to word for plans and deadlines, as in 「あさってまでに出してください|あさってまでにだしてください」, please hand it in by the day after tomorrow, used with non-past verbs."
    ],
    ja: "[あさって]テストがあります。",
    en: "I have a test the day after tomorrow." },
  "まいしゅう|毎週": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「毎|まい」, means every. You know it from 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, and 「毎晩|まいばん」, every night. The second, 「週|しゅう」, means week. Look at its shape. The bottom left has the movement radical, the one that looks like a winding road. Inside sits a part meaning around, or a full circle. So a week is time going around one full cycle. You know this kanji from a whole family: 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week, 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week, and 「週末|しゅうまつ」, the weekend, literally the end of the week. Put them together and 「毎週|まいしゅう」 is every week. Grammar note: like other time words built with 「毎|まい」, you don't use 「に」 after it. Just say 「毎週テニスをします|まいしゅうテニスをします」. You can do the same with month and year too, once you meet those words: every plus month, every plus year. Recap: every plus week, the same 「毎|まい」 as every day and the same 「週|しゅう」 as this week." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The every kanji, 「毎|まい」, gives you a complete set of routine words. 「毎朝|まいあさ」 is every morning, 「毎日|まいにち」 every day, 「毎晩|まいばん」 every night, and 「毎週|まいしゅう」 every week. Going bigger, every month is usually 「毎月|まいつき」, and every year is 「毎年|まいとし」, though 「毎年|まいねん」 is also heard. Notice that month and year switch to native readings after 「毎|まい」 in their most common forms. Now, a close cousin that works differently. If you want to say how many times per week, use 「週に|しゅうに」 plus a count with 「回|かい」: 「週に二回|しゅうににかい」, twice a week. And for a length of time, add 「間|かん」: 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」 is one week long. So every week, twice a week, and for a week are three different tools. Wrap-up: 「毎|まい」 makes the routine, 「週に|しゅうに」 counts it, and 「週間|しゅうかん」 measures it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is perfect for talking about habits, and with habits you use the plain present form of the verb, the 「ます」 form, not a special ongoing form. 「毎週映画を見ます|まいしゅうえいがをみます」, I watch a movie every week. You can make it more precise by adding a day of the week. 「毎週月曜日|まいしゅうげつようび」 is every Monday. Here's the subtle point. 「毎週|まいしゅう」 alone never takes 「に」, but once you add the day, the day can take 「に」: 「毎週月曜日にジムに行きます|まいしゅうげつようびにジムにいきます」. Lots of things in Japanese life run on a weekly cycle. Famous manga magazines come out every week. And garbage collection is set by the day: burnable trash goes out on certain days every week, and if you miss it, you wait. Wrap-up: use 「毎週|まいしゅう」 with plain present verbs for habits, and add a day for precision." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. Picture your neighbor's enormous, goofy dog. Every single week, on the same day, it sneaks into your entryway, where everyone in Japan leaves their shoes, and it steals one of yours. Monday morning, you reach for your sneakers, and there's only one. You run outside and shout, my shoe! My shoe! Down the street, the dog is trotting away with your shoe in its mouth, tail wagging. This happens every week like clockwork. The neighbors set their watches by your yelling. My shoe, every week. That's 「まいしゅう」. And the kanji fit the scene. 「毎|まい」 is every, and 「週|しゅう」 is week. So whenever you need to say every week, hear yourself yelling about that shoe. Wrap-up: my shoe, stolen every single week." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「毎|まい」 means every, as in 「毎日|まいにち」, and 「週|しゅう」 means week, as in 「今週|こんしゅう」, so it's every week, and it takes no 「に」.",
      "「毎|まい」 makes the routine, 「週に|しゅうに」 plus a count like 「週に二回|しゅうににかい」 says how often, and 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」 measures a length of a week.",
      "Use it with plain present verbs for habits, and add a day for precision: 「毎週|まいしゅう」 alone takes no 「に」, but 「毎週月曜日に|まいしゅうげつようびに」 does.",
      "Picture a goofy dog stealing one of your shoes every single week while you shout, my shoe! My shoe, every week, is 「まいしゅう」."
    ],
    ja: "[毎週|まいしゅう]テニスをします。",
    en: "I play tennis every week." },
  "こんげつ|今月": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「今|こん」, means now or this. On its own it's 「いま」, now, but in compounds it often becomes 「こん」, like in 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, and 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester. The second, 「月|げつ」, means moon or month. Its native reading is 「つき」, the moon. It also shows up as 「げつ」 in 「月曜日|げつようび」, Monday, the moon day. A month is one cycle of the moon, which is why one kanji covers both. So 「今月|こんげつ」 is this month. Now swap the first kanji and you get a whole set. 「来月|らいげつ」 is next month, using 「来|らい」, coming. It works exactly like 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week. Watch out for one trap: the names of months, like January and February, use a different reading, 「がつ」. So it's 「一月|いちがつ」, January, but 「今月|こんげつ」, this month. Recap: this plus moon equals this month, with the moon read 「げつ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Months come in a neat little timeline. Last month is 「先月|せんげつ」, using 「先|せん」, the same kanji as in 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week, and in 「先生|せんせい」, a teacher, someone who went before you. This month is 「今月|こんげつ」. Next month is 「来月|らいげつ」. All three use the reading 「げつ」 for month, and they match the week words one for one: last week, this week, next week. Learn them as a grid and you get six words for the price of three. For every month, the usual form is 「毎月|まいつき」, which switches to the native reading of the moon kanji. And when you count a length of months, you use 「か月|かげつ」, as in 「三か月|さんかげつ」, three months. Wrap-up: last, this and next work the same for weeks and months, so learn them together." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up whenever people talk about money, schedules and deadlines. A very relatable complaint is 「今月はお金がありません|こんげつはおかねがありません」, I'm broke this month. Rent and bills are due by the end of the month, which is 「今月の終わり|こんげつのおわり」, or more crisply, 「今月末|こんげつまつ」. You know 「末|まつ」 already from 「週末|しゅうまつ」, the weekend, the end of the week. Another handy expression is 「今月中に|こんげつちゅうに」, within this month, sometime before it ends. It works just like 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」, by the end of today. At work, you might hear a manager say a report is due within this month. And with a specific date, add 「の」 and the day: 「今月の十日|こんげつのとおか」, the tenth of this month. Wrap-up: this month comes up with rent, deadlines and the end of the month." },
      { title: "Three moon readings", text: "The moon kanji is a bit of a shape shifter, and this word is a good place to sort it out. Read 「つき」, it's the moon in the sky, and it's also the native reading in 「毎月|まいつき」, every month. Read 「げつ」, it's a month counted relative to now, as in 「今月|こんげつ」 and 「来月|らいげつ」. It's also the reading in 「月曜日|げつようび」, Monday. Read 「がつ」, it names a month of the calendar, like 「四月|しがつ」, April. That one also appears in 「お正月|おしょうがつ」, the New Year holiday. Finally, when you count months as a length of time, you get 「か月|かげつ」, so two months is 「二か月|にかげつ」. A quick self-test: this month uses 「げつ」, April uses 「がつ」, and the moon itself is 「つき」. Wrap-up: moon, relative month, calendar month, each with its own reading." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「今|こん」 means this and 「月|げつ」 means moon or month, since a month is one moon cycle. Watch out: calendar months use 「がつ」, as in 「一月|いちがつ」.",
      "Last, this and next month, 「先月|せんげつ」, 「今月|こんげつ」 and 「来月|らいげつ」, match the week words one for one, so learn them as a grid of six.",
      "It comes up with money and deadlines, as in 「今月はお金がありません|こんげつはおかねがありません」, plus 「今月末|こんげつまつ」 for the end of the month and 「今月中に|こんげつちゅうに」 for within this month.",
      "The moon kanji has three readings: 「つき」 for the moon itself, 「げつ」 for months relative to now like 「今月|こんげつ」, and 「がつ」 for calendar months like 「四月|しがつ」."
    ],
    ja: "[今月|こんげつ]はいそがしいです。",
    en: "I'm busy this month." },
  "らいげつ|来月": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「来|らい」, means coming. It's the kanji from the verb 「来る|くる」, to come, which uses the native reading. In time words it becomes 「らい」 and points to the one that's coming next. The second, 「月|げつ」, means moon or month. A month is one turn of the moon. You know it from 「月曜日|げつようび」, Monday, and from 「今月|こんげつ」, this month. So 「来月|らいげつ」 is the coming month: next month. Here's the family that makes it easy. 「来週|らいしゅう」 is next week, 「来年|らいねん」 is next year, and 「来学期|らいがっき」 is next semester. They all use 「来|らい」 for next. And there's 「将来|しょうらい」, the future, where 「来|らい」 means what's coming. Like other time words, don't put 「に」 after it. Just say 「来月行きます|らいげついきます」. Recap: the coming moon is next month, part of the same family as next week and next year." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji is a fun one because a single shape takes several different native readings depending on how the verb 「来る|くる」 is conjugated. In the dictionary form it's 「来る|くる」. In the polite form it becomes 「来ます|きます」. In the negative it's 「来ない|こない」. Same kanji, three different sounds, and you just have to read the kana that follows to know which one. Then in time words like this one it switches to the Chinese-origin reading, 「らい」. As for the shape, many scholars trace it back to a drawing of a stalk of grain, like wheat. The meaning of coming was borrowed onto it later, because the two words sounded alike in old Chinese. Traditional Chinese still writes it with a slightly fancier version of that plant. Wrap-up: one kanji, read 「く」, 「き」 or 「こ」 as a verb, and 「らい」 when it means next." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Next month is the word for future plans, and a few patterns make it easy to use. On its own, it doesn't take 「に」, but if you add a date, the date does: 「来月の五日に行きます|らいげつのいつかにいきます」, I'll go on the fifth of next month. Another favorite is 「来月から|らいげつから」, starting next month: 「来月から新しい仕事を始めます|らいげつからあたらしいしごとをはじめます」, I start a new job next month. And here's a bonus. To go one step further, add 「再|さ」 in front. 「再来月|さらいげつ」 is the month after next. The same trick gives you 「再来週|さらいしゅう」, the week after next, and 「再来年|さらいねん」, the year after next. That one prefix doubles your time vocabulary. Wrap-up: next month alone takes no particle, but a date inside next month does." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. Picture a friend who owes you money. Every time you ask for it back, he puts on his most sincere face and says, next month. I'll get it to you next month, I promise. You know it's a lie. He knows it's a lie. Lie, get. That's 「らいげつ」. Now picture the scene repeating, month after month, his promise never changing. Next month, next month. Each time he says it, a little sign pops up over his head that reads lie, with a cartoon bag of money he'll get you someday. If you want, make it even funnier by imagining him using the word himself: 「来月返します|らいげつかえします」, I'll pay you back next month. Wrap-up: lie, get, the friend who'll always pay you back next month." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「来|らい」 means coming and 「月|げつ」 means month, so it's the coming month, part of the same family as 「来週|らいしゅう」 and 「来年|らいねん」.",
      "As the verb 「来る|くる」, this kanji is read 「く」, 「き」 or 「こ」 depending on the ending, but in time words it switches to 「らい」, meaning next.",
      "Next month alone takes no particle, but a date inside it does, as in 「来月の五日に|らいげつのいつかに」, and adding 「再|さ」 gives 「再来月|さらいげつ」, the month after next.",
      "Picture a friend who owes you money and always promises to pay you back next month, which is a lie, so lie, get, 「らいげつ」."
    ],
    ja: "[来月|らいげつ]{日本|にほん}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'm going to Japan next month." },
  "カメラ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a loanword from English, camera. The sound fit into Japanese very easily: three beats, each one a consonant plus a vowel. The English r becomes the Japanese 「ラ」 sound, which sits somewhere between an English r and l. The English word itself has a fun history. It comes from Latin, where camera simply meant a room or chamber. Early cameras were literally dark rooms with a tiny hole that let in light and projected an image on the wall. Later the room shrank into a box, and the name stuck. Japan has a special connection to cameras. Many of the world's best-known camera makers are Japanese, and taking photos everywhere you go is a big part of travel culture there. You'll also hear 「デジカメ」, a shortened form of digital camera. Japanese loves chopping long loanwords into short, snappy ones, just like 「カラオケ」 uses a shortened orchestra. To take a photo, people say 「しゃしんをとる」. Recap: from English camera, which goes back to the Latin word for a room." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "With a camera, the verb you need is 「撮る|とる」, to take a photo. It sounds the same as 「取る|とる」, to take or grab, but it's written with a different kanji. A very useful request when traveling is 「写真を撮ってもいいですか|しゃしんをとってもいいですか」, may I take a photo? And to ask a stranger to snap one of you, try 「写真を撮ってくれませんか|しゃしんをとってくれませんか」, could you take our picture? Some places don't allow photos, especially inside temples, museums and some shrines, so look for a sign with a crossed out camera. Another surprise for visitors: phones sold in Japan make a loud shutter sound that you can't switch off. It's an industry custom meant to stop people from secretly taking photos. Wrap-up: ask politely with 「撮ってもいいですか|とってもいいですか」, and expect the click." },
      { title: "Photo words", text: "A little family of photo words grows around this loanword. 「カメラマン」 is a photographer, not only someone who films video. A video camera is 「ビデオカメラ」, and as you know, a digital camera gets shortened to 「デジカメ」. These days most photos are taken on a 「スマホ」, a smartphone, another chopped loanword. When it's time for a group shot, the person holding the camera says 「はい、チーズ」, just like say cheese. And as the shutter clicks, lots of people flash a V sign with two fingers. In Japanese that's called 「ピース」, from the English word peace. The native word for a photo is 「写真|しゃしん」. Its first kanji means to copy and the second means truth, so a photo is a copy of the truth. Wrap-up: 「カメラ」 brings along a whole set of photo words, from 「デジカメ」 to 「ピース」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English camera, which goes back to the Latin word for a room, since early cameras were literally dark rooms with a tiny hole letting in light.",
      "The verb is 「撮る|とる」, so ask 「写真を撮ってもいいですか|しゃしんをとってもいいですか」 before snapping, and expect Japanese phones to make a shutter sound you can't turn off.",
      "A whole set of photo words comes with it, like 「デジカメ」 for a digital camera, 「はい、チーズ」 for say cheese, and 「ピース」 for the V sign."
    ],
    ja: "{新しい|あたらしい}[カメラ]をかいました。",
    en: "I bought a new camera." },
  "ところ|所": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, and the kanji 「所|ところ」 uses its native reading here. Its left side looks like the kanji for a door, and its right side is an old element for an axe. The kanji's origin is debated, so take this as a hook rather than history. A way to remember it: a door marks a spot, a place where something is. It's a very general word. It can be a physical place, like 「きれいなところ」, a pretty place, or 「しずかなところ」, a quiet place. A great phrase to learn is 「どんなところですか」, what kind of place is it? You can ask it about someone's hometown and get them talking. You'll often see it written in kana only, especially when it's used in a more abstract way. Both spellings are correct. The kanji shows up with a Chinese-origin reading in other words you'll meet later, like the words for an address and a kitchen, so keep an eye out for it. A common mistake is to think it only means a big place like a city. It can also be a tiny spot, like the place on a map or a spot by the window. Recap: a general, everyday word for a place or spot, and you'll often see it in kana." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「しょ」, which turns up in lots of words you'll meet. 「住所|じゅうしょ」 is an address, the place where you live. 「場所|ばしょ」 is a location, and it's a bit more concrete than 「ところ」, like the location of a meeting. 「近所|きんじょ」 is your neighborhood, the nearby places. Notice the sound softens to 「じょ」 there. And here's a great example of how Japanese sounds change. The kitchen is 「台所|だいどころ」. That's the native reading, 「ところ」, but when it comes second in the word, the first sound softens and becomes 「どころ」. This softening happens a lot in Japanese compounds. It's the same reason 「すし」 becomes 「ずし」 in some sushi names. Wrap-up: alone it's 「ところ」, and inside compounds it becomes 「しょ」, 「じょ」 or 「どころ」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's a mistake almost every English speaker makes. In English you can say I'm going to the teacher. In Japanese you can't use 「に」 directly after a person when you mean going to them. A person isn't a place. So you add 「のところ」, the person's place: 「先生のところに行きます|せんせいのところにいきます」, I'll go to the teacher. The same works for staying at someone's home. 「友だちのところに泊まります|ともだちのところにとまります」, I'll stay at my friend's place. The word can also refer to a point in time rather than space. 「今、出かけるところです|いま、でかけるところです」 means I'm just about to go out. With the ongoing form, 「食べているところです|たべているところです」, I'm in the middle of eating. Wrap-up: to go to a person, add 「のところ」, and later you'll use it for moments in time too." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This little word shows up in some very common set phrases. The best known is 「ところで」, which means by the way. It's literally at this point, and people use it to switch topics: 「ところで、あしたはひまですか」, by the way, are you free tomorrow? When someone arrives just when you need them, you say 「いいところに来ましたね|いいところにきましたね」, you came at a good time. To point out someone's good qualities, people talk about their 「いいところ」, their good points. To be modest when inviting someone home, you might hear 「何もないところですが|なにもないところですが」, it's a place with nothing special, but please come in. That's humble talk, not a real complaint about the house. Wrap-up: from by the way to a good point, this word fits into many everyday phrases." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's a general everyday word for a place or spot, big or small, often written in kana, and 「どんなところですか」 is a great way to ask what kind of place it is.",
      "Alone it's 「ところ」, but in compounds the kanji becomes 「しょ」 in 「住所|じゅうしょ」, 「じょ」 in 「近所|きんじょ」, and 「どころ」 in 「台所|だいどころ」.",
      "A person isn't a place, so to go to someone add 「のところ」, as in 「先生のところに行きます|せんせいのところにいきます」, I'll go to the teacher.",
      "It shows up in set phrases like 「ところで」, by the way, 「いいところ」, someone's good points, and the humble 「何もないところですが|なにもないところですが」."
    ],
    ja: "きれいな[所|ところ]ですね。",
    en: "It's a beautiful place, isn't it?" },
  "トマト|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This loanword came into Japanese from English tomato. The sounds fit Japanese perfectly, because each syllable is already a simple consonant plus a vowel. So Japanese keeps all three beats, each one short and even. English speakers tend to stress the middle beat. Japanese gives each beat the same length, so try saying it smooth and flat. The word has a long journey behind it. English got it from Spanish, and Spanish got it from the Aztec language of Mexico, Nahuatl, where the plant was first grown. Spanish explorers carried the plant, and the word, to Europe. Many vegetables and fruits in Japanese come from English this way, written in katakana. Think of 「バナナ」 or 「レタス」. In Japan you'll often see it in salads, sandwiches and bento lunches. In a recipe, you might cut it with 「切る|きる」: 「トマトを切ります|トマトをきります」. Recap: from English tomato, which came from Spanish and the Aztec language, three even beats in Japanese." },
      { title: "Fun with sound", text: "Here's a fun fact you can share at parties. Written in kana, this word is a palindrome. Read it backwards and it's exactly the same: 「ト」, 「マ」, 「ト」. Japanese kids love word games like this, and the most famous Japanese palindrome is 「しんぶんし」, newspaper paper. Another classic is 「たけやぶやけた」, the bamboo grove burned down. Palindromes in Japanese work beat by beat, so they're easier to make than in English, where you need letters to line up. A memory trick: picture a tomato sliced exactly in half. Both halves look the same, just like the word looks the same from both ends. When you say it, keep each beat even and flat, with no heavy stress in the middle. Wrap-up: the same forwards and backwards, just like a tomato sliced in half." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Tomatoes reached Japan in the Edo period, centuries ago, but at first they were grown mainly to look at, as a decorative plant. Eating them didn't really catch on until much later, as Western food spread in modern times. Today Japan loves them, and the Japanese twist is sweetness. Supermarkets sell small, very sweet tomatoes that are grown with very little water, and some are priced like luxury fruit. You'll also see 「ミニトマト」, cherry tomatoes, a made in Japan loanword combining mini and tomato. They're a favorite in boxed lunches, because the bright red adds color next to green vegetables and yellow egg. Japanese home cooks care a lot about color in a lunch box. And in summer, a whole chilled tomato with a little salt is a classic snack. Wrap-up: once a decoration, now a sweet treat and the red spot in every lunch box." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It came from English tomato, which came from Spanish and the Aztec language Nahuatl, and Japanese keeps three even beats with no stress in the middle.",
      "Written in kana it's a palindrome, the same forwards and backwards, just like a tomato sliced exactly in half.",
      "Tomatoes reached Japan as a decorative plant in the Edo period, and today they're prized for sweetness and as the red spot in lunch boxes, like 「ミニトマト」."
    ],
    ja: "[トマト]をきってください。",
    en: "Please cut the tomato." },
  "はし|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word. It does have a kanji, 「箸|はし」, with the bamboo radical on top, because chopsticks were made of bamboo. But it's very often written in kana. Here's the tricky part. The sound 「はし」 also means bridge, written 「橋|はし」, and edge, written 「端|はし」. Japanese tells them apart with pitch. In standard Tokyo speech, chopsticks start high and drop. Bridge starts low and rises. So listen for the melody. A way to remember it: picture your chopsticks laid across your bowl like a little bridge. You'll often hear the polite form 「おはし」 at the table. Some etiquette that every visitor should know. Don't stick your chopsticks straight up in a bowl of rice. That's done at funerals, as an offering to the dead. Also, don't pass food from your chopsticks to someone else's. That too recalls a funeral custom. When you're done, lay them down neatly across your bowl or on the chopstick rest. Japanese chopsticks are usually shorter and more pointed than Chinese ones, which makes them good for picking bones out of fish. Recap: same sound as bridge, a different melody, and some important table manners." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a Japanese table, chopsticks come with their own little vocabulary. The disposable wooden ones you get at restaurants and convenience stores are 「割りばし|わりばし」, from the verb 「割る|わる」, to split, because you snap them apart before eating. The small stand you rest them on is a 「はし置き|はしおき」, literally a chopstick place. Now a trap every visitor falls into. As soon as you pick up chopsticks in front of Japanese people, someone will probably say 「おはしが上手ですね|おはしがじょうずですね」, you're good with chopsticks! It's meant kindly, but it can feel funny if you've used them all your life. The graceful response is modest: 「いいえ、まだまだです」, oh, not really. Laughing it off works too. And when you want a pair, ask for 「おはしをください」. Wrap-up: snap your disposable pair, rest them neatly, and take the compliment modestly." },
      { title: "Etiquette deep dive", text: "Beyond the two big funeral taboos, Japanese table manners have a whole list of small chopstick don'ts, and many of them even have names. Hovering your chopsticks over the dishes while you decide what to take is called 「迷いばし|まよいばし」, wandering chopsticks, and it looks indecisive. Stabbing a piece of food instead of picking it up is frowned on. So is pulling a dish toward you with your chopsticks, or licking them. Pointing at someone with your chopsticks, the way you might gesture with a fork, is considered rude. When you share from a big plate, some people flip their chopsticks around and use the clean upper ends, or use separate serving chopsticks if there are any. None of this will get you thrown out of a restaurant, and friends will forgive a visitor. But knowing it shows real respect. Wrap-up: pick up, don't stab, don't hover, and never point." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The same sound means bridge, 「橋|はし」, but chopsticks start high and drop while bridge starts low and rises. Never stick chopsticks upright in rice, since that's a funeral custom.",
      "Disposable chopsticks are 「割りばし|わりばし」, from 「割る|わる」, to split, and when someone says 「おはしが上手ですね|おはしがじょうずですね」, answer modestly with 「いいえ、まだまだです」.",
      "Beyond the funeral taboos, don't hover over dishes, called 「迷いばし|まよいばし」, don't stab food, and never point at people with your chopsticks."
    ],
    ja: "[はし]でごはんを{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat rice with chopsticks." },
  "パーティー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English party. Japanese can't end a syllable on an r sound, so the r in party disappears. The vowel before it gets stretched instead, which is what the long dash after 「パ」 does. The tricky bit is the tea sound in the middle. Traditional Japanese only has the sound chi there, so to write a real tea sound, Japanese uses 「テ」 with a small 「ィ」 after it. That small 「ィ」 is a modern tool for sounds that native Japanese words don't have. Then a second long dash stretches the final vowel. You may also see it spelled with no final dash. Both spellings are used and both are correct. You use it with 「する」 or with 「ある」: 「パーティーがあります」, there's a party. A cultural note: in Japan, parties with coworkers or classmates are often held at a pub-style restaurant, where everyone starts with a toast of 「乾杯|かんぱい」. There are welcome parties in spring and year-end parties in December. Recap: English party, with the r gone, the vowels stretched, and a small 「ィ」 for the tea sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This loanword is used for Western-style events like a birthday party, 「誕生日パーティー|たんじょうびパーティー」, or a Christmas party. You'll also hear 「ホームパーティー」, a party at someone's home, a combination made in Japan. But many of the most important Japanese get-togethers use native words ending in 「会|かい」, gathering. 「飲み会|のみかい」 is a drinking party with friends or coworkers, literally a drinking gathering. 「歓迎会|かんげいかい」 is a welcome party for a new member. 「送別会|そうべつかい」 is a farewell party for someone leaving. And 「忘年会|ぼうねんかい」 is the year-end party, literally a forget-the-year gathering, where you put the troubles of the year behind you. So if your office invites you to one of these, it's a party even without the loanword. Wrap-up: the loanword for birthdays and Christmas, and 「会|かい」 words for office and school gatherings." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how a party invitation might go, using patterns you know. A friend asks: 「土曜日にパーティーをするんですが、来ませんか|どようびにパーティーをするんですが、きませんか」, I'm having a party on Saturday, won't you come? Notice that you go to a party with 「に」: 「パーティーに行きます|パーティーにいきます」. If you accept, you might ask 「何か持っていきましょうか|なにかもっていきましょうか」, shall I bring something? If you can't make it, Japanese people rarely say no directly. Instead they trail off: 「土曜日はちょっと|どようびはちょっと」, Saturday is a little, and let the silence finish the sentence. Your friend will understand completely. After the party, it's polite to thank the host the next time you meet, with 「この間はありがとうございました|このあいだはありがとうございました」. Wrap-up: get invited with 「ませんか」, decline with a gentle 「ちょっと」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese parties, especially with coworkers, have a structure worth knowing. Someone is the organizer, the 「幹事|かんじ」, who books the restaurant and collects the money. Nobody drinks until everyone has a glass and the toast is made. Then there's the custom of pouring for others. You fill your neighbor's glass, and they fill yours, rather than serving yourself. The bill is often split evenly, called 「割り勘|わりかん」, though a boss may chip in more. When the first party ends, a smaller group often heads somewhere else for round two, the 「二次会|にじかい」, maybe a karaoke box. To finish the evening, people don't say the party is over, since that sounds too final. They use a nicer phrase, 「お開き|おひらき」, literally opening up, to mean it's time to wrap up. Wrap-up: wait for the toast, pour for others, and expect a round two." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English party, the r drops and the vowel stretches with a dash, while 「テ」 plus a small 「ィ」 writes the tea sound that native Japanese lacks.",
      "The loanword covers Western events like birthdays and Christmas, but office and school gatherings use 「会|かい」 words, like 「飲み会|のみかい」 and 「忘年会|ぼうねんかい」.",
      "Get invited with 「来ませんか|きませんか」, go to a party with 「に」, and decline gently with a trailing 「土曜日はちょっと|どようびはちょっと」 instead of a direct no.",
      "At Japanese parties you wait for the toast, pour for others, often split the bill with 「割り勘|わりかん」, and expect a round two, the 「二次会|にじかい」."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}に[パーティー]があります。",
    en: "There's a party on the weekend." },
  "バーベキュー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English barbecue. Watch how the sounds get adapted. The r at the end of the first syllable disappears, and the vowel stretches instead, shown by the dash after 「バ」. The b sounds stay as the Japanese b sound, and each consonant gets a vowel after it, so 「ベ」 gets its own beat. Then the last part, cue, uses 「キ」 plus a small 「ュ」, which squeezes the sound into one beat, and a final dash stretches it. The English word itself comes from Spanish, which borrowed it from a native Caribbean language. It was originally a wooden frame for cooking or drying meat over a fire. You do one with 「する」: 「バーベキューをする」. A cultural note: barbecue in Japan is a popular group outing, especially by a river or at a park in summer. Groups of friends and coworkers bring grills, meat and vegetables, and cook together. It's very social, so you'll often hear it with 「みんなで」, everyone together. Japanese barbecue usually means grilling thin slices of meat and vegetables, not slow-smoked meat. Recap: English barbecue, with the r dropped, long vowels stretched, and a small 「ュ」 for the cue sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This loanword sits next to some native Japanese grilling words, and it helps to know the differences. The verb for grilling or roasting is 「焼く|やく」. From it come lots of food names. 「焼き鳥|やきとり」 is chicken grilled on skewers, a classic at pubs and festivals. 「焼き肉|やきにく」 is grilled meat, usually at a restaurant where each table has its own grill and you cook bite-sized pieces yourself. It comes from Korean barbecue. So what's the difference? 「焼き肉|やきにく」 usually means eating out, indoors, at a restaurant. 「バーベキュー」 means cooking outdoors, at a park, a campsite or by a river, with your own grill and friends. And 「焼きそば|やきそば」, fried noodles, is a favorite side dish at a barbecue. Wrap-up: 「焼く|やく」 is to grill, 「焼き肉|やきにく」 is indoors, and 「バーベキュー」 is outdoors." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Planning a barbecue in Japan comes with some practical vocabulary. To invite people: 「今度の日曜日、バーベキューをしませんか|こんどのにちようび、バーベキューをしませんか」, how about a barbecue this Sunday? Then come the questions of who brings what. 「私は肉を持っていきます|わたしはにくをもっていきます」, I'll bring the meat. Japanese barbecues are usually team efforts, with everyone sharing the cooking and the cleanup. Something to know: you can't barbecue just anywhere. Many parks ban grills, and others only allow them in a marked area, sometimes with a reservation. Garbage is a big deal too. Signs often say take your trash home, since there may be no bins. Some places even rent out the whole setup, so you can show up empty-handed. That service is advertised as 「手ぶら|てぶら」, empty hands. Wrap-up: invite with 「ませんか」, split the shopping, and take your trash home." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English barbecue, the r drops and the vowel stretches, and 「キ」 plus a small 「ュ」 squeezes the cue sound into one beat, ending with a long dash.",
      "It's built around 「焼く|やく」, to grill: 「焼き肉|やきにく」 usually means eating grilled meat indoors at a restaurant, while 「バーベキュー」 means cooking outdoors with friends.",
      "Invite with 「バーベキューをしませんか」, split who brings what, and remember many parks ban grills and expect you to take your trash home."
    ],
    ja: "みんなで[バーベキュー]をしましょう。",
    en: "Let's all have a barbecue together." },
  "ホームステイ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is made from two English words, home and stay. Look at how each half was squeezed into Japanese. Home becomes 「ホーム」. The long o vowel gets a dash, and the m gets its own beat with an extra vowel added after it, 「ム」. Stay becomes 「ステイ」. Japanese can't start with two consonants together like s and t, so it adds a little vowel after the s, giving 「ス」. Then the ending is written with 「テイ」. You've got a sibling in 「ホームシック」, homesick, which uses the same 「ホーム」. And for the people you stay with, you've got 「ホストファミリー」, your host family. You do a homestay with 「する」: 「ホームステイをする」. A cultural note: many students who study in Japan do a homestay. It's a great way to see daily life, eat home cooking, and practice Japanese every day. Common tips include taking off your shoes at the door, offering to help, and bringing a small gift from home when you arrive. Recap: home plus stay, each word reshaped to fit Japanese sounds." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A homestay comes with a set of daily phrases that make you sound like part of the family. When you first arrive, bow and say 「お世話になります|おせわになります」. It means something like, thank you for taking care of me, and it's exactly what Japanese guests say. Each morning when you leave, say 「いってきます」, I'm off, and the family answers 「いってらっしゃい」, see you later. When you come home, it's 「ただいま」, I'm home, and they reply 「おかえりなさい」, welcome back. Before eating, 「いただきます」, and after, 「ごちそうさまでした」. On your last day, switch to the past tense: 「お世話になりました|おせわになりました」, thank you for everything. Many students call their host parents 「お母さん|おかあさん」 and 「お父さん|おとうさん」, just like the kids do. Wrap-up: arrive with 「お世話になります|おせわになります」 and leave with 「お世話になりました|おせわになりました」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A few house customs will make your homestay go smoothly. Bring 「お土産|おみやげ」, a small gift from your home country. Something local and packaged, like sweets, is perfect. Japanese homes have slippers for indoors, but you take them off before stepping onto tatami mats. And there's a separate pair of slippers just for the toilet, which you must not wear back out into the hallway. That's a classic and very funny mistake. The bath is shared, so wash outside the tub and never drain the water when you're done, because the next person uses it. Families may offer the guest the first bath as an honor. Many families also have a curfew, so ask what time you should be home, and tell them if you'll miss dinner. Wrap-up: bring a gift, mind the slippers, and never pull the bath plug." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's home plus stay reshaped for Japanese: home gets a long vowel and an extra 「ム」, and stay gets a vowel after the s, giving 「ス」, since Japanese can't start with s and t together.",
      "Arrive saying 「お世話になります|おせわになります」, use 「いってきます」 and 「ただいま」 every day, and leave on your last day with 「お世話になりました|おせわになりました」.",
      "Bring 「お土産|おみやげ」 from home, never wear the toilet slippers into the hallway, and never drain the shared bath water, because the next person uses it."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}で[ホームステイ]をしました。",
    en: "I did a homestay in Japan." },
  "スペイン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The name of the country, a loanword from English Spain. Japanese can't start a word with the cluster s and p together, so it adds a small vowel after the s, giving 「ス」. Then the rest, pain, becomes 「ペイン」. Interestingly, older Japanese used a name that was closer to the country's own Spanish name. The modern name follows English. Country names give you a really useful pattern. Add 「語|ご」 for the language, 「スペイン語|スペインご」, Spanish. Add 「人|じん」 for the people, 「スペイン人|スペインじん」, a Spanish person. You can do the same with 「日本|にほん」: 「日本語|にほんご」 and 「日本人|にほんじん」. It's the same trick for any country, so it's worth practicing a few. A historical note: Spanish and Portuguese traders and missionaries were among the first Europeans to reach Japan, in the 1500s. Some everyday Japanese words came in from that time, like the word for bread, which comes from Portuguese. Recap: English Spain with an extra vowel after the s, and add 「語|ご」 or 「人|じん」 for the language and the people." },
      { title: "Kanji nicknames", text: "Long ago, Japanese wrote many foreign country names with kanji picked mainly for their sounds. Spain was written with four kanji, starting with the one for west, 「西|にし」. Today the country name is always in katakana, but that west kanji survives as a one character nickname for Spain, in newspapers and dictionaries. For example, a Spanish-Japanese dictionary may be called 「西和辞典|せいわじてん」. You already use another one of these nicknames every day. The 「英|えい」 in 「英語|えいご」, English, comes from the old kanji spelling of England. In the same way, America is nicknamed with the rice kanji, 「米|べい」, so the United States is often 「米国|べいこく」 in the news. These sound spellings are a little like puns, so don't look for deep meanings in them. Wrap-up: Spain's old kanji nickname is the west, just as England's nickname gives you 「英語|えいご」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Country names plug right into patterns you've learned. 「スペインに行ったことがあります|スペインにいったことがあります」, I have been to Spain. 「スペイン語が話せます|スペインごがはなせます」, I can speak Spanish. And 「スペイン料理|スペインりょうり」 is Spanish food. Paella is a favorite in Japan, written 「パエリア」. There's a remarkable bit of history connecting the two countries. In the early 1600s, a samurai named Hasekura Tsunenaga sailed all the way across the Pacific and the Atlantic on a diplomatic mission to Spain and Rome. Some members of his group stayed behind in a town near Seville. To this day, hundreds of people there carry the surname Japón, meaning Japan, and many believe they descend from those travelers. Wrap-up: a samurai once sailed to Spain, and you can talk about it with the patterns you already know." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English Spain, with a vowel added after the s to give 「ス」, and like any country you add 「語|ご」 for the language and 「人|じん」 for the people.",
      "Spain's old kanji spelling survives as the one-character nickname 「西|にし」, west, seen in words like 「西和辞典|せいわじてん」, just as England's gives you 「英語|えいご」.",
      "Plug it into patterns you know, like 「スペインに行ったことがあります|スペインにいったことがあります」, and remember the samurai Hasekura who sailed to Spain in the early 1600s."
    ],
    ja: "{友|とも}だちは[スペイン]の{人|ひと}です。",
    en: "My friend is from Spain." },
  "なにか|何か": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「何|なに」, what, plus the little particle 「か」. You know 「か」 as the question marker. Here it adds a feeling of uncertainty. What, I'm not sure, some sort of thing. So it becomes something. The same trick works with other question words. 「だれ」 plus 「か」 is 「だれか」, someone. 「どこ」 plus 「か」 is 「どこか」, somewhere. 「いつ」 plus 「か」 is 「いつか」, someday. One pattern, a whole family. Now compare it with 「何も|なにも」, which you also know. With a negative verb, it means not anything. So the question 「何か食べましたか|なにかたべましたか」, did you eat something?, has two natural answers: 「はい、食べました|はい、たべました」, or 「いいえ、何も食べませんでした|いいえ、なにもたべませんでした」, I didn't eat anything. A common mistake is to answer with 「何か|なにか」 in the negative. Use 「何も|なにも」 for nothing. You'll often drop the particle 「を」 after it in speech, so 「何か飲みますか|なにかのみますか」 sounds natural. Recap: what plus 「か」 means something, and the same 「か」 makes someone and somewhere." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "English puts the description after something: something cold, something interesting. Japanese puts the description in front of a noun, so you usually add 「もの」, a thing. 「何か冷たいもの|なにかつめたいもの」, something cold. 「何か面白いもの|なにかおもしろいもの」, something interesting. Or more specifically, 「何か冷たい飲み物|なにかつめたいのみもの」, something cold to drink. Another very common pattern combines it with the conditional 「たら」: 「何かあったら、電話してください|なにかあったら、でんわしてください」, if anything happens, give me a call. And when a friend looks upset, you can ask 「何かありましたか|なにかありましたか」, did something happen? With particles, 「を」 and 「が」 are often dropped right after it, but others stay, like 「で」 or 「に」: 「何かで|なにかで」, by some means. Wrap-up: put the description before a noun, and pair it with 「たら」 for if anything happens." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The what kanji has two readings, and this is a good moment to sort them. 「何|なに」 is the full form. 「何|なん」 is used before sounds made with the tip of the tongue, like 「で」, 「の」 and 「じ」. So it's 「何で|なんで」, why, 「何の|なんの」, what kind of, and 「何時|なんじ」, what time. But something is always written and read 「何か|なにか」. Now, in casual speech you'll also hear 「なんか」. It can mean something, just like 「何か|なにか」, but it's also a filler word, like the English like or kind of. A young person might say 「なんか、ねむい」, I'm kind of sleepy. Use 「なにか」 in polite speech and save 「なんか」 for chatting with friends. Wrap-up: polite something is 「なにか」, and casual 「なんか」 doubles as like." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this word constantly in everyday service and classroom Japanese. At the end of a class, the teacher asks 「何か質問がありますか|なにかしつもんがありますか」, are there any questions? Notice that in questions, English often says any, and Japanese still uses this word. In a café, the server may ask 「何かお飲み物は|なにかおのみものは」, anything to drink? It's polite to leave the end of the question unsaid. When you want to offer help, say 「何か手伝いましょうか|なにかてつだいましょうか」, can I help with anything? And if you're shopping and just browsing, a clerk might ask if you're looking for something, and you can reply that you're just looking. Wrap-up: in questions, this word covers both something and anything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Adding 「か」 to 「何|なに」 adds uncertainty, so what becomes something, just as 「だれか」 is someone and 「どこか」 is somewhere. For a negative answer, use 「何も|なにも」, not this word.",
      "Japanese puts the description before a noun, so say 「何か冷たいもの|なにかつめたいもの」 for something cold, and pair it with 「たら」 in 「何かあったら|なにかあったら」, if anything happens.",
      "Something is always read 「なにか」, not with the 「なん」 reading, while casual 「なんか」 can mean something but also works as a filler like the English like.",
      "In questions it covers both something and anything, as in 「何か質問がありますか|なにかしつもんがありますか」, are there any questions?"
    ],
    ja: "[何か|なにか]{食べましょう|たべましょう}。",
    en: "Let's eat something." },
  "じょうず|上手": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's look at the kanji. 「上|うえ」 means up or above, and 「手|て」 means hand. Picture a hand that's above the rest, a skilled hand. The reading is special, though. 「上|じょう」 uses its Chinese-origin reading, and 「手|ず」 uses an unusual reading you won't see much elsewhere. So learn the sound as a whole word. Its mirror image is 「下手|へた」, poor at, made from down plus hand. Learn the pair together. You've got other words with the hand kanji too: 「歌手|かしゅ」, a singer, a person skilled with songs, and 「手紙|てがみ」, a letter. And the kanji for up shows up in 「年上|としうえ」, someone older. Grammar note: it's a na-adjective, and the skill takes 「が」: 「テニスが上手です|テニスがじょうずです」, good at tennis. A cultural tip: don't say it about yourself. It sounds like bragging. Use it to praise other people. And when someone praises your Japanese with 「上手ですね|じょうずですね」, the modest reply is 「いいえ、まだまだです」, no, I still have a long way to go. Recap: an upper hand is a skilled hand, said about others, never yourself." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "Japanese has several ways to say good at, and they aren't interchangeable. 「上手|じょうず」 praises someone's skill from the outside, which is why it sounds like bragging about yourself. So what do you say about your own strengths? Use 「得意|とくい」, a strong point. 「料理が得意です|りょうりがとくいです」, cooking is my thing. It sounds confident but not boastful, and you can use it about anyone. Then there's 「できる」, which simply means can do. 「スペイン語ができます|スペインごができます」 means I can speak Spanish, with no judgment about how well. In casual speech you'll also hear 「うまい」, which means skillful, and also tasty. Friends might say a guitar player is 「うまい」. A good rule: praise others with 「上手|じょうず」, describe yourself with 「得意|とくい」, and state plain ability with 「できる」. Wrap-up: three ways to say good at, depending on whose skill you're talking about." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since this is a na-adjective, it changes shape depending on its job. Before a noun, add 「な」: 「上手な人|じょうずなひと」, a skilled person. To describe how someone does something, use 「に」: 「上手に話します|じょうずにはなします」, she speaks skillfully. To talk about improvement, combine it with 「なる」: 「日本語が上手になりました|にほんごがじょうずになりました」, my Japanese got better. Wait, that's about yourself, isn't it? It's fine here, because you're describing progress, not bragging. To say someone is good at doing an activity, turn the verb into a noun with 「の」: 「田中さんは歌うのが上手です|たなかさんはうたうのがじょうずです」, Mr. Tanaka is good at singing. And in the past, 「上手でした|じょうずでした」. Wrap-up: 「な」 before a noun, 「に」 before a verb, 「になる」 to improve, and 「のが」 to turn an action into a skill." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a diner run by a man named Joe. Joe is absurdly skilled at everything. He flips five pancakes at once, juggles the salt shakers, and plays guitar while the coffee brews. Customers come in just to watch his hands. Above the door hangs a big neon sign: Joe's. And under it, in smaller letters, the slogan: the upper hand. So the sound, Joe's, gives you 「じょうず」, and the slogan matches the kanji, 「上|じょう」 for up and 「手|ず」 for hand. Whenever you watch someone do something really well, picture Joe's diner and say, that's Joe's level. And remember, Joe never calls himself skilled. The customers do. Wrap-up: Joe's diner, where the upper hand is always skilled." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Up plus hand makes an upper hand, a skilled hand, read as a whole word. Use it to praise others, never yourself, and answer praise with 「いいえ、まだまだです」.",
      "Praise others with 「上手|じょうず」, describe your own strengths with 「得意|とくい」, and state plain ability with 「できる」, with casual 「うまい」 for skillful too.",
      "As a na-adjective it takes 「な」 before a noun, 「に」 before a verb, 「になる」 to talk about improvement, and 「のが」 to make an action a skill, as in 「歌うのが上手です|うたうのがじょうずです」.",
      "Picture Joe's diner, where Joe is absurdly skilled and the sign reads the upper hand, so Joe's gives you 「じょうず」, up plus hand."
    ],
    ja: "{田中|たなか}さんはテニスが[上手|じょうず]です。",
    en: "Mr. Tanaka is good at tennis." },
  "へた|下手": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's look at the kanji. 「下|した」 means down or below, and 「手|て」 means hand. Picture a hand that's down low, not much skill. But the reading is special. Neither kanji uses one of its usual readings here, so the whole word is read 「へた」. Learn it as one sound. It's the mirror image of 「上手|じょうず」, skillful, which is up plus hand. Up hand, down hand. That pair is one of the easiest ways to remember both words. You know the down kanji from 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, the subway, the train that runs under the ground. Grammar note: it's a na-adjective, and the skill takes 「が」: 「歌が下手です|うたがへたです」, bad at singing. A cultural tip: unlike 「上手|じょうず」, this word is fine to use about yourself, and Japanese people do it all the time to sound modest. But saying it directly to someone else about them can sound rude, so be careful. A softer way to say you're not good at something is 「あまり上手じゃないです|あまりじょうずじゃないです」, not very good. Recap: a low hand is a clumsy hand, and it's the modest word you use about yourself." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "There's a close cousin you'll meet soon: 「苦手|にがて」, written with the kanji for bitter or painful plus hand. It also means not good at, but it adds a feeling of dislike, something you'd rather avoid. 「数学が苦手です|すうがくがにがてです」, math isn't my thing. It's softer than 「下手|へた」, and it can even be used about people or food: a person you find hard to deal with, or a food you don't like. 「下手|へた」, by contrast, is a flat judgment of poor skill. One more fun piece of trivia: the same two kanji, read differently, 「下手|しもて」, name one side of a theater stage, and 「上手|かみて」 names the other. So the up hand and down hand pair even shows up at the theater. Wrap-up: 「下手|へた」 is poor skill, while 「苦手|にがて」 is not good at and would rather avoid." },
      { title: "Proverbs", text: "Two proverbs make a perfect pair with this word. The first is 「下手の横好き|へたのよこずき」. It describes someone who loves a hobby they're terrible at. Think of the uncle who sings karaoke every weekend, loudly and off key, and enjoys every minute. People often use it about themselves with a laugh: my golf? 「下手の横好き|へたのよこずき」. Very modest and very Japanese. The other proverb is the hopeful flip side: 「好きこそものの上手なれ|すきこそもののじょうずなれ」. It means that what you love, you'll become good at. Passion leads to skill. So if you love Japanese, even if you feel 「下手|へた」 now, the proverb says you're on your way to 「上手|じょうず」. You'll hear people say 「下手ですが|へたですが」, I'm not good, but here goes, before they sing or show their work. Wrap-up: loving something you're bad at is charming, and loving it is how you get good." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. Picture your uncle Hector at a family party. Someone hands him a microphone, and before anyone can stop him, he launches into a love song. He's off key. He forgets the words. He knocks over a vase with a dramatic arm swing. The whole family winces and whispers his name: Hector, Hector, please. Heh-ta. That's your hook for 「へた」, bad at. Now add the kanji. Hector's hand is down low, holding the microphone at his belly button, because he doesn't even hold it right. Down plus hand, 「下手|へた」. And just like a modest Japanese speaker, Hector proudly announces before every song that he's terrible. He's right. Wrap-up: Uncle Hector, low hand, terrible singing, and that's 「へた」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Down plus hand is a clumsy hand, read as one word, the mirror of 「上手|じょうず」. Unlike its partner, it's the modest word to use about yourself, but it can sound rude about others.",
      "「下手|へた」 is a flat judgment of poor skill, while 「苦手|にがて」 means not good at with a feeling of wanting to avoid it, and works for people and foods too.",
      "「下手の横好き|へたのよこずき」 describes loving a hobby you're bad at, and 「好きこそものの上手なれ|すきこそもののじょうずなれ」 says what you love, you'll get good at.",
      "Picture Uncle Hector singing off key at a party while the family whispers Hector, please, and holding the mic down low: heh-ta, 「へた」, down plus hand."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは{歌|うた}が[下手|へた]です。",
    en: "I'm bad at singing." },
  "いう|言う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「言|い」 uses its native reading here. Its shape has a box at the bottom that looks like a mouth, 「口|くち」, with lines stacked on top. The real origin of the shape is debated, so treat this as a hook. A way to remember it: picture words coming out of a mouth in lines. You'll see this kanji as a building block on the left of lots of kanji about speech, like the ones in 「話す|はなす」, to speak, and 「語|ご」, language. And it's the first kanji in 「言葉|ことば」, a word or language. Here's a sound quirk that surprises learners. It's written 「いう」, but people actually say it like 「ゆう」 in normal speech. Write it one way and say it the other. The key grammar pattern is quoting with 「と」: 「先生はあした休みだと言いました|せんせいはあしたやすみだといいました」, the teacher said there's no class tomorrow. Compare it with 「話す|はなす」. That one is about talking or speaking a language, while 「言う|いう」 is about saying specific words. For the humble version, you already know 「と申します|ともうします」, used for your name. Recap: words coming out of a mouth, written 「いう」 but said like 「ゆう」, and it quotes with 「と」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a u-verb, and it has a quirk shared by every verb that ends in 「う」. In the negative, the 「う」 doesn't become 「あ」. It becomes 「わ」: 「言わない|いわない」, not say. The same happens with 「買う|かう」, which becomes 「買わない|かわない」, and 「会う|あう」, which becomes 「会わない|あわない」. The te-form doubles the consonant: 「言って|いって」. Careful, that's spelled exactly like the te-form of 「行く|いく」, to go, so context tells you which one it is. Another super useful pattern is 「という」, called. 「田中という人|たなかというひと」 is a person called Tanaka. 「これは何という食べ物ですか|これはなんというたべものですか」, what is this food called? Wrap-up: the negative uses 「わ」, the te-form sounds like going, and 「という」 names things." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In class and around Japan, a few phrases with this verb will save you again and again. To ask how to say something: 「これは日本語で何と言いますか|これはにほんごでなんといいますか」, how do you say this in Japanese? When you didn't catch something: 「もう一度言ってください|もういちどいってください」, please say it once more. Add 「ゆっくり」 to ask them to say it slowly. To pass on a message, use the ongoing form: 「田中さんは来ないと言っていました|たなかさんはこないといっていました」, Mr. Tanaka said he isn't coming. That form is the natural way to report what someone else said. And notice the pronunciation again. In all these phrases, the dictionary form sounds like 「ゆう」, but the polite form is clearly 「いいます」. Wrap-up: ask how to say it, ask to hear it again, and pass on messages with 「と言っていました|といっていました」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The say kanji anchors a set of words about language and speech. You know 「言葉|ことば」, a word or language, where the kanji uses an old native reading, 「こと」, followed by the leaf kanji. So words are like leaves of speech, a lovely image, though its exact history is debated. 「一言|ひとこと」 is a single word or a brief remark, as when someone is asked to say a few words at a party. 「方言|ほうげん」 is a dialect, the way people speak in one region, like the famous dialect of Osaka. 「言い訳|いいわけ」 is an excuse, literally a reason you say. And 「伝言|でんごん」 is a message left for someone, a word passed along. In compounds the kanji often takes its Chinese-origin reading, 「げん」 or 「ごん」. Wrap-up: from a single word to a dialect, this kanji is all about what comes out of your mouth." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji suggests lines of words coming out of a mouth, and though it's written 「いう」, people say it like 「ゆう」. Quote with 「と」, as in 「と言いました|といいました」.",
      "Like every verb ending in 「う」, its negative uses 「わ」, 「言わない|いわない」, its te-form 「言って|いって」 looks like going's, and 「という」 names things, as in 「田中という人|たなかというひと」.",
      "Ask 「日本語で何と言いますか|にほんごでなんといいますか」 for how to say something, 「もう一度言ってください|もういちどいってください」 to hear it again, and pass on messages with 「と言っていました|といっていました」.",
      "The say kanji anchors speech words like 「言葉|ことば」, a word, 「一言|ひとこと」, a brief remark, 「方言|ほうげん」, a dialect, and 「言い訳|いいわけ」, an excuse."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}はあした{休み|やすみ}だと[言いました|いいました|いう]。",
    en: "The teacher said there's no class tomorrow." },
  "おそくなる|遅くなる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is two pieces you know put together. 「遅い|おそい」 is the adjective late or slow. Change the final 「い」 to 「く」 and you get 「遅く|おそく」. Then add 「なる」, to become. So the whole thing literally means to become late. This adjective plus 「なる」 pattern is everywhere. 「寒くなる|さむくなる」 is to get cold. 「暑くなる|あつくなる」 is to get hot. Once you know the pattern, you can describe any change. The kanji 「遅|おそ」 uses its native reading here. Look at its bottom left. That's the movement radical, the winding road shape you also see in 「週|しゅう」, week. Here it's about how slowly you're moving along. Its sibling 「遅刻する|ちこくする」 means to be late for an appointment, like class or work, and there the kanji uses its Chinese-origin reading. The most useful phrase of all: 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」, sorry for being late. Or in the past, 「遅くなりました|おそくなりました」, I'm late. It can also mean it's gotten late in the evening: 「遅くなりましたから、帰ります|おそくなりましたから、かえります」. Recap: late plus become means to be late, and it's the go-to word for apologizing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several late words, and choosing the right one matters. 「遅い|おそい」 is the adjective, late or slow: a slow bus, or a late hour. 「遅くなる|おそくなる」 is becoming late, perfect for apologies and for saying the time is getting late. 「遅れる|おくれる」 is to be delayed or behind schedule. It's the word for trains: 「電車が遅れています|でんしゃがおくれています」, the train is delayed. Notice it uses a different native reading of the same kanji. 「遅刻する|ちこくする」 is specifically to be late for a fixed time, like school or work. Teachers and bosses use it. And 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」, to ride plus be late, means to miss a train or bus. So a full excuse might be: I missed the train, so I was late for class. Wrap-up: 「遅い|おそい」 describes, 「遅れる|おくれる」 is delayed, and 「遅刻する|ちこくする」 is late for an appointment." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Punctuality in Japan is taken very seriously, and that shapes how this phrase is used. Trains run so precisely that when one is even a minute late, the staff apologize over the speakers. If a train is badly delayed, stations hand out official delay slips, which passengers show to their school or company to prove the lateness wasn't their fault. Because being on time matters so much, people arrive early, often five or ten minutes before a meeting. And if you're going to be late, the polite thing is to send a message ahead, using the future: 「すみません、少し遅くなります|すみません、すこしおそくなります」, sorry, I'll be a little late. Then, when you arrive, you apologize again with the past form. Even a few minutes deserves an apology. Wrap-up: message ahead with 「遅くなります|おそくなります」, and apologize again when you arrive." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The form 「遅く|おそく」 is the adverb version of the adjective, and it's useful well beyond this phrase. With an action verb it means late: 「きのうは遅く起きました|きのうはおそくおきました」, I got up late yesterday. With 「まで」 it means until late: 「遅くまで勉強しました|おそくまでべんきょうしました」, I studied until late. All i-adjectives work this way. 「早い|はやい」 becomes 「早く|はやく」, early, so 「早く寝ます|はやくねます」, I'll go to bed early. And notice the pairing with 「なる」 works in any tense. 「遅くなりますから|おそくなりますから」, because it'll get late. 「遅くなる前に|おそくなるまえに」, before it gets late. You can even say 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」, sorry for being late, using the te-form to give the reason for apologizing. Wrap-up: change 「い」 to 「く」 and the adjective describes how or when you do things." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「遅い|おそい」 with 「い」 turned to 「く」 plus 「なる」, to become, so literally to become late, and 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」 is the go-to apology.",
      "「遅い|おそい」 describes something late or slow, 「遅れる|おくれる」 means delayed, like a train, and 「遅刻する|ちこくする」 means late for a fixed time like school or work.",
      "Punctuality matters so much in Japan that you message ahead with 「少し遅くなります|すこしおそくなります」, then apologize again when you arrive, even for a few minutes.",
      "Changing 「い」 to 「く」 turns any i-adjective into an adverb, so 「遅く起きました|おそくおきました」 is I got up late and 「遅くまで|おそくまで」 is until late."
    ],
    ja: "すみません、[遅くなりました|おそくなりました|おそくなる]。",
    en: "Sorry I'm late." },
  "おふろにはいる|お風呂に入る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's take it apart. 「お」 is the polite prefix. 「風呂|ふろ」 is the bath. 「に」 marks where you go, and 「入る|はいる」 means to enter. So you literally enter the bath. English says take a bath, but Japanese thinks of it as getting into the tub. About the kanji. The first one, 「風|ふ」, normally means wind. You know it from 「台風|たいふう」, a typhoon. But in 「風呂|ふろ」, the kanji are mostly a way of spelling the word, so don't look for a wind meaning in your bath. The verb 「入る|はいる」 uses the native reading. You also know 「入れる|いれる」, to put something in, from the same kanji. A grammar trap: 「入る|はいる」 looks like a ru-verb but it's actually a u-verb. So it's 「入ります|はいります」 and 「入って|はいって」. A cultural note: in Japan, you wash and rinse yourself outside the tub first, then soak in clean hot water. The whole family often uses the same bathwater, one after another, so keeping it clean matters. Many people bathe every night to relax before bed. Recap: you enter the bath, and you wash before you get in." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese bathing comes with its own little phrases. The hot water in the tub is 「お湯|おゆ」. Many modern bathtubs fill and heat themselves, and when the bath is ready, a recorded voice announces 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」, the bath is ready, often with a little tune. If you're a guest, you might ask 「お風呂に入ってもいいですか|おふろにはいってもいいですか」, may I take a bath? For a shower, the verb changes completely. You don't enter a shower. You pour it over yourself: 「シャワーを浴びる|シャワーをあびる」. Many Japanese people bathe in the evening, to wash off the day and warm up before bed, rather than showering in the morning. A host might ask 「お風呂、どうぞ|おふろ、どうぞ」, the bath is yours. Wrap-up: you enter a bath, you pour a shower, and the tub tells you when it's ready." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「入る|はいる」 means to enter, and it stretches further than English enter. You enter a room, 「部屋に入る|へやにはいる」. You also enter a university, which means getting in or starting there: 「大学に入る|だいがくにはいる」. You join a club the same way: 「クラブに入る|クラブにはいる」. You even get into insurance: 「保険に入る|ほけんにはいる」, to take out a policy. In each case, 「に」 marks what you go into. The partner verb is 「入れる|いれる」, to put something in, like sugar in coffee. And with the Chinese-origin reading 「にゅう」, the kanji appears in 「入院|にゅういん」, being admitted to hospital. Its native reading 「いり」 shows up in 「入口|いりぐち」, entrance, the enter mouth of a building. Wrap-up: enter a bath, a school, a club or insurance, all with 「に入る|にはいる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Bathing outside the home is a big part of Japanese life. Neighborhood public baths, 「銭湯|せんとう」, were once essential when many homes had no tub, and some still thrive today. Hot springs, 「温泉|おんせん」, are a favorite getaway, often with outdoor pools. The etiquette is the same as at home but stricter. Wash thoroughly at the shower stations first. Don't let your small towel touch the water. Many people fold it and set it on their head. Bathing is done nude, in separate areas for men and women. Many places don't allow people with tattoos, though this is slowly changing. There's even a phrase, 「裸の付き合い|はだかのつきあい」, naked relations, meaning friends or coworkers bond by bathing together, with nothing to hide. Wrap-up: wash first, towel on your head, and enjoy the naked relations." }
    ],
    short: [
      "You literally enter the bath with 「に入る|にはいる」, and 「入る|はいる」 is a u-verb despite its look. In Japan you wash outside the tub first, because the family shares the water.",
      "You enter a bath, but you pour a shower over yourself with 「シャワーを浴びる|シャワーをあびる」, and many tubs announce 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」 when ready.",
      "「入る|はいる」 stretches beyond rooms: you enter a university, a club, even insurance, always with 「に」 marking what you go into.",
      "At public baths, 「銭湯|せんとう」, and hot springs, 「温泉|おんせん」, wash thoroughly first and keep your towel out of the water, often folded on your head."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[お風呂に入ります|おふろにはいります|おふろにはいる]。",
    en: "I take a bath every night." },
  "おもう|思う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji uses its native reading, 「おも」, followed by 「う」. Look at its shape. The top looks like a field, 「田|た」, and the bottom is the heart, 「心|こころ」. A way to remember it: thoughts are what grow in the field of your heart. The heart at the bottom is a real meaning clue. You also see 「心|しん」 in 「心配する|しんぱいする」, to worry, and it's the bottom part of 「念|ねん」 in 「残念|ざんねん」. Kanji about feelings and thoughts often have a heart in them. The key pattern is 「と思います|とおもいます」, I think that. You put your opinion before 「と」: 「あしたは雨だと思います|あしたはあめだとおもいます」, I think it will rain tomorrow. Notice that with nouns and na-adjectives you add 「だ」 before 「と」. Japanese uses this a lot to soften statements. Saying I think, instead of stating something flat out, sounds more modest. For someone else's thoughts, use 「思っています|おもっています」: they think that. Recap: a field over a heart, and 「と思います|とおもいます」 is how you give your opinion." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Two patterns with this verb are especially useful. First, to talk about your plans, combine the volitional form with 「と思っています|とおもっています」: 「日本に行こうと思っています|にほんにいこうとおもっています」, I'm thinking of going to Japan. It's gentler than saying flatly that you will go, and Japanese people use it all the time for plans and intentions. Second, a habit that surprises English speakers. In English we say I don't think it will rain. Japanese usually puts the negative inside the opinion instead: 「雨は降らないと思います|あめはふらないとおもいます」, I think it won't rain. Saying 「と思いません|とおもいません」 is possible, but it sounds stronger, like disagreeing. And to ask for an opinion, use 「どう」: 「どう思いますか|どうおもいますか」, what do you think? Wrap-up: plans with the volitional plus 「と思っています|とおもっています」, and keep the negative inside the opinion." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "English uses think for everything, but Japanese splits it. 「思う|おもう」 is for opinions, feelings and impressions, things that just come to you. 「考える|かんがえる」 is for thinking something through, considering it with effort. If a friend asks you to go on a trip and you need time to decide, say 「考えます|かんがえます」, I'll think about it. You can't really use 「思う|おもう」 there. If you're asked what you think of a movie, though, it's 「面白いと思います|おもしろいとおもいます」. The think kanji also gives you 「思い出す|おもいだす」, literally to bring a thought out, which means to recall or remember. And the noun 「思い出|おもいで」 means memories, the warm kind, like memories of a homestay. Wrap-up: 「思う|おもう」 is what you feel, 「考える|かんがえる」 is what you work out, and 「思い出|おもいで」 is what you keep." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In conversation, this verb helps you agree, compare and react. When someone gives an opinion and you agree, say 「私もそう思います|わたしもそうおもいます」, I think so too. It's warm and very natural. When something turns out different from what you expected, use 「思ったより|おもったより」, than I thought: 「テストは思ったより簡単でした|テストはおもったよりかんたんでした」, the test was easier than I thought. And when you're surprised, 「そう思わなかった|そうおもわなかった」, I didn't think so, works in casual talk. Japanese speakers add 「と思います|とおもいます」 to almost any statement in meetings and class to sound polite and open. Even facts sometimes get it, which can sound odd to English ears, but it's simply good manners. A tiny exchange: 「この映画、どう思う|このえいが、どうおもう」, what do you think of this movie? 「面白いと思う|おもしろいとおもう」, I think it's fun. Wrap-up: agree with 「そう思います|そうおもいます」 and compare with 「思ったより|おもったより」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a field over a heart, so thoughts grow in the field of your heart, and 「と思います|とおもいます」 gives your opinion modestly, with 「だ」 before 「と」 after nouns.",
      "Use the volitional plus 「と思っています|とおもっています」 for plans, as in 「行こうと思っています|いこうとおもっています」, and keep the negative inside the opinion: 「降らないと思います|ふらないとおもいます」.",
      "「思う|おもう」 is for opinions and feelings that come to you, while 「考える|かんがえる」 is for thinking something through, and 「思い出|おもいで」 means warm memories.",
      "Agree with 「私もそう思います|わたしもそうおもいます」, I think so too, and compare with expectations using 「思ったより|おもったより」, than I thought."
    ],
    ja: "あしたは{雨|あめ}だと[思います|おもいます|おもう]。",
    en: "I think it will rain tomorrow." },
  "きる|切る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「切|き」 uses its native reading here. Look at its right side. That's 「刀|かたな」, a sword or blade. Anything with a blade shape in a kanji often has to do with cutting. The left side looks like the number seven. A way to remember it: seven quick strokes with a blade. This kanji shows up in some words you know with surprising meanings. 「切符|きっぷ」 is a train ticket, which used to be a slip cut from paper. And 「親切|しんせつ」 means kind. That one's a bit abstract, so just enjoy the connection. Now an important trap. 「着る|きる」, to wear, sounds exactly the same. But they're different verbs and conjugate differently. 「切る|きる」 is a u-verb, so you say 「切ります|きります」 and 「切って|きって」. 「着る|きる」 is a ru-verb, so it's 「着ます|きます」 and 「着て|きて」. Context helps too. You cut vegetables, paper or hair. You wear clothes. You'll also hear it for hanging up the phone, cutting the connection. Recap: a blade on the right means cutting, and it's a u-verb, unlike wear." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The cutting kanji turns up in more words than you'd expect, and it changes its sound along the way. In 「切符|きっぷ」, a ticket, and 「切手|きって」, a postage stamp, the 「き」 is followed by a small 「っ」, a little pause, because the next sound is a hard consonant. Both are small slips of paper, and the cutting kanji fits that nicely. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「せつ」, the kanji appears in 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, and 「大切|たいせつ」, precious or important. Those meanings have drifted far from cutting, so treat them as separate words. Then there's 「締め切り|しめきり」, a deadline. It's built from tighten plus cut, the point where things get cut off. Writers and students dread it. And 「売り切れ|うりきれ」 means sold out, a sign you'll see on vending machines and at bakeries. Wrap-up: cut paper gives you tickets and stamps, and cut off gives you deadlines and sold out." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has an intransitive partner, 「切れる|きれる」, for when something gets cut or runs out on its own. You cut the string with 「を」 and 「切る|きる」. The string breaks with 「が」 and 「切れる|きれる」. The partner has some everyday uses. When your phone battery dies, it's 「電池が切れました|でんちがきれました」. When a knife is sharp, people say it cuts well, 「よく切れる|よくきれる」. Back to the main verb: besides hanging up the phone, you also use it to switch off power: 「スイッチを切る|スイッチをきる」. And when you drain pasta or wash vegetables, you cut the water, 「水を切る|みずをきる」. Finally, added to another verb's stem, it means doing something all the way. 「売り切れる|うりきれる」 is to sell out completely. Wrap-up: you cut with 「を」, things get cut or run out with 「切れる|きれる」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. Picture a tiny locksmith's shop with a huge, gleaming blade mounted on the counter. A customer hands over an old brass key. The locksmith grins, slams it under the blade, and chop, chop, chop, he cuts a brand new key in seconds, sparks flying everywhere. Key. That's the start of 「きる」. Now match it to the kanji. The blade on the right side of 「切|き」 is the locksmith's blade, the sword shape 「刀|かたな」, and the seven-like shape on the left is the key being shaped. Every time you cut something in Japanese, a carrot, a piece of paper, a phone call, imagine that blade coming down and making a key. And remember it's a u-verb: 「切ります|きります」. Wrap-up: the locksmith's blade cuts a key, and that's how you cut." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The blade 「刀|かたな」 on the right of the kanji means cutting. It's a u-verb, 「切ります|きります」, unlike 「着る|きる」, to wear, which is a ru-verb.",
      "Cut paper slips give you 「切符|きっぷ」, a ticket, and 「切手|きって」, a stamp, while cut off gives you 「締め切り|しめきり」, a deadline, and 「売り切れ|うりきれ」, sold out.",
      "You cut something with 「を」 and 「切る|きる」, while things get cut or run out on their own with 「切れる|きれる」, as in 「電池が切れました|でんちがきれました」.",
      "Picture a locksmith slamming an old key under a huge blade and cutting a new one: key, 「きる」, with the blade on the right of 「切|き」."
    ],
    ja: "{肉|にく}を[切ります|きります|きる]。",
    en: "I cut the meat." },
  "つくる|作る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「作|つく」 uses its native reading here, followed by 「る」. On its left side is a squeezed-in person, the same narrow person shape you see in many kanji. A way to remember it: picture a person busy at work, making something with their hands. You've got a sibling in 「作家|さっか」, a writer, someone who makes books for a living. There the kanji uses its Chinese-origin reading. It's a regular u-verb: 「作ります|つくります」, 「作って|つくって」. It's used for making all sorts of things. Food, like 「ケーキを作る|ケーキをつくる」, to make a cake. Things, like making a chair. Even making friends uses this verb. A common question from English speakers is when to use it and when to use 「する」. Use 「作る|つくる」 when something new comes into existence. Use 「する」 for activities, like doing homework. And for general cooking, there's also 「料理する|りょうりする」. Recap: a person at work, making something new." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has two Chinese-origin readings besides its native one, and you'll meet both. 「さく」 appears in 「作文|さくぶん」, a composition, the essay your teacher assigns, made of writing. It's also in 「作品|さくひん」, a work, like a painting or a novel. 「さ」 appears in 「作家|さっか」, a writer, and 「作業|さぎょう」, work or a task, like hands-on work. Here's something else interesting. The verb 「つくる」 can be written with other kanji, depending on what's being made. For large-scale making, like building ships or brewing sake, you'll see 「造る|つくる」. For creating something original and new, sometimes 「創る|つくる」. But 「作る|つくる」 is the everyday one, the safe choice for food, crafts, plans and almost anything else. Wrap-up: 「さく」 for a composition, 「さ」 for a writer, and 「作る|つくる」 for everyday making." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb covers far more than cooking. To make a meal, 「ご飯を作る|ごはんをつくる」. To make friends, 「友だちを作る|ともだちをつくる」. Busy people talk about making time: 「時間を作る|じかんをつくる」. Add 「方|かた」 to the stem and you get the way to make: 「作り方|つくりかた」, a recipe or instructions, as in 「ケーキの作り方|ケーキのつくりかた」, how to make a cake. One of the nicest words in the family is 「手作り|てづくり」, handmade or homemade, literally hand making. Notice the sound softens to 「づ」 when it comes second. You'll see it on bakery signs and hear it as praise: 「手作りですか|てづくりですか」, did you make this yourself? Bringing a homemade gift or a homemade lunch is a warm gesture in Japan. Wrap-up: make food, friends and time, and learn 「作り方|つくりかた」 and 「手作り|てづくり」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A memory trick for the sound. Picture a tiny restaurant kitchen with exactly two cooks. They're twins, they wear tall white hats, and they rule this kitchen like kings. Nobody else is allowed in. Orders fly in, and the two cooks make everything from scratch: dumplings, cakes, noodles, even the plates. A big sign over the door says, two cooks rule. Two, cook, rule. Squash it together and you get 「つくる」, to make. Now link it to the kanji. The narrow person shape on the left of 「作|つく」 is one of the cooks, busy at work. Whenever you make something in Japanese, a cake, a plan, or a friend, picture those two cooks ruling their kitchen. Wrap-up: two cooks rule, and they make everything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The narrow person on the left suggests someone busy making something with their hands. Use 「作る|つくる」 when something new comes into existence, and 「する」 for activities.",
      "Besides 「つくる」, the kanji is read 「さく」 in 「作文|さくぶん」, a composition, and 「さ」 in 「作家|さっか」, a writer, while 「作る|つくる」 is the everyday spelling for making.",
      "You make food, friends and even time with it, and the family includes 「作り方|つくりかた」, how to make something, and 「手作り|てづくり」, homemade.",
      "Picture twin cooks ruling a tiny kitchen under a sign saying two cooks rule: two, cook, rule squashes into 「つくる」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}とケーキを[作りました|つくりました|つくる]。",
    en: "I made a cake with my mother." },
  "ふる|降る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「降|ふ」 uses its native reading here, followed by 「る」. Look at its left side. That shape is a radical for a hill or a set of steps. The right side is about feet coming down. Together they picture coming down from a hill. That's why this kanji is all about descending. It's used for things that come down from the sky: 「雨が降る|あめがふる」, it rains, and 「雪が降る|ゆきがふる」, it snows. Don't try to use 「する」 with rain. In Japanese, rain and snow always fall. You've got a sibling with the same kanji, 「降りる|おりる」, to get off a train or a bus. It's the same idea of coming down, but with a different native reading. The ongoing form is used for weather happening right now: 「雨が降っています|あめがふっています」, it's raining. It's a u-verb: 「降ります|ふります」, 「降って|ふって」. Recap: coming down the hill, the verb for rain and snow falling, and the same kanji as getting off a train." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Since this verb is all about weather, it pairs with weather patterns you've learned. When the sky looks dark, say 「雨が降りそうです|あめがふりそうです」, it looks like it's going to rain. To make plans that depend on the weather, use 「たら」: 「雨が降ったら、うちにいます|あめがふったら、うちにいます」, if it rains, I'll stay home. When rain stops, Japanese uses a separate verb, 「やむ」: 「雨がやみました|あめがやみました」, the rain stopped. For heavy rain, add the adverb 「強く|つよく」 or use 「大雨|おおあめ」, a downpour. The opposite of rain falling is the sky clearing up, 「晴れる|はれる」. A short exchange for the end of a workday: 「雨、降っていますか|あめ、ふっていますか」, is it raining? 「ええ、かさを持っていったほうがいいですよ|ええ、かさをもっていったほうがいいですよ」, yes, you'd better take an umbrella. Wrap-up: it looks like rain with 「そう」, it stops with 「やむ」, and it clears up with 「晴れる|はれる」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There's another verb pronounced exactly the same way that you'll hear a lot: 「振る|ふる」, to wave or shake. You wave your hand to say goodbye, 「手を振る|てをふる」, and you shake a bottle of dressing. Here's the fun part. In romance, 「振る|ふる」 also means to dump someone. So 「振られました|ふられました」 means I got dumped. Imagine the confusion if you mixed them up! Context usually makes it obvious. Rain and snow fall with 「降る|ふる」, using 「が」. Hands and partners get waved or dumped with 「振る|ふる」, using 「を」. Both are u-verbs, so their forms match too. And don't confuse either with 「古い|ふるい」, old, which has an extra 「い」 and is an adjective. Wrap-up: rain falls, hands wave, and partners get dumped, all with the same sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「こう」, which you'll meet in weather reports. 「降水確率|こうすいかくりつ」 is the chance of precipitation, literally the probability of falling water. Forecasters give it as a percentage, and people use it to decide whether to take an umbrella. The same reading shows up in 「以降|いこう」, from then on, in schedules and notices, like from nine o'clock onward. Rain itself has a family too. 「大雨|おおあめ」 is heavy rain. 「小雨|こさめ」 is light rain, and notice the sound changes to 「さめ」 there. 「にわか雨|にわかあめ」 is a sudden shower. And 「雨降り|あめふり」 is rainy weather, rain plus fall, a word in a famous children's song. Wrap-up: 「こう」 in forecasts, and a whole family of rain words to go with the falling." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji pictures coming down from a hill, so rain and snow fall with 「降る|ふる」, never 「する」, and it shares a kanji with 「降りる|おりる」, to get off a train.",
      "Say 「雨が降りそうです|あめがふりそうです」 when it looks like rain, use 「やむ」 when it stops, and 「晴れる|はれる」 when the sky clears.",
      "「振る|ふる」 sounds the same but means to wave or shake, and in romance to dump someone, while rain falls with 「降る|ふる」 and 「が」.",
      "The reading 「こう」 shows up in forecasts as 「降水確率|こうすいかくりつ」, the chance of rain, and rain words include 「大雨|おおあめ」 and 「小雨|こさめ」."
    ],
    ja: "{雨|あめ}が[降っています|ふっています|ふる]。",
    en: "It's raining." },
  "もっていく|持っていく": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is two verbs joined together. 「持って|もって」 is the te-form of 「持つ|もつ」, to hold or carry. 「いく」 is to go. So the literal meaning is to go while holding something. In other words, you take it with you. The kanji 「持|も」 uses its native reading here. On its left is the hand radical, which shows up in lots of action kanji, like 「捨てる|すてる」, to throw away. A hand that holds: that's 「持つ|もつ」. Now flip the direction. Swap 「いく」 for 「くる」, to come, and you get 「持ってくる|もってくる」, to bring something here. Going away from where you are means take. Coming toward where you are means bring. Think about which way you're moving. To a friend's party, you take a dish: 「持っていきます|もっていきます」. If your friend says bring a dish, from their point of view it's 「持ってきて|もってきて」. One important limit: this is for things, not people. For taking a person somewhere, Japanese uses a different verb. Recap: hold it and go, and you take it along; hold it and come, and you bring it." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese splits take along into two verbs, one for things and one for people. For things, it's 「持っていく|もっていく」, hold and go. For people and animals, it's 「連れていく|つれていく」. 「連れる|つれる」 means to bring someone along, so 「友だちを連れていく|ともだちをつれていく」 is to take a friend along, and 「犬を連れていく|いぬをつれていく」 is to take the dog. Using the holding verb for a person sounds like you're carrying them in your arms. Both have bring versions with 「くる」: 「持ってくる|もってくる」 and 「連れてくる|つれてくる」. And there's a third direction: 「持って帰る|もってかえる」, to take something home. Since 「いく」 conjugates like 「行く|いく」, the past is 「持っていった|もっていった」. Wrap-up: things are held with 「持つ|もつ」, people are led with 「連れる|つれる」, and both go or come." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb shows up in advice and planning all the time. Giving advice: 「かさを持っていったほうがいいですよ|かさをもっていったほうがいいですよ」, you'd better take an umbrella. Asking what to bring: 「何を持っていけばいいですか|なにをもっていけばいいですか」, what should I take? Schools send home lists of things to take on a trip, called 「持ち物|もちもの」, the things you carry. And here's one you'll hear at every fast food counter in Japan. The clerk asks 「こちらでお召し上がりですか、お持ち帰りですか|こちらでおめしあがりですか、おもちかえりですか」, will you eat here or take it out? 「お持ち帰り|おもちかえり」 is takeout, literally holding it and going home. Just answer with one word, 「持ち帰りで|もちかえりで」, takeout please. Wrap-up: take an umbrella, check the list of things to bring, and order your food to go." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The holding verb, 「持つ|もつ」, means much more than holding in your hands. It also means to own. 「車を持っています|くるまをもっています」, I have a car. So someone with lots of money is an 「お金持ち|おかねもち」, a money holder, a rich person. The same stem appears in one of the most important feeling words in Japanese, 「気持ち|きもち」, a feeling or mood. You know the spirit kanji, 「気|き」, from 「元気|げんき」. So a feeling is what your spirit is holding. 「気持ちがいい|きもちがいい」 means it feels good, like a hot bath or a cool breeze. With the te-form, 「持って|もって」 also combines with verbs other than go and come. 「持って帰る|もってかえる」, to take home, is the one you'll use most. Wrap-up: hold, own, and even feel, all from the same holding verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「持って|もって」, holding, plus 「いく」, going, so you take it along, while 「持ってくる|もってくる」 brings it here. It's for things, not people.",
      "Things are held with 「持っていく|もっていく」, but people and animals are led with 「連れていく|つれていく」, and 「持って帰る|もってかえる」 means take home.",
      "You'll hear it in advice like 「かさを持っていったほうがいいですよ|かさをもっていったほうがいいですよ」, and 「お持ち帰り|おもちかえり」 means takeout at fast food counters.",
      "「持つ|もつ」 also means to own, giving 「お金持ち|おかねもち」, a rich person, and 「気持ち|きもち」, a feeling, what your spirit is holding."
    ],
    ja: "かさを[持っていきます|もっていきます|もっていく]。",
    en: "I'll take an umbrella." },
  "すてる|捨てる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「捨|す」 uses its native reading here, followed by 「てる」. On its left side is the hand radical, the same one you see in 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, and 「掃除|そうじ」, cleaning. A way to remember it: picture your hand opening and letting go of something you don't want. It's a ru-verb, so it's 「捨てます|すてます」 and 「捨てて|すてて」. The most common phrase is 「ごみを捨てる|ごみをすてる」, to throw away the garbage, using 「ごみ」, which you know. A cultural note: throwing away garbage in Japan is serious business. Trash has to be sorted into burnable and non-burnable items, bottles, cans and plastic, and each type goes out only on its own day of the week. Neighbors notice if you get it wrong, and public trash cans are rare, so many people carry their trash home. If you do a homestay, ask your host family about the rules on your first day. Recap: a hand letting go means to throw away, and in Japan you sort it first." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "A few patterns from your lessons combine naturally with this verb. To ask someone not to throw something away: 「捨てないでください|すてないでください」, please don't throw it out. Signs say 「ごみを捨てないでください|ごみをすてないでください」, no littering. To express regret about something you threw away by mistake, use 「てしまう」: 「大事な手紙を捨ててしまいました|だいじなてがみをすててしまいました」, I accidentally threw away an important letter. That form adds a sense of oops, or it's all gone now. The opposite verb is 「拾う|ひろう」, to pick up, as in picking up trash or finding a wallet on the street. And you'll hear a casual word for littering, 「ポイ捨て|ポイすて」. 「ポイ」 is the sound of carelessly tossing something away, plus our verb. Wrap-up: please don't throw it, oops I threw it, and the opposite, pick it up." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb combines with others to make some vivid words. 「使い捨て|つかいすて」 means disposable, literally use and throw away, like disposable chopsticks or paper cups. 「見捨てる|みすてる」 means to abandon someone, literally to see and throw away, to turn your back on them. It's a strong, emotional word, often heard in dramas: don't abandon me! A 「捨て猫|すてねこ」 is a stray kitten someone left behind, a common sight in old manga, sitting in a cardboard box in the rain. And the dramatic 「捨てる|すてる」 can be used for bigger things than trash. People throw away a dream, or their pride, or even their hometown. So the verb has a cold edge when it's not about garbage. Wrap-up: use and toss, see and toss, and the poor kitten in the box." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Before throwing something away in Japan, you might hear one of the most beloved Japanese words: 「もったいない」. It expresses regret over waste, the feeling that it's a shame to throw away something that still has value. Leftover rice, a half-used notebook, a sweater with one small hole, all could prompt it. Children are taught to finish every grain of rice in their bowl, and grandparents save wrapping paper and rubber bands. The word has even become an international slogan for environmental causes. So you might hear 「捨てるのはもったいない|すてるのはもったいない」, it's a waste to throw it out. Many Japanese households also hand items down, sell them at secondhand shops, or repair them instead. Wrap-up: before you toss something, ask yourself whether it's 「もったいない」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The hand radical on the left suggests a hand letting go of something unwanted. In Japan, garbage must be sorted by type and put out only on its set day.",
      "Use 「捨てないでください|すてないでください」 for please don't throw it away, and 「捨ててしまいました|すててしまいました」 for an oops, I threw it out, with 「拾う|ひろう」 as the opposite.",
      "It combines into vivid words like 「使い捨て|つかいすて」, disposable, 「見捨てる|みすてる」, to abandon someone, and 「捨て猫|すてねこ」, a stray kitten left behind.",
      "Before throwing something away, Japanese people may say 「もったいない」, regret that something still useful is being wasted."
    ],
    ja: "ごみを[捨てます|すてます|すてる]。",
    en: "I throw away the trash." },
  "はじめる|始める": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「始|はじ」 uses its native reading here, followed by 「める」. Its left side is the kanji for woman. The origin of the full shape is not something to rely on, so let's use meaning and sound to anchor it. The key grammar point is that 「始める|はじめる」 takes an object with 「を」. You begin something: 「仕事を始めます|しごとをはじめます」, I start work. It's a ru-verb, so 「始めます|はじめます」 and 「始めて|はじめて」. There's also a partner verb you'll meet, for when something begins on its own, like a class starting. That one takes 「が」 instead. So you begin a class, but a class begins. Here's a lovely connection. The phrase 「はじめまして」, nice to meet you, comes from the same native root, meaning for the first time. It's usually written with a different kanji, one that means first, but the sound and idea are the same. So when you say it, you're saying this is our beginning. Another useful phrase is 「始めましょう|はじめましょう」, let's begin, which teachers say at the start of class. Recap: to begin something with 「を」, from the same root as the greeting for a first meeting." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb belongs to a family of transitive and intransitive pairs that share a pattern. The transitive one ends in 「める」 and takes 「を」. The intransitive one ends in 「まる」 and takes 「が」. 「授業を始める|じゅぎょうをはじめる」, the teacher starts the class. 「授業が始まる|じゅぎょうがはじまる」, the class starts. You know another pair just like it: 「ドアを閉める|ドアをしめる」, to close the door, and 「ドアが閉まる|ドアがしまる」, the door closes. Others follow the same shape, like to decide and to be decided. And there's a super handy add-on. Attach 「始める|はじめる」 to any verb stem to mean start doing: 「食べ始める|たべはじめる」, start eating, and 「雨が降り始めました|あめがふりはじめました」, it started to rain. Wrap-up: 「める」 with 「を」, 「まる」 with 「が」, and a verb stem plus 「始める|はじめる」 means start doing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this verb at the start of every class, test and meeting. A teacher handing out a quiz says 「始めてください|はじめてください」, please begin. In a formal exam, the proctor may simply call out 「始め|はじめ」, begin, and later 「やめ」, stop. At a meeting: 「では、始めましょう|では、はじめましょう」, well then, let's begin. To say where something starts from, use 「から」: 「一ページから始めます|いちページからはじめます」, we'll start from page one. In daily life, people use it for new habits and new chapters: 「ジョギングを始めました|ジョギングをはじめました」, I've started jogging. And for something you began a while ago and still do: 「日本語の勉強を始めて二年です|にほんごのべんきょうをはじめてにねんです」, it's been two years since I started studying Japanese. Wrap-up: begin the test, begin the meeting, and begin new hobbies." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The idea of beginning is so prized that 「はじめ」 is a traditional boy's name in Japan. It's often written with the kanji for one, 「一|はじめ」, read with this special name reading, or with other kanji meaning origin or beginning, including this verb's own kanji. Parents sometimes gave it to a firstborn son, the beginning of the family line. New beginnings are celebrated in Japan, especially in April, when the school year and the work year both start. Cherry blossoms are blooming, new students arrive with crisp uniforms, and new employees join companies. That's why so many beginnings in Japan feel tied to spring. Even New Year's has 「書き初め|かきぞめ」, the first calligraphy of the year, written with the other first kanji. Wrap-up: in Japan, beginnings come with spring, a fresh start, and sometimes a name." }
    ],
    short: [
      "You begin something with 「を」, as in 「仕事を始めます|しごとをはじめます」, and the greeting 「はじめまして」 comes from the same native root meaning for the first time.",
      "The 「める」 verb takes 「を」 and its partner 「始まる|はじまる」 takes 「が」, and adding 「始める|はじめる」 to a verb stem means start doing, as in 「食べ始める|たべはじめる」.",
      "Teachers say 「始めてください|はじめてください」 to start a test, meetings open with 「始めましょう|はじめましょう」, and people use it for new hobbies like 「ジョギングを始めました|ジョギングをはじめました」.",
      "Beginnings are so prized that 「はじめ」 is a traditional boy's name, and in Japan new starts come in April, when school and work years begin."
    ],
    ja: "{九時|くじ}にしごとを[始めます|はじめます|はじめる]。",
    en: "I begin work at nine." },
  "うんてんする|運転する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「運|うん」, means to carry or to transport. It has the movement radical at the bottom left, the winding road shape. It can also mean luck, as in good fortune that carries you along. The second, 「転|てん」, means to turn or to roll. Look at its left side: it's 「車|くるま」, a car or vehicle. A vehicle with turning wheels. Together they suggest moving something along by turning wheels. That's driving. Add 「する」 to make the verb. Here's a great sibling: 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, a bicycle. It has the same 「転|てん」, plus 「自|じ」, self, and 「車|しゃ」, vehicle. A vehicle whose wheels you turn yourself. The thing you drive takes 「を」: 「車を運転する|くるまをうんてんする」. A cultural note: in Japan, people drive on the left side of the road, and you can get a driver's license at eighteen. In big cities like Tokyo, many people don't drive at all, because trains are so convenient. Recap: carry plus turning wheels, the same turning kanji as in bicycle." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each of these two kanji has relatives worth knowing. The first one, 「運|うん」, shows up in 「運動|うんどう」, exercise, which is literally carrying yourself into motion. With its native reading it becomes the verb 「運ぶ|はこぶ」, to carry something, like boxes when you move house. And on its own, 「運|うん」 means luck. The second kanji, 「転|てん」, appears with a native reading in a verb you know: 「転ぶ|ころぶ」, to fall down or tumble. That's the rolling, turning idea again, only this time it's your whole body that rolls. Add one more piece to driving and you get a person word: 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」, a driver, like a taxi or bus driver. So one family covers moving, carrying, luck, tumbling and drivers. Learn them together and each one props up the others." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word isn't only for cars. Listen at any Japanese train station and you'll hear it in announcements about train service. When trains stop because of an accident or heavy snow, the screen says 「運転見合わせ|うんてんみあわせ」, service suspended. Machines and trains are operated, or driven, with the same word. Now for people. To say you can drive, use the potential form: 「運転できます|うんてんできます」. To praise someone, say 「運転が上手ですね|うんてんがじょうずですね」, you're a good driver. Signs along the road remind you of 「安全運転|あんぜんうんてん」, safe driving. One thing to know before you visit: Japan is extremely strict about drinking and driving. Even a small amount is illegal, and passengers who knowingly ride with a drinking driver can be punished too. So at a party, the driver sticks to tea. In short, people, trains and machines all get driven with this one word." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a giant cartoon car wheel rolling down a winding road. Sitting on top of it, steering with a tiny wheel, is a very serious cat. Every time the car goes over a bump, the cat grunts, oon. And every time it takes a sharp turn, the wheel squeals, ten! Oon, ten. Oon, ten. Bump, turn. Bump, turn. That's the rhythm of 「うんてん」. Now remember the shapes. The winding road at the bottom left of 「運|うん」 is the road the wheel rolls along. The car on the left side of 「転|てん」 is the wheel itself, turning and turning. So whenever you see these two kanji together, picture that cat on the winding road, bumping and turning. That silly driver will bring the word straight back." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「運|うん」 means carry and 「転|てん」, with a car on its left, means turn, so it's moving along on turning wheels, the same 「転|てん」 as in 「自転車|じてんしゃ」.",
      "「運|うん」 appears in 「運動|うんどう」, exercise, and 「運ぶ|はこぶ」, to carry, while 「転|てん」 gives 「転ぶ|ころぶ」, to tumble, and the pair gives 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」, a driver.",
      "It's used for trains and machines too, as in 「運転見合わせ|うんてんみあわせ」, service suspended, and you can say 「運転できます|うんてんできます」 for I can drive.",
      "Picture a cat steering a giant wheel down a winding road, grunting oon at bumps while the wheel squeals ten at turns: 「うんてん」."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は{車|くるま}を[運転します|うんてんします|うんてんする]。",
    en: "My father drives a car." },
  "うん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the casual way to say yes. It's the everyday version of 「はい」. Use it with friends, family and people younger than you. With teachers, bosses or strangers, stick with 「はい」. Switching to 「うん」 with someone you should be polite to can sound too familiar. It also works as a listening sound while someone talks. Japanese listeners make little sounds of agreement all the time, to show they're following along. With friends, that sound is often 「うん、うん」. A way to remember it: it's basically the English mm-hmm, the little grunt of agreement. Just say it with a nod. Watch out for its opposite, 「ううん」, which means no. They look almost the same, but the extra 「う」 at the start and the change in pitch flip the meaning. Listen closely to the melody. It pairs naturally with casual speech forms: 「うん、いいよ」, yeah, sure, or 「うん、行く|うん、いく」, yeah, I'll go. Recap: the friendly, casual yes, like mm-hmm, and only with people close to you." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a little ladder of ways to say yes, and each step fits a different relationship. At the top is 「はい」, the safe, polite yes for teachers, customers and strangers. Just below it is 「ええ」, which many adults use in polite conversation. It sounds softer and a bit more relaxed than 「はい」, but it's still polite. Then comes 「うん」, the casual yes for friends and family. There's also 「そう」, which isn't really yes at all. It means that's so, and in casual talk it often works as a yes, as in 「うん、そう」, yeah, that's right. When you aren't sure which one to use, 「はい」 is never wrong. But using 「うん」 with your friends shows you feel close to them. So pick your yes to match the person in front of you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Listen to two friends chatting in Japanese and you'll hear this little sound constantly. Japanese has a habit called 「あいづち」, small listening noises that keep a conversation flowing. Silence on the phone can make the speaker worry you've hung up or stopped caring. So friends sprinkle in 「うん」, 「うんうん」 and 「そうそう」 every few seconds. It doesn't always mean I agree. Often it just means I'm listening, keep going. That trips up English speakers both ways. Japanese listeners may seem to agree with everything, when they're only showing attention. And English speakers who listen in silence can seem cold or distracted. A mini dialogue: 「きのう映画見たんだ|きのうえいがみたんだ」, I saw a movie yesterday. 「うん、うん」. 「すごくおもしろかった」, it was really good. 「へえ、いいね」, oh, nice. So try nodding along with a soft sound of your own." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the casual yes, like a friendly mm-hmm, for friends, family and younger people, while teachers and strangers get 「はい」. Don't mix it up with 「ううん」, which means no.",
      "Yes comes in a ladder: polite 「はい」, softer but still polite 「ええ」, and casual 「うん」, so pick the one that matches the person in front of you.",
      "Friends sprinkle 「うん」 and 「うんうん」 into conversation as 「あいづち」, little listening sounds that often just mean I'm listening, keep going, not I agree."
    ],
    ja: "[うん]、いいよ。",
    en: "Yeah, sure." },
  "ううん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the casual way to say no, the everyday version of 「いいえ」. It looks almost the same as 「うん」, yes, but there's an extra 「う」 at the start. It's also said with a different melody. That little change flips the meaning completely. A way to remember it: think of English uh-uh versus uh-huh. Two sounds that are almost identical, but one means no. The Japanese pair works just the same way. Use it with friends and family, never with your teacher or boss. With them, stick with 「いいえ」. It's often followed by a casual negative: 「ううん、行かない|ううん、いかない」, no, I'm not going. Careful not to mix it up with a long, thinking sound like 「うーん」, which is more like hmm, let me think. That one is just a pause, not a no. People usually shake their head a little as they say 「ううん」, which helps make it clear. Recap: the casual no, with an extra 「う」 and a dip in pitch, just like uh-uh." },
      { title: "Negative questions", text: "Here's where this casual no gets tricky for English speakers. Imagine your friend asks 「行かないの|いかないの」, you're not going? In English, you'd say no to agree: no, I'm not. In Japanese, the answer responds to whether the question was right. So if you really aren't going, you say 「うん、行かない|うん、いかない」, right, I'm not going. If you actually are going, you say 「ううん、行くよ|ううん、いくよ」, no, I'm going. It feels backwards at first. A trick is to hear the casual yes as that's right and the casual no as that's wrong. Then it all makes sense. The polite pair works the same way, with 「はい」 and 「いいえ」. This is one of the most common mix-ups, even for intermediate learners. So think of it as right or wrong, not yes or no." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Besides a plain no, this little word is a kind way to wave something off. When a friend says 「ごめんね」, sorry, you can answer 「ううん、だいじょうぶ」, no, it's fine. When a friend thanks you, 「ううん」 can mean oh, it was nothing. It brushes the apology or thanks away gently, with a little head shake. In polite speech, the same job is done by 「いいえ」, as in 「いいえ、どういたしまして」. Japanese also has a rougher no, 「いや」. It's casual and can sound blunt, so it's better to hear it than to use it for now. And in writing, especially in comics and text messages, you'll often see this word spelled with a small 「ぅ」 or a long dash to show the melody. So the casual no doubles as a gentle, friendly no worries." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the casual no, like uh-uh versus uh-huh, with an extra 「う」 and a different melody from 「うん」. Use 「いいえ」 with teachers and bosses.",
      "With a negative question like 「行かないの|いかないの」, answer whether the question was right: 「うん、行かない|うん、いかない」 if you're not going, 「ううん、行くよ|ううん、いくよ」 if you are.",
      "It gently waves off apologies and thanks, as in 「ううん、だいじょうぶ」, no, it's fine, working like a friendly no worries."
    ],
    ja: "[ううん]、{行かない|いかない}。",
    en: "No, I'm not going." },
  "いつも|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「いつ」, the question word when, plus the particle 「も」. When you add 「も」 to a question word and use a positive verb, it covers every case. So 「いつ」 plus 「も」 means at any time, every time: always. Compare it with 「何も|なにも」, which you also know. There, 「も」 with a negative verb means not anything. So 「も」 after a question word can mean all or none, depending on the verb. It sits at the top of a ladder of frequency words you've learned. 「いつも」, always. 「よく」, often. 「ときどき」, sometimes. 「あまり」 with a negative, not much. And 「ぜんぜん」 with a negative, not at all. Learning them as a ladder makes them easy to use. You usually put it before the verb, or near the start of the sentence: 「いつも七時に起きます|いつもしちじにおきます」, I always get up at seven. A common everyday phrase is 「いつもありがとう」, thanks for always being there. People say it to friends and family. Recap: when plus 「も」 means every time, which is always." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The question word 「いつ」, when, changes meaning with each little particle you attach. Add 「も」 and you get always. Add 「か」 instead and you get 「いつか」, someday, sometime. Add 「でも」 and you get 「いつでも」, anytime at all, as in 「いつでもどうぞ」, come anytime. The same trick works across the question words. 「だれか」 is someone, 「どこか」 is somewhere, and 「なにか」 is something. Now here's a sound-alike trap. 「いつか」, someday, sounds exactly like 「五日|いつか」, the fifth day of the month, which you also know. Context sorts them out. One is a calendar date, the other is a vague future. So 「いつ」 plus a particle gives a whole set: always, someday and anytime. Memorize them as a set and you get three words for the price of one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Add 「の」 and this word becomes a noun meaning the usual. At a café where they know you, you can say 「いつものお願いします|いつものおねがいします」, the usual, please. 「いつもの店|いつものみせ」 is our usual place, and 「いつも通り|いつもどおり」 means as usual. Now a grammar point that surprises English speakers. Put this word with a negative verb and you don't get never. You get always not. 「いつも朝ごはんを食べません|いつもあさごはんをたべません」 means I always skip breakfast. To say never, Japanese prefers other words, like 「ぜんぜん」 with a negative. Also notice that 「いつも」 doesn't need a particle after it. You don't say 「いつもに」. It just sits in the sentence. So learn the usual as well as always." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「いつ」, when, plus 「も」, which with a positive verb covers every case, so every time means always, at the top of the ladder above 「よく」 and 「ときどき」.",
      "Change the particle after 「いつ」 and the meaning changes: 「いつも」 is always, 「いつか」 is someday, and 「いつでも」 is anytime.",
      "Add 「の」 and it means the usual, as in 「いつものお願いします|いつものおねがいします」, and with a negative verb it means always not, rather than never."
    ],
    ja: "[いつも]{七時|しちじ}に{起きます|おきます}。",
    en: "I always get up at seven." },
  "おそく|遅く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the adjective 「遅い|おそい」, late or slow, with its final 「い」 changed to 「く」. That change turns an i-adjective into an adverb, a word that describes an action. So 「遅い|おそい」 describes something, and 「遅く|おそく」 describes doing something late. For example, 「遅く寝ました|おそくねました」, I went to bed late. 「遅く起きました|おそくおきました」, I got up late. You know the same pattern with 「早い|はやい」, early: 「早く|はやく」, early or quickly. So 「早く起きる|はやくおきる」 and 「遅く起きる|おそくおきる」 make a perfect pair. It also forms 「遅くなる|おそくなる」, to become late, which you've learned. A useful phrase is 「遅くまで|おそくまで」, until late: 「遅くまでべんきょうしました|おそくまでべんきょうしました」, I studied until late. The kanji 「遅|おそ」 uses its native reading. It has the movement radical, the winding road at the bottom left, about moving slowly along. Recap: change the 「い」 of late to 「く」, and you can do things late." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for late shows up in several words you know or will soon meet, each with a slightly different flavor. 「遅い|おそい」 is the adjective, late or slow. 「遅く|おそく」 is how you do something late. Then there's a verb, 「遅れる|おくれる」, to be late for something, like a train or a meeting. Notice the reading changes to 「おく」 there. 「遅刻する|ちこくする」 uses the Chinese-origin reading 「ち」 and means to be late for school or work, an official late mark. And 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」 means to miss a train or bus, literally to be late in getting on. One last tip. 「遅い|おそい」 means both late and slow, but its opposites are written differently. Early is 「早い|はやい」, and fast is 「速い|はやい」, with a different kanji. Same sound, two kanji. So one late kanji gives you a whole set of ways to be behind." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The most useful phrase with this word is an apology. When you arrive after everyone else, say 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」, sorry I'm late. It literally says sorry for having become late, and it's what Japanese people actually say. Another common pair is 「夜遅く|よるおそく」, late at night. 「夜遅くまで起きていました|よるおそくまでおきていました」, I was up until late at night. At work, you'll also hear 「遅くまでおつかれさま|おそくまでおつかれさま」, thanks for staying so late. And for students, the classic excuse is about the night before: 「遅く寝たから、眠いです|おそくねたから、ねむいです」, I went to bed late, so I'm sleepy. One mistake to avoid. Don't use this word for being late to an appointment as a fact about yourself. For that, use 「遅れる|おくれる」 or 「遅刻する|ちこくする」. So keep this word for doing something late, like sleeping, eating or working." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「遅|おそ」 has two parts. At the bottom left is the movement radical, the winding road you see in many kanji about going and coming. You know it from 「近い|ちかい」, near, and 「週|しゅう」, week. On the right, the modern shape looks like a sheep under a roof or a body shape. In the older, traditional form of this kanji, that right side was a different component: the kanji for rhinoceros. That part was chosen mostly for its sound, but it makes a perfect memory trick. Picture a huge rhinoceros plodding slowly down a winding road, holding up all the traffic behind it. Everyone arrives late, because of the rhino. The modern kanji simplified the rhino away, but you can still imagine it there. So remember it as the slow rhino on the road." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Changing the 「い」 of 「遅い|おそい」 to 「く」 turns it into an adverb, so you can do things late, as in 「遅く寝ました|おそくねました」, paired with 「早く|はやく」.",
      "The late kanji gives 「遅い|おそい」, late or slow, 「遅く|おそく」, doing something late, 「遅れる|おくれる」, to be late for something, and 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, late for school or work.",
      "The key phrase is 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」, sorry I'm late, and 「夜遅く|よるおそく」 means late at night, but use 「遅れる|おくれる」 for being late to an appointment.",
      "The old form of 「遅|おそ」 had a rhinoceros on the right, so picture a slow rhino plodding down the winding road and making everyone late."
    ],
    ja: "きのうは[遅く|おそく]{寝ました|ねました}。",
    en: "I went to bed late yesterday." },
  "かんぱい|乾杯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「乾|かん」, means dry. The second, 「杯|ぱい」, means a cup or a glass. Notice the tree radical on its left side, because cups were often made of wood. So the word literally says dry the cup, which is just like the English bottoms up. Drink until the cup is dry. About the sound: 「杯|はい」 starts with an h sound on its own, but after the 「ん」 of 「かん」, it changes to a p sound, 「ぱい」. Sound changes like this are common when kanji join together. The same kanji works as a counter for cups and glasses, so you'll hear it counting drinks later. Chinese uses the same pair of kanji for its toast, too. A cultural note: at a Japanese party, nobody drinks until the toast. Someone gives a short speech, everyone raises their glass, says 「乾杯|かんぱい」 together, and then drinks. It's polite to pour drinks for others, and let them pour for you, instead of filling your own glass. Recap: dry the cup, the Japanese bottoms up, and wait for the toast before you drink." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji have other readings you'll meet. The first, 「乾|かん」, means dry. With its native reading, it becomes the verb 「乾く|かわく」, to dry out, as in laundry drying in the sun. It's also in 「乾電池|かんでんち」, a dry cell battery, the kind you put in a remote. The second, 「杯|はい」, is the counter for cupfuls and glassfuls. Here's where the sounds get lively. One glass is 「一杯|いっぱい」, two is 「二杯|にはい」, three is 「三杯|さんばい」. So the h sound can become p or b, depending on the number in front. And 「いっぱい」 has a second life, too. It also means full, or a lot, as in my stomach is full. That makes sense: one full cup. So the dry kanji and the cup kanji both lead into everyday words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A Japanese toast follows a few unwritten rules. At company parties and big dinners, someone is chosen to lead it. That person may give a short speech, then raises their glass and calls out the word, and everyone repeats it. Often the first drink is beer for the whole table, a habit summed up in the phrase 「とりあえずビール」, beer for now. When you clink glasses with a boss or an older person, it's polite to hold your glass a little lower than theirs. That shows respect. After that, people keep an eye on each other's glasses. When a neighbor's glass is getting low, you pour for them, and they'll pour for you. If you can't drink alcohol, it's fine to toast with tea or juice. Nobody minds, as long as you join the toast. So raise your glass with everyone, and keep it a little lower for the boss." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「乾|かん」 means dry and 「杯|ぱい」 means cup, so it's dry the cup, the Japanese bottoms up, and nobody drinks until everyone says it together.",
      "「乾|かん」 becomes 「乾く|かわく」, to dry out, while 「杯|はい」 counts glassfuls, shifting to 「一杯|いっぱい」 and 「三杯|さんばい」, and 「いっぱい」 also means full.",
      "Someone leads the toast, often with 「とりあえずビール」 for the first round, and when clinking with a boss, hold your glass a little lower to show respect."
    ],
    ja: "{誕生日|たんじょうび}おめでとう！[乾杯|かんぱい]！",
    en: "Happy birthday! Cheers!" },
  "みんなで|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「みんな」, everyone, plus the particle 「で」. You know 「で」 for places and for tools, like eating with chopsticks. Here it marks how many people take part, or the group that does something. So 「みんなで」 means doing it as everyone, all together. You know a sibling: 「一人で|ひとりで」, alone, by yourself. It's the same 「で」. One person doing it, or everyone doing it. A typical use: 「みんなで食べましょう|みんなでたべましょう」, let's all eat together. Or 「みんなで歌いました|みんなでうたいました」, we all sang together. Watch for a difference. 「みんなが」 just means everyone does it, maybe separately. 「みんなで」 means they do it together, as one group. Everyone has lunch versus everyone has lunch together. A way to remember it: think of 「で」 as with, as in with everyone. It fits the Japanese love of group activities, like a barbecue by the river, karaoke with coworkers, or a class trip. Recap: everyone plus 「で」 means all together, the group version of by yourself." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The particle 「で」 after a group of people tells you who does something together, and it works with any number. 「一人で|ひとりで」 is alone. 「二人で|ふたりで」 is the two of us together. 「三人で|さんにんで」 is the three of us. 「家族で|かぞくで」 means as a family, as in 「家族で旅行しました|かぞくでりょこうしました」, we traveled as a family. And 「自分で|じぶんで」 means by yourself, without help. The same 「で」 also marks a total. 「全部で|ぜんぶで」 means all together, in total, which you hear at the register: 「全部で千円です|ぜんぶでせんえんです」, that's a thousand yen in total. So 「で」 bundles things into one unit, whether people or prices. Remember it as the bundling 「で」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "First, a politeness note about the base word. 「みんな」 is the everyday word for everyone. When a teacher or a speaker addresses a group politely, they say 「みなさん」 instead, as in 「みなさん、こんにちは」. You can't really put 「さん」 on yourself, so when you talk about your own group of friends, 「みんな」 is the natural choice. Now, where you'll hear 「みんなで」. It's the go-to word for invitations and plans: 「みんなで行こう|みんなでいこう」, let's all go. 「みんなで写真をとりましょう|みんなでしゃしんをとりましょう」, let's take a picture all together. At the end of a party, someone might say 「みんなで片付けよう|みんなでかたづけよう」, let's all clean up. Teachers love it, too: 「みんなで読みましょう|みんなでよみましょう」, let's read it together. So it's the word that pulls a group into one activity." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「みんな」 plus 「で」, marking the group that does something, so it means all together, the group version of 「一人で|ひとりで」, alone.",
      "The 「で」 after a group bundles people into one unit, as in 「二人で|ふたりで」 or 「家族で|かぞくで」, and the same 「で」 makes a total in 「全部で|ぜんぶで」.",
      "It's the go-to word for invitations like 「みんなで行こう|みんなでいこう」, while a speaker addressing a group politely uses 「みなさん」 instead."
    ],
    ja: "[みんなで]{歌|うた}を{歌いました|うたいました}。",
    en: "We all sang a song together." },
  "ざんねん|残念": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「残|ざん」, means to remain or be left over. The second, 「念|ねん」, means a thought or feeling. Look at its shape: on top is 「今|いま」, now, and at the bottom is the heart, 「心|こころ」. A feeling in your heart right now. That bottom heart is the same one in 「思う|おもう」, to think. So together, the word is a feeling that remains, something that stays with you. That's regret or disappointment. You use it to react when something doesn't work out. If a friend can't come to your party, you say 「残念ですね|ざんねんですね」, that's too bad. If you didn't win, 「残念でした|ざんねんでした」. It's also a gentle way to turn down an invitation. Starting with 「残念ですが|ざんねんですが」, unfortunately, sounds kind and polite. A common mistake is to use it for serious tragedies. It's for everyday disappointment, like missing out or bad luck. For deeper loss, Japanese uses other expressions. Recap: a feeling left behind in your heart, the everyday that's too bad." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「残|ざん」, has a radical on its left that looks a bit like a little evening shape with a stroke on top. It's often called the bone or death radical, and it appears in kanji connected with remains and endings. On the right is a shape linked to cutting. So 「残|ざん」 is about what's left over. With its native reading it becomes 「残る|のこる」, to remain, and 「残す|のこす」, to leave something behind, like leaving food on your plate. You also know 「残業|ざんぎょう」, overtime, which is literally leftover work. The second kanji, 「念|ねん」, appears in 「記念|きねん」, a commemoration, and 「記念日|きねんび」, an anniversary, a day you keep in mind. So this word lives in a family of leftovers and lasting thoughts." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this word in several tones. A gentle 「残念ですね|ざんねんですね」 shows sympathy. 「残念ながら|ざんねんながら」, unfortunately, is a softer, more formal way to deliver bad news, and it starts many polite refusals and announcements. For example, 「残念ながら、雨でしあいは中止です|ざんねんながら、あめでしあいはちゅうしです」, unfortunately, the game is cancelled because of rain. On Japanese quiz shows, you'll hear 「残念でした|ざんねんでした」 with a playful, teasing tone when someone gets an answer wrong. Friends use it that way too. In casual slang, younger people even call someone a 「残念な人|ざんねんなひと」, a person who's a bit of a letdown, like someone handsome but with terrible manners. So this word can be kind, formal or teasing, depending on the voice." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Imagine you've been saving the last slice of cake in the fridge all day. You come home, open the door, and there's just an empty plate with a few crumbs. Your roommate is on the couch, eating it. You groan, ZAN, like a heavy door slamming. Then you sigh, nen, as your head drops. Zan, nen. The slam, then the sigh. That's the sound of disappointment. Now tie it to the kanji. The first one means leftover, and the leftover cake is exactly what you lost. The second one has a heart at the bottom, and your heart is still feeling it. So whenever something falls through, picture that empty plate, the slam, and the sigh." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「残|ざん」 means remain and 「念|ねん」, now over a heart, means a feeling, so it's a feeling that stays with you, the everyday that's too bad, not for serious tragedies.",
      "「残|ざん」 is about leftovers, as in 「残る|のこる」, to remain, and 「残業|ざんぎょう」, overtime, while 「念|ねん」 appears in 「記念日|きねんび」, an anniversary.",
      "It can be sympathetic in 「残念ですね|ざんねんですね」, formal in 「残念ながら|ざんねんながら」, unfortunately, or playful in a quiz show's 「残念でした|ざんねんでした」.",
      "Picture finding an empty plate where your saved cake was: the door-slam ZAN and the sigh nen, with the leftover kanji and the heart that still feels it."
    ],
    ja: "あした{来ません|きません}か。[残念|ざんねん]ですね。",
    en: "You're not coming tomorrow? That's too bad." },
  "まだ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This native word has two main uses, and Genki focuses on one of them. With a negative verb, it means not yet: 「まだ食べていません|まだたべていません」, I haven't eaten yet. Notice the form. In Japanese, not yet uses the te-form plus 「いません」, not the past tense. A very common mistake is to say 「まだ食べませんでした|まだたべませんでした」. That sounds wrong. The idea is that you're still in the state of not having eaten. Its partner is 「もう」, already. So the question is 「もう食べましたか|もうたべましたか」, have you eaten already? If yes, 「はい、食べました|はい、たべました」. If no, the short answer is 「いいえ、まだです」, no, not yet. With a positive sentence, 「まだ」 means still: 「まだ学生です|まだがくせいです」, I'm still a student. A way to remember it: Ma, dinner's not ready yet. Picture someone asking if it's done, and the answer is, not yet. Recap: with a negative, not yet, and remember the te-form with 「いません」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "These two little words, 「まだ」 and 「もう」, each flip their meaning depending on the verb. Here's the full picture. 「まだ」 with a positive verb means still: 「まだ寝ています|まだねています」, he's still sleeping. 「まだ」 with a negative means not yet: 「まだ起きていません|まだおきていません」, he hasn't gotten up yet. Now its partner. 「もう」 with a positive means already: 「もう起きました|もうおきました」, he's already up. 「もう」 with a negative means not anymore: 「もう食べません|もうたべません」, I won't eat any more. Notice how they pair up. Still is the opposite of not anymore, and not yet is the opposite of already. Drawing these four boxes on paper helps a lot. So keep this pair together in your head." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's a phrase you'll hear from Japanese people again and again. When someone praises your Japanese, saying 「日本語が上手ですね|にほんごがじょうずですね」, the classic humble answer is 「いいえ、まだまだです」. It means no, I still have a long way to go. Doubling the word makes it stronger: still, still. Japanese people say it about their own cooking, sports, English and work, and it's considered a modest, mature response. Accepting praise with a simple thank you can sound a little too confident. The word also shows up in time phrases. 「まだ時間があります|まだじかんがあります」 means there's still time. And when someone offers you more food, 「まだ食べられます|まだたべられます」, I can still eat, means yes please. So for compliments on your Japanese, you now have the perfect humble reply." }
    ],
    short: [
      "With a negative it means not yet, using the te-form plus 「いません」, as in 「まだ食べていません|まだたべていません」, never the past tense. With a positive it means still.",
      "「まだ」 means still or not yet, and its partner 「もう」 means already or not anymore, so still pairs with not anymore and not yet with already.",
      "When someone praises your Japanese, the classic humble reply is 「いいえ、まだまだです」, I still have a long way to go."
    ],
    ja: "[まだ]{食べていません|たべていません}。",
    en: "I haven't eaten yet." },
  "〜について|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You put this right after a noun to mean about or concerning that topic. It's built from the particle 「に」 and the te-form of an old verb meaning to attach or follow. So the idea is attached to that topic, sticking to it. You don't need to use the verb, just the whole chunk. Some typical patterns. 「日本について話しましょう|にほんについてはなしましょう」, let's talk about Japan. 「かぞくについて書きます|かぞくについてかきます」, I'll write about my family. It works with verbs like talk, write, think, read and ask. So it's great for essays, presentations and class discussions. To modify a noun, add 「の」: 「日本についての本|にほんについてのほん」, a book about Japan. A common mistake for English speakers is to use it for casual chatter where English about means roughly, like around ten o'clock. For approximate times, Japanese uses 「ごろ」 instead. 「について」 is only for topics. Recap: noun plus 「について」 means about that topic, perfect for talks and essays." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This chunk is flexible, and a few extra pieces change how it works. Add 「は」 to make the topic stand out: 「しゅくだいについては、あした話します|しゅくだいについては、あしたはなします」, as for the homework, I'll talk about it tomorrow. Add 「の」 to describe a noun: 「日本の音楽についてのレポート|にほんのおんがくについてのレポート」, a report about Japanese music. It also goes with a verb you'll use a lot for essays: 「調べる|しらべる」, to look into. 「日本の祭りについて調べました|にほんのまつりについてしらべました」, I researched Japanese festivals. Don't confuse it with 「の」 alone. 「日本の本|にほんのほん」 can mean a book from Japan, while 「日本についての本|にほんについてのほん」 clearly means a book about Japan. So use this chunk whenever you mean the subject matter." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The 「ついて」 part is the te-form of the verb 「つく」, which means to attach or to stick to something. The same verb shows up in modern Japanese in phrases like a stain sticking to your shirt, or a light coming on. Long ago, people used it in the sense of following something closely, staying attached to it. Add the particle 「に」, and the phrase meant sticking close to this. Over time, the whole chunk froze into a fixed expression meaning concerning or about. That's very similar to English. We say a report on Japan, or a talk on music, using on, a word about touching. Japanese uses attached to. So when you talk about something, picture your words stuck tightly to the topic." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Put it after a noun to mean about that topic, as in 「日本について話しましょう|にほんについてはなしましょう」, but use 「ごろ」 for approximate times.",
      "Add 「は」 to highlight the topic or 「の」 to describe a noun, since 「日本についての本|にほんについてのほん」 clearly means a book about Japan, unlike 「日本の本|にほんのほん」.",
      "The 「ついて」 part comes from 「つく」, to stick to, so talking about something means your words stick to the topic, like English on."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}[について]{話しましょう|はなしましょう}。",
    en: "Let's talk about Japan." },
  "〜ど|〜度": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a counter for degrees, placed after a number. The kanji 「度|ど」 uses its Chinese-origin reading. Its basic idea is a measure or a degree, how much of something there is. So 「三十度|さんじゅうど」 is thirty degrees, and 「何度|なんど」 asks how many degrees. You'll pair it with 「気温|きおん」, temperature: 「気温は二十度です|きおんはにじゅうどです」. Japan uses Celsius. Thirty degrees is a hot summer day, twenty is pleasant, and zero is freezing. If you're used to Fahrenheit, that can be confusing at first. You also use it for a fever: thirty-eight degrees is a real fever. Here's a sibling you know: 「もう一度|もういちど」, one more time. In that phrase, the same kanji counts times. One time, two times. So 「度|ど」 can measure degrees or count times. For below zero, Japanese uses a word for minus before the number, so cold winter forecasts are easy to follow. Recap: number plus 「度|ど」 is degrees, and the same kanji counts times in one more time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji does a lot of work, and you've already seen its counting-times side. 「一度|いちど」 is once. 「二度|にど」 is twice. 「何度も|なんども」 means many times, again and again. 「もう一度|もういちど」 is one more time. Soon you'll meet 「今度|こんど」, which means next time, or this time, depending on context. 「今度の日曜日|こんどのにちようび」 is this coming Sunday. Then there's the measuring side. 「温度|おんど」 means temperature in general, like the temperature of water or a room. Air temperature, the weather kind, is 「気温|きおん」. And with a fever, Japanese asks 「熱は何度ですか|ねつはなんどですか」, what's your temperature? So the kanji measures heat and counts times." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you grew up with Fahrenheit, Japanese weather forecasts take some getting used to. Here's a handy rule. To roughly convert Celsius to Fahrenheit, double the number and add thirty. So 「二十度|にじゅうど」 is about seventy, and 「三十五度|さんじゅうごど」, a brutal summer day in Tokyo, is about a hundred. In Japan, a day that reaches thirty-five or more even has a special name in the forecasts, meaning an extremely hot day. For body temperature, normal is around thirty-six and a half. Anything from thirty-seven and a half up counts as a fever, and many schools and offices ask you to stay home. So when a Japanese friend says they have 「三十八度|さんじゅうはちど」, they're really sick. Double it and add thirty, and the numbers stop being scary." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Put it after a number for degrees Celsius, as in 「三十度|さんじゅうど」 for a hot day, and the same kanji counts times in 「もう一度|もういちど」.",
      "The kanji counts times, as in 「一度|いちど」 and 「何度も|なんども」, and measures heat, as in 「温度|おんど」 and 「熱は何度ですか|ねつはなんどですか」.",
      "To convert roughly to Fahrenheit, double the Celsius number and add thirty, so 「三十八度|さんじゅうはちど」 means a friend is really sick."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[三十五度|さんじゅうごど|〜ど]です。",
    en: "It's thirty-five degrees today." },
  "どう|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the question word how. It belongs to the family of words that start with 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」 and 「ど」. You know 「これ」, 「それ」, 「あれ」 and 「どれ」. In the same way, 「こう」 means this way, 「そう」 means that way, 「ああ」 means that way over there, and 「どう」 asks which way, or how. That's why 「そうですね」 means that's right: it's literally that way, isn't it. The most common use is asking how something is: 「どうですか」, how is it? Or in the past, 「どうでしたか」, how was it? 「テストはどうでしたか」, how was the test? It's also used for offering things: 「コーヒーはどうですか」, how about some coffee? And it builds other question words. 「どうして」 means why. In very polite situations, like in a restaurant or with a guest, you'll hear a fancier word for how in its place. But 「どう」 is perfectly fine for everyday polite speech. Recap: the 「ど」 question word for how, used to ask how things are and to offer things." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This question word builds a whole set of useful expressions. 「どうして」 asks why. 「どうやって」 asks how, in the sense of by what method: 「どうやって行きますか|どうやっていきますか」, how do you get there? 「どうぞ」, please go ahead, and 「どうも」, thanks, also start with the same sound and are related to it. Then there's the difference between 「どう」 and 「どんな」. 「どんな」 goes right before a noun: 「どんな人ですか|どんなひとですか」, what kind of person is he? 「どう」 stands on its own as the comment: 「その人はどうですか|そのひとはどうですか」, how is that person? A good rule: if a noun comes next, use 「どんな」. So the 「ど」 family gives you how, why, what kind and by what means." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A few short phrases with this word carry a lot of everyday weight. If a friend looks upset, ask 「どうしたの」, what's wrong? or in polite speech 「どうしたんですか」. At a doctor's office, the doctor might start with 「どうしましたか」, what seems to be the problem? When you're stuck and panicking a little, you'll hear people mutter 「どうしよう」, what should I do? It's what someone says when they've missed the last train. Then there's the polite upgrade. In stores, restaurants and when offering things to guests, you'll hear 「いかがですか」 instead of 「どうですか」. 「お茶はいかがですか|おちゃはいかがですか」, would you care for some tea? It means the same thing, just more refined. So learn these little phrases, and you'll understand a lot of real conversations." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the 「ど」 member of the 「こう」, 「そう」, 「ああ」 family, asking which way, or how, as in 「どうですか」 for how it is or to offer something.",
      "It builds 「どうして」, why, and 「どうやって」, by what method, while 「どんな」 goes before a noun and 「どう」 stands on its own as the comment.",
      "Short phrases carry a lot: 「どうしたの」 asks what's wrong, 「どうしよう」 means what should I do, and stores use the politer 「いかがですか」."
    ],
    ja: "テストは[どう]でしたか。",
    en: "How was the test?" },
  "かいしゃいん|会社員": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first two make 「会社|かいしゃ」, a company. 「会|かい」 means to meet or gather. You know it from 「会う|あう」, to meet, which uses the native reading. 「社|しゃ」 means a company or society, and you also see it in 「神社|じんじゃ」, a shrine, and 「社長|しゃちょう」, a company president. So a company is a gathering of people working together. The last kanji, 「員|いん」, means member, a person on the staff. You know it from 「駅員|えきいん」, a station attendant, a member of the station staff. Put it together: a member of a company. That's an office worker, a company employee. It's the standard answer for someone who works at a company and isn't a boss. On forms in Japan, it's one of the job boxes you can tick. You'll also hear a loanword for male office workers, built from English salary and man. A cultural note: being a company employee in Japan has traditionally meant long hours and evenings out with coworkers. That's slowly changing. Recap: company plus member equals office worker, with the same member kanji as station staff." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The member kanji at the end is a handy job maker. Put it after a workplace and you get a person who works there. 「店員|てんいん」 is a shop clerk, a member of the shop. 「駅員|えきいん」 is station staff. 「銀行員|ぎんこういん」 is a bank employee. 「公務員|こうむいん」 is a government worker, a civil servant, which you'll hear a lot because it's a very popular and stable career in Japan. The company kanji has its own family. 「社長|しゃちょう」 is the company president, the head of the company. And when 「会社|かいしゃ」 comes second in a compound, the first sound gets voiced, so a travel agency is 「旅行会社|りょこうがいしゃ」. So the member kanji turns places into people, which makes many job words easy to guess." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When Japanese people introduce their job, they often keep it vague. Instead of naming a job title, many simply say 「会社員です|かいしゃいんです」, I'm an office worker, or they name the company: 「〜で働いています|〜ではたらいています」, I work at such and such. People also call their own company 「うちの会社|うちのかいしゃ」, our company, even when talking to outsiders. You'll also hear the loanword 「サラリーマン」, made in Japan from English salary and man. It's very common in conversation, though it mostly suggests a man in a suit. On official forms, the neutral word is the one in this lesson. Office workers also exchange business cards, called 「名刺|めいし」, when they meet someone new. They hold the card out with both hands, facing the other person. So this is the plain, neutral way to say I work for a company." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「会社|かいしゃ」 is a company, a gathering of people, and 「員|いん」 means member, as in 「駅員|えきいん」, so it's a company member, an office worker.",
      "The member kanji turns workplaces into people: 「店員|てんいん」, a shop clerk, 「銀行員|ぎんこういん」, a bank employee, and 「公務員|こうむいん」, a civil servant.",
      "Many people describe their job simply as 「会社員です|かいしゃいんです」, while the made-in-Japan 「サラリーマン」 is common in conversation but suggests a man in a suit."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[会社員|かいしゃいん]です。",
    en: "My older brother is an office worker." },
  "しごと|仕事": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji. The first, 「仕|し」, means to serve or to do work for someone. On its left is the narrow person radical, so it's a person who serves. The second, 「事|ごと」, means a thing or a matter. Its native reading is 「こと」, but in this word it becomes 「ごと」, with the sound softened because it comes second. You know the same kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「じ」 in 「用事|ようじ」, an errand, and 「返事|へんじ」, a reply. So the word is the things you do in service, your work. It covers a job, a task, and an occupation. Use it for both. 「仕事があります|しごとがあります」 can mean I have work to do today, and 「仕事は何ですか|しごとはなんですか」 asks what someone's job is. To be polite, add 「お」: 「お仕事は|おしごとは」. A common English speaker mistake is to use it like a countable job title. Japanese tends to say 「会社員|かいしゃいん」 or 「先生|せんせい」 for the role, and 「仕事|しごと」 for the work itself. After work, people say 「おつかれさまでした」 to thank each other for their hard work. Recap: things you do in service, your job and your work." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「事|じ」 or 「こと」, means a thing or a matter, and it pops up all over Japanese. With its native reading, 「事|こと」 means thing, an abstract matter, as in something that happened. With the Chinese-origin reading, it makes 「食事|しょくじ」, a meal, an eating matter. 「火事|かじ」, a fire, is literally a fire matter, and notice the reading is just 「じ」 here. 「大事|だいじ」 means important, a big matter, and you know it from 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care. The first kanji, 「仕|し」, is in 「仕方|しかた」, a way of doing something. That gives the famous phrase 「仕方がない|しかたがない」, it can't be helped. People say it when something bad happens that nobody can change. So the work word sits in a big family of matters." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the verbs this word likes. To work is 「仕事をする|しごとをする」. To go to work is 「仕事に行く|しごとにいく」. Work finishing is 「仕事が終わる|しごとがおわる」, and something you'll hear a lot is 「仕事が忙しい|しごとがいそがしい」, work is busy. If you want to ask someone politely what they do, the gentle way is 「お仕事は何ですか|おしごとはなんですか」. In the evening, many office workers stay late, doing 「残業|ざんぎょう」, overtime. And after work, coworkers sometimes go drinking together, at a party called a 「飲み会|のみかい」. Talk about the job flows more freely there. On payday, the money you get is 「給料|きゅうりょう」, your salary. So with this word you can talk about your whole working day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「仕|し」, a person who serves, plus 「事|ごと」, a thing, softened from 「こと」, gives the things you do in service, your work, a job or a task.",
      "「事|じ」 means a matter, as in 「食事|しょくじ」, a meal, and 「大事|だいじ」, important, while 「仕|し」 appears in 「仕方がない|しかたがない」, it can't be helped.",
      "Talk about your workday with 「仕事をする|しごとをする」 and 「仕事が忙しい|しごとがいそがしい」, and ask politely with 「お仕事は何ですか|おしごとはなんですか」."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}の[仕事|しごと]はいそがしいです。",
    en: "My father's job keeps him busy." },
  "カラオケ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one's special, because it's a Japanese invention, and the word went from Japanese into English, not the other way around. It's made of two parts. 「から」 is a native Japanese word meaning empty. You know it from 「空手|からて」, karate, literally empty hand, fighting with no weapon. 「オケ」 is short for 「オーケストラ」, orchestra, a loanword from English. Japanese loves chopping long loanwords down to short ones. So the word means empty orchestra. The music is all there, but the singer's part is empty, so you fill it in. Karaoke machines spread in Japan in the 1970s, and from there to the rest of the world. Notice how English speakers say it compared with Japanese, which keeps each beat short and even. A cultural note: in Japan, people usually go to karaoke boxes, private rooms you rent by the hour with friends. Nobody has to sing in front of strangers. It's a popular way to end a night out with coworkers or classmates, and you go with 「行く|いく」: 「カラオケに行く|カラオケにいく」. Recap: an empty orchestra, the same empty as in karate, and a gift from Japan to the world." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Going to karaoke in Japan is a ritual with its own vocabulary. You go to a karaoke place, often a multi-story building full of small private rooms, with friends or coworkers. You pay by the half hour or hour, and drinks are often included or ordered by phone from the room. A tablet lets you search and queue songs. The verb is 「歌う|うたう」, to sing: 「カラオケで歌いましょう|カラオケでうたいましょう」. Everyone has a signature song, and Japanese has a fun word for it: 「十八番|おはこ」. It's written with the numbers eighteen, but read in a special way. It's often linked to a famous set of eighteen Kabuki plays, though the full story is debated. Many people also go to karaoke alone, just to practice. So grab a friend, book a room, and pick your signature song." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "English speakers usually say this word with a squashed middle, something like carry-okey, with stress on the O. In Japanese, every beat gets the same length and there's no big stress. Four beats: 「か」, 「ら」, 「お」, 「け」. The O is its own clear beat, not blended into the A before it. Say it slowly, clap four times, and you'll hear it. Japanese people sometimes don't recognize the English version at first. Also notice the mixed spelling. It's written all in katakana, even though the first half, empty, is a native Japanese word. That's because the whole thing is treated as a modern, foreign-feeling word. So clap out four even beats, and you'll sound like a local." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's a Japanese invention: 「から」, empty, as in 「空手|からて」, plus 「オケ」, short for orchestra, so an empty orchestra waiting for your voice.",
      "In Japan you rent a private room with friends, sing with 「歌う|うたう」, and everyone has a signature song, 「十八番|おはこ」.",
      "Japanese gives it four even beats, 「か」, 「ら」, 「お」, 「け」, with no big stress, unlike the squashed English carry-okey."
    ],
    ja: "{友|とも}だちと[カラオケ]に{行きました|いきました}。",
    en: "I went to karaoke with friends." },
  "きせつ|季節": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「季|き」, means season. On top is the grain radical, a plant with a drooping head of grain, and below it is 「子|こ」, child. A way to remember it: the grain grows and ripens with the seasons. The second, 「節|せつ」, has the bamboo radical on top. It first meant a joint or node of bamboo, the rings that divide a bamboo stalk into sections. From there it came to mean a division or a period of time. So the year is like a bamboo stalk, divided into sections, and each section is a season. The four seasons you know are 「春|はる」, 「夏|なつ」, 「秋|あき」 and 「冬|ふゆ」. All four use native readings. A typical question: 「どの季節が好きですか|どのきせつがすきですか」, which season do you like? A cultural note: Japan is very proud of its four distinct seasons. Food, decorations and even snacks change with each one: cherry blossoms in spring, fireworks in summer, red leaves in fall, and hot pots in winter. Traditional poems often include a seasonal word, so every reader knows what time of year it is. Recap: grain that grows with the year, and a bamboo joint that divides it, give you season." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji show up in other seasonal words. The first one appears in 「四季|しき」, the four seasons, a word Japanese people use with pride. The second one, 「節|せつ」, appears in 「節分|せつぶん」, a festival in early February. Its name literally means season division, because it falls on the day before spring starts in the old calendar. With its native reading, the same kanji becomes 「ふし」, a joint or knot, like the joint of a bamboo stalk or a knuckle. In haiku, poets use a 「季語|きご」, a season word. And in cooking, you'll hear 「旬|しゅん」, the time when a food is at its peak. Japanese menus often highlight foods that are in season. So the season kanji link festivals, poems and food." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Let's look at 「節分|せつぶん」, the festival of the season division. It happens around the third of February. Families throw roasted soybeans, either out the door or at someone wearing a demon mask, usually dad. As they throw, they shout 「鬼は外|おにはそと」, demons out, and 「福は内|ふくはうち」, good luck in. Then each person eats the same number of beans as their age, for good health. Some people also eat a long, thick sushi roll, facing the lucky direction for that year, in silence. The festival exists because the old calendar treated this day as the end of winter. The next day is the official start of spring, even though it's usually still cold. So in Japan, the change of season is something you can celebrate with a handful of beans." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「季|き」 shows grain ripening with the seasons, and 「節|せつ」, a bamboo joint, divides the year into sections, so together they mean season.",
      "The kanji appear in 「四季|しき」, the four seasons, 「節分|せつぶん」, the season division festival, and 「季語|きご」, the season word in haiku.",
      "At 「節分|せつぶん」 in early February, families throw roasted soybeans shouting 「鬼は外|おにはそと」 and 「福は内|ふくはうち」, then eat one bean for each year of their age."
    ],
    ja: "どの[季節|きせつ]が{好き|すき}ですか。",
    en: "Which season do you like?" },
  "はる|春": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here the kanji is read with its native Japanese reading. Look at the bottom of 「春|はる」 and you will find the sun, 「日|ひ」. The top part is an old shape whose history scholars still argue about, so here is just a hook instead. A way to remember it: picture new plants pushing up toward a warming sun. Spring is one of a set of four you already know. 「夏|なつ」 is summer, 「秋|あき」 is fall, and 「冬|ふゆ」 is winter, and all four are short native words. You also know 「休み|やすみ」, a break, so 「春休み|はるやすみ」 is spring break. In Japan spring matters even more than usual, because the school year and the working year both start in April. So spring means cherry blossoms, new classes, new jobs and fresh starts all at once. People hold picnics under the blossoms, called 「はなみ」, flower viewing. To recap: the sun at the bottom, and the season when everything begins." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Besides the native reading, this kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「しゅん」, which you'll meet in compounds. 「春分の日|しゅんぶんのひ」 is the spring equinox, a national holiday in March. A lovely word is 「青春|せいしゅん」, youth, the springtime of life. It's literally blue spring, or green spring, since the blue kanji also covers the fresh green of new plants. Japanese movies and songs about school days are full of this word. Then there's the four-season set written together: 「春夏秋冬|しゅんかしゅうとう」, spring, summer, fall, winter, all in Chinese-origin readings. And watch for 「春休み|はるやすみ」, spring break, which uses the native reading again. So two readings: the native one for the season, and the Chinese-origin one inside compounds." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. In Japan, spring brings beautiful cherry blossoms, but also massive amounts of tree pollen, especially from cedar trees. Millions of people suffer from hay fever, called 「花粉症|かふんしょう」. So picture it. You're standing under the cherry blossoms on a sunny April day. The pollen hits you, your nose tickles, and you sneeze: HAAA... ROO! HA-RU. A spring sneeze. Everyone around you is wearing masks and sneezing too. The whole park is going ha-ru, ha-ru. Now look at the kanji. The sun at the bottom is the warm spring sun shining down on all that pollen. So every time you sneeze in spring, remember that the sneeze itself says spring." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese spring has its own small seasonal events, and the weather forecast tracks them carefully. In late winter, the first strong, warm southern wind of the year is called 「春一番|はるいちばん」, spring number one. When it blows, people know warm weather is coming. Then comes the cherry blossom front. The forecast shows a map line moving north, from the southern islands in March to the far north in May, predicting when the blossoms will open in each city. Companies and families reserve spots in parks for flower viewing parties, sometimes sending a junior employee early in the morning to save space with a blue tarp. And because the school year starts in April, spring is also when everyone wears new uniforms and suits. So spring in Japan has a wind, a moving front and a fresh start." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The sun, 「日|ひ」, sits at the bottom of the kanji, so picture new plants pushing up toward a warming sun. In Japan, spring is also when the school and work year begins.",
      "The everyday season is 「はる」, but in compounds it's 「しゅん」, as in 「春分の日|しゅんぶんのひ」, the spring equinox, and 「青春|せいしゅん」, youth.",
      "Picture yourself under cherry blossoms when the pollen hits and you sneeze HAAA ROO, a spring sneeze, 「はる」.",
      "Spring brings 「春一番|はるいちばん」, the first warm southern wind, a cherry blossom front moving north on forecast maps, and a fresh start in April."
    ],
    ja: "[春|はる]はさくらがきれいです。",
    en: "Cherry blossoms are pretty in spring." },
  "あき|秋": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native reading of the kanji. 「秋|あき」 has two clear parts. On the left is a stalk of grain, the shape called 「のぎへん」, which shows up in many kanji about crops. On the right is fire, 「火|ひ」, the same fire you know from 「火曜日|かようび」, Tuesday. A way to remember it: picture fields of ripe grain glowing red and orange at harvest, as if they were on fire. Fall is harvest time, so grain and fire make a nice picture. It sits with the other seasons, 「春|はる」, 「夏|なつ」 and 「冬|ふゆ」. In Japan fall is famous for red maple leaves, for good food, and for being pleasantly 「涼しい|すずしい」, cool, after the hot summer. People even talk about fall as the season for eating and the season for reading. To recap: grain plus fire, the glowing harvest season." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading too, 「しゅう」. It's in 「秋分の日|しゅうぶんのひ」, the autumn equinox holiday in September, and in the four-season phrase 「春夏秋冬|しゅんかしゅうとう」. A tasty member of the family is 「秋刀魚|さんま」, a slim, silver fish eaten grilled in the fall. It's written with the kanji for autumn, sword and fish, because it's shaped like a blade, and it's read in a special way you just have to learn. And here's a beautiful one. Put the heart kanji under this one, and you get a kanji meaning sorrow or melancholy. Fall plus heart equals a wistful, sad feeling. Poets have loved that connection for centuries. So the fall kanji brings holidays, grilled fish and a touch of sadness." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. It's a crisp fall afternoon, and you're walking through a park covered in red and yellow leaves. You reach into your pocket. Your house key is gone. You look down at the giant pile of leaves you just kicked through. You groan, AH, KEY! A-KI. Now you're on your hands and knees, digging through the leaves, which are glowing orange like fire in the setting sun. That's the fire on the right side of the kanji. And the leaves are mixed with stalks of dry grain, the radical on the left. Ah, key! Fall. So whenever you see a pile of fall leaves, check your pocket for your key." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Fall in Japan has a long list of things to enjoy. Like cherry blossom viewing in spring, there's leaf viewing in fall. The word 「紅葉|こうよう」 means the changing leaves, and the same kanji can be read 「もみじ」, the Japanese maple. People travel to Kyoto and mountain areas just to see them. In early fall, there is moon viewing, 「月見|つきみ」, with rice dumplings and pampas grass. Food matters too. Chestnuts, sweet potatoes, matsutake mushrooms, and grilled saury fish all mark the season. People say 「食欲の秋|しょくよくのあき」, fall of the appetite. You'll also hear phrases calling it the fall of reading, the fall of sports, and the fall of art. Schools hold sports days in the fall weather. So fall in Japan is a season of leaves, moons, books and good food." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is grain on the left and fire, 「火|ひ」, on the right, so picture harvest fields glowing like fire. Fall in Japan means red leaves, good food and cool 「涼しい|すずしい」 weather.",
      "In compounds it's read 「しゅう」, as in 「秋分の日|しゅうぶんのひ」, and the family includes 「秋刀魚|さんま」, a blade-shaped fall fish, plus a kanji where fall over heart means melancholy.",
      "Picture kicking through a pile of fiery orange leaves and realizing your house key is gone: AH, KEY, 「あき」.",
      "Fall brings leaf viewing for 「紅葉|こうよう」, moon viewing, 「月見|つきみ」, and so much seasonal food that people call it 「食欲の秋|しょくよくのあき」."
    ],
    ja: "[秋|あき]はすずしいです。",
    en: "Fall is cool." },
  "ぎゅうにゅう|牛乳": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings here. The first, 「牛|ぎゅう」, means cow. On its own, with the native reading, a cow is 「うし」. The shape is said to be a simple picture of a cow's head with horns, which makes it easy to spot. The second, 「乳|にゅう」, means milk, the milk that a mother animal gives. Put them together and you get cow milk, which is exactly what this word is. You will see the cow kanji again on menus, because beef is 「牛肉|ぎゅうにく」, cow meat. In everyday talk people also say 「ミルク」, from English milk, but on the carton at the supermarket it usually says this kanji word. A small cultural note: Japanese school lunches traditionally come with a little carton of milk every day, so almost every adult in Japan grew up with this word printed in front of them. To recap: cow plus milk, plain cow milk." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The cow kanji is all over Japanese menus. 「牛肉|ぎゅうにく」 is beef. 「牛丼|ぎゅうどん」 is a bowl of rice topped with thin slices of simmered beef and onion, a cheap and famous fast food. The milk kanji has relatives too. 「豆乳|とうにゅう」 is soy milk, literally bean milk, which you'll find in every convenience store in Japan, often in flavors like banana or coffee. 「乳製品|にゅうせいひん」 means dairy products, a word you'll see on food labels. Now for the near-synonym. 「ミルク」, from English, is used for milk in coffee or tea, for baby formula, and in drink names like 「ミルクティー」. But the plain cow milk you buy in a carton is usually this kanji word. So one cow kanji and one milk kanji open up a whole shelf of the supermarket." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick that uses a real Japanese sound word. In Japanese, 「ぎゅう」 is the sound of squeezing something tight. People say 「ぎゅっと」 for gripping hard, and 「ぎゅう」 for a big tight hug. So picture a farmer giving a cow a huge, tight hug, squeezing and saying gyuu! And out pops a stream of fresh milk. Then the milk splashes into a bucket with a soft noo sound. Gyuu, nyuu. Squeeze, splash. The cow kanji and the squeeze sound happen to share the same sound, so the picture fits perfectly. Just remember that the cow reading actually comes from Chinese, and the squeeze link is only a trick. So hug the cow, and the milk comes out." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Drinking cow milk wasn't a big part of traditional Japanese life. There were short periods, long ago, when nobles drank milk products, but for most of history, milk wasn't an everyday drink. That changed in the late eighteen hundreds, when Japan opened up to the West. The new government encouraged milk and dairy, partly because they believed it would make people stronger and healthier, like Westerners. Milk shops and dairy farms spread, especially on the northern island of Hokkaido, which is still famous for milk and ice cream today. After the Second World War, milk became part of school lunches, and it became a daily drink for a whole generation. So this word grew from a rare foreign habit into everyday breakfast." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「牛|ぎゅう」 means cow, natively 「うし」, and 「乳|にゅう」 means milk, so it's plain cow milk, the word printed on cartons and school lunch milk.",
      "The cow kanji gives 「牛肉|ぎゅうにく」, beef, and 「牛丼|ぎゅうどん」, the milk kanji gives 「豆乳|とうにゅう」, soy milk, and 「ミルク」 is for milk in coffee or drink names.",
      "Picture a farmer giving a cow a tight hug, 「ぎゅう」, and milk splashing into a bucket with a soft noo, though the cow reading really comes from Chinese.",
      "Milk wasn't an everyday drink in Japan until the late eighteen hundreds, when the government promoted dairy, and school lunches after the war made it a daily habit."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[牛乳|ぎゅうにゅう]を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink milk every morning." },
  "ケーキ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word cake. Japanese has no single sound for the A in cake, so it became a long E sound, written with 「ケ」 and the long dash. English words that end in a hard K sound need a vowel added in Japanese, so the final K became 「キ」. That is the usual pattern: Japanese syllables end in a vowel, so extra vowels get tacked on. It is written in katakana because it is a loanword. In Japan the word most often means a Western style sponge cake with cream, and cake shops are everywhere, with beautiful little slices in glass cases. A fun cultural note: on Christmas Eve, many Japanese families buy a strawberry shortcake, white cream with red berries on top. Shops take orders weeks ahead. You might also see 「チーズケーキ」, cheesecake, which follows the same pattern. To recap: cake, with a stretched vowel and an extra one at the end." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a sound-alike that catches learners. 「ケーキ」, cake, is pronounced exactly like 「景気|けいき」, which means the state of the economy, business conditions. That's because Japanese often pronounces a written 「えい」 as a long E sound. So on the news you'll hear about whether the economy is good or bad with the same sounds as cake. A good economy is 「景気がいい|けいきがいい」. Context almost always makes it clear, but there's a classic pun in there: when business is good, people buy cake. Don't confuse it with 「けち」, either, which means stingy, with a short E and a different ending. Listen for the long vowel. So the sweets shop and the stock market share a sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Many cake words in Japan look English but mean something a little different. 「ショートケーキ」, shortcake, in Japan is a light sponge cake with whipped cream and strawberries, not the biscuit-style shortcake of America. It's the most classic cake in the country. 「ホットケーキ」, literally hot cake, means pancakes, the thick fluffy kind. You'll also see 「パンケーキ」 for pancakes in trendy cafés. 「チーズケーキ」 comes in a famous Japanese style that's soft, jiggly and cotton-light. A cake shop is 「ケーキ屋|ケーキや」, using the same shop ending as in 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore. When you buy a slice, you count it with 「一つ|ひとつ」 or with 「一切れ|ひときれ」, one piece. So learn these as Japanese words, not just English ones." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English cake, the vowel stretches to a long E with a dash and an extra vowel turns the final K into 「キ」, since Japanese syllables end in vowels.",
      "It sounds exactly like 「景気|けいき」, the state of the economy, because a written 「えい」 is often pronounced as a long E, so cake shops and the stock market share a sound.",
      "Japanese cake words have their own meanings: 「ショートケーキ」 is sponge with cream and strawberries, and 「ホットケーキ」 means pancakes."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}と[ケーキ]を{作りました|つくりました}。",
    en: "I made a cake with my mother." },
  "すし|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, usually written in kana or with a special kanji you don't need yet. It is generally linked to an old word meaning sour, the same idea as 「すっぱい」, sour, which you might hear in a kitchen. The reason is the rice. Sushi rice is seasoned with vinegar, so it has a light sour taste. Long ago, sushi was a way of preserving fish packed in fermenting rice, and that was very sour indeed. Here is a sound change worth knowing. When the word comes second in a compound, the first sound becomes voiced, so it turns into 「ずし」. Rolled sushi is 「まきずし」, and conveyor belt sushi is 「かいてんずし」. English speakers often think sushi means raw fish, but the word is really about the vinegared rice. Raw fish on its own is 「さしみ」. To recap: the sour vinegared rice, which becomes 「ずし」 inside longer words." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Thanks to the sound change, the sushi family is full of 「ずし」 words. 「まきずし」 is rolled sushi. 「手巻きずし|てまきずし」 is hand-rolled sushi, the cone-shaped kind you roll yourself at home parties. 「ちらしずし」 is scattered sushi, a bowl of sushi rice with toppings spread on top, often served on the Girls' Day festival in March. 「いなりずし」 is sushi rice stuffed in a sweet fried tofu pouch, named after the fox spirits of Inari shrines, who are said to love fried tofu. And the conveyor belt sushi word, 「回転ずし|かいてんずし」, uses the same turning kanji you know from driving and bicycles. Rotating sushi. Finally, the usual kanji spelling, 「寿司|すし」, is chosen for the sound, with a lucky meaning of long life in the first kanji. So once you know 「すし」, you know the whole 「ずし」 family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a sushi restaurant, a few habits and words will help you. It's fine to eat nigiri sushi with your fingers. When you dip it in soy sauce, turn it over and dip the fish side, not the rice, so it doesn't fall apart. The pickled ginger, 「ガリ」, is for cleansing your palate between pieces, not a topping. The green tea is often called 「あがり」 in sushi shops, a bit of special counter slang. And wasabi is usually already tucked under the fish by the chef. At conveyor belt places, you just grab plates, and the bill is counted by the color or number of plates. Many now have tablets to order. To order at the counter, say 「〜をください」. So relax and enjoy it, fish side down." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The word is linked to an old word for sour, because the rice is seasoned with vinegar, and it becomes 「ずし」 inside compounds like 「まきずし」. Raw fish alone is 「さしみ」.",
      "Thanks to the sound change, the family is full of 「ずし」 words, like 「手巻きずし|てまきずし」, 「ちらしずし」, 「いなりずし」 and 「回転ずし|かいてんずし」.",
      "Eat nigiri with your fingers and dip the fish side, not the rice, in soy sauce, and use the pickled ginger, 「ガリ」, to cleanse your palate."
    ],
    ja: "[すし]を{食べに|たべに}{行きましょう|いきましょう}。",
    en: "Let's go eat sushi." },
  "てんぷら|天ぷら": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Most likely this word came with Portuguese traders and missionaries who reached Japan in the fifteen hundreds. Several Portuguese words have been suggested as the source, and nobody is completely sure which one, so treat the details as uncertain. The Portuguese did fry food in batter, and Japanese cooks took the idea and made it their own. The kanji 「天|てん」 means heaven or sky, the same kanji as in 「天気|てんき」, weather. In this word it is just borrowed for its sound, so don't look for a heavenly meaning. That is also why the rest is written in kana. Tempura is shrimp, fish and vegetables in a very light, crispy batter, often eaten with a dipping sauce and grated radish. A bowl of rice topped with tempura is called 「てんどん」, from the first part of this word plus the word for a rice bowl. To recap: a likely Portuguese import, with the heaven kanji borrowed only for its sound." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Tempura shows up in a lot of dishes, and the words build on the first part of the name. 「天丼|てんどん」 is tempura on rice with a sweet soy sauce. 「天ぷらそば|てんぷらそば」 and 「天ぷらうどん|てんぷらうどん」 are noodle soups topped with tempura. And 「天かす|てんかす」 are the little crispy bits of fried batter, often sprinkled on noodles for crunch. At a nice tempura restaurant, the chef fries each piece and hands it to you right away, so it's eaten piping hot. You dip it in a light sauce mixed with grated radish, or you just touch it to a little salt. Good tempura isn't greasy. The batter is made with cold water and mixed only a little, which keeps it light. So learn the short 「てん」 prefix, and you'll understand a whole menu." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The heaven kanji, 「天|てん」, is a simple picture-like shape: a person standing with arms spread, with a line above the head. What's above a person's head is the sky. With its Chinese-origin reading, it appears in 「天気|てんき」, weather, the energy of the sky. 「天国|てんごく」 is heaven, the sky country. And 「天才|てんさい」 is a genius, someone with a gift from heaven. With a native reading, it shows up in old and poetic words for the sky. In tempura, though, it's only borrowed for the sound. The rest of the word stays in kana because there's no fixed kanji for it in everyday use. So this one kanji gives you weather, heaven, genius and fried shrimp." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Today tempura can be a fancy restaurant meal, but it started as cheap street food. In the Edo period, the old name for Tokyo, sixteen hundreds to eighteen hundreds, the city was full of busy working people. Tempura stands sold fried seafood on skewers, and customers ate it standing up, dipped in a shared sauce, like fast food. Frying indoors was dangerous because of the risk of fire in a wooden city, which is one reason it was sold from outdoor stalls. Fresh fish from Tokyo Bay made it a local favorite. Over time, it moved into restaurants and became more refined. Even now, the tempura counter, where the chef fries in front of you, keeps a bit of that street stall feeling. So the fancy dish has humble roots." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It most likely came with Portuguese traders in the fifteen hundreds, and the heaven kanji 「天|てん」 is borrowed only for its sound, which is why the rest is in kana.",
      "The short 「てん」 prefix builds a whole menu, like 「天丼|てんどん」, tempura on rice, 「天ぷらうどん|てんぷらうどん」, and 「天かす|てんかす」, crispy batter bits.",
      "「天|てん」 pictures a person with a line above the head, the sky, giving 「天気|てんき」, 「天国|てんごく」 and 「天才|てんさい」, though tempura only borrows its sound.",
      "Tempura began as cheap Edo period street food, fried seafood on skewers eaten standing at outdoor stalls, since frying indoors risked fire in a wooden city."
    ],
    ja: "[天ぷら|てんぷら]はおいしいです。",
    en: "Tempura is delicious." },
  "りんご|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Today this word is almost always written in kana. There is a kanji spelling, but it is rare and hard, so kana is what you will see in shops. The word came into Japanese long ago from Chinese, and the sounds were softened over time. A way to remember it: picture an apple with a ring around it, ready to go. Ring, go. Apples in Japan are often very large, sweet and a bit expensive, and they are sometimes given as gifts. Aomori, at the northern tip of the main island, grows more apples than anywhere else in the country, and its name is almost a synonym for apples. When counting apples, use the general counter 「一つ|ひとつ」, 「二つ|ふたつ」 and so on, as in the example. It belongs with 「くだもの」, fruit, and with 「ケーキ」 as a dessert word from this lesson. To recap: a kana word for apple, picture the ring on the apple that you take to go." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan grows some of the world's most famous apples. The Fuji apple, one of the most popular varieties on the planet, was developed in Japan, in Aomori, and released in the nineteen sixties. Its name comes from the town where it was bred, though many people link it to Mount Fuji. Japanese apples are often huge and very sweet. Some farmers even put bags over each apple as it grows, to control its color. In lunch boxes, parents love making 「うさぎりんご」, rabbit apples. They cut apple slices and carve the red skin into two little ears, so each slice looks like a bunny. And just after the Second World War, a cheerful song about apples became a huge hit, a symbol of hope in hard times. So the humble apple has a big place in Japanese hearts." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here's a basket of fruit words to learn with this one. Most native fruit names are written in hiragana in daily life. 「みかん」 is a mandarin orange, the classic winter fruit, eaten under the heated table. 「いちご」 is a strawberry. 「ぶどう」 is grapes, and 「もも」 is a peach. Then come the loanwords in katakana, like 「バナナ」, banana, and 「メロン」, melon. Melons in Japan can be very expensive gifts. And the word for fruit in general is 「くだもの」. There's also a newer loanword, 「フルーツ」, used in shops and desserts. A juicy word to add: apple juice is 「りんごジュース」. So 「りんご」 sits in a family of kana fruit words." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's almost always written in kana, so picture an apple with a ring on it, ready to go: ring, go. Aomori grows more apples than anywhere else in Japan.",
      "The famous Fuji apple was developed in Aomori, and parents carve apple slices into 「うさぎりんご」, rabbit apples with red-skin ears, for lunch boxes.",
      "It joins a basket of kana fruit words like 「みかん」, 「いちご」, 「ぶどう」 and 「もも」, all under 「くだもの」, fruit."
    ],
    ja: "[りんご]を{一つ|ひとつ}ください。",
    en: "One apple, please." },
  "りょうり|料理": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「料|りょう」, is made of rice, 「米|こめ」, on the left and a measuring dipper on the right. Measuring out rice gives the idea of materials or ingredients, and also of fees, things that are measured out. The second, 「理|り」, means reason, logic or putting things in order. So the word is ingredients put in order: taking raw materials and arranging them into a dish. It means both the act of cooking and the food that comes out of it, a cuisine. Add 「する」 and you get 「料理する|りょうりする」, to cook, which you already know. Put a country in front and you get a cuisine, like 「日本料理|にほんりょうり」, Japanese food. A common mix up: 「料理|りょうり」 is cooking a real meal. For just making something simple, people often say 「作る|つくる」. To recap: measured ingredients put in order, so cooking and cuisine." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word has a big family. The first one, about measuring out materials, often means a fee. 「料金|りょうきん」 is a charge or fare. 「無料|むりょう」 is free of charge, literally no fee, and you'll see it on signs everywhere. 「給料|きゅうりょう」 is your salary. And 「材料|ざいりょう」 means ingredients or materials, the things you measure out before cooking. The second kanji, 「理|り」, is about reason and order. 「無理|むり」 means impossible or unreasonable, literally without reason. 「理由|りゆう」 is a reason, why something happened. So the cooking word sits between fees and reasons. That might seem odd, but the measuring and the putting in order make perfect sense in a kitchen." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people actually talk about cooking. To say someone is a good cook: 「料理が上手です|りょうりがじょうずです」. To say you like cooking: 「料理が好きです|りょうりがすきです」. To say you cooked: either 「料理しました|りょうりしました」 or 「料理を作りました|りょうりをつくりました」, I made a dish. A warm word is 「手料理|てりょうり」, home cooking, food made with your own hands for someone. People often say it about a partner's or a mother's food. On menus and restaurant signs, you'll see a country name in front: 「中華料理|ちゅうかりょうり」 is Chinese food, and 「イタリア料理|イタリアりょうり」 is Italian. Don't confuse it with 「ご飯|ごはん」, which means a meal or rice in general. So use this word for a dish someone made, and for whole cuisines." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「料|りょう」, rice plus a measuring dipper, means ingredients, and 「理|り」 means putting in order, so it's ingredients put in order, both cooking and cuisine.",
      "「料|りょう」 often means a fee, as in 「無料|むりょう」 and 「材料|ざいりょう」, and 「理|り」 means reason, as in 「無理|むり」 and 「理由|りゆう」.",
      "Use it for a dish someone made, as in 「料理が上手です|りょうりがじょうずです」 and 「手料理|てりょうり」, home cooking, and for cuisines like 「中華料理|ちゅうかりょうり」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}の[料理|りょうり]はおいしいです。",
    en: "My mother's cooking is delicious." },
  "サッカー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word soccer. Here is a fun fact. Soccer itself is British slang, made from the middle of the word association, as in association football, plus an E R ending. Japanese uses this word because it came in through American English, where football means something else. Now the sounds. The small 「ッ」 is a pause that doubles the next consonant, so you get a strong K sound in the middle. The E R ending of English turns into a long A sound, written with the dash. You see the same pattern in 「コンピューター」, computer, and in other words that end in E R. Use 「する」 with it: 「サッカーをする」, to play soccer. Japan's national teams are popular, and the men's team is often nicknamed after the color blue. To recap: from English soccer, with a doubled K and a long ending." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Sports names in Japanese are mostly loanwords, and many get chopped short. 「テニス」 is tennis. 「バスケットボール」, basketball, is usually shortened to 「バスケ」. 「バレーボール」 is volleyball, often just 「バレー」. And then there's an old kanji word for soccer, 「蹴球|しゅうきゅう」, kick ball, from before the loanword took over. You'll rarely hear it now, but compare it with 「野球|やきゅう」, field ball, which stuck around. Same ball kanji, different fate. For all of these, you play with 「する」: 「テニスをする」, 「バスケをする」. And a player is a 「選手|せんしゅ」, as in 「サッカー選手|サッカーせんしゅ」, a soccer player. So learn the long loanwords, but expect to hear the short ones." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Soccer grew a lot in Japan from the nineteen nineties on. The professional league, the J League, started in nineteen ninety-three, and it made the sport hugely popular with young people. In two thousand two, Japan and South Korea hosted the men's World Cup together, the first time it was held in Asia. The men's national team is known as 「サムライブルー」, samurai blue, after their blue uniforms. The women's national team has its own nickname, 「なでしこジャパン」, named after a pink flower that symbolizes the ideal Japanese woman. In twenty eleven, just months after the big earthquake and tsunami, they won the Women's World Cup. It was an emotional moment for the whole country. So soccer in Japan comes with samurai, flowers and a famous victory." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It came from American English soccer, with a small 「ッ」 doubling the K and the English ending stretched into a long A, and you play it with 「する」.",
      "Sports loanwords often get chopped short, like 「バスケ」 and 「バレー」, and the old kanji word for soccer faded while 「野球|やきゅう」 stuck around.",
      "Soccer boomed after the J League began in nineteen ninety-three, and the women's team, 「なでしこジャパン」, won the World Cup months after the twenty eleven earthquake."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}に[サッカー]をします。",
    en: "I play soccer on weekends." },
  "やきゅう|野球": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji are read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「野|や」, means field or open country. You know it from 「野菜|やさい」, vegetables, which is literally field greens. The second, 「球|きゅう」, means ball or sphere. So the word is field ball, a ball game played on a big open field. The word was made in Japan in the late eighteen hundreds when the American game arrived. Baseball is one of the most loved sports in Japan. Every summer the national high school tournament is held at a famous stadium near Osaka, and the whole country watches on TV. Players who lose often scoop up a little of the field's dirt to take home. Use 「する」 to play it and 「見る|みる」 to watch it. To recap: field plus ball, the game played on a big open field." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji have native readings you'll meet. The field kanji, 「野|や」, reads 「の」 in 「野原|のはら」, an open field or meadow. The ball kanji, 「球|きゅう」, reads 「たま」 natively, meaning a ball or a sphere. Its left side is the jewel radical, a form of the king shape, which often appears in kanji for precious stones and round shiny things. On the right is 「求|きゅう」, to seek, which mostly gives the sound. Now a lovely compound: 「地球|ちきゅう」, the Earth, literally the ground ball. Our planet is a ball of earth. And 「電球|でんきゅう」 is a light bulb, an electric ball. So the ball kanji covers baseballs, light bulbs and the whole planet." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Baseball arrived in Japan in the eighteen seventies, brought by an American teacher at a school in Tokyo. Students loved it right away. For a while, people just used the English name. The Japanese word, field ball, was coined in the eighteen nineties by a school baseball player named Chuman Kanoe, who wanted a name for the sport. Around the same time, the famous haiku poet Masaoka Shiki was a passionate player. He helped translate baseball terms into Japanese and wrote poems about the game, and he's now in the Japanese baseball Hall of Fame. Japanese baseball even has its own made-up English words. A night game is called 「ナイター」, from night plus er, a word you won't hear in America. So the field ball has a long, poetic history in Japan." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The national high school tournament mentioned before is held at a stadium called 「甲子園|こうしえん」, near Osaka. The name is so famous that it's become shorthand for the tournament itself. Players dream of going to it the way college players in America dream of the big finals. In professional baseball, two of the most famous rival teams are the 「阪神タイガース|はんしんタイガース」 from the Osaka area and the 「読売ジャイアンツ|よみうりジャイアンツ」 from Tokyo. Fans are famous for their organized cheering. Each player has his own cheer song, sung by the whole section with trumpets and drums. At Tigers games, fans release long balloons into the air during the seventh inning. So a baseball game in Japan is a big, noisy party." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「野|や」 means field, as in 「野菜|やさい」, and 「球|きゅう」 means ball, so it's field ball, a word coined in Japan when the American game arrived.",
      "The ball kanji, natively 「たま」, has the jewel radical for round shiny things and gives 「地球|ちきゅう」, the Earth, and 「電球|でんきゅう」, a light bulb.",
      "Baseball arrived in the eighteen seventies, the name field ball was coined by a school player in the eighteen nineties, and the poet Masaoka Shiki helped translate its terms.",
      "The high school tournament at 「甲子園|こうしえん」 is a national obsession, and pro games are noisy parties with cheer songs for every player."
    ],
    ja: "テレビで[野球|やきゅう]を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch baseball on TV." },
  "いしゃ|医者": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji are read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「医|い」, means medicine or healing. The second, 「者|しゃ」, means person, someone who does something. You have this same person kanji in 「研究者|けんきゅうしゃ」, researcher, someone who does research. So a doctor is literally a medicine person. The word for hospital is a different kanji, 「病院|びょういん」, a building for illness, so don't mix the two up. Politeness matters with this word. Talking about your own job, you can say 「医者|いしゃ」 plainly. But for someone else's doctor, people usually say 「お医者さん|おいしゃさん」, with the polite pieces added. And when you speak directly to a doctor, you call them 「先生|せんせい」, the same word as for a teacher. Being a doctor is a classic dream job in Japan, and it often comes up when people talk about what they want to become. To recap: medicine plus person, the person who heals." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The person kanji at the end has two common readings. In 「医者|いしゃ」, 「研究者|けんきゅうしゃ」 and 「学者|がくしゃ」, a scholar, it's read 「しゃ」. But with its native reading it's 「もの」. You know 「怠け者|なまけもの」, a lazy person. 「若者|わかもの」 means young people. So the same kanji can be a professional in a lab coat or a lazy person on the couch, depending on the reading. The medicine kanji has its own family. 「医学|いがく」 is medicine as a field of study. And here's a fun one: 「歯医者|はいしゃ」, a dentist, literally a tooth doctor. Doctors who treat animals are called 「獣医|じゅうい」, beast doctors. So one person kanji makes doctors, scholars and lazy people." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people talk about seeing a doctor in Japan. Japanese often say 「病院に行く|びょういんにいく」, go to the hospital, even for a small clinic visit. 「医者に行く|いしゃにいく」 is also fine. When the doctor examines you, the phrase is 「お医者さんにみてもらう|おいしゃさんにみてもらう」, to have the doctor look at you. At the start, the doctor might ask 「どうしましたか」, what seems to be the problem? You can answer with your symptoms, like 「熱があります|ねつがあります」, I have a fever. When you leave, the staff may say 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care of yourself. Most clinics in Japan take walk-ins, and you show your health insurance card at reception. So with a few phrases, a visit to the doctor feels a lot less scary." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「医|い」 means medicine and 「者|しゃ」 means person, so a doctor is a medicine person. Say 「お医者さん|おいしゃさん」 for someone else's doctor and call doctors 「先生|せんせい」 directly.",
      "The person kanji reads 「しゃ」 in 「学者|がくしゃ」 but 「もの」 in 「若者|わかもの」, and the medicine family includes 「歯医者|はいしゃ」, a dentist.",
      "People often say 「病院に行く|びょういんにいく」 even for a clinic, the doctor opens with 「どうしましたか」, and the staff send you off with 「お大事に|おだいじに」."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[医者|いしゃ]です。",
    en: "My older sister is a doctor." },
  "おかねもち|お金持ち": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one is built from two words you already know. The first is 「お金|おかね」, money. The kanji 「金|かね」 means gold or metal, the same one as in 「金曜日|きんようび」, Friday, gold day. The 「お」 at the front is the polite prefix, and with money it is almost always used. The second part, 「持ち|もち」, comes from the verb 「持つ|もつ」, to hold or to have. You know it from 「持ってくる|もってくる」, to bring. When the verb drops its ending and joins a noun, it means someone who has that thing. So this word is a money haver, someone who holds a lot of money. The same pattern gives 「力持ち|ちからもち」, a person who has strength, a strong person. It is fine in normal conversation, but saying it straight to a person's face can sound a little rude, so be careful. To recap: money plus holding, the person who holds lots of money." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The holding part, 「持ち|もち」, is the stem of the verb 「持つ|もつ」, the form you get by dropping 「ます」 from 「持ちます|もちます」. Many Japanese nouns are built this way, from a verb stem. Attached to a noun, this one means someone who has that thing, or the having of it. The biggest relative is 「気持ち|きもち」, a feeling, literally the holding of your spirit. You know it from 「気持ちがいい|きもちがいい」, it feels good. There's also 「力持ち|ちからもち」, a strong person. And when you add 「大|おお」 for big, the next sound gets voiced: 「大金持ち|おおがねもち」 is a super rich person, with 「が」 instead of 「か」. So the verb stem 「持ち|もち」 turns things into owners." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this word a lot in daydreams and jokes. 「お金持ちになりたい|おかねもちになりたい」, I want to be rich, is something people say when buying a lottery ticket. Japan has a big lottery at the end of every year, and long lines form at lucky ticket booths. A rich family is 「お金持ちの家|おかねもちのいえ」. Without the polite 「お」, the word sounds rougher, and you might hear it in comics or arguments. A careful way to describe someone is to avoid the word and say something softer, like they seem to have a nice lifestyle. A popular lucky charm for money is the 「招き猫|まねきねこ」, the beckoning cat with its paw raised, found in many shops. People say a raised right paw invites money, and a raised left paw invites customers. So money talk in Japan comes with lotteries and lucky cats." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「お金|おかね」, money, plus 「持ち|もち」 from 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, so a money haver, a rich person, like 「力持ち|ちからもち」, a strong person.",
      "「持ち|もち」 is the verb stem of 「持つ|もつ」, making owners of things, as in 「気持ち|きもち」, and with 「大|おお」 it becomes 「大金持ち|おおがねもち」, super rich.",
      "People say 「お金持ちになりたい|おかねもちになりたい」 when buying lottery tickets, and the beckoning 「招き猫|まねきねこ」 is a lucky charm for money."
    ],
    ja: "あの{人|ひと}は[お金持ち|おかねもち]です。",
    en: "That person is rich." },
  "ゆうめいじん|有名人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「有|ゆう」, means to have or to exist. The second, 「名|めい」, means name. The same kanji gives you 「なまえ」, name. Together they make 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, which you already know. Being famous is literally having a name. Then comes 「人|じん」, person. This is the same reading as in 「日本人|にほんじん」, a Japanese person, and 「外国人|がいこくじん」, a foreigner. So the whole word is a person who has a name, a famous person, a celebrity. Note that 「有名|ゆうめい」 is a na-adjective, so to say a famous person in a normal phrase you can also say 「有名な人|ゆうめいなひと」. This compound version is shorter and is the usual word on TV. Japanese TV is full of celebrities on variety shows, and they are often just called 「タレント」, from English talent. To recap: having a name plus person." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each piece of this word has relatives. The have kanji, 「有|ゆう」, also makes 「有料|ゆうりょう」, which means there's a fee. You'll see it on signs for parking lots and restrooms, opposite 「無料|むりょう」, free. The name kanji, 「名|めい」, appears in 「名刺|めいし」, a business card, literally a name card. You know it in 「〜名様|〜めいさま」, the polite counter for customers, as in a restaurant asking how many people. And the person kanji at the end, read 「じん」, has a cousin reading 「にん」 used for counting: 「三人|さんにん」, three people. The rule of thumb: 「じん」 for a type of person, like nationality or celebrity, and 「にん」 for counting heads. So this one word connects to fees, business cards and counting people." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese has several words for famous people, and they're not all the same. This one is the general term, anyone with a famous name, whether a politician, an athlete or a writer. 「芸能人|げいのうじん」 is more specific: entertainers, people in show business, like TV stars and singers. Gossip shows and magazines are full of this word. 「タレント」, from English talent, means a TV personality, someone famous for appearing on variety shows. And idols, 「アイドル」, are young singers and performers with huge fan bases. If you spot a famous person on the street, people say 「あ、有名人だ|あ、ゆうめいじんだ」, oh, it's a celebrity! Sound tip: in the word itself, both vowels are long, so give each one its full time. So pick the right word for the kind of fame." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, is literally having a name, and adding 「人|じん」, person, gives a celebrity, a shorter version of 「有名な人|ゆうめいなひと」.",
      "「有|ゆう」 gives 「有料|ゆうりょう」, there's a fee, 「名|めい」 gives 「名刺|めいし」, a business card, and 「人|じん」 is for types of people while 「にん」 counts heads.",
      "This is the general word for anyone famous, while 「芸能人|げいのうじん」 means entertainers, 「タレント」 TV personalities, and 「アイドル」 young performers."
    ],
    ja: "きのう[有名人|ゆうめいじん]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw a celebrity yesterday." },
  "かお|顔": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese reading. The kanji 「顔|かお」 has two halves. The right half is a shape that appears in many kanji about the head. You can see it in 「頭|あたま」, head, as in 「頭がいい|あたまがいい」, smart. Both share that same right side. So when you see that shape, think head. The left half helps with the sound and meaning, but you don't need its history to remember it. A way to remember the sound: picture a cow with a big round face looking at you. Cow, face. This word also shows up in useful phrases. To wash your face is 「顔を洗う|かおをあらう」. And the Japanese idea of face as in reputation is just as strong as in English. Losing face is a real concern. Japanese has lots of idioms with face, like having a wide face, meaning knowing lots of people. To recap: the head shape on the right, and a cow's big face for the sound." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The right side of this kanji is a component called the head or page radical. Its old meaning was a head, and today it also stands alone as the kanji for a page of a book. It's on the right side of many kanji about the head and things at the top. You know 「頭|あたま」, head. You also know 「宿題|しゅくだい」, homework, and the second kanji there, meaning topic, has the same head shape on its right. A topic is the head of a discussion. Other kanji with it include words for forehead and neck. The left side of the face kanji mainly gives the sound in the original Chinese. So whenever you see that shape on the right, look for a link to heads." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is full of everyday idioms. 「顔を出す|かおをだす」, literally to put out your face, means to show up briefly, like dropping by a party. 「顔色|かおいろ」, face color, is your complexion, and if you look sick, someone will say 「顔色が悪いですよ|かおいろがわるいですよ」, you look pale. A smiling face is 「笑顔|えがお」, a lovely word with an unusual reading, where the face part becomes 「がお」. And in text messages, Japanese people use 「顔文字|かおもじ」, face characters, little faces made from symbols. They look at you straight on, so you can read them without tilting your head. So with this one word, you can show up, look pale, smile and send emoticons." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here's a set of face and head words to learn together. 「目|め」 is eye, 「耳|みみ」 is ear, 「口|くち」 is mouth, and 「鼻|はな」 is nose. Careful, because nose is spelled with the same kana as flower. The face is also the home of 「頭|あたま」, the head, and 「髪|かみ」, hair. Many body-part phrases are built with adjectives. 「目が大きい|めがおおきい」 means having big eyes. 「顔が小さい|かおがちいさい」, a small face, is actually a compliment in Japan. It's said about stylish people and models. And people describe someone well connected as having a wide face, as you learned earlier. When talking about someone's looks, the pattern uses 「は」 for the person and 「が」 for the part. So build your body vocabulary around the face." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The right half is the head shape also found in 「頭|あたま」, so think head, and for the sound, picture a cow with a big round face: cow, face.",
      "The right side is the head radical, which also means page, and it appears in kanji about heads, like 「頭|あたま」 and the topic kanji in 「宿題|しゅくだい」.",
      "Face idioms include 「顔を出す|かおをだす」, to show up briefly, 「顔色|かおいろ」, complexion, 「笑顔|えがお」, a smile, and 「顔文字|かおもじ」, emoticons.",
      "Learn face words together, like 「目|め」, 「耳|みみ」, 「口|くち」 and 「鼻|はな」, and note that 「顔が小さい|かおがちいさい」 is a compliment in Japan."
    ],
    ja: "[顔|かお]を{洗います|あらいます}。",
    en: "I wash my face." },
  "としうえ|年上": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji are read with their native Japanese readings here. The first, 「年|とし」, means year, and by extension age, how many years old you are. With the Chinese-origin reading it is 「ねん」, as in 「来年|らいねん」, next year. The second, 「上|うえ」, means above or on top. It is the same kanji as in 「上手|じょうず」, good at, though there it is read differently. So this word is above in age, someone older than you. The opposite is 「年下|としした」, below in age, someone younger. The pattern is: person, 「は」, me, 「より」, then this word, as in the example. This matters a lot in Japan. Age decides who uses polite speech to whom, and even a single year can change how people talk at school or work. That is why people often ask your age early on. To recap: year plus above, the person above you in age." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To say exactly how much older someone is, put the number right in front of the word. 「三つ年上|みっつとしうえ」 means three years older, and 「五歳年上|ごさいとしうえ」 means five years older. So you might hear 「姉はわたしより二つ年上です|あねはわたしよりふたつとしうえです」, my big sister is two years older than me. It is a noun, so before another noun it takes 「の」: 「年上の友だち|としうえのともだち」, an older friend. Here is a trap for English speakers. In English, older can describe things, like an older car or an older house. This word is only for people, and sometimes pets. For an old thing, Japanese uses 「古い|ふるい」. And for someone who is simply old, an elderly person, you would say 「お年寄り|おとしより」 instead. This word is always a comparison, older than someone. So keep it for people, and put the number of years in front." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, being a little older comes with a role. At school and at work there is a pair of words you will hear constantly: 「先輩|せんぱい」, a senior, someone who came before you, and 「後輩|こうはい」, a junior, someone who came after you. In school clubs, even one grade makes a difference. Younger members usually speak politely to older ones, with 「です」 and 「ます」, and often help carry equipment or clean up. The older ones may answer casually, and in return they are expected to look after and guide the younger ones. Because of this, people often want to know early on who is older, so they can choose the right way to talk. That is why a question about your age or school year is not rude here. It helps everyone find their place. Being older in Japan means both respect and responsibility." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「上|うえ」 changes its reading a lot, and you already know several of its faces. In this word it is 「うえ」, above. In 「上手|じょうず」, good at, it is read 「じょう」. In the honorific verb 「召し上がる|めしあがる」, to eat, it is read 「あ」, as in the verb 「上がる|あがる」, to go up. A close cousin of this word is 「目上|めうえ」, someone above you. That one is about rank or status, like your boss or your teacher, not about age. So a boss who is younger than you is still your 「目上|めうえ」, but not your 「年上|としうえ」. And the mirror word is 「年下|としした」, younger, with 「下|した」, below. Remember the pair: age goes with 「年|とし」, status goes with 「目|め」, and both can be above you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「年|とし」, year or age, plus 「上|うえ」, above, so someone above you in age; the opposite is 「年下|としした」, someone younger.",
      "Put the number of years right in front, as in 「三つ年上|みっつとしうえ」, three years older, and keep it for people, since old things are 「古い|ふるい」.",
      "Being older comes with a role in Japan, like 「先輩|せんぱい」 and 「後輩|こうはい」 at school, where even one grade decides who speaks politely and who looks after whom.",
      "Don't mix it up with 「目上|めうえ」, someone above you in rank or status, so a younger boss is your 「目上|めうえ」 but not your 「年上|としうえ」."
    ],
    ja: "{田中|たなか}さんはわたしより[年上|としうえ]です。",
    en: "Mr. Tanaka is older than me." },
  "えき|駅": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji is read with its Chinese-origin reading, but it is used like a plain everyday word. On the left of 「駅|えき」 is a horse, the kanji 「馬|うま」. You can spot the mane and the four legs at the bottom. That horse is there for a real historical reason. Long ago, a station was a post stop on the main roads, where messengers and travelers changed to fresh horses. When the railways came, the same word was used for train stations. So a station is still, in its shape, a place with horses. You will see it in 「駅員|えきいん」, station attendant, a member of the station staff. Big stations in Japan are like small cities, full of shops and restaurants. And meeting someone at a station exit is a very common plan. To ask the way, say 「駅はどこですか|えきはどこですか」. To recap: the horse on the left, from the days of changing horses at the post stop." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Stations are how people give directions in Japan. Instead of a street address, someone will say 「駅から歩いて五分です|えきからあるいてごふんです」, it is a five minute walk from the station. The area right outside is 「駅前|えきまえ」, in front of the station, and many shops and clinics even use that word in their names. To name a particular station, just add this word after the place name, like 「東京駅|とうきょうえき」, Tokyo Station. Big stations have many exits, so when you meet a friend, say which one: 「東口|ひがしぐち」, the east exit, or 「西口|にしぐち」, the west exit. Otherwise you may wait at the wrong side for half an hour. On the train you will hear 「次の駅|つぎのえき」, the next station. In short, the station is the center of daily life and the anchor for every meeting." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "One of the joys of train travel in Japan is 「駅弁|えきべん」. It joins this word with 「弁当|べんとう」, a boxed lunch, so it is a station lunch box. Stations all over the country sell their own, made with local specialties. One town is famous for crab, another for beef, another for a pot of rice cooked in a little clay jar. People buy one before a long train ride and eat it in their seat, watching the countryside go by. Some fans travel just to try new ones, and department stores hold special fairs selling station lunches from across Japan. Many stations also have a rubber stamp with a local picture, and collectors carry little books to fill with them. So a station is not only a place to change trains. It is a little taste of the town it serves." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture an old post station on a dusty road, long ago. A tired horse plods in, the one you can see on the left side of the kanji. The traveler jumps off and pats his pockets. Eh? Key? Eh? Key? He has lost the key to his luggage. The station keeper laughs and points to a hook on the wall, where it has been hanging all along. Now jump forward to a modern station. You are patting your pockets for your train pass, muttering the same thing. Eh? Key? Every station, then and now, has someone searching their pockets at the gate. So the horse on the left and the panicked traveler give you both the shape and the sound. Picture the horse, hear the panic, and you have the station." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The horse, 「馬|うま」, on the left is there because a station was once a post stop where travelers changed to fresh horses.",
      "Japanese directions are given from the station, like 「駅から歩いて五分です|えきからあるいてごふんです」, and when meeting someone, always say which exit, such as 「東口|ひがしぐち」.",
      "Stations sell 「駅弁|えきべん」, station lunch boxes made with local specialties, which people eat in their seats on long train rides.",
      "Picture a traveler at an old post station, horse on the left, patting his pockets and muttering eh, key, eh, key, for the sound."
    ],
    ja: "[駅|えき]までバスで{十分|じゅっぷん}です。",
    en: "It's ten minutes to the station by bus." },
  "しんかんせん|新幹線": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「新|しん」, means new. You know it from 「新しい|あたらしい」, new, and 「新聞|しんぶん」, newspaper, which is new things heard. The second, 「幹|かん」, means trunk, like a tree trunk, and so the main part of something. The third, 「線|せん」, means line. It has the thread shape on its left, a long line of thread. So the word is new trunk line, the new main railway line. The first one opened in nineteen sixty four, just before the Tokyo Olympics, running between Tokyo and Osaka. It was the fastest train in the world at the time, and it has an amazing safety record. Trains are famously on time, often to within a minute. In English it is called the bullet train because of the rounded nose of the early trains. To recap: new plus trunk plus line, the new main line." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Each of these kanji has more than one face. The first, 「新|しん」, has a native reading in 「新しい|あたらしい」, new. It also hides in place names with the old reading 「にい」, as in 「新潟|にいがた」, a prefecture on the Japan Sea side. The second kanji has a native reading too: 「幹|みき」, the trunk of a tree. That is the picture behind the main line, the trunk that the smaller branch lines grow from. The third, 「線|せん」, is a good example of how many kanji are built. The thread on the left gives the meaning, a line. The right side is 「泉|いずみ」, a spring of water, but here it is there for its sound, because its Chinese-origin reading is also 「せん」. You will see this kanji again in words like 「線路|せんろ」, railway track. So a sound part and a meaning part make the line, and the trunk line is the backbone of the network." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you take the bullet train, you need a few words. Trains come in types with names. On the main Tokyo to Osaka line, the fastest are called 「のぞみ」, which means hope. A bit slower is 「ひかり」, light. The one that stops everywhere is 「こだま」, echo. Seats come in two kinds. 「指定席|していせき」 are reserved seats, which you book ahead, using 「予約|よやく」 from this lesson. 「自由席|じゆうせき」 are unreserved seats, free seats, where you sit wherever there is space. In busy seasons the free seats fill up fast, and people end up standing. There is also a more comfortable first class car, called the green car. To get on, use 「に」 and 「乗る|のる」: 「新幹線に乗る|しんかんせんにのる」. So learn the three names and the two kinds of seats, and you are ready to ride." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "A few fun facts make this train even more memorable. When a bullet train pulls into Tokyo Station and turns around, a cleaning crew in neat uniforms has only about seven minutes to clean the whole train. They bow to the train as it arrives, sweep every car, turn all the seats to face the other way, and bow again as it leaves. Visitors love to watch them. If you travel from Tokyo toward Osaka on a clear day, sit on the right side of the train, and you may see Mount Fuji. Many stations on the line have names starting with 「新|しん」, like 「新大阪|しんおおさか」, new Osaka. They were often built just for the bullet train, a little away from the old city center. So the word new shows up again in the station names along the way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「新|しん」, new, plus 「幹|かん」, trunk, plus 「線|せん」, line, so the new main railway line, first opened in nineteen sixty four.",
      "The trunk is 「幹|みき」, a tree trunk that branch lines grow from, and 「線|せん」 joins a thread part for meaning with 「泉|いずみ」 for its sound.",
      "Learn the train names 「のぞみ」, 「ひかり」 and 「こだま」, plus the two seat types, reserved 「指定席|していせき」 and unreserved 「自由席|じゆうせき」.",
      "Cleaning crews turn a whole train around in about seven minutes at Tokyo Station, and many stops are new stations like 「新大阪|しんおおさか」."
    ],
    ja: "[新幹線|しんかんせん]で{東京|とうきょう}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to Tokyo by bullet train." },
  "ちかてつ|地下鉄": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「地|ち」, means ground or earth. You know it from 「地図|ちず」, map, a ground picture, and 「地震|じしん」, earthquake, the ground shaking. The second, 「下|か」, means below. Together 「地下|ちか」 means underground, and it is also the word for a basement floor in a department store. The third, 「鉄|てつ」, means iron. You can see the metal shape, the left side of 「金|かね」, on its left. In Japan, iron is used as a short way of saying railway, an iron road. So this word is under the ground iron, the underground railway. Tokyo's subway network is one of the busiest in the world, and at rush hour staff sometimes help push people onto the cars. Use 「で」 to say by subway, as in the example. To recap: ground plus below plus iron, the railway under the ground." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word breaks neatly into two parts, and each part has its own family. First, 「地下|ちか」, underground. On its own it also means the basement levels of a building. A basement floor is 「地下一階|ちかいっかい」, the first floor down. Department store basements are famous for their amazing food halls, and people call them 「デパ地下|デパちか」, department store underground. Next, the iron part, 「鉄|てつ」. It is in 「鉄道|てつどう」, railway, which is an iron road. Railway fans are even nicknamed 「てっちゃん」, from this kanji. Also notice the first kanji, 「地|ち」. It reads 「じ」 in 「地震|じしん」, earthquake, but 「ち」 in 「地図|ちず」, map. So one word gives you three useful pieces: underground, iron, and the ground itself." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at 「鉄|てつ」. On the left is the metal shape, from 「金|きん」, which means gold or metal. On the right is a shape that, as its own kanji, means to lose, as in the verb 「失う|うしなう」. Put metal, or money, next to lose, and you get something no business wants to see: lose money. Because of this, several railway companies in Japan write their logo with a slightly different kanji, where the right side is changed to the kanji for arrow. It is a well known bit of company superstition, and you can spot it on signs and trains if you look. The old, traditional form of the kanji was more complicated and did not have this problem. So the next time you see the subway sign, check the right side, and smile at the railway that does not want to lose its money." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Big Japanese cities have subway networks that look scary at first but are very well organized. In Tokyo, each line has a color and a letter, and every station has a number, so you can follow signs even without reading the names. The key word for changing lines is 「乗り換え|のりかえ」, a transfer. You will hear it in announcements and see it on yellow signs. To find the entrance, ask 「地下鉄の駅はどこですか|ちかてつのえきはどこですか」, where is the subway station? Most people pay with a rechargeable card that they just touch at the gate. In rush hour, cars get very crowded, and there are women only cars at busy times on many lines. A common sentence pattern is going somewhere by subway, with 「で」, or getting on, with 「に乗る|にのる」. Know the color, the number, and the word for transfer, and the city opens up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「地|ち」, ground, plus 「下|か」, below, plus 「鉄|てつ」, iron, which stands for railway, so the railway under the ground.",
      "It splits into 「地下|ちか」, underground, as in the food halls called 「デパ地下|デパちか」, and 「鉄|てつ」, iron, as in 「鉄道|てつどう」, railway.",
      "The right side of 「鉄|てつ」 looks like the kanji for lose, so metal plus lose reads as losing money, and some railways change it in their logos.",
      "Tokyo lines have a color, a letter and station numbers, and the key word for changing lines is 「乗り換え|のりかえ」, a transfer."
    ],
    ja: "[地下鉄|ちかてつ]で{学校|がっこう}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to school by subway." },
  "ふね|船": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. On the left of 「船|ふね」 is a smaller kanji that is also read 「ふね」 and also means boat. That small one is an old picture of a little boat, and in its full form it looks a bit like a narrow boat seen from above. So the boat shape on the left tells you the topic right away. The bigger kanji is the everyday one for ships of any size, from ferries to huge ocean liners. A way to remember the sound: picture a funny little boat rocking on the waves. Funny boat. Japan is an island country, so boats matter a lot. Ferries connect the main islands with thousands of smaller ones. Use 「で」 to say by boat, as in 「船で行きます|ふねでいきます」, and use 「乗る|のる」, to ride, when you get on: 「船に乗る|ふねにのる」. Notice that you get on with 「に」, the same way you get on a train or a bus. To recap: the little boat on the left, inside the big word for ship." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word has a little sound trick. On its own it is 「ふね」. But when it comes first in a compound, the last vowel often changes to 「ふな」. For example, 「船便|ふなびん」 is sea mail, sending a package by ship, slow but cheap. And 「船乗り|ふなのり」 is a sailor, a person who rides on ships. This E to A change happens in a few other native words too. Rain, 「雨|あめ」, becomes 「あま」 in 「雨傘|あまがさ」, an umbrella for rain. And sake, 「酒|さけ」, becomes 「さか」 in 「酒屋|さかや」, a liquor shop. These are old patterns in the language, so you just learn them word by word. If you hear 「ふな」 at the start of a word, think boat. So remember, a boat on its own, and a slightly different sound when it leads a longer word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading too, 「せん」. You meet it in a fun everyday word: 「風船|ふうせん」, a balloon, which is literally a wind ship. You also meet it in travel words like 「客船|きゃくせん」, a passenger ship. Now compare two kanji that both mean boat. 「舟|ふね」, the simple one you can see on the left of our kanji, is for a small boat, the kind you row with oars, like a little boat on a pond. The bigger kanji, the one in this word, is for ships of any size, from ferries to cargo ships. In speech they sound exactly the same, so you only choose when writing. Most of the time, the bigger one is the safe choice. So the little rowboat lives inside the big ship, and the wind ship floats in the sky." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases you need around boats. To travel by boat, use 「で」. To board, use 「に乗る|にのる」. To get off, use 「を降りる|をおりる」: 「船を降りる|ふねをおりる」, to get off the boat. And if the waves are rough, you may need this word: 「船酔い|ふなよい」, seasickness, which is boat drunkenness, using the 「ふな」 sound again. A common sentence is 「船酔いしました|ふなよいしました」, I got seasick. Ferries in Japan are often big and comfortable, and some long distance ones have shared carpeted rooms where passengers simply lie down on the floor and sleep for the night. There are also short ferries to small islands, which are part of daily life for people who live there. So you ride with 「に」, get off with 「を」, and hope you do not need the word for seasick." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The smaller kanji on the left, also read 「ふね」, is an old picture of a little boat, and you get on with 「に乗る|にのる」.",
      "When it leads a compound the sound shifts to 「ふな」, as in 「船便|ふなびん」, sea mail, the same E to A change as 「雨|あめ」 in 「雨傘|あまがさ」.",
      "Its Chinese-origin reading 「せん」 gives 「風船|ふうせん」, a balloon or wind ship, while the simpler 「舟|ふね」 is for a small rowing boat.",
      "Board with 「に乗る|にのる」, get off with 「を降りる|をおりる」, and if the waves are rough you get 「船酔い|ふなよい」, seasickness."
    ],
    ja: "[船|ふね]で{島|しま}に{行きました|いきました}。",
    en: "I went to the island by boat." },
  "ひこうき|飛行機": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「飛|ひ」, means to fly. The shape is often said to look like a bird's wings, which makes a handy picture. The second, 「行|こう」, means to go. You know it from 「行く|いく」, to go, and from 「旅行|りょこう」, travel. Together, 「飛行|ひこう」 means flight, flying and going. The third, 「機|き」, means machine. So the whole word is a flying machine, which is a perfect description of an airplane. You have a close cousin in 「宇宙飛行士|うちゅうひこうし」, astronaut, which shares the same flying part, a space flight person. And the place planes leave from is 「空港|くうこう」, airport, which is a sky harbor. Use 「で」 for by plane, and 「乗る|のる」 for getting on: 「飛行機に乗る|ひこうきにのる」. To recap: fly plus go plus machine, the flying machine." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is the airplane vocabulary you will actually use. To board, 「飛行機に乗る|ひこうきにのる」. To get off, 「飛行機を降りる|ひこうきをおりる」. The plane itself flies with the verb 「飛ぶ|とぶ」, to fly, which uses the same first kanji with its native reading. When you check in, the staff may ask if you want a window seat, 「窓側|まどがわ」, or an aisle seat, 「通路側|つうろがわ」. To ask how long a flight is, combine it with 「かかる」 from this lesson: 「飛行機で何時間かかりますか|ひこうきでなんじかんかかりますか」, how many hours does it take by plane? Many Japanese people travel inside the country by plane too, for example from Tokyo to Okinawa or Hokkaido, because those are a long way by train. So remember to ride with 「に」, get off with 「を」, and let the plane do the flying." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word opens a family. The first, to fly, gives the verb 「飛ぶ|とぶ」. The pair 「飛行|ひこう」, flight, also appears in 「宇宙飛行士|うちゅうひこうし」, astronaut, and in 「飛行場|ひこうじょう」, an airfield, a flying place. The last kanji, 「機|き」, machine, is a very busy one. It is in 「機械|きかい」, machine in general. It is the ending of many home appliances, like 「洗濯機|せんたくき」, washing machine. And it is the last part of 「自動販売機|じどうはんばいき」, vending machine, which is literally an automatic selling machine. So when you see 「き」 at the end of a gadget word, think machine. Learn this one kanji, and a whole shop full of machines becomes easier to read." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a small plane on the runway. The pilot climbs in, reaches for the ignition, and his pocket is empty. He lets out a high squeak. Hee! Then he turns to his co-pilot, who is calmly holding up the key. Co-key! The co-pilot has the key! Hee, co-key. The pilot grabs it, the engines roar, and the flying machine lifts into the sky. Keep the long middle sound in mind. It is not a quick co, but a stretched co-oh, so the co-pilot really takes his time. Each time you see a plane overhead, imagine that squeak of panic and the co-pilot with the key. A squeak, a co-pilot, a key, and you are flying." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「飛|ひ」, fly, plus 「行|こう」, go, plus 「機|き」, machine, so literally a flying machine.",
      "Ride with 「に乗る|にのる」, get off with 「を降りる|をおりる」, and at check-in choose a window seat, 「窓側|まどがわ」, or aisle seat, 「通路側|つうろがわ」.",
      "The last kanji, 「機|き」, machine, ends many gadget words like 「洗濯機|せんたくき」, washing machine, and 「自動販売機|じどうはんばいき」, vending machine.",
      "A pilot squeaks hee when he finds no key, then sees his co-pilot holding it, co-key, with a long stretched co sound."
    ],
    ja: "[飛行機|ひこうき]で{日本|にほん}に{来ました|きました}。",
    en: "I came to Japan by plane." },
  "よやく|予約": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「予|よ」, means beforehand or in advance. You know it from 「天気予報|てんきよほう」, the weather forecast, which tells you the weather before it happens. It is also in 「予定|よてい」, a plan or schedule, something set beforehand. The second, 「約|やく」, means promise or agreement. It has the thread shape on the left, like tying something together. You know it from 「約束|やくそく」, a promise or appointment. So a reservation is a promise made ahead of time. Add 「する」 to make the verb: 「予約する|よやくする」, to make a reservation. You use it for restaurants, hotels, tickets, and even doctors and hair salons. Popular restaurants in Japan can be booked out for weeks, so this is a very useful word. To recap: beforehand plus promise, a promise made in advance." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how a reservation usually goes. On the phone, you might start with 「予約をお願いします|よやくをおねがいします」, I would like to make a reservation, please. Then give the time and the number of people: 「七時に二人です|しちじにふたりです」, seven o'clock, two people. When you arrive, you say your name with this word in front: 「予約した田中です|よやくしたたなかです」, I am Tanaka, I made a reservation. Staff check the list, and you are shown to your table. If a place is full, you may hear 「予約でいっぱいです|よやくでいっぱいです」, we are fully booked. On tables in restaurants you will often see a little sign that says 「予約席|よやくせき」, a reserved seat, so do not sit there. And to cancel, people simply use the loanword 「キャンセル」. So start with please, give the time and the people, and say your name when you arrive." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to useful families. The first, beforehand, is in 「予定|よてい」, a plan, and 「天気予報|てんきよほう」, the forecast. Students also meet 「予習|よしゅう」, preparing for a class before it happens, learning in advance. The second kanji has a surprise. On its own, as a prefix before a number, 「約|やく」 means about or approximately. 「約十分|やくじゅっぷん」 is about ten minutes, and you will see it all the time on maps and timetables. Now compare two words with this kanji. 「約束|やくそく」 is a promise or appointment between people, like meeting a friend at six. 「予約|よやく」 is a booking with a business, like a table or a hotel room. You keep a promise with a friend, but you make a reservation with a restaurant. Same promise kanji, two different kinds of agreement." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word works both as a noun and, with 「する」, as a verb. As a verb it takes 「を」 for the thing you book: 「レストランを予約する|レストランをよやくする」, to book a restaurant. As a noun it has its own handy phrases. 「予約がある|よやくがある」 means to have a reservation. 「予約が取れる|よやくがとれる」 means you managed to get one, which is good news for a popular place. And 「予約が取れない|よやくがとれない」 means you could not, because it is full. You can also combine it with the past plain form before a noun: 「予約したホテル|よやくしたホテル」, the hotel I booked. And with 「ておく」, from your later lessons, you get 「予約しておく|よやくしておく」, to book in advance so it is ready. So learn it as a verb with 「を」, and as a noun with have and get." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「予|よ」, beforehand, as in 「予定|よてい」, plus 「約|やく」, promise, as in 「約束|やくそく」, so a promise made in advance.",
      "Start with 「予約をお願いします|よやくをおねがいします」, give the time and number of people, and on arrival say 「予約した田中です|よやくしたたなかです」 with your name.",
      "You keep a 「約束|やくそく」 with a friend but make a 「予約|よやく」 with a business, and before a number 「約|やく」 alone means about.",
      "As a verb it takes 「を」 for what you book, and as a noun it pairs with 「予約がある|よやくがある」, to have one, and 「予約が取れる|よやくがとれる」, to get one."
    ],
    ja: "ホテルを[予約しました|よやくしました|よやく]。",
    en: "I reserved a hotel." },
  "ツアー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word tour. Japanese has no true T U sound, so it uses 「ツ」, which has a little hiss after the T. Then the vowel is stretched with 「ア」 and the long dash, to match the drawn out sound of tour. It is written in katakana because it is a loanword. In Japanese it usually means a package tour, a guided trip that a travel company sets up, with a bus, a guide and a fixed plan. Group tours with a guide holding up a little flag are a classic image of Japanese travelers abroad. You join one with 「に」 and 「参加する|さんかする」, to take part, or simply with 「行く|いく」. It goes nicely with 「旅行|りょこう」, travel, from this same lesson. A tour is one kind of trip, organized by someone else. To recap: from English tour, with a hissy 「ツ」 and a long stretched ending." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word joins easily with other words to make lots of trip types. A bus tour is 「バスツアー」. A day trip, where you go and come back the same day, is 「日帰りツアー|ひがえりツアー」, a same day return tour. A guide is 「ツアーガイド」. The verb most often used with it is 「参加する|さんかする」, to take part, with 「に」: 「ツアーに参加しました|ツアーにさんかしました」, I joined a tour. Notice that the word also means a concert tour, just like in English. A band going around the country is on a 「ツアー」, and fans may travel to several cities to see the same show. When people talk about a package tour, the kind with flights and hotels arranged, you will also hear 「パックツアー」. So this one word can be a bus trip, a day trip, or your favorite band on the road." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The first sound of this word is the one English speakers struggle with most. It is not 「す」 and not 「つ」 said like the T in to. It is a T with a little hiss, like the end of the English word cats. Try saying cats, then hold onto that last bit and add the vowel. You will meet the same katakana sound in many other loanwords. Tuna becomes 「ツナ」, as on a sandwich. A Christmas tree is a 「ツリー」. And two, as in a two shot photo, is 「ツー」. Then there is the long dash at the end. Do not cut it short, or the word sounds clipped. Give the last vowel two full beats. So the whole word has three beats: the hissy start, the vowel, and the stretch. Practice it with tuna and tree, and the tour will follow." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English tour, with a hissy 「ツ」 and a long stretched ending, and usually means a package tour with a guide and fixed plan.",
      "It combines into trip types like 「日帰りツアー|ひがえりツアー」, a day trip, and takes 「に参加する|にさんかする」, to join, and it also means a concert tour.",
      "The first sound is a T with a hiss, like the end of cats, the same 「ツ」 as in 「ツナ」, tuna, and the dash needs two full beats."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}の[ツアー]に{行きました|いきました}。",
    en: "I went on a tour of Kyoto." },
  "どうぶつえん|動物園": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「動|どう」, means to move. It has the shape for power on the right, so think of moving with force. You know it from 「活動|かつどう」, activity, and 「感動する|かんどうする」, to be moved emotionally. The second, 「物|ぶつ」, means thing. With the native reading it is 「もの」, as in 「食べ物|たべもの」, food. So 「動物|どうぶつ」, animal, is literally a thing that moves. That is a neat way to describe an animal. The third, 「園|えん」, means garden or park. It is the same kanji as in 「公園|こうえん」, park. You can even see a big box around it, like a fence around a garden. So the whole word is an animal park, a zoo. Ueno Zoo in Tokyo is the most famous, and its giant pandas have drawn huge crowds for decades. To recap: moving thing plus garden, a garden of animals." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word has a perfect partner. 「動物|どうぶつ」 is a moving thing, an animal. And 「植物|しょくぶつ」 is a planted thing, a plant. Add the garden kanji, and you get 「植物園|しょくぶつえん」, a botanical garden. Two gardens, one for things that move, and one for things that grow in place. The moving kanji also gives the verb 「動く|うごく」, to move, and 「自動車|じどうしゃ」, a car, which is literally a self moving vehicle. The garden kanji has another lovely member: 「幼稚園|ようちえん」, kindergarten, a garden for little children. And of course 「公園|こうえん」, a park, a public garden. So whenever you see 「えん」 at the end of a place, picture a fenced garden, full of children, plants, or animals." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Walk around a Japanese zoo and you will notice the animal names on the signs are usually written in katakana, even native Japanese names. This is the custom for names of living species. So you will see 「ゾウ」, elephant, 「キリン」, giraffe, 「サル」, monkey, and of course 「パンダ」 and 「ライオン」, lion, which are loanwords anyway. Fun fact: the giraffe's name is the same word as a legendary creature from old Chinese stories, and it is also a famous beer brand. To say you are going, use 「に」: 「動物園に行きましょう|どうぶつえんにいきましょう」. To say what you saw there, use 「で」 for the place. For counting small animals, use 「匹|ひき」, and for big ones like elephants, use 「頭|とう」. So read the katakana signs, count with the right counter, and enjoy the visit." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's oldest zoo is Ueno Zoo in Tokyo, which opened in eighteen eighty two. In nineteen seventy two, two giant pandas arrived there from China as a sign of friendship. People lined up for hours just to see them for a few seconds, and the country went panda crazy. Panda goods, panda snacks, and panda pictures were everywhere, and pandas have stayed hugely popular ever since. Another famous zoo is in Asahikawa, in snowy Hokkaido. It became popular by showing animals moving naturally, and in winter its penguins go for a walk through the snow, waddling past the visitors in a little line. A trip to the zoo is a classic family day out and a common school trip. So in Japan, a zoo can mean pandas, penguins in the snow, and a big day out for children." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「動物|どうぶつ」, animal, literally a moving thing, plus 「園|えん」, garden or park, as in 「公園|こうえん」, so an animal park.",
      "Its partner is 「植物|しょくぶつ」, a plant, which gives 「植物園|しょくぶつえん」, a botanical garden, one garden for things that move and one for things that grow.",
      "Zoo signs write animal names in katakana, like 「ゾウ」 and 「キリン」, and you count small animals with 「匹|ひき」 and big ones with 「頭|とう」.",
      "Ueno Zoo, Japan's oldest, set off a national panda craze when two pandas arrived in nineteen seventy two."
    ],
    ja: "[動物園|どうぶつえん]でパンダを{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw pandas at the zoo." },
  "じかん|時間": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「時|じ」, means time or hour. It has the sun, 「日|ひ」, on the left, a nice reminder that people once told time by the sun. You know it from telling the time, like 「三時|さんじ」, three o'clock. With the native reading it is 「とき」, a time when something happens. The second, 「間|かん」, means interval or space between. Look at the shape: a sun inside a gate, light shining through the gap. With the native reading it is 「あいだ」, as in 「この間|このあいだ」, the other day. So the word is a stretch of time, the space between two moments. That is why it also counts hours: 「一時間|いちじかん」 is one hour long. A common mix up: 「三時|さんじ」 is three o'clock, but 「三時間|さんじかん」 is three hours. To recap: hour plus gap, a span of time." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese clearly separates a point in time from a length of time, and this word is the key. A point is 「三時|さんじ」, three o'clock. A length is 「三時間|さんじかん」, three hours. The same pattern works with other units. 「三日|みっか」 can be the third of the month, but 「三日間|みっかかん」 makes it clearly a three day period. For weeks you already have 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」, one week. For years, 「一年間|いちねんかん」. To ask how long, say 「何時間|なんじかん」, how many hours. And this word teams up with two verbs you know. 「時間がある|じかんがある」 means to have time, and 「時間がかかる|じかんがかかる」 means it takes time. Notice that both use 「が」. So the extra 「かん」 turns a moment into a stretch, and that is the whole trick." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond hours, this word means the time for something. 「時間ですよ|じかんですよ」 means it is time, as in time to go or time is up. A teacher says it at the end of a test. You can also say lunch time, 「昼ごはんの時間|ひるごはんのじかん」. When you want to ask someone politely for a moment of their time, say 「今、お時間ありますか|いま、おじかんありますか」, do you have a moment now? It is a very polite and useful opener with a teacher or a coworker. Japan is also known for punctuality. Keeping time is 「時間を守る|じかんをまもる」, literally to protect the time. Arriving five minutes early is normal, and trains apologize for a delay of only a minute or two. So use this word for hours, for moments, and for being on time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji have surprising readings worth knowing. The first, time, is 「じ」 in o'clock, and 「とき」 for a time when something happens. It repeats in 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes. And it has a special reading in 「時計|とけい」, a clock or watch, which you simply learn as a whole. The second kanji, the gap, reads 「あいだ」, between, and 「かん」 in this word. It also reads 「ま」, a space or pause, in 「間に合う|まにあう」, to be in time, literally to fit into the gap. You see that same reading in 「間違い|まちがい」, a mistake, a gap that went wrong. And in 「人間|にんげん」, a human being, it is read 「げん」, with the idea of a person living among others. So one gate with a sun inside gives you between, a pause, a human, and being on time." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「時|じ」, hour, with a sun on the left, plus 「間|かん」, gap, a sun inside a gate, so the span between two moments.",
      "The extra 「かん」 turns a point into a length, so 「三時|さんじ」 is three o'clock but 「三時間|さんじかん」 is three hours.",
      "It also means the time for something, and 「今、お時間ありますか|いま、おじかんありますか」 is a polite way to ask for a moment of someone's time.",
      "The gap kanji reads 「ま」 in 「間に合う|まにあう」, to be in time, and 「げん」 in 「人間|にんげん」, a human being."
    ],
    ja: "[時間|じかん]がありません。",
    en: "I don't have time." },
  "せかい|世界": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「世|せ」, means world, society, or a generation of people. The second, 「界|かい」, means boundary or realm. It has a rice field, 「田|た」, on top, and fields have edges and borders. So the word is the realm of the world, everything within the borders of human society. The word actually came from Buddhist texts, where it described the universe, and it later came to mean the world as we know it. A very useful add on is 「中|じゅう」, all through. 「世界中|せかいじゅう」 means all over the world. You have the same 「中|じゅう」 in 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day long. To say the world's best, you can say 「世界で一番|せかいでいちばん」, number one in the world, using 「一番|いちばん」 from this lesson. A typical sentence: 「世界は広いです|せかいはひろいです」, the world is big. To recap: world plus boundary, the whole realm of the world." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up in many set phrases. Number one in the world is often shortened to 「世界一|せかいいち」, and you will see it all over ads and TV: the world's best ramen, the world's tallest tower. A place on the World Heritage list is a 「世界遺産|せかいいさん」, and Japan has many, like Mount Fuji and the old temples of Kyoto. A world map is 「世界地図|せかいちず」, using the map word you already know. World history at school is 「世界史|せかいし」. And if someone is famous around the globe, they are 「世界的に有名|せかいてきにゆうめい」, world famous. The ending 「的|てき」 turns the noun into a describing word, a bit like the English ending ical. So say number one, heritage, map and famous, and the whole world is at your fingertips." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「世|せ」, has a traditional explanation worth knowing. Its shape is often described as three tens joined together, which is thirty. Thirty years was thought of as one generation, so the kanji came to mean a generation, an age, and then the world of people. It has a native reading too, 「よ」, which you will meet in 「世の中|よのなか」, society, the world we live in. The second kanji, 「界|かい」, has a rice field on top. Underneath is a part that you also see in 「紹介|しょうかい」, introduction. It gives the sound 「かい」 here, while the field gives the meaning of a border, like the edge of a field. You will meet this kanji again in words about different worlds or spheres. So a generation plus a border makes the world." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "Japanese has a few words that English might translate as the world, and they are not the same. This word is the big one, the whole planet, all countries, and also a particular sphere, like the world of fashion or the world of a novel. 「世の中|よのなか」 is the world of people and society, the way things are. You might sigh and say the world is a strange place, and use 「世の中|よのなか」. For between countries, use 「国際|こくさい」, international, as in an international student or an international phone call. And 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, literally outside the sea, is for going abroad from Japan. So if you say you want to travel the world, use this word. If you want to go abroad, use overseas. And if you are talking about society and how life works, use the people world." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「世|せ」, world or generation, plus 「界|かい」, boundary, with a rice field on top, so the whole realm within the world's borders.",
      "It shows up in set phrases like 「世界一|せかいいち」, number one in the world, and 「世界遺産|せかいいさん」, a World Heritage site.",
      "The first kanji looks like three tens joined, thirty years or one generation, which grew to mean the world of people.",
      "Use this word for the whole world, 「海外|かいがい」 for going overseas, and 「世の中|よのなか」 for society and how life works."
    ],
    ja: "[世界|せかい]は{広い|ひろい}です。",
    en: "The world is big." },
  "びよういん|美容院": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "All three kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「美|び」, means beauty. You will meet it again in 「美術館|びじゅつかん」, art museum. The second, 「容|よう」, means appearance or looks. Together, 「美容|びよう」 means beauty care, making your looks beautiful. The third, 「院|いん」, means an institution or building. It is the same kanji as in 「病院|びょういん」, hospital, and 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school. Here is a trap. 「美容院|びよういん」 and 「病院|びょういん」 sound very close. The beauty parlor has a full 「よ」 sound, while the hospital has a small 「ょ」 squeezed in. Listen carefully, or you may end up at the wrong place. In Japan this word is mostly used for a hair salon, the place to get your hair cut and styled. A barber shop for men has its own word. To recap: beauty plus looks plus institution, the place that makes you look good." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is what you need at the salon. Book ahead, using 「予約|よやく」 from this lesson. When you arrive, the stylist, 「美容師|びようし」, will ask what you want. The basic menu uses loanwords: 「カット」, a cut, 「カラー」, coloring, and 「パーマ」, a perm. To ask for shorter hair, say 「短くしてください|みじかくしてください」, please make it short, using the 「く」 form of the adjective. To ask for just a little, add 「少し|すこし」 at the start. Many salons also give you a hair wash with a short head massage. There is no tipping, so do not leave extra money. You will also hear 「美容室|びようしつ」, which means the same thing, with room instead of institution. For a traditional men's barber, people say 「床屋|とこや」. So book, say short or just a little, and enjoy the head massage." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Look at the example sentence again. In English you say I got my hair cut, because someone else cut it. But Japanese simply says 「髪を切りました|かみをきりました」, I cut my hair, even when a stylist did all the work. This is normal in Japanese for services. You also say you built a house, even if a builder did it. Nobody will think you cut your own hair. So when you come back from the salon, just say 「髪を切りました|かみをきりました」. Friends may notice and say 「髪、切った|かみ、きった」, did you cut your hair? in the casual past. If you really did it yourself, add 「自分で|じぶんで」, by myself. So the simple sentence covers the service, and an extra word covers doing it yourself. Keep it short, just like the new haircut." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The beauty kanji at the start of this word is in some lovely words. Its native reading is in 「美しい|うつくしい」, beautiful, a more elegant word than 「きれい」. With 「人|じん」, it makes 「美人|びじん」, a beautiful woman. It is in 「美術館|びじゅつかん」, an art museum, a hall of beautiful arts. And, in a special whole word reading, it is in 「美味しい|おいしい」, delicious, which is beautiful taste. The last kanji, 「院|いん」, an institution, shows up in 「病院|びょういん」, hospital, 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school, and 「寺院|じいん」, a Buddhist temple. Notice the sound pattern: so many institutions end in 「いん」. So when you hear 「いん」 at the end, think building, and check the first part to know whether you will be cured, educated, or made beautiful." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「美容|びよう」, beauty care, plus 「院|いん」, institution, and careful, since 「病院|びょういん」, hospital, sounds almost the same but with a small 「ょ」.",
      "At the salon ask for 「カット」 or 「カラー」, say 「短くしてください|みじかくしてください」 for shorter hair, and leave no tip.",
      "Japanese says 「髪を切りました|かみをきりました」, I cut my hair, even when a stylist did it, and adds 「自分で|じぶんで」 only if you really did it yourself.",
      "The beauty kanji is in 「美しい|うつくしい」 and 「美術館|びじゅつかん」, while many institutions end in 「いん」, like 「病院|びょういん」 and 「大学院|だいがくいん」."
    ],
    ja: "[美容院|びよういん]で{髪|かみ}を{切りました|きりました}。",
    en: "I got my hair cut at the beauty parlor." },
  "てぶくろ|手袋": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their native Japanese readings. The first, 「手|て」, means hand. You know it from 「手紙|てがみ」, letter, and 「お手洗い|おてあらい」, restroom, the place to wash your hands. The second, 「袋|ふくろ」, means bag or sack. Look at the bottom of the kanji and you can see the shape for clothing. So gloves are literally hand bags, little bags for your hands. It is a very Japanese way of naming something, just by describing it. Notice the sound change. On its own, bag is 「ふくろ」. When it comes second in a compound, the first sound gets voiced and becomes 「ぶくろ」. This happens a lot in Japanese, just like 「すし」 becoming 「ずし」. Gloves go on with the verb 「する」, as in the example, or with 「はめる」. To recap: hand plus bag, with 「ふくろ」 turning into 「ぶくろ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both halves of this word have big families. The hand kanji appears in 「手紙|てがみ」, letter, and 「手伝う|てつだう」, to help, lending a hand. With the Chinese-origin reading 「しゅ」, it often means a skilled person, as in 「歌手|かしゅ」, a singer, and 「選手|せんしゅ」, an athlete. The bag kanji gives you a whole shopping trip of words. 「紙袋|かみぶくろ」 is a paper bag. A plastic shopping bag is 「ビニール袋|ビニールぶくろ」. And a sleeping bag is 「寝袋|ねぶくろ」, a sleep bag. Notice that in all of these, the bag sound turns into 「ぶくろ」 when it comes second, just like in gloves. There is even a casual, slightly old fashioned word some men use for their mother, 「お袋|おふくろ」. So one bag kanji holds paper, plastic, sleep, and even mom." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You put gloves on with 「する」 or 「はめる」, and you take them off with 「外す|はずす」 or 「取る|とる」. So 「手袋を外してください|てぶくろをはずしてください」 means please take off your gloves. Gloves come in pairs, so you might hear 「片方|かたほう」, one of the pair. That is the word you need when you lose one: 「手袋を片方なくしました|てぶくろをかたほうなくしました」, I lost one of my gloves. In winter, single lost gloves are often placed on a fence or a post near where they were found, so the owner can spot them on the way back. Mittens, the kind without fingers, are 「ミトン」, a loanword. Cotton work gloves, the cheap white ones used for gardening or moving house, are 「軍手|ぐんて」. So you put them on, take them off, and maybe lose just one." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan you will notice white gloves in some surprising places. Many taxi drivers wear neat white gloves while driving. So do some bus drivers, train conductors, and staff at department stores and hotels. The gloves give a clean, formal, careful image, like a promise of good service. Politicians on the campaign trail often wear white gloves too, waving from the top of a car. At home, cheap cotton work gloves are a must for cleaning, gardening, and the big end of year house cleaning. And in cold places like Hokkaido, children wear thick mittens, sometimes joined by a string through the coat sleeves so they cannot lose them. So gloves in Japan are not only for warmth. They can also be a uniform, a sign of good manners, and a tool for hard work." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「手|て」, hand, plus 「袋|ふくろ」, bag, so little hand bags, with 「ふくろ」 voicing to 「ぶくろ」 when it comes second.",
      "The bag kanji turns into 「ぶくろ」 in 「紙袋|かみぶくろ」, a paper bag, and 「寝袋|ねぶくろ」, a sleeping bag, just like in gloves.",
      "Put gloves on with 「する」 or 「はめる」, take them off with 「外す|はずす」, and if you lose one, it is 「片方|かたほう」, one of the pair.",
      "White gloves are a sign of careful service in Japan, worn by many taxi drivers, conductors and department store staff."
    ],
    ja: "{冬|ふゆ}は[手袋|てぶくろ]をします。",
    en: "I wear gloves in winter." },
  "せいかつ|生活": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「生|せい」, means life or being born. It is the same kanji as in 「学生|がくせい」, student, and 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, someone born before you. It also appears in 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, birthday, with a different reading. The second, 「活|かつ」, means activity or being lively. It has the water shape on its left, and you can think of water flowing, full of life. You know it from 「活動|かつどう」, activity. So the word is life plus activity, the everyday business of living. It means your daily life and lifestyle, like student life or life in Japan. A common mistake: English life can also mean being alive or a whole lifetime, but this word is mostly about the daily routine of living. Add 「する」 and it means to live or get by, as in living on a small budget. To recap: life plus lively activity, your day to day living." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is used a lot when talking about where and how you live. 「学生生活|がくせいせいかつ」 is student life, and 「日本の生活|にほんのせいかつ」 is life in Japan. A very common sentence for someone who has moved is 「日本の生活に慣れました|にほんのせいかつになれました」, I have gotten used to life in Japan. The particle is 「に」 for what you get used to. Money for living is 「生活費|せいかつひ」, living costs, like rent and food. Someone with a healthy routine has a good 「生活|せいかつ」, and someone who stays up all night has an unhealthy one. A question a new friend might ask you is how your life here is going. You can answer that it is busy but fun. So use this word for the routine of your days, the place you live, and the money it takes." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "English life covers many ideas, and Japanese splits them into separate words. This word is daily life, the routine of living. 「暮らし|くらし」 is very close, but softer and more everyday, the way you get by, often used about living alone or a simple life. Living alone is 「一人暮らし|ひとりぐらし」. Then there is 「人生|じんせい」, a person's life as a whole story, from birth to old age. If you say life is short, or my life changed, use 「人生|じんせい」. And 「命|いのち」 is life as being alive, the life that can be saved or lost. Doctors save a 「命|いのち」. So student life is 「生活|せいかつ」, a whole lifetime is 「人生|じんせい」, and being alive is 「命|いのち」. Choose by asking: is it my routine, my story, or my heartbeat?" },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「生|せい」, may be the kanji with the most readings in common use. Here it is 「せい」. In 「一生|いっしょう」, a whole lifetime, it is 「しょう」. In the verb 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born, it is 「う」. In 「生きる|いきる」, to live, it is 「い」. And in 「生ビール|なまビール」, draft beer, it is 「なま」, raw or fresh. Its picture is often explained as a plant sprouting up from the ground, which fits nicely with being born and growing. The core idea in all these words is fresh, living, coming into being. When you meet it in a new word, you can usually guess it has to do with life or birth, and then you only need to guess the reading. So one kanji covers being born, living a whole life, and a fresh cold beer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「生|せい」, life, plus 「活|かつ」, lively activity, with water flowing on its left, so the everyday business of living.",
      "It is the word for daily life in a place, as in 「日本の生活に慣れました|にほんのせいかつになれました」, I have gotten used to life in Japan.",
      "Use 「生活|せいかつ」 for your routine, 「人生|じんせい」 for your whole life story, and 「命|いのち」 for being alive.",
      "The first kanji has many readings, like 「しょう」 in 「一生|いっしょう」 and 「なま」 in 「生ビール|なまビール」, all around the idea of fresh, living things."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[生活|せいかつ]はたのしいです。",
    en: "Life in Japan is fun." },
  "ことし|今年": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has a special reading that belongs to the whole word, not to each kanji. The first kanji, 「今|いま」, means now. The second, 「年|とし」, means year. So the meaning is easy: the year we are in now, this year. But the sound 「ことし」 can't be split neatly into the two kanji, so it is best to learn it as one unit. The same thing happens with 「今日|きょう」, today, and 「今朝|けさ」, this morning, which also have special whole-word readings. Other words with the same kanji use the normal sound 「こん」, as in 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, and 「今月|こんげつ」, this month. For the years around it, you have 「来年|らいねん」, next year. Notice that last year and next year use 「ねん」, but this year is special. A common greeting at New Year is to ask someone to look after you well again this year. To recap: now plus year, read with its own special sound." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a grammar point that trips up many learners. Time words that are relative to now, like this year, next year, and last year, do not take the particle 「に」. You say 「今年日本に行きます|ことしにほんにいきます」, with no particle after this year. The same goes for 「来年|らいねん」, 「今日|きょう」, and 「毎日|まいにち」. But a specific year or date does take 「に」: 「二〇二七年に|にせんにじゅうななねんに」, in twenty twenty seven. So the rule is simple. If the word moves with today, no 「に」. If it is a fixed point on the calendar, use 「に」. You can also put 「の」 after it to describe something: 「今年の夏|ことしのなつ」, this summer, or 「今年の目標|ことしのもくひょう」, this year's goal. And add 「は」 to contrast it with other years. So no 「に」, but 「の」 and 「は」 are fine." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here is the full set of year words around this one. Last year is 「去年|きょねん」. The year before last has a native reading, 「おととし」. Next year is 「来年|らいねん」, and the year after next is 「再来年|さらいねん」, literally next year again. Notice the pattern of sounds. The native years, this year and the year before last, both end in 「とし」, the native word for year. The Chinese-origin years end in 「ねん」. Every year can be read two ways: 「毎年|まいとし」 or 「まいねん」, and both are correct. You may also hear 「昨年|さくねん」 in formal speech, which means last year too. So for daily life, learn this lineup: the year before last, last year, this year, next year, and the year after next. Two of them sound native, and the rest sound Chinese." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This word is everywhere around New Year in Japan. Right after midnight, people say 「あけましておめでとうございます」, happy New Year, and then add 「今年もよろしくお願いします|ことしもよろしくおねがいします」, please treat me well again this year. That second line goes in nearly every New Year card, and Japanese people send many of these cards to arrive on the first of January. Before the year ends, when you see someone for the last time, you say 「よいお年を|よいおとしを」, have a good year, using 「年|とし」 again. And every December, a kanji is chosen to sum up the year that just passed, based on votes from the public. A famous monk writes it with a giant brush at a temple in Kyoto, and it is shown on the news. So this year is a word of greetings, cards, and one big kanji." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「今|いま」, now, plus 「年|とし」, year, but 「ことし」 is a special whole-word reading, like 「今日|きょう」, so learn it as one unit.",
      "Relative time words like this one take no 「に」, so say 「今年日本に行きます|ことしにほんにいきます」, while a fixed year does take 「に」.",
      "The lineup is 「おととし」, 「去年|きょねん」, this year, 「来年|らいねん」 and 「再来年|さらいねん」, with the native ones ending in 「とし」.",
      "At New Year people say 「今年もよろしくお願いします|ことしもよろしくおねがいします」, please treat me well again this year, a line in nearly every New Year card."
    ],
    ja: "[今年|ことし]{日本|にほん}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'm going to Japan this year." },
  "あたたかい|暖かい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading of the kanji. On the left of 「暖|あたた」 is the sun, 「日|ひ」. That sun is the key. This kanji is for warmth in the air and in the weather, like the warmth of a sunny spring day. There is a second kanji with exactly the same sound, 「温かい|あたたかい」. That one has the water shape on its left, and it is for warm things you touch or drink, like soup or a bath. You know it from 「温泉|おんせん」, hot spring. So remember: sun for warm weather, water for warm food and drinks. In speech they sound identical, so you only need to choose when writing. In casual speech people often shorten it to 「あったかい」. It fits between 「暑い|あつい」, hot weather, and 「涼しい|すずしい」, cool weather, as a nice in between. Warm can also describe a kind heart, with the water version. To recap: the sun on the left for the warmth of the weather." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an i-adjective, and its long shape makes the endings a good tongue twister. Past is 「暖かかった|あたたかかった」, it was warm, with two 「か」 sounds in a row. Negative is 「暖かくない|あたたかくない」. With 「なる」, you get 「暖かくなる|あたたかくなる」, to get warm. That is the classic phrase for spring arriving: 「暖かくなりましたね|あたたかくなりましたね」, it has gotten warm, hasn't it? Change the ending to 「さ」 and you get a noun, 「暖かさ|あたたかさ」, warmth. There is also a verb partner pair. 「暖める|あたためる」 means to warm something, like a room, and takes 「を」. 「暖まる|あたたまる」 means to warm up by itself, like a room getting warmer. So practice the past tense slowly, and use the 「なる」 form when spring comes." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The sun warmth kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「だん」. You will see it in 「暖房|だんぼう」, heating, the warm air setting on an air conditioner. Its opposite is 「冷房|れいぼう」, cooling, using the cold kanji. In winter you switch your unit to heating, and in summer to cooling. A mild winter is 「暖冬|だんとう」, a warm winter. Meanwhile, the other warm kanji, the one with water, reads 「おん」. It is in 「温泉|おんせん」, hot spring, 「気温|きおん」, air temperature, and 「温度|おんど」, temperature in general. Notice the interesting twist. Air temperature uses the water kanji, even though warm weather uses the sun kanji. You simply have to learn that one. So when you set the heating, think sun, and when you check the temperature, think water." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Many Japanese homes heat only the room people are using, not the whole house. That makes some homes chilly in winter, and it explains a much loved piece of furniture: the 「こたつ」. It is a low table with a heater underneath and a thick blanket over the top. You sit on the floor with your legs under the blanket, warm and cozy, often with a bowl of mandarin oranges on top. Once you get in, it is very hard to get out. There is also a lovely seasonal phrase, 「小春日和|こはるびより」. It means a mild, sunny day in late autumn or early winter, like a little spring that pops up when it is supposed to be cold. English sometimes calls this an Indian summer. So warmth in a Japanese winter means a heated table, oranges, and a surprise spring day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The sun, 「日|ひ」, on the left marks warmth in the air and weather, while 「温かい|あたたかい」, with water on the left, is for warm food, drinks and baths.",
      "Its past is the tongue twister 「暖かかった|あたたかかった」, and 「暖かくなりましたね|あたたかくなりましたね」 is the classic line when spring arrives.",
      "The sun kanji reads 「だん」 in 「暖房|だんぼう」, heating, while the water kanji reads 「おん」 in 「気温|きおん」, air temperature, the twist you just have to learn.",
      "Many homes heat only one room, which explains the 「こたつ」, a low table with a heater and blanket, and a mild late autumn day is 「小春日和|こはるびより」."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[暖かい|あたたかい]です。",
    en: "It's warm today." },
  "すずしい|涼しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. On the left of 「涼|すず」 is the water shape, three little drops. On the right is the kanji for capital, the same one at the start of the city name Kyoto, which you may know. A way to remember it: picture a cool breeze blowing in off the water, the most refreshing place in the capital. This word is for pleasantly cool weather, the kind you enjoy. It is used after the summer heat, for an autumn evening, or for shade on a hot day. Compare it with 「寒い|さむい」, which is unpleasantly cold, and 「冷たい|つめたい」, which is cold to the touch. A common mistake is to use this word for a cold drink. For that, Japanese uses 「冷たい|つめたい」. The season of cool is 「秋|あき」, fall, as in the example. Japanese summers are so hot and humid that people really look forward to this feeling. To recap: water on the left, a cool breeze you are happy to feel." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese summers are hot and sticky, so people have many traditional ways to feel cool. One is the 「風鈴|ふうりん」, a little wind bell hung by a window. When a breeze passes, it rings softly, and just the sound makes people feel cooler. Another is 「打ち水|うちみず」, sprinkling water on the street or garden in front of a house to cool the air and settle the dust. People fan themselves with a flat round fan called 「うちわ」, often handed out free at summer festivals. And on a hot day, nothing beats cold thin noodles, 「そうめん」, served with ice. All of these aim for the same feeling: this word, a pleasant coolness. So in Japan, cool is not only the weather. It is a sound, a splash, a breeze, and a bowl of noodles." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is a favorite for small talk. When the heat finally breaks in September, everyone says 「涼しくなりましたね|すずしくなりましたね」, it's gotten cool, hasn't it? It is a perfect, friendly greeting to a neighbor or coworker. It also describes a room or a place: 「この部屋は涼しいです|このへやはすずしいです」, this room is nice and cool. And there is a fun idiom: 「涼しい顔|すずしいかお」, a cool face. It means looking calm and unbothered, as if nothing happened, especially when you should be worried or feel guilty. Picture someone who broke the vase, standing there with a cool face. Remember the trap too: never use this word for a cold drink or cold hands. That needs the touch word. So use it for weather, places, and that calm, innocent face." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. The first part sounds like 「鈴|すず」, a little bell. To be clear, that is only a sound match, not where the word comes from. Now picture a hot summer evening. You are sitting on a wooden porch, sweating. Then a breeze comes in off the river, and the wind bell above you rings: suzu, suzu. Instantly the air feels cooler. The bell keeps ringing as the breeze blows, and you sigh with relief, a long shh sound, like the end of the word. Bell, bell, then a happy sigh. Whenever you hear this word, imagine that little bell over the porch and the cool wind that makes it ring. So the ringing bell and the happy sigh give you the cool evening breeze." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Water drops on the left and capital on the right suggest a cool breeze off the water, and it is for pleasantly cool weather, never a cold drink, which is 「冷たい|つめたい」.",
      "To feel cool in sticky summers, people hang a 「風鈴|ふうりん」, a wind bell, sprinkle water with 「打ち水|うちみず」, and eat cold 「そうめん」 noodles.",
      "「涼しくなりましたね|すずしくなりましたね」 is the go-to small talk when the heat breaks, and 「涼しい顔|すずしいかお」 is a calm, innocent face when you should feel guilty.",
      "The start sounds like 「鈴|すず」, a little bell, so picture a wind bell ringing on a hot porch as a cool breeze arrives, then a happy sigh."
    ],
    ja: "{秋|あき}は[涼しい|すずしい]です。",
    en: "Fall is cool." },
  "つめたい|冷たい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. On the left of 「冷|つめ」 are two little strokes. This shape stands for ice, and it shows up in kanji about cold. So this word is about cold you can touch, like ice. Use it for cold drinks, cold water, cold hands, or a cold wind on your skin. It also works for people, like in English, for someone who is cold or unkind. Here is the big trap for English speakers. Cold weather is a different word, 「寒い|さむい」. If you say the day itself is cold, use 「寒い|さむい」. If you touch something cold, use this word. And for pleasantly cool weather you have 「涼しい|すずしい」. The opposite of this word is 「温かい|あたたかい」, warm to the touch. Vending machines in Japan sell both cold drinks, with blue labels, and hot drinks, with red labels, right next to each other. To recap: the ice strokes on the left, for cold you can touch." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese temperature words come in two sets, and this word belongs to the touch set. For the weather and the air around you, the scale goes 「暑い|あつい」, hot, 「暖かい|あたたかい」, warm, 「涼しい|すずしい」, cool, and 「寒い|さむい」, cold. For things you touch or drink, the scale goes 「熱い|あつい」, hot, written with a different kanji, 「温かい|あたたかい」, warm, 「ぬるい」, lukewarm, and finally this word, cold. Lukewarm is the useful one learners forget. It is what you say when your bath or your coffee is not hot enough. So a cold day is 「寒い|さむい」, but a cold beer is this word. A hot day is one kind of 「あつい」, and hot soup is the other. Picture two thermometers, one in the sky and one in your hand, and pick the right one." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has several readings, all about cold. In this word it is 「つめ」. In the verbs 「冷える|ひえる」, to get cold, and 「冷やす|ひやす」, to chill, it is 「ひ」. These two verbs are a pair. 「ビールを冷やす|ビールをひやす」 means to chill the beer, which you do. 「ビールが冷えている|ビールがひえている」 means the beer is chilled, the result. With a different reading, 「冷める|さめる」 means to cool down, like food going cold before you eat it, so you might hear 「冷めないうちに|さめないうちに」, before it gets cold, eat it. And the Chinese-origin reading 「れい」 is in 「冷蔵庫|れいぞうこ」, refrigerator, a cold storage box, and 「冷房|れいぼう」, air conditioning. So one icy kanji covers the beer, the fridge, and the dinner going cold." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a Japanese restaurant, a glass of cold water is often brought to you for free. Staff and customers sometimes call it 「お冷|おひや」, using the same cold kanji with a polite 「お」 in front. So you can ask 「お冷をください|おひやをください」, water, please. When ordering a drink, you may be asked if you want it hot or cold: 「温かいのと冷たいの、どっちがいいですか|あたたかいのとつめたいの、どっちがいいですか」, which do you want, hot or cold? The word also works for people, but be careful. Calling someone a 「冷たい人|つめたいひと」 is a strong criticism. It means they are unkind or distant. So use it freely for drinks and water, and carefully for people. Ask for cold water by its polite name, and you will sound like a local." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The two ice strokes on the left mark cold you can touch, like drinks or hands, while cold weather is a different word, 「寒い|さむい」.",
      "Picture two thermometers, one for the air and one for your hand, where this word is the cold end of the touch scale, just past 「ぬるい」, lukewarm.",
      "The same icy kanji gives 「冷やす|ひやす」, to chill, 「冷める|さめる」, to cool down, and 「冷蔵庫|れいぞうこ」, the refrigerator.",
      "Ask for free water with 「お冷をください|おひやをください」, and be careful with people, since 「冷たい人|つめたいひと」 is a strong criticism."
    ],
    ja: "[冷たい|つめたい]{水|みず}を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink cold water." },
  "おそい|遅い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. The kanji 「遅|おそ」 has the road shape wrapping around the bottom and left side. This shape appears in kanji about moving along a road. A way to remember it: picture someone dragging their feet on a long road, slow and late. That is the double meaning of this word. It can mean slow, as in a slow bus, and it can mean late, as in a late hour. You have already met the adverb 「遅く|おそく」, doing something late, and 「遅くなる|おそくなる」, to be late. The same kanji shows up in 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, to be late for an appointment, and 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」, to miss a train. The opposite for both meanings is 「はやい」, with one kanji for early and another for fast. A common phrase at night is 「もう遅いです|もうおそいです」, it's already late. To recap: the road shape for slow going, so slow or late." },
      { title: "Late or delayed", text: "This kanji appears in a little family of lateness words, and each one has its own job. This adjective describes something as slow or late in general: a slow bus, a late hour. But if your train is running behind schedule, Japanese uses the verb 「遅れる|おくれる」, to be delayed, with the same kanji read differently. So say 「電車が遅れています|でんしゃがおくれています」, the train is delayed. If you said the train is 「遅い|おそい」, it would sound like the train is just slow. If you are late for school, work, or a meeting, there is a special verb, 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, to arrive late for something with a set start. And if you miss the train completely, that is 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」, to ride late. So slow is this adjective, delayed is 「おくれる」, late for class is 「ちこくする」, and gone without you is 「のりおくれる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this word a lot in everyday complaints and apologies. Waiting for a friend who has not shown up, you might mutter 「遅いですね|おそいですね」, they are late, aren't they? When the friend finally arrives, a casual friend might simply shout 「遅い|おそい」, you are late! And the late friend says 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」, sorry for being late, which is the standard apology. It uses the 「く」 form plus 「なる」, so literally, sorry for having become late. Another common phrase is 「もう遅い|もうおそい」, it is already too late, as in, there is nothing we can do now. And with 「まで」, you get 「遅くまで|おそくまで」, until late: 「遅くまで働きました|おそくまではたらきました」, I worked until late. So apologize with the 「く」 form, and complain with the plain one." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular i-adjective, so it follows all the usual patterns. Past: 「遅かった|おそかった」, it was slow or late. Negative: 「遅くない|おそくない」, not slow. To use it before a verb, change the last 「い」 to 「く」: 「遅く起きる|おそくおきる」, to get up late. That adverb form is how you describe when you did something, like eating dinner late or going to bed late. With 「なる」 it means to become late: 「帰りが遅くなる|かえりがおそくなる」, I will be home late, a very common message to family. And in a comparison, it works with 「より」 just like other adjectives: 「バスは電車より遅いです|バスはでんしゃよりおそいです」, the bus is slower than the train. So the plain form describes, the 「く」 form tells when, and the 「なる」 form warns your family you will be home late." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The road shape wraps around the kanji, like someone dragging their feet on a long road, so it means both slow and late.",
      "A train behind schedule is 「遅れる|おくれる」, being late for class is 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, and missing the train is 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」, while this adjective just means slow or late.",
      "The standard apology for being late is 「遅くなってすみません|おそくなってすみません」, using the 「く」 form plus 「なる」.",
      "The 「く」 form tells when, as in 「遅く起きる|おそくおきる」, to get up late, and 「帰りが遅くなる|かえりがおそくなる」 warns your family you will be home late."
    ],
    ja: "このバスは[遅い|おそい]です。",
    en: "This bus is slow." },
  "ねむい|眠い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. On the left of 「眠|ねむ」 is the eye, 「目|め」. Eyes are what close when you get sleepy. A way to remember it: picture heavy eyes slowly drooping shut. Another hook for the sound: it is like the long sleepy moan you make when you have to get up early. This is an i-adjective, so it describes how you feel: 「眠いです|ねむいです」, I'm sleepy. The verb for going to bed is 「寝る|ねる」. It sounds similar but is a different kanji, so don't mix them up. Being sleepy is the feeling, and 「寝る|ねる」 is the action. In Japan you often see people napping on trains, even standing up. Long commutes and late nights make this word very common. A related useful word is 「朝寝坊する|あさねぼうする」, to oversleep, what happens when you stay sleepy too long. To recap: the eye on the left, heavy and ready to close." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This adjective has a verb behind it: 「眠る|ねむる」, to sleep. Here is how it differs from 「寝る|ねる」, which you already know. 「寝る|ねる」 is the action of going to bed or lying down. You can 「寝る|ねる」 and still be awake, staring at the ceiling. 「眠る|ねむる」 is actually being asleep, the state of sleep itself. So if you went to bed but could not fall asleep, you say 「眠れませんでした|ねむれませんでした」, I could not sleep. Another fun word is 「居眠り|いねむり」, dozing off while you are supposed to be awake, like in class or on the train. The feeling of drowsiness is 「眠気|ねむけ」, and a cup of coffee to fight it off is often called a drowsiness waker. So feeling sleepy is this adjective, going to bed is 「寝る|ねる」, and actually sleeping is 「眠る|ねむる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan you will see people sleeping in public all the time, and nobody minds. Commuters doze off on trains, sitting or even standing, and often wake up just in time for their stop. Students sometimes nod off in class, and office workers may close their eyes in long meetings. There is even a word for this, 「居眠り|いねむり」, sleeping while present. Some researchers say that dozing off at work can be read as a sign of how hard someone has been working, since they are so tired, rather than as laziness. Of course, it depends on the place and the boss. Japan is also known for short sleep hours, and many people catch up with naps on the way to work. Trains are safe, so people feel relaxed enough to sleep. So a sleepy country has made napping in public perfectly normal." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an i-adjective that describes a feeling, so it is usually about yourself. 「眠いです|ねむいです」 means I am sleepy. To talk about how sleepy you were, use the past: 「眠かったです|ねむかったです」, I was sleepy. To say you are getting sleepy, use the 「く」 form with 「なる」: 「眠くなりました|ねむくなりました」, I have gotten sleepy. That one is perfect after a big lunch or a boring lecture. For other people, Japanese prefers not to state their feelings directly. Instead, you add 「そう」 to the stem: 「眠そうですね|ねむそうですね」, you look sleepy. That is a kind thing to say to a tired friend. You will learn that pattern properly later. So say it plainly about yourself, use the 「なる」 form when the feeling comes on, and say looks sleepy about others." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The eye, 「目|め」, on the left is what droops shut when you are sleepy, and don't confuse it with 「寝る|ねる」, the action of going to bed.",
      "The verb behind it is 「眠る|ねむる」, actually being asleep, so if you went to bed but stayed awake you say 「眠れませんでした|ねむれませんでした」.",
      "Sleeping in public is normal in Japan, with commuters and even office workers dozing in what is called 「居眠り|いねむり」, sleeping while present.",
      "Say 「眠いです|ねむいです」 about yourself, 「眠くなりました|ねむくなりました」 when sleepiness comes on, and 「眠そうですね|ねむそうですね」, you look sleepy, about others."
    ],
    ja: "{今朝|けさ}はとても[眠い|ねむい]です。",
    en: "I'm very sleepy this morning." },
  "かんたん|簡単": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「簡|かん」, has bamboo on top. Long ago people wrote on thin strips of bamboo, and this kanji came to mean brief and simple, like a short note. The second, 「単|たん」, means single or one alone. Put them together and you get brief and single, nothing complicated, so simple or easy. This is a na-adjective. Before a noun, add 「な」: 「簡単な料理|かんたんなりょうり」, simple cooking. The opposite is 「難しい|むずかしい」, difficult. You also know 「やさしい」, easy. 「やさしい」 often means gentle and easy, and can also mean kind. 「簡単|かんたん」 is more about something being simple and quick to do. A very common phrase is to say a dish is easy to make, or a test was easy, as in the example. To recap: bamboo note plus single, short and simple." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, single or simple, is in a word every learner needs: 「単語|たんご」, a vocabulary word, a single word. It is also in 「単純|たんじゅん」, simple, but often in the sense of simple minded or too simple. So a simple test is 「簡単|かんたん」, but a simple person who does not think things through is 「単純|たんじゅん」. Now compare three words for easy. This word means simple to do, not complicated. 「やさしい」, written with the kanji for easy, is gentle and easy, like an easy book. And 「楽|らく」 means easy in the sense of comfortable or not tiring, like an easy job or a comfortable chair. A math problem can be 「簡単|かんたん」. A lazy day is 「楽|らく」. So simple to do, gentle, and comfortable are three different kinds of easy." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a na-adjective, so it follows the na patterns. Before a noun, add 「な」: 「簡単な問題|かんたんなもんだい」, an easy question. As a sentence ending, use 「です」 or 「でした」: 「宿題は簡単でした|しゅくだいはかんたんでした」, the homework was easy. The negative is 「簡単じゃないです|かんたんじゃないです」, it is not easy. Watch out for a common mistake. Some learners say 「かんたんい」 or try to use the i-adjective past ending. That is wrong, because it is a na-adjective. To use it before a verb, add 「に」: 「簡単に作れます|かんたんにつくれます」, you can make it easily. And with 「なる」, 「簡単になりました|かんたんになりました」, it became easy. So remember 「な」 before nouns, 「に」 before verbs, and never an i-adjective ending." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up in some handy set phrases. 「簡単に言うと|かんたんにいうと」 means simply put, or in short, a great way to start summarizing. A teacher might say 「簡単に説明します|かんたんにせつめいします」, I will explain briefly. On cooking sites and TV, you constantly see easy recipes labeled with this word, promising dinner in ten minutes. And when someone serves you a simple meal, they may modestly say 「簡単なものですが|かんたんなものですが」, it is only something simple. That kind of modesty is very typical when offering food or gifts. If a friend is nervous, you can encourage them: 「簡単ですよ|かんたんですよ」, it's easy, don't worry. And if something is harder than it looks, people say 「簡単じゃない|かんたんじゃない」, it is not that simple. So use it to summarize, to encourage, and to be modest about your cooking." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「簡|かん」, a brief note written on bamboo strips, plus 「単|たん」, single, so brief and single, nothing complicated.",
      "Something easy to do is 「簡単|かんたん」, comfortable and not tiring is 「楽|らく」, and 「単純|たんじゅん」 often means too simple or simple minded.",
      "It is a na-adjective, so use 「な」 before nouns and 「に」 before verbs, as in 「簡単に作れます|かんたんにつくれます」, and never an i-adjective ending.",
      "It fits handy phrases like 「簡単に言うと|かんたんにいうと」, simply put, and the modest 「簡単なものですが|かんたんなものですが」 when serving food."
    ],
    ja: "このテストは[簡単|かんたん]でした。",
    en: "This test was easy." },
  "かかる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is usually written in kana. It tells you how much time or money something takes. The amount comes right before it, with no particle: 「一時間かかります|いちじかんかかります」, it takes an hour. With money, it is the same: 「千円かかります|せんえんかかります」, it costs a thousand yen. To ask, combine it with 「どのぐらい」, how long, from this lesson: 「どのぐらいかかりますか」. Use 「から」 and 「まで」 to give the start and end: from home to school, it takes ten minutes. A common mistake for English speakers is to try to say it with 「とる」 or 「する」, because English says take. In Japanese, time and money hang on this verb instead. Notice that the thing taking the time is not the person. The trip or the task takes the time. A way to remember it: it sounds a little like car, car. Picture a line of cars, car after car, stuck in traffic, and the trip taking ages. To recap: put the amount first, then this verb." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a group one verb: 「かかります」, 「かかった」, 「かからない」. It is intransitive, so the time or the money is the thing doing the taking, and if you mark it, you use 「が」: 「時間がかかる|じかんがかかる」, it takes time. It has a transitive partner, 「かける」, with 「を」. 「時間をかける|じかんをかける」 means to spend time on something on purpose, to take your time. So compare: 「この料理は時間がかかる|このりょうりはじかんがかかる」, this dish takes a long time, just a fact. 「この料理に時間をかける|このりょうりにじかんをかける」, I put time into this dish, a choice. You already know 「かける」 from making a phone call, 「電話をかける|でんわをかける」. That is the same verb, another face of it. So the time takes itself with 「が」, and you spend it with 「を」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb has a surprising number of meanings, and all of them share the idea of something hanging on or catching onto something. A picture hanging on the wall: 「絵がかかっています|えがかかっています」. A door that is locked has a lock hanging on it: 「かぎがかかっています」, it is locked. When the engine catches, 「エンジンがかかる」, the car starts. A phone call coming in is 「電話がかかってくる|でんわがかかってくる」. And to see a doctor is 「医者にかかる|いしゃにかかる」, to be under a doctor's care. Time and money are just one more use: the cost hangs on the trip. In kanji, these are written in a few different ways, which is one reason people often just use kana. So when you hear this verb, think of things hanging, catching, and costing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a typical exchange at a travel desk. You ask 「東京から大阪まで新幹線でどのぐらいかかりますか|とうきょうからおおさかまでしんかんせんでどのぐらいかかりますか」, from Tokyo to Osaka by bullet train, about how long does it take? The answer: 「二時間半ぐらいかかります|にじかんはんぐらいかかります」, it takes about two and a half hours. Notice that 「ぐらい」, about, goes right after the amount. For money, you ask 「いくらかかりますか」, how much does it cost? You might hear 「全部で一万五千円ぐらいです|ぜんぶでいちまんごせんえんぐらいです」, about fifteen thousand yen in total. And after a trip, use the past: 「三時間かかりました|さんじかんかかりました」, it took three hours. You can also say 「時間がかかりますね|じかんがかかりますね」, it takes a while, doesn't it? So ask how long, ask how much, and add about after the number." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The amount of time or money comes right before it with no particle, as in 「一時間かかります|いちじかんかかります」, and the trip takes the time, not the person.",
      "It pairs with 「が」 for time that simply takes, while its partner 「時間をかける|じかんをかける」 means spending time on purpose.",
      "All its meanings share the idea of hanging or catching, like a picture on the wall, a lock on a door, or an engine starting with 「エンジンがかかる」.",
      "Ask 「どのぐらいかかりますか」 for time or 「いくらかかりますか」 for money, and put 「ぐらい」, about, right after the amount."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}まで{十分|じゅっぷん}[かかります|かかります|かかる]。",
    en: "It takes ten minutes to the station." },
  "とまる|泊まる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading of the kanji. On the left of 「泊|と」 is the water shape. On the right is 「白|しろ」, white. The kanji first meant a boat anchoring in a harbor for the night. Today it means staying somewhere overnight, at a hotel, an inn, or a friend's place. The place takes 「に」: 「ホテルに泊まる|ホテルにとまる」, to stay at a hotel. Be careful. There is another verb with the same sound, 「止まる|とまる」, to stop, like a car stopping. A way to keep them apart: a boat stops in the harbor for the night, so the watery one is the overnight stop. Also note that this verb is about spending the night. For staying a long time somewhere, Japanese uses other words. In Japan, a classic trip means staying at a 「旅館|りょかん」, a traditional inn, with tatami floors, a hot bath and dinner served in your room. Hotels count nights with a special counter, so a one night stay is 「一泊|いっぱく」. To recap: the water shape, a boat anchoring for the night." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a group one verb: 「泊まります|とまります」, 「泊まった|とまった」, 「泊まらない|とまらない」. The place takes 「に」, and you can add how many nights with a special counter. One night is 「一泊|いっぱく」, two nights is 「二泊|にはく」, and three nights is 「三泊|さんぱく」. Notice the sound changes, just like with other counters. Travel ads constantly use the pattern 「一泊二日|いっぱくふつか」, one night, two days, a classic short trip. There is also a transitive partner, 「泊める|とめる」, to let someone stay. So 「友だちを泊めました|ともだちをとめました」 means I let my friend stay over at my place. And the friend would say they stayed at your place, with this verb and 「に」. So you stay with 「に」, let someone stay with 「を」, and count the nights with the 「はく」 counter." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji, 「はく」, shows up in several travel words. On hotel websites and signs, you will see 「宿泊|しゅくはく」, lodging or staying, a formal word. The price for one night is written as the lodging fee. 「外泊|がいはく」 means staying out overnight somewhere other than home, a word parents and dormitories care about. The kanji at the start of lodging, 「宿|やど」, is itself a word for an inn or a place to stay. For kids, a sleepover at a friend's house is a cute phrase, 「お泊まり会|おとまりかい」, a staying over party. And private homes rented to travelers are called 「民泊|みんぱく」, people lodging. So all these words share the same watery kanji, and all of them are about spending the night somewhere that is not your own bed." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has some unique places to spend the night. A capsule hotel gives you a bed inside a small pod, stacked two high in long rows, with a curtain or a screen for a door. It is cheap, clean, and popular with travelers and with workers who missed the last train home. At the other end, you can stay overnight at a Buddhist temple. This is called 「宿坊|しゅくぼう」, temple lodging, and the mountain temple area of Koyasan is the most famous place for it. Guests eat vegetarian temple food and can join the monks' morning prayers. Then there are business hotels, simple and tidy, near almost every station. And of course the traditional inn, with its hot baths. So in Japan, staying overnight can mean a pod, a temple, or a futon on tatami." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The water on the left comes from a boat anchoring for the night, so it means staying overnight, with the place marked by 「に」, unlike 「止まる|とまる」, to stop.",
      "Count nights with 「一泊|いっぱく」 and 「二泊|にはく」, as in the classic 「一泊二日|いっぱくふつか」, and use 「泊める|とめる」 for letting someone stay.",
      "The reading 「はく」 appears in lodging words like 「宿泊|しゅくはく」, 「外泊|がいはく」, staying out overnight, and 「民泊|みんぱく」, homes rented to travelers.",
      "An overnight stay in Japan can mean a capsule hotel pod, temple lodging called 「宿坊|しゅくぼう」, or a futon on tatami at a traditional inn."
    ],
    ja: "ホテルに[泊まりました|とまりました|とまる]。",
    en: "I stayed at a hotel." },
  "なる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is usually written in kana. It is one of the most useful verbs in Japanese. It shows a change, something turning into something else. After a noun, use 「に」: 「医者になる|いしゃになる」, to become a doctor. After a na-adjective, also use 「に」: 「元気になる|げんきになる」, to get well. After an i-adjective, change the final 「い」 into 「く」: 「寒くなる|さむくなる」, to get cold. You already know 「遅くなる|おそくなる」, to be late, which uses exactly this pattern. A very common mistake for English speakers is to say the i-adjective with 「に」. Remember, the 「い」 becomes 「く」 instead. English often says get or become, like get cold or get better. Japanese uses this verb for all of them. It is also used for things that happen by themselves, like seasons changing: 「春になる|はるになる」, spring comes. A way to remember it: picture a caterpillar turning into a butterfly. Change happens, and this verb is the change. To recap: noun or na-adjective plus 「に」, or the 「く」 form, then this verb." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a group one verb: 「なります」, 「なった」, 「ならない」. It has a natural partner in 「する」, and the pair shows a big idea in Japanese. 「する」 is when someone makes a change happen. This verb is when a change happens by itself. Compare: 「部屋を暗くする|へやをくらくする」, I make the room dark, maybe by turning off the lights. And 「部屋が暗くなる|へやがくらくなる」, the room gets dark, maybe because the sun is setting. Notice the particles: 「を」 with 「する」, but 「が」 with this verb. Japanese often prefers the version that just happens, because it sounds softer and less pushy. You will also use it for age: 「二十歳になりました|はたちになりました」, I turned twenty, with the special reading for age twenty. So make it with 「する」, let it happen with this verb, and turn twenty with 「になる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb shows up in some very common set phrases. 「好きになる|すきになる」 means to come to like someone or something, even to fall for someone. 「気になる|きになる」 means something is on your mind, you are curious or bothered about it. You might hear 「どうなりましたか」, how did it turn out? when someone asks about your job interview. At shops and restaurants, you will hear staff say 「千円になります|せんえんになります」, that comes to a thousand yen. Some people grumble that this is not proper polite Japanese, but you hear it every day. And a classic phrase when starting with a new group or teacher is 「お世話になります|おせわになります」, thank you for looking after me, which uses this verb to say I will be in your care. So this little verb carries liking, curiosity, prices, and gratitude." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful, because a few verbs sound like this one. 「鳴る|なる」 sounds exactly the same, but means to ring or make a sound, like a phone or an alarm: 「電話が鳴っています|でんわがなっています」, the phone is ringing. Context almost always tells you which one is meant. If a phone or a bell is the subject, it is ringing, not becoming. Then there is 「なれる」, which can be the verb 「慣れる|なれる」, to get used to something. 「日本の生活に慣れました|にほんのせいかつになれました」, I got used to life in Japan. Do not mix it up with this verb in the 「た」 form, 「なった」. And 「なく」, from 「泣く|なく」, is to cry. So listen for the subject. A phone rings, a person gets used to things, and almost anything else simply becomes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It shows change, with 「に」 after nouns and na-adjectives, as in 「医者になる|いしゃになる」, while i-adjectives switch 「い」 to 「く」, as in 「寒くなる|さむくなる」.",
      "With 「する」 someone makes a change, with 「を」, while this verb lets it happen by itself, with 「が」, as in 「部屋が暗くなる|へやがくらくなる」.",
      "It fills set phrases like 「気になる|きになる」, to be on your mind, 「千円になります|せんえんになります」 at the register, and 「お世話になります|おせわになります」.",
      "Listen for the subject, since 「鳴る|なる」 is a phone or bell ringing and 「慣れる|なれる」 means getting used to something."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は{医者|いしゃ}に[なりました|なりました|なる]。",
    en: "My older brother became a doctor." },
  "きめる|決める": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native Japanese reading. On the left of 「決|き」 is the water shape. The kanji originally had to do with water breaking through a bank, and from that came the idea of something being settled for good. A way to remember it: picture a river bursting through, no going back. Once you decide, it's done. It is a transitive verb, so the thing you decide takes 「を」: 「日を決める|ひをきめる」, to decide on the date. To decide on one choice from several, use 「に」: 「これに決めます|これにきめます」, I'll go with this one. You also know that 「する」 can mean to decide on an item, as in ordering this one, which is the same idea in a more casual way. There is a partner verb, 「決まる|きまる」, which means something gets decided, without saying who did it. Japanese often prefers that one, which sounds softer. To recap: the water shape, breaking through to a final decision." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a group two verb, a ru-verb, so the forms are easy: 「決めます|きめます」, 「決めた|きめた」, 「決めない|きめない」, and the te-form 「決めて|きめて」. Its partner, 「決まる|きまる」, is a group one verb, and that difference matters. With this verb, a person decides something, and the thing takes 「を」. With the partner, the thing gets decided, and it takes 「が」. So 「日にちを決めました|ひにちをきめました」 means we decided the date, and 「日にちが決まりました|ひにちがきまりました」 means the date has been decided. In meetings and announcements, the second one is very common, because it focuses on the result and not on who made the choice. The same pattern happens with many verb pairs, like 「開ける|あける」 and 「開く|あく」. So a person decides with 「を」, and the decision settles itself with 「が」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a restaurant, once you have had time with the menu, the server will come back and ask if you have decided. You may hear 「お決まりですか|おきまりですか」, the polite version, using the partner verb. If you have not chosen yet, you can say 「まだ決めていません|まだきめていません」, I have not decided yet, or more simply, 「まだです」. When you are ready, use 「にします」 for your choice, like 「カレーにします」, I will have the curry. The noun 「決まり|きまり」 means a rule, as in school rules or house rules. And in casual speech, 「決まってる|きまってる」 means of course, obviously, it is already settled. If someone asks whether you are coming to the party, you can laugh and say that of course you are. So decide your order, mention rules, and say of course, all from one family." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「けつ」, and it often becomes 「けっ」 with a small 「っ」 before certain sounds. It appears in strong, important words. 「決心|けっしん」 is a firm resolution, deciding in your heart. 「解決|かいけつ」 is solving a problem, untying and deciding it. 「決定|けってい」 is a decision, a formal word you see in the news or in business. And in sports, the final match of a tournament is 「決勝|けっしょう」, deciding the win. Notice how all of these feel final and serious, like the water bursting through the bank in the original meaning. Compare everyday choices, where people just use the native verb. You decide what to eat, but a company announces a decision. So the native reading is for daily choices, and 「けつ」 is for big, final ones." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The water shape comes from water breaking through a bank, settled for good, and you decide something with 「を」 or pick one choice with 「これに決めます|これにきめます」.",
      "A person decides with 「を」, as in 「日にちを決めました|ひにちをきめました」, while the partner 「決まる|きまる」 takes 「が」 and just says it got decided.",
      "Servers ask 「お決まりですか|おきまりですか」, you can answer 「まだ決めていません|まだきめていません」, and 「決まり|きまり」 means a rule.",
      "Its reading 「けつ」 is for big, final decisions, as in 「決心|けっしん」, a resolution, and 「決勝|けっしょう」, a final match."
    ],
    ja: "{旅行|りょこう}の{日|ひ}を[決めました|きめました|きめる]。",
    en: "We decided the date of the trip." },
  "ごろごろする|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a sound and feeling word, a mimetic word, plus 「する」. Japanese has hundreds of these doubled words that paint a picture with sound. The core image of this one is something heavy rolling around. So when people use it about themselves, it means lazing around at home, rolling over on the sofa or floor, doing nothing in particular. It is a casual, friendly word. You might say it about your day off: 「週末はうちでごろごろします|しゅうまつはうちでごろごろします」. The same sound has other uses that share the rolling, rumbling feel. It is the sound of thunder rumbling in the distance, and also the sound of a cat purring. A way to remember it: picture a big cat rolling around on the floor and purring, completely relaxed. That's you on a lazy Sunday. A common mistake is to use this word for being busy at home. It means the opposite: no plans, no effort. To recap: rolling around like a heavy, happy cat, so lazing at home." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Japanese sound words often come in pairs, one with a clear sound and one with a voiced, buzzy sound. The voiced one usually feels bigger, heavier, or rougher. This word has a light twin: 「ころころ」. That is something small and round rolling lightly, like a marble or a little ball. It also describes a cheerful, tinkling laugh, or someone whose opinions keep changing. Now add the voicing, and you get the heavy version, something big tumbling or someone heavily lounging. You can hear the same pattern in other pairs. 「きらきら」 is a pretty twinkle, like stars. 「ぎらぎら」 is a harsh glare, like the summer sun. So the little dots on the kana make the picture heavier. When you lounge on the sofa, you are not a light marble. You are a heavy log, rolling around." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several sound words for taking it easy, and each one has its own flavor. This word is lounging at home, lying around and rolling over, doing nothing. 「のんびり」 is relaxed and leisurely, in a positive way, like a slow, happy day in the countryside. 「ぶらぶら」 is wandering around with no particular goal, like strolling through town window shopping. 「だらだら」 is dragging on lazily, usually in a bad way, like a meeting that goes on and on, or working without any focus. So if you spend Sunday lying on the sofa, you used this word. If you spend it walking around a shopping street, you used 「ぶらぶら」. If you enjoyed a quiet day at a hot spring, you used 「のんびり」. And if you should have been studying but kept checking your phone, that was 「だらだら」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Besides lazing around, this word has a few other common uses. When there is something in your eye, like a speck of dust or an eyelash, and it feels gritty and uncomfortable, people say 「目がごろごろする|めがごろごろする」. It is the feeling of something rolling around in there. It is also used when lots of something is just lying around everywhere, like rocks on a mountain path. You might hear that there are rocks lying all over the place, with this word. And it can be said of people or things that are surprisingly common, the kind you find everywhere. Still, the use you will hear most is lounging at home on a day off. A friend asks what you did on the weekend, and you smile and say you just lazed around. So it is a lazy day, a gritty eye, or rocks everywhere." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a sound word for something heavy rolling around, so it means lazing at home like a big, relaxed cat, the opposite of being busy.",
      "Its light twin 「ころころ」 is a small marble rolling, and the voicing makes it heavier, just as 「きらきら」 twinkles but 「ぎらぎら」 glares.",
      "Lounging at home is this word, while 「のんびり」 is a relaxed day, 「ぶらぶら」 is aimless wandering, and 「だらだら」 is dragging on lazily.",
      "It also describes a gritty eye, 「目がごろごろする|めがごろごろする」, or rocks lying everywhere, but mostly it is a lazy day off."
    ],
    ja: "{日曜日|にちようび}はうちで[ごろごろします|ごろごろします|ごろごろする]。",
    en: "On Sundays I laze around at home." },
  "りょこうする|旅行する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「旅|りょ」, means journey or trip. You will see it again in 「旅館|りょかん」, a traditional Japanese inn, a building for travelers. The second, 「行|こう」, means to go. You know it from 「行く|いく」, to go, and it is also in 「飛行機|ひこうき」, airplane. So the word is to go on a journey. Without 「する」, 「旅行|りょこう」 is the noun, a trip. With 「する」, it becomes the verb, to travel. The place you travel around takes 「を」, as in the example, a little like traveling across or through Japan. If you just say where you are going, use 「に」 with 「行く|いく」 instead. Japanese travelers love to bring back small gifts from trips for friends and coworkers, so a trip usually means shopping too. To recap: journey plus go." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word has some useful relatives. 「旅|たび」, using the first kanji alone with its native reading, is a journey. It sounds more poetic and personal, like a long trip of discovery. A school trip, which nearly every Japanese student takes, is 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」. A honeymoon is 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」, a newly married trip. A company that arranges trips is 「旅行会社|りょこうがいしゃ」, a travel agency. But be careful. A business trip is not this word at all. It is 「出張|しゅっちょう」, going out for work. If you say you are traveling for work, people will expect 「出張|しゅっちょう」. And the traditional inn, 「旅館|りょかん」, uses the same journey kanji. So travel for fun, a poetic journey, a school trip, a honeymoon, and a business trip that is not travel at all." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese people travel a lot inside their own country, and there are big travel seasons. The first is Golden Week, a string of holidays around the start of May. The second is the summer period called 「お盆|おぼん」, in mid August, when many people go back to their hometowns to honor their ancestors. The third is New Year. At these times, trains, roads, and airports are packed, and the news reports traffic jams that stretch for many kilometers. Another big tradition is the school trip. Many students visit Kyoto and Nara to see temples, or Hiroshima to learn about peace, and they travel with their whole grade. Those trips are a favorite memory for a lot of people. So if you want to travel in Japan, avoid the three big holiday seasons, or book very early." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Besides the verb with 「する」, there is another common pattern: 「旅行に行く|りょこうにいく」, to go on a trip. The 「に」 here shows the purpose of going. You can add the place with 「へ」 or 「に」: 「沖縄へ旅行に行きます|おきなわへりょこうにいきます」, I am going on a trip to Okinawa. You can also use 「中|ちゅう」, during, after the noun: 「旅行中|りょこうちゅう」 means while traveling, or away on a trip. You might see that on an automatic email reply. With the 「たい」 form, 「旅行したい|りょこうしたい」, I want to travel, is a sentence every language learner says sooner or later. And to talk about past trips, use the past: 「去年京都を旅行しました|きょねんきょうとをりょこうしました」. So use the verb, go on a trip, or be away on one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「旅|りょ」, journey, as in 「旅館|りょかん」, plus 「行|こう」, go, and the place you travel around takes 「を」.",
      "Relatives include 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」, a school trip, and 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」, a honeymoon, but a business trip is 「出張|しゅっちょう」 instead.",
      "The big travel seasons are Golden Week, 「お盆|おぼん」 in August, and New Year, when trains and roads are packed, so avoid them or book early.",
      "Besides the verb, say 「旅行に行く|りょこうにいく」, to go on a trip, or 「旅行中|りょこうちゅう」, away on a trip."
    ],
    ja: "{夏|なつ}に{日本|にほん}を[旅行します|りょこうします|りょこうする]。",
    en: "I'll travel around Japan in the summer." },
  "れんしゅうする|練習する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「練|れん」, has the thread shape on its left. It originally meant working raw silk until it became soft and fine, and from there it came to mean training or polishing a skill. The second, 「習|しゅう」, means to learn. It has feathers on top. A common hook is a young bird flapping its wings again and again until it learns to fly. You know this kanji from 「習う|ならう」, to learn from someone, and 「習慣|しゅうかん」, a custom or habit, something learned by repetition. So the word is polishing what you learn, which is what practice is. Use 「を」 for what you practice: 「ピアノを練習する|ピアノをれんしゅうする」. A common mix up: 「勉強する|べんきょうする」 means to study, mostly from books, while this word is for skills you repeat, like music, sports or speaking. To recap: polishing the silk plus a bird learning to fly." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for practice and training, and they fit different worlds. This word is the general one, for music, sports, language, anything you repeat to get better. In your textbook, the exercises are 「練習問題|れんしゅうもんだい」, practice questions. For traditional arts, like martial arts, tea ceremony, or calligraphy, people often say 「稽古|けいこ」, a word with a classic, disciplined feel. For drills and serious training, like a fire drill or military training, the word is 「訓練|くんれん」, which shares the polishing kanji with this word. You also hear the loanword 「トレーニング」 for working out at the gym. So you practice piano with this word, train in karate with 「稽古|けいこ」, run a fire drill with 「訓練|くんれん」, and lift weights with the loanword." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, practice has a special place in school life, through club activities, called 「部活|ぶかつ」. Students join a club, like baseball, brass band, or kendo, and practice almost every day, often on weekends and during holidays too. Many sports clubs even have practice before classes start, called 「朝練|あされん」, a short form of morning practice. Students might arrive at school at seven to run laps. The teams take it very seriously, and the national high school baseball tournament is watched across the whole country every summer. Some people now say this is too much and want shorter hours, and schools have started to set limits. Still, for many adults, those years of daily practice with friends are some of their strongest memories. So practice in Japan can mean early mornings and long summers with a team." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There are two ways to say what you are practicing. You can put the thing with 「を」, as in practicing piano. Or you can make it a noun with 「の」: 「ピアノの練習をする|ピアノのれんしゅうをする」, to do piano practice. Both are natural. To talk about a habit, use the 「ている」 form: 「毎日日本語を練習しています|まいにちにほんごをれんしゅうしています」, I am practicing Japanese every day. A friendly match in sports, played for practice, is 「練習試合|れんしゅうじあい」. And if you want to ask someone to practice speaking with you, try 「一緒に練習しませんか|いっしょにれんしゅうしませんか」, shall we practice together? That is a great line for a language exchange. So use 「を」 or 「の」, add 「ている」 for your routine, and invite friends to practice with you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「練|れん」, working silk until fine, plus 「習|しゅう」, learning, like a bird flapping until it flies, and it is for repeated skills, not book study like 「勉強|べんきょう」.",
      "This is the general word for practice, while traditional arts use 「稽古|けいこ」, drills use 「訓練|くんれん」, and the gym uses 「トレーニング」.",
      "School club activities, 「部活|ぶかつ」, mean practice almost every day, sometimes with morning practice called 「朝練|あされん」.",
      "Mark what you practice with 「を」 or 「の」, and invite a partner with 「一緒に練習しませんか|いっしょにれんしゅうしませんか」, shall we practice together?"
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}ピアノを[練習します|れんしゅうします|れんしゅうする]。",
    en: "I practice piano every day." },
  "いちばん|一番": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「一|いち」, is one, a single line. The second, 「番|ばん」, means number or turn in order. You know it from 「ばんごう」, number. So the word is number one, first in line, the best. It is the main way to say most or best in Japanese. Just put it before an adjective: 「一番好き|いちばんすき」, like the most, or 「一番高い|いちばんたかい」, the most expensive. For a group, use 「の中で|のなかで」, among: 「季節の中で夏が一番好きです|きせつのなかでなつがいちばんすきです」. English speakers sometimes look for a special ending for words like biggest or best. Japanese doesn't change the adjective at all. It just adds this word in front. It can also mean number one in a line, like the first person in a queue. To recap: one plus number, the number one, the most." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The number kanji in this word shows up in many everyday places. 「番号|ばんごう」 is a number, like a phone number. 「交番|こうばん」 is a small police box on a street corner, where officers take turns on duty. 「番組|ばんぐみ」 is a TV or radio program. 「順番|じゅんばん」 is order or your turn, like waiting in line. And with 「目|め」 added, it becomes an ordinal, first, second, third: 「二番目|にばんめ」, the second one. So 「一番|いちばん」 can be number one, the best, while 「一番目|いちばんめ」 is just the first in a line, with no idea of best. At a station, platforms are counted with this kanji too. Platform two is 「二番線|にばんせん」, number two line. So the same kanji gives you numbers, turns, TV shows, police boxes and train platforms." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "To ask about the best or the most, combine this word with a question word and 「が」. 「何が一番好きですか|なにがいちばんすきですか」, what do you like the most? 「誰が一番背が高いですか|だれがいちばんせがたかいですか」, who is the tallest? 「どこが一番よかったですか|どこがいちばんよかったですか」, where was the best? And 「いつが一番いいですか|いつがいちばんいいですか」, when is best? Notice that the question word always takes 「が」, never 「は」, because it is the new information. In the answer, the thing that is best also takes 「が」. If you are choosing among things like seasons or foods, you can start with the group and 「の中で|のなかで」. For only two choices, Japanese switches to a different pattern with 「どっち」 and 「ほう」, so save this word for three or more. So question word, 「が」, then this word, and the adjective." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond best, this word also works like very or the most with position words. 「一番前|いちばんまえ」 is the very front, like the front row of a concert. 「一番後ろ|いちばんうしろ」 is the very back. 「一番上|いちばんうえ」 is the very top, like the top shelf, and 「一番下|いちばんした」 is the bottom. You will hear 「一番近い駅|いちばんちかいえき」, the nearest station, all the time when asking directions. It also appears in songs and poems, where each verse is counted: verse one is 「一番|いちばん」, verse two is 「二番|にばん」. At a station, the announcement may tell you the train leaves from track number one, 「一番線|いちばんせん」. So the same word can mean the best, the very front, the nearest, the first verse, or track one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「一|いち」, one, plus 「番|ばん」, number in order, and you put it before an unchanged adjective, as in 「一番高い|いちばんたかい」, the most expensive.",
      "The number kanji shows up in 「交番|こうばん」, a police box, and 「番組|ばんぐみ」, a TV program, while 「一番目|いちばんめ」 is just first in line, not best.",
      "Ask with a question word plus 「が」, as in 「何が一番好きですか|なにがいちばんすきですか」, and save it for three or more choices.",
      "With position words it means the very, as in 「一番前|いちばんまえ」, the very front, and 「一番近い駅|いちばんちかいえき」, the nearest station."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは{夏|なつ}が[一番|いちばん]{好き|すき}です。",
    en: "I like summer the best." },
  "どっち／どちら|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both of these ask which one when there are two choices. They belong to the big set of words that start with 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」 and 「ど」. The 「ど」 words ask questions. 「どっち」 matches 「こっち」, this way, and 「どちら」 matches 「こちら」, this person or this way, politely. 「どっち」 is the casual form. You use it with friends. 「どちら」 is the polite form. You use it in shops, with strangers, and in polite speech. The typical pattern is A 「と」 B 「と」, then this word 「が」, as in the example: coffee and tea, which is better? Here is a common mistake. For three or more choices, use 「どれ」, which one, instead. So choose these words for exactly two options. 「どちら」 can also politely mean where, as in asking where someone is from. A way to remember it: two options, two words, one casual, one polite. To recap: which of the two, with a casual and a polite version." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "These two words belong to the four way family of 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」 and 「ど」, and it helps to see the whole grid. For things, you have 「これ」, 「それ」, 「あれ」 and 「どれ」. For places, 「ここ」, 「そこ」, 「あそこ」 and 「どこ」. For directions and choices between two, casually, 「こっち」, 「そっち」, 「あっち」 and 「どっち」. And the polite versions are 「こちら」, 「そちら」, 「あちら」 and 「どちら」. The polite ones are very flexible. 「こちら」 can mean this way, this one, or even this person, as in introductions: 「こちらは田中さんです|こちらはたなかさんです」, this is Mr. Tanaka. In the same way, 「どちら」 can mean which one, which way, where, or even who, when said politely. So learn the grid four by four, and remember that the polite row does extra jobs." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "When someone asks you which of two things is better, the answer uses 「ほう」, side. The question: 「犬と猫とどっちが好きですか|いぬとねことどっちがすきですか」, which do you like better, dogs or cats? The answer: 「猫のほうが好きです|ねこのほうがすきです」, I like cats better, literally the cat side is liked. If you like both, say 「どっちも好きです|どっちもすきです」, I like both, with 「も」 after the question word. If you do not care, say 「どっちでもいいです」, either one is fine. In polite speech, swap in the polite form: 「どちらでもいいです」. And if you like neither, there is a pattern with 「どちらも」 and a negative verb. So ask with this word, answer with 「ほう」, and use 「も」 for both or 「でも」 for either." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The polite form has some very common uses beyond which one. When you answer the door or the phone and do not know who it is, you say 「どちら様ですか|どちらさまですか」, who is it, please? It is much more polite than asking who directly. When meeting someone new, you might be asked 「お国はどちらですか|おくにはどちらですか」, which country are you from? And neighbors meeting on the street may ask 「どちらへ」, where are you off to? It is more of a friendly greeting than a real question, and a vague answer like just over there is fine. In shops, staff use the polite form all the time, asking which one you would like. Meanwhile, with friends and family, the casual form is everywhere. So use the polite version for strangers, doors and phones, and the casual one at home." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Both ask which of exactly two, with 「どっち」 casual and 「どちら」 polite, while three or more choices need 「どれ」.",
      "They fill the 「ど」 slot in the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 grid, and the polite row does extra jobs, so 「こちら」 can even mean this person.",
      "Answer with 「ほう」, as in 「猫のほうが好きです|ねこのほうがすきです」, and use 「どっちも」 for both or 「どっちでもいいです」 for either.",
      "The polite form gives 「どちら様ですか|どちらさまですか」 at the door or phone and 「お国はどちらですか|おくにはどちらですか」, which country are you from."
    ],
    ja: "コーヒーとこうちゃと[どっち]がいいですか。",
    en: "Which is better, coffee or tea?" },
  "はやく|早く／速く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, one sound, and both use the native reading. The first, 「早|はや」, has the sun, 「日|ひ」, on top. Think of the sun just rising. This one means early, as in getting up early. The second, 「速|はや」, has the road shape along the bottom. Think of moving fast along a road. This one means fast, as in speed. Both come from the adjectives you know, 「早い|はやい」, early, and 「速い|はやい」, fast. Change the final 「い」 to 「く」 and you get the adverb, the way you do something: early or quickly. Like in English, early and fast are related ideas, which is why they share one sound. In speech you can't hear the difference, so you only need to pick when writing. A very common phrase is 「早く|はやく」 on its own, said to someone who is slow: hurry up. The opposite is 「遅く|おそく」, late or slowly. To recap: sun for early, road for fast." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a pattern you can use with almost every i-adjective. Drop the last 「い」, add 「く」, and the adjective now describes how an action is done. So 「おそい」 becomes 「おそく」, and 「おそく起きる|おそくおきる」 means to get up late. 「大きい|おおきい」 becomes 「大きく|おおきく」, and 「大きく書く|おおきくかく」 means to write big. There is one irregular case to watch: 「いい」 becomes 「よく」, because its older form is 「よい」. This same 「く」 form also joins with 「なる」, to become. 「早くなる|はやくなる」 means to get earlier, and 「寒くなる|さむくなる」 means to get colder. Na-adjectives do the same job with 「に」 instead, like 「しずかに」, quietly. So whenever you see an adjective ending in 「く」 right before a verb, think of it as telling you the how. One small 「く」 turns a describing word into a doing word." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "You say this word every single morning without noticing. The greeting 「おはようございます」 really does come from it. The older polite phrase was 「お早くございます|おはやくございます」, roughly it is early. Over the centuries, in polite speech, the 「く」 at the end of adjectives softened into 「う」. So 「はやく」 became 「はやう」, and then the vowels melted together into 「はよう」. That is how you get 「おはよう」. The same sound change gave Japanese two other greetings you already know. 「ありがとう」 comes from 「ありがたく」, meaning it is rare and precious. And 「おめでとう」 comes from 「めでたく」, meaning it is happy and auspicious. So three of the most common phrases in the language are fossils of this same adverb form. Next time you say good morning, you are really saying how early it is. Good morning is, quite literally, you are early." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This little word shows up in a handful of phrases you will hear constantly. A parent at the door says 「早く！|はやく！」, hurry up. A softer, more polite version is 「早くしてください|はやくしてください」, please be quick. If you want someone to feel better, you can say 「早く元気になってください|はやくげんきになってください」, get well soon, which is literally become healthy quickly. At work, people often apologize for leaving with 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」, but a boss might say 「今日は早く帰ってください|きょうははやくかえってください」, go home early today. When writing, use the sun version for time, like waking, sleeping and going home early. Use the road version for speed, like running or speaking fast: 「速く走る|はやくはしる」. If you are unsure, the sun version is far more common in everyday writing. Just remember that hurry up and get well soon both start with this word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Write 「早く|はやく」, with the rising sun, for early, and 「速く|はやく」, with the road shape, for fast, both adverbs made from the 「い」 adjectives.",
      "Drop the final 「い」 and add 「く」 to turn almost any i-adjective into a how word, with 「いい」 becoming 「よく」 as the one exception.",
      "「おはよう」 comes from 「お早くございます|おはやくございます」, where the 「く」 softened to 「う」, the same change behind 「ありがとう」 and 「おめでとう」.",
      "On its own 「早く|はやく」 means hurry up, and 「早く元気になってください|はやくげんきになってください」 means get well soon."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[早く|はやく]{起きます|おきます}。",
    en: "I'll get up early tomorrow." },
  "あるいて|歩いて": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the te-form of 「歩く|あるく」, to walk, read with its native reading. The kanji 「歩|ある」 comes from an old picture of two feet, one stepping in front of the other. You can still see the shape for stopping, a foot, on top. Used before a verb of motion, the te-form tells you how you go: 「歩いて行く|あるいていく」, to go by walking, on foot. This is the te-form linking two actions, walking and going, so it means going while walking. A common mistake is to say on foot with 「で」, like you do for transportation. With a bus you say 「バスで」, but for walking you use this te-form instead. You can also use it with 「来る|くる」, to come on foot, and 「帰る|かえる」, to walk home. Japanese cities are very walkable, and many people walk to the station every day. To recap: two feet stepping, in the te-form for going on foot." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The dictionary form is 「歩く|あるく」, a u-verb ending in 「く」. Verbs like this make their te-form by changing 「く」 to 「いて」, just like 「書く|かく」 becomes 「書いて|かいて」 and 「聞く|きく」 becomes 「聞いて|きいて」. The one famous exception is 「行く|いく」, which becomes 「行って|いって」, so don't let that one confuse you here. The polite form is 「歩きます|あるきます」, and the past is 「歩いた|あるいた」. Now a particle surprise. When you name the path or area you walk through, Japanese uses 「を」, even though walking is not something you do to an object. So 「公園を歩く|こうえんをあるく」 means to walk through the park, and 「道を歩く|みちをあるく」 means to walk along the road. You use 「を」 the same way with crossing a street or going through a gate. The 「を」 here marks the ground you cover. So: a regular 「く」 verb, with 「を」 for the path under your feet." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for walking has a second, Chinese-origin reading, 「ほ」, which often turns into 「ぽ」 after other sounds. You may already know the most common example, 「散歩|さんぽ」, a stroll or a walk for pleasure, as in 「犬と散歩する|いぬとさんぽする」, to walk the dog. Notice the difference from this lesson's word. 「歩いて|あるいて」 is about getting somewhere on foot. 「散歩|さんぽ」 is wandering around just for fun, with no destination. Another useful one is 「歩道|ほどう」, a sidewalk, literally a walking road. And 「一歩|いっぽ」 means one step, as in taking one step forward. On signs and apartment ads you will also see 「徒歩|とほ」, on foot, in a phrase like 「駅から徒歩五分|えきからとほごふん」, five minutes on foot from the station. In speech, people would just say 「歩いて五分|あるいてごふん」. So the same feet take you on a stroll, a step, and a sidewalk." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, distance is usually given in walking minutes, not miles or blocks. Ask someone how far the station is, and you will likely hear 「歩いて十分ぐらいです|あるいてじゅっぷんぐらいです」, about ten minutes on foot. If it is close, they might add 「歩いて行けますよ|あるいていけますよ」, you can walk there. If it is far, they may say 「歩いて行くのはちょっと…|あるいていくのはちょっと」, walking is a bit much, which is a polite way of saying don't. A very natural mini dialogue goes like this. One person asks 「駅までどうやって行きますか|えきまでどうやっていきますか」. The other answers 「歩いて行きます|あるいていきます」. Many people walk to the station and then take a train, so you will also hear things like 「家から駅まで歩いて、電車に乗ります|いえからえきまであるいて、でんしゃにのります」. That last sentence uses the te-form twice, linking walking and riding in order. Measure your city in walking minutes, and you will sound local." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the te-form of 「歩く|あるく」, to walk, whose kanji shows two feet stepping, and before a motion verb like 「行く|いく」 it means on foot, never with 「で」.",
      "It follows the 「く」 to 「いて」 rule like 「書いて|かいて」, and the path you walk through takes 「を」, as in 「公園を歩く|こうえんをあるく」.",
      "The reading 「ほ」 or 「ぽ」 gives 「散歩|さんぽ」, a stroll for fun, 「歩道|ほどう」, a sidewalk, and 「徒歩|とほ」 on apartment ads.",
      "Distance in Japan is given in walking minutes, as in 「歩いて十分ぐらいです|あるいてじゅっぷんぐらいです」, about ten minutes on foot."
    ],
    ja: "[歩いて|あるいて]{学校|がっこう}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I walk to school." },
  "〜で|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a particle, so it comes right after a noun. It marks the means you use to do something. After a vehicle, it means by: 「バスで」, by bus, 「電車で|でんしゃで」, by train. After a tool, it means with: 「はしで」, with chopsticks, or 「日本語で|にほんごで」, in Japanese. You already know this same particle for the place where something happens, like studying at the library. The connecting idea is setting: the place you act in, or the tool you act with. A common mistake is to use it for walking. For on foot, Japanese uses 「歩いて|あるいて」 instead. To ask how, use 「何で|なにで」, by what means, or 「どうやって」, from this lesson. Watch out: 「何で|なんで」, with a different reading, usually means why. A way to remember it: think of English with or by, the tool you hold in your hand. To recap: after a vehicle or tool, it means by or with." },
      { title: "More jobs for de", text: "This particle wears several hats, and they all share one idea: the setting or means of an action. Beyond vehicles and tools, here are three more you will meet. First, language. 「日本語で話してください|にほんごではなしてください」 means please speak in Japanese, and 「英語で何ですか|えいごでなんですか」 asks what it is in English. Second, how many people. 「ひとりで」 means by yourself, and 「みんなで」 means all together, as in 「みんなで食べましょう|みんなでたべましょう」, let's all eat together. Third, a total. At a shop, 「全部で千円です|ぜんぶでせんえんです」 means that is a thousand yen altogether. In each case, the word before this particle is the frame the action happens inside: a language, a group, or a sum. Once you see it as a frame, the different English translations stop feeling random. So: by bus, in Japanese, with everyone, all together, one frame each time." },
      { title: "Particle contrast", text: "Two classic mix-ups involve this particle. The first is about getting on a vehicle. To go by bus is 「バスで行く|バスでいく」. But to get on the bus, you use 「に」: 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」. English speakers often say 「バスを乗る|バスをのる」, which sounds wrong. Think of it this way: 「に」 points at where you end up, inside the bus. The second mix-up is place. With an action, use this particle: 「図書館で勉強する|としょかんでべんきょうする」, study at the library. With simple existence, use 「に」: 「図書館にいる|としょかんにいる」, be at the library. The verbs 「ある」 and 「いる」 almost always take 「に」 for the place. Most action verbs, like eating, working, reading and meeting, take this one. A few verbs, such as living somewhere, are exceptions that take 「に」. A quick test helps. If something is happening there, choose this particle. If something is just there, choose 「に」. So: ride by bus with this one, get on the bus with 「に」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how this particle sounds in everyday situations. At a restaurant, you might ask 「はしで食べてもいいですか|はしでたべてもいいですか」, may I eat with chopsticks. At a register, the clerk may ask how you'll pay, and you answer 「カードでおねがいします」, by card, please. In class, the teacher says 「ペンで書いてください|ペンでかいてください」, please write in pen. And when you don't know a word, the most useful sentence of all is 「これは日本語で何と言いますか|これはにほんごでなんといいますか」, what do you call this in Japanese? A short dialogue: 「何で来ましたか|なにできましたか」, how did you come here? 「電車で来ました|でんしゃできました」, I came by train. Notice the question uses the same particle as the answer, so you can just swap the question word for the real thing. It is one of the busiest particles you'll use, so it pays to hear it everywhere." }
    ],
    short: [
      "After a vehicle or tool it means by or with, as in 「電車で|でんしゃで」 or 「はしで」, the same particle that marks where an action happens.",
      "It marks the frame an action happens inside, like a language in 「日本語で|にほんごで」, a group in 「みんなで」, or a total in 「全部で|ぜんぶで」.",
      "Go by bus with 「バスで」 but get on with 「バスに乗る|バスにのる」, and use this particle where something happens but 「に」 where something just is.",
      "Ask 「何で来ましたか|なにできましたか」 and answer 「電車で来ました|でんしゃできました」, simply swapping the question word for the real thing."
    ],
    ja: "バス[で]{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'll go by bus." },
  "どうやって|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is built from two pieces you know. The first, 「どう」, means how. The second, 「やって」, is the te-form of 「やる」, to do. So together it means doing it how, by what means, in what way. Use it to ask about method or route: 「どうやって行きますか|どうやっていきますか」, how do you get there? The answer usually uses 「で」 for a vehicle, like 「バスで」, or 「歩いて|あるいて」 for on foot. You can also use it for doing something, like how do you make this dish. A common mix up: 「どう」 on its own asks how something is, like how was the movie. This word asks for the way you do something. It is fine in polite speech when followed by a polite verb. To recap: how plus doing, the question about method." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb hiding inside this word is 「やる」, a casual cousin of 「する」, to do. Its te-form is 「やって」, just like 「帰る|かえる」 becomes 「帰って|かえって」. The te-form here works the same way as in 「歩いて行く|あるいていく」. It describes the manner of the next verb. So the full question is really in doing what way do you go. After it, you can put almost any action verb. 「どうやって作りますか|どうやってつくりますか」, how do you make it. 「どうやって読みますか|どうやってよみますか」, how do you read this. When you are curious and want an explanation, add the 「んです」 pattern: 「どうやって作るんですか|どうやってつくるんですか」, so how do you actually make it? For an answer, give the means with 「で」 or describe steps with te-forms: 「電車に乗って、駅から歩きます|でんしゃにのって、えきからあるきます」. One question, and the answer often comes back as a chain of te-forms." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This question word belongs to the ko-so-a-do family, the set that means this, that, that over there, and which. There is a matching set built the same way. 「こうやって」 means like this, doing it this way. People say it all the time while demonstrating, holding chopsticks and saying 「こうやって持ちます|こうやってもちます」, you hold them like this. 「そうやって」 means that way, the way you just did it, often said by a teacher: 「そうやって書きます|そうやってかきます」, write it like that. And 「ああやって」 points at something over there, like someone else's method. Two more relatives use the same 「どう」. 「どうして」 means why, literally doing how. And 「どうやって」 and 「どうして」 are easy to confuse, so remember that one asks for a method and the other for a reason. When you know one member of the family, you can guess the others. So ask with 「どう」, and show with 「こう」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture yourself lost in a Japanese city. You stop someone and say 「すみません、駅までどうやって行けばいいですか|すみません、えきまでどうやっていけばいいですか」. Even if that ending is new, the start is enough. A shorter version, 「駅までどうやって行きますか|えきまでどうやっていきますか」, works perfectly. The answer might be 「まっすぐ行って、右に曲がってください|まっすぐいって、みぎにまがってください」, go straight and turn right. At home, you might ask a friend about cooking: 「このケーキ、どうやって作ったの|このケーキ、どうやってつくったの」, how did you make this cake? That casual version drops 「ですか」 and ends with 「の」. In a formal speech or in writing, you may meet a stiffer cousin, 「どのように」, which means the same thing. In daily conversation, though, this is the everyday choice. Anytime you need directions or a recipe, this is your opener." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「どう」, how, plus 「やって」, doing, so it asks about method or route, unlike plain 「どう」, which asks how something is.",
      "The hidden verb is 「やる」, and the answer often comes back as means with 「で」 or a chain of te-forms describing the steps.",
      "Its family includes 「こうやって」, like this, said while demonstrating, and don't confuse it with 「どうして」, which asks why.",
      "It is your opener for directions and recipes, as in 「駅までどうやって行きますか|えきまでどうやっていきますか」, with 「どのように」 as the stiffer written version."
    ],
    ja: "[どうやって]{来ました|きました}か。",
    en: "How did you get here?" },
  "どのぐらい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built from two pieces you know. The first, 「どの」, means which, before a noun. The second, 「ぐらい」, means about or roughly, as in 「一時間ぐらい|いちじかんぐらい」, about an hour. So together it is about which amount, which is a natural way to ask how much or how long. You can also say 「どのくらい」, with a 「く」. Both are correct and mean the same thing. It pairs perfectly with 「かかる」, from this lesson: 「どのぐらいかかりますか」, how long does it take, or how much does it cost. You can use it for time, money, distance and amounts. A typical answer includes 「ぐらい」 again, like about ten minutes. A related word you will see later is 「このぐらい」, about this much. A way to remember it: you ask with 「ぐらい」, and you answer with 「ぐらい」. To recap: which plus about, how much or how long." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This question belongs with three answers built the same way. 「このぐらい」 means about this much, often said while holding your hands apart. 「そのぐらい」 means about that much, and 「あのぐらい」 means about as much as that over there. You may also hear 「どれぐらい」, which means the same thing as the question word here, and is just as common. Now an important neighbor. 「ぐらい」 is about an amount or a length: 「三時間ぐらい|さんじかんぐらい」, about three hours. Its look-alike 「ごろ」 is about a point in time: 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」, around three o'clock. So if you are asking how long, use 「ぐらい」, and if you are asking about when, use 「ごろ」 with 「いつ」: 「いつごろ」. Mixing them up changes the meaning from a clock time to a duration. So 「ぐらい」 measures, while 「ごろ」 points at the clock." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This question opens many everyday conversations. At a ticket counter, 「新宿までどのぐらいかかりますか|しんじゅくまでどのぐらいかかりますか」, how long does it take to Shinjuku? A friend might ask about your stay: 「日本にどのぐらいいますか|にほんにどのぐらいいますか」, how long are you in Japan? And a doctor or coach may ask about habits: 「どのぐらい運動しますか|どのぐらいうんどうしますか」, how much do you exercise? Answers usually echo the same 「ぐらい」. 「二十分ぐらいです|にじゅっぷんぐらいです」, about twenty minutes. 「一週間に三回ぐらいです|いっしゅうかんにさんかいぐらいです」, about three times a week. Japanese people tend to give rough numbers rather than exact ones, so expect 「ぐらい」 a lot even when the speaker knows the precise answer. Giving a little wiggle room sounds natural and modest. So ask with it, answer with it, and keep it a little vague." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「どの」, which, plus 「ぐらい」, about, so about which amount, and it pairs perfectly with 「かかる」 to ask how long or how much.",
      "Use 「ぐらい」 for an amount or length, but 「ごろ」 for a point in time, so 「三時間ぐらい|さんじかんぐらい」 measures while 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」 points at the clock.",
      "Answers echo 「ぐらい」, as in 「二十分ぐらいです|にじゅっぷんぐらいです」, because Japanese speakers prefer rough, modest numbers."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}まで[どのぐらい]かかりますか。",
    en: "How long does it take to the station?" },
  "〜しゅうかん|〜週間": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「週|しゅう」, means week. It has the road shape on its left, like the week going round and round. You know it from 「週末|しゅうまつ」, weekend, 「毎週|まいしゅう」, every week, and 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week. The second, 「間|かん」, means span or interval. It is the same kanji as in 「時間|じかん」, time or hours. So this counter means a span of weeks, a length of time in weeks. Put a number in front: 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」, one week, 「二週間|にしゅうかん」, two weeks. Notice the small 「っ」 with one, and with eight and ten, which squeeze into the next sound. Here is the trap. Without 「間|かん」, like 「来週|らいしゅう」, it is a point in time, next week. With 「間|かん」, it is a length of time, for weeks. That matches 「時間|じかん」 for hours. To recap: week plus span, how many weeks long." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Lengths of time behave differently from points in time. A point in time, like Monday or three o'clock, often takes 「に」. A length of time, like two weeks, usually takes no particle at all. So you say 「二週間日本にいました|にしゅうかんにほんにいました」, I was in Japan for two weeks, with nothing after the counter. Add 「ぐらい」 if it is approximate: 「一週間ぐらい|いっしゅうかんぐらい」. Now for frequency. Put 「に」 after the length, then the number of times: 「一週間に三回|いっしゅうかんにさんかい」, three times a week. That 「に」 means per. You can also add 「前|まえ」 or 「後|ご」 to place an event: 「二週間前|にしゅうかんまえ」, two weeks ago, and 「二週間後|にしゅうかんご」, two weeks later. So the same counter can tell you how long, how often, or how far back. So: no particle for how long, and 「に」 for how often." },
      { title: "Counting out loud", text: "The counting itself has a few bumps worth practicing. One is 「いっしゅうかん」, with a small 「っ」, so the 「い」 and the 「しゅ」 squeeze together. Two through seven are regular: 「にしゅうかん」, 「さんしゅうかん」, 「よんしゅうかん」, 「ごしゅうかん」, 「ろくしゅうかん」, 「ななしゅうかん」. Eight is usually 「はっしゅうかん」, again with a small 「っ」. Nine is 「きゅうしゅうかん」. Ten is 「じゅっしゅうかん」, sometimes said 「じっしゅうかん」. To ask how many weeks, say 「何週間|なんしゅうかん」. Also listen for the long vowel in the middle. The word for week is 「しゅう」, with two beats, so the whole counter is four beats long. Cut that vowel short, and you get 「しゅ」, a different sound, as in 「歌手|かしゅ」, singer. Say it slowly at first, clapping the beats. So: small 「っ」 on one, eight and ten, and keep that vowel long." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the first kanji. It has the movement radical, the long swooping stroke that wraps around the bottom left, used in kanji about going and traveling. Inside sits 「周|しゅう」, which means around or a full circuit, and it also gives the sound. So the week is a trip all the way around and back to the start. Now the second kanji. It is a gate, 「門|もん」, with the sun, 「日|ひ」, inside. The traditional explanation is light shining through the gap between two gate doors, so a gap, an interval, a span. Its native reading is 「あいだ」, between, and you also hear it as 「ま」 in some words. With the Chinese reading 「かん」, it turns time words into lengths: 「時間|じかん」, 「週間|しゅうかん」, and also 「年間|ねんかん」. Once you know the gap kanji, you can spot it as the length marker. So: going around, plus a gap in the gate." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「週|しゅう」, week, plus 「間|かん」, span, so 「来週|らいしゅう」 is a point in time but 「二週間|にしゅうかん」 is a length.",
      "A length takes no particle, as in 「二週間日本にいました|にしゅうかんにほんにいました」, while 「一週間に三回|いっしゅうかんにさんかい」 uses 「に」 for per.",
      "One, eight and ten get a small 「っ」, as in 「いっしゅうかん」, and keep the long vowel in 「しゅう」 so it doesn't sound like 「しゅ」.",
      "The week kanji is a trip all the way around, and the gap kanji, a sun inside a gate, turns time words into lengths."
    ],
    ja: "[二週間|にしゅうかん|〜しゅうかん]{日本|にほん}にいました。",
    en: "I was in Japan for two weeks." },
  "〜かげつ|〜か月": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This counter is for lengths of time in months. The kanji 「月|げつ」 means month or moon, read here with a Chinese-origin reading. The small 「か」 in the middle is a counting marker. You may also see it written with a tiny katakana 「ヶ」, and it is still read 「か」. So 「三か月|さんかげつ」 is three months long. Some numbers squeeze sounds together: 「一か月|いっかげつ」, one month, and 「六か月|ろっかげつ」, six months. Here is the big trap. Without 「か」, you get the names of the months: 「三月|さんがつ」 is March. So 「さんがつ」 is a point on the calendar, but 「さんかげつ」 is a length of time. You also know 「今月|こんげつ」, this month, and 「来月|らいげつ」, next month, with the same reading 「げつ」. A way to remember it: the little 「か」 is like a tape measure, it turns a month into a length. To recap: number plus 「か」 plus month, for how many months long." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "That little 「か」 in the middle has an interesting history. It stands for an old counter word, 「箇|か」, which simply means an item or a piece, something you count. Writers got tired of the full kanji and abbreviated it. One shortcut was a simple three stroke shape, and over time that shape came to look exactly like a tiny katakana 「ケ」. That is why you see 「ヶ月|かげつ」 on calendars and signs, and it is still read 「か」, not 「け」. It is not really katakana at all, just a look-alike shorthand. Today you may see the same word written four ways: with hiragana 「か」, with a small 「ヶ」, with a small 「ヵ」, or with the full old kanji. All of them are read the same. Genki uses hiragana, which is the safest choice for you. So the tiny 「ヶ」 is a disguised kanji, not a typo." },
      { title: "Counting out loud", text: "Counting months has some sound changes worth drilling. One is 「いっかげつ」, with a small 「っ」. Two is 「にかげつ」, three is 「さんかげつ」, four is 「よんかげつ」, and five is 「ごかげつ」. Six squeezes: 「ろっかげつ」. Seven is 「ななかげつ」. Eight is often 「はっかげつ」, and nine is 「きゅうかげつ」. Ten squeezes again: 「じゅっかげつ」. To ask how many months, say 「何か月|なんかげつ」. For a month and a half, add 「半|はん」 at the end: 「一か月半|いっかげつはん」. For half a year, though, people usually don't say six months. They say 「半年|はんとし」, using the native reading of year. One more twist: every month is 「毎月|まいつき」, with the native reading 「つき」, moon. So month has three readings, 「がつ」, 「げつ」 and 「つき」, depending on the word. Practice the small 「っ」 numbers and you have it." },
      { title: "Time patterns", text: "Once you can count months, you can plug the counter into several useful patterns. For how long, use no particle: 「三か月日本語を勉強しました|さんかげつにほんごをべんきょうしました」. For how often, add 「に」 and the number of times: 「一か月に一回|いっかげつにいっかい」, once a month. For ago, add 「前|まえ」: 「二か月前|にかげつまえ」, two months ago. For later, add 「後|ご」: 「二か月後|にかげつご」, two months later. And for about, add 「ぐらい」: 「三か月ぐらい|さんかげつぐらい」. A natural conversation might go like this. 「どのぐらい日本にいましたか|どのぐらいにほんにいましたか」, how long were you in Japan? 「六か月ぐらいいました|ろっかげつぐらいいました」, I was there about six months. Each pattern just sticks a small word onto the same block. So one counter, five patterns, and you can talk about any stretch of months." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The little 「か」 turns month into a length, so 「三か月|さんかげつ」 is three months long while 「三月|さんがつ」 is March.",
      "The tiny 「ヶ」 in 「ヶ月|かげつ」 is a shorthand for the old counter 「箇|か」, not real katakana, so it is still read 「か」.",
      "Six and ten squeeze, as in 「ろっかげつ」, half a year is usually 「半年|はんとし」, and month has three readings, 「がつ」, 「げつ」 and 「つき」.",
      "The counter plugs into patterns like 「一か月に一回|いっかげつにいっかい」, once a month, and 「二か月前|にかげつまえ」, two months ago."
    ],
    ja: "[三か月|さんかげつ|〜かげつ]{日本語|にほんご}をべんきょうしました。",
    en: "I studied Japanese for three months." },
  "〜ねん|〜年": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「年|ねん」 means year, read here with its Chinese-origin reading. After a number it counts years: 「一年|いちねん」, one year, 「三年|さんねん」, three years. With the native reading it is 「とし」, as in 「年上|としうえ」, someone older. You know the Chinese-origin reading from 「来年|らいねん」, next year. Here is something to watch. The same number plus this kanji can be a length of time, for three years, or a calendar year, the year two thousand. To make the length clear, add 「間|かん」, span, just like with weeks and hours: 「三年間|さんねんかん」, for three years. Unlike weeks and months, years don't need the extra piece. Both forms are used for lengths of time. Also, a student's year in school uses this same counter plus 「生|せい」, so a third year student is 「三年生|さんねんせい」. Japan also counts years by the emperor's era name, which you will see on forms. To recap: number plus year, with 「間|かん」 added when you want to stress the length." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The oldest forms of this kanji show a person with a bundle of grain on top. The usual explanation is a farmer carrying in the ripe harvest. Since crops were gathered once a year, the harvest came to stand for the year itself. The modern shape has been simplified so much that you can no longer see the person or the grain, but the idea of one harvest, one year, is a nice way to hold onto it. There are two main readings. The Chinese-origin 「ねん」 is used for counting years and in most compounds. The native 「とし」 is used when it stands alone, as in 「年をとる|としをとる」, to get older, literally to take on years. That native reading is why age words, like being older than someone, use 「とし」. So a bundle of grain, brought in once a year." },
      { title: "Counting out loud", text: "Counting years is mostly regular, with one big trap. One is 「いちねん」, two is 「にねん」, three is 「さんねん」. Four is the trap: 「よねん」, not 「よんねん」. Five is 「ごねん」, six is 「ろくねん」. Seven can be 「ななねん」 or 「しちねん」. Eight is 「はちねん」, nine is 「きゅうねん」, ten is 「じゅうねん」. To ask how many years, or which year, say 「何年|なんねん」. Now a few year words that break the pattern completely. This year is 「今年|ことし」, a special reading you just have to learn. Last year is 「去年|きょねん」. Every year can be 「毎年|まいとし」 or 「まいねん」, and both are common. The four trick also shows up in 「四時|よじ」, four o'clock, so if you know that one, you know this. So remember 「よねん」 for four, and 「ことし」 for this year." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan uses two year systems side by side. One is the familiar western calendar. The other counts years by the emperor's era name. The current era is 「令和|れいわ」, which began in 2019 when a new emperor took the throne. Before that came 「平成|へいせい」, from 1989 to 2019, and before that 「昭和|しょうわ」. Each era starts over at year one, and the first year has a special name, 「元年|がんねん」. So 2019 was 「令和元年|れいわがんねん」, and 2020 was 「令和二年|れいわにねん」. You will see era years on government forms, driver's licenses, bank documents, and some receipts, so it is worth knowing when you fill in your birth year. Many people mix both systems in daily life, and some even think of their age through the era they were born in. When a form asks for a date, check which system it wants. So in Japan, a year can have two names at once." }
    ],
    short: [
      "After a number it counts years, and adding 「間|かん」, as in 「三年間|さんねんかん」, stresses the length, though years work without it too.",
      "The oldest form shows a farmer carrying the harvest, gathered once a year, and the native reading 「とし」 appears alone, as in 「年をとる|としをとる」.",
      "The trap is four, which is 「よねん」, not 「よんねん」, and this year is the special 「今年|ことし」.",
      "Japan also counts years by era name, like 「令和|れいわ」, where the first year is 「元年|がんねん」, so check which system a form wants."
    ],
    ja: "[二年|にねん|〜ねん]{日本|にほん}にすんでいました。",
    en: "I lived in Japan for two years." },
  "このごろ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built from two pieces you know. The first, 「この」, means this, before a noun. The second, 「ごろ」, means around or about a point in time. You know it from 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」, around three o'clock. So together it is around this time, which means these days or lately. Use it at the start of a sentence to talk about how things are recently: 「このごろいそがしいです」, I'm busy these days. It describes an ongoing situation, so it often goes with the 「ています」 form or with adjectives. A close cousin is 「最近|さいきん」, recently. 「最近|さいきん」 can be used for a single event that just happened, but this word is for a state that continues over a period. So don't use it for something that happened once yesterday. A way to remember it: 「ごろ」 is around, so this word means the time around now. To recap: this plus around, how things are these days." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second half of this word comes from 「ころ」, an old word for a time or a period. After 「この」, its first sound becomes voiced, giving 「ごろ」. On its own, the plain 「ころ」 is still very much alive. 「子どものころ|こどものころ」 means when I was a child, in my childhood days. 「あのころ」 means back then, those days, often said with a little nostalgia. And 「そのころ」 means around that time, in a story about the past. The question form 「いつごろ」 asks roughly when. Notice the pattern: 「この」 points to now, so you get these days. 「あの」 points far away in time, so you get back in those days. Put these side by side and you have a little time machine: these days, back then, and around when. So this word is one member of a family of rough time markers, all built from the old word for a period." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this word in small talk about how life is going. A neighbor might say 「このごろ寒いですね|このごろさむいですね」, it's been cold lately, hasn't it. A friend might complain 「このごろ、ぜんぜん寝ていません|このごろ、ぜんぜんねていません」, I haven't been sleeping at all lately. You might share a new habit: 「このごろ毎朝走っています|このごろまいあさはしっています」, these days I run every morning. Notice the 「ています」 form there, because it describes something ongoing. A mini dialogue: 「このごろどうですか」, how have things been lately? 「ちょっといそがしいです」, a bit busy. In casual speech, younger people more often reach for 「最近|さいきん」, but this word is perfectly natural and sounds slightly softer and more reflective. Use it for a trend or a state, not a single event. So: a gentle word for how things have been going." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「この」, this, plus 「ごろ」, around, so the time around now, for an ongoing state rather than a single event like 「最近|さいきん」 can be.",
      "The 「ごろ」 comes from the old word 「ころ」, a period, which also gives 「子どものころ|こどものころ」, in childhood, and 「あのころ」, back then.",
      "It fits small talk about ongoing things, often with 「ています」, as in 「このごろ毎朝走っています|このごろまいあさはしっています」."
    ],
    ja: "[このごろ]いそがしいです。",
    en: "I'm busy these days." },
  "〜ご|〜後": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「後|ご」 means after or behind. Here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. After a length of time, it means that much time later: 「三日後|みっかご」, three days later, 「一時間後|いちじかんご」, one hour later. You can also use it to mean in that much time from now, as in the example: please come in one hour. It is the same kanji you know from 「後で|あとで」, later on, and 「後|あと」, after an event, with the native reading. It also hides in the word for P.M., 「ごご」, written 「午後|ごご」, which is literally after noon. The kanji has the step shape on its left, a shape made of short slanted strokes that is used in kanji about walking and going. A way to remember it: someone walking behind, coming after. A common mistake: use this one after a length of time. After an event, like after class, use 「の後|のあと」 instead. To recap: length of time plus this, that much later." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has more readings than most, and you will meet all of them. With the Chinese-origin 「ご」, it follows a length of time, as here, and it appears in 「午後|ごご」, afternoon. With the native 「あと」, it means after or later, as in 「後で|あとで」, later on. With another native reading, 「うしろ」, it means behind, in space: 「いすの後ろ|いすのうしろ」, behind the chair. And in weather forecasts you may see 「のち」, meaning later in the day. Its natural partner is 「前|まえ」, before. That pairing runs all through the language: 「午前|ごぜん」 and 「午後|ごご」, a.m. and p.m., and 「三日前|みっかまえ」, three days ago, against 「三日後|みっかご」, three days later. Learn them as a pair and you get twice the words. So one kanji covers behind, later, and after, depending on the reading." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There are three ways to say after, and they attach to different things. After a length of time, use this suffix: 「一週間後|いっしゅうかんご」, a week later. To set a time point in a sentence, it often takes 「に」: 「一時間後に来てください|いちじかんごにきてください」. After a noun for an event, use 「の後で|のあとで」: 「授業の後で|じゅぎょうのあとで」, after class. After a verb, use the past tense plus 「後で|あとで」: 「食べた後で|たべたあとで」, after eating. Notice that the verb is always in past tense there, even when talking about the future. A common mistake is saying 「授業後|じゅぎょうご」 in casual speech. It exists, but it sounds stiff and written, like a notice board. In conversation, stick to 「の後で|のあとで」. The suffix also works for the past, telling a story: 「三日後に手紙が来ました|みっかごにてがみがきました」, three days later a letter came. So match the after to what comes before it." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Japanese has a whole pile of words read 「ご」, and this suffix sits right in the middle of them. There is 「五|ご」, five. There is 「語|ご」, a language, as in 「日本語|にほんご」. And there is 「午|ご」, noon, the first half of 「午後|ごご」. Put them together and you get a famous tongue twister. Five p.m. is 「午後五時|ごごごじ」, three 「ご」 sounds in a row, then 「じ」. Now try five hours later: 「五時間後|ごじかんご」. And five p.m., five hours later, would be 「午後五時の五時間後|ごごごじのごじかんご」. Japanese speakers rely on context and rhythm to keep these apart. For you, the trick is position. If 「ご」 comes right after a length of time, it means later. If it comes after a country, it is a language. If it is a number, it is five. So listen to where the 「ご」 sits, and the meaning sorts itself out." }
    ],
    short: [
      "After a length of time it means that much later, as in 「三日後|みっかご」, while after an event you use 「の後|のあと」 instead.",
      "The kanji reads 「ご」, 「あと」 and 「うしろ」, and it pairs with 「前|まえ」, as in 「三日前|みっかまえ」 against 「三日後|みっかご」.",
      "Use this suffix after a length of time, 「の後で|のあとで」 after an event noun, and the past tense plus 「後で|あとで」 after a verb.",
      "Many words are read 「ご」, like five and language, so tell this one by position, coming right after a length of time, as in 「五時間後|ごじかんご」."
    ],
    ja: "[一時間後|いちじかんご|〜ご]に{来て|きて}ください。",
    en: "Please come back in an hour." },
  "〜か〜|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the particle 「か」 used between two nouns to mean or. The pattern is A 「か」 B: 「コーヒーかこうちゃ」, coffee or tea. You already know 「か」 at the end of a sentence, where it makes a question. Here it is doing a related job. A question is choosing between yes or no, and this one offers a choice between two things. You can use it with more than two options, too, like A or B or C, putting 「か」 between each one. After the second item, add the particle the sentence needs, like 「を」 in the example. Here is a contrast. 「と」 between nouns means and, both things. 「か」 means or, one or the other. So 「コーヒーとこうちゃ」 is coffee and tea, but 「コーヒーかこうちゃ」 is coffee or tea. A way to remember it: the question sound, offering you a choice. To recap: put 「か」 between nouns for or." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The same little 「か」 that offers a choice also turns question words into vague words. Put it after 「何|なに」, and you get 「何か|なにか」, something. After 「だれ」, you get 「だれか」, someone. After 「どこ」, 「どこか」, somewhere. After 「いつ」, 「いつか」, someday. So 「何か食べますか|なにかたべますか」 means will you eat something? And 「だれか来ました|だれかきました」 means someone came. The thread is the same as in the or pattern. 「か」 keeps something open and unsettled. A question leaves the answer open. An or leaves the choice open. And these words leave the identity open: some thing, not yet chosen. When you hear 「か」 right after a question word, it usually means some, not which. So 「か」 is the particle of the open choice, wherever it shows up." },
      { title: "Lists compared", text: "Japanese has three common ways to join nouns, and each paints a different picture. 「と」 gives a complete list: 「パンと卵を買いました|パンとたまごをかいました」 means I bought bread and eggs, and nothing else. 「や」 gives a sample list, with more unnamed: 「パンや卵を買いました|パンやたまごをかいました」 means I bought things like bread and eggs. And 「か」 gives a choice, one or the other: 「パンか卵を買います|パンかたまごをかいます」 means I'll buy bread or eggs. If you are setting a meeting, 「月曜日か火曜日|げつようびかかようび」 offers Monday or Tuesday. If you say 「と」 there instead, you are meeting on both days. A quick check helps. Ask yourself, is it all of them, some of them, or just one? So: 「と」 for all, 「や」 for a sample, and 「か」 for just one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This pattern is everywhere in polite offers. A host might ask 「コーヒーか紅茶、どちらがいいですか|コーヒーかこうちゃ、どちらがいいですか」, coffee or tea, which would you like? Notice that 「どちら」, which of two, often follows the choice. A waiter might say 「ごはんかパンがえらべます」, you can choose rice or bread. In planning, you will hear 「明日か明後日|あしたかあさって」, tomorrow or the day after. Japanese people also use this pattern to sound less pushy. Offering two options instead of one gives the other person room to choose. When you answer, you can simply name your choice and add 「をおねがいします」: 「コーヒーをおねがいします」. If either is fine, say 「どちらでもいいです」. So when someone offers you a choice with 「か」, pick one and say please." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Placed between nouns, 「か」 means or, as in 「コーヒーかこうちゃ」, while 「と」 means and, both things.",
      "After a question word the same 「か」 makes vague words like 「何か|なにか」, something, and 「だれか」, someone, always leaving a choice open.",
      "Use 「と」 for a complete list, 「や」 for a sample list, and 「か」 for just one of them.",
      "Offering two options with 「か」 sounds less pushy, and you answer by naming one with 「をおねがいします」 or saying 「どちらでもいいです」."
    ],
    ja: "コーヒー[か]こうちゃを{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink coffee or tea." },
  "おんせん|温泉": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「温|おん」, warm. Look at its left side: three little strokes, the water radical, so warm water is built right into it. You already know this kanji from 「気温|きおん」, the air temperature in a weather report. The second kanji is 「泉|せん」, a spring, a place where water comes up out of the ground. Its bottom part is 「水|みず」, water. A way to remember the top: it looks like the kanji for white, so picture clear white water bubbling up. Put them together and you get warm spring water, a hot spring. Japan sits on a chain of volcanoes, so hot springs are everywhere, and a trip to one is a classic weekend getaway. People often stay at a traditional inn, a 「旅館|りょかん」, eat a big dinner, and soak in the bath several times. You wash first, then get in, and you never put your towel in the water. So, warm plus spring: a hot spring, and a very Japanese way to relax." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Walking into a hot spring for the first time, you'll see cloth curtains over two doorways. Blue usually marks the men's bath, 「男湯|おとこゆ」, and red the women's, 「女湯|おんなゆ」. The hiragana 「ゆ」 on those curtains means hot water. Inside, the most prized bath is often the outdoor one, the 「露天風呂|ろてんぶろ」, where you can soak while looking at mountains or snow. People bring a small towel and often fold it on their heads while soaking. Many places also have strict rules about tattoos, because of their old link with gangs, though some are now more relaxed. After the bath, guests often walk around in a light cotton robe, a 「ゆかた」, and sometimes enjoy eggs slowly cooked in the spring water. A whole trip can be built around soaking, eating and sleeping. So look for the 「ゆ」 sign, and get ready to relax." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each half of this word has relatives you can use. The first kanji means warm, and with its native reading it gives 「温かい|あたたかい」, warm to the touch, like soup or a kind heart. Weather uses a different kanji for warm, 「暖かい|あたたかい」, so when it's a warm day, the spelling changes. You know 「気温|きおん」, air temperature, and you'll also see 「温度|おんど」, temperature in general, on air conditioners. The second kanji, spring, has the native reading 「いずみ」, a spring of water. That is also a common given name and family name. In place names, this word often comes at the end, as in 「草津温泉|くさつおんせん」 and 「別府温泉|べっぷおんせん」, two of the most famous hot spring towns. Spotting this ending on a train map tells you there's a bath nearby. So warm plus spring, and both halves open up new words." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Imagine your Japanese teacher, your 「先生|せんせい」, on a weekend trip. Picture them sitting ON a steaming rock in a hot spring, with a towel folded on their head, cheeks pink, still holding a red pen and grading homework. You walk in and gasp, because there is your teacher, sitting on the steamy rock. On, plus the first half of 「先生|せんせい」: that gives you the sound 「おんせん」. Now add the kanji. The three water drops on the left of the first kanji are splashing on the homework, so the ink runs. The second kanji has water at its base, bubbling up under the rock. Every time you hear this word, picture the teacher on the rock, soaking and grading. It is silly, but that is what makes it stick. So: your teacher, on a rock, in the steam." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「温|おん」, warm, with water on its left, plus 「泉|せん」, a spring with water at its base, so warm spring water, a hot spring.",
      "Look for the 「ゆ」, hot water, on the curtains, blue for men and red for women, and seek out the outdoor 「露天風呂|ろてんぶろ」.",
      "The warm kanji gives 「温かい|あたたかい」, and this word ends famous town names like 「草津温泉|くさつおんせん」, a sign there is a bath nearby.",
      "Picture your teacher sitting on a steamy rock grading homework, so on plus the start of 「先生|せんせい」 gives the sound."
    ],
    ja: "やすみに[温泉|おんせん]にいきたいです。",
    en: "I want to go to a hot spring on my break." },
  "つり|釣り": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the noun form of the verb 「釣る|つる」, to catch a fish with a hook and line. Many Japanese verbs turn into nouns this way: take the stem, and the 「り」 is left at the end, just like 「休む|やすむ」 gives 「休み|やすみ」, a break. The kanji is worth a close look. On the left is the metal radical, the same piece that is the whole kanji in 「お金|おかね」, money. Metal makes sense, because the key tool in fishing is a little metal hook. So the kanji is basically metal for catching. You use it with 「する」 or with 「行く|いく」: 「釣りをする|つりをする」, to fish, and 「釣りに行く|つりにいく」, to go fishing. That second pattern, a noun plus 「に行く|にいく」, is the same one you use for 「買い物に行く|かいものにいく」. Lakes, rivers and the sea all count, so it pairs nicely with 「川|かわ」 and 「湖|みずうみ」 from this same lesson. In short: a metal hook, a verb turned noun, and a quiet day by the water." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb behind this noun is 「釣る|つる」, a u-verb. Its forms are regular: 「釣ります|つります」, 「釣って|つって」, 「釣った|つった」. It is transitive, so the fish takes 「を」: 「魚を釣る|さかなをつる」, to catch a fish. There are two ways to say go fishing, and both use the purpose pattern with 「に行く|にいく」. With the noun, it is 「釣りに行く|つりにいく」. With a verb stem and an object, it is 「魚を釣りに行く|さかなをつりにいく」, to go to catch fish. The stem of the verb and the noun look identical here, which makes this pattern easy. To say where you fish, use 「で」 for the place of action: 「川で釣りをする|かわでつりをする」. A very similar verb is 「祭る|まつる」, which gives 「お祭り|おまつり」, also in this lesson. Both turn into nouns by keeping the stem. So one stem does double duty, as a noun and as the purpose before 「に行く|にいく」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a surprise you'll meet at every cash register. When you pay and get change back, the clerk hands it to you with 「お釣りです|おつりです」, here is your change. That word for change is written with the same kanji as fishing, with a polite 「お」 in front. It has nothing to do with fish. Most explanations trace it to an older sense of the verb, hanging things so they balance, as in the word 「釣り合う|つりあう」, to be in balance. The change you get back balances what you paid against the price. So in daily life, you will hear 「お釣り|おつり」 far more often than you go fishing. At the register, you might also hear 「お釣りはいりません|おつりはいりません」, keep the change, though tipping is not a custom in Japan, so this is rare. So one kanji, two very different meanings: fishing by the river, and coins at the counter." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Fishing in Japan is not just for the countryside. In the middle of Tokyo, you can find fishing ponds where you pay by the hour, sit on a wooden platform, and catch carp or goldfish. These are called 「つりぼり」, fishing moats, and one famous spot sits right beside the train tracks near Ichigaya station. People also fish from piers and breakwaters along the coast, often for small fish to fry at home. A long-running comedy film series was even built around an office worker who was obsessed with fishing, showing how familiar the hobby is. Families sometimes go to river parks where you catch trout and grill them right there on skewers with salt. And at summer festivals, children play a scooping game for goldfish, a cousin of the real thing. So fishing in Japan can mean a mountain river, a seaside pier, or a pond between office towers." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the noun form of 「釣る|つる」, with metal for the hook on the left, and you say 「釣りに行く|つりにいく」, to go fishing.",
      "The fish takes 「を」, as in 「魚を釣る|さかなをつる」, and the place of fishing takes 「で」, as in 「川で釣りをする|かわでつりをする」.",
      "The same kanji gives 「お釣り|おつり」, your change at the register, likely from an older sense of balancing, as in 「釣り合う|つりあう」.",
      "Fishing in Japan can mean pay-by-the-hour ponds called 「つりぼり」 right in Tokyo, seaside piers, or grilling trout at river parks."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}と[釣り|つり]をしました。",
    en: "I went fishing with my father." },
  "キャンプ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from the English word camp. Listen to how it got squeezed into Japanese sounds. The ca at the start becomes 「キャ」, a small ya sound tucked in, because that is the closest Japanese match for the flat English a in camp. The m before the p becomes 「ン」, since Japanese has just one sound for a nasal at the end of a syllable. And the final p gets a vowel added, 「プ」, because Japanese words cannot end on a bare consonant, apart from 「ン」. You will see this extra vowel on lots of borrowed words, like 「テープ」 and 「ゲーム」. To go camping, you say 「キャンプに行く|キャンプにいく」, or to do it, 「キャンプをする」. Camping is popular in Japan, especially in the mountains and by lakes in summer, and there is a whole gear culture around it. A way to remember the spelling: the little 「ャ」 is the only tricky part, everything else is just English with vowels. So: camp, plus Japanese vowels, gives you a weekend in a tent." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Camping in Japan has its own vocabulary, mostly borrowed. A campsite is 「キャンプ場|キャンプじょう」, where the kanji means a place. A campfire is 「キャンプファイヤー」, and grilling outdoors is 「バーベキュー」. In recent years, a fancy version called 「グランピング」, glamping, has become very popular, with ready-made tents and comfortable beds. There is also a sports meaning. Every February, professional baseball teams hold their 「キャンプ」, spring training camp, many of them in warm Okinawa, and the news covers it every day. In conversation, you might say 「週末、キャンプに行きませんか|しゅうまつ、キャンプにいきませんか」, want to go camping this weekend? And someone may answer 「いいですね。どこでしますか」, sounds good, where shall we do it? Remember you can 「する」 it or 「行く|いく」 to it. So one borrowed word covers tents, campfires and even baseball training." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Japanese counts words in beats, not syllables, and this word is a good one to clap out. It has three beats. The first is 「キャ」, a single beat even though it is written with two characters, because the small 「ャ」 just blends into the one before. The second beat is 「ン」, which counts as a full beat on its own, even though it has no vowel. The third is 「プ」. So it is 「キャ」, 「ン」, 「プ」, three equal claps. English speakers often rush the 「ン」, making the word sound like two beats. Give it its full time. Compare 「キャップ」, a cap, also three beats, but with a small 「ッ」 pause instead of 「ン」. You'll see small 「ャ」, 「ュ」 and 「ョ」 in many borrowed words, like 「シャツ」 and 「ジュース」, and they always make one beat with the sound before. So clap three times: camp in Japanese rhythm." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English camp, with the flat a as 「キャ」, the m as 「ン」, and an extra vowel on 「プ」, and you go with 「キャンプに行く|キャンプにいく」.",
      "A campsite is 「キャンプ場|キャンプじょう」, glamping is 「グランピング」, and baseball teams hold their spring training 「キャンプ」 every February.",
      "Clap three equal beats, 「キャ」, 「ン」, 「プ」, since the small 「ャ」 blends into one beat and 「ン」 counts as a full beat."
    ],
    ja: "{山|やま}で[キャンプ]をしました。",
    en: "I went camping in the mountains." },
  "やま|山": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is one of the kanji that really is a picture. Three upward strokes, the middle one tallest, sitting on a base: a mountain range with one high peak. Here it uses its native Japanese reading, 「やま」. In names of mountains, though, it switches to its Chinese-origin reading, 「さん」, so Mount Fuji is 「富士山|ふじさん」. Sometimes that softens to 「ざん」, as in 「登山|とざん」, mountain climbing. It is also extremely common in family names, like 「山田|やまだ」, literally mountain rice field, and 「山下|やました」, below the mountain. Because so much of Japan is mountains, around three quarters of the land, this kanji is everywhere on maps and signs. The verb that goes with it is 「登る|のぼる」, to climb, from this lesson: 「山に登る|やまにのぼる」. You can also go camping there, or visit a hot spring at its foot. A way to remember the readings: the native 「やま」 is the mountain itself, the Chinese 「さん」 is the mountain's title. A picture of peaks, read 「やま」 on its own and 「さん」 in names." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji appears in more words than you might expect. You've seen it in mountain names and in climbing. A volcano is 「火山|かざん」, fire mountain, which makes sense for a country full of them. A mountain path is 「山道|やまみち」, and mountain climbing, said in plain native words, is 「山登り|やまのぼり」. Now the surprise. The common word 「たくさん」, a lot, is sometimes written with kanji as 「沢山|たくさん」, and the second kanji is this very one. That spelling borrows kanji mostly for their sounds, so don't read too much into the meaning, but you will see it in older texts and on some menus. Another hidden appearance: the kanji for island, 「島|しま」, has this mountain tucked under a bird shape. The traditional explanation is a mountain in the sea where birds rest. So keep an eye out, because these three peaks hide in plenty of places." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Mountains run through Japanese culture as well as its map. In the old Shinto tradition, many mountains are sacred, home to spirits, and some have shrines right on their summits. Mount Fuji is the most famous. Its official climbing season is short, usually from early July to early September, and many people climb overnight to watch the sunrise from the top. That sunrise has its own name, 「ご来光|ごらいこう」. The start of a mountain's climbing season is celebrated with a ceremony called 「山開き|やまびらき」, the opening of the mountain. Since 2016, Japan has even had a national holiday for mountains, 「山の日|やまのひ」, in August. In autumn, people head to the mountains to see the red and yellow leaves. So in Japan, a mountain can be a hike, a holy place, and a holiday all at once." },
      { title: "Particles on the mountain", text: "Different particles paint different pictures with this word. The most common is 「に」 with climbing: 「山に登る|やまにのぼる」. Here the mountain is the goal, the place you end up. You will also hear 「山を登る|やまをのぼる」. With 「を」, the focus shifts to the slope you move along, the path under your feet, just as with walking through a park. Both are natural. For going there, use 「に」 or 「へ」: 「山へ行く|やまへいく」. For doing something on the mountain, use 「で」: 「山でキャンプをする|やまでキャンプをする」, camp in the mountains. And to say there are mountains, use 「に」 with 「ある」: 「近くに山があります|ちかくにやまがあります」, there's a mountain nearby. Notice how one noun stays still while the particles move the action around it. So pick the particle by asking: goal, path, place of action, or simply there." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a picture of peaks with the middle one tallest, read 「やま」 on its own but 「さん」 in mountain names like 「富士山|ふじさん」.",
      "It hides in 「火山|かざん」, a volcano or fire mountain, in the spelling 「沢山|たくさん」, and under the bird in 「島|しま」, island.",
      "Many mountains are sacred in Shinto, and climbers on Fuji watch the sunrise called 「ご来光|ごらいこう」, while August even has 「山の日|やまのひ」.",
      "Climb with 「山に登る|やまにのぼる」 for the goal or 「を」 for the slope, use 「へ」 for going, and 「で」 for doing something there."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[山|やま]にのぼりました。",
    en: "I climbed a mountain with a friend." },
  "みずうみ|湖": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Listen to the native word first, because it explains itself. It sounds just like 「水|みず」, water, joined to 「海|うみ」, sea. That is a fair way to think of it: a sea of fresh water, sitting inland. Both of those words are ones you already know, from lessons three and five. The kanji has the water radical, three short strokes on the left, the same as in 「海|うみ」 and in 「温泉|おんせん」. The right side mainly gives the sound in Chinese-style compounds, where this kanji is read 「こ」. The most famous example is 「琵琶湖|びわこ」, the biggest lake in Japan, near Kyoto, named for its lute-like shape. So when you see 「こ」 at the end of a place name with this kanji, think lake. On its own, though, you say 「みずうみ」. You can swim in one, fish in one, or camp beside one, all words from this lesson. A way to remember it: water plus sea, a little sea you can see across. So, read it as water sea, and you have a lake." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Here is a chain of clues hidden in this kanji. On the left is the water radical, the three short strokes. On the right is a part that gives the sound, 「胡|こ」. Look closely at that right side and you'll find 「古|ふる」 inside it, the kanji from 「古い|ふるい」, old. Its Chinese-origin reading is also 「こ」. So the sound 「こ」 passes from old, to the right side, to lake. That is a real pattern in kanji: many share a sound piece, and spotting it helps you guess the reading. The other part of that right side is the shape 「月|つき」, which is just part of the sound piece here, not the moon. So when you see this kanji in a place name, read it 「こ」, and you'll be right almost every time. On its own, it is 「みずうみ」. So: water on the left, and old hiding on the right to give the sound." },
      { title: "Water words", text: "This lesson is full of water, so let's line up the words by size and type. The smallest is 「池|いけ」, a pond, the kind you see in a Japanese garden with carp. Then comes this word, a lake, big and inland. A river is 「川|かわ」, water that flows. And the biggest is 「海|うみ」, the sea, salty and endless. Notice that all of these kanji except river have the water radical on the left. River is so basic it is a picture of water itself. Near Mount Fuji there is a famous group called 「富士五湖|ふじごこ」, the five lakes of Fuji, where people go to see the mountain reflected in still water. One of them is 「河口湖|かわぐちこ」, a popular stop on day trips. Japanese people also take boats out on lakes, rent swan pedal boats, and fish in winter through the ice. So pond, lake, river, sea, from small to huge." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The native word sounds like 「水|みず」, water, plus 「海|うみ」, sea, an inland sea of fresh water, and in place names it reads 「こ」, as in 「琵琶湖|びわこ」.",
      "Water sits on the left, and the right side hides 「古|ふる」, old, whose reading 「こ」 passes along as the sound of lake.",
      "From small to huge, it goes 「池|いけ」, pond, then lake, 「川|かわ」, river, and 「海|うみ」, sea, with lakes like 「河口湖|かわぐちこ」 reflecting Mount Fuji."
    ],
    ja: "[湖|みずうみ]でおよぎました。",
    en: "I swam in the lake." },
  "じんじゃ|神社": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「神|じん」, means god or spirit, the kind of spirits Shinto honors. On its own it is read 「かみ」. The second, 「社|じゃ」, means shrine. On its own that would be 「しゃ」, but it softens to 「じゃ」 here. Now look at both kanji: they share the same left side, a narrow radical that is linked to altars and religious things. So both halves are about the sacred, a spirit and its home. That second kanji may look familiar, because it is also in 「会社|かいしゃ」, company, and 「社長|しゃちょう」 from this lesson. Its older sense is a gathering place, which grew into both a shrine and a business. Shrines are Shinto. You can spot one by the big gate at the entrance, often painted red. Buddhist temples are 「お寺|おてら」, which you met in lesson four. At a shrine, people bow twice, clap twice, make a wish, and bow once more. At New Year, nearly everyone visits one. So: spirit plus sacred place, a home for the gods." },
      { title: "Shrine walk-through", text: "Let's walk through a shrine step by step. First you pass under the gate, the 「鳥居|とりい」, and many people bow lightly before stepping through. Try not to walk in the very center of the path, because that is said to be the way of the gods. Soon you'll see a pair of stone guardian animals, the 「狛犬|こまいぬ」, one with its mouth open and one with it closed. Next is a water basin with ladles. You rinse your left hand, then your right, then pour a little into your cupped hand to rinse your mouth. Then you go to the main hall, toss a coin into the offering box, and pray. Before leaving, you can buy a protective charm, an 「お守り|おまもり」, or draw a paper fortune, an 「おみくじ」. If the fortune is bad, people often tie it to a rack and leave it behind. So: gate, guardians, water, prayer, and a fortune to take home." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, spirit or god, has several readings. Alone it is 「かみ」, and respectfully 「神様|かみさま」, which you'll hear in songs and stories. In this word it is 「じん」. In 「神道|しんとう」, the name of the Shinto religion, it is 「しん」. And in the city name 「神戸|こうべ」, it becomes 「こう」. So Kobe literally contains the god kanji. The second kanji, shrine or company, also has relatives. You know 「会社|かいしゃ」 and 「社長|しゃちょう」. There is also 「社会|しゃかい」, society, which uses the same two kanji as company, just in reverse order. Big shrines sometimes use different endings. 「大社|たいしゃ」, as in 「出雲大社|いずもたいしゃ」, means a grand shrine, and 「神宮|じんぐう」, as in 「明治神宮|めいじじんぐう」 in Tokyo, means a shrine linked to the imperial family. So learn these two kanji, and you can read shrine names all over Japan." },
      { title: "Shrine or temple", text: "Learners often mix up shrines and temples, so here is a quick way to tell them apart by name. Shinto shrines end in 「神社|じんじゃ」, 「神宮|じんぐう」, or 「大社|たいしゃ」. Buddhist temples usually end in 「寺|じ」 or 「でら」, as in 「清水寺|きよみずでら」 in Kyoto, or 「金閣寺|きんかくじ」, the Golden Pavilion. You can also tell by sight. A shrine has the gate and the guardian animals. A temple often has a big incense burner, statues of the Buddha, and sometimes a tall pagoda. At a shrine, you clap your hands when you pray. At a temple, you press your palms together quietly, without clapping. Most Japanese people visit both, for different occasions. Weddings and New Year are often at shrines, while funerals are usually Buddhist. Don't worry if it feels mixed, because for many people it is. So: clap at the shrine, stay quiet at the temple." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「神|じん」, spirit, plus 「社|じゃ」, shrine, both with the altar radical on the left, so a home for the gods, marked by a gate.",
      "Bow at the 「鳥居|とりい」, pass the guardian 「狛犬|こまいぬ」, rinse at the basin, pray, then get an 「お守り|おまもり」 or an 「おみくじ」 fortune.",
      "The spirit kanji reads 「かみ」, 「しん」 in 「神道|しんとう」 and 「こう」 in 「神戸|こうべ」, and grand shrines may end in 「大社|たいしゃ」 or 「神宮|じんぐう」.",
      "Shrines end in 「神社|じんじゃ」, 「神宮|じんぐう」 or 「大社|たいしゃ」 while temples end in 「寺|じ」, and you clap at a shrine but pray quietly at a temple."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}の[神社|じんじゃ]にいきました。",
    en: "I went to a shrine in Kyoto." },
  "しゃしんをとる|写真を撮る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You already know 「写真|しゃしん」, a photo, from lesson four. Its first kanji, 「写|しゃ」, means to copy, and the second, 「真|しん」, means true or real. So a photo is a true copy of what is in front of you. Nice and logical. The verb is where it gets interesting. Japanese has several verbs pronounced 「とる」, and they are written with different kanji depending on what kind of taking you mean. For photos and videos it is 「撮る|とる」. Its left side is the hand radical, like a hand holding a camera. For taking a class, or picking something up, it is 「取る|とる」, which is also in this lesson. They sound the same, so you only notice the difference when reading or writing. The pattern is just like English: 「写真を撮る|しゃしんをとる」, to take a picture. A very useful polite question when traveling is 「写真を撮ってもいいですか|しゃしんをとってもいいですか」, may I take a picture? That is the te-form plus 「もいい」 for permission, from lesson six. So: a true copy, taken with the camera kind of 「とる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb here is a u-verb, so it conjugates like 「帰る|かえる」: 「撮ります|とります」, 「撮って|とって」, 「撮った|とった」. Since photos are the object, 「写真|しゃしん」 takes 「を」. When you want someone to take a photo for you, the most useful pattern is 「てもらう」. A polite request sounds like 「写真を撮ってもらえませんか|しゃしんをとってもらえませんか」, could you take a picture for us? To offer to take one for somebody else, use 「ましょうか」: 「撮りましょうか|とりましょうか」, shall I take it for you? And when it's not allowed, you'll hear the te-form plus 「はいけません」: 「ここで写真を撮ってはいけません|ここでしゃしんをとってはいけません」. With these three, you can ask, offer and understand the rules. So one verb, with three patterns: asking, offering, and not allowed." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Photos are a big part of travel in Japan, and they come with set phrases. When a group lines up, the photographer usually says 「はい、チーズ」, the Japanese version of say cheese. Lots of people, young and old, flash a peace sign. A selfie is 「自撮り|じどり」, literally taking yourself. At museums, temples and some shops, look for signs saying 「撮影禁止|さつえいきんし」, no photography. That 「さつ」 is the Chinese reading of the same kanji as the verb here. If you want to photograph a person, especially a stranger or someone working, always ask first. A typical exchange: 「すみません、写真を撮ってもいいですか|すみません、しゃしんをとってもいいですか」. 「はい、どうぞ」. And if someone offers to take your group's photo, a simple 「ありがとうございます、おねがいします」 does the trick. So: ask first, say cheese, and watch for the no photos sign." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in the noun opens up other words. The first, copy, has the native verb 「写す|うつす」, to copy something down or to capture an image. The second, true or real, is in the native word 「真ん中|まんなか」, right in the middle, the true middle. It also adds emphasis in color words: 「真っ白|まっしろ」, pure white, and 「真っ赤|まっか」, bright red. And the kanji for taking photos has a formal reading, 「さつ」, as in 「撮影|さつえい」, filming or photography, a word you'll see on signs and in news about movies. Compare it with its sound-alike sibling 「取る|とる」, to take or pick up. Both share the hand radical on the left, but the photo one has 「最|さい」, the kanji from 「最近|さいきん」, on its right. So learn these kanji, and the camera, the center, and pure white all connect." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A photo is a true copy, 「写|しゃ」 plus 「真|しん」, and you take it with 「撮る|とる」, the hand-holding-a-camera kind of 「とる」, not 「取る|とる」.",
      "Ask someone to take one with 「写真を撮ってもらえませんか|しゃしんをとってもらえませんか」, offer with 「撮りましょうか|とりましょうか」, and watch for 「てはいけません」.",
      "Groups say 「はい、チーズ」, a selfie is 「自撮り|じどり」, and 「撮影禁止|さつえいきんし」 signs mean no photos, so ask before photographing people.",
      "The true kanji gives 「真ん中|まんなか」 and 「真っ白|まっしろ」, while the photo kanji reads 「さつ」 in 「撮影|さつえい」, photography."
    ],
    ja: "ここで[写真を撮っても|しゃしんをとっても|しゃしんをとる]いいですか。",
    en: "May I take a picture here?" },
  "がいこく|外国": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「外|がい」, means outside. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「そと」, the outside of a building. The second, 「国|こく」, means country. On its own it is 「くに」, as in 「国に帰る|くににかえる」, to go back to your home country, from lesson six. In its modern form, the kanji is a box, a border, with a jewel inside: a way to remember it is that a country is the precious land inside the border. Put them together and you get a country outside, a foreign country. This little word is the root of a whole family. Add 「人|じん」, person, and you get 「外国人|がいこくじん」, a foreigner. Add 「語|ご」, language, and you get 「外国語|がいこくご」, a foreign language, the same 「語|ご」 as in 「日本語|にほんご」. Learning one gives you the others for free. A common question is 「外国に行ったことがありますか|がいこくにいったことがありますか」, have you ever been abroad? So: outside plus country, and you are abroad." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Two words for foreigner deserve a careful note. The full form, 「外国人|がいこくじん」, is neutral and polite, and it is what you'll see in news reports and on official forms. A shorter form, 「外人|がいじん」, is very common in casual talk, but many people living in Japan find it a bit distancing, like calling someone an outsider. Some speakers use it without any bad intent, but the full form is always the safer choice. There is also a close cousin of the country word itself: 「海外|かいがい」, literally beyond the sea. Because Japan is an island nation, going anywhere abroad means crossing water, so people often say 「海外旅行|かいがいりょこう」, an overseas trip. In daily speech, 「海外|かいがい」 is often more natural than this lesson's word when talking about travel. So say 「外国人|がいこくじん」 for people, and think about 「海外|かいがい」 for trips." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, country, has a sound change worth noticing. On its own it is 「くに」. In compounds it is usually 「こく」, as here and in 「韓国|かんこく」, South Korea. But after some sounds it softens to 「ごく」, as in 「中国|ちゅうごく」, China. You'll also meet 「国際|こくさい」, international, as in an international student. The first kanji, outside, has its own set. Alone it is 「そと」, the outside: 「外で遊ぶ|そとであそぶ」, to play outside. As 「がい」 it appears in 「以外|いがい」, other than, and in 「外食|がいしょく」, eating out. Interestingly, eating out uses the same 「がい」 as foreign country, both describing something outside your own home. So outside and country each lead you to a little family, and they meet in the word for abroad." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the first kanji. On the left is 「夕|ゆう」, evening, the same shape at the bottom of 「夢|ゆめ」, dream. On the right is a simple shape, a vertical line with a little tick, which in old Chinese stood for divination, reading cracks in heated bones. Scholars disagree about how the two pieces combine, so treat what follows as a memory trick, not history. Picture a fortune teller who is only allowed to work in the evening, outside the town walls. Evening plus fortune telling, out beyond the gate. The second kanji is a box, a border, with a jewel inside, as the main lesson described. So the whole word becomes the fortune teller's land, out past the border at dusk. It is a fun image that ties the shapes together, and the readings 「がい」 and 「こく」 sit on top of it. So remember evening on the left, a fortune on the right, and the world outside." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「外|がい」, outside, plus 「国|こく」, country, a border with a jewel inside, and it gives 「外国人|がいこくじん」 and 「外国語|がいこくご」.",
      "Say 「外国人|がいこくじん」 rather than the casual 「外人|がいじん」, which can feel distancing, and use 「海外|かいがい」, beyond the sea, for trips abroad.",
      "The country kanji softens to 「ごく」 in 「中国|ちゅうごく」, and the outside kanji reads 「がい」 in 「外食|がいしょく」, eating out.",
      "As a memory trick, not history, picture evening on the left and a fortune teller's mark on the right, working at dusk outside the town walls."
    ],
    ja: "[外国|がいこく]にいったことがありますか。",
    en: "Have you ever been to a foreign country?" },
  "かわ|川": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Another kanji that really is a picture. Three vertical lines, the left one curving, like water flowing between two banks, seen from above. Here it uses its native Japanese reading, 「かわ」. When it comes after another word in a place name, it often softens to 「がわ」, like 「品川|しながわ」, a big station in Tokyo. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「せん」, but you will mostly see that in longer compound words, so for now just keep 「かわ」. Compare it with the water radical, the three little drops on the left of 「湖|みずうみ」 and 「温泉|おんせん」. Both are about water, but here the three lines stand tall and run downstream. A typical sentence from this lesson is 「川で釣りをしました|かわでつりをしました」, I went fishing at the river. Notice the particle 「で」, because the river is the place where the action happens. Rivers in Japan are short and fast, rushing from the mountains to the sea, so a riverside walk or barbecue is a classic summer outing. A way to remember it: three lines, flowing water. So: a picture of a stream, read 「かわ」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Several different words are all pronounced 「かわ」, so the kanji matters. This one, three flowing lines, is the everyday river. A different kanji, 「河|かわ」, with the water radical, is used for big rivers, especially famous ones abroad, but it is read the same. Then there is 「皮|かわ」, skin or peel, as in the skin of an apple. And there is 「革|かわ」, leather, as in a leather bag. On top of that, the very common word 「かわいい」, cute, starts with these same sounds, though it is a completely different word. And remember that in place names, the river becomes 「がわ」, which is also the sound of 「側|がわ」, side, as in 「右側|みぎがわ」, the right side. In speech, context sorts it out: you fish in one, peel the other, and wear the third. So when you hear 「かわ」, think river, skin, or leather, and let the sentence decide." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji shows up in many names. 「小川|おがわ」, a small river or brook, is also a common family name. The Kyoto river 「鴨川|かもがわ」 is famous for couples sitting evenly spaced along its banks. Tokyo's 「隅田川|すみだがわ」 is known for its summer fireworks. In most of these names, notice the sound change to 「がわ」. Now look at a related kanji, 「州|しゅう」. It is the river shape with three little dots between the lines. The traditional explanation is sandbanks or islands in a river, so it came to mean a region or land. You see it in 「九州|きゅうしゅう」, one of Japan's four main islands, and in the word for a U.S. state, as in 「カリフォルニア州|カリフォルニアしゅう」. So a river with a few dots of land becomes a whole region." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese has a lovely expression built from the shape of this kanji. When parents sleep side by side with their small child in the middle, people say they sleep 「川の字|かわのじ」, in the shape of the river kanji. The two long lines are the parents, and the short one in the center is the child. Families often sleep together on futons laid out on the floor, so the picture is very real. Rivers also shape summer life. Big fireworks shows are often held along rivers, and people spread out picnic sheets on the banks hours in advance. In Kyoto, restaurants build wooden platforms over the river in summer, so diners can eat above the cool water. So this simple kanji carries both the river itself and a warm picture of a family asleep." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The three lines are a picture of water flowing between banks, read 「かわ」 but softened to 「がわ」 in place names like 「品川|しながわ」.",
      "Many words are read 「かわ」, like 「皮|かわ」, skin, and 「革|かわ」, leather, so you fish in one, peel another, and wear the third.",
      "It is in names like 「鴨川|かもがわ」 and 「隅田川|すみだがわ」, and with three dots between the lines it becomes 「州|しゅう」, a region, as in 「九州|きゅうしゅう」.",
      "Parents sleeping on either side of a small child are said to sleep 「川の字|かわのじ」, in the shape of the river kanji."
    ],
    ja: "[川|かわ]でつりをしました。",
    en: "I went fishing in the river." },
  "ドライブ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from the English word drive. Listen to how the sounds changed. The d and r run together into 「ドラ」, with an extra vowel after the d, because Japanese does not put two consonants side by side. The English v becomes a b sound, 「ブ」, because traditional Japanese has no v. And the final vowel is added so the word does not end on a consonant. The meaning also shifted. In Japanese, this is a drive for fun, a road trip or a pleasure ride through the countryside or along the coast. You say 「ドライブに行く|ドライブにいく」, to go for a drive. It is a classic date activity. If you just mean the act of operating a car, to drive to work, use 「運転する|うんてんする」, which you met in lesson eight. A common mistake is to say you do a 「ドライブ」 to the office, which sounds like you are going sightseeing on the way to work. A way to remember it: the 「ドライブ」 is the trip, 「運転|うんてん」 is the steering. So: a drive, but always the fun kind." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Several other driving words came into Japanese with this one, and some changed in surprising ways. 「ドライブスルー」 is a drive-through, at fast food places and even some pharmacies. 「ドライブイン」 is a roadside rest stop with a restaurant, and it often doesn't involve eating in your car at all. Then there's 「ドライバー」. It means a driver, the person behind the wheel. But it also means a screwdriver, and in golf, the club called a driver. So if someone in a workshop asks you to pass the 「ドライバー」, they want a tool, not a chauffeur. Finally, there's a native word for the act of driving, 「運転|うんてん」, and the person who drives for work is 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」, like a bus or taxi driver. So the loanword family stays mostly on the fun side, while the native words do the work." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, going for a drive is a popular weekend activity, especially along the coast or into the mountains. You can use the word with 「に行く|にいく」 or with 「する」. 「海までドライブしました|うみまでドライブしました」, we drove out to the sea. 「ドライブに行きませんか|ドライブにいきませんか」, want to go for a drive? Couples often go on a drive as a date, stopping at rest areas famous for their local snacks and soft-serve ice cream. Remember that Japan drives on the left, and highways have tolls, so a long drive can be pricey. A mini dialogue: 「週末、何をしましたか|しゅうまつ、なにをしましたか」. 「友だちとドライブに行きました|ともだちとドライブにいきました」. 「いいですね。どこに行きましたか|いいですね。どこにいきましたか」. So it's a word for fun on the road, with friends, a date, or family." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The English v sound has a habit of turning into a b sound in Japanese. That is why drive ends in 「ブ」. You'll find the same change in many words you already know. Television is 「テレビ」. Video is 「ビデオ」. Violin is 「バイオリン」. And Valentine's Day is 「バレンタインデー」. Modern Japanese can write a v sound using 「ヴ」, a 「ウ」 with two dots, and you'll sometimes see it on signs or menus. But most people still pronounce it as a b. Notice also that this word is four beats: 「ド」, 「ラ」, 「イ」, 「ブ」. English drive is one syllable, so the Japanese version feels much longer. Don't rush the 「イ」. Once you hear the v to b change, lots of loanwords become easier to guess. So listen for the b, and think of the v." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English drive but means a drive for fun, while simply operating a car, like driving to work, is 「運転する|うんてんする」.",
      "Its family includes 「ドライブスルー」 and 「ドライブイン」, and 「ドライバー」 can mean a driver, a screwdriver, or a golf club.",
      "A drive to the coast or mountains is a classic weekend or date, as in 「ドライブに行きませんか|ドライブにいきませんか」, want to go for a drive?",
      "The English v turns into a b sound, giving 「ブ」, just as in 「テレビ」 and 「バイオリン」, and the word has four beats."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}に[ドライブ]にいきました。",
    en: "I went for a drive on the weekend." },
  "びじゅつかん|美術館": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「美|び」, means beauty. A way to remember its shape: the top looks like the kanji for sheep, and the bottom is 「大|だい」, big, so picture a big, fine sheep, something admired. You have seen this kanji in 「美容院|びよういん」, a beauty salon, from lesson ten. The second, 「術|じゅつ」, means art, technique, a skill. Together, 「美術|びじゅつ」 means fine art, the art of beauty. The third, 「館|かん」, means a large public building. It is the same one as in 「図書館|としょかん」, the library, 「映画館|えいがかん」, the movie theater, and 「旅館|りょかん」, a Japanese inn. Whenever you see 「かん」 with this kanji at the end, think of a big building with a purpose. So the whole word is a building for fine art. A museum of history or science uses a different word, so this one is really for paintings and sculpture. A typical sentence is 「美術館で絵を見ました|びじゅつかんでえをみました」. So: beauty, art, building." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first two kanji each lead to useful words. Beauty, with its native reading, gives 「美しい|うつくしい」, beautiful, a slightly more elegant word than 「きれい」. It also appears in the usual spelling of 「おいしい」, written 「美味しい|おいしい」, beautiful taste, though that spelling just borrows the kanji for their meaning. The second kanji, art or technique, shows up in 「芸術|げいじゅつ」, the arts in general, and in 「手術|しゅじゅつ」, surgery, which is literally hand technique. That second one is easy to say wrong, so practice it slowly. Put beauty and technique together and you get 「美術|びじゅつ」, fine art, which is also the name of the school subject, art class. A high schooler might say 「美術の授業|びじゅつのじゅぎょう」, art class. So beauty, technique, and a building, each one with relatives of its own." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is for art. For history, science or natural history, Japanese uses a different word, 「博物館|はくぶつかん」, literally a building of broad things. So the big national museum in Tokyo with samurai swords and pottery is a 「博物館|はくぶつかん」, while the one with western paintings nearby is a 「美術館|びじゅつかん」. At the entrance, you pay an admission fee, and you'll often see a special exhibition, a 「展覧会|てんらんかい」. Museums in Japan are very quiet, and many ban photos, so look for the no photography sign. People love to buy postcards and souvenirs in the museum shop afterwards. A typical sentence: 「日曜日に美術館に行きませんか|にちようびにびじゅつかんにいきませんか」, shall we go to the art museum on Sunday? So for paintings, this word, and for dinosaurs, the other one." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word has a few spots that trip learners up. The middle sound is 「じゅ」, short, not the long 「じゅう」 you hear in ten. So it's 「び」, 「じゅ」, 「つ」, 「か」, 「ん」, five beats in all. Next, listen for the 「つ」. Between the 「ゅ」 and the 「か」, it is often whispered, almost silent, because a 「う」 sound between two voiceless consonants tends to lose its voice in Japanese. It is still a full beat, though, so keep its timing even when it goes quiet. Also, don't confuse it with 「美容院|びよういん」, a beauty salon. They both start with the beauty kanji, but one is for hair and one is for art. Practice the five beats slowly, then speed up. So: short 「じゅ」, a whispered 「つ」, and a building for art." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「美術|びじゅつ」, fine art, the art of beauty, plus 「館|かん」, a large public building, as in 「図書館|としょかん」.",
      "Beauty gives 「美しい|うつくしい」, and the technique kanji is in 「芸術|げいじゅつ」, the arts, and 「手術|しゅじゅつ」, surgery or hand technique.",
      "This word is only for art, while history and science museums are 「博物館|はくぶつかん」, so paintings go here and dinosaurs go there.",
      "Say a short 「じゅ」, not 「じゅう」, and keep the whispered 「つ」 as a full beat, for five beats in all."
    ],
    ja: "[美術館|びじゅつかん]で{絵|え}を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I looked at paintings at the art museum." },
  "しゃちょう|社長": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「社|しゃ」, means company here. It is the second half of 「会社|かいしゃ」, a company, and also part of 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, a company employee, from lesson one. The second, 「長|ちょう」, means the head or the chief. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「長い|ながい」, long, from lesson seven. A way to link them: the long one, the elder, becomes the leader. So the word is the head of the company, the president. In Japan, this word is used almost like a title. Staff will call the boss 「社長|しゃちょう」 directly, instead of using a name, the same way students call their teacher 「先生|せんせい」. The same 「長|ちょう」 shows up in lots of leader words, like the head of a department or a school principal, so it is a handy kanji to know. Picture the company's tallest figure standing at the front. So: company plus chief, the person at the very top." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, head or chief, is a real workhorse. Add it to almost any group, and you get the person in charge. A school principal is 「校長|こうちょう」, the head of the school. A department manager is 「部長|ぶちょう」, and a section chief is 「課長|かちょう」. A store manager is 「店長|てんちょう」, the head of the shop. A mayor is 「市長|しちょう」, head of the city. A ship's captain is 「船長|せんちょう」. And above the company president, many firms have a chairman, 「会長|かいちょう」, head of the association. Even school clubs have a 「部長|ぶちょう」, the club captain. Each time, the first kanji tells you the group, and this one tells you that you're talking about the boss. You can often guess new words from that alone. So whenever you see 「ちょう」 with this kanji at the end, look for the person in charge." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a Japanese company, titles do the work that names do in English. Employees usually call the president just 「社長|しゃちょう」, with no name and no 「さん」, because the title is already respectful. The same goes for 「部長|ぶちょう」 and 「課長|かちょう」. You might hear 「社長、おはようございます|しゃちょう、おはようございます」 in the elevator. There's a funny twist outside the office. Some shop staff, taxi drivers or bar hosts call middle-aged male customers 「社長|しゃちょう」 as a playful flattering nickname, like calling someone boss in English. People from outside the company often add 「さん」 when talking about another company's president: 「社長さん|しゃちょうさん」. A typical question is 「お父さんのお仕事は|おとうさんのおしごとは」, what does your father do? and an answer could be 「社長です|しゃちょうです」. So inside the company it's a title, and outside it can even be a compliment." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji have pictures behind them. The second, head or long, is usually explained as an old drawing of an elderly person with long, flowing hair. Long hair means long life, which explains both meanings: 「長い|ながい」, long, and the elder or chief. In many societies the oldest person led the group, so the picture fits nicely. The first kanji has the altar radical on the left, the same one you saw in 「神社|じんじゃ」, and 「土|つち」, earth, on the right. The usual explanation is an altar to the god of the land, a place where the local community gathered. That gathering sense grew into society and company. So the company president is, in a way, the elder of the gathering. So: an altar to the earth, and an elder with long hair at its head." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「社|しゃ」, company, from 「会社|かいしゃ」, plus 「長|ちょう」, chief, so the head of the company, used as a title like 「先生|せんせい」.",
      "Add the chief kanji to a group to get its boss, as in 「校長|こうちょう」, principal, 「店長|てんちょう」, store manager, and 「市長|しちょう」, mayor.",
      "Staff call the president just 「社長|しゃちょう」, with no name or 「さん」, and some shop staff even use it as a flattering nickname for customers.",
      "The chief kanji is an elder with long flowing hair, and the company kanji is an altar to the earth god where people gathered."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は[社長|しゃちょう]です。",
    en: "My father is a company president." },
  "かしゅ|歌手": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「歌|か」, means song. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「歌|うた」, a song, and the verb is 「歌う|うたう」, to sing, both from lesson seven. You also met this 「か」 reading in 「歌舞伎|かぶき」, where the three kanji are song, dance and skill. The second kanji, 「手|しゅ」, is hand. On its own it is 「て」, as in 「手紙|てがみ」, a letter. But in many job words, this kanji means a person who is skilled at something, someone who does it with their hands, so to speak. You will see it again in 「選手|せんしゅ」, an athlete, later in this lesson. So the word is a song person, a singer, usually meaning a professional one. To say you want to be one: 「歌手になりたいです|かしゅになりたいです」, with 「になる」 for becoming. A way to remember it: the hand holding the microphone. So: song plus skilled person, a singer." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The hand kanji, read 「しゅ」, turns many words into people with a skill. You know this word and 「選手|せんしゅ」, an athlete. A driver for work is 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」, a bus or taxi driver. An assistant is 「助手|じょしゅ」, a helping hand. The same kanji also gives skill words with special readings. 「上手|じょうず」, good at something, is literally upper hand. 「下手|へた」, bad at something, is literally lower hand. In those two, the hand kanji is read 「ず」 and 「た」, which are irregular, so just learn them as whole words. In all of these, the hand stands for what you can do. A singer doesn't sing with their hands, of course, but the kanji means a person of skill. So 「歌が上手な人|うたがじょうずなひと」 is someone good at singing, while this word is someone who sings for a living." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word usually means a professional singer, not someone who just likes singing. A common small talk question is 「好きな歌手はだれですか|すきなかしゅはだれですか」, who is your favorite singer? You'll also hear 「アイドル」 for pop idols, and 「バンド」 for bands, but this word covers soloists of every style. Japan has a huge karaoke culture, and singing with friends or coworkers is a normal night out. On New Year's Eve, many families watch a long TV singing contest, 「紅白歌合戦|こうはくうたがっせん」, where famous singers split into a red team and a white team. That program name contains the native reading of the song kanji, 「うた」. A child might say 「歌手になりたいです|かしゅになりたいです」 when asked about their dream. So it is a job title, and a common dream for kids." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a big black crow, cawing 「カー」 at the top of its lungs, standing on a stage. On its feet it wears a pair of shiny new shoes. Ka, plus shoe, gives you something close to 「かしゅ」. Now the crow lifts one wing like a hand and grabs a microphone, because the second kanji is the hand. The first kanji, song, is what the crow is belting out, loud and off key. The audience throws flowers anyway, because this crow is a famous professional. Every time you hear 「かしゅ」, picture the shoe-wearing crow with a microphone in its hand. Just remember the real word is short, two beats, so don't stretch the shoe sound out. So: a cawing crow, in shoes, holding a mic." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「歌|か」, song, plus 「手|しゅ」, hand, which in job words means a skilled person, so a professional singer.",
      "The hand kanji makes skill people like 「選手|せんしゅ」 and 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」, and gives 「上手|じょうず」 and 「下手|へた」, with irregular readings.",
      "It means a professional singer, as in 「好きな歌手はだれですか|すきなかしゅはだれですか」, and New Year's Eve brings the 「紅白歌合戦|こうはくうたがっせん」 singing contest.",
      "Picture a crow cawing 「カー」 on stage in shiny shoes, holding a microphone, but keep the word to two short beats."
    ],
    ja: "{将来|しょうらい}、[歌手|かしゅ]になりたいです。",
    en: "I want to become a singer in the future." },
  "ルームメイト|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword straight from the English word roommate. Watch how the sounds got reshaped. The English r and l both become the Japanese r sound, so room is 「ルーム」. The long oo becomes a long vowel, shown by the dash. Then mate becomes 「メイト」, with the English long a written as 「メイ」 and an extra vowel added after the t, because Japanese words do not end on a consonant. So one short English word becomes six beats of Japanese. The meaning is the same as in English, someone you share a room or an apartment with. It is common for students living in dorms or for people sharing a place abroad. Japanese often borrows English words for things linked with student life and living arrangements, like 「アパート」 and 「ホームステイ」. A way to remember it: say roommate slowly, giving every consonant its own vowel, and you are there. A typical sentence is 「ルームメイトはアメリカ人です|ルームメイトはアメリカじんです」. So: room plus mate, stretched into Japanese rhythm." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Sharing a place with a roommate is less common in Japan than in America, but it's growing. Many students live alone in a tiny studio apartment, called a 「ワンルーム」, a word made in Japan from English parts. Others live in a student dorm, a 「寮|りょう」, where sharing a room is normal. In big cities, young people also live in a 「シェアハウス」, a house where everyone has their own room but shares the kitchen and bathroom. Sharing an apartment with a friend is often called 「ルームシェア」. A typical conversation might go like this. 「ルームメイトはどんな人ですか|ルームメイトはどんなひとですか」, what is your roommate like? 「やさしくて、おもしろい人です|やさしくて、おもしろいひとです」, kind and funny. Notice the adjective te-form linking two descriptions. So the word is easy, but the living arrangements around it are a little different in Japan." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both halves of this word show up in other loanwords, so learning one helps with the rest. The mate part appears in 「クラスメイト」, a classmate, and 「チームメイト」, a teammate. The room part appears in 「ルームサービス」, room service at a hotel, and in 「ワンルーム」, a studio apartment. Japanese also mixes and matches English parts in new ways, so you'll sometimes see combinations English doesn't use. When reading katakana, try to split long words into chunks you recognize. A long word like 「ルームメイト」 becomes much less scary once you spot room and mate inside it. The same chunk trick works for 「ホストファミリー」, also in this lesson. A native word you might also hear is 「同じ部屋の人|おなじへやのひと」, the person in the same room, though that sounds more like a description. So look for the English building blocks, and long katakana words break apart easily." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English roommate, with each consonant getting its own vowel, so one short word becomes six beats of Japanese.",
      "Sharing is less common in Japan, where students often live in a 「ワンルーム」 studio, a dorm called 「寮|りょう」, or a 「シェアハウス」.",
      "Split long katakana into chunks, like mate in 「クラスメイト」 and room in 「ルームサービス」, and the word stops looking scary."
    ],
    ja: "[ルームメイト]はアメリカ{人|じん}です。",
    en: "My roommate is American." },
  "ホストファミリー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from the English phrase host family, the family a study abroad student lives with. Look at how each part was squeezed into Japanese. Host becomes 「ホスト」. The s and the t each get their own beat, with a vowel added, because Japanese does not stack consonants. Family becomes 「ファミリー」. Japanese did not have an f sound like English, so it uses 「フ」 with a small 「ァ」 to make the fa sound. The l becomes an r sound, and the y sound at the end becomes a long vowel. This word goes hand in hand with 「ホームステイ」, a homestay, from lesson eight, and with 「留学する|りゅうがくする」, to study abroad, from this lesson. Many Genki characters stay with one, so you hear it in the dialogues. When you talk about your host mother, you can say 「ホストファミリーのおかあさん」. A way to remember it: host and family, said slowly with a vowel after every consonant. So: the family that hosts you while you study." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Living with a host family comes with a set of phrases that make life smoother. When you arrive for the first time, say 「よろしくおねがいします」, and a more grateful version is 「お世話になります|おせわになります」, thank you in advance for looking after me. Coming home, you say 「ただいま」, and the family answers 「おかえり」. Before eating, 「いただきます」, and after, 「ごちそうさまでした」. Many students bring a small gift from their home country, an 「お土産|おみやげ」, to give on the first day. When you leave, you say 「お世話になりました|おせわになりました」, thank you for everything you did for me. Little habits matter too: taking off your shoes at the door, and asking before using the bath. So a few set phrases, used every day, will make your host family feel at home with you." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word is part of a whole set. Your host mother is 「ホストマザー」, your host father is 「ホストファーザー」, and a host brother or sister is 「ホストブラザー」 or 「ホストシスター」. In conversation, though, many students just say 「おかあさん」 and 「おとうさん」, which sounds warm. The family half also appears in a very Japanese shortening. A family restaurant, the kind of casual chain with big menus and drink bars, is 「ファミリーレストラン」, but nearly everyone shortens it to 「ファミレス」. Japanese loves to clip long loanwords this way, keeping the first part of each piece. A convenience store, 「コンビニエンスストア」, becomes 「コンビニ」, and a smartphone becomes 「スマホ」. So when you hear a short katakana word that sounds unfamiliar, try stretching it back out. So: host and family, and a family that also eats at the 「ファミレス」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English host family, with 「フ」 and a small 「ァ」 for the fa sound, and it goes hand in hand with 「ホームステイ」.",
      "Arrive with 「お世話になります|おせわになります」, use 「ただいま」 and 「いただきます」 daily, and leave with 「お世話になりました|おせわになりました」.",
      "The family has a 「ホストマザー」 and 「ホストファーザー」, and family restaurants get clipped to 「ファミレス」, like 「コンビニ」 and 「スマホ」."
    ],
    ja: "わたしの[ホストファミリー]はやさしいです。",
    en: "My host family is kind." },
  "しょうらい|将来": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「将|しょう」, has a few meanings. One is a general, the leader of an army. Another, from old Chinese grammar, is about to or going to. That second meaning is the one at work here. The second kanji, 「来|らい」, means to come. You know it from 「来年|らいねん」, next year, 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week, and the verb 「来る|くる」. Put them together and you get what is about to come, the future. Japanese has more than one word for future, and this one is usually about a person's own life ahead, careers, plans, hopes. That is why it is the word for questions like what do you want to be in the future: 「将来、何になりたいですか|しょうらい、なにになりたいですか」. It often sits at the start of a sentence, with a comma after it, just like the example. A way to remember it: think of the general looking ahead to what is coming. So: about to plus come, your future." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several ways to talk about the future, and each has its own feel. This word is about a person's life ahead: jobs, goals, family plans. Another word, 「未来|みらい」, is the future in a bigger, more abstract sense, the future of humanity, or a science fiction future with flying cars. A third, 「今度|こんど」, means next time or this coming time, as in 「今度、映画を見ましょう|こんど、えいがをみましょう」, let's see a movie sometime. And 「これから」 means from now on, starting right now. So if a child talks about becoming a doctor, they use this word. If a scientist talks about robots, they use 「未来|みらい」. And if a friend suggests dinner next week, it's 「今度|こんど」. Notice that this word and 「未来|みらい」 both end in the same kanji, come. So choose your future by how big and how personal it is." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word often comes as part of a pair: 「将来の夢|しょうらいのゆめ」, your dream for the future. Japanese children write essays with exactly that title in elementary school, and surveys of children's dream jobs make the news every year. Common answers include soccer player, doctor, pastry chef and, lately, video creator. Adults ask young people 「将来、何になりたいですか|しょうらい、なにになりたいですか」, and job interviewers ask similar questions in a more formal way. A short exchange might go: 「将来、何がしたいですか|しょうらい、なにがしたいですか」. 「日本で働きたいです|にほんではたらきたいです」. Another useful phrase is 「近い将来|ちかいしょうらい」, the near future, which you'll hear in the news. Notice the comma after the word in many sentences, giving it a little pause. So it is the word for big life questions and childhood dreams." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word has four beats: 「しょ」, 「う」, 「ら」, 「い」. The first part is a long vowel, so hold it. If you cut it short, it sounds wrong. Here's the tricky part. Many kanji are read 「しょう」, so you'll hear this sound everywhere. In this very lesson, 「正月|しょうがつ」, New Year, starts with the same sound but a different kanji. 「小学校|しょうがっこう」, elementary school, begins with yet another. And the word for introduce, 「紹介|しょうかい」, uses another still. The second half, 「らい」, is the same as in 「来年|らいねん」 and 「来週|らいしゅう」, so it carries a hint of coming, next. If you remember that, you can guess the meaning from the sound. Listen for 「らい」 at the end, and you'll know something is on its way. So hold the long 「しょう」, and let the 「らい」 tell you it's coming." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「将|しょう」, about to, plus 「来|らい」, come, so what is about to come, usually your own life ahead, as in 「将来、何になりたいですか|しょうらい、なにになりたいですか」.",
      "This word is your personal future, 「未来|みらい」 is the big abstract future, 「今度|こんど」 is next time, and 「これから」 is from now on.",
      "It often pairs as 「将来の夢|しょうらいのゆめ」, the title of children's school essays, and 「近い将来|ちかいしょうらい」 means the near future.",
      "Hold the long 「しょう」 for four beats total, and let the 「らい」, as in 「来年|らいねん」, hint that something is coming."
    ],
    ja: "[将来|しょうらい]、{先生|せんせい}になりたいです。",
    en: "In the future, I want to be a teacher." },
  "ゆめ|夢": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese word, and just like in English, it covers two things: the dreams you have when you sleep, and the dreams you have for your life. The verb is the surprise. In English you have a dream, but in Japanese you see one: 「夢を見る|ゆめをみる」. That makes sense, because dreams are mostly pictures. For a life dream, people say 「夢があります|ゆめがあります」, I have a dream, or ask 「夢は何ですか|ゆめはなんですか」, what is your dream? It pairs nicely with 「将来|しょうらい」, the future, from this lesson. Now the kanji. At the very bottom is 「夕|ゆう」, evening, a real part of this character. A way to remember the rest: picture an eye above it that stays open into the night, still seeing pictures while you sleep. That ties together the kanji and the verb 「見る|みる」. A common mistake is to say 「夢をする|ゆめをする」. So: something you see at night, or see in your future." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The first dream of the New Year has a special name, 「初夢|はつゆめ」, and people say it can predict your luck for the year. There's even a traditional ranking of the luckiest things to dream about. First is Mount Fuji, second is a hawk, and third is an eggplant. The saying is 「一富士二鷹三茄子|いちふじにたかさんなすび」. Nobody is completely sure why those three were chosen, and there are several competing explanations, but many people still know the phrase and joke about it at New Year. There's also 「正夢|まさゆめ」, a dream that comes true, and the opposite, a dream that turns out to be nothing. Some people even put a picture of a lucky ship under their pillow to invite good dreams. So in Japan, even the first dream of the year has its own little tradition." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since this word uses 「見る|みる」, all the verb forms follow that ru-verb: 「夢を見ました|ゆめをみました」, I had a dream, and 「夢を見ています|ゆめをみています」, I'm dreaming. A sweet way to say good night is 「いい夢を見てね|いいゆめをみてね」, sweet dreams, literally see a good dream. Inside a dream uses 「の中で|のなかで」: 「夢の中で、空を飛びました|ゆめのなかで、そらをとびました」, in my dream, I flew. For life dreams, the key verb is 「かなう」, to come true: 「夢がかなう|ゆめがかなう」, a dream comes true. If you make it come true yourself, the verb changes to 「かなえる」: 「夢をかなえる|ゆめをかなえる」. That pair is just like 「開く|あく」 and 「開ける|あける」, one happening on its own, one done on purpose. So you see your dreams at night, and you make them come true by day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It covers both sleeping and life dreams, and you see one rather than have one, 「夢を見る|ゆめをみる」, never 「夢をする|ゆめをする」.",
      "The first dream of the year, 「初夢|はつゆめ」, predicts your luck, with Mount Fuji, a hawk, and an eggplant ranked luckiest.",
      "Say sweet dreams with 「いい夢を見てね|いいゆめをみてね」, and a dream comes true with 「夢がかなう|ゆめがかなう」 or you make it so with 「夢をかなえる|ゆめをかなえる」."
    ],
    ja: "{昨日|きのう}、こわい[夢|ゆめ]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I had a scary dream yesterday." },
  "おまつり|お祭り": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The core word is 「祭り|まつり」, and the 「お」 in front is just polite and friendly, like in 「お茶|おちゃ」. It comes from the verb 「祭る|まつる」, to worship, to honor the gods. That is the heart of it: traditionally, a festival is held to honor the spirit of a local shrine. Look at the kanji. The bottom is a radical connected with altars and offerings, the same one you see squeezed onto the left side of 「神社|じんじゃ」. The top is traditionally explained as a piece of meat and a hand, making an offering on the altar. So the word itself carries the idea of celebrating the gods. Today festivals are big community parties. In summer you will see people in light cotton robes, food stalls with grilled snacks, games, dancing, and often fireworks. Some have portable shrines carried through the streets. Kyoto's 「祇園祭|ぎおんまつり」 is one of the most famous. Inside a festival name, the 「お」 drops. So: honoring the gods, with food and fireworks." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Some festivals are so famous they have a ranking, the three great festivals of Japan. One is Kyoto's 「祇園祭|ぎおんまつり」 in July, with huge floats pulled through the streets. Another is Osaka's 「天神祭|てんじんまつり」, with boats on the river and fireworks. The third is usually Tokyo's 「神田祭|かんだまつり」, with portable shrines carried by shouting crowds. In summer, local neighborhoods hold smaller festivals with stalls selling 「たこ焼き|たこやき」, 「焼きそば|やきそば」, and shaved ice, 「かき氷|かきごおり」. Children try to scoop goldfish with paper scoops that tear easily. Around the Obon holiday, people dance in a circle in a dance called 「盆踊り|ぼんおどり」. In winter, Sapporo holds a famous snow festival with giant ice sculptures. So festivals happen in every season, from floats in July to snow statues in February." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for festival has a Chinese reading too, 「さい」. You'll meet it in school life. A school culture festival, where classes run cafes and haunted houses for visitors, is 「文化祭|ぶんかさい」. A university festival is 「学園祭|がくえんさい」 or 「大学祭|だいがくさい」. Sports day at school, though, is 「運動会|うんどうかい」, which uses a different word, a meeting, not a festival. With the native reading, you can name a festival by season: 「夏祭り|なつまつり」, a summer festival, and 「秋祭り|あきまつり」, an autumn festival. Many festivals are named after the place or the shrine, like 「雪まつり|ゆきまつり」, snow festival, and as the main lesson said, the polite 「お」 drops inside those names. So when you see 「さい」 at the end of a school event, think festival, and when you see 「まつり」, think food stalls and summer nights." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's a classic particle trap. To say there is a festival somewhere, you use 「ある」, the verb for things. That makes sense, because a festival is an event, not a living thing. But the place does not take 「に」, as it usually does with 「ある」. It takes 「で」: 「京都でお祭りがあります|きょうとでおまつりがあります」, there is a festival in Kyoto. That's because an event happens at a place, so it's treated like an action. The same rule applies to parties, concerts, tests and meetings: 「学校でテストがあります|がっこうでテストがあります」. Compare 「京都にお寺があります|きょうとにおてらがあります」, there is a temple in Kyoto, where the temple just sits there. So use 「に」 for buildings, and 「で」 for events. So a festival happens, and things that happen take 「で」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「祭る|まつる」, to worship, and the kanji shows an offering on an altar, so a festival honors the local shrine's spirit.",
      "The three great festivals are Kyoto's 「祇園祭|ぎおんまつり」, Osaka's 「天神祭|てんじんまつり」 and Tokyo's 「神田祭|かんだまつり」, with summer stalls and 「盆踊り|ぼんおどり」 dancing.",
      "Read 「さい」, it names school events like 「文化祭|ぶんかさい」, while 「まつり」 names seasonal ones like 「夏祭り|なつまつり」.",
      "An event takes 「で」 even with 「ある」, as in 「京都でお祭りがあります|きょうとでおまつりがあります」, while a building that just sits there takes 「に」."
    ],
    ja: "{夏|なつ}に[お祭り|おまつり]があります。",
    en: "There is a festival in the summer." },
  "おしょうがつ|お正月": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji plus a polite 「お」. The first kanji, 「正|しょう」, usually means correct or proper. You will often see it read 「せい」, but here it takes the reading 「しょう」. In this word it carries the sense of the proper beginning, so 「正月|しょうがつ」 originally meant the first month of the year. The second kanji, 「月|がつ」, means month, as in 「一月|いちがつ」, January, and it is also in 「今月|こんげつ」 and 「来月|らいげつ」. Today the word means the New Year holidays, roughly the first few days of January, and it is the biggest holiday of the year. Families gather, eat special boxed New Year food, children get envelopes of money from relatives, and most people visit a shrine, a 「神社|じんじゃ」, to pray for a good year. Businesses close and trains fill with people going home. New Year in Japan is a quiet family time, more like an American Christmas, while Christmas is more for couples. So: the proper first month, and the most important holiday in Japan." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The New Year comes with greetings that you'll hear everywhere. Before the year ends, people say 「よいお年を|よいおとしを」, have a good New Year, as a farewell. Once the year turns, everyone says 「あけましておめでとうございます」, happy New Year, literally congratulations on the opening. This is usually followed by 「今年もよろしくおねがいします|ことしもよろしくおねがいします」, please treat me kindly again this year. A traditional custom is sending New Year's postcards, 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, which the post office delivers right on New Year's Day. Children receive 「お年玉|おとしだま」, money in small decorated envelopes. A short exchange between neighbors: 「あけましておめでとうございます」. 「おめでとうございます。今年もよろしく|おめでとうございます。ことしもよろしく」. So learn two greetings, one for before and one for after, and you're ready for the holiday." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The New Year season is full of special objects and foods. Outside homes and shops, people set up pine and bamboo decorations, 「門松|かどまつ」, to welcome the year's god. Inside, they place two round rice cakes stacked on each other, with a small citrus fruit on top, called 「鏡餅|かがみもち」. The New Year meal is 「おせち料理|おせちりょうり」, many small dishes in stacked boxes, each with a lucky meaning, like long life or good harvests. There is also a soup with rice cakes, 「お雑煮|おぞうに」, whose recipe changes by region and family. On New Year's Eve, Buddhist temples ring their great bells one hundred and eight times. The first shrine visit of the year is 「初詣|はつもうで」, and famous shrines get millions of visitors. So the holiday is a mix of decorations, special food, bells and prayers for a good year." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji has two readings, and both are useful. Its core meaning is correct. With its native reading it gives 「正しい|ただしい」, correct. As 「せい」, it is in 「正解|せいかい」, the right answer, which quiz shows shout out. As 「しょう」, it shows up in this word and in 「正午|しょうご」, exactly noon. Notice that 「正午|しょうご」 shares its second kanji with 「午前|ごぜん」 and 「午後|ごご」, so noon sits right between a.m. and p.m. The month kanji also has three readings to keep straight. 「がつ」 for names of months, like 「一月|いちがつ」. 「げつ」 for 「今月|こんげつ」 and 「か月|かげつ」. And 「つき」 for 「毎月|まいつき」, every month, or the moon itself. In this word, it is 「がつ」, like a month name. So the proper reading 「しょう」 plus the month name 「がつ」 gives you the proper month, New Year." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「正|しょう」, proper, plus 「月|がつ」, month, originally the proper first month, and now the New Year holidays, Japan's biggest family holiday.",
      "Say 「よいお年を|よいおとしを」 before the year ends and 「あけましておめでとうございます」 after, followed by 「今年もよろしくおねがいします|ことしもよろしくおねがいします」.",
      "The season brings 「門松|かどまつ」 decorations, 「おせち料理|おせちりょうり」 in stacked boxes, and the first shrine visit, 「初詣|はつもうで」.",
      "The proper kanji reads 「ただ」 in 「正しい|ただしい」, 「せい」 in 「正解|せいかい」, and 「しょう」 here, joined to the month name reading 「がつ」."
    ],
    ja: "[お正月|おしょうがつ]に{神社|じんじゃ}にいきます。",
    en: "I go to a shrine at New Year's." },
  "おかし|お菓子": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The core word is 「菓子|かし」, and the 「お」 in front makes it polite and soft, which is how almost everyone says it. The first kanji, 「菓|か」, has the grass radical on top, the plant marker, and underneath it is 「果|か」, fruit. That is the same kanji as in 「果物|くだもの」, fruit, from lesson five. The history fits: long ago, the sweet things people snacked on were fruit and nuts, so the word for snacks was built on fruit. The second kanji is 「子|し」, child, used here as a little suffix for small things. You have seen that same suffix in 「帽子|ぼうし」, a hat. Today the word covers all kinds of snacks and sweets: candy, chocolate, cookies, chips, rice crackers. Japanese convenience stores have a whole aisle of them, with new flavors every season. Traditional Japanese sweets, often made with red bean paste, have their own name, but they are still part of this family. A way to remember it: fruit from a plant, made small and cute. So: snacks, born from fruit." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan splits its sweets into two big camps. 「和菓子|わがし」 are the traditional Japanese sweets, and the 「わ」 at the front is the same one as in 「和食|わしょく」, Japanese food. They are often made from rice flour and sweet red bean paste, shaped like leaves or flowers, and they change with the seasons. In spring you see pink cherry blossom sweets, and in autumn you see little chestnuts and maple leaves. They are made to go with green tea. The other camp is 「洋菓子|ようがし」, Western sweets, like cake, cookies and pudding. Notice how the 「か」 softens to 「が」 when it comes second. Sweets are also a big part of gift giving. When people come back from a trip, they bring 「お土産|おみやげ」 for coworkers, and very often that souvenir is a box of individually wrapped local sweets, one for each person in the office. So a whole culture of sharing travels inside those little boxes." },
      { title: "Snack time word", text: "Here is a close cousin you will hear from every Japanese child: 「おやつ」, a snack, or snack time. Kids have it in the afternoon, after school, and parents ask 「おやつ、食べる|おやつ、たべる」, want a snack? The story behind it is well established. Old Japan counted the hours of the day differently, and the mid afternoon hour, roughly two to three o'clock, was called the eighth hour, 「八つ時|やつどき」. People had a light bite then, and with the polite 「お」 in front, the time itself became the word for the snack. Today the two words overlap. 「お菓子|おかし」 is the thing itself, the candy or the cookies. 「おやつ」 is the snack you eat between meals, which might be sweets, but could also be fruit or a rice ball. A useful phrase is 「お菓子を作る|おかしをつくる」, to bake or make sweets. So remember the pair: the sweets, and the snack hour." },
      { title: "Polite little o", text: "The 「お」 at the front of this word deserves its own moment. It is a beautifying prefix. It does not change the meaning, it just makes the word sound softer and more refined. With some words it is optional. With others, people almost never drop it. You already know a whole group of these. 「お茶|おちゃ」, tea. 「お金|おかね」, money. 「お酒|おさけ」, alcohol. 「お風呂|おふろ」, a bath. 「お土産|おみやげ」, a souvenir. For sweets, the bare word 「菓子|かし」 does exist, but it sounds stiff and technical, the kind of word printed on a factory label. In conversation, you say it with the 「お」. One exception you might meet is 「駄菓子|だがし」, the cheap penny candy sold in old fashioned corner shops, where the prefix is replaced by another piece. So when in doubt, keep the polite 「お」. It makes the sweets sound sweeter." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The first kanji 「菓|か」 is grass on top of 「果|か」, fruit, because long ago people snacked on fruit and nuts, and the second kanji 「子|し」 is a small-thing suffix, so snacks were born from fruit.",
      "Japan splits sweets into traditional 「和菓子|わがし」, seasonal treats of rice flour and red bean paste made for green tea, and Western 「洋菓子|ようがし」 like cake and cookies, with the 「か」 softening to 「が」.",
      "Its cousin 「おやつ」 is snack time, named after the old mid-afternoon eighth hour, so 「お菓子|おかし」 is the sweets themselves while 「おやつ」 is whatever you eat between meals.",
      "The 「お」 is a beautifying prefix that softens the word, and the bare 「菓子|かし」 sounds stiff like a factory label, so in conversation always keep the polite 「お」."
    ],
    ja: "{子供|こども}は[お菓子|おかし]がすきです。",
    en: "Children like sweets." },
  "ビール|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword, but not from English. It comes from the Dutch word bier. That explains why it sounds closer to the Dutch or German word than to the English beer. For about two centuries, while Japan kept most of the world out, the Dutch were the only Europeans allowed to trade there, through a small island in Nagasaki. Quite a few words came in that way, and beer is one of them. Listen to the sounds. The b stays, the long ee becomes 「ビー」 with a long vowel, and the final r turns into 「ル」, with an extra vowel, because Japanese does not end words on a consonant. Today beer is the most popular alcoholic drink in Japan. At a work party, the first round is almost always beer, and everyone raises a glass and says 「乾杯|かんぱい」, cheers, from lesson eight. It falls under the wider word 「お酒|おさけ」, which means alcohol in general as well as rice wine. A way to remember it: think of the old Dutch ships bringing barrels in. So: Dutch bier, poured into Japanese." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a Japanese pub, an 「居酒屋|いざかや」, on a Friday night. Before anyone even looks at the menu, someone says 「とりあえずビール」, beer for now, to start. It is almost a ritual. Draft beer is 「生ビール|なまビール」, and the 「生|なま」 means raw or fresh, the same as in raw fish. People often shorten the order to just 「生|なま」. To order, you can say 「生ビールを二つください|なまビールをふたつください」, two draft beers, please. Notice you count drinks with the general counter, 「一つ|ひとつ」, 「二つ|ふたつ」. A bottle is 「びん」 and a can is 「缶|かん」. The legal drinking age in Japan is twenty, so your younger host siblings will be drinking juice. A friendly mini dialogue: 「何を飲みますか|なにをのみますか」, what will you drink? 「ビールをお願いします|ビールをおねがいします」, beer, please. So the first word of the night is very often this one." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful with the long vowel here. This word has three beats, 「ビ」, a held 「ー」, and 「ル」. If you cut the middle beat short, you get 「ビル」, which is a different word: a building, a modern multi story building. That one comes from the end of the English word building. So 「あのビル」 is that building, and 「あのビール」 is that beer. Imagine telling a friend you want to drink the building. The fix is to really hold the long vowel, like a little sigh of happiness after the first sip: bee, held, ru. Japanese counts every beat, so a long vowel is not decoration, it changes the word. Here is a memory trick: the beer is longer than the building because you want to make it last. Say both out loud a few times, short and long, until the difference feels natural. So: hold the vowel for beer." },
      { title: "Pouring manners", text: "Drinking beer in Japan comes with its own manners. At a group meal, beer often arrives in big bottles shared around the table. The custom is that you do not pour your own glass. You pour for the people around you, and they pour for you. When someone older or more senior lifts the bottle toward you, you pick up your glass with both hands and hold it out. Then you return the favor. If you notice a neighbor's glass is getting low, offering a refill is a kind gesture. Nobody drinks until everyone has a glass and the group says 「乾杯|かんぱい」 together. At the end of the evening, people often thank each other for the company. If you do not drink alcohol, it is perfectly fine to ask for 「ウーロン茶|ウーロンちゃ」, oolong tea, and join the toast with that. So with beer, the real skill is filling other people's glasses." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It came from Dutch bier, not English, because the Dutch were the only Europeans trading with Japan for two centuries, and the final r picked up a vowel to become 「ル」.",
      "At an 「居酒屋|いざかや」 the night often starts with 「とりあえずビール」, beer for now, and draft beer is 「生ビール|なまビール」, often shortened to just 「生|なま」.",
      "Hold the long middle beat, because if you cut it short you get 「ビル」, a building, so remember the beer is longer because you want to make it last.",
      "At a group meal you never pour your own glass, you pour for others and hold yours out with both hands, and nobody drinks until everyone says 「乾杯|かんぱい」 together."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[ビール]はおいしいです。",
    en: "Japanese beer is tasty." },
  "おもちゃ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese word, almost always written in kana. There is a formal kanji word for toys, but it is read differently and you mostly see it on signs in stores, so for daily life just use this one. It is a friendly, everyday word, used for anything a child plays with: blocks, cars, dolls, games. A toy store is 「おもちゃ屋|おもちゃや」, with the same shop ending as 「本屋|ほんや」, a bookstore. Stuffed animals have their own word, 「ぬいぐるみ」, which you will meet in lesson fourteen. A typical verb to go with it is 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, to play: 「おもちゃで遊ぶ|おもちゃであそぶ」, to play with toys. Notice the particle 「で」, because the toy is what you play with, like a tool. A way to remember it: picture a little kid in a store tugging your sleeve and begging, oh, more toys, oh, more cha! The sound-alike gets you most of the way there. So: anything a child plays with, oh more toys." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This one has a charming history. Japanese dictionaries generally trace it back to an older word, 「もちあそび」, which meant something you hold in your hands and play with. It combined the idea of holding, as in 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, from lesson eight, with 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, to play. In the speech of the old imperial court, women often shortened words and added a polite 「お」 in front, and this word was squeezed down to 「もちゃ」. With the 「お」 attached, it became the word we use today. So the polite front piece is not a separate word you can drop. It has fused into the toy. That is why you never hear just 「もちゃ」 on its own. It is a nice reminder that some everyday words started as elegant court language. So: a thing to hold and play with, squeezed small and made polite." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Look closely at the small 「ゃ」 at the end. It joins the 「ち」 to make one sound, 「ちゃ」. So this word has three beats: 「お」, 「も」, 「ちゃ」. If you wrote the 「や」 full size, you would get 「おもちや」, four beats, which sounds like a rice cake shop, a place that sells 「もち」. A small kana change, and the toy store becomes a mochi store. Funnily enough, a toy store is 「おもちゃ屋|おもちゃや」, so there you really do say 「ちゃ」 and then a full 「や」, four beats then a fifth. Practice saying it slowly: o, mo, cha, ya. The same small 「ゃ」 shows up in 「お茶|おちゃ」 and 「お客さん|おきゃくさん」, so you already have the habit. Here is a memory trick: the small letter is a little toy sitting next to the big one. So: three neat beats, with the tiny 「ゃ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The basic meaning is a child's toy, but Japanese stretches it in a few fun ways. Something that looks cheap or fake can be described as like a toy: 「おもちゃみたいな車|おもちゃみたいなくるま」, a car that looks like a toy. People also use it for gadgets that adults play with. A new camera or game console might be called a grown up's toy, 「大人のおもちゃ|おとなのおもちゃ」, though be careful, because that exact phrase also has a racier meaning, so you may get a laugh. There is also an expression for teasing someone or toying with them: 「人をおもちゃにする|ひとをおもちゃにする」, to treat a person like a plaything. And at every station and shopping center you will see rows of capsule toy machines, where you put in coins, turn a handle, and get a tiny surprise toy. So the word moves easily from the toy box to adult life." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A friendly native word written in kana for anything a child plays with, and you remember it by picturing a kid begging, oh, more toys, oh, more cha!",
      "It comes from the old word 「もちあそび」, a thing you hold and play with, which court ladies squeezed to 「もちゃ」 and topped with a polite 「お」 that has fused on for good.",
      "The small 「ゃ」 makes three beats, 「お」, 「も」, 「ちゃ」, and writing a full-size 「や」 instead turns the toy into a mochi shop, 「おもちや」.",
      "Beyond children's toys, it describes cheap-looking things like 「おもちゃみたいな車|おもちゃみたいなくるま」, adult gadgets, and teasing someone in 「人をおもちゃにする|ひとをおもちゃにする」, to treat a person like a plaything."
    ],
    ja: "{子供|こども}の[おもちゃ]がたくさんあります。",
    en: "There are lots of children's toys." },
  "こんがっき|今学期": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji. The first, 「今|こん」, means now or this. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「今|いま」, now. But at the front of time words it often takes its Chinese-origin reading 「こん」: 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, and 「今月|こんげつ」, this month. So you already know this pattern. The rest is 「学期|がっき」, a school term. 「学|がく」 means learning, as in 「学生|がくせい」 and 「大学|だいがく」. 「期|き」 means a period of time. When these two meet, 「がく」 shrinks to 「がっ」, a little pause before the 「き」. That happens a lot in Japanese when a ku sound runs into a k sound. You will see 「期|き」 again in 「期末試験|きまつしけん」, final exams, in lesson sixteen, literally the end of the period exam. So the whole word is this learning period, this semester. Its partner is 「来学期|らいがっき」, next semester. A typical sentence: 「今学期はいそがしいです|こんがっきはいそがしいです」. So: this, plus learning period." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The Japanese school year does not start in September. It starts in April, right when the cherry blossoms bloom, so the first day of school and the pink trees go together in everyone's memory. Elementary, middle and high schools usually split the year into three terms, called 「一学期|いちがっき」, 「二学期|にがっき」 and 「三学期|さんがっき」, first, second and third term. Summer vacation falls between the first and second, and winter break between the second and third. Universities usually have two semesters, spring and fall. The start of any new term is 「新学期|しんがっき」, and in April you see it on posters and in stores selling notebooks and backpacks. So when a Japanese student talks about this term, they may mean one of three, not one of two. A nice phrase to borrow: 「今学期もよろしく|こんがっきもよろしく」, please be kind to me this term too. So the school year begins with the blossoms." },
      { title: "Many faces of now", text: "The first kanji, 「今|いま」, is a shape shifter. You know its native reading, 「いま」, now, and its Chinese-origin reading 「こん」 in words like this one. But it also hides inside some special readings that you simply have to memorize as whole words. 「今日|きょう」, today, does not use either reading. 「今朝|けさ」, this morning, is another special one. And 「今年|ことし」, this year, is read as one special unit too. Then there is 「今度|こんど」, next time or this time, which uses the regular 「こん」. So when you see this kanji at the front of a time word, do not just guess. Week, month and term take 「こん」. Day, morning and year are special. Here is a memory trick: the three short, everyday units, the day, the morning, and the year, got worn smooth into nicknames from constant use. So: this kanji has more than one way to say now." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The last part, 「がっき」, has a twin. 「楽器|がっき」 means a musical instrument, like a guitar or a piano. Same sound, same small pause, totally different kanji: the first one there is music, as in 「音楽|おんがく」. So 「学期|がっき」 and 「楽器|がっき」 are pronounced the same. Context almost always makes it clear. You do not play a semester, and you do not get busy during a guitar. Also listen carefully to the small 「っ」. Without the pause, 「がき」 is a rude slang word for a brat, a little kid. So that tiny pause matters. Say it as three beats: 「が」, a silent beat, then 「き」. Here is a memory scene to tie them together: a student sits down on the first day of the term, opens a guitar case, and says, this term I am learning an instrument. Both words are in one sentence. So: same sound as instrument, and keep the pause." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「今|こん」 means this, as in 「今週|こんしゅう」 and 「今月|こんげつ」, and 「学期|がっき」 is a learning period, with 「がく」 shrinking to 「がっ」, so it is literally this learning period.",
      "The Japanese school year starts in April with the cherry blossoms, and schools usually have three terms, so this term may mean one of three, not one of two.",
      "The kanji 「今|いま」 takes 「こん」 before week, month and term, but day, morning and year have special readings, 「今日|きょう」, 「今朝|けさ」 and 「今年|ことし」, worn smooth from constant use.",
      "「学期|がっき」 sounds exactly like 「楽器|がっき」, a musical instrument, and dropping the small pause gives 「がき」, a rude word for a brat, so keep that silent beat."
    ],
    ja: "[今学期|こんがっき]はいそがしいです。",
    en: "I am busy this semester." },
  "らいがっき|来学期": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji. The first, 「来|らい」, means coming, and it is the key to a whole set of time words you already know: 「来週|らいしゅう」, next week, 「来月|らいげつ」, next month, and 「来年|らいねん」, next year. The verb 「来る|くる」, to come, uses the same kanji with its native reading. So whenever you see 「らい」 at the front of a time word, think the one that is coming next. The rest is 「学期|がっき」, a school term. 「学|がく」 is learning, as in 「学生|がくせい」, and 「期|き」 is a period of time. When they join, 「がく」 shrinks to 「がっ」, with a little pause. Put it all together: the coming learning period, next semester. It pairs with 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester. Notice the neat system: 「今|こん」 for this one, 「来|らい」 for the next one, and you can plug in week, month or semester. A typical sentence: 「来学期、日本語をとります|らいがっき、にほんごをとります」, next semester I will take Japanese. So: coming, plus learning period." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Time words in Japanese often come in neat sets of three: last, this, and next. For weeks it is 「先週|せんしゅう」, 「今週|こんしゅう」, 「来週|らいしゅう」. For months it is 「先月|せんげつ」, 「今月|こんげつ」, 「来月|らいげつ」. For terms it follows the same pattern: 「先学期|せんがっき」, last semester, 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester, and this word, next semester. The first kanji, 「先|せん」, means ahead or previous, the same one as in 「先生|せんせい」. But watch out for years, because they break the pattern. Last year is 「去年|きょねん」, not a 「せん」 word. This year is 「今年|ことし」, with a special reading. Only next year, 「来年|らいねん」, behaves. So the coming kanji is the most reliable member of the family. Whenever you hear 「らい」 in front of a time unit, you know it is the next one. So: last, this, next, with next always steady." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a small grammar point that trips up many learners. Time words that are relative to now, like today, tomorrow, next week and next semester, do not take the particle 「に」. You just say 「来学期、日本に行きます|らいがっき、にほんにいきます」, I will go to Japan next semester. Compare that with fixed points in time, like dates, days of the week and clock times, which do take 「に」: 「四月に|しがつに」, in April, 「三時に|さんじに」, at three. If you want to make it the topic, you can add 「は」: 「来学期は忙しいです|らいがっきはいそがしいです」, next semester, I will be busy. That 「は」 gives a sense of contrast, as if to say this semester was fine, but next semester will be tough. It also pairs nicely with plans: 「来学期、留学するつもりです|らいがっき、りゅうがくするつもりです」, I plan to study abroad next semester. So: no 「に」 for next semester." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a huge semester calendar on the wall. It is the last day of class, and the next semester is rolling toward you down a long hallway like a bowling ball. You shout, it is coming! And the kanji at the front, 「来|らい」, is exactly the coming kanji, the same one in 「来る|くる」, to come. Now add the sound: it rolls in like a lie detector beeping, rai, rai, rai, and then lands with a sharp click on your desk, 「がっき」. You open it and find a new class schedule, new textbooks, and a note saying more kanji. Then think of the other two in the set. This semester is right here, 「今|こん」, now. Next semester is still on its way, 「来|らい」, coming. When you need the word, picture that ball rolling toward you. So: the semester that is coming." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「来|らい」 is the coming kanji behind 「来週|らいしゅう」, 「来月|らいげつ」 and 「来年|らいねん」, so plus 「学期|がっき」, a learning period, it means the coming semester.",
      "Terms follow the last, this, next set, 「先学期|せんがっき」, 「今学期|こんがっき」 and this word, and while years break the pattern, the 「らい」 member always stays steady as next.",
      "Relative time words like this one take no 「に」, so just say 「来学期、日本に行きます|らいがっき、にほんにいきます」, while adding 「は」 gives a sense of contrast.",
      "Picture next semester rolling toward you like a bowling ball while you shout it is coming, because 「来|らい」 is the same kanji as in 「来る|くる」, to come."
    ],
    ja: "[来学期|らいがっき]、{日本語|にほんご}をとります。",
    en: "I will take Japanese next semester." },
  "じゅぎょう|授業": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「授|じゅ」, means to give or to pass on, and it has the hand radical on the left, a hand handing something over. The second, 「業|ぎょう」, means work, business, or a field of study. You will meet it again in 「卒業|そつぎょう」, graduation, in lesson fifteen, which is finishing your studies. So the whole word is passing on studies, a teacher handing knowledge to students, a class or a lesson. How is this different from 「クラス」? This word is the actual session of teaching, the hour in the classroom. 「クラス」 is more the course, or the group of students. So you say 「授業がある|じゅぎょうがある」, I have class, or 「授業に遅刻する|じゅぎょうにちこくする」, to be late for class. You will see it again in 「授業中に|じゅぎょうちゅうに」, during class, in lesson sixteen. A way to remember it: picture the teacher's hand passing the work across the desk. So: give plus studies, a class." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Students in Japan talk about class with a handful of set verbs. To attend class, you 「授業に出る|じゅぎょうにでる」, literally go out to class. To take or receive a class, you 「授業を受ける|じゅぎょうをうける」. A teacher, on the other hand, 「授業をする|じゅぎょうをする」, does a class, teaches it. When class starts, 「授業が始まる|じゅぎょうがはじまる」, and when it ends, 「授業が終わる|じゅぎょうがおわる」. And in this very lesson you have the naughty one: 「授業をサボる|じゅぎょうをサボる」, to skip class. A quick dialogue between friends: 「午後、授業ある|ごご、じゅぎょうある」, do you have class this afternoon? 「うん、三時まで|うん、さんじまで」, yeah, until three. Notice that in casual talk, the particle often drops out. It is also part of the word for tuition, 「授業料|じゅぎょうりょう」, the class fee. So: one word, many verbs around it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "University classes in Japan are organized by periods. The day is divided into fixed slots, and students say first period, second period, and so on. At many universities each slot is about ninety minutes, much longer than a typical American class meeting, and there is a lunch break between the second and third periods. A popular student phrase is that a class was 「休講|きゅうこう」, cancelled for the day, which is always good news. You will often see a notice board at the entrance listing cancelled classes. In high school, the rhythm is different: students usually stay in one homeroom, and the teachers come to them for each subject. At the start and end of class, a student calls out, everyone stands, and the class bows to the teacher together. So a Japanese school day has a clear, shared ritual around each 「授業|じゅぎょう」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「業|ぎょう」, means work, business, or a field of study, and it turns up in many useful words. 「卒業|そつぎょう」, graduation, from lesson fifteen, is finishing your field of study. 「卒業式|そつぎょうしき」 is the graduation ceremony. 「授業料|じゅぎょうりょう」, tuition, adds the fee kanji 「料|りょう」, the same one as in 「料理|りょうり」, cooking. Outside school, this kanji appears in words about jobs and industry: 「職業|しょくぎょう」, an occupation, and 「作業|さぎょう」, work or a task, which you will see on signs at construction sites. So when you spot 「ぎょう」 at the end of a word, think work being done. The first kanji, 「授|じゅ」, is rarer for beginners, but it appears in 「教授|きょうじゅ」, a professor, the one who passes on knowledge. So the family splits neatly: one side is passing on, the other side is the work." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「授|じゅ」 is a hand passing something on and 「業|ぎょう」 is work or studies, so it is a teacher handing knowledge across, the actual class session rather than the course or group of students.",
      "Learn the set verbs around it, like 「授業に出る|じゅぎょうにでる」 to attend, 「授業を受ける|じゅぎょうをうける」 to take a class, and the naughty 「授業をサボる|じゅぎょうをサボる」, to skip class.",
      "Japanese university classes run in fixed periods of about ninety minutes, a cancelled class is a welcome 「休講|きゅうこう」, and in high school everyone stands and bows to the teacher together.",
      "「業|ぎょう」 means work being done, as in 「卒業|そつぎょう」 and 「職業|しょくぎょう」, while 「授|じゅ」 means passing on, as in 「教授|きょうじゅ」, a professor."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[授業|じゅぎょう]がありません。",
    en: "I have no class today." },
  "こちら|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word belongs to the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 family. The 「こ」 words point to things near the speaker: 「これ」, this thing, 「ここ」, here. This one literally means this direction, this way. Its siblings are 「そちら」, that way near you, 「あちら」, that way over there, and 「どちら」, which way, which you met in lesson ten. Here is the useful trick. Pointing toward a direction is softer and more polite than pointing at a person or thing directly. So when you introduce someone, you say 「こちらは田中さんです|こちらはたなかさんです」, this is Mr. Tanaka, instead of using 「これ」, which would be rude for a person. It also means here, this place, politely, and on the phone it can mean our side, as in we. A common mistake English speakers make is saying 「これは」 when introducing a friend. Another nice phrase is 「こちらこそ」, the pleasure is mine. So: this way, the polite way to point to people." },
      { title: "Politeness ladder", text: "This word sits at the top of a little politeness ladder. At the bottom is 「ここ」, here, plain and simple. In the middle is 「こっち」, this way, which is casual and friendly, what you say to a friend: 「こっちこっち」, over here, over here! At the top is this word, the polite version. The same ladder exists for the whole family. 「そこ」, 「そっち」, 「そちら」. 「あそこ」, 「あっち」, 「あちら」. And for questions, 「どこ」, 「どっち」, 「どちら」. Notice the casual middle step always has the small 「っ」 pause. So if you ask a shop clerk where something is, you might say 「どちらですか」, and the clerk will answer politely with this word or one of its siblings. With a friend, 「どっち」 is perfectly natural. Choosing the right rung is a quick way to sound like you know the social setting. So: three levels, and this is the polite top step." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Walk into any Japanese shop or restaurant and you will hear this word constantly. A server greets you and leads you to a table with 「こちらへどうぞ」, this way, please. At a fast food counter, the clerk asks 「こちらでお召し上がりですか|こちらでおめしあがりですか」, will you be eating here? That is the very polite way of asking for here or to go. When the food arrives, a waiter might set it down and say 「こちら、ラーメンです」, here is your ramen. On the phone or in an email, a company refers to itself as this side: 「こちらこそ」 means the pleasure is ours, and a person might say 「こちらは田中です|こちらはたなかです」, this is Tanaka speaking. You only need to understand the fancy shop phrases, not produce them. But recognizing this word helps you follow what is happening. So: the polite word that guides you through shops and phone calls." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Picture a very polite butler in white gloves at the door of a fancy hotel. He bows deeply, sweeps his open palm toward the lobby, and with each word he points: this way, please. Now notice his hand. He never points a finger at you, that would be rude. He gestures with his whole open hand toward the direction. That is exactly what this word does in language. Instead of pointing at a person with 「これ」, you gesture toward their direction. Now imagine the butler's name is Coach Ira, and every guest who arrives says, thank you, Coach Ira. The sound-alike gets you close to the word. So when you introduce someone, picture Coach Ira sweeping his gloved hand toward your friend, saying this way, this person. So: the polite open hand toward this side." }
    ],
    short: [
      "As the polite this way member of the 「こ」, 「そ」, 「あ」, 「ど」 family, it lets you point toward a direction instead of at someone, so introduce people with 「こちらは」, never 「これは」.",
      "It is the polite top rung of a ladder, plain 「ここ」, casual 「こっち」 with its small pause, then this word, and the whole family follows the same three levels.",
      "Shops use it constantly, from 「こちらへどうぞ」, this way please, to 「こちらでお召し上がりですか|こちらでおめしあがりですか」, eating here, and on the phone it means our side.",
      "Picture a polite butler named Coach Ira sweeping his open gloved hand toward the lobby, never pointing a finger, which is exactly how this word gestures toward a person."
    ],
    ja: "[こちら]は{田中|たなか}さんです。",
    en: "This is Mr. Tanaka." },
  "うそをつく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A set phrase made of two pieces. 「うそ」 is a lie. The verb 「つく」 is the one Japanese uses for telling lies. You do not say a lie or talk a lie in Japanese, you 「つく」 one. There is a kanji for lie, but it is usually written in kana, so learn it that way. Add 「つき」 to the end and you get 「うそつき」, a liar, which children shout at each other on the playground. You will also hear 「うそ」 on its own all the time, but not as an accusation. When a friend tells you surprising news, you say 「うそ！」 or 「うそでしょう」, meaning no way, you are kidding. It is like saying shut up in English to mean I cannot believe it. So do not be hurt if someone says it to your good news. The past form is 「うそをつきました」, someone lied. A way to remember it: picture someone poking the truth with a stick, a little jab, and that jab is the 「つく」. So: lie, plus the verb for telling it." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb here, 「つく」, is a regular u-verb, so it follows the same pattern as 「書く|かく」. The te-form is 「ついて」, and the plain negative is 「つかない」. That lets you plug it into patterns you know. To say do not lie, use the request form: 「うそをつかないでください」, please do not lie. To give a rule, use the must not pattern from lesson six: 「うそをついてはいけません」, you must not lie, which is what parents and teachers say. To confess, use the past: 「うそをつきました。すみません」, I lied, sorry. And to describe a habit, use the noun form: 「彼はうそつきです|かれはうそつきです」, he is a liar. Notice the particle 「を」 marks the lie, as the thing being told. If you want to say who you lied to, use 「に」: 「母にうそをついた|ははにうそをついた」, I lied to my mom. So: a simple u-verb, ready for every pattern." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese has two famous sayings about lying that pull in opposite directions. The first is 「うそも方便|うそもほうべん」, a lie can also be a means. It comes from Buddhist teaching, the idea that a small untruth can be justified if it helps someone. People use it to excuse a white lie, like praising a gift you do not really like. The second is stricter, and every child hears it: 「うそつきはどろぼうの始まり|うそつきはどろぼうのはじまり」, a liar is the beginning of a thief. In other words, lying today leads to stealing tomorrow. Together they show a realistic attitude: honesty matters, but social harmony matters too, and a gentle white lie to spare feelings is widely accepted. So when someone says your Japanese is excellent, you can smile and wonder which saying they had in mind. So: two sayings, one strict and one forgiving." },
      { title: "True or false", text: "The opposite of a lie is 「本当|ほんとう」, the truth, or really. These two words often appear side by side in conversation. When a friend tells you something surprising, you might say 「本当|ほんとう」 or the casual 「ほんと」 with rising intonation, really? Or you can say 「うそ」, no way. Both mean roughly the same thing as a reaction: I can hardly believe it. Then the friend answers 「本当だよ|ほんとうだよ」, it is true, or 「うそだよ」, just kidding, I made it up. A useful pattern is 「本当に|ほんとうに」, really, truly, as in 「本当にありがとう|ほんとうにありがとう」, thank you so much. And when you want to insist you are not lying, say 「うそじゃないよ」, I am not lying. So learn the pair together: the lie, and the truth, and you can react to almost any surprising news." }
    ],
    short: [
      "You do not say or tell a lie in Japanese, you 「つく」 one, like poking the truth with a stick, and 「うそ！」 alone is a friendly no way rather than an accusation.",
      "「つく」 is a regular u-verb like 「書く|かく」, so it slots into patterns like 「うそをつかないでください」 and 「うそをついてはいけません」, with 「に」 for the person lied to.",
      "Two sayings pull opposite ways, the forgiving 「うそも方便|うそもほうべん」, a lie can be a means, and the strict 「うそつきはどろぼうの始まり|うそつきはどろぼうのはじまり」, a liar is the beginning of a thief.",
      "Its opposite is 「本当|ほんとう」, the truth, and both 「ほんと」 and 「うそ」 work as reactions to surprising news, while 「うそじゃないよ」 insists you are not lying."
    ],
    ja: "{弟|おとうと}は[うそをつきました|うそをつきました|うそをつく]。",
    en: "My little brother told a lie." },
  "おなかがすく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A set phrase made of three pieces. 「おなか」 is your stomach or belly, which you will also meet as a body word in lesson twelve. 「が」 marks it as the subject. And 「すく」 means to become empty. So the literal picture is your stomach empties out. That is how Japanese says to get hungry. The grammar is the tricky part. Because 「すく」 is about a change, becoming empty, Japanese uses the past form to say you are hungry right now: 「おなかがすきました」, my stomach has emptied out, I am hungry. Or the ongoing form, 「おなかがすいています」. A common mistake is to say 「おなかがすきます」, which sounds like a general fact or a future, instead of right now. The same logic applies to 「つかれる」, to get tired, also in this lesson. In casual speech people often say 「おなかすいた」, dropping the 「が」. A cute extra: the sound for a rumbling, empty stomach is 「ペコペコ」. So: belly becomes empty, and you say it in the past." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Genki writes this phrase in kana, but both parts have kanji you will see in daily life. The belly is 「お腹|おなか」. The kanji has the flesh radical on the left, a squeezed form of 「月|つき」 that in many body kanji actually comes from meat or flesh, not the moon. The verb 「すく」 is written 「空く|すく」, and that is a kanji you already know: 「空|そら」, the sky, and 「空港|くうこう」, the airport. Its core idea is empty, open space. The sky is the big empty space above you, and an empty stomach is empty space inside you. The same kanji shows up when a train or a restaurant is not crowded: 「電車が空いています|でんしゃがすいています」, the train is empty, there are seats. So the very same verb works for a quiet train and a hungry belly. So: the flesh radical for the belly, and the sky kanji for empty." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese describes a lot of body states with this same shape: a body part, 「が」, and then what it does. You will meet several. 「のどがかわく」, to get thirsty, is literally the throat dries out. Like this one, it describes a change, so you say 「のどがかわきました」, I am thirsty. When you have eaten enough, you say 「おなかがいっぱいです」, my stomach is full. And you already know 「頭がいい|あたまがいい」, smart, literally the head is good, and 「背が高い|せがたかい」, tall, literally the back is tall, from lesson seven. Once you see the pattern, you can describe your body in a natural Japanese way, instead of translating I am hungry word for word. A handy pair to remember for any meal: before, 「おなかがすいた」, and after, 「おなかがいっぱい」. So: body part, 「が」, and what that body part is doing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In daily life, saying you are hungry is often a gentle way of suggesting food. Friends walking around town might say 「おなかすいたね」, we are getting hungry, huh? That is really an invitation: shall we eat something? The natural reply is 「うん、何か食べよう|うん、なにかたべよう」, yeah, let us eat something. Children say it nonstop when they get home from school. You may also hear a rougher version from men, using a different word for belly, 「腹|はら」, and a different verb: 「腹へった|はらへった」, I am starving. It is very casual and a bit masculine, so recognize it but use it carefully. To be polite with a host, you can say 「少しおなかがすきました|すこしおなかがすきました」, I am a little hungry, which sounds modest. Remember the opposite too: when someone offers more food and you are full, 「もうおなかがいっぱいです」. So: the stomach empties, and dinner plans begin." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Literally your belly becomes empty, and since 「すく」 is a change, you use the past form 「おなかがすきました」 or 「おなかがすいています」 to say you are hungry right now.",
      "The belly is 「お腹|おなか」 with the flesh radical, and 「すく」 is 「空く|すく」, the empty sky kanji, the same verb used for a train that is not crowded.",
      "Many body states use body part, 「が」, then what it does, like 「のどがかわく」, to get thirsty, and the handy meal pair is 「おなかがすいた」 before and 「おなかがいっぱい」 after.",
      "Saying 「おなかすいたね」 is really a gentle invitation to go eat, while the rough masculine 「腹へった|はらへった」 is something to recognize but use carefully."
    ],
    ja: "[おなかがすきました|おなかがすきました|おなかがすく]。なにかたべたいです。",
    en: "I am hungry. I want to eat something." },
  "かう|飼う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is only for keeping animals, raising a pet or livestock. The tricky part is that it sounds exactly like 「買う|かう」, to buy, from lesson four. In speech you tell them apart by context: 「犬を飼う|いぬをかう」 is to keep a dog, and 「犬を買う|いぬをかう」 would be buying one at a store. In writing the kanji solves it. Look at the left side of this kanji: it is the eating radical, a squeezed version of 「食|しょく」, food, the same one in 「食べる|たべる」. That fits nicely, because keeping an animal is mostly about feeding it. To say you have a pet now, use the ongoing form: 「犬を飼っています|いぬをかっています」, I have a dog. That is how you talk about a pet you keep. It goes with 「ペット」, a pet, from lesson fifteen, and with 「猫|ねこ」 and 「犬|いぬ」. A way to remember it: the food radical means feeding. So: same sound as buy, but with food in the kanji, keeping an animal." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is the verb you use when people chat about pets. The natural question is 「何か飼っていますか|なにかかっていますか」, do you have any pets? Literally, are you keeping anything? And a natural answer: 「猫を二匹飼っています|ねこをにひきかっています」, I have two cats. Notice 「匹|ひき」, the counter for small animals like cats, dogs and fish. It changes sound with some numbers: 「一匹|いっぴき」, 「三匹|さんびき」. This is a u-verb, so it conjugates just like 「買う|かう」, to buy: 「飼います|かいます」, 「飼って|かって」, 「飼わない|かわない」. That means the spoken forms of the two verbs are identical in every shape. If you want to say you would like a pet, use the 「たい」 form: 「犬を飼いたいです|いぬをかいたいです」, I want to have a dog. And you will often see 「ペット可|ペットか」 or no pets signs on apartment listings. So: the verb for every pet conversation." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you have this verb, you can build several related words. Use the stem, 「飼い|かい」, and add something after it. 「飼い主|かいぬし」 is a pet owner, literally the keeping master. 「飼い犬|かいいぬ」 is a pet dog, someone's dog, and 「飼い猫|かいねこ」 a pet cat. The opposite of a pet is a stray, 「野良|のら」, so a stray cat is 「野良猫|のらねこ」, and you will spot them all over Japanese towns and temples. There is also a famous saying: 「飼い犬に手をかまれる|かいいぬにてをかまれる」, to be bitten on the hand by your own dog. It means to be betrayed by someone you trusted and took care of. Now look at the right side of the kanji: that shape is the same one in the second kanji of 「寿司|すし」, sushi. Here it mainly helps with the sound in Chinese. So: keeping, owners, pet dogs, and a dog that bites the hand that feeds it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Pets are very popular in Japan, and today there are more pet cats and dogs than children under fifteen. But keeping one is not always easy. Many apartments forbid pets, so people check listings carefully for pet friendly buildings. Small dogs are especially popular, and you will see them carried in bags or pushed in little strollers. For people who cannot keep a cat at home, there are cat cafes, where you pay by the hour to sit with cats and have a drink. Japan also has some very famous animals. Hachiko, the loyal dog who waited at Shibuya station for years after his owner died, has a statue there that is a classic meeting spot. And a cat once served as an honorary stationmaster at a small rural train station. So a nation of apartment dwellers has found many creative ways to live with animals, even without keeping them." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It sounds exactly like 「買う|かう」, to buy, but its kanji has the eating radical on the left because keeping an animal is mostly about feeding it, and 「犬を飼っています|いぬをかっています」 means I have a dog.",
      "Ask 「何か飼っていますか|なにかかっていますか」 to find out about pets, and count small animals with 「匹|ひき」, as in 「猫を二匹飼っています|ねこをにひきかっています」.",
      "The stem builds words like 「飼い主|かいぬし」, a pet owner, and 「飼い犬|かいいぬ」, a pet dog, plus the saying about being bitten by your own dog, meaning betrayal by someone you cared for.",
      "Pets outnumber children under fifteen in Japan, yet many apartments forbid them, so people find creative ways to be with animals, like cat cafes and the famous loyal dog Hachiko."
    ],
    ja: "{犬|いぬ}を[飼っています|かっています|かう]。",
    en: "I have a dog." },
  "サボる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a fun one. It comes from the French word sabotage, which Japanese borrowed as 「サボタージュ」, meaning a worker slowdown or strike. People chopped it down to just the first two sounds, 「サボ」, and then stuck the Japanese verb ending 「る」 on the end. Now it works like a normal verb, and it means to skip, to slack off, especially to skip class or work. Because it is a borrowed word, it is written in katakana, with hiragana for the endings, like 「サボります」 and 「サボりました」. It conjugates as a regular u-verb, not an ru-verb, so the te-form is 「サボって」. It is casual, and a little naughty. You would say it with friends, not to your teacher. Japanese makes verbs from borrowed words this way now and then, and the same trick turns google into a slangy verb for searching online. A typical sentence: 「授業をサボりました|じゅぎょうをサボりました」, I skipped class. A way to remember it: you are sabotaging your own grades. So: French sabotage, shortened, turned into a verb for skipping." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word belongs to a playful group of verbs made by chopping a borrowed word and adding 「る」. They are all u-verbs, and all fairly casual. 「メモる」 is to jot down a memo, from memo. 「ミスる」 is to make a mistake, from miss. 「ダブる」 is to overlap or to be doubled, from double, and students also use it to mean to repeat a school year. 「ハモる」 is to sing in harmony. And as the main lesson mentioned, there is one from google for searching online. The noun form is handy too. Take the stem, 「サボり」, and it means skipping or slacking: 「サボりはだめ」, no slacking! A related native word is 「ずる休み|ずるやすみ」, faking a sick day, staying home when you are not really sick. So: a whole family of loanword verbs, all with the little 「る」 tail, all best used with friends." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Because it ends in 「る」, it is easy to treat this as a ru-verb, but it is a u-verb. The difference shows up in every form. The polite form is 「サボります」, with 「り」 before 「ます」. The negative is 「サボらない」, not dropping the 「る」. The te-form is 「サボって」, with the small 「っ」. Compare 「食べる|たべる」, a real ru-verb, which becomes 「食べない|たべない」 and 「食べて|たべて」. Here is a quick test: almost all loanword verbs made with 「る」 are u-verbs, so you can conjugate them like 「帰る|かえる」. You can combine it with patterns from your lessons. 「授業をサボってはいけません|じゅぎょうをサボってはいけません」, you must not skip class. 「サボらないでください」, please do not skip. And a casual confession among friends: 「今日サボっちゃった|きょうサボっちゃった」, I ended up skipping today. So: looks like a ru-verb, behaves like a u-verb." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, perfect attendance is genuinely celebrated. Many schools give an award at the end of the year to students who never missed a day, and some adults proudly remember earning it every year. Showing up, even when you are tired, is seen as a sign of good character and commitment to the group. That makes skipping feel a little more rebellious than it might in English. At the same time, everyone knows the reality: university students in particular are famous for skipping lectures, especially the first period in the morning, and plenty of office workers quietly slack off when the boss is out. Using this verb is a lighthearted, slightly guilty way to admit it. If you say it about yourself with a grin, people will laugh. If you say it about a coworker to the boss, that is another story. So: a playful word in a culture that admires showing up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the French word sabotage chopped down to 「サボ」 with the Japanese verb ending 「る」 added, a casual, slightly naughty u-verb for skipping class or work, like sabotaging your own grades.",
      "It belongs to a playful family of loanword verbs with a 「る」 tail, like 「メモる」, 「ミスる」 and 「ハモる」, and its noun form 「サボり」 means slacking.",
      "It looks like a ru-verb but behaves as a u-verb, so it becomes 「サボります」, 「サボらない」 and 「サボって」, like nearly every loanword verb made with 「る」.",
      "Japan celebrates perfect attendance, which makes skipping feel a bit rebellious, so using this verb about yourself with a grin is a lighthearted, slightly guilty confession."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は{授業|じゅぎょう}を[サボりました|サボりました|サボる]。",
    en: "I skipped class today." },
  "とる|取る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the general, everyday take. You take a class, 「クラスを取る|クラスをとる」, get a grade, grab something off a shelf, or pass something over: 「塩を取ってください|しおをとってください」, please pass the salt. There are several verbs pronounced 「とる」, and Japanese uses a different kanji for each kind of taking. For photos it is 「撮る|とる」, from lesson four and in 「写真を撮る|しゃしんをとる」 in this lesson. This one is the all-purpose version. Now the kanji. On the left is 「耳|みみ」, ear. On the right is a simple shape for a hand. The traditional explanation is a little grim: in ancient China, soldiers took the ear of a defeated enemy as proof, so a hand grabbing an ear meant to take. Whether or not you like the story, it is a memorable picture: a hand reaching out and taking hold. Another handy phrase is 「年を取る|としをとる」, to get older, literally to take on years. So: a hand grabbing an ear, the everyday verb for taking." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb is a workhorse, and it pairs with many nouns in fixed phrases. Learn them as chunks. 「メモを取る|メモをとる」, to take notes. 「休みを取る|やすみをとる」, to take a day off. 「免許を取る|めんきょをとる」, to get a license, like a driver's license. 「連絡を取る|れんらくをとる」, to get in touch with someone. 「点を取る|てんをとる」, to score points, as on a test, which connects to the counter for points in this lesson. And for school, 「いい成績を取る|いいせいせきをとる」, to get good grades. At the table, it is the polite way to ask for something out of reach: 「しょうゆを取ってください|しょうゆをとってください」, could you pass the soy sauce? Notice how many of these are about getting or obtaining something, not just grabbing it physically. A common mistake is to use the verb for photos; for those, the kanji is different. So: one verb, many useful set phrases." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Even though it ends in 「る」, this is a u-verb, one of the sneaky ones. The polite form is 「取ります|とります」, the negative 「取らない|とらない」, and the te-form 「取って|とって」, with the small 「っ」. You can tell because the vowel before 「る」 is an o sound, not an e or i sound, and ru-verbs always have e or i there. The te-form unlocks some very natural combinations. 「取ってくる|とってくる」 means to go and get something and come back: 「本を取ってきます|ほんをとってきます」, I will go get my book. Another is the purpose pattern, going somewhere to get something: 「本を取りに行きます|ほんをとりにいきます」. You can also join it to other verbs: 「取り出す|とりだす」, to take something out, as from a bag. So: an o before the 「る」, which means it is a u-verb." },
      { title: "Many kinds of taking", text: "Japanese has several verbs pronounced 「とる」, each with its own kanji, and they show how carefully the language separates kinds of taking. The one in this lesson is the general one, used for everyday taking and getting. For photos, it is 「撮る|とる」, which you already know. For catching animals, fish, or a ball, it is 「捕る|とる」: in baseball, a fielder catches a fly ball with this one. For picking or gathering, like collecting mushrooms in the forest or hiring new staff, it is 「採る|とる」. You do not need to write the last two yet, but it helps to know they exist. When you hear the sound, the meaning comes from context, and when you are unsure how to write it, this general kanji is usually the safest choice. So: one sound, several kanji, and this one is the everyday all-rounder." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the all-purpose take, and its kanji is a hand grabbing 「耳|みみ」, an ear, from the grim old story of soldiers taking ears as proof, while photos use a different kanji, 「撮る|とる」.",
      "It is a workhorse in set phrases about obtaining things, like 「メモを取る|メモをとる」, 「休みを取る|やすみをとる」 and 「いい成績を取る|いいせいせきをとる」, so learn them as chunks.",
      "With an o sound before 「る」 it must be a u-verb, so its te-form 「取って|とって」 unlocks combinations like 「取ってくる|とってくる」, to go get something and come back.",
      "Several verbs share this sound with different kanji, for photos, catching and gathering, but this general one is the everyday all-rounder and usually the safest choice."
    ],
    ja: "{日本語|にほんご}のクラスを[取っています|とっています|とる]。",
    en: "I am taking a Japanese class." },
  "ならう|習う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb means to learn from someone, to take lessons. You learn piano, karate or cooking from a teacher: 「母に料理を習いました|ははにりょうりをならいました」. Notice the particle 「に」 for the person you learn from. Compare it with 「勉強する|べんきょうする」, which is studying on your own, with books and homework. And with 「覚える|おぼえる」, from lesson nine, which is memorizing, getting something into your head. A common mistake is using 「習う|ならう」 for studying alone at your desk. Now the kanji. The top is 「羽|はね」, wings. The traditional reading of the shape is a young bird beating its wings over and over, practicing until it can fly. That idea of repetition is the core of the kanji. You can see it in two words you know: 「練習|れんしゅう」, practice, from lesson ten, and 「習慣|しゅうかん」, a habit, from lesson fifteen, where it is read 「しゅう」. Both are about doing something again and again. So: little wings flapping, learning from a teacher by practice." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese children are famously busy after school, and a big reason is 「習い事|ならいごと」, lessons, the things you learn from a teacher outside regular school. The word is built from this verb's stem plus 「事|こと」, thing. Popular choices include swimming, piano, English conversation, and calligraphy, 「書道|しょどう」. Many kids also learn the abacus, 「そろばん」, which builds amazing mental math skills. Older kids add cram school, 「塾|じゅく」, to prepare for entrance exams. Adults take lessons too: tea ceremony, flower arranging, and cooking classes are classic hobbies. A natural question to ask a Japanese friend is 「何か習っていますか|なにかならっていますか」, are you taking any lessons? And they might answer 「ピアノを習っています|ピアノをならっています」, I take piano lessons. Notice the ongoing form for something you do regularly. So: a whole culture of after school lessons, built on this one verb." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a u-verb ending in 「う」, and that group has one special twist. In the negative, the 「う」 does not become 「あ」. It becomes 「わ」. So the plain negative is 「習わない|ならわない」, not an a sound. You already know this from 「買う|かう」, which becomes 「買わない|かわない」, and 「会う|あう」, which becomes 「会わない|あわない」. The te-form is 「習って|ならって」, with the small 「っ」. The person you learn from can take 「に」 or 「から」: 「先生に習う|せんせいにならう」 or 「先生から習う|せんせいからならう」, both meaning to learn from the teacher. The thing you learn takes 「を」. You can also say you went somewhere to learn: 「料理を習いに行きます|りょうりをならいにいきます」, I go to learn cooking. And to talk about wanting to learn, use the 「たい」 form: 「空手を習いたいです|からてをならいたいです」. So: watch for the 「わ」 in the negative." },
      { title: "A famous saying", text: "Japanese has a well known proverb that uses this verb: 「習うより慣れよ|ならうよりなれよ」. Word by word, it says rather than learning, get used to it. The idea is that you master a skill by doing it again and again, not just by being taught. Listening to a teacher explain how to ride a bike only gets you so far. You learn by wobbling around the parking lot until your body knows how. The verb at the end, 「慣れる|なれる」, means to get used to something, and you will hear it a lot as a learner: 「日本の生活に慣れましたか|にほんのせいかつになれましたか」, have you gotten used to life in Japan? This saying fits nicely with the kanji, which is all about repeated practice. It is also good advice for language study: your teacher can explain a pattern, but you only own it after using it many times. So: learn from a teacher, then practice until it is yours." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means learning from a teacher, with 「に」 for the person, unlike studying alone, and its kanji has 「羽|はね」, wings, like a young bird flapping again and again to practice.",
      "Japanese kids are busy with 「習い事|ならいごと」, after-school lessons like piano or swimming, so a natural question is 「何か習っていますか|なにかならっていますか」, are you taking any lessons?",
      "As a u-verb ending in 「う」, its negative uses 「わ」, giving 「習わない|ならわない」, just like 「買わない|かわない」, so watch for that twist.",
      "The proverb 「習うより慣れよ|ならうよりなれよ」 says rather than learning, get used to it, meaning you truly master a skill by practicing it again and again."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}にりょうりを[習いました|ならいました|ならう]。",
    en: "I learned cooking from my mother." },
  "のぼる|登る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb means to climb, to go up something with some effort: a mountain, a tree, a tall staircase. The thing you climb takes the particle 「に」: 「山に登る|やまにのぼる」, to climb a mountain. You can also use 「を」 when you mean climbing along a path, but 「に」 is the safe choice for now. The same kanji appears in 「登山|とざん」, mountain climbing as a hobby, where it is read 「と」 and the mountain kanji is read 「ざん」. A cultural note: every summer, many people climb Mount Fuji overnight to watch the sunrise from the top. There is a saying that a wise person climbs Fuji once, and only a fool climbs it twice. A way to remember the verb: picture your legs pushing up the slope. Its opposite, coming down, is 「降りる|おりる」, from lesson six. So: climbing up, usually to a mountain with 「に」." },
      { title: "Three kinds of up", text: "There are three common kanji for the verb 「のぼる」, and each paints a different kind of going up. This one, 「登る|のぼる」, is climbing with effort, using your arms and legs, like a mountain, a tree, or a ladder. A second one, 「上る|のぼる」, uses the simple up kanji, the same as in 「上|うえ」. It is for going up in a more general way: up the stairs, up a slope, or upstream on a river. A third, 「昇る|のぼる」, is for rising smoothly, like the sun coming up in the morning, or an elevator rising. So a sunrise uses the third, climbing Mount Fuji uses this one, and walking up a station staircase can use the second. In everyday writing people often mix up the first two, but for mountains, this kanji is the classic choice. So: one sound, three pictures of going up, and this one is the hard climb." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the top of the kanji. That spreading shape is a radical that represents two feet, set apart as if stepping or straddling. You will see it again in 「発|はつ」, as in 「出発|しゅっぱつ」, departure, where it also suggests setting off on foot. The bottom of the kanji looks exactly like 「豆|まめ」, bean. Most scholars say that in this kanji, the shape originally showed a tall stand or vessel rather than an actual bean, so the old picture was feet stepping up onto something raised. The details are debated, so take that as background rather than certainty. For memory, a beginner can enjoy a trick: picture two feet standing on top of a giant bean, scrambling to stay balanced as they climb. Besides the native reading, you will see 「と」 in 「登山|とざん」 and 「とう」 in 「登場|とうじょう」, to appear on stage. So: two feet at the top, climbing onto something tall." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Even though it ends in 「る」, this is a u-verb. The vowel before 「る」 is an o sound, which ru-verbs never have, so you can tell right away. The polite form is 「登ります|のぼります」, the negative 「登らない|のぼらない」, and the te-form 「登って|のぼって」. It works beautifully with the experience pattern from lesson eleven: 「富士山に登ったことがありますか|ふじさんにのぼったことがありますか」, have you ever climbed Mount Fuji? And a natural answer: 「いいえ、まだです|いいえ、まだです」, no, not yet. You can also use the 「たい」 form for dreams: 「いつか富士山に登りたいです|いつかふじさんにのぼりたいです」, someday I want to climb Mount Fuji. Remember the mountain takes 「に」, as the goal you are climbing to. And the opposite verb for coming back down a mountain is 「下りる|おりる」, written with the down kanji. So: an o before 「る」, and a u-verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means climbing with effort, and the thing you climb takes 「に」, as in 「山に登る|やまにのぼる」, with the same kanji read 「と」 in 「登山|とざん」, mountain climbing.",
      "Of three kanji for this sound, 「上る|のぼる」 is general going up and 「昇る|のぼる」 is smooth rising like the sun, but this one is the hard climb, the classic for mountains.",
      "The top of the kanji is two feet stepping and the bottom looks like 「豆|まめ」, bean, so picture two feet scrambling up onto a giant bean.",
      "The o sound before 「る」 marks it as a u-verb, and it pairs nicely with the experience pattern, 「富士山に登ったことがありますか|ふじさんにのぼったことがありますか」."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と{山|やま}に[登りました|のぼりました|のぼる]。",
    en: "I climbed a mountain with a friend." },
  "はしる|走る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese verb, meaning to run. Even though it ends in 「る」, it is a u-verb, so the te-form is 「走って|はしって」, not a ru-verb shape. This is one of the classic exceptions, like 「帰る|かえる」 and 「入る|はいる」. Now the kanji. In its old forms, the top was a person swinging their arms, and the bottom was a foot. Today the top looks like soil and the bottom part looks like the lower half of 「足|あし」, foot, from lesson twelve. So the kanji is a moving body over a foot, running. Its slower partner is 「歩く|あるく」, to walk, also from lesson twelve, and you already know 「歩いて|あるいて」, on foot. With places, 「を」 marks where you run through: 「公園を走る|こうえんをはしる」, to run in the park. It is not only for people. Trains and cars 「走る|はしる」 too, which is how Japanese says a train runs on a line. A way to remember it: arms swinging, feet pounding. So: a body over a foot, running." },
      { title: "Hidden in thanks", text: "This kanji hides inside a phrase you say after every meal: 「ごちそうさまでした」. Written in full, the key part is 「ご馳走|ごちそう」, a feast or a treat. The second kanji there is this one, running, read 「そう」. The first kanji means to gallop, like a horse. So a feast is literally galloping and running around. The traditional explanation is that in the old days, preparing a fine meal for a guest meant a host had to run all over, on foot or on horseback, to gather the ingredients. So after eating, you thank the cook for all that running around on your behalf. The word 「ごちそう」 on its own also means a treat or a delicious spread, and 「ごちそうする」 means to treat someone to a meal. So the next time you say thank you after dinner, picture the host racing around town for your food. So: running, hidden in the thank you for a meal." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Running has a special place in Japanese culture, and the biggest example is the 「駅伝|えきでん」, a long distance relay race. The name comes from the old relay stations where messengers handed off along the main roads. Teams of runners pass a cloth sash from one runner to the next instead of a baton. The most famous is the Hakone Ekiden, held every year on the second and third of January. University teams run from Tokyo to the mountain town of Hakone and back over two days, and millions of people watch it on television during the New Year holiday. Spectators line the roads waving flags in the cold. City marathons are also hugely popular, and the Tokyo Marathon draws enormous numbers of applicants. Many people run in parks or along rivers before work. So in Japan, running is not just exercise, it is a New Year tradition." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The te-form, 「走って|はしって」, opens up some very useful combinations. Add 「いく」 or 「くる」 and you show the direction of the running. 「走っていく|はしっていく」 is to run off, away from here. 「走ってくる|はしってくる」 is to come running toward here: a dog that sees you coming home 「走ってきます|はしってきます」, comes running. You can also turn the verb into a noun with 「の」 to talk about liking it: 「走るのが好きです|はしるのがすきです」, I like running. And with the te-form for a request, a teacher at school might say 「廊下を走らないでください|ろうかをはしらないでください」, please do not run in the hallway, which is a sign you will see in every Japanese school. Notice 「を」 for the place you run through, the hallway. So: add come or go to show which way you run, and add 「の」 to talk about running as an activity." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Its kanji is a moving body over a foot, and although it ends in 「る」 it is a u-verb with the te-form 「走って|はしって」, and trains run too.",
      "This kanji hides in 「ご馳走|ごちそう」, a feast, because a host once had to gallop and run all over to gather food, so 「ごちそうさまでした」 thanks them for that running.",
      "The 「駅伝|えきでん」 relay race is a New Year tradition in Japan, especially the Hakone Ekiden that millions watch on television in early January.",
      "Add 「いく」 or 「くる」 to the te-form to show direction, as in 「走ってくる|はしってくる」, to come running, and use 「の」 to talk about running as an activity."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}{公園|こうえん}を[走ります|はしります|はしる]。",
    en: "I run in the park every morning." },
  "つかれる|疲れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb means to get tired. It is a ru-verb, and like 「おなかがすく」, it describes a change: you become tired. So to say you are tired right now, use the past: 「疲れました|つかれました」, I have gotten tired, I am tired. Or the ongoing form, 「疲れています|つかれています」. A common mistake is to say 「疲れます|つかれます」, which sounds like a general fact, this job tires me out, rather than how you feel now. You will hear the casual 「疲れた|つかれた」 a lot, said with a sigh at the end of a long day. Now the kanji. On the outside is the sickness radical, a slanted roof shape with two little dots on the left. It is the same one as in 「病気|びょうき」, illness, from lesson twelve, and it marks many words about the body feeling bad. A way to remember it: being tired is a mini sickness, the body under a heavy roof. At work, people say 「お疲れ様です|おつかれさまです」 to each other, a thank you for your hard work. So: the sickness roof, and you say it in the past." },
      { title: "Work greeting", text: "The phrase 「お疲れ様です|おつかれさまです」 is one of the most used phrases in any Japanese workplace, and it does far more than thank someone. Coworkers say it when they pass in the hallway, almost like hello. They say it at the start of a phone call or an email to a colleague. And when someone leaves for the day, the people staying behind say 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」, in the past, you have worked hard. Friends and students shorten it to just 「おつかれ」. Here is a subtle point. A similar phrase, 「ご苦労様|ごくろうさま」, also praises hard work, but it is traditionally said from a higher person to a lower one. So many people feel it is rude to say it to your boss. The phrase with this verb is the safe choice for everyone. So: the tired word that became the glue of office life." },
      { title: "Shaking it off", text: "The noun form of this verb is 「疲れ|つかれ」, tiredness or fatigue, and Japanese talks about it almost like a substance that builds up and then gets removed. When you have been working too hard, 「疲れがたまる|つかれがたまる」, tiredness piles up. After a good rest, 「疲れが取れる|つかれがとれる」, the tiredness is taken away. That second phrase uses the same kanji as 「取る|とる」, to take, from this lesson. So what removes tiredness in Japan? A long soak in a hot spring, an 「温泉|おんせん」, is the classic answer. A deep bath at home in the evening is a daily habit for many families for exactly this reason. Massage chairs at hot spring inns, a cold drink after the bath, and an early night all help. A friend might ask 「疲れは取れましたか|つかれはとれましたか」, has your tiredness gone away? So: tiredness piles up, and then the bath takes it away." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a ru-verb, so the forms are regular: 「疲れない|つかれない」, 「疲れて|つかれて」. The te-form is very handy for giving a reason. 「疲れて、寝ました|つかれて、ねました」, I was tired, so I went to sleep. Or with 「から」: 「疲れたから、休みます|つかれたから、やすみます」, I am tired, so I will rest. To describe a person who is tired right now, use the ongoing form before a noun: 「疲れている人|つかれているひと」, a tired person. And to describe something tiring as a general fact, use the plain present: 「この仕事は疲れる|このしごとはつかれる」, this job is tiring. That is where the present tense really fits. Compare the three: 「疲れた|つかれた」, I am tired now; 「疲れている|つかれている」, I am in a tired state; 「疲れる|つかれる」, it tires me out in general. So: past for now, present for in general." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Getting tired is a change, so say 「疲れました|つかれました」 for how you feel now, and the kanji wears the sickness radical, the body under a heavy roof.",
      "「お疲れ様です|おつかれさまです」 is the glue of office life, used like hello and goodbye, and unlike 「ご苦労様|ごくろうさま」, it is safe to say to anyone, including your boss.",
      "Japanese treats tiredness like a substance, so 「疲れがたまる|つかれがたまる」, it piles up, and after a hot spring soak, 「疲れが取れる|つかれがとれる」, it is taken away.",
      "Use the past 「疲れた|つかれた」 for being tired now and the plain present 「疲れる|つかれる」 for something tiring in general, like 「この仕事は疲れる|このしごとはつかれる」."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}はとても[疲れました|つかれました|つかれる]。",
    en: "I got very tired today." },
  "やめる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb means to quit or to stop doing something. It is a ru-verb, and the thing you stop takes 「を」: 「たばこをやめる」, to quit smoking, 「アルバイトをやめる」, to quit a part time job. It can be written with kanji, and which one depends on the meaning: one for quitting a job, another for stopping an action. Genki uses kana, which is safe for both. You will also hear it on its own a lot. 「やめて」 or 「やめてください」 means stop it, please stop, and 「やめましょう」 means let us not do it, let us call it off. How is it different from 「止まる|とまる」 or 「止める|とめる」? Those are about stopping movement, like a car stopping, while this one is about giving up an activity or a habit. A common mistake is using it to say a bus stopped. A way to remember it: when you quit something, you say yikes, I am done, and walk away. So: giving up an activity, with 「を」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Genki writes this in kana, but you will see two kanji for it, and they split the meaning neatly. For quitting a job, a school, or a position, it is 「辞める|やめる」. That kanji is the same one as in 「辞書|じしょ」, dictionary, where it means words. So 「会社を辞める|かいしゃをやめる」 is to quit your company. For stopping an action or a habit, like smoking or a plan, it is 「止める|やめる」. That is the kanji for stop, the same one you see on red stop signs, 「止まれ|とまれ」. Be careful, because the same kanji with okurigana 「める」 can also be read 「とめる」, to stop something like a car. Context and the meaning tell you which reading is meant. Since it is easy to mix these up, writing it in kana is perfectly acceptable, and many native speakers do. So: the dictionary kanji for quitting a job, and the stop kanji for stopping a habit." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "To quit doing an action, you need to turn a verb into a noun first, and the tool for that is 「の」. Put the plain form before it: 「たばこを吸うのをやめる|たばこをすうのをやめる」, to stop smoking, or 「お菓子を食べるのをやめました|おかしをたべるのをやめました」, I stopped eating sweets. You cannot attach this verb straight to another verb, so the 「の」 is essential. This is a ru-verb, so the forms are regular: 「やめない」, 「やめて」, 「やめた」. The volitional form is very common in conversation: 「やめよう」, let us not, let us call it off. And there is a useful phrase for changing your mind: 「やっぱりやめた」, actually, never mind, I changed my mind. You hear that when someone almost orders dessert and then thinks better of it. So: verb, plus 「のを」, plus this verb, to quit doing something." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a mini scene. A little boy is poking his sister. She shouts 「やめて」, stop it! He keeps going. She gets louder: 「やめてよ」, I said stop! Their mother turns around and says 「やめなさい」, stop that, which is the firm form parents use with children. Notice the ladder of strength. 「やめてください」 is a polite request. 「やめて」 is casual, between friends or family. 「やめなさい」 is an instruction from someone in charge. A related word, 「だめ」, means no, not allowed, and it is often said together: 「だめ、やめて」. As an adult, you might use the polite request if a stranger is bothering you. And at work, 「仕事を辞めました|しごとをやめました」, I quit my job, is serious news, often followed by a question about what you will do next. So: one verb that ranges from a playground shout to a career change." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means giving up an activity or habit, with 「を」 for what you quit, as in 「たばこをやめる」, unlike stopping movement, which is a different verb.",
      "Quitting a job or school is written 「辞める|やめる」, with the dictionary kanji, while stopping a habit or action is 「止める|やめる」, with the stop kanji, so kana is a safe choice.",
      "To quit doing something, turn the verb into a noun with 「の」, as in 「たばこを吸うのをやめる|たばこをすうのをやめる」, since you cannot attach it straight to another verb.",
      "It runs up a ladder of strength, from the polite 「やめてください」 to the casual 「やめて」 to the parent's firm 「やめなさい」, stop that."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}はたばこを[やめました|やめました|やめる]。",
    en: "My father quit smoking." },
  "けんかする|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The noun 「けんか」 means a fight, and with 「する」 it becomes a verb, to fight. It covers both shouting arguments and real fistfights, so context tells you which. A couple arguing over the dishes and two drunk men brawling are both 「けんか」. There are kanji for it, but they are hard, so it is usually written in kana. The person you fight with takes 「と」, the with particle: 「兄とけんかしました|あにとけんかしました」, I had a fight with my brother. A common mistake is using 「を」, which sounds like you are fighting the person as an object. There is a nice compound for fights between brothers and sisters: 「きょうだいげんか」. Notice the sound softens to 「げんか」 when it comes second, the way 「川|かわ」 becomes 「がわ」. There is also a well known saying that fights and fires are the flowers of old Edo, the old name for Tokyo, because both happened so often there. A way to remember it: think of a tense, crackling ken, ka. So: a fight, done with someone using 「と」." },
      { title: "Making up", text: "Where there is a fight, there is making up, and Japanese has a lovely word for that: 「仲直り|なかなおり」. It combines 「仲|なか」, a relationship, the same as in 「仲がいい|なかがいい」, to get along well, with 「直る|なおる」, to be fixed. So making up is literally the relationship getting repaired. You can say 「友だちと仲直りしました|ともだちとなかなおりしました」, I made up with my friend. Notice it uses 「と」, just like the fighting verb. There is also a popular saying: 「けんかするほど仲がいい|けんかするほどなかがいい」, the more you fight, the closer you are. People say it, half joking, about couples or siblings who bicker constantly but are obviously devoted to each other. Other useful compounds: 「口げんか|くちげんか」 is a verbal argument, a mouth fight, and 「夫婦げんか|ふうふげんか」 is a husband and wife quarrel. So: you fight with someone, and then you fix the relationship." },
      { title: "Buying a fight", text: "Japanese treats a fight like something you can buy and sell. If someone provokes you, insults you on purpose, or tries to start trouble, they are 「けんかを売る|けんかをうる」, selling a fight. If you take the bait and fight back, you are 「けんかを買う|けんかをかう」, buying the fight. So a tough guy in a movie might growl 「けんか売ってるの|けんかうってるの」, are you trying to pick a fight with me? And a calm person might say they refuse to buy it. It is a vivid image: one person offering trouble like a street vendor, and the other deciding whether to pay. It also gives you a good memory link to two verbs you already know, 「売る|うる」, to sell, and 「買う|かう」, to buy. Of course, the wise choice is to keep your wallet closed. So: a fight is sold by the one who starts it and bought by the one who answers." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji for this word are 「喧嘩|けんか」, and they are not on the beginner list, so Genki uses kana. But they are fun to look at. Both kanji have the mouth radical, 「口|くち」, squeezed onto the left side. That is the same radical you see in many words about sounds and speaking. Two mouths side by side make a good picture for a noisy quarrel. Each kanji on its own carries a sense of noise and clamor, so the pair together is something like a loud commotion. In real life you will often see the word written in kana or with just one of the kanji, since the full version is hard to write by hand. You are not expected to write them yet. Just notice the two little mouths when you see them on a manga page or a sign. So: two shouting mouths, a quarrel." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It covers both arguments and fistfights, and the person you fight with takes 「と」, not 「を」, as in 「兄とけんかしました|あにとけんかしました」.",
      "Making up is 「仲直り|なかなおり」, the relationship getting fixed, and the saying 「けんかするほど仲がいい|けんかするほどなかがいい」 claims the more you fight, the closer you are.",
      "Japanese treats a fight like goods, so whoever provokes trouble is 「けんかを売る|けんかをうる」, selling a fight, and whoever answers is 「けんかを買う|けんかをかう」, buying it.",
      "Its hard kanji 「喧嘩|けんか」 each have the mouth radical on the left, two shouting mouths side by side for a noisy quarrel, which is why Genki uses kana."
    ],
    ja: "{昨日|きのう}、{兄|あに}と[けんかしました|けんかしました|けんかする]。",
    en: "I had a fight with my big brother yesterday." },
  "しょうかいする|紹介する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first, 「紹|しょう」, has the thread radical on the left, a small twisted thread, which often marks ideas about tying and connecting. The second, 「介|かい」, means to be in between, to act as a go-between. So the word is to tie two people together by standing in the middle: to introduce. The pattern puts the person you introduce with 「を」 and the listener with 「に」: 「友だちを先生に紹介します|ともだちをせんせいにしょうかいします」, I introduce my friend to the teacher. When you introduce yourself, you do a 「自己紹介|じこしょうかい」, a self introduction, which is a big part of the first day of class, a new job, or any meeting in Japan. It usually goes: 「はじめまして」, then your name, a little about yourself, and 「よろしくおねがいします」. You also hear it in a polite request: 「紹介してください|しょうかいしてください」, please introduce me. A way to remember it: the thread connecting you to someone new. So: a thread and a go-between, introducing people." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, who introduces you can matter as much as who you are. Business and social life run on connections, and an introduction from a trusted person opens doors. A company may be much more willing to meet you if someone they know says, I can vouch for this person. Even job hunting and apartment hunting can go more smoothly with a personal introduction. When meeting in business, introductions come with a ritual: exchanging business cards, 「名刺|めいし」. You hold your card with both hands, facing the other person so they can read it, bow slightly, and say your name and company. You receive their card with both hands too, look at it carefully, and set it on the table in front of you during the meeting, never stuffing it straight into a pocket. Traditionally, even marriage introductions were arranged through a go-between. So: an introduction in Japan is a small bridge built on trust." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb combines beautifully with the giving and receiving patterns from lesson sixteen. If someone does you the favor of introducing a person to you, use 「てもらう」: 「友だちに先生を紹介してもらいました|ともだちにせんせいをしょうかいしてもらいました」, I had my friend introduce the teacher to me. If you do the favor for someone, use 「てあげる」: 「田中さんに友だちを紹介してあげました|たなかさんにともだちをしょうかいしてあげました」, I introduced my friend to Tanaka for him. And to ask for an introduction, use 「てくれませんか」: 「いい先生を紹介してくれませんか|いいせんせいをしょうかいしてくれませんか」, could you introduce me to a good teacher? There is also a set phrase for formal settings, 「紹介させてください|しょうかいさせてください」, please let me introduce. You do not need to build that form yet, just recognize it. So: introductions are favors, and Japanese grammar marks who is doing the favor for whom." },
      { title: "Not only people", text: "In English, we introduce people. In Japanese, this verb is used much more widely. You can introduce a place, a product, a book, a dish, or an idea. A travel show on television might say 「今日は京都のお寺を紹介します|きょうはきょうとのおてらをしょうかいします」, today we will introduce temples in Kyoto. A friend might say 「いいレストランを紹介します|いいレストランをしょうかいします」, let me recommend a good restaurant. And in class, a teacher might ask you to present a famous place in your country, which is also this verb. Magazines have pages introducing new shops, and websites introduce products. In English, we would often say recommend, present, show, or feature instead. So if you see this word on a poster or hear it on the news, think showing something to someone new, not just shaking hands. So: introducing works for anything you want to share with someone." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「紹|しょう」 has the thread radical for connecting and 「介|かい」 means a go-between, so it ties two people together, with the person introduced taking 「を」 and the listener taking 「に」.",
      "In Japan an introduction from a trusted person opens doors, and business introductions come with the ritual of exchanging 「名刺|めいし」, offered and received with both hands.",
      "Introductions are favors, so use 「てもらう」 when someone introduces a person to you, 「てあげる」 when you do it for someone, and 「てくれませんか」 to ask for one.",
      "Unlike English, you can introduce places, products, books and dishes, so on a poster or the news it often means recommend, present or feature."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}を[紹介します|しょうかいします|しょうかいする]。",
    en: "Let me introduce my friend." },
  "ダイエットする|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from the English word diet, made into a verb with 「する」. Listen to the sounds. Diet becomes 「ダイエット」. The die part stays close to English, the e gets its own beat, and the t is doubled with a small 「ッ」, a little pause, then gets an extra vowel, because Japanese does not end on a consonant. The meaning shifted. In English, your diet can just be whatever you normally eat. In Japanese, this word always means trying to lose weight, going on a diet. So 「ダイエットしています」 means I am on a diet. If you want to talk about what you eat in general, you would use a different expression, so do not say your diet is mostly vegetables with this word. It goes naturally with 「やせる」, to lose weight, and 「太る|ふとる」, to gain weight, both from lesson seven. A common pairing: 「ダイエットのために、走っています|ダイエットのために、はしっています」. A way to remember it: in Japanese, a diet is always a mission. So: diet, but only the weight loss kind." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The most common way to talk about dieting is with the noun plus 「中|ちゅう」, in the middle of: 「ダイエット中|ダイエットちゅう」, I am on a diet. It is the perfect excuse when someone offers you dessert: 「ごめん、今ダイエット中なんだ|ごめん、いまダイエットちゅうなんだ」, sorry, I am on a diet right now. You might also hear that someone succeeded, 「ダイエットに成功した|ダイエットにせいこうした」, or failed. And there is a loanword that gained a new meaning in Japanese: 「リバウンド」, from rebound, meaning gaining the weight back after a diet. Diet versions of drinks and foods use this word too, though you will also see the loanword for zero, as in zero sugar. A common mistake is thinking it can describe what you normally eat. If you want to talk about eating habits, Japanese uses 「食生活|しょくせいかつ」, your eating life. So: on a diet, the perfect reason to say no to cake." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb belongs to a big and very handy family: loanwords plus 「する」. Japanese borrows a noun from English or another language and simply adds 「する」 to make it a verb. You already know many. 「アルバイトする」, to work part time, from the German word for work. 「デートする」, to go on a date. 「コピーする」, to make a copy. 「ジョギングする」, to go jogging. 「メールする」, to send a message. All of these conjugate exactly like 「する」: 「ダイエットします」, 「ダイエットして」, 「ダイエットしない」. That makes them easy. The tricky part is knowing which English words Japanese has borrowed, and whether the meaning changed on the way, as it did with diet. You can also often put 「を」 between the noun and 「する」, as in 「ダイエットをする」. So: borrow a noun, add 「する」, and you have a new verb." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a famous traditional piece of advice about eating: 「腹八分目|はらはちぶんめ」. It means eat until your stomach is eight parts out of ten full, about eighty percent, and then stop. It is often linked with Okinawa, which is known for the long lives of its older residents. Many Japanese people grow up hearing it from parents and grandparents. Traditional Japanese meals also help, with small portions served in many little dishes, lots of vegetables, fish and soup. At the same time, Japan has endless diet trends. Every few years a new one sweeps the country, promoted on television, and suddenly a particular food sells out in every supermarket. So: the old wisdom is simple, stop at eighty percent, and the modern world keeps inventing new diets anyway." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English diet, but in Japanese it always means trying to lose weight, never just what you normally eat, so a diet is always a mission.",
      "Say 「ダイエット中|ダイエットちゅう」, I am on a diet, as the perfect excuse to refuse dessert, and talk about general eating habits with 「食生活|しょくせいかつ」 instead.",
      "It belongs to the big family of loanword plus 「する」 verbs, like 「デートする」 and 「コピーする」, which all conjugate just like 「する」.",
      "The traditional advice 「腹八分目|はらはちぶんめ」 says to stop eating at eighty percent full, even as modern Japan keeps inventing new diet trends."
    ],
    ja: "{今|いま}、[ダイエットしています|ダイエットしています|ダイエットする]。",
    en: "I am on a diet now." },
  "ちこくする|遅刻する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first, 「遅|ち」, means late or slow. On its own, with its native reading, it is 「遅い|おそい」, slow or late, from lesson ten. You also know it from 「遅くなる|おそくなる」, to be late, and you will meet it in 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」, to miss a train, in lesson sixteen. Look at the bottom left of the kanji: that long sweeping stroke is the movement radical, often found in kanji about going and walking. The second kanji, 「刻|こく」, means to carve, and from there, a precise moment of time, as if time were carved into marks. So the word is late for the set moment: to be late for something scheduled, like class or work. The thing you are late for takes 「に」: 「授業に遅刻しました|じゅぎょうにちこくしました」. Culturally, this matters a lot in Japan. Trains run to the minute, and being even a few minutes late is a real thing to apologize for. So: late for the carved moment." },
      { title: "Late versus delayed", text: "Japanese has two common ways to talk about being late, and they are not interchangeable. This word, 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, is for a person who is late for a scheduled start, like class, work, or a meeting. It sounds a bit official, almost like a mark on your record. The other is 「遅れる|おくれる」, a native verb with the same first kanji. It is more general: a person can be late, but so can a train, a bus, or a plan. 「電車が遅れました|でんしゃがおくれました」, the train was delayed. You would not use the first word for a train. When you apologize to a friend for keeping them waiting, the gentle phrase is 「遅れてごめん|おくれてごめん」, sorry I am late. And notice that the same kanji also gives the adjective 「遅い|おそい」, slow or late. One kanji, three readings, three words. So: trains are delayed, and students are tardy." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese trains are famous for being on time, so on time that a delay of a minute or two can make the news. That creates an interesting system. When a train is significantly delayed, the railway company hands out a 「遅延証明書|ちえんしょうめいしょ」, a delay certificate. Notice the first kanji, the late kanji again. You take this slip and show it to your school or your boss, and it proves that your lateness was the train's fault, not yours. These days many companies post them online too. This shows how seriously punctuality is taken. Being late without a good reason is considered disrespectful, because it wastes other people's time. Many people arrive five or ten minutes early to be safe. Some schools even have teachers at the gate in the morning, noting who comes in after the bell. So in Japan, being late can require paperwork." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how lateness sounds in real conversation. Running toward the school gate, a student groans 「やばい、遅刻しそう|やばい、ちこくしそう」, oh no, I am going to be late. That 「そう」 form means it looks like something is about to happen. A teacher warns the class, 「明日は遅刻しないでください|あしたはちこくしないでください」, please do not be late tomorrow. At work, if you will be late, it is good manners to call ahead before the start time. When you arrive, the apology is direct: 「遅刻して、すみません|ちこくして、すみません」, I am sorry for being late. Notice the te-form gives the reason for the apology. And a classic excuse, true or not: 「寝坊しました|ねぼうしました」, I overslept. A common mistake is to forget 「に」 for the thing you are late for. So: call ahead, apologize clearly, and maybe set two alarms." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「遅|ち」 means late and 「刻|こく」 means to carve, hence a precise moment, so it is being late for the carved moment, with 「に」 for what you are late for.",
      "This word is for a person late to a scheduled start, while 「遅れる|おくれる」 also covers delayed trains and plans, so trains are delayed and students are tardy.",
      "Japanese trains are so punctual that a delayed train comes with a 「遅延証明書|ちえんしょうめいしょ」, a delay certificate proving your lateness was not your fault.",
      "If you will be late, call ahead, and when you arrive apologize directly with 「遅刻して、すみません|ちこくして、すみません」, using the te-form to give the reason."
    ],
    ja: "{授業|じゅぎょう}に[遅刻しました|ちこくしました|ちこくする]。",
    en: "I was late for class." },
  "りゅうがくする|留学する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first, 「留|りゅう」, means to stay, to remain somewhere. The second, 「学|がく」, means learning, the same kanji as in 「学生|がくせい」 and 「大学|だいがく」. So the word is to stay somewhere to learn, to study abroad. You actually met this word on day one of Genki. 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, an international student, is just this word plus 「生|せい」, student. Many of the Genki characters are exactly that. The place you go takes 「に」: 「日本に留学したいです|にほんにりゅうがくしたいです」, I want to study abroad in Japan. That is the 「たい」 form for wanting to do something. It is used for a longer stay at a school, a semester or a year, not for a short tourist trip, which would be 「旅行|りょこう」. When you go, you might stay with a 「ホストファミリー」, also in this lesson. A way to remember it: you are staying to study, and you stay long enough to become a 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」. So: stay plus learn, studying abroad." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「留|りゅう」, carries the idea of staying or being kept in place. You will meet it in another very useful word: 「留守|るす」, being away from home. Notice it has a different reading there, 「る」. The idea is that someone stays behind to guard the house while the owner is out. So 「留守です|るすです」 means nobody is home. An answering machine was called 「留守番電話|るすばんでんわ」, a phone that watches the house while you are out. The same kanji can also be read 「とめる」, to fasten or hold something in place, as with a pin or a clip. In this word it is read 「りゅう」, one of its Chinese-origin readings. The bottom part of the kanji is 「田|た」, a rice field, which you know from 「田中|たなか」. A memory trick: picture yourself staying put in a rice field abroad, studying. So: the staying kanji, with more than one reading." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases you will hear when people talk about study abroad. To say how long, put the time first: 「一年間留学しました|いちねんかんりゅうがくしました」, I studied abroad for one year. For something happening now, use 「中|ちゅう」: 「今、日本に留学中です|いま、にほんにりゅうがくちゅうです」, I am currently studying abroad in Japan. To share an experience, use the pattern from lesson eleven: 「留学したことがありますか|りゅうがくしたことがありますか」, have you ever studied abroad? An exchange program, where students trade places between schools, is 「交換留学|こうかんりゅうがく」, and the first part means exchange. And a person who has come back from studying abroad might be asked, how was it? The proud answer is usually about friends, food, and how hard the language was at first. So: phrases for how long, now, and ever." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Studying abroad has deep roots in Japanese history. Long ago, in the seventh to ninth centuries, Japan sent official missions to China, and young monks and scholars traveled with them to study Buddhism, writing, law, and medicine. Some stayed for many years. They brought back knowledge that shaped Japanese culture, including much of the writing system you are learning now. Later, in the late eighteen hundreds, the government sent students to Europe and America to learn science, engineering, and government as the country modernized quickly. Today the flow goes both ways. Japanese universities welcome students from all over the world, and many Japanese students spend a semester or a year abroad. So when you dream of studying in Japan, you are joining a very long tradition of people crossing the sea to learn." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「留|りゅう」 means to stay and 「学|がく」 means learning, so it is staying somewhere to learn, and adding 「生|せい」 gives 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, an international student.",
      "The staying kanji also appears as 「る」 in 「留守|るす」, being away from home, because someone stays behind to guard the house.",
      "Put the length first in 「一年間留学しました|いちねんかんりゅうがくしました」, use 「留学中|りゅうがくちゅう」 for now, and 「留学したことがありますか|りゅうがくしたことがありますか」 to ask about experience.",
      "Studying abroad is an old Japanese tradition, from monks sailing to China centuries ago to students sent to the West as the country modernized."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}に[留学したい|りゅうがくしたい|りゅうがくする]です。",
    en: "I want to study abroad in Japan." },
  "しゅっしん|出身": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「出|しゅつ」, means to come out, to go out. Here it shrinks to 「しゅっ」, with a little pause, before the next sound. You know this kanji from 「出かける|でかける」, to go out, and 「出す|だす」, to take out, where it uses its native readings. The second, 「身|しん」, means body, or oneself. So the word is where you yourself came out from: your hometown, home country, or even your old school. It is a noun, and it attaches right after a place: 「東京出身です|とうきょうしゅっしんです」, I am from Tokyo. To ask someone politely, add the honorable 「ご」: 「ご出身はどちらですか|ごしゅっしんはどちらですか」, where are you from? That uses 「どちら」, the polite which way. It is a staple of self introductions in Japan, right after your name. A common mistake is to say you are 「出身|しゅっしん」 something without a place in front. A way to remember it: picture yourself stepping out of your hometown gate. So: come out plus self, where you are from." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is not only for countries and cities. You can use it for your school too. 「東京大学出身|とうきょうだいがくしゅっしん」 means a graduate of Tokyo University, someone who came out of that school. Related words build on it. 「出身地|しゅっしんち」 is your place of origin, with the kanji for ground or place. And 「出身校|しゅっしんこう」 is the school you graduated from. In casual talk, people also use 「地元|じもと」, which means your home area, the local place you grew up. A friend might say 「地元に帰ります|じもとにかえります」, I am going back home, to my hometown. A mini dialogue: 「ご出身はどちらですか|ごしゅっしんはどちらですか」, where are you from? 「大阪です。スミスさんは|おおさかです。スミスさんは」, Osaka, and you? Then the conversation often turns to local food. So: where you came out from, a place or even a school." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, where you are from is a big topic of conversation, and people often identify strongly with their prefecture. Japan has forty seven prefectures, and each one has its own famous foods, dialect, festivals, and stereotypes. Television even has shows comparing prefectures. So when two people discover they are from the same place, they get excited and start chatting about local spots and dialect words. When someone says they are from Osaka, the next comment might be about takoyaki or the Osaka sense of humor. From Hokkaido, maybe the cold winters and great seafood. As a foreigner, you will often be asked this question too, and naming your city and one thing it is famous for is a great conversation starter. Bringing a small treat from your hometown as a gift is a nice touch. So: your hometown is part of your identity and a ready topic of small talk." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let us look at both kanji more closely. 「出|しゅつ」 is often described as a foot stepping out of an enclosure, and the shape looks like two mountains stacked, which is a popular memory trick. With its Chinese-origin reading, it shows up in 「出発|しゅっぱつ」, departure, and 「出席|しゅっせき」, attendance, where it also shrinks to 「しゅっ」. The second kanji, 「身|しん」, means body or oneself. A common explanation is that it pictures a person with a rounded belly, and some say a pregnant woman. You will see it in 「身長|しんちょう」, height, the length of your body, and in 「自身|じしん」, oneself. With its native reading, it is 「み」, as in 「中身|なかみ」, the contents inside something. So: a step out, plus your own body, the place you yourself came from." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「出|しゅつ」, come out, shrinks to 「しゅっ」 before 「身|しん」, oneself, so it is where you came out from, attached after a place as in 「東京出身です|とうきょうしゅっしんです」.",
      "It works for schools as well as places, so 「東京大学出身|とうきょうだいがくしゅっしん」 means a graduate of Tokyo University, while the casual 「地元|じもと」 means your home area.",
      "People in Japan identify strongly with their prefecture, so where you are from is a ready topic of small talk, often turning to local food.",
      "「出|しゅつ」 looks like a foot stepping out or two stacked mountains, and 「身|しん」 pictures a person with a rounded belly, so it is your own body stepping out."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは{東京|とうきょう}[出身|しゅっしん]です。",
    en: "I am from Tokyo." },
  "ひさしぶり|久しぶり": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces. 「久し|ひさし」 comes from the adjective 「久しい|ひさしい」, a slightly literary word meaning long-lasting, long ago. The kanji carries that same sense of a long time. Then 「ぶり」 is a little ending that means after a gap of. You can put it after any length of time: 「三年ぶり|さんねんぶり」, for the first time in three years, or 「一週間ぶり|いっしゅうかんぶり」, after a week. So this word is after a long gap, it has been a long time. Its most common use is as a greeting when you see someone again after ages: 「お久しぶりです|おひさしぶりです」 is the polite version, with 「お」 in front, and 「久しぶり|ひさしぶり」 or 「久しぶりだね|ひさしぶりだね」 is casual, for friends. It is often followed by 「お元気ですか|おげんきですか」, how have you been? You can also use it for doing something again: 「久しぶりに映画を見ました|ひさしぶりにえいがをみました」, I watched a movie for the first time in a long while. A way to remember it: a long gap, then hello again. So: long time, plus after a gap." },
      { title: "Formal cousin", text: "In business or very polite situations, people often add a more formal phrase when they meet again: 「ご無沙汰しております|ごぶさたしております」. It means I have been out of touch with you for a long time, and it takes the blame on yourself, as if to say, it is my fault we have not spoken. You will see it at the start of emails and letters, especially to a teacher, a client, or an older relative. The middle part, 「無沙汰|ぶさた」, means a lack of news or contact. You do not need to use this phrase yet, but it is good to recognize. Compare the levels: 「久しぶり|ひさしぶり」, casual, with friends. 「お久しぶりです|おひさしぶりです」, polite, for most adults. And this formal one, for when you want to be especially humble. So: three steps, from friends to formal letters, all about meeting again after a long time." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There are three handy patterns with this word. First, as a greeting, it ends with 「です」 or 「だね」, as you know. Second, with 「に」, it becomes an adverb, describing an action you do for the first time in a long while: 「久しぶりに映画館に行きました|ひさしぶりにえいがかんにいきました」, I went to a movie theater for the first time in ages. Third, with 「の」, it describes a noun: 「久しぶりの休み|ひさしぶりのやすみ」, a day off, the first in a long while. You can also swap the first part for an exact length of time: 「五年ぶりに|ごねんぶりに」, for the first time in five years. 「十年ぶりの雪|じゅうねんぶりのゆき」, the first snow in ten years, is the kind of phrase you hear on the news. So: 「に」 for an action, 「の」 for a noun, and a number in place of the first part when you know exactly how long." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji at the front, 「久|ひさ」, is a simple three stroke shape, and its core meaning is a long time. With its Chinese-origin reading 「きゅう」, it appears in 「永久|えいきゅう」, forever, eternity, where the first kanji also means long lasting. You might see that word in phrases like a permanent address or a lifetime guarantee. There is also 「持久力|じきゅうりょく」, stamina or endurance, literally the power to hold on for a long time. The native reading, 「ひさ」, is the one you meet in this greeting. Notice the okurigana: the kanji is followed by 「し」, which comes from the old adjective form, and then 「ぶり」. That is why you write it with the kanji plus 「しぶり」 and never as just one kanji and 「ぶり」. A memory trick: the shape looks like a person taking long, slow strides. So: the long time kanji, read 「ひさ」 here." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「久し|ひさし」 means a long time and 「ぶり」 means after a gap of, so it is the greeting after ages apart, polite 「お久しぶりです|おひさしぶりです」 or casual 「久しぶり|ひさしぶり」.",
      "Its formal cousin 「ご無沙汰しております|ごぶさたしております」 humbly takes the blame for being out of touch and often opens letters and emails.",
      "Add 「に」 for an action done for the first time in ages, 「の」 to describe a noun, or swap in an exact time, as in 「五年ぶりに|ごねんぶりに」.",
      "The kanji 「久|ひさ」 means a long time, read 「きゅう」 in 「永久|えいきゅう」, forever, and it is always followed by 「しぶり」 in this greeting."
    ],
    ja: "[久しぶり|ひさしぶり]ですね。おげんきですか。",
    en: "Long time no see. How are you?" },
  "まあまあ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese word with no kanji. It means so-so, okay, not great and not bad. It is the answer when someone asks how something was and you do not want to sound too excited or too negative. 「テストはどうでしたか」, how was the test? 「まあまあでした」, it was okay. It also works for people and things: a restaurant that is 「まあまあ」 is fine but nothing special. It comes from the little word 「まあ」, which on its own is a soft hesitation, like well, or a mild oh my. Doubling it makes a gentle, shrugging sound. Japanese loves this kind of doubled word, like 「ときどき」 and 「いろいろ」. Here is a cultural tip: Japanese people often say 「まあまあ」 about their own skills to sound modest, even when they are very good. So if a friend says their English is 「まあまあ」, it may be excellent. A common mistake is using it as a strong negative, but it is gentle. A way to remember it: picture a shoulder shrug with each 「まあ」. So: a doubled shrug, so-so." },
      { title: "Now, now", text: "This word has a second life that has nothing to do with so-so. Said in a soothing voice, with a calming gesture of the hands, it means now, now, or easy, calm down. Picture two friends starting to argue at a dinner table. A third friend raises both palms and says 「まあまあ、落ち着いて|まあまあ、おちついて」, now, now, calm down. Or a grandmother patting a crying child. Here the doubled sound is like a gentle pat on the shoulder. The single 「まあ」 also has its own everyday uses. 「まあ、いいか」 means oh well, never mind, when you decide not to worry about something. And at the start of a sentence, 「まあ」 softens what comes next, like well, sort of. Tone tells you which meaning is intended. A flat, shrugging voice is so-so. A soft, soothing voice with raised hands is calm down. So: the same sound can rate a meal or calm a quarrel." },
      { title: "Scale of good", text: "It helps to place this word on a scale. At the top, 「とても」 or 「すごく」, very: 「とてもおいしい」, really delicious. Next, 「けっこう」, fairly, surprisingly: 「けっこうおいしい」, pretty good, better than I expected. In the middle sits this word: okay, so-so. Below that, you switch to negative sentences. 「あまりおいしくない」, not very good, with 「あまり」 and a negative ending. And at the bottom, 「ぜんぜんおいしくない」, not good at all. Notice the last two require a negative form after them, while this word does not. It can stand alone as an answer, or come before an adjective: 「まあまあおいしい」, reasonably tasty. Because Japanese conversation values not sounding too strong, this middle word is extremely useful. It lets you answer honestly without praising too much or complaining. So: right in the middle of the scale, between pretty good and not very." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for this word. Picture a strict food critic sitting at a table. The waiter brings out a plate of noodles. The critic takes one bite, chews slowly, and tilts a hand back and forth, palm down, the universal sign for so-so. As the hand tilts left, the critic says 「まあ」. As it tilts right, again 「まあ」. Left, right, so-so. The waiter's face falls. Now imagine the critic's mother, sitting next to him, saying in a soft voice, now now, be nice, and the same two sounds come out but gently. A tilting hand for the rating, two soft pats for the calming. If you remember the tilting hand, you will remember the rhythm of the doubled word. So: a hand tilting left and right, so-so." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a doubled, shrugging 「まあ」 meaning so-so, and Japanese people often use it about their own skills to sound modest even when they are very good.",
      "Said softly with raised palms, it means now, now, calm down, as in 「まあまあ、落ち着いて|まあまあ、おちついて」, so tone tells you which meaning is intended.",
      "It sits right in the middle of the scale, between 「けっこう」, pretty good, and 「あまり」 with a negative, and unlike those lower words it needs no negative ending.",
      "Picture a food critic tilting his hand left and right, saying 「まあ」 each way, the universal so-so gesture that gives the doubled rhythm."
    ],
    ja: "テストは[まあまあ]でした。",
    en: "The test was so-so." },
  "もっと|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese word, an adverb meaning more, or even more. It goes right before what you want more of: a verb, an adjective, or another adverb. 「もっと食べてください|もっとたべてください」, please eat more. 「もっと大きいのがいいです|もっとおおきいのがいいです」, a bigger one would be good. And a very useful phrase for learners: 「もっとゆっくり話してください|もっとゆっくりはなしてください」, please speak more slowly. Notice it comes before the word it boosts, just like English more. Compare it with 「たくさん」, a lot, which is about a big amount, while this one is always about going beyond where you are now. A way to remember it, as a sound-alike hook: it sounds like the English word motto, and the motto of a hungry learner is always more. So: always more, placed before the word." },
      { title: "More versus one more", text: "A very common mix up for English speakers is between this word and 「もう」. Both can translate as more, but they work differently. 「もう」 goes with a number to mean one more or another: 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」, one more, and 「もう一度|もういちど」, one more time. This word, on the other hand, means more in degree or amount, without a specific count: 「もっとください」, give me more, and 「もっと大きい|もっとおおきい」, bigger. So at a restaurant, if you want another bowl of rice, say 「もう一杯ください|もういっぱいください」, one more bowl, please. If you want a bigger serving in general, use this word. And to ask a teacher to repeat something, use 「もう一度お願いします|もういちどおねがいします」, not this word. A quick test: if there is a number after more, use 「もう」. So: one more uses 「もう」, and more in general uses this word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word shows up in many everyday situations. Shopping, it is your best friend: 「もっと安いのはありますか|もっとやすいのはありますか」, do you have a cheaper one? 「もっと小さいのはありますか|もっとちいさいのはありますか」, do you have a smaller one? With a teacher: 「もっと練習しましょう|もっとれんしゅうしましょう」, let us practice more. To encourage someone, people double it: 「もっともっと」, more and more, as a cheer or a request. In a photo shoot, a photographer might say smile more. A child might pull on your sleeve after a story and say just 「もっと」, again, more. You can also use it to show you want something to continue: 「もっと日本にいたかったです|もっとにほんにいたかったです」, I wish I could have stayed in Japan longer. So: a word for shops, classrooms, and children who never want the story to end." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen to the small 「っ」 in the middle. It is a full beat of silence, a tiny hold before the 「と」. So the word has three beats: 「も」, a pause, 「と」. If you drop the pause, you get 「もと」, which is a different word meaning origin or base, the place something came from. And if you add 「も」 at the end, you get 「もっとも」, which means the most, the very most, a more formal word you will meet in writing. So the three are connected in sound but differ in meaning: 「もと」, origin; this word, more; 「もっとも」, most. Practice saying it with a clear little hitch, as if you are holding your breath for a moment before asking for more. Here is a memory trick: the small 「っ」 is you pausing to decide whether you really need more cake. So: three beats, with a pause in the middle." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is an adverb placed before what you want more of, as in 「もっとゆっくり話してください|もっとゆっくりはなしてください」, and it sounds like motto, the hungry learner's motto.",
      "Use 「もう」 with a number for one more, like 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」, and this word for more in amount or degree without a count.",
      "It is a shopper's best friend in 「もっと安いのはありますか|もっとやすいのはありますか」, do you have a cheaper one, and children use it alone to ask for more.",
      "The small 「っ」 is a full silent beat, and dropping it gives 「もと」, origin, while adding 「も」 gives 「もっとも」, the most."
    ],
    ja: "[もっと]{日本語|にほんご}を{勉強したい|べんきょうしたい}です。",
    en: "I want to study Japanese more." },
  "あと|後": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese reading of the kanji, 「あと」, and it means after or later. It follows a noun with 「の」: 「授業の後|じゅぎょうのあと」, after class. Or it follows a verb in the past: 「食べた後|たべたあと」, after eating. Notice that the verb before it is always in the past form, even when you talk about the future. You already know a close relative from lesson six: 「後で|あとで」, later, as in see you later. The same kanji has other readings too. With its Chinese-origin reading 「ご」, it is in 「〜後|〜ご」 from lesson ten, as in 「三日後|みっかご」, three days later. And with another native reading, it is in 「後ろ|うしろ」, behind. So the kanji is all about what comes after, in time or in space. Look at the left side: it is the going person radical, the same one as in 「行く|いく」. A way to remember it: someone walking along, falling behind, coming after. So: after, following a noun with 「の」 or a past verb." },
      { title: "Five more minutes", text: "This word has a very useful second meaning: the rest, what remains, or more to go. Put it in front of an amount: 「あと五分|あとごふん」, five more minutes. That is exactly what a sleepy student says to a parent in the morning. 「あと一つ|あとひとつ」, one more left. 「あと三日で夏休みです|あとみっかでなつやすみです」, three more days until summer vacation. Here the picture is the time or amount that comes after now. In casual speech, people also start a sentence with this word to add one more thing, like and also: 「あと、パンも買ってきて|あと、パンもかってきて」, and also, get some bread. You will hear it constantly in shops and in everyday lists. A shop clerk might say 「あと一つしかありません|あとひとつしかありません」, there is only one left. So: besides after, this word also means more to go, or what is left." },
      { title: "Before and after", text: "This word has a mirror image: 「前|まえ」, before. They work as a pair, but with one big grammar difference. Before uses the dictionary form of a verb: 「寝る前に|ねるまえに」, before going to sleep. After uses the past form: 「寝た後で|ねたあとで」, after going to sleep. The logic is that before something happens, it has not happened yet, so it stays in the plain present. After something happens, it is done, so it takes the past. With nouns, both use 「の」: 「授業の前|じゅぎょうのまえ」, before class, and 「授業の後|じゅぎょうのあと」, after class. A nice daily routine sentence uses both: 「ご飯の前に手を洗って、ご飯の後で歯をみがきます|ごはんのまえにてをあらって、ごはんのあとではをみがきます」, I wash my hands before meals and brush my teeth after. So: before with the plain form, after with the past form." },
      { title: "Kanji family", text: "This kanji appears in some very common words where it means after, behind, or later. The one you see every day on clocks and schedules is 「午後|ごご」, afternoon, literally after noon. Its partner is 「午前|ごぜん」, morning, before noon. Then there is 「最後|さいご」, the last, the very end, as in the last train, 「最後の電車|さいごのでんしゃ」. In schools and workplaces, the kanji gets yet another reading, 「こう」, in 「後輩|こうはい」, a junior, someone who joined after you. The opposite is 「先輩|せんぱい」, a senior, someone who came first. That relationship is very important in Japanese clubs and offices, and juniors use polite language with seniors. So this kanji has three readings you will meet often: 「あと」, 「ご」, and 「こう」, plus 「うしろ」 for behind. So: afternoon, the very last, and the junior who came after you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means after, following a noun with 「の」 or a verb that is always in the past form, as in 「食べた後|たべたあと」, and its kanji has the going person radical for falling behind.",
      "In front of an amount it means more to go, as in 「あと五分|あとごふん」, five more minutes, and at the start of a sentence it adds and also.",
      "Its mirror 「前|まえ」 takes the dictionary form, while this word takes the past, because after something happens, it is done.",
      "The kanji appears in 「午後|ごご」, afternoon, 「最後|さいご」, the last, and 「後輩|こうはい」, a junior who came after you."
    ],
    ja: "{授業|じゅぎょう}の[後|あと]、テニスをします。",
    en: "I play tennis after class." },
  "そして|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese connecting word. It links two sentences in order, like and then, or and also. 「本を読みました|ほんをよみました」. 「そして、寝ました|そして、ねました」. I read a book, and then I went to sleep. It comes from 「そう」, in that way, plus 「して」, the te-form of 「する」, so roughly having done that. That is why it carries a sense of sequence. Like other sentence connectors, it goes at the start of the second sentence, usually followed by a comma. How is it different from 「それから」, from lesson four? They are very close. 「それから」 leans toward after that, the next thing in time. This one can mean the next step, but also just adding another point: this restaurant is cheap, and also delicious. It sounds a little more written and neat than 「それから」. A common mistake is to use it to join two nouns, like cats and dogs. For that, Japanese uses the particle 「と」 instead. A way to remember it: having done so, then the next thing. So: and then, between sentences." },
      { title: "Connector family", text: "This word is part of a family of sentence starters that link ideas, and knowing a few lets you tell longer stories. You already have 「それから」, and then, after that. 「でも」 means but, for a contrast. 「だから」 means so, therefore, for a result. 「それに」 means besides, on top of that, for adding a stronger reason. And in writing you will see 「しかし」, a more formal but. Here is a short example that uses several: 「日本語はむずかしいです。でも、おもしろいです。そして、友だちができます。だから、勉強します|にほんごはむずかしいです。でも、おもしろいです。そして、ともだちができます。だから、べんきょうします」. Japanese is hard. But it is interesting. And you make friends. So I study. Each connector starts a new sentence, usually with a comma after it. So: a small set of starter words, and your sentences can tell a story." },
      { title: "In stories", text: "This word has a slightly neat, storybook feel, and you will see it a lot in written Japanese, from children's books to novels. It marks the next important step in a story. A famous example: the Japanese title of Agatha Christie's mystery And Then There Were None is 「そして誰もいなくなった|そしてだれもいなくなった」, which starts with this very word. It creates a sense of something dramatic happening at last. In speeches and essays, it often introduces the final point: and finally, and most importantly. In casual conversation, people use it less than 「それから」 or the simple te-form, and you might hear 「で」 instead, a short, casual and so. So if your writing sounds a little choppy, adding this word between sentences gives it a smooth, organized flow. So: a connector that gives your sentences the feel of a well told story." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese already has a powerful way to link actions inside one sentence: the te-form. 「起きて、シャワーを浴びて、学校に行きました|おきて、シャワーをあびて、がっこうにいきました」, I got up, took a shower, and went to school. That is all one sentence. So when do you need this word? Mainly when you start a new sentence, or when you want to put a little extra weight on the next step. You can even combine the two: 「シャワーを浴びて、そして、学校に行きました|シャワーをあびて、そして、がっこうにいきました」, which emphasizes the final step a bit. Another point: this word links clauses and sentences, not single nouns. For nouns, use 「と」 for a complete list, or 「や」 for a sample list, such as among other things. So: the te-form links inside a sentence, and this word links across sentences." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「そう」 plus 「して」, having done that, so it starts the next sentence with and then or and also, while nouns are joined with 「と」 instead.",
      "It belongs to a family of sentence starters, along with 「でも」, but, 「だから」, so, and 「それに」, besides, that let you tell longer stories.",
      "It has a neat, storybook feel common in writing, like the Japanese title of And Then There Were None, 「そして誰もいなくなった|そしてだれもいなくなった」.",
      "The te-form links actions inside one sentence, while this word links across sentences or adds weight to the next step."
    ],
    ja: "{本|ほん}を{読みました|よみました}。[そして]、ねました。",
    en: "I read a book. And then I went to sleep." },
  "〜だけ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A small grammar word, a particle, meaning only or just. It goes right after the thing you are limiting, usually a noun or a number. 「一つだけ|ひとつだけ」, just one. 「水だけ|みずだけ」, only water. 「日本語だけ話します|にほんごだけはなします」, I speak only Japanese. When it comes after a noun, it often replaces 「を」 or 「が」, so you do not need them. It is a neutral word. It just states the limit, without any feeling of too little or too much. In later lessons you will meet another word for only that carries more of a not enough feeling, but this one is calm and simple. It can also follow a verb in the plain form: 「見るだけ|みるだけ」, just looking, which is perfect when a shop clerk comes to help you and you are only browsing. A common mistake English speakers make is putting it in front of the word, like English only. In Japanese it always comes after. A way to remember it: picture a fence put up right after the word, and nothing gets past. So: only, placed after the thing." },
      { title: "Only, two ways", text: "Japanese has a second way to say only, and it is a great partner to learn alongside this one. That second way is 「しか」, which always takes a negative verb. Compare: 「一つだけ買いました|ひとつだけかいました」, I bought just one, and 「一つしか買いませんでした|ひとつしかかいませんでした」, I bought only one, nothing but one. The meaning is similar, but the feeling differs. This word is neutral, simply stating the limit. The other one carries a sense of not enough, as if to say, I wish it were more. So if you have only a little money and are disappointed, 「千円しかありません|せんえんしかありません」 sounds natural. If you are just explaining calmly, 「千円だけあります|せんえんだけあります」 works. The big trap: with 「しか」, the verb must be negative, even though the English is positive. So: this word for a calm only, and the other for only, and it is not enough." },
      { title: "Handy phrases", text: "This little word builds several phrases you will use all the time. 「少しだけ|すこしだけ」 or 「ちょっとだけ」 means just a little. It is a polite way to accept food, or to describe your skills modestly: 「日本語は少しだけ話せます|にほんごはすこしだけはなせます」, I can speak just a little Japanese. 「これだけ」 means just this, or this much, as when you put one item on the counter. 「一回だけ|いっかいだけ」, just once, is a classic plea: please, just once! And 「できるだけ」 means as much as possible, as best you can: 「できるだけ日本語で話します|できるだけにほんごではなします」, I will speak in Japanese as much as I can. That one is a great phrase for a language learner. So: a little bit, just this, just once, and as much as you can, all built on this word." },
      { title: "Particle traffic", text: "What happens when this word meets other particles? With 「を」 and 「が」, it usually replaces them: 「水だけ飲みます|みずだけのみます」. But other particles stay, and the order matters. With 「に」, 「で」 and 「と」, this word usually comes after the particle. 「日曜日にだけ|にちようびにだけ」, only on Sundays. 「家でだけ|いえでだけ」, only at home. 「友だちとだけ|ともだちとだけ」, only with friends. So 「日本でだけ買えます|にほんでだけかえます」 means you can buy it only in Japan. With 「は」, the pattern flips: this word comes first, then 「は」, to highlight one thing in contrast: 「これだけは忘れないで|これだけはわすれないで」, at least do not forget this. A memory trick: think of this word as a fence that goes up after the little particle signs, so the place or time sign stays visible in front of the fence. So: replace 「を」 and 「が」, but keep 「に」, 「で」 and 「と」 in front." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a neutral particle meaning only that always comes after the thing it limits, often replacing 「を」 or 「が」, as in 「見るだけ|みるだけ」, just looking.",
      "Its partner 「しか」 also means only but needs a negative verb and carries a not enough feeling, while this word calmly states the limit.",
      "It builds handy phrases like 「少しだけ|すこしだけ」, just a little, 「一回だけ|いっかいだけ」, just once, and 「できるだけ」, as much as possible.",
      "It replaces 「を」 and 「が」 but comes after 「に」, 「で」 and 「と」, as in 「日本でだけ買えます|にほんでだけかえます」, only in Japan."
    ],
    ja: "{日本語|にほんご}[だけ]{話します|はなします}。",
    en: "I speak only Japanese." },
  "〜てん|〜点": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A counter for points and scores. It goes right after a number: 「九十点|きゅうじゅってん」, ninety points, or 「百点|ひゃくてん」, a hundred points. Japanese school tests are usually scored out of a hundred, so a hundred points is a perfect score, and you hear that phrase a lot from proud kids. Watch for the little sound changes, the same ones you meet with other counters. One point is 「一点|いってん」, three is 「三点|さんてん」, and ten is 「十点|じゅってん」, where the number shrinks and leaves a small pause. The kanji itself means a dot, a point, a mark. The four little strokes at the bottom are like dots. The same word is used for points in a sports match, and for a point in the sense of a point in an argument. To ask about a score: 「テストは何点でしたか|テストはなんてんでしたか」, how many points did you get on the test? A way to remember it: each point is a little dot on the scoreboard. So: dots on a scoreboard, a counter for points." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for points, 「点|てん」, has a rich family of words. 「点数|てんすう」 is a score, the number of points. 「満点|まんてん」 is a perfect score, with the first kanji meaning full. Japanese people also use that word for anything excellent: a meal can be 「満点|まんてん」, a perfect ten. 「弱点|じゃくてん」 is a weak point, using the weak kanji from 「弱い|よわい」. Out on the street, you will see 「交差点|こうさてん」, an intersection, literally the point where roads cross. That one is great for giving directions: turn right at the intersection. And the same kanji with okurigana can be read 「つける」, to turn on a light, as if putting a little dot of light in the dark, though most people write that in kana. So: score, perfect score, weak point, and the point where roads meet, all with the dot kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "If you ever get a Japanese test back, one detail might confuse you. In Japan, correct answers are marked with a circle, called 「まる」. Wrong answers are often marked with a check mark or a slash, which looks to an English speaker like a sign of being correct. So a page full of check marks is bad news. The circle as a sign of correctness goes beyond school: on forms and quizzes, and in everyday speech, 「まる」 means right, and 「ばつ」, an X, means wrong. A failing score is sometimes called 「赤点|あかてん」, red points, because teachers mark it in red. At the top, a score of a hundred is a cause for celebration, and kids often hang perfect test papers on the fridge. So: in Japan, circles mean correct, and check marks mean try again." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how scores come up in real conversation. After a test, friends ask each other 「何点だった|なんてんだった」, how many points did you get? The answer might be 「八十点|はちじゅってん」, eighty points. Notice the shrinking sound again, eighty uses 「はちじゅっ」 before 「てん」. To say a score is out of a hundred, Japanese says 「百点満点で|ひゃくてんまんてんで」, on a scale where a hundred is perfect. In sports, a scoreboard might show the teams' points, and to score a point is 「点を取る|てんをとる」, the same taking verb from this lesson, or 「点が入る|てんがはいる」, a point goes in. When a teacher praises your answer, you might hear 「いい点ですね|いいてんですね」, that is a good score. So: what score did you get, out of a hundred, and taking points, the score conversation in Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It counts points after a number, with sound changes like 「一点|いってん」 and 「十点|じゅってん」, and its kanji means a dot, like dots on a scoreboard.",
      "The dot kanji builds 「点数|てんすう」, a score, 「満点|まんてん」, a perfect score, 「弱点|じゃくてん」, a weak point, and 「交差点|こうさてん」, an intersection.",
      "On Japanese tests a circle, 「まる」, means correct, while a check mark often means wrong, and a failing score is 「赤点|あかてん」, red points.",
      "After a test friends ask 「何点だった|なんてんだった」, and scores are out of a hundred, 「百点満点で|ひゃくてんまんてんで」, while scoring is 「点を取る|てんをとる」."
    ],
    ja: "テストは[九十点|きゅうじゅってん|〜てん]でした。",
    en: "I got ninety points on the test." },
  "さっか|作家": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「作|さく」, means to make, to create. Here it shrinks to 「さっ」 before the 「か」, with a little pause. You know it with its native reading in 「作る|つくる」, to make, from lesson eight. The second, 「家|か」, usually means house, as in 「家|いえ」. But at the end of a word, read 「か」, it means a person who does something as their profession, a specialist. You will see that use in 「漫画家|まんがか」, a manga artist, from this same lesson. The house sense is in 「家族|かぞく」, family, where it is also read 「か」. So this word is a maker by profession, a creator of writing, a writer or novelist. It is more for authors of books and stories than for journalists, who are 「ジャーナリスト」. Japan has a big literary tradition and a famous literary prize for new writers, so this word comes up often. A way to remember it: the maker who works from home all day. So: make, plus professional, an author." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「家|か」 turns a field into a person who has made it their life's work. You already have 「漫画家|まんがか」, a manga artist. Add a few more and the pattern jumps out. 「画家|がか」 is a painter, a person of pictures. 「音楽家|おんがくか」 is a musician, a person of music. 「政治家|せいじか」 is a politician, a person of politics. And 「専門家|せんもんか」 is an expert, a person of a specialty. Notice how this ending feels different from the other job endings in this lesson. 「者|しゃ」, as in 「研究者|けんきゅうしゃ」, just means the one who does it. 「士|し」 and 「師|し」 suggest training and a qualification. But 「家|か」 has a flavor of devotion, a person whose whole house, so to speak, is built around one art or calling. That is why it shows up so often with creative work. So when you hear 「か」 at the end of a job, think of someone who has made that thing their home." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "How do people actually use this word? When you talk about a writer you like, you will often hear it softened with 「さん」: 「あの作家さん|あのさっかさん」, that author. Fans say this naturally, even about famous people they have never met. A classic small-talk question is 「好きな作家はだれですか|すきなさっかはだれですか」, who is your favorite author? It works well on a first date or with a new classmate. If you want to be more specific, a novelist is 「小説家|しょうせつか」, built from 「小説|しょうせつ」, a novel, plus the same professional ending. The verb that goes with this job is simply 「書く|かく」, to write. So you might say 「この作家は本をたくさん書きました|このさっかはほんをたくさんかきました」, this author wrote many books. One small trap: to say you want to become a writer, use 「なりたい」, not 「したい」. So the job word, plus 「さん」 for warmth, and 「書く|かく」 for the work itself." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan takes its writers seriously, and you can even find them on money. The novelist Natsume Soseki, author of the famous cat novel 「吾輩は猫である|わがはいはねこである」, I am a cat, was on the thousand-yen note for about twenty years. Literature also has its own big events. Twice a year, newspapers and TV announce the winners of the 「芥川賞|あくたがわしょう」, a prize for promising new writers, named after the short story writer Akutagawa Ryunosuke. A winning book can suddenly become a bestseller, and the news coverage is surprisingly big. Bookstores in Japan are everywhere, and many people read paperbacks on the train, often with a paper cover from the shop wrapped around the book so nobody can see the title. So an author in Japan is not a distant figure. Their faces show up on money, on the news and on train platforms. That is the world this word lives in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「作|さく」, to make, shrinks to 「さっ」, and 「家|か」 at the end of a word marks a professional, so it is a maker by profession, an author of books and stories.",
      "The ending 「家|か」, as in 「画家|がか」 and 「音楽家|おんがくか」, has a flavor of devotion, a person whose whole house is built around one art or calling.",
      "Fans warmly say 「作家さん|さっかさん」, a novelist is specifically 「小説家|しょうせつか」, and the work itself is simply 「書く|かく」, to write.",
      "Japan takes writers seriously, putting a novelist on the thousand-yen note and treating the 「芥川賞|あくたがわしょう」 for new writers as big news."
    ],
    ja: "あの[作家|さっか]の{本|ほん}がすきです。",
    en: "I like that writer's books." },
  "ジャーナリスト|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword straight from the English word journalist. Listen to how the sounds were fitted into Japanese. The j sound becomes 「ジャ」, a small ya tucked after a ji sound. The ur in the middle of journal becomes a long vowel, 「ジャー」, since Japanese has no ur sound. The n gets its own vowel, 「ナ」, then the l becomes an r sound, 「リ」. And the st at the end splits into two beats, 「ス」 and 「ト」, because Japanese does not stack consonants. So three syllables in English turn into six beats in Japanese. The meaning is the same as English: someone who reports news, for a newspaper, a magazine, TV or online. That links to 「新聞|しんぶん」, a newspaper, and 「雑誌|ざっし」, a magazine, from earlier lessons. A writer of novels, by contrast, is a 「作家|さっか」. A way to remember it: just say journalist slowly and give every consonant its own vowel. A typical sentence: 「姉はジャーナリストです|あねはジャーナリストです」. So: journalist, stretched to Japanese rhythm." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is something that surprises learners. In everyday news talk, Japanese people more often say 「記者|きしゃ」 for a reporter. It is built from 「記|き」, to record, and 「者|しゃ」, a person, the same ending as in 「医者|いしゃ」. A press conference is 「記者会見|きしゃかいけん」, literally a meeting with the reporters. So what does the loanword do? It tends to suggest someone more independent, a freelance writer, a commentator, or someone doing deep investigative work, the kind of person who writes books about politics or war. A newspaper staff member covering a baseball game is usually a 「記者|きしゃ」, while a famous independent reporter on a talk show is introduced as a 「ジャーナリスト」. Both are fine for describing your job. A typical exchange: 「お仕事は|おしごとは」, and the answer, 「ジャーナリストです」. So two words for one field, the plain staff reporter and the more independent journalist." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word belongs to a big family of loanwords that end in 「スト」, the English ist. Once you notice it, you will hear it everywhere. 「ピアニスト」 is a pianist, and 「ギタリスト」 is a guitarist. 「アーティスト」 is an artist, often used for pop singers and bands. 「スタイリスト」 is a stylist, the person who picks clothes for a photo shoot or TV show. And the field itself, journalism, is 「ジャーナリズム」, with the ism ending turned into 「ズム」. In every case the final st of English is split into two beats, a 「ス」 and a 「ト」, because Japanese wants a vowel after each consonant. So if you know an English word ending in ist, you can often guess the Japanese one, and you will usually be right. Just remember the rhythm: lots of short, even beats. That is your loanword shortcut for people and their professions." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English journalist stretched to Japanese rhythm, with a long 「ジャー」 and the final st split into 「ス」 and 「ト」, so three syllables become six beats.",
      "Everyday news talk more often uses 「記者|きしゃ」 for a staff reporter, while the loanword suggests a more independent or investigative journalist.",
      "It belongs to the family of loanwords ending in 「スト」, the English ist, like 「ピアニスト」 and 「アーティスト」, so you can often guess them from English."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[ジャーナリスト]です。",
    en: "My older sister is a journalist." },
  "けいさつかん|警察官": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「警|けい」, means to warn or to guard. Look at the bottom: it contains 「言|げん」, the speech part, which fits giving a warning. The second, 「察|さつ」, means to observe, to look into, to inspect. Together, 「警察|けいさつ」 is the police, the ones who guard and watch over things. The third, 「官|かん」, means a government official. So the full word is a police official, a police officer. It is the formal job title. In everyday speech, people usually say 「おまわりさん」, a friendly word that literally means the honorable one who goes around, because officers patrol the neighborhood. Kids learn that one first. A big part of Japanese policing is the tiny neighborhood police box, where officers help people with directions and lost items. That is why the Genki example asks an officer for directions. If you lose a wallet in Japan, there is a good chance it ends up there. A way to remember it: guard, observe, official. So: the official who guards and watches, a police officer." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's build around the pieces of this word. 「警察|けいさつ」 alone is the police as an organization. 「警察署|けいさつしょ」 is a police station, where 「署|しょ」 means a government office. The small neighborhood police box is a 「交番|こうばん」, which you will see on street corners all over Japan. Now the last kanji, 「官|かん」, a government official. You can also shorten the whole job to 「警官|けいかん」, police officer, which you will hear in news reports. The same official ending is in 「外交官|がいこうかん」, a diplomat, an official of foreign relations. Then compare the friendly word 「おまわりさん」, which kids and adults use when speaking to an officer politely and warmly. The job title sounds like a form or a news story, while 「おまわりさん」 sounds like your local neighborhood helper. So pick the formal title when describing the job, and the warm nickname when you are actually talking to one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you need the police in Japan, the number to call is one one zero, and people say it as 「ひゃくとおばん」. Notice the special reading 「とお」 for the zero here, borrowed from the word for ten, so it sounds smooth and is hard to mishear. For everyday problems, though, you walk to the 「交番|こうばん」. Officers there give directions, help with lost items and chat with locals. Japan has a strong habit of turning in lost things, and people really do get wallets back with the money inside. A mini dialogue: you say 「すみません、駅はどこですか|すみません、えきはどこですか」, excuse me, where is the station? The officer points and explains. You finish with 「ありがとうございました」. When you talk about them afterwards, you might say 「警察官に道を教えてもらいました|けいさつかんにみちをおしえてもらいました」, a police officer showed me the way. So in Japan, the police are as much about directions as about crime." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on sound, so treat it as just a hook. The word starts with 「けい」, which sounds a bit like the English word keg. Then 「さつ」, which sounds like sat. Then 「かん」, like can. Picture this: a police officer in full uniform catches a man who has stolen a beer keg. The officer sat on the keg, holding a can of soda in one hand and a whistle in the other, waiting for backup. Keg, sat, can. Now link it to the kanji. The first one has the speech part at the bottom, so the officer is shouting warnings at the thief. The second is about observing, so he keeps his eyes fixed on him. The third is the official, with his badge shining. So see the officer sitting on the keg, sipping from a can, warning and watching. That is your police officer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「警察|けいさつ」, guarding and observing, is the police, and 「官|かん」 is a government official, so this is the formal title, while people usually say the friendly 「おまわりさん」.",
      "The police station is 「警察署|けいさつしょ」, the street-corner police box is 「交番|こうばん」, and the job title shortens to 「警官|けいかん」 in news reports.",
      "Call 「ひゃくとおばん」 in an emergency, but for everyday problems walk to the 「交番|こうばん」, where officers give directions and handle lost items.",
      "As a sound hook, picture an officer who sat on a stolen keg holding a can, keg, sat, can, warning and watching the thief."
    ],
    ja: "[警察官|けいさつかん]にみちを{聞きました|ききました}。",
    en: "I asked a police officer for directions." },
  "しょうぼうし|消防士": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「消|しょう」, means to put out, to extinguish. You know it with its native reading in 「消す|けす」, to turn off or erase, from lesson six. Look at its left side: the water radical, three little drops. Perfect, since water puts out fire. The second, 「防|ぼう」, means to prevent or to defend against. Together, 「消防|しょうぼう」 means firefighting, both putting fires out and preventing them. The third, 「士|し」, means a trained person, a professional. You will see the same ending in 「弁護士|べんごし」, a lawyer, from lesson thirteen, and in 「宇宙飛行士|うちゅうひこうし」, an astronaut, from this lesson. So the word is a trained person who fights fires, a firefighter. A fun fact: in Japan, the emergency number for fire and ambulances is one one nine, while the police use one one zero. Kids in Japan often say they want to be a firefighter when they grow up. A way to remember it: water to put it out, a wall to prevent it. So: extinguish, prevent, professional." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「消|しょう」, brings a whole family with it. You know 「消す|けす」, to turn off or erase, which is what you do to something. Its partner is 「消える|きえる」, to go out or disappear, which is what something does by itself. So the firefighter makes the fire go out, and then the fire disappears. A pencil eraser is 「消しゴム|けしゴム」, the rubber that erases. Now the second kanji. 「防|ぼう」, to prevent, is in 「予防|よぼう」, prevention, as in prevention of illness. Put the first two kanji together with new endings and you get the gear and places of the job. 「消防車|しょうぼうしゃ」 is a fire engine, with 「車|しゃ」 for vehicle. 「消防署|しょうぼうしょ」 is the fire station, the same office ending as in police station. So once you have the first two kanji, the whole firefighting world opens up." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Firefighting has deep roots in Japan, because old cities were built of wood and paper and burned easily. In the Edo period, the city that later became Tokyo had famous firefighting crews called 「火消し|ひけし」, fire putter-outers, made from 「火|ひ」, fire, and the same kanji you see in this word. These crews were local heroes, known for their courage, their bold coats and their tall banners. There was even a saying that fires and fights were the flowers of Edo, because both happened so often and drew such crowds. Today, besides the professionals, many towns have a volunteer fire corps, the 「消防団|しょうぼうだん」, made of local people with regular jobs. And every January, fire departments hold a New Year parade where performers do acrobatics on top of tall bamboo ladders, a tradition from the old crews. So the modern firefighter carries a long and proud history." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In daily talk, people use this word for any firefighter. Interestingly, inside the fire department, it is also the name of the lowest rank, the starting level, and a broader official word for fire service staff is 「消防官|しょうぼうかん」, with the same official ending as in 「警察官|けいさつかん」. You do not need to worry about that. For conversation, this word is the natural one. In an emergency, you call one one nine and say 「火事です|かじです」, it's a fire, using 「火事|かじ」, a fire in a building. Or you say 「救急車をお願いします|きゅうきゅうしゃをおねがいします」, an ambulance, please, because the same number covers both. Children often say 「消防士になりたい|しょうぼうしになりたい」 when asked about their dreams. Remember that 「火事|かじ」 is the fire you call about, and this word is the person who comes. So one one nine, a fire, and the firefighter on the way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「消|しょう」, with its water radical, means to put out, 「防|ぼう」 means to prevent, and 「士|し」 is a trained professional, so it is a trained person who fights fires.",
      "The first kanji gives 「消す|けす」, to put out, and 「消える|きえる」, to go out, and the first two kanji build 「消防車|しょうぼうしゃ」, a fire engine, and 「消防署|しょうぼうしょ」, the fire station.",
      "Old wooden Edo had heroic crews called 「火消し|ひけし」, and today volunteer corps and New Year ladder acrobatics carry on that proud history.",
      "In an emergency call one one nine and say 「火事です|かじです」, remembering 「火事|かじ」 is the fire you call about and this word is the person who comes."
    ],
    ja: "[消防士|しょうぼうし]はかっこいいです。",
    en: "Firefighters are cool." },
  "きょうし|教師": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「教|きょう」, means to teach. You know it with its native reading in 「教える|おしえる」, to teach, from lesson six. It is also in 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, a textbook, and 「教室|きょうしつ」, a classroom. The second, 「師|し」, means master or expert, a person who has mastered something and passes it on. The same kanji is in 「看護師|かんごし」, a nurse, later in this lesson. So this word is a teaching master, a teacher. Here is the key difference from 「先生|せんせい」. This word is the job title, how you describe the profession: 「母は教師です|はははきょうしです」, my mother is a teacher. But you never call your own teacher by it. To their face, or when talking about them with respect, you say 「先生|せんせい」. A common mistake is to say 「教師|きょうし」 when greeting your teacher, which sounds cold and odd. And when talking about your own teaching job, you use this word, never 「先生|せんせい」, since you would not give yourself that title of respect. A way to remember it: the job title on a business card. So: teach plus master, a teacher as a profession." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the second kanji, 「師|し」, because it carries a special kind of respect. It marks someone who has mastered a skill and can guide others, usually with real training or a license behind it. You will meet it in many job words. 「看護師|かんごし」 is a nurse, from this same lesson. 「医師|いし」 is a doctor, the formal written word you see on hospital signs, while 「医者|いしゃ」 is the everyday one. 「美容師|びようし」 is a hairdresser, a master of beauty. And 「講師|こうし」 is a lecturer or instructor, someone who teaches classes, often part time. Notice the reading stays 「し」 in all of them. Now the first kanji, 「教|きょう」, gives you 「教室|きょうしつ」, a classroom, and 「教会|きょうかい」, a church, a place of teaching. So this word is literally a master of teaching, and the ending puts it among the trained professionals." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for people who teach, and each has its own place. 「先生|せんせい」 is the respectful title you use to address your teacher, and also doctors, lawyers and even famous authors. 「教師|きょうし」 is the neutral job title, the profession. 「講師|こうし」 is an instructor or lecturer, someone who leads classes, like at a language school or cram school, or a university lecturer below professor. 「教授|きょうじゅ」 is a full university professor. And 「コーチ」 is a sports coach, borrowed from English. Here is one more that combines with this word: 「家庭教師|かていきょうし」, a private tutor who comes to your home, from 「家庭|かてい」, household. So if you tutor kids in math, you are a 「家庭教師|かていきょうし」. Choose the word by the setting: a title of respect, a job, a class, a university, or a home." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Teachers in Japan have a big role, often bigger than in many Western countries. Homeroom teachers may visit students' homes, supervise after-school clubs on weekends, and keep an eye on student behavior even outside school. Teaching is also known as a very demanding job with long hours, and that is something Japanese news talks about often. Students greet their teacher together at the start and end of class, standing and bowing, led by the student on duty. Then there is the world outside school. Many students also attend a 「塾|じゅく」, a cram school, in the evenings, where instructors help them prepare for entrance exams. A favorite private option is the 「家庭教師|かていきょうし」, a tutor who comes to your house, often a university student earning extra money. So this profession reaches into classrooms, clubs, homes and evening cram schools. Teaching in Japan is a full-time commitment in every sense." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「教|きょう」 means teach and 「師|し」 means master, so it is the job title for a teacher, but you always address your own teacher as 「先生|せんせい」.",
      "The ending 「師|し」 marks a trained master, as in 「看護師|かんごし」, 「医師|いし」 and 「美容師|びようし」, placing this word among the licensed professionals.",
      "Pick by setting, 「先生|せんせい」 for respect, this word for the profession, 「講師|こうし」 for an instructor, 「教授|きょうじゅ」 for a professor, and 「家庭教師|かていきょうし」 for a home tutor.",
      "Japanese teachers play a big role, supervising clubs and even visiting homes, and the job is known for its long, demanding hours."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は{高校|こうこう}の[教師|きょうし]です。",
    en: "My mother is a high school teacher." },
  "けんきゅうしゃ|研究者": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「研|けん」, means to polish or to grind, and it has the stone radical on the left, like grinding a stone smooth. The second, 「究|きゅう」, means to go to the very bottom of something, to investigate fully. Together, 「研究|けんきゅう」 means research, polishing an idea and digging down to the bottom of it. You will meet that word by itself in lesson sixteen. The third kanji, 「者|しゃ」, means a person who does something. It is the same ending as in 「医者|いしゃ」, a doctor, from lesson ten. So this word is a person who does research, a researcher or scientist. It is used for people at universities, labs and companies. Many students in a 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school, hope to become one. A typical sentence: 「研究者になりたいです|けんきゅうしゃになりたいです」, I want to be a researcher. A way to remember it: polish the stone, dig to the bottom, and you are the person doing it. So: polish, dig deep, person." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「者|しゃ」 means a person, and it is a busy ending. You know 「医者|いしゃ」, a doctor, the person of medicine. 「学者|がくしゃ」 is a scholar, a person of learning, often used for experts in history or literature. 「科学者|かがくしゃ」 is a scientist, a person of science. And 「記者|きしゃ」 is a news reporter, a person who records. Now here is a twist. The same kanji can also take its native reading, 「もの」. You have it in 「怠け者|なまけもの」, a lazy person, and in 「若者|わかもの」, young people. When it is 「しゃ」, the word tends to be a neutral or professional label. When it is 「もの」, it often describes a type of person, sometimes with a bit of judgment. So a researcher and a lazy person share the same kanji, but they sound quite different. That is a good pair to remember." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The heart of this word is 「研究|けんきゅう」, research, and it is one of those nouns that become verbs with 「する」. So 「研究する|けんきゅうする」 means to do research. The most natural way to talk about what you study is 「を研究しています|をけんきゅうしています」, I am researching something, in the ongoing form. For example, 「日本の歴史を研究しています|にほんのれきしをけんきゅうしています」, I research Japanese history. To talk about a goal, you combine it with 「なる」: 「研究者になりたいです|けんきゅうしゃになりたいです」. Notice the difference. The person is a noun, and you become one. The research is an action, and you do it. A university professor's office or lab is 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」, with the room kanji you know from 「教室|きょうしつ」. So a researcher does research, in a research room. One root, three useful words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a proud research tradition, and Japanese scientists have won many Nobel Prizes, especially in physics, chemistry and medicine. When one wins, it is huge news, and the winner's old school and hometown celebrate. Inside Japanese universities, research life centers on the lab or seminar group. Students in their final years join a professor's group and spend much of their time there, sometimes late into the night. The group is called a 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」, the same word as the room itself, so it means both the place and the team. Many researchers also work at big companies, developing cars, electronics or medicine, which is a big part of Japan's economy. If you meet someone who says 「研究者です|けんきゅうしゃです」, a good follow-up question is 「何を研究していますか|なにをけんきゅうしていますか」, what do you research? So the lab is both a room and a family, and research is a respected path." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「研|けん」 is polishing a stone, 「究|きゅう」 is digging to the bottom, together research, and 「者|しゃ」 is the person doing it, a researcher.",
      "Read 「しゃ」, the kanji 「者|しゃ」 gives neutral professional labels like 「学者|がくしゃ」, but read 「もの」 it often judges a type of person, like 「怠け者|なまけもの」, a lazy person.",
      "You become the person with 「なる」 but do the research with 「する」, as in 「日本の歴史を研究しています|にほんのれきしをけんきゅうしています」, in a 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」.",
      "In Japanese universities the 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」 is both the lab room and the team, and Japanese scientists' many Nobel Prizes make research a respected path."
    ],
    ja: "[研究者|けんきゅうしゃ]になりたいです。",
    en: "I want to become a researcher." },
  "うちゅうひこうし|宇宙飛行士": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A long word, but it breaks into three easy chunks, all with Chinese-origin readings. First, 「宇宙|うちゅう」, outer space, the universe. Both of these kanji have the same roof radical on top, the one that shows up in many kanji about houses and covering. So a way to picture it: space is the great roof over everything. Next, 「飛行|ひこう」, flying. The first kanji is to fly, and the second is to go. You already know this pair from 「飛行機|ひこうき」, an airplane, from lesson ten, which is a flying machine. And the second kanji, 「行|こう」, is the same as in 「旅行|りょこう」, a trip, and 「行く|いく」. Last, 「士|し」, a trained professional, the same ending as in 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, a firefighter, and 「弁護士|べんごし」, a lawyer. So the whole word is a trained person who flies in space, an astronaut. Japan has sent several astronauts to the space station, and many children dream of becoming one. A way to remember it: space, flying, professional. So: three chunks stacked together, a space flyer." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each chunk of this long word opens its own little family. Start with 「宇宙|うちゅう」, space. Add 「人|じん」, person, and you get 「宇宙人|うちゅうじん」, a space person, which means an alien. Add 「船|せん」, ship, and you get 「宇宙船|うちゅうせん」, a spaceship. Now the flying part. The first kanji of 「飛行|ひこう」 has a native reading in the verb 「飛ぶ|とぶ」, to fly, which birds and planes do. The second kanji, here read 「こう」, is the same one as in 「行く|いく」, to go, in 「旅行|りょこう」, a trip, and even in 「銀行|ぎんこう」, a bank. Finally the ending 「士|し」, a trained professional, shared with 「消防士|しょうぼうし」 and 「弁護士|べんごし」. Notice how the word is really a chain: space, fly, go, trained person. Once you know each link, you can read 「宇宙船|うちゅうせん」 or 「宇宙人|うちゅうじん」 the first time you see them. Long words in Japanese are often just short ones snapped together." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a real astronaut story, and it has a fun twist that links to this lesson. The first Japanese person in space was not a trained astronaut at all. In nineteen ninety, a TV news reporter, a 「ジャーナリスト」, flew to the Soviet space station Mir, paid for by his TV company. Two years later, Mohri Mamoru flew on an American space shuttle as Japan's first astronaut from the national space program. Since then, several Japanese astronauts have lived on the International Space Station, and one of them, Wakata Koichi, became the station's first Japanese commander in two thousand fourteen. Japan's space agency is called JAXA, and you will hear its name read like a word on the news. Astronaut launches are big events on Japanese TV, and children follow them closely. So the dream of 「宇宙飛行士になりたい|うちゅうひこうしになりたい」 is a very real one in Japan." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Seven beats is a lot to say smoothly, so let's practice the rhythm. Break it into three steps: 「うちゅう」, then 「ひこう」, then 「し」. In the first chunk, the small 「ゅ」 is tucked into the 「ち」, making one beat, 「ちゅ」, and then the 「う」 stretches it, so it is 「う」, 「ちゅ」, 「う」, three beats, not four. Don't say it like the English word chew with a quick vowel. Give that long vowel its full length. In 「ひこう」, also stretch the final 「こう」 into a long o sound, like in 「飛行機|ひこうき」. Many learners swallow these long vowels and the word comes out too short. Try tapping your fingers on the table: one tap for each beat, seven taps in total. Then say it a few times faster, keeping the taps even. Japanese rhythm is very regular, and long words become easy once each beat gets the same time. So: three chunks, seven even taps, and two stretched vowels." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It stacks three chunks, 「宇宙|うちゅう」, space, 「飛行|ひこう」, flying, as in 「飛行機|ひこうき」, and 「士|し」, a trained professional, so it is a space flyer.",
      "Each chunk opens a family, like 「宇宙人|うちゅうじん」, an alien, 「宇宙船|うちゅうせん」, a spaceship, and 「飛ぶ|とぶ」, to fly, so long words are short ones snapped together.",
      "The first Japanese person in space was actually a TV reporter, and later national astronauts flew, with one becoming commander of the International Space Station.",
      "Say it in three chunks with seven even beats, 「うちゅう」, 「ひこう」, 「し」, giving both long vowels their full length."
    ],
    ja: "{将来|しょうらい}、[宇宙飛行士|うちゅうひこうし]になりたいです。",
    en: "I want to be an astronaut in the future." },
  "スポーツせんしゅ|スポーツ選手": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A mix of a loanword and a kanji word. The first part, 「スポーツ」, comes from the English word sports, with a long vowel for the or sound and extra vowels so each consonant gets its own beat. You have known it since lesson three. The second part, 「選手|せんしゅ」, uses Chinese-origin readings. 「選|せん」 means to choose, to select. Look at its bottom left: the long movement stroke you also see in 「遅刻|ちこく」. 「手|しゅ」 means hand, and here a skilled person, the same as in 「歌手|かしゅ」, a singer, from this lesson. So 「選手|せんしゅ」 is a chosen skilled person, the player picked for the team, an athlete. You can swap in a specific sport: a baseball player is 「野球選手|やきゅうせんしゅ」, and a soccer player is 「サッカー選手|サッカーせんしゅ」. Baseball is huge in Japan, and the summer high school tournament is a national event. A way to remember it: the coach chooses you, and you are the hand that plays. So: sports, plus chosen player, an athlete." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is one of the most useful things about 「選手|せんしゅ」: it works as a title after a name. On TV, sports commentators and reporters call athletes by their family name plus 「選手|せんしゅ」, just as you would add 「さん」 or 「先生|せんせい」. So a baseball star named Tanaka becomes 「田中選手|たなかせんしゅ」 on the news. It sounds respectful and neutral, perfect for interviews and broadcasts. You can also put any sport in front: 「テニス選手|テニスせんしゅ」, a tennis player, 「水泳選手|すいえいせんしゅ」, a swimmer. And for school teams, a team member is often just 「選手|せんしゅ」. If you want to say you play a sport as a hobby, though, don't call yourself this. Just say 「テニスをします」. The word is for athletes who compete seriously. So: a job, a title after a name, and a word for serious competitors." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in 「選手|せんしゅ」 have useful relatives. The first, 「選|せん」, to choose, has a native reading in 「選ぶ|えらぶ」, to choose, which you will learn soon. It is also in 「選挙|せんきょ」, an election, where people choose their leaders. Now 「手|しゅ」, hand. With its native reading 「て」, it is the hand on your body. But it also stands for a person with a skill, someone whose hands do the work. You know 「歌手|かしゅ」, a singer, from this lesson. 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」 is a driver, a person who drives. And look at 「上手|じょうず」, good at, and 「下手|へた」, bad at, which use the same kanji to talk about skill level. Even 「相手|あいて」, an opponent or partner, uses it. So 「手|て」 is not just a hand. It is the person and the skill behind the hand, and an athlete is a chosen pair of skilled hands." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Baseball is probably Japan's most beloved sport, and the national high school tournament is a summer tradition. It is played at a stadium called 「甲子園|こうしえん」 near Osaka, and the name of the stadium is used for the tournament itself. Teams from every prefecture compete, and the whole country watches on TV. A famous custom: players from losing teams often scoop up a little of the stadium's dirt and take it home as a memory. Many professional players first became famous there as teenagers. Soccer, sumo, figure skating and marathon running are also hugely popular. Sumo wrestlers, though, are usually called 「力士|りきし」, with the professional ending 「士|し」, rather than 「選手|せんしゅ」, because sumo is treated as a traditional art as much as a sport. So when you hear 「選手|せんしゅ」, think of the high school pitcher at 「甲子園|こうしえん」 dreaming of the big leagues." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「選|せん」 means choose and 「手|しゅ」 means a skilled person, so 「選手|せんしゅ」 is the player chosen for the team, and you can swap in any sport, like 「野球選手|やきゅうせんしゅ」.",
      "On the news 「選手|せんしゅ」 is a respectful title after a name, like 「田中選手|たなかせんしゅ」, but for a hobby just say 「テニスをします」 instead.",
      "「手|て」 is not just a hand but the skilled person behind it, as in 「歌手|かしゅ」, 「運転手|うんてんしゅ」 and 「上手|じょうず」, so an athlete is a chosen pair of skilled hands.",
      "The summer high school baseball tournament at 「甲子園|こうしえん」 is a national event, while sumo wrestlers are called 「力士|りきし」 rather than 「選手|せんしゅ」."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[スポーツ選手|スポーツせんしゅ]です。",
    en: "My older brother is an athlete." },
  "はいゆう|俳優": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. Both have the person radical on the left, the thin standing person, so you know right away this word is about people. The first, 「俳|はい」, means a performer or entertainer in old usage. You may know it from 「俳句|はいく」, the famous short poem. The second, 「優|ゆう」, also meant a performer long ago. Today it also means excellent or gentle. With its native reading it is 「優しい|やさしい」, kind, the same sound as the gentle 「やさしい」 from lesson five. So the word is two old words for performer, put together: an actor. It is used for film, TV and stage actors, and for both men and women. If you want to say actress specifically, there is 「女優|じょゆう」, a woman performer, which is still very common. Japan also has famous traditional actors in 「歌舞伎|かぶき」, all of them men, even in women's roles. A typical sentence: 「あの俳優はとても有名です|あのはいゆうはとてもゆうめいです」. A way to remember it: two people standing on a stage. So: performer plus performer, an actor." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「優|ゆう」, builds several words about performers. 「女優|じょゆう」, a woman performer, is an actress. 「声優|せいゆう」, a voice performer, is a voice actor, a hugely popular job in Japan because of anime and video games. Famous voice actors have big fan clubs and give concerts. There is also 「男優|だんゆう」, a male actor, but it is less common, since 「俳優|はいゆう」 already covers men. Then the same kanji takes another meaning, excellent, in 「優勝|ゆうしょう」, winning a championship. And in its native reading, it is 「優しい|やさしい」, kind and gentle. Now compare some related words. 「役者|やくしゃ」 is another word for an actor, a person who plays a role, often used for stage and traditional theater. And 「芸能人|げいのうじん」 is a celebrity, anyone in show business. So pick 「俳優|はいゆう」 for the profession, and 「声優|せいゆう」 for the voices behind your favorite anime." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji have the standing person radical on the left, the thin shape that looks like a person in profile. Now look at the right sides. The right side of 「俳|はい」 is 「非|ひ」, a kanji meaning not or wrong, which you will meet in words like 「非常口|ひじょうぐち」, an emergency exit. Here it mostly helps give the sound 「はい」, rather than the meaning. The right side of 「優|ゆう」 is a more complex shape, the kanji 「憂|ゆう」, which means sorrow or worry. Again, it gives the sound, so a person plus the 「ゆう」 sound. Many kanji work like this. One side gives a hint about meaning, like the person radical, and the other side gives a hint about the sound. Once you spot this, you can often guess the reading of a new kanji. Just be careful, because the sound hint is not always reliable. So: two standing people, each with a sound part, and together, an actor." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When Japanese people talk about actors, the word comes up in a few common ways. To ask about someone's favorite, you say 「好きな俳優はだれですか|すきなはいゆうはだれですか」. Fans often use 「さん」 after an actor's name, even for big stars. To talk about the job itself: 「俳優になりたいです|はいゆうになりたいです」, I want to become an actor. Many Japanese actors appear in TV dramas, which often run for one season of about ten or eleven episodes, so a hit drama can make an actor famous overnight. They also appear in commercials constantly, so you will see the same faces on trains and billboards. Another word you will hear is 「タレント」, a TV personality, from the English talent, for people who appear on variety shows but may not act at all. So an actor plays roles, and a 「タレント」 just appears on TV being entertaining." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Both kanji carry the person radical and both once meant performer, so the word is performer plus performer, an actor of either gender, with 「女優|じょゆう」 for actress.",
      "The 「優|ゆう」 kanji builds 「声優|せいゆう」, a hugely popular voice actor job thanks to anime, and also 「優勝|ゆうしょう」, championship, and 「優しい|やさしい」, kind.",
      "Each kanji pairs the person radical for meaning with a sound part, 「非|ひ」 giving 「はい」 and 「憂|ゆう」 giving 「ゆう」, a common way kanji hint at their readings.",
      "Ask 「好きな俳優はだれですか|すきなはいゆうはだれですか」, and remember an actor plays roles while a 「タレント」 is a TV personality who may not act at all."
    ],
    ja: "あの[俳優|はいゆう]はとても{有名|ゆうめい}です。",
    en: "That actor is very famous." },
  "かんごし|看護師": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「看|かん」, means to watch over carefully. Its shape is traditionally explained as a hand held over an eye, shading it to look closely. You can see the eye 「目|め」 at the bottom. The second, 「護|ご」, means to protect. It is also in 「弁護士|べんごし」, a lawyer, from lesson thirteen, someone who protects you in court. Together, 「看護|かんご」 means nursing care, watching over and protecting a patient. The third, 「師|し」, means a professional, a master of a skill. It is the same one as in 「教師|きょうし」, a teacher, from this lesson. So the whole word is a nursing professional, a nurse. Here is a nice bit of history. The old word ended in a kanji meaning woman, because nurses were assumed to be female. Around two thousand two, the official title changed to this one, so it covers everyone. You will still hear people say 「看護婦|かんごふ」 sometimes. A way to remember it: a hand over the eye, watching the patient. So: watch, protect, professional, a nurse." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The pieces of this word build a family about care. 「看病|かんびょう」 is looking after someone who is sick, usually at home, like caring for a child with a fever. It combines the watching kanji with 「病|びょう」 from 「病気|びょうき」. Then the second kanji, 「護|ご」, to protect. 「介護|かいご」 means caring for the elderly or disabled, a very important word in Japan, where the population is aging fast. 「保護|ほご」 is protection, as in protecting animals or children. And you know it in 「弁護士|べんごし」, a lawyer. The first kanji also shows up somewhere unexpected: 「看板|かんばん」, a signboard, the board that people look at, outside a shop. So the core idea is careful watching and protecting. Put it together with the professional ending 「師|し」, and you get a trained expert in watching over and protecting people. That's a nurse." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a Japanese hospital, how do you address the staff? Doctors are called 「先生|せんせい」, just like teachers. Nurses are usually called 「看護師さん|かんごしさん」, using the job title plus 「さん」. That's the polite, normal choice, and you can use it to get a nurse's attention: 「すみません、看護師さん|すみません、かんごしさん」. A common mistake is calling a nurse 「先生|せんせい」, which would sound a bit odd, since that title belongs to the doctor. A typical hospital moment: the nurse calls your name in the waiting room, takes your temperature and asks about your symptoms, and you say things like 「熱があります|ねつがあります」 or 「のどがいたいです」. When talking about someone's job, you say 「姉は看護師です|あねはかんごしです」. So remember: doctors are 「先生|せんせい」, nurses are 「看護師さん|かんごしさん」, and the symptoms from this lesson are what you tell them." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「看|かん」 is a hand over an eye watching closely, 「護|ご」 is protect, and 「師|し」 is a professional, and this gender-neutral title replaced the older 「看護婦|かんごふ」.",
      "The pieces build a family about care, like 「看病|かんびょう」, nursing a sick person at home, and 「介護|かいご」, elder care, all about careful watching and protecting.",
      "In a Japanese hospital, doctors are 「先生|せんせい」, but nurses are called 「看護師さん|かんごしさん」, so calling a nurse 「先生|せんせい」 sounds odd."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は{病院|びょういん}の[看護師|かんごし]です。",
    en: "My older sister is a nurse at a hospital." },
  "シェフ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one comes from French. The French word chef simply means chief or head, and it is the same root as the English word chief. A French head cook is the chef de cuisine, the head of the kitchen, and English and Japanese both kept just the first word. Look at how the sounds fit into Japanese. Japanese has no native sheh sound, so it uses a special combination, a big 「シ」 with a small 「ェ」, made for foreign words. And because a Japanese word can't end on an f sound, the final sound gets an extra u vowel and becomes 「フ」. So you get 「シェフ」. It's also a loanword sibling of 「レストラン」, another French word that came in through cooking and dining. In Japanese, 「シェフ」 has a stylish feel. It suggests a trained cook at a proper restaurant, often Western-style, and on TV you'll hear famous cooks called by name plus 「シェフ」, almost like a title. A plain everyday cook is more often 「コック」, which came from Dutch. To recap: French chef, head of the kitchen, with an extra vowel tacked on the end." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for cooks, and each paints a different picture. 「シェフ」 suggests a head chef in a Western-style restaurant, French or Italian. 「コック」, from Dutch, is a cook in general, also Western-style, and a bit old fashioned now. 「料理人|りょうりにん」 is a cooking person, a neutral word for a professional cook of any kind, built from 「料理|りょうり」, cooking. For Japanese cuisine, especially sushi and traditional restaurants, the cook is an 「板前|いたまえ」, literally the one in front of the board, meaning the cutting board. And for desserts, there is 「パティシエ」, a pastry chef, also from French, a popular dream job for Japanese kids. So if you go to a sushi bar, don't call the chef 「シェフ」. Call them an 「板前|いたまえ」, or more casually, 「大将|たいしょう」, boss. The word you choose tells everyone what kind of food is being made." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "On a restaurant menu, you might see 「シェフのおすすめ」, the chef's recommendation. That's a very common phrase, and ordering it is a safe bet. At a nice Western-style restaurant, you might also see 「シェフ」 used as a title after a name, like a famous chef introduced on TV as Name plus 「シェフ」. Cooking shows are very popular in Japan, and some chefs become celebrities. When you enjoy a meal, you might say to the staff 「とてもおいしかったです」, it was delicious, and they may pass it on to the chef. Sometimes the chef comes out to greet guests. A natural sentence about the job: 「兄はレストランのシェフです|あにはレストランのシェフです」, my brother is a chef at a restaurant. Notice the 「の」 linking the restaurant and the chef. So the word fits menus, TV shows and job talk, always with a stylish, Western-restaurant feel." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From French chef, meaning head of the kitchen, it uses the special 「シェ」 sound plus an extra vowel in 「フ」, and it has a stylish Western-restaurant feel compared with the plainer 「コック」.",
      "Each cook word paints a picture, so at a sushi bar call the cook an 「板前|いたまえ」, not this word, and a pastry chef is a 「パティシエ」.",
      "On menus 「シェフのおすすめ」, the chef's recommendation, is a safe order, and on TV famous cooks get this word as a title after their name."
    ],
    ja: "あの[シェフ]のりょうりはおいしいです。",
    en: "That chef's food is delicious." },
  "まんがか|漫画家": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's break it into three kanji. 「漫|まん」 means rambling, free-flowing, aimless, using its Chinese-origin reading. 「画|が」 means picture, and you know it from 「映画|えいが」, a movie, literally moving pictures, and from 「映画館|えいがかん」. Put them together and 「漫画|まんが」 is free, loose pictures: drawings done for fun rather than serious fine art. The great woodblock artist Hokusai used this word for his famous sketchbooks over two hundred years ago, long before modern comics. Then comes 「家|か」. On its own with its native reading, 「家|いえ」 is a house, and it's in 「家族|かぞく」, family. But tacked on the end of a word with the Chinese-origin reading 「か」, it means a person who makes a profession of something, a specialist. You've met it in 「作家|さっか」, a writer, literally a person of making, and you'll see it in 「政治家|せいじか」, a politician. So 「漫画家|まんがか」 is a professional of loose, playful pictures. In Japan this is a serious and respected career, and famous ones are household names. To recap: 「漫画|まんが」, comics, plus 「家|か」, the professional who makes them." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「漫|まん」, has the water radical on the left, the three drops, and its meaning of spreading out freely, aimlessly, fits that flowing water. It shows up in a fun word: 「漫才|まんざい」, a traditional style of stand-up comedy done by a duo, one silly person and one straight person who corrects them. Then the second kanji, 「画|が」, picture. You know 「映画|えいが」, a movie, and 「映画館|えいがかん」, a movie theater. 「画家|がか」 is a painter, and here 「画|が」 is followed by the same 「家|か」 professional ending as in this word. So a painter and a manga artist are close cousins in Japanese: picture professional, and loose picture professional. Also notice 「漫画|まんが」 is often written in katakana, 「マンガ」, on shop signs and covers, to look fun and modern. So: flowing water, pictures, and the person who makes them." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Manga is everywhere in Japan, read by children and adults alike. The most famous manga artist of all is Tezuka Osamu, called the god of manga, who created Astro Boy after World War Two and shaped how modern Japanese comics look and tell stories. Today many popular series first appear in thick weekly magazines, cheap enough to read on the train. The best known is 「少年ジャンプ|しょうねんジャンプ」, a magazine for boys, though everyone reads it. A manga artist's life is famously hard. Many produce a full chapter every week, with a team of assistants helping with backgrounds and details, and the word for the deadline, 「締め切り|しめきり」, is something every artist fears. Popular series are then made into anime, movies and games. So becoming a 「漫画家|まんがか」 is a dream for many young people, but one that means a lot of late nights." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, a silly scene to link sound and meaning. The word starts with 「まん」, which sounds like the English man. Then 「が」, like a little gasp. Then 「か」, like a crow's caw. Picture a man at his drawing desk at three in the morning. He's on deadline, surrounded by piles of paper, ink on his face. He finishes the last page, and gasps, ga! And then a crow outside his window says caw! Man, ga, caw. Now add the kanji. The first one has water drops on its left side, so imagine his coffee spilling across the desk in a flowing stream. The second one is a picture, his finished comic page. And the last is a house, because he hasn't left his house in a week. So: a man, a gasp, a crow, spilled coffee and a finished picture. That is your manga artist." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「漫画|まんが」 is loose, free-flowing pictures, a word Hokusai used for his sketchbooks, and 「家|か」 marks the professional, so it is the person who makes comics as a career.",
      "「漫|まん」 has the water radical for spreading freely, as in the comedy style 「漫才|まんざい」, and with 「画|が」 it makes a close cousin of 「画家|がか」, a painter.",
      "Manga artists like Tezuka Osamu are household names, but the life is famously hard, with weekly chapters and a dreaded 「締め切り|しめきり」, deadline.",
      "Picture a man finishing his last page at three in the morning, gasping ga as a crow caws, man, ga, caw, with coffee spilling across his desk."
    ],
    ja: "[漫画家|まんがか]になりたいです。",
    en: "I want to become a cartoonist." },
  "だいとうりょう|大統領": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with their Chinese-origin readings. 「大|だい」 means big or great, the same as in 「大学|だいがく」, college, and 「大好き|だいすき」. 「統|とう」 means to unite or bring together under one control; it has the thread radical on the left, like gathering many threads into one. 「領|りょう」 means to govern or to hold territory. The pair 「統領|とうりょう」 already meant a leader who commands everyone, and putting 「大|だい」 in front makes it the great leader. Japanese built this word in the eighteen-hundreds, when it needed a way to talk about the head of the United States. That's why it's used for elected heads of countries, like the American or French president. Here's a common mix-up for English speakers: president in English covers companies too, but in Japanese the head of a company is 「社長|しゃちょう」, with the 「社|しゃ」 of 「会社|かいしゃ」. Japan itself has no 「大統領|だいとうりょう」; its leader is the prime minister, 「総理大臣|そうりだいじん」. To recap: the great, 「大|だい」, uniting leader, for countries only." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「大|だい」, is a picture of a person standing with arms and legs spread wide, making themselves big. It's one of the most useful kanji you know, and it has several readings. In this word it is 「だい」, as in 「大学|だいがく」 and 「大好き|だいすき」. But it can also be 「たい」, as in 「大変|たいへん」, tough, and 「大切|たいせつ」, important. With its native reading, it becomes 「おお」, as in 「大きい|おおきい」, big, and 「大家さん|おおやさん」, a landlord. And there is a special reading you just have to memorize: 「大人|おとな」, an adult. So how do you know which one to use? Mostly, you learn each word as a unit. A rough rule: when 「大|だい」 or 「たい」 appears, the next kanji usually has a Chinese-origin reading too, like here. So the big person standing tall at the front of this word is a good picture for a nation's leader." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "On the news, this word is very often used as a title after a name, just like 「さん」 or 「先生|せんせい」. So Abraham Lincoln becomes 「リンカーン大統領|リンカーンだいとうりょう」, President Lincoln. When there is an election, it's 「大統領選挙|だいとうりょうせんきょ」, the presidential election, using 「選挙|せんきょ」, an election, which shares a kanji with 「選手|せんしゅ」. For Japan's own leader, you will hear 「首相|しゅしょう」, the prime minister, on the news, or 「総理|そうり」 for short in conversation. People also say 「総理大臣|そうりだいじん」 in full. A typical classroom question is 「アメリカの大統領はだれですか|アメリカのだいとうりょうはだれですか」. You could answer with a name plus 「です」. A common mistake is using this word for Japan's leader. Japan has a prime minister, and an emperor as a symbol of the state, but no president. So presidents abroad, prime minister at home." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's compare words for people at the top. 「大統領|だいとうりょう」 is the head of a country with a president. 「首相|しゅしょう」 is a prime minister, using 「首|しゅ」, neck or head. 「大臣|だいじん」 is a government minister, like the minister of education. 「社長|しゃちょう」 is the president of a company, the head of the 「会社|かいしゃ」. 「会長|かいちょう」 is a chairperson, the head of a group or the chairman of a company board. You may recognize 「長|ちょう」, meaning chief or head, in both of those. And for a kingdom, there is 「王様|おうさま」, a king, with the respectful 「様|さま」. So in English, president covers a nation and a company, but Japanese splits them very clearly. If you call a company boss 「大統領|だいとうりょう」, everyone will laugh. Remember the 「長|ちょう」 family for companies and groups, and save this word for countries." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「大|だい」, great, plus 「統領|とうりょう」, a uniting leader, makes the head of a country, while a company president is 「社長|しゃちょう」 and Japan's leader is the prime minister.",
      "「大|だい」 is a person standing with arms spread wide, with readings 「だい」, 「たい」, 「おお」 and the special 「大人|おとな」, so learn each word as a unit.",
      "On the news it is a title after a name, like 「リンカーン大統領|リンカーンだいとうりょう」, but Japan's own leader is 「首相|しゅしょう」 or 「総理|そうり」, since Japan has no president.",
      "Japanese splits English president clearly, keeping this word for countries and the 「長|ちょう」 family, like 「社長|しゃちょう」 and 「会長|かいちょう」, for companies and groups."
    ],
    ja: "アメリカの[大統領|だいとうりょう]はだれですか。",
    en: "Who is the president of America?" },
  "おなか|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is two pieces. First the polite prefix 「お」, the same softening 「お」 you hear in 「お金|おかね」 and 「お茶|おちゃ」. Then 「なか」, which you know as middle or inside, the word written 「中|なか」. So your stomach is, literally, your honorable middle. It's the soft, polite, everyday way to talk about your belly, and you'll hear it from everyone, kids and adults. There is a kanji spelling, 「お腹|おなか」, but kana is very common. You already met it in 「おなかがすく」, to get hungry, literally the stomach becomes empty. Two other patterns are worth knowing. 「おなかがいたい」 means my stomach hurts, and 「おなかがいっぱい」 means I'm full. A common mistake for English speakers is using it only for the organ inside. In Japanese it covers the whole tummy area, so a baby's round belly or a cat's fluffy underside is also 「おなか」. There's a blunter word for belly, 「はら」, but that one sounds rough, so stick with 「おなか」 to be safe. To recap: polite 「お」 plus 「なか」, the middle of you." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Many useful phrases are built on this word, and each one uses a different verb or adjective. 「おなかがすく」 means to get hungry, literally the stomach becomes empty. Usually you say it in the past, 「おなかがすきました」, I'm hungry. 「おなかがいっぱい」 means full, the stomach is full up. 「おなかがいたい」 is a stomachache. And 「おなかをこわす」, literally to break your stomach, means to have an upset stomach, often from eating something bad or too much. There is also the blunt native word 「はら」, written 「腹|はら」, with the flesh radical on the left, the same shape that looks like the moon. That one shows up in strong idioms like 「腹が立つ|はらがたつ」, literally the belly stands up, meaning to get angry. It sounds rough in normal speech, so men use it casually, but you are safest with the polite one. So: empty, full, hurting and broken, all with this soft, friendly stomach word." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese parents have a famous warning for kids during summer thunderstorms. They say that the thunder god, 「かみなりさま」, will come and steal your belly button if your tummy is showing. The belly button is 「おへそ」, so children cover their tummies and hide their 「おへそ」 when thunder rumbles. One common explanation is that it was a way to stop children from sleeping with their stomachs uncovered and catching a chill when the temperature suddenly drops before a storm. Keeping your stomach warm is a real habit in Japan. Some people, especially older people, wear a 「腹巻き|はらまき」, a knitted band that wraps around the belly to keep it warm, and you can buy them in stores everywhere. The idea is that a cold stomach leads to illness. So if a Japanese friend tells you to cover your tummy at night, they're passing on a very old piece of advice." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just for fun. Picture a cartoon character, a big round man with a round belly, who shouts 「お」 in surprise every time someone pokes his tummy. Now remember that 「なか」 means the middle or the inside. So he's the guy with the famous middle: poke it, and he says oh! Now imagine him in each of the phrases you know. Before lunch, he pats his belly and moans, his middle is empty. After a huge bowl of ramen, he leans back in his chair, his middle is full. After eating too much ice cream, he groans and clutches his middle, it hurts. And when the thunder rolls, he quickly pulls his shirt down to cover his belly button. So whenever you need the word, picture the man with the famous middle, saying oh. That's your polite, honorable middle." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The polite 「お」 plus 「なか」, middle, makes your honorable middle, the soft everyday word for the whole tummy area, while the blunt 「はら」 sounds rough.",
      "Phrases built on it include 「おなかがすく」, hungry, 「おなかがいっぱい」, full, 「おなかがいたい」, a stomachache, and 「おなかをこわす」, an upset stomach.",
      "Japanese kids hear that the thunder god will steal their 「おへそ」 if their tummy shows, a warning tied to the real habit of keeping your stomach warm.",
      "Picture a round man who shouts oh whenever someone pokes his famous middle, then see him hungry, full and hurting, to remember the honorable middle."
    ],
    ja: "[おなか]がいたいです。",
    en: "My stomach hurts." },
  "あし|足": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is a picture of a leg. The bottom part looks like a foot taking a step, and the box on top is usually explained as the knee. That's a handy way to see it: knee on top, foot at the bottom, the whole leg in one character. And that matches the word. English splits leg and foot, but Japanese 「足|あし」 covers both, from the hip all the way to the toes. So 「足がいたい|あしがいたい」 could mean your feet hurt after a long walk or your legs ache after a run, and context tells you which. The reading here is the native Japanese one, 「あし」. The same kanji also carries the idea of being enough, as in 「足りる|たりる」, to be sufficient. A way to remember it: having enough is like standing on your own two feet. You'll also see 「足|あし」 used for animals and furniture, like the legs of a table. Look at 「歩く|あるく」, to walk: its top part is 「止|し」, another old drawing of a foot. Feet and walking keep showing up in these shapes. To recap: one kanji, one word, for the whole leg down to the foot." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The same kanji shows up in several words where its meaning shifts from leg to adding or enough. 「足りる|たりる」 means to be enough. 「足す|たす」 means to add, as in adding numbers, two plus three. A school field trip is 「遠足|えんそく」, literally a far walk, from 「遠い|とおい」, far, and here the kanji takes its Chinese-origin reading, 「そく」. That same reading appears when counting pairs of shoes or socks: 「一足|いっそく」, one pair, 「二足|にそく」, two pairs, and 「三足|さんぞく」, three pairs, with the sound changing to 「ぞく」. Finally, there's 「足|あし」 used for transportation. People say 「足がない|あしがない」, I don't have legs, meaning I don't have a way to get there, no car and no train. So a single kanji covers legs, walking, pairs of shoes, enough and adding. The key reading for your body part is still 「あし」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some everyday phrases with this word. 「足が速い|あしがはやい」 means a fast runner, literally fast legs. 「足が遅い|あしがおそい」 is a slow runner. After a long day of sightseeing, you might say 「足がつかれました|あしがつかれました」, my legs are tired. In Japan, many homes, schools, temples and some restaurants ask you to take off your shoes at the entrance, so your feet are on display more than you might expect. Wear socks without holes. A fun spot to rest tired feet is an 「足湯|あしゆ」, a foot bath, often free, found in hot spring towns and sometimes at train stations, where you sit and soak just your feet in warm spring water. And here is a spelling note: when people want to be clear they mean the leg rather than the foot, they sometimes write a different kanji, 「脚|あし」. But in daily writing, 「足|あし」 covers both. So fast legs, tired legs, and a warm foot bath at the end of the day." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on sound. The word 「あし」 sounds a little like the English word ash. Picture yourself walking barefoot across a beach after a bonfire. The sand is covered in gray ash, and with every step, your legs get ashy from your toes up to your knees. Look down: ash on your feet, ash on your ankles, ash on your shins. That reminds you that the Japanese word covers the whole leg, not just the foot. Now match it to the kanji. The box on top is your ashy knee, and the bottom part is your foot stepping forward through the ash. Each time you take a step, a little cloud of ash puffs up. So when you hear 「あし」, think ash all the way up your legs, and see the knee and foot of the kanji walking along the beach. That's your leg, and your foot." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji pictures a whole leg, the box on top as the knee and a stepping foot below, and the word covers everything from hip to toes, both leg and foot.",
      "The same kanji means enough in 「足りる|たりる」 and add in 「足す|たす」, and read 「そく」 it counts pairs of shoes, as in 「一足|いっそく」.",
      "Phrases like 「足が速い|あしがはやい」, a fast runner, and 「足がつかれました|あしがつかれました」 are everyday staples, and hot spring towns offer an 「足湯|あしゆ」, a foot bath, for tired feet.",
      "It sounds like ash, so picture walking barefoot through bonfire ash that coats your legs from toes to knees, reminding you the word covers the whole leg."
    ],
    ja: "たくさん{歩いて|あるいて}、[足|あし]がいたいです。",
    en: "I walked a lot, and my legs hurt." },
  "は|歯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The reading here is the native Japanese one, just 「は」, a single sound. Look at the kanji. The bottom part shows rows of teeth inside an open mouth, a frame with little marks lined up inside it. The 「止|し」 sitting on top originally helped give the character its sound. So when you see the boxy bottom with teeth inside, think of a mouth full of teeth. The best sibling word is 「歯医者|はいしゃ」, a dentist. You know 「医者|いしゃ」, a doctor, and putting 「歯|は」 in front makes a tooth doctor. It's an easy one to build yourself. To brush your teeth is 「歯をみがく|はをみがく」, using the verb for polishing. Watch out for one thing: there are many words pronounced 「は」 in Japanese, such as 「葉|は」, a leaf, and of course the particle 「は」. Context sorts them out, and the tooth one is almost always followed by something like 「がいたい」 or 「をみがく」. A small cultural note: when a child loses a baby tooth in Japan, the tradition is to throw a lower tooth up onto the roof and an upper tooth down below, so the new one grows in straight. To recap: one sound, 「は」, a mouth full of teeth." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji teams up with other words to make a family of tooth vocabulary. 「歯医者|はいしゃ」 is a dentist, a tooth doctor. 「歯ブラシ|はブラシ」 is a toothbrush, mixing the kanji with the English loanword brush. 「歯みがき|はみがき」 is brushing your teeth, and also toothpaste. Now listen to what happens when a word comes before it. A cavity is 「虫歯|むしば」, literally an insect tooth, from 「虫|むし」, a bug, and the 「は」 changes to 「ば」. That sound change, where the start of the second word gets voiced, is very common in Japanese compounds. It also happens in 「八重歯|やえば」, a snaggletooth, literally an eight-layer tooth, meaning a tooth growing in front of the others. So once the tooth word comes second, listen for 「ば」. One more: wisdom teeth are 「親知らず|おやしらず」, literally parents don't know, because they come in after you've grown up and left your parents. So the dentist, the cavity, and the tooth your parents never saw." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At the dentist in Japan, the key phrase is 「歯がいたいです|はがいたいです」, my tooth hurts. You can point and say 「この歯です|このはです」, this tooth. The dentist will probably ask if you have a cavity, using 「虫歯|むしば」. In daily life, brushing your teeth is 「歯をみがく|はをみがく」, using 「みがく」, to polish. Japanese office workers and students often brush their teeth after lunch, right in the office or school bathroom, so don't be surprised to see people with toothbrushes at the sink at work. Many elementary schools even have a brushing time after school lunch. Here is a mistake to avoid: the particle 「は」 and this word sound the same. In the sentence 「歯は白いです|ははしろいです」, teeth are white, you get two 「は」 sounds in a row, one a word and one a particle. Japanese people handle this fine, so don't worry. So: hurts, polish, and brush after lunch." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows rows of teeth inside an open mouth, and putting it before 「医者|いしゃ」 gives 「歯医者|はいしゃ」, a tooth doctor, a dentist.",
      "When it comes second it often voices to 「ば」, as in 「虫歯|むしば」, a cavity, literally an insect tooth, while wisdom teeth are 「親知らず|おやしらず」, parents don't know.",
      "At the dentist say 「歯がいたいです|はがいたいです」, and don't be surprised to see Japanese workers and students brushing their teeth, 「歯をみがく|はをみがく」, after lunch."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[歯|は]をみがきます。",
    en: "I brush my teeth every day." },
  "のど|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, usually written in kana in everyday life, though it does have a kanji, 「喉|のど」, with the mouth radical on the left. It covers your throat from the inside, where you swallow and where your voice comes from. A way to remember it: 「のど」 starts like 「のむ」, to drink, and your throat is exactly where drinks go down. That's a hook, not a word history. The two big patterns in this lesson are about being sick or thirsty. 「のどがいたい」 means I have a sore throat, a classic symptom you'd tell a doctor along with 「せき」, a cough, and 「熱|ねつ」, a fever. And 「のどがかわく」 means your throat dries out, which is how Japanese says you're thirsty. A common mistake for English speakers is to say thirsty with an adjective. Japanese describes the throat instead. One fun cultural note: Japan has a very long-running TV singing contest for ordinary people called 「のど自慢|のどじまん」, literally bragging about your throat, meaning showing off your singing voice. So 「のど」 is also your singing voice. To recap: the throat, where you swallow, where you sing, and what gets dry when you're thirsty." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The thirsty phrase has a grammar twist worth knowing. The verb in 「のどがかわく」 is 「かわく」, to dry out. It's a change verb, like 「おなかがすく」. That means when you are thirsty right now, Japanese uses the past tense: 「のどがかわきました」, or casually, 「のどがかわいた」. Literally, my throat has dried out, and that's my current state. If you say 「のどがかわきます」 in the present tense, it sounds like a general habit or a future, like my throat gets dry, which isn't what you mean. The same thing happens with 「おなかがすきました」, I'm hungry. Another useful form is 「のどがかわいています」, my throat is dry, the ongoing state. And you can make it a reason: 「のどがかわいたから、水を飲みます|のどがかわいたから、みずをのみます」, I'm thirsty, so I'll drink water. So for being thirsty right now, use the past tense, just like being hungry." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In winter, when colds go around, you'll see 「のどあめ」 everywhere in Japanese convenience stores and pharmacies. These are throat candies, from 「あめ」, candy, and they come in many flavors, from honey lemon to strong herbal ones. People offer them to friends with a sore throat, and singers carry them in their bags. Another throat word: 「のど仏|のどぼとけ」, literally throat Buddha, is the Adam's apple. When you see a doctor, they might say 「のどを見せてください|のどをみせてください」, please show me your throat, and you open your mouth and say ah. Japanese people also gargle often to prevent colds, and the word for that is 「うがい」. Kids are taught to wash their hands and gargle when they come home, 「手洗いうがい|てあらいうがい」. So a sore throat in Japan means candies, gargling and a quick look by the doctor." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is your throat, where you swallow and sing, so 「のどがいたい」 is a sore throat and 「のどがかわく」, the throat dries out, is how you say thirsty.",
      "Because 「かわく」 is a change verb like 「おなかがすく」, say 「のどがかわきました」 for being thirsty right now, not the present tense.",
      "A sore throat in Japan brings out 「のどあめ」, throat candies, plus the habit of gargling, 「うがい」, and a doctor asking to see your throat."
    ],
    ja: "かぜで[のど]がいたいです。",
    en: "My throat hurts because of a cold." },
  "ねつ|熱": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the bottom of 「熱|ねつ」. Those four little dots are the fire radical, the same dots you see under many heat-related characters. So this kanji is about heat. Here it uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「ねつ」. When it means a fever, it's simply your body having extra heat. The same kanji with its native reading gives you 「熱い|あつい」, hot to the touch, like hot tea or a hot pan. Be careful: hot weather uses a different kanji, 「暑い|あつい」. Both are pronounced 「あつい」, but 「熱い|あつい」 is for things you touch, and 「暑い|あつい」 is for the air around you. The fever pattern is 「熱がある|ねつがある」, literally there is heat, which you also learn as its own phrase in this lesson. Japanese people usually measure fever in Celsius under the arm, so you'll hear something like 「三十八度|さんじゅうはちど」, using the counter 「〜度|〜ど」, degrees. The same kanji also shows up in 「熱心|ねっしん」, enthusiastic, a heart full of heat. To recap: fire dots at the bottom, heat in the body, a fever." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you're sick, you'll need several phrases built around fever. The basic one is 「熱がある|ねつがある」, I have a fever. When a fever starts, you say 「熱が出た|ねつがでた」, a fever came out. When it goes down, 「熱が下がった|ねつがさがった」, the fever went down, using 「下がる|さがる」. To take your temperature, you say 「熱を測る|ねつをはかる」. Japanese people usually know their normal temperature, called 「平熱|へいねつ」, the flat or ordinary heat, and they compare against it. Thirty-seven degrees and a bit is a slight fever, and thirty-eight is a real one. Schools and workplaces often ask you to stay home if you have a fever, and many people take their temperature every morning in flu season. A mini dialogue: the teacher asks 「どうしましたか」, what's wrong? And you answer, 「熱があります|ねつがあります」. They'll say 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care. So: comes out, goes down, measure it, and compare with your normal heat." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for fever is really the kanji for heat, and it builds a hot family of words. 「熱心|ねっしん」, a heart full of heat, means enthusiastic or devoted, like a hardworking student. Notice the 「ねつ」 shrinks to 「ねっ」 there, with a small pause. 「熱中|ねっちゅう」 means to be absorbed in something, totally into it, like a game or a hobby. 「情熱|じょうねつ」 is passion, the heat of feelings. On food packages and in microwave instructions, you'll see 「加熱|かねつ」, adding heat, meaning to heat or cook something. And with its native reading, 「熱い|あつい」 is hot to the touch. So whether it's your body temperature, your passion or your microwave, the same kanji with the four fire dots underneath is working. That gives you one simple picture for many words: heat." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese summers are very hot and humid, and one word you'll hear constantly on summer news is 「熱中症|ねっちゅうしょう」, heatstroke. It's built from the fever kanji, the middle kanji, and 「症|しょう」, a symptom or condition, which has the sickbed radical you see in 「病気|びょうき」. So it's literally the condition of heat getting inside you. Every summer, the weather news gives heatstroke warnings, telling people to drink water often, take salt, use air conditioning and stay out of the sun. Schools change their sports practices, and people carry small fans and cooling towels. Elderly people are especially at risk. A useful phrase: 「熱中症に気をつけてください|ねっちゅうしょうにきをつけてください」, please watch out for heatstroke. People say this as a kind farewell in summer, almost like saying take care. So the fever kanji also shows up in Japan's summer survival vocabulary." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The four dots at the bottom are the fire radical, so the kanji is about heat, a fever is 「熱がある|ねつがある」, and 「熱い|あつい」 is hot to the touch, unlike hot weather, 「暑い|あつい」.",
      "A fever comes out, 「熱が出た|ねつがでた」, goes down, 「熱が下がった|ねつがさがった」, and is compared with your normal temperature, 「平熱|へいねつ」.",
      "The heat kanji builds 「熱心|ねっしん」, enthusiastic, 「熱中|ねっちゅう」, absorbed, 「情熱|じょうねつ」, passion, and 「加熱|かねつ」, heating food.",
      "Summer news constantly warns about 「熱中症|ねっちゅうしょう」, heatstroke, and 「熱中症に気をつけてください|ねっちゅうしょうにきをつけてください」 works as a kind summer farewell."
    ],
    ja: "{子供|こども}は[熱|ねつ]があります。",
    en: "My child has a fever." },
  "せき|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, nearly always written in kana. There is a kanji, but it's rare, so don't worry about it. It's a noun, the cough itself, not the action. To say you're coughing, Japanese says the cough comes out: 「せきが出る|せきがでる」, which is its own phrase in this lesson. You can also say 「せきがひどい」, my cough is bad, when you see a doctor, along with 「のどがいたい」, sore throat, and 「熱がある|ねつがある」, I have a fever. A way to remember it: say it out loud with a sharp ending, something like seh-KEE, and it sounds a little like a dry cough clearing your throat. That's a sound-alike hook, nothing more. Watch out for other words with the same sound. 「せき」 can also be a seat, and it's the end of 「成績|せいせき」, grades, and of 「親せき|しんせき」, relatives. Those are completely different words that happen to sound alike. In Japan, when you have a cough, it's considered good manners to wear a mask in public, so you'll often see people masked up during cold season. To recap: the noun for cough, which comes out with 「出る|でる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There are two main ways to talk about coughing, and both treat the cough as a thing. The first is 「せきが出る|せきがでる」, literally a cough comes out. It uses 「出る|でる」, the verb that something does by itself, so the cough is the subject with 「が」. That's the natural way to describe a symptom that you can't control: 「せきが出ます|せきがでます」, I have a cough. The second is 「せきをする」, to do a cough, which sounds more like a single action, like coughing once on purpose to clear your throat or get attention. Then you can describe it with adjectives. 「せきがひどい」, the cough is bad. 「せきが止まらない|せきがとまらない」, the cough won't stop, using 「止まる|とまる」, to stop. When you're telling a doctor your symptoms, you can link them with the te-form: 「熱があって、せきが出ます|ねつがあって、せきがでます」, I have a fever and a cough. So the cough comes out, or you do one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, coughing in public comes with expectations. If you have a cough, people expect you to wear a mask on trains, in offices and in shops. There's even a common phrase for it, cough etiquette, said in Japanese as 「せきエチケット」, mixing the native word with a French loanword. It means covering your mouth with a mask, a tissue or your sleeve, never your bare hand. Posters in stations and hospitals remind people of it. Related symptoms you might want: 「くしゃみ」 is a sneeze, and 「鼻水|はなみず」 is a runny nose, literally nose water. A typical scene at work: a coworker coughs a few times, and someone says 「大丈夫ですか|だいじょうぶですか」, are you okay? They answer 「ちょっとかぜです」, just a little cold, and put on a mask. So in Japan a cough is not only a symptom. It's also a little social signal to grab a mask." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick, just to anchor the sound. Imagine a tiny dragon living in your throat. Every time it breathes out smoke, you cough. The dragon's name is Sekki, and whenever he sneezes fire, out comes a dry cough. Picture yourself in a quiet library, trying to be silent, and Sekki the throat dragon decides to stretch. One cough, two coughs, three coughs, and everyone turns to look. You put on a mask, and the dragon is trapped inside, grumbling. Now link it to the grammar. The cough comes out by itself, because Sekki has a mind of his own. That's why you say 「せきが出る|せきがでる」, the cough comes out, not I cough it. So when you hear 「せき」, think of Sekki the dragon in your throat, puffing out coughs whenever he likes. And remember to keep a mask ready for him." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the noun for a cough, usually in kana, and the cough comes out with 「せきが出る|せきがでる」, while in Japan a cough means wearing a mask in public.",
      "Use 「せきが出る|せきがでる」 for a symptom you cannot control and 「せきをする」 for a single deliberate cough, as in 「熱があって、せきが出ます|ねつがあって、せきがでます」.",
      "In Japan 「せきエチケット」, cough etiquette, means covering your mouth with a mask, tissue or sleeve, never your bare hand, so a cough signals grabbing a mask.",
      "Picture Sekki, a tiny dragon in your throat puffing out coughs whenever he likes, which is why the cough comes out by itself."
    ],
    ja: "かぜで[せき]がでます。",
    en: "I have a cough from my cold." },
  "びょうき|病気": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. 「病|びょう」 means sickness. Look at its outer frame, the shape that leans like a bed tipped on its side with dots on the left. That frame is the sickness radical, and it's often explained as a sickbed. You'll find the same 「病|びょう」 in 「病院|びょういん」, hospital, a building for the sick. The second kanji is 「気|き」, spirit, energy, the feeling in the air. It's everywhere: 「元気|げんき」, healthy and full of energy, 「天気|てんき」, weather, the feeling of the sky, and 「電気|でんき」, electricity. So 「病気|びょうき」 is sick energy, the opposite of 「元気|げんき」. Grammar tip: it's a noun, so you say 「病気です|びょうきです」, I'm sick, or 「病気になる|びょうきになる」, to get sick. A common mistake is treating it like an adjective and adding 「い」, which doesn't work. It's also a little serious. For a simple cold, people usually just say 「かぜ」, and save 「病気|びょうき」 for real illness or being sick in general. To recap: 「病|びょう」, the sickbed kanji, plus 「気|き」, energy. Sick energy." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「気|き」, is one of the most hardworking in Japanese, and you already know many of its words. 「元気|げんき」 is healthy and energetic, and 「元気がない|げんきがない」 means low on energy, down. 「気分|きぶん」 is your mood or how you feel, and 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」 means you feel sick or unwell. 「気をつける|きをつける」 means to be careful, to attach your attention. 「天気|てんき」 is weather. So 「気|き」 is a kind of energy, feeling or atmosphere. Now the first kanji, 「病|びょう」, sickness. You know 「病院|びょういん」, a hospital. 「看病|かんびょう」 is nursing someone who is sick. Here's the useful contrast: 「病気|びょうき」 is actual illness, while 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」 is just feeling unwell right now, maybe from a crowded train or too much food. So the energy kanji tells you whether you're full of life or sick in bed." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since this word is a noun, it takes noun patterns, and two verbs go with it. 「病気になる|びょうきになる」 means to become sick, to get ill. That's the change. And 「病気です|びょうきです」 describes the state, being sick. You can also say 「病気をする|びょうきをする」, which means to have an illness, often used when looking back: 「子供のころ、よく病気をしました|こどものころ、よくびょうきをしました」, I was often sick as a child. To describe the illness, you put 「の」 before something, or use 「な」 adjectives in front, like 「大変な病気|たいへんなびょうき」, a serious illness. A useful word is 「病気がち|びょうきがち」, prone to illness, sickly. And as a reason, you can say 「病気で学校を休みました|びょうきでがっこうをやすみました」, I missed school because of illness, using 「で」 for the cause. So: become sick, be sick, have been sick, and skip school because of it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "When someone is in the hospital in Japan, people visit and bring a gift. This visit is called 「お見舞い|おみまい」, and it comes with some interesting rules. The most famous one is: never bring a potted plant. A plant in a pot puts down roots, and the word for taking root, 「根付く|ねづく」, sounds like 「寝付く|ねつく」, being stuck in bed. Nobody wants that wish for a sick friend. Some people also avoid flowers with strong smells, or white flowers, which are linked to funerals. Fruit, magazines and small treats are safer gifts. When you leave, you say 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care of yourself. And when the person recovers, they often give a small return gift to say thank you. So being 「病気|びょうき」 in Japan brings visitors, careful gift choices, and kind wishes to get better." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「病|びょう」 has the sickbed radical and 「気|き」 is energy, so it is sick energy, the opposite of 「元気|げんき」, a noun used for real illness rather than a simple cold.",
      "「病気|びょうき」 is actual illness, while 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」 is just feeling unwell right now, both built on the hardworking energy kanji 「気|き」.",
      "As a noun it takes 「病気になる|びょうきになる」 to get sick, 「病気です|びょうきです」 to be sick, and 「で」 for a cause, as in 「病気で学校を休みました|びょうきでがっこうをやすみました」.",
      "On an 「お見舞い|おみまい」 hospital visit never bring a potted plant, since taking root sounds like being stuck in bed, and leave with 「お大事に|おだいじに」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は[病気|びょうき]です。",
    en: "My mother is sick." },
  "プレゼント|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word present, in the sense of a gift. Watch how the sounds get squeezed into Japanese. The pr cluster needs a vowel between the letters, so pre becomes 「プレ」. In English the s in present is actually spoken as a z, and Japanese keeps that with 「ゼ」. The n stays as 「ン」, and the final t can't end a word in Japanese, so it gets an o vowel and becomes 「ト」. Put together: 「プレゼント」. The meaning is narrower than English. It's only the gift, never the present moment and never the verb to present. To give a present, you use 「あげる」 or 「もらう」, as in 「プレゼントをあげる」. It's very common for birthdays, 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, and Christmas. Japan also has a related idea, 「お土産|おみやげ」, a souvenir gift you bring back from a trip for coworkers and friends, which is almost an obligation. And there's White Day, March fourteenth, when people who received chocolates on Valentine's Day give a return present a month later. To recap: English present, the gift kind, with a z sound and an extra o at the end." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch out for a near twin of this word. 「プレゼン」 is short for 「プレゼンテーション」, a presentation, like the kind you give at work with slides. It's the same English word, present, but chopped right before the 「ト」. So 「プレゼント」 is a gift, and 「プレゼン」 is a presentation. Imagine the confusion if your boss says 「明日はプレゼンがあります|あしたはプレゼンがあります」, there's a presentation tomorrow, and you think there's a gift! The difference is just that one final beat, so listen carefully. Another sound note: unlike English, the Japanese word doesn't stress one syllable much. Each of the five beats gets equal time. Practice saying 「プ」, 「レ」, 「ゼ」, 「ン」, 「ト」 evenly, and don't drop the last 「ト」, or you'll end up in a meeting instead of a party. So: with 「ト」, a gift. Without it, slides and a projector." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word teams up with the giving and receiving verbs you know. Use 「あげる」 when you give a present to someone: 「友だちにプレゼントをあげました|ともだちにプレゼントをあげました」, I gave my friend a present. Use 「もらう」 when you receive one: 「母にプレゼントをもらいました|ははにプレゼントをもらいました」. And use 「くれる」 when someone gives one to you or your family: 「姉がプレゼントをくれました|あねがプレゼントをくれました」. Remember, 「くれる」 always points toward me. You can also use 「プレゼントする」 as a verb, to give as a gift, and the thing you give comes with 「を」: 「花をプレゼントしました|はなをプレゼントしました」, I gave flowers as a present. So the noun works with the three giving verbs, and it can also turn into its own verb with 「する」. Choose the verb based on who gives and who receives." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Gift-giving in Japan follows the seasons and has its own rules. Wrapping matters a lot. Department stores wrap gifts beautifully, and the look of a present is part of the gift itself. Twice a year, people send seasonal gifts to people who have helped them: 「お中元|おちゅうげん」 in summer and 「お歳暮|おせいぼ」 at the end of the year, often food like fruit, coffee or beer. Then there's the custom of returning gifts, called 「お返し|おかえし」. If you receive something, you often give something back, usually worth about half as much for wedding or funeral gifts. White Day in March is a famous example. Another custom: when giving a present, people sometimes say 「つまらないものですが」, it's a boring thing, but please take it, as a humble phrase. Today many younger people find it a bit old fashioned. So a present in Japan is wrapped with care and often answered with another one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English present, keeping the z sound as 「ゼ」 and adding an o for 「ト」, it only ever means a gift, given with 「あげる」 or received with 「もらう」.",
      "Without the final 「ト」 you get 「プレゼン」, a work presentation, so keep all five even beats or you will end up in a meeting instead of a party.",
      "It pairs with 「あげる」, 「もらう」 and 「くれる」 depending on who gives to whom, and 「プレゼントする」 makes it a verb on its own.",
      "Japanese gifts are carefully wrapped, follow the seasons with 「お中元|おちゅうげん」 and 「お歳暮|おせいぼ」, and are often answered with a return gift, 「お返し|おかえし」."
    ],
    ja: "{彼女|かのじょ}に[プレゼント]をかいました。",
    en: "I bought a present for my girlfriend." },
  "ふつかよい|二日酔い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Let's break it apart. 「二日|ふつか」 means two days or the second day of the month, with the special native reading you learned for dates, like 「三日|みっか」. Then 「酔い|よい」, the noun form of the verb 「酔う|よう」, to get drunk. Look at the left side of the kanji in 「酔い|よい」. That's the same part you see in 「お酒|おさけ」, alcohol. It's a drawing of a jar for liquor, and it shows up in lots of drinking-related kanji. So the whole thing is second-day drunkenness: you drank last night, and now on the next day you're still paying for it. It's a perfect description, honestly. The usual pattern is 「二日酔いです|ふつかよいです」, I'm hungover, or 「二日酔いになる|ふつかよいになる」. In Japan, after-work drinking parties are a big part of office culture, so this word comes up a lot, and convenience stores sell little energy drinks marketed as hangover helpers. Don't confuse it with 「にさんにち」, written 「二三日|にさんにち」, two or three days, which uses the Chinese-origin reading for the numbers. To recap: 「二日|ふつか」, the second day, plus 「酔い|よい」, drunkenness, still drunk the day after." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「酔う|よう」, to get drunk, has a bigger meaning in Japanese: to feel dizzy and sick. That's why it shows up in words for motion sickness. 「車酔い|くるまよい」 is carsickness, from 「車|くるま」, car. 「船酔い|ふなよい」 is seasickness. Notice 「船|ふね」, a ship, changes to 「ふな」 there. And 「乗り物酔い|のりものよい」 covers motion sickness on any vehicle. Then there's 「酔っ払い|よっぱらい」, a drunk person, the kind you might see on a late-night train. So the feeling of being drunk, the day-after headache, and the queasy feeling on a bus are all the same verb in Japanese. Now the left side of the kanji: it's the same shape as in 「お酒|おさけ」, and it's an old picture of a jar for alcohol. You'll see it in other drinking kanji too. So whenever you see that jar shape, think of drinks and wobbly feelings." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Drinking parties, called 「飲み会|のみかい」, are a big part of Japanese work and student life. They often start with everyone raising a glass and saying 「かんぱい」, cheers. At big parties, there may be a second round at another bar, the 「二次会|にじかい」, and sometimes a third. That's where hangovers come from. The next morning, Japan has its own remedies. A classic is miso soup made with 「しじみ」, small freshwater clams, said to be good for a tired liver. Convenience stores sell small bottles of drinks advertised for before and after drinking, and some people swear by them. Also, Japan's last trains run around midnight, so people at parties often watch the clock carefully, or stay out until the first train in the morning. So if a coworker comes in pale and says 「二日酔いです|ふつかよいです」, offer them water and maybe point them toward some clam soup." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb behind this word, 「酔う|よう」, is a u-verb. Its te-form is 「酔って|よって」, and its past is 「酔った|よった」. A very common form is 「酔っている|よっている」, to be drunk, the ongoing state. Just like 「結婚している|けっこんしている」 for being married, the change already happened, and now you're in that state. So 「彼は酔っています|かれはよっています」 means he is drunk right now. The noun form, 「酔い|よい」, comes from the verb stem, the part before 「ます」. Many Japanese nouns are made this way: 「よいます」 becomes 「よい」, drunkenness. Now careful. 「よい」 also sounds like the adjective for good, another form of 「いい」. But that's a different word, so don't think a hangover is a good day. And to describe how you feel, you can say 「頭がいたい|あたまがいたい」, my head hurts, which goes nicely with a hangover. So: a u-verb, a state of being drunk, and a noun made from the stem." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「二日|ふつか」, the second day, plus 「酔い|よい」, drunkenness, makes second-day drunkenness, still paying for last night, and the kanji has the liquor jar shape from 「お酒|おさけ」.",
      "「酔う|よう」 really means to feel dizzy and sick, so it also gives 「車酔い|くるまよい」, carsickness, 「船酔い|ふなよい」, seasickness, and 「酔っ払い|よっぱらい」, a drunk.",
      "Hangovers come from 「飲み会|のみかい」 drinking parties with second rounds, 「二次会|にじかい」, and a classic remedy is miso soup with 「しじみ」 clams.",
      "「酔う|よう」 is a u-verb whose ongoing form 「酔っている|よっている」 means being drunk now, and the noun 「酔い|よい」 comes from its stem."
    ],
    ja: "{今朝|けさ}は[二日酔い|ふつかよい]です。",
    en: "I have a hangover this morning." },
  "しけん|試験": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. 「試|し」 means to try or to test. It has the speech radical on the left, the same one in many words about language, which fits: testing often means asking questions. You'll see it again in this very lesson in 「試合|しあい」, a match, where people try their strength against each other. 「験|けん」 means to check or verify, to see if something holds up. It's also in 「経験|けいけん」, experience, things you've actually checked out by living through them. So 「試験|しけん」 is trying you out and checking the results. It sounds more formal than the loanword 「テスト」. A quiz in class might be a 「テスト」, while big official exams are 「試験|しけん」. Finals are 「期末試験|きまつしけん」, the end-of-term exam. The pattern is 「試験がある|しけんがある」, there's an exam, or 「試験を受ける|しけんをうける」, to take an exam. A cultural note: Japanese university entrance exams are so intense that people have long called them exam hell, and students often buy good-luck charms at shrines before them. To recap: try, 「試|し」, plus check, 「験|けん」. Trying you out and checking." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「試|し」, means to try, and it pops up in some very handy shopping words. 「試着|しちゃく」 is trying on clothes, from the wearing kanji in 「着る|きる」. The fitting room is the 「試着室|しちゃくしつ」, and you can ask 「試着してもいいですか|しちゃくしてもいいですか」, may I try it on? 「試食|ししょく」 is trying food, the free samples in supermarkets and department store food halls. You know 「試合|しあい」, a match. And the native verb is 「試す|ためす」, to try something out. Now the second kanji, 「験|けん」, to check. You know 「経験|けいけん」, experience. And 「実験|じっけん」 is a science experiment, a real check. So an exam is trying and checking, a fitting room is a try-on room, and a free sample is a try-taste. One kanji, and you can survive a clothing store, a supermarket and a final exam." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Students talk about exams constantly, so here are the key verbs. To take an exam is 「試験を受ける|しけんをうける」, literally to receive the exam. To pass is 「合格する|ごうかくする」, often with 「に」: 「試験に合格しました|しけんにごうかくしました」. To fail is 「試験に落ちる|しけんにおちる」, literally to fall. Because of that, students avoid saying the word for fall or slip near exam time, as it's bad luck. Midterms are 「中間試験|ちゅうかんしけん」, and finals are 「期末試験|きまつしけん」. Before a test, you say 「試験の前に勉強します|しけんのまえにべんきょうします」. And to cheer someone on, say 「がんばって」. A mini dialogue: 「試験はどうでしたか|しけんはどうでしたか」, how was the exam? 「むずかしかったです」, it was hard. So you receive the exam, and then you either pass or fall. Learn those verbs, and you can talk about school life." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Exams in Japan come with good-luck customs, and some are based on puns. The chocolate bar Kit Kat is famous for this. Its name in Japanese, 「キットカット」, sounds like 「きっと勝つ|きっとかつ」, you will surely win. So students receive Kit Kats from family and friends before big exams, and the company even makes special exam packaging. Another food pun: 「カツ丼|カツどん」, a pork cutlet rice bowl, is eaten before exams, because 「カツ」 sounds like 「勝つ|かつ」, to win. At shrines, students buy charms for exam success and write wishes on wooden plaques, especially at shrines to the god of learning. And families carefully avoid saying words like fall or slip in front of test takers. Winter is exam season for university entrance, and snow on the exam day is big news. So exams in Japan come with chocolate, cutlets, charms and a lot of hope." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「試|し」 means try and 「験|けん」 means check, so an exam is trying you out and checking the results, more formal than a 「テスト」.",
      "The try kanji gives handy shopping words like 「試着|しちゃく」, trying on clothes, and 「試食|ししょく」, free samples, while 「験|けん」 gives 「実験|じっけん」, an experiment.",
      "You receive the exam with 「試験を受ける|しけんをうける」, then pass with 「合格する|ごうかくする」 or fall with 「試験に落ちる|しけんにおちる」, a word students avoid near exam time.",
      "Exam luck runs on puns, like Kit Kat, 「キットカット」, sounding like 「きっと勝つ|きっとかつ」, you will surely win, and 「カツ丼|カツどん」 for 「勝つ|かつ」, to win."
    ],
    ja: "あしたは[試験|しけん]があります。",
    en: "I have an exam tomorrow." },
  "インフルエンザ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This came into Japanese from Italian, not from English. In Italian, influenza means influence. Centuries ago, people in Italy thought epidemics were caused by the influence of the stars, so the disease got that name. English took the word too, and then shortened it to flu. Japanese kept the whole long form. Look at how the sounds were fitted in. The l becomes an r-like sound in 「ル」, since Japanese doesn't separate l and r. Each consonant that sits next to another one gets its own vowel, which is why it grows to seven kana. The final za sound stays as 「ザ」. In casual speech, people often shorten it themselves to 「インフル」, just like English did with flu, but they chopped off the other end. In Japan, flu season is taken seriously. Many people get a flu shot every autumn, schools sometimes close classes when too many kids are out, and wearing a mask on the train is normal. A common pattern is 「インフルエンザになる」, to come down with the flu, or 「インフルエンザで休む|インフルエンザでやすむ」, to take time off because of it. To recap: the Italian word for influence, kept whole in Japanese, often clipped to 「インフル」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Every winter, flu becomes a daily topic in Japan. In autumn, many people get a flu shot, called 「予防接種|よぼうせっしゅ」, preventive vaccination, using 「予防|よぼう」, prevention, which shares a kanji with 「消防士|しょうぼうし」. If you get sick, clinics usually do a quick test with a swab in your nose to confirm it. Once you have the flu, schools have rules for how long you must stay home, counted from when the fever started and when it went away. Many companies have similar rules. And when too many students in one class are absent, the school closes that class for a few days, which is called 「学級閉鎖|がっきゅうへいさ」, class closure. It makes the news when it spreads. A mini dialogue: 「どうしたんですか」, what happened? 「インフルエンザで休みました|インフルエンザでやすみました」, I was off with the flu. So the flu in Japan means shots, quick tests, and a strict stay-home rule." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Like many medical words, this one has loanword relatives, and some came from German or Latin rather than English. 「ウイルス」, a virus, sounds closer to the German or Latin pronunciation, which is why it isn't like the English vai-rus. 「ワクチン」, a vaccine, comes from German too, with its 「チン」 ending. You may have heard 「ノロウイルス」, the norovirus, a stomach bug that spreads in winter. And like English, Japanese shortens long words. People clip this one to 「インフル」, and you will hear it in casual talk like 「インフルになった」, I got the flu. Compare that with the native word 「かぜ」, a cold. A cold is a normal, mild illness, while the flu is treated as serious, with tests and time off. People sometimes say 「ただのかぜです」, it's just a cold, to show it isn't the flu. So one Italian loanword, surrounded by German ones, and clearly more serious than a cold." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It came from Italian, where it means influence, from the old belief that the stars caused epidemics, and Japanese kept the whole word, often clipped to 「インフル」.",
      "Flu in Japan means autumn shots, 「予防接種|よぼうせっしゅ」, a quick nose-swab test, strict stay-home rules, and even 「学級閉鎖|がっきゅうへいさ」 when too many students are absent.",
      "Its medical relatives like 「ウイルス」 and 「ワクチン」 came from German, and the flu is treated as far more serious than an ordinary 「かぜ」."
    ],
    ja: "[インフルエンザ]で{学校|がっこう}をやすみました。",
    en: "I missed school because of the flu." },
  "かぜ|風邪": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has a fun story. The first kanji, 「風|かぜ」, means wind, and on its own it's read 「かぜ」 with its native reading. The second kanji, 「邪|じゃ」, means wicked or evil; on its own its Chinese-origin reading is 「じゃ」. So it's written as an evil wind. That idea comes from old Chinese medicine, where illnesses like colds were thought to blow into the body on a bad wind. Here's the tricky part. In this word, the two kanji together are simply read 「かぜ」. The second kanji doesn't get its 「じゃ」 sound at all; it's silent here and just adds meaning. So 「風|かぜ」, wind, and 「風邪|かぜ」, a cold, sound the same, and the writing tells you which you mean. The key pattern is 「風邪をひく|かぜをひく」, to catch a cold, literally to pull in a cold. Don't use the verb for catch a ball here. The same wind kanji also shows up with its Chinese-origin readings in words you know, like 「お風呂|おふろ」, bath, and 「台風|たいふう」, typhoon, a big wind. If you're sick with a cold, you say 「風邪です|かぜです」, and the reply might be 「お大事に|おだいじに」. To recap: an evil wind blows in, and you pull in a cold." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb in 「風邪をひく|かぜをひく」 is 「ひく」, to pull, a u-verb. When you've caught a cold, you usually say it in the past, 「風邪をひきました|かぜをひきました」, because catching is a moment of change, and the cold is the result. If you want to say you have a cold right now, use the ongoing form: 「風邪をひいています|かぜをひいています」. Notice the te-form of 「ひく」 is 「ひいて」, with the 「く」 turning into 「い」, the regular pattern for verbs ending in 「く」. To give a reason, you can use 「で」: 「風邪で学校を休みました|かぜでがっこうをやすみました」, I missed school because of a cold. And for just a touch of a cold, there's 「風邪気味|かぜぎみ」, a bit cold-ish, a very handy word when you feel a little off but not truly sick. So you pull in a cold, you are in the state of having pulled it, and sometimes you're just cold-ish." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The wind kanji, 「風|かぜ」, makes a whole windy family. With its Chinese-origin reading 「ふう」, it gives you 「台風|たいふう」, a typhoon, and 「風船|ふうせん」, a balloon, literally a wind boat. You'll also see it as 「ふ」 in 「お風呂|おふろ」, a bath. And it can mean style or manner, as in 「和風|わふう」, Japanese style, or 「洋風|ようふう」, Western style, words you'll see on restaurant menus and in home design. Now the cold itself makes compounds too. 「風邪薬|かぜぐすり」 is cold medicine. Notice 「くすり」, medicine, changes to 「ぐすり」 after another word, a common sound change. 「夏風邪|なつかぜ」 is a summer cold, which people say is harder to shake off. So the wind kanji gives you weather, balloons, baths and styles, and the cold word gives you medicine and summer colds. Remember: alone it's wind, with the evil kanji attached it's a cold." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has many home remedies for colds, and some are delightfully specific. The classic sick-day food is 「おかゆ」, a soft rice porridge, often with a pickled plum, 「うめぼし」, on top. It's gentle on the stomach, and parents make it for their kids. Another is 「たまござけ」, warm sake mixed with egg and sugar, an old-fashioned drink for adults with a cold. A famous folk remedy is wrapping a grilled green onion around your neck with a cloth, said to help a sore throat. Most people today think of it as grandma's advice rather than real medicine. Hot ginger drinks and honey are popular too. And of course, people wear masks and gargle. There is even a teasing old saying that fools never catch colds, which people use as a joke when a friend is sick. So if a Japanese friend has a cold, porridge and a warm drink are the kind thing to offer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is written as an evil wind, from old Chinese medicine, but read simply 「かぜ」 with the second kanji silent, and you catch one with 「風邪をひく|かぜをひく」, to pull in a cold.",
      "Catching is a change, so say 「風邪をひきました|かぜをひきました」 or 「風邪をひいています|かぜをひいています」, and 「風邪気味|かぜぎみ」 means a bit cold-ish.",
      "The wind kanji builds 「台風|たいふう」, 「風船|ふうせん」 and 「和風|わふう」, while the cold gives 「風邪薬|かぜぐすり」, cold medicine, and 「夏風邪|なつかぜ」, a summer cold.",
      "The classic Japanese cold remedy is 「おかゆ」, soft rice porridge with a 「うめぼし」, alongside folk cures like warm egg sake and a green onion around the neck."
    ],
    ja: "[風邪|かぜ]をひきました。",
    en: "I caught a cold." },
  "ホームシック|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "From the English word homesick. Look at how the sounds were fitted in. Home gets a long vowel, 「ホーム」, shown by the long dash. Sick gets a small 「ッ」 before the final sound, which gives that little catch you hear before the k, and then an extra u vowel at the end, because Japanese words don't end in a k sound. So you get 「シック」. Here's a grammar difference. In English, homesick is an adjective, I am homesick. In Japanese it's a noun, so you become it: 「ホームシックになる」, to get homesick. You might also hear 「ホームシックです」. You know 「ホーム」 from another loanword, 「ホームステイ」, a homestay with a local family, which is exactly the situation where homesickness tends to hit. A cute side note: 「ホーム」 on its own in Japanese often means a train platform, from the English word platform, so the same sounds can mean two different things. If a friend studying abroad says 「ホームシックになりました」, a kind reply is to ask about their family or suggest a video call. To recap: English homesick, made into a noun, that you become with 「になる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first half, 「ホーム」, appears in lots of loanwords, but it doesn't always mean home. 「ホームステイ」 is a homestay, living with a local family. 「ホームページ」 is a website, or the front page of one. 「ホームラン」 is a home run in baseball, Japan's favorite sport. But here's the twist: 「ホーム」 alone often means a train platform. That comes from the English platform, shortened from 「プラットホーム」. So at a station, a sign might say which 「ホーム」 your train leaves from, and that has nothing to do with home. The second half, 「シック」, is sick. You'll also see 「シック」 meaning chic, stylish, from French, as in a chic outfit. Same katakana, different source. So 「ホームシック」 could look at first like a stylish platform. Only context tells you, and with 「になる」 after it, it's clearly the feeling of missing home." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Homesickness comes up a lot for exchange students, and the word often appears with 「留学|りゅうがく」, studying abroad. A useful pattern: 「留学して、ホームシックになりました|りゅうがくして、ホームシックになりました」. To say what you miss, Japanese often uses 「さびしい」, lonely, or 「なつかしい」, nostalgic, longing for something from the past. You could say 「家族に会いたいです|かぞくにあいたいです」, I want to see my family, which is a very natural way to express it. Japanese students who move from the countryside to Tokyo for college get homesick too, and they might talk about missing their mother's cooking, 「おふくろの味|おふくろのあじ」, the taste of mom's food. A kind response to a homesick friend is 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」 with a gentle question about their family. So the loanword names the feeling, and native words like 「さびしい」 describe it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English homesick, it is a noun in Japanese, so you become it with 「ホームシックになる」, often during a 「ホームステイ」 abroad.",
      "「ホーム」 alone often means a train platform and 「シック」 can mean chic, so only context, like 「になる」 after it, shows it is missing home.",
      "The loanword names the feeling, while native words like 「さびしい」, lonely, and 「家族に会いたいです|かぞくにあいたいです」, I want to see my family, describe it."
    ],
    ja: "{留学|りゅうがく}して、[ホームシック]になりました。",
    en: "I studied abroad and got homesick." },
  "アレルギー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word came from German, not English. The German word is Allergie, said roughly al-ler-GEE, with a hard g. That's why the Japanese version has 「ギ」 in it, while English allergy has a soft j-like sound. The sounds were fitted in the usual way. The double l becomes 「レ」 and 「ル」, since Japanese uses its r-like sound for l. The r in the middle gets its own vowel, and the long ee at the end becomes 「ギー」 with a long dash. Why German? In the late eighteen-hundreds, Japan modeled its modern medicine on Germany, so many medical words came straight from German. Another example is 「カルテ」, a patient's medical chart, from German Karte. The usual pattern is 「アレルギーがある」, to have an allergy, with the trigger in front plus 「の」: 「卵のアレルギーがある|たまごのアレルギーがある」, I'm allergic to eggs. A very common one in Japan is hay fever from cedar pollen every spring, which millions of people suffer from. People also use the word jokingly for a strong dislike, like being allergic to math. To recap: German Allergie, with its hard g, from Japan's German-influenced medicine." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word is part of a group of German loanwords that came in with medicine and science. 「カルテ」, a patient's chart, from German Karte. 「ギプス」, a plaster cast for a broken bone, from German Gips. 「ウイルス」, virus. And here is one you know well: 「アルバイト」, a part-time job, from German Arbeit, meaning work. Students in Japan shorten it to 「バイト」. So when a Japanese word seems weirdly different from its English cousin, German is often the reason. In this word, the clue is the hard 「ギ」 sound, where English uses a soft j. Another German one in daily life is 「エネルギー」, energy, also with that hard 「ギ」. Compare the English-based 「エナジードリンク」, an energy drink, which uses the English sound. So if a loanword has a hard g where English is soft, think German. That little sound is a fingerprint of its origin." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you have a food allergy in Japan, these phrases are essential. 「えびのアレルギーがあります」, I'm allergic to shrimp. Or more simply, 「えびアレルギーです」, I have a shrimp allergy, with the food stuck right on the front. To ask if something contains an ingredient: 「卵が入っていますか|たまごがはいっていますか」, does this have egg in it? Japanese packaged food lists the major allergens clearly on the label, such as egg, milk, wheat, shrimp, crab, buckwheat and peanuts, so you can check before you buy. Buckwheat, 「そば」, is a big one in Japan, since 「そば」 noodles are everywhere. Many restaurants also have allergy charts if you ask. A key word is 「入っていない|はいっていない」, not included. So say what you're allergic to, ask if it's inside, and check the label for the list." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It came from German Allergie, which explains the hard 「ギ」, because Japan modeled its modern medicine on Germany, and you say 「卵のアレルギーがある|たまごのアレルギーがある」.",
      "It belongs to German loanwords like 「カルテ」, 「ギプス」 and 「アルバイト」, and a hard g where English is soft, as in 「エネルギー」, is a fingerprint of German origin.",
      "Say 「えびアレルギーです」 with the food stuck on the front, ask 「卵が入っていますか|たまごがはいっていますか」, and check Japanese labels, which list major allergens like 「そば」."
    ],
    ja: "{卵|たまご}の[アレルギー]があります。",
    en: "I have an egg allergy." },
  "ジュース|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "From the English word juice. The j sound at the start is 「ジュ」, and the long oo in the middle becomes a long vowel, shown by the dash. The final s sound can't end a word in Japanese, so it gets an extra u vowel and becomes 「ス」. That gives you 「ジュース」. Here's the meaning shift that surprises English speakers. In everyday Japanese, 「ジュース」 often means any sweet, cold soft drink, so a kid might call a cola or a melon soda 「ジュース」, not just fruit juice. On packaging, though, the word is strictly reserved for real one hundred percent fruit juice. Other drinks have to call themselves something else. So you'll see a big gap between the label and how people talk. Typical phrases are 「オレンジジュース」, orange juice, and 「りんごジュース」, apple juice. Japan is famous for its vending machines, and you'll find rows of juices and soft drinks on almost every street corner, hot and cold. It's a sibling to other drink loanwords you know, like 「コーヒー」, which came from Dutch koffie. To recap: English juice, but in casual Japanese it stretches to cover sweet soft drinks too." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a surprise. In tennis and volleyball, 「ジュース」 also means deuce, the score when both sides are tied near the end of a game. It came from the English word deuce, but it ended up sounding just like juice in Japanese. So a tennis commentator saying 「ジュース」 is not offering you a drink. Context makes it clear: at a sports match it's a tied score, and at a café it's a drink. Another sound tip: the first beat is 「ジュ」, a single beat with a small 「ュ」, then a long vowel. So it's 「ジュ」, then stretch, then 「ス」. Three beats in all. Don't confuse it with 「じゅう」, the number ten, which has a similar start. 「ジュース」 has that extra 「ス」 at the end. So listen for the final 「ス」 to know it's juice, and listen for the tennis court to know it might be a tied score." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Let's look at how people order and talk about this drink. At a café or restaurant, you might say 「オレンジジュースをください」, orange juice, please. When offered drinks, a host might ask 「何を飲みますか|なにをのみますか」, what will you drink? Kids often answer 「ジュース」. Among friends, a fun casual custom is to bet a drink, like the loser buys everyone a juice from the vending machine. You'll hear 「ジュースおごって」, buy me a juice, as a lighthearted favor. At drinking parties, people who don't drink alcohol order 「ソフトドリンク」, soft drinks, and 「ジュース」 is a normal choice. Another common one is 「野菜ジュース|やさいジュース」, vegetable juice, very popular as a healthy drink in Japan. So whether it's a café order, a little bet, or a healthy choice, this word is a part of daily life." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English juice, but in casual Japanese it stretches to any sweet cold soft drink, while packaging reserves it strictly for one hundred percent fruit juice.",
      "In tennis and volleyball 「ジュース」 also means deuce, a tied score, and its three beats end in a 「ス」 that sets it apart from 「じゅう」, ten.",
      "Order with 「オレンジジュースをください」, bet friends a vending machine drink with 「ジュースおごって」, or pick the healthy 「野菜ジュース|やさいジュース」."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}、オレンジ[ジュース]を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink orange juice in the morning." },
  "たまご|卵": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, using the native reading 「たまご」. The kanji 「卵|たまご」 is used for eggs of all kinds, chicken eggs, fish eggs, frog eggs. A way to remember the shape: it looks like two rounded halves side by side, each with a little dot inside, like two eggs with yolks. That's a hook, not the kanji's history. There's a second common spelling, 「玉子|たまご」, which you'll often see on menus and food packaging for cooked eggs. Here 「玉|たま」 means a ball or something round, and 「子|ご」 is child, the same kanji as in 「子供|こども」, but softened to 「ご」. So that spelling reads as little round child, which matches the sound nicely. In grammar it works like any noun: 「卵を食べる|たまごをたべる」, to eat eggs. A fun cultural note: a very popular simple breakfast in Japan is 「卵かけご飯|たまごかけごはん」, a raw egg cracked over hot rice with a little soy sauce. Japanese eggs are produced to be safe to eat raw, so this is normal there. It sits next to 「ご飯|ごはん」, rice or meal, from your vocab. To recap: the native word for egg, written 「卵|たまご」, or as round ball child for cooked ones." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Egg dishes give you a great little family of words. 「卵焼き|たまごやき」 is a rolled omelet, a sweet or savory layered egg roll, a lunchbox classic. 「ゆで卵|ゆでたまご」 is a boiled egg, from 「ゆでる」, to boil. 「生卵|なまたまご」 is a raw egg, with 「生|なま」, raw. And the best one: a fried egg is 「目玉焼き|めだまやき」, literally a grilled eyeball, because a sunny-side-up egg looks like a big eye staring at you. Then there's 「親子丼|おやこどん」, a parent-and-child bowl, which is chicken and egg cooked together over rice. The chicken is the parent, and the egg is the child. It's a little dark when you think about it, but very tasty. So egg words are full of pictures: rolled, boiled, raw, an eyeball, and a parent with its child. Remember these, and you'll be able to read half of a Japanese breakfast menu." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Eggs are everywhere in Japanese food, so here are some useful situations. At a ramen shop, you can often add a soft-boiled marinated egg as a topping, sometimes listed as 「味玉|あじたま」, a flavored egg, using the round 「玉|たま」 kanji. At a sushi restaurant, there's 「玉子|たまご」, a sweet egg omelet on rice, and some people say you can judge a sushi shop by its egg. When counting eggs, use 「個|こ」: 「卵を二個ください|たまごをにこください」, two eggs, please. In supermarkets, eggs are sold in packs of ten, and many people check the date carefully because they might eat them raw. If you're allergic, you'd say 「卵アレルギーです|たまごアレルギーです」. So eggs on ramen, eggs on sushi, eggs counted with 「個|こ」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on sound. The word sounds a bit like tomato go. Picture this: a chicken in a farm kitchen lays an egg on the counter. The egg rolls, wobbles, and bumps right into a big red tomato. The tomato says, go! And the egg rolls off the counter and lands, unbroken, in a frying pan, where it becomes a perfect fried egg, staring up at you like an eyeball. Tomato, go, egg. Now add the kanji. Imagine the two halves of the shape as two eggs sitting side by side on the counter, each with a dot of yolk inside. One of them is the egg that rolled away. So whenever you need the word, see the tomato shouting go and the egg rolling into the pan. That's your egg, 「たまご」, ready for breakfast." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「卵|たまご」 covers eggs of all kinds and looks like two eggs with yolks, while cooked eggs are often written 「玉子|たまご」, little round child.",
      "Egg dishes are full of pictures, like 「卵焼き|たまごやき」, a rolled omelet, 「目玉焼き|めだまやき」, a fried egg that looks like an eyeball, and 「親子丼|おやこどん」, a parent-and-child bowl.",
      "Eggs top ramen as 「味玉|あじたま」, sit on sushi as sweet 「玉子|たまご」, and are counted with 「個|こ」, as in 「卵を二個ください|たまごをにこください」.",
      "It sounds like tomato go, so picture an egg bumping into a tomato that shouts go, sending it rolling into a frying pan."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[卵|たまご]を{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat eggs every morning." },
  "ふく|服": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「ふく」, even standing alone, which is a little unusual for a one-kanji word. It's the general word for clothes, what you wear on your body. The verb that goes with it is 「着る|きる」, to wear on the upper body or the whole body, so you say 「服を着る|ふくをきる」, to put on clothes. Don't confuse it with 「着物|きもの」. That word is built from 「着|き」, wearing, and 「物|もの」, thing, so it originally meant simply things you wear, but today it means traditional Japanese dress, the kimono. In contrast, Western-style clothes are 「洋服|ようふく」, where 「洋|よう」 means ocean, standing for the lands across the ocean, the West. Traditional clothing in general is 「和服|わふく」, where 「和|わ」 means Japanese. Both share 「服|ふく」. A cultural note: Japanese schools often have uniforms, called 「制服|せいふく」, again with the same kanji, and many company workers wear a suit almost like a uniform too. A common mistake: 「服|ふく」 is clothing in general, so to name a specific item, use its own word, like 「シャツ」, a shirt, or 「ぼうし」, a hat. To recap: the general word for clothes, read 「ふく」, found inside lots of clothing words." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese is picky about wearing verbs, and 「服|ふく」 connects to the most general one. 「着る|きる」 is for clothes on your upper body or your whole body: a shirt, a jacket, a dress, or clothes in general, so 「服を着る|ふくをきる」. But for things on your lower body, like pants, skirts, socks and shoes, you use 「はく」. For hats, you use 「かぶる」, to put on your head. For glasses, 「かける」, to hang them on. For a tie or a watch, 「する」. And to take off clothes, you use 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, which works for almost everything, clothes and shoes. Another thing: the ongoing form describes what someone is wearing now. 「赤い服を着ています|あかいふくをきています」 means she is wearing red clothes. It describes the state, not the act of dressing. So: 「着る|きる」 for tops, 「はく」 for bottoms, 「かぶる」 for hats, and 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」 to take it all off." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Clothing in Japan follows the seasons. Twice a year, on June first and October first, many schools and companies switch between summer and winter uniforms. This is called 「衣替え|ころもがえ」, changing clothes, and it's an old custom that started in the imperial court. At home, people pack away winter coats and bring out summer clothes. Then at New Year, clothing stores sell 「福袋|ふくぶくろ」, lucky bags, sealed bags filled with random items, often clothes, for a fixed price, usually worth much more. People line up early to buy them. The first part, 「福|ふく」, means good fortune, and it sounds the same as clothes, though it's a different kanji. That's a nice coincidence to remember. Also, in Japanese shops you can ask to try things on: 「試着してもいいですか|しちゃくしてもいいですか」. So Japan changes clothes with the seasons, and starts the year with lucky bags of clothes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the general word for clothes, read 「ふく」 even alone, put on with 「着る|きる」, and it sits inside 「洋服|ようふく」, Western clothes, 「和服|わふく」, and 「制服|せいふく」, a uniform.",
      "Japanese is picky about wearing verbs, using 「着る|きる」 for tops and clothes in general, 「はく」 for bottoms, 「かぶる」 for hats, and 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」 to take anything off.",
      "Japan changes clothes with the seasons in 「衣替え|ころもがえ」, and starts the year with 「福袋|ふくぶくろ」, lucky bags, whose 「福|ふく」 sounds just like clothes."
    ],
    ja: "あたらしい[服|ふく]をかいました。",
    en: "I bought new clothes." },
  "もの|物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the native reading, 「もの」, a thing you can touch, a physical object. Look at the left side of the kanji. That's the cow radical, the same piece that sits on the left of a lot of animal-related characters. A way to remember it: picture a cow as the most basic valuable thing a farmer owns. That's a hook, not the official history. The real anchor is the family of words you already know, because 「物|もの」 sits at the end of so many of them. 「食べ物|たべもの」 is eating things, food. 「飲み物|のみもの」 is drinking things, drinks. 「買い物|かいもの」 is buying things, shopping. 「着物|きもの」 is wearing things, a kimono. And 「果物|くだもの」, fruit, uses it too. Then with its Chinese-origin readings, you see it in 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, the zoo, a garden of moving things, and in 「荷物|にもつ」, baggage. Here's a key point for English speakers. Japanese splits thing into two words. 「もの」 is for concrete objects, while 「こと」 is for abstract things, like events, facts, or ideas. You can see 「こと」's kanji in 「用事|ようじ」, things to do. To recap: 「もの」, a concrete thing, the ending of food, drinks, and shopping." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closer at the right side of 「物|もの」. The cow radical sits on the left, and the right-hand piece is mainly there for sound. Dictionaries list this kanji as a sound-plus-meaning character, so don't read too much into that right side. What matters more for you is that this one kanji has three readings you will meet constantly. The native reading 「もの」 appears at the end of everyday compounds, and also in 「建物|たてもの」, a building, literally a built thing, and 「品物|しなもの」, goods or merchandise. The Chinese-origin reading 「ぶつ」 shows up in more formal or scientific words: 「動物|どうぶつ」, an animal, and 「生物|せいぶつ」, living things, the subject of biology class. The third reading, 「もつ」, is rarer, and you mainly see it in 「荷物|にもつ」, luggage. A rough rule: if the word is a homey compound built from native verbs, expect 「もの」. If it sounds technical or comes in a pair of Chinese-origin readings, expect 「ぶつ」. So one kanji, three voices: 「もの」, 「ぶつ」, and 「もつ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Some of the most useful words built on 「物|もの」 are about stuff you leave behind. 「忘れ物|わすれもの」 is a forgotten thing, something you left somewhere by accident. On Japanese trains you will hear the announcement 「お忘れ物のないようにご注意ください|おわすれもののないようにごちゅういください」, please be careful not to leave anything behind. A teacher might ask the class 「忘れ物はありませんか|わすれものはありませんか」 before a field trip ends. Its cousin is 「落とし物|おとしもの」, a dropped thing, something that fell out of your pocket. Lost and found counters handle both. Another handy one is 「本物|ほんもの」, the real thing, the genuine article, as opposed to a fake. And when you are craving something, 「何か甘い物が食べたい|なにかあまいものがたべたい」 means I want to eat something sweet. Notice how naturally 「物|もの」 slots in after an adjective there. In casual writing you will often see the word in kana alone when it's general, and in kanji when it's a definite physical item. So listen for it in train stations and lost and found offices." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture yourself coming home from a giant shopping trip. Your arms are full of bags, boxes, a lamp, a rolled-up rug, and a houseplant balanced on your head. With every step you let out a long moan. Moan, oh. Moan, oh. That moaning sound is 「もの」, and every single item crushing your arms is a 「物|もの」, a thing. Now add the kanji. Waiting at your front door is a patient cow, the radical on the left of the character, offering to carry some of your things. You load the bags onto the cow and finally stop moaning. When you need the word, replay the scene: the moan of a person buried in things, and the friendly cow beside them. Remember, this is just a trick for memory, not where the word comes from. A moaning shopper with too many things, and a cow to help." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji carries the cow radical, and it ends a whole family of everyday words: 「食べ物|たべもの」 food, 「飲み物|のみもの」 drinks, 「買い物|かいもの」 shopping. Use 「もの」 for concrete objects and 「こと」 for abstract things.",
      "One kanji, three readings: 「もの」 in homey native compounds like 「建物|たてもの」, 「ぶつ」 in formal words like 「動物|どうぶつ」, and the rare 「もつ」 in 「荷物|にもつ」.",
      "Listen for 「忘れ物|わすれもの」, a forgotten thing, in train announcements, and its cousin 「落とし物|おとしもの」, a dropped thing, at lost and found counters.",
      "Picture a shopper buried in bags, moaning \"moan, oh\" with every step, while a friendly cow, the radical on the left, offers to carry the things."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}でいろいろな[物|もの]をかいました。",
    en: "I bought various things in Japan." },
  "きっぷ|切符": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji. 「切|きっ」 is the cutting kanji, the same one as in 「切る|きる」, to cut, and here it's squeezed down to 「きっ」 with a small 「っ」. You also know it from 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, and 「締め切り|しめきり」, a deadline, literally the point where things are cut off. The second kanji, 「符|ぷ」, means a token or tally, a little marker that proves something. Its reading is 「ふ」, but after the small 「っ」 it hardens to 「ぷ」. Notice the bamboo radical on top: long ago, tallies were made from strips of bamboo. Put together, 「切符|きっぷ」 is a cut-off token, a slip of paper that proves you paid. A cultural note: for decades, station staff stood at the ticket gates and punched or clipped every passenger's ticket by hand with special clippers. Today automatic gates swallow the ticket, and many people use rechargeable IC cards instead. You'd still buy a 「切符|きっぷ」 for a long trip or the bullet train. It's mostly for train, bus, and event tickets. A related word is 「切手|きって」, a postage stamp, with the same 「切|きっ」. To recap: the cutting kanji plus a token, a cut slip that proves you paid." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how buying a ticket actually goes. At most stations there is a row of ticket machines, called 「券売機|けんばいき」. Above them hangs a big fare map. You find your destination, check the price, and press that amount on the machine. If you'd rather ask a person, go to the ticket window and say 「大阪までの切符を一枚ください|おおさかまでのきっぷをいちまいください」, one ticket to Osaka, please. Notice the counter 「枚|まい」, the one for flat things like paper, since a ticket is a flat slip. Two words to know are 「片道|かたみち」, one way, and 「往復|おうふく」, round trip. If you pay too little and ride further than planned, don't panic. Before the exit gate there is a fare adjustment machine, 「精算機|せいさんき」, where you pay the difference. And if you lose your ticket, you usually have to pay again, so keep it safe until you leave the gate. So: find your fare, buy a 「切符|きっぷ」, and hold onto it until the end." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word is a great example of the little pause, the small 「っ」. Say it with a clear beat in the middle: 「き」, a tiny silent hold, then 「ぷ」. Three beats, not two. If you skip the pause and soften the end, you get 「きふ」, which is actually a different word, 「寄付|きふ」, a donation. That's a real mix-up waiting to happen. The sound change here follows a pattern you will see again and again. When a kanji reading ending in 「つ」 meets a reading starting with 「ふ」, the 「つ」 shrinks to a small 「っ」 and the 「ふ」 hardens to 「ぷ」. You already know the same thing with numbers: 「一分|いっぷん」, one minute, and 「六分|ろっぷん」, six minutes, where 「ふん」 becomes 「ぷん」. Its cousin 「切手|きって」, a stamp, has the same pause, and if you drop it you get 「来て|きて」, come. So tap your foot on the silent beat: 「き」, hold, 「ぷ」. The pause is part of the word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words that all translate as ticket, and each has its own territory. 「切符|きっぷ」 is the classic word for train and bus tickets. 「チケット」, from English, is the usual word for concerts, movies, sports events, and plane tickets, the kind you might buy online. Then there's the kanji 「券|けん」, which means a ticket or voucher and appears in lots of compounds. 「乗車券|じょうしゃけん」 is the official term printed on a train ticket, literally a riding ticket. 「入場券|にゅうじょうけん」 is an admission ticket. And at many ramen shops and cafeterias you buy a 「食券|しょっけん」, a meal ticket, from a vending machine by the door, then hand it to the cook. The ticket machine itself, 「券売機|けんばいき」, also uses 「券|けん」. A quick rule: trains and buses, say 「切符|きっぷ」. Events and flights, say 「チケット」. Signs and printed forms, look for 「券|けん」. Three words, three territories." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The cutting kanji 「切|きっ」 plus 「符|ぷ」, a token or tally, makes a cut-off slip that proves you paid, from the days when staff clipped every ticket by hand.",
      "Find your fare on the map above the 「券売機|けんばいき」, buy your ticket, and keep it until the exit gate, using the 「精算機|せいさんき」 if you underpaid.",
      "Say it in three beats with a silent hold: 「き」, pause, 「ぷ」. Drop the pause and you get 「寄付|きふ」, a donation.",
      "Use 「切符|きっぷ」 for trains and buses, 「チケット」 for events and flights, and look for 「券|けん」 on signs and printed forms."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}で[切符|きっぷ]をかいました。",
    en: "I bought a ticket at the station." },
  "〜だい|〜代": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a suffix, so it attaches to the end of a noun to mean the charge or fee for that thing. It uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「だい」. The pattern is very simple: the thing you pay for plus 「代|だい」. 「電話代|でんわだい」 is the phone bill, from 「電話|でんわ」, telephone. 「電気代|でんきだい」 is the electricity bill, from 「電気|でんき」. 「バス代|バスだい」 is bus fare. You can even say 「本代|ほんだい」, money spent on books. It's very handy in daily life when you talk about bills and expenses, and you can make your own combinations. The same kanji has another big meaning: generation or a span of time. You'll hear 「二十代|にじゅうだい」, in one's twenties, and 「時代|じだい」, an era. A way to remember both meanings: the native reading of this kanji is in 「代わりに|かわりに」, instead of, in exchange. A fee is what you hand over in exchange for something, and a generation is one group taking the place of another. That's a memory hook, not a history lesson. Don't confuse it with the counter 「〜台|〜だい」 for machines, which sounds the same. To recap: put 「代|だい」 after a noun, and it becomes the fee for it." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a whole set of suffixes for money you pay, and they're worth sorting out. 「代|だい」 is the friendly, everyday one for what you spend on something: 「電話代|でんわだい」, the phone bill, or 「タクシー代|タクシーだい」, taxi fare. Then there's 「料|りょう」, which sounds more official and is used for a fee charged for a service: 「授業料|じゅぎょうりょう」, tuition, and 「入場料|にゅうじょうりょう」, an admission fee. You'll see 「料金|りょうきん」 on price signs, meaning a charge or rate. Next, 「費|ひ」 is for categories of expenses, as in a budget: 「交通費|こうつうひ」, transportation costs, which companies often pay for their workers' commutes. Finally, rent has its own special word, 「家賃|やちん」, house fee. In daily chat, 「代|だい」 is the one you'll reach for most. You can even say 「電話代|でんわだい」 or the more official 「電話料金|でんわりょうきん」 for the same bill, depending on the tone. Different suffixes, same idea: money going out." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The left side of 「代|だい」 is the person radical, the slim standing figure you also see in 「休む|やすむ」, to rest, and 「住む|すむ」, to live. The right side is a simple shape that dictionaries treat as mainly giving the sound. The person radical fits nicely with the kanji's core meaning of substitute, one person standing in for another, though that's a way to see it rather than a history lesson. With its native reading, it's the verb 「代わる|かわる」, to take someone's place, and the expression 「代わりに|かわりに」, instead. Its Chinese-origin reading has two forms. Usually it's 「だい」, as here. But in a few words it's 「たい」, like 「交代|こうたい」, taking turns or switching shifts, as when a soccer player is substituted. So when you see this kanji, think of someone stepping into another person's place. Money takes the place of goods, a new generation takes the place of the old, and a substitute player takes the place of a tired one. One standing figure, several meanings." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Listen at a Japanese dinner party and you'll hear this suffix constantly. 「飲み代|のみだい」 is money for drinks, as in 「今日の飲み代はいくら|きょうののみだいはいくら」, how much was tonight's drinking? Friends talk about rising bills with 「今月は電気代が高い|こんげつはでんきだいがたかい」, the electricity bill is high this month. Students complain about 「本代|ほんだい」, money for textbooks. Notice the noun before 「代|だい」 can be almost anything you spend on, including loanwords like 「ガス代|ガスだい」, the gas bill. In shops, older people sometimes say 「お代|おだい」 alone, meaning the price or payment. A kind shopkeeper might say 「お代はけっこうです|おだいはけっこうです」, no charge, it's on the house. You'd also hear 「お代はいくらですか|おだいはいくらですか」 in old-fashioned settings. To use the word, just put what you spent money on in front, then 「代|だい」, and talk about whether it's 「高い|たかい」 or 「安い|やすい」. A tiny suffix that turns almost anything into a bill." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Put 「代|だい」 after whatever you pay for and it becomes the fee: 「電話代|でんわだい」 is the phone bill and 「電気代|でんきだい」 the electricity bill.",
      "Of the money suffixes, 「代|だい」 is the everyday one, while 「料|りょう」 is a more official service fee, 「費|ひ」 a budget category, and rent is 「家賃|やちん」.",
      "The person radical fits the core idea of substitute, one person standing in for another, as in 「代わりに|かわりに」, instead, so money stands in for goods.",
      "You hear it constantly in casual talk, like 「飲み代|のみだい」 for drinks money or 「今月は電気代が高い|こんげつはでんきだいがたかい」, the electricity bill is high this month."
    ],
    ja: "{電話|でんわ}[代|だい|〜だい]がたかいです。",
    en: "My phone bill is expensive." },
  "ようじ|用事": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with their Chinese-origin readings. 「用|よう」 means use, purpose, or business to do. 「事|じ」 means thing or matter, the abstract kind of thing, not an object you can hold. So 「用事|ようじ」 is a matter of business, something you have to take care of: an errand, an appointment, a task. The second kanji is worth tracking, because it shows up in lots of words you know with different readings. In 「仕事|しごと」, a job, it's read 「ごと」. In 「返事|へんじ」, a reply, and in 「お大事に|おだいじに」, get well soon, it's 「じ」 again, just like here. The usual pattern is 「用事がある|ようじがある」, I have something to do. And here's the cultural part. Japanese people often turn down invitations politely by saying something vague like 「ちょっと用事があって|ちょっとようじがあって」, I have a little something to take care of, and then trailing off. Nobody asks what the errand is. It's a gentle, face-saving no, and it's useful for you too. A mistake to avoid: don't use it for a full-time job, which is 「仕事|しごと」. To recap: a matter of use, 「用|よう」, plus thing, 「事|じ」. Something you have to do." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「用|よう」, use or business, appears in many words you'll actually see around you. On a train or airplane toilet, the little sign 「使用中|しようちゅう」 means occupied, literally in use. 「利用|りよう」 means to make use of something, like a library or a service, and signs often say thanks for using us. 「用意|ようい」 means preparation: at a race, the starter says 「用意、ドン|ようい、ドン」, get set, go! And 「急用|きゅうよう」 is urgent business, a sudden errand that can't wait. 「用|よう」 alone is also used: a shop clerk might ask 「何かご用ですか|なにかごようですか」, can I help you with something, literally do you have some business here? A friend might ask casually 「何か用|なにかよう」, what do you want? So the common thread is a purpose, a use, a reason to do something. 「用事|ようじ」 is simply a matter of that kind of purpose. Keep that in mind and you'll spot 「用|よう」 everywhere." },
      { title: "Near-synonyms", text: "Several words overlap with 「用事|ようじ」, and it helps to know how they differ. 「用事|ようじ」 is a task or errand you need to take care of, something with a purpose, like going to the bank or the post office. 「予定|よてい」 is a plan or schedule, anything you've planned to do, including fun things. 「予定がある|よていがある」 just means I've got plans. 「約束|やくそく」 is a promise or appointment with another person. If you're meeting a friend at six, that's a 「約束|やくそく」. And 「仕事|しごと」 is your work or job. So if you're going shopping with a friend tomorrow, it's both a 「予定|よてい」 and a 「約束|やくそく」, but it sounds a bit odd to call it a 「用事|ようじ」, because it's fun rather than a chore. If you need to renew your visa, that's a perfect 「用事|ようじ」. Picking the right word tells people what kind of thing is filling your time. A chore, a plan, a promise, or your job." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's build some real sentences with this word using patterns from your lessons. To give a reason, combine it with 「ので」: 「用事があるので、先に帰ります|ようじがあるので、さきにかえります」, I have something to do, so I'll head home first. That's a polite way to leave early. To say why you went somewhere, use 「で」 for the reason: 「用事で東京に行きました|ようじでとうきょうにいきました」, I went to Tokyo on business. To ask about someone's errand gently, try 「用事は終わりましたか|ようじはおわりましたか」, did you finish your errand? You'll also hear 「用事がすむ|ようじがすむ」, the errand gets done. And to turn down an invitation with a little more detail, add 「ちょっと」 and 「んです」: 「すみません、その日はちょっと用事があるんです|すみません、そのひはちょっとようじがあるんです」. The 「んです」 makes it sound like an explanation rather than a flat refusal. These patterns let one simple noun do a lot of social work." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「用|よう」, use or business, plus 「事|じ」, an abstract matter, makes something you have to take care of, and 「ちょっと用事があって|ちょっとようじがあって」 is a gentle, face-saving way to decline.",
      "The common thread of 「用|よう」 is purpose, as in 「使用中|しようちゅう」 on an occupied toilet or a clerk's 「何かご用ですか|なにかごようですか」, can I help you?",
      "A 「用事|ようじ」 is a chore-like errand, unlike 「予定|よてい」 plans, a 「約束|やくそく」 with a person, or 「仕事|しごと」, your job.",
      "Pair it with 「ので」 to leave politely: 「用事があるので、先に帰ります|ようじがあるので、さきにかえります」, I have something to do, so I'll head home first."
    ],
    ja: "あしたは[用事|ようじ]があります。",
    en: "I have things to take care of tomorrow." },
  "おてあらい|お手洗い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three pieces. First, the polite 「お」. Then 「手|て」, hand, with its native reading. Then 「洗い|あらい」, from the verb 「洗う|あらう」, to wash. Literally it's the honorable hand washing, the place where you wash your hands. It's a gentle, indirect way to name the restroom without saying what you actually do there, a lot like English speakers say bathroom or washroom. 「手|て」 is in many words you know: 「手紙|てがみ」, a letter, paper by hand, and 「手伝う|てつだう」, to help, lending a hand. The washing kanji also appears in 「洗濯する|せんたくする」, to do laundry, with its Chinese-origin reading 「せん」. On politeness: 「トイレ」, from English toilet, is the neutral everyday word and is perfectly fine. 「お手洗い|おてあらい」 is a step politer, good for asking a stranger, a shopkeeper, or your teacher. In department stores and hotels you might also see signs with an even fancier word, the powder room. The classic question is 「お手洗いはどこですか|おてあらいはどこですか」, where's the restroom. To recap: polite 「お」, hand, washing: the place where you wash your hands." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a whole ladder of words for the restroom, from blunt to very refined. At the bottom is 「便所|べんじょ」, an old, rather crude word, a bit like the john. You'll hear it in old movies, but it's best avoided. 「トイレ」 is the everyday, neutral word everyone uses. 「お手洗い|おてあらい」 is a polite step up, soft and indirect. Above that is 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」, literally a makeup room, which is what you'll see on signs in department stores, hotels, and on airplanes, much like powder room in English. At home, there's also 「洗面所|せんめんじょ」, the washroom area with the sink, where you brush your teeth. In many Japanese homes, the toilet is in its own tiny room, separate from the bath and the sink, so these really are different places. On signs, look for 「男|おとこ」 for men and 「女|おんな」 for women, often in blue and red. Choose your word for the setting: 「トイレ」 with friends, 「お手洗い|おてあらい」 when being polite." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese restrooms can surprise first-time visitors. Many toilets have a control panel with a row of buttons for a warm seat, a bidet spray, and drying. The key one to find is 「流す|ながす」, to flush. Sometimes it's a button on the wall rather than a handle. Some women's restrooms have a button that plays a flushing or running water sound, so no one hears you. In private homes there's a special custom: you take off your shoes at the door and wear indoor slippers, but the toilet has its own separate pair of slippers. You switch into them at the toilet door and switch back when you leave. Walking out into the living room still wearing toilet slippers is a classic mistake, and people will notice. On the bright side, public restrooms in stations, parks, and convenience stores are free and usually very clean. So if you need one, just ask 「お手洗いを借りてもいいですか|おてあらいをかりてもいいですか」, may I use the restroom, literally may I borrow it." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The 「洗い|あらい」 in this word is a neat bit of grammar you can reuse. Take the polite form of a verb, 「洗います|あらいます」, drop 「ます」, and you have the stem 「洗い|あらい」, which can work as a noun: washing. Japanese does this all the time. 「皿洗い|さらあらい」 is dishwashing. 「休む|やすむ」, to rest, gives 「休み|やすみ」, a break or day off. 「帰る|かえる」 gives 「帰り|かえり」, the way home. 「買う|かう」 gives the 「買い|かい」 in 「買い物|かいもの」, shopping. And 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」 gives 「遊び|あそび」, play. Once you notice this pattern, a lot of long words suddenly break into pieces you already know. 「お手洗い|おてあらい」 is just polite 「お」, plus 「手|て」, plus the noun form of washing. You'll also see 「手洗い|てあらい」 without 「お」 on signs that remind you to wash your hands. Verb stems as nouns: a small trick that unlocks many words." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Polite 「お」, plus 「手|て」, hand, plus 「洗い|あらい」, washing, makes the place where you wash your hands, a step politer than 「トイレ」.",
      "On the restroom ladder, 「便所|べんじょ」 is crude, 「トイレ」 is neutral, 「お手洗い|おてあらい」 is polite, and 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」 appears on fancy signs.",
      "Look for the 「流す|ながす」 button to flush, and in Japanese homes switch into the separate toilet slippers and back again when you leave.",
      "Drop 「ます」 from 「洗います|あらいます」 and the stem 「洗い|あらい」 works as a noun, the same trick behind 「休み|やすみ」 and 「帰り|かえり」."
    ],
    ja: "[お手洗い|おてあらい]はどこですか。",
    en: "Where is the restroom?" },
  "しあい|試合": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, with an unusual mix of readings. 「試|し」 uses its Chinese-origin reading and means to try or test. It's the same kanji as in 「試験|しけん」, an exam, from this same lesson. 「合|あい」 uses its native reading and comes from the verb 「合う|あう」, to fit together, to match, to come together. You've seen 「合う|あう」 in 「似合う|にあう」, to look good on someone, when clothes fit you, and 「付き合う|つきあう」, to go out with someone, keeping each other company. So 「試合|しあい」 is two sides coming together to try their strength. It's used for sports and competitions with an opponent: soccer, baseball, tennis, judo. Two verbs to pair with it are 「勝つ|かつ」, to win, and 「負ける|まける」, to lose. A typical pattern is 「試合がある|しあいがある」, there's a game, or 「試合を見る|しあいをみる」, to watch a game. A common mistake for English speakers is using it for game in the sense of a video game or board game. That's 「ゲーム」. 「試合|しあい」 is a match against someone. A cultural note: Japan's high school baseball tournament at Koshien is huge, and the whole country watches. To recap: trying, 「試|し」, plus coming together, 「合|あい」. A match." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in 「試合|しあい」 leads into its own family. The first, 「試|し」, try or test, is in 「試験|しけん」, an exam, and in the verb 「試す|ためす」, to try something out. In clothing stores you'll see 「試着室|しちゃくしつ」, the fitting room, where you try clothes on. The second, 「合|あい」, coming together or fitting, is even bigger. 「間に合う|まにあう」 means to be in time, literally to fit into the gap. 「話し合う|はなしあう」 is to talk something over together. 「場合|ばあい」 means a case or situation, as in in case of fire. With the Chinese-origin reading 「ごう」, 「合格|ごうかく」 means passing an exam. And there's 「似合う|にあう」, to suit someone. Notice that the idea of things meeting or matching runs through all of them. So a 「試合|しあい」 is a test where two sides meet. Next time you see either kanji, think: trying, or coming together." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people talk about a match. To play in one, say 「試合に出る|しあいにでる」, literally to go out into the match. A coach might say 「明日の試合に出てください|あしたのしあいにでてください」, please play in tomorrow's match. To win is 「試合に勝つ|しあいにかつ」, and to lose is 「試合に負ける|しあいにまける」. Notice 「に」 there, not 「を」. A tie is 「引き分け|ひきわけ」. For a bigger event made up of many matches, the word is 「大会|たいかい」, a tournament or meet. Here's a quick exchange. One friend asks 「昨日の試合、どうだった|きのうのしあい、どうだった」, how was yesterday's game? The other says 「三対二で勝った|さんたいにでかった」, we won three to two. That 「対|たい」 is how scores are read, like three to two. If you just watched on TV, 「試合を見た|しあいをみた」 works fine. So: play in it with 「に出る|にでる」, win or lose it with 「に」, and read scores with 「対|たい」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese sports and martial arts carry a lot of ceremony around a match. In judo, kendo, and karate, players bow to each other before and after every bout. A well-known saying in the martial arts is 「礼に始まり礼に終わる|れいにはじまりれいにおわる」, it begins with a bow and ends with a bow. Even in baseball, high school teams line up at home plate and bow to each other before the first pitch and after the last out. At the famous summer tournament at Koshien Stadium, players from losing teams often kneel and scoop the infield dirt into bags to take home as a keepsake. Sumo is a special case. A sumo bout isn't usually called a 「試合|しあい」; it's called a 「取組|とりくみ」. And wrestlers perform purification rituals, like throwing salt, before each one. So a 「試合|しあい」 in Japan is not just about winning. Respect for the opponent is built right into the ritual." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「試|し」, to try or test, plus 「合|あい」 from 「合う|あう」, to come together, is two sides meeting to test their strength, a match, not a video game.",
      "「試|し」 brings 「試験|しけん」 and 「試着室|しちゃくしつ」, while 「合|あい」 brings meeting words like 「間に合う|まにあう」 and 「話し合う|はなしあう」.",
      "Play in a match with 「試合に出る|しあいにでる」, win or lose it with 「に」, and read scores with 「対|たい」, as in 「三対二|さんたいに」.",
      "Respect is built into the ritual, with players bowing before and after, though a sumo bout is called a 「取組|とりくみ」 rather than a 「試合|しあい」."
    ],
    ja: "あした、サッカーの[試合|しあい]があります。",
    en: "There is a soccer match tomorrow." },
  "せいじ|政治": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. 「政|せい」 means government or governing affairs. 「治|じ」 means to rule, to manage, to bring under control. The same kanji with a native reading also gives the verb 「治る|なおる」, to heal or get better, A way to remember it: governing a country well is a bit like keeping a body healthy. Together, 「政治|せいじ」 is the business of governing, politics. A great sibling here is 「政治家|せいじか」, a politician. You know 「家|か」 from 「漫画家|まんがか」, cartoonist, and 「作家|さっか」, writer: the person whose profession it is. So a politician is a professional of politics. You'll also see 「治|じ」 in the name of the Meiji era, 「明治|めいじ」, which means enlightened rule. That was the period when Japan modernized in the late eighteen-hundreds. The usual pattern in this lesson is 「政治にきょうみがある|せいじにきょうみがある」, to be interested in politics, with 「に」 marking what you're interested in. Politics is also connected to 「大統領|だいとうりょう」, a president of a country. To recap: government, 「政|せい」, plus rule, 「治|じ」. The business of governing." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's open up the first kanji, 「政|せい」. On the left is 「正|せい」, the kanji for correct or right, which you know from 「正しい|ただしい」 and from 「お正月|おしょうがつ」, New Year. It gives this kanji its sound, 「せい」. On the right is a radical that represents a hand holding a stick, a sign of action or making something happen. Dictionaries commonly explain the pair as making things right, which is a fitting picture of governing. Now the second kanji, 「治|じ」. Its left side is the water radical. Some explanations connect it with controlling rivers and floods, an ancient job of rulers, but the origin is debated, so take that as a nice picture rather than a fact. This kanji has a second Chinese-origin reading, 「ち」, in 「治安|ちあん」, public safety, as in Japan's 「治安がいい|ちあんがいい」, safe streets. So: making things right, plus bringing things under control. That's the work of politics." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Politics is a topic many Japanese people avoid in casual conversation, especially at work or with people they've just met. If someone asks your opinion and you'd rather not say, 「よく分かりません|よくわかりません」, I'm not really sure, is a perfectly normal dodge. A few key words help you follow the news. Japan's parliament is called 「国会|こっかい」, the Diet. The head of government is the prime minister, 「首相|しゅしょう」. An election is 「選挙|せんきょ」, and to vote is 「投票する|とうひょうする」. In 2016, the voting age was lowered from twenty to eighteen. And during election season you'll notice something very Japanese: candidates drive around in vans with loudspeakers on the roof, calling out their own names over and over and waving white gloves. They also stand outside train stations in the morning, bowing to commuters. Politics may be quiet at the dinner table, but it gets loud on the streets in election season." },
      { title: "Academic cousins", text: "If you study in Japan or talk about university majors, 「政治|せいじ」 shows up in a little family of subjects. Add 「学|がく」, study, and you get 「政治学|せいじがく」, political science. You already know 「経済|けいざい」, economics, and its subject name 「経済学|けいざいがく」. Many Japanese universities have a department that combines them, 「政治経済学部|せいじけいざいがくぶ」, the school of politics and economics, often shortened in conversation. Other majors you know follow the same shape: 「文学|ぶんがく」, literature, and 「歴史|れきし」, history. So when someone asks 「専攻は何ですか|せんこうはなんですか」, what's your major, you might answer 「政治学です|せいじがくです」. Notice how 「学|がく」 turns a topic into a field of study. And like 「政治家|せいじか」 for the person, these patterns let you build words from pieces. The topic, plus 「学|がく」 for studying it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「政|せい」, government, plus 「治|じ」, to rule or bring under control, is the business of governing, and add 「家|か」 for a politician, 「政治家|せいじか」.",
      "「政|せい」 pairs 「正|せい」, correct, with a hand holding a stick, making things right, and 「治|じ」 has the water radical and the reading 「ち」 in 「治安|ちあん」.",
      "Many people avoid politics in casual talk, but election season gets loud, with candidates calling out their names from vans and bowing to commuters at stations.",
      "Add 「学|がく」 and it becomes a field of study, 「政治学|せいじがく」, political science, alongside 「経済学|けいざいがく」, economics."
    ],
    ja: "[政治|せいじ]にきょうみがあります。",
    en: "I am interested in politics." },
  "せいせき|成績": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. 「成|せい」 means to become, to accomplish, to complete. 「績|せき」 means achievement, the results you pile up. Look at its left side: that's the thread radical, the same one in many fabric and string kanji. The old meaning of this kanji was spinning thread, twisting fibers together little by little. A way to remember it: your grades are like thread you spin bit by bit all semester, and at the end you have a whole spool of results. So 「成績|せいせき」 is accomplished results, your grades or overall performance. It's often your record over a term or a year, not just a single score on one test, though people use it for test results too. The patterns are 「成績がいい|せいせきがいい」, good grades, and 「成績が悪い|せいせきがわるい」, bad grades. Notice it uses 「が」, because it's describing the grades. A cultural note: Japanese students get a report card at the end of each term, and some kids dread bringing it home before summer vacation. Be careful: 「せき」 at the end sounds like 「せき」, cough, from this lesson, but it's a totally different word. To recap: accomplished results, spun up bit by bit. Your grades." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「成|せい」, is worth knowing well because it pops up in some important places. Its native reading is in the verb 「成る|なる」, to become, which you usually write in kana. Its Chinese-origin reading 「せい」 is in 「成人|せいじん」, an adult, a person who has become grown up. Japan has a national holiday in January called 「成人の日|せいじんのひ」, Coming of Age Day, when young adults dress up in kimono and suits for ceremonies. It's also in 「完成|かんせい」, completion, when something is finished. And if you fly into Tokyo, you might land at 「成田|なりた」 airport, where the kanji has a different native reading. So the core idea is becoming or completing something. Add 「績|せき」, the spun-up results, and your 「成績|せいせき」 is what your efforts have become by the end of the term. Watch for this kanji in holidays, airports, and finished projects." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people actually talk about grades. When they improve, say 「成績が上がった|せいせきがあがった」, my grades went up. When they slip, 「成績が下がった|せいせきがさがった」, they went down. In Japanese schools the report card is called 「通知表|つうちひょう」, and many use a five-point scale where five is the top mark. Be careful not to mix up 「成績|せいせき」 with a single test score. For one test, people talk about points, 「点|てん」: 「テストで九十点を取った|テストできゅうじってんをとった」, I got ninety points on the test. 「成績|せいせき」 is the bigger picture. A quick dialogue: a mother asks 「今学期の成績はどう|こんがっきのせいせきはどう」, how are your grades this term? The child mumbles 「まあまあ」, so-so. Adults use the word too, for performance at work, like sales results. So it's points for one test, and 「成績|せいせき」 for the overall record." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a seesaw in a schoolyard. On one end sits your report card. Every time you study hard, the report card end swings up. Every time you skip homework, it crashes down. Seesaw, seesaw. That's the 「せい」 part. Now, at the very end of the term, you hand your report card to your parents, and they read it and let out a nervous little cough. That cough is 「せき」. Put it together: the seesaw of your grades, and your parents' cough when they see them. Seesaw, then cough: 「せいせき」. As a bonus, the cough reminds you that 「せき」 alone means a cough, a separate word, so you'll keep them apart. Remember, this is only a memory trick built on the sounds, not anything about the word's origin. A grade seesaw and a worried cough." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「成|せい」, to accomplish, plus 「績|せき」, achievement with the thread radical, is results spun up bit by bit over a term, used as 「成績がいい|せいせきがいい」.",
      "「成|せい」 means becoming or completing, as in 「成人|せいじん」, an adult, and 「完成|かんせい」, completion, so your grades are what your efforts became.",
      "Grades go up with 「成績が上がった|せいせきがあがった」 and down with 「成績が下がった|せいせきがさがった」, while a single test is counted in 「点|てん」, points.",
      "Picture a grade seesaw for 「せい」, then your parents' nervous cough, 「せき」, as they read your report card."
    ],
    ja: "[成績|せいせき]がよかったです。",
    en: "My grades were good." },
  "かのじょ|彼女": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji. 「彼|かの」 is an old word for that, the far-away that, just like the modern 「あの」. 「女|じょ」 means woman, using its Chinese-origin reading here. You know the native reading in 「女の人|おんなのひと」. So 「彼女|かのじょ」 is literally that woman. Here's a neat bit of history. Before the late eighteen-hundreds, Japanese didn't really have a word for she. When Japanese writers began translating European novels, they needed one, so this word was put to use for she. It took a while to feel natural in everyday speech. Today it has two meanings. It can be she, a woman being talked about, or girlfriend. In conversation, girlfriend is extremely common: 「彼女がいる|かのじょがいる」 means I have a girlfriend. A common mistake for English speakers is using it all the time for she, like in English. Japanese usually just uses the person's name plus 「さん」, or skips the subject entirely. Saying 「彼女|かのじょ」 about your boss or teacher can sound distant or odd. Its partner word is 「彼|かれ」, he or boyfriend. To recap: that woman, she, and very often your girlfriend." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「女|じょ」, woman, is one of the oldest pictures in the writing system. It's traditionally explained as a drawing of a woman kneeling with her arms crossed in front of her. It has several readings. The native one is 「おんな」, as in 「女の人|おんなのひと」 and 「女の子|おんなのこ」. The Chinese-origin reading 「じょ」 appears here and in 「女性|じょせい」, a woman, the polite, formal word, and in 「女子|じょし」, girls, which you'll see on restroom signs and school teams. There's also 「め」 in a few words like 「女神|めがみ」, a goddess. As a radical, 「女|おんな」 appears on the left side of many kanji you know: 「好き|すき」, to like, 「姉|あね」, older sister, 「妹|いもうと」, younger sister, and 「始める|はじめる」, to begin. In many of them it's a meaning clue, but in some it's just part of the shape. Look for the kneeling figure, and you'll start spotting it everywhere." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In casual conversation among young people, 「彼女|かのじょ」 meaning girlfriend is everywhere. A friend might ask 「彼女いるの|かのじょいるの」, do you have a girlfriend? And someone hoping to find one might say 「彼女がほしい|かのじょがほしい」, I want a girlfriend. When you start dating someone, the natural verb is 「できる」: 「彼女ができた|かのじょができた」, I got a girlfriend, literally a girlfriend came into being. An ex-girlfriend is 「元カノ|もとカノ」, using 「元|もと」, former, plus a clipped 「カノ」. The ex-boyfriend version is 「元カレ|もとカレ」. In texting and manga you'll see the girlfriend meaning written in katakana, 「カノジョ」, to make it clear. And here's a curious modern habit: some people now say 「彼女さん|かのじょさん」 to politely refer to someone else's girlfriend. To sum up: with 「いる」 you have one, with 「できる」 you get one, and with 「元|もと」 she's in the past." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Let's look at the first half, 「かの」. It's an old pointing word from classical Japanese, meaning that one over there. It hasn't disappeared. You'll still hear it in a slightly grand phrase, 「かの有名な|かのゆうめいな」, that famous, as in that famous painter, the one everyone knows. When writers in the late eighteen-hundreds needed a word for she, early texts often read this pair of kanji as 「かのおんな」, that woman, using the native reading for both halves. Over time, the reading settled into 「かのじょ」, which mixes a native reading for the first kanji with a Chinese-origin reading for the second. That mixed pattern is unusual, which is part of why this word feels a little different. By the early twentieth century it was firmly established, and soon the girlfriend meaning grew alongside she. So today's casual word for a girlfriend began as a scholarly translation tool. An old pointing word, a woman, and a modern romance." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「彼|かの」, an old far-away that, plus 「女|じょ」, woman, was put to use for she in the late eighteen-hundreds, and today it very often means girlfriend.",
      "「女|じょ」 is drawn as a kneeling woman and appears as a radical in kanji like 「好き|すき」, 「姉|あね」, and 「始める|はじめる」.",
      "Have one with 「彼女がいる|かのじょがいる」, get one with 「彼女ができた|かのじょができた」, and an ex is 「元カノ|もとカノ」.",
      "Early texts read the pair as 「かのおんな」, that woman, before it settled into the unusual mixed reading 「かのじょ」."
    ],
    ja: "[彼女|かのじょ]はやさしいです。",
    en: "She is kind." },
  "かれ|彼": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading 「かれ」. Long ago, 「かれ」 meant that one over there, pointing at a person or thing far away, and it could be used for anyone, man or woman. It's part of the same far-away family as 「あれ」, that over there. When Japanese writers began translating European languages in the late eighteen-hundreds, they used 「彼|かれ」 for he, and the separate word 「彼女|かのじょ」, that woman, was used for she. So now 「彼|かれ」 is mainly for men. Like its partner, it has two meanings: he, and boyfriend. In conversation, 「彼|かれ」 alone is often heard as my boyfriend, though 「彼氏|かれし」 is clearer. Here's the big tip for English speakers. Japanese doesn't use he nearly as much as English does. It's normal to just use the person's name, 「田中さん|たなかさん」, or leave the subject out when everyone knows who you mean. Using 「彼|かれ」 for someone older or higher in status, like your teacher, sounds too casual and distant. The left side of the kanji is the step radical, the one for going or walking somewhere. A way to remember it: he's the guy who walked over there. That's a hook. To recap: that one over there, now he or boyfriend." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The old pointing word 「かれ」 left some fun fossils in modern Japanese. One is 「かれこれ」, literally that and this, which now means roughly or about, as in 「かれこれ一時間待った|かれこれいちじかんまった」, I waited about an hour. It works like 「あれこれ」, this and that. The prettiest fossil is the word 「たそがれ」, dusk or twilight. Its accepted origin is an old phrase meaning who is that, over there? At dusk the light fades, and you can't quite make out the face of the person approaching, so you ask, who is that? The 「かれ」 in that phrase is the same far-away pointer. So the image of a figure in the dim evening light became the word for twilight itself. Today 「たそがれ」 is poetic, often used for the twilight years of life, or for gazing wistfully at a sunset. A tiny old word for that one over there still hides inside these expressions." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "We know the left side is the step radical. The right side is 「皮|かわ」, skin or hide, which here mainly gives the kanji its Chinese-origin sound, 「ひ」. You rarely hear that reading, but there's one important word that uses it: 「彼岸|ひがん」, literally that shore, the far bank. In Buddhism it means the other shore of enlightenment, beyond this world of suffering. In Japan, 「お彼岸|おひがん」 is a week around the spring and autumn equinoxes when families visit their ancestors' graves, clean them, and leave flowers and offerings. There's a saying, 「暑さ寒さも彼岸まで|あつささむさもひがんまで」, heat and cold last only until the equinox week, meaning the weather finally turns mild after that. The red spider lily, which blooms in autumn around this time, is often called the equinox flower. So the same kanji that means he can carry you to the far shore. A pointing word, a sound piece, and a seasonal custom." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese nouns don't usually change for plural, but personal pronouns can take plural endings. For he, add 「ら」: 「彼ら|かれら」, they, for a group of men or a mixed group. For she, you get 「彼女ら|かのじょら」 or the softer 「彼女たち|かのじょたち」, for a group of women. 「たち」 is the same ending as in 「私たち|わたしたち」, we, and 「子供たち|こどもたち」, children. 「ら」 can sound a little rougher or more written than 「たち」. Just like the singular, these are used less than English they. In conversation, people say things like 「あの人たち|あのひとたち」, those people, or name the group: 「田中さんたち|たなかさんたち」, Tanaka and the others. You'll mostly hear 「彼ら|かれら」 in news reports, writing, and story narration. One more pattern: 「彼の|かれの」 with 「の」 makes his, as in 「彼の車|かれのくるま」, his car. So: add 「ら」 for they, 「の」 for his, and remember names usually win in speech." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「かれ」 once meant that one over there for anyone, but translators made it he, so today it means he or boyfriend and is used far less than English he.",
      "The old pointer survives in 「かれこれ」, roughly, and in 「たそがれ」, twilight, from an old phrase asking who is that, over there?",
      "The right side 「皮|かわ」 gives the sound 「ひ」, heard in 「彼岸|ひがん」, the far shore, and 「お彼岸|おひがん」, the equinox week of grave visits.",
      "Add 「ら」 for they, 「彼ら|かれら」, or 「の」 for his, but in speech names usually win over pronouns."
    ],
    ja: "[彼|かれ]は{大学生|だいがくせい}です。",
    en: "He is a college student." },
  "かれし|彼氏": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji. 「彼|かれ」 is he or that one over there. 「氏|し」 is a title used after a person's name, a lot like Mister in formal writing. You'll see it in newspapers, where someone is called their family name plus 「氏|し」. Put them together, and 「彼氏|かれし」 is like Mister He. It started as a playful, jokey word in the early twentieth century and stuck. Today it's the clear, everyday word for boyfriend. Why is it useful? 「彼|かれ」 alone can mean he or boyfriend, so it can be ambiguous. 「彼氏|かれし」 only means boyfriend, never just he. Its partner is 「彼女|かのじょ」, which does double duty for she and girlfriend. The usual patterns are 「彼氏がいる|かれしがいる」, to have a boyfriend, and in this lesson 「彼氏と別れる|かれしとわかれる」, to break up with a boyfriend, using 「と」 for with. To recap: he plus a Mister title. Mister He is your boyfriend." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「氏|し」, is a small but useful one. Its ancient meaning is clan or family line, and with its native reading 「うじ」 it still means a clan name, a word you'll meet in Japanese history, where powerful families had clan names. Its Chinese-origin reading 「し」 shows up in a word you'll see on almost every form in Japan: 「氏名|しめい」, full name. When filling out paperwork at a city office or a hospital, the box labeled with this word is where your full name goes. In news and formal writing, 「氏|し」 is attached after a family name as a respectful title, a bit like Mr. or Ms. in a newspaper. So 「彼氏|かれし」 takes that formal title and sticks it onto 「彼|かれ」, he. Japanese dictionaries usually trace the coinage to around 1930, credited to a popular film narrator and entertainer named Tokugawa Musei, and it began as a joke. A clan word, a form label, and a playful boyfriend." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how the word lives in everyday talk. When someone starts dating, the natural phrase is 「彼氏ができた|かれしができた」, I got a boyfriend. Wanting one is 「彼氏がほしい|かれしがほしい」. A friend might tease, 「彼氏とはどう|かれしとはどう」, so how are things with your boyfriend? Near-synonyms are worth knowing. 「恋人|こいびと」 means a romantic partner and works for anyone, but it sounds a bit serious or bookish in casual speech. Some people avoid labels and say 「付き合っている人|つきあっているひと」, the person I'm going out with, which is gentle and gender-neutral. An ex-boyfriend is 「元カレ|もとカレ」, from 「元|もと」, former. Here's a short exchange. One friend asks, 「週末、何するの|しゅうまつ、なにするの」, what are you doing this weekend? The other answers, 「彼氏と映画を見に行く|かれしとえいがをみにいく」, going to see a movie with my boyfriend. Use 「できる」 for getting one, 「と」 for doing things with him, and 「恋人|こいびと」 when you want something more neutral." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「彼|かれ」, he, plus the formal title 「氏|し」 makes a jokey Mister He that stuck as the clear word for boyfriend, never just he.",
      "「氏|し」 means clan, read 「うじ」, and appears in 「氏名|しめい」, full name, on forms, and the word began as a joke around 1930.",
      "Get one with 「彼氏ができた|かれしができた」, do things with him using 「と」, and say 「恋人|こいびと」 when you want something more neutral."
    ],
    ja: "[彼氏|かれし]とえいがを{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I watched a movie with my boyfriend." },
  "いみ|意味": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. Look at the first one, 「意|い」. It's built from two pieces you can see: 「音|おと」, sound, stacked on top of 「心|こころ」, heart or mind. A way to remember it: the sound of your heart, what you really have in mind. Its meaning is intention, will, what's in your mind. You'll see it again in 「注意する|ちゅういする」, to watch out, literally to pour your mind onto something. The second kanji is 「味|み」, taste or flavor. With its native reading it's 「味|あじ」, the flavor of food, and it has the mouth radical on the left. It's also in 「興味|きょうみ」, interest, from this same lesson. So 「意味|いみ」 is the flavor of what's in someone's mind, the meaning behind their words. The handiest question you can learn is 「どういう意味ですか|どういういみですか」, what does that mean, or 「この言葉の意味は何ですか|このことばのいみはなんですか」, what does this word mean. Another useful phrase is 「意味がない|いみがない」, it's pointless, literally there's no meaning. To recap: the mind's intention, 「意|い」, plus taste, 「味|み」. The flavor of what someone means." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The most useful pattern with this word is explaining what something means. You use 「という意味です|といういみです」, it means such-and-such. For example, 「すてきは、とてもいいという意味です|すてきは、とてもいいといういみです」, the word lovely means very good. You can also ask about a sentence someone said: 「それはどういう意味|それはどういういみ」, what do you mean by that? Careful with tone. Said sharply, that question can sound like you're offended, like what's that supposed to mean? To say you understand the meaning, use 「が」: 「意味が分かります|いみがわかります」, I understand the meaning, or the negative 「意味が分かりません|いみがわかりません」. In formal writing, 「意味|いみ」 also works as a 「する」 verb, 「意味する|いみする」, to mean or signify: 「赤は危険を意味する|あかはきけんをいみする」, red signifies danger. So in conversation, use 「という意味|といういみ」, and in writing, you'll see 「意味する|いみする」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「味|み」, taste, is in a word you probably use a lot: 「趣味|しゅみ」, a hobby. Literally it's the taste of what you enjoy. Together with 「興味|きょうみ」, interest, and 「意味|いみ」, meaning, that's a little family where taste becomes a way of describing what attracts the mind. The first kanji, 「意|い」, mind or intention, has its own family. 「意見|いけん」 is an opinion, literally what your mind sees. 「用意|ようい」 is preparation, setting your mind on what you'll use. 「意外|いがい」 means unexpected, outside of what your mind expected. And 「注意|ちゅうい」 is caution. In each case, 「意|い」 is about what's going on inside your head. So when you see 「意|い」, think mind, and when you see 「味|み」, think taste, literal or figurative. Your hobby is the taste of fun, your interest is a rising taste, and meaning is the taste of the mind." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Young Japanese people use this word in some lively ways. 「意味ない|いみない」, there's no point, is a common complaint: studying the night before is pointless, or waiting is pointless. 「意味分かんない|いみわかんない」, with the casual shortened ending, is like I don't get it, or that makes zero sense, often said with a laugh at something absurd. There's also a four-kanji slang phrase, 「意味不明|いみふめい」, meaning unclear, which people use for something baffling or nonsensical, and online it even gets clipped to 「いみふ」. On the more serious side, a teacher might say 「いい意味で|いいいみで」, in a good way, as in he's crazy, in a good way. And the opposite is 「悪い意味で|わるいいみで」, in a bad way. These are just as useful as the dictionary meaning. So whether you're questioning a test answer or laughing at a strange video, this word has you covered. Pointless, baffling, or in a good way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「意|い」, sound over heart, is what's in your mind, and 「味|み」 is taste, so meaning is the flavor of what someone means, as in 「どういう意味ですか|どういういみですか」.",
      "Explain a meaning with 「という意味です|といういみです」, and in formal writing you'll see 「意味する|いみする」, to signify.",
      "「味|み」 links 「趣味|しゅみ」, 「興味|きょうみ」, and 「意味|いみ」, while 「意|い」 is about the mind, as in 「意見|いけん」, an opinion.",
      "Young people say 「意味ない|いみない」 for pointless and 「意味不明|いみふめい」 for baffling, while 「いい意味で|いいいみで」 means in a good way."
    ],
    ja: "このことばの[意味|いみ]はなんですか。",
    en: "What is the meaning of this word?" },
  "せまい|狭い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is an i-adjective with the native reading 「せまい」. It means a space is cramped, tight, narrow, not roomy. Its opposite is 「広い|ひろい」, wide or spacious, also in this lesson. Look at the right side of the kanji. It's the same piece found in 「挟む|はさむ」, to squeeze something between two things. That's a good picture: a narrow space squeezes you in on both sides. Here's the important tip for English speakers. When a room or an apartment is small, Japanese usually says 「狭い|せまい」, not 「小さい|ちいさい」. 「小さい|ちいさい」 is about the size of an object, like a small box or a small dog. 「狭い|せまい」 is about how little space there is to move around in. So 「部屋が狭い|へやがせまい」, my room is cramped, is the natural thing to say. It also works for roads, 「道が狭い|みちがせまい」, a narrow street, which you'll see a lot in older Japanese neighborhoods. A cultural note: apartments in big Japanese cities like Tokyo are famously compact, so you'll hear this word often. Some one-room places are so tight you can almost touch both walls. There's also a phrase for narrow-minded: a narrow heart. To recap: squeezed in on both sides. Cramped, not roomy." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's put this adjective through the patterns from your lessons. It's a regular i-adjective, so the past is 「狭かった|せまかった」, it was cramped, and the negative is 「狭くない|せまくない」, it's not cramped. To link it to another description, use the te-form 「狭くて|せまくて」: 「この部屋は狭くて、暗いです|このへやはせまくて、くらいです」, this room is cramped and dark. To compare, use 「より」: 「私の部屋は兄の部屋より狭い|わたしのへやはあにのへやよりせまい」, my room is smaller than my older brother's. To say something has become cramped, add 「なる」: 「荷物が多くて、車が狭くなった|にもつがおおくて、くるまがせまくなった」, with all the luggage, the car got cramped. And for too cramped, drop the 「い」 and add 「すぎる」: 「狭すぎる|せますぎる」. All of these follow the same i-adjective rules you already know, with no surprises. One adjective, five patterns: past, negative, linking, comparing, and too much." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, room sizes are often measured in tatami mats, the woven straw mats on traditional floors. The counter is 「畳|じょう」, and one mat is roughly the size of a person lying down. A classic small room is 「四畳半|よじょうはん」, four and a half mats, about seven square meters, famous as the cramped room of a poor student in novels and songs. Apartment listings use codes like 1K or 2LDK, with the number counting rooms besides the kitchen, living, and dining area. A one-room apartment in Tokyo might be just six mats. Japanese homes deal with the squeeze cleverly: futons are folded away into a closet every morning, turning the bedroom back into a living room. And a 1979 European report famously described Japanese homes as rabbit hutches, a phrase that stung and was quoted for years. Capsule hotels take it even further, with sleeping pods barely big enough to sit up in. Cramped spaces are part of daily life, and people are very good at living in them." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond rooms and roads, this adjective appears in some lovely expressions. When you bump into a friend far from home, or discover a coworker knows your cousin, people say 「世界は狭いですね|せかいはせまいですね」, it's a small world, literally the world is narrow. The variation 「世間は狭い|せけんはせまい」 means the same thing. About people, 「心が狭い|こころがせまい」, a narrow heart, means petty or intolerant, someone who can't let small things go. Similarly, 「視野が狭い|しやがせまい」, a narrow field of view, means narrow-minded. And a very Japanese one: 「肩身が狭い|かたみがせまい」, literally your shoulders and body feel narrow, means to feel small, ashamed, or out of place, like being the only one who forgot the gift. Notice how the physical feeling of being squeezed becomes a feeling about the mind. So use it for apartments and alleys, but also for small worlds, small hearts, and feeling small. Squeezed space, squeezed feelings." }
    ],
    short: [
      "For a cramped room say 「狭い|せまい」, not 「小さい|ちいさい」, because it's about little space to move, and the kanji shares a piece with 「挟む|はさむ」, to squeeze.",
      "It's a regular i-adjective, so you get 「狭かった|せまかった」, 「狭くない|せまくない」, 「狭くて|せまくて」, and 「狭すぎる|せますぎる」 with no surprises.",
      "Rooms are measured in tatami mats, and the classic cramped student room is 「四畳半|よじょうはん」, four and a half mats.",
      "The squeeze becomes feelings in 「世界は狭いですね|せかいはせまいですね」, it's a small world, and 「心が狭い|こころがせまい」, petty."
    ],
    ja: "わたしのへやは[狭い|せまい]です。",
    en: "My room is small." },
  "ひろい|広い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "An i-adjective with the native reading 「ひろい」. It means wide or spacious, lots of room to move around. Its opposite is 「狭い|せまい」, cramped, from this same lesson. Look at the kanji. The outer part, the dot and the line with a long sweep down the left, is a radical that represents a building with a roof. You'll see it in other kanji for buildings and shops. A way to remember it: a big building with a wide roof, plenty of room inside. That's a hook built from the shape. With its Chinese-origin reading 「こう」, the same kanji appears in 「広告|こうこく」, an advertisement, which you know from your vocab: an announcement spread out widely to everyone. The city name 「広島|ひろしま」, Hiroshima, also uses it, meaning wide island. Like 「狭い|せまい」, it's used for spaces, not for the size of objects. So 「部屋が広い|へやがひろい」 is a roomy room, and 「この公園は広い|このこうえんはひろい」 is a big, spacious park. It also works for wide streets and broad knowledge. There's even a phrase for generous: a wide heart. A common mistake is using 「大きい|おおきい」, big, for a spacious room. That sounds odd. To recap: a wide roof, lots of room. Spacious." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a matched pair of verbs that are great to learn together. 「広げる|ひろげる」 is transitive, meaning you spread something out or widen it: 「地図を広げる|ちずをひろげる」, to spread out a map, or 「手を広げる|てをひろげる」, to open your arms wide. 「広がる|ひろがる」 is intransitive, meaning something spreads or widens on its own: 「うわさが広がる|うわさがひろがる」, a rumor spreads, or a view opening up before you. This 「える」 versus 「がる」 pattern is common, so recognizing it helps. Then there's 「広場|ひろば」, a plaza or open square, literally a wide place, where people gather in front of stations. And you know 「広島|ひろしま」 and 「広告|こうこく」. Notice that when you see the 「ひろ」 sound with this kanji, it's the native reading, and 「こう」 is the Chinese-origin one, used in more formal compounds. So one wide roof gives you to spread, to spread out, a plaza, and a city." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Some of the most useful phrases with 「広い|ひろい」 describe people rather than places. 「顔が広い|かおがひろい」, literally a wide face, means someone knows lots of people, someone well connected. If you need a good dentist or a part-time job, ask a friend whose face is wide. 「心が広い|こころがひろい」, a wide heart, means generous and forgiving, the opposite of petty. And 「幅広い|はばひろい」 means wide-ranging, as in a wide range of hobbies or fans of all ages. In travel, 「広い|ひろい」 describes big open places: 「北海道は広いです|ほっかいどうはひろいです」, Hokkaido is vast, which is what people say about Japan's spacious northern island. Notice how all of these use 「が」: something is wide. A small dialogue: one friend says 「田中さんは顔が広いね|たなかさんはかおがひろいね」, Tanaka knows everyone, huh. The other laughs, 「本当に|ほんとうに」, really. So a wide face is a social network, and a wide heart is a generous person." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a superhero standing on top of a hill. As the music swells, he throws his cape open, and it spreads out wider and wider until it covers an entire valley below, a vast, spacious field shaded by red fabric. The hero shouts, Hero, eee! That's 「ひろい」. Now look at the kanji's shape: the dot and the long sweeping line on the left of the roof radical are like the edge of that huge cape, stretched over everything underneath. Whenever you need the word for wide or spacious, picture that hero and his enormous cape, shouting his own name as it covers the whole countryside. It's silly, which is exactly why it sticks. Just remember it's a memory trick built from the sound, not where the word comes from. A hero with a cape wide enough for a whole valley." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji sits under a roof radical, a big building with a wide roof, and like 「狭い|せまい」 it describes space, so a roomy room is 「広い|ひろい」, not 「大きい|おおきい」.",
      "Learn the verb pair together: 「広げる|ひろげる」, you spread something out, and 「広がる|ひろがる」, something spreads on its own.",
      "「顔が広い|かおがひろい」, a wide face, means well connected, and 「心が広い|こころがひろい」, a wide heart, means generous.",
      "Picture a hero throwing open a cape wide enough to cover a whole valley, shouting \"Hero, eee!\" for 「ひろい」."
    ],
    ja: "このこうえんは[広い|ひろい]です。",
    en: "This park is spacious." },
  "わるい|悪い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "An i-adjective with the native reading 「わるい」, and the opposite of 「いい」, good. Look at the kanji. The bottom is 「心|こころ」, heart. A way to remember it: something sitting on top of your heart and weighing it down, a bad feeling. That's a hook from the shape. You'll see 「悪い|わるい」 in lots of everyday phrases. 「天気が悪い|てんきがわるい」 is bad weather. 「成績が悪い|せいせきがわるい」 is bad grades. And 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」 means to feel sick, literally my feeling is bad, which is a great phrase for this lesson about illness. Here's a cool usage English speakers don't expect. In casual speech, 「悪い|わるい」 or 「悪いね|わるいね」 can mean sorry, my bad, or thanks for the trouble. It's a quick, friendly apology among friends, mostly used by men, and too casual for your teacher. The negative 「悪くない|わるくない」, not bad, works much like in English. Conjugation is the regular i-adjective pattern: 「悪かった|わるかった」, it was bad. Note that unlike 「いい」, which becomes 「よかった」, 「悪い|わるい」 has no irregular forms. To recap: a weight on the heart, the opposite of good. Bad." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「あく」, this kanji appears in some strong words. 「悪魔|あくま」 is a devil or demon. 「悪口|わるぐち」, badmouthing, uses the native reading, but 「悪人|あくにん」, a villain or bad person, uses 「あく」. The word you'll hear most, though, is 「最悪|さいあく」, the worst. It's used constantly in casual speech: you miss your train, it starts raining, you've lost your umbrella, and you sigh, 「最悪|さいあく」, this is the worst. Its opposite is 「最高|さいこう」, the best. The top part of the kanji, sitting over 「心|こころ」, is a shape that dictionaries mostly describe as giving the sound. As a radical, 「心|こころ」 at the bottom marks words about feelings, like 「思う|おもう」, to think, and 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry. So a feeling-word kanji with two voices: 「わる」 in everyday adjectives, and 「あく」 in villains, devils, and the worst day ever." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Several set phrases built on 「悪い|わるい」 are very handy. 「具合が悪い|ぐあいがわるい」 means to feel unwell, literally my condition is bad. It's a gentle way to say you're sick, perfect for calling in to work. 「気持ちが悪い|きもちがわるい」 means to feel nauseous, and also gross or creepy. Young people shorten it to 「キモい」. Careful: 「頭が悪い|あたまがわるい」, a bad head, means stupid, and it's rude about someone else. To admit fault, say 「私が悪かったです|わたしがわるかったです」, it was my fault, literally I was the bad one. If something isn't anyone's fault, 「誰も悪くない|だれもわるくない」, nobody's to blame. And 「悪口|わるぐち」 is badmouthing, saying mean things behind someone's back: 「悪口を言う|わるぐちをいう」. Notice that most of these use 「が」 to mark what is bad. So 「悪い|わるい」 can describe your health, your stomach, your head, or whose fault it was." },
      { title: "Near-synonyms", text: "English uses bad in many places where Japanese uses a different word, so let's sort it out. If you are bad at something, like singing or soccer, don't use 「悪い|わるい」. Use 「下手|へた」: 「サッカーが下手です|サッカーがへたです」, I'm bad at soccer. If you want to say something is terrible or awful, or someone was cruel, 「ひどい」 is often a better fit: 「ひどい天気|ひどいてんき」, awful weather, or 「ひどい」 alone, how mean! If food has gone bad, Japanese says it has spoiled, using the verb 「腐る|くさる」. And if a test score is bad, you can say 「成績が悪い|せいせきがわるい」, which is correct, but for a skill, it's 「下手|へた」. So 「悪い|わるい」 is mostly for the quality of conditions, behavior, and luck, like 「運が悪い|うんがわるい」, unlucky. Bad at a skill, use 「下手|へた」. Shockingly bad, use 「ひどい」. Generally bad, 「悪い|わるい」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji puts something on top of 「心|こころ」, heart, a weight on the heart, and among friends 「悪いね|わるいね」 can even mean sorry or my bad.",
      "With the reading 「あく」 it appears in strong words like 「悪魔|あくま」, a devil, and the everyday sigh 「最悪|さいあく」, this is the worst.",
      "Set phrases use 「が」 to mark what's bad: 「具合が悪い|ぐあいがわるい」, feeling unwell, and 「私が悪かったです|わたしがわるかったです」, it was my fault.",
      "For being bad at a skill use 「下手|へた」, for something awful use 「ひどい」, and keep 「悪い|わるい」 for conditions, behavior, and luck."
    ],
    ja: "{天気|てんき}が[悪い|わるい]です。",
    en: "The weather is bad." },
  "いたい|痛い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "An i-adjective with the native reading 「いたい」, for something that hurts. Look at the kanji. The outer frame, leaning like a bed on its side with dots on the left, is the sickness radical. You know it from 「病気|びょうき」, illness, and 「病院|びょういん」, hospital. So this kanji lives in the family of sickness and pain words. The key grammar pattern is body part plus 「が」 plus 「痛い|いたい」. 「頭が痛い|あたまがいたい」 is a headache, my head hurts. 「おなかが痛い|おなかがいたい」 is a stomachache. 「歯が痛い|はがいたい」 is a toothache. 「のどが痛い|のどがいたい」 is a sore throat. A common mistake for English speakers is trying to say I have a pain. Japanese just says the body part hurts. In real life, when you stub your toe or bump your head, people shout it as a reflex, often chopped short to 「いたっ」, ouch. There's also a modern slang use: calling someone or something 「いたい」 means cringe, painfully embarrassing to watch, and it's often written in katakana for that meaning. To recap: the sickness radical, and body part plus 「が痛い|がいたい」. It hurts." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Inside the sickness frame, the remaining piece of this kanji mostly gives the sound for the Chinese-origin reading, 「つう」. That reading shows up in some words you'll hear at the doctor. 「頭痛|ずつう」 is a headache, and notice that 「頭|あたま」 is read 「ず」 here, an unusual reading. 「腹痛|ふくつう」 is a stomachache, used on medicine boxes. 「腰痛|ようつう」 is lower back pain. These sound more medical than saying something hurts. There's also a noun with the native reading, 「痛み|いたみ」, pain, and the verb 「痛む|いたむ」, to ache, as in an old injury that aches when it rains. Medicine for pain is 「痛み止め|いたみどめ」, literally a pain stopper. So on a pharmacy shelf, look for 「つう」 compounds on the boxes, and in conversation use the adjective. Everyday speech says the body part hurts, and the medicine box uses 「つう」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you see a Japanese doctor, you'll probably be asked what kind of pain you have, and Japanese answers with sound words, repeated pairs that imitate the feeling. 「ずきずき」 is a throbbing pain, like a toothache or a pounding wound. 「がんがん」 is a pounding, banging headache, the kind after a late night. 「ちくちく」 is a prickling pain, like a needle or a scratchy sweater. 「ひりひり」 is stinging, like a sunburn or a spicy burn on your tongue. And 「しくしく」 is a dull, nagging ache, often used for the stomach. You can put them right before 「痛い|いたい」: 「頭ががんがん痛い|あたまががんがんいたい」, my head is pounding. The doctor might ask, 「どんな痛みですか|どんないたみですか」, what kind of pain is it? Using one of these words gives a clear picture. So learn a few of these sound words, and your doctor will know exactly how it hurts." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "When a small child falls and scrapes a knee in Japan, a parent will rub the spot and say a little chant: 「痛いの痛いの飛んでいけ|いたいのいたいのとんでいけ」, pain, pain, fly away! At the last word they make a tossing gesture, as if throwing the pain far into the sky. It's the Japanese version of kissing it better, and almost everyone grew up with it. There's also a useful grammar pattern from your lessons that fits here. When you see someone else get hurt, add 「そう」 to the stem: 「痛そう|いたそう」, that looks painful. People say it reflexively when watching sports injuries or seeing a big bruise. And for a sharp ouch, the clipped 「痛っ|いたっ」 is the instant reaction. So pain has its own little rituals in Japanese: a reflexive yelp, a sympathetic looks-painful, and a chant to send it flying away." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji sits in the sickness radical, and the key pattern is body part plus 「が痛い|がいたい」, as in 「頭が痛い|あたまがいたい」, my head hurts.",
      "The reading 「つう」 gives medical-sounding words on medicine boxes, like 「頭痛|ずつう」 and 「腹痛|ふくつう」, while everyday speech just says the body part hurts.",
      "Describe the pain with sound words before 「痛い|いたい」, like 「ずきずき」 for throbbing or 「がんがん」 for a pounding headache.",
      "Parents chant 「痛いの痛いの飛んでいけ|いたいのいたいのとんでいけ」 to send a child's pain flying away, and 「痛そう|いたそう」 means that looks painful."
    ],
    ja: "{頭|あたま}が[痛い|いたい]です。",
    en: "I have a headache." },
  "あまい|甘い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "An i-adjective with the native reading 「あまい」. Look at the kanji: it's traditionally explained as a mouth with something held inside it, a line in the middle of an open shape, like a tasty thing you keep in your mouth to enjoy. Its core meaning is sweet, the taste of sugar, like 「お菓子|おかし」, snacks and sweets, and 「ケーキ」. 「甘い物|あまいもの」 is sweet stuff, desserts. You know 「物|もの」, thing, from this lesson. Someone with a sweet tooth is called 「甘党|あまとう」, a member of the sweet party. Then there's the meaning that surprises English speakers. 「甘い|あまい」 can also mean too lenient, too soft on someone. A parent who spoils their kids is 「子供に甘い|こどもにあまい」, sweet toward the children. And if your plan is naive or your guess is too optimistic, someone might say it's 「甘い|あまい」, you're being too easy on yourself. There's a related verb 「甘える|あまえる」, to act like a spoiled child and depend on someone's kindness, a very Japanese idea about relationships. It can also describe a sweet voice or a sweet smell. To recap: something tasty held in the mouth. Sweet, and sometimes too soft." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This adjective belongs to a small family of taste words that are great to learn as a set. 「甘い|あまい」 is sweet. 「辛い|からい」 is spicy hot. 「苦い|にがい」 is bitter, like strong coffee or medicine. 「すっぱい」 is sour, like lemons or pickled plums. And 「しょっぱい」 is salty. Two compounds with 「甘|あま」 are worth knowing. 「甘口|あまくち」, a sweet mouth, means mild or sweet-flavored. At a Japanese curry restaurant, you'll often choose between 「甘口|あまくち」, mild, 「中辛|ちゅうから」, medium, and 「辛口|からくち」, hot. The same words describe sake and wine: 「辛口|からくち」 means dry. Then there's 「甘酒|あまざけ」, a sweet, thick drink made from rice, traditionally served at New Year and at shrines. So know your five tastes, and remember 「甘口|あまくち」 when ordering curry. Sweet, spicy, bitter, sour, and salty." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Here's something that surprises many visitors. When Japanese people praise a dessert, one of the highest compliments is 「甘すぎない|あますぎない」, not too sweet. Japanese cakes and pastries are often lighter and less sugary than American ones, and people really appreciate that balance. Traditional Japanese sweets, called 「和菓子|わがし」, are often made from sweet bean paste, rice flour, and seasonal flavors, and they're served with bitter green tea to balance the sweetness. So if you bring a box of cookies to a Japanese friend and they say 「甘すぎなくて、おいしい|あますぎなくて、おいしい」, they're giving you real praise. On the other side, someone who loves sweets is a 「甘党|あまとう」, and someone who prefers drinking to dessert is a 「辛党|からとう」, a member of the spicy party. So in Japan, sweet is good, but not too sweet is often even better." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond taste and spoiling kids, 「甘い|あまい」 shows up in some sharp everyday phrases. 「考えが甘い|かんがえがあまい」, your thinking is sweet, means you're being naive or too optimistic. A coach might say it to a player who thinks they can win without practicing. 「甘く見る|あまくみる」, to look at something sweetly, means to underestimate it: 「試験を甘く見ないで|しけんをあまくみないで」, don't underestimate the exam. In romance, 「甘い言葉|あまいことば」 are sweet words, sweet talk, sometimes with the hint that they can't be trusted. And there's a famous phrase about life: 「人生は甘くない|じんせいはあまくない」, life isn't sweet, meaning life isn't easy. Notice how the negative and the adverb form 「甘く|あまく」 do a lot of work. If someone says one of these to you, it's a warning to take things seriously. Sweet thinking, sweet looking, sweet words, and a life that isn't sweet." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a mouth holding something tasty, so it means sweet, but it also means too lenient, as with a parent who is 「子供に甘い|こどもにあまい」.",
      "Learn the five tastes together, and remember 「甘口|あまくち」, mild, versus 「辛口|からくち」, hot, when ordering curry.",
      "A top compliment for a dessert is 「甘すぎない|あますぎない」, not too sweet, since Japanese sweets favor balance.",
      "「考えが甘い|かんがえがあまい」 means your thinking is naive, and 「甘く見る|あまくみる」 means to underestimate something."
    ],
    ja: "このケーキは[甘い|あまい]です。",
    en: "This cake is sweet." },
  "おおい|多い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "An i-adjective with the native reading 「おおい」. Look at the kanji. A way to remember it: it looks like two copies of 「夕|ゆう」, evening, stacked on top of each other, as if evenings were piling up into many. That's a hook from the shape, not the kanji's real history. With its Chinese-origin reading 「た」, the same kanji is in 「多分|たぶん」, probably, from this lesson: a large share, so most likely. Here's the big grammar tip, and it trips up almost every English speaker. 「多い|おおい」 is used as a predicate, at the end of a sentence: 「東京は人が多い|とうきょうはひとがおおい」, Tokyo has a lot of people, literally in Tokyo, people are many. But you usually can't put it straight in front of a noun the way you'd say many people in English. Saying 「多い人|おおいひと」 sounds wrong. Instead, use 「たくさんの」, as in 「たくさんの人|たくさんのひと」, many people. The pattern is place or topic plus 「は」, then thing plus 「が多い|がおおい」. You can say 「宿題が多い|しゅくだいがおおい」, there's a lot of homework. Its opposite is 「少ない|すくない」, few. To recap: many, but used at the end, in the pattern something 「が多い|がおおい」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "We know you can't just say 「多い人|おおいひと」 for many people. But there are two ways 「多い|おおい」 does go before a noun, and they're very natural. First, when it's part of a relative clause with its own subject: 「人が多い町|ひとがおおいまち」, a town with many people, or more naturally 「人の多い町|ひとのおおいまち」, where 「が」 changes to 「の」. Likewise 「雨の多い季節|あめのおおいきせつ」, a rainy season, a season with lots of rain. Second, the form 「多くの|おおくの」, many, is used in writing and speeches: 「多くの人が来ました|おおくのひとがきました」, many people came. In casual speech, 「たくさんの」 is still more common. Also handy: 「多すぎる|おおすぎる」, too many, as in 「宿題が多すぎる|しゅくだいがおおすぎる」, way too much homework. And compare with 「より」: 「東京は大阪より人が多い|とうきょうはおおさかよりひとがおおい」. So add a subject, or use 「多くの|おおくの」, and 「多い|おおい」 can sit before a noun." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's get a little more accurate about this kanji's shape. The ancient Chinese dictionary explained it as two evenings stacked up, the idea of nights piling up over time. Many modern scholars instead read the two shapes as pieces of meat heaped on top of each other, perhaps an offering. The honest answer is that the origin is debated. Either way, two of the same thing stacked together is a natural picture for many. The Chinese-origin reading 「た」 is in several useful words. 「多少|たしょう」, many and few, means somewhat or to some extent: 「多少分かります|たしょうわかります」, I understand a bit. 「多数|たすう」 is a majority. And 「多分|たぶん」, probably, you already know. Its opposite kanji, 「少|しょう」, few, sits right next to it in 「多少|たしょう」. So stacked shapes, a debated origin, and the reading 「た」 in words about amounts." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The long vowel is the key to this word. Say it with a held 「お」: 「おお」, then 「い」. Three beats. If you shorten the first vowel, you get 「おい」, which is a rough way of saying hey, the call you'd use to get a friend's attention. A man shouting 「おい」 across the street is not saying many. A few other words are close neighbors too. 「大きい|おおきい」, big, starts with the same long sound, and English speakers sometimes mix the two up, since big and many feel related. 「遠い|とおい」, far, has the same rhythm with a different first sound. And 「多い|おおい」 versus 「青い|あおい」, blue, can blur when said fast. Practice by tapping three even beats for 「おおい」, four for 「おおきい」, and three for 「とおい」. A clear long vowel keeps your many from turning into a shout of hey. Hold the 「お」, and the word is yours." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Use 「多い|おおい」 at the end, as in 「東京は人が多い|とうきょうはひとがおおい」, because 「多い人|おおいひと」 sounds wrong, so say 「たくさんの人|たくさんのひと」 instead.",
      "It can go before a noun inside a clause with its own subject, as in 「人の多い町|ひとのおおいまち」, or in the written form 「多くの|おおくの」.",
      "The origin is debated, two evenings or two pieces of meat stacked up, but the reading 「た」 appears in amount words like 「多少|たしょう」 and 「多分|たぶん」.",
      "Hold the long vowel for three beats, 「おおい」, or it turns into 「おい」, a rough shout of hey."
    ],
    ja: "{東京|とうきょう}は{人|ひと}が[多い|おおい]です。",
    en: "There are many people in Tokyo." },
  "すてき|素敵": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a na-adjective, a warm, slightly elegant compliment. You'll often hear it as 「すてきですね」, how lovely, or 「すてきな服|すてきなふく」, lovely clothes. The kanji are interesting. 「素|す」 means plain or natural, and 「敵|てき」 actually means enemy. Obviously nice has nothing to do with an enemy. These kanji were chosen mainly for their sounds, not their meanings, a practice Japanese uses for some words. So don't try to build the meaning from the pieces. That's also why the word is very often written in kana. A way to remember it: picture someone saying it with stars in their eyes, looking at a beautiful dress or a sweet gift. That's a hook. How does it differ from 「いい」? 「いい」 is plain good. 「すてき」 adds a touch of charm and admiration, like lovely or wonderful. It's great for complimenting someone's clothes, a nice café, a beautiful view, or a person's personality. A common mistake is to use it for something merely functional, like a good price. That's just 「いい」 or 「安い|やすい」. You'll hear it used by everyone, though it has a slightly soft, refined feel. To recap: written with sound-only kanji, used to praise something lovely." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Where does this word come from? Honestly, nobody knows for sure. It appears in the late Edo period, in the early eighteen-hundreds, as slang among city people, and it seems to have meant something like tremendous or extraordinary, sometimes even too much. Over time it softened into lovely and wonderful. One popular theory says it grew out of the beginning of 「すばらしい」, splendid, with an ending added on. Another theory links it to a different word entirely. Dictionaries mention these ideas but don't settle on one, so treat any story you hear as a guess. What's certain is that the kanji came later and were chosen for their sounds, which is why their meanings, plain and enemy, don't fit. That's also why many writers prefer kana for it. So this is a word with a fuzzy past: Edo slang for something striking, now a gentle compliment." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a set of everyday compliments, and choosing the right one makes you sound natural. 「かわいい」 is cute, for babies, pets, small things, and clothes with a playful look. 「きれい」 is beautiful or clean, for faces, flowers, views, and tidy rooms. 「かっこいい」 is cool or good-looking, often for men, cars, and stylish things. 「すてき」 sits a bit apart: it's lovely or charming, with a sense of admiration and good taste. It works for an elegant restaurant, a thoughtful gift, someone's way of living, or a person's warm character: 「すてきな人|すてきなひと」, a wonderful person. A nice set phrase is 「すてきな一日を|すてきないちにちを」, have a lovely day. Grammar note: it's a na-adjective, so it's 「すてきな」 before a noun and 「すてきでした」 for the past. A common slip is treating it like an i-adjective. So cute, beautiful, cool, and then lovely, with a touch of admiration." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji, plain plus enemy, were picked for sound, not meaning, so it's often written in kana, and it adds charm and admiration beyond plain 「いい」.",
      "Its origin is uncertain, but it began as Edo-period city slang for something tremendous before softening into lovely, with the kanji added later.",
      "Among compliments like 「かわいい」, 「きれい」, and 「かっこいい」, 「すてき」 means lovely with good taste, and as a na-adjective it takes 「な」 before nouns."
    ],
    ja: "[素敵|すてき]な{服|ふく}ですね。",
    en: "What nice clothes!" },
  "あるく|歩く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A verb with the native reading 「あるく」. It's a u-verb, so the forms are 「歩きます|あるきます」 and the te-form 「歩いて|あるいて」. You already know 「歩いて|あるいて」 as on foot, as in 「歩いて行く|あるいていく」, to go on foot. Look at the kanji. The top part, 「止|し」, is an old drawing of a foot. The bottom part is also a foot, turned around. So the kanji shows two footprints, one stepping in front of the other: walking. Feet show up in other kanji too, like 「足|あし」, leg or foot, from this same lesson. Its faster sibling verb is 「走る|はしる」, to run. With its Chinese-origin reading 「ほ」 or 「ぽ」, the same kanji is in 「散歩|さんぽ」, a stroll, like walking the dog. Grammar tip: when you walk along a path, use 「を」, not 「に」. So 「道を歩く|みちをあるく」, to walk along the road, or 「公園を歩く|こうえんをあるく」, to walk through the park. The 「を」 marks the space you move through. A cultural note: Japanese cities are very walkable, and it's common to walk ten or fifteen minutes to the station every day. To recap: two footprints, one in front of the other. To walk." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a regular u-verb ending in 「く」, so its te-form is 「歩いて|あるいて」. That's worth noting because 「行く|いく」, which also ends in 「く」, is the famous exception with 「行って|いって」. Don't let 「行く|いく」 make you say the wrong thing here. The short past is 「歩いた|あるいた」, and the negative is 「歩かない|あるかない」. The potential form, can walk, is 「歩ける|あるける」: 「駅まで歩けますか|えきまであるけますか」, can I walk to the station? A doctor might say 「もう歩けますよ|もうあるけますよ」, you can walk now. For doing two things at once, use the stem plus 「ながら」: 「歩きながら話す|あるきながらはなす」, to talk while walking. There's even a modern word built this way, 「歩きスマホ|あるきスマホ」, walking while staring at your smartphone, which posters at stations warn against. So: 「歩いて|あるいて」, not like 「行く|いく」, plus 「歩ける|あるける」 and 「歩きながら|あるきながら」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji, 「ほ」, often changing to 「ぽ」, appears in some words you'll see on the street. 「歩道|ほどう」 is a sidewalk, a walking road. 「横断歩道|おうだんほどう」 is a crosswalk, literally a walking road for crossing sideways. 「一歩|いっぽ」 is one step, as in 「一歩ずつ|いっぽずつ」, one step at a time, a common encouraging phrase. Notice the small 「っ」 and the 「ぽ」 sound there. And 「進歩|しんぽ」 means progress, steps forward. You know 「散歩|さんぽ」, a stroll. In Japanese chess, called shogi, the humble pawn uses this same kanji with a special reading, 「ふ」, because the pawn moves one step at a time. Notice how the meaning of stepping runs through all of them: sidewalks, single steps, progress, strolls. So when you see this kanji, think of steps." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Walking in Japan comes with some unwritten rules. On escalators, people traditionally stand on one side and leave the other side open for those walking up. In Tokyo it's usually stand on the left, and in Osaka, stand on the right, though railway companies now ask everyone to stand still on both sides for safety. Eating while walking used to be considered bad manners, and in some places it still is, so people often eat their snack right next to the shop or vending machine. Yet in busy shopping streets near temples, 「食べ歩き|たべあるき」, eating your way down the street, has become a popular pastime. Some city areas in Tokyo also ban smoking while walking on the street, with signs and fines. And busy crossings, like the famous one in Shibuya, have scramble crosswalks where everyone crosses in every direction at once. So walk with an eye on the local customs." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji shows two footprints, one stepping in front of the other, and the path you walk along takes 「を」, as in 「道を歩く|みちをあるく」.",
      "It's a regular 「く」 verb, so the te-form is 「歩いて|あるいて」, not like the exception 「行って|いって」, and you get 「歩ける|あるける」 and 「歩きながら|あるきながら」.",
      "The reading 「ほ」 or 「ぽ」 means steps, as in 「歩道|ほどう」, a sidewalk, 「一歩|いっぽ」, one step, and 「散歩|さんぽ」, a stroll.",
      "Walking has unwritten rules, like standing on one side of escalators, and eating while walking was once rude, though 「食べ歩き|たべあるき」 is now a popular pastime."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}まで[歩きます|あるきます|あるく]。",
    en: "I walk to the station." },
  "ねつがある|熱がある": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a set phrase built from two parts you know. 「熱|ねつ」 is heat or fever, the kanji with the fire dots at the bottom. 「ある」 is the verb for things existing, there is. So literally, there is heat. That's how Japanese says I have a fever. The pattern is the same one you use for lots of things in this lesson. 「用事がある|ようじがある」, there's an errand, I have something to do. 「興味がある|きょうみがある」, there's interest, I'm interested. 「アレルギーがある」, I have an allergy. Japanese often uses 「ある」 for having things, especially abstract ones. A common mistake for English speakers is trying to translate have directly with the verb for holding. Just remember: there is heat. You'll also hear 「熱が出る|ねつがでる」, a fever comes out, for when a fever starts, and 「熱が下がる|ねつがさがる」, the fever goes down. To ask someone, say 「熱がありますか|ねつがありますか」, do you have a fever. In Japan, a temperature of thirty-seven point five degrees Celsius or higher is often the line for staying home from school or work. To recap: fever plus 「ある」. There is heat, so you have a fever." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "With its native reading, the fever kanji becomes 「熱い|あつい」, hot to the touch, like a hot bath, hot coffee, or a hot stove. Here's where English speakers get tangled. Japanese has three different words pronounced 「あつい」. 「熱い|あつい」 is for things you touch or drink. 「暑い|あつい」 is for hot weather and hot rooms, the heat you feel all around you. And 「厚い|あつい」 means thick, like a thick book. The opposites help keep them apart. The opposite of hot-to-touch is 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch. The opposite of hot weather is 「寒い|さむい」. And the opposite of thick is 「薄い|うすい」, thin. In speech, the pitch differs a little and context does most of the work. In writing, the kanji tell you which one. So a fever, a hot drink, a hot summer, and a thick book." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Taking your temperature is a big part of daily life in Japan. A thermometer is 「体温計|たいおんけい」, and to take your temperature is 「熱を測る|ねつをはかる」. Japanese thermometers usually go under the armpit, not in the mouth. People know their normal temperature, which is 「平熱|へいねつ」, often around thirty-six and a half degrees. A slight fever is 「微熱|びねつ」, and a high fever is 「高い熱|たかいねつ」 or 「高熱|こうねつ」. Here's a typical exchange. A parent asks 「熱、測った|ねつ、はかった」, did you take your temperature? The child answers 「三十八度ある|さんじゅうはちどある」, it's thirty-eight. Notice that you can just use 「ある」 with the number. In summer you'll also hear 「熱中症|ねっちゅうしょう」, heatstroke, a serious danger on hot days, with warnings on the news. So: measure with 「測る|はかる」, report with 「ある」, and know your normal temperature." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The fever kanji, 「熱|ねつ」, also means passion, and it shows up in some positive words. 「熱心|ねっしん」, a hot heart, means enthusiastic or dedicated: 「熱心な学生|ねっしんながくせい」, a hard-working, eager student. Teachers love using it. 「熱中する|ねっちゅうする」 means to be absorbed in something, to be crazy about it, like a kid who's totally into a video game. And 「情熱|じょうねつ」 is passion, the kind in love songs and dramatic speeches. Notice the sound change in 「熱心|ねっしん」 and 「熱中|ねっちゅう」: the 「つ」 shrinks into a small 「っ」 before the next sound. That's the same pattern as in 「切符|きっぷ」. So the same heat that gives you a fever also describes passion and dedication. One kanji, from sickbed to enthusiasm. A fever, a hot heart, and passion." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese says there is heat, 「熱|ねつ」 plus 「ある」, the same pattern as 「用事がある|ようじがある」, rather than translating have directly.",
      "Three words sound like 「あつい」: 「熱い|あつい」 hot to the touch, 「暑い|あつい」 hot weather, and 「厚い|あつい」 thick, each with its own opposite.",
      "Take your temperature with 「熱を測る|ねつをはかる」, report the number with 「ある」, and know your normal temperature, 「平熱|へいねつ」.",
      "The same heat means passion in 「熱心|ねっしん」, enthusiastic, 「熱中する|ねっちゅうする」, to be absorbed, and 「情熱|じょうねつ」."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[熱があります|ねつがあります|ねつがある]。",
    en: "I have a fever today." },
  "のどがかわく|喉が渇く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A phrase with two pieces. 「喉|のど」 is throat, written with the mouth radical on the left. 「渇く|かわく」 is to become dry, especially from thirst. Look at its left side: those three short strokes are the water radical. So it's a water kanji about water running out. Together, my throat becomes dry, which is how Japanese says I'm thirsty. Here's the grammar twist. Because 「渇く|かわく」 describes a change, becoming dry, you usually put it in the past to say you're thirsty right now: 「のどがかわきました」 or casually 「のどがかわいた」. It means my throat has gotten dry, so I'm thirsty now. Saying the present form 「かわきます」 sounds like it will get dry later. English speakers often make that mistake. It's just like 「おなかがすく」, to get hungry, where you say 「おなかがすきました」 to mean I'm hungry. These two phrases are a matched pair. One more thing: there's another kanji for the same verb, 「乾く|かわく」, used for things drying out, like laundry. Don't confuse them in writing; the thirsty one is the water radical version. To recap: the throat dries out, and you say it in the past to mean you're thirsty now." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how this phrase lives in daily conversation. Among friends, the quick version is 「のどかわいた」, dropping the 「が」. If it's really bad, add a sound word: 「のどがからから」, my throat is bone dry, using the dry rattling sound. A host might notice you looking tired and ask 「何か飲みますか|なにかのみますか」, would you like something to drink? And luckily, Japan has vending machines on nearly every corner, so you're never far from a drink. Here's a mini dialogue on a hot day. One friend says 「暑いね。のどがかわいた|あついね。のどがかわいた」, it's hot, I'm thirsty. The other says 「あそこに自動販売機があるよ|あそこにじどうはんばいきがあるよ」, there's a vending machine over there. On a lighter note, 「のど」 can also mean a singing voice. There's a long-running TV singing contest for amateurs whose name means proud of their throat. So 「のど」 is for thirst, for sore throats, and for singing." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. The verb 「かわく」 starts with the same sound as 「川|かわ」, a river. Picture a traveler crossing a desert, tongue hanging out, desperate for water. At last she spots a river ahead. She runs to it, and finds it completely dried up, nothing but cracked mud. The river, 「かわ」, has gone dry, 「かわく」. She clutches her throat and croaks 「のどがかわいた」. Now link the kanji: those three water drops on the left side of the character are the last drops of the river, disappearing. To make it funnier, she then spots a cute puppy and says 「かわいい」, cute, but her voice is so dry it comes out as a croak. Remember, this is a memory trick built on sounds; the river and the verb aren't related. A dried-up river and a thirsty traveler." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「渇く|かわく」 describes becoming dry, so say it in the past, 「のどがかわいた」, to mean you're thirsty now, just like 「おなかがすきました」.",
      "Friends drop the 「が」 for 「のどかわいた」, add 「のどがからから」 for bone dry, and 「のど」 can also mean a singing voice.",
      "Picture a desert traveler finding a 「川|かわ」, a river, dried up, 「かわく」, with the water radical's three drops as its last trickle."
    ],
    ja: "[喉が渇きました|のどがかわきました|のどがかわく]。みずをください。",
    en: "I am thirsty. Water, please." },
  "はらう|払う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A u-verb with the native reading 「はらう」. Look at the kanji. The left side is the hand radical, the same one in tons of action kanji. A way to remember it: picture your hand sweeping money across the counter. That's a hook. In fact the verb's broader meaning is to sweep away or brush off, like brushing dust off your clothes, and you can think of paying as clearing away what you owe. Watch the forms: 「払います|はらいます」, polite, and 「払って|はらって」, te-form. The usual patterns are 「お金を払う|おかねをはらう」, to pay money, with 「を」 for what you pay, and 「カードで払う|カードではらう」, to pay by card, with 「で」 for the means. You'll also hear 「現金で払う|げんきんではらう」, to pay in cash. A cultural note: Japan has long been a cash-heavy country, though cards and phone payments are now common. And there's no tipping. In shops you often put your money on a little tray instead of handing it over directly. A useful related word is 「〜代|〜だい」, the fee, from this lesson: 「電話代を払う|でんわだいをはらう」, to pay the phone bill. To recap: a hand sweeping money over. To pay." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Verbs ending in 「う」 have one quirk you must watch. In the negative, the 「う」 becomes 「わ」, not 「あ」. So it's 「払わない|はらわない」, don't pay, not some form with 「あ」. The same goes for 「買う|かう」, 「買わない|かわない」, and 「会う|あう」, 「会わない|あわない」. The te-form uses a small 「っ」: 「払って|はらって」. The short past is 「払った|はらった」. Now let's use it with patterns you know. To ask someone to pay: 「ここで払ってください|ここではらってください」, please pay here. To say you must pay: 「払わなければいけない|はらわなければいけない」. And with 「てしまう」 for regret: 「高いお金を払ってしまった|たかいおかねをはらってしまった」, I ended up paying a lot. Once you've got the 「わ」 negative down, the rest of this verb is perfectly regular. Remember: 「う」 verbs turn into 「わ」 in the negative." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The noun form 「払い|はらい」, payment, combines into some useful compounds, and it often changes to 「ばらい」. 「支払い|しはらい」 is payment, the formal word you see on bills and websites. 「前払い|まえばらい」 is paying in advance, and 「後払い|あとばらい」 is paying afterward. 「現金払い|げんきんばらい」 is cash payment. At a restaurant, the staff might ask 「お支払いは|おしはらいは」, how will you be paying? Then there's a surprising relative: 「酔っ払い|よっぱらい」, a drunk person, from the verb 「酔っ払う|よっぱらう」, to get drunk. It shares this kanji, though how the drunk meaning developed isn't clear. You may also hear 「払う|はらう」 in its sweeping sense: 「ほこりを払う|ほこりをはらう」, to brush off dust. And 「注意を払う|ちゅういをはらう」 means to pay attention, quite like English. So 「払い|はらい」 for payment, 「ばらい」 in compounds, and one tipsy cousin." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Paying at Japanese restaurants works differently from many Western ones. Usually you don't pay at the table. You take the bill, which is often left on your table in a small holder, up to the register by the door. To ask for the bill, say 「お会計お願いします|おかいけいおねがいします」. Among friends, splitting the bill evenly is called 「割り勘|わりかん」: 「割り勘にしよう|わりかんにしよう」, let's split it. If someone treats you, they 「おごる」: 「今日は私がおごります|きょうはわたしがおごります」, it's on me today. Often the senior person, like a boss or an older student, pays for younger ones, and the younger ones say 「ごちそうさまでした」 to thank them. At many ramen shops you pay first at a ticket machine, then eat. And remember, no tipping, so if you leave money on the table, a server may run after you to return it. So: pay at the register, split with 「割り勘|わりかん」, and thank whoever treats you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The hand radical suggests a hand sweeping money across the counter, with 「を」 for what you pay and 「で」 for how, as in 「カードで払う|カードではらう」.",
      "Verbs ending in 「う」 turn the 「う」 into 「わ」 in the negative, so it's 「払わない|はらわない」, just like 「買わない|かわない」.",
      "In compounds the noun often becomes 「ばらい」, as in 「前払い|まえばらい」, and the formal word is 「支払い|しはらい」, with a tipsy cousin, 「酔っ払い|よっぱらい」.",
      "Pay at the register by the door, split the bill with 「割り勘|わりかん」, thank whoever treats you, and never leave a tip."
    ],
    ja: "カードで[払います|はらいます|はらう]。",
    en: "I will pay by card." },
  "なくす|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A u-verb, usually written in kana. It means to lose something you had, like a wallet, keys, or a ticket. Look at the sound: it starts with 「ない」, not exist. It's related to that idea: you make something not be there anymore. That makes it easy to remember. It's the active, transitive verb, meaning someone loses something, so it takes 「を」: 「さいふをなくす」, to lose your wallet, or 「切符をなくしました|きっぷをなくしました」, I lost my ticket. There's a matching verb 「なくなる」, to disappear or run out, where the thing itself goes missing. For example, 「お金がなくなった|おかねがなくなった」, the money ran out. Here's a big mistake for English speakers. In English, lose also means losing a game. In Japanese, that's a totally different verb, 「負ける|まける」. You'd never use 「なくす」 for losing a match or 「試合|しあい」. Also, for getting lost on the way somewhere, use 「道に迷う|みちにまよう」, which you know. A cultural note: Japan is famous for returning lost items. People hand in lost wallets at the police box, often with all the cash still inside. To recap: make something not be there. To lose an item." },
      { title: "Kanji choices", text: "We usually write this verb in kana, but you'll meet two kanji spellings, and the difference matters. 「無くす|なくす」 uses the kanji for nothing or without, the one in 「無料|むりょう」, free of charge. It's the general lose or get rid of: 「さいふを無くす|さいふをなくす」. The other spelling, 「亡くす|なくす」, uses the kanji for death. It means to lose a person, to have someone die. 「父を亡くしました|ちちをなくしました」 means I lost my father, he passed away. Its partner is 「亡くなる|なくなる」, to pass away, the polite, gentle way to say someone died. Because these sound identical, context does the work in speech. In writing, using the right kanji avoids a very awkward mistake. So: lose your keys with kana or 「無|な」, and for the loss of a loved one, use 「亡|な」. Same sound, very different feeling." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Losing things is a perfect fit for a pattern from your later lessons, 「てしまう」, which adds regret or an accident. 「さいふをなくしてしまいました」 means I went and lost my wallet, oh no. In casual speech it shrinks to 「なくしちゃった」, which you'll hear constantly. To warn someone, combine with 「ないように」: 「なくさないように、気をつけて|なくさないように、きをつけて」, be careful not to lose it. Notice the negative 「なくさない」 with 「さ」, the regular pattern for verbs ending in 「す」. The partner 「なくなる」 shines when something is used up: 「時間がなくなった|じかんがなくなった」, we ran out of time. That's the thing itself vanishing, so 「が」 marks it. So: 「なくしちゃった」 when it's your fault, 「なくなった」 when it just ran out, and 「なくさないように」 as a warning." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "English uses lose very broadly, but Japanese has separate words for different ways things go missing. 「落とす|おとす」 is to drop something, like dropping your phone, or dropping your wallet without noticing. 「忘れる|わすれる」 is to forget, and it's also used for leaving something behind: 「電車にかさを忘れた|でんしゃにかさをわすれた」, I left my umbrella on the train. 「なくす」 is the general word: it's gone, and you don't know where. If you lose something in Japan, go to a police box, a 「交番|こうばん」, or the station's lost and found. You might say 「さいふを落としました|さいふをおとしました」 to the officer. Thanks to honest finders, there's a good chance it comes back. So: dropped, 「落とす|おとす」. Left behind, 「忘れる|わすれる」. Simply gone, 「なくす」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It starts with 「ない」, making something not be there, and it takes 「を」, but losing a game is 「負ける|まける」, never 「なくす」.",
      "Lose objects with kana or 「無くす|なくす」, but 「亡くす|なくす」 with the death kanji means losing a person.",
      "Use 「なくしちゃった」 when it's your fault, 「なくなった」 when something just ran out, and 「なくさないように」 as a warning.",
      "Japanese splits lose up: 「落とす|おとす」 for dropping, 「忘れる|わすれる」 for leaving behind, and 「なくす」 when it's simply gone."
    ],
    ja: "さいふを[なくしました|なくしました|なくす]。",
    en: "I lost my wallet." },
  "きょうみがある|興味がある": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces. 「興味|きょうみ」 is interest, and 「ある」 is there is. So literally, there is interest. Let's break 「興味|きょうみ」 apart. 「興|きょう」 means to rise up, to get excited or stirred up. 「味|み」 means taste, the same kanji as 「味|あじ」, flavor, and the one in 「意味|いみ」, meaning, from this lesson. So interest is a taste for something rising up in you. That's a nice way to picture it. The grammar pattern is very important: the thing you're interested in takes 「に」. So 「日本に興味がある|にほんにきょうみがある」, I'm interested in Japan, or 「政治に興味があります|せいじにきょうみがあります」, I'm interested in politics. A common mistake for English speakers is using 「が」 or 「を」 for the topic of interest. It's always 「に」. To say not interested, use 「興味がない|きょうみがない」. It's in the same family of 「ある」 phrases as 「熱がある|ねつがある」, have a fever, and 「用事がある|ようじがある」, have something to do. It's a slightly more serious, thoughtful phrase than 「好き|すき」, to like. You might be interested in history without loving it. To recap: a taste rising up, and you put the topic before 「に」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「興|きょう」, is a dense, busy-looking character. It's traditionally explained as several hands lifting something up together, which fits its meaning of rising up or getting going. Don't worry about writing it yet; just learn to recognize its shape. It has a second Chinese-origin reading, 「こう」, which appears in 「興奮|こうふん」, excitement, being worked up: 「興奮して眠れない|こうふんしてねむれない」, I'm too excited to sleep. You'll also see it in words about business booming or a region recovering, which shows the rising-up idea. So in 「興味|きょうみ」 it's 「きょう」, in excitement it's 「こう」. Same kanji, two readings, both about something rising inside you or around you. Picture many hands lifting a taste up toward you, and you have interest. A rising feeling, lifted by many hands." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some ways people use this phrase beyond the basics. To ask someone about their interests, say 「何に興味がありますか|なににきょうみがありますか」, what are you interested in? It comes up in self-introductions and job interviews. To say you've become interested, use 「興味を持つ|きょうみをもつ」, to take an interest: 「日本の歴史に興味を持ちました|にほんのれきしにきょうみをもちました」, I became interested in Japanese history. Notice the topic still takes 「に」. If something is fascinating, call it 「興味深い|きょうみぶかい」, deeply interesting, a slightly formal word. And here's a key difference from 「趣味|しゅみ」, a hobby. A hobby is something you actually do regularly, like cooking or tennis. An interest can be something you just want to know more about. So you might be interested in politics without it being your hobby. Ask with 「何に|なにに」, start one with 「持つ|もつ」, and keep hobbies separate." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「興|きょう」, rising up, plus 「味|み」, taste, is a taste rising in you, and the topic always takes 「に」, as in 「日本に興味がある|にほんにきょうみがある」.",
      "「興|きょう」 is many hands lifting something up, and its other reading 「こう」 appears in 「興奮|こうふん」, excitement.",
      "Ask 「何に興味がありますか|なににきょうみがありますか」, start one with 「興味を持つ|きょうみをもつ」, and remember an interest isn't necessarily a 「趣味|しゅみ」, a hobby."
    ],
    ja: "{政治|せいじ}に[興味があります|きょうみがあります|きょうみがある]。",
    en: "I am interested in politics." },
  "せきがでる|せきが出る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A set phrase with two parts. 「せき」 is a cough, the native word usually written in kana. 「出る|でる」 is to come out, the same verb you know from 「出かける|でかける」, to go out, and its partner 「出す|だす」, to take something out or hand it in. So literally, a cough comes out. That's the natural Japanese way to say I'm coughing. Notice the cough is the subject, marked with 「が」, and it comes out on its own. You're not doing the coughing on purpose, it just happens to you. Japanese often describes symptoms this way. Compare 「熱が出る|ねつがでる」, a fever comes out, meaning a fever starts. Since 「出る|でる」 is a ru-verb, the forms are easy: 「せきが出ます|せきがでます」 and 「せきが出て|せきがでて」. A typical sentence to a doctor would be 「せきが出て、のどが痛いです|せきがでて、のどがいたいです」, I'm coughing and my throat hurts. A common mistake for English speakers is trying to turn cough into a verb with 「する」. You may hear that sometimes, but 「せきが出る|せきがでる」 is much more natural. A quick cultural note: if you cough in public in Japan, it's polite to wear a mask. To recap: the cough comes out on its own." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The sound 「せき」 belongs to several different words, so context is everything. The cough is written with a kanji built from the mouth radical, though people usually just write it in kana. Then there's 「席|せき」, a seat: 「席に座る|せきにすわる」, to sit in your seat, and a reserved seat on a train is 「指定席|していせき」. There's also the 「せき」 at the end of 「成績|せいせき」, grades. And a barrier or checkpoint was once called 「関|せき」, which survives in place names. With the cough, the verb almost always gives it away: a cough comes out, 「出る|でる」. A seat is something you sit in or leave, as in 「席を立つ|せきをたつ」, to get up from your seat. So if you hear 「せきが」 followed by 「出る|でる」, it's a cough. If it's followed by sitting or standing, it's a seat. Coughs come out; seats are sat in." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The pattern of a symptom plus 「出る|でる」 is a whole family of useful phrases for the doctor's office. 「熱が出る|ねつがでる」 is when a fever starts. 「鼻水が出る|はなみずがでる」, nose water comes out, means a runny nose. 「くしゃみが出る|くしゃみがでる」 is sneezing. 「涙が出る|なみだがでる」 is tears coming out, from allergies or from emotion. And 「血が出る|ちがでる」 is bleeding. In each case, the symptom is the subject with 「が」, and it comes out on its own. You can chain them with the te-form to describe a cold: 「せきが出て、鼻水も出ます|せきがでて、はなみずもでます」, I'm coughing, and my nose is running too. Notice 「も」, also, replacing 「が」 in the second part. For stopping symptoms, medicine for coughs is 「せき止め|せきどめ」, a cough stopper. So: cough, fever, runny nose, sneezing, tears, and blood all come out with 「出る|でる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a strong sense of manners around coughing. If you have a cough, it's expected that you'll wear a mask on the train, at work, and in shops, to protect others. This was common long before the pandemic, especially during cold and flu season and spring allergy season. There's even a term, cough etiquette, promoted on posters: cover your mouth with a tissue or your sleeve, not your bare hand. Another related word is 「せきばらい」, clearing your throat with a little cough. People sometimes do it on purpose, to get attention politely, or to show mild disapproval, like when someone is talking loudly on the train. Pharmacies sell many kinds of cough drops and syrups, and convenience stores carry throat lozenges. So if you start coughing in Japan, put on a mask, cover your mouth, and you'll fit right in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The cough is the subject and simply comes out on its own, so 「せきが出る|せきがでる」 is much more natural than turning cough into a 「する」 verb.",
      "Many words sound like 「せき」, such as 「席|せき」, a seat, but if 「出る|でる」 follows, it's a cough, since coughs come out and seats are sat in.",
      "Symptoms come out with 「出る|でる」, like 「熱が出る|ねつがでる」, 「鼻水が出る|はなみずがでる」, and 「くしゃみが出る|くしゃみがでる」.",
      "If you have a cough in Japan, wear a mask and cover your mouth with a tissue or sleeve, not your bare hand."
    ],
    ja: "かぜで[せきが出ます|せきがでます|せきがでる]。",
    en: "I am coughing because of a cold." },
  "わかれる|別れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A ru-verb with the native reading 「わかれる」. The kanji 「別|べつ」 means separate or different. With its Chinese-origin reading 「べつ」, you know it from 「別に|べつに」, not particularly, nothing special, which is a separate matter. You may also know 「別々|べつべつ」, separately, like when you split the bill. So 「別れる|わかれる」 is to become separate. It has two main uses. Two people parting ways after meeting, like saying goodbye at a station. And couples breaking up. The partner is marked with 「と」: 「彼氏と別れた|かれしとわかれた」, I broke up with my boyfriend. A common mistake: English says broke up with, but Japanese uses 「と」, together with, not 「を」. Both people separate from each other. There's a sound-alike to watch out for. 「分かれる|わかれる」, written with the dividing kanji, means a road or a group splits into parts. Same sound, different kanji. A way to remember it: 「分かる|わかる」, to understand, sounds the same at the start. Picture understanding as splitting a problem apart into clear pieces. That's a hook. And the polite goodbye 「さようなら」 is often said at a parting. To recap: the separate kanji, two people becoming separate." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, spring is known as the season of meetings and partings, 「出会いと別れの季節|であいとわかれのきせつ」. The school and business year starts in April, and graduation is in March. So March is full of goodbyes: students leave their classmates, coworkers transfer to new branches, and farewell parties called 「送別会|そうべつかい」 fill the calendar. Notice 「別|べつ」 inside that word too. It all happens while the cherry blossoms bloom and fall, which makes partings feel especially bittersweet, and many famous graduation songs are about it. Here's another surprise: the farewell 「さようなら」 is used less than textbooks suggest. It can sound final, like goodbye for a long time. Friends say 「じゃあね」 or 「またね」, see you. Coworkers say 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」, excuse me for leaving first. So partings are seasonal, ceremonial, and full of different goodbyes." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「別|べつ」, separate or different, appears in several words you know or will meet soon. 「特別|とくべつ」 means special, literally particularly separate from the rest: 「特別な日|とくべつなひ」, a special day. 「別の|べつの」 means another or a different one: 「別の色はありますか|べつのいろはありますか」, do you have it in a different color? That's a very useful shopping phrase. 「区別|くべつ」 means telling things apart: 「わかれる」 and 「わかる」 can be hard to 「区別する|くべつする」. And the noun 「別れ|わかれ」 means a parting or farewell, with the native reading. The common thread is something set apart from the rest. A special day is separate from ordinary days, another color is separate from this one, and a farewell sets two people apart. So when you see this kanji, think set apart." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's look at how this ru-verb works with grammar from your lessons. To say you parted at a place, use 「で」: 「駅で別れました|えきでわかれました」, we said goodbye at the station. To mention a time, 「九時に別れた|くじにわかれた」, we split up at nine. For breaking up, it's often used with 「ことにする」, to decide to: 「彼と別れることにしました|かれとわかれることにしました」, I decided to break up with him. And with 「たい」: 「別れたい|わかれたい」, I want to break up, which is a heavy sentence to hear. There's also a noun, 「別れ話|わかればなし」, the breakup talk, that difficult conversation. Notice that 「別れる|わかれる」 is intransitive, meaning it's about people separating. The transitive verb with a related meaning is 「分ける|わける」, to divide something, like splitting snacks. So: 「で」 for where you parted, 「と」 for who with, and 「ことにする」 when it's a decision." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「別|べつ」 means separate, so it's two people becoming separate, and the partner takes 「と」, as in 「彼氏と別れた|かれしとわかれた」.",
      "Spring is the season of meetings and partings, with March graduations and 「送別会|そうべつかい」 farewell parties under the cherry blossoms.",
      "「別|べつ」 means set apart, as in 「特別|とくべつ」, special, 「別の|べつの」, another one, and 「区別|くべつ」, telling things apart.",
      "Use 「で」 for where you parted, 「と」 for who with, and 「ことにする」 when breaking up is a decision."
    ],
    ja: "{彼氏|かれし}と[別れました|わかれました|わかれる]。",
    en: "I broke up with my boyfriend." },
  "きんちょうする|緊張する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. 「緊|きん」 means tight or tense. Look at the bottom of it: that's the thread radical, like a string pulled tight. 「張|ちょう」 means to stretch, to pull out. Its left side is the bow radical, as in a bow and arrow. Picture drawing back a bowstring until it's stretched taut. So 「緊張|きんちょう」 is pulled tight and stretched: tension. When you're nervous, your body and mind feel exactly like that string. A great sibling word is 「頑張る|がんばる」, to do your best, which uses the same stretching kanji with a native reading. When you 「頑張る|がんばる」, you stretch yourself. The verb is 「緊張する|きんちょうする」, and it's often used in the past for how you felt: 「緊張しました|きんちょうしました」, I was nervous. It fits right before a 「試験|しけん」, an exam, a 「試合|しあい」, a match, a speech, or a first date. A common mistake: Japanese doesn't use the word for worried here; 「心配する|しんぱいする」 is about worrying over something, while 「緊張する|きんちょうする」 is the jittery, tense feeling in the moment. A cultural note: people often encourage each other with 「頑張って|がんばって」 right before something nerve-wracking. To recap: tight plus stretched. Tense, like a bowstring." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people actually talk about nerves. Right before a big moment, someone might say 「緊張してきた|きんちょうしてきた」, I'm starting to get nervous. That 「てくる」 shows a feeling building up. To calm a friend, say 「緊張しないで|きんちょうしないで」, don't be nervous, or 「リラックスして」, relax. A close cousin is 「あがる」, which means to get stage fright, to freeze up in front of people: 「人前であがってしまう|ひとまえであがってしまう」, I freeze up in front of people. Japanese also uses a sound word for a pounding heart, 「ドキドキ」: 「ドキドキする」 can be nervousness or a crush. Here's a mini dialogue. Before a speech, one student says 「すごく緊張してる|すごくきんちょうしてる」, I'm super nervous. Her friend says 「大丈夫。頑張って|だいじょうぶ。がんばって」, you'll be fine, good luck. So: building nerves with 「てくる」, stage fright with 「あがる」, and a pounding heart with 「ドキドキ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「張|ちょう」, stretch, shows up in a word every office worker knows: 「出張|しゅっちょう」, a business trip. Literally, it's stretching out to go somewhere. 「来週、大阪に出張します|らいしゅう、おおさかにしゅっちょうします」, I'm going on a business trip to Osaka next week. With its native reading, it's the verb 「張る|はる」, to stretch or spread, as in pitching a tent, or ice forming over a pond. And of course 「頑張る|がんばる」, to hang in there. The first kanji, 「緊|きん」, is rarer, but you'll see it in 「緊急|きんきゅう」, emergency, on signs for emergency exits and emergency phones. Tight plus hurry: a situation that's pulled taut and urgent. So the tension in 「緊張|きんちょう」 connects to the urgency of an emergency, and the stretch connects to business trips and hard work. Tight things and stretched things." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「する」 verb, so it conjugates just like 「勉強する|べんきょうする」. The interesting part is choosing the right tense. 「緊張しています|きんちょうしています」 describes your state right now: I'm nervous at this moment. 「緊張しました|きんちょうしました」 is how you felt during something that's over: I was nervous during the interview. And 「緊張します|きんちょうします」 is a general habit: I always get nervous before tests. The noun 「緊張|きんちょう」 also works alone. 「緊張がとれる|きんちょうがとれる」 means the tension melts away, when you finally relax. With 「すぎる」 you get 「緊張しすぎて、何も言えなかった|きんちょうしすぎて、なにもいえなかった」, I was so nervous I couldn't say anything. That's a perfect sentence for a bad first date or an oral exam. So match the tense to the moment: right now, back then, or always." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「緊|きん」, tight with the thread radical, plus 「張|ちょう」, stretch with the bow radical, is a bowstring pulled taut, the jittery feeling in the moment.",
      "Nerves build with 「緊張してきた|きんちょうしてきた」, stage fright is 「あがる」, and a pounding heart is 「ドキドキ」.",
      "「張|ちょう」 appears in 「出張|しゅっちょう」, a business trip, and 「頑張る|がんばる」, while 「緊|きん」 appears in 「緊急|きんきゅう」, emergency.",
      "Match the tense to the moment: 「緊張しています|きんちょうしています」 right now, 「緊張しました|きんちょうしました」 back then, and 「緊張します|きんちょうします」 always."
    ],
    ja: "{試験|しけん}のまえに[緊張しました|きんちょうしました|きんちょうする]。",
    en: "I got nervous before the exam." },
  "しんぱいする|心配する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. 「心|しん」 is heart or mind. With its native reading it's 「心|こころ」, which you'll hear in songs and stories. 「配|ぱい」 means to distribute, to hand out, to pay attention to. On its own its reading is 「はい」, but after the 「ん」 it hardens to 「ぱい」. So 「心配|しんぱい」 is spreading your heart out over something, giving your attention and feelings to it, which is what worrying is. The person or thing you worry about is marked with 「を」: 「母を心配する|ははをしんぱいする」, to worry about my mother. Or use 「が心配です|がしんぱいです」 to say something is a worry: 「試験が心配です|しけんがしんぱいです」, I'm worried about the exam. Two phrases you'll use constantly are 「心配しないで|しんぱいしないで」, don't worry, and the more formal 「ご心配なく|ごしんぱいなく」, please don't worry. A common mix-up is with 「緊張する|きんちょうする」, to get nervous. That's the tense feeling in the moment. 「心配する|しんぱいする」 is worrying about something that might go wrong. And remember 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, it's okay, which is the natural answer when someone worries about you. To recap: heart, 「心|しん」, spread out over something. To worry." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The opposite of 「心配|しんぱい」 is a great word to learn alongside it: 「安心|あんしん」, relief or peace of mind, literally a calm heart. 「安心しました|あんしんしました」 means I'm relieved, what a relief. You'll also see 「安心|あんしん」 in advertising, promising safe, worry-free products. Notice that both words share 「心|しん」, heart, but in 「心配|しんぱい」 it comes first and in 「安心|あんしん」 it comes second. Another sibling is 「熱心|ねっしん」, enthusiastic, a hot heart. The second kanji, 「配|はい」, distribute, has its own family. The verb 「配る|くばる」 means to hand out, like passing out flyers or tests. And 「配達|はいたつ」 is delivery, as in newspaper or pizza delivery. So worry is a heart handed out over things, and relief is a heart at peace. A spread-out heart and a calm one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There's a very common polite phrase built on this word: 「ご心配をおかけしました|ごしんぱいをおかけしました」, I'm sorry to have caused you worry. People say it when they return to work after being sick, or after any trouble that made others concerned. It's a key piece of Japanese politeness. Its gentle opposite, when you're reassuring someone, is 「心配いりません|しんぱいいりません」, no need to worry. Someone who worries about everything is called 「心配性|しんぱいしょう」, a worrier by nature: 「母は心配性です|はははしんぱいしょうです」, my mom is a worrywart. A quick exchange: your coworker says 「もう大丈夫ですか|もうだいじょうぶですか」, are you all right now? You answer 「はい、ご心配をおかけしました|はい、ごしんぱいをおかけしました」. That combination shows you understand the social side of worry. So apologize for causing worry, reassure with 「いりません」, and tease the family worrier." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a nervous baker named Shin. He's made a giant pie for an important customer, and he's so worried about it that he can't stop pacing. He paces so much he trips and the pie flies through the air and lands right on his shin. Shin, pie. 「しんぱい」. Now he's even more worried: about the customer, about the pie, and about his bruised shin. The worry spreads out over everything, just like the meaning of the kanji. Whenever you need the word for worry, picture poor Shin with pie on his shin, fretting about everything at once. And remember the answer you'd give him: 「心配しないで|しんぱいしないで」, don't worry. This is just a memory trick from the sounds, not the real origin. A worried baker, a flying pie, and a sore shin." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「心|しん」, heart, plus 「配|ぱい」, to distribute, is your heart spread out over something, and the classic reassurance is 「心配しないで|しんぱいしないで」.",
      "Its opposite is 「安心|あんしん」, a calm heart, and 「配|はい」 also gives 「配る|くばる」, to hand out.",
      "Returning after illness, say 「ご心配をおかけしました|ごしんぱいをおかけしました」, sorry to have caused you worry.",
      "Picture a worried baker named Shin whose pie lands on his shin: shin, pie, 「しんぱい」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}はいつも[心配します|しんぱいします|しんぱいする]。",
    en: "My mother always worries." },
  "おだいじに|お大事に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A set phrase. The core is 「大事|だいじ」, important or precious. Its pieces are 「大|だい」, big, like in 「大学|だいがく」 and 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, and 「事|じ」, matter or thing, like in 「用事|ようじ」 and 「返事|へんじ」. So 「大事|だいじ」 is a big matter, something important. Add polite 「お」 in front and the particle 「に」 at the end, which turns it into how to treat something. The full idea is please treat yourself as precious, please take good care. It's what you say to someone who is sick or injured. In English we'd say get well soon or take care. You'll hear it everywhere around illness. Doctors and pharmacists in Japan say it at the end of every visit, as you leave with your medicine. Friends say it when you mention a cold. It's polite enough for anyone, and you can add 「どうぞ」 in front for extra warmth. A common mistake for English speakers is to use it as a general goodbye, like take care on any normal day. It's only for when someone is unwell. Also, you don't say it about yourself. Typical exchange: 「風邪をひきました|かぜをひきました」, I caught a cold, and the reply is 「お大事に|おだいじに」. To recap: treat yourself as precious. Get well soon." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The core word 「大事|だいじ」 is useful on its own. As a na-adjective, it means important or precious: 「大事な物|だいじなもの」, an important thing, or 「大事な話|だいじなはなし」, an important talk. Its near-synonym is 「大切|たいせつ」, also important or precious. They overlap a lot, but 「大切|たいせつ」 often feels a little warmer and more emotional, as in 「大切な人|たいせつなひと」, someone dear to you, while 「大事|だいじ」 can lean toward something that matters practically. Add 「に」 and 「する」, and you get 「大事にする|だいじにする」, to take good care of something, to cherish it: 「物を大事にする|ものをだいじにする」, to look after your things. That's exactly where 「お大事に|おだいじに」 comes from: it's a shortened request to take good care of yourself. So the phrase is really an important thing, treated carefully. Important, dear, and cared for." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Visiting someone who is sick or in the hospital is called 「お見舞い|おみまい」, and it comes with customs. Visitors bring gifts like fruit, flowers, or something to read. But there are things to avoid. Potted plants are traditionally a bad choice, because a plant with roots suggests the illness will take root and the patient will stay in bed. Flowers like chrysanthemums are linked with funerals, and white flowers can feel too somber. Red flowers can suggest blood. When you leave, you say 「お大事に|おだいじに」. For extra politeness, the full form is 「お大事になさってください|おだいじになさってください」, please take good care of yourself, using a respectful verb. Among friends, 「お大事にね|おだいじにね」 sounds warm and casual. So when someone is sick, bring a cut-flower bouquet or fruit, skip the potted plant, and leave with 「お大事に|おだいじに」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The 「に」 at the end of this phrase is the same 「に」 that turns na-adjectives into adverbs. 「静か|しずか」 becomes 「静かに|しずかに」, quietly. 「きれい」 becomes 「きれいに」, neatly. And 「大事|だいじ」 becomes 「大事に|だいじに」, carefully, preciously. The full idea is 「お大事にしてください|おだいじにしてください」, please do it carefully, meaning please take care of yourself, and everyday speech simply chops off the end. Japanese often drops the final verb in set phrases like this, the same way 「どうぞよろしく」 is a shortened version of a longer request. You can also use the adverb with other verbs: 「大事に使ってください|だいじにつかってください」, please use it carefully, when lending someone something precious. So 「お大事に|おだいじに」 is really a sentence with its verb tucked away. Na-adjective, plus 「に」, plus an unspoken please do." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「大事|だいじ」, a big matter, plus polite 「お」 and 「に」 means please treat yourself as precious, said only to someone who is sick, never as an everyday goodbye.",
      "「大事|だいじ」 means important, slightly more practical than the warmer 「大切|たいせつ」, and 「大事にする|だいじにする」 means to take good care of something.",
      "On a hospital visit, 「お見舞い|おみまい」, bring cut flowers or fruit, skip potted plants that suggest the illness will take root, and leave with 「お大事に|おだいじに」.",
      "The 「に」 turns the na-adjective into an adverb, and the full idea is 「お大事にしてください|おだいじにしてください」 with the verb dropped."
    ],
    ja: "かぜですか。[お大事に|おだいじに]。",
    en: "You have a cold? Get well soon." },
  "げんきがない|元気がない": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A phrase made of two pieces. 「元気|げんき」 is health and energy, the word you know from 「お元気ですか|おげんきですか」, how are you. Let's break it apart. 「元|げん」 means origin or source. 「気|き」 is spirit or energy, the same kanji as in 「天気|てんき」, weather, and 「病気|びょうき」, sickness. So 「元気|げんき」 is the source of your energy, your vitality. Then 「ない」 is there isn't. So 「元気がない|げんきがない」 is there's no energy. It describes someone who seems low, down, or tired, not their usual self. It's often about mood as much as health. You'd say it when a friend is quiet and gloomy: 「元気がないですね|げんきがないですね」, you don't look so good, or the casual 「どうしたの、元気ないね|どうしたの、げんきないね」, what's up, you seem down. A common mistake: 「元気がない|げんきがない」 isn't a diagnosis. For actual illness use 「病気|びょうき」, sick. And note the sentence pattern, 「元気|げんき」 plus 「が」 plus 「ない」, just like 「熱がある|ねつがある」 uses 「が」 plus 「ある」. It's a gentle, caring thing to notice and say. To recap: the source of energy, and there isn't any. You seem down." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The last piece of this phrase, 「ない」, bends exactly like an adjective ending in 「い」, and that opens up a lot of useful forms. In the past, it becomes 「元気がなかった|げんきがなかった」, he seemed down. To link it to another sentence, use the form ending in 「て」, 「元気がなくて|げんきがなくて」, as in 「元気がなくて、何も食べません|げんきがなくて、なにもたべません」, she's down and isn't eating anything. With the looks-like pattern from your lessons, it turns into 「元気がなさそう|げんきがなさそう」, he looks a bit down. Notice the extra 「さ」 that appears before 「そう」. That little 「さ」 is a famous exception, and this is one of the most common places you'll hear it. In casual speech the particle 「が」 often drops out completely, leaving 「元気ない|げんきない」. Finally, the positive side. 「元気|げんき」 by itself is an adjective that takes 「な」, so a lively person is 「元気な人|げんきなひと」. So the no-energy phrase bends like an adjective, and it even has a looks-that-way form." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji of 「元気|げんき」, 「気|き」, is one of the busiest kanji in the language. It covers spirit, mood, air and feeling. 「気分|きぶん」 is your mood or how you feel right now. 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」 means I feel sick or I feel unwell. 「人気|にんき」 is popularity, literally the spirit of the people. A popular shop is 「人気がある|にんきがある」. 「電気|でんき」 is electricity or the lights. 「空気|くうき」 is air, the spirit of the sky. And a phrase you'll use constantly, 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care, is literally attach your spirit, pay attention. Notice how many of these use the same 「が」 plus 「ある」 or 「ない」 shape as our phrase. 「人気がある|にんきがある」 and 「元気がない|げんきがない」 are built the same way. So when you see this little kanji, think of an invisible energy, and you'll be in the right neighborhood." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Noticing that someone seems down is only half the conversation. Here's how it usually goes on. A friend says 「元気ないね。どうしたの？|げんきないね。どうしたの？」, you seem down, what happened? A typical answer is a soft one: 「ちょっと疲れてて|ちょっとつかれてて」, I'm just a bit tired. Japanese people often play it down rather than share a big problem straight away. Then comes the encouragement. The classic line is 「元気出して|げんきだして」, cheer up, literally put out your energy. More politely, 「元気を出してください|げんきをだしてください」. And when someone recovers, you'll hear 「元気になった|げんきになった」, I'm feeling better now. One more set phrase with the same word: when you say goodbye to someone you won't see for a long time, you can say 「お元気で|おげんきで」, take care, stay well. So the conversation runs from noticing, to a soft answer, to put out your energy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「元気|げんき」, the source of your energy, plus 「ない」 means there's no energy, a gentle way to notice someone seems down, not a diagnosis.",
      "The 「ない」 bends like an i-adjective, giving 「元気がなかった|げんきがなかった」 and the looks-that-way form 「元気がなさそう|げんきがなさそう」 with its famous extra 「さ」.",
      "「気|き」 is invisible energy or spirit, found in 「気分|きぶん」, mood, 「人気|にんき」, popularity, and 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care.",
      "After noticing someone is down, the classic encouragement is 「元気出して|げんきだして」, cheer up, literally put out your energy."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[元気がない|げんきがない|げんきがない]ですね。",
    en: "You don't look well today." },
  "できるだけ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces you know. 「できる」 is can, be able to. 「だけ」 is only or just, as in 「〜だけ」. Together, it's only as much as you can, which means as much as possible. Put it right before what you're trying to do. 「できるだけ早く|できるだけはやく」 is as soon as possible. 「できるだけ毎日運動します|できるだけまいにちうんどうします」 is I exercise every day as much as I can. You'll hear it a lot in instructions from teachers and doctors, like 「できるだけ休んでください|できるだけやすんでください」, please rest as much as possible, which fits right in with this lesson about being sick. It's a great phrase for making plans or promises without overpromising: you're saying you'll do your best, within your ability. It's a little like 「頑張る|がんばる」, to try hard, in spirit. A common mistake for English speakers is putting it at the end of the sentence, like in English, as possible. In Japanese, it goes before the action or the adverb it modifies. Remember the order: can, only, then the thing. It's polite enough for any situation. A way to remember it: only as far as you can go, and not one step less. That's a hook. To recap: can, only. As much as you're able." },
      { title: "Two building blocks", text: "Each half of this phrase has more going on than it first seems. 「できる」 isn't only can. It also means to be finished, as in 「宿題ができた|しゅくだいができた」, my homework is done, and to come into being, as in 「友達ができた|ともだちができた」, I made a friend. The core idea is something becomes possible or comes to exist. 「だけ」 is the limit word. 「一つだけ|ひとつだけ」 is just one, and 「少しだけ|すこしだけ」 is just a little. Be careful not to mix it up with 「しか」, which also means only but needs a negative verb, as in 「一つしかない|ひとつしかない」, there's only one. 「だけ」 is neutral, while 「しか」 sounds like it's not enough. So in our phrase, 「だけ」 sets the limit at whatever is possible. You go right up to that line. Two small words, each with a bigger life of its own." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This phrase is flexible about what follows it. Most often it sits before an adverb. 「できるだけ早く|できるだけはやく」 is as early as possible, and 「できるだけたくさん」 is as many as possible. It can also go before a whole verb phrase. 「できるだけ日本語で話します|できるだけにほんごではなします」, I'll speak in Japanese as much as I can. It even works before 「の」 and a noun. 「できるだけのことをします」 means I'll do everything I can, literally the things that are as much as possible. It also pairs well with a pattern you'll meet, 「ようにする」, try to make a habit of. 「できるだけ歩くようにしています|できるだけあるくようにしています」, I try to walk as much as I can. There's a near-synonym, 「なるべく」. It means much the same, but sounds a touch softer and more like if possible. 「できるだけ」 feels more like pushing to your limit. So it slides in front of an adverb, a verb, or even a noun." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「できる」, can, plus 「だけ」, only, means as much as you're able, and it goes before the action, as in 「できるだけ早く|できるだけはやく」.",
      "「できる」 also means to be finished or come into being, and 「だけ」 is a neutral limit word, unlike 「しか」, which needs a negative.",
      "It can sit before an adverb, a verb phrase, or even 「の」 plus a noun, and 「なるべく」 is a softer near-synonym meaning if possible."
    ],
    ja: "[できるだけ]{毎日|まいにち}{運動|うんどう}します。",
    en: "I exercise every day as much as possible." },
  "たぶん|多分": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. 「多|た」 means many or a lot, the same kanji as in 「多い|おおい」, there are many, from this lesson, but here using its 「た」 reading. 「分|ぶん」 means part or portion. You see it in 「気分|きぶん」, mood, and 「自分|じぶん」, oneself. So 「多分|たぶん」 is a large portion, which turned into most likely or probably. Picture most of the chances pointing one way. In real life it's very often written in kana. The usual position is at the start of the sentence, and it pairs naturally with 「でしょう」 or 「と思います|とおもいます」 at the end. 「たぶん雨でしょう|たぶんあめでしょう」, it'll probably rain. 「たぶん来ると思います|たぶんくるとおもいます」, I think he'll probably come. The ending reinforces the guess. How sure is it? It's somewhere between maybe and probably, a reasonable guess, not certain. A cultural note: Japanese speakers often soften statements to avoid sounding too pushy, and 「たぶん」 is one of the handiest softeners. It can even be a gentle, polite way to say I'm not sure. A common mistake is using it alone with a firm statement that's actually certain. To recap: a large portion of the chances. Probably." },
      { title: "How sure are you", text: "Japanese has a whole ladder of words for how sure you are, and it helps to know where this one sits. At the bottom is 「かもしれない」, maybe, it might be. 「雨が降るかもしれない|あめがふるかもしれない」, it might rain. That's about a coin flip. Next up is 「たぶん」, probably, more likely than not. Above that is 「きっと」, surely, I'm quite confident. 「きっと晴れる|きっとはれる」, I'm sure it'll clear up. It often carries hope or belief. At the top is 「絶対|ぜったい」, definitely, absolutely. These can be mixed. 「たぶん」 with 「かもしれない」 sounds very hesitant, and that's fine in conversation. A useful trick is to listen for which rung a speaker picks. Choosing 「たぶん」 over 「きっと」 tells you they're leaving room to be wrong. So remember the ladder: might, probably, surely, definitely." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In conversation, this word often stands alone as a complete answer. Someone asks 「明日来る？|あしたくる？」, are you coming tomorrow? You answer 「たぶん」, probably. Or 「たぶんね」, probably, yeah. It's also a common way to answer when you're unsure. 「たぶん大丈夫|たぶんだいじょうぶ」, it's probably fine. Here's a short exchange. 「田中さんはどこ？|たなかさんはどこ？」, where's Tanaka? 「たぶん図書館だと思う|たぶんとしょかんだとおもう」, probably at the library, I think. Notice that the 「と思う|とおもう」 at the end matches the guess at the start. Japanese likes that kind of frame, guess word up front, guessing ending at the back. One thing to avoid: don't use it in requests or commands, which aren't guesses. And if your boss asks whether the report will be ready, a bare 「たぶん」 can sound careless. Something like 「たぶん間に合うと思います|たぶんまにあうとおもいます」 is safer. So it's a perfect one-word answer, as long as a guess is what's wanted." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the second kanji, 「分|ぶん」, which changes its sound a lot. In this word it's read 「ぶん」. With minutes, it's 「ふん」 or 「ぷん」. 「五分|ごふん」 is five minutes, and 「十分|じゅっぷん」 is ten minutes. Now here's a surprise. The same two kanji, read 「じゅうぶん」, mean enough. So 「十分|じゅうぶん」 can be ten minutes or plenty, depending on the reading. The kanji is also the first half of 「分かる|わかる」, to understand. The shape helps. On top is a pair of strokes spreading apart, and below is the knife shape, 「刀|かたな」. It's about dividing, cutting into parts. Understanding is like splitting a problem into pieces you can handle. The first kanji, 「多|た」, is two of the evening shape stacked on top of each other, and the stacked look matches its meaning, many. So many parts, divided out: probably." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「多|た」, many, plus 「分|ぶん」, portion, is a large share of the chances, probably, and it pairs naturally with 「でしょう」 or 「と思います|とおもいます」.",
      "On the ladder of certainty, 「かもしれない」 is maybe, 「たぶん」 is probably, 「きっと」 is surely, and 「絶対|ぜったい」 is definitely.",
      "It works alone as a one-word answer, but with your boss add a guessing ending, as in 「たぶん間に合うと思います|たぶんまにあうとおもいます」.",
      "「分|ぶん」 is a knife dividing things and changes its sound a lot, as in 「五分|ごふん」, 「十分|じゅっぷん」, and 「十分|じゅうぶん」, enough."
    ],
    ja: "あしたは[多分|たぶん]{雨|あめ}でしょう。",
    en: "It will probably rain tomorrow." },
  "もうすぐ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces you know. 「もう」 means already, and 「すぐ」 means right away. Put them together and you get it's almost already here, coming right up. It means very soon, in a few moments, or even in a few days or weeks, depending on what's coming. The usual patterns are 「もうすぐ」 plus a noun plus 「です」: 「もうすぐ夏休みです|もうすぐなつやすみです」, summer vacation is coming soon. Or 「もうすぐ」 plus a verb: 「もうすぐ来ます|もうすぐきます」, they'll be here soon. You can also use it for time and events: 「もうすぐ誕生日です|もうすぐたんじょうびです」, my birthday is coming up. Here's a tip for English speakers. 「すぐ」 alone is immediately, often used for actions: 「すぐ行きます|すぐいきます」, I'll go right now. 「もうすぐ」 is more about something approaching on its own. Something is about to happen, rather than you doing it right now. Watch out for 「もう」 in other combinations, like 「もう一度|もういちど」, one more time, where it means more instead of already. Context tells you which. A way to remember it: already, and right away, so it's practically here. That's a hook. To recap: already plus right away. Very soon." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first half, 「もう」, is a little word with a lot of jobs, and it helps to see them side by side. With a positive verb, it means already. 「もう食べました|もうたべました」, I already ate. With a negative verb, it means not anymore. 「もう食べません|もうたべません」, I won't eat any more. With an amount, it means more. 「もう少し|もうすこし」, a little more, and 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」, one more. Its natural partner is 「まだ」, still or not yet. 「まだ食べていません|まだたべていません」, I haven't eaten yet. So 「もう」 and 「まだ」 are opposites on the same timeline. In our phrase, 「もう」 is the already idea, pushed forward. The event isn't here, but it's so close it might as well be. So already, not anymore, and more, all from one tiny word." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "On Japanese trains, you won't hear this exact phrase in the announcements. You'll hear its formal cousin, 「まもなく」, shortly. 「まもなく電車が参ります|まもなくでんしゃがまいります」, the train will be arriving shortly. It means the same thing, but sounds official. In everyday talk, 「もうすぐ」 is the natural choice. Friends say 「もうすぐ着く|もうすぐつく」, I'm almost there, in a text when they're running late. It's also great on its own as a reply. 「まだ？」, not yet? 「もうすぐ！」, almost! Don't confuse it with 「そろそろ」. That word means it's about time to do something, and it's often a polite signal to leave: 「そろそろ帰ります|そろそろかえります」, I should be heading home soon. 「もうすぐ」 describes something approaching. 「そろそろ」 suggests it's time for you to act. So trains say 「まもなく」, friends say 「もうすぐ」, and guests getting ready to go say 「そろそろ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「もう」, already, plus 「すぐ」, right away, means it's practically here, used for something approaching on its own rather than an action you take right now.",
      "「もう」 means already with a positive verb, not anymore with a negative, and more with an amount, and its opposite partner is 「まだ」.",
      "Trains say the formal 「まもなく」, friends text 「もうすぐ着く|もうすぐつく」, and 「そろそろ」 means it's about time for you to act."
    ],
    ja: "[もうすぐ]{夏休み|なつやすみ}です。",
    en: "Summer vacation is coming soon." },
  "はじめて|初めて": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The native reading here is 「はじめて」. Look at the kanji. The left side is the clothing radical, and the right side is 「刀|かたな」, a knife or blade. The traditional explanation is that cutting the cloth is the first step in making clothes. That's a lovely picture for beginnings. You already know this kanji from a phrase you say all the time: 「はじめまして」, nice to meet you, literally this is the first time. The 「はじめ」 part is the same word. It's also in 「初詣|はつもうで」, with the reading 「はつ」, the first shrine visit of the new year, a big cultural tradition in early January. There's a sound-alike: 「始める|はじめる」, to begin, uses a different kanji. 「初めて|はじめて」 is about doing something for the first time in your life, while 「始める|はじめる」 is about starting an activity. So 「初めて日本に行きました|はじめてにほんにいきました」 means I went to Japan for the first time. A common mistake is using it to mean first, as in first of all, in a list of steps. That's 「最初に|さいしょに」 or 「まず」. To recap: cutting the cloth, the very first step. For the first time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The 「初|はつ」 reading of this kanji gives a whole set of special firsts. 「初雪|はつゆき」 is the first snow of the season. 「初恋|はつこい」 is your first love. 「初日の出|はつひので」 is the first sunrise of the new year, and many people stay up or travel to the coast to watch it. The kanji can also be read 「しょ」, as in 「最初|さいしょ」, the very first, and 「初級|しょきゅう」, beginner level. Your textbook is a 「初級|しょきゅう」 book. Then there's the form 「初めに|はじめに」, at the beginning, which is about the start of a period or an event, not your lifetime. So 「初めて|はじめて」 is the first time ever, 「初めに|はじめに」 is at the start, and 「最初|さいしょ」 is the first one in an order. All of them share the beginning kanji, but each points at a different kind of first." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Two patterns make this word really useful. The first is 「初めての|はじめての」 plus a noun. 「初めての旅行|はじめてのりょこう」, my first trip. 「初めてのデート|はじめてのデート」, a first date. The 「の」 lets it describe a thing. The second is a very natural way to say it's my first time. Turn the verb into a noun with 「の」, and add 「は初めてです|ははじめてです」. 「日本に来たのは初めてです|にほんにきたのははじめてです」, this is my first time coming to Japan. That sounds more natural than a direct translation of the English. It also lines up neatly with the experience pattern, 「〜たことがある」. If someone asks 「すしを食べたことがありますか|すしをたべたことがありますか」, have you ever eaten sushi, you can reply 「いいえ、今日が初めてです|いいえ、きょうがはじめてです」, no, today's my first time. So for any first experience, reach for the noun with 「の」 and 「は初めてです|ははじめてです」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this question a lot in Japan as a customer: 「初めてですか|はじめてですか」, is this your first time here? Hair salons, clinics, gyms and some restaurants ask it so they can explain the system or give you a form to fill in. The polite version is 「初めてのご利用ですか|はじめてのごりようですか」, is this your first time using our service? A simple answer is 「はい、初めてです|はい、はじめてです」. If you've been before, say 「二回目です|にかいめです」, it's my second time. The word also appears in introductions. The full set phrase 「初めまして|はじめまして」 is said only the first time you meet someone. You never say it to someone you've met before, even if it's been years. For that, use 「お久しぶりです|おひさしぶりです」, long time no see. So shops ask if it's your first time, and you only say the greeting once." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a blade cutting cloth, the first step in making clothes, and it means the first time ever, unlike 「最初に|さいしょに」 or 「始める|はじめる」.",
      "The reading 「はつ」 gives special firsts like 「初雪|はつゆき」 and 「初恋|はつこい」, while 「しょ」 appears in 「最初|さいしょ」 and 「初級|しょきゅう」.",
      "Say it's your first time by turning the verb into a noun with 「の」: 「日本に来たのは初めてです|にほんにきたのははじめてです」.",
      "Shops ask 「初めてですか|はじめてですか」, and 「はじめまして」 is said only the first time you ever meet someone."
    ],
    ja: "[初めて|はじめて]{日本|にほん}にいきました。",
    en: "I went to Japan for the first time." },
  "にさんにち|二三日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is simple once you see it. 「二|に」 is two, 「三|さん」 is three, and 「日|にち」 is the counter for days. Put them side by side and you get two or three days, a couple of days. Notice it uses the Chinese-origin readings for the numbers, 「に」 and 「さん」, and 「にち」 for day. Compare the special native reading for two days, 「二日|ふつか」, and three days, 「三日|みっか」, from your vocab, or 「二日酔い|ふつかよい」, hangover. Those are the precise counts, while this one is a rough estimate. That's an important difference. A common mistake for English speakers is to try to say two to three days with something like a to between them. Japanese simply puts the numbers side by side, no extra word needed. The same trick works with other counters too. 「二三人|にさんにん」, two or three people. 「二三回|にさんかい」, two or three times. You'll hear this a lot at the doctor: 「二三日休んでください|にさんにちやすんでください」, please rest for a couple of days, which fits this lesson about being sick. It's also used in everyday plans, like being away for a couple of days. To recap: two, three, days. A couple of days, as a rough guess." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Putting two numbers side by side is a whole family of rough estimates, and some pairs have their own sounds. 「一二回|いちにかい」, once or twice. 「二三人|にさんにん」, two or three people. 「四五日|しごにち」, four or five days. Notice four is read 「し」 here, not 「よん」. The old reading comes back in these pairs. 「五六人|ごろくにん」, five or six people. 「七八人|しちはちにん」, seven or eight people. For a vaguer number, there's 「数|すう」, several. 「数日|すうじつ」 is a few days, and 「数人|すうにん」 is a few people. These sound a bit more written or formal. And you can always add 「ぐらい」, about, for extra fuzziness, like 「二三日ぐらい|にさんにちぐらい」, about two or three days. So just pair neighboring numbers, and the counter covers both." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase is common whenever someone gives a rough time frame. At the doctor: 「二三日で治りますよ|にさんにちでなおりますよ」, it'll get better in two or three days. The 「で」 here marks how long it takes. At work: 「二三日中にお返事します|にさんにちちゅうにおへんじします」, I'll get back to you within a couple of days. The ending 「中に|ちゅうに」 means within. On a trip: 「二三日東京にいます|にさんにちとうきょうにいます」, I'll be in Tokyo for a couple of days. In writing, you'll usually see it with ordinary digits and a small comma between them, rather than kanji. Either way, it's read the same. One more tip. If you want to be exact, don't use this. Say 「二日|ふつか」 or 「三日|みっか」. This phrase is for when you honestly don't know, and that vagueness is often what makes it polite. So it's the go-to way to say a couple of days without promising which." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese just puts the numbers side by side, two, three, days, for a rough couple of days, unlike the exact 「二日|ふつか」 or 「三日|みっか」.",
      "Pair neighboring numbers with any counter for an estimate, like 「一二回|いちにかい」 or 「四五日|しごにち」, where four goes back to its old reading 「し」.",
      "Doctors say 「二三日で治りますよ|にさんにちでなおりますよ」, and its vagueness is part of what makes it polite."
    ],
    ja: "[二三日|にさんにち]やすんでください。",
    en: "Please rest for two or three days." },
  "それに|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces. 「それ」 is that, the same word as in 「それから」, and then, which you know. 「に」 here works like onto or on top of. So 「それに」 is on top of that, which means moreover or besides. It's a conjunction that starts a new sentence, and it's used for stacking up one more reason or point in the same direction. For example, 「このみせはやすいです。それに、おいしいです。」, this shop is cheap, and on top of that, it's tasty. Both points support the same idea, that it's a good shop. A common mistake for English speakers is to use 「それに」 for and then, in time order. That's 「それから」. 「それから」 is about what happens next, while 「それに」 is about adding another reason. It's also great for excuses. In this lesson, someone might say they can't go out: they have a cold, and besides, they have an exam tomorrow. 「それに」 lets you pile up reasons naturally. It's neutral and polite enough for most situations. In casual speech, you might hear it at the start of an extra complaint too. A way to remember it: picture placing one more brick on top of a stack. That's a hook. To recap: that, plus on top of. Moreover." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a small set of words for joining sentences, and each one has its own job. 「そして」 is plain and, used to line up facts calmly, often in writing. 「それから」 is and then, next in time. 「でも」 is but, turning in the opposite direction. 「だから」 is so, therefore, giving a result. 「それで」 is and so, often used when the result is what happened next in a story. 「それに」 is the one that piles on. It adds another point that pushes in the same direction as the first. It feels stronger than 「そして」, a little like and what's more. Here's a quick test. If the second sentence makes your case stronger, 「それに」 fits. If it just continues a story, reach for 「そして」 or 「それから」. So 「それに」 is the stacker in the family, the one that builds up an argument." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word loves to team up with the 「し」 pattern from your lessons, where you list reasons with 「し」 at the end of each one. Imagine a friend asks why you don't want to go to a party. 「今日は疲れているし、それに、明日テストがあるし|きょうはつかれているし、それに、あしたテストがあるし」. I'm tired today, and besides, I have a test tomorrow. The 「し」 says these are reasons, and 「それに」 adds weight to the next one. It's also handy for praise. 「この部屋は広いです。それに、駅から近いです|このへやはひろいです。それに、えきからちかいです」, this room is spacious. Plus, it's close to the station. In casual speech, you'll often hear a small pause after it, like taking a breath before the clinching point. So use it to add the final reason that seals the deal." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a trap. The very same sounds can mean something completely different. At a restaurant, someone points at the menu and says 「それにします」. That doesn't mean moreover, I'll do it. It means I'll have that one. It comes from the pattern noun plus 「にする」, to decide on something. So 「それ」, that, plus 「に」, plus 「します」, I decide on. You probably know 「コーヒーにします」, I'll have coffee. How do you tell them apart? Position and rhythm. The connector stands at the very start of a sentence, usually with a little pause after it, and points back to what was just said. The ordering phrase is a whole sentence, and 「それ」 means a real thing in front of you. So 「それに、安いです|それに、やすいです」 is besides, it's cheap. But 「それにします」 is I'll take that. Same sounds, different jobs. Listen for the pause." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「それ」, that, plus 「に」, on top of, means moreover, stacking another reason in the same direction, unlike 「それから」, which means and then.",
      "Among the sentence joiners, 「それに」 is the stacker, adding a point that makes your case stronger rather than just continuing a story like 「そして」.",
      "It teams up with the 「し」 pattern for piling reasons, adding the final one that seals the deal.",
      "Don't confuse the connector at the start of a sentence with 「それにします」, I'll have that one, from the pattern noun plus 「にする」."
    ],
    ja: "このみせはやすいです。[それに]、おいしいです。",
    en: "This shop is cheap. Moreover, it is tasty." },
  "おなじ|同じ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native reading 「おなじ」. It's a special word grammatically, and that's the most important thing to learn. It acts like an adjective, but when it comes directly before a noun, you add nothing, no 「な」 and no 「の」. So 「同じクラス|おなじクラス」, the same class, and 「同じ大学|おなじだいがく」, the same college. A very common mistake for English speakers is to say 「同じなクラス|おなじなクラス」 or 「同じのクラス|おなじのクラス」. Both are wrong. Just put the noun right after it. At the end of a sentence, you use 「です」: 「同じです|おなじです」, they're the same. To compare, use 「と」: 「これとそれは同じです|これとそれはおなじです」, this and that are the same, or 「兄と同じ|あにとおなじ」, the same as my older brother. You can also say 「同じ物|おなじもの」, the same thing, with 「物|もの」 from this lesson. Its opposite is 「違う|ちがう」, to be different. Look at the kanji: a frame with a single 「口|くち」, mouth, inside, under a line. A way to remember it: everyone under one roof speaking with one mouth, all saying the same thing. That's a hook. To recap: the same, and it goes straight before a noun with nothing in between." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When this kanji is in a compound, it usually switches to its Chinese-origin reading, 「どう」. 「同時|どうじ」 means at the same time. 「同級生|どうきゅうせい」 is a classmate, someone in the same grade. 「同僚|どうりょう」 is a coworker. 「同意|どうい」 means agreement, the same opinion. So whenever you hear 「どう」 at the start of a word and it has to do with sameness or togetherness, think of this kanji. Be careful though. 「どう」 is also how, as in 「どうですか」, and there are other kanji read 「どう」, like the one in 「動物|どうぶつ」, animal. Context will guide you. On its own, as the everyday word for same, it keeps the native reading 「おなじ」. And in more formal writing there's a related form, 「同じく|おなじく」, likewise, as in 「私も同じく|わたしもおなじく」. So same alone is 「おなじ」, but inside compounds it turns into 「どう」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here are two patterns that take this word further. The first is 「同じぐらい|おなじぐらい」, about the same amount. Use it to compare. 「兄は父と同じぐらい背が高いです|あにはちちとおなじぐらいせがたかいです」, my older brother is about as tall as my father. The 「と」 marks what you're comparing with. The second is 「同じように|おなじように」, in the same way. 「先生と同じように言ってください|せんせいとおなじようにいってください」, please say it the same way as the teacher. You can also make it a noun with 「の」. 「同じのをください|おなじのをください」, the same one, please. This 「の」 means one, not the joining 「の」, so it doesn't break the no-particle-before-nouns rule. So same as is 「と同じ|とおなじ」, about as much is 「同じぐらい|おなじぐらい」, and in the same way is 「同じように|おなじように」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is everywhere in small daily moments. At a café, when your friend orders, you can just say 「私も同じので|わたしもおなじので」, I'll have the same. Very natural, and it saves you reading the whole menu. When you discover something in common, people say 「同じだ！|おなじだ！」 or 「一緒！|いっしょ！」, same here! It's a quick way to bond, like finding out you were born in the same year. Here's a mini dialogue. 「どこの大学？|どこのだいがく？」, which college? 「東京大学|とうきょうだいがく」. 「えっ、同じ！|えっ、おなじ！」, no way, same! The word 「一緒|いっしょ」 overlaps with it. In casual speech it can mean the same too, but its main meaning is together. So whether you're ordering or making friends, same is a word that brings people closer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Put the noun straight after it with no 「な」 or 「の」, as in 「同じクラス|おなじクラス」, and compare with 「と」, as in 「兄と同じ|あにとおなじ」.",
      "In compounds it switches to 「どう」, as in 「同時|どうじ」, at the same time, and 「同級生|どうきゅうせい」, a classmate.",
      "Use 「と同じ|とおなじ」 for same as, 「同じぐらい|おなじぐらい」 for about as much, and 「同じように|おなじように」 for in the same way.",
      "At a café, 「私も同じので|わたしもおなじので」 means I'll have the same, and 「同じ！|おなじ！」 is a quick way to bond over something in common."
    ],
    ja: "わたしと{兄|あに}は[同じ|おなじ]{大学|だいがく}です。",
    en: "My older brother and I go to the same college." },
  "おとな|大人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the two kanji. The first is the big from 「大きい|おおきい」 and 「大学|だいがく」. The second is the person from 「人|ひと」 and 「外国人|がいこくじん」. Put them together and you get a big person, a grown-up. That part is easy. The tricky part is the sound. Neither kanji is read the way you would expect. Normally the big kanji is read 「だい」 or 「おお」, and the person kanji is 「ひと」, 「じん」 or 「にん」. But this word is an old native Japanese word, 「おとな」, that was simply given these two kanji because they fit the meaning. The reading belongs to the pair as a whole, not to each kanji. You already know another word like this: 「一人|ひとり」, one person, where the reading also belongs to the whole word. So when you see these two kanji side by side, do not try to build the sound from the parts. Just remember the pair. You will see it on ticket machines and price boards, for example 「大人|おとな」 next to 「子供|こども」, adult price and child price. So, a big person is a grown-up, and the pair has its own special reading." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The same kanji pair shows up in a few words that grow out of this one. 「大人っぽい|おとなっぽい」 means grown-up looking or mature, said about a teenager who dresses or acts older. The ending 「っぽい」 means like or -ish. 「大人しい|おとなしい」 is an adjective meaning quiet, calm or well-behaved. A quiet child or a gentle dog is 「おとなしい」. Yes, it's literally acting like an adult. 「大人げない|おとなげない」 is the opposite: childish, said about an adult who acts badly, like a grown man sulking after losing a game. And the natural opposite of our word is 「子供|こども」, child. So from one grown-up pair you get mature, quiet, and immature. That's a whole family built on what an adult is supposed to be." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Becoming an adult in Japan has a public celebration, 「成人の日|せいじんのひ」, Coming of Age Day, on the second Monday of January. For many years, the legal age of adulthood was twenty. In 2022, it was lowered to eighteen. But some rules stayed at twenty. You still have to be twenty to drink alcohol or smoke. Most towns still hold their ceremony for twenty-year-olds, partly because eighteen-year-olds are busy with entrance exams. Young women often wear a bright long-sleeved kimono called a 「振袖|ふりそで」, and young men wear suits or traditional dress. The formal word for an adult in legal or official contexts is 「成人|せいじん」, literally a completed person. 「大人|おとな」 is the everyday word. So becoming a grown-up is marked with a ceremony, a kimono, and a day off in January." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word shows up a lot in prices and tickets. At a museum or a train station, you'll see two prices side by side, one for adults and one for children. To buy a ticket, say 「大人一枚|おとないちまい」, one adult, please. For two, 「大人二枚|おとなにまい」. On Japanese trains, the adult fare usually starts at age twelve, and children's fares are about half. In everyday talk, kids say 「大人になったら|おとなになったら」, when I grow up. 「大人になったら、パイロットになりたい|おとなになったら、パイロットになりたい」, when I grow up, I want to be a pilot. And there's a nice phrase you'll hear about someone who grew up a lot: 「大人になったね|おとなになったね」, you've really grown up. A common mistake is using 「大人|おとな」 for older people. For seniors, use 「お年寄り|おとしより」 instead. So one adult ticket, and the dream of when I grow up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Big plus person is a grown-up, but the native word 「おとな」 was simply given these kanji, so the reading belongs to the pair as a whole.",
      "The pair grows into 「大人っぽい|おとなっぽい」, mature-looking, 「大人しい|おとなしい」, quiet, and 「大人げない|おとなげない」, childish.",
      "Adulthood is celebrated on 「成人の日|せいじんのひ」 in January, and though the legal age dropped to eighteen, drinking still starts at twenty.",
      "Buy a ticket with 「大人一枚|おとないちまい」, and kids dream with 「大人になったら|おとなになったら」, when I grow up."
    ],
    ja: "[大人|おとな]は{千円|せんえん}です。",
    en: "Adults are one thousand yen." },
  "べんごし|弁護士": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a three kanji job word, and all three readings are the Chinese-origin ones. The first, 「弁|べん」, has to do with speech and arguing a case, being able to talk your way through something. The second, 「護|ご」, means to protect or defend. The third, 「士|し」, marks a trained professional, someone with a qualification. Add them up: a qualified professional who speaks up to protect you. That is exactly what a lawyer does in court. Now the family. You already know 「看護師|かんごし」, nurse. It has the same 「護|ご」, protect, in the middle. A nurse watches over and protects patients, a lawyer protects clients with words. And the 「士|し」 at the end shows up in other job words you know, like 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, firefighter, and 「宇宙飛行士|うちゅうひこうし」, astronaut. Careful, though: the 「し」 in 「看護師|かんごし」 is a different kanji, 「師|し」, used for teachers and experts. Both sound the same, so listen for the meaning. So, speak, protect, professional: the one who argues your case." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「弁|べん」, appears in some surprising places. You know it from 「弁当|べんとう」, a boxed lunch. And you'll hear it in names of dialects: 「関西弁|かんさいべん」, Kansai dialect, and 「大阪弁|おおさかべん」, Osaka dialect. Why such different meanings, from lunch to speech to law? The modern form of this kanji is a simplified one. In the official reform of the 1940s, it took over the jobs of several older, more complicated kanji, one about speech and argument, another about sorting and handling. So today the one simple shape carries all of them. For our word, the speech meaning is the one that matters. A lawyer is someone who speaks, and a dialect is a way of speaking. So 「弁|べん」 can be a lunch box, a way of talking, or the voice that defends you in court." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Becoming a lawyer in Japan is famously hard. Everyone has to pass the national bar exam, 「司法試験|しほうしけん」, which has long been one of the toughest tests in the country. How do people address a lawyer? Not with 「さん」. Lawyers are called 「先生|せんせい」, just like teachers, doctors and politicians. So a lawyer named Sato is 「佐藤先生|さとうせんせい」. The everyday word for law is 「法律|ほうりつ」. A law office is 「法律事務所|ほうりつじむしょ」. If you want to say your mother is a lawyer, it's 「母は弁護士です|はははべんごしです」. And to say you want to become one, 「弁護士になりたいです|べんごしになりたいです」. In TV dramas, lawyer shows are very popular, often with a brilliant but eccentric lawyer winning impossible cases. So it's a tough exam, a title of 「先生|せんせい」, and lots of courtroom dramas." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. Picture a courtroom in Japan. The defense lawyer stands up, but instead of papers, he opens a giant lunch box, a 「弁当|べんとう」. The judge frowns. Then the lawyer starts speaking, and he's so persuasive that every word protects his client like armor. He even speaks in thick Osaka dialect, 「大阪弁|おおさかべん」. At the end, he points to the badge on his jacket that says qualified professional, the 「士|し」 kanji. The whole scene lines up with the word. 「弁|べん」, the lunch box and speech kanji. 「護|ご」, protecting the client. 「士|し」, the trained professional. And to hook the sound, imagine the judge calling out, Ben, go, she! as the lawyer marches to the front. Silly, but it ties lunch, speech, protection and a badge into one picture." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「弁|べん」, speech, plus 「護|ご」, protect, plus 「士|し」, a trained professional, makes a qualified professional who speaks up to protect you.",
      "「弁|べん」 took over several older kanji in a 1940s reform, so it covers 「弁当|べんとう」, dialects like 「関西弁|かんさいべん」, and speech in court.",
      "The bar exam, 「司法試験|しほうしけん」, is famously hard, and lawyers are addressed as 「先生|せんせい」, not 「さん」.",
      "Picture a lawyer opening a 「弁当|べんとう」 in court, protecting his client, and flashing a professional badge as the judge shouts \"Ben, go, she!\""
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は[弁護士|べんごし]です。",
    en: "My mother is a lawyer." },
  "わたくし|私": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the very same kanji as 「私|わたし」, the everyday word for I. The difference is only the reading. 「わたくし」 is the older, fuller form, and 「わたし」 is a shortened version of it that became the normal word. Because the long form sounds more careful and proper, it is now the formal way to say I. Use it when you need to be at your most polite. Think of a job interview, a speech, talking to a customer, or calling a company on the phone. It pairs naturally with the humble phrase 「と申します|ともうします」, so you might say 「わたくしは山田と申します|わたくしはやまだともうします」, my name is Yamada. With friends, it would sound stiff and a little funny, so stay with 「わたし」 or, for men, 「僕|ぼく」. The kanji also carries a sense of private or personal, the opposite of public. You see it in 「私たち|わたしたち」, we. A way to remember it: the extra 「く」 is like putting on a tie. Same person, dressed up. So, one kanji, two readings, and the longer one is the formal one." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has many ways to say I, and choosing one says a lot about you. 「わたくし」 is the most formal, for speeches, business and ceremonies. 「わたし」 is the standard polite I for everyone. 「あたし」 is a softer, casual version used mostly by women and girls. 「僕|ぼく」 is a casual or gentle I used mainly by boys and men. 「俺|おれ」 is a rough, very casual I used by men among friends. It can sound rude in the wrong place. 「自分|じぶん」 literally means oneself, and in some settings, like sports teams, it's used to mean I. Many people switch between them all day. A man might use 「俺|おれ」 with friends, 「僕|ぼく」 with his girlfriend's parents, and 「わたくし」 in a job interview. So picking your I is like picking your clothes for the occasion." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading too, 「し」, and with it the meaning private or personal comes forward. 「私立|しりつ」 means private, as in a private school or private university. 「私鉄|してつ」 is a private railway, as opposed to the big national lines. Now a fun problem. 「市立|しりつ」, city-run, sounds exactly the same as 「私立|しりつ」, private. That's confusing when talking about schools. So people often say 「わたくしりつ」 for private and 「いちりつ」 for city-run, borrowing the native readings just to tell them apart. The shape: on the left is the grain radical, 「禾|のぎへん」, a plant with drooping head. On the right is a small shape that's traditionally said to mean self or private. So the same kanji covers I and private, and it even borrows its formal reading to clear up confusion." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In business Japanese, you'll often hear 「わたくし」 on the phone. 「はい、わたくし、山田でございます|はい、わたくし、やまだでございます」, yes, this is Yamada speaking. For a company speaking of itself, the humble we is 「わたくしども」. A staff member might say it to a customer, meaning we at this company. But here's the bigger lesson. Even in polite Japanese, people often leave the word for I out entirely. If the subject is obvious, you skip it. Saying I in every sentence, the way English does, sounds unnatural and a bit self-focused. So in a self-introduction, you might use it once at the start, 「わたくしは」, then drop it for the rest. English speakers tend to overuse it. A good rule: say it when you need to make clear it's you, not someone else. So it's formal I on the phone, and often, no I at all." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the same kanji as 「わたし」, but the older, fuller reading is the formal I, like putting on a tie for interviews and speeches.",
      "Choosing your I is like choosing clothes, from formal 「わたくし」 and standard 「わたし」 to casual 「僕|ぼく」 and rough 「俺|おれ」.",
      "The reading 「し」 means private, as in 「私立|しりつ」, and to avoid confusion with city-run 「市立|しりつ」 people say 「わたくしりつ」.",
      "You'll hear it on business calls, but even polite Japanese often drops I entirely when the subject is obvious."
    ],
    ja: "[私|わたくし]は{田中|たなか}ともうします。",
    en: "My name is Tanaka." },
  "カレー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word curry, which itself came into English from India. To fit Japanese sounds, the r becomes the Japanese r sound, and the final y turns into a long vowel, written with the long dash. So curry becomes 「カレー」. The interesting part is how it arrived. Curry did not come to Japan straight from India. It came from Britain in the late 1800s, as a British style curry made with curry powder and thickened with flour. That is why Japanese curry is so different from Indian curry. It is a thick, mild, slightly sweet stew with potatoes, carrots and onions, poured over rice. That dish is called 「カレーライス」. The Japanese navy adopted it early, and even today Japan's navy is famous for eating curry on Fridays. Now it is one of the most common home meals and school lunches in Japan. Families buy blocks of curry roux in the supermarket, labeled mild, medium or hot. A handy link: a hot one is 「辛い|からい」, spicy, a word in this same lesson. So, an English word for an Indian dish, reshaped by Britain, and made Japan's comfort food." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, curry is a whole family of dishes, not just one. 「カレーライス」 is the classic, curry and rice on one plate. Old menus sometimes say 「ライスカレー」, with the words swapped. 「カツカレー」 adds a breaded pork cutlet on top. It's a favorite of hungry students. 「カレーうどん」 is thick wheat noodles in curry soup. Be careful, it splashes on your shirt. 「カレーパン」 is a deep-fried bread roll with curry inside, sold at every bakery and convenience store. When you order, you may be asked about spice. 「甘口|あまくち」 is mild, literally sweet mouth. 「中辛|ちゅうから」 is medium. 「辛口|からくち」 is hot. The usual side dish is 「福神漬|ふくじんづけ」, crunchy red pickles, or pickled shallots. So curry in Japan comes on rice, on noodles, and inside bread." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The long vowel at the end really matters, because similar sounds mean very different things. 「かれ」, without the long sound, is he or boyfriend, as in 「彼|かれ」. 「かれい」 is a type of flatfish, a flounder, often simmered in soy sauce. In normal speech it sounds almost the same as curry, so context does the work. 「華麗|かれい」 means splendid or elegant. Japanese ads love the pun, calling a curry dish a splendid curry, with the two words side by side. And 「辛い|からい」, spicy, shares the first sound and is the perfect partner word. To keep curry clear, stretch that final vowel firmly. Curry has two beats in English, but three in Japanese: 「カ」, 「レ」, and a held 「ー」. So, he, flounder, elegant and curry, all separated by vowel length and context." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Curry has special places in Japanese life. Ask almost any Japanese person, and they'll tell you curry tastes better on the second day. It's called 「二日目のカレー|ふつかめのカレー」, second-day curry. The flavors settle overnight, so families often make a big pot on purpose. Curry is also the classic camp food. On school trips and outdoor programs, children cook it outdoors together in big pots. For many people, that's a warm memory. School lunches, 「給食|きゅうしょく」, serve curry regularly, and it's often the most popular menu day. And there are regional curries all over Japan, with local ingredients, sold as souvenirs in boxed pouches. So curry is a family, school and camping memory, and it's best on day two." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Curry reached Japan from Britain in the late 1800s, not India, which is why 「カレーライス」 is a thick, mild, slightly sweet stew over rice.",
      "Curry comes as 「カツカレー」, 「カレーうどん」, and 「カレーパン」, and you choose 「甘口|あまくち」, 「中辛|ちゅうから」, or 「辛口|からくち」.",
      "Hold the final long vowel for three beats, or it blurs into 「かれ」, he, or 「かれい」, a flounder.",
      "Everyone agrees 「二日目のカレー|ふつかめのカレー」, second-day curry, tastes best, and it's a classic of camp and school lunches."
    ],
    ja: "この[カレー]はからいです。",
    en: "This curry is spicy." },
  "こうちゃ|紅茶": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here is a fun difference between languages. English calls it black tea, but Japanese calls it red tea. The first kanji, 「紅|こう」, means deep red or crimson, the color of the brewed tea in the cup. Notice the thread radical on its left side, the same one you see in 「約束|やくそく」 and 「手紙|てがみ」. It hints at dyed thread and cloth. The second kanji, 「茶|ちゃ」, is tea. It has the grass radical on top, because tea is made from leaves. Both readings here are Chinese-origin readings. Now the family. You already know 「お茶|おちゃ」, which in Japan means green tea by default. If you just ask for tea, you will get green tea. If you want the British style, you ask for this word. The same tea kanji also hides inside 「喫茶店|きっさてん」, a cafe, where it is read 「さ」 instead. Chinese also calls this kind of tea red tea, so the name came along with the kanji. So, black tea in English is red tea in Japanese, named for the color in your cup." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The tea kanji, 「茶|ちゃ」, builds a whole menu of drinks. 「抹茶|まっちゃ」 is powdered green tea, the bright, frothy kind used in the tea ceremony and in sweets. 「麦茶|むぎちゃ」 is roasted barley tea. It has no caffeine, and in summer almost every Japanese fridge has a cold jug of it. 「ほうじ茶|ほうじちゃ」 is roasted green tea, brown and toasty. 「ウーロン茶|ウーロンちゃ」 is oolong tea, a Chinese style tea that's very popular with meals. And here's a surprise. The word for the color brown, 「茶色|ちゃいろ」, is literally tea color. Brown hair is 「茶色い髪|ちゃいろいかみ」. So when you see 「茶|ちゃ」 at the end of a drink name, it's tea, and the first part tells you which kind." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「紅|こう」, is one you'll see again in some beautiful words. On its left is the thread radical, and on its right is a simple shape, 「工|こう」, the same one you see in 「工場|こうじょう」, factory. That right side gives the sound 「こう」. Japanese has many kanji where one side gives the meaning area and the other side hints at the sound. With its native reading 「べに」, it means crimson dye or rouge. 「口紅|くちべに」 is lipstick, literally mouth red. And 「紅葉|こうよう」 means autumn leaves turning red. The same two kanji can also be read 「もみじ」, which means the maple tree or its red leaves. Autumn leaf viewing is a big seasonal outing in Japan. So this kanji is a deep red, from your teacup to your lips to the maple trees." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japanese cafés, ordering this tea comes with a few choices. You'll often see 「ミルクティー」, tea with milk, and 「レモンティー」, tea with lemon. To order, just say 「紅茶をお願いします|こうちゃをおねがいします」, or name the style. Staff will often ask 「ホットとアイス、どちらにしますか」, hot or iced? Iced tea is very common in Japan, even in winter. Interestingly, green tea in Japan is drunk plain, with no sugar or milk. This red tea is the one people treat in a Western way, with sugar, milk, and sweets on the side. Convenience stores and vending machines sell bottled versions, both sweetened and unsweetened. Read the label for 「無糖|むとう」, no sugar, if you prefer it plain. So for British style tea, ask for this word, and choose hot or iced." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English says black tea but Japanese says red tea, 「紅|こう」, crimson, plus 「茶|ちゃ」, since plain 「お茶|おちゃ」 means green tea.",
      "「茶|ちゃ」 builds a menu like 「抹茶|まっちゃ」 and 「麦茶|むぎちゃ」, and brown is literally tea color, 「茶色|ちゃいろ」.",
      "「紅|こう」 takes its sound from 「工|こう」, and as 「べに」 it gives 「口紅|くちべに」, lipstick, while 「紅葉|こうよう」 is red autumn leaves.",
      "This is the tea people take Western style with milk, lemon, or sugar, and staff will ask whether you want it hot or iced."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[紅茶|こうちゃ]を{飲みます|のみます}。",
    en: "I drink black tea in the morning." },
  "きもの|着物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built from two native readings, and it is very literal. The first kanji, 「着|き」, comes from the verb 「着る|きる」, to wear. The second, 「物|もの」, means thing. So the word is just a thing to wear. Long ago it meant any clothes at all. Over time, as Western clothes became normal, the word narrowed to mean the traditional Japanese robe. Now look at the family. The ending 「物|もの」 is everywhere: 「食べ物|たべもの」, a thing to eat, food, 「飲み物|のみもの」, a thing to drink, and 「買い物|かいもの」, shopping. This word fits the same pattern. And the first kanji also appears in 「着く|つく」, to arrive, a word you will meet soon. Today most people wear a kimono only on special days, like New Year's, weddings, graduation, or the Coming of Age Day when twenty year olds dress up. In summer there is a light cotton version for festivals called 「ゆかた」. Putting on a formal kimono is so complicated that many people pay a professional to help. So, a thing to wear, which became the traditional robe." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese uses different verbs for wearing, depending on where the clothing goes. 「着る|きる」 is for things on your upper body or your whole body: shirts, jackets, dresses and of course the kimono. 「着物を着る|きものをきる」, to wear a kimono. Same kanji, twice. 「はく」 is for things below the waist: pants, skirts, socks and shoes. 「かぶる」 is for hats, things you put on your head. 「かける」 is for glasses. 「する」 covers accessories like ties, watches and scarves. To take off clothes, most use 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」. 「靴を脱ぐ|くつをぬぐ」, take off your shoes, is very important at Japanese homes. Here's a check: in 「着物|きもの」, the verb 「着る|きる」 is built right in, so you can always remember which verb goes with it. So the robe and the verb share a kanji: you 「着る|きる」 a 「着物|きもの」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Kimono come with rules that every Japanese person learns. The most important one is the wrap. The left side always goes over the right side, as seen by the wearer. The right side is tucked underneath first. Wrapping it the other way is done only for the dead at funerals, so getting it wrong is a big mistake. The wide sash tied around the waist is the 「帯|おび」. It can be very long and is tied in decorative knots at the back. Young unmarried women often wear a 「振袖|ふりそで」, with long swinging sleeves, at Coming of Age Day. In summer, people wear the light cotton version to fireworks festivals. At many tourist spots, like Kyoto, you can rent a kimono for a day and walk the old streets in it. So: left over right, a sash at the waist, and the swinging sleeves for celebrations." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The wearing kanji, 「着|き」, shows up in many other clothing words, often read 「ぎ」. 「上着|うわぎ」 is a jacket or outer layer, literally upper wearing. 「下着|したぎ」 is underwear, literally lower wearing. 「水着|みずぎ」 is a swimsuit, water wearing. 「普段着|ふだんぎ」 is everyday clothes, as opposed to dressing up. Notice how the 「き」 softens to 「ぎ」 at the end of each compound. That softening often happens when two words join. There's also a pair worth knowing. 「和服|わふく」 is a more formal word for Japanese clothing, and 「洋服|ようふく」 is Western clothing. Today 「洋服|ようふく」 just means clothes in general, since that's what most people wear. So from the swimsuit to the jacket, the wearing kanji is there." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「着|き」 from 「着る|きる」, to wear, plus 「物|もの」, thing, once meant any clothes, then narrowed to the traditional robe as Western clothes took over.",
      "Wearing verbs depend on body part, and the robe has its verb built in: you 「着る|きる」 a 「着物|きもの」.",
      "Always wrap left over right as the wearer sees it, since the other way is only for the dead at funerals.",
      "In clothing compounds the wearing kanji softens to 「ぎ」, as in 「上着|うわぎ」, 「下着|したぎ」, and 「水着|みずぎ」."
    ],
    ja: "{お正月|おしょうがつ}に[着物|きもの]を{着ました|きました}。",
    en: "I wore a kimono at New Year's." },
  "セーター|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is from the English word sweater. Watch how the sounds get squeezed. Japanese does not like the sw cluster at the start, so the w simply drops out and you get 「セ」. The t sound stays. Then the er at the end, which English barely pronounces, turns into a long vowel, written with the long dash. That same habit of making er into a long vowel shows up in lots of loanwords you know, like 「コンピューター」, computer, and 「スーパー」, supermarket. In Japanese this word is used for knitted tops, the kind made from wool or yarn. Be careful with a related false friend. A sweatshirt, the cotton kind with a fleece inside, is not called this. It is 「トレーナー」, from the English word trainer. So if you want a cozy knit, say this word. If you want the gym style top, say 「トレーナー」. This word also fits nicely with the verb 「編む|あむ」, to knit, from this same lesson. A handmade knitted sweater is a classic gift in Japanese stories and dramas. So, sweater with the w dropped and the ending stretched out." },
      { title: "Clothing loanwords", text: "Many everyday clothes in Japanese are English loanwords, and they fit together with this one. 「シャツ」 is a shirt. 「ティーシャツ」 is a T-shirt. 「スカート」 is a skirt. 「コート」 is a coat. 「ジャケット」 is a jacket. And 「ジーンズ」 is jeans. A hooded sweatshirt is 「パーカー」, from parka. In fashion magazines and shops, you'll often see the word 「ニット」, from knit. It's the trendy word for knitted tops, and it covers sweaters and cardigans. In everyday talk, both words are used. A cardigan is 「カーディガン」, and a turtleneck is 「タートルネック」. Most of these take the verb 「着る|きる」, since they're on the upper body. But jeans and skirts take 「はく」. So the sweater sits in a closet full of English words, and in stores it might hang under the label 「ニット」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a cold winter morning. Your grandmother has spent all autumn knitting you something. She holds up a huge, itchy, bright red sweater with a reindeer on it. You try to say thank you, but all that comes out is say... ta!, as you struggle to pull it over your head, your arms flailing. Say, ta. 「セー」 and 「ター」, with both vowels held long, just like you're stretching to get the sweater on. The long dashes in the katakana are the stretching. Two long sounds, two long pulls of yarn. And when it's finally on, grandma says the verb you know, 「着て|きて」, wear it, and takes a picture. Say, ta, stretched out and itchy: the Japanese sweater." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Sweater drops its w and stretches the final er into a long vowel, and it means a knitted top, while a cotton sweatshirt is 「トレーナー」.",
      "It hangs among clothing loanwords like 「シャツ」 and 「コート」, and shops often label knitted tops with the trendy word 「ニット」.",
      "Picture yourself struggling into grandma's itchy knitted gift while gasping \"say... ta!\", both sounds stretched long like the dashes in 「セーター」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}が[セーター]をあみました。",
    en: "My mother knitted a sweater." },
  "がっき|楽器": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first kanji, 「楽|がく」, is the music kanji. You know it from 「音楽|おんがく」, music. The same kanji also means fun or comfort, as in 「楽しみです|たのしみです」, I can't wait, because music and joy go together. The second kanji, 「器|き」, means a tool, a vessel, or a container. Both readings here are Chinese-origin. So the word is a music tool, a thing for making music. Now listen to the sound. 「がく」 plus 「き」 should give 「がくき」, but Japanese squeezes the 「く」 into a small pause, giving 「がっき」. The same squeeze happens in 「学校|がっこう」, where 「がく」 plus 「こう」 becomes 「がっこう」. Here is a fun trap. You already know 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester. That 「がっき」 is a different word, written with the learning kanji, but it sounds exactly the same. So 「がっき」 can be a semester or an instrument. Context makes it clear. You play string or keyboard instruments with the verb 「ひく」, like 「ピアノをひく」 or 「ギターをひく」. So, the music kanji plus the tool kanji: a music tool." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "In Japanese, the verb for playing an instrument depends on how you make the sound. For strings and keyboards, use 「弾く|ひく」, to pluck or press. 「ピアノを弾く|ピアノをひく」, 「ギターを弾く|ギターをひく」. For wind instruments, use 「吹く|ふく」, to blow. 「フルートを吹く|フルートをふく」. It's the same verb used for the wind blowing. For drums, use 「たたく」, to hit. 「ドラムをたたく」. There's also a general formal word, 「演奏する|えんそうする」, to perform, which works for any instrument. English uses play for all of them, and so does 「する」 sometimes in casual Japanese, but the specific verbs sound much more natural. To ask about it, use the ability form: 「何か楽器が弾けますか|なにかがっきがひけますか」, can you play any instrument? So pluck the strings, blow the flute, and hit the drums." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「器|き」, has a striking shape: four mouth-like boxes around a shape in the middle. The meaning to remember is a container, vessel or tool. It shows up in useful words. 「食器|しょっき」 is tableware, dishes, the containers you eat from. Notice the same little squeeze: 「しょく」 plus 「き」 becomes 「しょっき」. 「器用|きよう」 means skillful with your hands, good at handling tools. Someone good at crafts is 「器用|きよう」. And 「電器|でんき」 means electrical appliances. A shop that sells them can be called a 「電器屋|でんきや」. Don't mix it up with 「電気|でんき」, electricity, which sounds the same. On its own, with the native reading 「うつわ」, it means a bowl or dish, and also a person's capacity, their caliber. So this kanji is a vessel, whether it holds rice or music." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a rich set of traditional instruments, called 「和楽器|わがっき」, Japanese instruments. 「琴|こと」 is a long zither with thirteen strings, laid on the floor and plucked. 「三味線|しゃみせん」 is a three-stringed instrument with a long neck, played with a large pick. It's heard in kabuki theater. 「尺八|しゃくはち」 is a bamboo flute, with a breathy, haunting sound. 「太鼓|たいこ」 are the big drums you hear at festivals, sometimes in huge powerful groups. Most Japanese children also learn two instruments at school: the recorder, 「リコーダー」, and a keyboard you blow into, called 「鍵盤ハーモニカ|けんばんハーモニカ」. Almost every adult remembers them. So from the 「琴|こと」 to the school recorder, music tools are part of growing up in Japan." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The music kanji 「楽|がく」 plus 「器|き」, a tool, makes a music tool, with 「がく」 squeezed to 「がっ」 as in 「学校|がっこう」.",
      "The playing verb depends on the sound: 「弾く|ひく」 for strings and keys, 「吹く|ふく」 for wind instruments, and 「たたく」 for drums.",
      "「器|き」 means a vessel or tool, as in 「食器|しょっき」, tableware, and 「器用|きよう」, skillful with your hands.",
      "Traditional instruments, 「和楽器|わがっき」, include the 「琴|こと」 and 「三味線|しゃみせん」, while every child learns the recorder at school."
    ],
    ja: "なにか[楽器|がっき]をひけますか。",
    en: "Can you play any musical instrument?" },
  "からて|空手": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two simple kanji, both with native readings. The first, 「空|から」, means empty. The same kanji can also be read 「そら」, sky, and 「くう」 in 「空港|くうこう」, airport. The second, 「手|て」, is hand, as in 「手紙|てがみ」 and 「上手|じょうず」. So the word is empty hand: fighting with your bare hands, no weapons. There is a nice bit of history here. Karate grew up in Okinawa, and it was once written with a different first kanji meaning China, because of its Chinese roots. In the 1930s the name was changed to the empty kanji, which sounds the same and gives the meaning of fighting unarmed. Here is another member of the family you already know. 「カラオケ」 starts with the same empty, 「から」, plus a short form of orchestra. Karaoke is an empty orchestra, the music with the singer missing. So karate and karaoke share the very same empty. So, empty plus hand: the art of fighting with nothing in your hands." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you train in this art in Japan, you'll pick up a whole vocabulary. The training hall is a 「道場|どうじょう」. The belt is 「帯|おび」, and earning a black belt, 「黒帯|くろおび」, is a big goal. You'll hear 「押忍|おす」 shouted constantly. It's a short, strong word used in many karate dojos to mean hello, understood, and thank you. To say you do it, people say 「空手をやっています|からてをやっています」 or 「空手を習っています|からてをならっています」, I'm learning karate. The forms practiced alone, set patterns of moves, are called 「型|かた」. Karate became an Olympic sport for the first time at the Tokyo Games held in 2021. So it's a 「道場|どうじょう」, a belt, and a loud 「押忍|おす」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese martial arts often end with the kanji 「道|どう」, the way or path. 「柔道|じゅうどう」 is judo, the gentle way, focused on throws. 「剣道|けんどう」 is kendo, the way of the sword, with bamboo swords and armor. 「合気道|あいきどう」 is aikido, the way of harmonizing energy. Notice 「気|き」 in the middle, the spirit kanji. 「弓道|きゅうどう」 is Japanese archery, the way of the bow. Karate is sometimes called 「空手道|からてどう」, the way of the empty hand. The idea is that these aren't just fighting skills but a path for training your character. And the same kanji, 「道|みち」, means road in everyday language. So when you see 「道|どう」 at the end, it's a path for life, not just a sport." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The empty kanji, 「空|から」, has more uses than sky and empty, and they all connect. As a verb, 「空く|あく」 means to become free or vacant. 「席が空いています|せきがあいています」, the seat is free. You'll also hear 「すく」 with the same kanji, as in 「お腹が空いた|おなかがすいた」, I'm hungry, literally my stomach is empty. 「空っぽ|からっぽ」 means completely empty, said of a box, a fridge, or even a head. 「空|そら」 is sky, and 「空|くう」 appears in words about air and empty space. So the pictures link up: the sky is empty space, a seat is empty, a stomach is empty, and a karate hand holds nothing. On top of the kanji is the hole shape, and that fits the idea of something hollow. So one kanji gives you sky, empty, free seats and hunger, and the empty hand." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「空|から」, empty, plus 「手|て」, hand, means fighting with nothing in your hands, the same empty found in 「カラオケ」, an empty orchestra.",
      "Train in a 「道場|どうじょう」, aim for a black belt, 「黒帯|くろおび」, and shout 「押忍|おす」 for hello, understood, and thanks.",
      "Many martial arts end in 「道|どう」, the way, like 「柔道|じゅうどう」 and 「剣道|けんどう」, because they're a path for training character.",
      "The empty kanji links sky, a free seat in 「空いています|あいています」, an empty stomach in 「お腹が空いた|おなかがすいた」, and the empty hand."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[空手|からて]をれんしゅうします。",
    en: "I practice karate every day." },
  "ゴルフ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Straight from the English word golf. Watch what happens to the sounds. Japanese syllables almost always end in a vowel, so English consonants that sit alone need a vowel added. The l in the middle has no vowel after it, so it becomes 「ル」, using the Japanese r sound, since Japanese does not have a separate l. The f at the end also stands alone, so it becomes 「フ」. The result is three beats where English has one. You can hear the same pattern in other sport words you know, like 「テニス」 and 「サッカー」. In Japan golf has a strong image as a business sport. Office workers and company bosses traditionally played it with clients and coworkers on weekends, and courses can be expensive. There are also many driving ranges in cities, often with tall green nets you can see from the train. To say you play, use 「する」: 「ゴルフをします」. So, golf, stretched out to fit Japanese, with a vowel after every consonant." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Golf in Japan has some vocabulary you won't learn from the English. A driving range is an 「打ちっぱなし|うちっぱなし」, literally hit and leave them. You hit ball after ball and never go pick them up. Many are in cities, with tall green nets. Golf with clients is called 「接待ゴルフ|せったいゴルフ」, entertainment golf. In the past, a junior employee might even try not to beat his boss or an important client. To say you play, use 「ゴルフをする」. To say you're going golfing, 「ゴルフに行く|ゴルフにいく」. A golf course is 「ゴルフ場|ゴルフじょう」. The ending 「場|じょう」 means place, as in 「駐車場|ちゅうしゃじょう」, parking lot. Golf courses are often far from cities, so players leave very early in the morning. So it's a range where the balls stay, and a game where you might let your boss win." },
      { title: "Sports loanwords", text: "Lots of sports in Japanese are loanwords, and you can spot the same sound habits. 「テニス」 is tennis. 「サッカー」 is soccer, while American football is 「アメフト」, a chopped form of American football. 「バスケ」 is short for 「バスケットボール」, basketball. 「バレー」 is short for volleyball. 「スキー」 is skiing, and 「ボウリング」 is bowling. Most of these use 「する」 or the casual 「やる」: 「テニスをする」, play tennis. Japanese baseball, the most popular sport, has a native word too, 「野球|やきゅう」, literally field ball. Notice how often a vowel is added after a consonant, just like in this word, where one English beat becomes three. So Japanese plays sports in English, but chops them and adds vowels." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese adds a vowel after each lone consonant, so one-beat golf becomes three beats, 「ゴルフ」, and in Japan it has the image of a business sport.",
      "A driving range is an 「打ちっぱなし|うちっぱなし」, where you never collect the balls, and golf with clients is 「接待ゴルフ|せったいゴルフ」.",
      "Sports loanwords get chopped and filled with vowels, like 「バスケ」 and 「アメフト」, while baseball has the native word 「野球|やきゅう」."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は{日曜日|にちようび}に[ゴルフ]をします。",
    en: "My father plays golf on Sundays." },
  "バイク|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word bike, but there is a big meaning shift. In English, bike often means a bicycle. In Japanese, it means a motorbike or motorcycle. The English word was short for motorbike, and Japanese kept that meaning. The sounds come through nicely: the ai sound stays, and the k at the end gets a vowel, becoming 「ク」. Now, for a pedal bicycle, you use a word you already know, 「自転車|じてんしゃ」. Its kanji mean self, turning, and vehicle: a vehicle whose wheels you turn yourself. And 「車|くるま」, car, shares that last kanji. A way to remember it: in Japanese, if it has an engine, it is this loanword. If it needs your legs, it is 「自転車|じてんしゃ」. Small scooters are also usually called by this word. For riding, use the verb 「のる」, to ride, with 「に」: 「バイクにのります」. To say you go somewhere on one, use 「で」, like 「バイクで行きます|バイクでいきます」. So, a borrowed bike, but in Japan it always has a motor." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for two-wheelers, and each has its own picture. 「オートバイ」 is an older word for motorcycle. It was made in Japan from auto and bicycle, and you'll still see it in official or older writing. 「スクーター」 is a scooter, the kind with a flat floor for your feet. 「原付|げんつき」 is a small motor scooter of up to fifty cc. It's short for a long official name meaning a bicycle with a motor attached. Many students ride one because the license is easy to get. For pedal bicycles there's 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, and the everyday shopping bike with a basket is jokingly called a 「ママチャリ」, a mom's bike. 「チャリ」 is slang for a bicycle. So the motor ones are 「バイク」, 「オートバイ」 and 「原付|げんつき」, and the pedal ones are 「自転車|じてんしゃ」 and 「ママチャリ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how this word shows up in daily life. To ride one, you need a license, a 「免許|めんきょ」. 「バイクの免許を取りました|バイクのめんきょをとりました」, I got my motorcycle license. A helmet is a 「ヘルメット」, and wearing one is the law. In big cities, many motorcycle couriers rush documents around, a service called 「バイク便|バイクびん」, bike delivery. Parking is a real challenge in Japanese cities, so you'll see special parking areas just for two-wheelers. A short exchange: 「どうやって来ましたか|どうやってきましたか」, how did you get here? 「バイクで来ました|バイクできました」, I came by motorbike. And if a friend says they like bikes, check which kind. In Japanese, they almost certainly mean the motor kind. So license, helmet, and the fast couriers, all with an engine." }
    ],
    short: [
      "In Japanese it means a motorbike, never a pedal bicycle, which is 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, so if it has an engine, it's 「バイク」.",
      "Motor two-wheelers include 「オートバイ」 and the small 「原付|げんつき」, while pedal bikes are 「自転車|じてんしゃ」 or the basket-front 「ママチャリ」.",
      "You need a license, 「免許|めんきょ」, and a helmet, and if a friend says they like bikes, they almost certainly mean the motor kind."
    ],
    ja: "[バイク]で{大学|だいがく}に{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to college by motorcycle." },
  "ぞう|象": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is one of the classic picture characters. In its oldest form it was a drawing of an elephant standing on end, with the trunk at the top, the body in the middle, and legs and tail below. Over the centuries the drawing became squared off, but you can still imagine a long creature stacked vertically. The reading 「ぞう」 is the Chinese-origin reading. Later, the same kanji took on the meaning of shape or image, so you will meet it in advanced words about impressions and appearances. For now, just keep the elephant. A way to remember it: you go to the zoo to see one, and 「ぞう」 sounds like zoo. The zoo itself is 「動物園|どうぶつえん」. A cute fact: every Japanese child knows the song 「ぞうさん」, where a little elephant is told what a long nose it has, and it answers that its mother has a long nose too. In Japanese, an elephant's trunk is called its nose, 「はな」. So, an ancient drawing of an elephant, and a word that sounds like the place you see one." },
      { title: "Counting elephants", text: "How do you count elephants in Japanese? Not with the counter for small animals. Small animals, like cats and dogs, use 「匹|ひき」. One cat is 「一匹|いっぴき」. Large animals use 「頭|とう」, the same kanji as head. One elephant is 「一頭|いっとう」, two elephants 「二頭|にとう」, three 「三頭|さんとう」. This counter is used for cows, horses, and other big animals too. Birds have their own counter, 「羽|わ」, which means feather. So at the zoo, 「象が二頭います|ぞうがにとういます」, there are two elephants. Notice the verb 「いる」, since elephants are alive. Getting the counter right sounds very natural. Using the small-animal counter for an elephant would sound funny, like calling an elephant a little critter. So small animals get 「匹|ひき」, birds get 「羽|わ」, and the elephant gets a head, 「頭|とう」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This word has some sound-alike neighbors to watch out for. First, length. 「ぞう」 has a long vowel. Without it, 「ぞ」 is just a sentence ending some men use for emphasis, as in 「行くぞ|いくぞ」, let's go! Then voicing. 「そう」, without the dots, is the word in 「そうです」, that's right. Only the little dots separate yes from elephant. Next, the same sound in other kanji. 「像|ぞう」 means a statue or image. It's actually the elephant kanji with the person radical added. 「仏像|ぶつぞう」 is a Buddhist statue. And the elephant kanji itself also reads 「しょう」 in words about appearance: 「印象|いんしょう」 is an impression. 「現象|げんしょう」 is a phenomenon. So with dots, long vowel and the right kanji, 「ぞう」 is the elephant, not that's right." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Elephants have a surprising history in Japan. In 1728, an elephant brought by ship from Vietnam landed in Nagasaki. The next year it walked all the way to Edo, now Tokyo, which took months. Along the way, it visited Kyoto and was shown to the emperor. Since only people with court rank could meet the emperor, the elephant was famously given a rank for the visit. Crowds lined the roads to see it, and books and prints about it were very popular. A much sadder story is also well known. During World War Two, the elephants at Ueno Zoo in Tokyo were killed, mostly by starvation, because of fears that they might escape during air raids. A children's book about them, 「かわいそうなぞう」, the poor elephants, is read by many Japanese children. Today, elephants are beloved zoo stars. So the elephant was once a celebrity with court rank, and also a sad lesson from the war." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji began as a drawing of an elephant standing on end, trunk at the top, and 「ぞう」 sounds like the zoo where you see one.",
      "Large animals take the counter 「頭|とう」, so two elephants are 「二頭|にとう」, while small animals use 「匹|ひき」 and birds 「羽|わ」.",
      "Only the dots separate 「ぞう」, elephant, from 「そう」, that's right, and the kanji is read 「しょう」 in 「印象|いんしょう」.",
      "An elephant walked from Nagasaki to Edo in 1729 and was given court rank, while the Ueno Zoo elephants are remembered in 「かわいそうなぞう」."
    ],
    ja: "[象|ぞう]はとても{大きい|おおきい}です。",
    en: "Elephants are very big." },
  "からだ|体": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji. On the left is the person radical, the same one in 「休む|やすむ」, to rest. On the right is 「本|ほん」, the kanji for book, which originally means root or origin. A way to remember it: the person part plus the root part, the foundation of a person, which is the body. That is a hook, not the full history. The kanji you see is a simplified form of an older, more complicated one. In this word it has its native reading, 「からだ」. In other words it is read 「たい」, the Chinese-origin reading, as in words for physical education and body weight you will meet later. This word is used in very common phrases. 「体にいい|からだにいい」 means good for your body, healthy. 「体に悪い|からだにわるい」 means bad for your health, using 「悪い|わるい」, bad. And when someone is sick, you might ask 「体はだいじょうぶですか|からだはだいじょうぶですか」, is your body okay. So, a person plus a root, the body you live in." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The body kanji often switches to its Chinese-origin reading, 「たい」, in compounds. 「体重|たいじゅう」 is body weight, with the heavy kanji. 「体温|たいおん」 is body temperature, with the warm kanji. At the doctor, they'll ask you to take your 「体温|たいおん」. 「体育|たいいく」 is physical education, PE at school. And 「体育館|たいいくかん」 is the school gym. 「体調|たいちょう」 is physical condition. 「体調が悪い|たいちょうがわるい」, I'm not feeling well. And there's 「大体|だいたい」, which means roughly or mostly. It's big plus body, read 「たい」 again, the overall shape of things rather than the details. So weight, temperature, PE and condition all borrow the body kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some everyday expressions with this word. At the end of a letter or email, Japanese people often write 「お体に気をつけてください|おからだにきをつけてください」, please take care of yourself. With 「お」, it's polite. In winter, you'll see this a lot. Someone with weak health says 「体が弱い|からだがよわい」, my body is weak. Someone strong is 「体が丈夫|からだがじょうぶ」. If you can't touch your toes, you might say 「体が硬い|からだがかたい」, my body is stiff. Someone flexible is 「体が柔らかい|からだがやわらかい」. To exercise, you can say 「体を動かす|からだをうごかす」, move the body. 「たまには体を動かしましょう|たまにはからだをうごかしましょう」, let's get some exercise once in a while. Mini dialogue: 「最近、体の調子はどう？|さいきん、からだのちょうしはどう？」, how's your health lately? 「まあまあ」, so-so. So take care of your body, keep it flexible, and move it." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick that ties the sound to the meaning. The first part, 「から」, sounds like the particle from, and also like empty. Picture a doctor opening a patient's suitcase, and it's empty, totally 「から」. Then the patient's whole body jumps out of it with a big Da! like a magician's assistant. Empty, then da. 「から」, 「だ」. Or, if you prefer, imagine everything you do comes from your body: you walk from it, talk from it, eat from it. Everything starts 「から」, from, the body. Then look at the kanji, the person standing next to the root. The body is where a person is rooted. So an empty suitcase, and then, da, there's the body." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The person radical plus 「本|ほん」, root, is the foundation of a person, and you'll use it in 「体にいい|からだにいい」, good for your body.",
      "In compounds it switches to 「たい」, as in 「体重|たいじゅう」, body weight, 「体温|たいおん」, body temperature, and 「体育|たいいく」, PE.",
      "Letters often end with 「お体に気をつけてください|おからだにきをつけてください」, and stiff or flexible bodies are 「体が硬い|からだがかたい」 or 「体が柔らかい|からだがやわらかい」.",
      "Picture an empty suitcase, 「から」, that a whole body jumps out of with a big \"Da!\" for 「だ」."
    ],
    ja: "やさいは[体|からだ]にいいです。",
    en: "Vegetables are good for your body." },
  "がいこくご|外国語": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a three kanji word, and it is a great one, because you already know every piece. The first kanji, 「外|がい」, means outside. The second, 「国|こく」, means country, the same kanji as 「国|くに」. Together they make 「外国|がいこく」, foreign country, literally an outside country. Add the third kanji, 「語|ご」, language, and you get the language of an outside country. All the readings here are Chinese-origin. Now the family. You know 「外国人|がいこくじん」, foreigner, a person from an outside country. You know 「日本語|にほんご」, Japanese language, which uses the same 「語|ご」. And English, 「英語|えいご」, uses it too. Look at the left side of 「語|ご」. It is the speech radical, also found in 「言う|いう」, to say, and 「言葉|ことば」, word. So it makes sense that it means language. You can stick 「語|ご」 after almost any country name to name its language. A common question is 「外国語を話しますか|がいこくごをはなしますか」, do you speak a foreign language. So, outside, country, language: the language of another land." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The outside kanji, 「外|がい」, makes a whole group of useful words. 「海外|かいがい」 means overseas, literally outside the sea. 「海外旅行|かいがいりょこう」 is a trip abroad. 「外出|がいしゅつ」 is going out. The office board might say someone is 「外出中|がいしゅつちゅう」, out right now. And here's a great one for a language learner: 「外来語|がいらいご」, loanwords, literally words that came from outside. 「カレー」 and 「アパート」 are 「外来語|がいらいご」. Also, in Japanese schools, the subject where kids study their own language isn't called 「日本語|にほんご」. It's 「国語|こくご」, the national language. 「日本語|にほんご」 is the word usually used when teaching Japanese as a foreign language. So outside the sea, going outside, and words from outside." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the readings of the first two kanji, because they change a lot. 「外|がい」, outside. Its native reading is 「そと」, as in 「外で遊ぶ|そとであそぶ」, play outside. It's also read 「げ」 in 「外科|げか」, surgery, a hospital department. 「国|こく」, country. Its native reading is 「くに」. Watch how it softens to 「ごく」 in 「中国|ちゅうごく」, China, and squeezes to 「こっ」 in 「国会|こっかい」, the national parliament. The shape of 「国|くに」 is an enclosure with 「玉|たま」, a jewel, inside. The border wraps around what's precious. The last kanji, 「語|ご」, has the speech radical on the left, and on its right is 「吾|ご」, an old word for I, which here mostly gives the sound. So outside, country and language each have more than one sound, but here all three use their Chinese-origin readings." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how conversations about languages usually go. 「何語を話しますか|なにごをはなしますか」, what languages do you speak? You can answer 「英語と日本語を話します|えいごとにほんごをはなします」. To ask how to say something in another language, use 「で」: 「これは英語で何と言いますか|これはえいごでなんといいますか」, how do you say this in English? If someone speaks several languages, you might hear 「ペラペラ」, fluent, as in 「彼は英語がペラペラです|かれはえいごがペラペラです」. In Japan, English is taught from elementary school, and many Japanese also study a second foreign language, like Chinese, Korean, or French, at university. And the word for studying abroad is 「留学|りゅうがく」. So ask what language, how to say it, and praise someone as 「ペラペラ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「外|がい」, outside, plus 「国|こく」, country, plus 「語|ご」, language with the speech radical, is the language of an outside country.",
      "「外|がい」 builds 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, 「外出|がいしゅつ」, going out, and 「外来語|がいらいご」, loanwords that came from outside.",
      "Each kanji has more voices, like 「そと」 for outside and 「くに」 for country, but here all three use their Chinese-origin readings.",
      "Ask 「何語を話しますか|なにごをはなしますか」, use 「で」 for how to say it in a language, and praise fluency with 「ペラペラ」."
    ],
    ja: "なにか[外国語|がいこくご]を{話します|はなします}か。",
    en: "Do you speak any foreign language?" },
  "ことば|言葉": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first kanji is the speech kanji from 「言う|いう」, to say. Here it is read 「こと」, a native reading. The second kanji is leaf, with the grass radical on top. On its own it is 「は」, but when it follows another sound, it softens to 「ば」, a common sound change in Japanese compounds. So the word is literally saying leaves. Why leaves? There is a famous line from about eleven hundred years ago, in the preface to an old poetry collection. It says that Japanese poetry takes the human heart as its seed and grows into countless leaves of words. That image of words as leaves growing from the heart is a lovely way to remember it. This word covers both a single word and language in general, depending on context. You might ask 「この言葉はどういう意味ですか|このことばはどういういみですか」, what does this word mean. Compare 「外国語|がいこくご」, foreign language, which uses another kanji with the same speech radical on its left. So, speech plus leaves: words that grow from the heart like leaves." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words that overlap with word and language, and it helps to sort them out. 「言葉|ことば」 is the everyday word. It covers a single word, a phrase, or language in general. 「単語|たんご」 is a vocabulary word, one item on a list. Flashcards are full of 「単語|たんご」. 「言語|げんご」 is the formal, academic word for language, used in linguistics or official settings. 「語|ご」 at the end of a name is a specific language, like 「日本語|にほんご」. There's also 「言葉遣い|ことばづかい」, the way someone uses words, their manner of speaking. A parent might scold a child, 「言葉遣いに気をつけて|ことばづかいにきをつけて」, watch your language. So 「言葉|ことば」 is the warm everyday word, 「単語|たんご」 is for flashcards, and 「言語|げんご」 is for textbooks." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word appears in many set phrases. 「言葉が通じない|ことばがつうじない」 means we can't understand each other's language. You might say it about a trip abroad. 「言葉にできない|ことばにできない」 means I can't put it into words, often about a beautiful view or a strong feeling. 「お礼の言葉|おれいのことば」 is words of thanks, said at speeches and ceremonies. Japanese also separates 「話し言葉|はなしことば」, spoken language, from 「書き言葉|かきことば」, written language. The two are quite different in Japanese, and you'll notice it as you read more. And a children's game, 「しりとり」, is played with this word in mind. You say a word, and the next person says a word that starts with your last sound. So you can be lost for words, give words of thanks, or play a word game." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji here have other readings you'll meet. The speech kanji, 「言|げん」, reads 「いう」 as a verb, 「げん」 in words like 「言語|げんご」, language, and 「ごん」 in 「伝言|でんごん」, a message to pass on. You can spot a 「口|くち」, mouth, at the bottom of it. The leaf kanji, 「葉|は」, has its native reading 「は」. It also reads 「よう」, as in 「紅葉|こうよう」, autumn leaves turning red. Here's a nice detail: the leaves on trees are 「葉っぱ|はっぱ」 in casual speech. In 「言葉|ことば」 the leaf softens to 「ば」, a sound change that also happens in 「花火|はなび」, where the 「ひ」 of fire softens to 「び」. So the speech kanji has three voices, and the leaf can soften too." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Speech plus leaf, softened to 「ば」, comes from an old poetry preface describing words growing from the heart like countless leaves.",
      "「言葉|ことば」 is the warm everyday word, 「単語|たんご」 is a vocabulary item for flashcards, and 「言語|げんご」 is the formal word for language.",
      "Set phrases include 「言葉が通じない|ことばがつうじない」, we can't understand each other, and 「言葉にできない|ことばにできない」, I can't put it into words.",
      "The speech kanji has three voices, 「いう」, 「げん」, and 「ごん」, and the leaf 「は」 softens to 「ば」 just like fire does in 「花火|はなび」."
    ],
    ja: "この[言葉|ことば]は{英語|えいご}で{何|なん}ですか。",
    en: "What is this word in English?" },
  "ぶんぽう|文法": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first kanji, 「文|ぶん」, means writing, a sentence, or text. You know it from 「文化|ぶんか」, culture, and you also have 「ぶんがく」, literature, which uses the same kanji. The second kanji, 「法|ほう」, means law, rule, or method. It has the water radical on its left. Both readings are Chinese-origin. Put them together and you get the rules of sentences: grammar. Now listen to the sound. 「法|ほう」 is normally 「ほう」, but after the 「ん」 of 「ぶん」, it changes to 「ぽう」. Japanese often turns an h sound into a p sound after 「ん」 or a small pause. You can hear the same thing in counters, like 「一本|いっぽん」, where the h sound of 「本|ほん」 becomes a p. So if you ever hear 「ぽう」 in the middle of a word, think of 「ほう」 in disguise. You might say 「文法はむずかしいです|ぶんぽうはむずかしいです」, grammar is difficult. A way to remember it: grammar is the law of writing. So, sentence plus rule, with the sound hardened to 「ぽう」 after 「ん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「文|ぶん」, writing or sentence, builds some words you'll use as a student. 「作文|さくぶん」 is a composition, an essay, literally making sentences. Japanese classes often assign one. 「文|ぶん」 alone means a sentence. 「文を作る|ぶんをつくる」, make a sentence. 「注文|ちゅうもん」 is an order, at a restaurant or a shop. 「注文をお願いします|ちゅうもんをおねがいします」, I'd like to order. And look at 「文字|もじ」, letters or characters. Here the kanji is read 「も」, an unusual reading you'll see in few other places. The second kanji, 「法|ほう」, appears in 「方法|ほうほう」, method, and 「法律|ほうりつ」, law. So grammar sits among composition, sentence, order and characters." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you study Japanese, it helps to know the grammar words themselves. A noun is a 「名詞|めいし」, a name word. A verb is a 「動詞|どうし」, a moving word. An adjective is a 「形容詞|けいようし」. A particle is a 「助詞|じょし」, a helper word. Notice they all end in 「詞|し」, which means word or part of speech. Some useful sentences: 「この文法が分かりません|このぶんぽうがわかりません」, I don't understand this grammar. 「文法の間違いを直してください|ぶんぽうのまちがいをなおしてください」, please correct my grammar mistakes. A grammar book is a 「文法書|ぶんぽうしょ」. A tip: Japanese teachers often say grammar is less important than speaking. But knowing a few grammar terms makes asking questions much easier. So noun, verb, adjective and particle, all ending in the word kanji." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「文|ぶん」, sentence, plus 「法|ほう」, rule, makes the law of writing, with 「ほう」 hardening to 「ぽう」 after the 「ん」.",
      "「文|ぶん」 builds student words like 「作文|さくぶん」, a composition, and 「注文|ちゅうもん」, an order, while 「法|ほう」 gives 「方法|ほうほう」, method.",
      "The parts of speech all end in 「詞|し」, like 「名詞|めいし」, noun, and 「動詞|どうし」, verb, which makes asking grammar questions easier."
    ],
    ja: "{日本語|にほんご}の[文法|ぶんぽう]はむずかしいです。",
    en: "Japanese grammar is difficult." },
  "アプリ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is short for 「アプリケーション」, from the English word application. Japanese loves to take long loanwords and cut them down to the first few sounds. Here the long word keeps only its first three beats. You already know several words made the same way. 「パソコン」 is short for personal computer, cutting both words. 「エアコン」 is short for air conditioner. And 「スーパー」 is short for supermarket. Once you notice the pattern, many loanwords become easier to guess. Watch the sounds too. The p in application gets a vowel, becoming 「プ」, and the l becomes the Japanese r sound, giving 「リ」. In everyday Japanese this word means a phone or computer app. You download one, use one, delete one. People also say 「スマホ」 for smartphone, which is another chopped word, from smart and phone. A useful phrase is 「このアプリはべんりです」, this app is convenient. A way to remember it: Japanese takes the first bite of a long English word and keeps it. So, application cut down to three beats." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the verbs that go with this word in daily life. To get an app, you can say 「アプリをダウンロードする」, download it, or more casually, 「アプリを入れる|アプリをいれる」, put it in. That second one is very common in speech. To delete one, say 「アプリを消す|アプリをけす」, erase it, or 「アプリを削除する|アプリをさくじょする」, a slightly more technical word. To open one, use 「開く|ひらく」. In Japan, the most used messaging app is LINE. People ask 「ライン、やってる？」, are you on it? and exchange contacts by scanning a code. A mini dialogue: 「このアプリ、知ってる？|このアプリ、しってる？」, do you know this app? 「ううん、何のアプリ？|ううん、なんのアプリ？」, no, what kind of app is it? Asking 「何のアプリ|なんのアプリ」 is a handy pattern for any what kind of question. So you put apps in, erase them, and open them." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Phone vocabulary in Japanese is full of chopped words like this one. 「スマホ」 is a smartphone. 「ケータイ」, often written in katakana, is short for 「携帯電話|けいたいでんわ」, a portable phone, and it's still used for any mobile phone. Then there's a fun one: 「ガラケー」, the old flip phone. It's short for Galapagos phone, because Japanese flip phones developed in isolation, with special features no other country had, like animals evolving on the Galapagos Islands. 「充電|じゅうでん」 means charging the battery. 「充電が切れた|じゅうでんがきれた」, my battery died. And Wi-Fi is said 「ワイファイ」. So the word for app sits with 「スマホ」, 「ケータイ」 and the island-evolved 「ガラケー」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「アプリケーション」 cut to its first three beats, the same chopping that gives 「パソコン」, 「エアコン」, and 「スマホ」.",
      "Get an app with 「アプリを入れる|アプリをいれる」, delete it with 「アプリを消す|アプリをけす」, and ask what kind with 「何のアプリ|なんのアプリ」.",
      "It sits among chopped phone words like 「スマホ」, 「ケータイ」, and 「ガラケー」, the Galapagos flip phone that evolved in isolation."
    ],
    ja: "この[アプリ]はべんりです。",
    en: "This app is convenient." },
  "アパート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is from the English word apartment, cut short. The ending ment is dropped, and the ar sound turns into a long vowel, written with the long dash. You already know a perfect twin: 「デパート」, department store, is from department, cut in exactly the same place. A way to remember it: 「アパート」 and 「デパート」 are both English words with the ment chopped off. In Japan, though, this word has a specific image. It usually means a small, cheaper building of two or three floors, often made of wood or light steel, with outdoor stairs and thin walls. Many students and young workers live in one. A bigger, sturdier concrete building with many floors is called 「マンション」, from this same lesson. So Japanese splits English apartment into two words by building type. To say you live in one, use 「すむ」: 「アパートにすんでいます」. When you rent one, you deal with the 「大家さん|おおやさん」, landlord. So, apartment with its tail cut off, meaning a small, simple building." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Renting a place in Japan comes with its own vocabulary. Living alone is 「一人暮らし|ひとりぐらし」. Many students start this in a small apartment. Monthly rent is 「家賃|やちん」. Then come the move-in costs. 「敷金|しききん」 is a deposit, partly returned when you leave. 「礼金|れいきん」, key money, is a gift to the landlord that you never get back. Not every place charges it nowadays, but it surprises many foreigners. Floor plans use letters and numbers. A one-room place with a small kitchen is a one K, and a bigger one with a living room, dining room and kitchen is a one LDK. And apartments often have a separate toilet and bath. Listings point out if they're together or not. So in Japan you pay rent, a deposit, and sometimes key money." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for this word. Picture a young student moving out of their parents' home for the first time. They're finally living apart from the family. They arrive at a small two-story wooden building, climb the outdoor metal stairs, and open a tiny door. The walls are so thin they can hear the neighbor snoring. They shout, I'm living a-part! And the sound is 「アパート」. To keep it apart from its twin, imagine the student's mother at the 「デパート」 department store downtown, buying them a rice cooker as a housewarming gift. Both words lost their ending, and both rhyme. One is where you live apart, the other is where you shop. So: living apart, in a small wooden building." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Apartment loses its ment just like 「デパート」, and in Japan it means a small, cheaper two or three floor building, unlike the sturdier 「マンション」.",
      "Renting means monthly 「家賃|やちん」, a 「敷金|しききん」 deposit, and sometimes 「礼金|れいきん」, key money you never get back.",
      "Picture a student in a thin-walled wooden building shouting \"I'm living a-part!\" while mom shops at the rhyming 「デパート」."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}は[アパート]にすんでいます。",
    en: "I live in an apartment." },
  "マンション|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word mansion, but do not picture a huge house with a pool. In Japanese it means a larger, sturdier apartment building, usually made of reinforced concrete, with many floors, an elevator, and often an automatic locked entrance. Individual units can be rented or bought, so it also covers what English calls a condominium. The grand sounding name was a selling point when these newer, nicer buildings first appeared. Watch the sounds. The ending sion becomes 「ション」, the same way station becomes 「ステーション」. Compare it with 「アパート」, from this same lesson. That one is smaller, cheaper, often wood, with fewer floors. If you tell a Japanese friend you live in a mansion, they will think of a modern apartment, not a palace. A way to remember it: in Japan, a mansion is just a nice apartment block. To say you live in one, use 「すむ」, like 「マンションにすんでいます」. So, an English word for a grand house, scaled down to mean a solid apartment building." },
      { title: "Kinds of homes", text: "Japanese homes come in several types, each with its own word. 「一戸建て|いっこだて」 is a detached house, a single home on its own land. Many families dream of owning one. 「マンション」 is a concrete apartment building, and 「アパート」 is a smaller, simpler one. 「団地|だんち」 is a large public housing complex, rows of identical apartment blocks built mainly in the postwar decades. 「寮|りょう」 is a dormitory, for students or company employees. And 「シェアハウス」, from share house, is a shared home where young people rent rooms and share a kitchen. To talk about where you live, use 「住む|すむ」 with 「に」. In the progressive, 「マンションに住んでいます|マンションにすんでいます」. So from a 「一戸建て|いっこだて」 to a dorm, Japanese has a word for each home." },
      { title: "False friends", text: "This word is a classic false friend, an English word that changed meaning in Japan, and it has company. 「スマート」 in Japanese often means slim, not clever. Calling someone 「スマート」 is a compliment on their figure. 「クレーム」, from claim, means a complaint. 「ナイーブ」, from naive, usually means sensitive or delicate, not gullible. 「サイン」 means a signature or autograph, not a street sign. And this word, from mansion, means a solid apartment building, not a palace. These can trip up both sides. A Japanese person might tell you in English they live in a mansion, and they mean a nice condo. So when an English word shows up in Japanese, double-check its meaning before you trust it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Not a grand house, it's a sturdy concrete apartment building or condo, the bigger, nicer counterpart to the small 「アパート」.",
      "Homes range from the detached 「一戸建て|いっこだて」 to 「マンション」, 「アパート」, the public 「団地|だんち」, and the dorm, 「寮|りょう」.",
      "It's a classic false friend, like 「スマート」 meaning slim and 「クレーム」 meaning a complaint, so double-check English words in Japanese."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}は[マンション]にすんでいます。",
    en: "My friend lives in a condominium." },
  "くうこう|空港": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「空|くう」, means sky or air. On its own, sky is 「そら」, the native reading. The second, 「港|こう」, means port or harbor. Look at its left side: the water radical, because a harbor is where ships meet the water. On its own, harbor is 「みなと」. Put them together: a sky port, a harbor for planes instead of ships. English airport works the very same way, air plus port. Now the family. The same first kanji appears in 「空手|からて」, karate, but there it is read 「から」 and means empty. Sky and empty are linked in the kanji: the sky is the big empty space above you. And you have 「カラオケ」, empty orchestra, which uses that same 「から」, written in katakana. To get to the airport, you might take a train or bus, and then board a 「飛行機|ひこうき」, airplane. A useful phrase is 「空港まで行きます|くうこうまでいきます」, I'm going as far as the airport. So, sky plus harbor: a port for the sky." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's some vocabulary for a trip to the airport. Departure is 「出発|しゅっぱつ」, and arrival is 「到着|とうちゃく」. You'll see these on the big boards. To board a plane is 「飛行機に乗る|ひこうきにのる」. Picking someone up is 「迎えに行く|むかえにいく」. 「空港まで迎えに行きます|くうこうまでむかえにいきます」, I'll come pick you up at the airport. Seeing someone off is 「見送る|みおくる」. Japanese families often see off travelers at the airport, waving until the very end. The big airports near Tokyo are 「成田|なりた」 and 「羽田|はねだ」. Near Osaka, there's Kansai International Airport, built on an artificial island in the sea. Souvenirs bought at the airport are 「お土産|おみやげ」, and many travelers buy them there at the last minute. So you depart, arrive, pick people up and see them off." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The harbor kanji, 「港|こう」, has its own family. On its own with the native reading, it's 「みなと」, a harbor or port. A port town is a 「港町|みなとまち」. Yokohama and Kobe are famous port cities, with historic foreign districts. The kanji also appears in a place name: Hong Kong, written with the fragrant kanji and the harbor kanji, and read 「ホンコン」, from the Chinese pronunciation. Big international airports often have a longer name with 「国際|こくさい」, international, as in 「国際空港|こくさいくうこう」. And notice how this word pairs with others about travel: 「駅|えき」, station, and 「バス停|バスてい」, bus stop. Each is the place where a vehicle stops. So the sky harbor joins the sea harbor, the train station and the bus stop." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「空|くう」, sky, plus 「港|こう」, harbor with the water radical, makes a sky port, built exactly like English air plus port.",
      "Boards show 「出発|しゅっぱつ」 and 「到着|とうちゃく」, and you pick people up with 「迎えに行く|むかえにいく」 or see them off with 「見送る|みおくる」.",
      "Alone, the harbor kanji is 「みなと」, as in 「港町|みなとまち」, a port town, and big airports add 「国際|こくさい」, international."
    ],
    ja: "[空港|くうこう]まで{車|くるま}で{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I go to the airport by car." },
  "みせ|店": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji. The outer part, a dot and a line with a long stroke sweeping down on the left, is a radical that shows a building with a roof. Shops are buildings, so that fits. Here the word has its native reading, 「みせ」. Some say the word comes from the verb 「見せる|みせる」, to show, because a shop shows its goods to people passing by. That link is widely given, and it makes a great hook either way: a shop is where things are shown. The kanji's Chinese-origin reading is 「てん」, and you already know it at the end of 「喫茶店|きっさてん」, cafe, literally a tea drinking shop. Many shop words end in that 「てん」. Another way to name a shop is with 「屋|や」, as in 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore. When you walk into a Japanese shop, staff will greet you with 「いらっしゃいませ」, welcome. You do not need to answer, a small nod is fine. A useful sentence is 「この店はやすいです|このみせはやすいです」, this store is cheap. So, a building with a roof, where goods are shown." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The shop kanji's Chinese-origin reading, 「てん」, is everywhere. 「店員|てんいん」 is a store clerk, a shop member. 「書店|しょてん」 is a bookstore, a more formal word for 「本屋|ほんや」. 「売店|ばいてん」 is a kiosk or small stand, like the ones on station platforms. 「本店|ほんてん」 is the main store, and 「支店|してん」 is a branch. 「閉店|へいてん」 means closing time, or a store closing for good. You'll see it on signs. And remember the 「屋|や」 way of naming shops, as in 「本屋|ほんや」 and 「パン屋|パンや」. It attaches to what's sold, and adding 「さん」, like 「パン屋さん|パンやさん」, sounds friendly and can even mean the person who runs the shop. So the clerk, the kiosk, and the branch all come from one shop kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how shopping usually goes in Japan. When you walk in, staff say 「いらっしゃいませ」. To ask for something: 「すみません、傘はありますか|すみません、かさはありますか」, excuse me, do you have umbrellas? To pay, say 「お会計お願いします|おかいけいおねがいします」, the check, please. At the register you might be asked 「袋はいりますか|ふくろはいりますか」, do you need a bag? Bags usually cost a few yen now, so you can say 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」, I'm fine. People often say 「お店|おみせ」 with 「お」 to sound polite and gentle. For opening and closing times, use 「開く|あく」 and 「閉まる|しまる」. 「店は何時に閉まりますか|みせはなんじにしまりますか」, what time does the shop close? So greet, ask, pay, and decline the bag." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The roof radical shows a building, and the native word is often linked to 「見せる|みせる」, to show, since a shop shows its goods.",
      "The reading 「てん」 gives 「店員|てんいん」, a clerk, 「売店|ばいてん」, a kiosk, and 「支店|してん」, a branch, while 「屋|や」 names shops by what they sell.",
      "Staff greet you with 「いらっしゃいませ」, you can decline a bag with 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」, and 「お店|おみせ」 sounds gentle and polite."
    ],
    ja: "この[店|みせ]はやすいです。",
    en: "This store is cheap." },
  "ぶっか|物価": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「物|ぶつ」, is the thing kanji. You know it with its native reading 「もの」 in 「食べ物|たべもの」 and 「着物|きもの」. With the Chinese-origin reading it shows up in 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, zoo, the garden of moving things, animals. The second kanji, 「価|か」, means value or price. It has the person radical on the left. A way to picture it: people decide what things are worth. Put them together: the price of things. Listen to the sound change. 「ぶつ」 plus 「か」 should be 「ぶつか」, but the 「つ」 squeezes into a small pause, giving 「ぶっか」. That is the same squeeze you hear in 「学校|がっこう」. Now the meaning. This is not the price of one item. It is the general level of prices in a place, the cost of living. So you say a city's prices are high, 「物価が高い|ぶっかがたかい」, or low, 「物価が安い|ぶっかがやすい」. Tokyo is famous for high prices, while the countryside is cheaper. So, things plus value: how much things cost in general." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word comes with its own verbs and partners. Prices go up: 「物価が上がる|ぶっかがあがる」. They go down: 「物価が下がる|ぶっかがさがる」. You'll hear these on the news a lot. It's important to separate this word from 「値段|ねだん」. 「値段|ねだん」 is the price of one item, like a coffee. This word is the price level of everything in a place. So you'd say 「このコーヒーの値段|このコーヒーのねだん」, never this word for one coffee. Also useful is 「生活費|せいかつひ」, cost of living expenses. A mini dialogue: 「東京は物価が高いですね|とうきょうはぶっかがたかいですね」, prices are high in Tokyo, aren't they? 「そうですね。でも、田舎は安いですよ|そうですね。でも、いなかはやすいですよ」, yes, but the countryside is cheap. So prices rise and fall, but one item has a 「値段|ねだん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The thing kanji, 「物|もの」, has three readings, and you'll meet all of them. 「もの」, the native reading, as in 「建物|たてもの」, a building, a built thing. 「ぶつ」, as in 「動物|どうぶつ」, animal, and squeezed to 「ぶっ」 in this word. And 「もつ」, in 「荷物|にもつ」, luggage, baggage. That one surprises many learners. There's even a special reading for a whole word: 「果物|くだもの」, fruit, where the reading belongs to the pair. The second kanji, 「価|か」, value, also appears in 「価格|かかく」, price, a formal word you'll see in stores and ads. So the thing kanji is 「もの」, 「ぶつ」, 「もつ」, and in fruit, something special." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「物|ぶつ」, things, plus 「価|か」, value, squeezed to 「ぶっか」, is the general level of prices in a place, as in 「物価が高い|ぶっかがたかい」.",
      "Prices rise with 「物価が上がる|ぶっかがあがる」, but a single item's price is its 「値段|ねだん」, never this word.",
      "「物|もの」 has three readings, 「もの」, 「ぶつ」, and 「もつ」, plus the special whole-word reading in 「果物|くだもの」."
    ],
    ja: "{東京|とうきょう}は[物価|ぶっか]が{高い|たかい}です。",
    en: "Prices are high in Tokyo." },
  "こうこく|広告": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「広|こう」, means wide or broad. You know it with its native reading in 「広い|ひろい」, wide, spacious. Its outer part is the same roof radical you see in 「店|みせ」, a building with a roof. The second kanji, 「告|こく」, means to announce or tell. It has 「口|くち」, mouth, at the bottom, which fits, since you announce things with your mouth. Put them together: to announce widely. An advertisement spreads a message out to as many people as possible. That is a nice clean picture. Listen to the rhythm too: two very similar sounds, 「こう」 and 「こく」, back to back. A way to remember it: an ad is a loud mouth that speaks wide. You will see this word for ads in newspapers, magazines, on trains, and online. Trains in Japan are famous for being packed with hanging paper ads. You might say 「新聞で広告を見ました|しんぶんでこうこくをみました」, I saw an ad in the newspaper. Another common word for a TV ad is 「コマーシャル」, from English commercial, or just 「シーエム」. So, wide plus announce: telling everyone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each half of this word leads to a family of its own. The first kanji, read 「ひろ」 natively, gives you 「広い|ひろい」, wide, and 「広場|ひろば」, an open square or plaza, literally a wide place. It is also in the city name 「広島|ひろしま」, wide island. The second kanji, read 「こく」, is all about telling. 「報告|ほうこく」 is a report, the kind you give your boss after a job. And there is a famous one in Japanese romance: 「告白|こくはく」, a confession, especially telling someone you love them. In Japan, couples often start dating after one person makes a clear 「告白|こくはく」. So the same telling kanji appears in an ad, a work report, and a love confession. Each one is a message you deliberately put out there. That is the family: wide places, and things you announce." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Ads are everywhere in Japan, and this word comes with some handy partners. To place an ad is 「広告を出す|こうこくをだす」, literally to put out an ad. A newspaper ad is 「新聞広告|しんぶんこうこく」. On trains you will see ads hanging from the ceiling over the aisle. Those have their own name, 「中吊り広告|なかづりこうこく」, middle hanging ads. Online, pop-up ads and banner ads are also called by this word, and video sites tell you to skip the ad with a button marked 「広告をスキップ|こうこくをスキップ」. On a TV show, people talk about the ad break as 「シーエム」, but in business and writing, this kanji word is the standard, neutral term. A company might say, 「雑誌に広告を出しました|ざっしにこうこくをだしました」, we put an ad in a magazine. So when you mean the general idea of advertising, this is your word." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on sound. The word sounds a bit like coke, coke. Picture a giant billboard as wide as a whole building. On it is a huge cartoon mouth, and the mouth is shouting coke, coke, coke at everyone passing by. People on the street cover their ears, but they all end up buying a cola anyway. That is advertising: a wide space, a loud mouth, and a message repeated until you remember it. The two kanji fit the picture too. The first one means wide, like the billboard. The second one has a mouth at the bottom, like the shouting cartoon mouth. Next time you see an ad, hear that mouth shouting coke, coke. A wide billboard with a shouting mouth: that is an ad." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「広|こう」 means wide, as in 「広い|ひろい」, and 「告|こく」 means announce, with a mouth at the bottom. Together they mean announcing widely, which is exactly what an ad does.",
      "The first kanji gives wide places like 「広場|ひろば」 and 「広島|ひろしま」, while the second is about telling, as in 「報告|ほうこく」, a report, and 「告白|こくはく」, a love confession.",
      "To place an ad is 「広告を出す|こうこくをだす」, and this is the standard neutral word for advertising in business and writing, while TV ad breaks are casually called 「シーエム」.",
      "It sounds like coke, coke: picture a building-wide billboard with a huge cartoon mouth shouting coke at everyone, matching the wide kanji and the mouth kanji."
    ],
    ja: "{新聞|しんぶん}で[広告|こうこく]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw an advertisement in the newspaper." },
  "ぼしゅう|募集": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「募|ぼ」, means to call for, to invite people to come forward. Look at the bottom of it: it is 「力|ちから」, power or effort. The second kanji, 「集|しゅう」, means to gather. This one has a lovely real picture. The top part is a bird, and the bottom part is 「木|き」, tree. Birds gathering on a tree. You already know this kanji in 「集める|あつめる」, to collect, with its native reading. Put them together: calling people in and gathering them, which is recruitment. In daily life, you see this word on signs in shop windows, like 「アルバイト募集|アルバイトぼしゅう」, part-timers wanted. It is also used for clubs looking for members, companies hiring staff, and contests looking for entries. It works as a noun and as a verb with 「する」, as in 「募集しています|ぼしゅうしています」, they are recruiting. A way to remember it: picture a manager calling out with all their power, and new workers flocking in like birds to a tree. So, call for plus gather: bringing people in." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word has a perfect partner. When a company is recruiting, it is doing 「募集|ぼしゅう」. When you answer that call and apply, you do 「応募する|おうぼする」. Notice the shared first kanji, the one that means to call for. One side calls out, the other side responds. You will also see that kanji in 「募金|ぼきん」, collecting money for charity. On busy streets in Japan, volunteers stand with boxes asking for donations, and that is 「募金|ぼきん」, calling for money. The second kanji, gather, appears in 「集める|あつめる」, to collect, and 「集まる|あつまる」, to come together. A school trip will have a 「集合時間|しゅうごうじかん」, the time everyone must gather. So this family splits nicely: calling out to people, and people gathering in. Recruiting does both." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Walk past a convenience store or café in Japan and look at the door. You will often see a paper sign saying 「スタッフ募集中|スタッフぼしゅうちゅう」, staff wanted now. That ending, 「中|ちゅう」, means in progress, so the recruiting is happening right now. When the spots are full, the sign may change to 「募集終了|ぼしゅうしゅうりょう」, recruiting closed. The word works for people, but also for ideas and entries. A city might call for photos for a contest, or a TV show might ask for viewer questions. As a verb, it takes 「を」 for whatever is being sought, as in 「アルバイトを募集しています|アルバイトをぼしゅうしています」. It sounds a little official, like a notice, so in casual talk a friend might just say they are looking for people. When you see this word on a door, think: they want someone, maybe you." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. The word sounds a bit like boss shoe. Picture a cranky store boss standing outside his shop, holding up one glass slipper like in Cinderella. Above him hangs a sign saying help wanted. Everyone who wants the job has to try on the boss shoe. A long line of hopeful workers gathers on the sidewalk, like birds flocking to a tree. Whoever's foot fits gets hired on the spot. That is recruiting: the boss calls out with all his power, and people gather. And remember the kanji too. The first one has power at the bottom, the boss calling out. The second one is birds on a tree, the crowd gathering. Boss shoe: calling for workers and gathering them in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「募|ぼ」 means to call for, with power at the bottom, and 「集|しゅう」 is birds gathering on a tree. Calling people in and gathering them is recruitment.",
      "Its partner is 「応募する|おうぼする」, to apply, sharing the call-for kanji, which also appears in 「募金|ぼきん」, collecting donations, while the gather kanji gives 「集める|あつめる」 and 「集まる|あつまる」.",
      "Look for door signs like 「スタッフ募集中|スタッフぼしゅうちゅう」, staff wanted now, which change to 「募集終了|ぼしゅうしゅうりょう」 when recruiting closes, and it takes 「を」 for whatever is sought.",
      "It sounds like boss shoe: a cranky boss holds up a glass slipper under a help wanted sign, and job seekers flock like birds to a tree to try it on."
    ],
    ja: "あの{店|みせ}はアルバイトを[募集|ぼしゅう]しています。",
    en: "That shop is recruiting part-time workers." },
  "やくそく|約束": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「約|やく」, means an agreement or a promise. It has the thread radical on the left, like 「紅茶|こうちゃ」 and 「手紙|てがみ」. A promise is something that ties people together, like thread. The second kanji, 「束|そく」, means a bundle. Its shape is a tree, 「木|き」, with a band around its middle, sticks tied together into a bundle. Put them together: an agreement tied up tight. You already know the first kanji from 「予約|よやく」, reservation. A reservation is an agreement made in advance. So both words are about tying down a plan. Now the meaning. This word covers two English ideas: a promise and an appointment, a plan to meet. 「約束があります|やくそくがあります」 means I have plans, I'm meeting someone. Keeping a promise is 「約束を守る|やくそくをまもる」, literally guarding it, also in this lesson. Japanese kids seal promises with a pinky swear song. So, agreement plus bundle: a promise tied up tight between two people." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese children seal a promise with a pinky swear, and it comes with a surprisingly scary little song. Two kids hook little fingers and chant 「ゆびきりげんまん」. The first part means cutting fingers. Then comes the threat: 「うそついたら はりせんぼん のーます」, if you lie, you have to swallow a thousand needles. It ends with 「ゆびきった」, fingers cut, and the fingers let go. Kids sing it cheerfully, without thinking about the meaning at all. Adults still say 「ゆびきり」 jokingly when making a promise with a friend. Some people trace the custom back to old Edo period practices, but the details are debated, so just enjoy the song. When a Japanese friend holds out a pinky, you know what is coming: a promise you had better keep." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a whole set of verb partners. To make a promise is 「約束する|やくそくする」. To keep it is 「約束を守る|やくそくをまもる」. To break it is 「約束を破る|やくそくをやぶる」, using the verb for tearing something. The meeting time you agreed on is 「約束の時間|やくそくのじかん」. Here is a common mix up for English speakers. A plan in general is 「予定|よてい」. Saying 「予定があります|よていがあります」 just means I'm busy. Saying 「約束があります|やくそくがあります」 means I have plans with someone else, another person is involved. Here is a mini dialogue. Someone asks, 「今晩、飲みに行きませんか|こんばん、のみにいきませんか」, want to go for a drink tonight? You answer, 「すみません、友だちと約束があるんです|すみません、ともだちとやくそくがあるんです」, sorry, I'm meeting a friend. So, 「予定|よてい」 is your calendar, and this word is a date with another person." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's go deeper into both kanji, because each leads somewhere new. The first one, read 「やく」, has a second big job. It means approximately. Before a number, you will see 「約一時間|やくいちじかん」, about one hour, or 「約百人|やくひゃくにん」, about a hundred people. That use is very common in news and in writing. The connection is the idea of tying things down roughly, bundling a number into a round figure. The second kanji, the bundle, has a native reading too: 「たば」. A bouquet of flowers is 「花束|はなたば」, a flower bundle. So the same shape, sticks tied around the middle of a tree, can be a bundle of flowers or the bond of a promise. Next time you see a bouquet, think of a promise tied up with string." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「約|やく」 has the thread radical and means agreement, and 「束|そく」 is a tree with a band around it, a bundle. So it is an agreement tied up tight, covering both a promise and an appointment.",
      "Japanese kids seal promises by hooking pinkies and singing 「ゆびきりげんまん」, which threatens that a liar must swallow a thousand needles, and adults still jokingly say 「ゆびきり」.",
      "Make one with 「約束する|やくそくする」, keep it with 「約束を守る|やくそくをまもる」, and break it with 「約束を破る|やくそくをやぶる」. Unlike 「予定|よてい」, which is just your calendar, it means plans involving another person.",
      "The first kanji also means approximately, as in 「約一時間|やくいちじかん」, about one hour, and the bundle kanji read 「たば」 gives 「花束|はなたば」, a bouquet."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[約束|やくそく]があります。",
    en: "I have an appointment with a friend." },
  "うれしい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, and it is almost always written in hiragana. It is the bright, happy feeling you get when something good happens to you. A present, a good test score, a message from a friend, good news. It is about a moment, a reaction. Compare 「幸せ|しあわせ」, happy, also in this lesson, which is a deeper, lasting happiness about your life. You feel 「うれしい」 when you get a gift. You feel 「幸せ|しあわせ」 when your life is good. Here is a common mistake. Feeling words like this one are used for your own feelings. You can say 「うれしいです」, I am glad. But saying another person is glad sounds odd, because you cannot see inside their heart. For others, Japanese uses a form like 「うれしそう」, looks glad, which you meet in this same lesson. The opposite feeling is 「悲しい|かなしい」, sad. A way to remember it: picture shouting hooray when good news comes, and hear the 「うれ」 hiding in hooray. So, a quick burst of gladness, for your own feelings." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This word is usually written in hiragana, but it does have a kanji, and you will see it in novels, signs, and some messages. The kanji has two parts. On the left is 「女|おんな」, woman, the same shape that sits inside 「好き|すき」, to like. On the right is a kanji meaning joy, which you will meet in 「喜ぶ|よろこぶ」, to be delighted. So the written form combines woman and joy. Why the woman part is there is not something to build a story on, so just remember the shapes. The joy part is the useful link. 「喜ぶ|よろこぶ」 is the verb version of gladness, used to describe how someone reacts, as in 「母が喜びました|ははがよろこびました」, my mother was delighted. So the hidden kanji connects this little adjective to its bigger family of joy." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an 「い」 adjective, and it conjugates normally. Not glad is 「うれしくない」. Was glad is 「うれしかった」. To give the reason for your gladness, use the 「て」 form of a verb right before it. 「会えてうれしいです|あえてうれしいです」 means I'm glad I could see you. 「手紙をもらってうれしかったです|てがみをもらってうれしかったです」, I was happy to get the letter. That pattern, reason in 「て」 form, then the feeling, works for many emotion words. Now, a contrast. 「楽しい|たのしい」 means fun, enjoyable, and it describes an activity while you are in it, like a party. This word is the reaction to something that happens to you, like getting good news. A party is 「楽しい|たのしい」, and being invited to it makes you 「うれしい」. So, reason in 「て」 form, and then the gladness." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this word constantly in Japan, often as a short exclamation. Someone hands you a gift, and you say 「わあ、うれしい！」, oh, how nice! It is warm and sincere, and it shows the giver that their gesture landed. It is also a lovely reply to compliments. If someone praises your Japanese, a humble 「いえいえ」 is standard, but adding 「そう言ってもらえるとうれしいです|そういってもらえるとうれしいです」, I'm glad to hear you say that, sounds natural and gracious. In business emails, you will see a polite request form built from this word, meaning it would make me glad if you could, which is a soft way of asking a favor. That shows how central it is: gladness becomes a polite way to ask. So, use it freely for gifts, kind words, and good news." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the quick, bright gladness when something good happens to you, unlike the deeper lasting 「幸せ|しあわせ」. Use it for your own feelings, and 「うれしそう」 for others.",
      "Its rare kanji combines woman and a joy part, and that joy part links it to 「喜ぶ|よろこぶ」, the verb for being delighted.",
      "Give the reason with a 「て」 form first, as in 「会えてうれしいです|あえてうれしいです」. It is a reaction to what happens to you, while 「楽しい|たのしい」 describes a fun activity itself.",
      "Say 「わあ、うれしい！」 when handed a gift, and use it to reply graciously to compliments, since it warmly shows the giver their gesture landed."
    ],
    ja: "プレゼントをもらって、[うれしい]です。",
    en: "I got a present, so I'm glad." },
  "かなしい|悲しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji, because it tells a clear story. At the bottom is 「心|こころ」, heart. On top is a part that means not or wrong. A heart that is not right: sadness. That bottom heart is a real and useful pattern. Many feeling kanji have a heart at the bottom or on the left. You know 「心配する|しんぱいする」, to worry, which starts with the heart kanji itself. And in 「感動する|かんどうする」, to be moved, from this same lesson, the first kanji also has a heart at the bottom. Here the reading is native, 「かな」, with 「しい」 written in hiragana, the ending many feeling adjectives have, like 「うれしい」 and 「さびしい」. Just like 「うれしい」, it describes your own feelings directly. For someone else, you say they look sad, 「悲しそう|かなしそう」. This is the opposite of 「うれしい」, glad. A way to remember it: when something sad happens, your heart is not right. So, a heart marked wrong: sadness." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The top part of this kanji is a kanji of its own, read 「ひ」, meaning not or wrong. And that gives this kanji its Chinese-origin reading, 「ひ」, too. You will meet that reading in a few bigger words. 「悲劇|ひげき」 is a tragedy, like a sad play. 「悲鳴|ひめい」 is a scream, a cry of shock or pain. So when you see this kanji read 「ひ」, think of a dramatic sad word. When it is read 「かな」 with 「しい」, it is the everyday adjective. That top part is also handy on its own. It shows up as a prefix meaning non or un, as in 「非常口|ひじょうぐち」, the emergency exit sign you see in every building. So, the top piece says not, the bottom piece is the heart, and the reading 「ひ」 travels with that top piece." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "English has many words for unhappy feelings, and so does Japanese. Let's sort out three. This word is plain sadness, from a loss, bad news, or a tearjerker movie. 「さびしい」 means lonely, the empty feeling when someone is gone or you are by yourself. If your friend moves away, you might feel both, but 「さびしい」 focuses on missing them. Then there is 「つらい」, which means painful or hard to bear, written with the same kanji as spicy. A tough job, a breakup, or a long illness is 「つらい」. It is about suffering through something. To make a noun, change the ending to 「み」. 「悲しみ|かなしみ」 means sorrow, the sadness itself, used in songs and stories. And the verb to cry, 「泣く|なく」, is in this lesson too. So, sad is a feeling, lonely is missing someone, and hard is enduring something." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular 「い」 adjective, so it conjugates like any other. Was sad is 「悲しかった|かなしかった」. Not sad is 「悲しくない|かなしくない」. To link it to a result, use the 「て」 form, 「悲しくて|かなしくて」. For example, 「悲しくて、泣きました|かなしくて、なきました」, I was so sad that I cried. To describe a thing that causes sadness, put the adjective right before the noun: 「悲しい話|かなしいはなし」, a sad story, 「悲しい映画|かなしいえいが」, a sad movie. Here, the feeling belongs to the story, so this use is fine for anything. Only when you describe a person's inner feeling do you need the 「そう」 form for others, like 「悲しそうな顔|かなしそうなかお」, a sad looking face. So a story can simply be sad, but a friend only looks sad." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji puts a part meaning not or wrong on top of 「心|こころ」, heart, so a heart that is not right means sadness, and many feeling kanji carry that heart.",
      "The top part, read 「ひ」, gives the kanji its reading in dramatic words like 「悲劇|ひげき」, tragedy, and 「悲鳴|ひめい」, a scream, and it appears in 「非常口|ひじょうぐち」.",
      "This is plain sadness, while 「さびしい」 is lonely and missing someone, and 「つらい」 is painful or hard to bear. The noun 「悲しみ|かなしみ」 means sorrow.",
      "A story can simply be 「悲しい話|かなしいはなし」, but for another person's inner feeling you need the 「そう」 form, like 「悲しそうな顔|かなしそうなかお」."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}が{帰って|かえって}、[悲しい|かなしい]です。",
    en: "My friend went home, so I'm sad." },
  "きびしい|厳しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is a complicated one, and its history is not simple, so let's focus on how the word is used. Here it has its native reading, 「きび」, plus the adjective ending 「しい」. Its main use is for people and rules that are strict. A strict teacher, strict parents, strict school rules. You might say 「父はとても厳しいです|ちちはとてもきびしいです」, my father is very strict. But it goes beyond people. It also means harsh or severe. Very harsh cold weather is 「厳しい寒さ|きびしいさむさ」. A tough business situation, a hard schedule, or a tough competition can all be called this. Think of it as anything that does not let you off easy. It is a little different from 「むずかしい」, difficult. Something difficult is hard to understand or do. Something with this word is hard on you, demanding. The opposite for people is 「やさしい」, kind or gentle. A way to remember it: the strict teacher holds the key, and her words sting like a bee, key and bee. So, strict, harsh, and severe: something that does not go easy on you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a business secret. In Japanese workplaces, this word is often a polite way of saying no. If you ask for a lower price or a faster deadline, and someone sighs and says 「それはちょっと厳しいですね|それはちょっときびしいですね」, that's a bit tough, they usually mean it's not happening. Japanese tends to avoid a direct no, so describing the request as harsh does the job gently. Learn to hear it. The word also pairs with 「に」 to show what someone is strict about. 「時間に厳しい|じかんにきびしい」 means strict about time, someone who hates lateness. 「自分に厳しい|じぶんにきびしい」 means hard on yourself, often said as praise about a disciplined person. As an adverb, 「厳しく|きびしく」, it describes how someone scolds or trains. So, strict people, harsh weather, and a polite, gentle no." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji is complex, so rather than an origin story, let's look at where else you will meet it. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「げん」, and that reading shows up on warning signs. 「禁煙|きんえん」 means no smoking, but a sign saying 「火気厳禁|かきげんきん」 means fire strictly prohibited, often at gas stations and factories. That 「げんきん」 part means strictly forbidden, using this kanji. Careful, 「げんきん」 also sounds exactly like the word for cash, 「現金|げんきん」, which is written differently. Context will save you. The kanji also has a rare native reading, 「おごそか」, meaning solemn or dignified, like a grand ceremony. That fits the core feeling: serious, stern, not relaxed. So, read 「きび」 in the everyday adjective, and 「げん」 on the signs that tell you firmly what not to do." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's compare some hard words, because English hard covers too much. 「難しい|むずかしい」 means difficult to understand or do, like a hard test question. 「大変|たいへん」 means tough in the sense of a lot of work or trouble, like a busy week. 「きつい」 is casual and means tight, or physically and mentally draining, like a tough workout or tight shoes. This word is about strictness and severity, standards or conditions that are demanding and do not let up. A teacher can be strict, but not 「難しい|むずかしい」. A test can be difficult, and a grader can be strict. Winter can be severe, and a schedule can be strict or 「きつい」. The opposite for people is 「優しい|やさしい」, kind. So, difficult is hard to solve, and this word is hard on you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It describes people and rules that are strict, like 「厳しい|きびしい」 parents, and also harsh conditions like 「厳しい寒さ|きびしいさむさ」. Unlike 「むずかしい」, it means hard on you, not hard to do.",
      "In business, 「それはちょっと厳しいですね|それはちょっときびしいですね」 is often a gentle way of saying no, and with 「に」 it shows what someone is strict about, as in 「時間に厳しい|じかんにきびしい」.",
      "Its Chinese-origin reading 「げん」 shows up on warning signs like 「火気厳禁|かきげんきん」, fire strictly prohibited, so do not confuse it with 「現金|げんきん」, cash.",
      "「難しい|むずかしい」 is hard to solve, 「大変|たいへん」 is a lot of trouble, and 「きつい」 is draining or tight, but this word means demanding standards that do not let up."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}はとても[厳しい|きびしい]です。",
    en: "My father is very strict." },
  "きぶんがわるい|気分が悪い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a short phrase, so let's open it up. The first word, 「気分|きぶん」, means mood or how you feel inside. It has two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. 「気|き」 is spirit, energy, or air. You know it from many words: 「元気|げんき」, healthy, 「天気|てんき」, weather, 「病気|びょうき」, sickness, and 「電気|でんき」, electricity. All of them are about some kind of invisible energy. The second kanji, 「分|ぶん」, means part or portion. You know it in 「自分|じぶん」, oneself, and 「多分|たぶん」, probably. Then 「が」, and 「悪い|わるい」, bad, which you already know. So the phrase is literally your inner feeling is bad. In practice it usually means you feel sick, often a bit queasy, like you might throw up, or just unwell. It can also mean you are in a bad mood, upset by something. Context makes it clear. The opposite, 「気分がいい|きぶんがいい」, means you feel good or refreshed. Compare 「元気がない|げんきがない」, don't look well, which is about low energy. So, your inner spirit is bad: you feel sick." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a few bad feeling phrases, and they are easy to mix up. This one is about your inner state, usually feeling queasy or unwell, or sometimes just a bad mood. 「具合が悪い|ぐあいがわるい」 is about your physical condition. It is what you tell a doctor, or say when you have a cold. 「気持ちが悪い|きもちがわるい」 can also mean nauseous, but it has another very common use: gross or creepy. A slimy bug, a weird smell, or a creepy stranger can all be called that. Young people shorten it to 「キモい」, which is slang and a bit rude. So be careful. If you say a dish is 「気持ちが悪い|きもちがわるい」, you might sound like you are calling it disgusting. Saying you feel queasy with this lesson's phrase is safer. Mood and nausea, body condition, and gross: three different bads." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a long, winding bus ride in the mountains. Your friend turns pale and says 「ちょっと気分が悪い|ちょっときぶんがわるい」, I feel a little sick. That is exactly where this phrase lives. Motion sickness has its own word, 「乗り物酔い|のりものよい」, getting drunk on a vehicle. At work or school, the polite way to excuse yourself is 「すみません、ちょっと気分が悪いんですが|すみません、ちょっときぶんがわるいんですが」, sorry, I'm feeling a bit unwell. The 「んですが」 ending explains your situation and politely leaves the request unsaid, so the listener understands you want to rest or go home. Staff at a hotel or a nurse might ask you 「ご気分はいかがですか|ごきぶんはいかがですか」, how are you feeling? So, a soft little phrase that gets you sympathy and a seat by the window." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since the core of this phrase is the 「い」 adjective 「悪い|わるい」, you can conjugate the whole thing just by changing that last word. I felt sick is 「気分が悪かった|きぶんがわるかった」. I don't feel sick is 「気分が悪くない|きぶんがわるくない」, though people usually say 「気分はいい|きぶんはいい」 for that. To say you started feeling sick, use 「なる」, to become: 「気分が悪くなりました|きぶんがわるくなりました」, I got sick, I started feeling bad. That is very common, because nausea usually comes on suddenly. And to give a reason, use the 「て」 form: 「気分が悪くて、早く帰りました|きぶんがわるくて、はやくかえりました」, I felt sick, so I went home early. Notice the particle 「が」 marks the mood itself. When contrasting, it often becomes 「は」. So, swap the ending, and the phrase moves through time with you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「気分|きぶん」 is your inner feeling, built from 「気|き」, spirit or energy, and 「分|ぶん」, part, so with 「悪い|わるい」 it literally says your inner feeling is bad, usually meaning queasy or unwell.",
      "This phrase covers feeling queasy or a bad mood, 「具合が悪い|ぐあいがわるい」 is your physical condition, and 「気持ちが悪い|きもちがわるい」 can also mean gross or creepy, so be careful with it.",
      "It fits a winding bus ride, and the polite way to excuse yourself is 「すみません、ちょっと気分が悪いんですが|すみません、ちょっときぶんがわるいんですが」, leaving the request unsaid.",
      "Just conjugate 「悪い|わるい」 at the end, and use 「気分が悪くなりました|きぶんがわるくなりました」 to say you suddenly started feeling sick."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい]です。",
    en: "I feel sick today." },
  "からい|辛い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native word, written with a kanji plus 「い」. Its main meaning is hot and spicy, like chili, wasabi, or a hot curry. In some regions it is also used for salty, though people in the Tokyo area usually say 「しょっぱい」 for that. Here is something interesting about the kanji. The very same kanji, with a different native reading, is 「辛い|つらい」, which means painful or hard to bear. Food that is too spicy is painful, so the two meanings fit together. Just remember that the reading changes the meaning, so listen carefully. There is another fun link. Look at the kanji for 「幸せ|しあわせ」, happiness, also in this lesson. It looks almost the same, but it has one extra horizontal line on top. People in Japan love to point this out: add one line to pain, and you get happiness. The opposite of spicy is 「甘い|あまい」, sweet. And a mild curry is 「あまくち」, a sweet mouth. A way to remember it: 「カレー」 is 「からい」, the two even sound alike. So, spicy, sometimes salty, and the same kanji as painful." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan is famous for curry rice, and the boxes of curry roux at the supermarket come in spice levels with special names. Mild is 「甘口|あまくち」, sweet mouth. Medium is 「中辛|ちゅうから」, middle spicy. Hot is 「辛口|からくち」, spicy mouth. Notice that the medium and hot levels both use this kanji. The word 「辛口|からくち」 has a second life too. On a bottle of sake or wine, it means dry, not sweet. And for people, a 「辛口|からくち」 critic is one who gives harsh, biting reviews. So spicy food, dry drinks, and sharp words all share the same mouth burning idea. Many Japanese people prefer milder food than people in Thailand or Korea, so restaurant staff may warn you that a dish is hot. So, sweet mouth, middle spicy, spicy mouth: a whole culture of curry levels." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a restaurant, this word comes up a lot. A server might ask 「辛いのは大丈夫ですか|からいのはだいじょうぶですか」, are you okay with spicy food? If you love heat, say 「辛いのが好きです|からいのがすきです」. If you can't handle it, say 「辛いのは苦手です|からいのはにがてです」, spicy food is not my thing. That word 「苦手|にがて」 is a gentle way to say you are bad with something. Notice the 「の」 after the adjective. It turns spicy into spicy things, so it can act as a noun. When you take a bite and your mouth is on fire, the natural reaction is short and loud: 「からっ！」, with a little catch at the end. That cut off form is how people react to strong tastes in casual speech. So, ask about it, admit it, or yell it with your mouth burning." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's put this word with the other taste words, so you can describe any dish. Sweet is 「甘い|あまい」. Salty is 「しょっぱい」, though some regions use this word for salty too. Sour is 「すっぱい」. Bitter is 「苦い|にがい」, written with the same kanji as 「苦手|にがて」. And umami, the savory taste, is a Japanese word that English borrowed. The kanji of this word also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「しん」. You will see it in 「香辛料|こうしんりょう」, spices, literally fragrance and spice ingredients, on food labels. And remember its other native reading, 「つらい」, painful or hard to bear. Context tells you which one: food is spicy, a job or a breakup is painful. So, five tastes, plus spice, and a kanji that burns both your mouth and your heart." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means hot and spicy, and the same kanji read 「つらい」 means painful. Add one line to the top and you get the kanji of 「幸せ|しあわせ」, happiness.",
      "Curry roux comes as 「甘口|あまくち」, 「中辛|ちゅうから」, and 「辛口|からくち」, and 「辛口|からくち」 also means dry sake or a harsh critic.",
      "Ask or answer with 「辛いのは大丈夫ですか|からいのはだいじょうぶですか」 or 「辛いのは苦手です|からいのはにがてです」, and when your mouth is burning just yell 「からっ！」.",
      "The taste words are 「甘い|あまい」, 「しょっぱい」, 「すっぱい」, and 「苦い|にがい」, and this kanji read 「しん」 appears in 「香辛料|こうしんりょう」, spices, on food labels."
    ],
    ja: "このカレーは[辛い|からい]です。",
    en: "This curry is spicy." },
  "すごい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native word, normally written in hiragana. It is the all-purpose wow word, for anything amazing, impressive, or intense. A great view, a friend's skill, a huge crowd, a surprising price. Here is an interesting history. In older Japanese, this word meant something eerie, frightening, or chilling, the kind of thing that makes your skin crawl. Over time, it shifted from scary intense to impressive intense, and now it is mostly positive. English did something similar with awesome and terrific, which once meant scary too. Two useful forms. First, before an adjective or verb, it becomes 「すごく」, meaning very or really, as in 「すごくおいしい」, really delicious. You learned that form back in lesson five. Second, people in casual speech often stretch it out for feeling, like 「すごーい」. In polite speech, 「すごいですね」 is a nice way to compliment someone. Careful: in formal writing, use 「とても」 instead of 「すごく」, since it sounds more proper. A way to remember it: your jaw drops, wow. So, from eerie to awesome, the go-to word for amazing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's compare this word with its close relatives. 「すばらしい」 means wonderful or splendid. It is always positive and sounds a bit more formal and admiring. You might use it in a speech or about a performance. This word is more casual and more flexible, because it just means intense. It can even be negative: 「すごい雨|すごいあめ」 is a terrible downpour, and 「すごい人|すごいひと」 could be an amazing person or, with the right tone, a crowd that is way too big. Then there is 「さすが」, which you say when someone is as impressive as you expected. If a chef friend cooks a great meal, 「さすが」 means I knew you would, you are the expert. This word is surprise, while 「さすが」 is expectation confirmed. So, splendid is always nice, this word is just intense, and 「さすが」 means as expected." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is an 「い」 adjective, so it goes straight before a noun, as in 「すごい景色|すごいけしき」, an amazing view. To modify another adjective or a verb, you change it to 「すごく」, like 「すごく高い|すごくたかい」, really expensive. Here is something you will hear a lot from young people. They often skip that change and say 「すごいおいしい」 instead of 「すごくおいしい」. It is very common in casual speech, but textbooks and teachers consider it incorrect, so stick with the 「く」 form yourself. Past tense works as usual: 「すごかった」, it was amazing. That one is a classic reaction after a concert or a fireworks show. And the negative, 「すごくない」, can mean not that great, or with rising intonation, isn't it amazing? Tone matters a lot here. So, 「い」 before nouns, 「く」 before everything else." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japanese conversation, listeners give constant little reactions to show they are following. These are called 「あいづち」. This word, in the polite form 「すごいですね」, is one of the most useful of them. Your coworker says she ran a marathon. You say 「へえ、すごいですね」, wow, that's amazing. It keeps the conversation warm and shows interest. Here is a mini dialogue. A friend says 「この絵、私が描いたんです|このえ、わたしがかいたんです」, I painted this. You say 「えっ、すごい！」, what, amazing! When someone praises you this way, the usual reply is modest, like 「いえいえ、まだまだです」, no, no, I still have a long way to go. Accepting praise too directly can sound boastful. So, give this word generously, and when it comes back to you, wave it away politely." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the all-purpose wow word, which once meant eerie or frightening before shifting to impressive. Before adjectives it becomes 「すごく」, meaning really.",
      "「すばらしい」 is always positive and more formal, this word just means intense and can even be negative, and 「さすが」 means someone is as impressive as expected.",
      "Put it straight before nouns, as in 「すごい景色|すごいけしき」, but use 「すごく」 before adjectives and verbs, even though young people often say 「すごいおいしい」.",
      "「すごいですね」 is a great listener reaction to show interest, and when praised yourself, reply modestly with 「いえいえ、まだまだです」."
    ],
    ja: "{山田|やまだ}さんの{日本語|にほんご}は[すごい]です。",
    en: "Mr. Yamada's Japanese is amazing." },
  "ちかい|近い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji. The stroke that wraps along the bottom left is a radical for moving along a road. You see it in lots of kanji about movement or distance. You know it from 「週|しゅう」, as in 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week, from 「遅い|おそい」, slow or late, and from 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, to play. Inside sits a part that also appears in other kanji, but the key piece is the road. A way to remember it: something just a short walk down the road. Here the reading is native, 「ちか」, plus 「い」. The Chinese-origin reading is 「きん」. You will meet that soon in 「最近|さいきん」, recently, meaning near in time instead of space. Here is a grammar point people often miss. To say near something, use 「の近く|のちかく」, as in 「駅の近く|えきのちかく」, near the station. And to say A is close to B, use 「に」 or 「から」, like 「駅から近い|えきからちかい」, close to the station. The opposite is 「遠い|とおい」, far, which has the same road radical. So, a short way down the road: close." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a quirk that trips up many learners. In English, you say a nearby store. You might expect to put this adjective right before the noun, like any other 「い」 adjective. But Japanese usually prefers the noun form 「近く|ちかく」 with 「の」. So a nearby store is 「近くの店|ちかくのみせ」, not the plain adjective plus store. The same goes for far: 「遠くの町|とおくのまち」, a faraway town. The plain adjective works fine in the predicate, at the end: 「この店は駅から近いです|このみせはえきからちかいです」, this store is close to the station. It also works in comparisons, like 「一番近い駅|いちばんちかいえき」, the closest station, and with 「が」 clauses, like 「駅が近いアパート|えきがちかいアパート」, an apartment where the station is close. So, for plain nearby, reach for 「近くの|ちかくの」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word shows up in some fun everyday expressions. When searching for an apartment, you will see the slang 「駅近|えきちか」, station close, meaning just a short walk from the train. In Japanese cities, that is a huge selling point. A funny one: if someone says 「トイレが近い|トイレがちかい」, the toilet is close, it does not mean the bathroom is down the hall. It means they need to pee often. People say it before a long drive or movie. The word also works for time. 「近いうちに|ちかいうちに」 means sometime soon, as in let's get together soon. And 「もうすぐ」 is another way to say soon. For a neighborhood, use 「近所|きんじょ」, with the Chinese-origin reading. 「近所の人|きんじょのひと」 are your neighbors. So, close to the station, close to the toilet, and close in time." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. The word sounds a bit like cheek eye. Picture a stranger on a packed Tokyo rush hour train. The car is so crowded that his cheek is pressed right up against your eye. You can't move, he can't move, and you are both pretending this is normal. Now that is close. Way too close. Every time you hear this word, feel that cheek squashed against your eye. And remember the kanji has the road radical sweeping along the bottom left, so imagine the train track running under the two of you. A tiny distance along the road, and a cheek in your eye. Cheek eye: about as near as anything can get." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the road radical sweeping along the bottom left, so think of something a short walk down the road. Say near something with 「の近く|のちかく」, as in 「駅の近く|えきのちかく」.",
      "For a plain nearby thing, Japanese prefers 「近くの|ちかくの」, as in 「近くの店|ちかくのみせ」, keeping the plain adjective for the end of a sentence or comparisons.",
      "Apartment ads boast 「駅近|えきちか」, close to the station, and 「トイレが近い|トイレがちかい」 means needing to pee often, while 「近いうちに|ちかいうちに」 means sometime soon.",
      "It sounds like cheek eye: on a packed Tokyo train, a stranger's cheek is pressed right against your eye, about as close as anything gets."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}は[近い|ちかい]です。",
    en: "The station is close." },
  "いろいろ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is usually written in hiragana, but it comes from 「色|いろ」, color, said twice. When written with kanji, the second half uses the repeat mark, the same mark you see in 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes. So the word is literally colors, colors. Many colors means many kinds, a variety. That is a lovely picture: a world full of different colors. Japanese has many words that repeat a sound like this to show plurality or variety. 「時々|ときどき」 is times, times, sometimes. And people, 「人々|ひとびと」, is person, person. Here is how to use it. Before a noun, add 「な」, as a na-adjective: 「いろいろな所|いろいろなところ」, various places. You can also use it as an adverb with 「と」 or alone, like 「いろいろとありがとう」, thanks for everything. A very common use is when someone asks what you did, and you answer 「いろいろ」, all sorts of things. A way to remember it: picture a box of crayons, every color a different kind of thing. So, colors repeated: all kinds of things." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Since this word comes from color, let's look at the color family. You know 「色|いろ」, color, and color names built from it, like 「黄色|きいろ」, yellow, and 「茶色|ちゃいろ」, brown, literally tea color. The kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「しょく」 or 「しき」. You will meet 「景色|けしき」, scenery, the colors of a view. Now some near-synonyms. 「たくさん」 means a lot, a large amount. This word means many different kinds. You can eat a lot of one kind of cake, but trying several kinds of cake is this word. A more formal partner is 「様々|さまざま」, various, which you will see in writing and news, and it uses the same repeat mark. So, amount is 「たくさん」, variety is this word, and formal variety is 「様々|さまざま」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This little word is a master of polite vagueness. When someone asks what you did over the weekend and you don't want to list everything, 「いろいろ」 does it for you. Even more interesting is 「いろいろありました」, a lot happened. People say it when something complicated or painful went on, and it signals that they would rather not go into details. A kind listener usually does not push. Then there is the classic farewell: 「いろいろお世話になりました|いろいろおせわになりました」, thank you for taking care of me in so many ways. You say this when leaving a job, a host family, or a school. It thanks someone for everything without naming anything. And with a verb, 「いろいろ考えました|いろいろかんがえました」 means I thought about it from many angles. So, it can mean variety, but also, it's complicated." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen to the rhythm here. It is a doubled sound, two identical halves. Japanese loves this pattern, and many such words describe sounds, textures, or states. The repeat mark you see in the kanji spelling is a hint. Watch for a sound change in some doubled words, though. In 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes, and 「人々|ひとびと」, people, the second half becomes voiced: 「と」 turns into 「ど」, and 「ひ」 into 「び」. That change is called 「連濁|れんだく」. In this word there is no change, because 「い」 cannot be voiced. So don't be tempted to add one. The pitch is also worth noticing: the first syllable is low and then it rises, rather than starting high. Saying it smoothly, without a pause between the halves, makes it sound natural. So, two equal halves, no voicing change, one smooth flow." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「色|いろ」, color, said twice, so colors, colors means many kinds. Before a noun add 「な」, as in 「いろいろな所|いろいろなところ」.",
      "Coming from color, it links to 「黄色|きいろ」 and 「景色|けしき」. 「たくさん」 means a large amount, this word means many kinds, and 「様々|さまざま」 is the formal version.",
      "It is a master of polite vagueness: 「いろいろありました」 hints that something complicated happened, and 「いろいろお世話になりました|いろいろおせわになりました」 thanks someone for everything.",
      "Unlike 「時々|ときどき」 and 「人々|ひとびと」, this doubled word has no voicing change, because 「い」 cannot be voiced, so say both halves the same in one smooth flow."
    ],
    ja: "[いろいろ]な{所|ところ}に{行きました|いきました}。",
    en: "I went to various places." },
  "しあわせ|幸せ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji means good fortune or blessing. Here it has the native reading, 「しあわせ」, with 「せ」 written in hiragana. The Chinese-origin reading is 「こう」, which you will meet in more formal words later. A bit of history: long ago, this word was written differently and meant how things come together, your circumstances or luck, good or bad. Over time it came to mean only good luck, and then the happy state that comes from it. Now look at the kanji next to 「辛い|からい」, spicy, from this same lesson, which also means painful. The happiness kanji has one extra horizontal line at the top. Japanese people love this: one more line turns pain into happiness. It is a coincidence of shape, but a great hook. Now the meaning. This is deep, lasting happiness, not a quick moment. Compare 「うれしい」, glad, which is a reaction to a good thing. You are 「うれしい」 when you get a gift. You are 「幸せ|しあわせ」 when your life, family, or relationship is good. It is a na-adjective, so 「幸せな家族|しあわせなかぞく」, a happy family. So, good fortune, and a kanji one stroke away from pain." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has several readings you will meet. The Chinese-origin reading is 「こう」. 「幸運|こううん」 means good luck, a fortunate chance. Add 「不|ふ」, not, to the front, and you get 「不幸|ふこう」, misfortune, unhappiness. A third reading, 「さいわ」, gives 「幸い|さいわい」, fortunately, as in 「幸い、けがはありませんでした|さいわい、けがはありませんでした」, fortunately, no one was hurt. And it is popular in names. The girls' name 「幸子|さちこ」 reads it as 「さち」, meaning blessing. So one shape, four readings, all circling around good fortune. Each reading has its own job: 「しあわ」 for the happy state, 「こう」 in compounds, 「さいわ」 for fortunately, and 「さち」 in names. So, a kanji of blessing that pops up in luck, unluck, relief, and names." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up at life's big moments. At a Japanese wedding, guests say 「お幸せに|おしあわせに」 to the newlyweds, a set phrase meaning be happy together. Friends might say 「幸せになってね|しあわせになってね」, I hope you'll be happy, when someone gets married or starts a new life. A person overwhelmed with gratitude might say 「私は幸せ者です|わたしはしあわせものです」, I'm such a lucky person. And it shows up in small, everyday joy too. Someone biting into a perfect dessert might sigh 「幸せ|しあわせ」, pure bliss. That everyday use is very common, especially with food, and it is cute rather than dramatic. Fairy tales end with 「いつまでも幸せに暮らしました|いつまでもしあわせにくらしました」, they lived happily ever after. So, from weddings to desserts, a word for blessings big and small." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「な」 adjective, so it follows the usual rules. Before a noun, add 「な」: 「幸せな時間|しあわせなじかん」, a happy time. To describe how someone lives or becomes, add 「に」, which turns it into an adverb: 「幸せに暮らす|しあわせにくらす」, to live happily, and 「幸せになる|しあわせになる」, to become happy. In the past, it is 「幸せでした|しあわせでした」, and in the negative, 「幸せじゃない|しあわせじゃない」. It can also act as a noun on its own. 「幸せを見つける|しあわせをみつける」 means to find happiness. A common slip for English speakers is to use it for small, quick joys like getting a present, where 「うれしい」 fits better. The dessert sigh is the playful exception. So, 「な」 before nouns, 「に」 before verbs, and a noun for happiness itself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means good fortune, and it is one line away from the kanji of 「辛い|からい」, painful. This is deep lasting happiness, unlike the quick reaction of 「うれしい」.",
      "The kanji has four readings: 「しあわ」 for the happy state, 「こう」 as in 「幸運|こううん」 and 「不幸|ふこう」, 「さいわ」 in 「幸い|さいわい」, fortunately, and 「さち」 in names.",
      "Wedding guests tell newlyweds 「お幸せに|おしあわせに」, and people also sigh 「幸せ|しあわせ」 over a perfect dessert, a cute everyday use.",
      "As a 「な」 adjective, use 「な」 before nouns and 「に」 before verbs, as in 「幸せになる|しあわせになる」, and it also works as a noun for happiness itself."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}は{今|いま}とても[幸せ|しあわせ]です。",
    en: "I am very happy now." },
  "だめ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is usually written in hiragana or katakana. It is often said to come from the board game of Go. In Go, there are points between the two players' territories that count for neither side, useless spaces. Those were called by this word, and from there it spread to mean useless, no good. That is why the kanji used for it mean something like useless eye, and they are only a spelling for the sound. You will hear this word constantly in Japan. Parents say it to kids when something is not allowed, often doubled: 「だめだめ」. It covers several English ideas: no good, not allowed, does not work, hopeless. For not allowed, there is a key grammar pattern: verb te form plus 「は」 plus this word. For example, 「ここでたべてはだめです」, you can't eat here. It is a bit softer and more casual than 「いけません」. You can also say 「だめです」 to turn down an idea, or 「だめですか」 to ask if something is okay. A way to remember it: a useless spot on the game board, a dead end. So, from a useless Go point to the everyday word for no good." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When this word is written in kanji, the first character is worth a look. On its left side is 「馬|うま」, horse. That character, read 「だ」, originally had to do with a load carried by a packhorse, and it came to suggest something cheap or low grade. You will meet it in a few fun words. 「無駄|むだ」 means waste, something pointless, as in a waste of time. 「下駄|げた」 are traditional wooden sandals that clack on the street. And 「駄菓子|だがし」 are cheap old-fashioned candies. The second character is 「目|め」, eye. In this word the pair mostly works as a spelling for the sound, so don't look for a deep meaning in the eye. So, a horse radical that hints at cheapness, wooden sandals, and waste: the written form of no good." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Besides not allowed, this word helps form must. Take the negative condition of a verb, like 「行かなければ|いかなければ」, if you don't go, and add this word: if you don't go, it's no good. So it means you have to go. In casual speech it shrinks to 「行かなきゃだめ|いかなきゃだめ」. A parent might say 「薬を飲まなきゃだめだよ|くすりをのまなきゃだめだよ」, you have to take your medicine. Another pattern is 「だめになる」, to become no good, to be ruined. Food can go bad, a phone can stop working, or plans can fall apart. 「雨でピクニックがだめになりました|あめでピクニックがだめになりました」, the picnic was ruined by rain. It is a 「な」 adjective, so before a noun it takes 「な」: 「だめな人|だめなひと」, a hopeless person. So, you must, it got ruined, and a hopeless case." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a popular piece of slang built from this word: 「だめもと」. It is short for no good to begin with. You say it when you try something with almost no chance of success, because you have nothing to lose. 「だめもとで聞いてみます|だめもとできいてみます」, I'll ask anyway, nothing to lose. It is a cheerful, brave little phrase. You will also hear friends tease each other with 「だめじゃん」, that's no good, you messed up, in a playful tone. When asking for permission, a short 「だめですか」 is a gentle way to ask, isn't it okay? And the answer is often softened. Instead of a flat 「だめです」, people say 「ちょっと」 and let the sentence trail off, which politely means no. So, a word for refusing, teasing, and taking a shot with nothing to lose." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is said to come from the game of Go, where useless points between territories count for neither side. For not allowed, use a verb 「て」 form plus 「は」 plus this word.",
      "In kanji the first character has 「馬|うま」, horse, and hints at cheapness, as in 「無駄|むだ」, waste, and 「駄菓子|だがし」, while the pair is mostly just a spelling for the sound.",
      "Added to a negative condition, it means must, as in 「行かなきゃだめ|いかなきゃだめ」, and 「だめになる」 means to be ruined, like a rained-out picnic.",
      "「だめもと」 means trying anyway with nothing to lose, and friends tease with 「だめじゃん」, while a polite refusal often just trails off with 「ちょっと」."
    ],
    ja: "ここでしゃしんをとっては[だめ]です。",
    en: "You can't take pictures here." },
  "あむ|編む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the left side of the kanji. It is the thread radical, a little twisted strand of silk. You have already seen it in 「紅茶|こうちゃ」, 「約束|やくそく」, and 「手紙|てがみ」. Here it makes perfect sense, because you knit with yarn. The right side helps give the sound in other words, so focus on the thread. In this verb the reading is native, 「あ」, with 「む」 in hiragana. It is a regular u verb, so the polite form is 「編みます|あみます」, and the te form is 「編んで|あんで」, just like 「読む|よむ」 becomes 「読んで|よんで」. The Chinese-origin reading, 「へん」, shows up later in words about editing, which is a kind of knitting together of writing. The objects that go with it are what you would expect: a 「セーター」, sweater, from this same lesson, a 「マフラー」, scarf, or gloves, 「手袋|てぶくろ」. Hand-knitting a scarf for someone you love is a classic romantic gesture in Japanese dramas and manga, especially in winter. A way to remember it: picture your hands looping thread, over and over. So, the thread radical, and the verb for making things out of yarn." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji knits together a lovely little family. Add 「物|もの」, thing, and you get 「編み物|あみもの」, knitting as a hobby, or the knitted item itself. Grandmothers in Japan are often pictured doing 「編み物|あみもの」 by the heater in winter. Then there is 「三つ編み|みつあみ」, a braid, literally three strand knitting. Many school girls in old anime wear their hair in 「三つ編み|みつあみ」. With the Chinese-origin reading 「へん」, the kanji moves into the world of writing. 「編集|へんしゅう」 is editing, gathering and weaving pieces into a whole, and a 「長編|ちょうへん」 is a full-length novel or film. So the family runs from yarn and hair to books and movies. The shared idea is weaving separate strands into one piece. So, knitting, braiding, and editing: all weaving things together." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular 「う」 verb ending in 「む」, so it follows the same pattern as 「読む|よむ」 and 「飲む|のむ」. The negative is 「編まない|あまない」, and the past is 「編んだ|あんだ」. Because hand-knitting is usually done for someone, this verb pairs beautifully with the giving and receiving patterns. 「祖母がセーターを編んでくれました|そぼがセーターをあんでくれました」 means my grandmother knit me a sweater, kindly, for my benefit. 「彼女にマフラーを編んであげました|かのじょにマフラーをあんであげました」 means I knit a scarf for my girlfriend. And to say you can knit, use the potential form, 「編める|あめる」. 「私は編み物ができます|わたしはあみものができます」 works too. So, conjugate it like 「読む|よむ」, and add a giving verb for a warm, handmade gift." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. The word sounds like ah, moo. Picture a big, calm cow sitting in a rocking chair by the fireplace. She has two knitting needles in her hooves and a ball of yarn in her lap. Every time she finishes a row, she sighs with satisfaction, ah, moo. She is knitting a long striped scarf for the farmer, and it is already ten meters long, trailing out the barn door. The farmer's wife comes in, sees it, and just shakes her head. Every time you hear this verb, picture the cow, the needles, and that contented ah, moo. And remember the thread radical on the left side of the kanji, the yarn in her lap. Ah, moo: a cow knitting away." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the thread radical on the left, which fits since you knit with yarn. It is a regular verb, so its 「て」 form is 「編んで|あんで」, just like 「読んで|よんで」.",
      "The kanji weaves strands together in 「編み物|あみもの」, knitting, 「三つ編み|みつあみ」, a braid, and with the reading 「へん」, in 「編集|へんしゅう」, editing.",
      "Since knitting is usually for someone, pair it with giving verbs, as in 「祖母がセーターを編んでくれました|そぼがセーターをあんでくれました」, my grandmother knit me a sweater.",
      "It sounds like ah, moo: a calm cow knits a ten-meter scarf in a rocking chair, sighing ah, moo with every row, with the yarn in her lap like the thread radical."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}はマフラーを[編みました|あみました|あむ]。",
    en: "My mother knitted a scarf." },
  "がんばる|頑張る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This might be the most Japanese word in the book. Let's look at the kanji first. 「頑|がん」 means stubborn or firm, with a Chinese-origin reading. 「張る|はる」 is a native verb meaning to stretch something tight, like a tent or a rope. Together you can picture it as stretching yourself tight and holding on stubbornly. That picture is a great memory aid, but be aware that the kanji spelling may have been fitted to the word, and scholars disagree about its exact origin. So treat the picture as a hook. Now the use. You say it all the time. Before an exam or a game, friends cheer you on with 「がんばって」, good luck, do your best. Politely, 「がんばってください」. When you promise to try hard, you say 「がんばります」. Teams and fans shout it at sports events. It is less about winning and more about effort and not giving up, which is a value Japanese culture honors. A common mistake: English speakers use good luck, which is about chance. Japanese focuses on your effort instead. So, stretch yourself tight and hang on: do your best." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb looks like a 「る」 verb, but it is a 「う」 verb, so its forms follow that pattern. The negative is 「がんばらない」, and the te form is 「がんばって」, with a small pause. Each form has its own job. 「がんばって」 is the friendly cheer, do your best. The command form, 「がんばれ」, is what fans shout at sports games. It sounds rough, but in cheering it is totally normal and warm. The volitional form, 「がんばろう」, means let's do our best, and people say it to rally a group. And 「がんばりました」 is a proud report: I worked hard. Teachers love to praise students with 「よくがんばりましたね」, you did really well. So one verb gives you a cheer, a chant, a team rally, and a pat on the back." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This word is everywhere in Japan, but it has a delicate side. After the great earthquake and tsunami of 2011, slogans using this word appeared across the country, encouraging the affected regions to keep going. Many people found them inspiring. Others felt tired of being told to try harder when they had already lost so much. That tension is real in daily life too. Telling someone who is exhausted, grieving, or depressed to 「がんばって」 can feel like pressure. In those moments, many Japanese people choose 「無理しないでね|むりしないでね」, don't push yourself too hard, or 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care, for someone who is sick. Both show you care without asking for more effort. So, cheer people on with this word, but know when to say take it easy instead." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Nobody is completely sure where this word comes from, and two theories are often told. One says it comes from a phrase meaning to stubbornly insist on yourself, 「我を張る|がをはる」, sticking to your own will. Over time, the sounds blended into the verb we know. The other theory links it to 「眼張る|がんばる」, written with the kanji for eye, meaning to keep your eyes wide open and keep watch, staying in one spot without moving. Either way, the idea is holding firm and not budging. The kanji we use today, stubborn plus stretch, may have been chosen later to fit the sound and the feeling. So treat both stories as possibilities, not facts. What is clear is that it went from stubborn persistence to the positive do your best of today. So, from digging in your heels to giving it your all." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji are stubborn plus stretch tight, picturing holding on firmly, though that spelling may have been fitted later. It is about effort, not luck, as in the cheer 「がんばって」.",
      "It is a 「う」 verb, so each form has a job: 「がんばって」 cheers, 「がんばれ」 is the fans' shout, 「がんばろう」 rallies a group, and 「がんばりました」 reports hard work.",
      "Telling someone exhausted or grieving to 「がんばって」 can feel like pressure, so many people choose 「無理しないでね|むりしないでね」, don't push yourself, instead.",
      "One theory traces it to 「我を張る|がをはる」, insisting on your will, and another to 「眼張る|がんばる」, keeping your eyes open on watch, both meaning holding firm."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}のしけん、[頑張ります|がんばります|がんばる]。",
    en: "I'll do my best on tomorrow's exam." },
  "なく|泣く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the kanji. On the left are three drops of water, the water radical. On the right is 「立|た」, from 「立つ|たつ」, to stand. A way to remember it: a person standing there with water running down their face, tears. That is a hook, but the water part really does fit, since crying is all about tears. The reading here is native, 「な」, with 「く」 in hiragana. It is a regular u verb, so it becomes 「泣きます|なきます」, and the te form is 「泣いて|ないて」, just like 「書く|かく」 becomes 「書いて|かいて」. A crying baby is 「赤ちゃんが泣いています|あかちゃんがないています」. Here is a common mistake. Animal sounds use the same sound, 「なく」, but a different kanji, 「鳴く|なく」, a mouth next to a bird. So a cat meowing, a bird singing, or an insect chirping, all use the bird one. People crying use the water one. Japanese also has a nice word for a crybaby, 「泣き虫|なきむし」, a crying bug. So, water plus standing: a person in tears." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's gather the words around crying. Tears are 「涙|なみだ」, and that kanji also has the water radical on its left side. A crying face is 「泣き顔|なきがお」, and the sound of crying is 「泣き声|なきごえ」. If you are about to cry, you say 「泣きそう|なきそう」, I'm going to cry, using the looks like pattern. The potential form, 「泣ける|なける」, is used in a special way: 「この映画は泣ける|このえいがはなける」 means this movie will make you cry, a real tearjerker. People recommend sad movies that way. The opposite, of course, is 「笑う|わらう」, to laugh or smile. And remember 「泣き虫|なきむし」, the crybaby, from before. Japanese even has 「泣き笑い|なきわらい」, laughing and crying at the same time. So, tears, crying faces, and tearjerker movies: the family of crying." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Here is one of Japan's most unusual festivals: 「泣き相撲|なきずもう」, crying sumo. It is held at shrines and temples around the country, often in spring. Parents bring their babies, and sumo wrestlers or priests hold them up face to face. The goal is to make the babies cry. Sometimes a referee in a scary mask helps. The baby who cries first, or loudest, wins, depending on the local rules. Why? There is an old saying that a crying child grows well, and a baby's loud cry is thought to bring good health and drive away bad luck. The babies are not hurt, just startled, and the crowd laughs and cheers. So in this festival, crying is a blessing, and the loudest crier is the champion." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb shows up in several patterns from your lessons. To ask someone not to cry, use the negative request: 「泣かないで|なかないで」, don't cry, or politely, 「泣かないでください|なかないでください」. To describe someone else's tears, the 「ている」 form gives an ongoing action: 「子どもが泣いている|こどもがないている」, the child is crying. To say you ended up crying, add 「しまう」: 「泣いてしまいました|ないてしまいました」, or casually, 「泣いちゃった|ないちゃった」, I ended up crying, I couldn't help it. That form fits well, because crying is often not on purpose. And notice that this verb takes no object for tears. You cry, you do not cry tears. The cause often comes in the 「て」 form: 「うれしくて泣きました|うれしくてなきました」, I cried because I was happy. So, don't cry, is crying, and couldn't help crying." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the water radical next to 「立|た」, standing, so picture a person standing in tears. Animals use a different kanji, 「鳴く|なく」, with a mouth and a bird.",
      "The crying family includes 「涙|なみだ」, tears, 「泣き顔|なきがお」, a crying face, and 「泣きそう|なきそう」, about to cry, and 「この映画は泣ける|このえいがはなける」 calls a movie a real tearjerker.",
      "At the 「泣き相撲|なきずもう」 festival, wrestlers hold babies up to make them cry, because a crying child is said to grow well, and the loudest crier wins.",
      "Ask someone not to cry with 「泣かないで|なかないで」, and say you couldn't help crying with 「泣いてしまいました|ないてしまいました」, since crying is rarely on purpose."
    ],
    ja: "{赤ちゃん|あかちゃん}が[泣いています|ないています|なく]。",
    en: "The baby is crying." },
  "みがく|磨く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the bottom of the kanji. It is 「石|いし」, stone. A way to remember it: polishing something by rubbing it with a stone, the way people once ground and smoothed things. The top part is a building shape with hemp plants inside, but the stone at the bottom is the piece to remember. The reading here is native, 「みが」, with 「く」 in hiragana. It is a regular u verb: 「磨きます|みがきます」, and the te form 「磨いて|みがいて」. In English, this word splits into two ideas. For teeth, it means to brush. For shoes, glass, or a car, it means to polish. In Japanese, it is the same verb, because both are about scrubbing a surface clean and shiny. The most common phrase is 「歯を磨く|はをみがく」, to brush your teeth, using 「歯|は」, tooth, which you learned in lesson twelve. A common mistake: English speakers sometimes use 「あらう」, to wash, for teeth. That sounds strange. Teeth get brushed, not washed. The verb is also used for polishing a skill, like practicing a lot. So, stone at the bottom, rubbing something until it shines." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's build some words from this verb. The noun 「歯磨き|はみがき」 means brushing your teeth, and it is also used for toothpaste. You might hear a child told 「歯磨きした？|はみがきした？」, did you brush your teeth? Shoe polishing is 「靴磨き|くつみがき」, which is also the word for a shoeshine stand. And here is a good idiom: 「腕を磨く|うでをみがく」, literally polish your arm. It means to hone your skill, especially in a craft like cooking or carpentry. Think of a sushi chef who trains for years. Compare this verb with 「洗う|あらう」, to wash, which is for hands, faces, dishes, and clothes, using water. This verb is about rubbing and scrubbing until something shines. So, toothbrushing, shoe shining, and polishing your skills: the shining family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a habit that surprises many visitors. In Japan, lots of people brush their teeth after lunch, not just in the morning and at night. Many elementary schools have children brush their teeth together after school lunch. And in office restrooms, you may see coworkers brushing at the sink at midday, toothbrush cups lined up. So if a coworker excuses herself with a toothbrush, that is normal. This verb also fits nicely into daily routine talk. A typical morning: 「起きて、顔を洗って、歯を磨きます|おきて、かおをあらって、はをみがきます」, I get up, wash my face, and brush my teeth. Notice how each step uses the 「て」 form to link actions in order. So, brush after meals, and use 「て」 forms to walk through your routine." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. The word sounds like me, gah, coo. Picture yourself waking up and looking in the bathroom mirror. You see your teeth and shout, me? Gah! They are covered in last night's chocolate cake. So you grab a toothbrush and scrub and scrub. After a full minute, your teeth sparkle like jewels, and you smile at the mirror and coo softly, like a happy pigeon. Me, gah, coo. Shocked, then scrubbing, then pleased with the shine. And remember the stone at the bottom of the kanji: imagine your toothbrush is so tough it is made of stone. Me, gah, coo: scrubbing until it shines." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has 「石|いし」, stone, at the bottom, picturing rubbing something with a stone until it shines. The same verb covers brushing teeth and polishing shoes, so say 「歯を磨く|はをみがく」, never 「あらう」.",
      "「歯磨き|はみがき」 is toothbrushing or toothpaste, 「靴磨き|くつみがき」 is shoe polishing, and 「腕を磨く|うでをみがく」 means to hone your skill, while 「洗う|あらう」 is washing with water.",
      "Many people in Japan brush their teeth after lunch, even at school and at the office sink. Describe your routine with linked 「て」 forms, as in 「起きて、顔を洗って、歯を磨きます|おきて、かおをあらって、はをみがきます」.",
      "It sounds like me, gah, coo: you shout me, gah at your chocolate-covered teeth in the mirror, scrub with a stone-tough toothbrush, then coo at the shine."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}{歯|は}を[磨きます|みがきます|みがく]。",
    en: "I brush my teeth every night." },
  "やくそくをまもる|約束を守る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase has three parts. First, 「約束|やくそく」, a promise, from this same lesson, an agreement tied up like a bundle. Then 「を」, the object marker. Then the verb 「守る|まもる」, which on its own means to protect, to defend, or to guard. So the phrase is literally to guard a promise, keeping it safe so it does not break. Look at the kanji for guard. On top is a roof, the same roof you see in 「家|いえ」, house. Below is a part that is often linked to the hand. So a hand keeping things safe under a roof. That is a helpful picture, but treat it as a hook. The verb also shows up in a well-known cultural item: 「お守り|おまもり」, the little charms you buy at shrines and temples. They are meant to protect you, for safe travel, good health, or passing exams. The opposite phrase is 「約束をやぶる|やくそくをやぶる」, to break a promise, which literally means to tear it. You also use 「守る|まもる」 for rules: 「ルールを守る|ルールをまもる」, to follow the rules. So, guarding a promise so it stays whole: keeping your word." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji for guard has several readings, and they turn up in some surprising places. Read 「まも」, it is the verb in this phrase. Read 「す」, it hides in 「留守|るす」, being away from home. If you call someone and nobody answers, they are 「留守|るす」. A 「留守番電話|るすばんでんわ」 is an answering machine, literally a phone that guards the house while you are out. Read 「もり」, it gives 「子守唄|こもりうた」, a lullaby, literally a song for guarding a child. And 「見守る|みまもる」 means to watch over someone, like a parent watching a child ride a bike. Each word has the same idea: keeping someone or something safe. So, guarding a promise, guarding an empty house, and guarding a sleeping baby." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a trap. The verb 「守る|まもる」 ends in 「る」, but it is a 「う」 verb, not a 「る」 verb. So the polite form is 「守ります|まもります」, the negative is 「守らない|まもらない」, and the te form is 「守って|まもって」, with a small pause. If you treat it like 「食べる|たべる」, you will get it wrong. Some useful forms: 「約束を守ってください|やくそくをまもってください」, please keep your promise. 「約束を守らない人|やくそくをまもらないひと」, a person who doesn't keep promises. And with the potential, 「約束を守れなくて、すみません|やくそくをまもれなくて、すみません」, I'm sorry I couldn't keep my promise. That sentence is worth memorizing for apologies. So, a 「う」 verb in disguise, like 「帰る|かえる」 and 「入る|はいる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, the same verb covers keeping promises, following rules, and being on time. 「時間を守る|じかんをまもる」 means to be punctual, literally to guard the time. Punctuality is taken very seriously. Trains are famous for running on time to the minute, and a delay of just a few minutes can mean an apology over the speakers. Railway companies even hand out delay slips so passengers can show their boss why they were late. For meetings, many people arrive five or ten minutes early, because arriving exactly on time can feel like cutting it close. Children learn early to follow 「きまり」, the rules of the classroom. So, keeping your word in Japan is not just about big promises, but also about the small promise of showing up on time." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「守る|まもる」 means to guard or protect, so the phrase is literally guarding a promise so it doesn't break. The same verb gives 「お守り|おまもり」, shrine charms, and 「ルールを守る|ルールをまもる」, following rules.",
      "The guard kanji is read 「す」 in 「留守|るす」, being away from home, 「もり」 in 「子守唄|こもりうた」, a lullaby, and appears in 「見守る|みまもる」, to watch over, all about keeping something safe.",
      "Despite ending in 「る」, 「守る|まもる」 is a 「う」 verb, so it becomes 「守って|まもって」, and 「約束を守れなくて、すみません|やくそくをまもれなくて、すみません」 is a great apology to memorize.",
      "The same verb covers punctuality, 「時間を守る|じかんをまもる」, literally guarding the time, which Japan takes seriously, with trains apologizing for delays of just a few minutes."
    ],
    ja: "{田中|たなか}さんはいつも[約束を守ります|やくそくをまもります|やくそくをまもる]。",
    en: "Mr. Tanaka always keeps his promises." },
  "かんどうする|感動する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first, 「感|かん」, means feeling or sense. Look at its bottom: 「心|こころ」, heart. Like 「悲しい|かなしい」 from this same lesson, a feeling kanji with a heart underneath. The second kanji, 「動|どう」, means to move. On the right is 「力|ちから」, power. A way to picture it: moving things takes strength. You know this kanji from 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, zoo, a garden of moving things, animals, and from 「活動|かつどう」, activity. Put them together: your feelings move. English says the very same thing: to be moved or touched. It is a strong word, for when something really affects your heart. A great movie, a beautiful piece of music, a speech, a kind act, or a sports victory. Use 「に」 for the thing that moved you: 「その映画に感動しました|そのえいがにかんどうしました」, I was moved by that movie. Japanese TV and ads use this word a lot, promising a moving story. So, feeling plus moving: your heart is moved." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The feeling kanji at the start of this word builds a whole family of emotional words. 「感じる|かんじる」 is the verb to feel or sense. 「感想|かんそう」 means your impressions or thoughts about something, and teachers often ask 「感想はどうでしたか|かんそうはどうでしたか」, what did you think? 「感謝|かんしゃ」 is gratitude, a deep thank you. Now, three near-synonyms. This word is being moved, usually by art, stories, or performances. 「感激|かんげき」 is similar but more intense and excited, often from someone's kindness, like being thrilled to tears. 「感心|かんしん」 means being impressed by someone, admiring their effort or skill, like a child who studies hard. So, moved by a movie is this word, thrilled by kindness is 「感激|かんげき」, and impressed by effort is 「感心|かんしん」. Feelings, impressions, and gratitude, all from one heart kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan loves this word, and television runs on it. Every summer, the national high school baseball tournament at Koshien stadium fills TV screens with tearful players, scooping up dirt from the field as a memento after losing. Viewers across the country watch for exactly this feeling. After the Olympics, fans and the media often say 「感動をありがとう|かんどうをありがとう」, thank you for moving us, to their athletes. And there is a long-running summer charity TV marathon with emotional stories that has become famous for its moving moments. Some people even joke that these shows sell 「感動|かんどう」 like a product. Movie posters promise the most moving story of the year. So, in Japan, being moved is a shared national pastime, especially in summer." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Notice something interesting about how this verb works. English uses the passive: I was moved. Japanese uses an active verb. You yourself do the moving of feelings, and the cause is marked with 「に」. 「彼のスピーチに感動しました|かれのスピーチにかんどうしました」, I was moved by his speech. Don't try to make it passive. To describe something as moving, add 「的|てき」, which turns many nouns into 「な」 adjectives. 「感動的な映画|かんどうてきなえいが」 is a moving movie, and 「感動的な話|かんどうてきなはなし」 is a touching story. You can also use the plain noun with 「の」: 「感動の再会|かんどうのさいかい」, a moving reunion, a common phrase in news. So, an active verb with 「に」 for the cause, and 「的|てき」 for describing things that move you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「感|かん」 means feeling, with a heart at the bottom, and 「動|どう」 means move, with power on the right. Together your feelings move, so mark the cause with 「に」, as in 「その映画に感動しました|そのえいがにかんどうしました」.",
      "The feeling kanji gives 「感じる|かんじる」, 「感想|かんそう」, and 「感謝|かんしゃ」. This word is being moved by art, 「感激|かんげき」 is being thrilled by kindness, and 「感心|かんしん」 is being impressed by effort.",
      "Japanese TV runs on this feeling, from tearful Koshien baseball players to fans telling Olympians 「感動をありがとう|かんどうをありがとう」, thank you for moving us.",
      "Unlike the English passive, Japanese uses an active verb with 「に」 for the cause, and adding 「的|てき」 gives 「感動的な映画|かんどうてきなえいが」, a moving movie."
    ],
    ja: "その{映画|えいが}に[感動しました|かんどうしました|かんどうする]。",
    en: "I was moved by that movie." },
  "〜かい|〜回": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a counter for the number of times something happens. Look at the kanji: a small box inside a bigger box. It is often described as something going around and around, and that matches its meaning of turning or circling. The native verb with this kanji is 「回る|まわる」, to go around. So each time is one round. The reading as a counter is the Chinese-origin 「かい」. Here are the numbers, with the sound changes to watch. One time is 「一回|いっかい」, with a small pause. Two times is 「二回|にかい」. Three is 「三回|さんかい」. Six becomes 「六回|ろっかい」, and ten becomes 「十回|じゅっかい」. How many times is 「何回|なんかい」. To say how often in a period, use 「に」: 「一日に三回|いちにちにさんかい」, three times a day, or 「一週間に二回|いっしゅうかんににかい」, twice a week. A common mistake is mixing this up with 「〜ど」, which you learned for degrees of temperature. That one can also count times, but this counter is the everyday choice. You may also hear 「今回|こんかい」, this time. So, a box going around, one round each time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This round and round kanji turns up in some fun words. The best one is 「回転寿司|かいてんずし」, conveyor belt sushi, where plates of sushi circle the restaurant on a moving belt. 「回転|かいてん」 means rotation, spinning around. The native verbs are 「回る|まわる」, to go around, and 「回す|まわす」, to turn something, like a doorknob or a dial. Then there are the time words. 「今回|こんかい」 is this time. 「前回|ぜんかい」 is last time, and 「次回|じかい」 is next time. You will see these in TV dramas, where the narrator says 「次回もお楽しみに|じかいもおたのしみに」, look forward to next time. And in baseball, innings are counted with this word, so 「九回|きゅうかい」 is the ninth inning. So, sushi going around, a dial turning, and episodes rolling on." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the most useful phrases with this counter. When you didn't catch something, say 「もう一回お願いします|もういっかいおねがいします」, one more time, please. It is perfect for language learners. If you want to say many times, add 「も」: 「何回も|なんかいも」, over and over. 「何回も電話しました|なんかいもでんわしました」, I called many times. For just once, say 「一回だけ|いっかいだけ」. And here is a mini dialogue. Your friend asks 「京都に行ったことがありますか|きょうとにいったことがありますか」, have you ever been to Kyoto? You answer 「はい、二回あります|はい、にかいあります」, yes, twice. Notice that the experience pattern pairs naturally with this counter. A slightly more formal alternative is 「〜ど」, as in 「もう一度|もういちど」, which is a bit softer and politer. So, once more, over and over, and just once." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji looks like a box going around inside a box, like 「回る|まわる」, to go around, so each time is one round. Watch the sound changes in 「一回|いっかい」, 「六回|ろっかい」, and 「十回|じゅっかい」.",
      "The going-around kanji gives 「回転寿司|かいてんずし」, conveyor belt sushi, and the time words 「今回|こんかい」, 「前回|ぜんかい」, and 「次回|じかい」, this time, last time, and next time.",
      "Say 「もう一回お願いします|もういっかいおねがいします」 when you didn't catch something, 「何回も|なんかいも」 for over and over, and pair it with experiences, as in 「二回あります|にかいあります」."
    ],
    ja: "{一週間|いっしゅうかん}に[三回|さんかい|〜かい]{運動|うんどう}します。",
    en: "I exercise three times a week." },
  "〜キロ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from kilo, a word part meaning one thousand. It was created for the metric system in France, built from a Greek word for thousand. A kilogram is a thousand grams, and a kilometer is a thousand meters. In everyday speech, both English and Japanese shorten them. English says kilos for weight. Japanese goes further and uses this one short word for both weight and distance. So 「五キロ|ごキロ」 can mean five kilometers or five kilograms. How do you know which? Context does the work. If you are talking about running, walking, or how far a place is, it is distance. If you are talking about your weight, a bag of rice, or luggage, it is weight. The sounds are simple: the k gets its vowel, the l becomes the Japanese r sound, and you get 「キロ」. Other metric words come through the same way, like 「メートル」, meter, and 「グラム」, gram. Japan uses metric, so you will hear this a lot. Distances on road signs and weights at the supermarket are all metric. So, kilo means a thousand, and context tells you if it is meters or grams." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Since this one word covers both distance and weight, let's see it in action. At the supermarket, rice is sold in big bags of 「五キロ|ごキロ」 or 「十キロ|じゅっキロ」. On the highway, speed uses it too. 「時速百キロ|じそくひゃっキロ」 means a hundred kilometers per hour, with 「時速|じそく」 meaning speed per hour. When a friend says 「三キロ太りました|さんキロふとりました」, she means she gained three kilos, and when she says 「三キロ走りました|さんキロはしりました」, she ran three kilometers. The verb does all the work. If you need to be precise, you can use the full forms, 「キロメートル」 and 「キログラム」. And on food labels you will see 「キロカロリー」, kilocalories. So, rice bags, highway speeds, and running distances: one little word for all of them." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Counting with this word brings some small sound changes, the same kind you see with other counters that start with a 「か」 row sound. One is usually 「一キロ|いちキロ」, though you may hear a quick 「いっキロ」 in casual speech. Six becomes 「六キロ|ろっキロ」, with a small pause. Ten is 「十キロ|じゅっキロ」, also with a pause. A hundred is 「百キロ|ひゃっキロ」. Eight can be 「はちキロ」 or 「はっキロ」. The pattern is that the number gets clipped and a little stop slips in before the hard 「き」 sound. This is the same thing that happens with 「一回|いっかい」 and 「六回|ろっかい」. If you know one of these counters, you basically know them all. So, listen for that tiny pause, and your numbers will sound natural." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan uses the metric system, but some traditional units are still alive in daily life. Apartment rooms are measured in 「畳|じょう」, the size of a tatami mat, about one point six square meters. A listing might say 「六畳|ろくじょう」, a six mat room, even if the floor is wood. Land and office space is often given in 「坪|つぼ」, roughly two tatami mats. And when cooking rice, people measure it in 「合|ごう」, a cup of about a hundred and eighty milliliters, and rice cookers mark their lines in it. So while road signs and scales use metric, your apartment and your rice cooker may speak an older language. So, metric for distance and weight, but tatami mats for rooms." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from kilo, meaning a thousand, and Japanese uses this one short word for both kilometers and kilograms. Context tells you which, distance for running and weight for luggage or rice.",
      "Rice comes in 「五キロ|ごキロ」 bags and highway speed is 「時速百キロ|じそくひゃっキロ」, and the verb does the work: 「三キロ太りました|さんキロふとりました」 is weight, while 「三キロ走りました|さんキロはしりました」 is distance.",
      "Numbers clip before its hard sound, as in 「六キロ|ろっキロ」, 「十キロ|じゅっキロ」, and 「百キロ|ひゃっキロ」, the same tiny pause you hear in 「六回|ろっかい」.",
      "Japan is metric, but traditional units survive: rooms are measured in 「畳|じょう」, tatami mats, land in 「坪|つぼ」, and rice in 「合|ごう」 on rice cooker lines."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[五キロ|ごキロ|〜キロ]はしります。",
    en: "I run five kilometers every day." },
  "ぜんぶ|全部": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「全|ぜん」, means whole or complete. You know it from 「全然|ぜんぜん」, not at all, which is literally wholly, completely, used with a negative. The second kanji, 「部|ぶ」, means part, section, or department. You know it hidden in 「部屋|へや」, room, where it has an unusual reading. Rooms are parts of a house. Put them together: the whole of the parts, every single part, all of it. A nice clear picture. You use this word for things, not usually for people. For all the people, Japanese prefers 「みんな」. That is a common mistake for English speakers. For example, 「全部食べました|ぜんぶたべました」, I ate all of it. Add 「で」 and it means in total. At a shop, the clerk might tell you the total by starting with 「全部で|ぜんぶで」, all together it comes to. You might also hear 「全部でいくらですか|ぜんぶでいくらですか」, how much is it all together. So, whole plus part: every part, the whole thing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, meaning whole, builds many useful words. 「全員|ぜんいん」 means all members, everyone in a group. This is the one to use for all the people in a formal way, like 「全員来ました|ぜんいんきました」, everyone came. 「全国|ぜんこく」 is the whole country, nationwide. 「安全|あんぜん」 means safe, literally peaceful and whole. The second kanji, part or section, is just as busy. 「部活|ぶかつ」 is a school club, short for club activities, a huge part of Japanese school life. 「部長|ぶちょう」 is the head of a department at work, or the captain of a school club. And 「一部|いちぶ」 means one part, a portion. So you can say 「一部だけ|いちぶだけ」, only some of it, the opposite of this word. So, the whole country, all members, school clubs, and a portion." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word is flexible about where it sits. As an adverb, it goes before the verb: 「全部読みました|ぜんぶよみました」, I read all of it. Before a noun, it takes 「の」: 「全部の本|ぜんぶのほん」, all the books. And with 「で」, it means in total. Now an important contrast with the negative. 「全部わかりません|ぜんぶわかりません」 can sound like I understand none of it, but 「全部はわかりません|ぜんぶはわかりません」, with 「は」, means I don't understand all of it, only some. That little 「は」 makes it a partial negative. And don't confuse it with 「全然|ぜんぜん」, which means not at all, completely negative. So, 「全部じゃない|ぜんぶじゃない」 is not all, while 「全然|ぜんぜん」 is none. So, all, all of the, all together, and not quite all." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. The word sounds like zen boo. Picture a calm Zen monk sitting in front of a giant buffet table at a temple party. Everyone expects him to take a small, peaceful bowl of rice. Instead, he closes his eyes, takes a deep breath, and eats every single dish on the table. Sushi, noodles, cakes, all of it. When he is finished, the other guests stare at the empty table and shout, boo! But the monk just smiles and says he has reached oneness with the whole buffet. Zen, boo. A monk who ate the whole thing, every part. So, whenever you hear this word, see that empty table." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「全|ぜん」 means whole and 「部|ぶ」 means part, so it is every part, all of it. Use it for things rather than people, who take 「みんな」, and 「全部で|ぜんぶで」 means in total.",
      "The whole kanji gives 「全員|ぜんいん」, all members, 「全国|ぜんこく」, and 「安全|あんぜん」, while the part kanji gives 「部活|ぶかつ」, school clubs, and 「一部|いちぶ」, just a portion.",
      "With a negative, adding 「は」 makes 「全部はわかりません|ぜんぶはわかりません」 mean I don't understand all of it, which is different from 「全然|ぜんぜん」, not at all.",
      "It sounds like zen boo: a calm Zen monk eats every dish on a temple buffet, and the guests staring at the empty table shout boo."
    ],
    ja: "[全部|ぜんぶ]{食べました|たべました}。",
    en: "I ate all of it." },
  "〜ともうします|〜と申します": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a humble way to give your name. It is made of three parts. First, 「と」, the quoting particle, the same one you use with 「言う|いう」 to report what someone said. Then 「申します|もうします」, the polite form of 「申す|もうす」, which is a humble verb for to say. Humble means you lower yourself to show respect to the listener. So the phrase is literally I am humbly called, and it is a step more polite than 「といいます」. It shows up in specific situations: job interviews, business meetings, calling a company, speeches, or meeting your partner's parents. A typical line is 「わたくしは田中と申します|わたくしはたなかともうします」, my name is Tanaka. Notice it pairs naturally with 「わたくし」, the formal I, from this same lesson. Here is a common mistake. Never use this verb for someone else's name or words, because humble words are only for yourself and your own group. For other people you would use a respectful form instead. With friends, just say your name with 「です」. So, humbly saying your name, for your most polite moments." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The humble verb in this phrase turns up in some very important set expressions. The most famous is 「申し訳ありません|もうしわけありません」, I am terribly sorry. It is the formal apology you hear from shop staff, train announcements, and companies. Literally, it means there is no excuse I can humbly offer. A bit less formal is 「申し訳ない|もうしわけない」. Another family member is 「申し込む|もうしこむ」, to apply or sign up, as in signing up for a class or a contest. The noun is 「申込書|もうしこみしょ」, an application form. The humble verb also has relatives you will meet as you study humble language, like 「参る|まいる」 for go or come, and 「いたす」 for do. So, the same humble little verb gives you a name, a deep apology, and an application form." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase really shines on the telephone. When you call a company, you start with your name: 「もしもし、山田と申しますが|もしもし、やまだともうしますが」, hello, my name is Yamada. The 「が」 at the end softens it and leads into your request. Then you ask for the person: 「田中さんはいらっしゃいますか|たなかさんはいらっしゃいますか」, is Mr. Tanaka there? At work, people add their company first, like 「さくら銀行の山田と申します|さくらぎんこうのやまだともうします」, I'm Yamada from Sakura Bank. Notice they use only their family name, no title. Never add 「さん」 to your own name. It is also how you answer in a job interview, a formal meeting, or when you meet your partner's parents. So, company, then name, then this phrase: the polite way to say who you are." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese business, introducing yourself is a small ceremony. People exchange 「名刺|めいし」, business cards, almost every time they meet someone new. You hold your card with both hands, facing the other person so they can read it, and bow slightly while saying your name with this humble phrase. You receive their card with both hands too, look at it carefully, and keep it on the table during the meeting. Putting it straight in your pocket, or writing on it in front of them, is seen as rude. The introduction usually ends with 「よろしくお願いいたします|よろしくおねがいいたします」, a humbler version of the phrase you learned in lesson one. So, a card in both hands, a bow, a humble name, and a request for goodwill." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the quoting 「と」 plus 「申します|もうします」, a humble verb for say, making it a step politer than 「といいます」. Use it only for your own name, never for someone else's.",
      "The same humble verb gives the formal apology 「申し訳ありません|もうしわけありません」, literally no excuse I can offer, and 「申し込む|もうしこむ」, to apply or sign up.",
      "On the phone, start with 「もしもし、山田と申しますが|もしもし、やまだともうしますが」, give your company first at work, and never add 「さん」 to your own name.",
      "Business introductions mean exchanging 「名刺|めいし」 with both hands, bowing as you say your name with this phrase, and finishing with 「よろしくお願いいたします|よろしくおねがいいたします」."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}は{山田|やまだ}[と申します|ともうします|〜ともうします]。",
    en: "My name is Yamada." },
  "とくに|特に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji, 「特|とく」, means special or particular, with a Chinese-origin reading. Look at its parts. On the left is the cow radical, the same piece you see in 「牛乳|ぎゅうにゅう」, milk. On the right is 「寺|てら」, temple, the same kanji as in 「お寺|おてら」. A way to remember it: a special cow at the temple, standing out from the rest. That is a hook, not history. Then 「に」 makes the word an adverb, so it modifies a verb or a sentence. You use it to pick one thing out of a group. 「すしが好きです。特にまぐろが好きです|すしがすきです。とくにまぐろがすきです」. I like sushi, especially tuna. It also has a very useful negative use. When someone asks if you have a question or a request, you can answer 「特にないです|とくにないです」, nothing in particular. Compare 「別に|べつに」, from lesson seven, which can also mean nothing in particular. But 「別に|べつに」 can sound a little cold or uninterested, while this word sounds neutral and polite. So, a special cow at the temple: especially." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji, meaning special, appears in many words you will see around Japan. 「特別|とくべつ」 means special, as in a special day or a special price. On train platforms, you will see 「特急|とっきゅう」, the limited express, a faster train that skips small stations. Notice the sound change there: 「とく」 becomes 「とっ」 before the 「きゅう」. In supermarkets, a sign saying 「特売|とくばい」 means special sale, so prices are low. And 「特大|とくだい」 means extra large, often on drinks or burgers. In each word, the kanji marks something as standing out from the ordinary. So, special day, express train, bargain sale, and extra large: all things that stand apart." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adverb can go in a few places. At the start of a sentence, it picks one item from the previous one: I like fruit. 「特にりんごが好きです|とくにりんごがすきです」. In the middle, before an adjective, it means especially or particularly: 「今日は特に寒いですね|きょうはとくにさむいですね」, it's especially cold today. With a negative, it softens things: 「特に忙しくないです|とくにいそがしくないです」, I'm not especially busy. That is gentler than a flat not busy. And as a full answer, 「特にない|とくにない」 or 「特にありません|とくにありません」 means nothing in particular. In meetings, when the chair asks for any other business, people often reply 「特にありません|とくにありません」. So, especially before an adjective, and not particularly with a negative." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "A couple of words sound close to this one, so let's keep them apart. 「得意|とくい」 means good at something, as in 「料理が得意です|りょうりがとくいです」, I'm good at cooking. Same 「とく」 at the start, but a different kanji and a very different meaning. Then there is 「遠く|とおく」, far away, with a long 「とお」. That stretched vowel is the clue. Our word has a short 「と」 and a crisp 「く」, followed by 「に」. Try saying them in a row: 「とくに」, 「とくい」, 「とおく」. Especially, good at, and far away. Here is a silly sentence to practice: 「特に料理が得意です|とくにりょうりがとくいです」, I'm especially good at cooking. Two 「とく」 words side by side. So, listen for the ending and the vowel length." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「特|とく」 has a cow radical next to 「寺|てら」, picturing a special cow at the temple, and with 「に」 it picks one thing out of a group. 「特にないです|とくにないです」 politely means nothing in particular.",
      "The special kanji marks things standing out in 「特別|とくべつ」, special, 「特急|とっきゅう」, the limited express, 「特売|とくばい」, a sale, and 「特大|とくだい」, extra large.",
      "Before an adjective it means especially, as in 「今日は特に寒いですね|きょうはとくにさむいですね」, and with a negative it softens things, as in 「特に忙しくないです|とくにいそがしくないです」.",
      "Keep it apart from 「得意|とくい」, good at, and 「遠く|とおく」, far away, by listening for the short 「と」 and the 「に」 ending."
    ],
    ja: "すしが{好き|すき}です。[特に|とくに]まぐろが{好き|すき}です。",
    en: "I like sushi. I especially like tuna." },
  "いちにち|一日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two simple kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings: 「一|いち」, one, and 「日|にち」, day. Together: one day, as a length of time. You use it for durations and rates, like 「一日に三回|いちにちにさんかい」, three times a day, or 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day long, a word you will meet soon. Here is the big trap. The same two kanji, written exactly the same way, are also read 「ついたち」, which means the first day of the month, a date. That reading is very old. It is often traced to the idea of the moon rising, since old calendars started each month with the new moon. So when you see these two kanji, ask yourself: is this one day of time, or the first of the month? Listen and look at the context. The day kanji, 「日|にち」, is in many words you know: 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, 「日曜日|にちようび」, Sunday, and on its own as 「日|ひ」, day. A way to remember the split: counting one day is the plain number word, while the date has its own special name. So, one plus day: one day long." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The day kanji started as a real picture of the sun, a circle with a dot in the middle, which later became squared off. That is why it means both sun and day. It is one of the busiest kanji in Japanese, with many readings. Here it is 「にち」. Read 「ひ」, it means day or sun on its own, as in 「暑い日|あついひ」, a hot day. In 「日曜日|にちようび」, the second one turns into 「び」. In the date words like 「二日|ふつか」, it is 「か」. And read 「じつ」, it appears in 「休日|きゅうじつ」, a day off, and 「平日|へいじつ」, a weekday. Then 「今日|きょう」, today, is a special reading for the whole word. The first kanji, one, is just a single line. So, one stroke and a sun: one day, and a sun kanji with at least five voices." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word combines with particles in some very handy ways. With 「中|じゅう」, it becomes 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day long. With 「おき」, it becomes 「一日おき|いちにちおき」, every other day: 「一日おきに走ります|いちにちおきにはしります」, I run every other day. With 「も」 and a negative, it means not even one day. 「一日も休みませんでした|いちにちもやすみませんでした」, I didn't miss a single day. That pattern works with other amounts too, like 「一回も|いっかいも」, not even once. And with a positive, 「一日も早く|いちにちもはやく」 means as soon as possible, literally even one day sooner, often used in wishes like hoping someone gets well soon. So, all day, every other day, not a single day, and as soon as possible." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese has a short, catchy saying built on this word: 「一日一善|いちにちいちぜん」, one good deed a day. It is a motto you might see on a classroom wall or hear from a grandparent. Pick up some litter, help someone with a heavy bag, give up your seat on the train. Many sayings use this same four kanji rhythm, two pairs back to back. Another one you may meet is 「一期一会|いちごいちえ」, treasure each meeting, because it will never happen the same way again. It comes from the tea ceremony. The day word also gives the business phrase 「一日でも早く|いちにちでもはやく」, even one day sooner, used when people really want something done. So, one good deed a day, and a culture that likes to pack big ideas into four small kanji." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is one plus day, a length of time, as in 「一日に三回|いちにちにさんかい」. The same kanji read 「ついたち」 mean the first of the month, so check the context.",
      "The day kanji began as a picture of the sun and has many readings: 「にち」, 「ひ」, 「び」, 「か」 in dates, 「じつ」 in 「休日|きゅうじつ」, plus the special 「今日|きょう」.",
      "It makes 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day, 「一日おき|いちにちおき」, every other day, and with 「も」 and a negative, 「一日も休みませんでした|いちにちもやすみませんでした」, not a single day missed.",
      "The motto 「一日一善|いちにちいちぜん」 means one good deed a day, one of many four-kanji sayings like 「一期一会|いちごいちえ」, treasure each meeting."
    ],
    ja: "[一日|いちにち]{三時間|さんじかん}べんきょうします。",
    en: "I study three hours a day." },
  "ふつか|二日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "From two to ten, Japanese uses old native numbers for days, not the Chinese-origin ones you use for counting money or time. The native two is 「ふた」. You know it from 「二つ|ふたつ」, two things, and 「二人|ふたり」, two people. Add 「か」, an old native word for days, and the sound shortens a little to 「ふつか」. This word has two jobs. It means two days, a length of time, and also the second day of the month, a date. Context tells you which. 「京都に二日いました|きょうとにふつかいました」 means I was in Kyoto for two days. Here is a family member you already know: 「二日酔い|ふつかよい」, hangover. It is literally drunk into the second day, the morning after. And you know 「二三日|にさんにち」, two or three days, which switches back to the Chinese-origin numbers. That is because it is a rough range, not one of the fixed day words. A way to remember it: the native two that gives you 「二つ|ふたつ」 also gives you two days. So, native two, plus the old day sound." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a pair that confuses even advanced learners. This word is the second. But 「二十日|はつか」, the twentieth, sounds almost the same. Both have three beats and end in 「つか」. The difference is just the first sound. The second starts with 「ふ」, the soft breathy sound from 「ふたつ」. The twentieth starts with 「は」, like a laugh. Say them slowly: 「ふつか」, 「はつか」. And the twentieth has no connection to the native number two at all. It is a special word to memorize. If someone says they are coming on 「はつか」, they mean the twentieth, not the second. Mixing them up means arriving eighteen days off. A way to keep them apart: the second is soft, like blowing out a candle, and the twentieth is a laugh, ha. So, blow for two, laugh for twenty." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since this word means both two days and the second, Japanese has tools to make it clear. For a length of time, you can add 「間|かん」: 「二日間|ふつかかん」, a two day period. That removes all doubt. For dates, you often use 「に」: 「二日に会いましょう|ふつかにあいましょう」, let's meet on the second. To count backward or forward, add 「前|まえ」 or 「後|ご」. 「二日前|ふつかまえ」 is two days ago, and 「二日後|ふつかご」 is two days later. You can also say 「二日目|ふつかめ」, the second day of a trip or event, as in 「旅行の二日目に雨が降りました|りょこうのふつかめにあめがふりました」, it rained on the second day of the trip. So, add 「間|かん」 for duration, 「に」 for the date, and 「目|め」 for the second day of something." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, the second of January has its own traditions, because it falls in the New Year holidays. Traditionally, it is the day for 「書き初め|かきぞめ」, the first calligraphy of the year. People, especially children, write a lucky word or phrase with a brush, like a wish for the year. It is also linked to 「初夢|はつゆめ」, the first dream of the new year. Customs vary, but it is often said to be the dream on the night of the first or the second. A lucky first dream, according to an old saying, features Mount Fuji, a hawk, or an eggplant, in that order: 「一富士二鷹三茄子|いちふじにたかさんなすび」. Nobody fully agrees why eggplants, but the phrase is famous. So, the second of January: brush writing, and hoping to dream of Mount Fuji." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native two, 「ふた」, as in 「二つ|ふたつ」, plus the old day sound 「か」, meaning both two days and the second of the month. It also gives 「二日酔い|ふつかよい」, a hangover.",
      "The twentieth, 「二十日|はつか」, sounds almost the same, so remember the second starts with a soft blowing 「ふ」 and the twentieth with a laughing 「は」.",
      "Add 「間|かん」 for a duration, 「二日間|ふつかかん」, use 「に」 for the date, and 「目|め」 for the second day of something, as in 「二日目|ふつかめ」.",
      "January second is traditionally the day for 「書き初め|かきぞめ」, the first calligraphy of the year, and is linked to 「初夢|はつゆめ」, the lucky first dream."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}に[二日|ふつか]いました。",
    en: "I was in Kyoto for two days." },
  "みっか|三日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese three, 「み」. You know it from 「三つ|みっつ」, three things. Add the old native day sound 「か」, and a little pause slips in between, giving 「みっか」. That pause is the same one you hear in 「三つ|みっつ」. Like the other day words, it does double duty. It means three days, a length of time, and also the third day of the month, a date. Context will tell you which. Here is a fun expression with it: 「三日坊主|みっかぼうず」, a three day monk. It means someone who starts something, like a diet, a diary, or studying, and gives up after just three days. It comes from the idea of someone who becomes a monk but cannot stand the strict life and quits after three days. If you start a new habit, you can joke, I hope I don't become a three day monk. A way to remember it: three days is just long enough to quit. A related word for New Year: the first three days of January are a big holiday period in Japan, called 「三が日|さんがにち」, which uses the Chinese-origin three instead. So, native three plus the day sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The native three in this word appears in a beautiful sky word: 「三日月|みかづき」, a crescent moon, literally the third day moon. In the old lunar calendar, each month began with the new moon, so on the third day, the moon was just a thin curved sliver. Notice the sound shift: no pause here, and 「つき」 becomes 「づき」. There is also a history phrase, 「三日天下|みっかてんか」, a three day reign. It is used for anyone whose power or success lasts only a short time. It is often linked to a famous general who seized power in the sixteenth century but was defeated very soon after, though his rule actually lasted closer to two weeks. And you already know 「三つ|みっつ」, three things. So, three things, a crescent moon, and a reign that ended almost before it began." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word combines nicely with patterns you know. To say how often, use 「に」 plus the counter for times: 「三日に一回|みっかにいっかい」, once every three days. 「三日に一回、ジョギングをします|みっかにいっかい、ジョギングをします」, I jog once every three days. For a clear length of time, add 「間|かん」: 「三日間|みっかかん」, for three days. To ask how many days, use 「何日|なんにち」: 「何日休みましたか|なんにちやすみましたか」, how many days did you take off? Answer with 「三日です|みっかです」. And 「三日目|みっかめ」 is the third day, like the third day of a diet, which, if you remember the three day monk, might also be the last. So, once every three days, three days long, and the dangerous third day." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This word has a small pause in the middle: 「みっ」, then 「か」. Hold that pause for one beat. Without it, you get 「みか」, which sounds like the start of 「みかん」, the mandarin orange, a winter favorite in Japan. And with 「づき」 after it and no pause, it becomes 「三日月|みかづき」, the crescent moon. So these three are cousins in sound: the third, an orange, and the moon. Practice them in a row: 「みっか」, 「みかん」, 「みかづき」. Feel how only the date has that little stop. A way to remember it: imagine you are counting the days until you eat a mandarin, and on the third day, you stop for one beat, and then bite. So, a pause for the date, no pause for the orange or the moon." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native three, 「み」, with the same pause as 「三つ|みっつ」, plus the day sound 「か」. Remember 「三日坊主|みっかぼうず」, a three-day monk who quits a new habit after three days.",
      "The native three appears in 「三日月|みかづき」, the crescent moon, literally the third day moon, and in 「三日天下|みっかてんか」, a reign or success that lasts only a short time.",
      "Say how often with 「三日に一回|みっかにいっかい」, once every three days, use 「三日間|みっかかん」 for a clear duration, and ask with 「何日|なんにち」.",
      "Hold the pause for one beat, because without it you get the start of 「みかん」, the mandarin, or 「三日月|みかづき」, the moon, which have no pause."
    ],
    ja: "かぜで[三日|みっか]{休みました|やすみました}。",
    en: "I was off for three days with a cold." },
  "よっか|四日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese four, 「よ」. You know it from 「四つ|よっつ」, four things. Add the old native day sound 「か」, and a little pause slips in, giving 「よっか」, just like 「よっつ」. Like the other day words, it means four days as a length of time, and also the fourth day of the month as a date. Here is the big trap. It sounds a lot like 「八日|ようか」, eight days, or the eighth of the month. The difference is tiny but important. Four has a short 「よ」 and then a small pause, 「よっか」. Eight has a long vowel, 「よう」, and no pause, 「ようか」. If you make a date with a friend and mix them up, you will show up four days off. So when you hear it, listen for the pause or the long vowel. A way to remember it: four is short and choppy, with a little stop in it, while eight is stretched out long and smooth. So, native four, with a pause, not to be confused with eight." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The number four has an unlucky side in Japan. Its Chinese-origin reading, 「し」, sounds the same as 「死|し」, death. Because of that, some hospitals and hotels skip room numbers with four, and people avoid giving gifts in sets of four. That is also one reason the native reading 「よん」 is so common when counting, and why this date uses the native 「よ」. You will still see 「し」 in some words, but people tend to avoid it in sensitive places, like hospitals. Nine has a similar problem, since 「く」 sounds like 「苦|く」, suffering. A date like the fourth is perfectly normal and nobody worries about it, but the custom explains why the native sounds are everywhere. So, four can be unlucky, and the native reading helps keep things cheerful." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The number four has a surprising number of readings, and this date shows one of them. In 「四つ|よっつ」, four things, and this word, it is 「よ」 with a pause. In 「四人|よにん」, four people, and 「四時|よじ」, four o'clock, it is 「よ」 without a pause. When counting, it is 「よん」, as in 「四回|よんかい」, four times. And here is a surprise. The month April is 「四月|しがつ」, always with 「し」, never 「よん」. So April fourth is 「四月四日|しがつよっか」, two different readings of four in one date. There is even a city in Mie prefecture called 「四日市|よっかいち」, fourth day market. It got its name from markets held on days with a four. So, one number, many voices, and April is the odd one out." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for keeping four and eight apart. Four is 「よっか」, short and choppy with a stop in the middle. Picture a yo-yo that snaps and stops on the fourth try. Yo, stop, ka! Now eight, 「ようか」, is long and smooth. Picture a long, slow yawn, yooo-ka, stretched out like a lazy weekend. So the yo-yo that snaps is four, and the long yawn is eight. When you make plans, picture which one you hear. If you hear the snap, you are meeting on the fourth. If you hear the yawn, it's the eighth. This little scene can save you from showing up four days early or four days late. So, a snapping yo-yo for four, and a long yawn for eight." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native four, 「よ」, with the same pause as 「四つ|よっつ」, plus 「か」. Don't confuse it with 「八日|ようか」, which has a long vowel and no pause.",
      "Four is unlucky because its reading 「し」 sounds like 「死|し」, death, so hospitals may skip room fours, which helps explain why native readings like this date's 「よ」 are everywhere.",
      "Four has many readings: 「よ」 here and in 「四人|よにん」, 「よん」 when counting, and always 「し」 in 「四月|しがつ」, so April fourth is 「四月四日|しがつよっか」.",
      "Picture a yo-yo that snaps and stops for the choppy four, and a long slow yawn for the smooth eight, so you don't show up four days off."
    ],
    ja: "{旅行|りょこう}は[四日|よっか]です。",
    en: "The trip is four days." },
  "いつか|五日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese five, 「いつ」. You know it from 「五つ|いつつ」, five things. Add the old native day sound 「か」, and you get 「いつか」. Like the other day words, it means five days as a length of time, and also the fifth day of the month as a date. Now here is a fun coincidence. 「いつか」 also means someday, sometime, built from 「いつ」, when, plus 「か」, the question particle. So the same sound means the fifth day and someday. They are different words that happen to sound the same. In writing, the date one uses kanji, while someday is usually in hiragana. You can tell them apart from the sentence. 「五日に会いましょう|いつかにあいましょう」 is let's meet on the fifth. But 「いつか行きたいです|いつかいきたいです」 is I want to go someday. A way to remember it: someday, you will remember the fifth. Also note the native counting pattern: 「一つ|ひとつ」, 「二つ|ふたつ」, 「三つ|みっつ」, 「四つ|よっつ」, 「五つ|いつつ」. The day words follow those same native numbers. So, native five, and it sounds just like someday." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The fifth day of the fifth month is one of Japan's national holidays: 「こどもの日|こどものひ」, Children's Day. Said in full, the date is 「五月五日|ごがついつか」. Notice the month uses the Chinese-origin five, and the day uses the native five. Around this date, families fly 「こいのぼり」, huge carp streamers shaped like fish, from poles outside their homes. Carp are known for swimming up waterfalls in Chinese legend, so they stand for strength and success, and families hope their children will grow up strong. People also display samurai helmets and eat rice cakes wrapped in oak leaves. The holiday was traditionally a boys' festival, while girls had their own day in March, but today it is for all children. So, May fifth: carp swimming in the sky." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This word sits in a cluster of very similar sounds, all built from 「いつ」. 「いつ」 alone means when. 「いつか」, written in hiragana, means someday. 「いつも」 means always. 「いつつ」 means five things. And this word means the fifth, or five days. Five sounds, all starting with 「いつ」. Here is a sentence to practice: 「いつか、五日間旅行したいです|いつか、いつかかんりょこうしたいです」, someday I want to travel for five days. That sentence has both meanings side by side. A good way to tell them apart is kanji in writing, and the sentence frame when listening. If a number or date is involved, like 「五月|ごがつ」 before it, it is the fifth. If it sits with a wish, like want to, it is someday. So, when, someday, always, five things, and the fifth: one sound, many jobs." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Like the other day words, this one can be a date or a length of time. To make duration clear, add 「間|かん」: 「五日間|いつかかん」, for five days. For a date, 「五日に|いつかに」, on the fifth. To ask what date it is, use 「何日|なんにち」: 「今日は何日ですか|きょうはなんにちですか」, what's the date today? Answer 「五日です|いつかです」. For the fifth day of something, use 「目|め」: 「五日目|いつかめ」. And for ago and later, 「五日前|いつかまえ」 and 「五日後|いつかご」. Notice how all of these keep the native reading. Once you know the date word, the add-ons are the same across all the day words. So, learn the pattern once, and use it for every day from two to ten." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native five, 「いつ」, as in 「五つ|いつつ」, plus 「か」. It sounds exactly like 「いつか」, someday, so tell them apart by the sentence.",
      "May fifth, 「五月五日|ごがついつか」, is 「こどもの日|こどものひ」, Children's Day, when families fly 「こいのぼり」, carp streamers standing for strength and success.",
      "Many words start with 「いつ」: when, someday, 「いつも」, 「いつつ」, and this word, so a nearby date means the fifth, while a wish means someday.",
      "Like the other day words, add 「間|かん」 for duration, 「に」 for the date, 「目|め」 for the fifth day, and 「前|まえ」 or 「後|ご」 for ago and later."
    ],
    ja: "[五日|いつか]{東京|とうきょう}にいました。",
    en: "I was in Tokyo for five days." },
  "むいか|六日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese six, 「む」. You know it from 「六つ|むっつ」, six things. Then comes 「いか」 instead of the plain 「か」 you heard in the earlier days. That extra 「い」 is just part of how this word settled over time, so learn it as a set. Like the other day words, it means six days as a length of time, and also the sixth day of the month as a date. Here is the full native pattern so far: 「二日|ふつか」, 「三日|みっか」, 「四日|よっか」, 「五日|いつか」, 「六日|むいか」. Notice how each one starts with the native number from the counting set 「ふたつ」, 「みっつ」, 「よっつ」, 「いつつ」, 「むっつ」. The day words are like cousins of those numbers. A way to remember the odd 「い」: the ending 「いか」 sounds just like the Japanese word for squid. Picture a squid waiting patiently for six days. It is a silly picture, but it holds the sound. So, native six, with an extra 「い」 before the day sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's finish the native day series, since this word is right in the middle. After six comes 「七日|なのか」, from the native seven, 「なな」. Then 「八日|ようか」, eight, then 「九日|ここのか」, nine, and 「十日|とおか」, ten, with a long vowel. After ten, Japanese switches to Chinese-origin numbers plus 「にち」, like 「十一日|じゅういちにち」. But there are exceptions to watch. The fourteenth is 「十四日|じゅうよっか」, and the twenty-fourth is 「二十四日|にじゅうよっか」, keeping the native four. And the twentieth is the special word 「二十日|はつか」. So the native days run from two to ten, plus those few special cases. So, six sits in the middle of a series you can now finish: seven, eight, nine, and ten." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese calendars often carry a hidden six-day cycle called 「六曜|ろくよう」. Each day is labeled with one of six fortune names, repeating in order. The luckiest is 「大安|たいあん」, great peace, which is the most popular day for weddings, so wedding halls book up fast and charge more. The unluckiest is 「仏滅|ぶつめつ」, and some couples avoid marrying then, so venues may offer discounts. Another one, 「友引|ともびき」, is often avoided for funerals, because its name can be read as pulling a friend along. Many people say they don't believe in it, but they still check it for weddings and funerals. The system came from China and became popular in Japan much later. So, a six-day cycle of luck, quietly printed on many Japanese calendars." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The number six changes its sound depending on what follows it, so let's line them up. In this word, it is 「む」. In 「六つ|むっつ」, six things, it is 「むっ」, with a pause. When counting, it is 「ろく」, as in 「六時|ろくじ」, six o'clock, and 「六月|ろくがつ」, June. Before a hard 「か」 sound, it often clips to 「ろっ」, as in 「六回|ろっかい」, six times, and 「六個|ろっこ」, six pieces. So you have three voices: 「む」 in native words, 「ろく」 in normal counting, and 「ろっ」 before hard sounds. This word is also the only native day with that extra 「い」 before 「か」, so it has a rhythm all its own: 「む」, 「い」, 「か」, three even beats. So, three sixes, and one squid shaped date." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native six, 「む」, as in 「六つ|むっつ」, but with 「いか」 instead of plain 「か」. Remember the odd ending by picturing a squid, 「いか」, waiting six days.",
      "After six come 「七日|なのか」, 「八日|ようか」, 「九日|ここのか」, and 「十日|とおか」, then Chinese-origin numbers, except 「十四日|じゅうよっか」, 「二十四日|にじゅうよっか」, and 「二十日|はつか」.",
      "Calendars carry a six-day luck cycle called 「六曜|ろくよう」, where 「大安|たいあん」 is popular for weddings and 「仏滅|ぶつめつ」 is the unlucky day couples avoid.",
      "Six has three voices: 「む」 in native words, 「ろく」 in normal counting, and 「ろっ」 before hard sounds, as in 「六回|ろっかい」, and this date alone adds an extra 「い」."
    ],
    ja: "[六日|むいか]{雨|あめ}がふりました。",
    en: "It rained for six days." },
  "なのか|七日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This uses the native Japanese seven, 「なな」, which you know from 「七つ|ななつ」, seven things. In the day word, the second sound softens to 「なの」, and then the day sound 「か」 is added, giving 「なのか」. You may sometimes hear an older form, 「なぬか」, but 「なのか」 is the standard one today. Like the other day words, it means seven days as a length of time, and also the seventh day of the month as a date. Seven days, of course, make a week, 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」. Here is a nice cultural note. In Japan, the seventh of July is the star festival, 「七夕|たなばた」, when people write wishes on strips of paper and hang them on bamboo. It celebrates two star lovers who can meet only once a year. It is written with the seven kanji, but has its own special reading. Now look at the whole set you are learning: 「ふつか」, 「みっか」, 「よっか」, 「いつか」, 「むいか」, 「なのか」, and then 「ようか」, 「ここのか」, and 「とおか」. All native numbers. A way to remember it: seven days, one full week, 「なのか」. So, native seven, softened to 「なの」, plus the day sound." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji in this word are old friends, but they wear unusual readings here. The first kanji, seven, is a simple shape, just two strokes. It has two common readings. The Chinese-origin one is 「しち」, as in 「七時|しちじ」, seven o'clock, and 「七月|しちがつ」, July. The native one is 「なな」, as in 「七つ|ななつ」 and 「七人|ななにん」, seven people. In this day word, the native one shows up in its softened form, 「なの」. The second kanji, sun or day, has even more faces. It is 「にち」 in 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, 「び」 in 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, birthday, 「ひ」 when it stands alone as day or sun, and the little native 「か」 here. Because native readings are used for both halves, this word feels very Japanese, not borrowed from Chinese. Once you hit eleven, the Chinese-origin readings take over. So two tiny kanji, two native readings, and one very Japanese word for the seventh." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Seven days carries some special weight in Japanese life. On the seventh of January, many families eat 「七草がゆ|ななくさがゆ」, a light rice porridge with seven spring herbs. It is said to bring good health for the year and to rest a stomach tired from New Year's feasting. Notice that it uses the native 「なな」 again. Seven days also matters in Buddhist customs around death. After someone passes away, families hold a memorial on the seventh day, called 「初七日|しょなのか」, the first seventh day. Traditionally there were memorials every seven days until the forty-ninth day, which is a very important one. Nowadays the seventh-day service is often held right after the funeral, to save relatives another trip. And of course, a baby's naming has its own seventh-night celebration in some families. So this little date word links to fresh herbs in winter and to the quiet rhythm of mourning. Seven days in Japan are about renewal and remembrance." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There are a few words that can trip your ear here. First, the question word 「何日|なんにち」, what day or how many days, also starts with an n and has three beats. If someone asks 「何日ですか|なんにちですか」, the answer might be 「なのかです」, and the two sound oddly similar at first. Listen for the 「ん」 in the question. Second, there is the grammar word 「のに」, even though, which shares that 「の」 in the middle. In a sentence, the date word usually comes before particles like 「に」 or 「から」, so context will save you. Third, do not mix it with 「七か月|ななかげつ」, seven months, which has extra beats and uses the full 「なな」. A good habit is to say the date words out loud as a little chant, from 「ついたち」 to 「とおか」, and feel the rhythm. Your ear will learn the shape. Three beats, soft and round, and it belongs to the seventh." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native seven, 「なな」, softened to 「なの」, plus the day sound 「か」, meaning seven days or the seventh. Seven days make a full week, 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」.",
      "The seven kanji is 「しち」 or 「なな」, and the day kanji has many faces, so here both halves wear native readings, 「なの」 and 「か」, making it a very Japanese word.",
      "On January seventh families eat 「七草がゆ|ななくさがゆ」, porridge with seven spring herbs, and after a death a memorial is held on the seventh day, 「初七日|しょなのか」.",
      "Don't confuse it with the question 「何日|なんにち」, which has an 「ん」, or with 「七か月|ななかげつ」, seven months, which uses the full 「なな」."
    ],
    ja: "{一週間|いっしゅうかん}は[七日|なのか]です。",
    en: "One week is seven days." },
  "ようか|八日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Look at the two kanji first. 「八|はち」 is eight, and 「日|ひ」 is day or sun. But neither kanji uses its usual reading here. Instead, the word is built from the old native Japanese way of counting. The native eight is 「や」, the same sound you hear in 「八つ|やっつ」, eight things. Long ago this word was something like 「やうか」, and over time that 「やう」 smoothed out into the long 「よう」 sound you hear today. The 「か」 at the end is the native word for days, the same 「か」 you find in 「三日|みっか」 and 「五日|いつか」. This one word does two jobs. It can mean a length of time, eight days, or a date, the eighth of the month. The big trap is its neighbor four days, 「四日|よっか」. They sound very close, so listen for the long 「よう」 in eight versus the short, clipped 「よっ」 in four. A way to remember it: eight is the bigger number, so it gets the longer, stretched-out sound. To recap, native eight plus native days, with a long vowel that keeps it apart from four." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Eight is a famously lucky number in Japan. The reason is the shape of its kanji. Its two strokes spread apart as they go down, like a fan opening up or a road getting wider. Japanese people call this 「末広がり|すえひろがり」, widening toward the end, and it suggests a future that keeps growing and getting better. That is why eight is welcome at weddings, shop openings, and other new beginnings, and why some people like dates with an eight in them. Folding fans are given as gifts for the same reason. This is different from Chinese culture, where eight is lucky mainly because of its sound. In Japan, it is the shape that does the work. Compare this with four and nine, which are often avoided because they sound like death, 「し」, and suffering, 「く」. So when the eighth of the month rolls around, you can think of it as a quietly lucky day. A widening fan, a widening future." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The native eight, 「や」, hides in some useful words. The first is 「八つ|やっつ」, eight things, which you count with every time you list objects. Then there is a surprise. A greengrocer, the little shop that sells fruit and vegetables, is a 「八百屋|やおや」. It is literally eight hundred shop, because here eight hundred just means a huge number of things for sale. Both 「や」 and 「お」 come from native counting. Another lovely one is 「八重桜|やえざくら」, double-flowered cherry blossoms, with many layers of petals, literally eight layers. And there is 「八つ当たり|やつあたり」, which means taking your anger out on everyone around you, hitting out in all eight directions. In all of these, eight stands for many, or all around. The day word uses that same 「や」, which grew into the long 「よう」. So when you see eight with a native reading, think many, plenty, everywhere." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Picture an octopus with eight legs, sitting in a hotel lobby with eight tiny suitcases. It has booked an eight-day trip. The receptionist asks how long it is staying, and the octopus stretches out all eight legs, yawns a long, lazy yawn, and says 「ようか」. That long 「よう」 is the octopus stretching. Now picture a different guest, a small, nervous mouse with four little legs. It is staying for four days, and it answers quickly with a short, clipped 「よっか」, as if it is in a hurry. Long stretch for eight legs, short hop for four. Whenever you hear one of these two words, ask yourself whether it was a lazy octopus stretch or a quick mouse hop. That one picture keeps two easily confused words apart. The octopus stays eight days, and it takes its time saying so." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native eight, 「や」, as in 「八つ|やっつ」, which smoothed into a long 「よう」, plus 「か」. Eight is the bigger number, so it gets the longer sound, unlike the clipped 「よっか」 for four.",
      "Eight is lucky in Japan because its kanji spreads wider toward the bottom, 「末広がり|すえひろがり」, suggesting a future that keeps growing.",
      "The native eight means many in 「八百屋|やおや」, a greengrocer, 「八重桜|やえざくら」, layered cherry blossoms, and 「八つ当たり|やつあたり」, lashing out in all directions.",
      "Picture an eight-legged octopus stretching and yawning a long 「ようか」 for its eight-day stay, while a four-legged mouse answers with a quick, clipped 「よっか」."
    ],
    ja: "[八日|ようか]{旅行|りょこう}しました。",
    en: "I traveled for eight days." },
  "ここのか|九日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji are 「九|く」 nine and 「日|ひ」 day, but the word skips their usual readings and uses the old native Japanese count instead. The native nine is 「ここの」, which you already know from 「九つ|ここのつ」, nine things. Drop the 「つ」, add 「か」, the native word for days, and you get this word. That same 「か」 shows up all through the set: 「二日|ふつか」, 「三日|みっか」, 「八日|ようか」. Like the others, it works two ways. It can be a length of time, nine days, or a date, the ninth of the month. It is the longest of the native day words, four full beats, so give each beat its time. Do not confuse it with the Chinese-origin nine, 「九|きゅう」 or 「く」, which you use for clock times like 「九時|くじ」, nine o'clock. Those readings never appear in this word. A way to remember it: 「ここの」 sounds a bit like the word for here, 「ここ」, so picture yourself staying right here for nine days. To recap, native nine plus native days, four beats long." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Nine has a mixed reputation in Japan. One of its Chinese-origin readings is 「く」, which sounds the same as the word for suffering or hardship, 「苦|く」. Because of that, some hospitals and hotels skip room nine, and people may avoid giving sets of nine as gifts. But nine also has a happier side. In the old calendar borrowed from China, odd numbers were strong, and nine was the strongest single digit. So the ninth day of the ninth month became a seasonal festival, 「重陽の節句|ちょうようのせっく」, the festival of double nine. It is linked with chrysanthemums, and people once drank sake with chrysanthemum petals floating in it, wishing for long life. Today it is quiet compared with Girls' Day or Children's Day, but some shrines still celebrate it. Notice that the day word itself uses the native 「ここの」, so it carries none of the gloomy sound of 「く」. A gentle way to remember: the native nine is safe, soft, and four beats long." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will use this word in two kinds of sentences, and the particles change. When it is a date, a point on the calendar, you usually add 「に」: 「九日に会いましょう|ここのかにあいましょう」, let's meet on the ninth. When it is a length of time, you normally do not add 「に」: 「九日かかりました|ここのかかかりました」, it took nine days. Notice that you can get two 「か」 sounds in a row there, one from the day word and one from the verb, so say it slowly and clearly. If you want to make the length meaning crystal clear, add 「間|かん」 to get 「九日間|ここのかかん」, a nine-day period. Here is a mini dialogue. Someone asks, 「お休みは何日までですか|おやすみはなんにちまでですか」, until what day is your vacation? You answer, 「九日までです|ここのかまでです」, until the ninth. With the right particle, one word handles dates and durations." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native nine, 「ここの」, as in 「九つ|ここのつ」, plus 「か」, making the longest native day word at four beats. The readings 「きゅう」 and 「く」 never appear in it.",
      "Nine's reading 「く」 sounds like 「苦|く」, suffering, but the ninth of the ninth month is 「重陽の節句|ちょうようのせっく」, a chrysanthemum festival for long life, and this word uses the safe native nine.",
      "Add 「に」 for a date, as in 「九日に会いましょう|ここのかにあいましょう」, but not for a length, as in 「九日かかりました|ここのかかかりました」, and use 「九日間|ここのかかん」 to make duration clear."
    ],
    ja: "{手紙|てがみ}は[九日|ここのか]かかりました。",
    en: "The letter took nine days." },
  "とおか|十日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「十|じゅう」 is ten and 「日|ひ」 is day, but again the word uses the old native count. The native ten is 「とお」, the word you use for ten things. So when you count objects, one through ten, you finish with 「とお」, and this word is just that ten plus 「か」 for days. Spell it with 「お」, a long 「とお」, not a short one. This is where the native day words stop. From eleven on, you switch to the Chinese-origin numbers plus 「日|にち」, as in 「十一日|じゅういちにち」, eleven days or the eleventh. There are just a few leftovers from the native set. The fourteenth is 「十四日|じゅうよっか」, which reuses the native four days. And the twentieth is the special word 「二十日|はつか」. Like the other day words, it works for both a length of time and a date, so 「十日|とおか」 is ten days or the tenth of the month. A way to remember it: 「とお」 sounds a little like toe, and you have ten toes. To recap, native ten plus days, and the last stop on the native day train." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Because this word means both a date and a length of time, Japanese gives you a tool to make it clear. Add 「間|かん」, the kanji for interval or between, and you get 「十日間|とおかかん」, a ten-day period. That 「間|かん」 is the same piece you already know from 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」, one week, and 「一時間|いちじかん」, one hour. It turns a calendar point into a stretch of time. Particles help too. A date usually takes 「に」, as in 「十日に帰ります|とおかにかえります」, I go home on the tenth. A length often stands alone or takes 「ぐらい」, about: 「十日ぐらい|とおかぐらい」, about ten days. You can also say 「十日前|とおかまえ」, ten days ago, and 「十日後|とおかご」, ten days later. These little add-ons work with all the day words. So when you need to be precise, reach for 「間|かん」 for a length, and 「に」 for a date." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The tenth day of the first month is a big day in western Japan, especially Osaka. It is called 「十日戎|とおかえびす」, the tenth-day festival for 「えびす」, the cheerful god of business and good fortune. Ebisu is one of the seven lucky gods, and he is usually shown smiling, holding a fishing rod and a big red sea bream. Around the ninth to the eleventh of January, huge crowds visit Ebisu shrines to pray for success in business in the new year. They buy lucky bamboo branches decorated with charms, and people shout a famous chant asking the god to bring good business. Shopkeepers and company workers take it very seriously. Notice that the festival name uses exactly the date word you are learning, the native 「とおか」. So the tenth is not just the last of the native days, it is also a lucky day for anyone running a shop. Ten days in, and fortune is in the air." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick that ties the long sound to the number. Picture yourself on a long, ten-day hike. Each day you walk until your toes ache, and by the tenth day, all ten toes are complaining together, a long, groaning 「とおお」. Then a friend asks how long the hike was, and you pull off your boots, wiggle all ten sore toes, and say 「とおか」. The long vowel is the groan of ten tired toes. Now compare it with the date word for the twentieth, 「はつか」, which sounds nothing like it. Picture twenty fireworks on the twentieth, a short, bright pop. And the fourteenth goes back to the native four, 「じゅうよっか」. But the tenth is always the long groan. So stretch the vowel, think of ten aching toes, and you will never write it short. The tenth: ten toes, one long sigh of relief." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the native ten, a long 「とお」, plus 「か」, and it is the last native day word. From eleven on you switch to Chinese-origin numbers plus 「日|にち」, except 「十四日|じゅうよっか」 and 「二十日|はつか」.",
      "Add 「間|かん」 to make a ten-day period, 「十日間|とおかかん」, as in 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」, and use 「に」 for the date, as in 「十日に帰ります|とおかにかえります」.",
      "In Osaka, January tenth brings 「十日戎|とおかえびす」, a festival where crowds pray to the smiling god of business for success in the new year.",
      "Picture ten aching toes after a ten-day hike, groaning a long 「とおお」, so you always stretch the vowel and never write it short."
    ],
    ja: "[十日|とおか]{日本|にほん}にいます。",
    en: "I will be in Japan for ten days." },
  "おくさん|奥さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「奥|おく」, with its native reading, means the deep inside, the far back of a space. You can say the back of a room or the depths of a forest with the same kanji. In old Japan, the wife of a samurai or a lord lived and ran the household in the inner rooms at the back of the home, and people came to call her by that place, the one in the back. Add polite 「さん」 and you get the everyday word for someone else's wife. Here is the key rule. Use this word for other people's wives, like 「田中さんの奥さん|たなかさんのおくさん」, Mr. Tanaka's wife. When you talk about your own wife, you humble her, and say 「妻|つま」. There is an even more formal version, 「奥様|おくさま」, which you will hear in shops and on TV. Many people today find the idea of the wife in the back a bit old fashioned, so you may also hear neutral words like 「パートナー」. A way to remember it: think of the inner rooms, the heart of the home. To recap, the person of the inner house, for someone else's wife." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for the deep inside, 「奥|おく」, shows up in some handy words. Your back teeth, the molars far back in your mouth, are 「奥歯|おくば」. The depth of a room or a shelf, how far it goes back, is 「奥行き|おくゆき」, which you will see on furniture labels. Deep in the mountains is 「山奥|やまおく」, a place far from any town. And the polite word for someone's wife has a fancier form, 「奥様|おくさま」, where 「さま」 is the extra-polite version of 「さん」. Interestingly, one older word for your own wife has the same inner idea: 「家内|かない」, literally inside the house. So in Japanese, wives were linked to the inside of the home from two directions. In each of these words, 「奥|おく」 means the part that is furthest in, the back, the deep end. Keep that picture of depth, and the whole family makes sense." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a second life beyond someone's wife. Shopkeepers, especially at markets and in old-style neighborhoods, often call out to a woman customer with 「奥さん|おくさん」, roughly like calling out ma'am. A fish seller might shout, 「奥さん、今日は安いよ|おくさん、きょうはやすいよ」, ma'am, it's cheap today! It assumes she is married, so some people find it dated. When talking with a friend or neighbor, it is natural to ask about their wife this way: 「奥さんはお元気ですか|おくさんはおげんきですか」, is your wife well? The answer uses the humble word: 「はい、妻も元気です|はい、つまもげんきです」, yes, my wife is fine too. A very common English speaker mistake is saying 「私の奥さん|わたしのおくさん」 for my wife. It does happen in casual speech, but in polite talk it sounds like you are honoring your own family. So outward, use this word; inward, humble it." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Picture a huge old Japanese house with sliding paper doors, one after another, going back and back. You are a guest, and a servant leads you through door after door, deeper and deeper. At last, at the very back of the house, in a quiet inner room, sits the lady of the house, calmly arranging flowers. You bow and say 「おくさん」. Now hear the word 「おく」 as an echo of oak. Picture her room built of dark, polished oak wood, the deepest and most solid part of the house. Oak at the back, and the lady in the oak room. This scene gives you both the sound and the meaning, and also the kanji idea of the inner depths. So whenever you need the word for someone's wife, walk through those sliding doors to the oak room at the back." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「奥|おく」 means the deep inside, because a lord's wife once ran the household from the inner rooms. Use it for someone else's wife, and humbly say 「妻|つま」 for your own.",
      "The deep-inside kanji gives 「奥歯|おくば」, back teeth, 「奥行き|おくゆき」, depth, and 「山奥|やまおく」, deep in the mountains, while 「家内|かない」 links wives to the inside of the house too.",
      "Shopkeepers call out 「奥さん|おくさん」 to women customers like ma'am, and you ask a friend 「奥さんはお元気ですか|おくさんはおげんきですか」, but avoid 「私の奥さん|わたしのおくさん」 in polite talk.",
      "Walk through sliding door after sliding door to the oak room at the very back of the house, where the lady sits, and hear oak in 「おく」."
    ],
    ja: "{山田|やまだ}さんの[奥さん|おくさん]は{先生|せんせい}です。",
    en: "Mr. Yamada's wife is a teacher." },
  "ごしゅじん|ご主人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it into three pieces. First, the polite 「ご」 that goes in front of Chinese-origin words, as in 「ご飯|ごはん」. Then 「主|しゅ」, with its Chinese-origin reading, which means master, owner, or main. The kanji looks like a lamp with a flame on top, and you can picture the head of the house as the light at the center. That part is a hook, not a history. Last is 「人|じん」, person, read the same way as in 「外国人|がいこくじん」, foreigner, and 「有名人|ゆうめいじん」, celebrity. Put together, the master of the house. With 「ご」, it means someone else's husband: 「ご主人はお元気ですか|ごしゅじんはおげんきですか」, how is your husband. Without the 「ご」, 「主人|しゅじん」 is what many women still say for their own husband, though younger people often prefer 「夫|おっと」, which has no master idea in it. It pairs naturally with 「奥さん|おくさん」, someone else's wife. You may also hear 「主人|しゅじん」 used for the owner of a shop or an inn. To recap, polite 「ご」, plus master, plus person: the master of someone else's house." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「主|しゅ」 means main, master, or owner, and it builds a big family. A 「主婦|しゅふ」 is a housewife, literally the main woman of the household. The subject of a sentence, which you meet in grammar, is 「主語|しゅご」, the main word. The hero or main character of a story is 「主人公|しゅじんこう」, which contains this very word. With a native reading, the same kanji gives 「主に|おもに」, mainly or mostly, a very useful adverb. And the owner of a lost item is the 「持ち主|もちぬし」, the person who holds it as master, with yet another native reading, 「ぬし」. In every case, 「主|しゅ」 points to whoever or whatever is at the center, the one in charge. That is why the word for husband got built on it, back when husbands were seen as the master of the home. So one kanji, many readings, and always the idea of main." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There are several words for husband, and choosing well shows good manners. For your own husband in polite talk, many people use 「主人|しゅじん」 or the neutral 「夫|おっと」. In casual chat, women often say 「旦那|だんな」 for their own husband. For someone else's husband, the polite choices are 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」 or 「旦那さん|だんなさん」. A classic mistake is to put 「ご」 on your own husband. Saying 「私のご主人|わたしのごしゅじん」 sounds like you are honoring your own family, which is backwards. Here is a mini dialogue between neighbors. One asks, 「ご主人はお仕事ですか|ごしゅじんはおしごとですか」, is your husband at work? The other answers, 「ええ、主人は今日出張です|ええ、しゅじんはきょうしゅっちょうです」, yes, my husband is on a business trip today. Notice how the 「ご」 disappears in the answer. Add 「ご」 for theirs, drop it for yours." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "This word carries a piece of Japanese social history. In the traditional household system, before the Second World War, a family had an official head, usually the eldest man, who held legal authority over the family. Calling a husband the master of the house fit that system. After the war, the law changed and husbands and wives became equal, but the words stayed in everyday speech. Today many people use them without thinking about the old meaning, the way English speakers say goodbye without thinking of God be with you. Still, some younger couples avoid them and choose words like 「夫|おっと」 or 「パートナー」. You will also hear the word in a completely different setting: the owner of an inn or a small restaurant is the 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」 too, and his wife running the inn is the 「女将|おかみ」. So the word has moved from the head of the family to a polite everyday label." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is polite 「ご」, plus 「主|しゅ」, master, plus 「人|じん」, person, so the master of someone else's house. Without 「ご」, 「主人|しゅじん」 is what many women say for their own husband.",
      "「主|しゅ」 means main or master in 「主婦|しゅふ」, housewife, 「主語|しゅご」, subject, and 「主人公|しゅじんこう」, hero, and the native reading gives 「主に|おもに」, mainly.",
      "Add 「ご」 for someone else's husband and drop it for yours, as in 「ご主人はお仕事ですか|ごしゅじんはおしごとですか」 answered by 「ええ、主人は今日出張です|ええ、しゅじんはきょうしゅっちょうです」.",
      "It comes from the prewar household system with an official male head, and though the law changed, the word stayed, and it is also used for the owner of an inn."
    ],
    ja: "[ご主人|ごしゅじん]はお{元気|げんき}ですか。",
    en: "How is your husband?" },
  "パートナー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes straight from English partner, and it is a nice example of how English gets squeezed into Japanese sounds. English has an r sound after the vowel in both halves, part and ner. Japanese drops those r sounds and stretches the vowel instead, which is what the long marks 「ー」 are doing. So part becomes 「パー」, and the ending ner becomes 「ナー」. The t in the middle cannot sit on its own, so it picks up an extra vowel and becomes 「ト」. You will see the same pattern in 「セーター」, sweater, where the r ending also turns into a long vowel. In meaning, it is a lot like English. It can be a business partner, a dance partner, or the person you share your life with, married or not. In recent years many people use it as a neutral, modern way to talk about a spouse, instead of 「主人|しゅじん」 or 「奥さん|おくさん」, which carry old ideas of master and inner house. It works for any gender. To recap, English partner, with the r sounds turned into long vowels, and a nice neutral word for your other half." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "A few loanwords sound close to this one, and mixing them up can be funny. The biggest trap is 「パート」, which you might expect to mean part. In everyday Japanese it usually means part-time work, short for part-timer, especially the kind many parents do at supermarkets. So 「パートをしています」 means I work part-time, not I have a partner. Another one is 「パーティー」, party, which starts with the same long 「パー」 but ends with 「ティー」. Then there is 「アルバイト」, another word for a part-time job, which comes from German, not English. So listen carefully to the ending. If you hear the long 「ナー」 at the end, it is a person you share something with. If it stops short at 「ト」, it is a job. If it ends in 「ティー」, bring snacks. Four long beats with that final 「ナー」 is the person by your side." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has become more important in Japan over the last decade. Since 2015, when two Tokyo wards, Shibuya and Setagaya, started the idea, many cities and prefectures have introduced partnership certificate systems. These let same-sex couples, and in some places unmarried couples, register as partners and get some recognition, for example when renting an apartment or visiting each other in hospital. The system is called 「パートナーシップ制度|パートナーシップせいど」. Same-sex marriage is still not legal nationwide, so this word often appears in news about these debates. In business, you will hear 「ビジネスパートナー」, and in sports or dance, a 「パートナー」 is the person you practice with. Introducing someone this way is simple: 「こちらは私のパートナーです|こちらはわたしのパートナーです」, this is my partner. A small word, but one that carries a lot of modern Japan." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English partner with the r sounds turned into long vowels, 「パー」 and 「ナー」. Many people now use it as a neutral word for a spouse of any gender, instead of 「主人|しゅじん」 or 「奥さん|おくさん」.",
      "Listen to the ending: 「パート」 usually means part-time work and 「パーティー」 is a party, but the long 「ナー」 at the end means the person by your side.",
      "Many cities now have a 「パートナーシップ制度|パートナーシップせいど」 that lets same-sex couples register as partners, and introducing someone is simply 「こちらは私のパートナーです|こちらはわたしのパートナーです」."
    ],
    ja: "{私|わたし}の[パートナー]はアメリカ{人|じん}です。",
    en: "My partner is American." },
  "おじさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has two jobs. In the family, it is your uncle, your mother's or father's brother, or an aunt's husband. Outside the family, it is what children and young people call any man who looks middle aged, a bit like saying mister. A kid might point at a stranger and say 「あのおじさん」, that man over there. The big trap for English speakers is vowel length. With a short 「じ」, it is uncle. Stretch it to 「おじいさん」, and you have just called him grandfather, or an old man, which he will not enjoy. The same pattern shows up in its partner word: 「おばさん」, aunt, and 「おばあさん」, grandmother. When you write it in kanji, there are actually two spellings, depending on whether the uncle is older or younger than your parent, but in speech it is all the same word. Like other family words, add 「さん」 when talking about him politely. A way to remember it: short vowel, younger man; long vowel, older man. The longer the sound, the longer he has lived. To recap, uncle or a middle aged man, and keep that vowel short." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "In speech, uncle is one word, but in writing Japanese makes a distinction English does not. If the uncle is older than your parent, it is written 「伯父|おじ」. The first kanji there has the person piece on the left, and it is linked with the idea of the eldest brother. If he is younger than your parent, it is written 「叔父|おじ」, with a different first kanji used for younger siblings of a parent. Both end with the kanji for father, 「父|ちち」, so the spelling literally says older father or younger father. The same split exists for aunts, with the mother kanji instead. For a middle-aged man who is not a relative, you just write it in kana. Most people do not know off the top of their head which kanji their uncle needs, and they check when writing a formal letter. To recap, one sound, two spellings, and the kanji tell you whether he is your parent's older or younger brother." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here is the whole family tree around this word. Your uncle is 「おじ」, and your aunt is 「おば」. Your cousin is 「いとこ」, and the nice thing is that it covers any cousin, male or female, older or younger. Your nephew is 「おい」, and your niece is 「めい」. Your grandfather and grandmother, when you talk about your own, are 「祖父|そふ」 and 「祖母|そぼ」, and when you talk to them or about someone else's, they are 「おじいさん」 and 「おばあさん」. The same split applies here: for your own uncle, in polite talk with others, you say 「おじ」, without 「さん」. With 「さん」, it is how you address him, or how you talk about someone else's uncle. You may also hear the cute form 「おじちゃん」 from small children. So the uncle word sits in a neat set: 「おじ」, 「おば」, 「いとこ」, 「おい」, 「めい」. Learn them as one family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Outside the family, this word has a whole personality. A middle-aged man will often call himself this, especially when talking to kids: 「おじさんが教えてあげるよ|おじさんがおしえてあげるよ」, mister here will teach you. Men also use it jokingly about themselves to friends, meaning I am getting old. There is even a playful word for the stereotype of a tired middle-aged office worker. Kids in the neighborhood might call the man at the corner shop by his job plus this word, such as 「八百屋のおじさん|やおやのおじさん」, the vegetable shop man. For an English speaker, the safe rule is this: it is friendly from children, but an adult calling a stranger this to his face can sound a bit rude. Adults usually say 「すみません」 to get attention instead. So it is warm in the family, cozy from kids, and a little cheeky from grown-ups." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means uncle in the family and any middle-aged man outside it, like mister. Keep the 「じ」 short, because 「おじいさん」 means grandfather or an old man.",
      "In writing, an uncle older than your parent is 「伯父|おじ」 and a younger one is 「叔父|おじ」, both ending in the father kanji, while a non-relative is written in kana.",
      "The family set is 「おじ」, 「おば」, 「いとこ」, 「おい」, and 「めい」, and for your own uncle in polite talk you drop 「さん」.",
      "Middle-aged men call themselves this with kids, as in 「おじさんが教えてあげるよ|おじさんがおしえてあげるよ」, but an adult calling a stranger this can sound rude, so use 「すみません」."
    ],
    ja: "[おじさん]は{東京|とうきょう}にすんでいます。",
    en: "My uncle lives in Tokyo." },
  "おばさん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the female partner of 「おじさん」. In the family, it is your aunt, your mother's or father's sister, or an uncle's wife. Outside the family, it is what children call any woman who looks middle aged, like ma'am or lady. A child in a shop might say 「おばさん、これください」. The trap is the same as with uncle: vowel length. With a short 「ば」, it means aunt. Stretch it to 「おばあさん」, and you have just called her grandmother, or an old woman. Be a little careful with this word in real life. For a woman who is not a relative, being called this by an adult can sting, a bit like being told she looks old, so many adults avoid it and use polite words like 「すみません」 to get attention instead. With children, though, it is perfectly normal and warm. You might also hear it in a funny way, when a woman jokes that she is already 「おばさん」. A way to remember it: short vowel, younger woman; long vowel, older woman. To recap, aunt or a middle aged woman, with a short 「ば」 and a bit of care when you use it." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "In writing, the aunt word splits in two, just like uncle. If she is older than your parent, it is written 「伯母|おば」. If she is younger, it is 「叔母|おば」. The first kanji is the same pair used for uncles, marking older or younger, and the second kanji is mother, 「母|はは」. So the spelling says older mother or younger mother. Look at that mother kanji: many people see it as a woman figure with two dots, and that is a commonly taught picture, so it is easy to spot. In everyday speech, none of this matters, and for a non-relative woman you write it in kana. But on a wedding seating chart or a formal letter, families do pay attention to which kanji is right. Here is a memory trick: the older aunt's kanji is the same one as the older uncle's, so learn the pair together and you get both. One sound, two spellings, and the kanji tell you her place in the family." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Osaka, this word turns into a famous character: 「大阪のおばちゃん|おおさかのおばちゃん」, the Osaka auntie. The softer ending 「ちゃん」 makes it friendly and familiar. The stereotype, which Osaka people often joke about themselves, is a cheerful, talkative woman who loves animal prints, especially leopard, and who always carries candy in her bag. She will hand a piece to anyone, even strangers on the train, and calls it 「アメちゃん」, little candy, adding the cute 「ちゃん」 even to sweets. She bargains hard in shops and is not shy about giving advice. TV shows love this character. It is meant affectionately, and many women in Osaka embrace it with humor. In contrast, being called 「おばさん」 in a cold tone in Tokyo may feel like an insult. So the same family word can feel warm and funny or a little sharp. Tone and region change everything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means aunt in the family and any middle-aged woman outside it. Keep the 「ば」 short to avoid 「おばあさん」, grandmother, and since adults saying it to strangers can sting, use 「すみません」 instead.",
      "In writing, an aunt older than your parent is 「伯母|おば」 and a younger one is 「叔母|おば」, using the same older and younger kanji as uncle plus 「母|はは」, mother.",
      "In Osaka the word becomes the beloved 「大阪のおばちゃん|おおさかのおばちゃん」, a chatty auntie in leopard print handing out 「アメちゃん」, so tone and region change everything."
    ],
    ja: "[おばさん]がセーターをあんでくれました。",
    en: "My aunt knitted me a sweater." },
  "りょうしん|両親": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. 「両|りょう」 means both, a pair, the two sides. 「親|しん」 means parent, and also close or intimate. You already know that kanji by itself with its native reading, 「親|おや」, parent. You also know it in 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, which is about being close and warm to someone, and in 「親せき|しんせき」, relatives, the people close to you by blood. So the word is literally both parents, mother and father together. Use it plain like this for your own parents when you talk to other people: 「両親は東京にいます|りょうしんはとうきょうにいます」, my parents are in Tokyo. For someone else's parents, add the polite 「ご」 and say 「ご両親|ごりょうしん」. Note that this word is a bit formal and written in feel. In casual chat with friends, people often just say 「親|おや」. And when you talk about each parent separately, you use 「父|ちち」 and 「母|はは」. A way to remember it: picture a pair of scales with mother on one side and father on the other. To recap, both plus parent: your mom and dad as a pair." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「両|りょう」, both, gives you a bunch of handy words. Both hands is 「両手|りょうて」, and both sides of a street is 「両側|りょうがわ」. The everyday word for both, as in I like both, is 「両方|りょうほう」. Here is a fun one: currency exchange is 「両替|りょうがえ」. In the Edo period, 「両|りょう」 was also a unit of gold money, and changing coins of different kinds was called exactly this, so the word survives at every bank and airport counter. The parent kanji has a family too. Your best friend is 「親友|しんゆう」, a close friend. Parent and child together is 「親子|おやこ」. And your thumb is 「親指|おやゆび」, the parent finger, the biggest and strongest one. So in this word, both meets parent, and each half leads you to many other useful words." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The parent kanji is a big one, sixteen strokes, but it breaks into three pieces you can recognize. On the left top is stand, 「立|た」, and under it is tree, 「木|き」. On the right is see, 「見|み」. Many Japanese people learn a popular saying from this: a parent is someone who stands on a tree and watches over their child. Scholars say this is a folk explanation, not the real origin, but it is so well known that you will hear it in schools and on TV, and it makes a great memory trick. The real history is about sound and meaning parts and is more technical. The both kanji, 「両|りょう」, is simpler. It shows a balanced shape, and you can imagine a pair of things side by side, which is a handy picture even if it is not the full history. So remember the parent on the tree, watching, and you will never forget how to write this kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Talking about parents in Japanese takes a few different words depending on who is listening. To other people, about your own parents, the neutral word is 「両親|りょうしん」, or separately 「父|ちち」 and 「母|はは」. When talking to your own parents, or about someone else's, you use 「お父さん|おとうさん」 and 「お母さん|おかあさん」. For someone else's parents as a pair, add 「ご」: 「ご両親はお元気ですか|ごりょうしんはおげんきですか」, are your parents well? This is a very common, kind question to ask a friend or coworker. On forms and documents, you may also see 「父母|ふぼ」, father and mother, which looks very formal. And in casual talk among friends, it is just 「親|おや」, as in 「親に電話した|おやにでんわした」, I called my folks. So choose by direction: plain for yours, polite for theirs, casual with friends." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「両|りょう」 means both and 「親|しん」 means parent, as in 「親|おや」, so it is both parents together. Use it plainly for your own and say 「ご両親|ごりょうしん」 for someone else's.",
      "The both kanji gives 「両手|りょうて」, 「両方|りょうほう」, and 「両替|りょうがえ」, currency exchange, while the parent kanji gives 「親友|しんゆう」, best friend, and 「親指|おやゆび」, the thumb.",
      "The parent kanji breaks into stand, tree, and see, giving the popular folk saying that a parent stands on a tree watching over their child, a great memory trick even if not the true origin.",
      "Use 「両親|りょうしん」 or 「父|ちち」 and 「母|はは」 for your own to others, 「ご両親はお元気ですか|ごりょうしんはおげんきですか」 for theirs, and just 「親|おや」 casually with friends."
    ],
    ja: "[両親|りょうしん]に{電話|でんわ}をかけました。",
    en: "I called my parents." },
  "おおやさん|大家さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their native readings here, which is the trick. 「大|おお」 is big, as in 「大きい|おおきい」. 「家|や」 is house or home. You know that kanji with other readings too: 「家|いえ」 as house by itself, and 「か」 in 「家族|かぞく」, family, and 「家具|かぐ」, furniture. Here it takes the short native 「や」. The same 「や」 sound shows up in shop words like 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore, though that one is written with a different kanji. Put together, the big house, meaning the person who owns the building you live in. Add polite 「さん」, because this is someone you deal with face to face. In Japan, a landlord or landlady may live right next door to the apartment, and renting often involves a cultural surprise for foreigners: some places ask for a thank-you gift of money to the owner when you move in, called key money. A small gift of sweets when you first meet your landlord is also a nice gesture. A way to remember it: picture the owner living in the big house, while you rent the little room. To recap, big plus house, read the native way, plus 「さん」: the owner of your home." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a fun twist. The same two kanji can be read a completely different way: 「大家|たいか」. With the Chinese-origin readings, it means a great master or authority in some field, like a famous expert in calligraphy or a giant of literature. So the same spelling can mean your landlord or a grand master of the arts. Context and the reading tell you which. When there is 「さん」 after it, it is almost always the landlord. Another look-alike is 「大家族|だいかぞく」, a big family, where the big kanji takes yet another reading, 「だい」. You may also come across 「大屋|おおや」, written with the shop kanji, which is an older alternative spelling of the very same word. For daily life, remember this: 「おおや」 with 「さん」 is the person you pay rent to, and 「たいか」 is the master you bow to. Same kanji, two readings, two very different people." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Renting an apartment in Japan brings a few people into your life. The landlord, 「大家さん|おおやさん」, owns the building. Many buildings also have a 「管理人|かんりにん」, a building manager or caretaker, who handles trash rules and repairs. And you usually find the place through a 「不動産屋|ふどうさんや」, a real estate agent. When you sign the contract, you may pay a deposit called 「敷金|しききん」, which can come back when you leave, and gift money called 「礼金|れいきん」, which does not. You will also need a guarantor, or a guarantor company. If something breaks, you might say, 「大家さんに連絡します|おおやさんにれんらくします」, I will contact the landlord. A common surprise is that trash must be sorted carefully and put out on the right day, and the landlord or neighbors will notice. So renting in Japan is a small team, with the landlord at the top." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Picture a cartoon landlord who lives in a giant house on top of a hill, much bigger than any other. Every first day of the month, all the tenants climb up the hill with their rent money. When they finally reach the top, gasping, they look up at the huge house and cry out, 「おお」, oh! wow! Then the landlord swings open the door and says a cheerful 「や」, as in hey there. So the visit goes 「おお」, then 「や」: the huge house amazes you, and the landlord greets you. Put them together and you have 「おおや」, and add 「さん」 to be polite since you want to keep your deposit. The big kanji matches the giant house, and the house kanji matches his home. Climb the hill, say wow, hear hey, and pay the rent." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Both kanji use native readings: 「大|おお」, big, and 「家|や」, house, so the owner of the big house is your landlord. Add 「さん」, since you deal with them face to face.",
      "The same kanji read 「大家|たいか」 mean a great master of an art, but with 「さん」 after it, it is almost always your landlord.",
      "Renting involves the 「大家さん|おおやさん」, a 「管理人|かんりにん」 caretaker, and a 「不動産屋|ふどうさんや」 agent, plus 「敷金|しききん」, a refundable deposit, and 「礼金|れいきん」, gift money that never comes back.",
      "Tenants climb the hill to a giant house, gasp 「おお」 at its size, and hear the landlord greet them with a cheerful 「や」, giving 「おおや」."
    ],
    ja: "[大家さん|おおやさん]はやさしいです。",
    en: "My landlord is kind." },
  "みなさん|皆さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「皆|みな」, with its native reading, means all or everybody. Add polite 「さん」, the same one you put after names, and you get a respectful way to address a whole group at once. You hear it at the start of almost every speech, class, or announcement: 「皆さん、こんにちは|みなさん、こんにちは」, hello, everyone. You already know its casual cousin, 「みんな」, from 「みんなで」, all together. 「みんな」 is simply a relaxed form of 「みな」, so the two are really the same word wearing different clothes. Use 「みんな」 with friends, 「みなさん」 with a group you want to be polite to, and the even more formal 「皆様|みなさま」 in very polite settings, like an airline announcement. One common mistake: English speakers sometimes put 「さん」 on themselves or their own group. You never include yourself when you say 「みなさん」. It always means all of you, the people you are speaking to. A way to remember it: 「さん」 makes it a polite word aimed outward, at the audience. To recap, all plus polite 「さん」, the go-to greeting for a whole room." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「皆|みな」 has two parts stacked on top of each other. On top are two figures side by side, which look like two people standing in a line, and below is the shape for white, 「白|しろ」. The top piece is the same one you see in 「比べる|くらべる」, to compare, where two things stand next to each other. The exact origin of the whole kanji is debated, so it is best to treat that as a shape to recognize, not a story. You have heard its native reading, 「みな」. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「かい」, which you will meet in more advanced words like 「皆勤|かいきん」, perfect attendance, never missing a day, and 「皆無|かいむ」, absolutely none. Both of those carry the idea of all or entirely. So when you see this kanji, think everyone lined up together, and know it can be read 「みな」 or 「かい」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is the voice of teachers, guides, and anyone in front of a group. A teacher says 「皆さん、宿題を出してください|みなさん、しゅくだいをだしてください」, everyone, please hand in your homework. A tour guide says 「皆さん、こちらです|みなさん、こちらです」, everyone, this way. It is also used in letters and emails to a team, and when thanking people: 「皆さん、ありがとうございました|みなさん、ありがとうございました」. It can also mean everyone in the third person, as in 「家族の皆さん|かぞくのみなさん」, everyone in your family, a polite way to send regards. One more useful use: when a host welcomes guests, it is natural to say 「皆さん、どうぞ|みなさん、どうぞ」. Remember, though, that you are speaking to or about other people, never including yourself. So whenever you stand up in front of a room, this is your opening word." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The casual form 「みんな」 does something interesting. It can be a noun, everyone, but it can also act like an adverb meaning all of it, and then it can describe things, not just people. 「ケーキをみんな食べました|ケーキをみんなたべました」 means I ate all the cake. In that sentence, 「みんな」 floats after the object, just like a number would. The polite 「みなさん」 cannot do that. It only means people, and only the people you are addressing or honoring. Another pattern is 「みんなで」, together as a group, as in 「みんなで歌いましょう|みんなでうたいましょう」, let's all sing together. And 「みんなの」 makes a possessive, everyone's. With the polite form, you can say 「皆さんの|みなさんの」, as in your, all of you's. So the relaxed word is flexible and even counts cake, while the polite one stays focused on people." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「皆|みな」 means everybody, and polite 「さん」 aims it outward at a group, as in 「皆さん、こんにちは|みなさん、こんにちは」. 「みんな」 is the casual form, and it never includes yourself.",
      "The kanji stacks two side-by-side figures over 「白|しろ」, white, and its Chinese-origin reading 「かい」 appears in 「皆勤|かいきん」, perfect attendance, and 「皆無|かいむ」, absolutely none.",
      "It is the opening word of teachers and guides, as in 「皆さん、こちらです|みなさん、こちらです」, and it also sends polite regards, as in 「家族の皆さん|かぞくのみなさん」.",
      "Casual 「みんな」 can float after an object to mean all of it, as in 「ケーキをみんな食べました|ケーキをみんなたべました」, but polite 「みなさん」 only refers to people."
    ],
    ja: "[皆さん|みなさん]、おはようございます。",
    en: "Good morning, everyone." },
  "チョコレート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is English chocolate, run through Japanese sounds. English squeezes chocolate into two or three quick syllables, but Japanese gives each consonant its own vowel. So choc becomes 「チョコ」, and the ending late becomes 「レート」, with a long vowel and an extra o sound after the t, since a t cannot sit alone. That turns a short English word into six beats. In daily life, people very often cut it back down to just 「チョコ」. The big cultural note is Valentine's Day. In Japan, women traditionally give chocolate to men. There is 「本命|ほんめい」 chocolate, for the person you really like, and 「義理|ぎり」 chocolate, obligation chocolate, for coworkers and classmates. You may also hear about friend chocolate given between girls. Then a month later, on 「ホワイトデー」, men are expected to give a return gift. Department stores have huge chocolate fairs every February. A way to remember it: the word is long and luxurious, like a big box of chocolates, but in a hurry you just grab a 「チョコ」. To recap, English chocolate with extra vowels, the star of February in Japan." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The word has travelled a very long way. It began in Mexico, in the Nahuatl language of the Aztecs, where cacao drinks were prized, though the exact original word is still debated by scholars. Spanish took it as chocolate, and English and Dutch borrowed it from there. Chocolate first reached Japan through Nagasaki in the Edo period, when Dutch traders were the only Europeans allowed to trade there, and a few records mention it as an exotic medicine or treat. For a long time it was rare and expensive. In the early twentieth century, Japanese confectioners began producing chocolate from cacao beans at home, and it slowly became an everyday snack. The Japanese form follows English, not Spanish or Dutch, which is why it has the long 「レー」 in the middle. So this sweet word carries the voices of Aztec Mexico, Spain, the Netherlands, and England, all rolled into one Japanese bar." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "In daily speech, the short form 「チョコ」 is everywhere, and it builds lots of words. A plain chocolate bar is 「板チョコ|いたチョコ」, literally board chocolate, because it is flat like a board. Chocolate given between female friends on Valentine's Day is 「友チョコ|ともチョコ」, friend chocolate. A treat you buy for yourself is sometimes jokingly called 「自分チョコ|じぶんチョコ」, chocolate for me. Mint chocolate is 「チョコミント」, and fans and haters argue about it online. Notice how Japanese loves to cut long loanwords in half. You already know 「コンビニ」 for convenience store and 「パソコン」 for personal computer, and 「スマホ」 for smartphone works the same way. Shortening keeps words quick and friendly. So the long six-beat word is for packaging and formal writing, and the short two-beat 「チョコ」 is what you will actually hear. A big word, a small nickname." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English chocolate with each consonant given its own vowel, making six beats, though people often shorten it to 「チョコ」. On Valentine's Day, women traditionally give 「本命|ほんめい」 or 「義理|ぎり」 chocolate to men.",
      "The word began with the Aztecs in Mexico, passed through Spanish and English, and chocolate first reached Japan through Nagasaki with Dutch traders, though the Japanese form follows English.",
      "The short 「チョコ」 builds words like 「板チョコ|いたチョコ」, a chocolate bar, and 「友チョコ|ともチョコ」, friend chocolate, just as 「コンビニ」 and 「スマホ」 shorten long loanwords."
    ],
    ja: "バレンタインデーに[チョコレート]をあげました。",
    en: "I gave chocolate on Valentine's Day." },
  "みかん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is usually written in kana, but it does have kanji: 「蜜柑|みかん」. The first kanji means honey, and the second means citrus fruit. So the name is basically honey citrus, which suits this small, sweet, easy-to-peel orange. In English it is often called a satsuma, after Satsuma, an old province in the south of Japan, which shows how closely the fruit is linked to the country. It is the classic Japanese winter fruit. The famous picture is a family sitting at a 「こたつ」, a low table with a heater under it and a blanket over it, with a basket of these oranges on top. People eat so many in winter that some say their fingers turn a little yellow. You also see them at New Year's, in 「お正月|おしょうがつ」 decorations. They are counted with 「〜こ」 as small items: 「みかんを三個|みかんをさんこ」, three oranges. A way to remember it: picture a cozy winter night, the heater on, and a sweet orange in your hand. To recap, honey citrus, the sweet orange of a Japanese winter at the 「こたつ」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji spelling, 「蜜柑|みかん」, is rarely used in daily life, but it is charming once you look closely. The first kanji means honey or nectar, the same one in 「蜂蜜|はちみつ」, honey, where it is read 「みつ」. The second kanji is citrus. On its left is the tree piece, 「木|き」, which marks plants and wood. On its right is the kanji for sweet, 「甘い|あまい」. So the citrus kanji itself says sweet tree. Put them together, and you have honey sweet tree fruit. As for the reading, many dictionaries say the word was once 「みっかん」, using the Chinese-origin reading of honey, and the small 「っ」 dropped out over time to give the smooth three-beat word we have now. Because the kanji are hard, shops, packaging, and even the farmers who grow them mostly write it in kana. So remember the sweet tree hiding inside the second kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan grows a lot of these oranges, especially in warm, sunny prefectures facing the sea. The biggest producers are Wakayama and Ehime, along with Shizuoka. Ehime is so proud of its oranges that it has played with the idea of taps pouring orange juice instead of water, and you can find such juice taps as a fun attraction in some places there. The fruit also appears at New Year's. On top of the round rice cake decoration, 「鏡餅|かがみもち」, families traditionally place a small bitter orange called 「だいだい」, whose name sounds like generation after generation, as a wish for the family line. Many homes use a regular mandarin instead, because it is easier to find. And in many school lunches, children used to get frozen mandarins, 「冷凍みかん|れいとうみかん」, as a cool dessert. So this little fruit links farms, festivals, and schoolyards across Japan." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Eating one is a small ritual in Japan. You peel it with your fingers, no knife needed. People argue playfully about the best way to peel it, from the top, from the bottom, or in one long spiral. Inside, the white strings on the segments have their own name, 「すじ」, and some people carefully pick them all off while others eat them, saying they are good for you. You will often hear 「みかんをむく」, to peel a mandarin, using the verb for peeling. If someone hands you a box of them, a normal reaction is 「わあ、おいしそう」, oh, they look delicious. In winter, people buy them by the box, not one at a time, and a box sits in the hallway or under the table all season. Someone might say, 「みかん、もう一つ食べてもいい|みかん、もうひとつたべてもいい」, can I have one more? So it is a casual, shared, all-winter fruit." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Its rare kanji 「蜜柑|みかん」 mean honey and citrus, a fitting name for this small sweet orange. It is the classic winter fruit, eaten by the basketful at the 「こたつ」.",
      "The first kanji is the honey in 「蜂蜜|はちみつ」, and the citrus kanji is tree plus sweet, so the name says honey sweet tree fruit, though it is mostly written in kana.",
      "Wakayama and Ehime grow the most, a mandarin often tops 「鏡餅|かがみもち」 at New Year's in place of 「だいだい」, and schoolkids once got frozen 「冷凍みかん|れいとうみかん」.",
      "People peel them by hand, 「みかんをむく」, argue playfully over peeling methods and the white 「すじ」, and buy them by the box to share all winter."
    ],
    ja: "{冬|ふゆ}は[みかん]がおいしいです。",
    en: "Mandarin oranges are tasty in winter." },
  "シャツ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English shirt, and the changes are easy to hear. English has an r sound in the middle, as in sh-ir-t. Japanese simply drops that r. The sh sound is fine, so it becomes 「シャ」. The final t cannot stand on its own in Japanese, and English t sounds before a u vowel turn into 「ツ」, so the ending is 「ツ」. Two short beats, and you have the word. In Japanese, it is a broad word for shirts in general, and you will see it in compounds. Put the letter T in front and you get a T-shirt, 「ティーシャツ」. There is also a word you might not guess: 「ワイシャツ」, a dress shirt, which comes from white shirt, because office shirts used to be white. A shirt is something you wear on your upper body, so you use the verb for upper-body clothes, 「着る|きる」, to put it on, not 「はく」, which is for pants and shoes. A way to remember it: the word is just as short and plain as a basic shirt. To recap, English shirt, with the r dropped and the t turned into 「ツ」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese uses different verbs for wearing depending on where the item goes on your body, and this is a big one to get right. For things on your upper body, like shirts, sweaters, and jackets, the verb is 「着る|きる」. For things on your lower body, like pants, skirts, socks, and shoes, it is 「はく」. For hats, it is 「かぶる」. For glasses, it is 「かける」. For accessories like ties, watches, and rings, you often use 「する」. To say someone is wearing something right now, use the 「ている」 form: 「白いシャツを着ています|しろいシャツをきています」, he is wearing a white shirt. In Japanese this sounds like he has put it on and is in that state. To take it off, all these clothes use 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, except accessories, which often use 「外す|はずす」. A common mistake is using one verb for everything, the way English uses wear. So shirts get 「着る|きる」, and that one rule goes a long way." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This short loanword combines with lots of other words to name different shirts. A polo shirt is 「ポロシャツ」. A Hawaiian shirt is 「アロハシャツ」, from the Hawaiian greeting. A long sleeve shirt is 「長袖シャツ|ながそでシャツ」, and a short sleeve one is 「半袖シャツ|はんそでシャツ」, with the kanji for long and half in front of sleeve. In stores, you may also see 「ネルシャツ」, a flannel shirt. Here is a surprise. In Japanese, this word on its own can also mean an undershirt, the thin layer worn under your clothes, depending on context. Older people especially use it that way. A sleeveless undershirt is a 「ランニングシャツ」, a running shirt, since it looks like what runners wear. So when someone says they bought a shirt, it might be something fancy or something underneath. Context will tell you which. One tiny word, a whole closet of meanings." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English shirt with the r dropped and the final t turned into 「ツ」. A T-shirt is 「ティーシャツ」, a dress shirt is 「ワイシャツ」, from white shirt, and you put it on with 「着る|きる」.",
      "Wearing verbs depend on the body part: 「着る|きる」 for the upper body, 「はく」 for the lower body, 「かぶる」 for hats, 「かける」 for glasses, and 「する」 for accessories.",
      "It combines into 「ポロシャツ」, 「アロハシャツ」, and 「半袖シャツ|はんそでシャツ」, and on its own it can also mean an undershirt, especially for older people."
    ],
    ja: "{白い|しろい}[シャツ]を{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought a white shirt." },
  "ネクタイ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is English necktie, and you can hear both halves clearly. Neck becomes 「ネク」, with an extra vowel after the k, since a k cannot stand alone at the end of a syllable in Japanese. Tie becomes 「タイ」. Stick them together and you get four short beats. Note that Japanese keeps the full word necktie, where English speakers mostly just say tie today. That is common with loanwords: Japanese often keeps an older or fuller form of the English word. You put it on with the verb 「する」, as in 「ネクタイをする」, wear a tie. In Japan, the necktie is part of the classic salaryman look with a dark suit, though in summer many offices now follow a cool business style, with no jacket and no tie, to save air conditioning. It is also a very common gift for fathers, bosses, and on Father's Day, a bit like in the West. A way to remember it: neck plus tie, the thing you tie around your neck. To recap, English necktie, kept whole, with an extra vowel after neck." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful when you cut this word in half. In English, tie is fine on its own, but in Japanese 「タイ」 alone usually means something else. It is the name of the country Thailand. And written 「鯛|たい」, it is sea bream, a red fish served at celebrations, partly because its name echoes 「めでたい」, happy or auspicious. So asking for a tie in a shop with just 「タイ」 might confuse people. You can use it in a few set words, like 「タイピン」, a tie pin, but the full word is the safe choice. Another fun one is 「ノーネクタイ」, literally no necktie, a phrase made in Japan to mean going without a tie, often seen on invitations to casual summer events. And a bow tie is 「蝶ネクタイ|ちょうネクタイ」, butterfly necktie, because of its shape. So keep the neck in the word, unless you want a fish or a country." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In 2005, the Japanese government started a campaign called 「クールビズ」, cool business, to cut air-conditioning use in summer. Offices were encouraged to set air conditioners higher and let workers skip jackets and ties. It was a big change for a work culture where the dark suit and tie were almost a uniform. The prime minister and ministers appeared on TV without ties to set an example. It worked, and now the tie-free season is normal in many offices from spring to autumn. There is also a famous comic image of the tie. In old manga and TV comedies, a drunk office worker after a party staggers home with his necktie tied around his head like a headband. You still see this picture in jokes and illustrations, even if you rarely see it in real life. So the tie stands for serious office life, and also for letting go after work." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Besides the simple 「する」, there are more precise verbs for ties. To tie or tighten a necktie is 「締める|しめる」, the same verb you use for fastening a belt or tightening a screw. So 「ネクタイを締める|ネクタイをしめる」 is the classic way to say putting on a tie, and it has a feeling of getting serious, ready for work. In fact, 「気を引き締める|きをひきしめる」 means to brace yourself and focus. To loosen a tie is 「ゆるめる」, which suggests relaxing after a long day. To untie it is 「ほどく」, and to take it off is 「外す|はずす」. Notice that you do not use 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, the verb for taking off clothes, with a tie. Here is a tiny scene: after work, a man sits down, says 「疲れた|つかれた」, and loosens his tie. So tighten it to get serious, loosen it to relax." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English necktie kept whole, with 「ネク」 for neck and 「タイ」 for tie, and you wear it with 「する」, as in 「ネクタイをする」. It is part of the classic salaryman look and a common gift for fathers.",
      "Don't shorten it, because 「タイ」 alone means Thailand, or 「鯛|たい」, sea bream. Keep the neck, as in 「蝶ネクタイ|ちょうネクタイ」, a bow tie, or 「ノーネクタイ」, going without a tie.",
      "The 2005 campaign 「クールビズ」 let office workers skip jackets and ties in summer to save air conditioning, and old comedies show drunk workers wearing their tie around their head.",
      "Tighten a tie with 「締める|しめる」, which feels like getting serious, loosen it with 「ゆるめる」 to relax, and take it off with 「外す|はずす」, not 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}に[ネクタイ]をあげました。",
    en: "I gave my father a necktie." },
  "マフラー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the English word muffler. In English today, a muffler usually means the part of a car that quiets the engine, but it is also an old word for a warm scarf that muffles your neck against the cold. Japanese uses it in both senses, but in daily life it is almost always the winter scarf. Here is how the sounds shift. The English uh vowel becomes 「ア」, so muff becomes 「マフ」. The ending ler has an l and an r, and Japanese uses its r family sound for both, 「ラ」, and then stretches the vowel because of that final r, giving 「ラー」. A lighter summer or fashion scarf is a different word, 「スカーフ」, so this one is specifically the thick, cozy kind. You put it on with 「する」, as in 「マフラーをする」. Another nice cultural note: hand-knitting a scarf for someone you love is a classic romantic gift in Japanese dramas and manga. A way to remember it: the scarf muffles the cold wind. To recap, English muffler, l and r both turned into 「ラ」, meaning your winter scarf." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This scarf is part of a whole set of winter words. Gloves are 「手袋|てぶくろ」, literally hand bags, a lovely picture once you see the kanji for hand and bag. A knit hat is often called a 「ニット帽|ニットぼう」, and a regular hat is 「帽子|ぼうし」. A winter coat is 「コート」, and a sweater is 「セーター」. Then there is a Japanese winter essential: 「カイロ」, a small disposable heat pack that you shake and put in your pocket or stick inside your clothes. On the coldest days, people wear several of these at once. And earmuffs are 「イヤーマフ」, which shares the muff sound with this word. Many of these are loanwords, because modern winter fashion came from the West, but gloves and hats have native kanji names. So bundle up with the whole set, and you will have the words for a snowy day in Hokkaido." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There are a few ways to say you put on a scarf. The simplest is 「する」: 「マフラーをする」. But a more vivid verb is 「巻く|まく」, to wrap or wind around: 「マフラーを巻く|マフラーをまく」, to wrap a scarf around your neck. The same verb is used for winding a bandage or rolling sushi, as in 「巻き寿司|まきずし」, rolled sushi. To say you are wearing one right now, use the 「ている」 form: 「赤いマフラーをしています|あかいマフラーをしています」, she is wearing a red scarf. To take it off, use 「外す|はずす」 or 「取る|とる」, rather than the clothes verb 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」. And to knit one, the verb is 「編む|あむ」, so a hand-knitted scarf is a 「手編みのマフラー|てあみのマフラー」. So wrap it with 「巻く|まく」, wear it with 「する」, and knit it with 「編む|あむ」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The 「マフ」 sound at the start appears in a few other loanwords that are easy to mix up. 「マフィン」 is a muffin, the small cake. 「マフィア」 is the mafia. And in a car or motorcycle shop, this very word means the exhaust muffler, so if you talk about your scarf at a garage, people might think of engines first. In daily life, though, especially in winter, it almost always means the scarf. Compare it also with 「スカーフ」, a light scarf usually made of silk, worn for fashion, and 「ストール」, a wide wrap or stole. Here is a quick memory trick. A muffin is a warm, soft treat, a scarf is a warm, soft wrap, and both start with the same 「マフ」. Just remember that the scarf ends with a long 「ラー」, like a sigh of comfort in the cold. Warm sound, warm neck." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English muffler, an old word for a warm scarf, and in daily Japanese it almost always means a thick winter scarf. A light fashion scarf is 「スカーフ」 instead.",
      "It belongs to a winter set with 「手袋|てぶくろ」, gloves, 「ニット帽|ニットぼう」, a knit hat, 「コート」, 「セーター」, and 「カイロ」, the disposable pocket heat pack.",
      "Wear one with 「する」, wrap it on with 「巻く|まく」, the verb in 「巻き寿司|まきずし」, take it off with 「外す|はずす」, and knit it with 「編む|あむ」.",
      "Its 「マフ」 start is shared with 「マフィン」 and 「マフィア」, and at a garage it means the exhaust muffler, but in winter it is the scarf ending in a long 「ラー」."
    ],
    ja: "{寒い|さむい}から、[マフラー]をします。",
    en: "It's cold, so I'll wear a scarf." },
  "ゆびわ|指輪": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their native readings, and the meaning is easy to see once you split it. 「指|ゆび」 means finger. Look at the left side of the kanji: it has the hand shape, the same piece you see in many action kanji like 「持つ|もつ」, to hold. 「輪|わ」 means ring, loop, or wheel. Look at its left side, and you will find the kanji 「車|くるま」, car, which reminds you of a wheel going round. So the word is literally finger loop, a ring for your finger. The 「輪|わ」 kanji also shows up in other round things, like the Olympic rings. This is a real word for jewelry, so it is often a gift: an engagement ring after a 「プロポーズ」, or a wedding ring at a 「結婚式|けっこんしき」, wedding. To wear a ring, you use 「する」, as in 「指輪をする|ゆびわをする」. A way to remember it: picture your finger, 「ゆび」, poking through a little wheel, 「わ」. To recap, finger plus loop: a ring on your hand, with both parts read the native way." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The finger kanji, 「指|ゆび」, builds the names of all five fingers. The thumb is 「親指|おやゆび」, the parent finger. The index finger is 「人差し指|ひとさしゆび」, the finger that points at people. The middle finger is 「中指|なかゆび」. The ring finger is 「薬指|くすりゆび」, literally medicine finger. One common explanation is that people used it to mix or dab on medicine, though the exact origin is not certain. And the little finger is 「小指|こゆび」. Notice that the ring is usually worn on the medicine finger. The same kanji also makes the verb 「指す|さす」, to point at something. The ring kanji, 「輪|わ」, appears in 「輪ゴム|わゴム」, a rubber band, and with its Chinese-origin reading in 「五輪|ごりん」, the five rings, a common name for the Olympics. So fingers and rings both lead you into a whole hand of words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Western-style engagement and wedding rings are a fairly recent custom in Japan. They became popular after the Second World War, and especially from the 1960s and 70s. An engagement ring is 「婚約指輪|こんやくゆびわ」, and a wedding ring is 「結婚指輪|けっこんゆびわ」. A famous diamond advertising campaign in Japan promoted the idea that an engagement ring should cost about three months of salary, 「給料の三か月分|きゅうりょうのさんかげつぶん」, and that phrase is still well known, though most couples spend far less today. Rings are worn on the left ring finger, as in the West. Some couples skip the engagement ring and buy only matching wedding rings, which is common. There is also a fun custom of matching rings for young couples, called 「ペアリング」, a pair ring, a made-in-Japan word. So a ring in Japan mixes Western tradition with clever marketing and Japanese twists." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Both kanji use native readings: 「指|ゆび」, finger, with a hand shape, and 「輪|わ」, ring or wheel, with 「車|くるま」 inside. So it is a finger loop, worn with 「する」.",
      "The finger kanji names all five fingers, from 「親指|おやゆび」 to 「薬指|くすりゆび」, the medicine finger where rings go, and the ring kanji gives 「輪ゴム|わゴム」 and 「五輪|ごりん」, the Olympics.",
      "Engagement rings, 「婚約指輪|こんやくゆびわ」, became popular after the war, and a famous ad campaign claimed one should cost 「給料の三か月分|きゅうりょうのさんかげつぶん」, three months of salary."
    ],
    ja: "プロポーズのとき、[指輪|ゆびわ]をもらいました。",
    en: "I got a ring when he proposed." },
  "えんぴつ|鉛筆": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「鉛|えん」 means lead, the soft gray metal. Look at the left side: it is the metal piece, the same one you see in 「お金|おかね」, money, and in 「銀行|ぎんこう」, bank. 「筆|ひつ」 means writing brush. Look at the top: it has the bamboo piece, because traditional brushes have bamboo handles. So the word is literally a lead brush, a writing tool with lead inside. Fun fact: modern pencils actually contain graphite, not lead, but the name stuck in both English and Japanese. Notice the sound change. On its own, brush is 「ひつ」, but after the 「ん」 sound it becomes 「ぴつ」. Japanese often does this, turning an h sound into a p sound after 「ん」 or a small 「っ」. You can see the same pattern with the counter for long things: 「一本|いっぽん」, one pencil, which is exactly how you count these. A way to remember it: the metal piece on the left of the first kanji, and the bamboo hat on top of the second. To recap, lead plus brush, with 「ひつ」 popping into 「ぴつ」 after 「ん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The brush kanji, 「筆|ひつ」, is the star of a whole pencil case of words. With its Chinese-origin reading, a fountain pen is 「万年筆|まんねんひつ」, a ten-thousand-year brush, because it was meant to last forever. The writer of an article is the 「筆者|ひっしゃ」. With its native reading, 「ふで」, it is a real writing brush, 「筆|ふで」, and a pencil case is a 「筆箱|ふでばこ」, a brush box, even though it is full of pencils today. Then there are loanwords. A mechanical pencil is a 「シャープペンシル」, usually shortened to 「シャーペン」. The name comes from an early American mechanical pencil brand, the Ever-Sharp, and the Japanese electronics company Sharp was named after a mechanical pencil its founder made. An eraser is 「消しゴム|けしゴム」, erasing rubber. And a pencil sharpener is 「鉛筆削り|えんぴつけずり」, from the verb to shave. So the brush lives on in every pencil case in Japan." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Pencils play a big role in Japanese school life. In many elementary schools, children must use wooden pencils, not mechanical ones, partly to practice proper stroke pressure for kanji. Kids carry a pencil case with a neat row of sharpened pencils, often with their name on each one. Many big exams use answer sheets where you fill in bubbles, called 「マークシート」, and you are told to bring pencils, usually HB or soft ones. You count pencils with 「本|ほん」, the counter for long thin things, with its sound changes: 「一本|いっぽん」, 「二本|にほん」, 「三本|さんぼん」. In class, a teacher might say, 「鉛筆で書いてください|えんぴつでかいてください」, please write in pencil, using 「で」 for the tool. And if you forget yours, ask a classmate, 「鉛筆を貸してくれない|えんぴつをかしてくれない」, can you lend me a pencil? So the humble pencil is a daily companion from first grade to entrance exams." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「鉛|えん」 means lead, with the metal piece, and 「筆|ひつ」 means brush, with bamboo on top, so it is a lead brush. After 「ん」, 「ひつ」 changes to 「ぴつ」.",
      "The brush kanji gives 「万年筆|まんねんひつ」, a fountain pen, and read 「ふで」, 「筆箱|ふでばこ」, a pencil case, while a mechanical pencil is 「シャーペン」 and an eraser 「消しゴム|けしゴム」.",
      "Many elementary schools require wooden pencils, exams use pencils for 「マークシート」 answer sheets, and you count them with 「本|ほん」, as in 「一本|いっぽん」."
    ],
    ja: "[鉛筆|えんぴつ]で{名前|なまえ}を{書いて|かいて}ください。",
    en: "Please write your name with a pencil." },
  "ぬいぐるみ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, and it is built from two verbs. The first is 「ぬう」, to sew, which turns into 「ぬい」 when it joins another word. The second is 「くるむ」, to wrap something up, which softens into 「ぐるみ」. So the word is literally sewn and wrapped, a soft toy made by sewing fabric around stuffing. It is sometimes written with the kanji for sew, but kana is far more common. The same 「ぐるみ」 ending shows up in a fun related word, 「着ぐるみ|きぐるみ」, the big mascot costumes that people wear at theme parks and events, literally a wrap you wear. Japan loves mascot characters, and you will see their stuffed versions everywhere, from game centers to train station shops. Claw crane machines full of these toys are a classic sight in Japanese arcades. It is a common gift for children, and also for adults, especially as a cute souvenir. A way to remember it: picture someone sewing, 「ぬい」, and then wrapping a bear up snugly, 「ぐるみ」. To recap, sew plus wrap: a soft toy sewn up around its stuffing." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word shows two neat tricks for building Japanese words. First, verb stems can act like nouns. The stem of 「ぬう」, to sew, is 「ぬい」, and it appears in 「縫い物|ぬいもの」, sewing work, a thing that is sewn. Second, when two words join, the second one often starts with a voiced sound, so 「くるみ」 becomes 「ぐるみ」. This is called rendaku, sequential voicing, and you saw it in 「本棚|ほんだな」, bookshelf, where 「たな」 becomes 「だな」. The 「ぐるみ」 ending has another life as a suffix, meaning including everything, the whole lot. 「家族ぐるみの付き合い|かぞくぐるみのつきあい」 is a friendship that includes the whole family on both sides. The picture is the same: everything wrapped up together. In the toy word, the stuffing is all wrapped inside the fabric. So remember the stem trick and the voicing trick, and many long words become easy to unpack." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan treats stuffed animals with surprising respect. Throwing away an old, much-loved one can feel wrong, as if it had a bit of spirit in it. So some temples and shrines hold memorial services for dolls and soft toys, called 「人形供養|にんぎょうくよう」. People bring their old toys, a priest says prayers, and the toys are thanked before being respectfully disposed of. In recent years, there is also a popular hobby among adults of carrying a small soft toy, often a favorite character, when they go out or travel, and taking photos of it in cafés and famous places. Fans call this 「ぬい撮り|ぬいどり」, plush photography, using the short form 「ぬい」. Some even buy tiny clothes for their plush companions. So these toys are not only for children. In Japan, they can be travel buddies, friends, and even something to say thank you to when it is time to let go." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Arcades in Japan are full of claw machines, which Japanese calls 「ユーフォーキャッチャー」, after a famous brand name, or 「クレーンゲーム」. The prizes are mostly soft toys, and people may spend a lot of coins trying to win just one. If you win, you might say 「取れた|とれた」, I got it! Small plush toys hang from bags and phones as charms, and are often called 「マスコット」. When you buy one as a gift, you can say 「かわいいぬいぐるみを買いました|かわいいぬいぐるみをかいました」. A child might hug a teddy bear and say it is their best friend. Note that the word is quite long, so you will hear people shorten it to 「ぬい」, especially young fans. Teddy bears also have their own loanword, 「テディベア」. So whether it comes from a claw machine, a gift shop, or a temple service, this word is everywhere in Japanese life." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is built from two verbs, 「ぬう」, to sew, and 「くるむ」, to wrap, so it is a toy sewn and wrapped around stuffing. The same ending appears in 「着ぐるみ|きぐるみ」, mascot costumes.",
      "It shows two word-building tricks: a verb stem acting as a noun, and the voicing change that turns 「くるみ」 into 「ぐるみ」, which as a suffix means including everyone, as in 「家族ぐるみ|かぞくぐるみ」.",
      "Some temples hold 「人形供養|にんぎょうくよう」 to thank old toys before disposal, and adults take travel photos of their plush companions, called 「ぬい撮り|ぬいどり」.",
      "Arcades are full of claw machines called 「ユーフォーキャッチャー」 or 「クレーンゲーム」, mostly stocked with these toys, and young fans shorten the word to 「ぬい」."
    ],
    ja: "{妹|いもうと}に[ぬいぐるみ]をあげました。",
    en: "I gave my little sister a stuffed animal." },
  "まんが|漫画": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「画|が」 means picture or drawing, and you already know it from 「映画|えいが」, a movie, which is literally a projected picture. 「漫|まん」 means loose, casual, wandering, going wherever it likes. Look at its left side, the water piece, which suits something that spreads and flows freely. So the word is something like free, rambling pictures, drawings done for fun rather than serious art. The famous woodblock artist Hokusai used this word for his sketchbooks in the early 1800s, full of people, animals, and funny scenes. Today it means comics, from children's books to huge adventure series read by adults on the train. You also know 「漫画家|まんがか」, cartoonist, which adds 「家|か」, an expert or professional, as in 「作家|さっか」, writer. In English, the word manga has become a loanword itself, meaning Japanese style comics specifically. In Japanese it covers comics from any country. A way to remember it: water flowing freely, and pictures flowing freely across the page. To recap, free, rambling plus picture: comics." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji lead you to other words. The picture kanji, 「画|が」, has two common readings. As 「が」, it appears in 「画家|がか」, a painter, 「画面|がめん」, a screen, like a phone screen, and 「映画|えいが」, a movie. As 「かく」, it appears in 「計画|けいかく」, a plan, where it means to mark out or divide, a different sense of the same kanji. The free and wandering kanji, 「漫|まん」, shows up in 「漫才|まんざい」, the classic Japanese stand-up comedy done by a duo, one silly and one straight. Both comics and comedy share this playful, free-roaming kanji. Then you can add pieces to the comic word itself. A cartoonist is a 「漫画家|まんがか」, and a comic book store might be called a 「漫画専門店|まんがせんもんてん」, a specialist shop. So follow the picture kanji to movies and screens, and the playful kanji to comedy on stage." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Comics are part of everyday adult life in Japan. People read them on trains, in cafés, and on their phones. Big weekly magazines, thick as a phone book, publish many series at once, and the popular ones are later collected into small books. You count those books with 「冊|さつ」, and a numbered volume is 「巻|かん」, so volume three is 「三巻|さんかん」. There are also 「漫画喫茶|まんがきっさ」, manga cafés, where you pay by the hour to read from walls of comics, drink free soft drinks, and some people even stay overnight. Another habit is 「立ち読み|たちよみ」, reading while standing at a bookstore or convenience store shelf, though many shops now seal their comics in plastic. A friend might say, 「この漫画、面白いよ|このまんが、おもしろいよ」, this manga is fun, and lend you the whole set. Notice that 「アニメ」 means animation, a different thing. Comics are drawn, anime moves." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese comics are sorted by their target readers, and the labels are printed right on magazines. 「少年|しょうねん」 manga is for boys, full of action, friendship, and battles. 「少女|しょうじょ」 manga is for girls, often focused on romance and feelings, with sparkling eyes and flowers in the background. 「青年|せいねん」 manga is for young men and adults, with more complex stories. 「女性|じょせい」 manga, sometimes called 「レディース」, is for adult women. These are labels for the magazine, not strict rules, and many readers cross over happily. One huge influence was the artist 「手塚治虫|てづかおさむ」, known as the god of manga, who created Astro Boy in the 1950s and helped shape the movie-like style of modern comics. Today manga is a giant industry, and many anime, films, and games start as comics. So when you pick up a manga, the label on the cover tells you who it was made for." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「漫|まん」 means loose and free-flowing, with the water piece, and 「画|が」 means picture, as in 「映画|えいが」, so it is free, rambling pictures. Hokusai used the word for his sketchbooks.",
      "The picture kanji gives 「画家|がか」, 「画面|がめん」, and, read 「かく」, 「計画|けいかく」, while the free kanji gives 「漫才|まんざい」, duo stand-up comedy.",
      "Adults read manga everywhere, count books with 「冊|さつ」 and volumes with 「巻|かん」, and visit 「漫画喫茶|まんがきっさ」, manga cafés, though 「アニメ」 is the moving version.",
      "Manga is labeled by target reader, like 「少年|しょうねん」 for boys and 「少女|しょうじょ」 for girls, and 「手塚治虫|てづかおさむ」, the god of manga, shaped its modern style."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[漫画|まんが]を{読みます|よみます}。",
    en: "I read comic books every night." },
  "けしょうひん|化粧品": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. 「化|け」 means change or transform. Its usual reading is 「か」, as in 「文化|ぶんか」, culture, but in this word it takes the less common reading 「け」. The same kanji appears in 「お化け|おばけ」, a ghost, something that has transformed. 「粧|しょう」 means to adorn or dress up. Together, 「化粧|けしょう」 means makeup, literally transform and adorn yourself. You will hear it on its own too: 「化粧をする|けしょうをする」, to put on makeup. Then 「品|ひん」 means goods or products. The kanji is three little boxes stacked up, which is a nice hook for a pile of goods. You will see it in many shopping words, like food products or electronic products on store signs. So the whole word is makeup goods, everything from lipstick to skin cream. Japanese drugstores have huge cosmetics sections, and Japanese skin care is very popular with visitors, so this is a useful word for shopping. A way to remember it: picture a ghost, 「お化け|おばけ」, transforming into a beauty with a pile of products. To recap, transform plus adorn plus goods: cosmetics." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The goods kanji, 「品|ひん」, at the end of this word builds a whole shopping list. Food products are 「食品|しょくひん」, products for sale are 「商品|しょうひん」, a work of art is a 「作品|さくひん」, and machine parts are 「部品|ぶひん」. With its native reading 「しな」, it makes 「品物|しなもの」, goods or items. The makeup word itself, 「化粧|けしょう」, makes more compounds too. 「化粧水|けしょうすい」, makeup water, is the lotion or toner that is a key step in Japanese skin care. And here is a very useful one: 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」, makeup room, is a polite word for restroom, the way English says powder room. You will see it on signs in department stores and airports. And the opposite of wearing makeup has its own word, 「すっぴん」, a bare face. So from cosmetics, you can follow the goods kanji to food, art, and machines, or follow the makeup word straight to the restroom." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Shopping for cosmetics in Japan usually means a drugstore, 「ドラッグストア」, where entire aisles are filled with skin care, makeup, and sunscreen at all prices, or a department store counter for luxury brands. Japanese skin care often goes in steps. People wash with a cleanser, then use 「化粧水|けしょうすい」, the toner, then a milky lotion called 「乳液|にゅうえき」, and maybe a cream. Sunscreen, 「日焼け止め|ひやけどめ」, is very important, because pale skin is valued and the summer sun is strong. Men's cosmetics have also grown a lot, and many younger men use skin care. A shop worker might ask you, 「お肌のお悩みはありますか|おはだのおなやみはありますか」, do you have any skin concerns? If you are buying a gift, you might say, 「母に化粧品をプレゼントします|ははにけしょうひんをプレゼントします」. Many tourists buy Japanese cosmetics as souvenirs, so this word opens up a big part of shopping." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「化|け」 means transform, as in 「お化け|おばけ」, and 「粧|しょう」 means adorn, so 「化粧|けしょう」 is makeup. Add 「品|ひん」, goods, and you get cosmetics.",
      "The goods kanji gives 「食品|しょくひん」, 「商品|しょうひん」, and 「作品|さくひん」, while the makeup word gives 「化粧水|けしょうすい」, toner, and 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」, a polite word for restroom.",
      "Japanese skin care goes in steps, with cleanser, 「化粧水|けしょうすい」, then 「乳液|にゅうえき」, plus 「日焼け止め|ひやけどめ」, sunscreen, all found in huge drugstore aisles."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}に[化粧品|けしょうひん]を{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought cosmetics for my mother." },
  "ラジオ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is from English radio, and the vowels tell the story. English says ray-dee-oh. Japanese does not use the ray sound here, and keeps a simple short 「ラ」. The d sound before an ee vowel turns into 「ジ」, the way 「ディ」 used to be hard for older Japanese to say. So you get three short beats. Japanese also uses the English l and r sounds the same, as one r family sound, which is why this starts with 「ラ」. Japan has a famous radio tradition called 「ラジオ体操|ラジオたいそう」, radio exercises. It began in 1928. Every morning, a simple piano tune plays on the radio, and people all over the country do the same set of stretches. In summer vacation, many children gather in a local park early in the morning to do them together and get a stamp on a card. Many companies and older people still do them every day. A way to remember it: hear that cheerful morning piano and picture a whole park stretching in time. To recap, English radio, with a short 「ラ」 and 「ジ」 in the middle, and a country that exercises to it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Radio has played a dramatic role in Japanese history. Public radio broadcasting in Japan began in 1925, and soon the national broadcaster, which later became NHK, was reaching homes across the country. Then on August 15, 1945, came the most famous broadcast of all. For the first time, ordinary people heard the voice of the emperor, announcing over the radio that Japan accepted surrender, ending the Second World War. That speech is called 「玉音放送|ぎょくおんほうそう」, the jewel voice broadcast. Many families gathered around the radio to listen, and the recording is still played on TV every summer. Radio is still important today, especially during earthquakes and typhoons. When the power goes out, a battery or hand-crank radio keeps people informed, so many Japanese homes keep one in their emergency kit. So this small loanword connects to history, to daily morning exercises, and to staying safe in a disaster." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To listen to the radio, you use 「聞く|きく」: 「ラジオを聞く|ラジオをきく」. To say something was on the radio, use 「で」 for the medium: 「ラジオでニュースを聞きました|ラジオでニュースをききました」, I heard the news on the radio. You turn it on with 「つける」 and off with 「消す|けす」, the same verbs as for TV and lights. Radio sets are machines, so you count them with 「台|だい」: 「一台|いちだい」. Many people listen through phone apps today, and late-night radio talk shows have loyal fans, especially students studying for exams. Listeners send in funny messages and postcards, and hearing your name read on air is a thrill. There are also related words. A 「ラジカセ」 is a radio cassette player, a short form of radio plus cassette, and a 「ラジコン」 is a remote-controlled toy car, from radio control. So the radio word appears in old stereos and toy cars too." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English radio with a short 「ラ」 and 「ジ」 in the middle. Since 1928, people across Japan have done 「ラジオ体操|ラジオたいそう」, the same stretches to a morning piano tune.",
      "In 1945 ordinary people first heard the emperor's voice over the radio announcing surrender, the 「玉音放送|ぎょくおんほうそう」, and today radios remain vital in earthquakes and typhoons.",
      "You listen with 「聞く|きく」, mark the medium with 「で」, as in 「ラジオでニュースを聞きました|ラジオでニュースをききました」, and count sets with 「台|だい」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[ラジオ]を{聞きます|ききます}。",
    en: "I listen to the radio every morning." },
  "おさら|お皿": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "「皿|さら」 is a native reading, and the kanji is a picture you can still see. It looks like a dish or bowl seen from the side, sitting on a low stand. Put the polite 「お」 in front, the same one you see in 「お茶|おちゃ」, tea, and 「お酒|おさけ」, alcohol. Food and kitchen words often take this 「お」, and it makes the word sound softer and nicer. Without the 「お」, you will still hear 「皿|さら」 in compounds and in casual speech. The word covers plates, dishes, and saucers, and it can also mean a serving of food, a plateful. You can count dishes with native numbers, like 「一皿|ひとさら」, one plate. A useful phrase from daily life is 「お皿を洗う|おさらをあらう」, to wash the dishes. In Japanese homes, each person often has their own rice bowl and chopsticks, but plates are shared for side dishes. A way to remember it: look at the kanji and see the flat dish sitting on its little stand, ready for food. To recap, a picture of a dish, plus polite 「お」: plates and dishes." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The plate kanji builds a whole kitchen cupboard. A small plate is 「小皿|こざら」, and a big serving platter is 「大皿|おおざら」. Notice how 「さら」 turns into 「ざら」 when it comes second, the voicing change Japanese loves. A small personal plate, used to take food from shared dishes, is a 「取り皿|とりざら」, take plate. An ashtray is 「灰皿|はいざら」, ash plate. Washing dishes is 「皿洗い|さらあらい」. The kanji also appears as a part inside other kanji, usually at the bottom, about containers. To heap food up on a plate is 「盛る|もる」, with the plate shape at its base. And the kanji for blood, 「血|ち」, is the plate with an extra stroke on top. It is traditionally explained as blood in a ritual dish, though such old origins are always a little uncertain. So look for the dish at the bottom of kanji, and you will often find something being held." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Plates star in one of Japan's most famous ghost stories, 「皿屋敷|さらやしき」, the plate mansion. In the most popular version, a maid named 「お菊|おきく」 is blamed for losing one of a set of ten precious plates, and she dies in the garden well. Ever since, her ghost rises from the well at night, counting plates in a sad voice: 「いちまい、にまい、さんまい」, one plate, two plates, three plates, all the way to nine. Then she screams, because the tenth is missing. Notice the counter 「枚|まい」, used for flat things like plates, paper, and shirts. The story has been told in kabuki plays and comedy for centuries, and comedians love to twist the ending. In happier news, conveyor belt sushi uses plate colors to show prices, and at the end staff count your stack of plates to make the bill. So plates are counted in ghost stories and in sushi bars alike." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At home, a common request is 「お皿を出して|おさらをだして」, please get out the plates, or 「お皿を並べて|おさらをならべて」, please set the plates out. After dinner, someone says 「お皿を洗うね|おさらをあらうね」, I'll wash the dishes. At a restaurant, if you want an extra small plate to share food, ask, 「取り皿をください|とりざらをください」. Japanese meals often use many small dishes rather than one big plate, so tables can be full of little plates and bowls of different shapes. People also like to match tableware to the season, with cool glass dishes in summer and heavier pottery in winter. In casual speech you may hear the plain 「皿|さら」, especially from men or in compounds, but 「お皿|おさら」 is the normal everyday form for most people. And when you drop one, the sound is 「ガチャン」, crash! So set them out, share them, wash them, and do not drop them." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「皿|さら」 is a picture of a dish on a low stand, and the polite 「お」 softens it like 「お茶|おちゃ」. Count plates with 「一皿|ひとさら」, and wash them with 「お皿を洗う|おさらをあらう」.",
      "As a second word it voices to 「ざら」, as in 「小皿|こざら」, 「取り皿|とりざら」, and 「灰皿|はいざら」, and the plate shape sits at the bottom of kanji like 「盛る|もる」 and 「血|ち」.",
      "In the ghost story 「皿屋敷|さらやしき」, the maid 「お菊|おきく」 counts plates from a well and screams at the missing tenth, using the flat-thing counter 「枚|まい」.",
      "At a restaurant ask for a small sharing plate with 「取り皿をください|とりざらをください」, and at home say 「お皿を並べて|おさらをならべて」 to set the table."
    ],
    ja: "[お皿|おさら]を{洗いました|あらいました}。",
    en: "I washed the dishes." },
  "おかえし|お返し": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from a verb you already know, 「返す|かえす」, to return something. Take its stem, 「返し|かえし」, and add the polite 「お」, and you get a noun, the thing you give back. The kanji has the road piece along the bottom left, the same one you see in 「送る|おくる」, to send. Both are about something moving along a road, sent out or sent back. You also know the same kanji with its Chinese-origin reading in 「返事|へんじ」, a reply, an answer sent back. In Japanese culture, gift giving is a two way street. When someone gives you a present, it is polite to give something back later, an 「お返し|おかえし」, to show thanks. The famous example is 「ホワイトデー」, when men return Valentine's chocolates. For gifts at weddings or funerals, there is even a custom of returning something worth about half the original gift. It is also used for revenge in a joking way: getting someone back. A way to remember it: what goes down the road comes back up the road. To recap, polite 「お」 plus return: a thank-you gift sent back." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this word every time you pay at a shop. When the cashier gives you your change, they say something like 「三百円のお返しです|さんびゃくえんのおかえしです」, here is your change of three hundred yen. In that setting, the word simply means the money given back to you. So it covers both thank-you gifts and pocket change. In conversation, it also appears in the phrase 「お返しに|おかえしに」, in return. For example, 「お返しにクッキーをあげました|おかえしにクッキーをあげました」, I gave cookies in return. And with friends, it can mean payback in a playful way. If your friend tickles you, you can tickle back and shout 「お返し|おかえし」, here's yours! In sports, getting back at a rival is sometimes described the same way. So the core idea is always something coming back to you, whether it is coins, cookies, or a cheeky revenge." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Return gifts in Japan have special names for each occasion. When a baby is born or a couple marries, family and friends send congratulation gifts, and the family sends back a gift called 「内祝い|うちいわい」, literally inside celebration. Originally it meant sharing your own happiness, but today it usually means the thank-you gift. After a funeral, the family returns something to those who gave condolence money, and this is called 「香典返し|こうでんがえし」. A common rule of thumb is 「半返し|はんがえし」, returning about half the value of what you received. Popular return gifts are towels, tea, sweets, or catalog gifts where the receiver chooses something. Notice that 「かえし」 often becomes 「がえし」 when it comes second in a compound. This careful balance of giving and returning keeps relationships smooth. So in Japan, a gift is often the start of a conversation, and the return gift is the polite reply." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This noun comes from the verb 「返す|かえす」, to give back or return something. That verb is transitive, which means someone returns something: 「本を返す|ほんをかえす」, to return a book. It has an intransitive partner, 「返る|かえる」, where the thing itself comes back, as in an echo coming back or someone coming to their senses. Do not confuse these with 「帰る|かえる」, to go home, which sounds exactly the same as the intransitive one but uses a different kanji. Both 「返す|かえす」 and 「帰る|かえる」 are u-verbs. To make the noun, take the stem 「返し|かえし」 and add the polite 「お」. You can do the same with other verbs. For example, 「お知らせ|おしらせ」, a notice, comes from 「知らせる|しらせる」, to inform. So remember: 「返す|かえす」 for giving back, 「返る|かえる」 for coming back, 「帰る|かえる」 for going home, and this noun for what goes back." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the stem of 「返す|かえす」, to return, plus polite 「お」, meaning the thing you give back. Gifts in Japan call for a return gift, like on 「ホワイトデー」.",
      "Cashiers say 「三百円のお返しです|さんびゃくえんのおかえしです」 for your change, 「お返しに|おかえしに」 means in return, and with friends it can mean playful payback.",
      "Return gifts have names, like 「内祝い|うちいわい」 for births and weddings and 「香典返し|こうでんがえし」 after funerals, often following 「半返し|はんがえし」, returning about half the value.",
      "「返す|かえす」 is giving something back, 「返る|かえる」 is something coming back, and 「帰る|かえる」 is going home, while this noun is the verb stem plus 「お」."
    ],
    ja: "ホワイトデーに[お返し|おかえし]をあげました。",
    en: "I gave a return gift on White Day." },
  "りれきしょ|履歴書": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all Chinese-origin readings. 「履|り」 means to step on, or to put on footwear. You know a related native word, 「はく」, to put on items below your waist, which is often written with this kanji when it means shoes. Then 「歴|れき」 means history, things that have passed, as in the word for history, 「歴史|れきし」. Together, 「履歴|りれき」 is the path you have walked, your personal history. It is also used for things like your browser history. Last, 「書|しょ」 is writing or document, the same kanji as in 「辞書|じしょ」, dictionary, 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, and 「書く|かく」, to write. So the whole word is a document of the path you have walked. Japanese résumés are a bit different from Western ones. They usually follow a fixed form you can buy at a convenience store, with boxes for your education and work history, and a small ID photo glued in the corner. Some companies still prefer them handwritten. A way to remember it: picture footprints walking across a page. To recap, footsteps plus history plus document: your résumé." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The history kanji, 「歴|れき」, builds several words you will see on job applications. Your educational background is 「学歴|がくれき」, your history of study. Your work history is 「職歴|しょくれき」. Your career background in general is 「経歴|けいれき」. And 「歴史|れきし」 is history in the big sense. The first kanji, 「履|り」, is used for things you put on your feet. With its native reading it is 「履く|はく」, to wear shoes, and it appears in 「草履|ぞうり」, traditional straw or flat sandals. It also shows up in 「履修|りしゅう」, taking a course at school, the idea of stepping through a program. So the idea of stepping and walking runs through the whole family. On your résumé, you list your 「学歴|がくれき」 and 「職歴|しょくれき」 in neat rows, so it really is a record of where your feet have taken you. From sandals to careers, these kanji walk together." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Job hunting in Japan has its own rhythm. University students start in their third year, in a season called 「就活|しゅうかつ」, short for job hunting activities. They wear nearly identical dark suits, often called recruit suits, and attend company information sessions. The résumé is often filled in by hand, neatly, in black ink, and mistakes mean starting over rather than using correction tape. It includes a photo taken in a booth or studio, a list of education and jobs by year, and a section for 「志望動機|しぼうどうき」, your reason for applying. People changing jobs usually also submit a 「職務経歴書|しょくむけいれきしょ」, a separate document describing their work in detail, which is closer to a Western CV. Many part-time jobs also ask for one. A store manager might tell you, 「履歴書を持ってきてください|りれきしょをもってきてください」, please bring your résumé. So even for a café job, you will need this neat form." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「履|り」 is stepping or footwear, 「歴|れき」 is history, and 「書|しょ」 is document, so it is a document of the path you have walked. Japanese résumés use a fixed form with a small photo.",
      "The history kanji gives 「学歴|がくれき」, education, and 「職歴|しょくれき」, work history, while the step kanji gives 「履く|はく」 and 「草履|ぞうり」, sandals.",
      "In 「就活|しゅうかつ」, job hunting, résumés are often handwritten neatly in black ink, and even part-time jobs ask you to bring one, as in 「履歴書を持ってきてください|りれきしょをもってきてください」."
    ],
    ja: "[履歴書|りれきしょ]を{送りました|おくりました}。",
    en: "I sent my résumé." },
  "クリスマス|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is English Christmas, and the changes are clear. English starts with a cluster, chr, and Japanese splits it up with an extra vowel, giving 「クリ」. The t in the middle is silent in English too, so it disappears. Then mas becomes 「マス」, with an extra vowel at the end, since Japanese words do not end in s. Five short beats in total. The holiday itself took on a very different shape in Japan. It is not a religious or family holiday for most people, and it is not a day off work. Instead, Christmas Eve is a romantic night for couples, a bit like Valentine's Day in the West. Families often eat a Christmas cake, usually a white sponge cake with whipped cream and strawberries. And since the 1970s, a fried chicken chain made eating fried chicken a Christmas tradition, so people order buckets weeks in advance. The big family holiday comes a week later, with 「お正月|おしょうがつ」, New Year's. A way to remember it: picture a strawberry cake and a bucket of chicken under the lights. To recap, English Christmas with extra vowels, and a date night in Japan." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Christmas reached Japan much earlier than many people think. Christian missionaries arrived in the 1500s, starting with Francis Xavier in 1549, and the first Christmas mass in Japan is often said to have been held in Yamaguchi in 1552. But Christianity was banned in the early 1600s, and for over two centuries the holiday nearly vanished, kept only by hidden Christians in secret. After Japan reopened in the late 1800s, the holiday returned with foreign residents. Around 1900, shops in Tokyo, especially in the Ginza area, began using Christmas decorations to attract customers. By the 1920s and 30s, it was becoming a fun, commercial event in cities, and after the war it spread nationwide with cakes, presents, and lights. Throughout, it stayed mostly a cheerful seasonal event rather than a religious one. So this word carries centuries of history, from missionaries to department store windows." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The holiday comes with a bundle of loanwords. Christmas Eve is 「クリスマスイブ」, often just 「イブ」, and in Japan Eve can be bigger than the day itself. Santa Claus is 「サンタクロース」, but kids usually call him 「サンタさん」, adding the polite 「さん」 like a neighbor. A Christmas tree is 「クリスマスツリー」, and the greeting is 「メリークリスマス」. Presents are 「プレゼント」, and the lights that decorate city streets are called 「イルミネーション」, from illumination. Couples go out to see these famous light displays on date night. Shops sell decorated cakes with a small Santa doll on top. Notice how almost every word is English, because the holiday arrived as a Western custom. The few native words involved belong to winter in general, like 「雪|ゆき」, snow. So learn the set together, and you can talk about the whole season." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "One of the most striking things about the holiday in Japan is how quickly it ends. Christmas Day is a normal working day, and on the evening of the twenty-fifth, shops take down all the Christmas decorations. By the next morning, the twenty-sixth, they are replaced with New Year's decorations, like pine and bamboo arrangements, 「門松|かどまつ」, and the mood shifts to the biggest holiday of the year. Cake shops that sold Christmas cakes switch to New Year's foods. In conversation, people ask each other about their plans: 「クリスマスはどうしますか|クリスマスはどうしますか」, what are you doing for Christmas? A common answer from a single friend is a joke about spending it alone, which in Japan is a popular comic complaint, since the day is linked with couples. Many families give children presents from Santa that night. So the holiday is short, sweet, and a little romantic." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English Christmas with extra vowels, five beats long. In Japan it is not a day off, Christmas Eve is a date night for couples, and families eat strawberry cake and fried chicken.",
      "Missionaries brought Christmas in the 1500s, it vanished under the ban on Christianity, and it returned in the late 1800s, growing into a cheerful commercial event with Ginza shop decorations.",
      "The season comes with loanwords like 「クリスマスイブ」, 「サンタさん」, 「メリークリスマス」, and 「イルミネーション」, the light displays couples go to see.",
      "It ends fast, as decorations come down on the evening of the twenty-fifth and are replaced by 「門松|かどまつ」 for New Year's, the year's biggest holiday."
    ],
    ja: "[クリスマス]にケーキを{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat cake at Christmas." },
  "バレンタインデー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is English Valentine's Day, and it shows several sound changes at once. Japanese has no v sound in its basic set, so v becomes b: 「バ」. The l becomes Japanese r: 「レ」. Then tine becomes 「タイン」, and day becomes 「デー」, with a long vowel instead of the English glide. You can see the same day ending in 「ホワイトデー」. The custom is Japan's own twist. On February 14, women give chocolate to men. There is 「本命|ほんめい」 chocolate, often handmade, for someone you truly love, and 「義理|ぎり」 chocolate, obligation chocolate, for coworkers, bosses, and classmates. Friends also swap chocolates with each other. Chocolate companies promoted the day heavily from the 1950s onward, and it became a huge season for sweet shops. Then, one month later, men return the favor. Some offices have started banning obligation chocolate, since buying for everyone gets expensive. A way to remember it: in Japan, the arrow of love flies from women to men on this day, and comes back a month later. To recap, English Valentine's Day, v to b and l to r, and women give the chocolate." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The day is named after Saint Valentine, a Christian priest in ancient Rome. Little is known about him for certain, and the link to romance came much later, in medieval Europe. In Japan, the holiday arrived through business. One of the earliest known ads in Japan came from Morozoff, a sweets company in Kobe, which in 1936 printed an English-language ad in a newspaper for foreign residents, suggesting chocolate as a Valentine's gift. After the war, department stores and chocolate makers promoted the day in the 1950s and 60s. Exactly who first came up with women giving to men is debated, but by the 1970s, especially among school girls, it had become the custom. It caught on partly because it gave women a socially accepted chance to confess their feelings, which was rare at the time. So this day is a mix of Roman legend, European romance, and very Japanese marketing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "For many young people, this is the day for 「告白|こくはく」, confessing your feelings to someone. A girl might hand a boy chocolate behind the school building and say, 「好きです|すきです」, I like you. Boys often pretend not to care, while secretly counting how many chocolates they get. In recent years, new styles have appeared. 「逆チョコ|ぎゃくチョコ」, reverse chocolate, is when men give chocolate to women. Many women now spend more on chocolate for themselves or their friends than on obligation chocolate for coworkers. Big department stores hold huge chocolate fairs with famous chocolatiers from around the world. A typical question between friends is, 「誰にあげるの|だれにあげるの」, who are you giving it to? Notice the verb 「あげる」, to give, for chocolates going out from you. So the day mixes nervous confessions, office obligations, and treats for yourself." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "You may sometimes see this word written with a special katakana spelling, 「ヴァレンタインデー」, starting with 「ヴ」, which is the u kana with two dots. That letter was made to show the English v sound. In practice, most Japanese people say it exactly like 「バ」, so the two spellings sound the same. Shops sometimes use the fancy v spelling to look stylish and foreign. The government recommends the simpler 「バ」 spelling for most words, which is why it is the standard. You will see the same choice in words like 「バイオリン」, violin, sometimes written 「ヴァイオリン」. Also notice the ending. Japanese uses 「デー」 for day in made-up holiday names, but in other words, day often becomes 「デイ」, as in 「デイサービス」, day care for the elderly. So the spelling can change, but the sound stays put. Two spellings, one sweet holiday." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is English Valentine's Day with v becoming 「バ」 and day becoming 「デー」. In Japan, women give men chocolate, either 「本命|ほんめい」 for true love or 「義理|ぎり」 for obligation.",
      "The day arrived through business, with a Kobe sweets company advertising in 1936 and chocolate makers promoting it after the war, and by the 1970s women giving to men was the custom.",
      "It is a day for 「告白|こくはく」, confessing your feelings, and newer styles include 「逆チョコ|ぎゃくチョコ」, reverse chocolate from men, and treats for yourself.",
      "It is sometimes written 「ヴァレンタインデー」 to look stylish, but it is said the same as 「バ」, and made-up holiday names use 「デー」 rather than 「デイ」."
    ],
    ja: "[バレンタインデー]にチョコレートをあげました。",
    en: "I gave chocolate on Valentine's Day." },
  "ホワイトデー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This looks English, but it was made in Japan. English speakers do not have a White Day. It is built from English white, squeezed into 「ホワイト」, and day, 「デー」, with a long vowel, the same ending as in 「バレンタインデー」. Notice how white becomes 「ホワイト」: the w sound gets a 「ホ」 before it, and the final t gets an extra vowel. On March 14, exactly one month after Valentine's Day, men give a return gift, an 「お返し|おかえし」, to the women who gave them chocolate. Japanese candy and sweet makers started promoting it around 1980, and the name may come from white sweets like marshmallows and white chocolate. Popular gifts are cookies, candy, or white chocolate. There is a joking rule called three times return, meaning a man should give back something worth about three times the chocolate he got. The day has spread to some other countries in East Asia. A way to remember it: Valentine's is dark chocolate going out, and a month later the white sweets come back. To recap, made in Japan from white plus day, the day men give back." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Several companies claim credit for this day, and the story is a little tangled. A sweets maker in Fukuoka says it started a marshmallow day in 1978, suggesting men return Valentine's chocolate with marshmallows wrapped around chocolate. Around the same time, a national candy industry group promoted the idea of a return day, and in 1980 they launched it under the name White Day. Other confectioners also say they had similar ideas. Whichever came first, the color white was linked with sweets like marshmallows, white chocolate, and sugar candy, and also with the idea of pure, innocent love. The day quickly caught on, because it fit so well with the Japanese custom of returning gifts. It has since spread to South Korea, Taiwan, and China. So this holiday is a modern invention, born from a sweet industry idea and a very Japanese sense of fairness." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Choosing a gift for this day can be stressful. Men think about who gave them chocolate, and how much, and then buy something suitable for each. A man might ask a coworker, 「お返し、何にした|おかえし、なににした」, what did you get as your return gift? There is a well-known internet rumor that each sweet has a hidden meaning: candy means I like you, cookies mean let's be friends, and marshmallows mean I don't like you. It is just a popular myth, not a real rule, but some people do worry about it. Common gifts include cookies, candy, small accessories, or even handkerchiefs. Department stores set up special corners with gift ideas in early March. Office workers often pool money to buy a group return for colleagues. If you receive one, say 「ありがとう、うれしい」, thank you, I'm happy. So it is a day of careful thank-yous." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is made in Japan from white and day, and on March fourteenth men give an 「お返し|おかえし」 to women who gave them Valentine's chocolate. A joking rule says to return three times the value.",
      "Several companies claim it, from a Fukuoka marshmallow day in 1978 to a candy industry launch in 1980, and white links it to marshmallows and pure love.",
      "Men fret over who gave what, ask coworkers 「お返し、何にした|おかえし、なににした」, and some worry about a popular myth that each sweet carries a hidden meaning."
    ],
    ja: "[ホワイトデー]は{三月|さんがつ}{十四日|じゅうよっか}です。",
    en: "White Day is March fourteenth." },
  "ほしい|欲しい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「欲|よく」 means desire or greed, and here it is read with its native reading, 「ほ」, plus 「しい」. The word works like an i-adjective, not a verb, even though the English is to want. That is the key to using it. The thing you want is marked with 「が」, because you are describing it as wanted: 「車が欲しいです|くるまがほしいです」, I want a car. You can also see the same pattern in other feelings words, like 「好き|すき」, where the thing you like takes 「が」. It changes like any i-adjective: 「欲しくない|ほしくない」, I do not want it, and 「欲しかった|ほしかった」, I wanted it. Two common mistakes. First, this is for wanting things, nouns. For wanting to do something, use the 「たい」 form instead, like 「食べたい|たべたい」, I want to eat. Second, it describes your own feelings. Japanese avoids stating what is inside someone else's head, so for others you use other forms you will learn later. Also, asking a teacher if they want something with this word can sound too direct. To recap, desire as an adjective: the thing you want takes 「が」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji for desire has two parts. On the left is valley, 「谷|たに」. On the right is a part that looks like a person with an open mouth, often explained as a yawning or gaping person. You also see it in 「歌|うた」, song, and 「飲む|のむ」, to drink, both about the mouth. Many teachers use the picture of a wide-open mouth wanting to fill up, which is a handy memory trick, though the full origin is more technical. The Chinese-origin reading is 「よく」. It appears in 「食欲|しょくよく」, appetite, the desire to eat, and in 「欲張り|よくばり」, greedy, someone who wants too much. With the native reading, besides this adjective, there is the formal verb 「欲する|ほっする」, to desire, used in writing. Also note that when this word follows a te-form, as in want someone to do something, it is usually written in kana. So remember the open mouth next to the valley, hungry for more." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adjective is about your own wants, so you need a different form for other people. Later you will meet 「欲しがる|ほしがる」, which describes someone showing signs of wanting something. It is a verb, and the thing takes 「を」: 「弟は新しいゲームを欲しがっています|おとうとはあたらしいゲームをほしがっています」, my little brother wants a new game. Another pattern is attaching it to a te-form to mean you want someone else to do something. 「手伝ってほしい|てつだってほしい」 means I want you to help me. The person you want it from is marked with 「に」: 「母に来てほしい|ははにきてほしい」, I want my mother to come. In questions, it works fine for friends: 「何が欲しい|なにがほしい」, what do you want? You can also say 「欲しいもの|ほしいもの」, the thing I want. So the adjective handles your own wishes, and a few small add-ons stretch it to others." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Using this word politely takes care. With friends and family, it is perfectly natural. Before a birthday, someone asks, 「誕生日に何が欲しい|たんじょうびになにがほしい」, what do you want for your birthday? You answer, 「新しいかばんが欲しいな|あたらしいかばんがほしいな」, I'd like a new bag. But asking a teacher or a customer with this word sounds pushy, as if you are looking into their desires. So instead of asking your teacher if they want coffee with this word, you say 「コーヒーはいかがですか」, how about some coffee? Also, saying you want something very directly in a shop can sound abrupt. Shoppers usually say 「これをください」, or 「これがいいです」. Children, though, say it all the time, pointing at toys: 「これ欲しい|これほしい」. So use it freely with close people, and soften it with anyone above you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is an adjective, not a verb, so the thing you want takes 「が」, as in 「車が欲しいです|くるまがほしいです」. Use it for things, use 「たい」 for actions, and only for your own wants.",
      "The kanji has 「谷|たに」, valley, beside a gaping mouth, a handy picture of wanting to fill up, and read 「よく」 it appears in 「食欲|しょくよく」, appetite, and 「欲張り|よくばり」, greedy.",
      "For someone else's wants use 「欲しがる|ほしがる」 with 「を」, and after a 「て」 form it means wanting someone to do something, as in 「手伝ってほしい|てつだってほしい」.",
      "It is natural with friends, as in 「誕生日に何が欲しい|たんじょうびになにがほしい」, but with a teacher or customer offer 「コーヒーはいかがですか」 instead, since it sounds pushy."
    ],
    ja: "{新しい|あたらしい}{車|くるま}が[欲しい|ほしい]です。",
    en: "I want a new car." },
  "おしゃれ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a 「な」-adjective. Put 「な」 before a noun, as in 「おしゃれなシャツ」, a stylish shirt, or 「おしゃれな店|おしゃれなみせ」, a trendy shop. You can also use it on its own: 「山田さんはおしゃれです|やまださんはおしゃれです」, Ms. Yamada is stylish. It works for people, clothes, cafes, rooms, even a well designed website. It can also be a noun meaning dressing up, as in 「おしゃれをする」, to dress up for something. The word comes from 「お」 plus 「洒落|しゃれ」, an older word for being stylish or witty. That 「しゃれ」 also means a clever play on words, and lives on in 「だじゃれ」, a bad pun, the Japanese version of a dad joke. So being stylish and being witty come from the same root, the idea of something clever and well done. You will hear young people use it constantly as praise, a bit like English cool or chic. A way to remember it: picture someone so stylish they also tell clever jokes. To recap, a 「な」-adjective for stylish, from the same root as a clever pun." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Walk into a cafe in Tokyo with a friend, look around at the wooden tables and hanging plants, and the natural thing to say is 「おしゃれだね」. It is one of the most common compliments in everyday Japanese, and it is aimed at taste and design, not at beauty. Compare it with words you know. 「きれい」 is about being pretty or clean. 「かわいい」 is cute. 「かっこいい」 is cool or good-looking, often about a person's whole vibe. This word sits between them: it praises good sense and style. So you can tell a friend 「その靴、おしゃれ|そのくつ、おしゃれ」 about their shoes, but you would not use it for a sunset. There is also a verb use: 「おしゃれする」 means to dress up, for example before a date or a party. Someone who loves fashion can be called an 「おしゃれな人|おしゃれなひと」. Next time something in Japan looks tasteful and well put together, try this word." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture a very fancy friend named Shah, wearing a silk scarf and round sunglasses. Every time you meet, you gasp, oh, Shah, ray of style! Oh, Shah, ray. That is roughly how the word sounds, and the gasp is exactly the feeling it carries. Now add a second picture. Shah walks into a plain, boring room and in five minutes rearranges it with a plant, a lamp, and a nice rug, and the whole room becomes stylish too. That reminds you the word is not only for people. Rooms, shops, menus, even a well designed website can earn it. Keep the image of Shah, the ray of style, whenever you see something tasteful. One stylish friend, one gasp, one word." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Because this is a 「な」-adjective, it follows the same patterns you learned for 「きれい」 and 「げんき」. Polite present is 「おしゃれです」, and the negative is 「おしゃれじゃないです」. For the past, say 「おしゃれでした」, it was stylish. To link it with another adjective, use the 「で」 form: 「おしゃれで安い店|おしゃれでやすいみせ」, a shop that is stylish and cheap. A very handy pattern turns it into an adverb with 「に」: 「おしゃれに着る|おしゃれにきる」, to wear something stylishly. And if you want to say someone has become more stylish, use 「なる」: 「おしゃれになりました」. One trap for learners is treating it like an 「い」-adjective and adding 「くない」 to it for the negative. That form does not exist. Remember it is a 「な」-adjective all the way through." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「お」 plus 「洒落|しゃれ」, an old word for stylish or witty, the same root as 「だじゃれ」, a bad pun. Being stylish and being clever share one root.",
      "It is the go-to compliment for taste and design, like saying 「おしゃれだね」 in a nice cafe. Unlike 「きれい」 or 「かわいい」, it praises good sense and style, not beauty.",
      "Picture a fancy friend named Shah and gasp, oh, Shah, ray of style! Shah can make a whole room stylish too, so the word fits places as well as people.",
      "It is a 「な」-adjective, so say 「おしゃれじゃないです」 for the negative and 「おしゃれに着る|おしゃれにきる」 for wearing something stylishly. Never add 「くない」 to it."
    ],
    ja: "{山田|やまだ}さんはいつも[おしゃれ]です。",
    en: "Ms. Yamada is always stylish." },
  "けち|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Call someone this and you are saying they hate spending money or sharing. It is a bit rude, so use it about friends in a joking way, not to your boss. It works both as a noun, a stingy person, and as a 「な」-adjective: 「けちな人|けちなひと」, a stingy person. Kids shout 「けち」 at a friend who will not share their snacks, a bit like an English child saying meanie. You also hear it in the phrase about not being petty, like a small, cheap complaint. You might see it written in katakana as 「ケチ」 in manga or ads, which makes it feel more punchy. The opposite idea is someone who happily treats others, and you know a verb for that: 「おごる」, to treat someone to a meal. So the friend who never 「おごる」 might get called 「けち」. A way to remember it: it sounds like the noise of a tight purse snapping shut, ketch. Short and sharp, just like the stingy person who will not open their wallet. To recap, a short, cheeky word for stingy, best used in fun among friends." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture two friends splitting a bill. One wants to pay exactly for the three fries they ate, down to the last yen. The other laughs and says 「けちだなあ」, you are so stingy. That teasing tone is where this word lives. You also hear the doubled form, 「けちけちする」, which is a verb meaning to be tight with money or to pinch pennies. A friend might say 「けちけちしないで」, do not be so stingy, when you refuse to share dessert. There is also a useful set phrase, 「けちをつける」, which has drifted away from money. It means to find fault with something, to nitpick, as when someone criticizes every little thing about your plan. So the word can describe being small-minded in general, not just with cash. Keep it for friends and family, and use it with a smile." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let us place this word next to its neighbors. The friend who happily pays for everyone is the opposite, and you know the action: 「おごる」, to treat someone. A classic line is 「今日はおごるよ|きょうはおごるよ」, today is on me. Another opposite is the phrase 「気前がいい|きまえがいい」, generous, which literally means having a good spirit in front of others. Then there is a gentler, positive cousin of being careful with money: 「節約|せつやく」, saving or economizing. Someone who is 「節約|せつやく」 minded is sensible, while someone who is 「けち」 is annoying. The difference is that one saves for themselves wisely, and the other refuses to share. So when you want to compliment a careful spender, say they are good at saving, not that they are stingy. A small choice of word changes the whole mood." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A cheeky, slightly rude word for someone who hates spending or sharing, best used jokingly among friends. Think of a tight purse snapping shut, ketch, the opposite of a friend who will 「おごる」.",
      "Friends tease with 「けちだなあ」, and 「けちけちする」 means to pinch pennies. The phrase 「けちをつける」 drifts away from money and means to nitpick or find fault.",
      "Its opposites are 「おごる」, to treat someone, and 「気前がいい|きまえがいい」, generous. For a careful spender, praise their 「節約|せつやく」, saving, since 「けち」 means refusing to share."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[けち]です。",
    en: "My older brother is stingy." },
  "おくる|送る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「送|そう」 is read here with its native reading, 「おく」, plus 「る」. Look at the bottom left of the kanji. That curving piece is the road or movement piece, and you see it in many words about going places, like 「返す|かえす」, to return, and 「近い|ちかい」, near. So you can picture something moving off down the road. The word has two big meanings. First, to send things: 「手紙を送る|てがみをおくる」, to send a letter, or to send an email or a package. Second, to see someone off or take someone somewhere: 「駅まで送ります|えきまでおくります」, I will take you to the station. In Japan, it is polite to walk a guest to the door, or even to the station, rather than saying goodbye inside. The person you send to takes 「に」: 「母に送る|ははにおくる」, send to my mother. It is a regular u-verb, so 「送ります|おくります」 and 「送って|おくって」. Do not mix it up with 「おくれる」, to be late. A way to remember it: things travel down the road in the kanji, letters or people. To recap, sending things or people off down the road." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a Chinese-origin reading too, 「そう」, and it appears in some very practical words. 「送料|そうりょう」 is the shipping fee, the cost of sending something, which you will see on every online shop. 「放送|ほうそう」 is broadcasting, sending sound or pictures out over the air, so a TV or radio station is a place for 「放送|ほうそう」. 「送別会|そうべつかい」 is a farewell party, the party where you send a colleague off. With the native reading, you get 「見送る|みおくる」, to see someone off, literally to watch them as you send them away, as at an airport gate. And one that every learner meets: 「送り仮名|おくりがな」, the kana that follow a kanji in a word like this one, the little 「る」 that is sent along after it. In every one of these words, something leaves you and goes off somewhere. Shipping, broadcasting, farewells, all are sending." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb pairs beautifully with the giving patterns from the same part of your course. Add 「あげる」 to its 「て」 form and you get 「送ってあげる|おくってあげる」, to send something for someone as a favor, or to give someone a lift. 「駅まで送ってあげます|えきまでおくってあげます」, I will drive you to the station. Flip the direction with 「くれる」: 「父が駅まで送ってくれました|ちちがえきまでおくってくれました」, my dad kindly drove me to the station. And with 「もらう」: 「友だちに写真を送ってもらいました|ともだちにしゃしんをおくってもらいました」, I had my friend send me the photos. This is a great verb to practice those forms on, because sending is so often a kindness. Remember it is a regular u-verb, so the 「て」 form has a small 「っ」, like 「待って|まって」. One verb, three directions of kindness." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is something advanced but fun. Japanese has another verb pronounced exactly the same way, written with a different kanji, 「贈る|おくる」. That one means to give a present, especially a formal or heartfelt gift, like flowers on a birthday or a gift at a wedding. You will see it on gift wrap and in shop signs. So in speech, the same sound can mean sending a package or presenting a gift, and context tells you which. For your level, the common kanji is the one in this lesson, and it covers emails, letters, packages, and giving someone a ride. Also keep this word apart from 「おくれる」, to be late, which only adds one sound. A quick check: if something is going away from you, toward someone, you want this verb. If someone is not arriving on time, you want 「おくれる」. One sound, carefully placed." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the road or movement piece, so picture things heading off down the road. It means sending things, like 「手紙を送る|てがみをおくる」, and seeing people off, as in 「駅まで送ります|えきまでおくります」.",
      "With the reading 「そう」 it gives 「送料|そうりょう」, shipping fee, 「放送|ほうそう」, broadcasting, and 「送別会|そうべつかい」, a farewell party. In every word, something leaves you and goes somewhere.",
      "It pairs well with giving verbs: 「送ってあげる|おくってあげる」 and 「送ってくれました|おくってくれました」, as when someone kindly drives you to the station. Sending is so often a kindness.",
      "A same-sounding verb, 「贈る|おくる」, means to give a heartfelt gift. Also keep it apart from 「おくれる」, to be late: if something is going away toward someone, you want this verb."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}に{手紙|てがみ}を[送りました|おくりました|おくる]。",
    en: "I sent a letter to my mother." },
  "にあう|似合う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces, both native readings. 「似|に」 means to resemble. Look at the left side, the person piece, as in 「休む|やすむ」, to rest. Then 「合う|あう」 means to fit or match. You know that kanji from 「試合|しあい」, a match or game, where two sides meet, and 「付き合う|つきあう」, to date or keep company. Do not mix it up with 「会う|あう」, to meet, which sounds the same but uses a different kanji. So the word is something like resemble and fit: the clothes and the person match each other so well that they seem made for each other. The pattern is: the item takes 「が」, and the person takes 「に」: 「その色が田中さんに似合う|そのいろがたなかさんににあう」. Most often, though, people just say it as a compliment to someone's face: 「よく似合いますね|よくにあいますね」, that really suits you. It is a great phrase to use when a friend shows you new clothes or a new haircut. A way to remember it: the person and the outfit resemble each other and fit together. To recap, resemble plus fit: something looks good on someone." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Imagine you are clothes shopping in Japan. You step out of the fitting room, and the shop assistant immediately says 「お似合いですよ|おにあいですよ」, it suits you. That polite form with 「お」 in front is standard shop talk. With friends, you hear the plain version, 「似合う|にあう」, or with surprise, 「めっちゃ似合う|めっちゃにあう」, it looks amazing on you, though that one is slangy. The negative is also common and can be gently honest: 「あまり似合わないね|あまりにあわないね」, it does not really suit you. Another lovely phrase: 「お似合いのカップル|おにあいのカップル」, a well-matched couple, two people who look perfect together. And when someone compliments you, the humble response is to smile and say 「そうですか」 or 「ありがとうございます」 rather than agreeing too eagerly. Use this word whenever you want to make a friend feel good about a new look." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a compound, and the second half decides how it conjugates. 「合う|あう」 is a regular u-verb, so this one is too: 「似合います|にあいます」, 「似合って|にあって」, and the negative 「似合わない|にあわない」, with that classic 「わ」 that appears when a verb ends in 「う」. It is an intransitive verb, so it never takes 「を」. The thing that looks good takes 「が」, and the person takes 「に」. Very often both are left out in conversation, leaving just 「似合う|にあう」. Notice also the difference from 「着る|きる」 and 「かぶる」. Those are actions: you put on clothes or a hat. This one describes a result, how it looks once it is on. A typical mistake is using the 「ている」 form for this compliment. In fact the plain 「似合う|にあう」 or 「似合いますね|にあいますね」 is most natural. A small verb, a big compliment." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let us look harder at the first kanji. 「似|に」 means to resemble, and on its own it makes the verb 「似る|にる」. You will meet it often in the form 「似ている|にている」, to look like someone: 「私は母に似ています|わたしはははににています」, I look like my mother. Its Chinese-origin reading, 「じ」, appears in words like 「類似|るいじ」, similarity, which you will meet much later. On the left of the kanji is the standing person piece. On the right is a piece that also appears in 「以|い」, found in words like 「以上|いじょう」, more than. That right piece mostly gives the sound. Put together, the verb in this lesson is really resemble plus fit. When clothes suit you, it is as if they resemble you, matching your face and personality. So when you see the person piece, think of a person being matched with a look." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「似|に」, resemble, plus 「合う|あう」, fit: the outfit and the person match as if made for each other. The item takes 「が」 and the person takes 「に」, but usually you just say 「よく似合いますね|よくにあいますね」.",
      "Shop assistants say 「お似合いですよ|おにあいですよ」 when you leave the fitting room. You can also call a well-matched couple 「お似合いのカップル|おにあいのカップル」.",
      "Because 「合う|あう」 is a u-verb, the negative is 「似合わない|にあわない」. It is intransitive, never takes 「を」, and describes how something looks once on, unlike 「着る|きる」, which is the action.",
      "The first kanji alone makes 「似る|にる」, as in 「母に似ています|ははににています」, I look like my mother. Its left side is the person piece, so think of a person matched with a look."
    ],
    ja: "そのセーター、よく[似合います|にあいます|にあう]ね。",
    en: "That sweater looks good on you." },
  "あきらめる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a 「る」-verb, so 「あきらめます」, 「あきらめて」, 「あきらめない」. It has kanji, but they are hard, so kana is common. The thing you give up takes 「を」: 「ゆめをあきらめる」, to give up on a dream. You will hear the negative request a lot as encouragement: 「あきらめないで」, do not give up. Japanese sports anime and manga use that line constantly. Its opposite in spirit is 「頑張る|がんばる」, to keep trying, do your best. One interesting note: dictionaries link this verb to an older verb meaning to make something clear. The idea is that giving up comes from seeing the situation clearly and accepting it. So in Japanese, giving up can sound less like a failure and more like accepting reality calmly. Related to this, you will hear the phrase that it cannot be helped, which expresses the same calm acceptance. A common mistake: English speakers sometimes use it for quitting a habit, like smoking. For that, people usually say they stop it. This word is more about letting go of a hope or goal. A way to remember it: picture someone sighing, ah, and letting the dream float away. To recap, letting go of a goal, with the thing you drop marked by 「を」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "You will usually see this verb in kana, but you should recognize the kanji form, 「諦める|あきらめる」. The kanji has the speech piece on the left, the same one in 「話す|はなす」 and 「語|ご」. On the right is 「帝|てい」, which means emperor. That right part mainly supplies the sound. Here is the genuinely interesting history. In Buddhism, this kanji was used for truth, the deep truths about life, as in the Four Noble Truths. So the kanji carries a sense of seeing things as they really are. That fits the idea in older Japanese that giving up is really seeing clearly and accepting. The kanji is not on the everyday list most learners study early, which is why books and signs often use kana. Still, on a manga page with 「諦めない|あきらめない」 in bold, you will now know what you are looking at. Speech plus emperor, a word of truth." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Let us look at how people really say this. The most famous line comes from sports and anime. A coach tells the team that if you give up, the game is over right there. Encouraging lines like 「最後まであきらめないで|さいごまであきらめないで」, do not give up until the end, are everywhere. In daily life, the tone is often calmer. If a train is cancelled, someone might sigh, 「しかたがない。あきらめよう」, it cannot be helped, let us give up on it. That phrase, 「しかたがない」, often appears right beside this verb. There is also a fun expression about personality. 「あきらめが悪い|あきらめがわるい」 means someone is bad at giving up, stubborn, the type who keeps trying long after everyone else stopped. Depending on tone, that can be a criticism or quiet praise. Persistence and acceptance, both live in this one verb." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch out for a few look-alike sounds. First, 「あきる」, to get tired of something, to be bored with it. If you eat curry every day, eventually you 「あきる」. That is about losing interest, not about giving up a goal. Second, 「あける」, to open, which you know from opening a door or a window. Third, 「あかるい」, bright, cheerful. Interestingly, that brightness connects to the older idea mentioned in the main lesson, making things clear, because 「明るい|あかるい」 uses the kanji for clear and bright. But in daily use, keep them separate. Here is a memory scene to lock this verb in. Picture a hiker named Aki, staring up at a huge mountain. Aki says, I am Aki, and I am a lemon, I give up. Aki, lemon, give up. It is silly, but that is the point. One sigh at the bottom of a mountain." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is a 「る」-verb meaning to let go of a goal, with the thing you drop marked by 「を」, as in 「ゆめをあきらめる」. Its link to an old verb for making things clear makes giving up sound like calm acceptance.",
      "The kanji form 「諦める|あきらめる」 has the speech piece plus 「帝|てい」 for sound. In Buddhism the kanji meant truth, fitting the idea that giving up means seeing things as they really are.",
      "Coaches and anime cry 「あきらめないで」, do not give up, while daily life pairs it calmly with 「しかたがない」. Someone stubborn is 「あきらめが悪い|あきらめがわるい」, bad at giving up.",
      "Do not confuse it with 「あきる」, to get bored, 「あける」, to open, or 「あかるい」, bright. Picture hiker Aki at a huge mountain saying, I am Aki, and I am a lemon, I give up."
    ],
    ja: "[あきらめないで|あきらめないで|あきらめる]ください。",
    en: "Please don't give up." },
  "あげる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a giving verb, and the key is direction. It is for giving that moves outward, from you or someone on your side to someone else, or between two other people. The pattern is: giver 「は」 or 「が」, receiver 「に」, thing 「を」. So 「私は友だちに本をあげました|わたしはともだちにほんをあげました」, I gave my friend a book. The same verb also means to raise or lift, so a nice image is lifting a gift up and handing it out to someone. The big rule is that you never use it for something coming to you. If your friend gives you something, you must use 「くれる」. A very common mistake for English speakers is to say a friend 「あげました」 something to me. That sounds wrong in Japanese. A third verb, 「もらう」, is for receiving. Note one politeness point: offering to give something to a superior with this word can sound like you are doing them a favor, so be careful with teachers. For plants and pets, you will often hear the casual 「やる」 instead. A way to remember it: 「あげる」 goes up and away from me. To recap, giving outward, never toward me." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb becomes even more powerful when you attach it to another verb's 「て」 form. 「てあげる」 means to do something for someone, as a favor. 「妹に本を読んであげました|いもうとにほんをよんであげました」, I read a book to my little sister. 「友だちを手伝ってあげました|ともだちをてつだってあげました」, I helped my friend. The direction rule stays the same as with objects: the favor goes outward, away from you. Be careful, though. Saying you did something for someone with this form can sound like you are reminding them of your kindness. So people often avoid it when talking directly to the person they helped, and especially to someone of higher status. Offering help to a teacher with this form can sound pushy. With friends, it is perfectly natural and friendly. One favor, sent outward." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The giving system has more members than you might think. Toward the outside, you know this verb. For giving to someone of higher status, there is the humble 「さしあげる」, which you will meet with honorific speech: giving something to a teacher or a customer. For giving downward, to a younger sibling, a pet, or a plant, the casual 「やる」 is used, as in watering the flowers, which literally gives water to them. Toward you, there is 「くれる」, and its respectful form for when a superior gives you something, 「くださる」. You already know that one inside 「ください」, which is really a request form of it. And for receiving, there is 「もらう」. So 「ください」, the very first polite request you learned, is part of the same giving family. Three directions, each with polite and casual forms." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The same sound covers several verbs, written with different kanji. The basic meaning is to raise, 「上げる|あげる」, using the kanji for up. Lifting your hand, raising prices, turning up the volume. Then there is 「揚げる|あげる」, to deep fry, using a different kanji with the hand piece. That one hides in a very popular food, 「唐揚げ|からあげ」, Japanese fried chicken, and in 「揚げ物|あげもの」, fried food in general. A third spelling, 「挙げる|あげる」, means to give an example or to hold a ceremony, as in having a wedding. The giving verb is normally written in kana, which helps you tell it apart on the page. A memory scene to connect them: you lift up a plate of fried chicken and hand it to a friend. Raise it, fry it, give it, all with one sound." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Giving that moves outward, from you or your side to someone else, never toward you; for that use 「くれる」. The same verb means to raise, so picture lifting a gift up and away from you.",
      "Attach it to a 「て」 form to do a favor for someone, as in 「手伝ってあげました|てつだってあげました」. It can sound like reminding people of your kindness, so avoid it with superiors.",
      "The giving family has humble 「さしあげる」 upward, casual 「やる」 downward, and 「くれる」 and 「くださる」 toward you. Even 「ください」 belongs to this family.",
      "The same sound means raise, 「上げる|あげる」, deep fry, 「揚げる|あげる」, as in 「唐揚げ|からあげ」, and give an example, 「挙げる|あげる」. Picture lifting a plate of fried chicken to hand to a friend."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}にプレゼントを[あげました|あげました|あげる]。",
    en: "I gave a present to my friend." },
  "くれる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the other half of 「あげる」. It is giving that comes toward you, or toward someone in your group, like your family. Someone else is the giver, and you are the receiver. The pattern is: giver 「が」, receiver 「に」, thing 「を」, and often the receiver is just left out, because it is obviously you. 「友だちが本をくれました|ともだちがほんをくれました」, my friend gave me a book. You can include it too: 「私にくれました|わたしにくれました」. The most common mistake for English speakers is to use 「あげる」 for everything, because English uses give in both directions. In Japanese, it sounds odd and a bit cold to use 「あげる」 for something you received, as if you were an outsider. Using 「くれる」 shows that you see the gift as a kindness coming to you. Your in-group counts too, so if a friend gave something to your little sister, you can still use 「くれる」. It is a 「る」-verb: 「くれます」, 「くれて」. A way to remember it: the gift comes to my side. Picture an arrow pointing at you. To recap, giving toward me and my side, the opposite direction from 「あげる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Attach this verb to another verb's 「て」 form and it turns any action into a kindness someone did for you. 「友だちが手伝ってくれました|ともだちがてつだってくれました」, my friend helped me. 「母が料理を作ってくれました|ははがりょうりをつくってくれました」, my mother cooked for me. Without the ending, the sentence just reports facts. With it, you are quietly saying thank you. Japanese speakers add it constantly, and leaving it out can make you sound a little ungrateful. It also forms a polite request. 「手伝ってくれませんか|てつだってくれませんか」 means could you help me, literally won't you do me the favor of helping. For even more politeness, use 「くださいませんか」. A common thank you phrase is 「来てくれてありがとう|きてくれてありがとう」, thanks for coming. Remember: whenever an action lands on you as a favor, this ending is your gratitude marker." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb has a respectful older sibling: 「くださる」. You use it when the giver is someone above you, like a teacher or a boss. 「先生が本をくださいました|せんせいがほんをくださいました」, my teacher gave me a book. Notice the polite form is 「くださいます」, with an 「い」 where you might expect a 「り」, a special irregular spelling. And here is the surprise. The word 「ください」, which you have used since your first lessons when ordering coffee, is really the command form of 「くださる」. So every time you say 「コーヒーをください」, you are literally saying please give it to me, honorably. Meanwhile, the opposite direction has 「あげる」 and its humble form 「さしあげる」. Japanese gives you a full map of who gives to whom, and how polite to be about it." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the direction. Picture a crane, a big construction crane, swinging around a construction site. It picks up a giant gift box and slowly lowers it right onto your doorstep. Crane. Crane-ru. The gift always comes down to you. Now picture yourself trying to use the crane to send a gift away to your neighbor. It refuses. The crane only delivers to your house, and to your family's house next door. That is the rule: this verb points toward you and your inner circle, never away. When you want to send something out, the crane is no help, and you need 「あげる」 instead. Keep the picture of the crane lowering gifts onto your step, and you will never point it the wrong way. One crane, one direction: home." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The opposite direction from 「あげる」: someone gives to you or your in-group, so the receiver is often left out, as in 「友だちが本をくれました|ともだちがほんをくれました」. Using 「あげる」 here sounds cold, like an outsider.",
      "After a 「て」 form it turns an action into a kindness done for you, quietly saying thank you, as in 「来てくれてありがとう|きてくれてありがとう」. 「手伝ってくれませんか|てつだってくれませんか」 makes a polite request.",
      "Its respectful sibling 「くださる」 is for givers above you, with the special polite form 「くださいます」. The everyday 「ください」 is really its command form, please give it to me.",
      "Picture a construction crane lowering a gift box onto your doorstep, crane-ru, and refusing to send gifts to your neighbor. It points toward you and your inner circle, never away."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}が{本|ほん}を[くれました|くれました|くれる]。",
    en: "My friend gave me a book." },
  "できる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You already know this verb as can do, as in 「日本語ができる|にほんごができる」, I can speak Japanese. Here is its other big job: something comes into being. A new shop opens, a building goes up, or 「友だちができる|ともだちができる」, you make a friend. It is sometimes written with the kanji for come out and come, 「出来る|できる」, which fits this meaning nicely: something comes out into the world. The thing that appears is marked with 「が」, not 「を」, because nobody is doing it to an object. It just happens: 「駅の前にカフェができました|えきのまえにカフェができました」, a cafe opened in front of the station. You will also hear it in the kitchen. When food is ready, people say 「できた」, it is done, or 「ご飯ができましたよ|ごはんができましたよ」, dinner is ready. And with relationships: getting a boyfriend or girlfriend is also said with this verb. A common mistake for English speakers is to use 「を」 here, as if someone made the shop. Remember that the shop simply comes to exist. A way to remember it: picture something popping out into the world, ta-da. To recap, the same verb as can do, but with 「が」 it means something new appears." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a neat fact about this verb: it is the potential form of 「する」. Most verbs build their can do form by changing their ending, like 「食べる|たべる」 becoming 「食べられる|たべられる」, or 「書く|かく」 becoming 「書ける|かける」. But 「する」 is irregular, so instead of changing, it is replaced by this whole different verb. That is why 「勉強する|べんきょうする」 becomes 「勉強できる|べんきょうできる」, can study. There is also a longer pattern, plain verb plus 「ことができる」, which works with any verb: 「泳ぐことができる|およぐことができる」, can swim. It sounds a little more formal than the short potential form. In both uses, the skill or thing that you can do usually takes 「が」. And remember that this verb itself is a 「る」-verb, so 「できます」, 「できない」, 「できて」. One verb doing double duty for an irregular partner." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Listen in a Japanese office or kitchen and you will hear this verb in some lively forms. 「できたて」 means freshly made, straight out of the oven, as on a bakery sign for hot bread. 「できあがり」 means finished, the moment a dish or a project is complete, and TV cooking shows say it with a flourish at the end. When someone does excellent work, colleagues say they are 「できる人|できるひと」, literally a person who can, meaning a highly capable person. When a student finishes a test, a friend asks 「できた?」, how did it go, did you manage? And a nervous answer might be 「全然できなかった|ぜんぜんできなかった」, I could not do it at all. So the verb covers ability, readiness, and success. In everyday life, it is one of the most hardworking verbs in the language." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When this verb is written with kanji, it uses 「出|で」 from 「出る|でる」, to come out, and 「来|き」 from 「来る|くる」, to come. Notice the second one is read 「き」 here, not 「く」. This spelling is what scholars call a borrowed spelling: the kanji were chosen to fit the sound and feel, and modern style guides usually prefer kana. But the same pair appears in a common noun with a clear meaning: 「出来事|できごと」, an event or something that happened, literally a thing that came out. You might hear someone describe the events of their day with it. Another is 「出来上がる|できあがる」, to be completed. So even if you always write the verb in kana, it helps to recognize the kanji pair, because you will see it in these related words. Coming out, coming into being." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Besides can do, it means something comes into being, like 「友だちができる|ともだちができる」 or a new cafe opening. The thing that appears takes 「が」, not 「を」, because it simply comes to exist.",
      "It is the potential form of 「する」, so 「勉強する|べんきょうする」 becomes 「勉強できる|べんきょうできる」. For any verb, plain form plus 「ことができる」 also means can, a little more formally.",
      "It covers ability, readiness, and success: 「できたて」 is freshly made, 「できる人|できるひと」 is a capable person, and 「できた?」 asks how a test went.",
      "The kanji spelling 「出来る|できる」 is come out plus come, with 「来|き」 read 「き」. Modern style prefers kana, but the pair appears in 「出来事|できごと」, an event."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}のそばに{新しい|あたらしい}{店|みせ}が[できました|できました|できる]。",
    en: "A new store opened near the station." },
  "そうだんする|相談する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both Chinese-origin readings. 「相|そう」 means mutual, each other, together. 「談|だん」 means talk or conversation. Look at its left side: the speech piece, the same one you see in 「話す|はなす」, to speak, and in 「電話|でんわ」, telephone. Words with that piece are usually about words and talking. So the word is literally talking together, sitting down and working through a problem with someone. That is why it covers both to consult and to discuss. Add 「する」 and it becomes a verb. The person you consult takes 「に」, and the topic often comes with about: 「先生に相談する|せんせいにそうだんする」, to talk it over with my teacher. You can also use it as a noun, as in having a little consultation. In Japan, talking things over before acting is very important. Before a big decision at work, people quietly consult everyone involved first, so no one is surprised. A nice phrase to know is asking someone if you can consult them about something, a polite way to ask for advice. A way to remember it: two people facing each other, talking. To recap, mutual plus talk: talking a problem over together." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word leads to other useful words. The first one, 「相|そう」, also has a native reading, 「あい」, in 「相手|あいて」, the other party, your partner or opponent, the person facing you. In the title for a prime minister, 「首相|しゅしょう」, the same kanji is read 「しょう」. The second kanji, 「談|だん」, lives in 「冗談|じょうだん」, a joke. You will hear 「冗談でしょう|じょうだんでしょう」, you must be joking, quite often. It also appears in 「面談|めんだん」, a face to face meeting, such as a parent teacher conference. So the first kanji is about two sides facing each other, and the second is about talk. A partner, a joke, a meeting: all of them involve people and words. Your word sits right in the middle of that family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, the polite way to bring up a problem is often with a soft opener. People say 「ちょっと相談があるんですが|ちょっとそうだんがあるんですが」, I have something I would like to talk over with you. That 「が」 at the end, trailing off, makes it gentle. The listener will usually reply 「いいですよ。何ですか|いいですよ。なんですか」, sure, what is it. When someone gives you advice, there is a lovely phrase: 「相談に乗る|そうだんにのる」, literally to ride on the consultation, meaning to listen and help. You might say a friend always listens to your problems using that phrase. You will also see 「無料相談|むりょうそうだん」, free consultation, on signs at banks, real estate offices, and law offices. So the word covers both warm personal chats and formal professional advice. A friendly opener, a helpful ear." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese business culture has a famous habit that grows from this word. Before a meeting where a big decision will be made, the person proposing it quietly visits each colleague and talks it over one on one. This groundwork is called 「根回し|ねまわし」, which literally means going around the roots, a gardening term for carefully digging around a tree before moving it. By the time the official meeting happens, everyone already agrees, and nobody is embarrassed by a surprise. Not consulting people first can be seen as rude, even if your idea is good. That is why this verb is so common at work. Saying you did not consult your boss is a real problem. For you as a learner, the lesson is simple. In Japan, talking things over early is a sign of respect." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「相|そう」, mutual, plus 「談|だん」, talk, with the speech piece: literally talking a problem over together. The person you consult takes 「に」, as in 「先生に相談する|せんせいにそうだんする」.",
      "The first kanji gives 「相手|あいて」, the other party, and the second gives 「冗談|じょうだん」, a joke, and 「面談|めんだん」, a face to face meeting. All involve people and words.",
      "A gentle opener is 「ちょっと相談があるんですが|ちょっとそうだんがあるんですが」. Someone who listens and helps is said to 「相談に乗る|そうだんにのる」, literally ride on the consultation.",
      "Before big decisions, Japanese workers quietly consult each colleague, a groundwork called 「根回し|ねまわし」, digging around the roots. Talking things over early is a sign of respect."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}に[相談しました|そうだんしました|そうだんする]。",
    en: "I consulted my teacher." },
  "ちゅういする|注意する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both Chinese-origin readings. 「注|ちゅう」 means to pour. Look at its left side, the three drops of the water piece, which fits pouring. 「意|い」 means mind, intention, or meaning, the same kanji you know from 「意味|いみ」, meaning. So the word is literally pour your mind into something: pay close attention, be careful. Add 「する」 and it becomes a verb. The thing you watch out for takes 「に」: 「車に注意する|くるまにちゅういする」, watch out for cars. You will see this word all over Japanese signs, sometimes with a single word in front, warning you to mind your step or watch for falling objects. The second meaning comes from pointing that attention at someone else: you warn them, or give them a light scolding. If a teacher 「注意する|ちゅういする」 a student, it means they told them off or cautioned them. A close cousin is 「気をつける|きをつける」, be careful, which is softer and used more in daily speech, as in take care. A way to remember it: picture pouring water carefully into a cup, eyes fixed on it, so you do not spill. To recap, pour plus mind: watch out, or warn someone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji appear in words you will meet often. The first, 「注|ちゅう」, shows up in 「注文|ちゅうもん」, an order at a restaurant or shop. Think of pouring your request onto a document. It also appears in 「注射|ちゅうしゃ」, an injection at the doctor's, where medicine is literally poured into you. The second kanji, 「意|い」, is all over the place. 「意見|いけん」 is an opinion, the mind's view. 「用意|ようい」 means preparation, as in getting things ready. And 「意味|いみ」, meaning, you already know. Its shape is worth a look too: the sound kanji 「音|おと」 on top and the heart 「心|こころ」 underneath, a sound of the heart, which fits mind and intention. So this word pours that heart and mind into whatever you are doing. Order, injection, opinion, preparation, meaning: one family of pouring and minding." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will see this word printed everywhere in Japan. Train platforms warn 「足元注意|あしもとちゅうい」, watch your step. Construction sites say 「頭上注意|ずじょうちゅうい」, watch your head. Weather reports issue an advisory, something like a heads up, using this same word. On medicine packets, 「注意|ちゅうい」 is the label for precautions. In speech, the meaning of telling someone off is very common too. If a student talks in class, they might later say 「先生に注意されました|せんせいにちゅういされました」, I got told off by the teacher. That uses the passive, which you will meet later. It is a mild scolding, not a big punishment. To warn a friend kindly, people often prefer 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, which sounds caring rather than official. Signs say this word, friends say be careful." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick using the sound. Picture a tiny mouse in a white lab coat, holding a teapot over a very small cup. The mouse goes chu, chu, and pours with total concentration, eyes wide, not spilling a single drop. Chu, for the squeaking mouse, and pouring, for the first kanji. Then the mouse looks up and squeaks a sharp ee, a warning to a careless cat who is about to knock the cup over. That is the second meaning, warning someone. So the scene has both halves of the word: careful attention while pouring, and a quick warning to someone else. Chu plus i. Mouse focus, mouse warning. Whenever you see a sign with this word, picture the careful little mouse with the teapot, keeping everyone safe." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「注|ちゅう」, pour, with the water piece, plus 「意|い」, mind: pour your mind into something and be careful. Pointed at someone else, it means to warn or lightly scold them.",
      "The first kanji gives 「注文|ちゅうもん」, an order, and 「注射|ちゅうしゃ」, an injection, while 「意|い」 is in 「意見|いけん」 and 「用意|ようい」. Its shape is sound over heart, a sound of the heart.",
      "Signs say 「足元注意|あしもとちゅうい」, watch your step, and 「頭上注意|ずじょうちゅうい」, watch your head. Getting told off is 「先生に注意されました|せんせいにちゅういされました」, while friends prefer 「気をつけて|きをつけて」.",
      "Picture a tiny mouse going chu as it pours tea with total focus, then squeaking ee to warn a careless cat. Careful attention plus a warning, chu plus i."
    ],
    ja: "{車|くるま}に[注意して|ちゅういして|ちゅういする]ください。",
    en: "Please watch out for cars." },
  "プロポーズする|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is English propose, plus 「する」, the do verb that turns loanwords into verbs. Listen to the sounds. The pr cluster gets split with an extra vowel, 「プロ」. The po part becomes 「ポー」, with a long vowel for the English oh glide. And the se at the end, with its z sound, becomes 「ズ」, again with an extra vowel. The big thing to know is the meaning shift. In English, you can propose an idea at a meeting, a plan, or a toast. In Japanese, this word almost only means proposing marriage. For suggesting an idea, Japanese uses a different word. You can also use it as a noun, as in getting a proposal. The person you propose to takes 「に」: 「彼女にプロポーズした|かのじょにプロポーズした」, he proposed to his girlfriend. Japanese dramas love a big proposal scene, often with a ring, a 「指輪|ゆびわ」, in a fancy restaurant or with a city view at night. A way to remember it: in Japan, only one kind of proposal comes with a ring. To recap, English propose with extra vowels, used only for asking someone to marry you." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, a proposal is often followed by a series of traditional steps. The engaged couple will visit each other's parents, and the visit to the woman's family can be quite nervous, sometimes with a formal request for permission to marry. Some families still hold a ceremony called 「結納|ゆいのう」, an exchange of engagement gifts between the families. The engagement itself is 「婚約|こんやく」, and the engagement ring is 「婚約指輪|こんやくゆびわ」. A classic proposal line is very direct: 「結婚してください|けっこんしてください」, please marry me. Another famous line asks the partner to make miso soup for you every day, an old fashioned way of saying spend your life with me. Today, many couples also register their marriage at the city office on a meaningful date. A proposal opens the door to a whole series of family traditions." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This word is a good example of a common pattern in Japanese loanwords: the meaning shrinks. English propose has many uses, but Japanese borrowed just one, the romantic one. The same thing happens with other words. 「マンション」 comes from mansion, but in Japan it means an ordinary apartment in a concrete building. 「ハンドル」 comes from handle, but means a steering wheel. 「サイン」 comes from sign, and usually means a signature or an autograph. So when you meet a katakana word, do not assume it means everything the English word does. For suggesting an idea at work, Japanese uses 「提案|ていあん」, a proposal or suggestion. If you say 「プロポーズ」 to your boss about a new plan, expect some surprised faces. Same sound, much narrower meaning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English propose with extra vowels, but in Japanese it almost only means proposing marriage. The person takes 「に」: 「彼女にプロポーズした|かのじょにプロポーズした」, often with a ring, a 「指輪|ゆびわ」.",
      "A proposal leads to family traditions: visiting parents, sometimes a 「結納|ゆいのう」 gift exchange, and 「婚約|こんやく」, engagement. The classic line is simply 「結婚してください|けっこんしてください」.",
      "Loanword meanings often shrink, like 「マンション」 for an ordinary apartment. To suggest an idea at work, use 「提案|ていあん」, not 「プロポーズ」, or expect surprised faces."
    ],
    ja: "{山下|やました}さんは{恋人|こいびと}に[プロポーズしました|プロポーズしました|プロポーズする]。",
    en: "Mr. Yamashita proposed to his girlfriend." },
  "〜くん|〜君": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「君|くん」 is read here with its Chinese-origin reading. Its native reading is 「きみ」, which means you, a casual word a man might use to a friend, a younger person, or a partner. Long ago, the kanji meant a lord or ruler, but over time it became a friendly, casual title. Put it after a name, like 「たけし君|たけしくん」, and it is a casual version of 「さん」. It is used mostly for boys and young men, by friends, classmates, older people, and teachers talking to students. Girls usually get 「ちゃん」 instead when they are young. At some Japanese companies, a boss may use it for younger staff of any gender, and in the Japanese parliament it is used for all members, so it is not strictly a boy word. The big rules: never use it for your boss, a teacher, or anyone older than you, because it would sound like talking down. And never use it for yourself, just like 「さん」. A common mistake is using it with someone you have just met, which can feel too familiar. A way to remember it: think of a friendly older classmate calling out to a younger boy. To recap, a casual name ender for boys and juniors." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This ending belongs to a whole family of name endings, each sending a different signal. 「さん」 is the safe, neutral choice for almost anyone. 「さま」 is very respectful, used for customers and in letters, as in 「お客様|おきゃくさま」. 「ちゃん」 is cute and affectionate, for small children, close friends, and pets. 「先生|せんせい」 works as a title for teachers, doctors, and lawyers. And then there is having no ending at all, called 「呼び捨て|よびすて」, which only close friends or family use, because it is very familiar. This ending sits among them as the friendly, slightly boyish title, used by peers and from seniors to juniors. Choosing the right one is about your relationship with the person. In Japan, the end of a name is where you show how close you are." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji is usually explained as two parts. On top is a piece showing a hand holding a stick or staff, a sign of authority. Below is 「口|くち」, the mouth. So the picture is someone with authority giving orders, which matches the old meaning of ruler or lord. Over many centuries, the word softened. With its native reading, 「君|きみ」, it became a pronoun for you, used to equals or juniors, and you will hear it all the time in Japanese pop songs, where singers sing to their 「君|きみ」. In fact the Japanese national anthem begins with this same kanji, where it refers to the emperor, the ruler meaning. As a name ending, it uses the Chinese-origin reading. From an emperor to a classmate, it is quite a journey for one kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a Japanese elementary school classroom. The teacher takes attendance: 「田中くん|たなかくん」, 「山田さん|やまださん」, 「鈴木くん|すずきくん」. Boys get this ending and girls get 「さん」, though many schools now use 「さん」 for everyone. Among friends, a boy might be called by his first name plus this ending, like 「けんたくん」, while a closer friend just uses his name with nothing added. Girls often use it for boys in their class too. In anime, you will hear it constantly, and hearing a character switch from it to a bare name signals that two people have grown closer. As an adult in Japan, you will mostly hear it from older people talking about younger men. If you are unsure, use 「さん」. It is never wrong, and you can switch once someone tells you to." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Once meaning lord, the kanji became a friendly name ender, a casual 「さん」 mostly for boys and juniors. Never use it for your boss, a teacher, someone older, or yourself.",
      "It sits in the family of name endings: neutral 「さん」, respectful 「さま」, affectionate 「ちゃん」, and no ending at all, 「呼び捨て|よびすて」. The ending you choose shows how close you are.",
      "The kanji shows a hand holding a staff over 「口|くち」, a mouth giving orders, so it first meant ruler. Read 「きみ」, it became you, sung to in countless pop songs.",
      "Teachers call boys 「田中くん|たなかくん」 and girls 「山田さん|やまださん」, though many schools now use 「さん」 for all. If unsure, use 「さん」, since it is never wrong."
    ],
    ja: "[たけし君|たけしくん|〜くん]はわたしの{友だち|ともだち}です。",
    en: "Takeshi is my friend." },
  "〜たち|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Stick this on words for people to make them plural: 「子どもたち|こどもたち」, children, 「学生たち|がくせいたち」, students. It is sometimes written with the kanji 「達|たち」, but kana is common. Here is a fun connection: the word you already know for friend, 「友だち|ともだち」, is actually friend plus this very ending, with the sound softened to 「だち」. Long ago it meant friends, but today it is just one friend, and the ending lost its plural feel. A few points to know. First, it is mostly for people and sometimes animals, not things. You do not say books plus this ending. Japanese nouns usually do not need a plural at all, so 「本|ほん」 can be one book or many. Second, after a name, it means that person and their group: 「田中さんたち|たなかさんたち」, Tanaka and the others. So it is more like and company than a strict plural. Third, you can add it to pronouns: 「私たち|わたしたち」, we, and 「あなたたち」, you all. A way to remember it: picture a little crowd of people gathered behind the word. To recap, a plural ender for people, also hiding inside 「友だち|ともだち」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When this ending is written in kanji, it is 「達|たち」. On the left and bottom you can see the road and movement piece, the same one in 「送る|おくる」 and 「近い|ちかい」. The kanji's own meaning is to reach or to attain, to arrive at a goal down the road. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「たつ」, it shows up in words like 「上達|じょうたつ」, improvement in a skill, and 「配達|はいたつ」, delivery, as in a pizza delivery reaching your door. Its use as a plural ender is a separate job, where the kanji was simply borrowed for the sound. That is why you will often see the ending in kana, while 「友達|ともだち」 is very commonly written with the kanji. So when you meet this kanji in a word for skill or delivery, do not look for a crowd of people. Same kanji, two very different jobs." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several ways to talk about a group of people, each with its own feel. This ending is the everyday, neutral one. 「ら」 is shorter and a bit rougher or more matter of fact: 「彼ら|かれら」, they, or 「僕ら|ぼくら」, we, is common in songs and among young men. 「がた」 is the polite one: 「あなたがた」, you all, or 「先生がた|せんせいがた」, the teachers, showing respect. 「ども」 is humble, and is used about your own side: a shop employee might say 「私ども|わたくしども」, we at this store. And for things, Japanese sometimes doubles a word instead: 「人々|ひとびと」, people, or 「山々|やまやま」, mountains, with the little repeat mark. So the plural you choose quietly signals respect, humility, or casualness. Pick the ending that fits the people you are talking about." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It pluralizes words for people, like 「子どもたち|こどもたち」, and after a name means that person and company. It also hides inside 「友だち|ともだち」, which today means just one friend.",
      "The kanji 「達|たち」 has the road piece and means to reach, as in 「上達|じょうたつ」, improvement, and 「配達|はいたつ」, delivery. As a plural ender it was simply borrowed for its sound.",
      "Other plural endings shift the tone: rougher 「ら」 as in 「彼ら|かれら」, polite 「がた」, and humble 「ども」. Things sometimes double instead, as in 「人々|ひとびと」."
    ],
    ja: "[子どもたち|こどもたち|〜たち]は{公園|こうえん}であそんでいます。",
    en: "The children are playing in the park." },
  "わたしたち|私たち": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Just 「私|わたし」 plus the plural ender 「たち」, so it is literally I and my group. 「私|わたし」 uses its native reading here. You also know its more formal reading, 「わたくし」, and the kanji has a Chinese-origin reading, 「し」, used in words for private things, like private schools. So the kanji is about the self and personal matters. You can make other we words the same way. Boys and men often say 「僕たち|ぼくたち」, we, in casual speech. And for you all, people say 「あなたたち」. The plural can mean two people or a whole nation, depending on context, a bit like English. One cultural point: Japanese often leaves the subject out completely when it is obvious. So in conversation, you will hear this word less than English speakers use we. When you do hear it, it often stresses the group, as in we, as opposed to them. In formal speeches, you may hear 「私たち|わたしたち」 or the old formal word for we. A way to remember it: one 「私|わたし」, then a little crowd of 「たち」 standing behind it. To recap, I plus the people plural: we." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "English speakers say we and our all the time, but in Japanese, other words often step in. When talking about your family or your company, people say 「うち」: 「うちの母|うちのはは」, our mother, or 「うちの会社|うちのかいしゃ」, our company. That word carries a warm, in-group feeling. In speeches, news, and formal writing, you will hear 「我々|われわれ」, a stiff, official we, often used by politicians and company presidents. Then there is the possessive. Japanese has no separate word for our. You just add 「の」: 「私たちの学校|わたしたちのがっこう」, our school. In casual speech, young women sometimes say 「あたしたち」, a softer version. So this word is the standard, polite, all-purpose we, but listen for its cousins, because the choice tells you a lot about the speaker and the setting." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word behaves like any noun, so it takes all the usual particles. 「私たちは|わたしたちは」 marks the topic, as in we are students. 「私たちが|わたしたちが」 can stress who did something: it was we who did it, not them. 「私たちに|わたしたちに」 marks a receiver, and 「私たちと|わたしたちと」 means with us: 「私たちと一緒に行きませんか|わたしたちといっしょにいきませんか」, would you like to go with us? That is a great invitation pattern to practice. Because the subject is often dropped, though, the word stands out when it does appear. One useful tip. With giving verbs, you and your group count as the inner side, so when someone gives something to your group, use 「くれる」: 「先生が私たちに本をくれました|せんせいがわたしたちにほんをくれました」. The group gets treated exactly like you. A plural noun with all the particles, and your inner circle in grammar." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Just 「私|わたし」 plus the plural ender 「たち」: I and my group. Since Japanese often drops the subject, you hear it less than English we, and it often stresses us as opposed to them.",
      "Other words often step in: 「うち」 for your family or company, stiff 「我々|われわれ」 in speeches. For our, just add 「の」, as in 「私たちの学校|わたしたちのがっこう」.",
      "It takes all the usual particles, and 「私たちと一緒に行きませんか|わたしたちといっしょにいきませんか」 is a handy invitation. With giving verbs your group counts as the inner side, so use 「くれる」."
    ],
    ja: "[私たち|わたしたち]は{学生|がくせい}です。",
    en: "We are students." },
  "こんな〜|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is part of the 「こ」「そ」「あ」「ど」 family you already know from 「これ」, 「それ」, 「あれ」, 「どれ」, and 「ここ」, 「そこ」, 「あそこ」, 「どこ」. The 「こ」 words point to things close to you. So 「こんな」 is this kind of, something near you or just mentioned by you. 「そんな」 is that kind, near the listener, 「あんな」 is that kind over there, and 「どんな」, which you learned already, asks what kind. They always need a noun right after them: 「こんな本|こんなほん」, a book like this. You cannot use them alone like 「これ」. You will also hear 「こんなに」, so much, like this, as in I did not know it was this hard. Watch the tone. Depending on context, it can sound excited, like I wanted something like this, or annoyed, like what is this kind of thing. A common mistake is mixing it up with 「この」. 「この本|このほん」 is this specific book. 「こんな本|こんなほん」 is a book of this kind. A way to remember it: 「この」 points at one thing, 「こんな」 points at the type. To recap, the 「こ」 word for this kind of, always followed by a noun." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word has a whole matching set of siblings that describe manner, how something is done. 「こう」 means in this way, 「そう」 in that way, 「ああ」 in that way over there, and 「どう」, which you know, means how. So 「こうします」 means I will do it like this, as you demonstrate with your hands. Then add 「に」 to the kind words and you get degree words. 「こんなに」 is this much, 「そんなに」 is that much, and 「あんなに」 is that much over there. 「そんなに」 is especially common in negative sentences, meaning not that much: 「そんなに高くないです|そんなにたかくないです」, it is not that expensive. And everyone uses 「そんなことない」, which literally means there is no such thing. It is the classic humble reply to a compliment, like oh, not at all. One pattern, many useful words." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word carries a lot of feeling in daily speech. Late at night, someone might say 「こんな時間に何してるの|こんなじかんになにしてるの」, what are you doing at this hour? That sounds surprised or a little annoyed. Looking at a beautiful view, a friend might sigh 「こんなきれいな所、初めて|こんなきれいなところ、はじめて」, my first time somewhere this beautiful. At a hair salon, you can show a photo and say 「こんな感じで|こんなかんじで」, like this, please. That phrase, with the word for feeling, is extremely useful for shopping, haircuts, and ordering. A softer, more neutral version you will also hear is 「こういう」, as in this type of. It is less emotional and more descriptive. Show someone a picture, point, and say 「こんな感じ|こんなかんじ」, and you will get exactly what you want." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the 「こ」 member of the 「こ」「そ」「あ」「ど」 family and means this kind of, always followed by a noun. 「この本|このほん」 points at one book, 「こんな本|こんなほん」 at the type.",
      "Its manner siblings are 「こう」, 「そう」, 「ああ」, 「どう」, and adding 「に」 gives degree words like 「そんなに」, not that much. 「そんなことない」 is the classic humble reply to a compliment.",
      "It carries feeling, surprised in 「こんな時間に|こんなじかんに」 or amazed at a view. Show a photo and say 「こんな感じで|こんなかんじで」 at a salon to get exactly what you want."
    ],
    ja: "[こんな]{本|ほん}が{欲しい|ほしい}です。",
    en: "I want a book like this." },
  "きゅうに|急に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「急|きゅう」 uses its Chinese-origin reading here, and it means hurry, sudden, or steep. You already know it with its native reading in 「急ぐ|いそぐ」, to hurry. Look at the bottom of the kanji: it has the heart piece, the same one at the bottom of 「思う|おもう」, to think. A way to remember it: a racing heart, the feeling when something rushes at you. Add 「に」 and it turns into an adverb describing how something happens: suddenly, all of a sudden. 「急に雨が降りました|きゅうにあめがふりました」, it suddenly rained. 「急に|きゅうに」 is great for any surprise: someone suddenly stops, a plan suddenly changes, a person suddenly calls. The same kanji shows up in express trains, the ones in a hurry that skip stations, which you will see at train stations all over Japan. Another useful phrase is something being urgent, which uses the same kanji. A common mistake is using 「急に|きゅうに」 for things that are just fast. It is about something being sudden and unexpected, not quick. For fast, use 「早く|はやく」. To recap, hurry or sudden, plus 「に」: all of a sudden." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji is a regular at Japanese train stations. 「急行|きゅうこう」 is an express train, hurry plus go. 「特急|とっきゅう」 is a limited express, with the kanji for special in front, so it is even faster, and usually needs an extra ticket. If you take the wrong one, you may fly past your stop. Then there is 「急用|きゅうよう」, urgent business, a common reason for cancelling plans. And 「救急車|きゅうきゅうしゃ」, an ambulance, which uses a different first kanji, rescue, followed by this one, hurry, and then car. Finally, the native verb 「急ぐ|いそぐ」 gives you the polite warning 「急いでください|いそいでください」, please hurry. So the kanji runs through trains, ambulances, and emergencies. Wherever something has to move fast or happens without warning, look for this heart-pounding kanji." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The base word is actually a 「な」-adjective, and the 「に」 you see here is the regular way 「な」-adjectives become adverbs, just like 「きれいに」 or 「しずかに」. As an adjective before a noun, it means sudden or urgent: 「急な用事|きゅうなようじ」, a sudden errand, the classic excuse for missing a party. It also means steep: 「急な坂|きゅうなさか」, a steep slope, and 「急な階段|きゅうなかいだん」, steep stairs, common in old Japanese houses. So the meanings are sudden, urgent, and steep, all with the idea of a sharp change. As an adverb, it goes right before the verb or at the start of the sentence: 「急に寒くなりました|きゅうにさむくなりました」, it suddenly got cold. That pattern with 「なる」 is very common for weather and feelings. Adjective for sudden or steep, adverb for all of a sudden." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture yourself standing in a quiet line at a Japanese bakery. Out of nowhere, a huge cue ball, the white ball from a pool table, comes rolling at full speed through the door, knocking over the bread rack. Everyone gasps. A cue ball, suddenly! Cue, for the sound at the start of the word. The whole point of the scene is surprise: nobody expected a pool ball in a bakery. Then the ball stops, and it has a little heart drawn on it, matching the heart piece at the bottom of the kanji, the heart racing from the shock. So whenever something happens without warning, imagine that cue ball crashing through the door. A cue, a crash, a racing heart." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji 「急|きゅう」 means hurry or sudden, with a heart piece at the bottom, a racing heart. With 「に」 it means all of a sudden, for surprises, not for things that are merely fast, which take 「早く|はやく」.",
      "The kanji runs through stations and emergencies: 「急行|きゅうこう」, an express train, 「特急|とっきゅう」, a limited express, and 「救急車|きゅうきゅうしゃ」, an ambulance.",
      "The base is a 「な」-adjective meaning sudden, urgent, or steep, as in 「急な用事|きゅうなようじ」 and 「急な坂|きゅうなさか」. As an adverb, 「急に寒くなりました|きゅうにさむくなりました」 is a very common pattern.",
      "Picture a cue ball suddenly crashing into a quiet bakery, with a little heart drawn on it like the heart piece in the kanji. Cue, crash, racing heart."
    ],
    ja: "[急に|きゅうに]{雨|あめ}がふりました。",
    en: "It suddenly rained." },
  "ちょうど|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This means right on the mark, nothing more, nothing less. It is usually written in kana, though you may see the kanji 「丁度|ちょうど」. Use it for times and amounts: 「ちょうど三時です|ちょうどさんじです」, it is exactly three o'clock, or 「ちょうど千円|ちょうどせんえん」, exactly a thousand yen. When you pay with the exact amount at a shop, the clerk often says the amount is exactly right, using this word. It also works for timing. 「ちょうどいい」 means just right, not too much, not too little, like a room at the perfect temperature or shoes that fit. And you can say something happened just as something else was happening: I was just about to call you. A common mistake for English speakers is using it for strong agreement, the way English says exactly. In Japanese, for that you would say 「そうですね」 instead. 「ちょうど」 is about a precise fit. A way to remember it: it sounds a bit like a little drum hit, cho-do, landing right on the beat. To recap, right on the dot, for times, amounts, and things that fit just right." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At any Japanese convenience store, pay with the exact change and the clerk will often say 「ちょうどいただきます」, I have received the exact amount. You will hear it so often it becomes background music. Another very common phrase is 「ちょうどよかった」, what perfect timing, said when someone arrives just when you needed them. 「ちょうど今|ちょうどいま」 means right now, at this very moment, as in I was just thinking about you. When a size is perfect, people say 「ちょうどいいサイズ」. The feeling every time is a satisfying click, two things fitting together at exactly the right moment. Listen for it at the register, and you will start hearing it everywhere." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words in this neighborhood, and the differences are useful. 「ぴったり」 is a sound word meaning a perfect, snug fit. Shoes that fit perfectly are 「ぴったり」, and it is a little more vivid than our word. The opposite direction, being approximate, uses 「ぐらい」 or 「ごろ」 after a number. 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」 is around three o'clock, while 「ちょうど三時|ちょうどさんじ」 is three on the dot. 「ぐらい」 is used for amounts and lengths of time, as in about an hour. And 「だいたい」 means roughly or mostly. So you can build a little scale: 「だいたい」 is roughly, 「ぐらい」 is about, our word is exactly, and 「ぴったり」 is a perfect fit. Knowing all four lets you be just as precise, or as vague, as you want." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word teams up nicely with the 「ところ」 pattern you will meet for describing a point in time. With the dictionary form of a verb, 「ちょうど出かけるところです|ちょうどでかけるところです」 means I am just about to go out. With the 「ている」 form, 「ちょうど食べているところです|ちょうどたべているところです」, I am right in the middle of eating. With the past, 「ちょうど帰ったところです|ちょうどかえったところです」, I have just this moment come home. In each case, this word sharpens the timing, like a finger pointing at the exact moment on a clock. You can also use it at the start of a sentence about time, as in it was just one year ago. Adding it is optional, but it makes your Japanese sound natural and precise." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means right on the mark, for times, amounts, and fits, as in 「ちょうど三時です|ちょうどさんじです」 or 「ちょうどいい」, just right. Do not use it to agree strongly; say 「そうですね」 instead.",
      "Pay with exact change and clerks say 「ちょうどいただきます」. 「ちょうどよかった」 means perfect timing, and 「ちょうど今|ちょうどいま」 means right at this moment.",
      "On a scale of precision, 「だいたい」 is roughly, 「ぐらい」 is about, this word is exactly, and 「ぴったり」 is a perfect snug fit. 「三時ごろ|さんじごろ」 is around three, not on the dot.",
      "With 「ところ」 it sharpens timing: 「ちょうど出かけるところです|ちょうどでかけるところです」, just about to go, and 「ちょうど帰ったところです|ちょうどかえったところです」, just got home."
    ],
    ja: "{今|いま}[ちょうど]{三時|さんじ}です。",
    en: "It's exactly three o'clock now." },
  "さあ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This little sound has two main jobs, and the tone tells you which one. The first, from this lesson, is a thinking sound when you do not know. Said slowly, low, often with a little head tilt, 「さあ」 means hmm, I am not sure. You can follow it with 「わかりません」, I do not know: 「さあ、わかりません」. The second job is completely different. Said brightly, with energy, it means come on, or well then, let's go, as in 「さあ、行こう|さあ、いこう」, come on, let's go. You will hear that one when someone is starting something, like a meal or a trip. For the not sure meaning, be careful with politeness. On its own, 「さあ」 can sound a bit dismissive, like I don't know and I don't care. To a teacher or boss, it is safer to add something softer, like 「ちょっとわかりません」. It is a casual, natural word, so it is great in conversation with friends. A way to remember it: picture a long, slow breath out, like a shrug turned into sound. To recap, a shrug word for I'm not sure, or, said brightly, come on." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a set of small filler words, and each one has a different job. 「ええと」 or 「えーと」 is what you say while searching your memory, like um. 「あのう」 is a polite way to get attention or to begin a hesitant request, like excuse me, um. 「まあ」 means well, more or less, and softens a statement, as in 「まあまあです」, it is so-so. 「へえ」 shows surprise or interest, like oh really. And 「ああ」 is ah, I see. This word fits into that group as the shrug or the call to action. A Japanese conversation is full of these little sounds, and using them makes you sound much more natural. Try mixing them in your next practice conversation, because they are the glue of spoken Japanese." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are two short scenes. In the first, a coworker asks if the boss is coming to the party. The other replies slowly, 「さあ、どうでしょう」, hmm, who knows. That set phrase, adding the words for how would it be, is a very common polite way of saying I have no idea. In the second scene, a mother claps her hands on a busy morning and says brightly, 「さあ、行くよ|さあ、いくよ」, right, let's go! Teachers start lessons with 「さあ、始めましょう|さあ、はじめましょう」, alright, let us begin. Game show hosts use it to build excitement before an answer is revealed. So pay attention to the voice. Slow, falling, and thoughtful means not sure. Quick, high, and bright means here we go. A single sound with two moods." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Said slowly with a head tilt, it is a shrug meaning hmm, I am not sure; said brightly, it means come on, as in 「さあ、行こう|さあ、いこう」. To a boss, soften it with 「ちょっとわかりません」.",
      "It belongs to the little filler words that glue spoken Japanese together, alongside 「ええと」, 「あのう」, 「まあ」, 「へえ」 and 「ああ」. This one is the shrug or the call to action.",
      "「さあ、どうでしょう」 is a polite way to say who knows, while 「さあ、始めましょう|さあ、はじめましょう」 kicks things off. Slow and falling means not sure; quick and bright means here we go."
    ],
    ja: "[さあ]、わかりません。",
    en: "Hmm, I don't know." },
  "どうしたらいい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a little phrase built from pieces you know. 「どう」 is how. 「したら」 is the if or when form of 「する」, to do. And 「いい」 is good. So it is literally how, if I do it, would it be good? In plain English: what should I do? You will learn the 「たら」 form properly a bit later, but you can use this whole phrase now as a set expression. Add 「ですか」 to make it polite: 「どうしたらいいですか」, what should I do. In casual speech, people just say 「どうしたらいい」 with a rising tone, or sigh it to themselves when they are stuck. It is great for asking for advice, which links nicely to 「相談する|そうだんする」, to consult. You can also put an action in front, like what should I do about this problem, or how should I say it. You will also hear the close cousin 「どうすればいい」, which means almost the same thing. A common mistake is translating should word for word, which Japanese does not need here. The 「いい」 does that job. A way to remember it: how plus do plus good, three little words for being stuck. To recap, how, if I do, is it good: what should I do?" },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This phrase has a small family of relatives, and the differences matter. 「どうすればいい」 means almost the same, what should I do, and is a little more about the method or the logical step. 「どうしよう」, with the volitional form, is what people say when they panic: oh no, what am I going to do? You might say it after missing the last train. It is more of a cry than a question. Then there is a pattern for giving advice: verb in the 「たら」 form plus 「どうですか」. 「薬を飲んだらどうですか|くすりをのんだらどうですか」, why don't you take some medicine? So the same 「たら」 piece lets you ask for advice and also give it. For a very polite version with a boss or a customer, people say 「どうしたらよろしいでしょうか」. Asking, panicking, suggesting: one little family of phrases." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a short scene. You are at a ticket machine in a Japanese station, and you cannot figure out how to buy a ticket. You turn to a station worker and say 「すみません、切符はどうしたらいいですか|すみません、きっぷはどうしたらいいですか」, excuse me, what should I do about a ticket? Notice how the topic, the ticket, comes first with 「は」. The staff member will kindly show you which buttons to press. You can use this pattern with almost any topic: the trash, the bath, the payment. In Japan, asking this way is perfectly natural and very polite, because you are not demanding anything, just asking for guidance. With friends, drop the 「ですか」 and let your voice rise. Start with the topic, add the phrase, and you can get help with almost anything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「どう」, how, plus 「したら」, if I do, plus 「いい」, good: what should I do? Add 「ですか」 to be polite; the 「いい」 does the job of should.",
      "「どうすればいい」 is nearly the same, while 「どうしよう」 is a panicked oh no. The same 「たら」 form gives advice too, as in 「薬を飲んだらどうですか|くすりをのんだらどうですか」.",
      "Put the topic first with 「は」, as in 「切符はどうしたらいいですか|きっぷはどうしたらいいですか」 at a ticket machine. It politely asks for guidance on almost anything."
    ],
    ja: "{財布|さいふ}をなくしました。[どうしたらいい]ですか。",
    en: "I lost my wallet. What should I do?" },
  "〜こ|〜個": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「個|こ」 uses its Chinese-origin reading, and it means individual, one single item. Look at the left side: it has the person piece, and you will see it in the word for individual person. As a counter, it is for smallish, often roundish or chunky things: apples, eggs, candies, 「みかん」, erasers, even ice cubes. It is also a handy fallback when you do not know the proper counter. Watch the sound changes, because this counter starts with a k sound and gets the small 「っ」 with certain numbers. One is 「一個|いっこ」, six is 「六個|ろっこ」, eight is often 「八個|はっこ」, and ten is 「十個|じゅっこ」. The others are regular: 「二個|にこ」, 「三個|さんこ」. To ask how many, say 「何個|なんこ」. You also know a native way to count things, 「ひとつ」, 「ふたつ」, and so on, and for small items both systems work. The native one stops at ten, though, and this one keeps going. A way to remember it: picture a pile of single, individual candies, each one its own little 「こ」. To recap, individual item, a counter for small things, with small 「っ」 for one, six, eight, and ten." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for this counter shows up in several words about being an individual. 「個人|こじん」 means an individual person, as opposed to a group or company, and you will see it in phrases like personal information. 「個室|こしつ」 is a private room, very popular in Japanese restaurants and karaoke, where your group gets its own room. 「個性|こせい」 means personality or individuality, the qualities that make you unique, and Japanese schools and companies often talk about respecting it. So the core idea is each one by itself. That is exactly what the counter does: it treats each apple or candy as a separate single item. When you count with it, you are lining up individuals. One kanji, from private rooms to personality." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This counter is the friendly workhorse of everyday Japanese. At a bakery, you might say 「これを二個ください|これをにこください」, two of these, please. At the supermarket, eggs come in packs of ten, 「十個入り|じゅっこいり」. Friends trade candies with 「一個あげる|いっこあげる」, here, have one. And there is a common casual use you might not expect: people use it for school years. 「一個上の先輩|いっこうえのせんぱい」 means a senior who is one year above me. Young people say this all the time. Compared with the native counting words like 「ひとつ」 and 「ふたつ」, this counter feels a bit more casual and concrete, and it works better for larger numbers. So when you are not sure which counter to use for a small object, reach for this one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means individual, one single item, and counts small chunky things like apples and eggs, and works as a fallback. One, six, eight and ten take a small 「っ」, as in 「一個|いっこ」.",
      "The kanji appears in 「個人|こじん」, an individual, 「個室|こしつ」, a private room, and 「個性|こせい」, personality. The core idea is each one by itself.",
      "It is the everyday workhorse, as in 「これを二個ください|これをにこください」 at a bakery. Young people even say 「一個上の先輩|いっこうえのせんぱい」 for someone one school year above."
    ],
    ja: "みかんを[三個|さんこ|〜こ]{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought three mandarin oranges." },
  "〜さつ|〜冊": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「冊|さつ」 is a picture you can still see. It shows strips of bamboo or wood, standing side by side, with a string tied through them. That is how books were bound in ancient China, before paper was common. So it makes perfect sense as the counter for bound volumes: books, notebooks, magazines, dictionaries like 「辞書|じしょ」, and textbooks like 「教科書|きょうかしょ」. It uses its Chinese-origin reading. Watch the sound changes. One is 「一冊|いっさつ」, eight is 「八冊|はっさつ」, and ten is 「十冊|じゅっさつ」, each with a small 「っ」. The rest are regular, like 「二冊|にさつ」 and 「三冊|さんさつ」. To ask how many, say 「何冊|なんさつ」. A common mistake is to use the general counter for things for books. That sounds off to Japanese ears. Note that flat sheets, like single pages or tickets, use a different counter, 「〜まい」, for thin flat things. Only bound items with pages get 「さつ」. A way to remember it: look at the kanji and see the bamboo strips tied together, an old book. To recap, a picture of bound strips, used to count books and notebooks." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji appears in a few other words about bound paper. 「冊子|さっし」 is a booklet or pamphlet, like the little guide you pick up at a museum. Notice the small 「っ」 there too, the same sound change you see in 「一冊|いっさつ」. 「別冊|べっさつ」 is a separate volume, such as a special supplement that comes with a magazine. Then think about the neighbors in the counting world. 「〜まい」 counts flat thin things: paper, tickets, shirts, plates. 「〜ページ」 counts pages inside a book. And this counter counts the whole bound book. So if you have a notebook, it is one 「冊|さつ」, made of many 「まい」 of paper, with many 「ページ」 to write on. Three counters, one notebook, each looking at it from a different angle." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a librarian named Sue, sitting in a huge armchair in a cozy library. She is surrounded by tall towers of books. Every time someone returns a book, she stacks it on a pile and announces how many she has sat on, because the armchair is actually built from books. Sat, Sue, sat. Say it fast and it sounds like the counter. Now look closely at the books in the stack. Each one is bound with string, like the old bamboo strips in the kanji. Sue counts, one book, two books, three books, all sat upon. Whenever you count notebooks or magazines, picture Sue on her armchair of books. One cozy library, one counter for bound things." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji pictures bamboo strips tied with string, an ancient bound book, so it counts books, notebooks and magazines. One, eight and ten take a small 「っ」, as in 「一冊|いっさつ」, while flat sheets use 「〜まい」.",
      "It appears in 「冊子|さっし」, a booklet, and 「別冊|べっさつ」, a separate volume. A notebook is one 「冊|さつ」, made of many 「まい」 of paper, with many 「ページ」.",
      "Picture librarian Sue on an armchair built from string-bound books, announcing how many she has sat on. Sat, Sue, the counter for bound things."
    ],
    ja: "{本|ほん}を[二冊|にさつ|〜さつ]{読みました|よみました}。",
    en: "I read two books." },
  "〜だい|〜台": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「台|だい」 uses its Chinese-origin reading, and it means a stand, platform, or base, something that holds things up. As a counter, it is for machines and vehicles: cars, bicycles, computers, TVs, washing machines, pianos. The idea is that each machine sits on its own stand or base, like a unit. The good news is that the numbers are regular, with no sound changes: 「一台|いちだい」, 「二台|にだい」, 「三台|さんだい」, and so on. To ask how many, say 「何台|なんだい」. You know the same kanji with a different reading in 「台風|たいふう」, typhoon. Different reading, different job, so do not go looking for a stand inside the typhoon. A useful example: 「車が二台あります|くるまがにだいあります」, there are two cars. Note that people riding in the car are counted with 「〜人|にん」, not this counter. A common mistake is to count cars or bikes with the general counter for small things. For machines, 「台|だい」 always sounds right. A way to remember it: picture each machine on its own little stand, like a display in an electronics store. To recap, a stand or base, the counter for machines and vehicles." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji for this counter appears in some very common words with different readings. The one you probably know is 「台所|だいどころ」, the kitchen, literally the stand place, from an old word for the room where the food tables were kept. 「舞台|ぶたい」 is a stage in a theater, where the reading is 「たい」, a dance platform. 「屋台|やたい」 is a food stall, those little carts selling ramen or grilled squid at festivals. You already met 「台風|たいふう」, typhoon. And 「台湾|たいわん」 is Taiwan. In the words about stands, stages, and stalls, the core meaning of a raised platform is easy to see. So you have the kitchen, the stage, and the street stall, all built on the same idea of a platform that holds things up. Kitchen, stage, stall, and the machines you count." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch out for a couple of sound-alikes. This counter sounds exactly like 「〜代|〜だい」, used for decades of age or time periods. 「二十代|にじゅうだい」 means in your twenties, while 「二十台|にじゅうだい」 means twenty machines. Same sound, very different meaning, and only context tells you which. If someone says 「私は三十だいです|わたしはさんじゅうだいです」, they mean they are in their thirties, not that they own thirty cars. Another sound-alike is 「題|だい」, a title or topic, found in 「宿題|しゅくだい」, homework. Then there is 「大|だい」, big, as in 「大学|だいがく」, university. Japanese has many Chinese-origin readings that sound like this, so always listen for the noun in front. Cars and computers? It is the counter. A person's age? It is the decade. Context first, then meaning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means a stand or base, so it counts machines and vehicles, each sitting on its own stand. The numbers are regular, as in 「車が二台あります|くるまがにだいあります」.",
      "The kanji is in 「台所|だいどころ」, kitchen, 「舞台|ぶたい」, stage, and 「屋台|やたい」, food stall. All share the idea of a raised platform holding things up.",
      "It sounds exactly like 「〜代|〜だい」 for decades of age, so 「二十代|にじゅうだい」 is in your twenties while 「二十台|にじゅうだい」 is twenty machines. Listen for the noun in front."
    ],
    ja: "うちに{車|くるま}が[二台|にだい|〜だい]あります。",
    en: "We have two cars at home." },
  "〜ひき|〜匹": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「匹|ひき」 uses its native reading as a counter, and it is for small animals: cats, dogs, fish, insects, mice. The kanji itself is simple, and its origin is not something to rely on, so use a hook. A way to remember it: picture a little box with something wriggling inside. The sound changes are the big thing to learn, because this counter starts with an h sound. One is 「一匹|いっぴき」, with a p sound. Three is 「三匹|さんびき」, with a b sound. Six is 「六匹|ろっぴき」, eight is 「八匹|はっぴき」, and ten is 「十匹|じゅっぴき」. To ask how many, say 「何匹|なんびき」, again with a b sound. It is the same pattern as the long-object counter, 「本|ほん」: 「いっぽん」, 「さんぼん」, 「ろっぽん」. If you learn one, you get the other free. Big animals like horses, cows, and elephants use a different counter, 「頭|とう」, literally heads. So a 「猫|ねこ」, cat, is counted with 「ひき」, but an elephant is not. Medium dogs can go either way. To recap, the counter for small animals, with the same p and b sound changes as 「ほん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese counts animals in several ways. This counter is for small and medium animals: cats, dogs, fish, insects. For large animals like cows, horses, and elephants, the counter is 「頭|とう」, the head counter. For birds, it is 「羽|わ」, using the kanji for feather or wing. A famous oddity is that rabbits have traditionally been counted with that bird counter too, a habit with several competing stories behind it, so treat any one explanation with caution. Today many people count rabbits with this counter instead. People, of course, use 「人|にん」. And fish on a plate at a sushi counter can switch to other counters entirely. So when you count living things in Japanese, you are also sorting them by size and type. A cat is a 「匹|ひき」, a cow is a 「頭|とう」, and a bird is a 「羽|わ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Pet talk is where this counter really lives. A typical question when making friends is 「ペットを飼っていますか|ペットをかっていますか」, do you have a pet? And the answer might be 「猫を二匹飼っています|ねこをにひきかっています」, I have two cats. Notice the verb, 「飼う|かう」, to keep a pet, which sounds just like 「買う|かう」, to buy, but uses a different kanji. At the aquarium, children count fish with this counter. There is also a great idiom: 「一匹狼|いっぴきおおかみ」, a lone wolf, someone who likes to work alone. You may hear that phrase in sports, business, or about a solitary character in a drama. So the counter shows up in pet chats, at the aquarium, and even in personality descriptions. Two cats, one lone wolf." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It counts small animals, with sound changes: 「一匹|いっぴき」, 「三匹|さんびき」, and 「何匹|なんびき」. It follows the same p and b pattern as 「本|ほん」, so learn them together.",
      "Animals are sorted by size and type: small ones take 「匹|ひき」, large ones like cows take 「頭|とう」, and birds take 「羽|わ」. Rabbits traditionally took the bird counter too.",
      "Pet talk is its home, as in 「猫を二匹飼っています|ねこをにひきかっています」, using 「飼う|かう」, to keep a pet. A loner is 「一匹狼|いっぴきおおかみ」, a lone wolf."
    ],
    ja: "{猫|ねこ}が[三匹|さんびき|〜ひき]います。",
    en: "There are three cats." },
  "〜ほん|〜本": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You know this kanji as book, 「本|ほん」, and in 「日本|にほん」, Japan. Its shape is a tree, 「木|き」, with an extra line near the bottom marking the root. So its core meaning is root or origin, which is why Japan is the origin of the sun. As a counter, it uses the long, straight shape of a tree trunk: pencils like 「鉛筆|えんぴつ」, bottles, umbrellas, trees, bananas, even roads and rivers. It also counts some things you might not expect, like movies and phone calls, which are worth just memorizing. The sound changes are important. One is 「一本|いっぽん」, three is 「三本|さんぼん」, six is 「六本|ろっぽん」, eight is 「八本|はっぽん」, and ten is 「十本|じゅっぽん」. To ask how many, say 「何本|なんぼん」. The others stay 「ほん」. It is the same pattern as 「匹|ひき」 for small animals, so learn them together. A common mistake is saying 「さんほん」 instead of 「さんぼん」. A way to remember it: a tree with its root, long and straight. To recap, the root-of-a-tree kanji, counting long thin things with p and b sound changes." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This counter goes far beyond pencils. A phone call is counted with it: 「電話を一本かける|でんわをいっぽんかける」, to make a phone call, is a set phrase. Emails sometimes get this counter too. Movies are counted this way, as in two films, and so are home runs in baseball, and goals in soccer, as in scoring a goal. In judo, 「一本|いっぽん」 is the perfect winning point, the clean full score. There is also 「一本道|いっぽんみち」, a single straight road with no turns. And at a bar, ordering bottles of beer uses it: 「ビールを二本|ビールをにほん」. Many of these share the idea of a line: a phone line, the arc of a ball, a long straight road. When something is long, or travels in a line, this counter is a good guess." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Let us look at the sound changes as a rule, because they appear in many counters. When a counter starts with an h sound, numbers one, six, eight, and ten add a small 「っ」 and the h becomes a p: 「いっぽん」, 「ろっぽん」, 「はっぽん」, 「じゅっぽん」. After three and after the question word, the h becomes a b: 「さんぼん」, 「なんぼん」. The same rule works for 「匹|ひき」 and for 「杯|はい」, the counter for cups and glasses. That last one gives you a famous word: 「いっぱい」, one cupful, which also means full or a lot. So if you know the pattern once, you can apply it to many counters. Practice by tapping out the rhythm of one, two, and three, and feel the little pause before the p. One rule, three counters, and a whole lot of cups." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is a tree with a mark at the root, so it counts long straight things like pencils, bottles and umbrellas. Watch 「一本|いっぽん」, 「三本|さんぼん」 and 「六本|ろっぽん」, not 「さんほん」.",
      "It counts things that travel in a line, like phone calls, as in 「電話を一本かける|でんわをいっぽんかける」, movies, and goals. In judo, 「一本|いっぽん」 is the perfect winning point.",
      "When a counter starts with an h sound, one, six, eight and ten switch to p, while three and how many switch to b. The rule also covers 「杯|はい」, giving 「いっぱい」, a cupful or full."
    ],
    ja: "{鉛筆|えんぴつ}を[三本|さんぼん|〜ほん]ください。",
    en: "Three pencils, please." },
  "がいこくじん|外国人": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「外|がい」, outside. On its own, with the native reading, it is 「外|そと」, the outdoors. The second is 「国|こく」, country. Alone it is 「国|くに」. A way to remember its shape: a border box with a jewel inside, a country guarding its treasure. The last is 「人|じん」, person, the same piece that turns a country into its people, as in 「日本人|にほんじん」 or 「アメリカ人|アメリカじん」. So, a person from outside the country. You already know the first two together as 「外国|がいこく」, a foreign country, and you have seen 「外国語|がいこくご」, foreign language. Swap the last kanji and you change the meaning: country, language, person. A cultural note. People sometimes shorten this to 「外人|がいじん」. That short form is very common, but it can sound blunt or a bit excluding, so 「外国人|がいこくじん」 is the safe, neutral word to use yourself. To recap: outside, country, person, a person from a foreign land." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each of the three kanji opens a door to other words. The first, 「外|がい」, appears in 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, literally outside the sea. Japanese people say they want to travel 「海外|かいがい」 or that they studied abroad there. It is also in 「外出|がいしゅつ」, going out of the house. The second, 「国|こく」, appears in 「国際|こくさい」, international, as in an international airport, and in country names like 「中国|ちゅうごく」, China, and 「韓国|かんこく」, Korea. The third kanji has three readings to keep straight: 「じん」 for nationality, as in 「日本人|にほんじん」, 「にん」 for counting, as in 「三人|さんにん」, and 「ひと」 on its own. So outside, country, and person each lead to many more words. Learn the pieces, and the vocabulary grows by itself." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, you will see this word on signs at city offices and immigration counters, and in news about tourism, where foreign visitors are called 「外国人観光客|がいこくじんかんこうきゃく」. Some cities have help centers just for foreign residents. If you live in Japan, you will hear it often, mostly in a neutral, factual way. Japanese people meeting you might ask 「お国はどちらですか|おくにはどちらですか」, a polite way to ask what country you are from, using the honorific 「お」. A student from abroad is a 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」, which is often the word used for you on campus instead of this one. Notice that 「海外|かいがい」 is about places abroad, while this word is about people. Keep this one as your clear, neutral, official word, and you will always be understood." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「外|がい」, outside, plus 「国|こく」, country, plus 「人|じん」, person: a person from outside the country. The short 「外人|がいじん」 can sound blunt, so this full form is the safe neutral word.",
      "The kanji lead to 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, 「国際|こくさい」, international, and person read 「じん」 for nationality, 「にん」 for counting, and 「ひと」 alone.",
      "It is the neutral, official word you see at city offices and in tourism news. People may politely ask 「お国はどちらですか|おくにはどちらですか」, and students abroad are often called 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」."
    ],
    ja: "[外国人|がいこくじん]の{友だち|ともだち}がいます。",
    en: "I have a friend who is a foreigner." },
  "そば|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "These are thin noodles made from buckwheat flour, usually grayish brown, with a nutty taste. Japanese normally writes them in kana, though there is a kanji spelling you may see on shop signs, 「蕎麦|そば」. You can eat them two main ways. Hot, in a soy based broth, or cold, piled on a bamboo tray and dipped into a cool sauce, which is called 「ざるそば」. Slurping loudly is perfectly polite. It cools the noodles and shows you are enjoying them. Two customs make this word stick. On New Year's Eve, many families eat 「年越しそば|としこしそば」, year crossing soba. The long noodles stand for a long life, and because they break easily, they also stand for cutting off the troubles of the old year. And when people move into a new home, there is an old custom of giving neighbors soba. One trap. 「焼きそば|やきそば」, fried noodles, is actually made with wheat noodles, not buckwheat, even though it carries the name. Also, a different word that sounds the same, 「そば」, means right next to something. Recap: buckwheat noodles, hot or cold, and the noodle of New Year's Eve." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Walk into a soba shop and the menu has a set of names that sound like a puzzle. 「天ぷらそば|てんぷらそば」 comes topped with crispy tempura, often shrimp. 「きつねそば」, fox soba, has sweet fried tofu on top, because foxes are said to love fried tofu in Japanese folklore. 「たぬきそば」, raccoon dog soba, has crunchy tempura bits in it, though the topping varies by region. Near stations, you will find stand-up soba counters, 「立ち食いそば|たちぐいそば」, where busy workers slurp a bowl in five minutes. And after eating cold soba, many shops bring a pot of the cloudy cooking water, called 「そば湯|そばゆ」, which you pour into your leftover dipping sauce and drink. It is warm, nutty, and a lovely way to end the meal. Fox, raccoon, tempura, and a cup of noodle water." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The name for these noodles is usually explained as a shortening of an older word for the buckwheat plant, 「そばむぎ」. The second half, 「むぎ」, is the word for wheat or grain. The first half is widely explained as an old word for edges or corners, because buckwheat seeds have a distinctive three-cornered, angular shape. Over time, people dropped the grain part and kept the short name. Buckwheat grows well in cool mountain areas with poor soil, which is why regions like Nagano became famous for soba. For centuries, it was the plant and its grain that people ate as porridge or dumplings. Noodles made from it became popular later, especially in the city that became Tokyo, where soba shops became a classic part of everyday life. From an angular seed to a city's favorite fast food." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "As the main lesson hinted, the same sound also means near or beside, sometimes written 「側|そば」. Let us look at that other word more closely so you never mix them up. 「駅のそばに住んでいます|えきのそばにすんでいます」 means I live near the station. It feels close and personal, right next to. Compare it with 「ちかく」, nearby, which is a little wider, and 「となり」, which means directly next door, side by side. So you have three closeness words: 「となり」 is touching, 「そば」 is right beside, 「ちかく」 is in the area. How do you tell the noodles apart? Easy: the particles and the verbs. If someone is eating or ordering it, it is noodles. If it comes with 「の」 after a place and is followed by a location, it is beside. Noodles you eat, nearness you stand in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Thin buckwheat noodles, eaten hot in broth or cold as 「ざるそば」, and slurped loudly. On New Year's Eve, 「年越しそば|としこしそば」 stands for long life, though 「焼きそば|やきそば」 is actually wheat noodles.",
      "Menus offer 「天ぷらそば|てんぷらそば」, 「きつねそば」 with fried tofu that foxes love, and 「たぬきそば」 with tempura bits. Cold soba often ends with 「そば湯|そばゆ」, the cooking water mixed into the sauce.",
      "The name is usually explained as a shortening of 「そばむぎ」, with the first half an old word for corners, after the angular buckwheat seed. Buckwheat thrives in cool mountains like Nagano.",
      "The same sound means right beside, as in 「駅のそばに住んでいます|えきのそばにすんでいます」. 「となり」 is touching, 「そば」 is right beside, and 「ちかく」 is in the area."
    ],
    ja: "{昼ごはん|ひるごはん}に[そば]を{食べました|たべました}。",
    en: "I ate soba for lunch." },
  "え|絵": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A single sound, and a surprise. It looks like a native word, but this 「え」 is actually a Chinese-origin reading. Look at the kanji in two parts. On the left is the thread part, the same one found in 「糸|いと」, thread. On the right is 「会|かい」, the kanji from 「会う|あう」, to meet or gather. A way to remember it: threads of many colors gathered together into one picture, like embroidery. It covers any kind of picture made by hand, paintings, drawings, sketches, even a child's crayon doodle. A photo is not this word. A photo is 「写真|しゃしん」. Its partner verb is 「描く|かく」, to draw or paint, so you say 「絵を描く|えをかく」. You will also meet it inside other words, like 「絵本|えほん」, a picture book, with 「本|ほん」, book, and 「絵はがき|えはがき」, a picture postcard. If you want to say someone is good at art, the natural phrase is 「絵が上手です|えがじょうずです」. Recap: colored threads gathered together, a hand made picture." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Here is a word that went from Japanese into English. 「絵文字|えもじ」 is picture plus 「文字|もじ」, written character. So the word emoji literally means picture character. Many English speakers assume it has something to do with the English word emotion, but that is just a coincidence of sound. Emoji were developed for Japanese mobile phones in the late 1990s, and later spread worldwide when smartphones adopted them. Japanese also has 「顔文字|かおもじ」, face characters, the little faces built from punctuation marks and symbols. The picture kanji also appears in 「絵の具|えのぐ」, paints, the colors you use for painting, and 「似顔絵|にがおえ」, a portrait or caricature, the kind street artists draw. Notice how 「え」 sits at the end there and is still easy to hear. One tiny sound inside a word the whole world uses." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a rich tradition of pictures, and this kanji names many of them. 「浮世絵|うきよえ」 are the famous woodblock prints of the Edo period, showing actors, beautiful people, and landscapes. The well known image of a huge wave with Mount Fuji in the background is one of them, by Hokusai. Visit a Shinto shrine and you will see 「絵馬|えま」, small wooden plaques where people write their wishes. The name means picture horse, because long ago people offered real horses to the gods, then pictures of horses instead. Today, many plaques show the animal of the year instead. Students write wishes to pass exams on them. And 「絵本|えほん」, picture books, are a big part of Japanese childhood. From woodblock waves to wishing plaques, the picture kanji is everywhere." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Because this word is a single vowel, it can sound like other things. First, the particle 「へ」, the direction marker meaning to, is pronounced just like this word. So 「どこへ」 sounds like where plus picture, but context makes it clear. Second, the little sound of surprise, 「え?」, which means huh, or what. Said with a rising tone, it is a reaction, not a noun. Third, there is a separate word for a handle, like the handle of an umbrella, which also sounds the same. The trick is to listen for particles. The picture word is a noun, so it will usually come with 「を」, 「が」, or 「の」 after it, as in drawing a picture or the picture is beautiful. Surprise comes alone with a rising voice. Listen for the particle, and the picture will reveal itself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Despite looking native, 「え」 is a Chinese-origin reading. The kanji is thread plus 「会|かい」, gather: colored threads gathered into a hand made picture, drawn with 「描く|かく」, while a photo is 「写真|しゃしん」.",
      "「絵文字|えもじ」 is picture plus character, so emoji has nothing to do with emotion. It is also in 「顔文字|かおもじ」, face characters, and 「似顔絵|にがおえ」, a portrait.",
      "The kanji names Japan's picture traditions: 「浮世絵|うきよえ」 woodblock prints like Hokusai's wave, 「絵馬|えま」 wishing plaques at shrines, and 「絵本|えほん」, picture books.",
      "It sounds like the particle 「へ」 and the surprised 「え?」. As a noun, it usually comes with 「を」, 「が」 or 「の」, while surprise stands alone with a rising voice."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は[絵|え]がじょうずです。",
    en: "My older brother is good at drawing pictures." },
  "ちず|地図": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「地|ち」, ground or land. Look at its left side and you will see the earth part, 「土|つち」, soil. You already know it in 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, the subway, which is literally underground iron. The second is 「図|ず」, a drawing, a diagram or a plan. It is the same kanji that starts 「図書館|としょかん」, the library, but notice the reading changes. There it is 「と」, here it is 「ず」. Both are Chinese-origin readings of the same kanji, and each word just uses one. Put them together and you get a drawing of the land. Simple. Fun detail. The kanji 「地|ち」 and 「池|ち」, the pond in 「電池|でんち」, share the same right side and the same reading, so they are easy to mix up. Just check the left side. Earth for land, water drops for a pond. Useful phrases are 「地図を見る|ちずをみる」, to look at a map, and 「地図アプリ|ちずアプリ」, a map app on your phone. Recap: a drawing of the land, a map." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji also has another Chinese-origin reading, 「じ」, and you will meet both. With 「ち」, it appears in 「地下|ちか」, underground, 「地球|ちきゅう」, the Earth, literally the ground ball, and 「土地|とち」, a plot of land. With 「じ」, it appears in 「地震|じしん」, an earthquake, the ground shaking, and in 「地元|じもと」, your hometown or local area, a word Japanese people love when talking about their roots. The second kanji also has a less common reading, 「と」, which you already know from 「図書館|としょかん」, and it appears as 「ず」 in 「合図|あいず」, a signal. So this little map word is built from two very productive kanji. Earth, earthquakes, hometowns, signals, and the library are all in its extended family." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Maps matter more in Japan than you might expect, because most streets have no names. Addresses work by numbered blocks inside neighborhoods, not by street names, so even locals can get lost. That is why shops often put a small map on their website and business cards. If you are lost, the classic solution is the 「交番|こうばん」, a small neighborhood police box, where officers help with directions and often have detailed local maps on the wall. Japanese maps also use special symbols. A temple is often marked with a swastika-like symbol that long predates its European misuse, and a post office uses the same T-shaped mark you see on mailboxes. Today, of course, people mostly use phone maps. But the paper map at the station exit is still a lifesaver. No street names, plenty of maps." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases you need when you are lost in Japan. First, find someone and say 「すみません、地図を書いてもらえませんか|すみません、ちずをかいてもらえませんか」, excuse me, could you draw me a map? People will often sketch one for you. If you are looking at your phone, you can point and ask 「この地図のどこですか|このちずのどこですか」, where on this map is it? At hotels and tourist offices, ask for a free map: 「地図をください|ちずをください」. Many Japanese people, especially in big cities, will even walk you there. Notice that drawing a map uses 「書く|かく」 or 「描く|かく」, both pronounced the same. Looking at a map uses 「見る|みる」. With these three sentences, you can find your way almost anywhere in Japan." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「地|ち」, land, with the earth piece, plus 「図|ず」, drawing, the same kanji read 「と」 in 「図書館|としょかん」. A drawing of the land; just do not confuse 「地|ち」 with 「池|ち」, pond.",
      "The first kanji gives 「地球|ちきゅう」, the Earth, and with 「じ」, 「地震|じしん」 and 「地元|じもと」, hometown. The second appears as 「ず」 in 「合図|あいず」, a signal.",
      "Most Japanese streets have no names and addresses use numbered blocks, so maps matter a lot. If lost, head to a 「交番|こうばん」, a police box with detailed local maps.",
      "Ask 「地図を書いてもらえませんか|ちずをかいてもらえませんか」 for someone to sketch one, or point at your phone with 「この地図のどこですか|このちずのどこですか」. Looking at a map uses 「見る|みる」."
    ],
    ja: "この[地図|ちず]を{見て|みて}ください。",
    en: "Please look at this map." },
  "じしょ|辞書": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「辞|じ」, which is about words and expressions. The second is 「書|しょ」, writing or a written book. A book of words. You have seen 「書|しょ」 in lots of places. It is in 「図書館|としょかん」, the library, in 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, the textbook, and in 「履歴書|りれきしょ」, a resume. With its native reading it becomes the verb 「書く|かく」, to write. Notice that the learner list first gave you this word in kana only, back in the early lessons. Now you get the kanji too. A nice idiom. In Japanese you do not just use a dictionary, you pull it. The phrase is 「辞書を引く|じしょをひく」, to pull the dictionary, which comes from flipping and pulling pages to find a word. You can also say 「辞書で調べる|じしょでしらべる」, to look something up in the dictionary. A related word is 「電子辞書|でんしじしょ」, an electronic dictionary, which Japanese students used for years before phones took over. Recap: a written book of words." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji is worth a careful look. On its left is 「舌|した」, the tongue, which you will meet as its own word. A tongue in a kanji about words fits nicely: words come off the tongue. The kanji itself means words, speech, but also to decline or leave. That second meaning shows up in the verb 「辞める|やめる」, to quit a job. You might hear 「会社を辞めました|かいしゃをやめました」, I quit my company. It also appears in 「お世辞|おせじ」, flattery or empty compliments, words said just to be nice. When someone praises your Japanese, you might laugh and say they are just flattering you, using that word. So the kanji connects dictionaries, quitting, and flattery. The tongue on the left reminds you that all of them are about words coming out of the mouth." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a few words for dictionaries. This one is the most common everyday word. 「辞典|じてん」 is a bit more formal and is often used in titles of dictionaries, like an English Japanese dictionary on the shelf of a bookstore. Then there is 「事典|じてん」, which sounds exactly the same but uses the kanji for things, and means an encyclopedia, a book of things rather than words. 「字典|じてん」 is a dictionary of characters. Learners will mostly use 「漢和辞典|かんわじてん」, a kanji dictionary, to look up characters. A famous dictionary in Japan is 「広辞苑|こうじえん」, a huge, respected volume, a bit like the Oxford English Dictionary in reputation. For everyday speech, though, just say the word in this lesson. It is always correct and always understood." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「辞|じ」, words, plus 「書|しょ」, writing: a written book of words. You do not just use one, you pull it, 「辞書を引く|じしょをひく」, from flipping pages to find a word.",
      "The first kanji has 「舌|した」, tongue, on its left, since words come off the tongue. It also means to leave, as in 「辞める|やめる」, to quit, and appears in 「お世辞|おせじ」, flattery.",
      "「辞典|じてん」 is a more formal word for dictionary, while same-sounding 「事典|じてん」 is an encyclopedia. For everyday speech, 「辞書|じしょ」 is always correct."
    ],
    ja: "{漢字|かんじ}を[辞書|じしょ]でしらべます。",
    en: "I look up kanji in the dictionary." },
  "かぐ|家具": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「家|か」, house or family. With its native reading it is 「家|いえ」, house, and you know it in 「家族|かぞく」, family. A way to remember the shape: a roof on top, and under it the old kanji for a pig, as if the most precious thing in an old farmhouse was kept indoors. The second is 「具|ぐ」, tools, equipment, or the fillings in a dish. Japanese people even call the ingredients floating in a soup the 「具|ぐ」. So the equipment of a house. Tables, chairs, beds, shelves and sofas. Like English furniture, it is a general, uncountable feeling word. You would not use it for small things like cups or a phone. A related word is 「道具|どうぐ」, tools, which shares the same 「具|ぐ」. Cultural note. Many Japanese apartments come completely empty, sometimes without even a light fixture, so buying 「家具|かぐ」 is a big part of moving. Recap: house plus equipment, everything that fills a home." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji lead to more useful words. 「家|か」 appears in 「家庭|かてい」, home or household, and in 「作家|さっか」, a writer, literally a maker by profession. With another reading, 「け」, it marks a family name, as in the Tanaka family, 「田中家|たなかけ」. With 「や」, it appears in 「大家|おおや」, a landlord, the person you rent your apartment from. The second kanji, 「具|ぐ」, appears in 「文房具|ぶんぼうぐ」, stationery, pens, erasers, and notebooks, and in 「雨具|あまぐ」, rain gear. And a very handy word: 「具合|ぐあい」, condition. When you feel sick, you can say 「具合が悪いです|ぐあいがわるいです」, I am not feeling well. So this word links your landlord, your pens, and your health to the furniture in your room." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There is a verb with exactly the same sound: 「嗅ぐ|かぐ」, to smell or sniff something. 「花のにおいを嗅ぐ|はなのにおいをかぐ」 means to smell a flower. So 「かぐ」 can be a noun for furniture or a verb for sniffing. In a sentence, they are easy to tell apart, because the verb conjugates: 「かぎます」, 「かいで」, while the noun takes particles like 「を」 or 「が」 in front of a verb. Here is a memory scene that ties them together, clearly a trick. Picture a dog walking into a brand new furniture store, sniffing every sofa and table, nose working hard. The dog smells the furniture. Same sound, two meanings, one sniffing dog. When you see 「家具屋|かぐや」, a furniture store, imagine that dog and smile." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Apartment hunting in Japan, you will see the phrase 「家具付き|かぐつき」, furnished, literally with furniture attached. Furnished places are popular with students and people on short stays. When you move out, big furniture can be hard to throw away. You often have to call the city office, pay a fee, and put a special sticker on it, because large items are 「粗大ごみ|そだいごみ」, oversized trash. Many people sell old furniture online or give it to friends instead. When shopping, you will visit a 「家具屋|かぐや」, a furniture store. There, the store staff might ask whether you need delivery, using 「配達|はいたつ」. So furniture in Japan means finding a furnished place, buying wisely, and planning ahead for moving day. Buy it, move it, and know how to get rid of it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「家|か」, house, plus 「具|ぐ」, tools or equipment: the equipment of a house, like tables and beds, not small items. Many Japanese apartments come completely empty, so buying it is a big part of moving.",
      "The first kanji gives 「家庭|かてい」 and 「大家|おおや」, landlord, while the second gives 「文房具|ぶんぼうぐ」, stationery, and 「具合が悪いです|ぐあいがわるいです」, I am not feeling well.",
      "The verb 「嗅ぐ|かぐ」 means to sniff, and it conjugates while the noun takes particles. Picture a dog sniffing every sofa in a furniture store, a 「家具屋|かぐや」.",
      "Look for 「家具付き|かぐつき」, furnished, when apartment hunting. Big old furniture counts as 「粗大ごみ|そだいごみ」, oversized trash, needing a fee and a sticker to throw away."
    ],
    ja: "{新しい|あたらしい}[家具|かぐ]を{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought new furniture." },
  "でんち|電池": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「電|でん」, electricity. Look at the top and you will see the rain part, 「雨|あめ」. The kanji began as a picture of lightning in a storm, the first electricity people ever saw. You know it from a whole family of words. 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, 「電車|でんしゃ」, train, and 「電気|でんき」, electricity or lights. The second kanji is 「池|ち」, a pond. Its left side is the water part, three drops. With the native reading, it is 「池|いけ」, a pond in a garden or park. So a battery is a pond of electricity, a little pool where power is stored until you need it. That is a lovely, accurate picture. A sharp eye will notice that 「池|ち」, pond, and 「地|ち」, land, as in 「地図|ちず」, share the same right side and the same sound. Water on the left for pond, earth on the left for land. A handy phrase: 「電池が切れました|でんちがきれました」, the battery ran out, literally the battery got cut. Recap: lightning plus pond, stored electricity." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's zoom in on the first kanji, 「電|でん」, and look past the rain on top. The bottom part is 「申|しん」, which today means to say, as in the humble verb 「申す|もうす」. But scholars agree that in the oldest Chinese writing this shape was a picture of a lightning bolt, a jagged line with branches coming off it. Later it took on other meanings, so the rain part was added on top to make it clear that this was lightning from the sky. That makes the kanji a little weather report. Rain above, a flash below. The second kanji, 「池|ち」, is much simpler. Water on the left, and a sound part on the right that also appears in 「地|ち」 and 「他|た」, as in 「他|ほか」, other. So when you see this word, you are looking at a storm and a pond. One kanji brings the power, the other holds it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Shops sell batteries by size, and the size names are worth knowing. The common double A battery is 「単三|たんさん」, and the thinner triple A is 「単四|たんよん」. So you might ask a clerk for 「単三電池|たんさんでんち」. Long round batteries are often counted with 「本|ほん」, the counter for long thin things, so two of them is 「二本|にほん」. For phones, people usually switch to the loanword 「バッテリー」, and charging is 「充電|じゅうでん」, filling with electricity. You will hear 「充電がない|じゅうでんがない」, I'm out of charge, constantly. When a battery dies in a remote control, you go back to this word: 「電池が切れた|でんちがきれた」. Old batteries should not go in the normal trash. Many electronics stores and town offices have special collection boxes for them. So in daily life, this word lives in clocks, remotes and toys, while phones get the loanword." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a cozy den, the kind a bear sleeps in. In the middle of the den is a little pond. And in that pond, tiny lightning bolts are swimming around like goldfish, glowing yellow. The bear reaches in, scoops up a handful of them, and stuffs them into the back of its television remote. Click, the TV comes on. The den gives you 「でん」, and the bolts in the water remind you of the two kanji, lightning and pond. Every time your remote stops working, imagine the bear grumbling that the pond has run dry. Den, pond, lightning fish, power for your remote." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「電|でん」, electricity, which began as lightning under rain, plus 「池|ち」, pond: a little pond of stored electricity. When it dies, say 「電池が切れました|でんちがきれました」.",
      "The bottom of 「電|でん」 is 「申|しん」, which originally pictured a lightning bolt, so rain was added on top to show it came from the sky. A storm and a pond: one brings power, the other holds it.",
      "AA batteries are 「単三|たんさん」 and AAA are 「単四|たんよん」, counted with 「本|ほん」. Phones use the loanword 「バッテリー」 and 「充電|じゅうでん」, while this word lives in clocks and remotes.",
      "Picture a bear's den with a pond where lightning bolts swim like goldfish, scooped up to power the TV remote. Den, pond, lightning."
    ],
    ja: "[電池|でんち]を{買いに|かいに}{行きます|いきます}。",
    en: "I'm going to buy batteries." },
  "ジャケット|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English, jacket. Look at how the sounds were squeezed to fit Japanese. The ja sound becomes 「ジャ」. The ck in the middle turns into a small 「ッ」, which is a tiny pause, a held breath before the next sound. Then the final t cannot stand alone in Japanese, because almost every syllable has to end in a vowel, so it gets an o sound added and becomes 「ト」. You will see the same pattern in 「ペット」 and 「ポケット」, pocket. Since a jacket goes on your upper body, the verb is 「着る|きる」, to wear, the same one you use for a shirt or a sweater: 「ジャケットを着る|ジャケットをきる」. Meaning shift. In Japanese, this word also means the cover of a record or CD. Music fans talk about the cover art as the 「ジャケット」. For a heavy winter coat, people more often say 「コート」. Recap: an English word with a small pause and an extra vowel, the thing you put on when it gets cool." },
      { title: "Clothes verbs", text: "English uses put on and wear for everything, but Japanese picks the verb by the part of the body. A jacket covers the upper body, so it goes with 「着る|きる」. That same verb works for shirts, sweaters and coats. Things you step into below the waist, like trousers, skirts and shoes, take 「はく」. A hat on your head takes 「かぶる」. Glasses take 「かける」. Taking almost any of them off is 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, so 「ジャケットを脱ぐ|ジャケットをぬぐ」 is to take off your jacket. Here's a common mix up. Learners sometimes say they wear their shoes with 「着る|きる」, which sounds odd to Japanese ears. To describe what someone has on right now, use the te form plus 「いる」: 「黒いジャケットを着ています|くろいジャケットをきています」, he is wearing a black jacket. So remember, a jacket is a top, and tops take 「着る|きる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The word turns up in lots of combinations. A puffy down jacket is a 「ダウンジャケット」, very popular in winter. A leather jacket is often 「革ジャン|かわジャン」, where the word is shortened to just 「ジャン」. That shortcut also gives 「ジージャン」, a denim jacket, from the letter G for jeans. And in many schools and companies, a suit jacket is simply called the 「上着|うわぎ」, the upper garment, a native word that covers any outer top. In a shop, you might hear a clerk offer to let you try it on: 「試着しますか|しちゃくしますか」, would you like to try it on. And if it's chilly in the evening, a friend may remind you to bring one: 「ジャケットを持っていったほうがいいですよ|ジャケットをもっていったほうがいいですよ」. Leather, denim, down, it's all the same jacket at heart." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English jacket, the ck becomes a small 「ッ」 pause and the final t gains a vowel as 「ト」. It goes on the upper body, so use 「着る|きる」, and it can also mean a CD cover.",
      "Japanese picks clothing verbs by body part, so a jacket, like any top, takes 「着る|きる」, while shoes take 「はく」 and hats 「かぶる」. Taking it off is 「ジャケットを脱ぐ|ジャケットをぬぐ」.",
      "It forms 「ダウンジャケット」 and gets clipped to 「ジャン」 in 「革ジャン|かわジャン」 and 「ジージャン」. A native word for any outer top is 「上着|うわぎ」."
    ],
    ja: "さむいですから、[ジャケット]をきます。",
    en: "It's cold, so I'll wear a jacket." },
  "ペット|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English, pet. The short e stays as 「ペ」. The final t, which Japanese cannot leave hanging, becomes a small 「ッ」 pause plus 「ト」, adding a little o sound at the end. It is the same squeeze you hear in 「ジャケット」. Grammar tip. Pets are alive, so they take 「いる」 not 「ある」: 「ペットがいます」, I have a pet. The verb for keeping an animal is 「飼う|かう」, which sounds like 「買う|かう」, to buy, but uses a different kanji. And small animals like dogs and cats are counted with 「匹|ひき」. A common trap. When you see 「ペットボトル」, it has nothing to do with animals. That is a plastic drink bottle, named after the plastic it is made of, called P E T. Many people in Japan live in apartments that ban pets, so finding a pet friendly apartment can be a real search. Recap: an English word with a clipped ending, and it lives with you, so use 「いる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Pets are very popular in Japan, and in recent years there have been more pet cats and dogs than children under fifteen. Small dog breeds suit small homes, so you see lots of toy poodles and chihuahuas, and of course the native 「柴犬|しばいぬ」, the fox faced shiba. Because many apartments ban animals, animal cafés became a big thing. At a 「猫カフェ|ねこカフェ」, a cat café, you pay by the hour to drink coffee and relax with resident cats. There are also cafés with rabbits, owls and even hedgehogs. Another sight that surprises visitors is small dogs riding in pet strollers, pushed along like babies. Many families treat a pet as a true family member, and some temples even hold memorial services for pets that have died. So in Japan, a pet can be a companion, a café visit, or part of the household." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's build some pet vocabulary around this word. A dog is 「犬|いぬ」 and a cat is 「猫|ねこ」. A goldfish, the classic first pet from a summer festival game, is 「金魚|きんぎょ」, gold fish. A bird is 「鳥|とり」, and a hamster is just 「ハムスター」. The verb to keep or raise an animal is 「飼う|かう」. Its kanji has the food part on the left, because keeping an animal means feeding it. A pet shop is a 「ペットショップ」, and a vet is 「獣医|じゅうい」, though most people just say the animal hospital, 「動物病院|どうぶつびょういん」. One more fun expression. Someone who loves cats is a 「猫好き|ねこずき」, and a dog lover is an 「犬好き|いぬずき」. People often ask each other which side they're on. So, are you a dog person or a cat person?" }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English pet, with a small 「ッ」 and 「ト」 at the end. Pets are alive, so say 「ペットがいます」, keep them with 「飼う|かう」, and note that 「ペットボトル」 is just a plastic bottle.",
      "Japan now has more pet cats and dogs than children under fifteen. With many apartments banning pets, animal cafes like the 「猫カフェ|ねこカフェ」 became popular.",
      "Build vocabulary around it: 「犬|いぬ」, 「猫|ねこ」, 「金魚|きんぎょ」, and 「飼う|かう」, whose kanji has the food piece. People love asking if you are 「猫好き|ねこずき」 or 「犬好き|いぬずき」."
    ],
    ja: "うちには[ペット]がいません。",
    en: "We don't have any pets at home." },
  "わりびきけん|割引券": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, and the first two come from native verbs. 「割|わり」 comes from 「割る|わる」, to split or divide. 「引|びき」 comes from 「引く|ひく」, to pull, or in math, to subtract. The 「ひ」 turns into 「び」 here because it is joined onto the word before it. Together, 「割引|わりびき」 means a discount, splitting off a part of the price and pulling it away. The last kanji uses its Chinese-origin reading, 「券|けん」, a ticket or voucher. It appears on all kinds of tickets, like the meal tickets you buy from machines at noodle shops, called 「食券|しょっけん」. So a ticket that takes some off the price. A useful bonus. 「割|わり」 on its own also means ten percent. So 「二割引|にわりびき」 means twenty percent off, and 「三割引|さんわりびき」 means thirty percent off. You will see these signs in supermarkets in the evening, stuck on bento and sushi. You know 「引|ひく」 from other places too, like 「辞書を引く|じしょをひく」, to look up a word. Recap: split, pull off, ticket, a coupon." },
      { title: "Ticket family", text: "The last kanji, 「券|けん」, means a ticket or certificate, and it builds a big family you will meet at train stations. A basic train ticket is 「乗車券|じょうしゃけん」, a riding ticket. For a fast limited express, you also need a 「特急券|とっきゅうけん」. A commuter pass is a 「定期券|ていきけん」, a fixed period ticket, usually shortened to 「定期|ていき」. An admission ticket for a museum or a platform is an 「入場券|にゅうじょうけん」, an entering place ticket. A free invitation ticket is a 「招待券|しょうたいけん」. And at noodle shops you buy a 「食券|しょっけん」 from a machine. Even a passport is a 「旅券|りょけん」, a travel ticket, in official language. The pattern is always the same. Put what the ticket is for in front, and close with 「券|けん」. So a discount ticket is just one more member of this busy family." },
      { title: "Splitting the bill", text: "The first kanji, 「割|わり」, from 「割る|わる」, to split, shows up in a phrase every student uses when eating out with friends: 「割り勘|わりかん」. It means splitting the bill equally. A friend might say 「割り勘にしよう|わりかんにしよう」, let's split it. In Japan it is very common for friends and coworkers to divide the bill this way, though a boss or senior person may pay for everyone. The same kanji also gives 「学割|がくわり」, a student discount, short for 「学生割引|がくせいわりびき」. Show your student card at a movie theater, museum or even a train ticket office, and you may get a cheaper price. And the verb itself means breaking things too, so 「皿を割る|さらをわる」 is to break a plate. Splitting a price, splitting a bill, splitting a plate, they all come from the same idea." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Discount tickets in Japan come in many forms. Fast food chains hand them out in apps and paper flyers. Restaurants may give you one with the receipt to bring you back next time. Hot springs and bowling alleys print them on websites. Many people today simply say 「クーポン」, coupon, especially for app discounts, while this word sounds a bit more like a printed paper ticket. At the register, you can ask 「この割引券は使えますか|このわりびきけんはつかえますか」, can I use this discount ticket. The verb is 「使う|つかう」, to use. Watch the fine print, though. Common notes say a ticket cannot be combined with other offers, or that it has an expiry date, a 「有効期限|ゆうこうきげん」. Staff will often ask before you pay whether you have any coupons. Hand it over before they ring you up, not after." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「割|わり」, split, plus 「引|びき」, pull off, plus 「券|けん」, ticket: a ticket that takes some off the price. 「割|わり」 alone means ten percent, so 「二割引|にわりびき」 is twenty percent off.",
      "Put what a ticket is for in front of 「券|けん」: 「乗車券|じょうしゃけん」, a train ticket, 「定期券|ていきけん」, a commuter pass, and 「食券|しょっけん」, a meal ticket. Even a passport is a 「旅券|りょけん」.",
      "The split kanji gives 「割り勘|わりかん」, splitting the bill equally, and 「学割|がくわり」, a student discount. The verb 「割る|わる」 also means breaking, as in 「皿を割る|さらをわる」.",
      "Many people say 「クーポン」 for app discounts, while this word suggests a paper ticket. Ask 「この割引券は使えますか|このわりびきけんはつかえますか」 before they ring you up, and check the 「有効期限|ゆうこうきげん」."
    ],
    ja: "[割引券|わりびきけん]がありますか。",
    en: "Do you have a discount coupon?" },
  "インターネット|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English, internet. Watch how it gets squeezed. The in becomes 「イン」. The ter loses its r sound, because Japanese usually drops an r at the end of a syllable, and becomes 「ター」 with a long vowel. Then net becomes 「ネット」, with a small 「ッ」 pause and an o sound added to the final t. In everyday talk, people almost always cut it down to just 「ネット」. They say 「ネットで買う|ネットでかう」, to buy online, and 「ネットで調べる|ネットでしらべる」, to look something up online. Notice the particle. You do things on the internet with 「で」, the particle for the means or place of an action. Japanese loves to shorten long loanwords this way. 「パソコン」 is a shortened personal computer, and 「エアコン」 is a shortened air conditioner. Words for online life are mostly loanwords too, like 「アプリ」, app, and 「メール」, email. Recap: an English word with a dropped r and an extra vowel, and most people just say 「ネット」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The everyday verb for being online is 「つながる」, to be connected. So 「ネットにつながらない」 means I can't get online, and it's what you'll say when the Wi-Fi in your hotel fails. Wi-Fi itself is usually said 「ワイファイ」. Free Wi-Fi spots are common in stations and convenience stores. Shopping online is 「ネットショッピング」, and booking online is 「ネット予約|ネットよやく」. Here's a fun place word. A 「ネットカフェ」, or net café, is a café with private booths, computers, comics and drinks, and some people even sleep there overnight because it's cheaper than a hotel. If you want to ask a friend if they found something online, try 「ネットで見たの|ネットでみたの」, did you see it online. In daily speech, the long form sounds a little formal or technical, almost like something from the news, so most people just say the short form." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word splits into two English pieces, and each piece has its own family in Japanese. The front half, 「インター」, from inter, between, shows up in a few useful loanwords. An 「インターホン」 is the intercom by an apartment door, and you'll press it every time you visit someone. A highway exit is an 「インターチェンジ」, often just called the 「インター」. And an international school is an 「インターナショナルスクール」. The back half, 「ネット」, means net, so it also means a real net, like a tennis net or a fishing net. Japanese builds online words from it too. Online games are 「ネットゲーム」, often 「ネトゲ」. And someone who spends a lot of time on the internet can jokingly be called a 「ネット中毒|ネットちゅうどく」, an internet addict. So one long word, two families." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English internet, with the r dropped and a vowel added after the final t. People nearly always shorten it to 「ネット」 and use 「で」, as in 「ネットで買う|ネットでかう」.",
      "Being online is 「つながる」, so 「ネットにつながらない」 means you cannot get online. The short 「ネット」 is the everyday form, as in 「ネット予約|ネットよやく」, while the long form sounds technical.",
      "Each half has its own family: 「インター」 gives 「インターホン」 and 「インターチェンジ」, while 「ネット」 also means a real net and builds words like 「ネットゲーム」."
    ],
    ja: "[インターネット]で{調べました|しらべました}。",
    en: "I looked it up on the internet." },
  "じしん|地震": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「地|じ」, ground or earth. Look closely at the reading. In 「地図|ちず」, map, and 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, subway, it is 「ち」. Here it is 「じ」. Both are real readings of the same kanji, and this word simply uses 「じ」. The second kanji is 「震|しん」, to shake or tremble. It has the rain part on top, like 「電|でん」 and 「雪|ゆき」, a reminder of thunder shaking the sky. So the ground shakes. A cultural note. Japan sits on several tectonic plates and feels many small earthquakes every year. There is an old saying listing the four scariest things in life: 「地震|じしん」, 「雷|かみなり」, thunder, 「火事|かじ」, fire, and 「親父|おやじ」, the old man, meaning a strict father. Schools hold earthquake drills where children hide under their desks. A sound alike to watch for. 「自信|じしん」, with different kanji, means self confidence. Context tells them apart. Recap: earth plus shake." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When the ground starts moving, people say 「揺れてる|ゆれてる」, it's shaking, from the verb 「揺れる|ゆれる」, to sway. The shaking itself is called 「揺れ|ゆれ」. After any noticeable quake, TV channels show a map with numbers, and those numbers are the 「震度|しんど」, the intensity of shaking at each place. Notice the second kanji of this word again. The scale runs from zero to seven, and the levels five and six are each split into a weaker and a stronger half. In everyday talk, people often use these numbers rather than the magnitude. You might hear 「震度三でした|しんどさんでした」, it was a three. To say an earthquake happened, use 「ある」 or 「起きる|おきる」: 「地震が起きました|じしんがおきました」. Phones also give loud alarms, called earthquake early warnings, a few seconds before strong shaking arrives. Those seconds are enough to duck under a table." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "September first in Japan is 「防災の日|ぼうさいのひ」, Disaster Prevention Day. The date remembers the Great Kanto Earthquake of 1923, which destroyed much of Tokyo and Yokohama, mostly through fires that spread after the shaking. On that day and around it, schools, offices and towns hold drills. Families are encouraged to check their emergency kits, with water, food, a flashlight, a radio and a whistle. Many homes keep this bag near the door. Schools teach children to protect their heads, often with padded hoods that double as seat cushions. Every neighborhood has a marked evacuation site, often a school or a park. The constant small quakes mean most people stay fairly calm during a minor shake, but everyone knows the drill. Preparation is just part of everyday life in Japan." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「地|ち」, combines the earth part on the left, 「土|つち」, soil, with a right side that also appears in 「池|いけ」, pond, and 「他|た」, other. Its two readings split the family. With 「ち」 you get 「地図|ちず」, map, 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, subway, and 「地球|ちきゅう」, the planet Earth. With 「じ」, as in this word, you get 「地面|じめん」, the ground surface, and 「地元|じもと」, your hometown or local area. There's no simple rule for which reading appears, so learn them word by word. The second kanji, 「震|しん」, adds the rain part on top of 「辰|しん」, which gives the sound. The same shape appears in 「振る|ふる」, to wave or shake something, with a hand part instead of rain. So hand plus that shape is waving, and rain plus that shape is shaking. Earth that shakes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「地|じ」, earth, read 「じ」 here rather than 「ち」, plus 「震|しん」, shake, with the rain piece: the ground shakes. Do not confuse it with 「自信|じしん」, self confidence.",
      "When it shakes, people say 「揺れてる|ゆれてる」, and TV shows the 「震度|しんど」, the intensity, on a scale from zero to seven. To say one happened, use 「地震が起きました|じしんがおきました」.",
      "September first is 「防災の日|ぼうさいのひ」, remembering the 1923 Great Kanto Earthquake, with drills and emergency kit checks. Preparation is part of everyday life in Japan.",
      "「地|ち」 is read 「ち」 in 「地図|ちず」 but 「じ」 in 「地面|じめん」, so learn readings word by word. 「震|しん」 is rain over 「辰|しん」, while the hand version, 「振る|ふる」, means to wave."
    ],
    ja: "きのう[地震|じしん]がありました。",
    en: "There was an earthquake yesterday." },
  "ほけん|保険": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「保|ほ」, to protect, keep or maintain. Its left side is the person part, a person who looks after something. The second is 「険|けん」, steep, dangerous or risky. Its left side is the hill part, so you can picture a steep, risky slope. You see it in 「危険|きけん」, danger. Together, protection against danger. That is exactly what insurance is, a way of keeping yourself safe from risks you cannot see coming. In Japan, everyone who lives there, including foreign students, is expected to join the national health insurance. You get a card called 「保険証|ほけんしょう」, and you show it at every clinic visit. With it you usually pay only about thirty percent of the cost. The verb is a little unusual. You do not buy insurance in Japanese, you enter it: 「保険に入る|ほけんにはいる」. Travelers often get 「旅行保険|りょこうほけん」, travel insurance. Recap: protect plus danger, protection against risk." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a trap that catches even Japanese children. There is a second word pronounced exactly the same way, 「保健|ほけん」, written with a different second kanji. It means health care or hygiene. The first kanji is shared, protect, but the second is 「健|けん」, healthy, from 「健康|けんこう」, health. So one word protects against danger, and the other protects your health. You meet the second one at school. The school nurse's room is the 「保健室|ほけんしつ」, where kids go when they feel sick. Here's where it gets tricky. Your national health insurance card is the 「保険証|ほけんしょう」, using the danger kanji, because it is insurance. And the public health center in a town is a 「保健所|ほけんじょ」, using the health kanji. In speech, context tells you which is meant. In writing, check the second kanji. Danger means insurance, health means health care." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese has a whole lineup of insurance words, all ending in this one. Health insurance is 「健康保険|けんこうほけん」. Life insurance is 「生命保険|せいめいほけん」, life plus insurance. Car insurance is 「自動車保険|じどうしゃほけん」. The money you pay for it, the premium, is 「保険料|ほけんりょう」, with 「料|りょう」 meaning a fee. At a clinic, a very useful question is 「保険はききますか|ほけんはききますか」, does insurance cover this, using the verb 「きく」, to be effective, the same one you use when medicine works. Some treatments, like many dental cleanings for looks, are not covered. And when you sign up, remember the verb is entering, not buying: 「保険に入っています|ほけんにはいっています」, I'm insured. So whether it's your health, your car or your life, the word for the coverage stays the same." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word opens its own family. 「保|ほ」, to keep or protect, appears in 「保育園|ほいくえん」, a daycare center, a place that protects and raises little children. You also see it on computers: 「保存|ほぞん」 means to save a file, to keep it safe. And 「保証|ほしょう」 is a guarantee or warranty, the paper that protects you if a new appliance breaks. The second kanji, 「険|けん」, danger or steepness, shows up in 「危険|きけん」, danger, on warning signs. It also gives 「冒険|ぼうけん」, an adventure, literally risking danger, a word you'll see in the titles of many games and anime. And with its native reading, it becomes the adjective 「険しい|けわしい」, steep or harsh, used for a mountain path or a stern face. So insurance sits right where keeping safe meets facing danger." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「保|ほ」, protect, plus 「険|けん」, danger, with the hill piece: protection against risk. You do not buy insurance, you enter it, 「保険に入る|ほけんにはいる」, and show a 「保険証|ほけんしょう」 at clinics.",
      "Same-sounding 「保健|ほけん」 uses 「健|けん」, healthy, and means health care, as in the school nurse's 「保健室|ほけんしつ」. In writing, danger means insurance and health means health care.",
      "Insurance types all end in this word, like 「健康保険|けんこうほけん」 and 「生命保険|せいめいほけん」, and the premium is 「保険料|ほけんりょう」. At a clinic, ask 「保険はききますか|ほけんはききますか」.",
      "「保|ほ」 gives 「保育園|ほいくえん」, daycare, and 「保存|ほぞん」, saving a file, while 「険|けん」 gives 「危険|きけん」 and 「冒険|ぼうけん」, adventure. Insurance sits where keeping safe meets danger."
    ],
    ja: "[保険|ほけん]はたかいです。",
    en: "Insurance is expensive." },
  "ぜいきん|税金": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「税|ぜい」, tax. Look at its left side and you will see the grain part, the stalk of a rice plant. That fits history well. For centuries in Japan, taxes were paid in rice, not coins. The second is 「金|きん」, gold or money. You know it with its native reading in 「お金|おかね」, money, and in 「お金持ち|おかねもち」, a rich person. With the same Chinese-origin reading as here, it gives you 「金曜日|きんようび」, Friday, gold day. So tax money. The verb you use is 「払う|はらう」, to pay: 「税金を払う|ぜいきんをはらう」. A practical note for shopping. Japan has a consumption tax, which is the sales tax added to prices. Price tags often show two numbers, one before tax and one including tax, so look for the label that says 「税込|ぜいこみ」, tax included. Recap: grain on the left, money on the right, the money you owe the government." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the right side of 「税|ぜい」. It's a shape you have already met in two other kanji. It sits on the right of 「説|せつ」, as in 「説明|せつめい」, explanation, where the left side is the words part. And it sits on the right of 「脱|だつ」, as in 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, to take off clothes, where the left side is the body or flesh part. In all three, that right side mostly carries sound, which is why their readings are similar. So you can think of a small team. Words plus that piece is explaining. Body plus that piece is undressing. Grain plus that piece is tax. Grain on the left makes sense because, as you heard before, taxes were once paid in rice. Spotting shared pieces like this makes new kanji far less scary. When you see that right side, expect a reading that sounds a little like 「ぜい」, 「せつ」 or 「だつ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Shopping in Japan, you'll see the tax kanji everywhere. The national sales tax is 「消費税|しょうひぜい」, consumption tax. Prices can be marked 「税込|ぜいこみ」, tax included, or 「税抜|ぜいぬき」, tax pulled out, meaning tax will be added at the register. Then there's 「免税|めんぜい」, tax exemption. Many stores for tourists show a sign for tax free shopping, and a duty free store is a 「免税店|めんぜいてん」. Visitors with a passport can often get the consumption tax back on larger purchases. For residents, the big one is 「所得税|しょとくぜい」, income tax. Notice how this kanji simply attaches to the end of a word to name a kind of tax. On its own, though, you'll usually say the whole word, tax money, when talking about paying: 「税金が高い|ぜいきんがたかい」, taxes are high, is a complaint you'll hear in many conversations." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has an unusual tax system called 「ふるさと納税|ふるさとのうぜい」, hometown tax payment. The word 「ふるさと」 means hometown, and 「納税|のうぜい」 means paying tax. Here's how it works. Instead of sending part of your local tax to the city where you live, you can donate it to almost any town in Japan that you choose. In return, that town sends you a thank you gift, often local food. People pick towns for their beef, crab, rice, fruit or sake. It was started to help rural areas whose young people had moved to big cities. Now it is hugely popular, with websites that look like online shopping malls. Each year, many people choose towns just by browsing for the tastiest gifts. So in Japan, paying taxes can actually end with a box of fresh scallops arriving at your door." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「税|ぜい」, tax, with the grain piece since taxes were once paid in rice, plus 「金|きん」, money. You 「払う|はらう」 it, and price tags marked 「税込|ぜいこみ」 include tax.",
      "The right side of 「税|ぜい」 also appears in 「説|せつ」 and 「脱|だつ」, mostly carrying sound. Words plus it is explaining, body plus it is undressing, and grain plus it is tax.",
      "This kanji attaches to name kinds of tax: 「消費税|しょうひぜい」, consumption tax, 「所得税|しょとくぜい」, income tax, and 「免税|めんぜい」, tax free. Prices may be 「税込|ぜいこみ」 or 「税抜|ぜいぬき」.",
      "With 「ふるさと納税|ふるさとのうぜい」, you can send part of your local tax to a town you choose and get a thank you gift, often local food. It was started to help rural areas."
    ],
    ja: "{毎年|まいとし}[税金|ぜいきん]をはらいます。",
    en: "I pay taxes every year." },
  "きょうしつ|教室": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「教|きょう」, to teach. With its native reading it becomes the verb 「教える|おしえる」, to teach, which you already know. It also starts 「教科書|きょうかしょ」, textbook, and 「教師|きょうし」, teacher. The second is 「室|しつ」, a room. It has the roof part on top, a covered space. So a teaching room. Two uses worth knowing. The first is the plain classroom at a school. The second is a class or lesson school outside regular education. A cooking class is 「料理教室|りょうりきょうしつ」, and a place for English lessons can be called 「英会話教室|えいかいわきょうしつ」. A cultural note. In most Japanese schools, there are no janitors for the classrooms. The students clean their own classrooms every day, sweeping, wiping desks and even washing floors. It is seen as part of learning to care for shared spaces. Recap: teach plus room, a place where teaching happens." },
      { title: "Room family", text: "The second kanji, 「室|しつ」, room, closes a whole set of words for rooms with a special purpose. A meeting room is a 「会議室|かいぎしつ」. A waiting room at a clinic or station is a 「待合室|まちあいしつ」, a room for waiting together. At school, besides the classroom, there is the 「保健室|ほけんしつ」, the nurse's room, and the 「職員室|しょくいんしつ」, the teachers' room, where students go to see a teacher. At university, a professor's office and lab is the 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」, research room. At a hotel or inn, a traditional tatami room is a 「和室|わしつ」, a Japanese room, while a Western style room is a 「洋室|ようしつ」. Notice that in almost all of these, the ending names a room inside a bigger building. For a whole building, you'd use a different ending. So when you hear 「しつ」 at the end, picture a door with a label on it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "English uses class for both the room and the group of students, but Japanese splits them. The room is this word. The group of students is 「クラス」. So you are in the same 「クラス」 as your friend, but you meet in a 「教室|きょうしつ」. To talk about being there, use 「いる」 for people inside: 「先生はまだ教室にいます|せんせいはまだきょうしつにいます」, the teacher is still in the classroom. In Japanese high schools, students usually stay in their home classroom, and teachers move from room to room. Special subjects have their own rooms, like the science room, the 「理科室|りかしつ」, or the music room, the 「音楽室|おんがくしつ」. Lessons outside school use the word too, so if a friend says they go to a 「ヨガ教室|ヨガきょうしつ」, they mean yoga classes, not a yoga room. Room or lesson, context tells you." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the right side of 「教|きょう」. It is a common component often described as a hand holding a stick, and you see it in other action kanji like 「数|すう」, number, and 「放|ほう」, to release. In old writing it suggested tapping or urging someone on. The bottom of the left side contains 「子|こ」, child. A traditional reading of the shape is someone guiding a child, though the details of its origin are debated. Now the second kanji, 「室|しつ」. On top is the roof part, and underneath is 「至|し」, a kanji meaning to reach or arrive. A handy way to picture it, more a memory aid than history: the place you reach once you're under the roof, the inner room. So the classroom is the inner room where a child is guided. Two kanji, a hand urging and a roof sheltering." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「教|きょう」, teach, plus 「室|しつ」, room, with a roof on top: a teaching room. It also means a lesson school, like 「料理教室|りょうりきょうしつ」, and Japanese students clean their own classrooms.",
      "The kanji 「室|しつ」 labels special purpose rooms inside a building, like 「会議室|かいぎしつ」, 「待合室|まちあいしつ」, 「職員室|しょくいんしつ」, and 「和室|わしつ」, a tatami room.",
      "Japanese splits class in two: the room is 「教室|きょうしつ」 and the group of students is 「クラス」. A 「ヨガ教室|ヨガきょうしつ」 means yoga classes, not a yoga room.",
      "「教|きょう」 has a hand holding a stick on the right and 「子|こ」 on the left, traditionally read as guiding a child. 「室|しつ」 is a roof over 「至|し」, to reach, the inner room under the roof."
    ],
    ja: "[教室|きょうしつ]に{学生|がくせい}がいます。",
    en: "There are students in the classroom." },
  "たてもの|建物": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, and here both use their native readings, which is a little unusual for a compound. The first is 「建|たて」, from the verb 「建てる|たてる」, to build or put up a structure. The second is 「物|もの」, thing. So a built thing, a building. You already know 「物|もの」 from a big family of everyday words. 「食べ物|たべもの」 is an eaten thing, food. 「飲み物|のみもの」 is a drunk thing, a drink. 「買い物|かいもの」 is a bought thing, shopping. And 「着物|きもの」 is a worn thing, the kimono. In each one, a verb plus 「物|もの」 makes a thing. Careful, though. The same kanji switches to its Chinese-origin reading in other words, like 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, the zoo, and 「物価|ぶっか」, prices. This word is general. It covers houses, temples, schools and towers. For a modern multi story office or shopping building, people often say 「ビル」, short for the English word building. Recap: build plus thing, anything that was built." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There are two verbs pronounced 「たてる」, and they're easy to mix up. 「建てる|たてる」, with the kanji in this word, means to build a structure, like a house, a temple or a tower. 「立てる|たてる」, with the kanji of 「立つ|たつ」, to stand, means to stand something up, like a pole, a flag or even a plan, 「計画を立てる|けいかくをたてる」, to make a plan. So a building is something you put up permanently, while standing something up can be temporary. Each has an intransitive partner too. 「建つ|たつ」 means a building goes up: 「駅の前に新しいビルが建ちました|えきのまえにあたらしいビルがたちました」, a new building went up in front of the station. In speech they all sound the same, so ask yourself, is it a structure? If yes, it's the build kanji, the one in this word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The build kanji, 「建|たて」, gives a few useful words for talking about houses and cities. When it attaches to a number of floors, it changes to 「だて」 and describes how tall a building is. A two story house is 「二階建て|にかいだて」, and a ten story building is 「十階建て|じゅっかいだて」. A detached single family house is an 「一戸建て|いっこだて」, a one unit build, which many Japanese families dream of instead of an apartment. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「けん」, you get 「建築|けんちく」, architecture, and 「建築家|けんちくか」, an architect. Japan has many famous architects, and you might hear the word in a museum or on a city tour. There is also 「建設|けんせつ」, construction, which you see on signs at building sites. So the same kanji covers dream homes, skyscrapers and the sites where they rise." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Unusually, both kanji use native readings: 「建|たて」 from 「建てる|たてる」, to build, plus 「物|もの」, thing. A built thing, like 「食べ物|たべもの」 is an eaten thing; modern office buildings are often 「ビル」.",
      "「建てる|たてる」 means to build a structure, while 「立てる|たてる」 means to stand something up, even a plan. If it is a structure, use the build kanji.",
      "With floor counts it becomes 「だて」, as in 「二階建て|にかいだて」, and a detached house is an 「一戸建て|いっこだて」. Read 「けん」, it gives 「建築|けんちく」, architecture, and 「建設|けんせつ」, construction."
    ],
    ja: "あの[建物|たてもの]は{大きい|おおきい}です。",
    en: "That building is big." },
  "プール|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English, pool. The long oo sound is shown by the long mark 「ー」, which stretches the vowel. The final l becomes 「ル」, because Japanese has no separate l sound and uses its r sound instead, with an extra vowel on the end. You hear the same thing in 「ホテル」, hotel. Meaning shift. In Japanese this word only means a swimming pool. The game you play with cues and balls is called 「ビリヤード」, billiards. And a pool of water on the ground after rain is a different word again. The verb that goes with it is 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, to swim: 「プールで泳ぐ|プールでおよぐ」, swim at the pool, with 「で」 for the place of the action. A cultural note. Most Japanese elementary and junior high schools have an outdoor pool, and swimming lessons are a normal part of summer classes. Many towns also run cheap public pools that open in July and August. Recap: the English word pool with a long vowel and an l turned into r, and only for swimming." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you visit a public pool in Japan, a few rules may surprise you. Many pools require a swim cap, so bring one or buy one at the counter. Swimsuits are 「水着|みずぎ」, water clothes. Tattoos are often not allowed, or must be covered, though rules vary. At many public pools a whistle blows once an hour, and everyone has to get out for a short rest, called 「休憩|きゅうけい」. To get in the water, say 「プールに入る|プールにはいる」, enter the pool. Swimming laps is just 「泳ぐ|およぐ」. Many big cities also have huge summer water parks with slides and wave pools, packed during the school holidays. And remember, a puddle on the street after rain is not this word at all. That's a 「水たまり|みずたまり」, a gathering of water. So the pool means a place built for swimming, with rules, caps and hourly breaks." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a little memory scene. Picture a giant pulling a rope attached to a swimming pool. He groans, pull, pull, and drags the whole pool, water sloshing, across the playground and into the schoolyard. The kids cheer and jump straight in. The pulling sound reminds you of the word, with its long vowel stretched out like the long rope, shown by the dash. Then picture the kids' teacher calling out from the side, 「プールで泳ぎましょう|プールでおよぎましょう」, let's swim in the pool. Notice she uses 「で」, because the pool is the place where the swimming happens. A giant, a long rope, a long vowel, and a schoolyard full of splashing kids." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English pool, with a long vowel mark and the l turned into 「ル」. In Japanese it only means a swimming pool, so swim there with 「プールで泳ぐ|プールでおよぐ」.",
      "Public pools often need a swim cap, may ban tattoos, and clear everyone out hourly for a 「休憩|きゅうけい」. A street puddle is a 「水たまり|みずたまり」, not this word.",
      "Picture a giant pulling a pool across a schoolyard on a long rope, pull, pull, the rope like the long vowel. The teacher calls 「プールで泳ぎましょう|プールでおよぎましょう」, using 「で」 for the place."
    ],
    ja: "なつは[プール]で{泳ぎます|およぎます}。",
    en: "In summer I swim in the pool." },
  "えいがかん|映画館": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first two you already know together as 「映画|えいが」, movie. 「映|えい」 means to reflect or project. It has the sun part on the left, light shining on something. 「画|が」 means a picture. It is the same kanji in 「漫画|まんが」, comics. So a movie is a projected picture. Then comes 「館|かん」, a large building or hall. Its left side is the food part, because the kanji first meant a guesthouse where travelers were fed. That ending gives you a whole family of public buildings you have met. 「図書館|としょかん」, the library, a hall of books. 「美術館|びじゅつかん」, the art museum, a hall of fine art. And 「旅館|りょかん」, a Japanese inn, a hall for travelers. So here, a hall for projected pictures. A cultural note. Movie tickets in Japan are fairly expensive, so many theaters offer cheaper days, often a ladies' day or a discount on the first day of each month. Recap: projected picture plus hall." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Going to the movies in Japan has its own rhythm. You usually choose your seat when you buy your ticket, either online or at a machine, by tapping the seat map. The seat is a 「席|せき」. Many people buy tickets in advance, saying 「チケットを予約しました|チケットをよやくしました」. Popcorn and drinks are popular, often in flavors like caramel or even soy sauce and butter. Japanese audiences are famously quiet. They rarely talk or laugh loudly, and many stay seated until the end credits finish and the lights come on. Leaving early during the credits is not rude, but you will notice how many people stay. Foreign films are usually shown with Japanese subtitles, called 「字幕|じまく」, while children's films often use dubbing, 「吹き替え|ふきかえ」. So when buying a ticket for a foreign film, check whether it's subtitled or dubbed." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first two kanji each have more to offer. 「映|えい」, to project or reflect, becomes the native verb 「映る|うつる」, to be reflected or shown. A face reflected in a mirror, or a picture showing on a screen, uses this verb. Showing a film in a theater is 「上映|じょうえい」, and you'll see the word on posters with the show times, the 「上映時間|じょうえいじかん」. The second kanji, 「画|が」, picture, gives 「画面|がめん」, a screen, such as a phone or computer screen. It also gives 「動画|どうが」, moving pictures, the everyday word for online videos. And here's a twist. The same kanji has another reading, 「かく」, in 「計画|けいかく」, a plan, where the idea is drawing lines or dividing things up. So this kanji covers screens, videos and plans. Combine it all and you're in a hall of projected pictures." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory scene for the sound. Picture an old man named A, sitting in the very front row of a cinema. His name is pronounced like the letter A. Every time the movie gets exciting, he shouts, Ay, ga, Ay, ga, waving his popcorn bucket. Behind him, the whole audience turns and says, can you please be quiet. The usher walks over and gently tells him this is a hall, a 「館|かん」, and halls are for quiet watching. So: A shouting ga, inside the hall, the 「えいがかん」. And because Japanese audiences really do sit in silence, our noisy old A stands out even more. Shouting old man, popcorn, hush, a movie theater." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「映画|えいが」, a projected picture or movie, plus 「館|かん」, a large hall, like 「図書館|としょかん」 and 「旅館|りょかん」. A hall for projected pictures.",
      "You pick your 「席|せき」 when buying a ticket, and audiences stay quiet, often through the credits. Check whether a foreign film is 「字幕|じまく」, subtitled, or 「吹き替え|ふきかえ」, dubbed.",
      "「映|えい」 becomes 「映る|うつる」, to be reflected or shown, and gives 「上映|じょうえい」, screening. 「画|が」 gives 「画面|がめん」, a screen, and 「動画|どうが」, online videos.",
      "Picture an old man named A in the front row shouting Ay, ga, until the usher reminds him this is a 「館|かん」, a hall for quiet watching. A shouting ga in the hall."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と[映画館|えいがかん]に{行きました|いきました}。",
    en: "I went to the movie theater with a friend." },
  "りょかん|旅館": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「旅|りょ」, travel or a journey. You know it from 「旅行|りょこう」, a trip, and with its native reading it is 「旅|たび」, a journey, a slightly poetic word. The second is 「館|かん」, a hall or building, the same ending as 「図書館|としょかん」, 「美術館|びじゅつかん」 and 「映画館|えいがかん」. So a hall for travelers. But this word means something more specific than a hotel. A 「旅館|りょかん」 is a traditional Japanese inn. You take off your shoes at the entrance. Rooms have tatami mats, and staff lay out futons for you in the evening. Guests wear a light cotton robe called a 「浴衣|ゆかた」, and many inns have a large shared bath, often a hot spring, an 「温泉|おんせん」. Dinner is usually a long, beautiful set meal of many small dishes. A Western style place is a 「ホテル」. The verb for staying overnight is 「泊まる|とまる」: 「旅館に泊まる|りょかんにとまる」. Recap: travel plus hall, the traditional inn." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A first night at a Japanese inn comes with a few customs worth knowing. Inns usually sell stays as 「一泊二食付き|いっぱくにしょくつき」, one night with two meals, dinner and breakfast included. A staff member who looks after your room is often called a 「仲居さん|なかいさん」. You take off your shoes at the entrance and change into slippers, but you take the slippers off too before stepping onto the tatami. When you put on your cotton robe, wrap the left side over the right. Right over left is how the dead are dressed for a funeral, so staff may kindly fix it for you. Dinner may be served in your room or a dining hall, and while you eat, staff may come in and lay out the futons. At the end, you might say 「お世話になりました|おせわになりました」, thank you for taking care of me." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji, 「旅|りょ」, has a real and rather military history. Its left part is a fluttering flag or banner, the same piece seen in 「族|ぞく」, as in 「家族|かぞく」, family. Under and beside the flag, the old forms showed people gathered together. So the original picture was a group of people marching under a banner, a band of soldiers on the move. From there the meaning widened to traveling in general. The second kanji, 「館|かん」, has the food part on the left, because, as you heard, it once meant a lodging house where guests were fed. The right side, 「官|かん」, gives the sound and also appears in 「警官|けいかん」, a police officer. So the full picture is a group traveling under a flag, arriving at a house that feeds guests. Not a bad description of a tour group checking in for dinner." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The travel kanji has a native reading, 「たび」, and it builds some lovely words. A traveler is a 「旅人|たびびと」, a travel person, with the second part changing from 「ひと」 to 「びと」. A solo trip is a 「一人旅|ひとりたび」, very popular with young people. Now for places to stay. A 「ホテル」 is Western style. A 「民宿|みんしゅく」 is a small family run guesthouse, more homely and cheaper than an inn, often with shared bathrooms. A 「ゲストハウス」 is a budget hostel. The general old word for a place to stay is 「宿|やど」, and you'll see it in old town names and on signs. There's also the capsule hotel, the 「カプセルホテル」, a tiny sleeping pod. So when planning a trip, you'll pick from inns, guesthouses, hotels and capsules, and the traditional inn is the one with tatami, robes and a big dinner." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「旅|りょ」, travel, plus 「館|かん」, hall: a traditional Japanese inn with tatami, futons, 「浴衣|ゆかた」 robes, and often an 「温泉|おんせん」. You stay there with 「泊まる|とまる」.",
      "Stays often include two meals, 「一泊二食付き|いっぱくにしょくつき」, and slippers come off before tatami. Wrap your robe left over right, since right over left is for funerals.",
      "「旅|りょ」 originally showed people marching under a banner, then widened to travel. 「館|かん」 has the food piece, from a lodging house that fed its guests.",
      "Read 「たび」, the travel kanji gives 「旅人|たびびと」 and 「一人旅|ひとりたび」. Other stays include the 「民宿|みんしゅく」 guesthouse and 「カプセルホテル」, but the inn has tatami and a big dinner."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}の[旅館|りょかん]に{泊まりました|とまりました}。",
    en: "I stayed at a Japanese inn in Kyoto." },
  "にわ|庭": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here the kanji uses its native reading, a Japanese word that existed long before writing arrived. The kanji has the building part on top and to the left, a roof with one side open, and the rest sits underneath it. A way to remember it: the open space that belongs to a house. The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji is 「てい」, and you hear it in 「日本庭園|にほんていえん」, a formal Japanese garden. Notice that this word is about the yard or garden attached to a home or temple, not a vegetable plot or a public park. A park is 「公園|こうえん」. A cultural note. Japanese gardens are famous. Some temples in Kyoto have dry rock gardens, where raked white gravel stands for water and stones stand for islands or mountains. Many city homes have only a tiny garden, but people still fit in a small tree, a few stones and some moss. A memory hook, clearly just a hook: it sounds a bit like knee wah, so picture plants growing knee high in your garden. Recap: the open space by a house, a garden or yard." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The roof with one open side, wrapping the top and left of 「庭|にわ」, is a common component that marks buildings and spaces. You know it from 「店|みせ」, a shop, and 「広い|ひろい」, spacious, and it also appears in 「座る|すわる」, to sit, with two people sitting on the ground under the roof. Inside 「庭|にわ」 sits a smaller kanji, 「廷|てい」. It means a royal court, the open courtyard where officials gathered before a ruler, and it still appears in 「法廷|ほうてい」, a courtroom. It also gives this kanji its Chinese-origin reading. So the garden kanji is a courtyard under the shelter of a building, the open space at the heart of a home. You can almost see a palace yard, shrunk down to the patch of moss behind a Japanese house. Roof, courtyard, and the grass in between." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading, 「てい」, this kanji makes some surprising words. The biggest is 「家庭|かてい」, home plus garden, which means a household or a family home. Home cooking is 「家庭料理|かていりょうり」, the comforting food a family eats every day. School subjects even include 「家庭科|かていか」, home economics, where boys and girls learn to cook and sew. A schoolyard or sports ground is a 「校庭|こうてい」, a school garden. And a formal landscaped garden is a 「庭園|ていえん」. With the native reading, there's 「中庭|なかにわ」, an inner courtyard, common in old townhouses, temples and some schools, and 「裏庭|うらにわ」, a backyard, a garden behind the house. So the idea of a garden spreads out into family life, school life, and the quiet courtyards of temples. One small kanji, a whole world of open spaces." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses its native reading, and the kanji is a roof open on one side over a space: the yard or garden of a home or temple. A park is a 「公園|こうえん」, and a formal garden is 「日本庭園|にほんていえん」.",
      "The open roof piece also marks 「店|みせ」 and 「広い|ひろい」, and inside sits 「廷|てい」, a royal courtyard, which gives the reading 「てい」. A courtyard sheltered by a building.",
      "Read 「てい」, it gives 「家庭|かてい」, household, 「家庭料理|かていりょうり」, home cooking, and 「校庭|こうてい」, a schoolyard. Natively there are 「中庭|なかにわ」 and 「裏庭|うらにわ」."
    ],
    ja: "うちの[庭|にわ]にねこがいます。",
    en: "There is a cat in our garden." },
  "ボランティア|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English, volunteer. Watch the squeeze. Japanese has no v sound, so the v becomes a b, giving 「ボ」. The l becomes the Japanese r sound in 「ラン」. Then the teer part needs a sound that Japanese did not traditionally have. The plain 「チ」 would sound like chee, so a small 「ィ」 is added to 「テ」 to make a tee sound, 「ティ」. The ending r is dropped and becomes 「ア」. Meaning shift. In English, volunteer is mainly the person. In Japanese it is very often the activity itself, so you say 「ボランティアをする」, to do volunteering. You can also say 「ボランティア活動|ボランティアかつどう」, volunteer activities. A cultural note. The year 1995, after the great Kobe earthquake, is often called the first year of volunteering in Japan, because so many ordinary people came to help. Since then, volunteering after disasters has become a familiar part of Japanese life. Recap: v to b, a small vowel trick for tee, and it names the activity." },
      { title: "Sound family", text: "This word uses two sound tricks you'll meet in many loanwords. First, the v sound turns into b. You see it in 「バイオリン」, violin, 「ビデオ」, video, and 「テレビ」, television. Some writers use a special spelling with a small vowel added to 「ヴ」 for the v sound, but most everyday words just use the b row. Second, the little 「ィ」 added to 「テ」 makes a clean tee sound that older Japanese did not have. You'll hear it in 「パーティー」, party, 「アイスティー」, iced tea, and 「ティッシュ」, a tissue. The same trick works with d, as in 「ディズニー」, Disney. Once you know these two habits, you can guess how lots of English words will look in katakana. Try it: an English word with v becomes b, and an English tee sound gets the little 「ィ」 helper." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "People talk about many kinds of volunteering. There's 「災害ボランティア|さいがいボランティア」, disaster volunteering, helping clean up after floods or earthquakes. There's beach and park cleanup. Many university students volunteer as interpreters at big events or help foreign visitors. To join, the verb is 「参加する|さんかする」, to take part: 「ボランティアに参加しました|ボランティアにさんかしました」. People also use the word with 「で」 to mean unpaid, as in 「ボランティアで教えています|ボランティアでおしえています」, I teach as a volunteer, for free. In everyday speech, someone who keeps doing favors without any reward may joke, 「これじゃボランティアだよ」, this is basically volunteer work, meaning they aren't being paid enough. So the word covers both proud community service and a small complaint about unpaid effort." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English volunteer, the v becomes b and a small 「ィ」 makes a tee sound. In Japanese it often names the activity, so you say 「ボランティアをする」.",
      "It shows two loanword habits: v turns into b, as in 「バイオリン」 and 「テレビ」, and a small 「ィ」 after 「テ」 makes tee, as in 「パーティー」.",
      "Join with 「ボランティアに参加しました|ボランティアにさんかしました」, and with 「で」 it means unpaid. Someone joking 「これじゃボランティアだよ」 is complaining they are not paid enough."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}に[ボランティア]をします。",
    en: "I do volunteer work on weekends." },
  "かつどう|活動": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「活|かつ」, lively, active, full of life. It has the water part on the left, like a flowing stream. You already know it in 「生活|せいかつ」, daily life, literally living and being lively. The second is 「動|どう」, to move. A way to remember its shape: on the left is 「重い|おもい」, heavy, and on the right is 「力|ちから」, strength, so you use strength to move something heavy. You know it from 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, the zoo, where 「動物|どうぶつ」 is a moving thing, an animal. Together, lively moving, activity. It is used for organized activities, rarely for something small like reading a book. School club activities are called 「部活動|ぶかつどう」, usually shortened to 「部活|ぶかつ」, a huge part of Japanese school life. Japanese also loves this kind of shortcut. Job hunting is 「就活|しゅうかつ」, a job activity. Recap: lively plus move, organized activity." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji has more to give. 「活|かつ」, lively, has spawned a fun modern pattern of short words for organized efforts, building on the job hunting word. 「婚活|こんかつ」 is marriage hunting, going to events to find a partner. 「朝活|あさかつ」 is morning activities, like studying or exercising before work. Grammar students meet another member: 「活用|かつよう」, conjugation, how verbs change their endings. The second kanji, 「動|どう」, move, becomes the verb 「動く|うごく」, to move or to work, as in 「このパソコンは動かない|このパソコンはうごかない」, this computer won't work. It also gives 「運動|うんどう」, exercise, and 「自動|じどう」, automatic, as in 「自動ドア|じどうドア」, automatic doors. So 「活|かつ」 brings energy, 「動|どう」 brings motion, and you can find them both all over Japanese life." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This noun easily becomes a verb with 「する」. So 「活動する|かつどうする」 means to be active or to carry out activities, and it's common for groups and organizations. For example, 「このサークルは毎週活動しています|このサークルはまいしゅうかつどうしています」, this club meets every week. Notice that in Japanese, a club being active simply means it holds its meetings. Animals and volcanoes can be active too. A volcano that might erupt is an active volcano, 「活火山|かっかざん」. To describe a person who is energetic and always out doing things, add 「的|てき」 to make an adjective: 「活動的な人|かつどうてきなひと」, an active person. Be careful, though. For a person being busy in a single small way, like reading, this word sounds too big. Save it for organized, ongoing efforts, or for describing someone's general energy." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "School club activities shape Japanese teenage life far more than in many countries. Clubs practice almost every day after school, and sports clubs often train on weekends and during holidays too. The relationship between older students, 「先輩|せんぱい」, and younger ones, 「後輩|こうはい」, is important. Younger members often greet seniors politely and help with chores like collecting balls. The most famous club goal is the national high school baseball tournament, held at a stadium called 「甲子園|こうしえん」. Its name is so famous it stands for the whole tournament, and the games are shown on national TV every summer. There are also culture clubs, like brass band, art and tea ceremony. Students who don't join any club are jokingly called the 「帰宅部|きたくぶ」, the go home club. So when a Japanese friend mentions their old club, you're hearing about a big part of their youth." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「活|かつ」, lively, with the water piece, plus 「動|どう」, heavy plus strength, move: organized activity. School clubs are 「部活動|ぶかつどう」, or 「部活|ぶかつ」, and job hunting is 「就活|しゅうかつ」.",
      "「活|かつ」 spawned short words like 「婚活|こんかつ」 and 「朝活|あさかつ」, while 「動|どう」 gives 「動く|うごく」, 「運動|うんどう」, exercise, and 「自動|じどう」, automatic.",
      "With 「する」, it means a group is active or meets, as in 「毎週活動しています|まいしゅうかつどうしています」. For an energetic person, say 「活動的な人|かつどうてきなひと」, but not for small single acts.",
      "School clubs practice almost daily, with strong 「先輩|せんぱい」 and 「後輩|こうはい」 roles, and baseball dreams of 「甲子園|こうしえん」. Students in no club are the 「帰宅部|きたくぶ」, the go home club."
    ],
    ja: "ボランティア[活動|かつどう]をしています。",
    en: "I do volunteer activities." },
  "けいけん|経験": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「経|けい」. Its left side is the thread part, and it originally meant the long threads stretched on a loom, the ones everything else is woven through. From there it came to mean passing through, and also managing. You know it from 「経済|けいざい」, economics, from the very first lesson. The second is 「験|けん」, to test or check. Its left side is the horse part. You know it from 「試験|しけん」, an exam. So, things you have passed through and been tested by. That is experience. The key pattern is 「経験がある|けいけんがある」, to have experience. For example, work experience, or experience living abroad. This links to a grammar point you know: verb in the past plus 「ことがある」 means you have done something before. The noun says it in one word. In job interviews you will hear this a lot, and job ads often say experience is not needed, using this word. Recap: passed through and tested, experience." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "Japanese has two words that English translates as experience, and they're not the same. This word is experience you have built up, often over time, like work experience or life experience. The other is 「体験|たいけん」, body plus test, which is a direct, hands-on experience of something, often a one time try. Tourist programs offer a 「着物体験|きものたいけん」, trying on a kimono, or a 「料理体験|りょうりたいけん」, a cooking experience. You wouldn't use this word for those. A good test: if it's about doing something yourself once, to see what it feels like, use 「体験|たいけん」. If it's something that has become part of your background, use this word. Both share the second kanji, testing. One is testing with your body, the other is passing through and being tested." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「経|けい」, has a native reading you'll meet with time: 「経つ|たつ」, for time to pass. So 「三年経ちました|さんねんたちました」 means three years have passed. That fits the threads image. Time runs through your life like the long threads on a loom. It also appears in 「神経|しんけい」, nerves, the threads of the body, as in feeling nervous. The second kanji, 「験|けん」, test, gives you 「実験|じっけん」, an experiment, from real plus test. And of course, 「受験|じゅけん」, taking entrance exams, a huge topic for Japanese students. Students preparing for those exams are 「受験生|じゅけんせい」. So this word sits at the crossroads of threads that pass through time and the tests you face along the way. Put them together and you get the sum of what you've lived through." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In job ads, you'll see 「経験者|けいけんしゃ」, an experienced person, and 「未経験|みけいけん」, no experience. Ads that welcome beginners often say that 「未経験|みけいけん」 is fine. In an interview, you might hear 「アルバイトの経験はありますか|アルバイトのけいけんはありますか」, do you have part time job experience. A modest answer is 「少しあります|すこしあります」, I have a little. It also works for big life moments. When something was memorable, Japanese people often say 「いい経験になりました|いいけいけんになりました」, it was a good experience. People use that line even after something tough, as a gracious way of saying they learned from it. And a first time thing is 「初めての経験|はじめてのけいけん」. So remember that warm phrase for a hard day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「経|けい」, loom threads, passing through, plus 「験|けん」, test: what you have passed through and been tested by. The key pattern is 「経験がある|けいけんがある」, to have experience.",
      "This word is built-up background experience, while 「体験|たいけん」 is a hands-on, often one time try, like a 「着物体験|きものたいけん」. Both share the testing kanji.",
      "「経|けい」 gives 「経つ|たつ」, for time to pass, and 「神経|しんけい」, nerves, while 「験|けん」 gives 「実験|じっけん」 and 「受験|じゅけん」, entrance exams.",
      "Job ads say 「未経験|みけいけん」 is fine for beginners. After something memorable, even something tough, people graciously say 「いい経験になりました|いいけいけんになりました」."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}で{働いた|はたらいた}[経験|けいけん]があります。",
    en: "I have experience working in Japan." },
  "しゅうかん|習慣": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「習|しゅう」, to learn by practice. With its native reading it is the verb 「習う|ならう」, to learn from someone, and you know it in 「練習|れんしゅう」, practice. Look at the top. It is 「羽|はね」, feathers or wings. A way to remember it: a young bird flapping its wings again and again until it can fly. The second is 「慣|かん」, to get used to something. Its left side is the heart part, and with its native reading it gives 「慣れる|なれる」, to get used to. So, something practiced so often that you are used to it. This word covers both a personal habit, like jogging every morning, and a custom shared by a whole society. Some Japanese customs: taking your shoes off indoors, saying 「いただきます」 before meals, bowing when you greet people, and giving 「お土産|おみやげ」 after a trip. Recap: learned by practice plus got used to, a habit or custom." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "English uses habit for many things, but Japanese gives you choices. This word is a steady pattern, good or neutral, and it can belong to a person or to a whole society. A personal quirk or a bad little habit is usually 「癖|くせ」. Biting your nails, touching your hair, or saying um too much are all 「癖|くせ」. Even a messy hair curl in the morning is a 「寝癖|ねぐせ」, a sleep habit. So 「早起きの習慣|はやおきのしゅうかん」 is a habit of getting up early, but a strange way of talking is a 「くせ」. There's also a reversed word, 「慣習|かんしゅう」, the same two kanji swapped. It means an established custom or convention, sounding more formal and used for social or legal traditions. For everyday conversation, this word and 「癖|くせ」 are the two you need." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here are some handy patterns. To say you have a habit of doing something, put the plain verb before it and add 「がある」: 「毎朝走る習慣があります|まいあさはしるしゅうかんがあります」, I have a habit of running every morning. To say something has become a habit, use 「になる」: 「日記を書くのが習慣になりました|にっきをかくのがしゅうかんになりました」, writing a diary has become a habit. It pairs well with the verb from its second kanji, 「慣れる|なれる」, to get used to. The thing you get used to takes 「に」: 「日本の習慣に慣れました|にほんのしゅうかんになれました」, I've gotten used to Japanese customs. That sentence even uses the kanji twice. You might also hear 「〜ことにしている」, I make it a rule to, which describes a habit you chose on purpose. Practice these until they become, well, a habit." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory scene for the sound. Picture a young bird learning to fly, flapping its feathered wings, just like the wings on top of the first kanji. It practices every single morning on a tall tree. And every morning before it starts, it shouts, shoo, shoo, to clear the other birds off the branch. It's a habit. One day it lands on a can, a tin can, and kicks it off the branch, can, can, can, down to the ground. Shoo, then can. That's the sound, 「しゅうかん」. The bird has repeated it so many times that the whole forest is used to it. Wings that practice, a heart that gets used to it, shoo and can every morning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「習|しゅう」, learn by practice, topped with wings like a bird practicing flight, plus 「慣|かん」, get used to. Something practiced until it is natural: a personal habit or a social custom.",
      "This word is a steady habit or custom, while a personal quirk or bad habit is 「癖|くせ」, like bed hair, 「寝癖|ねぐせ」. The swapped 「慣習|かんしゅう」 is a formal convention.",
      "Say 「毎朝走る習慣があります|まいあさはしるしゅうかんがあります」 for a habit you have, and use 「になる」 for one that formed. Pair it with 「慣れる|なれる」, as in 「日本の習慣に慣れました|にほんのしゅうかんになれました」.",
      "Picture a young bird that shouts shoo every morning, then kicks a tin can off the branch, can, can. It repeats this so often that the whole forest is used to it."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[習慣|しゅうかん]はおもしろいです。",
    en: "Japanese customs are interesting." },
  "しめきり|締め切り": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two parts, both from native verbs, so both use native readings. The first is 「締め|しめ」, from 「締める|しめる」, to tighten, fasten or close up. It has the thread part on the left, like tying a cord tight. The second is 「切り|きり」, from 「切る|きる」, to cut. You know that kanji in 「切符|きっぷ」, a ticket, and in 「切手|きって」, a stamp. So, the point where things are closed up and cut off. After this moment, no more entries. It is used for homework, reports, job applications and work projects. Useful patterns. 「締め切りはいつですか|しめきりはいつですか」, when is the deadline. And 「締め切りを守る|しめきりをまもる」, to keep a deadline, which uses the same verb as 「約束を守る|やくそくをまもる」, to keep a promise. Writers and comic artists in Japan are famous for racing against their deadlines, and editors sometimes wait at their homes for the pages. Recap: close up plus cut off, the deadline." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Deadlines come with their own verbs. To make it in time, use 「間に合う|まにあう」: 「締め切りに間に合いました|しめきりにまにあいました」, I made the deadline. Missing it is passing it, 「過ぎる|すぎる」: 「締め切りを過ぎてしまいました|しめきりをすぎてしまいました」, the deadline has gone by, with 「てしまう」 adding a sense of regret. If you're lucky, the deadline gets pushed back, 「締め切りが延びる|しめきりがのびる」, the deadline stretches. On posters and forms, you'll often see the kanji written without the little 「め」 and 「り」, as 「締切|しめきり」, and the reading is the same. Stores use the word for orders too. A bakery might post a notice saying orders close on a certain date. And when an event is full, the sign may say applications are closed using the verb form, 「締め切りました|しめきりました」. So deadlines can be met, missed, stretched, or simply closed." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「締める|しめる」, to tighten or fasten, shows up in daily life. You tighten a necktie, 「ネクタイを締める|ネクタイをしめる」, and fasten a seat belt, 「シートベルトを締める|シートベルトをしめる」. Locking up the house at night is 「戸締まり|とじまり」, the door tightening. Careful, though. There's a different 「しめる」 with a different kanji, 「閉める|しめる」, to close, as in closing a door or window. Both sound the same. The thread kanji is for tightening and fastening, while the door kanji, with the gate shape, is for shutting. The second half, 「切る|きる」, to cut, also ends many words about stopping. 「売り切れ|うりきれ」 is sold out, everything cut off. And 「区切る|くぎる」 is to divide up into sections. So this word is fastened shut and cut off. No more entries, the line is closed." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory scene. Picture a tired comic artist at her desk at three in the morning. Behind her stands a stern editor holding a giant pair of scissors and a rope. The editor says, she may keep drawing until the clock strikes six. Then I will tie up the pages with this rope, tight, tight, and cut the rope with these scissors, snip. And that is the end. The tightening rope is 「締め|しめ」, and the snip of the scissors is 「切り|きり」. Picture her shouting shee, may, keep drawing, a sound hook for the start of the word. As the clock ticks toward six, she draws faster and faster. Tighten, snip, the deadline." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「締め|しめ」, from 「締める|しめる」, to tighten, plus 「切り|きり」, from 「切る|きる」, to cut: closed up and cut off. Keep one with 「締め切りを守る|しめきりをまもる」, like keeping a promise.",
      "You make it with 「間に合う|まにあう」, miss it with 「過ぎる|すぎる」, and hope it 「延びる|のびる」. Forms often write it 「締切|しめきり」, and 「締め切りました|しめきりました」 means applications are closed.",
      "「締める|しめる」 is for tightening, like a necktie, while same-sounding 「閉める|しめる」, with the gate shape, is for shutting doors. 「切る|きる」 ends stop words like 「売り切れ|うりきれ」, sold out.",
      "Picture an editor tying a comic artist's pages with a rope, tight, then snipping it with scissors at six. The tightening rope is 「締め|しめ」 and the snip is 「切り|きり」."
    ],
    ja: "[締め切り|しめきり]はあしたです。",
    en: "The deadline is tomorrow." },
  "よてい|予定": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「予|よ」, beforehand, in advance. It is in a nice little family you know. 「予約|よやく」, a reservation, a promise made in advance. And 「天気予報|てんきよほう」, the weather forecast, a report made in advance. The second is 「定|てい」, to fix or decide. It has the roof part on top, like something settled firmly in place. So, something fixed in advance. That is a plan or a schedule. Two very useful patterns. First, 「予定がある|よていがある」, to have plans. If someone invites you and you are busy, you can say 「その日は予定があります|そのひはよていがあります」, I have plans that day. Second, a plain verb plus 「予定です|よていです」 means I plan to, or it is scheduled. For example, 「来年日本に行く予定です|らいねんにほんにいくよていです」, I plan to go to Japan next year. It sounds a bit firmer than just wanting to. Recap: in advance plus fixed, a plan." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "Three words can sound like plans in English, but they work differently. This word is about something arranged or scheduled, often already on the calendar. 「計画|けいかく」 is a plan you designed, often detailed and bigger, like a trip plan or a business project. You make one with 「立てる|たてる」: 「旅行の計画を立てる|りょこうのけいかくをたてる」, to plan a trip. And 「つもり」, which you learned with verbs, is your personal intention, what you mean to do. 「行くつもりです|いくつもりです」 means I intend to go. Compare it with 「行く予定です|いくよていです」, I'm scheduled to go, which sounds more settled, as if arrangements are made. So intention is in your head, a schedule is in your calendar, and a plan is something you've designed. Pick whichever matches how fixed things really are." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「定|てい」, fixed, turns up all over everyday Japan. At a restaurant, a 「定食|ていしょく」 is a set meal, a fixed lineup of rice, soup, a main dish and pickles. Shop doors often show a 「定休日|ていきゅうび」, the regular closing day, a fixed day off each week. A commuter pass is a 「定期券|ていきけん」, a fixed period ticket. And with the native verb 「決める|きめる」, you get 「決定|けってい」, a decision, something decided and fixed. Notice that in every case, something is settled and won't move. That idea carries straight into this word. Things fixed in advance. Next time you order the fixed lunch set on a weekday, remember your plan was fixed in advance too." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how people use this word day to day. Things going as planned is 「予定通り|よていどおり」, as scheduled: 「電車は予定通りに着きました|でんしゃはよていどおりにつきました」, the train arrived on schedule. When something gets added to your calendar, people say 「予定が入る|よていがはいる」, a plan comes in. And to keep a day free for someone, you can say 「その日は空けておきます|そのひはあけておきます」, I'll keep that day open. A typical exchange between friends: 「週末、予定ある|しゅうまつ、よていある」, got plans this weekend. 「特にないよ|とくにないよ」, nothing in particular. Then comes the invitation. Japanese people also say 「予定が合わない|よていがあわない」, our schedules don't match, when it's hard to find a time. So this one word handles calendars, invitations and polite excuses all at once." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「予|よ」, in advance, as in 「予約|よやく」, plus 「定|てい」, fixed: something fixed in advance. Say 「予定があります|よていがあります」 for having plans, or verb plus 「予定です|よていです」 for I plan to.",
      "This word is something already scheduled, 「計画|けいかく」 is a plan you design, and 「つもり」 is a personal intention. 「行く予定です|いくよていです」 sounds more settled than 「行くつもりです|いくつもりです」.",
      "The fixed kanji appears in 「定食|ていしょく」, a set meal, 「定休日|ていきゅうび」, a regular closing day, and 「決定|けってい」, a decision. In every case, something is settled.",
      "「予定通り|よていどおり」 means as scheduled, and 「予定が入る|よていがはいる」 means a plan comes up. When it is hard to meet, people say 「予定が合わない|よていがあわない」."
    ],
    ja: "{週末|しゅうまつ}の[予定|よてい]はなんですか。",
    en: "What are your plans for the weekend?" },
  "そつぎょうしき|卒業式": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first two make 「卒業|そつぎょう」, graduation. 「卒|そつ」 means to finish, and 「業|ぎょう」 means work or studies. You know 「業|ぎょう」 from 「授業|じゅぎょう」, a class, literally giving studies. So finishing your studies. Then comes 「式|しき」, a ceremony. That ending makes other ceremony words too, like 「結婚式|けっこんしき」, a wedding. And the opposite of this one is 「入学式|にゅうがくしき」, the entrance ceremony when you start school. A cultural note. The Japanese school year starts in April, so graduation ceremonies happen in March, just as the cherry blossoms are about to bloom. They are formal and emotional. Students sing together, receive their diplomas one by one, and many cry. At university graduations, many women wear a 「袴|はかま」, a traditional pleated skirt over a kimono, which makes the day very colorful. Recap: finish studies plus ceremony, the graduation ceremony." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Graduation ceremonies come with sweet traditions. In schools where boys wear a black uniform with a stand-up collar, a girl may ask a boy she likes for the second button from the top of his jacket. It's called the 「第二ボタン|だいにボタン」, the second button, because it sits closest to the heart. A popular boy might end the day with no buttons left at all. Students also sign each other's albums and take endless photos under the cherry trees. Each student goes up to receive a diploma, the 「卒業証書|そつぎょうしょうしょ」, bowing to the principal. Classic songs bring everyone to tears, and favorite pop songs about parting become graduation anthems every spring. Afterward, many students hold one last class party. In Japan, a graduation feels like the end of a chapter written together." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ceremony kanji, 「式|しき」, closes many life event words. You know entrance and graduation ceremonies. There's also 「成人式|せいじんしき」, the coming of age ceremony held each January for young adults, where many women wear bright long-sleeved kimono. A funeral is 「葬式|そうしき」, usually said politely as 「お葬式|おそうしき」. Then the graduation part itself, 「卒業|そつぎょう」, becomes a verb with 「する」, and the school takes 「を」: 「大学を卒業しました|だいがくをそつぎょうしました」, I graduated from college. That 「を」 is the same one used with leaving places, which feels natural, because you are leaving school. Japanese also uses graduating playfully. Quitting a habit, like smoking, can be 「タバコを卒業する|タバコをそつぎょうする」. And when a member leaves a pop idol group, it is announced as their graduation. So the word means moving on, from school or from anything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「卒業|そつぎょう」, finishing studies, plus 「式|しき」, ceremony; the opposite is 「入学式|にゅうがくしき」. Since the school year starts in April, these emotional ceremonies happen in March.",
      "A girl may ask a boy she likes for his 「第二ボタン|だいにボタン」, the second button, closest to the heart. Each student bows to receive a 「卒業証書|そつぎょうしょうしょ」, a diploma.",
      "「式|しき」 closes many life events, like 「成人式|せいじんしき」 and 「お葬式|おそうしき」. You graduate with 「を」, and it is used playfully for quitting things, as in 「タバコを卒業する|タバコをそつぎょうする」."
    ],
    ja: "きょうは[卒業式|そつぎょうしき]です。",
    en: "Today is the graduation ceremony." },
  "けっこんしき|結婚式": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first two make 「結婚|けっこん」, marriage, which you know from 「結婚する|けっこんする」. 「結|けつ」 means to tie or bind, and it has the thread part on the left. Its sound shrinks to 「けっ」 here, a small pause before the next sound. 「婚|こん」 means marriage, and it has the woman part on the left. So two people tied together in marriage. Then comes 「式|しき」, a ceremony, the same ending as 「卒業式|そつぎょうしき」, the graduation ceremony. Notice the difference in use. 「結婚|けっこん」 is the marriage itself. This word is the wedding event. A cultural note. Many Japanese couples have a Western style chapel wedding, while others choose a Shinto ceremony at a shrine, a 「神社|じんじゃ」. Guests do not usually bring gifts from a registry. Instead they bring crisp new bills in a special decorated envelope, often around thirty thousand yen. Then there is a reception with speeches. Recap: tie, marriage, ceremony." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The marriage part, 「結婚する|けっこんする」, takes 「と」 for the partner: 「田中さんと結婚しました|たなかさんとけっこんしました」, I married Tanaka. English speakers sometimes try 「を」 here, but marrying is something two people do together, so 「と」 fits. Watch the te form too. 「結婚しています|けっこんしています」 means I am married, describing your current state. It doesn't mean you're getting married right now. Saying 「結婚しました|けっこんしました」 tells people that you got married, often as news. So someone asking whether you are married should use 「結婚していますか|けっこんしていますか」. The wedding itself is an event, so you have it or hold it, using 「ある」 or 「挙げる|あげる」: 「来月結婚式を挙げます|らいげつけっこんしきをあげます」, we're holding our wedding next month. Partner with 「と」, state with 「ている」, and the ceremony with 「挙げる|あげる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you're invited to a Japanese wedding, a few customs will help. The cash gift is usually in an odd amount, like thirty thousand yen, because even numbers can be split in two, which feels unlucky for a couple. The bills should be new and crisp. Guests who give speeches avoid certain words. Words about cutting, parting or breaking, like 「切れる|きれる」 and 「別れる|わかれる」, are avoided. So are doubled words like 「たびたび」, again and again, because they hint at marrying more than once. After the reception, younger friends often head to an after party, the 「二次会|にじかい」, the second gathering, which is more casual. Guests usually go home with a gift from the couple. A good phrase to say to them is 「ご結婚おめでとうございます|ごけっこんおめでとうございます」, congratulations on your marriage." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The tie kanji, 「結|けつ」, has a native verb, 「結ぶ|むすぶ」, to tie or connect. You tie a ribbon, or a train line connects two cities. The word 「おむすび」, a rice ball, comes from this verb, rice pressed and tied together in your hands. With the Chinese-origin reading, it gives 「結果|けっか」, a result, where things tie up into fruit. The marriage kanji, 「婚|こん」, appears in 「婚約|こんやく」, an engagement, a marriage promise, with the same promise kanji as 「約束|やくそく」. Sadly, it also appears in 「離婚|りこん」, divorce, separating from marriage. And as you may have heard, marriage hunting is 「婚活|こんかつ」. So the wedding sits in a chain of words: tie, promise, marriage, and, hopefully, a happy result." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「結|けつ」, tie, plus 「婚|こん」, marriage, plus 「式|しき」, ceremony: the wedding event, while 「結婚|けっこん」 is the marriage itself. Guests bring crisp new bills instead of registry gifts.",
      "Marry someone with 「と」, as in 「田中さんと結婚しました|たなかさんとけっこんしました」, and 「結婚しています|けっこんしています」 means you are married. Hold the ceremony with 「挙げる|あげる」.",
      "Cash gifts come in odd amounts since even numbers can be split, and speeches avoid words like 「切れる|きれる」 or doubled words. Congratulate with 「ご結婚おめでとうございます|ごけっこんおめでとうございます」.",
      "「結|けつ」 gives 「結ぶ|むすぶ」, to tie, 「おむすび」, and 「結果|けっか」, a result, while 「婚|こん」 gives 「婚約|こんやく」, engagement, and 「離婚|りこん」, divorce."
    ],
    ja: "{来月|らいげつ}{姉|あね}の[結婚式|けっこんしき]があります。",
    en: "My older sister's wedding is next month." },
  "うる|売る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese verb, so the kanji takes its native reading. It is the opposite of 「買う|かう」, to buy, and they make a perfect pair. The kanji's Chinese-origin reading is 「ばい」, and you can see both partners in the word 「売買|ばいばい」, buying and selling. It is a regular u verb, so the te form is 「売って|うって」, with a small pause. To say a store sells something, use the te form plus 「いる」: 「あの店で本を売っています|あのみせでほんをうっています」, they sell books at that store. A neat shape detail. In modern kanji, this exact kanji forms the right side of 「読む|よむ」, to read, next to the words part. A way to remember it: a seller calling out in words, reading off the goods. Related everyday words. 「売り場|うりば」 is a sales floor in a department store, and 「売り切れ|うりきれ」, sold out, is the sad sign you see on a vending machine, with 「切る|きる」, to cut. Recap: the opposite of buy, the right side of read." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has an intransitive partner, 「売れる|うれる」, which means to sell, in the sense of something selling well or selling out. 「この本はよく売れています|このほんはよくうれています」 means this book is selling well. The book does the selling, so it takes 「が」 or 「は」, not 「を」. Compare: 「本屋がこの本を売っています|ほんやがこのほんをうっています」, the bookstore sells this book. Same goods, different focus. A related casual word is 「売れっ子|うれっこ」, a person in high demand, like a busy actor or writer. You'll also hear 「売れ残り|うれのこり」, leftovers that didn't sell. And the store sign that matters most for shoppers, 「売り切れ|うりきれ」, sold out. So the seller sells with this verb, while the goods themselves sell well with 「売れる|うれる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji, 「ばい」, leads to some useful words. A small shop or kiosk in a station, hospital or school is a 「売店|ばいてん」, a selling shop. Business or trade in general is 「商売|しょうばい」, and someone might say their family runs a small business with this word. With the native reading, it combines freely. A sale, with prices cut, is 「安売り|やすうり」, cheap selling. A seller is a 「売り手|うりて」, the selling hand, and a buyer is a 「買い手|かいて」. Department stores mark each section with 「売り場|うりば」, so the shoe section is the 「靴売り場|くつうりば」. And a vendor calling out to customers on the street, or a person selling things from a cart, is part of the same family. So whenever you see this kanji, something is changing hands for money." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Shopping in Japan, you'll often need to ask where something is sold. The question is 「〜はどこで売っていますか|〜はどこでうっていますか」. For example, 「切手はどこで売っていますか|きってはどこでうっていますか」, where do they sell stamps. A clerk might answer 「コンビニで売っていますよ|コンビニでうっていますよ」, they sell them at convenience stores. Notice that Japanese leaves out who is selling. You don't need to say they. And the store itself rarely says we sell this in a sign. Instead you'll see 「販売中|はんばいちゅう」, now on sale, using a more formal word. Another phrase you'll hear at markets and festivals is 「全部売れちゃった|ぜんぶうれちゃった」, everything sold out, said happily by a vendor at the end of a busy day. Asking, answering, and celebrating sold out tables, the verb is always there." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The opposite of 「買う|かう」, buy, together in 「売買|ばいばい」. To say a store sells something, use 「売っています|うっています」, and remember this kanji forms the right side of 「読む|よむ」.",
      "Its intransitive partner 「売れる|うれる」 means something sells well, as in 「よく売れています|よくうれています」. The seller sells with this verb, while the goods sell with 「売れる|うれる」.",
      "Read 「ばい」, it gives 「売店|ばいてん」, a kiosk, and 「商売|しょうばい」, business, while native words include 「安売り|やすうり」 and 「売り場|うりば」. Something is changing hands for money.",
      "Ask 「切手はどこで売っていますか|きってはどこでうっていますか」, without saying who sells. Signs use the formal 「販売中|はんばいちゅう」, and happy vendors say 「全部売れちゃった|ぜんぶうれちゃった」."
    ],
    ja: "あのみせはくつを[売っています|うっています|うる]。",
    en: "That store sells shoes." },
  "おろす|下ろす": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native verb using the native reading of 「下|した」, down, below. You know that kanji from 「下手|へた」, unskilled, and 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, the subway. The verb means to bring something down or lower it. You take luggage down from a shelf, 「荷物を下ろす|にもつをおろす」. And you bring money down out of the bank: 「お金を下ろす|おかねをおろす」, to withdraw cash. A helpful cousin. You learned 「降りる|おりる」, to get off a train or bus. The sounds are close, because these native words are related, going down and bringing down. Notice that this verb takes 「を」 for the thing you are lowering. It is a regular u verb, so the past is 「下ろした|おろした」. A cultural note. Japan still uses a lot of cash, and many people withdraw money not at banks but at ATMs in convenience stores, which are open all night. Recap: down, so bring down, and with money it means withdraw." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb belongs to a family of up and down verbs that are easy to tangle. 「下りる|おりる」 means to go down yourself, like going down stairs or a hill. This verb, 「下ろす|おろす」, is the transitive partner, to bring something else down. There's a second pair with a different kanji. 「降りる|おりる」 is to get off a vehicle, and its partner 「降ろす|おろす」 means to let someone off. So a taxi passenger might say 「駅の前で降ろしてください|えきのまえでおろしてください」, please drop me off in front of the station. In speech, all of these sound similar, and the pattern is the same. The 「りる」 ending means you move yourself. The 「ろす」 ending means you move something or someone else. That pattern, intransitive ending in 「りる」 and transitive in 「ろす」, is worth remembering. Something goes down, or you bring it down." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At the ATM, this verb is the casual word for withdrawing money. The machine buttons usually show a more formal word, 「引き出し|ひきだし」, pulling out, so look for that button. Putting money in is 「預ける|あずける」, to deposit, literally to leave something in someone's care. You'll need your PIN, the 「暗証番号|あんしょうばんごう」, and you may pay a small fee, a 「手数料|てすうりょう」, especially at night or on weekends. Here's a lovely extra meaning. Using something new for the first time, like new shoes or a new shirt, can also use this verb. Something brand new, just worn for the first time, is 「おろしたて」. A friend might say 「この靴、おろしたてなんだ|このくつ、おろしたてなんだ」, these shoes are brand new, first time wearing them. Money from the bank, shoes from the box, both brought down into use." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "In the kitchen, the same sound means to grate. Grated daikon radish is 「大根おろし|だいこんおろし」, the little white heap served with grilled fish or tempura. Grated ginger is 「しょうがおろし」, and the grater itself is an 「おろし金|おろしがね」. The grating verb is usually written in kana or with a different kanji, but it shares the sound and the idea of bringing something down, rubbing it down into small pieces. There's also a business meaning with yet another kanji, wholesale, selling goods down the chain from makers to shops. And of course the cousins you already know, 「降りる|おりる」 for getting off and 「下りる|おりる」 for going down. So this one sound runs from banks to buses to a pile of grated radish. Whenever you hear it, ask what is being brought down." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses 「下|した」, down, to mean bring something down, like luggage from a shelf, or 「お金を下ろす|おかねをおろす」, withdraw cash. It is a cousin of 「降りる|おりる」, to get off.",
      "The 「りる」 verbs mean you move yourself, like 「下りる|おりる」, while 「ろす」 verbs move something else. A taxi passenger says 「駅の前で降ろしてください|えきのまえでおろしてください」.",
      "ATMs label withdrawal 「引き出し|ひきだし」, and depositing is 「預ける|あずける」. Using something new for the first time also uses this verb, so brand new shoes are 「おろしたて」.",
      "The same sound means to grate, as in 「大根おろし|だいこんおろし」 and the grater, an 「おろし金|おろしがね」. Whenever you hear it, ask what is being brought down."
    ],
    ja: "ぎんこうでお{金|かね}を[下ろしました|おろしました|おろす]。",
    en: "I withdrew money at the bank." },
  "かく|描く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native verb, and it sounds exactly like 「書く|かく」, to write, which you learned early on. Japanese uses different kanji to show which kind of marks you are making. 「書く|かく」 is for writing letters and words. This kanji is for pictures. Its left side is the hand part, the same one in 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, a hand moving across paper. So the natural pair is 「絵を描く|えをかく」, to draw or paint a picture. You can also draw a map, 「地図を描く|ちずをかく」. In speech the two verbs sound identical, so context does the work. If you hear about a picture, it is this one. If you hear about a letter or a report, it is writing. Both are regular u verbs that change the same way, so the te form is 「描いて|かいて」. You may also see this kanji read 「えがく」, a more literary word for depicting something. A good related word is 「漫画家|まんがか」, a comic artist, someone whose job is drawing. Recap: same sound as write, but the hand is making pictures." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The right side of 「描|かく」 is 「苗|なえ」, a seedling or young rice plant, grass on top of a field. In this kanji, it mostly carries sound, the Chinese-origin reading 「びょう」. Here's a delightful bonus. The same right side appears in 「猫|ねこ」, cat, with the animal part on the left. That's why cat also has the reading 「びょう」, though you'll rarely hear it. So drawing and cat are sound cousins in kanji. You meet the 「びょう」 reading of the drawing kanji in 「描写|びょうしゃ」, a description or depiction, used for scenes in novels and films. And 「素描|そびょう」 is a plain sketch. Most of the time, though, you'll use the native verb. A hand plus seedling for drawing, an animal plus seedling for a cat. Picture a hand drawing a cat in a rice field, and both kanji are yours." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "People use this verb for all kinds of image making. Drawing a picture is 「絵を描く|えをかく」, and painting with paints still uses the same verb, with 「絵の具|えのぐ」, paints. A comic artist draws manga, 「漫画を描く|まんがをかく」. At tourist spots, you'll see street artists who draw a 「似顔絵|にがおえ」, a caricature portrait, literally a picture that resembles your face. Many people, especially children, simply write this verb in kana, or even use the writing kanji, so don't be surprised. In more poetic speech, with the reading 「えがく」, people picture a future or a dream, as in 「夢を描く|ゆめをえがく」, to picture your dream. Song lyrics love that phrase. And a person who's good at drawing is described as 「絵が上手|えがじょうず」, good at pictures. Kids' drawings, manga pages, portraits and dreams all use the same drawing hand." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory scene to keep the two verbs apart. Picture a cat sitting at a desk, holding a pencil in its paw. On the left of the desk is a sheet of writing paper with the letters of a word, a letter to its friend. On the right is a big sketchbook. The cat says 「かく」 and starts writing. Then it says 「かく」 again and starts drawing a fish. Same sound, two jobs. Now remember that the drawing kanji has a hand on the left and a seedling on the right, just like the cat kanji has a seedling on its right. So the drawing hand and the cat share the same piece, and the cat is the artist. Writing is for letters. Drawing is the cat's favorite fish." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It sounds like 「書く|かく」, write, but this kanji with the hand piece is for pictures, as in 「絵を描く|えをかく」. Context tells them apart, and the literary reading 「えがく」 means to depict.",
      "Its right side is 「苗|なえ」, seedling, giving the reading 「びょう」, as in 「描写|びょうしゃ」. The same piece sits in 「猫|ねこ」, so picture a hand drawing a cat in a rice field.",
      "It covers drawing and painting, manga, and a street artist's 「似顔絵|にがおえ」. Read 「えがく」, it pictures dreams, as in 「夢を描く|ゆめをえがく」.",
      "Picture a cat at a desk saying 「かく」 to write a letter, then 「かく」 again to draw a fish. The drawing kanji and the cat kanji share the seedling piece, so the cat is the artist."
    ],
    ja: "{子ども|こども}が{絵|え}を[描いています|かいています|かく]。",
    en: "The child is drawing a picture." },
  "さがす|探す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native verb, using the native reading. The kanji has the hand part on the left, the same one in 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, a hand reaching and feeling around for something. A memory hook, just a hook: it sounds like saga, and looking for your lost keys can turn into a long saga. The pattern is simple. The thing you are looking for takes 「を」. You can look for an apartment, 「アパートを探す|アパートをさがす」, a job, 「仕事を探す|しごとをさがす」, or a lost wallet. A common mistake for English speakers. To look up information, like a word or a train time, Japanese uses a different verb, 「調べる|しらべる」. Use this one for finding a thing or a person. Also watch the difference between searching and finding. This verb is the search. When the thing turns up, Japanese uses 「見つかる|みつかる」, to be found. So you search with this verb, and with luck, it is found. Recap: a hand feeling around, to look for something." },
      { title: "Kanji choice", text: "There are two kanji for this verb, and both are read 「さがす」. This one, 「探す|さがす」, is for looking for something you want, like an apartment, a job, a good restaurant or a present. The other, 「捜す|さがす」, also with the hand part on the left, is for searching for something lost or missing, like a lost wallet, a missing child, or a suspect the police are hunting. In news reports you'll see the second kanji in 「捜査|そうさ」, a police investigation. Many people today simply use 「探す|さがす」 for both, and that's fine for everyday writing. But in a mystery novel or the news, notice which one appears. Seeking something you want, or tracking down something lost. Same sound, slightly different hunt." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading, 「たん」, this kanji starts some adventurous words. A detective is a 「探偵|たんてい」. Anime fans may know the long running series about a boy detective, whose title includes 「名探偵|めいたんてい」, a great detective. Exploration is 「探検|たんけん」, searching and inspecting, used for jungle expeditions or children exploring a cave. And with the native reading, the noun 「探し物|さがしもの」 means something you're looking for. A shop clerk may come up and ask 「何かお探しですか|なにかおさがしですか」, are you looking for something, in polite honorific form. You can answer 「ちょっと見ているだけです|ちょっとみているだけです」, I'm just looking. So the same kanji takes you from shopping to detective stories to jungle adventures. The idea in all of them is reaching out to find something." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "A few patterns help this verb shine. To say you're in the middle of looking, use the te form plus 「いる」: 「仕事を探しています|しごとをさがしています」, I'm job hunting. To go somewhere in order to look, use the stem plus 「に行く|にいく」: 「本を探しに行きます|ほんをさがしにいきます」, I'm going to look for a book. To try looking, add 「てみる」: 「ネットで探してみます|ネットでさがしてみます」, I'll try searching online. Notice here that when you search the internet, this verb works fine, because you're hunting for a page or a product. It's when you look up a fact, like a meaning or a time, that 「調べる|しらべる」 fits better. And remember the partner verb, 「見つける|みつける」, to find something. If you searched and found it, say 「見つけました|みつけました」. Searching is the journey, finding is the happy ending." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The hand piece shows a hand feeling around, and looking for lost keys can become a saga. The thing takes 「を」, but for looking up facts use 「調べる|しらべる」, and when found, 「見つかる|みつかる」.",
      "「探す|さがす」 is for seeking something you want, while 「捜す|さがす」 is for tracking down something lost, as in 「捜査|そうさ」, a police investigation. Many people use the first for both.",
      "Read 「たん」, it gives 「探偵|たんてい」, detective, and 「探検|たんけん」, exploration. Clerks ask 「何かお探しですか|なにかおさがしですか」, and you can reply 「ちょっと見ているだけです|ちょっとみているだけです」.",
      "Use 「探しています|さがしています」 for ongoing searching and 「探しに行きます|さがしにいきます」 for going to look. Searching the web for a product works, and finding it is 「見つけました|みつけました」."
    ],
    ja: "アパートを[探しています|さがしています|さがす]。",
    en: "I'm looking for an apartment." },
  "さそう|誘う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native verb using its native reading. Look at the left side of the kanji. It is the words part, the same piece on the left of 「話す|はなす」, to speak, and 「読む|よむ」, to read. A way to remember it: you use words to draw someone along. The pattern is: the person takes 「を」, and the event takes 「に」. So, to invite a friend to a movie is 「友だちを映画に誘う|ともだちをえいがにさそう」. A note on how it feels. This verb is about casually asking someone to join you, like going for a meal or a party. It is less formal than a printed invitation. In actual conversation, the invitation itself usually sounds like 「〜ませんか」, won't you, as in 「いっしょに行きませんか|いっしょにいきませんか」. A cultural note. When Japanese people turn down an invitation, they often avoid a direct no. They may just say 「ちょっと」, and let it trail off. Recap: words on the left, using words to draw someone along, to invite." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb often appears in the passive, because being invited is something that happens to you. The passive is 「誘われる|さそわれる」, and the person who invites takes 「に」: 「友だちに飲み会に誘われました|ともだちにのみかいにさそわれました」, I was invited to a drinking party by a friend. That sentence has two 「に」, one for the inviter and one for the event, so listen carefully. You can also say what you were invited to do with a volitional verb plus 「と」. For example, 「いっしょに行こうと誘いました|いっしょにいこうとさそいました」, I invited her, saying let's go together. And to thank someone warmly, use the te form plus 「くれる」: 「誘ってくれてありがとう|さそってくれてありがとう」, thanks for inviting me. That's a phrase you can use even if you have to say no." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The polite noun form is 「お誘い|おさそい」, an invitation. When declining gracefully, Japanese people often thank first: 「お誘いありがとうございます|おさそいありがとうございます」. Then they give a gentle reason, and finally leave the door open with 「また誘ってください|またさそってください」, please invite me again. Among friends, that becomes 「また誘ってね|またさそってね」. This last part is important. Without it, the other person may think you never want to be invited again. If you really do want to go, you can say 「ぜひ」, by all means, and sound eager. People also use the verb when someone else's actions tempt them. For example, a cozy bed or a sweet smell invites you, as in 「眠気を誘う|ねむけをさそう」, it makes you sleepy. Invitations from people, invitations from sleep, and graceful ways to answer both." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading, 「ゆう」, this kanji turns up in some useful words. 「勧誘|かんゆう」 means recruiting or soliciting. At the start of each school year, university clubs crowd the campus doing 「新歓|しんかん」, welcoming new students, and 「勧誘|かんゆう」, handing out flyers and trying to sign people up. On apartment doors, you may see a sticker refusing door-to-door salespeople, using this same word. Then there's 「誘惑|ゆうわく」, temptation, being drawn toward something you shouldn't, like a late night snack. On a darker note, the news uses 「誘拐|ゆうかい」 for kidnapping, drawing someone away by force or trickery. In every word, someone or something is pulling a person along. So the words part on the left leads people in, for better or for worse." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The words piece on the left shows using words to draw someone along. The person takes 「を」 and the event 「に」, as in 「友だちを映画に誘う|ともだちをえいがにさそう」, and declining is often a trailing 「ちょっと」.",
      "Being invited uses the passive 「誘われる|さそわれる」, with the inviter marked by 「に」. Thank someone with 「誘ってくれてありがとう|さそってくれてありがとう」, even when saying no.",
      "When declining, thank first with 「お誘いありがとうございます|おさそいありがとうございます」 and end with 「また誘ってください|またさそってください」 to leave the door open. Sleepiness can invite you too, 「眠気を誘う|ねむけをさそう」.",
      "Read 「ゆう」, it gives 「勧誘|かんゆう」, recruiting, 「誘惑|ゆうわく」, temptation, and 「誘拐|ゆうかい」, kidnapping. In every word someone is pulled along."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}を{映画|えいが}に[誘いました|さそいました|さそう]。",
    en: "I invited my friend to a movie." },
  "しゃべる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native verb, nearly always written in kana. There is a kanji spelling, 「喋る|しゃべる」, but you rarely need it. It is a casual, everyday word for talking and chatting, the easy flow of conversation between friends. Compare it with 「話す|はなす」, which is neutral, good for any situation, from a speech to a phone call. This verb is more relaxed, and it can hint at talking a lot. You would not use it for a formal speech. It is a regular u verb, so the te form is 「しゃべって」. You will also hear it for languages in casual speech, as in 「日本語がしゃべれる|にほんごがしゃべれる」, I can speak Japanese. A very common related word is 「おしゃべり」. As a noun it means a chat, and describing a person, it means a chatterbox. A way to remember it, as a hook: picture a group of friends at a café, chattering so much their coffee gets cold. Recap: casual talk, the chatty cousin of 「話す|はなす」." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "Japanese has several talking verbs, each with its own feel. 「言う|いう」 is to say, focused on the words that come out: say thank you, say a name. 「話す|はなす」 is to speak or talk, the neutral, all purpose verb, good in a meeting or a classroom. This verb is chatty and casual. It often suggests a lot of talking, or talking freely, even talking too much. A teacher might scold students with 「しゃべらないで」, stop chattering. And 「語る|かたる」 is to tell or narrate, like telling a story or talking deeply about your dreams. So a speech at a ceremony uses 「話す|はなす」. A friend gossiping at lunch uses this verb. Telling the tale of your trip with passion is 「語る|かたる」. One more tip. Saying 「しゃべっちゃった」 means I blabbed, I let a secret slip. So be careful who you chatter with." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular u verb, even though it ends in 「る」. That's an important point, because some 「る」 verbs are ru verbs. The test is the negative. A ru verb drops the 「る」, but this one changes it to 「ら」: 「しゃべらない」, doesn't talk. The masu form is 「しゃべります」, and the potential, can talk, is 「しゃべれる」. Watch out for 「しゃべりすぎる」, to talk too much, made with the stem plus 「すぎる」. You might confess 「昨日はしゃべりすぎた|きのうはしゃべりすぎた」, I talked too much yesterday. The noun 「おしゃべり」 can also be used with 「する」: 「友だちとおしゃべりする|ともだちとおしゃべりする」, to have a chat with a friend. Notice 「と」 marks your chat partner. So the verb, its noun, and a few endings give you all the chatter you need." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the sound. Picture a royal Persian king, a shah, sitting on his throne next to a giant bear. The shah and the bear chat all day long. They gossip about the court, laugh about the weather, and never stop talking. Shah, bear. Picture the two of them chattering so much that the servants roll their eyes, and the tea goes cold on the table. The court historian writes in his notebook: today, the shah and the bear talked again. The shah plus the bear gives you a hook for the start of the sound, 「しゃべ」, with 「る」 tagged on as the verb ending. Whenever you picture idle, happy chatter, see the shah and his bear, gossiping until midnight." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A casual verb for chatting, the relaxed cousin of neutral 「話す|はなす」, often hinting at lots of talk. A chat or a chatterbox is 「おしゃべり」.",
      "「言う|いう」 is to say, 「話す|はなす」 is all purpose speaking, this verb is chatty, and 「語る|かたる」 is to narrate. 「しゃべっちゃった」 means you let a secret slip.",
      "Though it ends in 「る」, it is a u-verb, so the negative is 「しゃべらない」. Talking too much is 「しゃべりすぎる」, and chatting with a friend is 「友だちとおしゃべりする|ともだちとおしゃべりする」.",
      "Picture a Persian shah and a giant bear gossiping on the throne all day while the tea goes cold. Shah plus bear gives 「しゃべ」, with 「る」 as the verb ending."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}と{一時間|いちじかん}[しゃべりました|しゃべりました|しゃべる]。",
    en: "I chatted with a friend for an hour." },
  "つきあう|付き合う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two native verbs joined together. The first is 「付く|つく」, to stick to or be attached. You know the same sound in 「気をつける|きをつける」, where you attach your attention, and in 「つける」, to turn on. The second is 「合う|あう」, to fit together or match. You know it in 「似合う|にあう」, to suit someone, where clothes fit you well. So, to stick together and fit with someone. It has two meanings. The first is to date someone, to be in a relationship. The pattern is 「〜と付き合う|〜とつきあう」, to go out with someone, often in the te form, 「付き合っています|つきあっています」, we are going out. The second is to keep someone company. If a friend says 「ちょっと付き合って|ちょっとつきあって」, they mean come along with me for a bit, maybe to a shop. A cultural note. In Japan, dating often begins with a clear confession of feelings, after which the couple officially starts going out. Recap: stick plus fit, to stay close to someone." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The particle changes the meaning, so watch it closely. With 「と」, it means to date or to associate with someone: 「田中さんと付き合っている|たなかさんとつきあっている」, I'm going out with Tanaka. With 「に」, it often means to keep someone company in an activity, and the activity takes 「に」: 「買い物に付き合ってくれる|かいものにつきあってくれる」, will you come shopping with me. Here the speaker is asking a favor, which is why 「てくれる」 appears. A classic confession line is 「付き合ってください|つきあってください」, please go out with me. Context matters, though, because the same words can also mean please come along with me. Many comedy scenes play on this mix up. Someone thinks they're being asked out, but the other person just wanted help carrying shopping bags." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The noun form, 「付き合い|つきあい」, means social relations, the ties you keep with people. A long friendship is described as 「付き合いが長い|つきあいがながい」, our relationship is long. Getting along with neighbors is 「近所付き合い|きんじょづきあい」, with a sound change to 「づ」. And someone who always skips the after work drinks is said to be 「付き合いが悪い|つきあいがわるい」, bad at socializing. The second verb, 「合う|あう」, also has a big family where it means doing something mutually. 「話し合う|はなしあう」 is to talk things over together. 「知り合う|しりあう」 is to get to know each other, and an acquaintance is a 「知り合い|しりあい」. 「助け合う|たすけあう」 is to help each other. So this word fits into a pattern of togetherness: stick together, talk together, help each other." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, a relationship often officially starts with a confession, called 「告白|こくはく」. One person tells the other they like them and asks to go out. Until that moment, even several dates might not count as a relationship. Holidays shape dating too. On Valentine's Day, women traditionally give chocolate to men, both true love chocolate and polite obligation chocolate for coworkers, called 「義理チョコ|ぎりチョコ」. Then a month later, on March fourteenth, comes White Day, when men return the favor with gifts. Christmas Eve is a romantic night for couples, more like Valentine's Day elsewhere, and people book restaurants weeks in advance. Many couples also celebrate monthly anniversaries in the first months. So dating in Japan has a clear starting line and a busy calendar." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「付く|つく」, stick, plus 「合う|あう」, fit: staying close to someone. With 「と」 it means dating, 「付き合っています|つきあっています」, and 「ちょっと付き合って|ちょっとつきあって」 means come along with me.",
      "With 「と」 it means dating, while with 「に」 it means keeping someone company in an activity. 「付き合ってください|つきあってください」 can be a confession or just a request to come along, a classic comedy mix up.",
      "The noun 「付き合い|つきあい」 means social ties, as in 「近所付き合い|きんじょづきあい」. 「合う|あう」 also means doing things mutually, as in 「話し合う|はなしあう」 and 「助け合う|たすけあう」.",
      "Relationships often start with a 「告白|こくはく」, a confession. Women give chocolate on Valentine's Day, including 「義理チョコ|ぎりチョコ」, and men return gifts on White Day."
    ],
    ja: "{二人|ふたり}は{去年|きょねん}から[付き合っています|つきあっています|つきあう]。",
    en: "The two of them have been dating since last year." },
  "つく|着く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native verb with the native reading. The kanji is the same one in 「着る|きる」, to wear, and 「着物|きもの」, kimono. The core idea of this kanji is to reach or attach to something. A way to remember the link, just a hook: clothes arrive on your body, and you arrive at a place. The pattern is: the destination takes 「に」. So 「駅に着く|えきにつく」, to arrive at the station, and 「家に着きました|いえにつきました」, I got home. A common mistake. English speakers sometimes use 「来る|くる」 or 「行く|いく」 when they mean the moment of arrival. Those two talk about the movement. This one is the moment you actually reach the place. Another tip. Several different verbs sound like 「つく」, including 「付く|つく」, to stick, which you see in 「付き合う|つきあう」. In speech, context sorts them out. It is a regular u verb, so the te form is 「着いて|ついて」. A handy line: 「着いたら電話します|ついたらでんわします」, I will call when I arrive. Recap: same kanji as wearing, reaching a place." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji's Chinese-origin reading, 「ちゃく」, gives you arrival words seen at every airport and station. Arrival is 「到着|とうちゃく」, and departure boards show it alongside departures. A plane landing is 「着陸|ちゃくりく」, reaching land. The same reading is also a counter, both for suits and dresses, as in 「一着|いっちゃく」, one outfit, and for finishing places in a race, where 「一着|いっちゃく」 means first to arrive, first place. That double use fits the kanji's core idea of reaching or attaching. Clothes reach your body, and runners reach the finish line. You also have native words from the wearing side, like 「下着|したぎ」, underwear, and 「水着|みずぎ」, swimsuit. So the same kanji covers arrival halls, landing planes, race winners and swimsuits, all things that reach their place." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "English uses arrive for both people and packages, but Japanese splits them. People, trains and planes arrive with this verb: 「バスが着きました|バスがつきました」, the bus has arrived. But mail, packages and messages reaching you use 「届く|とどく」, to be delivered: 「荷物が届きました|にもつがとどきました」, the package arrived. A common learner slip is using this verb for a parcel. It's understandable, but sounds a little off. The word 「届く|とどく」 also means to reach something with your hand, like the top shelf. On formal announcements, you'll hear the noun 「到着|とうちゃく」 instead of the verb: the train will soon arrive at Tokyo, said with 「まもなく東京に到着します|まもなくとうきょうにとうちゃくします」. So travelers arrive with this verb, packages are delivered with 「届く|とどく」, and announcements use the stiff noun." },
      { title: "Set phrases", text: "This verb shows up in some very common phrases where the idea is settling into place. 「席に着く|せきにつく」 means to take your seat. Teachers say 「席に着いてください|せきについてください」 at the start of class. The most useful one is 「落ち着く|おちつく」, literally to fall and arrive, which means to calm down or to settle. 「落ち着いて|おちついて」 means calm down, said to someone who is panicking. A place that makes you feel relaxed is described as 「落ち着く|おちつく」, as in 「この部屋は落ち着く|このへやはおちつく」, this room feels calming. And when you finally settle into a new life after moving, you can say 「やっと落ち着きました|やっとおちつきました」, I've finally settled in. So arriving isn't just about places. Your mind can arrive too, and settle down." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Its kanji is the one in 「着る|きる」, since clothes reach your body and you reach a place. The destination takes 「に」, as in 「駅に着く|えきにつく」, and it marks the moment of arrival, unlike 「来る|くる」.",
      "Read 「ちゃく」, it gives 「到着|とうちゃく」, arrival, and 「着陸|ちゃくりく」, landing. 「一着|いっちゃく」 counts one outfit or means first place in a race.",
      "People and trains arrive with this verb, but packages are delivered with 「届く|とどく」, as in 「荷物が届きました|にもつがとどきました」. Announcements use the stiff noun 「到着|とうちゃく」.",
      "It means settling into place in phrases like 「席に着く|せきにつく」, take your seat, and 「落ち着く|おちつく」, to calm down or settle. Your mind can arrive too."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}に[着きました|つきました|つく]。",
    en: "I arrived at the station." },
  "ほけんにはいる|保険に入る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a set phrase built from pieces you know. 「保険|ほけん」 is insurance, protection against danger. Then 「入る|はいる」, to enter, with the particle 「に」 marking what you go into. So, literally, to enter insurance. Where English says buy insurance or get insurance, Japanese thinks of it as joining something, like joining a group. You see the same idea in other phrases you know. 「お風呂に入る|おふろにはいる」, to take a bath, is literally to enter the bath. And people say 「大学に入る|だいがくにはいる」, to enter a university, or 「サークルに入る|サークルにはいる」, to join a club. A useful sentence: 「旅行の前に保険に入りました|りょこうのまえにほけんにはいりました」, I got insurance before the trip. Remember that 「入る|はいる」 is an u verb even though it ends in 「る」, so the past is 「入った|はいった」, with a small pause, just like 「帰った|かえった」. A cultural note. In Japan, residents, including foreign students, are expected to join the national health insurance system. Recap: enter insurance, you join it like a club." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's open up the two kanji of 「保険|ほけん」. The first, 「保|ほ」, has the person part squeezed onto its left side, and its core meaning is to keep, to protect, to look after. The second, 「険|けん」, has a different left side, the part that looks like a tall letter B. In this position it stands for a hill or a mound, and 「険|けん」 is about steep, rough, dangerous ground. You will meet it in 「危険|きけん」, danger, the word printed on warning signs, and with its native reading in 「険しい|けわしい」, steep, like a steep mountain path. So the word pictures a person keeping you safe on dangerous ground. Both kanji take their Chinese-origin readings here, which is typical for formal, institutional words like this one. In short, keep safe plus steep danger gives insurance." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan the most important piece of plastic in your wallet might be the 「保険証|ほけんしょう」, the health insurance card. 「証|しょう」 means proof. At any clinic, the first thing the receptionist says is 「保険証をお願いします|ほけんしょうをおねがいします」, your insurance card, please. With it, most adults pay only thirty percent of the bill. Health insurance in general is 「健康保険|けんこうほけん」, using 「健康|けんこう」, health. Before a trip abroad, people often buy 「旅行保険|りょこうほけん」, travel insurance, sometimes right at the airport. A mini dialogue between friends. One asks, 「保険に入った|ほけんにはいった」, did you get insurance? The other says, 「うん、入ったよ|うん、はいったよ」, yeah, I did. Notice how casual and short that is. In short, bring your card to the clinic, and join a plan before you travel." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb here, 「入る|はいる」, is intransitive. Something goes in by itself. Its transitive partner is 「入れる|いれる」, to put something in, like putting sugar in coffee. So you enter insurance with 「入る|はいる」, but you put a document into a bag with 「入れる|いれる」. Now a subtle and useful contrast. 「保険に入りました|ほけんにはいりました」 reports the moment you signed up. 「保険に入っています|ほけんにはいっています」, with the te form plus 「いる」, describes the result that continues, I am insured, I am covered. It works just like 「結婚しています|けっこんしています」, I am married, rather than I am getting married. So when a form or a doctor asks whether you have insurance, the natural answer is the te form plus 「いる」 version. In short, past tense for joining, te form plus 「いる」 for being covered." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese doesn't buy insurance, it enters it, with 「保険|ほけん」 plus 「入る|はいる」 and 「に」, just like joining a club or getting into a bath.",
      "The kanji are 「保|ほ」, a person keeping and protecting, and 「険|けん」, steep dangerous ground, as in 「危険|きけん」, so insurance pictures someone keeping you safe on risky ground.",
      "At any clinic you hand over your 「保険証|ほけんしょう」, your health insurance card, and before a trip abroad people join 「旅行保険|りょこうほけん」, travel insurance.",
      "Use 「保険に入りました|ほけんにはいりました」 for the moment you signed up, but 「保険に入っています|ほけんにはいっています」, with the te form plus 「いる」, to say you are covered now."
    ],
    ja: "りょこうの{前|まえ}に[保険に入りました|ほけんにはいりました|ほけんにはいる]。",
    en: "I bought insurance before the trip." },
  "きをつける|気をつける": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A set phrase. The first part is 「気|き」, one of the most useful kanji in Japanese. It means spirit, energy, mind or attention. It is in a big family you already know. 「元気|げんき」 is healthy, full of energy. 「天気|てんき」 is the weather, the energy of the sky. 「電気|でんき」 is electricity. 「気温|きおん」 is air temperature. And 「気分が悪い|きぶんがわるい」 means feeling sick. Then comes 「つける」, to attach or put on. So, to attach your mind to something, to pay attention. The thing you watch out for takes 「に」. For example, 「車に気をつけて|くるまにきをつけて」, watch out for cars. The most common form is 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care, which people say when someone leaves on a trip or heads home late. A memory tip: imagine pinning your attention onto something, like a magnet that will not let go. Recap: attach your mind, be careful." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「気|き」 has a real picture behind it. Its outer part comes from an old drawing of vapor or steam drifting upward in wavy lines. The older, traditional form had the kanji for rice, 「米|こめ」, tucked inside, like steam rising from a pot of cooking rice. In the modern simplified form that rice was replaced by a simple cross shape. So the core idea is something invisible that rises and flows, like breath or energy. That is why it shows up in the air, in the weather, in electricity and in your mood. When you pay attention, you are pointing that invisible energy at something. The reading here, 「き」, is the Chinese-origin reading, and it is so common that it feels completely native. Remember the steam rising from the rice pot, energy you cannot hold but can direct." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here is a tidy pair of phrases that differ only by one verb partner. 「気をつける|きをつける」 uses 「つける」, a transitive verb, to attach something on purpose. Its intransitive partner is 「つく」, to become attached, and that gives 「気がつく|きがつく」, to notice, to realize. Your attention attaches by itself. So you deliberately take care with one, and something just catches your attention with the other. A few more 「気|き」 phrases you will hear all the time. 「気に入る|きにいる」, something enters your mind, meaning to like it, as in 「このかばんが気に入りました|このかばんがきにいりました」, I really like this bag. 「気になる|きになる」, it becomes your mind, meaning it bothers you or makes you curious. And 「人気|にんき」, people's energy, means popularity. So remember the pair: you put on your attention on purpose, but you only notice when it attaches by itself." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Listen in a Japanese school classroom and you will hear this word as a command. At the start of class a student leader calls out 「起立|きりつ」, stand up, then 「気をつけ|きをつけ」, attention, and everyone stands straight and still, and finally 「礼|れい」, bow. Notice the command form drops the te and just ends in 「つけ」. Outside school, the te form is a warm goodbye. When a friend leaves for the airport, you say 「気をつけて|きをつけて」, take care, have a safe trip. To a customer or someone older, add the polite prefix, 「お気をつけて|おきをつけて」. Shops and hotels say 「お気をつけてお帰りください|おきをつけておかえりください」, please get home safely. It is less a warning than a small blessing. So in class it means stand straight, and at the door it means arrive safely." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「気|き」 means mind or attention, and 「つける」 means to attach, so you attach your mind to something, marking the danger with 「に」, as in 「車に気をつけて|くるまにきをつけて」.",
      "The kanji 「気|き」 began as a drawing of steam rising, once from cooking rice, so it means an invisible energy you can point at something when you pay attention.",
      "With transitive 「つける」 you take care on purpose, while intransitive 「つく」 gives 「気がつく|きがつく」, to notice, when your attention attaches by itself.",
      "In class 「気をつけ|きをつけ」 is the command to stand at attention, but at the door 「気をつけて|きをつけて」 is a warm wish to arrive safely, more blessing than warning."
    ],
    ja: "{車|くるま}に[気をつけて|きをつけて|きをつける]ください。",
    en: "Please watch out for cars." },
  "しらべる|調べる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native verb using the native reading of the kanji. The kanji has the words part on the left, the same one in 「話す|はなす」, to speak, 「読む|よむ」, to read, and 「誘う|さそう」, to invite. Words are a good clue here, because this verb is about checking and investigating information. The thing you look into takes 「を」. You look up a word in a dictionary, 「辞書で調べる|じしょでしらべる」, or look up something online, 「ネットで調べる|ネットでしらべる」, with 「で」 for the tool you use. It also works for bigger things, like researching a topic for a report, or the police investigating a case. A common mistake. English uses look for and look up as if they were similar. Japanese splits them. When you are searching for a physical thing, like keys or an apartment, use 「探す|さがす」. When you are finding out facts, use this one. It is a ru verb, so the te form is 「調べて|しらべて」 and the polite form is 「調べます|しらべます」. Recap: words on the left, investigating information." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look at the right half of 「調|しら」. That is the kanji 「周|しゅう」, around or circumference. It is mostly there for sound, and you can catch it doing the same job in a kanji you know well, 「週|しゅう」, week, where it sits on the moving along a road part. Both are read with a similar sound, 「しゅう」 or, in this kanji's case, 「ちょう」. On the left is the words part, taken from the kanji in 「言う|いう」, to say. So the kanji pictures words going all the way around something, checking it from every side. With its Chinese-origin reading you will meet it in 「調子|ちょうし」, condition, as in 「調子がいいです|ちょうしがいいです」, I am in good shape, and in 「体調|たいちょう」, physical condition. Here, in the verb, it takes the native reading 「しら」. So spot the week kanji's cousin on the right, and the words on the left." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "How do people actually use this verb day to day? Phones, mostly. 「スマホで調べる|スマホでしらべる」, to look it up on your phone, with 「で」 for the tool. Young people even have a slang verb, 「ググる」, to google, made from the company name plus the verb ending 「る」. The noun 「調べもの|しらべもの」 means looking things up or research work, as in 「図書館で調べものをします|としょかんでしらべものをします」, I do some research at the library. At work, a very handy combination is the te form plus 「おく」, which means doing something in advance. 「調べておきます|しらべておきます」, I will look into it, is what you say when someone asks a question you cannot answer yet. Here is a mini dialogue. Do you know when the store opens? 「調べておきますね|しらべておきますね」, I'll check for you. So remember the phone, the library, and the promise to check." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The words part on the left of the kanji points to checking information, so use this for looking up facts and 「探す|さがす」 for searching for physical things like keys.",
      "The kanji has the words part on the left and 「周|しゅう」, around, on the right, picturing words going all around something to check it from every side.",
      "Most looking up today is 「スマホで調べる|スマホでしらべる」, on your phone, and when you can't answer something yet, 「調べておきます|しらべておきます」 promises you will check."
    ],
    ja: "{電車|でんしゃ}の{時間|じかん}を[調べます|しらべます|しらべる]。",
    en: "I'll look up the train times." },
  "みえる|見える": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native verb built on 「見る|みる」, to look or watch. The difference is about intention. 「見る|みる」 is when you choose to look at something. This verb is when something comes into view on its own, whether you are trying or not. So the thing you can see becomes the subject, marked with 「が」. For example, 「山が見えます|やまがみえます」, the mountains are visible. A great sentence for a hotel room with a view. A common mistake is to say 「山を見えます|やまをみえます」 with 「を」. Use 「が」. Its sound partner is 「聞こえる|きこえる」, to be audible, from 「聞く|きく」, to listen. Another trap. 「見られる|みられる」 means you are able to see something, like a movie that is available to watch. This verb is about things physically appearing in your sight. It is a ru verb, so the negative is 「見えない|みえない」, I cannot see it. A cultural note. On clear winter days you can see Mount Fuji from Tokyo. Recap: seeing without trying, things come into view." },
      { title: "Another meaning", text: "This verb has a second life that surprises learners. Besides can be seen, it also means to look or to seem, judging by appearance. With an i adjective, change the final 「い」 to 「く」 and add the verb: 「若く見える|わかくみえる」, to look young. With a noun or a na adjective, use 「に」: 「学生に見えます|がくせいにみえます」, he looks like a student, and 「元気に見えます|げんきにみえます」, she looks healthy. A lovely compliment for someone older is 「お若く見えますね|おわかくみえますね」, you look so young. And a friendly tease is 「先生に見えない|せんせいにみえない」, you don't look like a teacher. Here the thing that looks some way is still the subject, so it often takes 「は」 or 「が」. In short, add 「く」 or 「に」 in front and the meaning shifts from visible to looks like." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「見|み」 builds a little team of verbs, and the members are easy to confuse. 「見る|みる」 is to look, on purpose. This one, 「見える|みえる」, is to be visible, happening by itself. 「見せる|みせる」 is to show, to make someone else see, as in 「写真を見せてください|しゃしんをみせてください」, please show me the photo. 「見つける|みつける」 is to find, and 「見つかる|みつかる」 is to be found, as in my keys turned up. Notice the pattern. The verbs ending in 「える」 and 「かる」 here describe things happening on their own, while 「せる」 and 「つける」 are actions you do to something. And with its Chinese-origin reading, 「けん」, the same kanji gives 「意見|いけん」, an opinion, the way you see things. Remember: look, be seen, show, find, be found, all from one eye on legs." },
      { title: "Polite and practical", text: "Two more uses round this word out. First, eyesight. 「よく見えません|よくみえません」 means I can't see well, and you will hear 「めがねがないと見えない|めがねがないとみえない」, without my glasses I can't see. Remember the thing you can't see takes 「が」, and the person who can or can't see often takes 「は」 or 「には」. Second, a surprise from honorific speech. In very polite situations, 「お見えになる|おみえになる」 means someone comes. A receptionist might say 「お客様がお見えになりました|おきゃくさまがおみえになりました」, your guest has arrived. It is a respectful alternative to 「いらっしゃる」, which you learn with honorifics. The idea is that the guest comes into view, so the polite thing is to say they appeared rather than came. So this little verb covers eyesight, appearances and even arriving guests." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「見る|みる」 is looking on purpose, while this verb means something comes into view by itself, so the visible thing takes 「が」, as in 「山が見えます|やまがみえます」.",
      "It also means to look or seem: add 「く」 to an i adjective, as in 「若く見える|わかくみえる」, or 「に」 after a noun, as in 「学生に見えます|がくせいにみえます」.",
      "From the same kanji come 「見る|みる」, look, 「見える|みえる」, be visible, 「見せる|みせる」, show, and 「見つける|みつける」, find, with 「える」 marking what happens by itself.",
      "It covers eyesight, as in 「よく見えません|よくみえません」, I can't see well, and in honorific speech 「お見えになる|おみえになる」 politely means a guest has arrived."
    ],
    ja: "へやから{山|やま}が[見えます|みえます|みえる]。",
    en: "You can see the mountains from the room." },
  "かんこうする|観光する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」, to do. The first is 「観|かん」, to view or observe carefully. It has the seeing part, 「見|み」, on the right. The second is 「光|こう」, light. With its native reading it is 「光|ひかり」, light, and the town of 「日光|にっこう」, sunlight, is a famous place for sightseeing. The word is usually traced to an old Chinese classic, which spoke of observing the light of a country, meaning its glory and culture. So, sightseeing is going to look at the bright sights of a place. The place you tour takes 「を」: 「京都を観光する|きょうとをかんこうする」, to sightsee in Kyoto. Related words are 「観光客|かんこうきゃく」, tourists, and 「観光地|かんこうち」, a tourist spot. A small contrast. 「旅行|りょこう」 is the whole trip, travel. This word is the looking around once you are there, visiting temples, shrines and famous views. Recap: view the light, sightseeing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At the airport in Japan, the immigration officer may ask the purpose of your visit. The expected answer is simply 「観光です|かんこうです」, sightseeing. In town, look for the 「観光案内所|かんこうあんないじょ」, the tourist information center, where 「案内|あんない」 means guidance. They hand out maps and can book tours. You can also ride a 「観光バス|かんこうバス」, a sightseeing bus. A very useful grammar pattern from your lessons is purpose plus 「に行く|にいく」. With this kind of noun you can drop the 「する」 and say 「京都へ観光に行きました|きょうとへかんこうにいきました」, I went to Kyoto for sightseeing. Here is a mini dialogue. 「休みに何をしましたか|やすみになにをしましたか」, what did you do over the break? 「大阪を観光しました|おおさかをかんこうしました」, I went sightseeing in Osaka. In short: tell immigration, find the info center, and go there for sightseeing." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's follow each kanji into its family. The first, 「観|かん」, is about watching attentively. With a native reading it can also be written 「観る|みる」, used especially for watching movies, plays and sports. You will meet 「観客|かんきゃく」, the audience at a game or show, adding the guest kanji you know from 「お客さん|おきゃくさん」. The second kanji, 「光|こう」, is light. Natively it is the noun 「光|ひかり」, and the verb 「光る|ひかる」, to shine or glow. Fans of trains know that one of the bullet train services is named 「ひかり」, light, because it is fast as a flash. And the famous temple town 「日光|にっこう」, sunlight, combines sun and light. Notice how 「にち」 shrinks to 「にっ」 before 「こう」. So one kanji means looking carefully, the other means light, and together you go to look at the shining sights." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "There is an old Japanese saying that every sightseer should know. 「日光を見ずして結構と言うなかれ|にっこうをみずしてけっこうというなかれ」. It means, do not say splendid until you have seen Nikko. It is a pun, because 「日光|にっこう」 and 「結構|けっこう」, splendid, rhyme. Nikko is home to the richly decorated Toshogu shrine, built for the first Tokugawa shogun. Japanese sightseeing has its own rhythm. Groups often visit famous places on a tight schedule, collect stamps at stations and temples, and buy edible souvenirs called 「おみやげ」 for coworkers back home. In recent years, cities like Kyoto have struggled with so many visitors that locals talk about tourism pollution, and some neighborhoods now limit entry to narrow streets. In short, see Nikko before you say splendid, and bring snacks home for the office." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「観|かん」 means to view carefully and 「光|こう」 means light, from an old saying about observing a country's glory, so sightseeing is looking at the bright sights once you arrive.",
      "Tell immigration 「観光です|かんこうです」 for sightseeing, and drop the 「する」 to say 「京都へ観光に行きました|きょうとへかんこうにいきました」, I went to Kyoto to sightsee.",
      "「観|かん」 means watching attentively, as in 「観客|かんきゃく」, the audience, and 「光|こう」 is light, as in 「光る|ひかる」, to shine, so you go to look at shining sights.",
      "An old pun says don't call anything 「結構|けっこう」, splendid, until you have seen 「日光|にっこう」, home of the richly decorated Toshogu shrine."
    ],
    ja: "{京都|きょうと}を[観光しました|かんこうしました|かんこうする]。",
    en: "I went sightseeing in Kyoto." },
  "よやくする|予約する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」, to do. You met the noun 「予約|よやく」 before, and now it becomes a verb. The first kanji is 「予|よ」, beforehand. It is the same one in 「予定|よてい」, a plan, and 「天気予報|てんきよほう」, the weather forecast. The second is 「約|やく」, a promise or agreement. It has the thread part on the left, like tying a knot to seal a deal. You know it from 「約束|やくそく」, a promise. So, a promise made in advance. That is exactly what a reservation is. You promise to come, and they promise to hold your place. The thing you reserve takes 「を」: 「レストランを予約する|レストランをよやくする」, to book a restaurant. You can also reserve a hotel, a train seat on the 「新幹線|しんかんせん」, or a haircut at the 「美容院|びよういん」. A handy phrase on the phone is 「予約したいんですが|よやくしたいんですが」, I would like to make a reservation. Recap: in advance plus promise, to book." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each of these two kanji opens a family. 「予|よ」, beforehand, gives 「予定|よてい」, a plan, 「予習|よしゅう」, preparing for a lesson before class, and 「予報|よほう」, a forecast. In every one, something happens ahead of time. Its partner 「約|やく」 has a second meaning you will love: about, approximately. Put it in front of a number, and 「約一時間|やくいちじかん」 means about one hour, a bit more formal than 「ぐらい」. You hear it in train announcements and on the news. So 「約|やく」 is both a promise and a rough estimate, and both make sense if you think of tying things loosely together. And of course it gives 「約束|やくそく」, a promise between friends. The difference? A reservation is a promise to a business, while a 「約束|やくそく」 is a promise to a person. In short, plans in advance, and promises tied with thread." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Reservation Japanese is a script you can memorize. When you call, the staff will say 「お電話ありがとうございます|おでんわありがとうございます」, then you say 「予約したいんですが|よやくしたいんですが」. They will ask 「何名様ですか|なんめいさまですか」, how many people, and 「お名前は|おなまえは」, your name. When you arrive, you announce yourself with 「予約した|よやくした」 plus your name, for example, I made a reservation under Smith. Some restaurants are 「予約制|よやくせい」, reservations only. Others accept walk ins, and the word for that is 「予約なし|よやくなし」, without a reservation. To cancel, use the loanword 「キャンセルする」. One extra tip. Popular sushi bars and hair salons in Japan can be booked weeks ahead, and many people book online. In short, learn the phone script, give your name, and book early." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「予|よ」 means beforehand, as in 「予定|よてい」, and 「約|やく」 means promise, as in 「約束|やくそく」, so a reservation is a promise made in advance.",
      "「約|やく」 also means about before a number, as in 「約一時間|やくいちじかん」, and while this word is a promise to a business, 「約束|やくそく」 is a promise to a person.",
      "On the phone say 「予約したいんですが|よやくしたいんですが」, then answer how many people and your name, and book popular places weeks ahead."
    ],
    ja: "レストランを[予約しました|よやくしました|よやくする]。",
    en: "I reserved a restaurant." },
  "さんかする|参加する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」, to do. The first is 「参|さん」, to go and join, or to visit respectfully. You may hear it in 「お参り|おまいり」, a visit to a shrine or temple to pray, with a different reading. The second is 「加|か」, to add. So, to go and add yourself to something. You join in and become one of the people taking part. The key grammar point is the particle. The event takes 「に」, because you are adding yourself to it. So 「パーティーに参加する|パーティーにさんかする」, to take part in a party, and 「ボランティア活動に参加する|ボランティアかつどうにさんかする」, to take part in volunteer activities. A common mistake is to use 「を」. Use 「に」. This word sounds a little more formal than just going. For a casual party with friends, people often just say 「行く|いく」 or 「出る|でる」. For events, tours, classes and meetings, this word fits well. Someone who participates is a 「参加者|さんかしゃ」. Recap: go and add yourself, to participate." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「加|か」, is easy to pick apart. On the left is 「力|ちから」, power, and on the right is 「口|くち」, mouth. Some books tell a story about adding words to strength, but treat that as a memory hook, since the origin is debated. Its native reading gives the verb 「加える|くわえる」, to add something. And in restaurants you will see 「追加|ついか」, an extra order, when you want one more plate. The first kanji, 「参|さん」, has a native reading you will meet in humble speech, 「参る|まいる」, the humble way to say I go or I come. A host might say 「すぐ参ります|すぐまいります」, I will come right away. You will also meet 「参考|さんこう」, reference, as in a study guide or reference book. So the first kanji humbly goes, the second adds, and together you go and add yourself." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Events in Japan come with their own vocabulary built on this word. A flyer for a workshop might list the 「参加費|さんかひ」, the participation fee, using 「費|ひ」, cost. A note saying 「参加自由|さんかじゆう」 means anyone may join freely. A tour might ask 「参加者は十人までです|さんかしゃはじゅうにんまでです」, up to ten participants. When someone invites you, a polite way to accept is 「ぜひ参加したいです|ぜひさんかしたいです」, I would love to take part. And if you can't make it, people often trail off softly: 「参加したいんですけど|さんかしたいんですけど」, I'd like to join, but, and leave the rest unsaid. Here is a mini dialogue. 「土曜日のイベントに参加しますか|どようびのイベントにさんかしますか」, will you join Saturday's event? 「はい、参加します|はい、さんかします」, yes, I will. So check the fee, check the limit, and answer with 「に」 and this verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「参|さん」 means to go and join and 「加|か」 means to add, so you add yourself to an event, which therefore takes 「に」, not 「を」.",
      "「加|か」 is power plus mouth and gives 「加える|くわえる」, to add, while 「参|さん」 gives the humble verb 「参る|まいる」, to go or come.",
      "Event flyers list the 「参加費|さんかひ」, the participation fee, and you can accept an invitation politely with 「ぜひ参加したいです|ぜひさんかしたいです」."
    ],
    ja: "パーティーに[参加します|さんかします|さんかする]。",
    en: "I will take part in the party." },
  "そつぎょうする|卒業する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」, to do. The first is 「卒|そつ」, to finish or complete. The second is 「業|ぎょう」, work or studies. You know it from 「授業|じゅぎょう」, a class. So, to finish your studies. The ceremony for it is 「卒業式|そつぎょうしき」. The grammar is the interesting part. The school you graduate from takes 「を」, because you are leaving it behind. So 「大学を卒業する|だいがくをそつぎょうする」, to graduate from college. English says from, so learners often want a different particle, but Japanese uses 「を」, the same as when you leave a place, like getting off a train. A cultural note. Japanese uses this word in a fun extended way. When a member leaves a pop idol group, they do not quit, they graduate. People also joke about graduating from a habit, like finally quitting junk food. It makes leaving sound like a proud step forward. Recap: finish plus studies, and the school takes 「を」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word lives in a big family. 「業|ぎょう」 means work, business, or a course of study. Besides 「授業|じゅぎょう」, class, you will meet 「職業|しょくぎょう」, occupation, 「工業|こうぎょう」, industry, and 「作業|さぎょう」, manual work or a task. The kanji 「卒|そつ」 is rare by itself, but it is the core of some very Japanese words. 「卒論|そつろん」 is a graduation thesis, short for a longer phrase. And 「新卒|しんそつ」, new plus graduate, means a fresh graduate. That word matters in Japan, because companies hire most of their new staff as a big group of fresh graduates who all start work together in April. The opposite step, entering a school, is 「入学|にゅうがく」, and its ceremony is 「入学式|にゅうがくしき」. In short, one kanji means finishing, the other means work or study, and their families cover a whole working life." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Graduation in Japan happens in March, right as the cherry blossoms begin to open, and the new school year starts in April. So spring is a season of goodbyes and new beginnings at the same time. At university ceremonies, many women wear a 「はかま」, the pleated skirt like garment over a kimono, often with boots, a style that recalls schoolgirls of about a hundred years ago. In junior high and high school there is a famous romantic custom. A boy's school jacket has buttons down the front, and a girl he likes may ask for his second button, the one closest to the heart. Everyone sings a farewell song, and many people cry. In short, graduation means March, blossoms, kimono skirts, and a button over the heart." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb combines nicely with patterns from your lessons. To talk about life after school, use the conditional 「たら」: 「大学を卒業したら、日本で働きたいです|だいがくをそつぎょうしたら、にほんではたらきたいです」, when I graduate from college, I want to work in Japan. Or use the te form plus 「から」 for after: 「卒業してから、旅行します|そつぎょうしてから、りょこうします」, after graduating, I'll travel. There is also a compact noun, 「卒業後|そつぎょうご」, after graduation, using 「後|ご」, after. And remember the particle trap, because it is easy to slip. Like other leaving verbs, such as 「出る|でる」, to leave a room, and 「降りる|おりる」, to get off a train, the place you leave takes 「を」. In short, use 「たら」 for plans, te form plus 「から」 for sequence, and always the leaving 「を」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「卒|そつ」 means to finish and 「業|ぎょう」 means studies, and the school you leave takes 「を」, as in 「大学を卒業する|だいがくをそつぎょうする」.",
      "「業|ぎょう」 means work or study, as in 「授業|じゅぎょう」 and 「職業|しょくぎょう」, while 「新卒|しんそつ」 means a fresh graduate, the group Japanese companies hire each April.",
      "Japanese graduation comes in March with the cherry blossoms, with women in 「はかま」 at university and girls asking for a boy's second button, closest to his heart.",
      "Use 「卒業したら|そつぎょうしたら」 for plans after graduating and 「卒業してから|そつぎょうしてから」 for sequence, and remember the place you leave takes 「を」."
    ],
    ja: "{来年|らいねん}{大学|だいがく}を[卒業します|そつぎょうします|そつぎょうする]。",
    en: "I will graduate from college next year." },
  "はっぴょうする|発表する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」, to do. The first is 「発|はつ」, to send out, to set off or to start. The second is 「表|ひょう」, to show or express. With its native reading it is 「表|おもて」, the surface or front side. Now watch the sounds change when they join. 「はつ」 shrinks to 「はっ」, a small pause, and 「ひょう」 turns into 「ぴょう」. So you get 「はっぴょう」. This kind of sound change is common when two Chinese-origin readings meet. So, to send something out and show it to people. That covers two uses. One is a presentation, like the talks students give in class. The other is making something public, like announcing news or results. A cultural note. In Japan, university entrance exam results are announced on a set day, and the moment when students check whether their number appears on the list is a big, emotional scene. Recap: send out plus show, to present or announce." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「発|はつ」, means to send out or set off. It gives 「出発|しゅっぱつ」, departure, where it shrinks to 「ぱつ」, and 「発音|はつおん」, pronunciation, literally sending out a sound. That is a word your teacher will use a lot. The second kanji, 「表|ひょう」, means to show or express. On its own, read the same way, 「表|ひょう」 means a chart or table, like a schedule table. In 「代表|だいひょう」, it means a representative, someone who stands in front and shows the group, like a national team. And its native verb 「表す|あらわす」 means to express, as in a face expressing feelings. So departure, pronunciation, chart, and representative all share these two kanji. In short, send out plus show, and the family grows from there." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Students in Japan give a lot of these. In university seminars, students take turns presenting their research, and the person presenting is the 「発表者|はっぴょうしゃ」. A useful sentence from your grammar is 「来週発表があるので、準備しなければいけません|らいしゅうはっぴょうがあるので、じゅんびしなければいけません」, I have a presentation next week, so I have to prepare. Children have the 「発表会|はっぴょうかい」, a recital, where piano students or dance students show what they have learned on stage, and parents film every second. For high school seniors there is the 「合格発表|ごうかくはっぴょう」, the announcement of who passed the entrance exam. Companies also use the word when they unveil a new product. Here is a mini dialogue. 「発表はどうでしたか|はっぴょうはどうでしたか」, how did your presentation go? 「ちょっと緊張しました|ちょっときんちょうしました」, I was a little nervous. In short, seminars, recitals and exam results are all occasions for it." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「発|はつ」 means to send out and 「表|ひょう」 means to show, and together they become 「はっぴょう」, sending something out to show people, as a presentation or an announcement.",
      "「発|はつ」 gives 「出発|しゅっぱつ」, departure, and 「発音|はつおん」, pronunciation, while 「表|ひょう」 gives 「代表|だいひょう」, a representative, and 「表す|あらわす」, to express.",
      "It covers seminar presentations, children's recitals called 「発表会|はっぴょうかい」, and the 「合格発表|ごうかくはっぴょう」, the announcement of who passed the entrance exam."
    ],
    ja: "{授業|じゅぎょう}で[発表しました|はっぴょうしました|はっぴょうする]。",
    en: "I gave a presentation in class." },
  "〜けど|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A small connecting word added to the end of a clause. It is the casual, conversational version of 「けれども」, and it means but, just like 「が」. For example, 「高いけど、おいしい|たかいけど、おいしい」, it is expensive, but tasty. It can follow plain forms or polite forms, so 「高いですけど|たかいですけど」 works too. 「が」 sounds a bit more formal and written, while this one feels softer and very natural in speech. Its second job is the tricky one. It often just gives background, more like and so, or leaves the rest of the sentence unsaid. For example, 「すみません、ちょっと聞きたいんですけど|すみません、ちょっとききたいんですけど」, excuse me, I have a quick question, and then the speaker waits. The listener fills in the rest. Stopping a sentence here sounds polite and gentle, because you are not pushing your point all the way. A common mistake is to think it always means but. Listen for whether there is a contrast. Recap: but, and also a soft way to set things up or leave them hanging." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is the full family, from formal to casual. 「けれども」 is the longest and most careful. Shorten it to 「けれど」, and then to this short form, which is what you will hear most. Now the most common mistake. After a noun or a na adjective in plain form, you must add 「だ」 first. So 「学生だけど|がくせいだけど」, I am a student, but, and 「静かだけど|しずかだけど」, it is quiet, but. Saying the noun with nothing in between sounds wrong. With 「です」 it is simple: 「学生ですけど|がくせいですけど」. It can also start a sentence when it carries 「だ」. 「だけど」 at the front means however, a casual cousin of 「でも」. For example, I want to go. 「だけど、お金がない|だけど、おかねがない」, but I don't have money. In short, remember to add 「だ」 after nouns, and you can even begin a sentence with it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese speakers use this little word as a cushion. When you turn down an invitation, saying no directly can feel harsh. Instead people say 「行きたいんですけど|いきたいんですけど」, I'd like to go, but, and let their voice fade. The listener understands perfectly. On the phone, people introduce themselves with it: 「田中ですけど|たなかですけど」, this is Tanaka, which gently opens the conversation. In a shop, 「これ、ほしいんですけど|これ、ほしいんですけど」 means I'd like this one, as a soft request. Notice how often it follows 「んです」, which explains your situation. Together they say, here is my situation, please take it from here. A mini dialogue. 「明日、ひまですか|あした、ひまですか」, are you free tomorrow? 「明日はちょっと用事があるんですけど|あしたはちょっとようじがあるんですけど」, tomorrow I have a little errand, so. That means no. In short, a trailing version of this word is a polite way to ask, refuse or introduce yourself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the casual form of 「けれども」, meaning but, yet it often just sets up background or leaves a sentence hanging, as in 「聞きたいんですけど|ききたいんですけど」.",
      "After a plain noun or na adjective you must add 「だ」, as in 「学生だけど|がくせいだけど」, and 「だけど」 can even start a sentence to mean however.",
      "A trailing version is a polite cushion, so 「行きたいんですけど|いきたいんですけど」 softly declines, and it also opens phone calls and gentle requests."
    ],
    ja: "{高い|たかい}です[けど]、{買います|かいます}。",
    en: "It's expensive, but I'll buy it." },
  "〜め|〜目": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This ending turns a number into an order, like first, second, third. The kanji is 「目|め」, eye, the same as the body part you learned, with its native reading. A way to remember it, just a hook: picture your eye moving along a row of things, counting one, two, three. It attaches to a number with a counter. So 「一つ目|ひとつめ」 is the first one, 「二回目|にかいめ」 is the second time, 「三日目|みっかめ」 is the third day, and 「一年目|いちねんめ」 is the first year. For order in a line or a ranking, you will often hear 「〜番目|〜ばんめ」, so 「二番目|にばんめ」 is the second. A common mistake. 「一番|いちばん」 on its own means number one, the best. Add this ending, 「一番目|いちばんめ」, and it just means first in order, with no idea of being the best. Directions use it a lot: 「二つ目の角|ふたつめのかど」, the second corner. Recap: number plus counter plus eye, the order of things." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「目|め」 is a real picture. It began as a drawing of an eye, an oval with the pupil in the middle, and over time it was turned up on its end, which is why it now stands tall with two lines across. You can spot it inside other kanji too. 「見る|みる」, to see, is an eye standing on two legs. Here it takes its native reading, 「め」. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「もく」, it appears in 「目的|もくてき」, a purpose or goal, literally the eye's target, and 「科目|かもく」, a school subject. And in 「目次|もくじ」 it means the table of contents. In short, a tall eye, read 「め」 for order, and 「もく」 in serious words." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This ending is everywhere in directions and daily counting. Asking the way, you will hear 「二つ目の信号を右に曲がってください|ふたつめのしんごうをみぎにまがってください」, turn right at the second traffic light. At a café, 「二杯目|にはいめ」 is your second cup, and some places offer the second cup at a discount. Speaking of people, 「一人目|ひとりめ」 is the first person, and parents say 「二人目の子ども|ふたりめのこども」, our second child. For trying again, 「二回目|にかいめ」 or 「二度目|にどめ」 mean the second time. One exception to watch. Floors and dates don't need it. 「三階|さんがい」 already means the third floor, and 「三日|みっか」 already means the third of the month. In short, add it to counted things, cups, people, lights, tries, but not to floors and dates." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch out for a lookalike ending that sounds the same. Attached to an adjective stem, 「め」 means slightly or on the more side. 「大きめ|おおきめ」 is on the large side, 「小さめ|ちいさめ」 is smallish, and 「早めに|はやめに」 means a little early, to be safe. It is often written in kana, sometimes with this same eye kanji, which is where confusion comes from. The trick is what comes before it. A number plus a counter means order, like 「三つ目|みっつめ」, the third one. An adjective without its final 「い」 means a degree, like 「長め|ながめ」, a bit long. A barber might ask if you want it 「短め|みじかめ」, a bit on the short side. In short, numbers before it give order, adjectives before it give slightly." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The eye kanji 「目|め」 added to a number and counter gives order, as in 「二回目|にかいめ」, the second time, and 「一番目|いちばんめ」 just means first in line, not the best.",
      "The kanji 「目|め」 began as a drawing of an eye turned on its end, read 「め」 for order and 「もく」 in serious words like 「目的|もくてき」, a goal.",
      "You hear it in directions like 「二つ目の信号|ふたつめのしんごう」, the second light, and with cups or children, but floors and dates like 「三階|さんがい」 don't need it.",
      "After an adjective stem, the same sound means slightly, as in 「大きめ|おおきめ」, on the large side, so numbers before it give order and adjectives give degree."
    ],
    ja: "{三つ|みっつ}[目|め]の{駅|えき}でおります。",
    en: "I get off at the third station." },
  "いちにちめ|一日目": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「一日|いちにち」, one day, plus the order ending 「目|め」, the eye kanji, which turns a count into first, second, third. So day number one of something, a trip, a course, a festival or a new job. Be careful, because Japanese has three similar words. 「一日|いちにち」 means one day, a length of time. 「一日|ついたち」, the same kanji, means the first day of the month, the date. And this word means the first day in a sequence. You can keep counting with the day words you learned. 「二日目|ふつかめ」 is the second day, and 「三日目|みっかめ」 is the third day. Notice they use the special date readings, 「ふつか」 and 「みっか」, before the ending. A typical sentence: 「旅行の一日目は京都でした|りょこうのいちにちめはきょうとでした」, the first day of the trip was in Kyoto. You will hear this a lot when someone starts a new job and talks about how their first day went. Recap: one day plus the order ending, day one." },
      { title: "Counting the days", text: "Once you know this word, you can count through any trip, but the day readings are irregular, so let's walk them. 「二日目|ふつかめ」, second day. 「三日目|みっかめ」, third. 「四日目|よっかめ」, fourth. 「五日目|いつかめ」, fifth. Notice they all borrow the special date readings, not regular counting. Interestingly, the first day does not use the date reading 「ついたち」. It uses the plain counting reading, 「いちにち」, because 「ついたち」 only means the first of the month. Trip lengths in Japan are often described with nights and days together, like 「二泊三日|にはくみっか」, two nights and three days. So you might say 「三日目に帰りました|みっかめにかえりました」, I went home on the third day. The particle 「に」 marks when something happens. In short, date readings for two onward, but plain 「いちにち」 for day one." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "There are close neighbors that mean almost the same thing. 「初日|しょにち」, beginning plus day, is the first day of an event that has a run, like a play, an exhibit, or a sumo tournament. Theater fans say they went on the opening day. The opposite is 「最終日|さいしゅうび」, the very last day, using 「最|さい」, most, the same kanji as in recently. And 「最初の日|さいしょのひ」 is a plain, everyday way to say the first day. The difference is this. 「初日|しょにち」 sounds official and tied to an event schedule. This word just counts days in order, so it fits your own trip, your diet, or your first week at a new job. Someone might say 「ダイエット三日目|ダイエットみっかめ」, day three of my diet, with a sigh. In short, count your own days with this ending, and save 「初日|しょにち」 for opening day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「一日|いちにち」 plus the order ending 「目|め」, meaning day one of a sequence, unlike 「一日|いちにち」, one day long, or 「一日|ついたち」, the first of the month.",
      "From day two on you use the date readings, as in 「二日目|ふつかめ」 and 「三日目|みっかめ」, but day one keeps the plain reading 「いちにち」.",
      "「初日|しょにち」 is the official opening day of an event like a play or sumo tournament, while this word just counts your own days, like day three of a diet."
    ],
    ja: "りょこうの[一日目|いちにちめ]は{東京|とうきょう}でした。",
    en: "The first day of the trip was in Tokyo." },
  "いちにちじゅう|一日中": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is 「一日|いちにち」, one day, plus 「中|じゅう」. You know 「中|なか」 with its native reading, inside, and the Chinese-origin reading is usually 「ちゅう」. But after a word of time or place, it often turns into 「じゅう」 and means all through, the whole of it. So, all through the day, all day long. Compare the two sounds, because they mean different things. 「授業中に|じゅぎょうちゅうに」, with 「ちゅう」, means during class. 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」, with 「じゅう」, means by the end of today, within the whole day. Here are more with the same ending: 「一年中|いちねんじゅう」, all year round, and 「世界中|せかいじゅう」, all over the world. Grammar tip. It works like an adverb, so it goes right before the verb with no particle: 「一日中寝ていました|いちにちじゅうねていました」, I slept all day. It often carries a feeling of too much, a whole day of work or rain. Recap: one day plus all through, all day long." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you know this all through ending, you can stretch it over any span of time or space. 「一晩中|ひとばんじゅう」 is all night long, using the native word for one night. 「日本中|にほんじゅう」 is all over Japan, as in the whole country is watching the game. 「家中|いえじゅう」 is all over the house, perfect for when you have searched everywhere for your keys. Now flip to the other reading, 「ちゅう」, which means in the middle of an activity. You will see it on signs. 「営業中|えいぎょうちゅう」 on a shop door means open for business. 「準備中|じゅんびちゅう」 means preparing, so not open yet. 「電話中|でんわちゅう」 means on the phone, and 「食事中|しょくじちゅう」 means in the middle of a meal. Here is a rough guide. Spans of time or place usually take 「じゅう」, and ongoing activities usually take 「ちゅう」. In short, all through a span, or busy in the middle of something." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a silly memory trick built on the sound. Break the word into three English-ish chunks: itchy, nichi, juice. Now picture a terrible day off. You wake up itchy from a mosquito bite. You scratch, you scratch, you scratch. The clock says morning, then noon, then evening, and you are still itchy. The only thing that helps is cold juice, so you drink juice all day long, glass after glass, while scratching on the sofa. That is a whole day ruined, from the first itch to the last sip of juice. Itchy, nichi, juice, all day long. The real word, of course, is 「いちにちじゅう」, one day plus all through. Keep the itchy juice day in mind and the meaning will stick. Remember, a scratchy, juicy day that never ends." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word loves complaints and confessions. 「一日中雨でした|いちにちじゅうあめでした」, it rained all day. 「一日中ゲームをしていました|いちにちじゅうゲームをしていました」, I played video games all day, maybe said a bit guiltily. A parent might say to a teenager, 「一日中寝ているの|いちにちじゅうねているの」, are you going to sleep all day? Notice how often it pairs with the te form plus 「いる」, because it describes something that went on and on. Be careful not to mix it up with 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, which is about repetition across many days. This word is about one single day, filled from start to finish. And plain 「一日|いちにち」 is just one day as a length of time, as in it takes one day. So you can say I study every day, but yesterday, I studied all day. In short, one day stretched from morning to night." }
    ],
    short: [
      "After a time or place word, 「中|じゅう」 means all through, so this is all day long, unlike 「ちゅう」, during, and it goes right before the verb with no particle.",
      "「じゅう」 stretches over spans like 「一晩中|ひとばんじゅう」, all night, and 「日本中|にほんじゅう」, all over Japan, while 「ちゅう」 marks ongoing activity, as in 「営業中|えいぎょうちゅう」.",
      "Picture an itchy day off where you sip juice all day long while scratching: itchy, nichi, juice, 「いちにちじゅう」.",
      "It loves complaints like 「一日中雨でした|いちにちじゅうあめでした」, it rained all day, and covers one full day, unlike 「毎日|まいにち」, every day."
    ],
    ja: "[一日中|いちにちじゅう]べんきょうしました。",
    en: "I studied all day long." },
  "さいきん|最近": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「最|さい」, the most. It makes superlatives, like 「最高|さいこう」, the best, literally the highest. The second is 「近|きん」, near. With its native reading it is 「近い|ちかい」, close by. Look at its bottom left, the moving along a road part, the same one in 「週|しゅう」, week. So the nearest stretch of time, recently, lately. A grammar tip. It often goes with a verb in the te form plus 「いる」, for something that has been going on: 「最近、日本語を勉強しています|さいきん、にほんごをべんきょうしています」, lately I have been studying Japanese. It also works with adjectives, like 「最近いそがしいです|さいきんいそがしいです」, I have been busy lately. And it can be used with the past for a single recent event. A related word you know is 「このごろ」, these days, which feels similar. The opposite idea would be long ago. Recap: the most plus near, the nearest stretch of time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to useful families. 「最|さい」, the most, builds a whole set of extremes. 「最高|さいこう」, the best. Its opposite, 「最低|さいてい」, literally the lowest, means the worst, and people say it about a terrible movie or a rude person. 「最初|さいしょ」, the very first, and 「最後|さいご」, the very last. You will hear these two constantly. The second kanji, 「近|きん」, near, has its own team. With its native reading you know 「近い|ちかい」, and the noun 「近く|ちかく」, the vicinity, as in near the station. With its Chinese-origin reading it gives 「近所|きんじょ」, the neighborhood, the near places. So the neighbors are the 「近所の人|きんじょのひと」. In short, 「さい」 pushes things to the extreme, and 「きん」 keeps them close, together the closest bit of time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A common mistake is using this word for the future. English recently only points backward, and so does this word. For soon, use 「もうすぐ」 instead. It also works as a noun before 「の」: 「最近の映画|さいきんのえいが」, recent movies, and 「最近のニュース|さいきんのニュース」, the latest news. Older people have a classic grumble, 「最近の若い人は|さいきんのわかいひとは」, young people these days, usually followed by a complaint. It's a cliché, and younger people often joke about it. It is also a perfect conversation opener. 「最近どう|さいきんどう」, how have you been lately, is what friends ask when they haven't met for a while. A typical answer might be 「最近、忙しいです|さいきん、いそがしいです」, or 「最近、ジムに行っています|さいきん、ジムにいっています」, lately I've been going to the gym. In short, it looks backward, works before 「の」, and opens a good chat." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「最|さい」 means the most and 「近|きん」 means near, so this is the nearest stretch of time, often with the te form plus 「いる」 for something ongoing lately.",
      "「最|さい」 builds extremes like 「最高|さいこう」, the best, and 「最初|さいしょ」, the first, while 「近|きん」 gives 「近所|きんじょ」, the neighborhood.",
      "It only looks backward, so use 「もうすぐ」 for soon, and 「最近どう|さいきんどう」 is a friendly opener meaning how have you been lately."
    ],
    ja: "[最近|さいきん]いそがしいです。",
    en: "I've been busy recently." },
  "もういちど|もう一度": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces. The first is 「もう」. You may know it as already, but before a number or an amount, it means more or another. For example, 「もう少し|もうすこし」, a little more, and 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」, one more. The second piece is 「一度|いちど」, one time. The kanji 「度|ど」 is the counter for times. It is the same counter you learned for degrees of temperature, so it does double duty. 「二度|にど」 is twice, or two degrees. So, one more time. It is perfect for class. If you did not catch something, say 「もう一度お願いします|もういちどおねがいします」, once more, please. Or 「もう一度言ってください|もういちどいってください」, please say it again. A related word is 「〜回|〜かい」, which also counts times, so 「もう一回|もういっかい」 means the same and sounds a little more casual. Kids shout it constantly when they want to play a game again. Recap: more plus one time, one more time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's sort out the again words. 「もう一回|もういっかい」 means the same as this phrase, but feels more casual. 「また」 simply means again, in general: 「また来ます|またきます」, I'll come again, or the friendly goodbye 「またね」, see you. It doesn't count anything. This phrase is more precise, exactly one more time, often right now. Now play with the counter. 「一度|いちど」 alone means once, and with a negative, 「一度も|いちども」 means not even once, never. With the pattern of having done something from your lessons, 「一度も行ったことがありません|いちどもいったことがありません」 means I have never been there, not even once. And 「今度|こんど」, now plus time, means next time or this coming time, as in 「今度、飲みに行きましょう|こんど、のみにいきましょう」, let's go for drinks sometime. In short, again, never, and next time, all from one little counter." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is a survival phrase for any learner, so let's polish it. With your teacher, 「もう一度お願いします|もういちどおねがいします」 is perfect. If someone speaks too fast, add a word for slowly: 「もう一度、ゆっくりお願いします|もういちど、ゆっくりおねがいします」, once more, slowly please. To be extra polite, ask 「もう一度言っていただけませんか|もういちどいっていただけませんか」, could you possibly say that again? That uses the receiving grammar, which sounds humble and soft. On the phone, if you couldn't hear, it pairs well with 「すみません」 at the start. Japanese people are used to repeating things for learners, so don't be shy. Asking clearly is better than nodding and guessing. And when someone says it to you, just repeat what you said, a little slower. In short, ask once more, ask slowly, and ask politely if needed." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Before a number 「もう」 means more, and 「一度|いちど」 is one time, so it is one more time, as in 「もう一度お願いします|もういちどおねがいします」 in class.",
      "「もう一回|もういっかい」 is the casual twin, 「また」 is general again, 「一度も|いちども」 with a negative means never, and 「今度|こんど」 means next time.",
      "Add slowly with 「もう一度、ゆっくりお願いします|もういちど、ゆっくりおねがいします」, or be extra polite with 「もう一度言っていただけませんか|もういちどいっていただけませんか」."
    ],
    ja: "[もう一度|もういちど]{言って|いって}ください。",
    en: "Please say it one more time." },
  "たのしみです|楽しみです": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from 「楽しい|たのしい」, fun or enjoyable, using the native reading of the kanji. Drop the adjective ending, add 「み」, and you get a noun, 「楽しみ|たのしみ」, something to enjoy, a pleasure still ahead of you. Add 「です」, and you are saying, this is something I am looking forward to. The thing you look forward to takes 「が」: 「旅行が楽しみです|りょこうがたのしみです」, I cannot wait for the trip. A longer form is 「楽しみにしています|たのしみにしています」, I am looking forward to it. A common mistake is to use it for something fun happening now. For right now, say 「楽しいです|たのしいです」. This one points to the future. Family of words. The same kanji, read with its Chinese-origin reading 「がく」, gives 「音楽|おんがく」, music, sound plus enjoyment, and with 「がっ」, 「楽器|がっき」, a musical instrument. Recap: the fun that is still coming, I cannot wait." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Compare two ways to turn this adjective into a noun. You may know the 「さ」 ending, which measures a quality. 「楽しさ|たのしさ」 means fun-ness, how much fun something has, as in the fun of learning Japanese. The 「み」 ending is different. It is much rarer, and it works only with certain adjectives, so you can't add it to just any word. It names a feeling or a thing you experience. Other examples are 「悲しみ|かなしみ」, sorrow, 「痛み|いたみ」, a pain, and 「甘み|あまみ」, sweetness as a taste. With this word it became something you enjoy, especially something still ahead. That is why the 「さ」 version describes how fun something is, while the 「み」 version is the treat waiting for you. Remember, 「さ」 measures, and 「み」 is the feeling you hold." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase is the polite way to end a lot of conversations and messages. After setting a date with someone, people say 「楽しみにしています|たのしみにしています」, I'm looking forward to it. In a business email, you might write 「お会いできるのを楽しみにしています|おあいできるのをたのしみにしています」, I look forward to meeting you. When you have a surprise for someone, you can tease them with 「楽しみにしててね|たのしみにしててね」, look forward to it! The noun alone can also mean a source of joy in life. 「毎朝のコーヒーが楽しみです|まいあさのコーヒーがたのしみです」, my morning coffee is my little pleasure. A quick dialogue. 「来週、日本ですね|らいしゅう、にほんですね」, next week you go to Japan, right? 「はい、とても楽しみです|はい、とてもたのしみです」, yes, I can't wait. In short, use it to close plans, sign off emails, and name life's little joys." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「楽|たの」 has a third reading you will love, 「らく」, meaning easy or comfortable. 「楽な仕事|らくなしごと」 is an easy job, used as a na adjective. When you sit on a soft sofa, you sigh 「楽だ|らくだ」, this is comfy. And 「気が楽になった|きがらくになった」 means I feel relieved. So this one kanji covers fun, music and ease. The traditional explanation is that the old form of the kanji pictured a musical instrument on a wooden stand, with the wood part still visible at the bottom. Scholars debate the details, so take the instrument picture as the common story rather than certain fact. Either way, it ties nicely to music, 「音楽|おんがく」. So, three readings: 「たの」 for fun, 「がく」 for music, and 「らく」 for ease. In short, a comfortable chair, a song, and a happy wait." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Adding 「み」 to the stem of 「楽しい|たのしい」 makes a noun for a pleasure still ahead, so the thing you're looking forward to takes 「が」, as in 「旅行が楽しみです|りょこうがたのしみです」.",
      "The 「さ」 ending in 「楽しさ|たのしさ」 measures how much fun something has, while the rarer 「み」 ending names the felt treat waiting for you.",
      "Use 「楽しみにしています|たのしみにしています」 to close plans or sign off emails, and the noun alone can name a little daily joy like your morning coffee.",
      "The kanji also reads 「がく」 for music, as in 「音楽|おんがく」, and 「らく」 for ease, as in 「楽な仕事|らくなしごと」, an easy job."
    ],
    ja: "{旅行|りょこう}が[楽しみです|たのしみです]。",
    en: "I'm really looking forward to the trip." },
  "えきいん|駅員": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「駅|えき」, station. Look at its left side and you'll find the horse part, 「馬|うま」. That's real history. Long before trains, a station was a relay post on the road where riders swapped tired horses for fresh ones. The word stayed when the railways came. The second kanji is 「員|いん」, a member or staff person. You already know it from 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, a company person, an office worker. So the pattern is simple. Place plus 「員|いん」 means a person who works there as staff. A station plus staff gives you the people in uniforms at the ticket gates. In Japan they're the ones to ask when you're lost, when your ticket won't go through the gate, or when you need to know which platform to use. They're famous for being polite and precise, and at big stations they even help push commuters onto packed trains at rush hour. To recap: 「駅|えき」, the old horse relay station, plus 「員|いん」, staff member, gives station staff." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「員|いん」, member or staff, is a real word maker. 「店員|てんいん」 is a shop clerk, shop plus staff. 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, as you know, is an office worker. 「銀行員|ぎんこういん」 is a bank employee, and 「公務員|こうむいん」 is a civil servant. It also means members as a group. 「全員|ぜんいん」, all members, means everyone, as in 「全員来ました|ぜんいんきました」, everyone came. And 「満員|まんいん」, full of members, means packed, full to capacity. That gives you the dreaded 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」, the packed rush hour train, where 「駅員|えきいん」 are hard at work. Notice that these are job titles, not polite ways to address someone. To call a clerk, people just say 「すみません」. In short, a place plus this kanji means the person who works there, and full plus this kanji means a packed train." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how to deal with station staff in practice. To get their attention, say 「すみません」. When people talk about them, they often add 「さん」: 「駅員さんに聞いてください|えきいんさんにきいてください」, please ask the station staff. They work at the window by the ticket gates, often in a small office. Common problems they help with are a card that won't open the gate, a lost item, which is a 「忘れ物|わすれもの」, or a wrong ticket. A mini dialogue. 「すみません、カードが使えないんですけど|すみません、カードがつかえないんですけど」, excuse me, my card isn't working. The staff member says 「ちょっと見せてください|ちょっとみせてください」, let me see it. One more distinction. The person checking tickets on the train itself is a conductor, 「車掌|しゃしょう」, not station staff. In short, find them at the gate, start with 「すみません」, and explain the problem." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「駅|えき」 has the horse part because stations were once relay posts for swapping horses, and 「員|いん」 means staff member, so this is the station staff.",
      "Place plus 「員|いん」 makes a job title, like 「店員|てんいん」, a clerk, while 「全員|ぜんいん」 means everyone and 「満員|まんいん」 means packed.",
      "Get their attention with 「すみません」, find them by the ticket gates for card problems or lost items, and remember the conductor on the train is a 「車掌|しゃしょう」."
    ],
    ja: "[駅員|えきいん]に{道|みち}を{聞きました|ききました}。",
    en: "I asked the station attendant for directions." },
  "おや|親": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here the kanji is read with its native reading, 「おや」. In other words it switches to the Chinese-origin reading 「しん」, and that's where its family lives. In 「両親|りょうしん」 it's parents, both of them, since 「両|りょう」 means both. In 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, and in 「親せき|しんせき」, relatives, the same kanji carries the idea of closeness, being dear and intimate. That's a nice way to hold it together. A parent is the closest person of all. Now the shape. A popular memory hook, and it is a hook, not the real history, splits the kanji into three parts. 「立|た」, stand, 「木|き」, tree, and 「見|み」, see. Picture a parent standing up on a tree, watching over their child from above. One usage note. When you talk about your own parents politely, 「両親|りょうしん」 is the usual word. 「親|おや」 by itself is a bit more general and plain, like saying parent or parents in a broad sense, as in 「親にでんわします|おやにでんわします」. Recap: native 「おや」 for parent, Chinese-origin 「しん」 for closeness in 「両親|りょうしん」 and 「親切|しんせつ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The native reading 「おや」 shows up in some charming compounds. 「親子|おやこ」 is parent and child. That gives the famous rice bowl 「親子丼|おやこどん」, chicken and egg over rice, literally a parent and child bowl, because the chicken and the egg are in it together. A bit dark, but people find it funny. Your thumb is 「親指|おやゆび」, the parent finger, the big one that watches over the others. With the Chinese-origin reading, 「しん」, there is 「親友|しんゆう」, a best friend, close plus friend. That is a stronger word than just a friend, so use it for your truly closest friend. And 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, is a word you know already. So the native reading stays with parents and things like parents, while the Chinese-origin reading carries closeness. In short, a chicken and egg bowl, a thumb, and a best friend." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Two well known phrases show how Japanese culture thinks about parents. The first is 「親孝行|おやこうこう」, being good to your parents, filial devotion. It comes from a Confucian ideal that was very strong in East Asia. People call a child who takes their parents on a trip or calls home often 「親孝行な子|おやこうこうなこ」, a devoted child. The opposite, 「親不孝|おやふこう」, means making your parents worry or suffer. The second phrase is 「親ばか|おやばか」, a parent fool, a doting parent who thinks their child is the cutest genius on earth. People say it about themselves with a laugh while showing baby photos. Also remember the respect rule. For someone else's parents, say 「ご両親|ごりょうしん」. For your own, say 「両親|りょうしん」 or 「親|おや」. In short, be devoted, laugh at your own doting, and add 「ご」 for other people's parents." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Read 「おや」 it means parent, and read 「しん」 it carries closeness, as in 「両親|りょうしん」, parents, and 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, since a parent is the closest person of all.",
      "The native reading gives 「親子丼|おやこどん」, the chicken and egg parent and child bowl, and 「親指|おやゆび」, the thumb, while 「しん」 gives 「親友|しんゆう」, a best friend.",
      "Japanese values 「親孝行|おやこうこう」, devotion to parents, laughs about being a doting 「親ばか|おやばか」, and uses 「ご両親|ごりょうしん」 for other people's parents."
    ],
    ja: "[親|おや]に{電話|でんわ}をかけました。",
    en: "I called my parents." },
  "しんせき|親せき": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first part is 「親|しん」, the same kanji as 「親|おや」, parent, but here it takes its Chinese-origin reading. It carries the idea of being close and dear, the same as in 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, which is literally close and caring, and 「両親|りょうしん」, parents. The second part, 「せき」, has its own kanji that also means relatives, but it's not on the everyday kanji list, so you'll usually see it written in kana, just like here. So the whole word is close family people. It covers your aunts, uncles, cousins, grandparents' side, everyone related to you beyond your own household. A cultural note that helps it stick. In Japan, the big times for seeing 「親せき|しんせき」 are 「お正月|おしょうがつ」, New Year's, and 「お盆|おぼん」 in summer, when people travel back to their hometowns. Kids often receive little envelopes of New Year's money from all their relatives, so for children, a big family means a good New Year. Recap: 「親|しん」, closeness, plus 「せき」, kin, gives your relatives." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「親|しん」 opens a whole family of words about closeness. With its native reading it is 「親|おや」, a parent, and it also shows up at the end of 「父親|ちちおや」, father, and 「母親|ははおや」, mother. With its Chinese-origin reading it gives 「親友|しんゆう」, a best friend, literally a close friend, and the adjective 「親しい|したしい」, close or intimate, uses the native reading again. Now an important contrast. 「家族|かぞく」 is your family, usually the people you live with or grew up with, like parents and siblings. 「親せき|しんせき」 reaches outward to everyone related to you beyond that circle. English speakers often say family for both, but in Japanese an aunt or a cousin is normally a 「親せき|しんせき」, not 「家族|かぞく」. So keep the two circles separate. The inner circle is family, and the outer circle is relatives." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are some phrases you will hear. 「親せきの家|しんせきのいえ」 is a relative's house, a common place to stay during holidays. 「遠い親せき|とおいしんせき」 means a distant relative, someone you are only loosely connected to. A gathering of the whole clan is 「親せきの集まり|しんせきのあつまり」, and for many people those gatherings mean weddings, funerals, and the New Year. Children often call relatives by role rather than name, like 「おじさん」 and 「おばさん」 for uncles and aunts. A short dialogue. One friend asks, 「お正月はどうするの|おしょうがつはどうするの」, what are you doing for New Year's? The other says, 「親せきの家に行くよ|しんせきのいえにいくよ」, I'm going to my relatives' place. The word itself is neutral, fine for both casual and polite speech. So when the holidays come, you head to the relatives' house." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on sound. The second half of the word sounds like 「せき」, which is also the word for a seat, written with a completely different kanji, 「席|せき」. They are not related at all, but the coincidence makes a good picture. Imagine New Year's Day at grandma's house. The doorbell keeps ringing. More aunts, more uncles, more cousins. Every one of them needs a seat, so the low table gets longer and longer, cushions spread across the whole floor, and somebody is sitting on the stairs. Grandma counts on her fingers and sighs that there are not enough seats for all these relatives. Relatives plus not enough seats. Remember that crowded floor, and the word will come right back." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「親|しん」 carries closeness, as in 「親切|しんせつ」, and 「せき」 means kin, so these are your close family people beyond your own household, often seen at New Year's and 「お盆|おぼん」.",
      "「家族|かぞく」 is the inner circle you live or grew up with, while 「親せき|しんせき」 reaches out to aunts, uncles and cousins, so keep the two circles separate.",
      "You will often hear 「親せきの家|しんせきのいえ」, a relative's house where people stay over the holidays, and 「遠い親せき|とおいしんせき」, a distant relative.",
      "Picture New Year's at grandma's house with so many relatives arriving that there aren't enough 「席|せき」, seats, a sound coincidence that brings back the word."
    ],
    ja: "お{正月|しょうがつ}に[親せき|しんせき]が{来ます|きます}。",
    en: "Relatives come over at New Year's." },
  "ごみ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one is a native word, and in daily life it's written in kana, often in katakana too, so you'll see it both ways on signs. There is an old kanji spelling, but you won't need it. What matters more is how much this word comes up in Japanese life. Japan sorts its garbage very carefully. Each neighborhood has a schedule with set days for burnable trash, plastics, cans and bottles, and big items. You put the right bag out on the right morning, at the collection spot, and neighbors really do notice if you get it wrong. You'll also notice that public trash cans are rare, so people often carry their 「ごみ」 home. The verbs to pair with it are 「すてる」, to throw away, as in 「ごみをすてる」, and 「出す|だす」, which is what you say for putting the garbage out for collection, 「ごみを出す|ごみをだす」. Recap: 「ごみ」 is garbage, it goes out on the right day, and you 「出す|だす」 it or 「すてる」 it." },
      { title: "More than garbage", text: "This word covers more than bags of household trash. It also means any small bit of dirt or a speck of something that does not belong. If something flies into your eye, you say 「目にごみが入った|めにごみがはいった」, I got something in my eye. A bit of lint on your sweater is also 「ごみ」. And there is a doubled form, 「ごみごみ」, which describes a place that is crowded and cluttered, like a maze of narrow streets packed with buildings, 「ごみごみした町|ごみごみしたまち」. Compare 「ほこり」, which is dust that settles on shelves. The container for it is 「ごみ箱|ごみばこ」, trash can, using 「箱|はこ」, box, which softens to 「ばこ」 when it joins the word. So think of it as anything unwanted, from a full garbage bag down to a tiny speck." },
      { title: "Sorting words", text: "If you live in Japan, a handful of labels will rule your week. 「燃えるごみ|もえるごみ」 is burnable garbage, using the verb 「燃える|もえる」, to burn. Its opposite is 「燃えないごみ|もえないごみ」, non-burnable garbage, the same verb in its negative form. Notice how neatly the grammar works, a plain verb sitting right in front of the noun to describe it. Big items like furniture or a broken bicycle are 「粗大ごみ|そだいごみ」, oversized garbage, and you usually have to book a pickup and pay a small fee with a sticker. Recyclables like cans and bottles often get the loanword 「リサイクル」. Some towns are famous for sorting into dozens of categories. You will also want the verb 「分ける|わける」, to separate, as in 「ごみを分ける|ごみをわける」. Learn burnable and non-burnable first, and the rest follows." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's tidiness starts in childhood. In most schools there are no janitors cleaning the classrooms every day. Instead, students have 「そうじ」 time, when they sweep floors, wipe desks, and take out the trash themselves. The idea is that if you clean a place, you take care of it. That habit shows up everywhere. After festivals and fireworks, volunteers and visitors pick up litter. Japanese soccer fans have made international news for staying after matches at the World Cup to collect trash in the stands. And on hiking trails you will often see signs asking you to take your trash home with you. So the word is not just a noun for something dirty. It carries a whole attitude of responsibility for shared spaces. Clean up after yourself, and you are acting very Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japan sorts its garbage onto set collection days, so you put it out with 「ごみを出す|ごみをだす」 on the right morning, or throw it away with 「すてる」.",
      "It also means any speck that doesn't belong, as in 「目にごみが入った|めにごみがはいった」, something got in my eye, and the trash can is a 「ごみ箱|ごみばこ」.",
      "Learn 「燃えるごみ|もえるごみ」, burnable garbage, and 「燃えないごみ|もえないごみ」, non-burnable garbage, first, with a plain verb sitting right before the noun.",
      "Japanese students clean their own classrooms, and that habit of caring for shared spaces shows up in fans collecting trash after matches and hikers taking litter home."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}[ごみ]を{捨てます|すてます}。",
    en: "I take out the garbage in the morning." },
  "さとう|砂糖": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「砂|さ」, sand. Its shape is a nice one. On the left is the stone part, and on the right is the kanji for little or few. Little bits of stone. That's sand. On its own, sand is the native word 「すな」, but in this word it's read 「さ」. The second kanji is 「糖|とう」, sugar itself, and you can spot the rice part on its left side, a part that shows up in lots of food and grain kanji. Put them together and you get grainy sugar, sugar that looks like sand. Think of how white granulated sugar pours just like fine sand. 「糖|とう」 also appears in science and health words, for example in words about blood sugar, so it's a useful one to recognize. Pair this word with 「甘い|あまい」, sweet, which you already know, and with the verb 「入れる|いれる」, to put in, as in putting 「砂糖|さとう」 in your coffee. In Japanese cooking, sugar goes into many savory dishes, like sukiyaki and teriyaki sauce, which is why lots of Japanese food has that sweet and salty balance. Recap: sand plus sugar gives sand-like sugar." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese home cooks learn a little rhyme for the order of adding seasonings, and sugar comes first. It is called 「さしすせそ」. 「さ」 is for 「砂糖|さとう」, sugar. 「し」 is for 「塩|しお」, salt. 「す」 is for 「酢|す」, vinegar. 「せ」 is for 「しょうゆ」, soy sauce, because its old spelling began with that sound. And 「そ」 is for 「みそ」, miso. The usual explanation is practical. Sugar goes in early because its particles are large and take longer to soak into food, while salt firms things up and makes it harder for sugar to get in. Soy sauce and miso go in near the end so their aroma does not cook away. So when you simmer a Japanese dish, remember the order of the kana row. Sugar first, miso last." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is something that surprises many learners. The same sound, 「さとう」, is also the most common family name in Japan, written 「佐藤|さとう」, with completely different kanji. There are millions of people named 「佐藤|さとう」, so you will certainly meet a 「佐藤さん|さとうさん」 at school or work. The two have nothing to do with each other. Context almost always makes it clear. If someone says 「佐藤さん|さとうさん」 with the polite title, it is a person. If it comes with 「入れる|いれる」, to put in, or 「甘い|あまい」, sweet, it is the sweetener. Japanese people even joke about it now and then. When you want to sound a little softer, as in the kitchen or at a café, you can also say 「お砂糖|おさとう」, adding the polite 「お」. So it is one sound with two lives, a sweetener and a very common last name." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word has relatives worth knowing. 「砂|さ」, sand, appears in 「砂漠|さばく」, desert, a place that is all sand. With its native reading 「すな」 it shows up in 「砂浜|すなはま」, a sandy beach. The other kanji, 「糖|とう」, is the one you will spot on food labels. 「糖分|とうぶん」 means sugar content, the amount of sugar in something, and drinks advertised as low sugar use this kanji in big letters. Health talk about blood sugar uses it too. Put those beside some kitchen words you know, like 「塩|しお」, salt, and 「甘い|あまい」, sweet, and you have a little set for talking about taste. One more useful word is 「和菓子|わがし」, traditional Japanese sweets, many of which are built on sugar and sweet bean paste. Remember sand for the grains and sugar for the sweetness on the label." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「砂|さ」 is sand, little bits of stone, and 「糖|とう」 is sugar with the rice part on its left, so this is sugar that pours like sand.",
      "Cooks add seasonings in the order 「さしすせそ」, starting with 「砂糖|さとう」 because its large particles soak in slowly, and ending with miso so the aroma survives.",
      "The same sound is Japan's most common family name, 「佐藤|さとう」, with different kanji, so a polite title means a person and 「甘い|あまい」 or 「入れる|いれる」 means the sweetener.",
      "「砂|さ」 appears in 「砂漠|さばく」, desert, and 「砂浜|すなはま」, a sandy beach, while 「糖|とう」 shows up on food labels as 「糖分|とうぶん」, sugar content."
    ],
    ja: "コーヒーに[砂糖|さとう]を{入れます|いれます}。",
    en: "I put sugar in my coffee." },
  "ファイル|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from the English word file. Japanese has no f sound of its own, so it uses 「フ」 with a small 「ァ」 to get close to fa. The English l sound becomes an r sound, and since Japanese words like to end in a vowel, an extra u is added at the end, giving the 「ル」. That's how file turns into this word. The meaning stretched a little in Japan. Yes, it means a computer file. But in everyday life it often means a physical folder or binder for papers. The most famous one is the 「クリアファイル」, the clear plastic sleeve folder that every Japanese student and office worker carries. Shops and anime stores sell them with characters printed on them, and companies give them away as free gifts. So if someone in Japan says they put a handout in their 「ファイル」, picture a plastic folder, not a computer. It's a cousin of other loanwords with the same squeeze, like 「ホテル」 from hotel, where the l also turns into r and gets a vowel. Recap: file, squeezed into Japanese sounds, meaning both a document folder and a computer file." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "On the computer side, the word pairs with a familiar set of verbs. You open a file with 「開く|ひらく」, as in 「ファイルを開く|ファイルをひらく」. You save it with 「保存する|ほぞんする」, and you send it with 「送る|おくる」. Attaching a file to an email is 「添付する|てんぷする」, and an attached file is often just called a 「添付ファイル|てんぷファイル」. On the paper side, you put a handout into your folder with 「入れる|いれる」, so 「プリントをファイルに入れる|プリントをファイルにいれる」, put the printout in the folder. Notice that a classroom handout in Japan is usually called a 「プリント」, another loanword. A quick office exchange. A coworker asks, 「ファイル、送ってもらえますか|ファイル、おくってもらえますか」, could you send me the file? You answer, 「はい、すぐ送ります|はい、すぐおくります」, sure, I'll send it right away. Open, save, send, and attach, the same four moves as in English." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Count the beats in this word and you get three, 「ファ」, 「イ」, 「ル」. The small 「ァ」 does not get a beat of its own. It just bends 「フ」 into a fa sound, and the two letters together take one beat. That small letter trick shows up in lots of modern loanwords, like 「ファン」, fan, 「ファッション」, fashion, and 「ファミリー」, family. The vowel sound of the English word comes across as two separate beats, 「ア」 then 「イ」, which is why it is not written with a long vowel mark. Compare 「フィルム」, from film, where English has a short i, so Japanese uses 「フィ」 instead. Say it evenly, with each beat about the same length, and avoid squeezing it into one English syllable. Three even beats, and the small letter rides along with the first one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from English file squeezed into Japanese sounds, and besides a computer file it often means a physical plastic folder, like the famous 「クリアファイル」.",
      "On a computer you 「開く|ひらく」, 「保存する|ほぞんする」 and 「送る|おくる」 a file, and attach one with 「添付する|てんぷする」, while paper handouts go into it with 「入れる|いれる」.",
      "It has three even beats, 「ファ」, 「イ」 and 「ル」, with the small 「ァ」 riding along on the first beat to bend it into a fa sound."
    ],
    ja: "この[ファイル]は{私|わたし}のです。",
    en: "This folder is mine." },
  "おおきさ|大きさ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built with a handy bit of grammar. Start with the adjective 「大きい|おおきい」, big. Drop the final 「い」 and add 「さ」. The 「さ」 turns an adjective into a noun meaning how much of that quality something has. So big becomes bigness, which in English we'd just call size. The kanji 「大|おお」 here uses its native reading, the same as in 「大きい|おおきい」. Compare 「大学|だいがく」, college, where the same kanji takes its Chinese-origin reading 「だい」. The 「さ」 pattern works on many adjectives. 「長い|ながい」, long, becomes 「長さ|ながさ」, length. 「高い|たかい」, high, becomes 「高さ|たかさ」, height. 「おもい」, heavy, becomes 「おもさ」, weight. Once you know the trick, you can make a whole set of measurement words on your own. To ask about size, combine it with 「どのぐらい」, as in 「大きさはどのぐらいですか|おおきさはどのぐらいですか」, about how big is it. Recap: 「大きい|おおきい」 minus 「い」 plus 「さ」 gives size." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The noun-making 「さ」 works on almost any adjective, not just 「い」 adjectives. With 「な」 adjectives, you simply attach it to the stem. 「静か|しずか」, quiet, becomes 「静かさ|しずかさ」, quietness. 「便利|べんり」, convenient, becomes 「便利さ|べんりさ」, convenience. Now one quirk to watch. The adjective 「いい」, good, has an old form 「よい」, and it is that form that takes the ending, giving 「よさ」, the goodness or the strong points of something. You never build it from 「いい」 for that meaning. There is also a cousin ending, 「み」, used with a smaller set of adjectives, such as 「甘み|あまみ」, a sweet taste, from 「甘い|あまい」. The difference is subtle. The 「さ」 version measures an amount, while the 「み」 version describes a felt quality. So reach for 「さ」 when you want to measure how much, and remember the little quirk with 「よさ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「大|おお」 has three readings you will meet constantly, and it helps to sort them. The native reading 「おお」 shows up in 「大きい|おおきい」, big, in 「大雨|おおあめ」, heavy rain, and in 「大勢|おおぜい」, a crowd of people. The Chinese-origin reading 「だい」 shows up in 「大学|だいがく」, college, and in 「大好き|だいすき」, to love something. And there is a third reading, 「たい」, in 「大切|たいせつ」, precious or important, and 「大変|たいへん」, tough or terrible. There is no simple rule for which one appears, so learn them word by word, but notice that 「おお」 often joins native words, while 「だい」 and 「たい」 tend to join Chinese-origin words. In this word it is 「おお」, because 「大きさ|おおきさ」 is built directly from the native adjective. Three readings, one simple shape." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Some handy combinations. 「同じ大きさ|おなじおおきさ」 means the same size. 「大きさが違う|おおきさがちがう」 means the sizes are different. And 「大きさを比べる|おおきさをくらべる」 is to compare sizes. A natural comparison pattern is to say something is about the same size as something else, using 「ぐらい」, as in 「ねこぐらいの大きさ|ねこぐらいのおおきさ」, about the size of a cat. For clothes and shoes, though, Japanese people usually use the loanword 「サイズ」 instead, so in a shop you ask about the 「サイズ」. And here is a fun habit. Japanese news often describes large areas by comparing them to a famous stadium in Tokyo, the Tokyo Dome, saying something is so many Tokyo Domes in size. Most people have no clear picture of that either, which is part of the joke. General size, 「大きさ|おおきさ」, clothes size, 「サイズ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Drop the 「い」 from 「大きい|おおきい」 and add 「さ」 to make a noun for how much of a quality something has, so bigness becomes size, just like 「長さ|ながさ」, length.",
      "The 「さ」 ending also attaches to na adjective stems, as in 「静かさ|しずかさ」, but good uses the old form to give 「よさ」, never a form built from 「いい」.",
      "The kanji reads 「おお」 with native words like 「大きい|おおきい」, but 「だい」 in 「大学|だいがく」 and 「たい」 in 「大切|たいせつ」, so learn readings word by word.",
      "Say 「同じ大きさ|おなじおおきさ」 for the same size or 「ねこぐらいの大きさ|ねこぐらいのおおきさ」 for about cat size, but use 「サイズ」 for clothes and shoes."
    ],
    ja: "[大きさ|おおきさ]はどのぐらいですか。",
    en: "About how big is it?" },
  "みち|道": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Here the kanji takes its native reading, 「みち」. Look at the shape. Along the left and bottom runs a part that means moving along a road. You already know it from many words. It's in 「近い|ちかい」, near, in 「週|しゅう」, week, which moves on and on, in 「返事|へんじ」, reply, and in 「遅れる|おくれる」, to be late. Whenever you see that sweeping road part, think motion. The top right of 「道|みち」 is the kanji for head, 「首|くび」, which on its own actually means neck. A way to picture it: a person going forward, head first, down a path. The word's meaning is broad. It's a road or street, the way to somewhere, and directions, as in asking someone the way, 「道を聞く|みちをきく」. With its Chinese-origin reading 「どう」, the same kanji means a way of life or discipline. That's the 「どう」 in judo, kendo, and the tea ceremony, which literally means the way of tea. Recap: the moving part plus head gives a path you travel, read 「みち」 for road, and 「どう」 for a discipline." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up in many everyday phrases. 「道に迷う|みちにまよう」 means to get lost, literally to be lost on the road. 「道を教える|みちをおしえる」 is to give someone directions. 「道を渡る|みちをわたる」 is to cross the street. 「帰り道|かえりみち」 is the way home, and 「近道|ちかみち」 is a shortcut, literally a near road. A dialogue you might really use. You stop someone and say, 「すみません、駅への道を教えてもらえませんか|すみません、えきへのみちをおしえてもらえませんか」, excuse me, could you tell me the way to the station? Now a useful contrast. 「通り|とおり」 is used for a named street, often a big one with shops. 「道路|どうろ」 is the formal word for a roadway, the kind you see in news reports about traffic. Everyday talk about getting somewhere uses 「道|みち」. So ask for the 「道|みち」, and you will find your way." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「どう」, this kanji builds a family of words for the traditional Japanese arts, each one a way or path of training. 「柔道|じゅうどう」 is judo, the gentle way. 「剣道|けんどう」 is kendo, the way of the sword. 「書道|しょどう」 is calligraphy, the way of writing, using 「書|しょ」 from 「書く|かく」. And the tea ceremony is 「茶道|さどう」, the way of tea. The same kanji also names practical routes. 「歩道|ほどう」 is a sidewalk, a walking path, and 「水道|すいどう」 is the water supply, the path that water travels to your tap. It even appears in place names, like 「北海道|ほっかいどう」, Japan's northern island. So whenever you see this kanji read 「どう」, think of a road, a route, or a lifelong path of practice." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick that ties the sound to the meaning. Picture yourself late for class, standing at a corner. The main road goes all the way around the block. But there is a tiny back path squeezed between two houses, the kind of shortcut you are not quite sure you are allowed to use. You glance around, grin, and whisper to yourself, me cheat. Then you dash down the little road and arrive just in time. Me cheat, the road. And the shortcut itself, in Japanese, is 「近道|ちかみち」, built from this same word, so the scene even teaches you a bonus word. Every time you see a narrow road, remember your guilty little whisper." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the moving along a road part plus 「首|くび」, a head going forward down a path, read 「みち」 for road and 「どう」 for a discipline like judo.",
      "Everyday phrases include 「道に迷う|みちにまよう」, to get lost, 「道を教える|みちをおしえる」, to give directions, and 「近道|ちかみち」, a shortcut.",
      "Read 「どう」, it names traditional arts as paths of training, like 「柔道|じゅうどう」 and 「茶道|さどう」, and practical routes like 「歩道|ほどう」, a sidewalk.",
      "Picture yourself sneaking down a narrow back path whispering me cheat, which sounds like 「みち」, and that shortcut is a 「近道|ちかみち」."
    ],
    ja: "[道|みち]がわかりません。",
    en: "I don't know the way." },
  "きまつしけん|期末試験": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Four kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings, and you know pieces of every one. 「期|き」 is a set period of time, a term. You've seen it in 「今学期|こんがっき」, this semester, where it's squeezed to 「き」 after a small 「っ」. 「末|まつ」 is the end, the tip. It's in 「週末|しゅうまつ」, weekend, the end of the week. So 「期末|きまつ」 is the end of the term. Then 「試験|しけん」, exam. 「試|し」 means to try or test, as in 「試合|しあい」, a match where teams test each other. It has the speech part on the left. 「験|けん」 means to check or verify, as in 「経験|けいけん」, experience. And look at its left side. That's the horse part, the same one you see in 「駅|えき」, station. So end of term plus exam gives the final exam. A note on Japanese school life. Most schools also have a mid-term exam, 「中間試験|ちゅうかんしけん」, the in-the-middle exam, and students cram hard for both. Recap: 「期|き」 term, 「末|まつ」 end, 「試験|しけん」 exam, so the end of term exam." },
      { title: "Kanji relatives", text: "Both halves of 「期末|きまつ」 lead to more useful words. 「期|き」, a set period, appears in 「期間|きかん」, a span of time, like a sale period or a campaign period. It also appears in 「期待|きたい」, expectation, waiting for something within a time, and in 「延期|えんき」, postponement, stretching the period out. Students dread hearing that a trip was 「延期|えんき」, but love hearing that an exam was. 「末|まつ」, the end, gives 「年末|ねんまつ」, the end of the year, the busy season before New Year's, and 「月末|げつまつ」, the end of the month, when bills are due. With its native reading 「すえ」 it appears in 「末っ子|すえっこ」, the youngest child, the one at the end of the line of siblings. So one kanji counts out stretches of time, and the other marks where they finish." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Exams come with their own set of verbs. You take an exam with 「受ける|うける」, so 「試験を受ける|しけんをうける」. A common mistake is to translate take with 「取る|とる」, which sounds odd here. You pass with 「合格する|ごうかくする」, and you fail with 「落ちる|おちる」, to fall, as in 「試験に落ちた|しけんにおちた」, I failed the exam. Studying for exams is 「試験勉強|しけんべんきょう」. In casual speech, students often shorten the word to 「期末|きまつ」, or say 「期末テスト|きまつテスト」, using the loanword for test. And there is a colorful word for cramming the night before, 「一夜漬け|いちやづけ」, literally pickled in one night, like vegetables soaked quickly in brine. A quick exchange. 「期末、どうだった|きまつ、どうだった」, how did finals go? 「一夜漬けだったから、だめだった|いちやづけだったから、だめだった」, I crammed overnight, so it went badly. Take it, pass it, and try not to pickle." },
      { title: "Exam culture", text: "Exams loom large in Japanese student life, and people have fun ways of wishing each other luck. The biggest tests are entrance exams, and taking them is called 「受験|じゅけん」. Many students attend 「塾|じゅく」, cram schools, in the evenings for years to prepare. Then come the puns. The chocolate bar KitKat became a popular good luck gift because its name in Japanese sounds like 「きっと勝つ|きっとかつ」, you will surely win. A pork cutlet bowl, 「カツ丼|カツどん」, is a classic meal the night before an exam, because 「カツ」 sounds like 「勝つ|かつ」, to win. Students also visit shrines and buy charms for academic success. Final exams are lower stakes than entrance exams, but the same spirit carries over. So if a Japanese friend has finals coming up, a KitKat makes a perfect little gift." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「期|き」 is a term and 「末|まつ」 is the end, as in 「週末|しゅうまつ」, so 「期末|きまつ」 is the end of term, plus 「試験|しけん」, an exam.",
      "「期|き」 counts stretches of time, as in 「期間|きかん」 and 「延期|えんき」, postponement, while 「末|まつ」 marks the end, as in 「年末|ねんまつ」, the end of the year.",
      "You take an exam with 「受ける|うける」, not 「取る|とる」, pass with 「合格する|ごうかくする」, and fail with 「落ちる|おちる」, and overnight cramming is 「一夜漬け|いちやづけ」.",
      "Students eat 「カツ丼|カツどん」 before exams because 「カツ」 sounds like 「勝つ|かつ」, to win, and KitKats make good luck gifts for the same punning reason."
    ],
    ja: "[期末試験|きまつしけん]はむずかしかったです。",
    en: "The final exam was difficult." },
  "けんきゅう|研究": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「研|けん」, has the stone part on its left side, and its core meaning is to grind or polish, like sharpening a blade on a whetstone. The second, 「究|きゅう」, has the hole or cave part on top, and it means to get to the bottom of something, to investigate all the way to the end. Put them together and research becomes polishing a question and digging down to its bottom. That's a nice picture of what scholars actually do, over and over, until the answer shines. You can grow the word easily. Add 「者|しゃ」, person, and you get 「研究者|けんきゅうしゃ」, researcher, which you also know. Add 「する」 and it becomes a verb, 「研究する|けんきゅうする」, to do research. A common pattern is the subject plus 「を研究しています|をけんきゅうしています」, I'm researching something. In Japanese universities, students in their final years join a professor's research group, often called a seminar or lab, and much of their life centers on it. Recap: grind and polish, then dig to the bottom, gives research." },
      { title: "Study versus research", text: "English speakers sometimes mix up this word with 「勉強|べんきょう」, because both can be translated as study. Here is the difference. 「勉強|べんきょう」 is learning things that are already known, like memorizing kanji or reviewing for a test. 「研究|けんきゅう」 is creating new knowledge, investigating a question that nobody has fully answered yet. So a student studies Japanese with 「勉強|べんきょう」, but a professor researches the history of Japanese with 「研究|けんきゅう」. A third verb, 「調べる|しらべる」, means to look something up or check into it, like searching a word in a dictionary or finding train times. That is quick and practical, while 「研究|けんきゅう」 takes months or years. Saying you are doing 「研究|けんきゅう」 on your homework would sound grand and a bit funny. Learn with 「勉強|べんきょう」, look up with 「調べる|しらべる」, and discover with 「研究|けんきゅう」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「研|けん」, has a native reading you may meet in the kitchen, 「研ぐ|とぐ」, to sharpen a knife, and also to wash rice by rubbing the grains together. That matches its grinding and polishing meaning. In 「研修|けんしゅう」, it means training, the polishing that new employees go through at a company. The second kanji, 「究|きゅう」, appears in 「究極|きゅうきょく」, ultimate, the very bottom or farthest point of something. Now the useful places. 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」 is a professor's office or lab, using 「室|しつ」, room, as in 「教室|きょうしつ」, classroom. 「研究所|けんきゅうじょ」 is a research institute, a whole building for research. So one kanji sharpens and polishes, the other goes all the way down, and together they fill rooms and buildings with discovery." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Picture a scientist named Ken in a white lab coat. Ken is obsessed with questions. Hanging over his desk is a giant glowing question mark, like a capital letter Q made of neon. Every morning he walks into the lab, stares up at it, and his assistant shouts, Ken, Q! That is his cue to start researching. Ken and his giant Q. Ken, Q. Research. And to tie in the kanji, imagine Ken grinding a stone to polish it, and then climbing down into a deep hole to get to the bottom of his question, just like the two parts of the word. Whenever you need this word, hear that assistant calling Ken to his question." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「研|けん」 means to grind and polish and 「究|きゅう」 means to dig to the bottom, so research is polishing a question until you reach its answer.",
      "「勉強|べんきょう」 is learning what's already known and 「調べる|しらべる」 is looking something up, while 「研究|けんきゅう」 creates new knowledge over months or years.",
      "「研|けん」 gives 「研ぐ|とぐ」, to sharpen a knife or wash rice, and 「究|きゅう」 gives 「究極|きゅうきょく」, ultimate, while 「研究室|けんきゅうしつ」 is a professor's lab.",
      "Picture a scientist named Ken whose assistant shouts Ken, Q, pointing at a giant neon question mark, to cue him to start his research."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の{文化|ぶんか}を[研究|けんきゅう]しています。",
    en: "I'm doing research on Japanese culture." },
  "だいがくいん|大学院": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Start with a word you know well, 「大学|だいがく」, college. Both kanji use Chinese-origin readings. 「大|だい」 is big, and 「学|がく」 is learning, so a big place of learning. Then add 「院|いん」. This kanji means an institution, a large formal building or establishment. You've met it in 「病院|びょういん」, hospital, the sickness institution, and in 「美容院|びよういん」, beauty parlor, the beauty institution. So 「大学院|だいがくいん」 is the institution attached to the university, the higher level you enter after finishing college. That's graduate school, for master's and doctoral degrees. Students there are called 「大学院生|だいがくいんせい」, adding 「生|せい」, the same as in 「学生|がくせい」, student. A cultural note. In Japan, far fewer people go on to graduate school than in the United States, especially outside science and engineering. Many companies traditionally hire students right after college and train them on the job. So saying you want to go to 「大学院|だいがくいん」 often means you're aiming for research or a specialist career. Recap: college plus institution gives graduate school." },
      { title: "Degrees and levels", text: "Once you are in graduate school, a few more words describe where you stand. Undergraduate study happens in a 「学部|がくぶ」, a faculty or department, and an undergraduate is a 「学部生|がくぶせい」. A master's program is 「修士課程|しゅうしかてい」, and a doctoral program is 「博士課程|はくしかてい」. The word for a doctoral degree or a person who holds one is 「博士|はくし」 in formal use, but in everyday speech it is often read 「はかせ」. That is the reading you hear for brilliant professor characters in anime and games, the scientist with wild hair who helps the hero. Kids even use 「はかせ」 for a classmate who knows everything about a subject, like a dinosaur expert. So undergraduate in the faculty, then master's, then doctorate, and maybe a nickname as the class 「はかせ」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "In casual talk, people often drop the first part and just say 「院|いん」. A graduate student becomes 「院生|いんせい」, and someone planning ahead might say 「院に行く|いんにいく」, I'm going to grad school. Now watch out for a sound-alike. 「員|いん」, written with a different kanji, means a member or staff person. You know it from 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, a company employee, and 「店員|てんいん」, a shop clerk. Same sound, completely different meaning. The first is an institution, a building or establishment, and the second is a person who belongs somewhere. Context usually settles it. If the word is about study or applications, it is the institution. If it is about a job title, it is the member. One more pair from the same institution kanji is 「入院|にゅういん」 and 「退院|たいいん」, entering and leaving the hospital. Institution versus member, same sound, different kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To talk about going to graduate school, Japanese uses a handful of patterns. 「大学院に入る|だいがくいんにはいる」 is to enter graduate school. 「大学院に進む|だいがくいんにすすむ」, to advance to graduate school, is very common and sounds natural, because it is the next step up. A more formal word for continuing your education is 「進学する|しんがくする」. Finishing is 「大学院を出る|だいがくいんをでる」, to graduate from it. Notice the particles, 「に」 for going into, and 「を」 for leaving. A dialogue between two seniors. One asks, 「卒業したら、どうするの|そつぎょうしたら、どうするの」, what are you doing after graduation? The other says, 「大学院に進もうと思ってる|だいがくいんにすすもうとおもってる」, I'm thinking of going on to grad school. That uses the volitional plus 「と思う|とおもう」, a pattern from your lessons. Enter with 「に」, leave with 「を」, and advance step by step." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「大学|だいがく」, college, plus 「院|いん」, an institution as in 「病院|びょういん」, gives the higher institution attached to the university, graduate school.",
      "Undergraduates study in a 「学部|がくぶ」, then come master's and doctoral programs, and 「博士|はくし」 is casually read 「はかせ」, the anime professor or class know-it-all.",
      "Casually it shrinks to 「院|いん」, as in 「院生|いんせい」, a grad student, so don't confuse it with 「員|いん」, a staff member, as in 「店員|てんいん」.",
      "Say 「大学院に進む|だいがくいんにすすむ」 to advance to grad school and 「大学院を出る|だいがくいんをでる」 to finish, with 「に」 for going in and 「を」 for leaving."
    ],
    ja: "[大学院|だいがくいん]に{行きたい|いきたい}です。",
    en: "I want to go to graduate school." },
  "しょうがくきん|奨学金": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「奨|しょう」, means to encourage or urge on. It's not a common kanji on its own, but it's the key to this word. The second is 「学|がく」, learning, the same one in 「学生|がくせい」, student, and 「大学|だいがく」, college. The third is 「金|きん」, money or gold. You know it from 「金曜日|きんようび」, Friday, and 「税金|ぜいきん」, tax. Notice that in 「お金|おかね」, money, the same kanji uses its native reading 「かね」, but in compound words it usually switches to 「きん」. Now put it together: encourage, learning, money. Money given to encourage study. That's a scholarship. One thing to know about Japan. Many things called 「奨学金|しょうがくきん」 there are actually student loans that you have to pay back after you graduate, and only some are true grants that you don't repay. So when a Japanese friend says they're paying back their 「奨学金|しょうがくきん」, that's why. The verb that goes with it is 「もらう」, to receive. Recap: encourage plus study plus money gives scholarship." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The last kanji, 「金|きん」, money, ends a whole row of useful money words, all with the same Chinese-origin reading. 「料金|りょうきん」 is a fee or charge, like a taxi fare or a phone bill. 「現金|げんきん」 is cash, literally present money, the money actually in your hand. 「貯金|ちょきん」 is savings, money stored up, and 「借金|しゃっきん」 is debt, borrowed money. Notice the small 「っ」 there, where 「しゃく」 squeezes into 「しゃっ」 before 「きん」. And from the learning side, 「学費|がくひ」 is tuition, study expenses, using 「費|ひ」, cost. A 「奨学金|しょうがくきん」 is often what pays the 「学費|がくひ」. If it has to be paid back, it can start to feel a lot like 「借金|しゃっきん」. So the ending 「きん」 tells you money is involved, and the front part tells you what kind." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Applying for a scholarship is 「奨学金に申し込む|しょうがくきんにもうしこむ」, using 「申し込む|もうしこむ」, to apply, with the particle 「に」. Paying it back is 「奨学金を返す|しょうがくきんをかえす」, and the formal word for repayment is 「返済|へんさい」. Japanese makes a clear distinction between two kinds. A 「給付型|きゅうふがた」 scholarship is the grant type, money given that you keep. A 「貸与型|たいよがた」 scholarship is the loan type, money lent to you. The ending 「型|がた」 means type or model. For international students, one of the best known is the Japanese government scholarship from the education ministry, 「文部科学省|もんぶかがくしょう」, often just called the MEXT scholarship in English. A quick exchange. 「その奨学金、返さなくてもいいの|そのしょうがくきん、かえさなくてもいいの」, you don't have to pay that back? 「うん、給付型だから|うん、きゅうふがただから」, right, it's a grant. Always ask which type before you sign." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word is a natural partner for the giving and receiving verbs from your lessons. When you receive it, you say 「もらう」. 「大学から奨学金をもらいました|だいがくからしょうがくきんをもらいました」, I received a scholarship from the university. Notice that the giver can be marked with 「から」 when it is an organization. When you want to be humble, perhaps in an interview or a thank-you letter, switch to 「いただく」, the humble form. 「奨学金をいただきました|しょうがくきんをいただきました」 sounds respectful toward whoever provided it. And when you talk about what the scholarship lets you do, a warm way to say it is with 「おかげで」, thanks to. 「奨学金のおかげで、留学できました|しょうがくきんのおかげで、りゅうがくできました」, thanks to the scholarship, I could study abroad. Receive with 「もらう」, be humble with 「いただく」, and give thanks with 「おかげで」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「奨|しょう」 means encourage, 「学|がく」 means learning and 「金|きん」 means money, so it is money given to encourage study, though in Japan many are loans you repay.",
      "The ending 「きん」 marks money words like 「料金|りょうきん」, a fee, 「現金|げんきん」, cash, 「貯金|ちょきん」, savings, and 「借金|しゃっきん」, debt.",
      "Japanese splits them into 「給付型|きゅうふがた」, grants you keep, and 「貸与型|たいよがた」, loans you repay, so always ask which type before you sign.",
      "Receive it with 「もらう」, be humble with 「いただく」, and give thanks with 「おかげで」, as in 「奨学金のおかげで|しょうがくきんのおかげで」, thanks to the scholarship."
    ],
    ja: "[奨学金|しょうがくきん]をもらいました。",
    en: "I received a scholarship." },
  "すいせんじょう|推薦状": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word has two parts, all read with Chinese-origin readings. First, 「推薦|すいせん」, recommendation. 「推|すい」 has the hand part on its left, and it means to push forward. 「薦|せん」 is a rare kanji that also means to recommend. So you're pushing someone forward, putting them up for something. That's what a recommendation is. Then 「状|じょう」, a formal letter or written document. You'll meet it in 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, the New Year's cards that people in Japan mail to friends, family, and coworkers so they all arrive on New Year's Day. So the whole word is a written push forward, a letter of recommendation. You'll need one for applying to 「大学院|だいがくいん」, graduate school, or for a 「奨学金|しょうがくきん」, a scholarship, so these three words come as a set in this lesson. The usual pattern is asking a teacher to write it for you, using the giving and receiving grammar, 「先生に推薦状を書いてもらいました|せんせいにすいせんじょうをかいてもらいました」. Recap: push forward plus recommend plus letter gives a letter of recommendation." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「状|じょう」, a formal letter, appears in several kinds of written messages. Besides New Year's cards, there is 「招待状|しょうたいじょう」, an invitation, using 「招待|しょうたい」, to invite, which you may get for a wedding. There is also 「礼状|れいじょう」, a thank-you letter, using 「礼|れい」, as in 「お礼|おれい」, thanks. The first kanji, 「推|すい」, push forward, shows up in 「推理|すいり」, reasoning or deduction, so a mystery novel is a 「推理小説|すいりしょうせつ」, a story where you push your reasoning forward to find the culprit. And here is a modern twist. The native verb 「推す|おす」, to push or recommend, gave Japanese fan culture the word 「推し|おし」, your favorite idol or character, the one you push and support. So the same kanji covers a formal letter for graduate school and your favorite pop star." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Asking someone to write this letter takes polite language. A natural request to a teacher is 「推薦状を書いていただけませんか|すいせんじょうをかいていただけませんか」, would you be so kind as to write me a letter of recommendation? That uses 「いただく」, the humble version of 「もらう」, which is just right when asking a favor from someone above you. Give your teacher plenty of time, bring your application details, and afterwards send thanks, perhaps with a 「礼状|れいじょう」, a thank-you note. In Japan, recommendations also matter for getting into schools. There is an admission route called 「推薦入試|すいせんにゅうし」, recommendation-based admission, where a student's high school recommends them and the usual entrance exam may be replaced by interviews or essays. So ask humbly, ask early, and always say thank you." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The first half of this word, 「すいせん」, has a pretty sound-alike. Written with different kanji, 「水仙|すいせん」 is the daffodil, or narcissus, the flower that blooms in late winter and early spring. The two words are not related at all. One means pushing someone forward, and the other is a flower. But the coincidence makes a nice memory picture, clearly just a trick. Imagine your favorite teacher handing you an envelope with your letter of recommendation, and taped to the front is a bright yellow daffodil for good luck. You walk into your graduate school interview holding the flower and the letter together. Recommendation and daffodil, same sound. Context will always tell you which one is meant, since you rarely ask a teacher to write you a flower. Picture that yellow flower on the envelope, and the word stays with you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「推|すい」 means to push forward and 「薦|せん」 means to recommend, plus 「状|じょう」, a formal letter as in 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, so it's a written push forward.",
      "「状|じょう」 also ends 「招待状|しょうたいじょう」, an invitation, and 「推|すい」 gives 「推す|おす」, to push, which became 「推し|おし」, your favorite idol.",
      "Ask a teacher humbly with 「推薦状を書いていただけませんか|すいせんじょうをかいていただけませんか」, give them plenty of time, and send thanks afterwards.",
      "「水仙|すいせん」, daffodil, sounds the same, so picture a yellow daffodil taped to the envelope holding your letter of recommendation."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}に[推薦状|すいせんじょう]を{書いて|かいて}もらいました。",
    en: "I had my teacher write me a letter of recommendation." },
  "たいふう|台風": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The second, 「風|ふう」, is wind. You know this kanji in its other readings too. In 「風邪|かぜ」, a cold, it's the native reading 「かぜ」, the same sound as the word for wind, as if a cold is something the wind blows in. And in 「お風呂|おふろ」, bath, it's read 「ふ」. The first kanji, 「台|たい」, usually means a stand or platform, and you know it as the counter for machines, 「〜台|〜だい」. In this word it's read 「たい」 and it's mostly there for its sound. The word sounds very much like the English typhoon, and that's a perfect hook. The history of which language borrowed from which is tangled, so just enjoy the match. Typhoons hit Japan mostly from late summer into fall. Japan numbers them each year in order, so the news says typhoon number one, number two, and so on. When one comes, trains stop, schools may close, and people stock up and stay home. Weather forecasts, 「天気予報|てんきよほう」, track them closely. Recap: stand plus wind, sounds like typhoon, comes in late summer and fall." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「風|ふう」 reaches well beyond weather. It appears in 「扇風機|せんぷうき」, an electric fan, where the sound shifts to 「ぷう」, and in 「風景|ふうけい」, scenery or landscape. It also means style or manner. 「和風|わふう」 means Japanese style, as in a Japanese style room or dressing, and 「洋風|ようふう」 means Western style. Restaurant menus use these all the time. Now the first kanji. 「台|たい」 usually takes the reading 「だい」, as in 「台所|だいどころ」, kitchen, and the counter for machines. The reading 「たい」 also shows up in 「台湾|たいわん」, Taiwan. For a near-synonym, 「嵐|あらし」 is a storm in general, any heavy wind and rain, while 「台風|たいふう」 is specifically the tropical storm that sweeps up from the Pacific. So the wind kanji can mean breeze, scenery, or style, and here it is a very big wind." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese weather reports have a whole vocabulary for these storms. 「台風が来る|たいふうがくる」 is a typhoon is coming. When it makes landfall, the news says 「上陸する|じょうりくする」, literally to go up onto land. The calm center is 「台風の目|たいふうのめ」, the eye of the typhoon, just like in English. Older houses have 「雨戸|あまど」, sliding storm shutters, which people close before the wind arrives. And there is a lovely set phrase, 「台風一過|たいふういっか」, which describes the clear blue sky the day after a typhoon passes. A dialogue between students. 「明日、台風が来るって|あした、たいふうがくるって」, they say a typhoon is coming tomorrow. 「じゃあ、学校は休みかな|じゃあ、がっこうはやすみかな」, so maybe school will be cancelled? That hopeful question is something every Japanese kid has asked. Shutters closed for the storm, and blue skies after." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a silly memory trick for the sound. Picture a businessman standing on a train platform in September, in a nice suit and a bright red tie. Suddenly the wind starts howling. His tie flips up over his shoulder, then whips straight off his neck and flies away down the tracks. He shouts after it, my tie! The wind just answers, foo, foo. Tie, foo. That is the typhoon. And to bring in the kanji, imagine the tie landing on a tall stand, a platform, which is what 「台|だい」 usually means, while the wind, 「風|かぜ」, keeps blowing around it. Every time you hear about a storm on the news, picture that red tie sailing away on the wind." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「風|ふう」 is wind and 「台|たい」 is mostly there for sound, and the word sounds just like typhoon, the storms that hit Japan from late summer into fall.",
      "The wind kanji also gives 「扇風機|せんぷうき」, an electric fan, and means style in 「和風|わふう」, Japanese style, while 「嵐|あらし」 is a storm in general.",
      "Weather reports say a typhoon will 「上陸する|じょうりくする」, make landfall, and 「台風一過|たいふういっか」 describes the clear blue sky the day after it passes.",
      "Picture a businessman's red tie blown off his neck as he shouts my tie and the wind answers foo, so tie plus foo gives 「たいふう」."
    ],
    ja: "[台風|たいふう]が{来ます|きます}から、{気をつけて|きをつけて}ください。",
    en: "A typhoon is coming, so please be careful." },
  "ぶんか|文化": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「文|ぶん」, writing, letters, sentences, the written arts. You've seen it in 「文法|ぶんぽう」, grammar, which is literally the rules of writing. The second is 「化|か」, to change or transform. It shows up in 「化粧品|けしょうひん」, cosmetics, things that change how you look. The left side of 「化|か」 is the person part, and its whole idea is turning into something else. So writing plus change. In old Chinese, this pairing meant changing and refining people through learning and the written word rather than by force. Later, when Japan needed a word for the Western idea of culture, this was the word it chose. It fits nicely. Culture is what people become through learning, art, and shared ways of life. You'll see it a lot in phrases like 「日本の文化|にほんのぶんか」, Japanese culture, and there's even a national holiday called Culture Day in early November. Recap: writing plus transformation, people shaped by learning, gives culture." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closer at the two shapes. The first, 「文|ぶん」, is a very old character. A common scholarly explanation is that it pictured a person standing with a pattern drawn on the chest, so its oldest sense was a pattern or design. From patterns came marks, and from marks came writing. You also meet it with the reading 「もん」 in 「文句|もんく」, a complaint, and in the old word 「文部科学省|もんぶかがくしょう」, the ministry of education. The second kanji, 「化|か」, has a person on the left, and the right side is usually explained as another person turned upside down. A person flipping into a different state. That is change. The same kanji is read 「け」 in 「化粧|けしょう」, make-up, and 「ば」 in the native verb 「化ける|ばける」, to transform, which is what foxes and raccoon dogs do in Japanese folk tales. A ghost or monster is 「お化け|おばけ」, literally a thing that has changed shape. So the kanji in culture is the same one in a Japanese ghost. Patterns and writing, plus a shape-shifter, give culture." },
      { title: "Culture Day", text: "November third is a national holiday called 「文化の日|ぶんかのひ」, Culture Day. Its history is layered. That date was originally the birthday of Emperor Meiji, the emperor who led Japan's rapid modernization, and it was celebrated as a holiday in his honor. After the war, Japan's new constitution was announced on November third, 1946, and two years later the date was renamed Culture Day, a day to love freedom and peace and to promote culture. On that day the government hands out the Order of Culture, a top honor for artists, writers and scientists. Many museums are free or open special exhibits. The weather around then is famously clear, and some people say early November is one of the sunniest stretches of the year in Tokyo. So if you're in Japan in early November, expect crowds at museums and parks. Culture Day, November third, an emperor's birthday turned into a celebration of the arts." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The word you're most likely to hear from a Japanese teenager is 「文化祭|ぶんかさい」, the school culture festival. Almost every junior high, high school and university has one, usually in autumn. Each class turns its classroom into something, a café, a haunted house, a play, a food stall selling fried noodles. Families and neighbors come to visit, and it's a huge memory for students. Anime and dramas set in schools almost always include one. Beyond school, you'll hear 「異文化|いぶんか」, a different culture, in talk about cultural exchange, and 「食文化|しょくぶんか」, food culture. Notice how freely this word attaches as a suffix too. Anime culture, coffee culture, work culture, all can be made with it. A natural thing to say as a learner is 「日本の文化に興味があります|にほんのぶんかにきょうみがあります」, I'm interested in Japanese culture. Keep that sentence handy, because people will ask why you study Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「文|ぶん」 means writing and 「化|か」 means change, an old pairing for refining people through learning, which Japan later chose for the Western idea of culture.",
      "「文|ぶん」 began as a pattern on a person's chest, and 「化|か」 is a person flipping into a new state, the same kanji as in 「お化け|おばけ」, a shape-shifting ghost.",
      "November third is 「文化の日|ぶんかのひ」, Culture Day, once Emperor Meiji's birthday and later renamed to celebrate freedom, peace and the arts.",
      "Teens know it from the autumn 「文化祭|ぶんかさい」, the school culture festival, and a handy line is 「日本の文化に興味があります|にほんのぶんかにきょうみがあります」."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[文化|ぶんか]がすきです。",
    en: "I like Japanese culture." },
  "へんじ|返事": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「返|へん」, to return or send back. It has the moving-along-a-road part on its left and bottom, which fits, because something travels back. You already know this kanji with its native reading in 「返す|かえす」, to return a thing, and in 「お返し|おかえし」, a return gift. The second is 「事|じ」, a thing or a matter. You know it from 「用事|ようじ」, business to take care of, and in 「仕事|しごと」, work, it takes its native reading 「こと」 with the sound softened to 「ごと」. So the whole word is the thing you send back. A reply. It's used for replying to letters, invitations and messages, and also for answering when someone calls your name. In Japanese classrooms, when the teacher calls roll, students answer 「はい」, and that is called giving a 「返事|へんじ」. A common pattern is 「返事をする|へんじをする」, to reply, or 「返事を書く|へんじをかく」, to write a reply. For email you may also hear a more technical word, but this one works everywhere. Recap: send back plus thing gives reply." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's take apart 「返|へん」. The road-moving part wraps the left and bottom, and inside it sits 「反|はん」, a kanji meaning to turn over or go against. You'll meet that inside 「反対|はんたい」, opposite or opposed. Here 「反|はん」 gives both sound and sense, since 「へん」 and 「はん」 are close, and turning back is the idea. So travel plus turn back is return. Now 「事|じ」. It's one of the kanji with lots of readings. 「じ」 in 「火事|かじ」, a fire, and 「食事|しょくじ」, a meal. 「こと」 on its own, as in 「好きなこと|すきなこと」, things I like. And 「ごと」 in 「仕事|しごと」. The stroke running straight down through the middle is a good shape to recognize. In this word, both kanji use Chinese-origin readings, which is typical for two-kanji nouns. So a returned matter, a turned-back thing. That's your reply." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word covers more than letters. When a Japanese teacher calls your name and you say 「はい」, that is your reply, and teachers will praise a loud, clear one by saying 「いい返事|いいへんじ」, a good reply. Coaches in school sports clubs expect a crisp one every time. For messages, the worry everyone knows is 「返事がない|へんじがない」, no reply. In polite emails you'll see 「お返事|おへんじ」 with the honorific 「お」, as in 「お返事をお待ちしています|おへんじをおまちしています」, I look forward to your reply. Don't confuse it with 「答え|こたえ」. An answer is what you give to a question, like on a test. A reply is what you send back to a person who reached out. You answer a quiz, but you reply to an invitation. You can also say 「すぐ返事します|すぐへんじします」, I'll reply right away. Reply to people, answer questions." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick built on the sound. The first part sounds like the English word hen. Picture a busy hen at a little post office desk. Every time a letter arrives, she reads it, scribbles something with her beak, and pushes it right back out through the window. That's the hen sending things back. Then a bee flies in, buzzing gee, gee, and the hen sends the bee back too. Hen, gee. She returns everything. She's the fastest replier in the barnyard and never leaves anyone on read. When you think of a reply, picture that hen shoving letters back out the window with her beak. And remember the kanji story too, where the first kanji has the road part, so the hen's reply is literally traveling back down the road. Hen sends it back, that's your reply." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「返|へん」 means to send back, as in 「返す|かえす」, and 「事|じ」 means a thing, so a reply is the thing you send back, including answering 「はい」 when your name is called.",
      "「返|へん」 is the road part wrapped around 「反|はん」, turn back, so travel plus turn back means return, while 「事|じ」 has many readings like 「こと」 and 「ごと」.",
      "You reply to a person who reached out, but give 「答え|こたえ」 to a question, and polite emails use 「お返事|おへんじ」, as in waiting for your reply.",
      "Picture a hen at a post office desk shoving every letter back out, then sending back a buzzing bee saying gee, so hen plus gee gives a reply."
    ],
    ja: "メールの[返事|へんじ]を{書きました|かきました}。",
    en: "I wrote a reply to the email." },
  "ひ|日": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji began as a picture of the sun, a circle with a dot in the middle, which got squared off over time into the box with a line you see now. Since one trip of the sun across the sky is one day, the kanji means both sun and day. Here it's the native reading 「ひ」, used on its own as the word for a day. You know this kanji in many other readings. It's 「にち」 in 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, and 「日本|にほん」, Japan, the land where the sun comes from. It's 「び」 in 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, birthday, and in the days of the week, like 「日曜日|にちようび」, Sunday, the sun day. With so many readings, the bare 「ひ」 shows up mainly in phrases where you describe what kind of day it was. For example 「雨の日|あめのひ」, a rainy day, 「休みの日|やすみのひ」, a day off, or 「その日|そのひ」, that day. One mistake to avoid. For dates like the fifth of the month, use the date words, not this one. Recap: a picture of the sun, one sun is one day, read 「ひ」 when it stands alone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has more readings than almost any other, so let's sort them. 「にち」 and 「じつ」 are Chinese-origin. You hear 「にち」 in 「日曜日|にちようび」 and 「毎日|まいにち」, and 「じつ」 in 「休日|きゅうじつ」, a holiday. Then the native readings. 「ひ」 stands alone, and it softens to 「び」 inside compounds like 「誕生日|たんじょうび」. There's also 「か」, used for counting days and for dates. 「二日|ふつか」, the second or two days, 「三日|みっか」, 「十日|とおか」. And there are special whole-word readings that you simply memorize. 「今日|きょう」, today. 「明日|あした」, tomorrow. 「昨日|きのう」, yesterday. 「一日|ついたち」, the first of the month. That last one comes from an old phrase about the moon rising, since months used to follow the moon. The bare 「ひ」 is the simplest member of the family, the day as an ordinary thing, while the others handle calendars and counting. One kanji, many jobs." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The sun matters a lot in Japanese culture. The national flag is called 「日の丸|ひのまる」, the circle of the sun, a red disc on white. The country's own name, 「日本|にほん」, means origin of the sun, because seen from China, Japan lay toward the sunrise. On New Year's morning, many people get up early to watch 「初日の出|はつひので」, the first sunrise of the year. They head to beaches, hilltops, or even climb Mount Fuji in the dark to catch it, and some pray to it. The phrase 「日の出|ひので」 alone just means sunrise, the sun coming out. Sunset is 「日の入り|ひのいり」, the sun going in. Notice how in all of these, 「ひ」 is the sun itself, not a day. That's the other half of this word's meaning. Context tells you which one. So the bare word can mean day, but in sunrise, sunset and the flag, it's the sun shining." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The bare word shines in a few patterns worth memorizing. Stories often start with 「ある日|あるひ」, one day, just like once upon a time in English. Use 「その日|そのひ」 for that day, and 「次の日|つぎのひ」 for the next day. Name a kind of day by putting a noun and 「の」 in front, like 「休みの日|やすみのひ」, a day off, or 「雪の日|ゆきのひ」, a snowy day. Holidays work the same way. 「母の日|ははのひ」 is Mother's Day, 「父の日|ちちのひ」 is Father's Day, and 「子どもの日|こどものひ」, Children's Day, is May fifth. You can also describe a day with an adjective, as in 「暑い日|あついひ」, a hot day. One more trap. 「ひ」 is also the word for fire, 「火|ひ」, as in 「火曜日|かようび」, Tuesday. They sound the same, so let the context decide. Days come before 「の」 phrases and adjectives, fire comes with cooking and burning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji began as a picture of the sun, and one trip of the sun is one day, so bare 「ひ」 describes kinds of days, like 「雨の日|あめのひ」, a rainy day.",
      "This kanji has many readings, 「にち」 and 「じつ」, 「ひ」 and 「び」, 「か」 for counting days, plus special words like 「今日|きょう」, while bare 「ひ」 is the ordinary day.",
      "In 「日の丸|ひのまる」, the flag, 「日の出|ひので」, sunrise, and 「初日の出|はつひので」, the year's first sunrise, 「ひ」 means the sun itself rather than a day.",
      "Stories start with 「ある日|あるひ」, one day, and holidays like 「母の日|ははのひ」 use a noun plus 「の」, but don't confuse it with 「火|ひ」, fire."
    ],
    ja: "{雨|あめ}の[日|ひ]は{家|いえ}にいます。",
    en: "On rainy days I stay home." },
  "きたない|汚い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「汚|きた」 here uses its native reading, followed by 「ない」 written in kana. Don't be fooled by that ending. It is not the negative 「ない」. This whole word is just a regular 「い」 adjective that happens to end in those sounds. So 「汚くない|きたなくない」 means not dirty. Now the kanji. On its left side is the three-drop water part, the same one you'll see in many kanji about water and liquids. A way to picture it: muddy, dirty water. The opposite is 「きれい」, which means both beautiful and clean. That makes a handy pair, a dirty room versus a clean room. The word has some useful stretches too. 「字が汚い|じがきたない」 means messy handwriting. And for a person or behavior, it can mean dirty in the sense of unfair, like a dirty trick, so be careful about saying it to people. In Japan, cleanliness matters a lot. Students clean their own classrooms every day, so a 「汚い|きたない」 classroom is the students' own fault. Recap: the water part, dirty water, an 「い」 adjective, opposite of 「きれい」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has a whole family of dirt words. The verb 「汚れる|よごれる」 means to get dirty, by itself, like a shirt getting dirty when you eat. Its partner 「汚す|よごす」 means to make something dirty, as in a child dirtying their clothes. The noun 「汚れ|よごれ」 is a stain or grime, a word you'll see on detergent packages. With its Chinese-origin reading 「お」, the kanji appears in 「汚染|おせん」, pollution. So 「汚い|きたない」 describes how something looks, dirty, while 「汚れる|よごれる」 describes the process of getting that way. Here's a nuance. A shirt with a small stain is 「汚れている|よごれている」, it's gotten dirty. Calling it 「汚い|きたない」 sounds stronger, closer to filthy or gross. On the clean side, besides 「きれい」, there's 「清潔|せいけつ」, hygienic, sanitary. One kanji, readings 「きた」, 「よご」 and 「お」, all about dirt." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese kids use this word the way English kids say ew, gross. Touch something on the ground and a parent says 「汚いよ|きたないよ」, that's dirty, don't. To make it sound extra strong in casual speech, people stretch it with a small pause, 「きったない」, really filthy. Be careful aiming it at people, though. 「汚い|きたない」 about a person's actions means sneaky or unfair, a dirty trick. A softer word for unfair is 「ずるい」, which kids shout when someone cheats at a game. 「ずるい」 is playful and light. 「汚い|きたない」 sounds like a real accusation. It can also describe sound and words, as in 「汚い言葉|きたないことば」, foul language. Mild use for rooms and handwriting is fine, as in 「私の字は汚いです|わたしのじはきたないです」, my handwriting is messy, which is a nice humble thing to say. Gross for things, unfair for people, so use it carefully." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick on the sound. Picture a fussy white cat standing at the doorway of a horribly messy room. Pizza boxes, socks, a muddy puddle on the floor. You call, kitty, come in. The cat looks at the mud, looks at you, and says, kitty? Nah, eye won't. Kitty, nah, eye. The cat turns around and stalks off, refusing to set a single paw in that filthy room. And remember the water part on the left of the kanji, the three drops. The puddle of muddy water is exactly what scared the kitty away. Whenever you see a disgusting room, imagine that cat saying kitty, nah, eye, and walking out. Muddy water, a cat who won't come in, that's dirty." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The ending isn't the negative 「ない」, it's a regular i adjective, and the water part on the left suggests muddy water, the opposite of 「きれい」.",
      "「汚い|きたない」 describes how dirty something looks, while 「汚れる|よごれる」 is the process of getting dirty, so a small stain is 「汚れている|よごれている」 rather than filthy.",
      "Kids use it like ew, gross, but aimed at a person it means unfair or sneaky, much stronger than the playful 「ずるい」, so use it carefully.",
      "Picture a fussy cat at a muddy room saying kitty, nah, eye won't, and stalking off rather than step in the dirty water."
    ],
    ja: "へやが[汚い|きたない]です。",
    en: "The room is dirty." },
  "おこす|起こす": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is the partner of one you know, 「起きる|おきる」, to get up or wake up. They share the kanji 「起|お」 with its native reading, and the shape is fun. On its left, wrapping underneath, is 「走|はし」, the kanji from 「走る|はしる」, to run. Getting up and getting going. The two verbs are a classic Japanese pair. 「起きる|おきる」 is something that happens to you. You wake up. 「起こす|おこす」 is something you do to someone or something else. You wake someone up. So 「私は七時に起きます|わたしはしちじにおきます」, I get up at seven, but 「母を起こします|ははをおこします」, I wake my mother. You'll see this kind of pairing again and again. One verb takes 「を」 and acts on something, the other just happens. A useful request is 「起こしてください|おこしてください」, please wake me up. Later you'll also meet it with the meaning to cause something, like causing an accident, which comes from the same idea of raising something up. Recap: 「起きる|おきる」 you wake, 「起こす|おこす」 you wake someone else." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "These two partner verbs belong to different groups, and that trips people up. 「起きる|おきる」 is a ru verb, so its te form is 「起きて|おきて」 and its negative is 「起きない|おきない」. But 「起こす|おこす」 is an u verb ending in 「す」. Its polite form is 「起こします|おこします」, its te form is 「起こして|おこして」, and its negative is 「起こさない|おこさない」. Now combine it with patterns from your lessons. If someone kindly wakes you, use 「起こしてもらう|おこしてもらう」, I got someone to wake me. 「母に起こしてもらいました|ははにおこしてもらいました」, my mom woke me up for me. If you wake someone as a favor, 「起こしてあげる|おこしてあげる」. And the classic complaint, 「どうして起こしてくれなかったの|どうしておこしてくれなかったの」, why didn't you wake me. Remember the endings: 「す」 for doing it to someone, 「る」 for it happening." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has many verb pairs like this one, and they follow loose patterns worth noticing. Often the version you do to something ends in 「す」. 「起きる|おきる」 and 「起こす|おこす」. 「出る|でる」, come out, and 「出す|だす」, take out. 「落ちる|おちる」, fall, and 「落とす|おとす」, drop something. 「直る|なおる」, get fixed, and 「直す|なおす」, fix it. Other pairs swap in different endings. 「入る|はいる」 and 「入れる|いれる」. 「見つかる|みつかる」, be found, and 「見つける|みつける」, find. The doing-to-something verb usually takes 「を」, and the happening verb usually takes 「が」 or no object at all. So when you learn one, look for its partner. It doubles your vocabulary for free. And when you hear a verb ending in 「す」 with a familiar kanji, there's a good chance it's the one where somebody does something to something. Learn the pairs together." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond waking people up, this verb means raising something or making something happen, and it shows up in everyday phrases. 「体を起こす|からだをおこす」 means to sit up in bed, raising your body. 「事故を起こす|じこをおこす」 means to cause an accident, which you'll hear in news reports about drivers. 「問題を起こす|もんだいをおこす」, to cause trouble, is what a parent might warn a teenager about. 「火を起こす|ひをおこす」, to start a fire, is what you do at a campsite. All share the image of making something rise up or come into being. In a hotel, you might ask the front desk for a wake-up call, though Japanese people usually just use the English word. At home, the polite request to family or a roommate is simply 「七時に起こして|しちじにおこして」, wake me at seven. Raise a person, raise a fire, raise trouble, it's all this one verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「起きる|おきる」 is waking up yourself, while 「起こす|おこす」 is waking someone else, taking 「を」, as in 「母を起こします|ははをおこします」.",
      "「起きる|おきる」 is a ru verb but 「起こす|おこす」 is an u verb in 「す」, so its forms are 「起こして|おこして」 and 「起こさない|おこさない」, as in 「起こしてもらう|おこしてもらう」.",
      "Many doing-to-something verbs end in 「す」 and take 「を」, like 「出す|だす」 and 「落とす|おとす」, while their partners just happen, so learn the pairs together.",
      "It also means to raise or cause, as in 「事故を起こす|じこをおこす」, to cause an accident, and 「火を起こす|ひをおこす」, to start a fire."
    ],
    ja: "あした{七時|しちじ}に[起こして|おこして|おこす]ください。",
    en: "Please wake me up at seven tomorrow." },
  "おごる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "There is a kanji for this verb, but it's rarely used, so it's normally written in kana. It's a casual, friendly word for paying for someone else's food or drinks. Who pays is a real social topic in Japan. Among friends, splitting the bill evenly is very common, and there's even a word for it, 「わりかん」. But senior people often pay for juniors. A boss treating the team, or an older student treating younger club members, is a familiar scene, and the younger ones are expected to thank them warmly, often with 「ごちそうさまでした」. The grammar is worth getting right. The person you treat takes 「に」, and the food takes 「を」, as in 「友だちに昼ごはんをおごる|ともだちにひるごはんをおごる」. To say someone treated you, use 「おごってもらう」, I received the treat. To offer, say 「今日は私がおごります|きょうはわたしがおごります」, today it's on me. A way to remember it: oh, go ahead, I'll get this. Recap: a casual verb for treating someone, person with 「に」, meal with 「を」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how a treat plays out between friends. One says 「今日は私がおごるよ|きょうはわたしがおごるよ」, today's on me. The other protests politely, 「えっ、いいの|えっ、いいの」, are you sure. Then 「ありがとう、ごちそうさま」 after the meal. Next time, the friend who was treated usually pays back with a treat of their own, to keep things even. The noun form is 「おごり」, a treat, and it appears in the handy line 「私のおごり|わたしのおごり」, my treat. In a group, the person treating is often the senior, and juniors say 「ごちそうになります」 before eating, which humbly accepts the meal. Notice that you don't use this word about your boss to their face, since it's casual. Talking to a superior, you'd say 「ごちそうさまでした」 instead. A casual tease between friends is 「おごって」, treat me. So it's friendly, a little cheeky, and always paired with thanks." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This verb is older than its treating meaning. The kanji sometimes used for it, which you'll rarely see, is the one that also means luxury or extravagance. The older sense of the verb was to live lavishly, to spend money with a flourish. From showing off with money, it came to mean spending your money on someone else's meal. There's also a homophone written with a different kanji that means to be proud or arrogant. The famous opening of the old war tale, The Tale of the Heike, says that the proud do not last for long, and the word for proud there is this very sound. So the two senses share a flavor of extravagance and showing off. Today, treating your friends has lost that arrogant ring, and it just sounds generous. But it's fun to know that a casual dinner word has roots in luxury and pride." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular u verb, so it conjugates like 「とる」 or 「乗る|のる」. Polite form 「おごります」, te form 「おごって」, past 「おごった」. The fun part is combining it with giving and receiving verbs. When a friend treats you, the warmest way to say so is 「友だちがおごってくれました|ともだちがおごってくれました」, my friend treated me, kindly, for my benefit. Or from your side, 「友だちにおごってもらいました|ともだちにおごってもらいました」, I got my friend to treat me. Notice the friend takes 「が」 with 「くれる」 and 「に」 with 「もらう」. When you treat someone, avoid 「おごってあげる」 to their face, since it can sound like you're making a big deal of your kindness. Just say 「おごるよ」. And watch the person and food. The person gets 「に」, the meal gets 「を」. Treats are perfect practice for those giving and receiving patterns." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's a casual verb for paying for someone's meal, often seniors treating juniors, with the person taking 「に」 and the meal taking 「を」, as in 「今日は私がおごります|きょうはわたしがおごります」.",
      "The noun 「おごり」 gives 「私のおごり|わたしのおごり」, my treat, but it's casual, so with a boss you just thank them with 「ごちそうさまでした」.",
      "The verb once meant living lavishly with money, and a homophone means proud, so a casual dinner word has roots in luxury and showing off.",
      "Say 「おごってくれました|おごってくれました」 when a friend treated you and 「おごってもらいました|おごってもらいました」 for receiving, but avoid 「おごってあげる」 to someone's face."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}に{昼ごはん|ひるごはん}を[おごりました|おごりました|おごる]。",
    en: "I treated my friend to lunch." },
  "わらう|笑う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is read with its native reading, 「わら」, followed by 「う」. Look at the top of the kanji. Those two little shapes are the bamboo part, the same top you see on many kanji for bamboo things. The origin of the bottom part is debated, so here's a memory hook instead. A way to remember it: picture bamboo bending and swaying in the wind, like a person doubled over, shaking with laughter. The verb covers laughing and also smiling. A smiling face is 「笑顔|えがお」, with 「顔|かお」, face, which you know. Here's a fun modern note. In Japanese online chat, people type the letter w after a message to mean lol. It comes from the first sound of this very word. Several w's in a row look like grass, so laughing online is jokingly called grass. If you see a Japanese comment full of w's, someone is laughing. One grammar point. To laugh at someone or something, use 「を」. Recap: bamboo top, bending with laughter, 「笑う|わらう」 for laughing and 「笑顔|えがお」 for a smiling face." },
      { title: "Sounds of laughing", text: "Japanese loves sound words, and laughing has a whole set of them. Each one paints a different kind of laugh. 「にこにこ」 is a warm, happy smile, the face of a cheerful shopkeeper. 「げらげら」 is loud belly laughter, people roaring at a comedy show. 「くすくす」 is quiet giggling, the kind you try to hide in class. 「にやにや」 is a sly grin or smirk, someone who knows a secret or is thinking something cheeky. And 「あはは」 is just ha ha. These usually come right before the verb, as in 「げらげら笑う|げらげらわらう」, to laugh loudly, or 「くすくす笑う|くすくすわらう」, to giggle. You'll also hear them with 「する」, as in 「にこにこしている」, to be all smiles. Notice how many of them are a sound repeated twice. That doubling is very typical of these words. Pick the sound and you pick the laugh." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Several words grow out of this kanji. 「笑い|わらい」 is the noun, a laugh or laughter. 「笑顔|えがお」, a smiling face, has an unusual reading. The kanji is read 「え」 there, an old reading tied to this one word, so just memorize it. Then there's 「微笑む|ほほえむ」, to smile gently, with no sound at all, the quiet smile of the Mona Lisa. So 「笑う|わらう」 can include sound, while 「微笑む|ほほえむ」 is silent and soft. A bitter or awkward smile, the face you make when something is embarrassing, is 「苦笑い|にがわらい」, using the kanji for bitter, 「苦い|にがい」. A funny story is 「笑い話|わらいばなし」. And the comedy world is 「お笑い|おわらい」. Notice how the verb's sound 「わら」 stays the same in most of these. So laugh, laughter, a gentle smile, a bitter smile, and comedy, one kanji ties them all together." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a thriving comedy scene called 「お笑い|おわらい」. Its most famous form is 「漫才|まんざい」, stand-up comedy by a duo. One partner plays the fool and says silly things. The other plays the straight man, and snaps back with a correction, sometimes with a smack on the head. That snappy comeback is called 「つっこみ」. Osaka is the heart of this tradition, and people from Osaka are stereotyped as natural comedians. On television, comedians appear on almost every variety show. There's also an old proverb, 「笑う門には福来る|わらうかどにはふくきたる」, which means good fortune comes to the gate of a house where people laugh. In other words, a happy home attracts luck. People quote it to cheer each other up. So laughter in Japan is serious business, from Osaka comedy duos to a proverb promising that laughing brings good luck." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has a bamboo top, so picture bamboo bending like someone doubled over laughing, and it covers smiling too, as in 「笑顔|えがお」, a smiling face.",
      "Sound words paint each laugh, from 「にこにこ」, a warm smile, and 「げらげら」, belly laughter, to 「くすくす」, giggling, and 「にやにや」, a smirk.",
      "The kanji also gives 「笑い|わらい」, laughter, 「苦笑い|にがわらい」, a bitter smile, and 「お笑い|おわらい」, comedy, while 「微笑む|ほほえむ」 is a silent gentle smile.",
      "Japan loves 「漫才|まんざい」 comedy duos, especially from Osaka, and the proverb 「笑う門には福来る|わらうかどにはふくきたる」 says luck comes to a laughing home."
    ],
    ja: "みんなが[笑いました|わらいました|わらう]。",
    en: "Everyone laughed." },
  "おちこむ|落ち込む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a compound verb, two verbs glued together, both with native readings. The first is 「落ちる|おちる」, to fall or drop. You'll know it from things falling off a desk, or failing an exam, which in Japanese is falling off it too. The second is 「込む|こむ」, which means to go into, to get packed in. On its own, it's the verb for a train being crowded. When it's attached after another verb, it adds the sense of going deep inside. So falling plus going in means sinking down into a hole. Literally, that's what it can mean, like ground caving in. But most often it's about feelings. You sink into a low mood. To feel down, to get depressed after something goes wrong. Note it's not usually a medical word. It's what you'd say after a bad test, a breakup, or a failed job interview. People commonly use the 「ている」 form, as in 「落ち込んでいる|おちこんでいる」, to say someone is feeling down right now. If a friend is 「落ち込んでいる|おちこんでいる」, you might cheer them up and say 「げんきを出して|げんきをだして」. Recap: fall plus sink in gives getting down in the dumps." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji are worth a closer look. 「落|お」 has the grass part across the top and the three drops of water on the left. It's usually explained as leaves and plants falling, dropping down. You'll meet its Chinese-origin reading 「らく」 in 「落第|らくだい」, failing a grade. And its partner verb 「落とす|おとす」 means to drop something, like your phone. Now the second kanji, 「込|こ」, is special. It's one of the kanji that were created in Japan, not imported from China. Look at it. The road-moving part on the left and bottom, and inside, the kanji from 「入る|はいる」, to enter. Moving and entering, going in. Because it was made in Japan, it has no Chinese-origin reading at all, only the native one. You also see it in 「込む|こむ」, crowded, and 「申し込む|もうしこむ」, to apply. So leaves falling plus a Japanese-made kanji for going in. Fall, then sink in." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second verb in this compound, 「込む|こむ」, is a busy helper. Stick it after another verb's stem and it adds the idea of going in or going deep. 「飛び込む|とびこむ」 is to jump into, like diving into a pool. 「書き込む|かきこむ」 is to write in, filling in a form, or posting a comment online. 「申し込む|もうしこむ」 is to apply or sign up, putting your name in. 「思い込む|おもいこむ」 is to be convinced of something, often wrongly, thinking yourself deep into an idea. And 「落ち込む|おちこむ」, falling in, sinking down. In each case, the first verb says how, and 「込む|こむ」 says into. Once you see this pattern, a lot of new verbs become easy to guess. If you know the first verb, you can usually figure out the compound. So remember the helper. Whatever verb comes before it, it's going in deep." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When a friend is feeling down, here's how the conversation might go. You notice and ask, 「どうしたの、落ち込んでるね|どうしたの、おちこんでるね」, what's wrong, you seem down. They explain about the bad test. You say 「そんなに落ち込まないで|そんなにおちこまないで」, don't be so down. Then the classic 「元気出して|げんきだして」, cheer up. Notice the 「ないで」 form for don't. A casual synonym young people use is 「へこむ」, which literally means to get dented, like a dented can. 「ちょっとへこんだ」 means I got a bit bummed out. It's lighter and more playful. And for the opposite, getting back on your feet, there's 「立ち直る|たちなおる」, to recover. Also notice that this word focuses on the mood itself, unlike 「悲しい|かなしい」, sad, which is an adjective for feeling. Down, dented, then back on your feet." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「落ちる|おちる」, to fall, plus 「込む|こむ」, to go deep inside, means sinking down, usually into a low mood, so 「落ち込んでいる|おちこんでいる」 means feeling down right now.",
      "「落|お」 pictures leaves falling, while 「込|こ」 is a kanji made in Japan from the road part and 「入る|はいる」, enter, so it has only a native reading.",
      "After a verb stem, 「込む|こむ」 adds going in deep, as in 「飛び込む|とびこむ」, to jump in, 「申し込む|もうしこむ」, to apply, and 「思い込む|おもいこむ」, to be convinced.",
      "Comfort a friend with 「そんなに落ち込まないで|そんなにおちこまないで」, while young people say 「へこむ」 for a lighter bummed out and 「立ち直る|たちなおる」 for recovering."
    ],
    ja: "{試験|しけん}がだめで、[落ち込みました|おちこみました|おちこむ]。",
    en: "The exam went badly, so I got depressed." },
  "こまる|困る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji is read with its native reading, 「こま」, plus 「る」. Its shape is easy to see. It's 「木|き」, tree, inside a closed box. The real origin of the kanji is debated, so take this as a memory hook. A way to remember it: a tree boxed in on every side has no room to grow. It's stuck. It's in trouble. That's the feeling of this verb. Having a problem, being at a loss, not knowing what to do. The grammar has a twist for English speakers. To say you're in trouble right now, Japanese uses the 「ている」 form, 「困っています|こまっています」, because it's an ongoing state. And the thing causing trouble usually comes first with a reason form, as in 「お金がなくて困っています|おかねがなくてこまっています」, I'm stuck because I have no money. A very common little phrase is 「困ったな|こまったな」, said to yourself when something goes wrong, like oh no, what now. You'll also hear 「困ったときは|こまったときは」, when you're in trouble, as in come to me anytime. Recap: a tree stuck in a box, stuck and in trouble, usually 「困っています|こまっています」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb has a polite superpower. When something someone does is a problem for you, Japanese people often don't say no directly. Instead, they say 「それは困ります|それはこまります」, that would be a problem for me. It's a soft but firm way to refuse or complain. A shop clerk might say it to a customer who's parking in the wrong place. A landlord might say it about noise. Hearing it means please stop. You also hear 「困るなあ|こまるなあ」, said with a sigh, which means come on, that's a pain. And kindness goes the other way. 「困っている人|こまっているひと」 means a person in need, someone who needs help. If you see someone lost, you can ask 「何か困っていますか|なにかこまっていますか」, are you having some trouble. So the same verb lets you complain politely and offer help kindly." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji in this verb has a Chinese-origin reading, 「こん」, that shows up in serious words. 「困難|こんなん」 means difficulty or hardship, a word you'll see in news and speeches. 「貧困|ひんこん」 means poverty. Both carry the boxed-in, stuck feeling. In daily conversation, though, you'll use the verb. Watch the past form too. 「困った|こまった」 often describes how you feel right now, not in the past, because it's a sudden realization. Oh no, I'm stuck. A close cousin is 「迷う|まよう」, to be unsure or lost. With 「迷う|まよう」, you can't decide between choices. With 「困る|こまる」, you have a problem and no answer. Another neighbor is 「大変|たいへん」, tough or hard, which describes the situation rather than your feeling. It's 「大変|たいへん」 and I'm 「困っている|こまっている」. So difficulty in the news, being stuck in daily life, one boxed-in kanji." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "There's a lovely saying that uses this verb. 「困った時はお互い様|こまったときはおたがいさま」. It means roughly, when you're in trouble, we're all in the same boat. The idea is that everyone gets stuck sometimes, so helping each other is natural and nobody should feel too guilty about asking. People say it when they help a stranger, or when someone apologizes for being a bother. It's a gentle way of saying don't worry, you'd do the same for me. Japanese culture puts a lot of weight on not being a burden, so people often hesitate to ask for help. This phrase is the cultural antidote. If you ever help a Japanese person and they keep thanking you and apologizing, you can smile and say it back to them. It will land beautifully. In trouble, we help each other, and that's just how it goes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Picture a tree boxed in with no room to grow, stuck and in trouble, and because it's an ongoing state you usually say 「困っています|こまっています」.",
      "「それは困ります|それはこまります」 is a soft but firm way to say please stop, while 「困っている人|こまっているひと」 means a person in need of help.",
      "Read 「こん」 it gives serious words like 「困難|こんなん」, difficulty, and in daily speech 「困った|こまった」 often expresses a sudden oh no right now.",
      "The saying 「困った時はお互い様|こまったときはおたがいさま」 means we all get stuck sometimes, so helping each other is natural and nobody should feel guilty."
    ],
    ja: "お{金|かね}がなくて、[困っています|こまっています|こまる]。",
    en: "I have no money, so I'm in trouble." },
  "だす|出す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is the partner of one you know, 「出る|でる」, to go out or come out. The kanji 「出|だ」 is the same, using its native reading. 「出る|でる」 is something that goes out by itself. You leave a room. 「出す|だす」 is you sending something out. You take it out, put it out, or hand it in. That's why the meanings look like a mixed bag in English, but they're all one idea: something moves out because you made it. You hand in homework, 「宿題を出す|しゅくだいをだす」. You mail a letter, 「手紙を出す|てがみをだす」. You put the garbage out, 「ごみを出す|ごみをだす」. You take something out of your bag. The same kanji also appears with its Chinese-origin reading 「しゅつ」 in 「出身|しゅっしん」, where you come from, and as 「で」 in 「出かける|でかける」, to go out. A nice phrase to know is 「げんきを出して|げんきをだして」, cheer up, which is literally put your energy out. Recap: 「出る|でる」 something goes out, 「出す|だす」 you send it out, homework, letters and garbage included." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has a second life as a helper. Put it after the stem of another verb, and it means to start doing something, often suddenly. 「泣き出す|なきだす」 means to burst into tears. 「笑い出す|わらいだす」 means to burst out laughing. 「降り出す|ふりだす」 means rain starting to fall. 「走り出す|はしりだす」, to break into a run. The feeling is that the action comes bursting out. Compare it with 「始める|はじめる」, which also follows a stem to mean begin. 「食べ始める|たべはじめる」 is a calm, deliberate start. 「泣き出す|なきだす」 is sudden and uncontrolled. There's also the very common 「思い出す|おもいだす」, which means to remember, as in recalling something from the past. Literally, it's to pull a thought out. Don't confuse it with 「覚える|おぼえる」, to memorize. You 「覚える|おぼえる」 new words, and later you 「思い出す|おもいだす」 them. Start suddenly, or pull out a memory." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, putting out the garbage, 「ごみを出す|ごみをだす」, is a serious skill. Towns have detailed rules. Burnable garbage goes out on certain days. Plastics on another. Cans, bottles and paper on yet another. Large items need a special sticker you buy at the convenience store. Each town hands out a calendar, and you must put the bags out on the right morning, often before eight, at a set collection spot. Put it out on the wrong day, and a neighbor may leave it there with a warning sticker. Newcomers, foreigners and Japanese alike, often find it confusing at first. The phrase 「ごみの日|ごみのひ」, garbage day, is something every household watches. So when you learn this verb, picture yourself in the early morning, carefully carrying the right bag to the right spot. Putting out the trash, done the proper way." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji gives you a handy group of words. 「出口|でぐち」 is an exit, the out mouth. Its partner is 「入口|いりぐち」, the entrance. In stations you'll follow signs for these all day. 「出発|しゅっぱつ」 means departure, used for trains and trips. 「出席|しゅっせき」 means attendance, being present at class. The verb 「出す|だす」 is also used for serving food, as in 「お茶を出す|おちゃをだす」, to serve tea to a guest, and for publishing or releasing, like a band putting out a new song. And 「声を出す|こえをだす」 means to speak up, to put out your voice. A teacher might say this to a quiet class. In all these, something is coming out into the world. Exit, departure, attendance, serving and publishing. The kanji's shape even looks like something rising up and out. When it comes out, it's this kanji." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「出る|でる」 is something going out by itself, while 「出す|だす」 is you sending it out, so you hand in homework, mail letters and put out garbage with it.",
      "After a verb stem it means to start suddenly, like 「泣き出す|なきだす」, to burst into tears, and 「思い出す|おもいだす」 means to pull out a memory, to remember.",
      "Putting out the garbage, 「ごみを出す|ごみをだす」, is a serious skill in Japan, with set days for each kind and bags out before eight on the right morning.",
      "The kanji gives 「出口|でぐち」, exit, and 「出発|しゅっぱつ」, departure, and the verb also serves tea, releases songs, and with 「声を出す|こえをだす」, speaks up."
    ],
    ja: "{宿題|しゅくだい}を[出しました|だしました|だす]。",
    en: "I handed in my homework." },
  "なおす|直す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading, 「なお」. Its core meaning is straight, direct, honest. To fix something is to make it straight again, so that it's right. That one idea covers many uses. You fix a broken bike. You correct mistakes in a paper. A teacher corrects your Japanese. The thing you fix takes 「を」. There's a related kanji to know about. When you cure an illness, the same sound 「なおす」 is usually written with a different kanji that means to govern or heal. Same sound, different spelling, so don't be surprised to see both. A very handy pattern comes later. 「直す|なおす」 can be added to the stem of another verb to mean redo it, do it again properly. For example, 「書き直す|かきなおす」, to rewrite, and 「やり直す|やりなおす」, to do over. It keeps the same idea of making it straight and right. Pair it with the intransitive partner 「直る|なおる」, which means something gets fixed by itself or is fixed. Recap: straight and right, so 「直す|なおす」 is to make something right again, fixing or correcting it." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji, with its core idea of straight, has several readings you'll meet. With the Chinese-origin reading 「ちょく」, it appears in 「直接|ちょくせつ」, directly, and 「直行|ちょっこう」, going straight there without stops. With 「じき」, it appears in 「正直|しょうじき」, honest, straight and correct. And there's the lovely word 「素直|すなお」, which describes someone open, honest and obedient, like a child who listens well. The idea of straight runs through them all. A straight path, a straight person, straightening out a mistake. Inside the kanji you can spot an eye, the box with lines, which you'll recognize from 「目|め」. One common explanation is a straight gaze, looking directly at something, but treat that as a helpful picture rather than settled fact. So 「ちょく」 for direct, 「じき」 for honest, and 「なお」 for fixing. Making things straight in every sense." },
      { title: "A Kansai twist", text: "Here's a regional surprise. In western Japan, including Osaka and Kyoto, this same verb has an extra meaning. It means to put something away. A mother in Osaka might say 「おもちゃをなおして」, and she doesn't mean fix your toys. She means put your toys back where they belong. The idea is still putting something back in its right place, so it connects to the meaning of making things right. In standard Japanese, you'd use 「片付ける|かたづける」, to tidy up, or 「しまう」, to put away. People from Tokyo sometimes get confused, and there are lots of jokes about it. A Kansai person asks a coworker to fix the documents, and the coworker starts checking for typos, when they really just meant file them away. If you visit Osaka and hear it about objects that aren't broken, now you know. Fix it, or put it back, depending on where you are." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are everyday situations for this verb. At school, you'll ask a teacher 「作文を直してください|さくぶんをなおしてください」, please correct my essay. At a repair shop, 「時計を直してもらいました|とけいをなおしてもらいました」, I had my watch fixed. Notice the 「てもらう」 pattern for getting someone else to fix it. In the mirror, you 「化粧を直す|けしょうをなおす」, touch up your make-up, and the women's restroom is sometimes politely called a place for exactly that. And for moods, 「機嫌を直す|きげんをなおす」 means to cheer up after being upset, fixing your bad mood. A partner might plead 「機嫌を直して|きげんをなおして」, please don't be mad anymore. It also works for habits, as in fixing a bad habit. In all these, something is crooked and gets put right. Essays, watches, make-up and moods, all fixed with one verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means straight, so fixing is making something straight and right again, and after a stem it means redo, as in 「書き直す|かきなおす」, to rewrite.",
      "The straight kanji also reads 「ちょく」 in 「直接|ちょくせつ」, directly, and 「じき」 in 「正直|しょうじき」, honest, and appears in 「素直|すなお」, open and obedient.",
      "In Osaka and Kyoto it also means to put something away, so 「おもちゃをなおして」 means put your toys back, not fix them.",
      "Ask 「作文を直してください|さくぶんをなおしてください」 to get an essay corrected, and use 「機嫌を直す|きげんをなおす」 for fixing a bad mood."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}が{私|わたし}の{日本語|にほんご}を[直しました|なおしました|なおす]。",
    en: "The teacher corrected my Japanese." },
  "みつかる|見つかる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is built on the kanji 「見|み」, to see, with its native reading, the same as in 「見る|みる」, to see, and 「見える|みえる」, to be visible. The word means to be found, or to turn up. Think of it as the thing becoming seen. It's the partner of 「見つける|みつける」, to find. Here's how they divide the job. With 「見つける|みつける」, a person does the finding, and the thing found takes 「を」. With 「見つかる|みつかる」, the thing just turns up, and it takes 「が」. So 「さいふが見つかりました|さいふがみつかりました」, my wallet was found, or my wallet turned up. Japanese often prefers this way of speaking, focusing on what happened rather than who did it. A common mistake for English speakers is using 「を」 with this verb. Don't. It also gets used in job hunting and housing. If you finally found a job or an apartment, you'd say it was found, using this verb. And the 「ない」 form, 「見つからない|みつからない」, is the go-to way to say I can't find it. Recap: something becomes seen, turns up, with 「が」. Partner of 「見つける|みつける」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This pair follows a pattern you'll see over and over. Intransitive verbs ending in 「あ」 sound plus 「る」, paired with transitive verbs ending in 「える」. 「見つかる|みつかる」 and 「見つける|みつける」. 「始まる|はじまる」, something begins, and 「始める|はじめる」, you begin it. 「閉まる|しまる」 and 「閉める|しめる」, to close. 「決まる|きまる」 and 「決める|きめる」, to be decided and to decide. 「集まる|あつまる」 and 「集める|あつめる」, to gather. The 「あ」 sound version just happens, with 「が」. The 「え」 sound version is someone doing it, with 「を」. Notice that the 「える」 ones are ru verbs, while the 「あ」 sound ones are u verbs. So 「見つかります|みつかります」 but 「見つけます|みつけます」. Learn this pattern once and dozens of verbs fall into place. Things happen with the 「あ」 sound, people act with the 「え」 sound." },
      { title: "Caught in the act", text: "This verb has a sneaky second use. It means to be caught, to be found out. Say a student is reading a comic in class. 「先生に見つかった|せんせいにみつかった」, I got caught by the teacher. Notice that the person who did the finding takes 「に」. A child hiding a broken vase worries about 「お母さんに見つかる|おかあさんにみつかる」, being discovered by mom. In the game of hide and seek, 「かくれんぼ」, the seeker shouts 「みつけた」, found you, often stretched as 「みーつけた」. That uses the partner verb, from the seeker's side. And the hider, from their side, could groan 「見つかった|みつかった」, I've been found. It's the same moment told from two directions. So the verb isn't only about lost wallets. It's also about secrets coming out. Found it, or got caught, depending on who you are in the story." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan is famous for lost things coming back. If you drop a wallet in a train station, there's a very good chance someone will take it to a police box or a station office, often with the cash still inside. Lost items are called 「落とし物|おとしもの」, literally dropped things, and police stations keep huge numbers of them every year, from umbrellas to phones to cash. In Tokyo alone, billions of yen in lost cash are handed in to the police each year. Many children are taught to bring found items to the police box, the 「交番|こうばん」. So if you lose something in Japan, don't panic. Ask at the station or police box, and quite often you'll get the happy news, 「見つかりましたよ|みつかりましたよ」, it's been found. Something lost, something turned up, thanks to honest strangers." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means something becomes seen and turns up, so the thing takes 「が」, as in 「さいふが見つかりました|さいふがみつかりました」, while its partner 「見つける|みつける」 takes 「を」.",
      "It follows a pattern where the 「あ」 sound plus 「る」 version just happens with 「が」, like 「始まる|はじまる」, and the 「える」 version is someone acting with 「を」.",
      "It also means to get caught, with the finder marked by 「に」, as in 「先生に見つかった|せんせいにみつかった」, I got caught by the teacher.",
      "Lost items in Japan are very often handed to a 「交番|こうばん」, so if you ask, you may well hear 「見つかりましたよ|みつかりましたよ」, it's been found."
    ],
    ja: "さいふが[見つかりました|みつかりました|みつかる]。",
    en: "My wallet was found." },
  "やくす|訳す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading, 「やく」, plus 「す」. Look at its left side. That's the speech or words part. It's the same part you see on 「話す|はなす」, to speak, 「言う|いう」, to say, 「読む|よむ」, to read, and 「語|ご」 in 「外国語|がいこくご」, foreign language. Whenever you see it, think words. So this verb is about doing something with words, turning them from one language into another. The grammar: the language you translate into takes 「に」, as in 「英語に訳す|えいごにやくす」, to translate into English. The language you translate from takes 「から」. The same kanji appears in words you'll meet later. Translation as a noun is 「翻訳|ほんやく」, and an interpreter is 「通訳|つうやく」. And here's a surprise. It also shows up in 「言い訳|いいわけ」, which means an excuse. There, the kanji means reason. You can think of an excuse as explaining yourself in other words. Recap: the words part on the left, turning words into another language, into with 「に」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's a surprise about this verb. The sound 「やく」 is actually this kanji's Chinese-origin reading, not a native one. The verb is made by taking a Chinese-origin sound and adding 「す」, a lot like adding 「する」. In fact, you'll sometimes hear 「訳する|やくする」 too. Several verbs work this way, such as 「愛す|あいす」, to love, and 「略す|りゃくす」, to abbreviate. The native reading of this kanji is 「わけ」, which you'll meet in other words. Grammatically, it now behaves as a regular u verb ending in 「す」. Polite form 「訳します|やくします」, te form 「訳して|やくして」, negative 「訳さない|やくさない」. Combine it with patterns you know. 「訳してもらえませんか|やくしてもらえませんか」, could you translate it for me. Or 「訳してみました|やくしてみました」, I tried translating it. So a Chinese sound plus 「す」, conjugating just like 「話す|はなす」. One small verb, a borrowed sound." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its native reading 「わけ」, this kanji means reason or meaning, and it pops up in very common phrases. You already saw 「言い訳|いいわけ」, an excuse. Even more important is 「申し訳ありません|もうしわけありません」, a very polite I'm sorry. Literally, it's something like I have no explanation to offer. You'll hear it constantly from shop staff, train announcements and business emails. A slightly softer version is 「申し訳ない|もうしわけない」. Then there's the word 「わけ」 itself, often in kana, meaning reason, as in 「そういうわけで」, and so, for that reason. Back on the translation side, 「直訳|ちょくやく」 means a literal, word for word translation, using the kanji for straight. Its opposite is 「意訳|いやく」, a free translation that follows the meaning. So this kanji links translation, reasons, excuses and apologies. All of them are about explaining what something really means." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "As a learner, you'll use this verb in two directions. Asking for help, say 「英語に訳してください|えいごにやくしてください」, please translate it into English. Or ask about a word, 「これはどう訳しますか|これはどうやくしますか」, how do you translate this. Some Japanese words famously don't translate neatly. 「よろしくおねがいします」 is the classic example, a greeting that can mean nice to meet you, please treat me well, or thanks in advance, depending on the situation. When something is hard to translate, people say 「訳しにくい|やくしにくい」, hard to translate, using the 「にくい」 ending. Also handy is the noun 「翻訳|ほんやく」 for written translation, and 「通訳|つうやく」 for spoken interpreting, which is also the word for the interpreter person. Remember the difference. Translators work with text, interpreters with speech. Translating is a great way to practice, so try translating short sentences into Japanese yourself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The words part on the left points to turning words into another language, and the language you translate into takes 「に」, as in 「英語に訳す|えいごにやくす」.",
      "「やく」 is actually the Chinese-origin reading plus 「す」, much like adding 「する」, and it conjugates as a regular u verb, like 「訳して|やくして」.",
      "With its native reading 「わけ」, meaning reason, the kanji appears in 「言い訳|いいわけ」, an excuse, and 「申し訳ありません|もうしわけありません」, a very polite apology.",
      "Ask 「英語に訳してください|えいごにやくしてください」, and remember 「翻訳|ほんやく」 is written translation while 「通訳|つうやく」 is spoken interpreting."
    ],
    ja: "{英語|えいご}に[訳して|やくして|やくす]ください。",
    en: "Please translate it into English." },
  "かす|貸す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading, 「か」, plus 「す」. Look at the bottom part of it. That's 「貝|かい」, shell. In ancient China, shells were used as money, so this shell part shows up in lots of kanji connected to money and value. Lending things, money especially, fits right in. This verb is the opposite partner of 「借りる|かりる」, to borrow. Notice they even start with the same sound, which makes them easy to mix up. The key is the direction. With 「貸す|かす」, the thing goes away from you. With 「借りる|かりる」, it comes to you. A very common mistake: when you want to ask a friend for their pen, you can say 「貸してください|かしてください」, which literally means please lend it to me. That's correct and very natural. Or you can say 「借りてもいいですか|かりてもいいですか」, may I borrow it. Both work, as long as the verb matches who is acting. The person you lend to takes 「に」, as in 「友だちにかさを貸す|ともだちにかさをかす」, to lend a friend an umbrella. Recap: shell money underneath, things going away from you, opposite of 「借りる|かりる」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. The sound of this verb is close to the English word cash. Picture a generous friend at a café, pulling bills out of their wallet and pressing them into your hand. Here, cash, take it, pay me back later. They're the lender. The cash leaves their hand and goes to you. Now picture the shell at the bottom of the kanji, the ancient money shell, sitting on top of that stack of bills like a paperweight. Shell money, cash money, it's all leaving the lender's wallet. That direction is what matters. When you think of lending, think of the cash going away from you. The other verb, 「借りる|かりる」, is the friend who receives it. Both start with 「か」, but only the lender's word sounds like cash. Cash going out from your hand, that's lending." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji turns up in some useful real-world words. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「たい」, as in 「賃貸|ちんたい」, rental, the word you'll see on apartment listings and real estate signs. 「貸し切り|かしきり」 means reserved for a private group. A restaurant or a bus with that on the door is booked by one party, so you can't go in. A rental bicycle is often called a 「貸し自転車|かしじてんしゃ」, a bike for lending. Then there's the cycle of lending. 「貸す|かす」, lend, 「借りる|かりる」, borrow, and 「返す|かえす」, give back. Three verbs, all starting with 「か」, which is actually handy for remembering them as a set. There's also the noun phrase 「貸し借り|かしかり」, lending and borrowing, which people use for favors too, as in we're even now. Lend, borrow, return, and a closed door marked private." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular u verb ending in 「す」. 「貸します|かします」, 「貸して|かして」, 「貸さない|かさない」. It works beautifully with the giving and receiving verbs. When a friend lends you something, say 「貸してくれました|かしてくれました」, they lent it to me, kindly. Asking casually, 「ちょっと貸して|ちょっとかして」, lend it to me for a sec, which is how kids ask for an eraser. More politely, 「貸してくれませんか|かしてくれませんか」, would you lend it to me. Even more polite, 「貸していただけませんか|かしていただけませんか」. When you lend something to someone, it's 「貸してあげました|かしてあげました」, though saying that about yourself can sound a little boastful. To ask for permission from your side, use the partner verb. 「借りてもいいですか|かりてもいいですか」. A common mistake is saying 「貸してもいいですか|かしてもいいですか」, which means may I lend it to you. Watch the direction, and pick your politeness." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has 「貝|かい」, shell, the ancient money, at the bottom, and lending sends something away from you, the opposite of 「借りる|かりる」, to borrow.",
      "The sound is close to cash, so picture a friend pressing cash into your hand, with the money leaving the lender, since only the lender's word sounds like cash.",
      "Its reading 「たい」 appears in 「賃貸|ちんたい」, rental, and 「貸し切り|かしきり」 means reserved for a private group, while 「貸す|かす」, 「借りる|かりる」 and 「返す|かえす」 form a set.",
      "Ask with 「貸してくれませんか|かしてくれませんか」 or 「借りてもいいですか|かりてもいいですか」, but not 「貸してもいいですか|かしてもいいですか」, which offers to lend."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}にかさを[貸しました|かしました|かす]。",
    en: "I lent my umbrella to a friend." },
  "つれていく|連れていく": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the te form of 「連れる|つれる」, to bring someone along, plus 「いく」, to go. The kanji 「連|つ」 uses its native reading. It has the moving-along-a-road part on the left and bottom, and 「車|くるま」, car or cart, in the middle. Its core meaning is to link up or accompany. A way to remember it: carts traveling together in a line. So the whole phrase means to go somewhere with a person in tow. You take your child to the park, or take a friend to a restaurant. Here's the key point for English speakers. Japanese splits take by what you're taking. For people and animals, use this verb. For things, use 「持っていく|もっていく」, take a thing, which you already know. So you don't 「持っていく|もっていく」 your little brother. The direction works the same as with things too. Its partner 「連れてくる|つれてくる」 means to bring someone here, toward where you are. Going away uses 「いく」, coming toward uses 「くる」. The place you take them takes 「に」. Recap: accompany plus go, for people only, while things use 「持っていく|もっていく」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji in 「連れる|つれる」 has a Chinese-origin reading, 「れん」, with the idea of linking things in a row. 「連絡|れんらく」 means contact, getting in touch, as in 「連絡してください|れんらくしてください」, please contact me. 「連休|れんきゅう」 means consecutive days off, a long weekend or a holiday stretch like Golden Week. You'll also see the native reading inside other words. 「家族連れ|かぞくづれ」 means families out together, a family with kids in tow, and the sound softens to 「づれ」. 「子ども連れ|こどもづれ」 means someone with children. Restaurant staff use 「お連れ様|おつれさま」 for your companion, as in your companion is waiting. So the kanji ties together contact, linked holidays, and people traveling together. A chain of carts, a chain of days, a chain of messages. It's all linking up." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There's a set of these taking verbs, all built from the te form of 「連れる|つれる」. Going away from here, 「連れていく|つれていく」. Coming toward here, 「連れてくる|つれてくる」. And heading home, 「連れて帰る|つれてかえる」, to take someone home. Mix them with giving and receiving. 「父が動物園に連れていってくれました|ちちがどうぶつえんにつれていってくれました」, my dad took me to the zoo. A child begging would say 「連れていって|つれていって」, take me with you. Shop staff use a humble form, 「お連れします|おつれします」, I'll take you there, when they walk you to your seat. Remember that the 「いく」 part conjugates like 「行く|いく」, so the te form is 「いって」, not the regular pattern. One more tip. In casual speech, people shorten it, dropping the 「い」 to say 「連れてく|つれてく」. Take, bring, take home, all built on one te form." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In daily Japanese life, you'll hear this verb with kids and dogs especially. 「犬を散歩に連れていく|いぬをさんぽにつれていく」 means to take the dog for a walk. Notice the purpose 「に」 there, for a walk. Parents talk about taking kids to the doctor, to swim class, to the park. Couples talk about taking a partner to meet their parents, a big step. A common learner mistake is using this verb for objects, like an umbrella. That sounds like the umbrella is your little friend. Another mistake is using it for people who are senior to you. Saying you'll take your teacher somewhere can sound like you're leading them around. Politer is 「ご案内します|ごあんないします」, I'll guide you. Here's a mini exchange. 「今度、いい店に連れていって|こんど、いいみせにつれていって」, take me somewhere good next time. 「いいよ」, sure. People and pets in tow, not umbrellas or bosses." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji pictures carts linked in a line, and the phrase means taking a person or animal along, while things use 「持っていく|もっていく」.",
      "Read 「れん」, the linking kanji gives 「連絡|れんらく」, contact, and 「連休|れんきゅう」, consecutive days off, and natively 「家族連れ|かぞくづれ」, families out together.",
      "Use 「連れていく|つれていく」 for taking away, 「連れてくる|つれてくる」 for bringing here, and 「連れて帰る|つれてかえる」 for taking someone home, as in 「連れていってくれました|つれていってくれました」.",
      "Use it for kids and dogs, as in 「犬を散歩に連れていく|いぬをさんぽにつれていく」, but not for umbrellas, and for seniors say 「ご案内します|ごあんないします」."
    ],
    ja: "{子ども|こども}を{公園|こうえん}に[連れていきました|つれていきました|つれていく]。",
    en: "I took my child to the park." },
  "みちにまよう|道に迷う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three pieces, all native readings. 「道|みち」 is the road or way. 「に」 marks where the problem happens. And 「迷う|まよう」 means to be lost, to wander, or to be unsure which way to go. So it's literally to be lost on the road. Look at the kanji 「迷|まよ」. Like 「道|みち」 itself, it has the moving-along-a-road part on the left and bottom. Two road kanji in one phrase, which fits a phrase about wandering. The middle of 「迷|まよ」 is the rice shape, but don't read too much into that. The verb 「迷う|まよう」 also has a second use, being unable to make up your mind. If you can't choose between two dishes on a menu, you're 「迷っている|まよっている」. A lost child is 「迷子|まいご」, which you'll hear in announcements at department stores and amusement parks. A note on Japan. Most streets there don't have names, and addresses go by blocks, so even locals get lost. The little police boxes called 「こうばん」 often help with directions. Recap: road plus wander, two road kanji, to get lost." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at 「道|みち」. Inside the road-moving part sits 「首|くび」, the kanji for neck or head. The way your head leads you along the road. With its Chinese-origin reading 「どう」, this kanji takes on a deeper meaning, the Way, a path of discipline. That's why so many Japanese arts end in it. 「柔道|じゅうどう」, the gentle way. 「剣道|けんどう」, the way of the sword. 「書道|しょどう」, the way of writing, calligraphy. 「茶道|さどう」, the way of tea. Notice that last one also has the reading 「ちゃどう」. Then there's 「北海道|ほっかいどう」, the island prefecture, where the kanji means a region or circuit. And 「水道|すいどう」, the water supply, the way the water travels. So a road can be a street, a pipe, a region, or a lifelong art. Getting lost on one is a lot easier when you know where it leads." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji in 「迷う|まよう」 has a Chinese-origin reading, 「めい」, and you'll meet it in one of the most important words in Japanese social life, 「迷惑|めいわく」, trouble or a nuisance to others. 「迷惑をかける|めいわくをかける」 means to cause trouble for someone. Japanese people are very careful not to do this, and you'll see signs asking you not to be a nuisance, for example by talking on your phone on the train. Literally, the word combines being lost with being confused, the confusion you cause others. Then there's the deciding sense of the verb, as in 「どれにするか迷っています|どれにするかまよっています」, I can't decide which one. And 「迷路|めいろ」 is a maze, a wandering road. So the kanji covers getting lost, mazes, indecision, and causing other people bother. Lost on the road, or lost in your choices." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how getting lost plays out. You walk up to someone and say 「すみません、道に迷ってしまいました|すみません、みちにまよってしまいました」. Excuse me, I've gotten lost. The 「てしまう」 form adds the feeling that it happened, unfortunately. Then ask 「駅はどこですか|えきはどこですか」, where is the station. They might reply with 「まっすぐ行って、右に曲がってください|まっすぐいって、みぎにまがってください」, go straight and turn right. Often, a kind person will just walk you there. Nowadays everyone uses phone maps, but people still get lost in giant stations like Shinjuku, which has many exits. A useful softer sentence is 「迷っています|まよっています」, I'm lost, using the 「ている」 form for an ongoing state. And if you guide someone, you can say 「迷わないでね|まよわないでね」, don't get lost. Lost, then asking, then found again." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「道|みち」, road, plus 「迷う|まよう」, to wander or be unsure, both with the road part, means to be lost on the road, and a lost child is a 「迷子|まいご」.",
      "「道|みち」 has 「首|くび」, head, inside the road part, and read 「どう」 it means a lifelong path of discipline, as in 「柔道|じゅうどう」 and 「茶道|さどう」.",
      "Read 「めい」, the lost kanji gives 「迷惑|めいわく」, a nuisance to others, and 「迷路|めいろ」, a maze, while the verb also means being unable to decide.",
      "Ask for help with 「すみません、道に迷ってしまいました|すみません、みちにまよってしまいました」, where the 「てしまう」 form adds that it unfortunately happened."
    ],
    ja: "きのう[道に迷いました|みちにまよいました|みちにまよう]。",
    en: "I got lost yesterday." },
  "むかえにいく|迎えに行く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase uses a pattern you know from 「買い物に行く|かいものにいく」, to go shopping. A verb stem or action noun, plus 「に」, plus a motion verb, means going in order to do that. Here the action is 「迎え|むかえ」, from the verb 「迎える|むかえる」, to meet, greet or welcome someone who is arriving. The kanji uses its native reading, and it has the moving-along-a-road part on the left and bottom, like in 「近い|ちかい」, near, and 「道|みち」, road. Someone is traveling toward you, and you go out to meet them. So the whole phrase means to go to welcome someone, which in daily life means to go pick them up. Going to the station to get your mother, going to the airport for a friend, going to school to pick up your kid. The place takes 「に」 and the person takes 「を」, as in 「駅に母を迎えに行きます|えきにははをむかえにいきます」. Its partner is 「迎えに来る|むかえにくる」, to come to pick up, for when someone comes to where you are. Recap: welcome plus go for that purpose, going out to get someone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「げい」, this kanji appears in 「歓迎|かんげい」, a warm welcome. You'll see big banners at hotels and airports for tour groups, and a 「歓迎会|かんげいかい」 is a welcome party, held when someone joins a company or club, usually in April. Its opposite is the farewell party, using the kanji for sending off. Speaking of which, the pairing of sending off and picking up gives 「送迎|そうげい」, shuttle service, which you'll see on hotel buses. The native version of that pair is 「送り迎え|おくりむかえ」, dropping off and picking up, a daily job for many parents. The noun 「出迎え|でむかえ」 means going out to greet someone, like waiting at an airport arrival gate. And 「お迎え|おむかえ」 alone is the pickup itself. So this kanji connects warm welcomes, parties, shuttle buses and parenting. Going out to meet people, again and again." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "At Japanese daycares and kindergartens, the pickup time is called 「お迎え|おむかえ」, and it's a daily ritual. Parents rush from work, often by bicycle with a child seat on the back, to arrive on time. Kids wait in matching hats and smocks. Teachers hand each child over with a report of the day. The phrase 「お迎えに来たよ|おむかえにきたよ」, I've come to get you, is what every child wants to hear. The verb 「迎える|むかえる」 also has a beautiful wider use. You welcome not only people but moments. 「新年を迎える|しんねんをむかえる」 means to greet the new year. 「誕生日を迎える|たんじょうびをむかえる」 means to reach your birthday. Older people talk about meeting their twentieth or sixtieth birthday in this way. So the same verb covers picking up a toddler and welcoming a new year. Going out to meet what's arriving, whether a child or a calendar day." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The pattern here is purpose plus motion, and it works with three motion verbs. 「行く|いく」, 「来る|くる」 and 「帰る|かえる」. With 「帰る|かえる」, you get going home to do something. For pickups, you'll also hear the nice combo 「迎えに行ってくる|むかえにいってくる」, I'll go pick them up and come back. That 「てくる」 ending means doing something and returning, a very natural way to tell your family where you're going. Another handy one is 「迎えに行ってあげる|むかえにいってあげる」, I'll go get you, as a favor. In the past tense, 「迎えに行きました|むかえにいきました」. The order inside the sentence is flexible but usually goes place with 「に」, person with 「を」, then the phrase. Notice two 「に」s in one sentence, one for the place and one for the purpose. That's normal. Go to get them, and come back with them." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「迎え|むかえ」, from 「迎える|むかえる」, to welcome, plus the purpose pattern with 「に」 and 「行く|いく」, means going to pick someone up, with the person taking 「を」.",
      "Read 「げい」, the kanji gives 「歓迎|かんげい」, a warm welcome, and 「送迎|そうげい」, shuttle service, while 「送り迎え|おくりむかえ」 is a parent's daily drop-off and pickup.",
      "Daycare pickup is called 「お迎え|おむかえ」, and the verb also welcomes moments, as in 「新年を迎える|しんねんをむかえる」, to greet the new year.",
      "The purpose pattern works with 「行く|いく」, 「来る|くる」 and 「帰る|かえる」, and 「迎えに行ってくる|むかえにいってくる」 means I'll go pick them up and come back."
    ],
    ja: "{駅|えき}に{母|はは}を[迎えに行きます|むかえにいきます|むかえにいく]。",
    en: "I'll go to the station to pick up my mother." },
  "あつめる|集める": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading, 「あつ」, plus 「める」. This is one of those kanji whose picture is real. On top is a bird part, the one used for short-tailed birds, and underneath is 「木|き」, tree. In older forms, there were even several birds on the tree. Birds flocking together on a branch, that's gathering. The verb means to gather things or people together, to collect. You collect stamps, gather information, or call people together for a meeting. The thing collected takes 「を」, and for a hobby you'd use the 「ている」 form, as in 「切手を集めています|きってをあつめています」, I collect stamps. It has an intransitive partner, 「集まる|あつまる」, which means to gather by themselves, like people gathering at a park. You'll find the same kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「しゅう」 in 「募集|ぼしゅう」, recruitment, gathering people for a job or a club. A cultural note: collecting is a big hobby in Japan, from train station stamps that travelers stamp in little notebooks to temple seals. Recap: birds gathering on a tree, collecting things together." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「しゅう」, this kanji builds lots of words about collections. 「集合|しゅうごう」 is gathering at a meeting point, a word on every school trip schedule. 「集中|しゅうちゅう」 means concentration, gathering your attention into the middle. A teacher might say 「集中してください|しゅうちゅうしてください」, please focus. 「写真集|しゃしんしゅう」 is a photo book, a collection of photos. 「全集|ぜんしゅう」 is the complete works of an author. And 「特集|とくしゅう」 is a special feature in a magazine or TV show, a special collection on one topic. The native verbs stay simple. 「集める|あつめる」 is you collecting, and 「集まる|あつまる」 is things or people gathering on their own. A teacher calls out 「みんな、集まって|みんな、あつまって」, everyone, gather round. From photo books to focused minds, it's all about bringing things together in one place." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb sits in a pattern you'll use constantly. 「集める|あつめる」 is a ru verb, and you do it to something with 「を」. 「集まる|あつまる」 is a u verb, and things gather by themselves with 「が」. So a teacher 「学生を集める|がくせいをあつめる」, gathers the students. But the students themselves 「学生が集まる|がくせいがあつまる」, gather. Where they gather takes 「に」, as in 「公園に集まる|こうえんにあつまる」. Pair it with patterns you know. For a hobby, 「集めています|あつめています」. For a goal, 「お金を集めたいです|おかねをあつめたいです」, I want to raise money. For trying something, 「集めてみる|あつめてみる」. And for asking people over, 「ちょっと集まってください|ちょっとあつまってください」, gather round please. Watch out for the te forms. 「集めて|あつめて」 and 「集まって|あつまって」. You collect with 「を」, they gather with 「が」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, gathering is very organized. On school trips, company outings and tours, everything runs on a 「集合時間|しゅうごうじかん」, the meeting time, and a 「集合場所|しゅうごうばしょ」, the meeting place. Being on time means being early. Many people arrive five or ten minutes ahead, and being late keeps the whole group waiting. Then there's the 「集合写真|しゅうごうしゃしん」, the group photo, which ends nearly every event. Everyone lines up in rows, often with the front row crouching, while someone counts down. And collecting as a hobby is everywhere. Kids collect trading cards and little capsule toys from vending machines called 「ガチャガチャ」. Adults collect limited edition snacks or train goods. Collecting is so popular that many shops design products to be gathered as complete sets. Meet on time, take the group photo, and collect the whole set." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji pictures a bird perched on 「木|き」, a tree, birds flocking together, so it means collecting things or gathering people, with the thing taking 「を」.",
      "Read 「しゅう」, the kanji gives 「集合|しゅうごう」, gathering at a meeting point, 「集中|しゅうちゅう」, concentration, and 「特集|とくしゅう」, a special feature.",
      "「集める|あつめる」 is a ru verb where you collect something with 「を」, while 「集まる|あつまる」 is an u verb where people gather by themselves with 「が」.",
      "Japanese group events run on a 「集合時間|しゅうごうじかん」, a meeting time, where being on time means early, and end with a 「集合写真|しゅうごうしゃしん」, a group photo."
    ],
    ja: "{古い|ふるい}{本|ほん}を[集めています|あつめています|あつめる]。",
    en: "I collect old books." },
  "いれる|入れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「入|い」 uses its native reading here. Its shape is simple, two strokes leaning into each other, and it means to enter or go in. You know it from 「入る|はいる」, to enter, as in 「お風呂に入る|おふろにはいる」, to take a bath, which is literally to enter the bath, and 「保険に入る|ほけんにはいる」, to buy insurance, which is to enter an insurance plan. Notice the same kanji is read 「はい」 there and 「い」 here. These two verbs are a classic pair. 「入る|はいる」 is something going in by itself. You enter a room. 「入れる|いれる」 is you putting something in. You put milk in coffee, or a book in your bag. The thing goes with 「を」 and the place with 「に」, as in 「かばんに本を入れる|かばんにほんをいれる」. A bonus use: 「お茶を入れる|おちゃをいれる」 means to make tea, because you're putting the tea into the pot and the hot water in. And when you get into a group or a club, someone lets you in, which is also this verb. Recap: 「入る|はいる」 you go in, 「入れる|いれる」 you put something in." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a ru-verb, so it's easy to conjugate. 「入れます|いれます」, 「入れて|いれて」, 「入れない|いれない」, 「入れた|いれた」. Its partner 「入る|はいる」 looks like a ru-verb too, but it's actually an u-verb, which is why its polite form is 「入ります|はいります」 and its te-form is 「入って|はいって」. Here's a sneaky trap. The same spelling can also be read 「入れる|はいれる」, and then it's the potential form of 「入る|はいる」, meaning can enter. So 「この部屋に入れます|このへやにはいれます」 means you can go into this room, while 「この部屋に入れます|このへやにいれます」 means I'll put something into this room. Same kanji, different reading, different meaning. Listen for the 「は」 sound. With the te-form you can make polite requests like 「砂糖を入れてください|さとうをいれてください」, please put sugar in. Wrap-up: a regular ru-verb, but watch out for its look-alike, 「はいれる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb goes far beyond milk in coffee. You turn something on with 「スイッチを入れる|スイッチをいれる」, putting the switch into the on position. At work, people say 「電話を入れる|でんわをいれる」, to give someone a quick call, as in I'll give the office a call. When someone tries hard, they 「力を入れる|ちからをいれる」, putting their strength into it. And brewing a hot drink is 「コーヒーをいれる」 or 「お茶をいれる|おちゃをいれる」. Here is a little dialogue at a café. 「砂糖を入れますか|さとうをいれますか」, do you take sugar? 「いいえ、けっこうです」, no, thank you. A common mistake for English speakers is to reach for 「入る|はいる」 when they mean put in. If you are the one doing the putting, and there's a 「を」 in the sentence, you want this verb. Wrap-up: switches, phone calls, effort and tea all get put in with this one verb." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「入|にゅう」 is only two strokes, and it is very easy to confuse with 「人|ひと」, person. Here's the difference. In the person kanji, the left stroke is the long one and the right stroke leans against it. In this kanji, it's the other way around, with the right stroke long and the left stroke tucked in at the top. This kanji has three readings you'll meet. 「い」, as in this verb, and 「いり」, as in 「入口|いりぐち」, the entrance, literally the entering mouth. Then 「はい」, as in 「入る|はいる」. And the Chinese-origin reading 「にゅう」, as in 「入学|にゅうがく」, entering a school, and 「入院|にゅういん」, being admitted to hospital. Its natural opposite is 「出|で」, to go out, and the two pairs mirror each other perfectly. 「入る|はいる」 and 「入れる|いれる」, 「出る|でる」 and 「出す|だす」. Wrap-up: watch which stroke is long, and remember the mirror pair with 「出|で」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「入る|はいる」 is something going in by itself, while 「入れる|いれる」 is you putting something in, with the thing taking 「を」 and the place 「に」.",
      "It's a regular ru verb, but the same spelling read 「はいれる」 is the potential form of 「入る|はいる」, can enter, so listen for the 「は」 sound.",
      "It covers far more than milk in coffee, like 「スイッチを入れる|スイッチをいれる」, to switch on, 「電話を入れる|でんわをいれる」, to give a call, and making tea.",
      "The kanji is easy to confuse with 「人|ひと」, but here the right stroke is the long one, and it mirrors 「出|で」 in the pairs 「入る|はいる」 and 「出る|でる」."
    ],
    ja: "コーヒーにミルクを[入れます|いれます|いれる]。",
    en: "I put milk in my coffee." },
  "みせる|見せる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Built on the kanji 「見|み」, to see, with its native reading, the same one in 「見る|みる」, to see, 「見える|みえる」, to be visible, and 「見つかる|みつかる」, to be found. This verb flips the seeing around. Instead of you seeing, you make someone else see. That's showing. You show your photos, your passport, your homework. The person you show it to takes 「に」, and the thing shown takes 「を」, as in 「友だちに写真を見せる|ともだちにしゃしんをみせる」. The most useful phrase by far is 「見せてください|みせてください」, please show me. Use it in a shop when you want to see an item behind the counter, as in 「それを見せてください|それをみせてください」, please show me that one. A common mistake is mixing it up with 「見る|みる」. If you say 「見てください|みてください」, you're asking someone to look at something. If you say 「見せてください|みせてください」, you're asking them to let you look. Here's a fun fact. The word for a shop, 「店|みせ」, has the same sound, and one common explanation links it to a place where goods are shown. Recap: 「見る|みる」 you look, 「見せる|みせる」 you make someone look, show." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a ru-verb, so its forms are regular. 「見せます|みせます」, 「見せて|みせて」, 「見せない|みせない」. Where it really shines is with the giving and receiving patterns. 「見せてくれる|みせてくれる」 means someone showed me, as a favor. 「友だちが写真を見せてくれました|ともだちがしゃしんをみせてくれました」, my friend showed me the photos. 「見せてあげる|みせてあげる」 means I show someone else, as a favor to them. And 「見せてもらう|みせてもらう」 means I got someone to show me. For a polite request to someone you don't know well, try 「見せていただけませんか|みせていただけませんか」, could you please show me? Notice how the person you show still takes 「に」 in all of these, as in 「妹に見せてあげました|いもうとにみせてあげました」. Wrap-up: one regular verb, and the favor patterns let you say exactly who showed what to whom." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Three verbs share the seeing kanji with its native reading, and they form a neat little set. 「見る|みる」 is you looking at something on purpose. 「見える|みえる」 is something being visible, coming into your eyes by itself, as in 「山が見える|やまがみえる」, the mountain is visible. And 「見せる|みせる」 is you making someone else see. The same kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「けん」. You'll meet it in 「意見|いけん」, an opinion, literally the way your mind sees something, and 「見学|けんがく」, a study visit, where you go and learn by looking. Then there's 「花見|はなみ」, cherry blossom viewing, back to the native reading. Here's one phrase worth knowing. 「顔を見せる|かおをみせる」, literally to show your face, means to drop by and visit someone. Wrap-up: look, be visible, show, all from one eye-filled kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll use this verb most in shops, offices and at the airport. At immigration, the officer says 「パスポートを見せてください|パスポートをみせてください」, please show your passport. At a shop, you point and say 「すみません、それを見せてもらえますか|すみません、それをみせてもらえますか」, excuse me, could you show me that one? Here's a mini dialogue. 「このかばん、ちょっと見せてください|このかばん、ちょっとみせてください」. 「はい、どうぞ」. Among friends it's casual and short. 「見せて見せて|みせてみせて」, let me see, let me see, said when a friend has new photos on their phone. A typical slip is to say 「見てください|みてください」 when you want to see something yourself. That asks the other person to look, not to let you look. Wrap-up: when you're the one who wants to see, ask them to show." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It flips seeing around so you make someone else see, so 「見せてください|みせてください」 asks to be shown, while 「見てください|みてください」 asks them to look.",
      "With favor patterns, 「見せてくれる|みせてくれる」 means someone showed me, 「見せてあげる|みせてあげる」 means I show someone, and the viewer always takes 「に」.",
      "The seeing kanji gives 「見る|みる」, look, 「見える|みえる」, be visible, and 「見せる|みせる」, show, and 「顔を見せる|かおをみせる」 means to drop by and visit.",
      "Immigration officers say 「パスポートを見せてください|パスポートをみせてください」, and friends with new photos hear an eager 「見せて見せて|みせてみせて」."
    ],
    ja: "{写真|しゃしん}を[見せて|みせて|みせる]ください。",
    en: "Please show me the photos." },
  "のりおくれる|乗り遅れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a compound verb, two verbs joined, both with native readings. The first is 「乗る|のる」, to ride or get on, which you know from riding trains and buses. Notice that 「乗る|のる」 marks the vehicle with 「に」, as in 「電車に乗る|でんしゃにのる」. That 「に」 carries over into this verb too. The second part is 「遅れる|おくれる」, to be late. Its kanji 「遅|おく」 has the moving-along-a-road part on its left and bottom, and you know it from 「遅い|おそい」, slow or late, 「遅く|おそく」, late, and 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, to be late for an appointment. Put them together: ride plus be late. Too late to ride. You missed it. So 「電車に乗り遅れました|でんしゃにのりおくれました」, I missed the train. Japanese trains are famous for leaving right on time, to the second, so if you're even a moment late, the doors close and you're left on the platform. The worst one to miss is the last train of the night, which might mean an expensive taxi or a night in a karaoke box. Recap: ride plus late, too late to ride, missing the train with 「に」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is made by taking the stem of 「乗る|のる」, which is 「乗り|のり」, and gluing another verb onto it. The whole thing then conjugates as a ru-verb, because the last part is 「遅れる|おくれる」. So 「乗り遅れます|のりおくれます」, 「乗り遅れて|のりおくれて」, 「乗り遅れた|のりおくれた」. You've seen this stem trick before in 「乗り換える|のりかえる」, to change trains, and the noun 「乗り物|のりもの」, a vehicle, literally a thing for riding. Here's a key difference to remember. 「電車が遅れる|でんしゃがおくれる」 means the train is late. That's the train's fault. 「電車に乗り遅れる|でんしゃにのりおくれる」 means you were too late for the train. That's your fault. The 「が」 versus 「に」 tells you who to blame. Wrap-up: a stem plus a verb, and the particle shows whose lateness it was." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Commuting in Japan comes with its own vocabulary. The first train of the day is 「始発|しはつ」, and the last one is 「終電|しゅうでん」. Station announcements often ask passengers not to make a dash for the closing doors, which is called 「駆け込み乗車|かけこみじょうしゃ」, a running jump onto the train. It's dangerous and holds everyone up. Interestingly, when a train itself is delayed, many stations hand out a little slip called a delay certificate, 「遅延証明書|ちえんしょうめいしょ」. Commuters show it at work or school to prove the lateness wasn't their fault. Notice the 「遅|ち」 in it, the same late kanji, with a Chinese-origin reading. But if you simply missed your train, there's no slip for that. You just apologize. Wrap-up: the railway will vouch for a late train, but never for a missed one." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. Picture yourself sprinting down the platform with a rice ball in your hand, wrapped in 「のり」, that crispy seaweed. As you run, the seaweed peels off and flaps behind you like a little black flag. The doors are closing. You shout, oh, curse it, I'm too late, which sounds a bit like 「おくれる」. The doors shut, the train pulls away exactly on time, and you're left on the platform holding a naked rice ball. Seaweed flapping, too late, train gone. To be clear, the seaweed word is a completely different word that just sounds like the ride part. But the picture of that flapping seaweed and the closing doors will stick. Wrap-up: flapping seaweed, closing doors, you missed the train." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「乗る|のる」, to ride, plus 「遅れる|おくれる」, to be late, means too late to ride, with the vehicle keeping 「に」, as in 「電車に乗り遅れました|でんしゃにのりおくれました」.",
      "「電車が遅れる|でんしゃがおくれる」 means the train is late, its fault, while 「電車に乗り遅れる|でんしゃにのりおくれる」 means you were too late, your fault.",
      "When a train is delayed you get a 「遅延証明書|ちえんしょうめいしょ」, a delay certificate, but if you simply missed it, there's no slip and you just apologize.",
      "Picture yourself sprinting with a rice ball, its 「のり」 seaweed flapping, shouting oh, curse it as the doors close and the train leaves exactly on time."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}、{電車|でんしゃ}に[乗り遅れました|のりおくれました|のりおくれる]。",
    en: "I missed the train this morning." },
  "アイロンをかける|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two parts. 「アイロン」 is a loanword from the English word iron, the tool for pressing clothes. It follows the English spelling more than the English pronunciation. In English we hardly say the r, but Japanese reads it letter by letter, with the r sound clearly in the middle, and adds the 「ン」 at the end. The second part is the very handy verb 「かける」. It's a verb for hanging or putting something onto something else, and you already know several of its uses. 「めがねをかける」 is to put on glasses, onto your face. 「電話をかける|でんわをかける」 is to make a phone call. And here, 「アイロンをかける」 is to run the iron over clothes. The clothes take 「に」, because you put the iron onto them, as in 「シャツにアイロンをかける」, to iron a shirt. A way to remember it: whenever something gets put onto something else, think 「かける」. Glasses onto your face, the iron onto your shirt. Recap: the iron, borrowed from English with a clear r, put onto the clothes with 「かける」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「かける」 is one of the hardest-working verbs in Japanese, and you'll keep finding new jobs for it. You can 「かぎをかける」, lock a door, putting the lock on. You can 「音楽をかける|おんがくをかける」, put on some music. You can 「時間をかける|じかんをかける」, spend time on something, and 「心配をかける|しんぱいをかける」, make someone worry, putting your worries onto them. Its intransitive partner is 「かかる」, and it mirrors a lot of these. 「かぎがかかっている」 means the door is locked. 「時間がかかる|じかんがかかる」 means something takes time, and 「電話がかかってくる|でんわがかかってくる」 means a call comes in. In each pair, 「かける」 is the ru-verb for the person doing it, and 「かかる」 is the u-verb for the thing it happens to. Wrap-up: lock, music, time and worry all get put on with 「かける」." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The word 「アイロン」 came into Japanese from English during the period when Western clothes and household tools were arriving. It was taken from the spelling, letter by letter, which is why the r sound is clearly there. Here's the fun part. Much later, the very same English word was borrowed a second time, for golf clubs. Golfers in Japan talk about 「アイアン」, an iron club, which is much closer to how English speakers actually say it. So one English word lives in Japanese twice, with two different sounds and two different meanings. And the metal itself isn't either of them. The metal iron is 「鉄|てつ」, a Chinese-origin word, as in 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, the subway, literally the underground iron. Wrap-up: the pressing iron and the golf iron, one English word borrowed twice." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Imagine two roommates on a Monday morning. One says 「このシャツ、しわしわだね」, this shirt is all wrinkled. 「しわ」 means a wrinkle, and doubling it paints a picture of a crumpled mess. The other replies 「アイロンをかけなきゃ」, I've got to iron it. That ending is a casual way of saying must. Office workers in Japan often wear white dress shirts, so ironing is a regular chore. Many people avoid it by buying shirts sold as wrinkle-resistant, or by taking their shirts to the dry cleaner, called 「クリーニング」 or 「クリーニング屋|クリーニングや」. You can iron trousers too, 「ズボンにアイロンをかける」. Remember the clothing takes 「に」 and the iron takes 「を」. Wrap-up: wrinkled shirt, iron onto it, and you're ready for Monday." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「アイロン」 is English iron read letter by letter with a clear r, and 「かける」 puts it onto your clothes, which take 「に」, just like glasses onto your face.",
      "「かける」 puts many things on, like 「かぎをかける」, to lock, and 「時間をかける|じかんをかける」, to spend time, while its partner 「かかる」 describes what happens.",
      "English iron was borrowed twice, as 「アイロン」 for pressing clothes and later 「アイアン」 for golf clubs, while the metal itself is 「鉄|てつ」.",
      "A wrinkled Monday shirt, 「しわしわ」, gets the casual must 「アイロンをかけなきゃ」, and remember the clothing takes 「に」 and the iron 「を」."
    ],
    ja: "シャツに[アイロンをかけました|アイロンをかけました|アイロンをかける]。",
    en: "I ironed my shirt." },
  "あさねぼうする|朝寝坊する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji with mixed readings, plus 「する」. 「朝|あさ」, morning, uses its native reading, as in 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast. 「寝|ね」, sleep, also uses its native reading, as in 「寝る|ねる」, to sleep. And 「坊|ぼう」 uses its Chinese-origin reading. That kanji originally meant a monk's lodging, and later came to be used for monks and then for little boys, as in 「赤ん坊|あかんぼう」, a baby. Added after another word, it can mean a person who's like that, a little affectionately. So 「寝坊|ねぼう」 is a sleepyhead, someone who sleeps late. Add 「朝|あさ」 and it's a morning sleepyhead. With 「する」, it becomes the verb, to oversleep. You'll often hear just 「寝坊する|ねぼうする」, and it means the same thing. It's the classic excuse for being late, so it pairs naturally with 「遅れる|おくれる」, as in 「寝坊して遅れました|ねぼうしておくれました」, I overslept and was late. A note on Japan: it's polite to apologize directly for this rather than make excuses. Recap: morning plus sleep plus little boy, a morning sleepyhead, to oversleep." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The sleep kanji has a whole family of useful words. 「寝る|ねる」 is to go to bed or sleep. 「昼寝|ひるね」 is a nap, a midday sleep. 「寝不足|ねぶそく」 is not getting enough sleep, with the kanji for not and enough. And there's a famous four-kanji phrase, 「早寝早起き|はやねはやおき」, early to bed and early to rise, which Japanese parents and teachers love to recommend. The morning kanji has a family too. 「毎朝|まいあさ」 is every morning. And watch out for 「今朝|けさ」, this morning, which has a special reading you just have to memorize. So 「今朝、寝坊しました|けさ、ねぼうしました」 means I overslept this morning. The opposite habit is to get up early, 「早起きする|はやおきする」. Wrap-up: sleep in, nap, sleep too little, or sleep early and rise early, the sleep kanji covers them all." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since this is a 「する」 verb, its forms follow 「する」. 「朝寝坊します|あさねぼうします」, 「朝寝坊して|あさねぼうして」, 「朝寝坊しない|あさねぼうしない」. The te-form is especially useful, because it can show a reason. 「寝坊して、電車に乗り遅れました|ねぼうして、でんしゃにのりおくれました」, I overslept and missed the train. And there's a polite apology pattern that fits perfectly. Add 「すみません」 straight after the te-form. 「寝坊してすみません|ねぼうしてすみません」, sorry for oversleeping. Among friends, the casual version is often a panicked 「やばい、寝坊した|やばい、ねぼうした」, oh no, I overslept! You can also use it as a plain noun. 「また寝坊|またねぼう」 means, overslept again? And someone who always oversleeps is just called 「寝坊|ねぼう」. Wrap-up: the te-form gives you the reason and the apology in one breath." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick using the sound. Picture an alarm clock that can talk. Every morning it shouts, ah, sa! ah, sa! Morning! Morning! Under the blanket is a sleepy little boy wearing a nightcap. He mumbles nay, nay, no, no, and gives the clock a tiny sleepy bow without getting up. Ah sa, nay, bow. The clock gives up, the sun climbs high, and the boy sleeps right through breakfast. That little boy fits the last kanji, which really is used for little boys and babies. Each time you hear the word, see that drowsy kid bowing from his pillow while the alarm shouts. Wrap-up: morning shouts, a sleepy no and a bow, and the boy oversleeps." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「朝|あさ」, morning, plus 「寝|ね」, sleep, plus 「坊|ぼう」, a little boy or person who's like that, makes a morning sleepyhead, so with 「する」 it means to oversleep.",
      "The sleep kanji gives 「昼寝|ひるね」, a nap, 「寝不足|ねぶそく」, lack of sleep, and 「早寝早起き|はやねはやおき」, early to bed and early to rise.",
      "The te form gives the reason and the apology in one breath, as in 「寝坊してすみません|ねぼうしてすみません」, sorry for oversleeping.",
      "Picture an alarm clock shouting ah, sa while a sleepy boy mumbles nay, nay and gives a tiny bow from his pillow, sleeping right through breakfast."
    ],
    ja: "きょうは[朝寝坊しました|あさねぼうしました|あさねぼうする]。",
    en: "I overslept today." },
  "あんないする|案内する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first, 「案|あん」, means a plan or an idea. You can see 「木|き」, tree, at the bottom, and it's more common in words about plans and proposals. The second, 「内|ない」, means inside or within. On its own, with its native reading, it's 「うち」, inside, home. So together it's like planning a route through the inside of a place. Leading someone around somewhere you know, as their guide. That covers giving someone a tour of your town or school, showing a guest to their seat, or guiding visitors through a building. The place takes 「を」 and the person you guide takes 「に」, as in 「友だちに町を案内しました|ともだちにまちをあんないしました」, I showed my friend around town. You'll also see the noun a lot on signs. An information desk at a station or department store is called 「案内所|あんないじょ」, and a store guide or directory board uses it too. When you visit Japan, look for it whenever you need help. Recap: plan plus inside, guiding someone through a place." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In shops, hotels and restaurants, staff use the humble form 「ご案内します|ごあんないします」, I will show you the way. When you walk into a restaurant, the waiter might ask how many people, then say 「お席にご案内します|おせきにごあんないします」, let me take you to your seat. At stations, announcements often start with words meaning we'd like to inform you, because this word also covers giving information, not just leading the way. Giving someone directions or leading them along a route is called 「道案内|みちあんない」. If a stranger kindly walks you to where you're going, that's this verb too. Among friends you can offer 「町を案内してあげるよ|まちをあんないしてあげるよ」, I'll show you around town. Wrap-up: staff say it humbly, friends say it warmly, and both mean let me guide you." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「内|ない」, means inside, and it builds a useful family. 「国内|こくない」 means within the country, domestic, so a domestic flight is a flight inside the country. 「以内|いない」 means within a limit, as in 「一時間以内|いちじかんいない」, within one hour. An older word, 「家内|かない」, literally inside the house, was used by a husband for his own wife, though many people now avoid it as old-fashioned. And with the native reading, it's 「うち」, inside or home. Compare it with 「中|なか」, which is the everyday word for inside a box or a room. This kanji feels a bit more formal and abstract, about being within a boundary. Wrap-up: inside the country, inside a time limit, inside the house, and here, guiding someone inside a place." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese hospitality, often called 「おもてなし」, shows up clearly in how people guide guests. At a traditional inn, a staff member will usually walk you all the way to your room, show you where the tea set and the bath are, and explain the dinner time. At a big company, a receptionist may take a visitor up in the elevator and right to the meeting room door rather than just pointing. When friends visit from abroad, many Japanese people take a full day off to show them around personally. If someone does this for you, thank them with 「案内してくださって、ありがとうございました|あんないしてくださって、ありがとうございました」. That's the polite version of thank you for showing me around. Wrap-up: in Japan, guiding a guest is a real act of care." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「案|あん」 means a plan and 「内|ない」 means inside, so it's like planning a route through a place to guide someone, with the place taking 「を」 and the person 「に」.",
      "Staff humbly say 「お席にご案内します|おせきにごあんないします」, let me show you to your seat, and giving directions is 「道案内|みちあんない」.",
      "「内|ない」 means within a boundary, as in 「国内|こくない」, domestic, and 「以内|いない」, within a limit, more formal than the everyday 「中|なか」.",
      "Japanese hospitality often means walking guests all the way to their room or meeting door, so thank your guide with 「案内してくださって、ありがとうございました|あんないしてくださって、ありがとうございました」."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}に{町|まち}を[案内しました|あんないしました|あんないする]。",
    en: "I showed my friend around town." },
  "せつめいする|説明する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first is 「説|せつ」, to explain or put forward an idea. On its left side is the speech or words part, the same one in 「話す|はなす」, to speak, 「言う|いう」, to say, and 「読む|よむ」, to read. So it's about using words. The second is 「明|めい」, bright or clear. Its shape is famous: 「日|ひ」, the sun, beside 「月|つき」, the moon. Two lights side by side make it bright. You know it from 「明日|あした」, tomorrow, the next bright day. So the whole word means to use words to make something clear. That's explaining. Things being explained take 「を」, and the person you explain to takes 「に」, as in 「先生が文法を説明しました|せんせいがぶんぽうをせつめいしました」. You'll also see it in 「説明書|せつめいしょ」, the instruction manual that comes with a product, with 「書|しょ」, writing, as in 「辞書|じしょ」. A handy request is 「説明してください|せつめいしてください」, please explain. Recap: words plus bright, making things clear with words, to explain." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji lead to words you'll keep meeting. The bright kanji, 「明|めい」, gives you 「明るい|あかるい」, bright or cheerful, with its native reading, and 「明日|あした」, tomorrow. The words kanji, 「説|せつ」, shows up in 「小説|しょうせつ」, a novel. That word literally means small talk in the old sense, minor stories as opposed to serious writings, and it was later chosen for the novel. You'll also meet 「伝説|でんせつ」, a legend, a story passed down. So the shared piece is speech, things told in words. Here's a near-synonym worth knowing. 「言い訳|いいわけ」 is an excuse. If you explain why something went wrong and it sounds like you're dodging blame, someone might say that's not an explanation, that's an excuse. Wrap-up: words that make things bright, and a family of told stories." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Explaining comes up everywhere, and Japanese has a few natural ways to ask for it. In class, a polite request is 「もう一度説明していただけませんか|もういちどせつめいしていただけませんか」, could you explain it once more? With a friend, 「ちょっと説明して|ちょっとせつめいして」 is enough. To say what the explanation is about, use 「について」, as in 「計画について説明します|けいかくについてせつめいします」, I'll explain the plan. You'll often hear people praise a teacher with 「説明がわかりやすい|せつめいがわかりやすい」, the explanations are easy to understand. And there's a well-known type of person who never reads 「説明書|せつめいしょ」, the instruction manual, and just starts pressing buttons. A common mistake is translating explain to me word for word and getting the particles tangled. Just keep the topic with 「を」 and the listener with 「に」. Wrap-up: ask for it politely, and praise it when it's clear." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「する」 verb, so it follows the irregular 「する」 pattern. 「説明します|せつめいします」, 「説明して|せつめいして」, 「説明しない|せつめいしない」. The noun on its own works too. 「説明を聞く|せつめいをきく」 is to listen to an explanation, and 「説明がある|せつめいがある」 means there will be an explanation. Combine it with the favor patterns and you can show gratitude. 「先生が説明してくれました|せんせいがせつめいしてくれました」, the teacher kindly explained it to me. 「友だちに説明してもらいました|ともだちにせつめいしてもらいました」, I had my friend explain it. And with the te-form plus 「みる」, you get 「説明してみてください|せつめいしてみてください」, try explaining it. Teachers love that one. Wrap-up: a regular 「する」 verb that teams up nicely with your favor patterns." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「説|せつ」 has the words part and 「明|めい」 is sun plus moon, bright, so explaining is using words to make something clear, as in 「説明書|せつめいしょ」, a manual.",
      "「明|めい」 gives 「明るい|あかるい」, bright, while 「説|せつ」 gives 「小説|しょうせつ」, a novel, and 「伝説|でんせつ」, a legend, all things told in words.",
      "Ask politely with 「もう一度説明していただけませんか|もういちどせつめいしていただけませんか」, keeping the topic with 「を」 and the listener with 「に」.",
      "It's a regular 「する」 verb that pairs well with favor patterns, as in 「先生が説明してくれました|せんせいがせつめいしてくれました」, the teacher kindly explained it."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}が{文法|ぶんぽう}を[説明しました|せつめいしました|せつめいする]。",
    en: "The teacher explained the grammar." },
  "むかえにくる|迎えに来る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「迎えに行く|むかえにいく」, to go to pick up. The first part, 「迎え|むかえ」, comes from 「迎える|むかえる」, to meet or welcome someone who's arriving. Its kanji uses the native reading and has the moving-along-a-road part on the left and bottom. Then comes the purpose pattern you know: 「に」 plus a motion verb, meaning coming in order to do something, like 「あそびに来る|あそびにくる」, to come to hang out. Here the motion verb is 「来る|くる」, to come. So someone comes to meet you and takes you back with them. The whole difference between the two phrases is the direction, the same as with 「連れていく|つれていく」 and 「連れてくる|つれてくる」. If you go out to get someone, use 「行く|いく」. If someone comes to where you are, use 「来る|くる」. So 「父が駅に迎えに来ました|ちちがえきにむかえにきました」 means my father came to the station to pick me up. A very natural request is 「迎えに来てください|むかえにきてください」, please come pick me up. In Japan, parents picking kids up from cram school late at night is a common sight. Recap: welcome plus come for that purpose, someone comes to get you." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The hard part here isn't the meeting, it's the coming. 「来る|くる」 is irregular, and it changes its vowel as it conjugates. 「来ます|きます」, 「来て|きて」, but 「来ない|こない」 and 「来られる|こられる」. So you get 「迎えに来ます|むかえにきます」, but 「迎えに来ない|むかえにこない」, doesn't come to pick me up. The te-form makes two very natural patterns. 「迎えに来てくれた|むかえにきてくれた」 means someone came to pick me up, as a kindness. 「迎えに来てもらった|むかえにきてもらった」 means I had someone come and get me. And a casual request on the phone is 「迎えに来て|むかえにきて」. Notice that the purpose 「に」 attaches to the stem 「迎え|むかえ」, and the destination can take its own 「に」 or 「まで」, as in 「駅まで迎えに来て|えきまでむかえにきて」. Wrap-up: watch the vowel change in 「来る|くる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The welcoming kanji shows up in some cheerful words. 「歓迎|かんげい」 means a warm welcome, using the Chinese-origin reading 「げい」. A welcome party for a new student or coworker is a 「歓迎会|かんげいかい」, and you'll see banners and signs saying welcome with this word. Then there's 「出迎える|でむかえる」, to go out and meet someone arriving, like greeting a guest at the door. And a lovely compound is 「送り迎え|おくりむかえ」, dropping off and picking up, all in one word. Parents spend a lot of time on the 「送り迎え|おくりむかえ」 of their kids. That pairs 「送る|おくる」, to see someone off, with this verb. Wrap-up: welcome parties, meeting guests at the door, and the daily drop-off and pick-up all share this kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a phone call. 「今、駅に着いたよ|いま、えきについたよ」, I just got to the station. 「迎えに来てくれる|むかえにきてくれる」, could you come pick me up? 「うん、十分で行くね|うん、じゅっぷんでいくね」, sure, I'll be there in ten minutes. Notice the parent says 「行く|いく」 from their side, because they're going. Outside big cities, where buses are few, this kind of call is a normal part of life. At a nursery school, parents simply call the pickup 「お迎え|おむかえ」. There's also an old expression where 「お迎えが来る|おむかえがくる」 means one's time has come, as if being collected for the next world. Older people sometimes say it jokingly about themselves. Wrap-up: a quick call, a kind ride, and a word every parent uses daily." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the partner of 「迎えに行く|むかえにいく」, and the only difference is direction, so use 「来る|くる」 when someone comes to where you are to pick you up.",
      "「来る|くる」 changes its vowel, so you get 「迎えに来ます|むかえにきます」 but 「迎えに来ない|むかえにこない」, and 「迎えに来てくれた|むかえにきてくれた」 thanks someone for coming.",
      "The kanji gives 「歓迎会|かんげいかい」, a welcome party, 「出迎える|でむかえる」, to meet someone at the door, and 「送り迎え|おくりむかえ」, drop-off and pickup.",
      "On the phone you ask 「迎えに来てくれる|むかえにきてくれる」, and the parent answers with 「行く|いく」 from their side because they are going."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}が{駅|えき}に[迎えに来ました|むかえにきました|むかえにくる]。",
    en: "My father came to the station to pick me up." },
  "きょうじゅうに|今日中に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three pieces. 「今日|きょう」, today, has a special reading that doesn't follow the usual sounds of its kanji, so learn it as a whole. Then 「中|じゅう」. This kanji means middle or inside, and its shape shows a line going right through the middle of a box. Here it takes the sound 「じゅう」, which after a time word means all through, the whole span. You know it from 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day long. Finally 「に」 marks a point in time. So within the whole of today, before it ends. By the end of today. It's a deadline phrase. You'll hear it at work and school, as in 「今日中に出してください|きょうじゅうにだしてください」, please hand it in by the end of today. You can swap in other time words too. 「今週中に|こんしゅうちゅうに」, usually said that way, means by the end of this week. Notice that one says 「ちゅう」, which brings up the tricky point. The same kanji is sometimes 「じゅう」 and sometimes 「ちゅう」, and the reading just has to be learned per word. Compare 「授業中に|じゅぎょうちゅうに」, during class. Recap: today plus all through, within today, a deadline." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This phrase sets a deadline, so it's worth comparing it with the time words around it. 「今日まで|きょうまで」 means until today, a span that keeps going right up to today, like a store being open until today. 「今日までに|きょうまでに」 means by today, a deadline. And 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」 means some time before today runs out. The little 「に」 is doing important work. It turns the whole span into a window in which something must happen. Without it, a word like 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」 describes something lasting all through the day, as in 「一日中寝ていました|いちにちじゅうねていました」, I slept all day. With a deadline, use a verb of finishing or handing in, like 「終わらせる|おわらせる」 or 「出す|だす」. Wrap-up: until is a span, by is a deadline, and this phrase is a deadline that ends at midnight." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The reading 「じゅう」 after a word of time or space means all through it. 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」 is all day long. 「一年中|いちねんじゅう」 is all year round, as in a place that's warm all year. It works with places too. 「世界中|せかいじゅう」 means all over the world, and 「日本中|にほんじゅう」 means all over Japan. So a famous singer might be known all over the world, 「世界中で有名|せかいじゅうでゆうめい」. With 「今週|こんしゅう」 and 「今月|こんげつ」, you'll hear both 「ちゅう」 and 「じゅう」, and people don't fully agree on which is better. When in doubt, follow what you hear around you. Wrap-up: after time or place, this little kanji means the whole stretch." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is the language of the office and the classroom. A boss might ask 「この書類、今日中にお願いできますか|このしょるい、きょうじゅうにおねがいできますか」, could I have this document by the end of today? The reply is a simple 「はい、わかりました」. In formal business emails, you'll see the stiffer version 「本日中に|ほんじつちゅうに」, where 「本日|ほんじつ」 is the formal word for today. Shops use it too, for same-day delivery. And here's a bonus about today's special reading. In 「こんにちは」, the old meaning is literally as for today, and it's written with the same two kanji, read 「こんにち」. So 「今日|きょう」 and 「今日|こんにち」 are the same kanji with different readings. Wrap-up: a polite little deadline you'll hear at every workplace." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「今日|きょう」, today, plus 「中|じゅう」, all through a span, plus 「に」 for a point in time means within today, a deadline, as in 「今日中に出してください|きょうじゅうにだしてください」.",
      "「今日まで|きょうまで」 is a span until today and 「今日までに|きょうまでに」 is by today, while 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」 is a window that closes at midnight.",
      "After a time or place word, 「じゅう」 means the whole stretch, as in 「一年中|いちねんじゅう」, all year, and 「世界中|せかいじゅう」, all over the world.",
      "Bosses ask for things 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」, and formal emails use 「本日中に|ほんじつちゅうに」, with 「本日|ほんじつ」 as the formal word for today."
    ],
    ja: "[今日中に|きょうじゅうに]{宿題|しゅくだい}を{出します|だします}。",
    en: "I'll hand in my homework by the end of today." },
  "じゅぎょうちゅうに|授業中に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three pieces. 「授業|じゅぎょう」, class, uses Chinese-origin readings. 「授|じゅ」 means to give or confer, and it has the hand part on its left. 「業|ぎょう」 means work, studies, or a course of learning, and you know it from 「卒業する|そつぎょうする」, to graduate, to finish your studies. So a class is giving out learning. Next is 「中|ちゅう」. This kanji means middle or inside, and its shape is a line going right through the middle of a box. After an activity, with the reading 「ちゅう」, it means in the middle of doing it, during. And 「に」 marks the time. So in the middle of class. You can use the same 「ちゅう」 after many activity words, like a meeting or a phone call. That's why a sign on a door might say that someone is in a meeting, or a phone call is in progress. Be careful with the reading. In 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」 and 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, the same kanji says 「じゅう」 and means all through a time period. Here it says 「ちゅう」, during an activity. A typical classroom rule is 「授業中に寝ないでください|じゅぎょうちゅうにねないでください」. Recap: class plus in the middle of, during class." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The reading 「ちゅう」 after an activity means in the middle of it, and Japan's signs are full of it. A door marked 「会議中|かいぎちゅう」 means a meeting is going on. 「電話中|でんわちゅう」 means someone is on the phone. A restaurant with a sign saying 「準備中|じゅんびちゅう」 is in the middle of preparing, so it's closed for now. When it opens, the sign flips to 「営業中|えいぎょうちゅう」, open for business. And road work is 「工事中|こうじちゅう」, construction in progress. 「食事中|しょくじちゅう」 means during a meal, as in 「食事中にすみません|しょくじちゅうにすみません」, sorry to bother you while you're eating. Once you know this pattern, you can read half the signs on a Japanese street. Wrap-up: put this kanji after an activity and it means in progress." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Notice what the 「に」 does. 「授業中に|じゅぎょうちゅうに」 marks the time when something happens, as in 「授業中に電話が鳴りました|じゅぎょうちゅうにでんわがなりました」, my phone rang during class. Without the 「に」, the word describes a state. 「今、授業中です|いま、じゅぎょうちゅうです」, I'm in class right now. That's handy for a quick text message. You can also say 「授業の間に|じゅぎょうのあいだに」, at some point during class, which feels a bit wordier. Compare the before and after patterns you know. 「授業の前に|じゅぎょうのまえに」 is before class, and 「授業の後で|じゅぎょうのあとで」 is after class. So you now have a full set for talking about the timing of anything around a class. Wrap-up: with 「に」 it's when something happens, without it, it's what's going on." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Classes in Japanese schools often begin with a small ceremony. A student on duty calls out 「起立|きりつ」, stand up, then 「礼|れい」, bow, and then 「着席|ちゃくせき」, sit down. Everyone bows to the teacher together, and the same thing happens at the end of class. After lessons, students clean their own classrooms, sweeping and wiping desks, a time called 「掃除|そうじ」. Using phones during class is usually not allowed in schools, and many schools collect them or ask students to keep them switched off. At university, things are looser, and a sleepy student nodding off in a big lecture hall is a familiar sight. Wrap-up: stand, bow, sit, and then the class is officially in progress." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「授業|じゅぎょう」, class, plus 「中|ちゅう」, in the middle of an activity, plus 「に」 means during class, unlike 「じゅう」, which means all through a time span.",
      "After an activity 「ちゅう」 means in progress, so signs say 「会議中|かいぎちゅう」, in a meeting, 「準備中|じゅんびちゅう」, preparing, and 「営業中|えいぎょうちゅう」, open.",
      "With 「に」 it marks when something happens, as in my phone rang during class, but without it, 「今、授業中です|いま、じゅぎょうちゅうです」 describes what's going on now.",
      "Japanese classes open and close with 「起立|きりつ」, 「礼|れい」 and 「着席|ちゃくせき」, stand, bow, sit, and phones during class are usually not allowed."
    ],
    ja: "[授業中に|じゅぎょうちゅうに]{寝ないで|ねないで}ください。",
    en: "Please don't sleep during class." },
  "このあいだ|この間": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces with native readings. 「この」 is this, the same as in 「この本|このほん」, this book. 「間|あいだ」 is the space between two things, a gap, or a span of time. Look at the shape of the kanji. It's 「門|もん」, a gate, with 「日|ひ」, the sun, inside. The traditional picture is sunlight shining through a gap in the gate. You know this kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「かん」 in 「時間|じかん」, time, and 「一時間|いちじかん」, one hour, and 「〜週間|〜しゅうかん」, for some weeks. So 「この間|このあいだ」 is this recent stretch of time. It points back to something that happened recently, a few days or a few weeks ago. In English, the other day, or recently. A common mistake is to use it for the future. It only points back. It's casual and very common in conversation, especially to bring up something you both remember, as in 「この間はありがとうございました|このあいだはありがとうございました」, thanks for the other day. In Japan, people say that a lot, thanking someone again for a meal or a favor the next time they meet. Recap: this plus gap in time, the other day, always in the past." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several ways to point back in time, and they're not quite the same. 「この前|このまえ」 is very close to this word. It means the other day, or the last time, as in 「この前の試験|このまえのしけん」, the last exam. 「先日|せんじつ」 is the polite, formal version, good for emails and business, as in thank you for the other day. 「最近|さいきん」 is different. It means lately, and it describes something going on over a recent period, not a single event, as in 「最近忙しい|さいきんいそがしい」, I've been busy lately. So if you met someone once a few days ago, use this word. If something has been going on for weeks, use 「最近|さいきん」. Wrap-up: the other day for a moment, lately for a stretch." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The word 「間|あいだ」 by itself is a real workhorse. You first met it for places, 「銀行と郵便局の間|ぎんこうとゆうびんきょくのあいだ」, between the bank and the post office. It also works for time. 「夏休みの間|なつやすみのあいだ」 means during the summer vacation, all through it. Add 「に」 and it becomes a point within that time. 「夏休みの間に|なつやすみのあいだに」 means at some point during the vacation. So 「この間|このあいだ」 is really this space of time, a gap close to now, and that's why it points to the recent past. Put 「の」 after it to describe a noun, as in 「この間の映画|このあいだのえいが」, that movie the other day. Wrap-up: between places, during a time, and here, the gap of time just behind us." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase is a great way to bring up shared memories. 「この間の話だけど|このあいだのはなしだけど」, about what we talked about the other day. It sets up the topic before you continue. Among friends, a quick thank you is just 「この間はどうも|このあいだはどうも」, thanks for the other day. Here is a short exchange. 「この間のパーティー、楽しかったね|このあいだのパーティー、たのしかったね」, the party the other day was fun, wasn't it? 「うん、また行きたい|うん、またいきたい」, yeah, I want to go again. Be careful when you're telling a story. It only works for things the listener can place in time, usually in the last few weeks. For something months ago, a word like 「前に|まえに」, before, sounds more natural. Wrap-up: a friendly way to reach back to something recent." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「この」 plus 「間|あいだ」, a gap or span, pictured as sunlight through a gate, means the recent stretch of time just behind us, the other day, never the future.",
      "「この前|このまえ」 is a close synonym and 「先日|せんじつ」 is the formal version, but 「最近|さいきん」 means lately, for something ongoing rather than one event.",
      "「間|あいだ」 means between places or during a time, so 「この間|このあいだ」 is the gap of time close to now, and with 「の」 it describes nouns, like that movie the other day.",
      "A quick 「この間はどうも|このあいだはどうも」 thanks a friend for the other day, but for months ago, 「前に|まえに」 sounds more natural."
    ],
    ja: "[この間|このあいだ]、{友だち|ともだち}に{会いました|あいました}。",
    en: "I met a friend the other day." },
  "これから|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces you know well. 「これ」 means this, the thing close to me, and here it points to this moment, right now. 「から」 means from, as in from a starting point. So from this point. From now on. It's part of a family you know. 「それから」, and then, is literally from that, moving on from what was just said. In fact, 「これから」 has two common uses. One is from now on, for changes going forward, like resolutions. 「これから毎日勉強します|これからまいにちべんきょうします」, from now on I'll study every day. The other is right after this, about to, as in 「これから出かけます|これからでかけます」, I'm about to go out. You'll hear it all the time on the phone or in messages, telling someone what you're doing next. It also shows up in a classic greeting when you start working with someone new, 「これからよろしくおねがいします」, which is like saying I look forward to working with you from now on. A way to remember it: this, then from, starting at this moment. Recap: 「これ」 plus 「から」, from this point forward." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word is part of a neat set built from the pointing words. 「これから」 looks forward from this moment. 「これまで」 looks back, until now, as in 「これまでありがとうございました」, thank you for everything up to now, something you might say when leaving a job. 「あれから」 means since then, from that point in the past, as in 「あれから十年|あれからじゅうねん」, ten years since then. And 「それから」, which you know as and then, moves on from what was just mentioned. A near-synonym is 「今から|いまから」, from now. It's mostly about the immediate moment, like starting right now. 「これから」 can mean that too, but it can also reach into the whole future, as in 「これからの日本|これからのにほん」, the Japan of the future. Wrap-up: this from, this until, that from, a little family of time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this word in many everyday moments. A friend phones and asks 「これからどうする」, so what should we do now? On a message app, someone types 「これから行くね|これからいくね」, heading over now. At the end of the year, or after you've worked with someone for a while, people say 「これからもよろしくお願いします|これからもよろしくおねがいします」. That extra 「も」 makes it from now on, too, meaning let's keep this good relationship going. It's a warm thing to say to a teacher or coworker. And at a ceremony or a big change in life, you might hear about 「これからの人生|これからのじんせい」, the rest of one's life ahead. Wrap-up: right now, from now on, and the whole road ahead, all in one little word." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「これ」, this moment, plus 「から」, from, means from now on for changes going forward, or right after this, as in 「これから出かけます|これからでかけます」.",
      "「これから」 looks forward, 「これまで」 means until now, and 「あれから」 means since then, while 「今から|いまから」 stays with the immediate moment.",
      "Adding 「も」 in 「これからもよろしくお願いします|これからもよろしくおねがいします」 means let's keep this good relationship going, a warm thing to say to a teacher or coworker."
    ],
    ja: "[これから]{毎日|まいにち}べんきょうします。",
    en: "From now on I'll study every day." },
  "このぐらい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces. 「この」 means this, near me. 「ぐらい」 means about or approximately, and you know it from 「〜ぐらい」 after amounts, as in 「一時間ぐらい|いちじかんぐらい」, about an hour. It can also be said as 「くらい」, without the extra sound mark, and both are fine. So the phrase means about this much, this amount, this size. It's really a phrase for gestures. You say it while holding your hands apart to show a size, or while pointing to an amount. It's part of the 「こそあど」 family you know, the this, that, that over there, which set. 「このぐらい」 is about this much, near me. 「そのぐらい」 is about that much, near you. 「あのぐらい」 is about that much, over there. And 「どのぐらい」 is the question, how much or how long, which you already know. So a typical exchange is 「どのぐらいですか」, about how big is it, and the answer, 「このぐらいです」, with your hands showing it. A way to remember it: point to your own hands and say this much. Recap: this plus about, said with a gesture, part of the question family with 「どのぐらい」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a hair salon in Japan. The stylist lifts a lock of your hair and asks how much to cut. You pinch your fingers a little apart and say 「このぐらい切ってください|このぐらいきってください」, please cut about this much. No numbers, no centimeters, just your fingers. That's the phrase doing its real job. Now picture a friend pouring you a drink. When the glass is full enough, you say 「このぐらいでいいです」, this much is fine. The 「で」 there means at this amount, and 「いいです」 means that's good. It's a polite way to say stop. There's one more use that surprises learners. The phrase can measure how serious something is, not just how big. Someone trips, apologizes, and you wave it off with 「このぐらい大丈夫です|このぐらいだいじょうぶです」, something this small is no problem. So it can shrink a worry down to size. Three scenes, then: the salon, the drink, and the little accident. In every one, your hands or your tone show the amount." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's see how this phrase plugs into a sentence. It behaves a lot like a noun of amount. Before another noun, it needs 「の」. So 「このぐらいの大きさ|このぐらいのおおきさ」, about this size, or 「このぐらいの箱|このぐらいのはこ」, a box about this big. Before a verb, it can sit right in front with nothing added, just like a number does. 「このぐらい食べます|このぐらいたべます」, I eat about this much. With 「です」 at the end, it becomes the answer to a size question. You'll also hear a twin, 「これぐらい」, built on 「これ」 instead of 「この」. It means the same thing and is just as common, so don't worry about choosing. The 「ぐ」 and 「く」 versions are interchangeable too, so 「これくらい」 is fine. A close relative is 「こんなに」, so much like this, which adds feeling, often surprise. 「こんなに大きい|こんなにおおきい」 is wow, this big. Keep 「このぐらい」 for calm measuring and 「こんなに」 for amazement. That's the whole toolkit for showing amounts with your hands." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「この」, this, plus 「ぐらい」, about, so about this much, usually said while your hands show the size; it belongs to the family with the question 「どのぐらい」.",
      "At a salon, pinch your fingers and say 「このぐらい切ってください|このぐらいきってください」, or stop a pour with 「このぐらいでいいです」; it can even shrink a worry, as in 「このぐらい大丈夫です|このぐらいだいじょうぶです」.",
      "Add 「の」 before a noun, as in 「このぐらいの箱|このぐらいのはこ」, but put it straight before a verb like a number; 「これぐらい」 is a twin, while 「こんなに」 adds surprise."
    ],
    ja: "[このぐらい]の{大きさ|おおきさ}です。",
    en: "It's about this big." },
  "じぶんで|自分で": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「で」. The first, 「自|じ」, means self. It started as a picture of a nose, and that connects to a lovely cultural habit. When Japanese people mean me, they often point to their own nose, not their chest. You know this kanji from 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, bicycle, the thing that turns its wheels by itself. The second, 「分|ぶん」, means a part or a share, and you know it from 「気分|きぶん」, mood, and 「多分|たぶん」, probably. So 「自分|じぶん」 is one's own part, oneself. Then 「で」 marks the means, how you do something, as in 「バスで行く|バスでいく」, to go by bus. So by oneself. Doing it on your own, without help. Compare a similar word, 「一人で|ひとりで」, alone. 「一人で|ひとりで」 is about being alone, with no one around. 「自分で|じぶんで」 is about doing it yourself, with no one helping. You could cook dinner 「自分で|じぶんで」 even with your family in the room. Recap: self plus part, oneself, plus 「で」, doing it by yourself without help." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closely at the second kanji, 「分|ぶん」, because it's one of the most useful in the language. Its top is two strokes spreading apart, the same shape as the number eight, 「八|はち」, which in old writing carried the idea of splitting. Underneath is a form of 「刀|かたな」, a knife or sword. A knife splitting something in two. That's the core idea, to divide. From dividing comes a share or part, which is the meaning in 「自分|じぶん」. The same kanji is read 「ふん」 or 「ぷん」 for minutes, as in 「五分|ごふん」, five minutes, a slice of an hour. And with its native reading it's the verb 「分かる|わかる」, to understand. Old thinking saw understanding as being able to divide things clearly, this from that. The first kanji, 「自|じ」, also has another reading you'll meet, 「し」, as in 「自然|しぜん」, nature. So two kanji, many readings, and here both are the sound-based Chinese ones. A knife makes a share, and your share is yourself." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you spend time around Japanese toddlers, you'll hear this word shouted. 「自分でやる|じぶんでやる」, I'll do it myself, as a small child grabs the spoon or the shoelaces. Parents say the other side of it, 「自分でやりなさい|じぶんでやりなさい」, do it yourself, when a child asks for help with something easy. Adults use it with pride too. 「自分で作りました|じぶんでつくりました」, I made it myself, about a cake or a shelf. One fun regional twist. In the Kansai area, around Osaka, 「自分|じぶん」 by itself is often used to mean you, talking to the other person. So someone from Osaka might ask 「自分、どこから来たの|じぶん、どこからきたの」, where are you from. And in sports clubs or the military, some men use it to mean I, in a stiff, respectful way. Standard Japanese uses it for oneself, though, and that's the sense you need. Just don't be confused when an Osaka friend points it at you. Wherever you go, 「自分で|じぶんで」 means with your own hands." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's how 「自分|じぶん」 changes with different particles, and each one gives you a new meaning. With 「で」, the means particle, it's by yourself, your own effort. 「自分で決めます|じぶんできめます」, I'll decide myself. With 「の」, it's your own, owned by you. 「自分の部屋|じぶんのへや」, one's own room. Notice that 「自分の|じぶんの」 can refer to whoever is the subject of the sentence. In 「田中さんは自分の車で来ました|たなかさんはじぶんのくるまできました」, Tanaka came in his own car, the own points back to Tanaka. With 「が」 or 「を」, it becomes the self as a topic. 「自分が好きです|じぶんがすきです」, I like myself. It also pairs well with the 「てみる」 pattern you know, try doing. 「自分で作ってみました|じぶんでつくってみました」, I tried making it myself. So keep three settings in mind: 「で」 for doing it yourself, 「の」 for your own, and the word alone for the self. Same word, three angles." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「自|じ」 is self, 「分|ぶん」 is a share, so 「自分|じぶん」 is oneself, and 「で」 marks the means: doing it yourself without help, unlike 「一人で|ひとりで」, which means being alone.",
      "「分|ぶん」 shows a knife splitting something, so it means divide, giving a share, minutes in 「五分|ごふん」, and understanding in 「分かる|わかる」; your share is yourself.",
      "Toddlers shout 「自分でやる|じぶんでやる」, I'll do it myself, and adults proudly say 「自分で作りました|じぶんでつくりました」; just know that in Osaka 「自分|じぶん」 alone often means you.",
      "The particle sets the angle: 「自分で|じぶんで」 is by yourself, 「自分の|じぶんの」 is your own and points back to the subject, and the bare word is the self, as in 「自分が好きです|じぶんがすきです」."
    ],
    ja: "[自分で|じぶんで]{料理|りょうり}をします。",
    en: "I cook by myself." },
  "ほかの|他の": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「他|ほか」 uses its native reading here. On its left is the person part, the same one you see in many kanji for people, and the whole kanji means other, as in other people, others. With its Chinese-origin reading 「た」 it appears in words like 「他人|たにん」, a stranger, literally an other person. The 「の」 links it to a noun, just like 「私の本|わたしのほん」, my book. So 「他の人|ほかのひと」, other people, 「他の店|ほかのみせ」, another shop. A common mistake is to drop the 「の」. You always need it before the noun. The word 「ほか」 also works without 「の」 in other patterns. 「ほかに」 means besides, else, as in a server asking if you'd like anything else, 「ほかに何かありますか|ほかになにかありますか」. You may also see 「ほか」 written with the kanji for outside, 「外|ほか」, the same kanji as in 「外国|がいこく」, so don't be thrown by that. A way to remember it: a person standing to one side, someone other than you. Recap: other, with the person part, always add 「の」 before a noun." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This little word is a shopper's best friend. You're in a clothing store and you like a sweater, but not the color. Ask 「ほかの色はありますか|ほかのいろはありますか」, do you have other colors. Or wrong size, 「ほかのサイズはありますか」. If you don't want to repeat the noun at all, say 「ほかのはありますか」, do you have other ones, with 「の」 standing in for the thing. At the end of a meeting, someone might ask 「ほかに質問はありますか|ほかにしつもんはありますか」, any other questions. And it's a gentle tool for turning down an invitation. If a friend suggests Friday and you can't make it, you can say 「じゃあ、ほかの日はどうですか|じゃあ、ほかのひはどうですか」, then how about another day. That keeps things friendly instead of just saying no. In a group, 「ほかの人|ほかのひと」 means the others, the rest of the people. So whenever you want options beyond what's in front of you, reach for this word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "A few words live close to this one, and the differences are worth knowing. First, 「別の|べつの」, which also translates as another. The difference is subtle. 「ほかの」 means others from the same group, the rest of them. 「別の|べつの」 means a separate, different one, something apart. If your coffee is cold and you want a fresh cup, 「別のコーヒー|べつのコーヒー」 fits, a separate one. If you're asking whether there are other flavors on the menu, 「ほかの」 is more natural. Next, 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」, one more. That adds to what you have, it doesn't replace it. Then there's 「他人|たにん」, other person, which uses the Chinese reading of the same kanji. It has a cool feel, someone unrelated to you, outside your circle. And finally 「そのほか」, besides that, the rest, used at the end of lists. Think of 「ほか」 as the rest of the set, 「別|べつ」 as something set apart, and 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」 as one more on the pile." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「他|ほか」 has the person part and means other, and you always need 「の」 before the noun, as in 「他の人|ほかのひと」; 「ほかに」 means besides or anything else.",
      "It's a shopper's tool, as in 「ほかの色はありますか|ほかのいろはありますか」, and a gentle way to decline, as in 「じゃあ、ほかの日はどうですか|じゃあ、ほかのひはどうですか」, how about another day.",
      "「ほかの」 is the rest of the same set, 「別の|べつの」 is a separate one set apart, and 「もう一つ|もうひとつ」 adds one more to the pile rather than replacing anything."
    ],
    ja: "[他の|ほかの]{人|ひと}はどこですか。",
    en: "Where are the other people?" },
  "ええと|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a filler word, the sound you make while you're thinking of what to say, like um, uh, or let me see. It has no kanji. You'll also see it written 「えーと」 or shortened to 「えと」, and stretched out in speech, 「ええーと」, as long as you need. It's casual but perfectly fine in polite conversation, for example when someone asks you a question and you need a second. A common mix-up: don't confuse it with 「ええ」 by itself, which means yes, a softer form of 「はい」. The 「と」 at the end is what makes it a thinking sound. Another filler you know, 「あのう」, is a little different. 「あのう」 is mostly used to get someone's attention politely, or before saying something a bit awkward. 「ええと」 is for searching your memory, like looking for a word or a number. Using Japanese fillers instead of English ums makes you sound much more natural, so it's worth practicing. A way to remember it: say um with a little hesitating eh at the start, and stretch it out while you look up at the ceiling. Recap: a thinking sound, not yes, stretch it as long as you need." },
      { title: "Thinking sounds", text: "Japanese has a whole toolbox of sounds for buying time, and each has its own flavor. 「ええと」 is searching for a fact, a name, a number, the right word. Someone asks your phone number and you say 「ええと」 while you remember it. A quicker, more casual version is 「えっと」, with a small 「っ」 that makes a little catch in the throat. Then there's 「そうですね」, which literally means that's right, but when someone asks your opinion it works as a polite let me think. 「好きな食べ物は|すきなたべものは」, what's your favorite food, 「そうですね、すしかな」, let me see, maybe sushi. That one sounds thoughtful and mature. 「うーん」 is a longer hmm, often a sign that you're unsure or about to say no. And when the word won't come, add 「なんだっけ」, what was it again. Put together, 「ええと、なんだっけ」 is the classic sound of a name on the tip of your tongue. Pick the sound that matches the kind of thinking you're doing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's a little scene at a train station ticket window. The clerk asks where you're going. You say 「ええと、京都まで|ええと、きょうとまで」, um, to Kyoto. The clerk asks how many. 「ええと、二枚お願いします|ええと、にまいおねがいします」, um, two please. Nobody blinks, because this is exactly how Japanese people talk when they're checking a detail. Now a few practical tips. In casual and everyday polite talk, it's completely natural, even helpful, because it tells the listener you heard them and you're working on an answer. Silence can feel more awkward than a filler. But in formal speeches or job interviews, people try not to say it too often, just as English speakers try to cut down their ums. And here's a funny thing. Japanese listeners hear an English um or uh as very foreign. Swapping in 「ええと」 is one of the quickest ways to make your Japanese sound like Japanese, even before your grammar is perfect. So practice it on purpose." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's a thinking sound like um, stretched as long as you need while you search your memory; don't confuse it with 「ええ」 alone, a soft yes, or with 「あのう」, which gets attention.",
      "「ええと」 searches for a fact, 「えっと」 is the quicker casual one, 「そうですね」 is a thoughtful let me think, and 「ええと、なんだっけ」 is a name on the tip of your tongue.",
      "At a ticket window, 「ええと、京都まで|ええと、きょうとまで」 sounds completely natural, and swapping English ums for this is one of the fastest ways to sound Japanese, though people limit it in formal speeches."
    ],
    ja: "[ええと]、ちょっとわかりません。",
    en: "Let me see, I'm not quite sure." },
  "じつは|実は": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「実|じつ」 uses its Chinese-origin reading. Its core meaning is fruit, and from there, substance, the real content of something, truth. With its native reading, 「み」, it means a fruit or nut on a tree. So think of the fruit as the real thing inside, the true part. Then 「は」, the topic particle, the same as in 「私は|わたしは」. So literally, as for the truth. That's what it means: actually, to tell the truth, in fact. It's the phrase you use right before you reveal something. A confession, a surprise, a request, or bad news. It softens what comes next and signals that you're about to say something real. It often pairs with the 「んです」 pattern, which explains a situation, as in 「実は、あした行けないんです|じつは、あしたいけないんです」, actually, I can't go tomorrow. Japanese speakers often say it before asking a favor, to set up the situation politely. If you hear it, get ready, something important is coming. Recap: fruit, the real content, truth, plus 「は」. As for the truth, actually." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「実|じつ」 has a roof on top, the same roof part you see on 「家|いえ」, house. In its older, fuller form, the part under the roof was a picture of strings of coins, so the traditional explanation is a house filled with wealth, full and solid. That gave meanings like full, substantial, real. Fruit fits this too, because fruit is the full, solid result of a plant. You'll meet this kanji in several useful words. 「実家|じっか」 is your parents' home, the real home you grew up in. Notice the small 「っ」 there, the sound gets clipped. 「実際|じっさい」 means in reality, actually, another clipped one. 「事実|じじつ」 is a fact, a true matter. And with its native reading, 「実|み」 is fruit or a nut, and 「実る|みのる」 means to bear fruit, to ripen. So the same character covers fruit on a tree and facts in a conversation. In 「実は|じつは」, the real stuff is about to come out." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Two words both translate as actually, and they're easy to mix up. 「実は|じつは」 introduces something new the listener doesn't know yet. A secret, a request, news. 「実は、結婚します|じつは、けっこんします」, actually, I'm getting married. The other word is 「本当は|ほんとうは」, the truth is, which corrects something. It points to a gap between how things seem and how they really are. 「本当は行きたくなかった|ほんとうはいきたくなかった」, the truth is, I didn't want to go, even though I went. So 「実は|じつは」 opens a door, and 「本当は|ほんとうは」 corrects the record. Here's a mini dialogue. A coworker comes over. 「実は、ちょっとお願いがあるんですが|じつは、ちょっとおねがいがあるんですが」, actually, I have a small favor to ask. And the other replies 「はい、何ですか|はい、なんですか」, sure, what is it. Among friends, it softens to 「実はね|じつはね」, with that friendly 「ね」. When you hear it, lean in. A reveal is coming." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick built on sound. The first part sounds a bit like the English word jitters, those nervous shakes. Picture a young man in a café, knees bouncing under the table, coffee cup rattling on its saucer. He has a big confession to make, and he's got the jitters. He takes a deep breath, leans forward, and says, well, actually. That's the moment. The jitters right before the truth comes out. Then imagine the kanji on top of his head like a little roof, keeping the real truth stored inside until he's ready. And on that roof, a single ripe piece of fruit, ready to drop. The fruit falls, the truth comes out, and he blurts his secret. Silly, but it ties three things together: the nervous sound, the fruit in the kanji, and the moment of revealing something real. Next time you hear this word, think of the jittery man and the falling fruit, and get ready for news." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「実|じつ」 means fruit, the real content, truth, plus the topic marker 「は」, so as for the truth; it signals a confession, request or news, often with 「んです」.",
      "「実|じつ」 is a roof over strings of coins, a full, solid house, giving real and fruit; it's in 「実家|じっか」, your parents' home, and 「事実|じじつ」, a fact.",
      "「実は|じつは」 opens a door to news the listener doesn't know, while 「本当は|ほんとうは」 corrects the record about how things only seemed; among friends it softens to 「実はね|じつはね」.",
      "Picture a jittery man in a café with a ripe fruit on the little roof over his head; the fruit drops and, well, actually, the truth comes out."
    ],
    ja: "[実は|じつは]、あした{行けません|いけません}。",
    en: "Actually, I can't go tomorrow." },
  "〜いがい|〜以外": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. 「以|い」 marks a point of reference, a starting line that you measure from. It shows up in useful words you'll meet later, like 「以上|いじょう」, that point and above, and 「以下|いか」, that point and below. The second kanji, 「外|がい」, means outside. You know it from 「外国|がいこく」, foreign country, the country outside, and 「外国人|がいこくじん」, foreigner. With its native reading, it's 「そと」, outside. So 「以外|いがい」 is everything outside of that point. Put it right after a noun to mean other than that, except that. 「肉以外|にくいがい」, anything other than meat. 「日曜日以外|にちようびいがい」, any day except Sunday. It's often followed by a word for all or anything, as in 「肉以外は何でも食べます|にくいがいはなんでもたべます」, I eat anything other than meat. Or by a negative, meaning only that, as in no one other than me knows. Watch out for a word with the same sound, 「意外|いがい」, which means unexpected. Recap: reference point plus outside, everything outside of that, other than." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word attaches straight onto a noun, with no 「の」 in between. You say 「肉以外|にくいがい」, never meat plus 「の」 plus this word. After it, different particles give different results. With 「は」, it sets up everything else as the topic. 「月曜日以外は暇です|げつようびいがいはひまです」, other than Monday, I'm free. With 「の」, it describes a noun. 「日本以外の国|にほんいがいのくに」, countries other than Japan. With 「に」, it means besides, in addition. 「英語以外に、日本語も話せます|えいごいがいに、にほんごもはなせます」, besides English, I can also speak Japanese. And with a negative it means only. 「私以外、だれも来ませんでした|わたしいがい、だれもきませんでした」, nobody came except me, so only I came. That last pattern is powerful because English says only, but Japanese says nobody except. So remember the four combos: 「は」 for the rest, 「の」 for describing, 「に」 for besides, and a negative for only." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「以|い」, is a little builder that sets a reference point. Attach it to another kanji and you get a whole family of measuring words. 「以上|いじょう」 is that point and above, with 「上|うえ」, up. You'll see it on signs like twenty years old and over. It also ends speeches and announcements, meaning that's all. 「以下|いか」 is that point and below, with 「下|した」, down. 「以内|いない」 is within, with 「内|うち」, inside, as in 「三日以内|みっかいない」, within three days. 「以前|いぜん」 is before, earlier, with 「前|まえ」. And 「以外|いがい」, the one in your lesson, is everything outside the point. Here's a detail that matters in real life. 「以上|いじょう」 and 「以下|いか」 include the point itself. Twenty and over includes twenty. That's different from English more than. Once you know the pattern, each new word in the family takes seconds to learn. Reference point, plus a direction." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Two traps hide in this word family. The first is 「意外|いがい」, unexpected, with exactly the same sound as 「以外|いがい」, other than. Its first kanji, 「意|い」, means mind or thought, the same as in 「意味|いみ」, meaning. So it's outside what you had in mind, unexpected. You'll hear it as 「意外とおいしい|いがいとおいしい」, surprisingly tasty. How to tell them apart? The one meaning other than always sits right after a noun. The one meaning unexpected is usually followed by 「と」 or 「に」, or 「な」 before a noun, and comes with a tone of surprise. The second trap is 「以内|いない」, within, which sounds exactly like 「いない」, the negative of 「いる」, not here. 「十分以内|じゅっぷんいない」, within ten minutes, versus 「先生がいない|せんせいがいない」, the teacher isn't here. Again, position saves you. The within word follows a number or amount. Listen to what comes just before, and the meaning clears up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「以|い」 marks a reference point and 「外|がい」 means outside, so it's everything outside that point, other than or except, as in 「肉以外は何でも食べます|にくいがいはなんでもたべます」.",
      "It attaches straight to a noun with no 「の」, then 「は」 gives the rest, 「の」 describes, 「に」 means besides, and a negative means only, as in nobody except me came.",
      "「以|い」 plus a direction builds a family: 「以上|いじょう」 and above, 「以下|いか」 and below, 「以内|いない」 within, 「以前|いぜん」 before; and above and below include the point itself.",
      "Same-sound traps are 「意外|いがい」, unexpected, usually followed by 「と」 or 「に」, and 「以内|いない」 versus not here; position saves you, since other than always follows a noun."
    ],
    ja: "にく[以外|いがい]はなんでも{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat anything other than meat." },
  "ごめん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a casual apology, used with friends and family. It's written in kana nowadays, but it comes from a kanji phrase meaning your honorable pardon. The 「ご」 is the polite prefix, like in 「ご飯|ごはん」, and 「めん」 comes from a kanji meaning pardon or exemption. So it was originally a way of asking forgiveness or permission. That's why you'll also hear the old visiting phrase 「ごめんください」, said at someone's door when you arrive, like asking pardon for intruding. Now, the apology family by level. 「ごめん」 is the casual one, for people close to you. 「ごめんなさい」 is a bit softer and fuller, still for friends and family, and also what kids say. 「すみません」 is the polite, all-purpose one for strangers, teachers, and coworkers. A common mistake is using 「ごめん」 with a teacher or a boss. It sounds too casual. Young people often stretch it, like 「ごめんね」, which feels gentler, or 「ごめんごめん」, a quick, light sorry for something small. Recap: from asking pardon, the casual sorry for close friends and family." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Today it's almost always written in kana, but the old kanji spelling is 「御免|ごめん」, and both pieces lead somewhere useful. The first kanji, 「御|ご」, is the honorable prefix itself. You usually see that prefix as kana, as in 「ごはん」, but in formal writing it's often this kanji. It can also be read 「お」, so the same character covers both polite prefixes you know. The second kanji, 「免|めん」, means to excuse, release, or permit. And here's the fun link. You'll meet it in 「運転免許|うんてんめんきょ」, a driver's license. A license is official permission, being released from the rule that you can't drive. So when you say sorry with this word, you're really asking for the other person's honorable permission or release, as if asking them to let you off. Knowing that, the visiting phrase 「ごめんください」 makes even more sense. You're asking permission to come in. A driver's license and a casual sorry share one kanji, the kanji of being let off." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how this word actually lives in conversation. When a friend says 「ごめん」, the natural replies are 「いいよ」, it's fine, or 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, no problem. You don't need anything fancy. The word also works as sorry, but, right before turning someone down. A friend asks you out for dinner and you say 「ごめん、今日はちょっと|ごめん、きょうはちょっと」, sorry, today's a bit, and you just trail off. That trailing off is itself the no, and it's very Japanese. There are small flavor differences in the endings, too. 「ごめんね」 is soft and warm. 「ごめんな」, with 「な」, sounds masculine and a little rough, the kind of thing an older brother might say. And 「ごめんごめん」, doubled, is a quick light sorry when you're a few minutes late or bump someone's arm. In texts between friends, it's often just the bare word. Remember the boundary: friends, family, younger people. Step up to 「すみません」 the moment you're talking to a teacher, a stranger, or a boss." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick that uses the sound. Picture a football coach on the sidelines, red-faced and fired up, yelling to his team, go, men! The whole team charges forward at once, straight off the field, and crashes into the snack table. Cups fly, a cake hits the ground, and a little old lady gets splashed with lemonade. The coach runs over, bows awkwardly, and says sorry, sorry, so sorry. So go men turns into sorry in an instant. That's your hook. Yell go, men, and an apology follows. The scene is loud and casual and a bit clumsy, which matches the word perfectly, because this is the casual, everyday apology, not the formal one. The coach is apologizing to a neighbor in a relaxed setting, not to the company president. If he'd knocked over the president's cake, he'd need a much more formal word. Go men, crash, sorry. That's the memory trick, and it should stick." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From the polite 「ご」 plus a kanji for pardon, it's the casual sorry for friends and family; use 「すみません」 with teachers, strangers and bosses.",
      "The old spelling 「御免|ごめん」 uses 「免|めん」, release or permit, the same kanji as a driver's license, 「運転免許|うんてんめんきょ」; you're asking to be let off.",
      "Friends reply 「いいよ」 or 「大丈夫|だいじょうぶ」, and 「ごめん、今日はちょっと|ごめん、きょうはちょっと」 trailing off is itself a no; 「ごめんね」 is warm, 「ごめんごめん」 quick and light.",
      "Picture a coach yelling go, men, his team crashing into the snack table, and him apologizing sorry, sorry; go men turns into the casual everyday apology."
    ],
    ja: "[ごめん]、ちょっとおくれる。",
    en: "Sorry, I'll be a little late." },
  "しつれいします|失礼します": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「します」. 「失|しつ」 means to lose or to fail at something. 「礼|れい」 means manners, courtesy, a bow. You'll meet it in 「お礼|おれい」, thanks, a gesture of gratitude. So 「失礼|しつれい」 is literally losing your manners, being rude. Add 「します」 and you're saying I'm about to do something rude. That sounds strange in English, but it's a humble way of excusing yourself. You're admitting that walking in or leaving bothers the other person a little. You say it when knocking and entering a teacher's office, when hanging up a phone call, or when leaving a room. When leaving, many people use the past form, 「失礼しました|しつれいしました」, I was rude, as in sorry for the interruption. In an office, people leaving work before their coworkers say 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」, excuse me for leaving ahead of you. And 「失礼ですが|しつれいですが」, it may be rude but, comes before a personal question, like asking someone's name. Recap: lose plus manners, a humble excuse me for entering, leaving or interrupting." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase is the backbone of a Japanese job interview. Here's the classic routine taught to students. You knock on the door, usually three times. When you hear 「どうぞ」, come in, you open the door and say 「失礼します|しつれいします」 before stepping inside. You walk to the chair but don't sit yet. You wait until the interviewer says 「どうぞ、おかけください」, please have a seat. At the end, you stand, bow, and at the door you turn and say 「失礼しました|しつれいしました」 before closing it quietly. That's two uses of one phrase, framing the whole meeting. On the phone it's just as important. Business calls end with 「失礼します|しつれいします」 or the more humble 「失礼いたします|しつれいいたします」, and you wait for the other person to hang up first if they're a client. The 「いたします」 is the humble version of 「します」, so it bows a little lower. Basically, any time you cross into someone's space or time, this phrase is your polite knock." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this phrase has relatives you'll meet. Start with 「礼|れい」, manners or a bow. 「お礼|おれい」 is thanks, a gift or words of gratitude. In Japanese schools, class starts with a student calling out 「起立|きりつ」, stand up, 「礼|れい」, bow, and then 「着席|ちゃくせき」, sit down. That middle word is this exact kanji on its own, meaning bow. Now 「失|しつ」, to lose. It appears in 「失敗|しっぱい」, a failure or mistake, where the sound gets clipped with a small 「っ」. With its native reading it's the verb 「失う|うしなう」, to lose something important. And 「失礼|しつれい」 itself works without 「します」. As a 「な」 adjective it means rude. 「失礼な人|しつれいなひと」 is a rude person. So the same word can be a humble excuse me or a sharp accusation, depending on how it's used. Saying it about yourself is polite. Saying it about someone else is a criticism. Lost manners, then, can be a polite bow or a complaint." },
      { title: "Close cousins", text: "Visiting a Japanese home uses a different phrase from visiting an office, and learners mix them up. When you step into someone's house, the set phrase is 「おじゃまします」. It comes from 「邪魔|じゃま」, a nuisance, a bother. So you're saying I'm going to be a bother. When you leave, it's 「おじゃましました」, I was a bother. Compare that with 「失礼します|しつれいします」, which belongs to offices, classrooms, and teachers' rooms, places with a work or school feel. So at a friend's family home, say 「おじゃまします」 at the door. At your professor's office, say 「失礼します|しつれいします」. Both are humble, and both admit you're disturbing someone a little. The difference is the setting. There's one more cousin. 「すみません」 can also mean excuse me, but it's more for getting attention, like calling a waiter, or squeezing past someone on a train. Three phrases, three settings: home, office, and getting someone's attention." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「失|しつ」 is lose and 「礼|れい」 is manners, so you're humbly saying I'm about to be rude when entering, leaving or hanging up; use 「失礼しました|しつれいしました」 when leaving.",
      "In a job interview you say 「失礼します|しつれいします」 entering and 「失礼しました|しつれいしました」 at the door leaving, and business calls end with it or the humbler 「失礼いたします|しつれいいたします」.",
      "「礼|れい」 alone is the bow called out in class, and 「失|しつ」 is in 「失敗|しっぱい」; 「失礼|しつれい」 as an adjective means rude, so about yourself it's polite but about others it's a criticism.",
      "Entering someone's home you say 「おじゃまします」, I'll be a bother, while 「失礼します|しつれいします」 belongs to offices and classrooms, and 「すみません」 is for getting attention."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}、[失礼します|しつれいします]。",
    en: "Excuse me, professor." },
  "あかちゃん|赤ちゃん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji here is 「赤|あか」, red, read with its native reading. It's the same red you find in the color word 「赤い|あかい」. Then comes 「ちゃん」, the cute, affectionate ending you hear on children's names and pets. So the word is literally something like little red one. Why red? Dictionaries explain it simply. A newborn's skin looks pink and reddish, so in old Japanese a baby was called 「赤子|あかご」, a red child. That older word still exists in books and set phrases, but in everyday speech people say 「赤ちゃん|あかちゃん」, which sounds warmer. One usage note. Because of the friendly 「ちゃん」 ending, this is the word you use for any baby, your own or a stranger's, and it never sounds rude. You'll often hear it with the verb 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born, which is also in this lesson. 「赤ちゃんが生まれました|あかちゃんがうまれました」 means a baby was born, and it's the kind of news people share with a big smile. To recap: 「赤|あか」, red, for a newborn's rosy skin, plus the cute 「ちゃん」, gives baby." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「赤|あか」 shows up in many everyday words. 「赤い|あかい」 is the adjective, red. 「赤信号|あかしんごう」 is a red traffic light. 「真っ赤|まっか」 means bright red, bright all the way through, with a small 「っ」 for emphasis, as in a face that turns bright red with embarrassment. The kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「せき」, which you'll see in 「赤十字|せきじゅうじ」, the Red Cross. Now for baby words. Besides 「赤ちゃん|あかちゃん」, there's 「赤ん坊|あかんぼう」, an older and plainer word for baby, which you'll hear from grandparents or in stories. It's not rude, just less sweet. When a baby grows into a toddler and a child, the word becomes 「子供|こども」. And remember, the cute ending is fixed. You can't swap in a different name ending. It's always this exact form. So red covers traffic lights, blushing faces, the Red Cross, and the newest people on earth." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a set of lovely customs for a new baby's first months. Many women go back to their own parents' home to give birth and rest afterward. That's called 「里帰り出産|さとがえりしゅっさん」, giving birth back home. About a month after birth, families often take the baby to a Shinto shrine, 「神社|じんじゃ」, for a first visit, called 「お宮参り|おみやまいり」, to pray for healthy growth. Around a hundred days, there's 「お食い初め|おくいぞめ」, the first meal ceremony. The baby can't actually eat yet, so the family just holds food up to the baby's lips, a wish that the child will never go hungry. Japanese also has its own baby talk. A dog is 「ワンワン」, after the barking sound, and a car is 「ブーブー」, after the engine. So if a parent points and says 「ワンワンだよ」, they're saying look, a doggy. These customs show how warmly a new arrival is welcomed. A month for the shrine, a hundred days for the first meal." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the phrases that come up again and again around babies. When a friend tells you their baby was born, say 「おめでとうございます」, congratulations. To ask about the baby, people often ask 「男の子ですか、女の子ですか|おとこのこですか、おんなのこですか」, is it a boy or a girl. To hold a baby in your arms, the everyday word is 「抱っこする|だっこする」, a soft, family word. A parent might ask, 「抱っこしますか|だっこしますか」, want to hold her. A crying baby is 「赤ちゃんが泣いています|あかちゃんがないています」, using 「泣く|なく」, to cry. And a sleeping baby is 「赤ちゃんが寝ています|あかちゃんがねています」. When you meet the baby, the natural thing is to tell the parent 「かわいいですね」, how cute. Parents often answer modestly, something like 「いえいえ」, oh no, not really, even while they beam with pride. That modest reply is normal, so don't take it as a disagreement. So, congratulations, boy or girl, hold, cry, sleep, cute. With those, you're ready for any baby visit." }
    ],
    short: [
      "A newborn's skin looks reddish, so it's 「赤|あか」, red, plus the cute 「ちゃん」, little red one; the older word was 「赤子|あかご」, red child.",
      "「赤|あか」 gives 「赤い|あかい」, 「赤信号|あかしんごう」 and 「真っ赤|まっか」, and reads 「せき」 in the Red Cross; 「赤ん坊|あかんぼう」 is a plainer, older word for baby.",
      "Families take a baby to a shrine at about a month for 「お宮参り|おみやまいり」, and around a hundred days hold food to its lips for 「お食い初め|おくいぞめ」.",
      "Around babies you'll need 「おめでとうございます」, 「抱っこする|だっこする」 to hold, and 「かわいいですね」; parents often answer 「いえいえ」 modestly, which isn't disagreement."
    ],
    ja: "[赤ちゃん|あかちゃん]が{泣いて|ないて}います。",
    en: "The baby is crying." },
  "おきゃくさん|お客さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The core of this word is one kanji, 「客|きゃく」, read with its Chinese-origin reading. It means guest or visitor. Look at the top of the kanji and you'll see the roof part, the same lid shape that sits on top of 「家|いえ」, house, and 「寮|りょう」, dormitory. A way to remember it, and this is a hook rather than history: a guest is someone who has come under your roof. Japanese then wraps 「客|きゃく」 in politeness on both sides. 「お」 in front makes it respectful, and 「さん」 at the end treats it like a person's name. That double politeness tells you something about Japanese culture. Guests and customers are treated with great care. The word stretches wider than English guest. It covers visitors to your home, customers in a shop, passengers, and clients of a business. In shops and restaurants staff go one step further and say 「お客様|おきゃくさま」, with the extra-polite 「様|さま」. That's who they're greeting when they call out 「いらっしゃいませ」 as you walk in. Recap: 「客|きゃく」, the person under your roof, plus polite 「お」 and 「さん」, gives guest or customer." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「客|きゃく」 is the core of a whole set of words about people who come and visit or use a service. 「お客様|おきゃくさま」, the extra-polite form, is what staff call customers. 「乗客|じょうきゃく」 is a passenger, with 「乗|じょう」 from 「乗る|のる」, to ride. So a riding guest. On trains you'll hear announcements addressed to passengers. 「観光客|かんこうきゃく」 is a tourist, a sightseeing guest, with 「観光|かんこう」, sightseeing. Famous spots like Kyoto are often crowded with them. In older Japanese homes there might be a 「客間|きゃくま」, a guest room, a formal room kept tidy for visitors. And 「客|きゃく」 alone also exists, used inside businesses, like when staff talk about how many customers came today. Notice that in all these words the kanji keeps its Chinese-origin sound. Once you can hear it, you'll start noticing it in train stations, shops and tourist spots. Riding guests, sightseeing guests, guests in the guest room, they're all the same idea of someone welcomed in." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Who says which form, and when? At home, a family uses 「お客さん|おきゃくさん」 for visitors. A mother might tell her kids 「今日はお客さんが来ます|きょうはおきゃくさんがきます」, we have guests coming today, which means clean your room. Shop staff talking to you directly say 「お客様|おきゃくさま」, never the 「さん」 version, because they need the highest level of respect. But a shop owner chatting with a friend about business might say 「今日はお客さんが少ない|きょうはおきゃくさんがすくない」, not many customers today. There's also a famous phrase, 「お客様は神様です|おきゃくさまはかみさまです」, the customer is a god. It came from a popular singer talking about his audience, but it spread as a business slogan. Today people sometimes use it critically, about customers who act demanding and expect staff to obey. So the 「さん」 form is the friendly everyday one, and the 「様|さま」 form is staff talking to you." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese hospitality has a special name, 「おもてなし」, wholehearted service to a guest, anticipating needs before they're spoken. You'll feel it in an inn where your futon appears while you're at dinner, or in a shop where your purchase is wrapped with great care. One part of this care is where the guest sits. In a room, the seat of honor is 「上座|かみざ」, usually the seat farthest from the door. The host or the lowest-ranking person takes 「下座|しもざ」, near the door. The idea is that the guest is safest and most comfortable away from the coming and going. This even applies in taxis and elevators in business settings. When you visit someone's home, you might be offered the best cushion and served tea first. The polite move is to wait to be shown where to sit. Knowing about 「上座|かみざ」 helps you understand why your host keeps gently steering you to a particular seat. As the guest, let yourself be steered." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「客|きゃく」 has a roof on top, a guest is someone under your roof, wrapped in polite 「お」 and 「さん」; it covers visitors, customers, passengers and clients.",
      "「客|きゃく」 builds 「乗客|じょうきゃく」, a riding guest or passenger, 「観光客|かんこうきゃく」, a sightseeing guest or tourist, and 「客間|きゃくま」, a guest room.",
      "Families and shop owners chatting use 「お客さん|おきゃくさん」, but staff speaking to you always say 「お客様|おきゃくさま」, as in the slogan 「お客様は神様です|おきゃくさまはかみさまです」.",
      "Japanese hospitality, 「おもてなし」, seats the guest at 「上座|かみざ」, the seat farthest from the door, so as the guest, let your host steer you."
    ],
    ja: "{店|みせ}に[お客さん|おきゃくさん]がたくさんいます。",
    en: "There are lots of customers in the shop." },
  "しゅしょう|首相": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「首|しゅ」. With its native reading, 「くび」, it means neck, and by extension head. From head it grows into the idea of chief or leader, the one at the top. The second kanji is 「相|しょう」. In this word it means a minister, a high official who helps run the state. You've met the same kanji with a different reading in 「相談する|そうだんする」, to consult, where it carries the idea of each other, two people talking things through. So 「首相|しゅしょう」 is the head minister, the chief among ministers. That's exactly what a prime minister is. In English prime means first, so the two languages built the idea the same way. A useful contrast. Back in lesson eleven you learned 「大統領|だいとうりょう」, president. Japan has no president. It has a 「首相|しゅしょう」, chosen by parliament, and the emperor is a separate symbolic role. So when you talk about Japan's leader on the news, this is the word. Recap: 「首|しゅ」, head or chief, plus 「相|しょう」, minister, gives the head minister, the prime minister." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「首|しゅ」 has a family that spans the human body and the map. With its native reading, 「首|くび」 is the neck. Add 「手|て」 in front and you get 「手首|てくび」, the wrist, literally the hand's neck. Add 「足|あし」 and you get 「足首|あしくび」, the ankle, the foot's neck. Notice how the 「く」 sound stays and the meaning is the narrow joint that connects. With its Chinese-origin reading, 「首|しゅ」 means head or chief, as in 「首相|しゅしょう」. And then 「首都|しゅと」, the capital city, the head city of a country. 「都|と」 is the same kanji as in 「東京都|とうきょうと」, the Tokyo metropolis. So 「日本の首都は東京です|にほんのしゅとはとうきょうです」, the capital of Japan is Tokyo. The kanji's shape is usually explained as a drawing of a head, with hair on top. Neck, wrist, ankle, capital city, prime minister. One kanji connects your body to the top of government." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "On the news, you'll hear more than one title for Japan's leader. 「首相|しゅしょう」 is the short, common one, used in headlines and newscasts. The full, official title is 「内閣総理大臣|ないかくそうりだいじん」, the prime minister of the cabinet. People often shorten that to 「総理大臣|そうりだいじん」 or just 「総理|そうり」. In parliament and in person, people address the leader as 「総理|そうり」. To refer to a specific leader, put the title after the family name, like a job title, so the name followed by 「首相|しゅしょう」. It works like 「先生|せんせい」 after a name. The same pattern works for foreign leaders. The British prime minister is also called 「首相|しゅしょう」. Other ministers are 「大臣|だいじん」. The foreign minister, for example, is 「外務大臣|がいむだいじん」. So the newscast might say the prime minister met with the foreign minister, and both words now make sense. Headlines say 「首相|しゅしょう」, and people in the room say 「総理|そうり」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「相|しょう」, is made of two parts you know. On the left is 「木|き」, tree. On the right is 「目|め」, eye. The traditional explanation is an eye looking closely at a tree, looking carefully, examining. From looking at each other comes the idea of mutual, and from someone who examines and advises comes minister, an official who helps the ruler. That's why this kanji fits a prime minister. It has three readings you'll meet. 「そう」, in 「相談|そうだん」, consultation, two people looking at a problem together. 「しょう」, here in 「首相|しゅしょう」, the minister sense. And the native reading 「あい」, in 「相手|あいて」, the other person, a partner or opponent, like the person you're playing tennis against. So tree plus eye gives looking, then each other, then advisor. One small shape, three meanings, all tied to looking carefully. When you see it, picture an eye studying a tree." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「首|しゅ」 is neck or head, so chief, and 「相|しょう」 is minister, giving the head minister; Japan has no president, its leader is chosen by parliament.",
      "Read 「くび」, 「首|くび」 is the neck, giving 「手首|てくび」, wrist, and 「足首|あしくび」, ankle; read 「しゅ」, it's head or chief, as in 「首都|しゅと」, the capital city.",
      "Headlines say 「首相|しゅしょう」, the official title is 「内閣総理大臣|ないかくそうりだいじん」, and people in the room say 「総理|そうり」; other ministers are 「大臣|だいじん」.",
      "「相|しょう」 is a tree plus an eye, looking carefully, which grows into each other in 「相談|そうだん」, partner in 「相手|あいて」, and the advising minister here."
    ],
    ja: "テレビで[首相|しゅしょう]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw the prime minister on TV." },
  "サラリーマン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This looks like an English loanword, but it's actually Japanese-made English. Japan took two English words, salary and man, and glued them together. English speakers don't say salary man, but in Japan it's an everyday word. Listen to how the sounds were squeezed into Japanese. Salary becomes 「サラリー」. The l turns into the Japanese r sound, and the final y becomes a long vowel. Man becomes 「マン」, with the vowel changed to the Japanese a. So someone who earns a salary, a man on a monthly paycheck. In practice it means a white-collar office worker at a company, the classic picture of a man in a dark suit riding a packed train in the morning and working late. It fits nicely with two other words in this lesson, 「給料|きゅうりょう」, salary, and 「残業|ざんぎょう」, overtime. You already know 「会社員|かいしゃいん」, company employee, from earlier. That's the neutral, official word you'd write on a form. 「サラリーマン」 is the casual, everyday one, and it carries a whole cultural image with it. Recap: salary plus man, made in Japan, means an office worker." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word belongs to a big category called 「和製英語|わせいえいご」, Japanese-made English. These are words built from English parts that English speakers don't actually use. This word was already in use in Japan early in the twentieth century, as office jobs in cities grew. There are plenty of cousins. 「ガソリンスタンド」 is a gas station, built from gasoline and stand. 「ナイター」 is a night baseball game, from night with an English-sounding ending. 「ペーパードライバー」, paper driver, is someone who has a license but never actually drives, so they're a driver only on paper. These words can trip up English speakers, because they look familiar but aren't. Try saying paper driver to an American and you'll get a blank stare. It also works the other way. When Japanese speakers use these in English, people get confused. So treat this category as Japanese words that happen to be made of English pieces. They feel familiar, but they belong to Japanese." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The classic image of this office worker comes with a whole kit of cultural details. There's the 「名刺|めいし」, the business card, exchanged with a bow and both hands at every first meeting. There's the 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」, the packed rush-hour train, where commuters are squeezed so tightly they can't move. There's the 「飲み会|のみかい」, the after-work drinking party with the boss and coworkers, long seen as part of the job. And for decades there was 「終身雇用|しゅうしんこよう」, lifetime employment, joining one company after school and staying until 「定年|ていねん」, retirement age. This system is changing. More people switch jobs, work from home, or skip the drinking parties. But the image is still everywhere in manga, TV dramas, and comedy. A tired man in a suit, loosening his tie on a late train, is instantly recognized. Business cards, packed trains, drinking parties, one company for life. That's the picture this word paints." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "How do people actually use this word? When describing a family member's job casually, it's common. 「父はサラリーマンです|ちちはサラリーマンです」, my dad's an office worker. When talking about your own job, though, many people say 「会社員です|かいしゃいんです」 or simply 「会社で働いています|かいしゃではたらいています」, I work at a company, which sounds more neutral. Sometimes people say it about themselves with a sigh, a bit self-mocking, like I'm just a regular office worker. There's also a great word built on it, 「脱サラ|だつサラ」. The first kanji, 「脱|だつ」, is the same one as in 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」, to take off clothes. So it's taking off the office-worker life, quitting a company job to open a café or start your own business. It's a popular dream. Note that the word fits men best. It's rarely used for women, who'd more often be called 「会社員|かいしゃいん」. So it's a casual word, a cultural image, and the starting point for that dream of escape." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's Japanese-made English, salary plus man, meaning a white-collar office worker; 「会社員|かいしゃいん」 is the neutral word for forms, while this one carries a whole cultural image.",
      "It belongs to 「和製英語|わせいえいご」, words built from English parts that English speakers don't use, like 「ガソリンスタンド」 and 「ペーパードライバー」, so treat them as Japanese.",
      "The classic image comes with business cards, 「名刺|めいし」, packed trains, 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」, drinking parties, 「飲み会|のみかい」, and lifetime employment, though that system is changing.",
      "People use it casually for a dad's job but say 「会社員です|かいしゃいんです」 about themselves, and 「脱サラ|だつサラ」 means taking off that life to start your own business."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は[サラリーマン]です。",
    en: "My father is an office worker." },
  "きゅうりょう|給料": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「給|きゅう」. It means to supply or to give. On its left side you can see the thread part, 「糸|いと」, which shows up in lots of kanji, but the main idea to hold onto is giving, handing something out. The second kanji is 「料|りょう」. It means a fee, a charge, or materials. You already know it from 「料理|りょうり」, cooking, where it's about ingredients and materials. Its shape is real and helpful. On the left is 「米|こめ」, rice, and on the right is a measuring dipper. Measuring out grain is the original idea, and it grew into measured amounts, like fees, charges, and materials. Put the two together and 「給料|きゅうりょう」 is the payment that's given out to you, your wages or salary. The day you get it is 「給料日|きゅうりょうび」, payday, with 「日|び」 for day. In Japan many companies pay once a month, often around the twenty-fifth. It pairs naturally with 「サラリーマン」 and 「残業|ざんぎょう」 in this lesson. Recap: 「給|きゅう」, give, plus 「料|りょう」, the measured payment, gives salary." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each half of this word has a big family, and once you see them, a lot of money words open up. Start with the second kanji, the one for a fee or charge. 「料金|りょうきん」 is a fee or fare, the price you pay for a service, like a train fare or a phone bill. 「授業料|じゅぎょうりょう」 is tuition, the fee for your classes. And 「無料|むりょう」 means free of charge, literally no fee. You'll see that one on signs everywhere. Now the first kanji, the one for giving out. 「月給|げっきゅう」 is a monthly salary. 「時給|じきゅう」 is an hourly wage, the word you'll see on every part-time job ad, so an 「アルバイト」 might pay a certain number of yen per hour as its 「時給|じきゅう」. And 「給食|きゅうしょく」 is school lunch, the meal that is given out to children at school. So one kanji is about handing something out, and the other is about a measured charge. Put them back together and you have your pay." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how people actually talk about pay. The simplest verb is 「もらう」, to receive, so you get your salary with 「給料をもらう|きゅうりょうをもらう」. To describe it, use 「高い|たかい」 and 「安い|やすい」, the same words as for prices. 「給料が安い|きゅうりょうがやすい」 means the pay is low, and it is one of the most common office complaints in Japan. English speakers sometimes say small or little pay, but Japanese prefers cheap and expensive. A favorite phrase is 「給料日前|きゅうりょうびまえ」, the days just before payday, when your wallet is nearly empty and you eat instant noodles. A tiny dialogue between coworkers might go like this. 「今日、飲みに行かない|きょう、のみにいかない」, want to go for a drink today? And the answer, 「ごめん、給料日前だから|ごめん、きゅうりょうびまえだから」, sorry, it's right before payday. So remember, salaries are high or cheap, and you receive them with 「もらう」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Pay in a Japanese company has a rhythm of its own. Salary usually arrives once a month, straight into a bank account. On top of that, many full-time employees get a 「ボーナス」, a bonus, twice a year, once in summer and once in winter. For many families the bonus is a big part of yearly income, and stores run special sales around bonus season to catch that money. There is also a special word for your very first paycheck in a career, 「初任給|しょにんきゅう」, the starting salary. A lovely custom goes with it. Many young people use part of their first real pay to buy a present for their parents or take them out to dinner, as a thank you for raising them. Newspapers even report the average starting salary every spring, when new graduates join companies. So salary in Japan is monthly, plus two bonuses, and the first one is often shared with your parents." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「給|きゅう」 means to give or supply, and 「料|りょう」 is rice plus a measuring dipper, a measured payment, so it's the pay given out to you; payday is 「給料日|きゅうりょうび」.",
      "「料|りょう」 gives 「料金|りょうきん」, a fee, and 「無料|むりょう」, free, while 「給|きゅう」 gives 「時給|じきゅう」, an hourly wage, and 「給食|きゅうしょく」, school lunch.",
      "You receive it with 「給料をもらう|きゅうりょうをもらう」 and describe it as high or cheap, as in 「給料が安い|きゅうりょうがやすい」; 「給料日前|きゅうりょうびまえ」 is the broke days before payday.",
      "Pay comes monthly plus a 「ボーナス」 in summer and winter, and many young people spend part of their first paycheck, 「初任給|しょにんきゅう」, on a thank-you for their parents."
    ],
    ja: "{毎月|まいつき}[給料|きゅうりょう]をもらいます。",
    en: "I get a salary every month." },
  "ざんぎょう|残業": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji you've already met in other words, both read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「残|ざん」, to remain or be left over. You know it from 「残念|ざんねん」, too bad, which is literally a lingering thought, a feeling that stays with you. The second is 「業|ぎょう」, work or business. You know it from 「授業|じゅぎょう」, a class, the work of giving lessons. So 「残業|ざんぎょう」 is leftover work, the work that remains after normal hours are over. That's overtime. Simple and logical. Culturally this word carries a lot of weight in Japan. Long overtime has been a famous part of office life for decades, the image of the 「サラリーマン」 still at his desk late at night, then catching the last train home. Recent laws and campaigns try to reduce it, but you'll still hear people sigh 「今日も残業です|きょうもざんぎょうです」, overtime again today. Use it with 「する」 to make a verb, 「残業する|ざんぎょうする」, to work overtime, or say 「残業があります|ざんぎょうがあります」, there's overtime. Recap: 「残|ざん」, remaining, plus 「業|ぎょう」, work, gives the work left over after hours, overtime." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closer at the first kanji, the one for remaining. On the left is a part that looks like a little skeleton or bare bone. It shows up in kanji connected with death or decay, and it is the same piece you see on the left of 「死ぬ|しぬ」, to die. On the right is the sound part. When this kanji stands alone as a verb, it uses its native reading. 「残る|のこる」 means to remain or be left, and 「残す|のこす」 means to leave something behind, like leaving food on your plate. In compounds it switches to the Chinese-origin sound you hear in this word. The second kanji, work or business, is a busy one too. Besides 「授業|じゅぎょう」, you know it from 「卒業|そつぎょう」, graduation, literally finishing your studies. So when you see the bony left side, think of what is left behind, and this word is the work left behind at the end of the day." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Overtime comes with a cluster of words worth knowing. First, the money. 「残業代|ざんぎょうだい」 is overtime pay. It uses the same 「〜代|〜だい」 you know for charges, like a bill for something. Next, the opposite of overtime. 「定時|ていじ」 means regular hours, the official quitting time. Leaving on the dot is 「定時に帰る|ていじにかえる」, going home at regular time, and some people feel a little guilty doing it even when their work is done. Then a darker one. 「サービス残業|サービスざんぎょう」 is unpaid overtime, work done as a free service to the company. It is a well-known social problem and a word you'll hear in the news. And at the extreme end is 「過労死|かろうし」, death from overwork, a word so tied to Japan that it has been borrowed into English. Finally, a polite phrase to escape the office, 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」, excuse me for leaving before you. So the family runs from overtime pay to leaving on time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Notice which particles and verbs go with this word. Overtime is something that exists, so you often hear 「残業がある|ざんぎょうがある」, there is overtime, or the happy version, 「今日は残業がない|きょうはざんぎょうがない」, no overtime today. When someone else makes you do it, the verb changes. A boss might say 「残業してくれる|ざんぎょうしてくれる」, could you do overtime for me? You can also measure it. 「二時間残業しました|にじかんざんぎょうしました」 means I did two hours of overtime. Here is a small dialogue between partners at home. One texts, 「今日、遅くなる|きょう、おそくなる」, I'll be late today. The other replies, 「また残業|またざんぎょう」, overtime again? And the answer comes back with a sigh, 「うん、ごめん」, yeah, sorry. A common mistake is translating overtime work with the verb for work. You don't need 「働く|はたらく」 here, because this word already contains the work. So just say it exists, or add 「する」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「残|ざん」, remaining, as in 「残念|ざんねん」, plus 「業|ぎょう」, work, as in 「授業|じゅぎょう」, gives the work left over after hours, overtime; make it a verb with 「する」.",
      "「残|ざん」 has a bony left side, as in 「死ぬ|しぬ」, so think of what's left behind; alone it's 「残る|のこる」, to remain, or 「残す|のこす」, to leave behind.",
      "The family runs from 「残業代|ざんぎょうだい」, overtime pay, and 「定時|ていじ」, regular quitting time, to 「サービス残業|サービスざんぎょう」, unpaid overtime, and 「過労死|かろうし」, death from overwork.",
      "Overtime exists, so say 「残業がある|ざんぎょうがある」 or add 「する」, as in 「二時間残業しました|にじかんざんぎょうしました」; you don't need 「働く|はたらく」 because the word already contains the work."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[残業|ざんぎょう]があります。",
    en: "I have overtime work today." },
  "パンダ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword that came into Japanese through English, so it's written in katakana. The English word panda fits Japanese sounds almost perfectly. Each syllable maps straight across: 「パ」, 「ン」, 「ダ」. Nothing needed squeezing, which makes it an easy one to remember. There's a fun bit of history behind why pandas are so loved in Japan. In nineteen seventy-two, when Japan and China restored diplomatic relations, China sent a pair of giant pandas to Ueno Zoo in Tokyo. Huge crowds lined up for hours just to see them for a few seconds, and Japan has been crazy about pandas ever since. Ueno is still full of panda souvenirs, panda bread, and panda everything. Whenever a baby panda is born at a Japanese zoo, it makes the national news. That links this word to two others you know. 「動物園|どうぶつえん」, zoo, from lesson ten, and 「赤ちゃん|あかちゃん」, baby, from this lesson. 「パンダの赤ちゃん|パンダのあかちゃん」, a baby panda, is a phrase you'll see in headlines. Recap: panda, borrowed straight from English, and a national favorite since the Ueno pandas arrived." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "This one comes with a built-in memory trick, so let's make a scene of it. Look at the first two sounds, 「パン」. That is the Japanese word for bread, which you already know. So picture this. A giant panda sits at Ueno Zoo, but instead of chewing bamboo, it is hugging a huge fluffy loaf of 「パン」. Then it finishes with a satisfied sigh, da. Bread, then da. 「パン」 plus 「ダ」. This is only a memory trick. The word has nothing to do with bread. But funnily enough, real Japanese bakeries do sell panda-shaped bread, with black and white dough, so the picture is easy to imagine. Every time you see a panda, think of it hugging its bread. 「パン」 becomes 「パンダ」 with one extra beat." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "There are actually two animals called panda, and Japanese names both clearly. The big black and white one is the 「ジャイアントパンダ」, giant panda. The smaller reddish-brown one with a striped tail, which looks a bit like a raccoon, is the 「レッサーパンダ」, the lesser panda, which English often calls the red panda. Here's an interesting fact. The name panda was first given to the small red one in the West, and the big famous one borrowed the name later. Where the word panda itself came from is not certain. It's often traced to a language of the Himalayan region, but that is debated. In daily Japanese, plain 「パンダ」 means the giant one. When you count pandas, you might use 「〜ひき」, the counter you know for animals. But in news reports about zoo pandas you'll often hear 「頭|とう」, the counter for large animals, as in 「二頭のパンダ|にとうのパンダ」, two pandas. So two pandas, giant and lesser, and a counter for big animals." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Pandas show up in Japanese life far beyond the zoo, and the word has a few fun uses. The black patches around a panda's eyes inspired a common joke. If someone stays up all night and gets dark circles under their eyes, friends might laugh and say they have panda eyes, 「パンダみたい」, like a panda. Japanese police cars are painted black and white, and people sometimes jokingly call them pandas. Panda-themed sweets, bread, and goods are sold in Ueno all year round. And when a zoo panda has a baby, the whole country waits for its name to be announced. A small dialogue at the zoo might go like this. 「パンダはどこですか」, where are the pandas? 「あそこです。でも、すごく混んでいますよ|あそこです。でも、すごくこんでいますよ」, over there, but it's really crowded. So listen for this word in jokes, on the road, and in zoo news, not just the animal itself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Borrowed from English, the syllables map straight across, and Japan has loved pandas since China sent a pair to Ueno Zoo in nineteen seventy-two.",
      "Picture a giant panda at Ueno hugging a loaf of 「パン」, bread, then sighing da, so 「パン」 plus 「ダ」; it's only a memory trick.",
      "Plain 「パンダ」 means the giant one, 「ジャイアントパンダ」, while the small reddish one is 「レッサーパンダ」; news reports count them with 「頭|とう」, the counter for large animals.",
      "Beyond the zoo, someone with dark circles is 「パンダみたい」, like a panda, and black and white police cars sometimes get jokingly called pandas."
    ],
    ja: "{動物園|どうぶつえん}で[パンダ]を{見ました|みました}。",
    en: "I saw a panda at the zoo." },
  "コンタクト|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English contact, as in contact lens. Japanese took the full phrase, contact lens, which becomes 「コンタクトレンズ」, and then did what it loves to do with long loanwords. It chopped it short. So in daily speech people just say 「コンタクト」. Notice how the sounds were adapted. English contact ends in two consonants, the c and the t, but Japanese syllables need vowels. So the k sound becomes 「ク」 and the t becomes 「ト」, giving four beats in all. You already know 「めがね」, glasses, from lesson seven. 「コンタクト」 is its partner. Now the verbs, which trip up English speakers. To say you wear contacts, use 「コンタクトをしています」, I'm doing contacts, which is the most natural. To put them in, use 「いれる」, the same verb as putting something inside. To take them out, people say 「はずす」 or 「とる」. Be careful. In English contact also means getting in touch with someone, and Japanese does use 「コンタクト」 that way sometimes, but in everyday talk it almost always means the lenses. Recap: contact lens, cut down to 「コンタクト」, the partner of 「めがね」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The most useful thing about this word is the verb 「する」 for wearing it. Japanese has a whole system of wearing verbs. 「きる」 for shirts, 「はく」 for shoes and pants, 「かぶる」 for hats, and 「かける」 for glasses. But for accessories and small things you put on, Japanese often just uses 「する」. You say 「ネクタイをする」 for a tie, 「指輪をする|ゆびわをする」 for a ring, 「マフラーをする」 for a scarf, and 「コンタクトをする」 for contacts. To describe what someone is wearing right now, use the 「〜ている」 form, which shows a continuing state. 「コンタクトをしています」 means I have contacts in, or I wear them. Compare 「めがねをかけています」, I'm wearing glasses. So if your friend has stopped wearing glasses, you could ask 「今日はコンタクトですか|きょうはコンタクトですか」, contacts today? So remember, most wearables have their own verb, but small add-ons like contacts take 「する」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Contact lenses are very common in Japan, and a few phrases come up all the time. Most people use disposable lenses, called 「使い捨てコンタクト|つかいすてコンタクト」, throwaway contacts, from 「使う|つかう」, use, and 「捨てる|すてる」, throw away. You'll also hear soft and hard lenses, 「ソフトコンタクト」 and 「ハードコンタクト」. Shops that sell them are everywhere near big stations, and you usually need an eye check first. A very common real-life moment is losing one. 「コンタクトを落としました|コンタクトをおとしました」, I dropped my contact. Then everyone gets down on the floor to search, 「探す|さがす」. Another is dry eyes after a long day in front of the computer, when people switch back to glasses at night. So in Japan the word usually means disposable soft lenses, and the classic drama is dropping one on the floor." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Let's count the beats carefully, because loanwords are where English speakers often rush. Japanese counts rhythm in beats, each one about the same length. This word has five of them: 「コ」, 「ン」, 「タ」, 「ク」, 「ト」. The 「ン」 gets a full beat of its own, even though it's just a hum. And the last two beats, 「ク」 and 「ト」, should be very light. Japanese speakers often whisper those u vowels between hard sounds, so it can sound almost like con-ta-k-to. Try clapping five even claps as you say it. Now compare the full phrase, 「コンタクトレンズ」, which has eight beats. That's a lot, which is exactly why people chop it down. The same chopping happened to 「エアコン」 and 「パソコン」, which you know. Long English phrases get cut to a handful of beats. So give it five even beats, keep the endings light, and you'll sound natural." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese chopped contact lens, 「コンタクトレンズ」, down to 「コンタクト」, the partner of 「めがね」; you wear them with 「する」, put them in with 「いれる」, and take them out with 「はずす」 or 「とる」.",
      "Most wearables have their own verb, like 「かける」 for glasses, but small add-ons take 「する」, so 「コンタクトをしています」 means I have contacts in.",
      "In Japan the word usually means disposable soft lenses, 「使い捨てコンタクト|つかいすてコンタクト」, and the classic drama is 「コンタクトを落としました|コンタクトをおとしました」, then everyone searching the floor.",
      "Give it five even beats, with 「ン」 getting a full beat and the light, almost whispered 「ク」 and 「ト」; the long eight-beat phrase is exactly why people chop it."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは[コンタクト]をしています。",
    en: "I wear contact lenses." },
  "ひげ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native Japanese word, and people usually write it in hiragana. There are kanji for it, but they're rare and complicated, so you can safely learn it as kana. The big thing to know is that 「ひげ」 is wider than English beard. It covers any facial hair, a beard, a mustache, stubble, sideburns. It's even used for the whiskers on a cat, 「ねこのひげ」. So think of it as face hair in general. A way to remember it, and this is just a hook: think of a he-goat, with that little tuft of hair on its chin. He-goat, hee-geh, face hair. Now the patterns. To say someone has a beard, use 「ひげがあります」. To grow one, people say 「ひげをはやす」. And in this same lesson you get the daily routine phrase 「ひげをそる」, to shave. Culturally, beards are less common among Japanese office workers than in some Western countries. Many companies expect a clean-shaven face, so a 「サラリーマン」 usually shaves every morning. Recap: 「ひげ」 is any facial hair, beard, mustache, or a cat's whiskers, and you shave it with 「そる」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Even though people write this word in kana, the kanji behind it are interesting, and they connect to a word you know. Traditionally Japanese had three different kanji, all read 「ひげ」. 「髭|ひげ」 was hair around the mouth, a mustache. 「鬚|ひげ」 was hair on the chin, a beard. And 「髯|ひげ」 was hair on the cheeks, side whiskers. In modern use people don't keep them strictly apart, and most just write kana. But look at what they share. All three have the same top part, a shape that means long flowing hair. You already know that piece. It sits on top of 「髪|かみ」, the hair on your head, from lesson seven. So head hair and face hair are written with the same hairy crown. You don't need to write these kanji. Just recognize that hairy top, and you'll spot hair words when you see them." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Since this word covers all facial hair, Japanese adds small words in front to say exactly which kind. 「口ひげ|くちひげ」 is a mustache, literally mouth whiskers, from 「口|くち」, mouth. 「あごひげ」 is a beard on the chin, from 「あご」, chin. And 「無精ひげ|ぶしょうひげ」 is stubble, literally lazy beard, the scruff you get when you skip shaving for a few days. The shaving tools come from the verb 「そる」. A razor is 「ひげそり」, a shaver, made by putting the noun and the verb stem together. Then there are animals. A cat's whiskers are 「ねこのひげ」, and the long feelers on a shrimp or a catfish are called by the same word too. So a learner should think of it as anything thin and hair-like growing from a face. So start from the general word, then add mouth, chin, or lazy to get specific." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Facial hair in Japan has gone in and out of fashion over the centuries. In the Meiji era, when Japan was rapidly adopting Western styles, big mustaches and beards became a sign of a modern, important man. Old photos of government officials and army officers are full of impressive whiskers. Today the picture is different. In many offices and service jobs, a clean-shaven face is the expected look, and some companies have rules against beards for staff who meet customers. So many workers shave every single morning, 「毎朝ひげをそる|まいあさひげをそる」. At the same time, among younger people, artists, and people in creative jobs, a neat beard or stubble can look stylish, and 「ひげが似合う|ひげがにあう」, a beard suits him, is a compliment. So facial hair says something about your job and image in Japan, from Meiji gentlemen to today's creative types." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Usually written in kana, it covers any facial hair, beard, mustache, stubble, even a cat's whiskers; you grow it with 「ひげをはやす」 and shave with 「ひげをそる」.",
      "Three old kanji, for mustache, beard and side whiskers, all share the long-flowing-hair crown that also tops 「髪|かみ」, so head hair and face hair look alike.",
      "Add a word in front to be specific: 「口ひげ|くちひげ」 is a mustache, 「あごひげ」 a chin beard, 「無精ひげ|ぶしょうひげ」 lazy stubble, and a razor is 「ひげそり」.",
      "Meiji officials wore grand whiskers, but many offices now expect 「毎朝ひげをそる|まいあさひげをそる」, while in creative circles 「ひげが似合う|ひげがにあう」 is a compliment."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は[ひげ]があります。",
    en: "My father has a beard." },
  "ブーツ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English boots. Notice it comes from the plural form. Japanese nouns don't change for plural, so 「ブーツ」 just means boots, whether it's one pair or many. Listen to how the sounds got squeezed in. The long oo becomes a long vowel, written with the dash, 「ブー」. The final ts sound, which English has but Japanese doesn't normally end a word with, becomes 「ツ」. So boots turns into two neat beats plus a long vowel. Now the verb, which is important. Japanese has different verbs for putting on different clothes. You learned in lesson seven that things you step into, like shoes, pants, and skirts, use 「はく」. Boots are footwear, so you say 「ブーツをはきます」. To take them off you use 「ぬぐ」, also in this lesson. Culturally, boots are a bit of a hassle in Japan, because you take your shoes off at the entrance of homes, many restaurants, and even some shops. Long lace-up boots mean a lot of time standing in the doorway. Recap: from English boots, put on with 「はく」, taken off with 「ぬぐ」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a pair of loanwords that are easy to mix up. 「ブーツ」, boots, and 「ブース」, booth, like a small stand at an event or a phone booth. They differ only in the last sound. 「ツ」 has a sharp t at the start, like the end of the English word cats. 「ス」 is a soft hiss. To keep them apart, think of the ts as the click of a boot heel hitting the floor. Click, 「ツ」. Also keep the long vowel. The dash in 「ブー」 means you hold the vowel for two beats. If you cut it short, you lose the shape of the word. Say it slowly as three beats, 「ブ」, held 「ー」, then 「ツ」. Japanese listeners hear the length clearly, so give that held vowel its full time. So the heel goes click at the end, and the vowel is long." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Boots belong to the shoe family, and Japanese mixes native words and loanwords here. The basic word is 「靴|くつ」, shoes, which you know. 「靴下|くつした」 are socks, literally under the shoes. Now look at a native word for boots. 「長靴|ながぐつ」 means rain boots or rubber boots, literally long shoes, and notice the k softens to g in the compound. For fashion boots, though, people use the loanword family. 「ロングブーツ」 are tall boots up to the knee. 「ショートブーツ」 are ankle boots. And 「レインブーツ」 are stylish rain boots, a fashionable version of 「長靴|ながぐつ」. So kids splashing in puddles wear 「長靴|ながぐつ」, while someone dressing up for the city wears 「ブーツ」. Same idea, different feel. Native words often sound practical, while loanwords sound fashionable. So shoes, socks, long shoes for rain, and stylish loanword boots." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Boots are popular in Japanese winter fashion, but the shoes-off culture makes them a real puzzle. Before you visit someone's home, or go to a restaurant with tatami rooms, people think twice about wearing tall lace-up boots. Many choose boots with a side zipper so they can slip them off quickly at the entrance. When you arrive, you might hear the host say 「どうぞ、上がってください|どうぞ、あがってください」, please come up in. That is your cue to take off your shoes. A small apology is natural if your boots take time, like 「すみません、ブーツなので」, sorry, it's because they're boots. Some restaurants and inns even keep a long-handled shoehorn by the door to help with tall boots. So if you know you'll be taking your shoes off, choose easy boots, and say sorry if you hold up the doorway." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English boots, it means one pair or many; boots are footwear you step into, so you put them on with 「はく」 and take them off with 「ぬぐ」.",
      "Don't confuse it with 「ブース」, booth: think of 「ツ」 as the click of a boot heel, and hold the long vowel in 「ブー」 for its full two beats.",
      "Kids splashing in puddles wear practical 「長靴|ながぐつ」, long shoes, while city dressers wear loanword 「ブーツ」, like 「ロングブーツ」 or 「ショートブーツ」.",
      "Because of the shoes-off culture, people pick side-zip boots, and if yours hold up the doorway, a small 「すみません、ブーツなので」 is natural."
    ],
    ja: "{冬|ふゆ}は[ブーツ]をはきます。",
    en: "I wear boots in winter." },
  "かぎ|鍵": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native word, and the kanji is read with its native reading here. Look at the left side of 「鍵|かぎ」. That's the metal part, the same piece that is the whole of 「金|かね」 in 「お金|おかね」, money. Lots of kanji for metal things carry it. Keys were made of metal, so that part makes perfect sense. One surprise for English speakers. 「かぎ」 means both key and lock. Japanese doesn't separate them sharply in daily talk. The context tells you which. This lesson also gives you the phrase 「鍵をかける|かぎをかける」, to lock. The verb 「かける」 is that useful hang-or-apply verb you've seen in 「めがねをかける」 and 「電話をかける|でんわをかける」. You're putting the lock on. To unlock, you say 「鍵をあける|かぎをあける」, to open the lock. A common worry sentence is 「鍵をなくしました|かぎをなくしました」, I lost my key, using 「なくす」 from lesson twelve. A way to remember the sound, and this is a hook: a key has a sharp hook shape, and 「かぎ」 also sounds like the word for a hook. Recap: metal part on the left, means both key and lock, and you lock with 「鍵をかける|かぎをかける」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The metal part on the left gets all the attention, so let's look at the right side. That piece is 「建|けん」, the kanji for building, which you'll meet in 「建物|たてもの」, a building. Here it isn't about buildings at all. It is there to give the sound. When this kanji is read with its Chinese-origin reading, it becomes 「けん」, matching that right side. You'll hear that reading in 「鍵盤|けんばん」, a keyboard, like the keys on a piano. Funny enough, English also calls those keys. But in normal daily speech, the word for the thing you lock a door with is always the native reading, 「かぎ」. So the left side tells you the meaning, metal, and the right side tells you the Chinese sound, 「けん」. That split, a meaning part plus a sound part, is how most kanji are built. So this kanji is metal for meaning, building for sound, read 「かぎ」 when it opens your door." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Locking comes with a neat verb pair. You lock something with 「鍵をかける|かぎをかける」, using the transitive verb 「かける」, where you do the action to the lock. But to say the door is locked, Japanese switches to the intransitive partner, 「かかる」. 「鍵がかかっている|かぎがかかっている」 means it's locked, a state, with nobody mentioned. Notice the particle flips from 「を」 to 「が」. This is the same pattern as 「開ける|あける」 and 「開く|あく」, or 「消す|けす」 and 「消える|きえる」, which you know. So imagine trying a door. You pull, it doesn't move, and you say 「あれ、鍵がかかっている|あれ、かぎがかかっている」, huh, it's locked. Then you might ask your roommate, 「鍵をかけた|かぎをかけた」, did you lock it? One verb for doing the locking, one for the state of being locked. So 「かける」 with 「を」 for the action, 「かかる」 with 「が」 for the result." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "A few everyday words are built on this one. 「合鍵|あいかぎ」 is a duplicate key, a spare copy. In Japan, giving your partner a copy of your apartment key is a meaningful step in a relationship, and dramas love the scene where someone hands over a 「合鍵|あいかぎ」. Then there is 「鍵っ子|かぎっこ」, a latchkey child, a kid who carries a key because their parents are still at work when school ends. Notice the small 「っ」 joining the two parts. The word can also be used in a figurative way, just like English. 「成功の鍵|せいこうのかぎ」 means the key to success. And the thing you hang keys on, a key ring or key chain, is usually the loanword 「キーホルダー」, made in Japan from English words. So the family runs from spare keys and latchkey kids to the key to success." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「鍵|かぎ」 has the metal part on the left, and it means both key and lock; you lock with 「鍵をかける|かぎをかける」 and unlock with 「鍵をあける|かぎをあける」.",
      "The metal side gives the meaning while the right side, 「建|けん」, gives the Chinese sound 「けん」, as in 「鍵盤|けんばん」, a keyboard, but your door key is always 「かぎ」.",
      "You lock it with 「を」 and 「かける」, but the locked state flips to 「が」 and 「かかる」, as in 「あれ、鍵がかかっている|あれ、かぎがかかっている」.",
      "「合鍵|あいかぎ」 is a spare key, a meaningful gift between partners, 「鍵っ子|かぎっこ」 is a latchkey kid, and 「成功の鍵|せいこうのかぎ」 is the key to success."
    ],
    ja: "[鍵|かぎ]をなくしました。",
    en: "I lost my key." },
  "たからくじ|宝くじ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two pieces. The first is the kanji 「宝|たから」, treasure, with its native reading. Its shape is real and easy to read. At the top is the roof part, and under it is 「玉|たま」, a jewel. A jewel kept under your roof, a treasure. The second piece is 「くじ」, written in kana. It means a lot, the kind you draw to decide something by chance. You may have seen 「おみくじ」 at a shrine, 「神社|じんじゃ」, the paper fortunes you draw for luck. Same 「くじ」, with a polite 「お」 and 「み」 in front. So 「宝くじ|たからくじ」 is a treasure lot, a ticket you draw hoping for treasure. That's a lottery. In Japan the most famous one is the big year-end lottery. Long lines form at popular ticket booths that are said to sell lucky tickets, and the drawing happens at the very end of the year. People buy tickets dreaming of quitting overtime forever. The matching phrase in this lesson is 「宝くじに当たる|たからくじにあたる」, to win the lottery, literally to hit the lottery. Recap: 「宝|たから」, jewel under a roof, treasure, plus 「くじ」, a drawn lot, gives lottery." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both halves of this word have relatives worth knowing. The treasure kanji gives you 「宝物|たからもの」, a treasure, something precious to you. A child might call a favorite toy their 「宝物|たからもの」. With its Chinese-origin reading it becomes 「ほう」, as in 「国宝|こくほう」, a national treasure, the official title given to famous temples, statues, and artworks. Now the lot half. 「くじ引き|くじびき」 means drawing lots, from 「引く|ひく」, to pull. Festivals and shopping streets often hold a 「くじ引き|くじびき」 where you draw a slip for a prize. A winning ticket is called 「当たり|あたり」, a hit, and a losing ticket is 「外れ|はずれ」, a miss. Those two words are printed on candy sticks and festival games all over Japan. So treasure for precious things, lots for drawing by chance, and every lot is either a hit or a miss." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Lotteries in Japan have a long history. In the Edo period, temples and shrines ran lotteries called 「富くじ|とみくじ」, wealth lots, often to raise money for repairs. They became hugely popular, and the government eventually cracked down on them in the eighteen-forties as part of a series of reforms. Lotteries then largely disappeared for about a century. The modern word took over after the Second World War. In nineteen forty-five the government began selling lottery tickets again to raise money in very hard times, and the name used was this word, treasure lots. Today the profits go to local governments to help pay for public projects. So when you buy a ticket, a bit of your money builds roads and parks, even if you don't win. So from temple wealth lots in old Edo to today's treasure lots, Japan has loved a lucky draw." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick to tie the sound to the meaning. Picture a pirate on a treasure island, digging in the sand. His shovel hits a chest. He throws it open, and inside, instead of gold coins, there is a single lottery ticket. He's furious and shouts tah-kara, like a pirate's grumble, which is close to 「たから」, treasure. Then a little parrot on his shoulder squawks koo-jee, koo-jee, which is close to 「くじ」, the lot. Treasure, then the parrot's lot. That's only a memory trick, but it ties the two halves together. And the kanji helps too. Remember the jewel sitting under a roof, safely kept at home, just like the pirate wishes his ticket were real gold. Next time you see a lottery booth, imagine the angry pirate and his squawking parrot." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「宝|たから」 is a jewel under a roof, treasure, and 「くじ」 is a drawn lot, as in 「おみくじ」, so a treasure lot; you win with 「宝くじに当たる|たからくじにあたる」.",
      "The treasure side gives 「宝物|たからもの」 and 「国宝|こくほう」, and the lot side gives 「くじ引き|くじびき」, where every slip is a hit, 「当たり|あたり」, or a miss, 「外れ|はずれ」.",
      "Edo temples ran 「富くじ|とみくじ」, wealth lots, until a crackdown, and in nineteen forty-five the government revived lotteries as treasure lots, with profits now funding local projects.",
      "A pirate digs up a chest holding only a lottery ticket and grumbles tah-kara, treasure, while his parrot squawks koo-jee, the lot."
    ],
    ja: "[宝くじ|たからくじ]を{買いました|かいました}。",
    en: "I bought a lottery ticket." },
  "かみ|紙": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, read here with its native reading. On the left side of 「紙|かみ」 you can see the thread part, 「糸|いと」. Many kanji about fibers, cloth, and string carry it, and paper fits, because paper is made by pressing plant fibers together into a sheet. You already know this kanji from 「手紙|てがみ」, letter, from lesson five. There it's still the native reading, but the k softens to g, 「がみ」, because it follows another word. Hand plus paper, a letter. Now a warning about sounds. Japanese has several words pronounced 「かみ」. 「髪|かみ」, hair, which you learned in lesson seven and which appears again in this lesson in 「髪をとかす|かみをとかす」. And 「神|かみ」, god, the kanji you see in 「神社|じんじゃ」, shrine. Context will tell you which one is meant, and the kanji make it clear in writing. Counting sheets of paper uses the counter 「〜まい」, the one for flat things, so one sheet of paper is 「紙一枚|かみいちまい」. Recap: thread part for fiber, the same kanji as in 「手紙|てがみ」, and watch out for its sound-twins hair and god." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The thread part on the left gives the meaning, fibers. The right side is 「氏|し」, a kanji meaning family name or clan, and here it mostly gives the sound. With its Chinese-origin reading this kanji becomes 「し」, and you'll meet it in several words. 「和紙|わし」 is traditional Japanese paper, with 「和|わ」 meaning Japanese style. 「表紙|ひょうし」 is the cover of a book. And 「用紙|ようし」 is a form, a printed sheet you fill in at an office or a bank. So when paper stands alone, it is the native word 「かみ」. When it joins a Chinese-style compound, it is often 「し」. And when it comes after a native word, it softens to 「がみ」, like in 「手紙|てがみ」. Three sounds, one kanji. So thread on the left for fiber, clan on the right for the sound 「し」, and the native reading 「かみ」 when it stands alone." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Paper holds a special place in Japanese culture. 「和紙|わし」, traditional handmade paper, is made from the inner bark of certain shrubs, and it is strong, soft, and long-lasting. It was used for books, letters, lanterns, umbrellas, and the sliding paper screens in traditional houses. The craft of making it in certain regions is even recognized by UNESCO. Then there is 「折り紙|おりがみ」, the art of folding paper, from 「折る|おる」, to fold. Notice the paper softens to 「がみ」 again. Children learn to fold cranes, and a string of a thousand paper cranes is a famous wish for health or peace. And at shrines, the zigzag white paper strips you see hanging from ropes are also made of paper, marking sacred space. So paper in Japan is not just for writing. It's craft, play, prayer, and even architecture." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Lots of everyday objects are made by putting this word in front. A paper bag is 「紙袋|かみぶくろ」, and a paper cup is 「紙コップ|かみコップ」, mixing a kanji word with a loanword. A paper plate is 「紙皿|かみざら」. When you're shopping, a clerk might ask whether you want a bag, and in some shops the bags are paper. When you need tissues, though, people usually say 「ティッシュ」, and toilet paper is 「トイレットペーパー」. So for those, Japanese uses loanwords. Use 「に」 for writing on paper, as in 「紙に書く|かみにかく」, write on paper. A teacher might say 「この紙に名前を書いてください|このかみになまえをかいてください」. When you need a sheet, ask for 「紙を一枚ください|かみをいちまいください」. Also notice that in office settings people often say 「用紙|ようし」 for a form. So paper goes in front to make bags, cups, and plates, and you write on it with 「に」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「紙|かみ」 has the thread part because paper is pressed plant fiber, it softens to 「がみ」 in 「手紙|てがみ」, and it shares its sound with hair, 「髪|かみ」, and god, 「神|かみ」.",
      "Thread gives the meaning and 「氏|し」 gives the sound 「し」 in compounds like 「和紙|わし」 and 「表紙|ひょうし」, while alone it's 「かみ」 and after a native word 「がみ」.",
      "Handmade 「和紙|わし」 from shrub bark went into screens, lanterns and books, and 「折り紙|おりがみ」 gives folded cranes, so paper in Japan is craft, play and prayer.",
      "Put it in front to make 「紙袋|かみぶくろ」, 「紙コップ|かみコップ」 and 「紙皿|かみざら」, and write on it with 「に」, as in 「紙に書く|かみにかく」."
    ],
    ja: "[紙|かみ]に{名前|なまえ}を{書いて|かいて}ください。",
    en: "Please write your name on the paper." },
  "スプーン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English spoon. Listen to how it fits into Japanese. English spoon starts with s and p squashed together, but Japanese needs a vowel after every consonant except n. So the s gets its own beat, 「ス」. The long oo becomes 「プー」, with the dash marking a long vowel. And the final n is the one consonant Japanese allows alone, 「ン」. So one English syllable becomes four Japanese beats. Culturally, the spoon has an interesting place in Japan. Most meals are eaten with 「はし」, chopsticks, which you learned in lesson eight. Soup like miso is sipped straight from the bowl. So when do you use a spoon? For Western or Western-influenced dishes, and above all for 「カレー」, Japanese curry rice, from lesson thirteen. Curry is always eaten with a spoon, never chopsticks. Ice cream, desserts, and fried rice often come with a spoon too. Use the particle 「で」 for the tool you eat with: 「スプーンで食べます|スプーンでたべます」, eat with a spoon, just like 「はしで」. Recap: English spoon stretched to four beats, the curry utensil, partner to 「はし」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has its own native word for spoon, and it still lives on in cooking. It's 「さじ」. You'll see it constantly in recipes. 「大さじ|おおさじ」 means a tablespoon, literally big spoon, and 「小さじ|こさじ」 means a teaspoon, small spoon. So a recipe might say 「しょうゆ大さじ一|しょうゆおおさじいち」, one tablespoon of soy sauce. There's also a famous idiom with it, 「さじを投げる|さじをなげる」, to throw the spoon, which means to give up on something as hopeless. It's said to come from doctors, who once mixed medicine with a spoon, throwing it down when a patient couldn't be helped. The rest of the cutlery family is mostly loanwords. 「フォーク」 is a fork, 「ナイフ」 is a knife, and of course 「はし」 are chopsticks. So the eating spoon is 「スプーン」, but the measuring spoon in a recipe is often 「さじ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "One of the most common places you'll hear this word is at a convenience store. When you buy something like yogurt, a pudding, or a bowl of rice, the clerk will often ask 「スプーンはおつけしますか」, shall I include a spoon? This is a polite, humble way of asking. You can answer 「はい、お願いします|はい、おねがいします」, yes please, or 「いいえ、大丈夫です|いいえ、だいじょうぶです」, no, I'm fine. That second answer is how Japanese people politely say no thanks. For ramen and Chinese dishes, you'll get a different kind of spoon, a wide, flat ceramic one called 「れんげ」, used to sip the soup. And for desserts at a cafe, a long thin spoon might come with your parfait. So at the register, listen for the spoon question, and say 「大丈夫です|だいじょうぶです」 if you don't need one." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "A quick memory scene, focused on the long vowel, because that's the part learners shorten. Imagine a giant spoon at a dinner table, so big that it acts like a swimming pool. You dive in from the edge and slide down its long curved bowl, shouting sp-oooooon all the way down, holding that long sound for as long as you can. That long slide is the dash in 「プー」. Then you land at the bottom with a little hum, 「ン」. Picture it again. Start with a little hiss, 「ス」, as you jump. Then the long slide, 「プー」. Then the hum at the bottom, 「ン」. This is just a memory trick, but it reminds you that the middle is stretched. Say it with that long slide, and it'll sound like real Japanese." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English spoon stretches to four beats, 「ス」, 「プー」, 「ン」; since most meals use 「はし」, the spoon is above all for 「カレー」, and you eat with it using 「で」.",
      "The native word 「さじ」 lives on in recipes as 「大さじ|おおさじ」, tablespoon, and 「小さじ|こさじ」, teaspoon, and 「さじを投げる|さじをなげる」 means to give up as hopeless.",
      "Convenience store clerks ask 「スプーンはおつけしますか」, and 「いいえ、大丈夫です|いいえ、だいじょうぶです」 is the polite no thanks; ramen comes with a flat ceramic 「れんげ」 instead.",
      "Picture sliding down a giant spoon shouting a long oooon: hiss 「ス」 as you jump, the long slide 「プー」, and a hum 「ン」 at the bottom."
    ],
    ja: "カレーを[スプーン]で{食べます|たべます}。",
    en: "I eat curry with a spoon." },
  "おゆ|お湯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「湯|ゆ」, with its native reading, plus the polite 「お」 in front. On the left side of the kanji you can see three little drops. That's the water part, the same one that runs down the side of 「海|うみ」, sea, and 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, swim. Lots of watery kanji carry it. Here's the key point for English speakers. Japanese treats hot water and cold water as two different words. 「水|みず」 is cool or room-temperature water. 「お湯|おゆ」 is hot water. Saying 「あつい水|あついみず」, hot water, sounds strange, like saying hot cold water. You simply switch words. You'll see this kanji all over Japan in an interesting place. Public baths and hot springs, 「温泉|おんせん」, often hang a cloth curtain at the entrance with a big 「ゆ」 written on it in hiragana. That one sound tells everyone, hot bath here. In this lesson the word comes with a verb, 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」, to boil water, which you'll do to make tea. Recap: water drops on the left, polite 「お」 in front, and a separate word from 「水|みず」 because it's hot." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The three water drops on the left give the meaning. The right side gives the sound, and it is the same piece you'll see in other kanji with similar sounds. With its Chinese-origin reading, this kanji is read 「とう」. You'll meet it in 「銭湯|せんとう」, a neighborhood public bath, where 「銭|せん」 is an old word for coins. So a public bath is literally hot water you pay coins for. Another is 「熱湯|ねっとう」, boiling hot water, a word you'll see on warning labels and instant noodle cups. But when the kanji stands alone, or appears with native words, it uses the native reading 「ゆ」. A bath curtain says 「ゆ」. Your teacup is 「湯のみ|ゆのみ」. So remember, water drops on the left, a sound piece on the right, and two readings, 「ゆ」 for everyday hot water and 「とう」 in compounds like the public bath." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Hot water shows up inside a lot of useful words. 「湯のみ|ゆのみ」 is a Japanese teacup, the kind without a handle, literally a hot water drinker. 「湯気|ゆげ」 is steam, the cloud that rises from a hot bath or a bowl of noodles. 「湯船|ゆぶね」 is the bathtub itself, literally a hot water boat, which is a lovely picture. At a hot spring, you'll find separate baths marked 「男湯|おとこゆ」, men's bath, and 「女湯|おんなゆ」, women's bath. The polite 「お」 drops off in these compounds. And this lesson gives you a verb pair. 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」 means to boil water, where you do it. 「お湯が沸く|おゆがわく」 means the water boils, all on its own. So the family covers teacups, steam, bathtubs, and baths for men and women, all built on hot water." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's a surprise for English speakers. This word often means a hot bath, not just hot water. At a hot spring someone might say 「いいお湯ですね|いいおゆですね」, this is a nice bath. And when family members come out of the bath, they might be asked 「お湯、どうだった|おゆ、どうだった」, how was the bath? Another very common use is with instant noodles. The instructions say to pour in hot water and wait three minutes. People say 「お湯を入れて三分|おゆをいれてさんぷん」, add hot water, three minutes. At a restaurant, if your tea gets cold, you could ask 「お湯をください|おゆをください」, hot water please, and staff will refill the pot. And at a hotel, a kettle in the room is for making 「お湯|おゆ」 for tea. So listen for this word at the bath, in instant noodle instructions, and over tea." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「湯|ゆ」 has the water drops plus polite 「お」, and Japanese uses a separate word for hot water, so never say hot 「水|みず」; bath curtains show a big 「ゆ」.",
      "Water drops give the meaning and the right side the sound 「とう」, as in 「銭湯|せんとう」, hot water you pay coins for, while alone it's 「ゆ」, as in 「湯のみ|ゆのみ」.",
      "It builds 「湯気|ゆげ」, steam, 「湯船|ゆぶね」, a hot-water boat or bathtub, and 「男湯|おとこゆ」 and 「女湯|おんなゆ」, plus the pair 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」 and 「お湯が沸く|おゆがわく」.",
      "It often means the bath itself, as in 「いいお湯ですね|いいおゆですね」, and you'll hear it in noodle instructions, 「お湯を入れて三分|おゆをいれてさんぷん」, and asking for tea refills."
    ],
    ja: "[お湯|おゆ]でおちゃをいれます。",
    en: "I make tea with hot water." },
  "でんしレンジ|電子レンジ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A fun hybrid word, half kanji and half loanword. The first half is 「電子|でんし」, electron, made of two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. 「電|でん」 is electricity. You know it well from 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, 「電車|でんしゃ」, train, 「電気|でんき」, lights or electricity, and 「電池|でんち」, battery. 「子|し」 normally means child, but in science words it means a tiny particle. So 「電子|でんし」 is a tiny electric particle, an electron. The second half, 「レンジ」, comes from English range, as in a kitchen range or cooking stove. The r becomes the Japanese r, the a becomes 「レ」, and the soft ge at the end becomes 「ジ」. So the whole thing is literally an electron range, a cooker that works with electricity in a new way. People often shorten it to just 「レンジ」. There's also a cute casual verb you'll hear, 「チンする」, to microwave something, named after the ding sound the old machines made when they finished. Recap: 「電|でん」, electricity, plus 「子|し」, particle, gives electron, plus range, a stove, equals microwave oven." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first half of this word, electron, turns up in many modern words, where it means electronic. 「電子辞書|でんしじしょ」 is an electronic dictionary, the little device that Japanese students carried everywhere before smartphones took over. 「電子マネー|でんしマネー」 is electronic money, the cards and phone apps people tap at stations and stores. 「電子メール|でんしメール」 is the official word for email, though in daily life people usually just say 「メール」. Now the second half. On its own, 「レンジ」 in Japanese usually means the microwave, not a whole cooking stove like in English. And a combination microwave and oven, very common in Japanese kitchens because space is tight, is called an 「オーブンレンジ」. So electronic plus a loanword makes dictionaries, money, and email, and the microwave is the kitchen star of the family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The phrase you'll hear most with a microwave is at a convenience store. When you buy a lunch box or a rice ball, the clerk will often ask 「温めますか|あたためますか」, shall I heat it up? They mean in the store's microwave. You answer 「はい、お願いします|はい、おねがいします」, or 「いいえ、大丈夫です|いいえ、だいじょうぶです」. At home, you use the particle 「で」 for the tool. 「レンジで温める|レンジであたためる」 means heat it in the microwave. The verb 「温める|あたためる」 means to warm something up. Packages often say how long, like 「レンジで二分|レンジでにふん」, two minutes in the microwave. And of course the casual way, 「チンする」, which friends and family use all the time. So listen for the convenience store question, and use 「で」 when you say how you heat something." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick for the second half. 「レンジ」 is hiding inside another loanword you probably know, 「オレンジ」, orange. Picture this. You're starving, so you throw a whole orange into the microwave, set it to two minutes, and press start. It spins around and around, getting puffier and puffier. Then ding, the door pops open and a cloud of orange steam fills the kitchen. The orange went into the 「レンジ」. Orange, range. This is only a trick for the sound, of course, and please don't really microwave an orange. But the picture links the two words strongly. Then remember the first half, electron, by imagining tiny glowing particles zooming around that orange. Electron range. So an orange in the 「レンジ」, with electrons buzzing around it, makes a microwave." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「電|でん」 is electricity and 「子|し」 is a tiny particle, so 「電子|でんし」 is an electron, plus 「レンジ」 from range, an electron range; casually it's 「レンジ」 or 「チンする」.",
      "「電子|でんし」 means electronic in 「電子辞書|でんしじしょ」 and 「電子マネー|でんしマネー」, while 「レンジ」 alone means the microwave and a combination oven is an 「オーブンレンジ」.",
      "Convenience store clerks ask 「温めますか|あたためますか」, shall I heat it, and at home you use 「で」, as in 「レンジで温める|レンジであたためる」.",
      "「レンジ」 hides inside 「オレンジ」, so picture an orange spinning in the microwave with tiny glowing electrons buzzing around it until ding."
    ],
    ja: "{毎日|まいにち}[電子レンジ|でんしレンジ]を{使います|つかいます}。",
    en: "I use the microwave every day." },
  "ヒーター|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English heater. Listen to how the sounds were adapted. The h and the long ee sound become 「ヒー」, with the dash stretching the vowel. The t plus the er at the end becomes 「ター」. English er endings usually turn into a long a sound in Japanese loanwords, the same way computer became 「コンピューター」, which you learned in lesson one. So heater becomes a tidy four beats. You already know a cousin from lesson six, 「エアコン」, air conditioner, which is short for air conditioner squeezed down. Many Japanese homes don't have central heating the way some American homes do. Instead, each room is warmed by its own device, an air conditioner that also heats, a gas or kerosene stove, or a small electric 「ヒーター」. And of course there's the famous heated table with a blanket over it, the kotatsu, where families sit in winter eating mandarin oranges, 「みかん」. To turn a heater on, use 「つける」, the same verb as turning on lights, and to turn it off use 「けす」. Recap: English heater, with er turning into a long a, warming one room at a time." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Heating words in Japanese hold a trap for English speakers. The biggest one is 「ストーブ」. It comes from English stove, but in Japan it does not mean a kitchen stove for cooking. It means a space heater, usually a gas or kerosene one that warms a room. So if a Japanese friend says they turned on the 「ストーブ」, they're warming up, not cooking dinner. The cooking stove is 「コンロ」, a gas burner. Then there is the general word for heating, 「暖房|だんぼう」, from 「暖かい|あたたかい」, warm. Its opposite is 「冷房|れいぼう」, air cooling. Air conditioners in Japan have buttons for both, and a sign in a train might say the cooling is on. 「ヒーター」 is the general word for a device that heats, often a small electric one. So heater, space heater, heating, and cooling, and remember that 「ストーブ」 warms the room, it doesn't cook." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Let's look at the long vowel at the end, because it has a curious history. English words ending in er usually come into Japanese with a long a sound, written with the dash, so heater gets 「ター」 at the end. You know the same pattern from 「コンピューター」. But for many years, technical writing in Japan often dropped that final dash. Engineers and manuals would write the word without the last long vowel, simply ending in 「タ」. You may still see that shorter spelling on product labels, instruction booklets, or in older technical documents. In everyday speech, though, people almost always say the full long ending. So when you speak, give the word four even beats. 「ヒ」, held 「ー」, 「タ」, held 「ー」. Two stretched vowels, two quick consonants. So say it long at both ends, and don't be surprised by the shorter spelling on a manual." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Winter in a Japanese home can feel surprisingly cold, because many older houses are not well insulated. So the heater becomes the center of family life. People gather around it, and the person who comes home first switches it on. A typical scene goes like this. Someone walks in from the snow and says 「寒い|さむい」, so cold! Then 「ヒーター、つけて」, turn on the heater, please. Another common phrase is about leaving it running, like 「ヒーターをつけたまま寝ました|ヒーターをつけたままねました」, I fell asleep with the heater on. That's something parents warn against, because of fire risk and the electricity bill. Many people also use small heaters under their desk at work or school. So the heater is where everyone gathers in winter, and you switch it on with 「つける」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English heater, with the er ending turning into a long 「ター」 like 「コンピューター」; Japanese homes warm one room at a time, and you switch it on with 「つける」 and off with 「けす」.",
      "The trap is 「ストーブ」, which means a space heater that warms a room, not a cooking stove, which is 「コンロ」; heating in general is 「暖房|だんぼう」 and cooling 「冷房|れいぼう」.",
      "Technical labels often drop the final dash and end in 「タ」, but in speech give it four even beats with both vowels stretched, 「ヒー」 and 「ター」.",
      "Poorly insulated homes make the heater the winter center, so someone coming in says 「ヒーター、つけて」, and parents warn against 「ヒーターをつけたまま寝ました|ヒーターをつけたままねました」."
    ],
    ja: "{寒い|さむい}ので[ヒーター]をつけました。",
    en: "It was cold, so I turned on the heater." },
  "ニュース|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword from English news. Here's how the sounds were squeezed in. The n plus the ew sound becomes 「ニュー」, with a small 「ュ」 to make the glide and a dash to stretch it. The final s needs a vowel after it in Japanese, so it becomes 「ス」. Like boots, it keeps the English s even though Japanese doesn't mark plurals. Now a nice connection. Back in lesson two you learned 「新聞|しんぶん」, newspaper. Its two kanji are 「新|しん」, new, and 「聞|ぶん」, hear. Newly heard things. That's a lovely native way of saying news, and it ended up meaning the paper you read them in. Today 「ニュース」 is the general word for news on TV, radio, or the internet. In this lesson you'll pair it with 「〜によると」, according to. 「ニュースによると」, according to the news, followed by 「そうです」, I hear that. That's the classic way to report something you learned from the news. A typical habit sentence is 「毎朝ニュースを見ます|まいあさニュースをみます」, I watch the news every morning. Recap: English news, with the s kept, the modern partner of 「新聞|しんぶん」, newly heard." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "The English word behind this loanword has a simple and real history. News comes from the word new. In older English, people talked about new things, and the plural form, news, came to mean fresh information. That's why the word ends in s even though we treat it as one thing today. You may have heard a popular story that news stands for north, east, west, and south, because news comes from all directions. That's a fun idea, but it's a myth, not the real origin. Japanese, of course, had its own way of saying news long before the loanword arrived, and it was built on the same idea of newness. 「新聞|しんぶん」 uses the kanji for new, too. So both languages built their word for news around the idea of something new. Remember the plural of new, not a compass." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here are the verbs and phrases that go with news. You can watch it, 「ニュースを見る|ニュースをみる」, on TV, or listen to it, 「ニュースを聞く|ニュースをきく」, on the radio. A news program is 「ニュース番組|ニュースばんぐみ」. To say you heard something on the news, use 「で」, as in 「ニュースで見ました|ニュースでみました」, I saw it on the news. Japanese TV news is famously calm and polite, and it is usually followed by the weather forecast, 「天気予報|てんきよほう」. People also talk about good and bad news. 「いいニュース」 and 「悪いニュース|わるいニュース」 both work. For personal news, though, you'll also hear 「知らせ|しらせ」, a notice or message, as in 「いい知らせ|いいしらせ」, good news. A small dialogue. 「今朝のニュース、見た|けさのニュース、みた」, did you see this morning's news? 「うん、びっくりした」, yeah, I was surprised. So watch it, hear it, and say you saw something on the news with 「で」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "News comes with a team of related words. The person who reads the news on TV is often called an 「アナウンサー」, an announcer, and in Japan announcers are well-known TV personalities. A presenter who leads a news show can also be called a 「キャスター」, from the English caster in newscaster. The people who go out and gather the stories are reporters. You know 「ジャーナリスト」, journalist, from lesson eleven, and there is also a kanji word, 「記者|きしゃ」, a reporter, from 「記|き」, to record, and 「者|しゃ」, person. And for news on paper, of course, you have 「新聞|しんぶん」. A newspaper article is 「記事|きじ」, which shares that recording kanji. So the family runs from the announcer on screen to the reporter in the field, with announcers, casters, and reporters all bringing you the news." }
    ],
    short: [
      "From English news, keeping the s; it's the modern partner of 「新聞|しんぶん」, newly heard, and pairs with 「ニュースによると」 plus 「そうです」 to report what you heard.",
      "News comes from the plural of new, fresh information, not north, east, west and south, which is a myth; Japanese 「新聞|しんぶん」 was built on newness too.",
      "You watch it with 「見る|みる」 or hear it with 「聞く|きく」, and say you saw something on the news with 「で」, as in 「ニュースで見ました|ニュースでみました」; personal news is often 「知らせ|しらせ」.",
      "The news team runs from the on-screen 「アナウンサー」 and 「キャスター」 to the reporter, 「記者|きしゃ」, recording person, who writes a newspaper article, 「記事|きじ」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[ニュース]を{見ます|みます}。",
    en: "I watch the news every morning." },
  "かじ|火事": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「火|か」, fire. Its shape is a little picture of flames. You know it from 「火曜日|かようび」, Tuesday, the fire day of the week, with exactly the same reading. The second is 「事|じ」, thing or matter, an event. You know it from 「用事|ようじ」, an errand or something you have to do. The same kanji also appears in 「仕事|しごと」, work, though there it's read with a different sound. So 「火事|かじ」 is a fire matter, a fire event. That's important. It doesn't mean fire in general, like a campfire or the flame on your stove. It means a fire as an accident, a building burning down. For flames themselves you'd use the native word 「火|ひ」. Japan has a long history with this word. In old Edo, now Tokyo, the city was built of wood and paper and fires were constant. There was even a saying that fires and fights were the flowers of Edo. The heroes who fight them are 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, firefighters, from lesson eleven. Recap: 「火|か」, fire, plus 「事|じ」, matter or event, gives a fire as an accident." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji here belongs to a big family. The fire kanji gives you 「花火|はなび」, fireworks, literally fire flowers, with the native reading softened to 「び」. There's also 「火山|かざん」, a volcano, fire mountain, using the same Chinese-origin reading as in this word. The matter kanji is even busier. You know 「用事|ようじ」, an errand, and 「仕事|しごと」, work. There's also 「大事|だいじ」, important, a big matter, and 「食事|しょくじ」, a meal, an eating matter. And a very close cousin of this word is 「事故|じこ」, an accident, like a car crash. A fire and an accident are both unwanted events, and both use that same kanji. So fire flowers, fire mountains, and fire events, and on the other side errands, meals, and accidents, all built from fire and matter." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you ever see a fire, the shout in Japanese is simply 「火事だ|かじだ」, fire! To call for help, you dial one one nine, which in Japan connects you to both the fire department and ambulances. The police number is different, one one zero. When describing a fire, you usually say it happened or existed, 「火事があった|かじがあった」, or that something turned into a fire, 「火事になった|かじになった」. Here's a short dialogue. 「あ、煙|あ、けむり」, oh, smoke! 「火事かな|かじかな」, is it a fire? 「早く電話して|はやくでんわして」, call quickly! A common mistake is using this word for a cozy fire, like a fireplace or a barbecue. That would sound alarming. For those, you'd use the native word 「火|ひ」. So shout 「火事だ|かじだ」 in an emergency, dial one one nine, and save the word for real, unwanted fires." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "There is a famous old Japanese saying listing the four scariest things in life. 「地震|じしん」, earthquakes. 「雷|かみなり」, thunder. 「火事|かじ」, fires. And 「親父|おやじ」, the old man, meaning dad. It's said with a bit of humor, but it shows how fires were feared, right up there with earthquakes. Another tradition still survives in some neighborhoods. On cold winter nights, volunteers walk the streets clapping two wooden blocks together and calling out 「火の用心|ひのようじん」, beware of fire. The sharp click of the wooden blocks is a classic winter sound in Japan, reminding families to check their heaters and stoves before bed. It goes back to the days when wooden houses could burn down whole districts. So fires sit in the saying of four fears, and in the winter call of beware of fire." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「火|か」, fire, as in 「火曜日|かようび」, plus 「事|じ」, matter or event, makes a fire as an accident, a building burning; flames themselves are the native 「火|ひ」.",
      "Fire gives 「花火|はなび」, fire flowers, and 「火山|かざん」, fire mountain, while the matter kanji gives 「用事|ようじ」, 「食事|しょくじ」 and its close cousin 「事故|じこ」, an accident.",
      "Shout 「火事だ|かじだ」 and dial one one nine, say 「火事があった|かじがあった」 or 「火事になった|かじになった」, and never use it for a cozy fireplace or barbecue.",
      "An old saying lists the four fears as earthquakes, thunder, fires and dad, and on winter nights volunteers clap wooden blocks calling 「火の用心|ひのようじん」, beware of fire."
    ],
    ja: "きのう、{近く|ちかく}で[火事|かじ]がありました。",
    en: "There was a fire nearby yesterday." },
  "りょこうがいしゃ|旅行会社": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is two words you already know, joined together. The first is 「旅行|りょこう」, travel, from lesson five. Its kanji are 「旅|りょ」, journey, and 「行|こう」, go. Going on a journey. The second is 「会社|かいしゃ」, company, from lesson seven. Its kanji are 「会|かい」, meet or gather, and 「社|しゃ」, company. People gathered together to do business. So a travel company, a travel agency. One sound change to notice. When 「会社|かいしゃ」 comes second in a compound, the k sound often softens into g, so it becomes 「がいしゃ」. You'll hear the same thing in other company words. This softening is very common in Japanese compounds and it just makes the word flow more smoothly. Travel agencies are still popular in Japan, even with online booking. Many people, especially families and older travelers, like booking a package 「ツアー」, a tour, through an agency, with the hotel, train, and sometimes even meals all arranged. The verb you'll use with them is 「予約する|よやくする」, to reserve, from lesson fifteen. Recap: 「旅行|りょこう」, travel, plus 「会社|かいしゃ」, company, softened to 「がいしゃ」, gives travel agency." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word is part of a whole pattern. Put a type of business in front of 「会社|かいしゃ」, and the k usually softens, giving 「がいしゃ」. So 「保険会社|ほけんがいしゃ」 is an insurance company, using 「保険|ほけん」, insurance, from lesson fifteen. 「自動車会社|じどうしゃがいしゃ」 is a car company. You can build your own with this pattern. Then there is the travel half. 「旅行|りょこう」 also appears in other useful words. 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」 is a school trip, the big class trip that nearly every Japanese student takes, often to Kyoto or Nara. 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」 is a honeymoon, a newlywed trip. The first kanji also gives you 「旅館|りょかん」, a Japanese-style inn. And the native word 「旅|たび」 means a journey, with a slightly poetic feel. So business plus company makes all kinds of companies, and travel plus a word makes school trips and honeymoons." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how a visit to a travel agency might go. You walk in and the staff greet you with 「いらっしゃいませ」. Then you explain what you want. 「北海道へのツアーはありますか|ほっかいどうへのツアーはありますか」, do you have tours to Hokkaido? The staff will bring out a 「パンフレット」, a brochure, and you'll talk about dates and prices. 「いくらですか」, how much is it? 「三泊四日で、八万円です|さんぱくよっかで、はちまんえんです」, three nights and four days, eighty thousand yen. Notice that Japanese counts trips by nights and days together. The counter 「泊|はく」 is for nights stayed, and you know the 「〜日|か」 days. Then you might say 「これにします」, I'll take this one, and make the booking with 「予約する|よやくする」. So at the agency, ask about tours, count nights and days together, and book with 「予約する|よやくする」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「旅行|りょこう」, travel, plus 「会社|かいしゃ」, company, which softens to 「がいしゃ」 in second position, giving a travel agency, where many people book package 「ツアー」 with 「予約する|よやくする」.",
      "Business plus a softened 「がいしゃ」 makes 「保険会社|ほけんがいしゃ」 and 「自動車会社|じどうしゃがいしゃ」, while travel builds 「修学旅行|しゅうがくりょこう」, a school trip, and 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」, a honeymoon.",
      "At the agency ask 「北海道へのツアーはありますか|ほっかいどうへのツアーはありますか」, notice that trips are counted by nights and days together, as in 「三泊四日|さんぱくよっか」, then book with 「予約する|よやくする」."
    ],
    ja: "[旅行会社|りょこうがいしゃ]でツアーを{予約しました|よやくしました}。",
    en: "I booked a tour at a travel agency." },
  "ショッピングモール|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A loanword built from two English words, shopping and mall. Let's look at each. Shopping becomes 「ショッピング」. The sh plus o becomes 「ショ」, the doubled p is marked with the small 「ッ」, a little pause, and the ing ending becomes 「イング」, because Japanese can't end on a bare ng sound. Mall becomes 「モール」. The English vowel turns into a long o, and the l becomes the Japanese r. Put together it's long, nine beats. 「ショッピング」 on its own is also used in Japanese to mean going shopping for fun, browsing and buying. You already have a native word for shopping from lesson four, 「買い物|かいもの」, literally buying things. That's the everyday one, used for groceries too. 「ショッピング」 has a more leisurely, fashionable feel. Big suburban malls became popular in Japan more recently than in America, and they're now common weekend destinations, often with cinemas, food courts, and supermarkets under one roof. Train stations in big cities also have huge shopping areas built right in. Recap: shopping, with ing becoming 「イング」, plus mall, becoming 「モール」, gives shopping mall." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "This is a long word, so let's count the beats properly. Japanese counts rhythm by even beats, and small sounds get a full beat too. 「ショ」 is one beat, even though it's written with two letters. The small 「ッ」 is a beat of silence, a tiny pause before the p. Then 「ピ」, 「ン」, and 「グ」. That's five beats for shopping. Then 「モ」, the long held vowel, and 「ル」, three more beats for mall. So the whole word is eight even beats. The trickiest part for English speakers is that little pause. If you skip it, the word sounds rushed and wrong. Try saying it slowly with a clap on each beat, including a silent clap for the pause. Because it's so long, people often just say 「モール」 in casual talk. So eight beats in all, with a silent beat for the small 「ッ」 and a long one in mall." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, big shopping malls are mostly in the suburbs, and people drive to them on weekends with the whole family. A mall day often means shopping, lunch at the food court, a movie, and groceries before heading home. In big cities, though, you'll find something a little different. Many large train stations have huge shopping buildings right on top of them, called 「駅ビル|えきビル」, station buildings. And department stores have famous basement food floors, called 「デパ地下|デパちか」, short for department store underground, where you can buy beautiful sweets and ready-made dishes. A weekend conversation might go 「週末、何するの|しゅうまつ、なにするの」, what are you doing this weekend? 「家族でショッピングモールに行くよ|かぞくでショッピングモールにいくよ」, going to the mall with my family. So suburban families go to the mall, while city people shop in station buildings." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The two halves of this word each make other compounds. Shopping appears in 「ネットショッピング」, online shopping, very popular in Japan, and 「ショッピングセンター」, a shopping center, often a slightly smaller or older version of a mall. A shopping street, a covered arcade of small local shops, is a native word, 「商店街|しょうてんがい」, from 「商店|しょうてん」, shop, and 「街|がい」, street. These traditional shopping streets were the heart of many towns before big malls arrived, and some are still lively today. On the mall side, Japanese uses 「モール」 mostly for big shopping complexes. And the word for the shopping itself, 「買い物|かいもの」, is used whatever the place. So shopping online, shopping centers, and old shopping streets, plus 「買い物|かいもの」 for the act itself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Shopping becomes 「ショッピング」, with a small 「ッ」 pause and ing turned into 「イング」, and mall becomes 「モール」; it's more leisurely and fashionable than the everyday 「買い物|かいもの」.",
      "Count eight even beats, five for shopping and three for mall, and don't skip the silent beat of the small 「ッ」; casually people just say 「モール」.",
      "Suburban families drive to malls on weekends, while city people shop in 「駅ビル|えきビル」, buildings on top of stations, and the famous basement food floors, 「デパ地下|デパちか」.",
      "Shopping builds 「ネットショッピング」 and 「ショッピングセンター」, while the traditional covered arcade of local shops is the native 「商店街|しょうてんがい」, and 「買い物|かいもの」 covers the act anywhere."
    ],
    ja: "[ショッピングモール]で{買い物|かいもの}をしました。",
    en: "I went shopping at the mall." },
  "りょう|寮": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A single kanji with its Chinese-origin reading. Look at the top of 「寮|りょう」. You'll see the roof part, the same one on top of 「家|いえ」, house, and 「客|きゃく」, guest. Lots of kanji about buildings and homes carry that roof. So you know straight away this is a place where people live. The rest of the kanji gives the sound, and you don't need to unpack it. 「寮|りょう」 is a dormitory, a shared building where people live together. In Japan it isn't only for students. Universities have student dorms, of course, but many companies also run dorms for young employees, often called company dorms, where new workers live cheaply for their first years. Some high schools, especially sports schools, have dorms too. Life in a Japanese dorm can come with rules, like curfews, set meal times, and shared baths. Use 「に住む|にすむ」 for living there: 「寮に住んでいます|りょうにすんでいます」, I live in a dorm. That uses 「住む|すむ」 from lesson seven. Its neighbors in your vocabulary are 「アパート」 and 「マンション」 from lesson thirteen. Recap: roof on top for a building, read 「りょう」, a shared dormitory for students or company workers." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Under the roof, this kanji has a sound part that you'll meet again in other kanji, all read 「りょう」. Take away the roof and add the person part on the left, and you get the kanji in 「同僚|どうりょう」, a coworker, someone you work alongside. Put the sickness part around it instead, and you get the kanji in 「治療|ちりょう」, medical treatment. Different meanings, but the same sound, because they share that same lower piece. This is a common pattern. Kanji that share a sound part often share a reading, even when their meanings are unrelated. So when you see that piece again, a good guess is 「りょう」. For this word, the roof on top tells you it's about a building where people live, and the rest tells you how to say it. So roof plus a sound part makes dormitory, and that sound part also shows up in coworker and treatment." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Dorms come in different types, and Japanese builds them by putting a word in front. 「学生寮|がくせいりょう」 is a student dorm, using 「学生|がくせい」, student, which you know from lesson one. 「社員寮|しゃいんりょう」 is a company dorm for employees, from 「社員|しゃいん」, a staff member. A person who lives in a dorm is a 「寮生|りょうせい」, and the monthly dorm fee is 「寮費|りょうひ」. Compare other places to live. An older type of student lodging is 「下宿|げしゅく」, a room rented in someone's home, often with meals. And you know 「アパート」, a modest apartment, and 「マンション」, a newer, nicer apartment building. Each has a different feel and price. So student dorm, company dorm, dorm residents, and dorm fees, all built on this one kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Life in a Japanese dorm often comes with rules, and one word you'll hear a lot is 「門限|もんげん」, curfew, the time the gate closes at night. If you come back late, you might be in trouble with the dorm manager. Many dorms also have shared meals in a dining hall, 「食堂|しょくどう」, and shared baths, so you get to know your neighbors quickly. Here's a short dialogue between students. 「どこに住んでいるの|どこにすんでいるの」, where do you live? 「大学の寮だよ|だいがくのりょうだよ」, in the university dorm. 「門限はある|もんげんはある」, is there a curfew? 「うん、十一時|うん、じゅういちじ」, yes, eleven o'clock. People often say dorm life is cheap and fun but a bit strict. And company dorms help new workers save money while they start their careers. So dorms in Japan mean curfews, shared meals, and close neighbors, for students and young workers alike." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「寮|りょう」 has the roof part, so it's a building where people live, a dormitory, not only for students but also for young company workers; say 「寮に住んでいます|りょうにすんでいます」.",
      "Its lower part is a sound piece read 「りょう」, which also appears in 「同僚|どうりょう」, coworker, and 「治療|ちりょう」, treatment, so shared parts often mean shared readings.",
      "Put a word in front for 「学生寮|がくせいりょう」, student dorm, or 「社員寮|しゃいんりょう」, company dorm; a resident is a 「寮生|りょうせい」 and the fee is 「寮費|りょうひ」.",
      "Dorm life means a curfew, 「門限|もんげん」, shared meals in the 「食堂|しょくどう」 and shared baths, so it's cheap and fun but a bit strict."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは[寮|りょう]に{住んで|すんで}います。",
    en: "I live in a dormitory." },
  "ちがい|違い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This noun comes straight from a verb you've heard many times, 「違う|ちがう」, to be different, or to be wrong. Think of the classic reply 「ちがいます」, no, that's not right. Japanese often makes a noun by taking the verb's stem, the part before 「ます」. 「ちがいます」 gives 「ちがい」, a difference. You've already seen this trick elsewhere. 「休みます|やすみます」 gives 「休み|やすみ」, a break or holiday. 「買い物|かいもの」, shopping, is built from 「買い|かい」, the stem of buy. So once you know the verb, the noun comes for free. The kanji 「違|ちが」 is read with its native reading here. Notice the part along its bottom and left side. That's the movement part, the one that runs under kanji like 「道|みち」, road, and 「近い|ちかい」, near. In this kanji the idea is things going different ways. The typical pattern puts two things before it with 「と」 and 「の」: 「日本とアメリカの違い|にほんとアメリカのちがい」, the difference between Japan and America. You'll use it when comparing cultures, words, or products. Recap: the stem of 「違う|ちがう」, to differ, used as a noun, gives difference." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Once you know 「違う|ちがう」, a whole family opens up. The most useful member is 「間違い|まちがい」, a mistake. The first kanji, 「間|ま」, is the one in 「時間|じかん」, but here it has its native reading 「ま」. So a mistake is literally a gap that went the wrong way. Its verb is 「間違える|まちがえる」, to get something wrong, as in 「漢字を間違えました|かんじをまちがえました」, I wrote the kanji wrong. Then there's 「大違い|おおちがい」, a huge difference, with 「大|おお」, big, on the front. Notice how the 「ち」 stays clear and doesn't change. And there's 「勘違い|かんちがい」, a misunderstanding, when your gut feeling, 「勘|かん」, pointed you the wrong way. You thought the party was on Friday, but it was Saturday. That's 「勘違い|かんちがい」. You'll also meet the same kanji read 「い」 in formal words like 「違反|いはん」, a violation, on traffic signs and in rule books. So one shape gives you difference, mistake, big difference, and misunderstanding. All of them are about things not lining up." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here are a few ways the noun works in a sentence. To say there is a difference, use 「違いがある|ちがいがある」. 「意味に違いがあります|いみにちがいがあります」, there is a difference in meaning. To say there's none, 「違いはありません|ちがいはありません」. To say you can tell two things apart, use 「分かる|わかる」: 「違いが分かります|ちがいがわかります」, I can tell the difference. That phrase is famous in Japan from an old instant coffee advertisement about a man who knows the difference, and people still joke with it. Later in your studies you'll meet a pattern built from this same noun, 「に違いない|にちがいない」. Literally it says there is no difference from this, and it means must be, without a doubt. 「あの人は先生に違いない|あのひとはせんせいにちがいない」, that person must be a teacher. You don't need to use it yet, but when you hear it, you'll know where it comes from. So the noun goes with 「ある」, 「ない」 and 「分かる|わかる」, and it hides inside one of the strongest ways to say I'm sure." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick, purely for fun. Picture two identical twin brothers standing side by side at a party. Everyone says they look exactly the same. But you know the secret. One of them is a cheeky guy. Chee guy. He has a tiny grin the other doesn't have. Every time someone mixes them up, the cheeky one winks at you and whispers, spot the difference. So whenever you hear 「ちがい」, picture that cheeky guy, the one small detail that makes two similar things different. You can even hear the verb in it. When someone calls the cheeky twin by his brother's name, he shakes his head and says 「ちがいます」, nope, wrong guy. Cheeky guy, the difference. A silly picture, but it ties the sound straight to the meaning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the stem of 「違う|ちがう」, to differ, used as a noun, just as 「休み|やすみ」 comes from 「休みます|やすみます」; compare with 「日本とアメリカの違い|にほんとアメリカのちがい」.",
      "The family includes 「間違い|まちがい」, a mistake, 「大違い|おおちがい」, a huge difference, and 「勘違い|かんちがい」, a misunderstanding when your gut pointed the wrong way.",
      "Use it with 「ある」, 「ない」 and 「分かる|わかる」, as in 「違いが分かります|ちがいがわかります」, and later it hides inside 「に違いない|にちがいない」, must be.",
      "Picture identical twins, one a cheeky guy with a tiny grin, the one small difference, who shakes his head with 「ちがいます」 when called by his brother's name."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}とアメリカの[違い|ちがい]は{何|なん}ですか。",
    en: "What is the difference between Japan and America?" },
  "ひみつ|秘密": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「秘|ひ」, hidden or secret. It's the idea of keeping something out of sight. The second is 「密|みつ」, which means dense, close, or tightly shut. Think of things packed together so tightly that nothing gets out. Put them together and 「秘密|ひみつ」 is something hidden and tightly kept, a secret. Both kanji point the same way, which is very common in Japanese compounds, two similar meanings doubled up to make the idea strong and clear. Now usage. The most common sentence is simply 「これは秘密です|これはひみつです」, this is a secret. To keep something secret you can say 「秘密にする|ひみつにする」, make it a secret. Children often say it with a finger to their lips, very much like in English. A friendly memory hook, and it is just a hook: picture someone whispering he, me, too, as they share a secret, he knows, me too. You'll also hear a cozier word, 「ないしょ」, in casual talk, meaning hush-hush or between us. Recap: 「秘|ひ」, hidden, plus 「密|みつ」, tightly closed, gives a secret." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at both kanji and you'll find a shared piece. In 「秘|ひ」, the right side is 「必|ひつ」, the kanji in 「必ず|かならず」, certainly. In 「密|みつ」, the same 「必|ひつ」 sits in the middle, with a roof on top and a mountain underneath. Hearing 「ひつ」 and 「みつ」 side by side, you can sense the shared component helping with the sound. The left side of 「秘|ひ」 today is the grain part, the one in many farming kanji. But older texts often wrote this kanji with the altar part instead, the one that appears in 「神|かみ」, god. That older form fits the meaning nicely, things of the gods, hidden from ordinary eyes. Both forms are real, and the grain version is simply the one used today. In 「密|みつ」, the roof on top gives a feeling of something closed in and covered. So the two kanji share the 「必|ひつ」 piece, one kept from view and one shut in tight. Knowing that, the pair becomes much easier to write." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here's a lovely coincidence between languages. 「秘書|ひしょ」 means a secretary, someone who handles the private papers, literally secret writing. And the English word secretary comes from the Latin word for a trusted person who keeps secrets. Both languages saw the job the same way. Now the second kanji. 「密|みつ」 means dense or close, and it appears in 「親密|しんみつ」, intimate, very close friends. In mystery novels you'll meet 「密室|みっしつ」, a locked room, the classic crime where no one could get in or out. The small 「っ」 appears because 「みつ」 shortens before 「し」. And during the pandemic, Japan used the phrase 「三密|さんみつ」, the three dense things to avoid: closed spaces, crowded places, and close contact. Everyone in Japan knows that word now. So 「秘|ひ」 brings the hidden side, and 「密|みつ」 brings the packed, close side. Put them in your memory together with secretary and locked room." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Secrets come with a set of everyday verbs. To keep one, you say 「秘密を守る|ひみつをまもる」, literally to protect the secret. To spill it, people often say 「秘密をばらす|ひみつをばらす」, a casual, slightly dramatic verb for blabbing. A sweet phrase is 「二人だけの秘密|ふたりだけのひみつ」, a secret just between the two of us. Children love 「秘密基地|ひみつきち」, a secret base, the hideout they build in the woods or under the stairs. Here's a tiny dialogue between friends. One says 「これ、秘密だよ|これ、ひみつだよ」, this is a secret, okay? The other answers 「うん、だれにも言わない|うん、だれにもいわない」, sure, I won't tell anyone. Notice how often the softer word 「ないしょ」 replaces it in this kind of chat. 「ないしょにしてね」, keep it between us, sounds warmer and more playful. 「秘密|ひみつ」 feels a little bigger and more serious, the word for real secrets, spies, and mysteries. Use 「ないしょ」 for gossip, and save 「秘密|ひみつ」 for the big stuff." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「秘|ひ」 is hidden and 「密|みつ」 is tightly shut, two similar meanings doubled into a secret; keep one with 「秘密にする|ひみつにする」, while 「ないしょ」 is the cozier casual word.",
      "Both kanji share 「必|ひつ」, helping the sounds 「ひ」 and 「みつ」, and 「秘|ひ」 was once written with the altar part, things of the gods hidden from view.",
      "「秘書|ひしょ」, secretary, echoes English, where a secretary keeps secrets too, and 「密|みつ」 gives 「親密|しんみつ」, 「密室|みっしつ」, a locked room, and the pandemic's 「三密|さんみつ」.",
      "Keep one with 「秘密を守る|ひみつをまもる」 and blab with 「秘密をばらす|ひみつをばらす」, but use 「ないしょ」 for gossip and save 「秘密|ひみつ」 for big secrets."
    ],
    ja: "これは[秘密|ひみつ]です。",
    en: "This is a secret." },
  "じゅんび|準備": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「準|じゅん」. It means a standard or a level, the idea of getting things lined up to the right level. The second is 「備|び」. It means to prepare or to equip, to have things ready in advance. On its left side you'll see the person part, the slim standing person. So together, 「準備|じゅんび」 is getting everything up to standard and ready. Preparation. It's a noun, but like many Chinese-origin nouns it becomes a verb with 「する」. 「準備する|じゅんびする」, to prepare. The usual pattern is thing plus 「の準備|のじゅんび」: 「パーティーの準備|パーティーのじゅんび」, preparation for the party, 「試験の準備|しけんのじゅんび」, preparing for an exam. When everything is ready, people say 「準備ができました|じゅんびができました」, the preparation is done. A common mistake for English speakers is using 「準備|じゅんび」 for small, quick actions. For little things like getting dressed, Japanese often just uses the action verb. 「準備|じゅんび」 feels like real planning, getting set for an event. Recap: 「準|じゅん」, up to standard, plus 「備|び」, ready in advance, gives preparation." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has three common words for getting ready, and they feel different. 「準備|じゅんび」 is the big one, preparation for an event, a trip, an exam. 「用意|ようい」 is about having the things ready at hand. 「飲み物を用意する|のみものをよういする」, to have drinks ready. You also hear it at the start of a race: 「よーい、ドン」, ready, go. Then 「支度|したく」 is personal getting ready, like dressing to go out or making dinner. 「出かける支度|でかけるしたく」, getting ready to head out. Now the kanji cousins. 「準|じゅん」 appears in 「基準|きじゅん」, a standard, and in 「準決勝|じゅんけっしょう」, the semifinal, the match just below the final. And 「備|び」 gives the native verb 「備える|そなえる」, to prepare for something like an earthquake, and 「予備|よび」, a spare. So 「準備|じゅんび」 is the planner's word, 「用意|ようい」 is the stuff, and 「支度|したく」 is you getting yourself together." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Walk past a Japanese restaurant before it opens and you'll see a small sign on the door: 「準備中|じゅんびちゅう」. Literally it means in the middle of preparation. In practice it means closed, come back later. When the sign flips, it reads 「営業中|えいぎょうちゅう」, open for business. That little sign is one of the first kanji compounds many visitors learn. In schools, before P.E. class, students do 「準備運動|じゅんびうんどう」, warm-up exercises, often stretching together in neat rows. Among friends, the quick question before heading out is 「準備はいい？|じゅんびはいい？」, are you ready? The answer might be 「うん、いいよ」, yep, all set, or 「まだ！ちょっと待って|まだ！ちょっとまって」, not yet, wait a sec. You will also hear people say the English word okay right after it, as a quick check. So keep an eye out for that door sign, and you'll know whether lunch is happening." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "There are two ways to build a sentence with this verb, and they mean slightly different things. With 「の準備をする|のじゅんびをする」, you name the event you're preparing for. 「旅行の準備をする|りょこうのじゅんびをする」, to prepare for the trip. With 「を準備する|をじゅんびする」, you name the actual things you get ready. 「お弁当を準備する|おべんとうをじゅんびする」, to prepare the lunch boxes. So the trip is the goal, and the lunch boxes are the items. This word also pairs beautifully with a pattern from lesson fifteen, the te-form plus 「おく」, doing something in advance. 「準備しておきます|じゅんびしておきます」, I'll get it ready ahead of time. Since preparation is all about doing things beforehand, those two belong together. And when you're done, 「準備ができた|じゅんびができた」 tells everyone it's finished. So remember: event with 「の」, items with 「を」, and add 「ておく」 for in advance." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「準|じゅん」 is a standard or level and 「備|び」 is ready in advance, so it's getting everything up to standard; it's real planning for an event, as in 「パーティーの準備|パーティーのじゅんび」.",
      "「準備|じゅんび」 is the planner's word, 「用意|ようい」 is having the things at hand, and 「支度|したく」 is getting yourself together, like dressing to head out.",
      "A restaurant door sign reading 「準備中|じゅんびちゅう」 means closed, come back later, and before going out friends check with 「準備はいい？|じゅんびはいい？」.",
      "Name the event with 「の準備をする|のじゅんびをする」, the items with 「を準備する|をじゅんびする」, and add 「ておく」 for in advance, as in 「準備しておきます|じゅんびしておきます」."
    ],
    ja: "パーティーの[準備|じゅんび]をしています。",
    en: "I'm preparing for the party." },
  "じぶん|自分": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「自|じ」, self. Here's a real and lovely detail. This kanji started as a picture of a nose. In Japan, when people point to themselves, they point at their own nose, not their chest. So the nose came to mean me, myself. You already know this kanji from 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, bicycle, literally a self-turning-wheel vehicle, something you power yourself. The second kanji is 「分|ぶん」, a part or portion. You know it from 「分かる|わかる」, to understand, and from minutes, 「分|ふん」. So 「自分|じぶん」 is one's own portion, oneself. You learned 「自分で|じぶんで」, by oneself, in lesson sixteen. 「自分の|じぶんの」 means one's own, as in 「自分の部屋|じぶんのへや」, one's own room. One thing to watch. 「自分|じぶん」 is not exactly I. It refers back to whoever the sentence is about, so it can mean myself, yourself, or himself. Recap: 「自|じ」, the nose, meaning self, plus 「分|ぶん」, portion, gives oneself." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word are busy members of big families. 「自|じ」 sometimes reads 「し」 instead. 「自然|しぜん」, nature, is things being as they are by themselves. 「自由|じゆう」, freedom, is acting from yourself. 「自動|じどう」, automatic, is moving by itself, which gives 「自動販売機|じどうはんばいき」, the vending machines you see on every corner in Japan. There's even a native reading, 「自ら|みずから」, personally, oneself, which you'll meet in newspapers. Then 「分|ぶん」 shifts its sound in different words. It's 「ぶん」 in 「半分|はんぶん」, half, 「ふん」 in 「五分|ごふん」, five minutes, and 「ぷん」 in 「十分|じっぷん」, ten minutes. With the native reading it becomes 「分かる|わかる」, where understanding is like dividing a problem into clear parts. In 「自分|じぶん」, it's your own share, your own portion of the world. So 「自|じ」 is the by-itself kanji, and 「分|ぶん」 is the share kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a couple of surprises in real life. In the Kansai region, around Osaka, people often use 「自分|じぶん」 to mean you. A friend might ask 「自分、何してんの？|じぶん、なにしてんの？」, what are you doing? That confuses learners from Tokyo textbooks every time. Some men in strict settings, like sports teams or the old-style military, use 「自分|じぶん」 as a very stiff, humble way to say I. You'll hear it in movies and from athletes in interviews. In everyday standard Japanese, though, it usually means oneself. A useful expression is 「自分勝手|じぶんかって」, selfish, doing whatever suits yourself. Another is 「自分らしい|じぶんらしい」, being true to yourself, a phrase you'll see in ads and self-help books. And parents tell kids 「自分でやりなさい|じぶんでやりなさい」, do it yourself. So listen for who is speaking and where. The same word can mean oneself, I, or even you." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's how 「自分|じぶん」 points back in a sentence. It usually refers to the subject, the person doing the main action. Take 「田中さんは自分の車を洗いました|たなかさんはじぶんのくるまをあらいました」, Tanaka washed his own car. Who owns the car? Tanaka. Now compare 「田中さんは山田さんに自分の話をしました|たなかさんはやまださんにじぶんのはなしをしました」. Even though Yamada is in the sentence, 「自分|じぶん」 most naturally points to Tanaka, the one doing the talking. So Tanaka talked about himself. This is why it's so flexible in English translation: it can be myself, yourself, herself, or themselves, depending on who the subject is. A handy contrast is with 「わたしの」. If you say 「自分の部屋|じぶんのへや」 in a question to a child, like 「自分の部屋をそうじした？|じぶんのへやをそうじした？」, it means your own room, not mine. So find the subject first, and that's who 「自分|じぶん」 is." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「自|じ」 began as a picture of a nose, since Japanese people point to their nose for me, plus 「分|ぶん」, portion, so one's own portion, oneself, pointing back to whoever the sentence is about.",
      "「自|じ」 is the by-itself kanji in 「自然|しぜん」, 「自由|じゆう」 and 「自動|じどう」, while 「分|ぶん」 is the share kanji, shifting to 「ふん」 and 「ぷん」 for minutes.",
      "In Kansai 「自分|じぶん」 often means you, some men in strict settings use it as a stiff I, and 「自分勝手|じぶんかって」 means selfish.",
      "It refers back to the subject, so in a sentence about Tanaka and Yamada, 「自分の|じぶんの」 points to Tanaka, the one doing the action; find the subject first."
    ],
    ja: "[自分|じぶん]の{部屋|へや}をそうじします。",
    en: "I clean my own room." },
  "あぶない|危ない": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「危|あぶ」, read with its native reading, followed by 「ない」. That ending is a trap for learners. It looks like the negative 「ない」, but it isn't. 「あぶない」 is just a regular i-adjective, like 「すくない」, few, also in this lesson. There's no positive form hiding inside it. The kanji means danger. A memory hook, and this is a hook, not a history lesson: look at the shape and picture a person crouching right at the edge of a cliff, with another person hunched below. Someone close to falling. That's danger. Now usage. Japanese uses 「あぶない」 in two ways. First, to describe something risky: 「この道は危ないです|このみちはあぶないです」, this road is dangerous. Second, as a quick shout, like watch out. If someone is about to step in front of a bike, you shout 「あぶない！」, and it works exactly like the English warning. You'll also see the formal word 「危険|きけん」 on signs, with the same first kanji. Recap: the person at the cliff's edge, a normal adjective despite the 「ない」, used both for risky things and as a shout of watch out." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The same kanji shows up with different readings in some words you'll meet. With its Chinese-origin reading 「き」, it gives 「危険|きけん」, danger, the stiff word you see on warning signs, often written in katakana as 「キケン」 for impact. It also gives 「危機|きき」, a crisis, a word from the news. With another native reading, you get 「危うく|あやうく」, nearly, by a hair. 「危うく遅れるところでした|あやうくおくれるところでした」, I very nearly missed it. So the kanji covers danger, crisis, and close calls. Now compare 「危ない|あぶない」 with 「危険|きけん」. The adjective is everyday and personal, the thing you'd say to a friend or child. 「危険|きけん」 is official and formal, the word on a construction fence or in a safety manual. You wouldn't shout 「きけん！」 at a friend stepping into traffic. You'd shout 「あぶない！」. So keep 「あぶない」 for the street and 「きけん」 for the signs." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This adjective stretches further than dangerous. The past form, 「危なかった|あぶなかった」, is what you say after a close call. You catch a falling glass just in time and breathe out, 「あぶなかった！」, phew, that was close. It also means risky in a softer, everyday sense. A student worried about failing might say 「数学の試験が危ない|すうがくのしけんがあぶない」, I'm in danger of failing math. A shaky plan, a sketchy job, a suspicious person, all of them can be 「危ない|あぶない」. 「危ない人|あぶないひと」 sounds like someone you'd want to keep away from. In fast casual speech, especially among young men, it gets clipped to 「あぶね！」 or 「あぶねえ」. Parents use it constantly with small children: 「走ったら危ないよ|はしったらあぶないよ」, it's dangerous to run. So the word moves from real danger to near misses to I might fail. Listen for the past tense especially, because that phew moment happens every day." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Despite the 「ない」, it's a regular adjective, not a negative; picture a person at a cliff's edge, and use it for risky things or shouted as watch out.",
      "Read 「き」, the kanji gives the formal 「危険|きけん」 on warning signs and 「危機|きき」, crisis, but you'd shout 「あぶない！」 at a friend, never 「きけん！」.",
      "After a close call you say 「あぶなかった！」, phew, that was close, and it softens to risky, as in 「数学の試験が危ない|すうがくのしけんがあぶない」, I might fail math."
    ],
    ja: "この{道|みち}は[危ない|あぶない]です。",
    en: "This road is dangerous." },
  "うらやましい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A native word, usually written in hiragana. Dictionaries trace it back to an old word 「うら」, meaning the heart or one's inner feelings, and 「やむ」, to be sick. So, roughly, a sick feeling in the heart. That's a vivid picture of envy, that little ache you feel when someone has what you want. Now the important grammar point. In Japanese, feelings like envy are usually about the speaker. 「うらやましい」 means I'm envious. If you want to say someone else is envious, Japanese uses a different form, 「うらやましがる」, to act envious. For now just use it about yourself. The thing you envy is marked with 「が」: 「友だちがうらやましい|ともだちがうらやましい」, I envy my friend. It's a very natural, friendly reaction in conversation. If your friend says they won the lottery, 「宝くじに当たりました|たからくじにあたりました」, you can say 「いいなあ、うらやましい」, oh, lucky you, I'm so jealous. It isn't bitter or rude. It's a warm way to show you're impressed. Recap: from an old phrase about a sick heart, used about your own feelings, and often paired with 「いいなあ」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words in the envy and jealousy area, and 「うらやましい」 is the lightest. Its verb partner is 「うらやむ」, to envy, used in writing and stories. Then there's 「ねたむ」, which is darker, envy mixed with resentment, wishing the other person didn't have it. 「嫉妬|しっと」 is jealousy, often romantic, the feeling when your partner talks to someone else. And there's a cute expression for that same romantic jealousy: 「やきもちを焼く|やきもちをやく」. 「やきもち」 is a grilled rice cake, which puffs up and swells as it toasts, so to grill a rice cake means to get jealous. So when you say 「うらやましい」, you're on the friendly end. It's admiration with a little wishing, the kind you say out loud with a smile. 「ねたむ」 and 「嫉妬|しっと」 are the ones people usually keep to themselves. If you remember just one, keep 「うらやましい」. It's the safe, social one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how this word lives in conversation. Your coworker says 「来週、ハワイに行きます|らいしゅう、ハワイにいきます」, I'm going to Hawaii next week. You answer 「えー、うらやましい！」, oh, I'm so envious! Then the polite move for them is to play it down. They might say 「いえいえ、仕事ですよ|いえいえ、しごとですよ」, no, no, it's for work. Being modest after hearing 「うらやましい」 is the natural rhythm. You can name exactly what you envy with 「の」: 「田中さんの声がうらやましい|たなかさんのこえがうらやましい」, I envy Tanaka's voice. There is a kanji for this word, but it's hard and not on the common list for learners, so hiragana is normal. Among young people you might even hear it chopped short as 「うらやま」. One mistake to avoid is using it as a complaint. It's not sour. It's closer to wow, I wish. So say it freely when friends share good news." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick, not real history. Split the sound into 「うら」 and 「やま」. In Japanese, 「うら」 can mean the back side, and 「やま」 is a mountain. Picture yourself stuck on the shady back side of a mountain, cold and damp, eating a soggy sandwich. Over the ridge you can see your friend sitting on the sunny front side, with a hot bowl of ramen, a view of the sea, and a dog wagging its tail. You stand in the shadows and sigh, I want that. That aching little sigh from the back of the mountain is 「うらやましい」. Then add the 「しい」 ending, the sound of your sigh, shee. Back-mountain-sigh. It's just a picture to hold the sound, but once you see yourself in that chilly shadow looking at your lucky friend, the word sticks." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It traces back to 「うら」, the heart, and 「やむ」, to be sick, a sick heart; it's about your own feelings, with what you envy marked by 「が」, and often paired with 「いいなあ」.",
      "It's the lightest, friendliest envy word, unlike darker 「ねたむ」 or romantic 「嫉妬|しっと」, which is also 「やきもちを焼く|やきもちをやく」, to grill a swelling rice cake.",
      "When a coworker shares good news, say 「えー、うらやましい！」, and they'll modestly play it down; it's not sour, it's closer to wow, I wish.",
      "Split it into 「うら」, back side, and 「やま」, mountain: you're stuck on the shady back of a mountain, sighing at your friend on the sunny side."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}が[うらやましい]です。",
    en: "I envy my friend." },
  "すくない|少ない": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「少|すく」, with its native reading, then the adjective ending. Look closely at the shape. It's the kanji for small, the one in 「小さい|ちいさい」, with one extra slanting stroke at the bottom. Small and few look almost the same, which makes sense. The same kanji appears in 「少し|すこし」, a little, a very common word. Its opposite is 「多い|おおい」, many or a lot, from lesson twelve. These two are a pair you'll use all the time. Now a common mistake English speakers make. In English you can say few people or a little money, putting the word right before the noun. In Japanese, 「少ない|すくない」 sounds odd directly in front of a noun like that. Instead, put it at the end of the sentence: 「人が少ないです|ひとがすくないです」, there are few people. Same with 「多い|おおい」. The pattern is thing plus 「が」, then 「少ない|すくない」. And don't let the 「ない」 fool you. Like 「あぶない」, it's just a normal adjective, not a negative. Recap: the kanji for small plus one stroke gives few, used at the end of the sentence, the opposite of 「多い|おおい」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a pair that trips up almost everyone. 「少ない|すくない」, few, and 「少し|すこし」, a little, use the same kanji, but the vowel changes. One is 「すく」 and the other is 「すこ」. Try saying them back to back, slowly. 「すくない」, 「すこし」. A tip is to link the sounds to the job each word does. 「すこし」 is a soft adverb you can put almost anywhere: 「すこし待ってください|すこしまってください」, please wait a little. 「すくない」 is a judgment at the end of a sentence: there are few. The same kanji also has a Chinese-origin reading, 「しょう」. You'll meet it in 「少年|しょうねん」, a boy, and 「少女|しょうじょ」, a girl, literally few years, young. And in shops you'll hear 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, please wait a moment, the formal version of 「すこし」. So that one shape gives you 「すく」, 「すこ」, and 「しょう」. Keep the vowels straight and you're set." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adjective conjugates like any other i-adjective, even with that sneaky 「ない」 at the end. The past is 「少なかった|すくなかった」, there were few. The negative is 「少なくない|すくなくない」, not few, which actually means quite a lot. That double 「ない」 sounds funny, but it's perfectly correct. Comparisons work too. 「東京より人が少ない|とうきょうよりひとがすくない」, there are fewer people than in Tokyo. And it can describe a noun when it comes inside a little clause with its own 「が」. 「休みが少ない仕事|やすみがすくないしごと」, a job with few days off. That sounds natural, even though putting it bare in front of a noun usually doesn't. One more gem: 「少なくとも|すくなくとも」 means at least. 「少なくとも三時間かかります|すくなくともさんじかんかかります」, it takes at least three hours. So the 「ない」 ending behaves like any adjective ending, and the word gives you at least as a bonus." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick to hold the sound. Picture a huge school building on the first day of term. The bell rings, the doors open, and in walks exactly one student, holding a tiny lunch box. The principal looks around the giant empty hallway and sighs. School, nai. School, but no one in it. Hardly any kids at all. That empty, echoing school is 「すくない」, few. To make it even sillier, picture the principal pointing at the kanji on the banner over the door, the one that looks like small with an extra stroke trailing off, like the last student wandering away. Few kids, small school, nearly nobody. Whenever you need the word for few, walk back into that lonely school and hear the principal sigh." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「少|すく」 is the kanji for small plus one extra stroke, the opposite of 「多い|おおい」; put it at the end, as in 「人が少ないです|ひとがすくないです」, not bare before a noun.",
      "「少ない|すくない」 and 「少し|すこし」 share a kanji but change the vowel, and the Chinese reading 「しょう」 gives 「少年|しょうねん」 and 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」.",
      "It conjugates like any i-adjective, so 「少なくない|すくなくない」 means quite a lot, it can describe a noun inside a clause with 「が」, and 「少なくとも|すくなくとも」 means at least.",
      "Picture a giant school on the first day where exactly one student walks in, and the principal sighs school, nai, hardly anyone at all."
    ],
    ja: "この{町|まち}は{人|ひと}が[少ない|すくない]です。",
    en: "There are few people in this town." },
  "つごうがわるい|都合が悪い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a phrase made of a noun and an adjective you already know. The noun is 「都合|つごう」, circumstances, how things fit for you. Its first kanji is 「都|つ」. Normally it means capital or big city, as in Tokyo's official name, but in this word it has an unusual short reading, 「つ」. The second kanji is 「合|ごう」, to fit or match, the same kanji as in 「合う|あう」, to fit, and 「似合う|にあう」, to suit. So 「都合|つごう」 is how things fit together, your situation or schedule. Then comes 「悪い|わるい」, bad, from lesson twelve. Bad fit, bad timing. So the phrase means it's inconvenient for me, or I have a scheduling conflict. Its opposite is 「都合がいい|つごうがいい」, convenient. This phrase is gold for polite Japanese. When you want to decline an invitation, you don't usually say no directly. You say 「その日はちょっと都合が悪いです|そのひはちょっとつごうがわるいです」, that day is a little inconvenient. Everyone understands it as a soft no. Recap: 「都合|つごう」, how things fit, plus 「悪い|わるい」, bad, gives inconvenient, the polite way to say I can't make it." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The first kanji here, 「都|つ」, is a real chameleon. Its main reading is 「と」. You see it in 「東京都|とうきょうと」, the official name for Tokyo as a metropolis, and in 「都会|とかい」, the big city. Its native reading is 「みやこ」, an old word for the imperial capital, which is why Kyoto is sometimes called the old capital. The short reading 「つ」 shows up in very few words. 「都合|つごう」 is the main one, and 「都度|つど」, each time, is another. So when you see this kanji in 「都合|つごう」, treat the 「つ」 reading as a special case to memorize. The second kanji, 「合|ごう」, also changes. It's 「あ」 in 「合う|あう」, and 「あい」 in 「試合|しあい」, a match, where two sides meet. In 「都合|つごう」 it's 「ごう」. Both kanji are using less common readings in this one word, which makes 「都合|つごう」 a word to learn as a single block." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word lives in calendars and invitations. When someone polite asks about your schedule, they'll often say 「ご都合はいかがですか|ごつごうはいかがですか」, how is your schedule? The 「ご」 on the front shows respect. To ask for a good day, people say 「ご都合のいい日はいつですか|ごつごうのいいひはいつですか」, which day is good for you? And if you can make time, the phrase is 「都合がつく|つごうがつく」, to be able to fit it in. You'll also see the word on shop doors. A sign reading 「都合により休みます|つごうによりやすみます」 means closed due to circumstances, with no further explanation. Here's a short exchange. 「土曜日、カラオケに行きませんか|どようび、カラオケにいきませんか」. 「すみません、土曜日はちょっと都合が悪くて|すみません、どようびはちょっとつごうがわるくて」. Notice how the answer ends with the te-form and just trails off. Nobody needs the rest of the sentence. That trailing reply is a real skill." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In many English-speaking places, if you turn down an invitation, people expect a reason. I'd love to, but I have a dentist appointment. In Japan, saying your schedule is inconvenient is usually reason enough. Asking why would feel nosy. The phrase works like a polite curtain. It closes the topic without giving details and without hurting anyone. That's why you'll hear it in offices, between friends, and between parents arranging play dates. The listener's job is to accept it gracefully and maybe suggest another day. There's a flip side, though. 「自分の都合|じぶんのつごう」 means your own convenience, and if someone does things 「自分の都合ばかり|じぶんのつごうばかり」, only what suits themselves, that's a criticism. They're being selfish. So the word can be gentle when you decline, but sharp when it describes someone who only thinks of their own schedule. Use the polite version, and you'll fit right in." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「都合|つごう」 is how things fit, your circumstances, plus 「悪い|わるい」, bad, so bad timing; 「その日はちょっと都合が悪いです|そのひはちょっとつごうがわるいです」 is everyone's soft no.",
      "Both kanji use rare readings here: 「都|と」 becomes 「つ」, found almost only in 「都合|つごう」 and 「都度|つど」, and 「合|あ」 becomes 「ごう」, so learn it as a single block.",
      "Polite schedulers ask 「ご都合はいかがですか|ごつごうはいかがですか」, and a reply like 「土曜日はちょっと都合が悪くて|どようびはちょっとつごうがわるくて」 can just trail off on the te-form.",
      "In Japan an inconvenient schedule is reason enough and asking why is nosy, but 「自分の都合ばかり|じぶんのつごうばかり」, only what suits yourself, is a criticism."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[都合が悪い|つごうがわるい]です。",
    en: "Tomorrow is not convenient for me." },
  "つよい|強い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「強|つよ」, read with its native reading, plus the adjective ending. On the left side of the kanji is the bow part, 「弓|ゆみ」, an archer's bow. A way to remember it, and this is a hook: pulling back a strong bow takes real strength. Now a surprising family member. You've been using this kanji since lesson three, in 「勉強|べんきょう」, study, where it has its Chinese-origin reading 「きょう」. The first kanji there, 「勉|べん」, means effort. In older Japanese, 「勉強|べんきょう」 meant forcing yourself, pushing hard with effort. Study is the strong effort you make. That old meaning still survives in shops, where a shopkeeper may say 「勉強します|べんきょうします」, meaning I'll make an effort for you, I'll give you a discount. Usage of 「強い|つよい」 is broad. People can be strong, teams can be strong, and so can wind and drinks. 「風が強い|かぜがつよい」 is the everyday way to say it's windy. Someone who can drink a lot is 「おさけに強い|おさけにつよい」, strong against alcohol. The opposite is 「よわい」, weak. Recap: the bow on the left for strength, the same kanji as in 「勉強|べんきょう」, effort." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji has several readings across words you'll meet. With 「きょう」, besides 「勉強|べんきょう」, it appears in 「強力|きょうりょく」, powerful, as in strong glue or a strong magnet. With 「ごう」, it appears in 「強引|ごういん」, pushy, forcing your way through. Its native verbs come in a pair. 「強める|つよめる」 means to strengthen something, like turning up the heat. 「強まる|つよまる」 means it gets stronger by itself, like rain or wind growing heavier, a word from weather reports. Then the opposite, 「弱い|よわい」, weak. Its kanji looks like two small bows sitting side by side, each with a couple of little marks inside. Since 「強|つよ」 also has a bow on its left, the pair is easy to spot on a page. And the noun form of strong is 「強さ|つよさ」, strength. So from one shape you get study, powerful, pushy, strengthen, and strength, with its weak twin right beside it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The most useful pattern here is something plus 「に強い|につよい」, meaning good at it or able to handle it. 「数学に強い|すうがくにつよい」, strong at math. 「寒さに強い|さむさにつよい」, someone who doesn't mind the cold. Houses in Japan are advertised as 「地震に強い|じしんにつよい」, earthquake resistant. The flip side is 「に弱い|によわい」, weak against something. 「甘いものに弱い|あまいものによわい」 means having a soft spot for sweets, you can't resist them. Here's a tiny conversation. One friend says 「田中さんはパソコンに強いですね|たなかさんはパソコンにつよいですね」, Tanaka's great with computers. The other laughs, 「でも、朝に弱いです|でも、あさによわいです」, but he's hopeless in the mornings. That pattern is everywhere in daily talk, from sports to cooking to drinking. So when you hear 「に強い|につよい」, think good at, tough against, or can handle, not just physically strong." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "As a regular i-adjective, this word bends into useful shapes. Drop the 「い」 and add 「く」, and you get an adverb: 「強く|つよく」, strongly, hard. 「ボタンを強く押してください|ボタンをつよくおしてください」, please press the button hard. Add 「なる」, and you get becoming stronger: 「強くなりたい|つよくなりたい」, I want to get stronger, a classic line from sports anime. The past is 「強かった|つよかった」, and the negative is 「強くない|つよくない」. With the comparison pattern from lesson ten, you can say 「犬より猫のほうが強い|いぬよりねこのほうがつよい」, the cat is stronger than the dog. With 「すぎる」 from lesson twelve, it's 「強すぎる|つよすぎる」, too strong, perfect for coffee or perfume. So the word fits neatly into patterns you already know. Practice the adverb especially, because pressing, pulling, and holding hard come up constantly." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「強|つよ」 has a bow on the left, pulling a strong bow, and it's the 「きょう」 in 「勉強|べんきょう」, once meaning forcing yourself with effort; it covers people, wind and drinks.",
      "Read 「きょう」 it gives 「強力|きょうりょく」, read 「ごう」 it gives 「強引|ごういん」, its verbs are 「強める|つよめる」 and 「強まる|つよまる」, and the weak twin 「弱い|よわい」 shows two little bows.",
      "Something plus 「に強い|につよい」 means good at or able to handle it, like 「地震に強い|じしんにつよい」, while 「甘いものに弱い|あまいものによわい」 is a soft spot for sweets.",
      "Change 「い」 to 「く」 for the adverb, as in 「ボタンを強く押してください|ボタンをつよくおしてください」, add 「なる」 for 「強くなりたい|つよくなりたい」, and 「すぎる」 for too strong."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は{風|かぜ}が[強い|つよい]です。",
    en: "The wind is strong today." },
  "さいあく|最悪": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first is 「最|さい」, most, the very top of a scale. You know it from 「最近|さいきん」, recently, from lesson fifteen, literally the most near, the closest in time. The second is 「悪|あく」, bad or evil. You know the same kanji from 「悪い|わるい」, bad, from lesson twelve, but there it has its native reading. Here it switches to the Chinese-origin reading, 「あく」. So 「最悪|さいあく」 is the most bad, the worst. Clear and logical. Its opposite is 「最高|さいこう」, the best, literally the most high, which you'll hear all the time when people are happy. Usage is very lively. In casual speech, people use 「最悪|さいあく」 as a one-word exclamation when something goes wrong, like ugh, that's the worst. You miss the last train, it starts to rain, you lost your key. 「最悪！|さいあく！」 It's strong, so be a bit careful using it about another person's work or cooking. It can sound harsh. Recap: 「最|さい」, most, as in 「最近|さいきん」, plus 「悪|あく」, bad, as in 「悪い|わるい」, gives the worst, the opposite of 「最高|さいこう」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「最|さい」, the most, builds a whole set of superlatives. 「最初|さいしょ」, the very first. 「最後|さいご」, the very last. 「最新|さいしん」, the newest, on phone ads. 「最低|さいてい」, the lowest, which is a close cousin of 「最悪|さいあく」. Here's the difference. 「最悪|さいあく」 is about situations: the weather, the timing, the luck. 「最低|さいてい」 often judges a person's behavior. If someone breaks a promise, a friend might snap 「最低！|さいてい！」, you're the lowest, you're terrible. That's a much more personal insult. There's also a native reading of the same kanji, 「最も|もっとも」, most, used in writing: 「最も高い山|もっともたかいやま」, the highest mountain. So with 「最|さい」 you can label the first, the last, the newest, the best, the worst, and the lowest. Just remember that 「最低|さいてい」 cuts closer to the person." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Beyond the exclamation, this word has a very practical use: planning for the worst. 「最悪の場合|さいあくのばあい」 means in the worst case. 「最悪の場合、電車で帰ります|さいあくのばあい、でんしゃでかえります」, worst case, I'll take the train home. In casual speech, people even use it alone at the start of a sentence: 「最悪、タクシーでいいよ|さいあく、タクシーでいいよ」, worst case, we can just take a taxi. That's reassuring, not gloomy. It's the backup plan. With 「の」 it also describes things: 「最悪の天気|さいあくのてんき」, awful weather, or 「最悪の一日|さいあくのいちにち」, the worst day ever. A quick exchange: one friend says 「財布を忘れた|さいふをわすれた」, I forgot my wallet. The other says 「最悪！|さいあく！」, ugh, no way. Then, being kind, 「大丈夫、払うよ|だいじょうぶ、はらうよ」, it's fine, I'll pay. So the word moves easily from complaining to planning." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「最|さい」, most, as in 「最近|さいきん」, plus 「悪|あく」, bad, as in 「悪い|わるい」, gives the worst, the opposite of 「最高|さいこう」; it's a lively exclamation but can sound harsh about someone's work.",
      "「最|さい」 builds 「最初|さいしょ」, 「最後|さいご」 and 「最新|さいしん」, and while 「最悪|さいあく」 is about situations, 「最低|さいてい」 judges a person and cuts much closer.",
      "It's also for backup plans, as in 「最悪の場合|さいあくのばあい」 or just 「最悪、タクシーでいいよ|さいあく、タクシーでいいよ」, and with 「の」 it describes things, like 「最悪の一日|さいあくのいちにち」."
    ],
    ja: "{雨|あめ}で{試合|しあい}がなくなりました。[最悪|さいあく]です。",
    en: "The game was canceled because of rain. It's the worst." },
  "えらぶ|選ぶ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「選|えら」, with its native reading, plus the verb ending 「ぶ」. It's an u-verb, so its forms are 「選びます|えらびます」 and 「選んで|えらんで」, like 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, to play. Look at the kanji's bottom and left edge. That's the movement part, the same one running under 「道|みち」, road. You've actually met this kanji before with its Chinese-origin reading, 「せん」, in 「スポーツ選手|スポーツせんしゅ」, an athlete, from lesson eleven. 「選手|せんしゅ」 is literally a chosen hand, a person selected to play. That's a great anchor. Athletes are the ones who were chosen. Now usage. The thing you choose takes 「を」: 「プレゼントを選ぶ|プレゼントをえらぶ」, to choose a present. If you're picking from a group, use 「から」: from these three, choose one. A common question in shops and restaurants is 「どれを選びますか|どれをえらびますか」, which one will you choose. A memory hook, and it's just a hook: think of choosing an era, era-boo. Which era would you pick to live in? Recap: the movement part, the same kanji as in 「選手|せんしゅ」, a chosen player, and an u-verb like 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The Chinese-origin reading of this kanji, 「せん」, leads to a famous pair of sound-alikes. 「選択|せんたく」 means a choice or selection. 「洗濯|せんたく」 means laundry. They sound exactly the same. The kanji are different: laundry uses the water-drop kanji 「洗|せん」, from 「洗う|あらう」, to wash. Japanese people actually make jokes about this pair. In a store, a 「選択|せんたく」 is a selection, and at home, 「洗濯|せんたく」 is the washing. Context almost always makes it clear. You'll also meet 「選挙|せんきょ」, an election, literally choosing and raising up, which you'll hear in the news. And in sports, 「選手|せんしゅ」, the athlete, keeps the same 「せん」. Then for the verb itself, with its native reading, you have 「選ぶ|えらぶ」. Compare its sound with 「えらい」, great or admirable, which is a totally different word. So one kanji gives you choose, choice, election, and athlete, and one sneaky laundry joke." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There's a useful difference between 「選ぶ|えらぶ」 and 「決める|きめる」, to decide. 「選ぶ|えらぶ」 means picking from options in front of you, this one, not that one. 「決める|きめる」 is making a decision, which may have no menu at all, like deciding to move abroad. In restaurants, you'll see the potential form on menus: 「選べる|えらべる」, can be chosen. 「ドリンクが選べます|ドリンクがえらべます」, you can choose your drink. Set meals often say 「ご飯かパンから選べます|ごはんかパンからえらべます」, choose rice or bread. A friendly line when giving a gift is 「好きなのを選んで|すきなのをえらんで」, pick whichever you like. And when a friend can't decide, you might hear 「選べない！|えらべない！」, I can't choose! A tiny exchange in a shop: 「どちらにしますか」, which will you have? 「うーん、こっちを選びます|うーん、こっちをえらびます」, hmm, I'll go with this one. So keep 「選ぶ|えらぶ」 for picking from a lineup." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's an u-verb like 「遊ぶ|あそぶ」, and the kanji is the 「せん」 in 「選手|せんしゅ」, an athlete, a chosen player; you choose with 「を」 and pick from a group with 「から」.",
      "Read 「せん」, it gives 「選択|せんたく」, a choice, which sounds exactly like 「洗濯|せんたく」, laundry, plus 「選挙|せんきょ」, an election, so one kanji brings a laundry joke.",
      "「選ぶ|えらぶ」 is picking from a lineup while 「決める|きめる」 is deciding with no menu at all, and menus use the potential form, as in 「ドリンクが選べます|ドリンクがえらべます」."
    ],
    ja: "{友だち|ともだち}のプレゼントを[選びました|えらびました|えらぶ]。",
    en: "I chose a present for my friend." },
  "おゆをわかす|お湯を沸かす": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is built from 「お湯|おゆ」, hot water, which is also in this lesson, plus the verb 「沸かす|わかす」, to bring to a boil. The kanji 「沸|わ」 is read with its native reading, and on its left you'll see the three water drops, just like in 「湯|ゆ」. Two watery kanji in one phrase. Here's the funny part for English speakers. Literally it says boil the hot water. Logically, you start with cold water. But Japanese names the water by what it becomes, the result you're aiming for. You heat water to make 「お湯|おゆ」. A nice verb pair to know. 「沸かす|わかす」 is the doing verb. You make the water boil. The partner verb 「沸く|わく」 describes it happening by itself. 「お湯が沸きました|おゆがわきました」, the water has boiled, is what you say when the kettle clicks off. 「沸かす|わかす」 is an u-verb, so the te-form is 「沸かして|わかして」. You also use it for the bath. 「おふろを沸かす|おふろをわかす」 means to heat the bath, a common evening job in Japanese homes. Recap: name the water by the result, 「お湯|おゆ」, and use 「沸かす|わかす」 to make it boil." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese splits water into two separate words. 「水|みず」 is cold or room temperature water, and 「お湯|おゆ」 is hot water. In English you just add hot, but in Japanese they really feel like different things. Asking for 「水|みず」 at a restaurant gets you a cold glass. The kanji 「湯|ゆ」 builds other everyday words. 「湯のみ|ゆのみ」 is a teacup, literally a hot water drinker. 「湯気|ゆげ」 is steam rising from a bowl or a bath. With the Chinese-origin reading, 「銭湯|せんとう」 is a public bath house, and you'll see the hiragana 「ゆ」 on the curtain at the door. The verb's other half, 「沸く|わく」, stretches too. When a crowd at a concert goes wild, people say the hall 「沸いた|わいた」, it boiled over with excitement. So from 「お湯|おゆ」 you get teacups, steam, and bath houses, and from 「沸く|わく」, a cheering crowd." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Hot water is part of daily life in Japanese homes in a way visitors notice right away. Many kitchens have an electric hot water pot that keeps water hot all day, ready for green tea, instant noodles, or a quick cup of miso soup. And then there's the bath. Japanese people usually wash before getting in, then soak in clean hot water, and the whole family may use the same bath water in turn. Modern bath systems heat it at the press of a button, and when it's ready, a little recorded voice announces 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」, the bath is ready, often with a cheerful tune. Many people in Japan can hum that melody. Before these systems, someone in the family had the job of heating the bath, and asking 「おふろ、沸いた？|おふろ、わいた？」 was an evening routine. So boiling water in Japan isn't just about cooking. It's tea, noodles, and that cozy soak at night." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb pair follows a pattern you can use to guess others. The doing verb ends in 「かす」, and its happening-by-itself partner ends in 「く」. 「沸かす|わかす」, to boil something, and 「沸く|わく」, to come to a boil. Look at more pairs with the same shape. 「乾かす|かわかす」, to dry something, like your hair with a dryer, and 「乾く|かわく」, to get dry, like laundry on the line. 「動かす|うごかす」, to move something, and 「動く|うごく」, to move by itself. With the doing verb, the thing takes 「を」. 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」. With the happening verb, the thing takes 「が」. 「お湯が沸く|おゆがわく」. And with the te-form plus 「いる」, the happening verb shows a state: 「お湯が沸いています|おゆがわいています」, the water is boiling. So whenever you see a verb ending in 「かす」, try dropping it to 「く」, and you may have found its partner." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Literally boil the hot water, since Japanese names the water by the result, 「お湯|おゆ」; 「沸かす|わかす」 is the doing verb and 「お湯が沸きました|おゆがわきました」 is said when the kettle clicks off.",
      "Cold 「水|みず」 and hot 「お湯|おゆ」 feel like different things, 「湯|ゆ」 builds 「湯のみ|ゆのみ」 and 「湯気|ゆげ」, and a cheering concert crowd 「沸いた|わいた」, boiled over.",
      "Homes keep hot water pots for tea and noodles, and modern baths announce 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」 with a cheerful tune many people can hum.",
      "Doing verbs ending in 「かす」 pair with happening verbs ending in 「く」, like 「乾かす|かわかす」 and 「乾く|かわく」, with 「を」 for the doing verb and 「が」 for the happening one."
    ],
    ja: "{今|いま}、[お湯を沸かしています|おゆをわかしています|おゆをわかす]。",
    en: "I'm boiling water now." },
  "かみをとかす|髪をとかす": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is 「髪|かみ」, hair, plus the verb 「とかす」, to comb. You learned 「髪|かみ」 in lesson seven. It's the native reading, and the top of the kanji has a special part showing long, flowing hair. Remember not to confuse it with 「紙|かみ」, paper, in this same lesson. Same sound, different kanji. The verb 「とかす」 is usually written in hiragana when it means combing. It's an u-verb, so 「とかします」 and 「とかして」. Be aware that Japanese has other verbs that sound exactly like it. The most common one means to melt or dissolve, like melting butter. Context tells you which. If 「髪|かみ」 is in front, it's combing, every time. A way to remember it, and this is a hook: think of a toe catching in a tangle, toe-kas, as you drag the comb through. This phrase fits the morning-routine set in this lesson. 「ひげをそる」, to shave, 「化粧する|けしょうする」, to put on makeup, and 「髪をとかす|かみをとかす」, to comb your hair. Recap: 「髪|かみ」, hair, plus 「とかす」, comb, an u-verb, part of getting ready in the morning." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Hair gets a whole set of verbs in Japanese, and 「髪|かみ」 sits in front of each one. 「髪を洗う|かみをあらう」, to wash your hair. 「髪を乾かす|かみをかわかす」, to dry it, usually with a hair dryer. 「髪をのばす|かみをのばす」, to grow it out. 「髪を染める|かみをそめる」, to dye it. And at the salon, since someone else cuts it, people say 「髪を切ってもらいました|かみをきってもらいました」, I had my hair cut. The tool for combing is 「くし」, a comb, and for brushing many people just use the loanword 「ブラシ」. So you can say 「くしで髪をとかす|くしでかみをとかす」, to comb your hair with a comb, using 「で」 for the tool. Collect these together and you can describe a whole bathroom routine: wash, dry, comb, and maybe dye. Each one is just 「髪を|かみを」 plus a verb. Learn them as a set and they'll come out naturally." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Both halves of this phrase have sound-alikes to keep straight. First, 「かみ」. Besides hair and paper, there's 「神|かみ」, a god or spirit, the kanji in 「神社|じんじゃ」, shrine. Three very different things with one sound. Japanese speakers tell them apart by context and partly by pitch, but as a learner, the verb usually settles it. You comb hair, you write on paper, and you pray to a god. Now 「とかす」. The most common sound-alike is 「溶かす|とかす」, to melt or dissolve, as in melting chocolate or dissolving sugar in coffee. There's also a related verb for untangling and loosening, which is close to the combing idea. That's actually a helpful picture. Combing is loosening the knots in your hair. So when you hear 「とかす」 with hair, think untangling. With chocolate or sugar, think melting. A funny mix-up to avoid is melting your hair. Context makes it obvious, but it's a good laugh to remember." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「髪|かみ」, hair, with its flowing-hair top, plus the u-verb 「とかす」, to comb, usually in hiragana; it belongs to the morning routine with 「ひげをそる」 and 「化粧する|けしょうする」.",
      "Hair gets a set of verbs, 「髪を洗う|かみをあらう」, 「髪を乾かす|かみをかわかす」, 「髪を染める|かみをそめる」, and you comb with 「くし」 using 「で」, as in 「くしで髪をとかす|くしでかみをとかす」.",
      "「かみ」 can be hair, paper or god, and 「とかす」 can mean 「溶かす|とかす」, to melt, so think untangling with hair and melting with chocolate, never melting your hair."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[髪をとかします|かみをとかします|かみをとかす]。",
    en: "I comb my hair every morning." },
  "ひげをそる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is 「ひげ」, facial hair, which you met earlier in this lesson, plus the verb 「そる」, to shave. Both are usually written in kana. The key grammar point is a trap. 「そる」 ends in 「る」, so it looks like a ru-verb, like 「食べる|たべる」. But it isn't. It's an u-verb, like 「帰る|かえる」 and 「切る|きる」. So the polite form is 「そります」, and the te-form is 「そって」. Say it with me in your head: 「そって」, not 「そて」. Remember 「ひげ」 covers any facial hair, so this phrase covers shaving a beard, a mustache, or just morning stubble. If you shave somewhere else, like your head, you'd put that body part in front of 「そる」 instead. A way to remember 「そる」, and this is a hook: after a hard shave your face might feel a little sore. Sore, 「そる」. This belongs to the morning-routine group in this lesson, with 「髪をとかす|かみをとかす」, to comb your hair, and 「化粧する|けしょうする」, to put on makeup. In Japanese offices many men are expected to be clean-shaven, so a 「サラリーマン」 does this every day. Recap: 「ひげ」, facial hair, plus 「そる」, an u-verb despite the 「る」, so 「そって」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese can get specific about facial hair. 「口ひげ|くちひげ」 is a mustache, literally mouth whiskers. 「あごひげ」 is a beard on the chin. 「無精ひげ|ぶしょうひげ」 is stubble, the lazy beard you get when you skip shaving for a few days. There are kanji for the different kinds of 「ひげ」, but they're hard, so kana is the norm. The verb 「そる」 also goes with other body parts. 「頭をそる|あたまをそる」 means to shave your head, as monks do. 「まゆをそる」 means to shave your eyebrows. And here's a fun connection. A razor is 「かみそり」, which comes from 「かみ」, hair, plus a form of 「そる」. A hair shaver. An electric shaver is often called 「電気かみそり|でんきかみそり」 or just 「シェーバー」. Be careful with another verb that sounds the same, meaning to bend or arch backward. It's spelled with a different kanji and has nothing to do with shaving." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since 「そる」 is an u-verb in disguise, it's worth gathering its look-alike friends in one place. These verbs all end in 「いる」 or 「える」 but are u-verbs anyway. 「帰る|かえる」, to go home. 「入る|はいる」, to enter. 「走る|はしる」, to run. 「知る|しる」, to know. 「切る|きる」, to cut. 「そる」 is a little different because it ends in 「おる」, which is always an u-verb, but learners still slip because the 「る」 looks familiar. Here's a silly sentence to bundle them: I came home, went in, cut my hair, and shaved. 「帰って、入って、髪を切って、ひげをそって|かえって、はいって、かみをきって、ひげをそって」. Every one of those has the doubled 「って」. You can also use the habit meaning of te-form plus 「いる」: 「毎朝ひげをそっています|まいあさひげをそっています」, I shave every morning. And at a barbershop, it becomes 「ひげをそってもらう」, to get a shave. Practice that string of 「って」 out loud and it sticks." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「そる」 looks like a ru-verb but is an u-verb like 「帰る|かえる」, so it's 「そります」 and 「そって」; after a hard shave your face feels sore, 「そる」.",
      "Be specific with 「口ひげ|くちひげ」 or 「あごひげ」, shave other parts as in 「頭をそる|あたまをそる」, and note a razor, 「かみそり」, is literally a hair shaver.",
      "Bundle the disguised u-verbs in one string, 「帰って、入って、髪を切って、ひげをそって|かえって、はいって、かみをきって、ひげをそって」, every one with a doubled 「って」."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は{毎朝|まいあさ}[ひげをそります||ひげをそる]。",
    en: "My father shaves every morning." },
  "ぬぐ|脱ぐ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「脱|ぬ」, with its native reading, plus the u-verb ending 「ぐ」. Its forms are 「脱ぎます|ぬぎます」 and 「脱いで|ぬいで」, like 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, to swim. On the left of the kanji is the flesh or body part, the same one that looks like 「月|つき」, moon, and shows up in many body-related kanji. The idea here is slipping out, getting free of something, and you're slipping your body out of your clothes. Here's what makes this verb so handy. Japanese has several verbs for putting things on, depending on where they go. 「着る|きる」 for shirts and jackets, 「はく」 for shoes and pants, and 「かぶる」 for hats, all from lesson seven. But for taking them off, one verb covers all of them. 「脱ぐ|ぬぐ」. Shirt, pants, shoes, hat. Easy. For glasses and small accessories, though, people use 「はずす」 or 「とる」 instead. Culturally, this is the verb at every Japanese doorway. You take your shoes off at the entrance of a home: 「靴を脱ぎます|くつをぬぎます」. Recap: flesh part on the left, an u-verb like 「泳ぐ|およぐ」, and one verb for taking off all your clothes and shoes." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Taking off your shoes is one of the first customs visitors learn in Japan. Just inside the front door of a home is the 「玄関|げんかん」, a small entry area, usually a step lower than the rest of the house. You take your shoes off there, step up onto the floor, and often slip on house slippers. Polite guests turn their shoes around to face the door, ready for leaving. Shoes come off in many other places too: some restaurants with tatami rooms, temples, schools, and traditional inns. In a tatami room, even the slippers come off, and you walk in socks. And in many homes, there's a separate pair of slippers just for the toilet, so don't wear those back into the living room. That's a classic visitor mistake. You may see a sign saying 「ここで靴を脱いでください|ここでくつをぬいでください」, please take your shoes off here. So keep this verb ready at every doorway." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「だつ」, this kanji means escaping or slipping out of something, and it builds some lively words. 「脱出|だっしゅつ」 is an escape, as in escape rooms or a hero escaping a trap. 「脱水|だっすい」 is losing water. In health news it means dehydration, and on a washing machine it's the spin cycle, where water is thrown out of the clothes. 「脱線|だっせん」 is a train going off the rails, and people also use it when a conversation goes off topic. Here we go, derailing again. Notice the small 「っ」 in these words, where 「だつ」 gets clipped before the next sound. Back to the native verb, it has a partner, 「脱げる|ぬげる」, to come off by itself. 「靴が脱げました|くつがぬげました」, my shoe came off, like when you run and a sandal flies away. So the kanji is about slipping out, whether it's your coat, a prisoner, or a train." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Two forms of this verb are worth practicing. First, the te-form. Because it ends in 「ぐ」, the te-form becomes 「いで」, with a voiced sound, just like 「泳ぐ|およぐ」 becomes 「泳いで|およいで」. So 「脱いで|ぬいで」, never 「脱いて|ぬいて」. Second, the ta-form, 「脱いだ|ぬいだ」, which follows the same rule. Now a nice pattern. With te-form plus 「から」, you can describe the order of actions at a doorway: 「靴を脱いでから、入ります|くつをぬいでから、はいります」, after taking off my shoes, I go in. And with te-form plus 「ください」, you get the polite request a host might use. Also watch the opposite direction. 「着ている|きている」 means wearing a shirt, but 「脱いでいる|ぬいでいる」 is less common, because once clothes are off, you just aren't wearing them. People say 「コートを脱ぎました|コートをぬぎました」 instead. So remember the voiced 「いで」, and use it for every item of clothing." }
    ],
    short: [
      "An u-verb like 「泳ぐ|およぐ」 with the body part on the left, slipping out; putting on needs 「着る|きる」, 「はく」 or 「かぶる」, but one verb takes off shirts, pants, shoes and hats.",
      "Shoes come off in the 「玄関|げんかん」, a step below the house, and in tatami rooms even slippers come off, and toilet slippers never go back to the living room.",
      "Read 「だつ」, it means slipping out, giving 「脱出|だっしゅつ」, escape, 「脱水|だっすい」, dehydration or spin cycle, and 「脱線|だっせん」, derailing, even in conversation.",
      "Its te-form is voiced, 「脱いで|ぬいで」, never 「脱いて|ぬいて」, and 「靴を脱いでから、入ります|くつをぬいでから、はいります」 describes the order at a doorway."
    ],
    ja: "{家|いえ}で{靴|くつ}を[脱ぎます|ぬぎます|ぬぐ]。",
    en: "I take off my shoes at home." },
  "こむ|混む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「混|こ」, with its native reading, plus the u-verb ending 「む」. On the left are the three water drops, and the kanji's core meaning is to mix, like things mixing together in water. A crowd is people all mixed and packed together. Now the important grammar habit. When you describe something being crowded right now, Japanese almost always uses the te-form plus 「いる」: 「混んでいます|こんでいます」, it is crowded. That's the state after the crowding happened. 「混む|こむ」 by itself is more like it gets crowded, as in a general habit: this train gets crowded in the morning. The te-form is 「混んで|こんで」, like 「読む|よむ」 and 「読んで|よんで」. The thing that's crowded takes 「が」 or 「は」: 「電車が混んでいます|でんしゃがこんでいます」, the train is crowded. Its opposite is 「すいています」, it's empty, not crowded. Culturally, this is a word every Tokyo commuter knows. Morning rush hour trains are famously packed, and you'll hear people complain, 「すごく混んでいました|すごくこんでいました」, it was really crowded. Recap: water drops for mixing, people packed together, usually said as 「混んでいます|こんでいます」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "With its Chinese-origin reading 「こん」, this kanji shows up in words you'll hear at stations. 「混雑|こんざつ」 is congestion. Train announcements say 「大変混雑しております|たいへんこんざつしております」, it is very crowded, in very polite language. 「混乱|こんらん」 is confusion or chaos, when things are mixed up in your head. The native verbs keep the mixing meaning. 「混ぜる|まぜる」 means to mix, as in stirring ingredients in a bowl. 「混ざる|まざる」 means to get mixed in. So the kanji really is about mixing, and crowding is people mixed together. One more spelling note. You'll sometimes see 「込む|こむ」 written for crowded, with the kanji meaning to go into or be packed in. It's the same kanji in 「申し込む|もうしこむ」, to apply. Both spellings for crowded are seen, and the water-drop version is very common on signs and news. So mixing, packing, crowding, and confusion all live together here." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Crowds are a daily topic in Japan, so this verb comes up in small talk all the time. At a station, a coworker might sigh 「今日も混んでいましたね|きょうもこんでいましたね」, it was crowded again today. A completely packed train is a 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」, a full-member train, so full that people are pressed against the doors. Station staff in Tokyo have been known to gently push passengers in so the doors can close. The opposite state uses a different verb: 「すいている」, not crowded, with plenty of empty seats. People plan around this. 「この時間はすいていますよ|このじかんはすいていますよ」, it's empty at this time. Roads count too: 「道が混んでいます|みちがこんでいます」, there's heavy traffic. A tiny exchange at a restaurant door: 「混んでいますね|こんでいますね」. 「じゃあ、ほかの店に行きましょうか|じゃあ、ほかのみせにいきましょうか」, let's try another place. So keep 「混んでいる|こんでいる」 and 「すいている」 as a pair." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick, just for fun. Picture a Tokyo commuter train at eight in the morning. The doors slide open, and instead of office workers, it's packed wall to wall with cows. Big, gentle cows in business ties, squeezed shoulder to shoulder, holding the straps with their hooves. Nobody can move. One cow tries to read a newspaper and can't lift it. And every few seconds, from somewhere in the crush, a low and miserable moo. Co-moo. That's the sound of a crowded train. So when you need the word for getting crowded, picture the cow train and hear that squished moo. Then add the te-form for the state, because those cows are still stuck in there right now. 「混んでいます|こんでいます」. A silly scene, but you won't forget it at rush hour." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「混|こ」 has water drops and means mix, so a crowd is people mixed together; for right now use 「混んでいます|こんでいます」, and the opposite is 「すいています」.",
      "Read 「こん」 it gives 「混雑|こんざつ」, congestion, and 「混乱|こんらん」, confusion, while 「混ぜる|まぜる」 means to mix, and crowded is sometimes written 「込む|こむ」, packed in.",
      "Crowds are daily small talk, from a packed 「満員電車|まんいんでんしゃ」 to traffic, 「道が混んでいます|みちがこんでいます」, so keep 「混んでいる|こんでいる」 and 「すいている」 as a pair.",
      "Picture a rush-hour train packed with cows in business ties, and from the crush a miserable co-moo, still stuck there now, 「混んでいます|こんでいます」."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}の{電車|でんしゃ}は[混んでいます|こんでいます|こむ]。",
    en: "The morning trains are crowded." },
  "たからくじにあたる|宝くじに当たる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase joins 「宝くじ|たからくじ」, lottery, from this lesson, with the verb 「当たる|あたる」, to hit. You remember 「宝|たから」, treasure, a jewel under a roof, and 「くじ」, a drawn lot. Now the verb. 「当|あ」 is read with its native reading, and 「当たる|あたる」 means to hit, to strike the target. When your lottery ticket hits the winning number, you've won. So Japanese says you hit the lottery. English has a similar idea with hit the jackpot. The key grammar point is the particle. Japanese uses 「に」, not 「を」, because 「に」 marks the target you hit. 「宝くじに当たる|たからくじにあたる」, to hit on the lottery. The past tense, 「当たりました|あたりました」, is what you'd shout with joy. It's an u-verb, so the te-form is 「当たって|あたって」. 「当たる|あたる」 is used for other kinds of hitting the mark too. A guess can 「当たる|あたる」, meaning it was right, and a weather forecast can too. A common daydream sentence is, if I win the lottery, I'll quit my job. Recap: 「宝くじ|たからくじ」, the treasure lot, plus 「に当たる|にあたる」, to hit it, means to win." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Lotteries are a big seasonal event in Japan. The most famous is the year-end jumbo lottery, sold in late autumn, with prizes worth hundreds of millions of yen. People line up for hours at certain ticket booths believed to be lucky, and one booth in Tokyo's Ginza district is famous for its long lines, since many big winners bought their tickets there. Some buyers even take their tickets to a shrine and pray for luck. Winnings from these lotteries are tax free in Japan, which makes the daydream even sweeter. The result is announced on New Year's Eve, so people end the year checking their numbers. A classic dinner-table question, and a favorite in Japanese classes too, is 「宝くじに当たったら、何をしますか|たからくじにあたったら、なにをしますか」, if you won the lottery, what would you do? Answers range from buying a house to traveling the world to quitting work. So the phrase carries a whole season of hope with it." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「当|あ」 is busier than you might think. With its Chinese-origin reading 「とう」, it's hiding in a word you use every day: 「本当|ほんとう」, true, really. Literally, the real hit, right on target. It also gives 「お弁当|おべんとう」, the boxed lunch, where the kanji keeps the reading 「とう」. The noun 「当たり|あたり」 means a hit or a winner. On a lottery scratch card or an ice cream stick, 「当たり|あたり」 means you won. The opposite is 「はずれ」, a miss. The doing verb is 「当てる|あてる」, to hit something on purpose, or to guess correctly. A friend might hold out closed hands and say 「当ててみて|あててみて」, try to guess. And sunlight 「当たる|あたる」 too: 「日が当たる部屋|ひがあたるへや」, a sunny room, where the sun hits. So whether it's a guess, a lottery, sunshine, or the truth itself, this kanji is about landing right on the mark." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This phrase is perfect for practicing conditionals. With the 「たら」 form from lesson seventeen, it becomes 「宝くじに当たったら|たからくじにあたったら」, if I win the lottery. Then you add your dream: 「宝くじに当たったら、大きい家を買いたいです|たからくじにあたったら、おおきいいえをかいたいです」, if I won the lottery, I'd want to buy a big house. You can also use the 「たら」 form with 「どうしますか」, what would you do? Keep the particle straight in every form. It's always 「に」, because the lottery is the target. Using 「を」 with this verb is a common slip, so watch for it. If you want 「を」, switch to the doing verb, 「宝くじを当てる|たからくじをあてる」, to land a lottery win. For a bigger prize, people say 「一等に当たる|いっとうにあたる」, to win first prize. And the same verb has a much less fun use. If a ball hits you, it's 「ボールが当たった|ボールがあたった」, and that's much less fun. So stick with 「に当たる|にあたる」, and use 「たら」 to dream big." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「当たる|あたる」 means to hit the target, so you hit the lottery, using 「に」 because it marks the target; guesses and forecasts can 「当たる|あたる」 too.",
      "The year-end jumbo lottery draws long lines at lucky booths like one in Ginza, winnings are tax free, and 「宝くじに当たったら、何をしますか|たからくじにあたったら、なにをしますか」 is a classic question.",
      "「当|あ」 hides in 「本当|ほんとう」, the real hit, and 「お弁当|おべんとう」, while 「当たり|あたり」 is a winner, 「はずれ」 a miss, and 「当ててみて|あててみて」 means try to guess.",
      "With 「たら」 it becomes if I win the lottery, and the particle stays 「に」; for 「を」, switch to the doing verb 「宝くじを当てる|たからくじをあてる」."
    ],
    ja: "[宝くじに当たりました|たからくじにあたりました|たからくじにあたる]！",
    en: "I won the lottery!" },
  "いれる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You already know this verb from lesson sixteen as 「入れる|いれる」, to put in. It's the same verb, being used in a special way. Think about how you make tea. You put the tea leaves in a pot, and you put hot water in. Coffee is the same. So Japanese uses 「いれる」 for making tea and coffee. It's literally the act of putting the drink together. There is a separate, special kanji for brewing, but it's uncommon and people usually just write this in hiragana. It's a ru-verb, so 「いれます」 and 「いれて」. Here's a common mistake English speakers make. They say make tea with 「作る|つくる」, to make. That sounds a bit odd, like you're manufacturing tea. For tea and coffee, 「いれる」 is natural. A very polite and useful offer is 「コーヒーをいれましょうか」, shall I make some coffee? Pair it with 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」 from this lesson. First you boil the water, then you make the tea. A gentle cultural note: offering to make tea for guests, 「お客さん|おきゃくさん」, is a basic act of hospitality in Japan. Recap: the same verb as put in, because you put leaves and water together, used for tea and coffee." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Because this verb is really 「入れる|いれる」, put in, it has a whole family. Its partner is 「入る|はいる」, to enter or go in by itself. You put sugar in, and the sugar goes in. The same kanji gives 「入口|いりぐち」, entrance, and 「入学|にゅうがく」, entering a school. With drinks, people often write 「お茶を入れる|おちゃをいれる」 using this everyday kanji. There is a special kanji just for brewing tea and coffee, which you may spot on cafe menus, but it's not one learners need to write. The verb also stretches in fun directions. 「スイッチを入れる|スイッチをいれる」 is to switch something on, putting the power in. 「電話を入れる|でんわをいれる」 is a casual business way to give someone a call. And 「お風呂に入る|おふろにはいる」, to take a bath, uses the partner verb, since you yourself go in. So making tea is just one branch of a big put-in family." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Making Japanese green tea has a few small customs worth knowing. First, the water. Good green tea is usually made with hot water that has cooled a little from boiling, because boiling water can make it bitter. Some people pour the water into the cups first, just to cool it, then into the teapot, a small pot called a 「急須|きゅうす」. When pouring for several guests, a careful host pours a little into each cup in turn, back and forth, so every cup gets the same strength. In offices, offering tea to visitors is still a standard courtesy, and the tea often appears without anyone asking. At home, a host might say 「お茶でもいれましょうか|おちゃでもいれましょうか」, shall I make some tea or something? The 「でも」 makes it light and casual. A guest can simply answer 「ありがとうございます」 and enjoy it. So this small verb carries a lot of hospitality." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the same verb as 「入れる|いれる」, put in, since you put leaves and water together, so use it for tea and coffee, not 「作る|つくる」, as in 「コーヒーをいれましょうか」.",
      "As put in, it partners with 「入る|はいる」, to go in, and stretches to 「スイッチを入れる|スイッチをいれる」, switch on, and 「電話を入れる|でんわをいれる」, give a call.",
      "Green tea is made with water cooled a little from boiling, poured back and forth so each cup is equal, and a host lightly offers 「お茶でもいれましょうか|おちゃでもいれましょうか」."
    ],
    ja: "コーヒーを[いれましょうか||いれる]。",
    en: "Shall I make some coffee?" },
  "うまれる|生まれる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「生|う」, with its native reading here, plus 「まれる」. 「生|せい」 is one of the hardest-working kanji in Japanese, with many readings, but its heart is always life, birth, and growing. You've met it everywhere. 「学生|がくせい」, student, a person living to learn. 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, the one born before you. 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, birthday, where it's read 「じょう」. And 「生活|せいかつ」, daily life. All of them are about life. So 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born, is life beginning. Now the grammar. English uses a passive, I was born. Japanese just uses a normal verb in the past: 「生まれました|うまれました」. It's a ru-verb, so 「生まれて|うまれて」. The time you were born takes 「に」: 「四月に生まれました|しがつにうまれました」, born in April. The place takes 「で」: 「東京で生まれました|とうきょうでうまれました」. And news of a birth sounds like 「赤ちゃんが生まれました|あかちゃんがうまれました」, the baby was born. Recap: 「生|う」, life, the same kanji as in 「学生|がくせい」 and 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, used as a normal past verb, not a passive." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Being born has a partner verb for the person doing the work, the mother. That verb is 「産む|うむ」, to give birth. It is a u-verb, so 「産みます|うみます」 and 「産んで|うんで」. The pair works like many you know. 「生まれる|うまれる」 says what happens to the baby, and 「産む|うむ」 says what the mother does. 「母が私を産みました|はははわたしをうみました」, my mother gave birth to me. 「私が生まれました|わたしがうまれました」, I was born. You will also see 「生む|うむ」 written with the life kanji, used in a wider sense, like producing a new idea. The stem 「生まれ|うまれ」 also works as a noun. 「東京生まれ|とうきょううまれ」 means born in Tokyo, a handy label you can use about yourself. 「生まれつき|うまれつき」 means by nature, from birth, as in 「生まれつき髪が茶色い|うまれつきかみがちゃいろい」, my hair has been brown since birth. So one verb gives you a partner, a label, and an adverb. Learn the trio together and birth talk becomes easy." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "When Japanese people talk about when they were born, the calendar can look unusual. Japan still uses era names alongside western years. People often describe a whole generation by era. 「昭和生まれ|しょうわうまれ」 means born in the Showa era, which ended in 1989. 「平成生まれ|へいせいうまれ」 means born in the Heisei era, from 1989 to 2019. Saying you are 「平成生まれ|へいせいうまれ」 instantly tells people you are on the younger side. Another common question uses the twelve animal signs of the Chinese zodiac. Someone may ask 「何年生まれですか|なにどしうまれですか」, which animal year were you born in? You might answer 「うさぎ年です|うさぎどしです」, the year of the rabbit. Notice that 「年|とし」 sounds like 「どし」 after the animal name. These signs come up at New Year, when cards and decorations show the coming year's animal. It is a friendly way to talk about age without asking it directly. Era names and zodiac animals give birth years a very Japanese flavor." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built purely on sound. Picture a delivery room. A nurse holds up a brand new baby. The baby opens its eyes, looks at its mother, and says, clear as a bell, ooh, mama! The doctors drop their clipboards. Ooh, ma, that is the start of 「うまれる」. Now imagine the baby adds a little burp at the end, ruh, and you have the whole word. Every time you hear 「うまれる」, see that astonishing baby greeting its mother on the day it was born. For the kanji, picture the life kanji 「生|う」 as a small plant pushing up through the ground, the way the shape is often described, with new leaves above a line of earth. The baby and the sprout both mean new life. So when you say 「生まれました|うまれました」, think of a talking baby and a fresh sprout. It is silly, but silly sticks." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「生|う」 is the life kanji of 「学生|がくせい」 and 「誕生日|たんじょうび」, and where English says I was born, Japanese uses a normal past verb, 「生まれました|うまれました」, with 「に」 for time and 「で」 for place.",
      "Its partner 「産む|うむ」 is what the mother does, while 「生まれる|うまれる」 is what happens to the baby, and the stem gives 「東京生まれ|とうきょううまれ」 and 「生まれつき|うまれつき」, by nature.",
      "People describe generations by era, like 「平成生まれ|へいせいうまれ」, and ask 「何年生まれですか|なにどしうまれですか」 to get your zodiac animal, a friendly way around asking age.",
      "A newborn looks at its mother and says ooh, mama, then burps ruh, while the life kanji sprouts like a small plant pushing up through the ground."
    ],
    ja: "わたしは{東京|とうきょう}で[生まれました|うまれました|うまれる]。",
    en: "I was born in Tokyo." },
  "かぎをかける|鍵をかける": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is 「鍵|かぎ」, lock or key, from this lesson, plus 「かける」. You remember that the kanji 「鍵|かぎ」 has the metal part on the left. Now 「かける」. This is one of Japanese's most flexible verbs. Its core idea is to hang something on, to attach or apply it. You've met it with 「めがねをかける」, to put on glasses, hanging them on your face. 「電話をかける|でんわをかける」, to make a phone call. And 「アイロンをかける」, to iron, from lesson sixteen, applying the iron to the cloth. So 「鍵をかける|かぎをかける」 is applying the lock, locking. It's a ru-verb, so 「かけます」 and 「かけて」. The thing you lock takes 「に」, because you put the lock on it: 「ドアに鍵をかけます|ドアにかぎをかけます」, I lock the door. To unlock, use 「あける」, to open: 「鍵をあける|かぎをあける」. A common worry when you're out is 「鍵をかけましたか|かぎをかけましたか」, did I lock it? Japan is famous for being safe, but people still lock their doors. Recap: 「鍵|かぎ」, the metal lock, plus 「かける」, the apply-it verb from glasses and phone calls, means to lock." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb 「かける」 has an intransitive partner, 「かかる」. This works like the pair 「あける」 and 「あく」, to open something and to be open. With 「かける」, you lock the door. With 「かかる」, the door is locked, and no one is mentioned. The thing takes 「が」 instead of 「を」. The most useful form is 「鍵がかかっています|かぎがかかっています」, it's locked. You might push on a door, find it won't open, and say just that. Compare that with 「鍵をかけておきました|かぎをかけておきました」, I locked it in advance, which focuses on what you did. In everyday speech many people also say 「鍵を閉める|かぎをしめる」, literally to close the lock, and it means the same thing. For losing your key, use 「鍵をなくしました|かぎをなくしました」. So you have one verb for doing, one for the result, and a casual alternative. Keep the doing and the result straight and your door talk will sound natural." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「鍵|かぎ」 has two parts. On the left is the metal part, the same one you see in 「銀行|ぎんこう」, bank, and in 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, subway, whose last kanji means iron. The metal part tells you the object is made of metal. On the right is 「建|けん」, which means to build. Here it mostly gives the sound. That's why the Chinese-origin reading of 「鍵|かぎ」 is 「けん」. You'll meet that reading in 「鍵盤|けんばん」, the keyboard of a piano. The same sound part appears in other kanji. 「建物|たてもの」, a building, uses it on its own. 「健康|けんこう」, health, puts the person part next to it, and it's read 「けん」 again. So the right side whispers the sound, and the left side gives the meaning. In our word, though, you hear the native reading, 「かぎ」, because it's the everyday Japanese word for a key or lock. Spot the metal on the left and the sound on the right, and this kanji gets easier." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture a Japanese couple leaving their apartment for the weekend. At the door one asks, 「鍵、かけた|かぎ、かけた」? The other says, 「うん、かけたよ」, yes, I locked it. Then, halfway to the station, a worry: 「あれ、鍵かけたかな|あれ、かぎかけたかな」, wait, did I lock it? That little drama is universal. A few related words come up all the time. A spare key is 「合鍵|あいかぎ」. Giving someone a copy of your key is a real sign of trust. Many newer apartment buildings have 「オートロック」, an entrance door that locks automatically and opens only with a key or code. And there's a word for children who carry their own house key because parents are at work, 「鍵っ子|かぎっこ」, a latchkey kid. Notice that 「鍵|かぎ」 covers both the key in your pocket and the lock on the door. Context tells you which one. With these words you can handle almost any door situation." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「かける」 is the hang-on, apply-it verb from glasses and phone calls, so locking is applying the lock; the door takes 「に」, as in 「ドアに鍵をかけます|ドアにかぎをかけます」.",
      "「かける」 is doing the locking, while its partner gives the result, 「鍵がかかっています|かぎがかかっています」, it's locked, with 「が」; casually people also say 「鍵を閉める|かぎをしめる」.",
      "「鍵|かぎ」 has the metal part, as in 「銀行|ぎんこう」, for meaning and 「建|けん」 for the sound 「けん」, heard in 「鍵盤|けんばん」 and 「健康|けんこう」.",
      "Halfway to the station comes the universal worry, 「あれ、鍵かけたかな|あれ、かぎかけたかな」, and related words include 「合鍵|あいかぎ」, 「オートロック」 and 「鍵っ子|かぎっこ」."
    ],
    ja: "ドアに[鍵をかけました|かぎをかけました|かぎをかける]。",
    en: "I locked the door." },
  "たりる|足りる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「足|た」, with its native reading here, plus 「りる」. You know this kanji from lesson twelve as 「足|あし」, foot or leg. But it has a second, very real meaning: to add, to be enough. The related verb 「足す|たす」 means to add, as in adding numbers. So 「足りる|たりる」 is to be enough, when what you've added up covers what you need. A way to remember the link, and this is a hook: your feet are enough to get you anywhere. It's a ru-verb, so 「足ります|たります」 and 「足りて|たりて」. Here's the important usage point. You'll hear the negative far more than the positive. 「足りない|たりない」, not enough, is everywhere. 「お金が足りません|おかねがたりません」, I don't have enough money. 「時間が足りない|じかんがたりない」, there's not enough time. The thing that's enough or not enough takes 「が」. Notice the shape of the sentence. English says I have enough money. Japanese makes the money the subject and says the money is enough, or isn't. Recap: 「足|た」, the foot kanji with its add-up meaning, a ru-verb mostly heard as 「足りない|たりない」, not enough." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The enough meaning of the foot kanji spreads into some very useful words, read with the Chinese-origin reading 「そく」. 「満足|まんぞく」 means satisfaction. Its first kanji means full, so it's literally full and enough. 「満足しました|まんぞくしました」, I'm satisfied. Notice the sound shifts to 「ぞく」 here. Then there is 「不足|ふそく」, a shortage, where 「不|ふ」 means not. Not enough. It sticks onto other nouns. 「睡眠不足|すいみんぶそく」 is lack of sleep, and 「運動不足|うんどうぶそく」 is lack of exercise. Doctors and tired students say these all the time. And the native verb 「足す|たす」, to add, gives 「足し算|たしざん」, addition, the arithmetic you did as a child. So from one kanji you get foot, add, enough, satisfaction, and shortage. They all share the idea of a total that measures up or falls short. Next time you see this kanji, ask yourself whether it's walking or counting." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb is everywhere in daily life, almost always in the negative. At a register, you might discover 「百円足りない|ひゃくえんたりない」, I'm a hundred yen short. Put the amount in front and the meaning is short by that much. At work, people complain 「人が足りない|ひとがたりない」, we're short-staffed. Checking chairs or plates, someone says 「一つ足りない|ひとつたりない」, we're one short. To ask whether an amount is enough, use 「で」 for the amount: 「千円で足りますか|せんえんでたりますか」, will a thousand yen be enough? A friend answers 「足りるよ|たりるよ」, that'll do. There's also a lovely word, 「物足りない|ものたりない」. It means something's missing, not quite satisfying. A meal that's tasty but too small is 「ちょっと物足りない|ちょっとものたりない」. A common mistake is trying to say I have enough with 「ある」 alone. Make the thing the subject and use this verb instead. Practice the short-by-this-much pattern and you'll use it every week." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick using an English sound-alike. Think of the old word tarry, meaning to linger. Picture yourself at a convenience store counter, tarrying. You dig through your pockets, counting coins one by one, while a line forms behind you. The clerk waits. You keep tarrying because the money just isn't enough. Tarry, 「たり」, linked to not enough, 「たりない」. Now give your feet a role, because the kanji is the foot. Imagine you finally take off your shoes and shake them, hoping a coin falls out of a sock. One does. Your feet saved the day, and now it's enough, 「足りる|たりる」. The scene is silly, but it ties the sound, the meaning, and the kanji shape together. So when you hear 「足りない|たりない」, see yourself tarrying at the register, shaking out your shoes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The foot kanji also means add, as in 「足す|たす」, so 「足りる|たりる」 is to be enough; you'll mostly hear 「足りない|たりない」, with the thing as subject, as in 「お金が足りません|おかねがたりません」.",
      "Read 「そく」, it gives 「満足|まんぞく」, full and enough, satisfaction, and 「不足|ふそく」, shortage, as in 「睡眠不足|すいみんぶそく」, lack of sleep.",
      "Put an amount in front for short by that much, as in 「百円足りない|ひゃくえんたりない」, ask 「千円で足りますか|せんえんでたりますか」, and 「物足りない|ものたりない」 means not quite satisfying.",
      "Picture yourself tarrying at a register, counting coins that aren't enough, until you shake out your shoes and a coin falls from your sock."
    ],
    ja: "{お金|おかね}が[足りません|たりません|たりる]。",
    en: "I don't have enough money." },
  "なれる|慣れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「慣|な」, with its native reading, plus 「れる」. On the left side of the kanji you'll see the heart part, a narrow standing form of the heart, the same one in 「忙しい|いそがしい」, busy. Many kanji about feelings and states of mind carry it. Getting used to something happens in your heart and mind. You've met this kanji with its Chinese-origin reading, 「かん」, in 「習慣|しゅうかん」, custom or habit, from lesson fifteen. That's a perfect anchor. A habit is something you've gotten used to. Now the grammar, which is important. The thing you get used to is marked with 「に」, not 「を」: 「日本の生活に慣れました|にほんのせいかつになれました」, I've gotten used to life in Japan. It's a ru-verb, so 「慣れます|なれます」 and 「慣れて|なれて」. Notice that Japanese uses the past tense, 「慣れました|なれました」, for I'm used to it now. You'll also hear 「慣れていません|なれていません」, I'm not used to it yet, very often from new arrivals. Be careful. 「なれる」 also sounds like the potential form of 「なる」, can become. Recap: heart part on the left, the same kanji as in 「習慣|しゅうかん」, habit, and take 「に」 for the thing you get used to." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese can attach this verb to the stem of another verb to say used to doing something. 「使う|つかう」, to use, becomes 「使い慣れる|つかいなれる」, to be used to using. 「使い慣れたパソコン|つかいなれたパソコン」 is the computer you know well. 「見る|みる」 gives 「見慣れる|みなれる」, to be used to seeing, and 「見慣れない人|みなれないひと」 is an unfamiliar face. 「聞き慣れない言葉|ききなれないことば」 is a word you're not used to hearing. These combinations are compact and very natural. If you want to say used to a whole activity, turn the verb into a noun with 「こと」 and add 「に」: 「早く起きることに慣れました|はやくおきることになれました」, I've gotten used to getting up early. Also notice the difference between 「慣れました|なれました」, which says the change has happened, and 「慣れています|なれています」, which describes the ongoing state of being used to it. Both are common. So this verb can stand alone or snap onto other verbs." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "If you spend any time in Japan, someone will ask you 「日本の生活にもう慣れましたか|にほんのせいかつにもうなれましたか」, have you gotten used to life in Japan yet? It's one of the most common friendly questions people ask newcomers. A modest, natural answer is 「はい、だいぶ慣れました|はい、だいぶなれました」, yes, I've gotten quite used to it. Or honestly, 「まだ慣れていません|まだなれていません」, I'm not used to it yet, perhaps followed by what's hard, like the trains or the honorific speech. The noun form is useful too. 「慣れ|なれ」 means familiarity. When you struggle with something, a kind coworker might say 「慣れですよ|なれですよ」, it's just a matter of getting used to it. That's encouragement. You'll also hear 「慣れない仕事|なれないしごと」, unfamiliar work, as a reason someone is tired. Practice the question and both answers, and you'll be ready for a conversation you're sure to have." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here is a real trap. Both 「慣れる|なれる」 and 「なれる」, the potential form of 「なる」, take the particle 「に」. 「先生になれる|せんせいになれる」 means can become a teacher. 「先生に慣れる|せんせいになれる」 means to get used to the teacher. Spoken aloud, they sound the same. How do you tell them apart? Context does most of the work. Becoming a teacher is a goal or dream, often with 「なりたい」 nearby. Getting used to a teacher is about time passing, often with words like 「もう」 or 「まだ」. In writing, the kanji settles it. There is also a neat connection with 「習う|ならう」, to learn. The word 「習慣|しゅうかん」, habit, joins the learn kanji with the get-used-to kanji. Learning something, then getting used to it, is how a habit forms. So keep the context in mind, watch for the kanji, and you won't be fooled by the twin sound." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「慣|な」 has the heart part and is the 「かん」 in 「習慣|しゅうかん」, habit; the thing you get used to takes 「に」, and 「慣れました|なれました」 means I'm used to it now.",
      "Attach it to a verb stem for used to doing, as in 「使い慣れたパソコン|つかいなれたパソコン」 or 「見慣れない人|みなれないひと」, or use 「ことに慣れる|ことになれる」 for a whole activity.",
      "Newcomers always get 「日本の生活にもう慣れましたか|にほんのせいかつにもうなれましたか」, answered with 「だいぶ慣れました|だいぶなれました」, and 「慣れですよ|なれですよ」 encourages that it's just practice.",
      "Can become, 「先生になれる|せんせいになれる」, sounds identical to get used to the teacher, so context helps: dreams versus time passing, with 「もう」 or 「まだ」."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の{生活|せいかつ}に[慣れました|なれました|なれる]。",
    en: "I've gotten used to life in Japan." },
  "おいのりする|お祈りする": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The core is the kanji 「祈|いの」, to pray, read with its native reading, as in the verb 「祈る|いのる」. Its noun form, 「祈り|いのり」, a prayer, takes the polite 「お」 in front and then 「する」 to become a verb: 「お祈りする|おいのりする」, to pray, to offer a prayer. On the left of the kanji is a real and meaningful part. It's the altar or spirit part, which shows up in kanji to do with gods and worship. You'll find it in 「神|じん」 of 「神社|じんじゃ」, shrine, and in 「社|しゃ」, the second kanji of 「神社|じんじゃ」, which on its own can also mean a shrine. So the kanji tells you this has to do with the divine. Culturally, Japanese people often pray at shrines and temples, especially at New Year, 「お正月|おしょうがつ」. At a shrine the common way is to toss a coin, bow twice, clap twice, make your wish or prayer silently, and bow once more. People pray for good health, passing exams, or a safe year. At temples, 「お寺|おてら」, you don't clap. Recap: the spirit part on the left, the noun 「祈り|いのり」 made polite with 「お」, plus 「する」, to pray." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Outside shrines and temples, this word lives in polite letters and emails. A very common closing line is 「ご健康をお祈りしています|ごけんこうをおいのりしています」, I pray for your good health. Another is 「ご成功をお祈りします|ごせいこうをおいのりします」, I wish you success. In these, it means something closer to sincerely wishing for someone, not religious prayer. You'll find these phrases at the end of business emails, holiday cards, and farewell messages. There's a funny side to this too. Japanese companies often end rejection letters with a line wishing the applicant success in the future. Because of that, job hunters jokingly call rejection emails 「お祈りメール|おいのりメール」, prayer emails. Getting one means you didn't get the job. The plain verb 「祈る|いのる」 is a u-verb, so 「祈ります|いのります」 and 「祈って|いのって」. Friends might say 「合格を祈ってるよ|ごうかくをいのってるよ」, I'm praying you pass. So this word covers both the shrine and the inbox." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The pattern here, polite 「お」 plus a noun plus 「する」, matches a phrase you've used since your first week, 「お願いします|おねがいします」. That comes from the verb 「願う|ねがう」, to wish or ask for. Its noun is 「願い|ねがい」, a wish, and with 「お」 it becomes 「お願い|おねがい」. So 「祈る|いのる」 and 「願う|ねがう」 run side by side. What's the difference? 「祈る|いのる」 points toward a god, or toward someone's well-being, with a serious, heartfelt feeling. 「願う|ねがう」 is wider. It's wishing or requesting, often from a person. You ask a favor from a friend with 「お願い|おねがい」, not with prayer. At shrines both appear. You make 「お願い|おねがい」, your wish, through 「お祈り|おいのり」, your prayer. Another word you may hear is 「願い事|ねがいごと」, the specific wish itself. Pair these two verbs in your mind, and both your polite requests and your prayers will make more sense." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The first shrine or temple visit of the new year has its own name, 「初詣|はつもうで」. Millions of people go in the first days of January, and famous shrines get huge crowds. After praying, many people buy an 「おみくじ」, a paper fortune. It tells you your luck for the year, from great luck to bad luck. If it's bad, people often tie it to a rack or a tree branch at the shrine and leave it behind. Many also buy an 「お守り|おまもり」, a small cloth charm. There are charms for health, safe driving, and passing exams. Students before entrance exams often carry one. Another custom is the 「絵馬|えま」, a small wooden plaque. You write your wish on it and hang it with many others at the shrine. Reading other people's wishes is oddly touching. So praying in Japan is often a cheerful, practical thing, tied to the new year and everyday hopes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「祈|いの」 has the altar or spirit part seen in 「神社|じんじゃ」, so the noun 「祈り|いのり」 plus polite 「お」 and 「する」 means to pray; at shrines you bow twice and clap twice.",
      "In letters it means sincerely wishing, as in 「ご健康をお祈りしています|ごけんこうをおいのりしています」, and job hunters call rejection emails 「お祈りメール|おいのりメール」.",
      "It runs beside 「お願いします|おねがいします」 from 「願う|ねがう」, but 「祈る|いのる」 is heartfelt and aimed at a god or someone's well-being, while 「願う|ねがう」 is wider wishing or requesting.",
      "New Year's first visit, 「初詣|はつもうで」, comes with an 「おみくじ」 fortune, an 「お守り|おまもり」 charm, and a wooden 「絵馬|えま」 for writing your wish."
    ],
    ja: "{神社|じんじゃ}で[お祈りしました|おいのりしました|おいのりする]。",
    en: "I prayed at the shrine." },
  "けしょうする|化粧する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first is 「化|け」. Its core meaning is to change or transform. The shape is usually explained as a person standing upright next to a person turned upside down, a person transformed. Its usual reading is 「か」, as in 「文化|ぶんか」, culture, from lesson sixteen. In this word, though, it takes an older, less common reading, 「け」. Watch out for that. The second kanji is 「粧|しょう」, to adorn or decorate oneself. So 「化粧|けしょう」 is changing your look by adorning yourself, putting on makeup. Add 「する」 and it's a verb. You already know a family member from lesson fourteen, 「化粧品|けしょうひん」, cosmetics, where 「品|ひん」 means goods. Makeup plus goods, makeup products. Same reading, 「け」, so the two words support each other. For the verb, you can say 「化粧する|けしょうする」, or 「化粧をする|けしょうをする」. Removing makeup is 「化粧を落とす|けしょうをおとす」, to drop it off. This belongs to the morning-routine set in this lesson, with 「髪をとかす|かみをとかす」 and 「ひげをそる」. Recap: 「化|け」, transform, with its unusual reading, plus 「粧|しょう」, adorn, gives putting on makeup, just like in 「化粧品|けしょうひん」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a word you will see on signs constantly: 「化粧室|けしょうしつ」. It literally means makeup room, but it's a polite word for restroom, used in department stores, hotels, and restaurants. The idea is the room where you fix your makeup. In fact, fixing your makeup has its own phrase, 「化粧を直す|けしょうをなおす」, to touch it up. Polite speakers add 「お」 and say 「お化粧|おけしょう」. Younger people often prefer the loanword 「メイク」, and say 「メイクする」, which sounds a bit more modern and casual. The opposite is a fun colloquial word, 「すっぴん」, a bare face with no makeup. Someone might laugh and say 「今日はすっぴんです|きょうはすっぴんです」, I'm not wearing any makeup today. One manners point: in Japan, putting on makeup on a train is often seen as bad manners. Train companies have even made posters about it. So you'll meet this word on restroom signs, in shops, and in daily chat." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The unusual reading 「け」 for the transform kanji isn't alone. It shows up in a word every Japanese child knows, 「お化け|おばけ」, a ghost or monster. It comes from the verb 「化ける|ばける」, to take on another form, to disguise yourself. In Japanese folklore, foxes and raccoon dogs are famous for changing into people. They 「化ける|ばける」. So putting on makeup and a fox disguising itself share the same kanji of transformation. With the more common reading 「か」, the kanji appears in serious words. 「文化|ぶんか」, culture. 「化学|かがく」, chemistry, the science of how substances change. And 「変化|へんか」, change, where both kanji mean change. Notice 「化学|かがく」 and 「科学|かがく」, science, sound identical. People sometimes call chemistry 「ばけがく」 to keep them apart, using the ghost reading as a joke. So this kanji turns up in cosmetics, culture, chemistry, and ghosts. All of them are about changing form." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Makeup in Japan has a long history, and some of it looks surprising today. For centuries, a white face powder called 「おしろい」 was used to make the skin look pale. You can still see it on geisha and on kabuki actors. Another historic custom was blackening the teeth, called 「お歯黒|おはぐろ」. In the Edo period, married women commonly blackened their teeth, and earlier, aristocratic men did too. Black teeth were considered beautiful and refined. The custom faded after Japan modernized in the late eighteen hundreds. Today, Japanese cosmetics are a huge industry, and many famous brands are Japanese. Natural looking makeup is popular, and skin care gets a lot of attention. For many women, wearing makeup to work is seen as part of looking professional, though attitudes are slowly changing. So this simple verb connects to geisha, old Edo customs, and a modern beauty business." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「化|け」, transform, uses an unusual reading 「け」, and 「粧|しょう」 means adorn, so changing your look with makeup, the same reading as 「化粧品|けしょうひん」; remove it with 「化粧を落とす|けしょうをおとす」.",
      "「化粧室|けしょうしつ」, makeup room, is a polite word for restroom, younger people say 「メイクする」, and a bare face is 「すっぴん」; making up on trains is seen as bad manners.",
      "The 「け」 reading also gives 「お化け|おばけ」 from 「化ける|ばける」, as shapeshifting foxes do, while 「か」 gives 「文化|ぶんか」 and 「化学|かがく」, jokingly called 「ばけがく」.",
      "Historic makeup included white 「おしろい」, still seen on geisha, and blackened teeth, 「お歯黒|おはぐろ」, once considered refined, while today Japanese cosmetics are a huge industry."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は{毎朝|まいあさ}[化粧します|けしょうします|けしょうする]。",
    en: "My mother puts on makeup every morning." },
  "しゅうしょくする|就職する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first is 「就|しゅう」. It means to take up, to settle into a position. The second is 「職|しょく」, a job, an occupation, your profession. So 「就職|しゅうしょく」 is taking up a job, settling into an occupation. With 「する」 it means to get a full-time job. That's a key nuance. It isn't for part-time work, which is 「アルバイト」 from lesson four. It means joining a company or organization properly, as a regular employee, the start of a career. The company you join takes 「に」: 「銀行に就職しました|ぎんこうにしゅうしょくしました」, I got a job at a bank. Culturally, this is a huge life event in Japan. University students go through 「就職活動|しゅうしょくかつどう」, job-hunting activity, usually shortened to 「就活|しゅうかつ」. You know 「活動|かつどう」, activity, from lesson fifteen. Students in their third and fourth years wear matching black suits to interviews, and many companies hire all their new graduates together, starting on the same day in April. Recap: 「就|しゅう」, take up, plus 「職|しょく」, occupation, means getting a real full-time job, with 「に」 for the company." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The job kanji 「職|しょく」 has the ear on its left side, the same shape as 「耳|みみ」, ear. It's a complicated kanji, so focus on that ear as your landmark. It appears in a group of work words. 「職業|しょくぎょう」 means occupation, the word you'd write on a form. 「職場|しょくば」 is your workplace, literally the job place. 「職員|しょくいん」 means staff, especially at schools and city offices, and you might see 「職員室|しょくいんしつ」, the teachers' room. Now the first kanji, 「就|しゅう」. It has a native reading too, 「つく」, meaning to take up a position. There's a formal phrase built from both kanji, 「職に就く|しょくにつく」, to take up a job. It's the same idea as our word, just spread out into a verb phrase. So both kanji have relatives you'll meet in workplaces and on forms. Remember the ear for the job kanji, and these work words will start to stand out." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has a whole family of career words built on the job kanji, and they tell the story of a working life. It starts with 「就職|しゅうしょく」, getting a job. Changing to a different company is 「転職|てんしょく」. The first kanji there means to turn or shift. Changing jobs used to be less common in Japan, where many people stayed at one company for life, but it's much more normal now. Leaving a job is 「退職|たいしょく」, where the first kanji means to withdraw. It covers quitting and also retiring. Retiring at the set age is 「定年退職|ていねんたいしょく」. There's also a near-synonym to know, 「入社|にゅうしゃ」, entering a company. 「就職|しゅうしょく」 is about getting employment in general, while 「入社|にゅうしゃ」 focuses on joining a specific company, and it's used for that first day, 「入社式|にゅうしゃしき」, the welcome ceremony. Learn the family as a timeline: get a job, change jobs, retire." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's how this word sounds in conversation. When a student finally gets a job offer, people say 「就職が決まりました|しゅうしょくがきまりました」, my job has been decided. Friends answer 「就職おめでとう|しゅうしょくおめでとう」, congratulations on the job. The company you'll be working for is your 「就職先|しゅうしょくさき」, literally your job destination. So someone might ask 「就職先はどこですか|しゅうしょくさきはどこですか」, where are you going to work? An informal job offer, given months before graduation, is called 「内定|ないてい」, and students compare how many they've received. A mini dialogue: 「卒業したら、どうするの|そつぎょうしたら、どうするの」? What will you do after graduating? 「東京の会社に就職するんだ|とうきょうのかいしゃにしゅうしょくするんだ」, I'm getting a job at a company in Tokyo. Notice the company takes 「に」, like a destination. With these phrases you can follow the big conversations around graduation." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「就|しゅう」, take up, plus 「職|しょく」, occupation, means getting a real full-time job, not part-time 「アルバイト」, with 「に」 for the company; students go through 「就活|しゅうかつ」 in matching black suits.",
      "「職|しょく」 has an ear on its left as a landmark, appearing in 「職業|しょくぎょう」, 「職場|しょくば」 and 「職員室|しょくいんしつ」, and the formal phrase 「職に就く|しょくにつく」 spreads the word into a verb.",
      "Career words make a timeline: 「就職|しゅうしょく」 get a job, 「転職|てんしょく」 change jobs, 「退職|たいしょく」 leave or retire, while 「入社|にゅうしゃ」 focuses on joining a specific company.",
      "Friends cheer 「就職が決まりました|しゅうしょくがきまりました」 with 「就職おめでとう|しゅうしょくおめでとう」, your future company is your 「就職先|しゅうしょくさき」, and an early informal offer is a 「内定|ないてい」."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は{銀行|ぎんこう}に[就職しました|しゅうしょくしました|しゅうしょくする]。",
    en: "My older brother got a job at a bank." },
  "りこんする|離婚する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings, plus 「する」. The first is 「離|り」, to separate, to leave, to move apart. The second is 「婚|こん」, marriage. On the left of 「婚|こん」 you'll see the woman part, 「女|おんな」, which appears in many kanji about family and marriage. You already know 「婚|こん」 well. It's in 「結婚|けっこん」, marriage, from lesson seven, and 「結婚式|けっこんしき」, wedding ceremony, from lesson fifteen. Here's a lovely pair to memorize together. 「結婚|けっこん」 is 「結|けっ」, to tie, plus marriage. Tying the knot, exactly like English. 「離婚|りこん」 is separating plus marriage. Untying it. Once you see the shared kanji, the two words lock into place. Both become verbs with 「する」. You'll often hear the past tense, 「離婚しました|りこんしました」, they got divorced. The person you divorce takes 「と」, with: 「彼と離婚した|かれとりこんした」, she divorced him. For breaking up as a dating couple, use 「別れる|わかれる」, from lesson twelve, instead. Recap: 「離|り」, separate, plus 「婚|こん」, marriage, the opposite of 「結婚|けっこん」, tying the marriage." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The separate kanji 「離|り」 has a native reading you'll find very useful, 「はなれる」, to be apart, to move away from. 「家族と離れて住んでいます|かぞくとはなれてすんでいます」, I live apart from my family. Its partner 「離す|はなす」 means to let go or set apart, as in letting go of someone's hand. With the Chinese-origin reading 「り」, it appears in 「距離|きょり」, distance, the amount of separation, and in 「離陸|りりく」, takeoff, an airplane leaving the land. On the right side of the kanji is a shape traditionally called the short-tailed bird. You'll see it again in 「難しい|むずかしい」, difficult, and in the first kanji of 「雑誌|ざっし」, magazine. It's a good landmark for spotting the kanji. So separation, distance, letting go, and taking off all share this kanji, and divorce is just separation applied to marriage. Learn 「離れる|はなれる」 next, since it's very common." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The marriage kanji 「婚|こん」 builds a whole little family of words about a couple's life together. 「婚約|こんやく」 is an engagement, literally a marriage promise, and the ring is a 「婚約指輪|こんやくゆびわ」. 「新婚|しんこん」 means newly married, and the honeymoon is 「新婚旅行|しんこんりょこう」, the newlywed trip. Then 「結婚|けっこん」, marriage, and sadly sometimes 「離婚|りこん」, divorce. After that, a person might choose 「再婚|さいこん」, remarriage. The first kanji there means again, the same one in 「再来週|さらいしゅう」, the week after next, though there it's read 「さ」. Notice how each word is just one meaningful kanji placed before the marriage kanji: promise, new, tie, separate, again. That makes them easy to guess when you meet them. Read them in order and you've got the whole story arc of a relationship in Japanese." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, the paperwork for marriage and divorce is surprisingly simple. Most divorces happen by mutual agreement, called 「協議離婚|きょうぎりこん」. The couple fills out a form called a 「離婚届|りこんとどけ」, a divorce notification, and submits it to the city office. No court is needed if both agree. Marriage works the same way, with a marriage form handed in at the city office. There's also a well known expression, 「熟年離婚|じゅくねんりこん」, divorce in later life. It refers to couples who split up after decades of marriage, often around the time the husband retires and suddenly spends all day at home. The media have talked about it a lot. Divorce used to carry more stigma than it does now, and it has become more common over the past few decades. So behind this word is a simple form, a city office counter, and changing ideas about marriage." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「離|り」, separate, plus 「婚|こん」, marriage, is the untying partner of 「結婚|けっこん」, tying the knot; the person takes 「と」, and dating couples breaking up use 「別れる|わかれる」 instead.",
      "「離|り」 natively is 「離れる|はなれる」, to be apart, and read 「り」 it gives 「距離|きょり」, distance, and 「離陸|りりく」, takeoff, with a short-tailed bird on the right as a landmark.",
      "Put one kanji before marriage for the whole story: 「婚約|こんやく」 engagement, 「新婚|しんこん」 newlyweds, 「結婚|けっこん」, 「離婚|りこん」, and 「再婚|さいこん」, remarriage.",
      "Most divorces are by mutual agreement, 「協議離婚|きょうぎりこん」, just a 「離婚届|りこんとどけ」 form at the city office, and 「熟年離婚|じゅくねんりこん」 is divorce after decades of marriage."
    ],
    ja: "ふたりは[離婚しました|りこんしました|りこんする]。",
    en: "The two of them got divorced." },
  "ずいぶん|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "An adverb usually written in hiragana. There are kanji for it, but you'll mostly see it in kana. It means very, quite, or considerably. What makes it different from 「とても」 or 「すごく」 is a feeling of surprise. 「ずいぶん」 is often used when something is more than you expected, especially compared to before. So 「ずいぶん寒いですね|ずいぶんさむいですね」 has the feel of wow, it's gotten pretty cold, hasn't it. 「ずいぶん上手になりましたね|ずいぶんじょうずになりましたね」, you've gotten so much better. That comparison idea makes it a great match for 「〜に比べて|〜にくらべて」, compared with, also in this lesson. A small caution. Because it carries surprise, it can sound a bit pointed if you use it about someone in a critical way, like you're quite late, aren't you. With praise and weather, it's warm and natural. In style it's a little more mature and gentle than casual 「すごく」. A memory hook, and it is just a hook: imagine something zooming way past what you expected, zoo-ee-boon. Recap: very or quite, with a touch of surprise, often meaning more than I expected or more than before." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This adverb is more flexible than 「とても」 in one important way. It works well with verbs that describe time or amount. 「ずいぶん待ちました|ずいぶんまちました」 means I waited quite a while. Try that with 「とても」 and it sounds odd. 「ずいぶん歩きましたね|ずいぶんあるきましたね」, we walked a long way, didn't we. It also sits naturally in front of words about time. 「ずいぶん久しぶりですね|ずいぶんひさしぶりですね」 is a classic greeting when you meet someone after ages. There's another, sharper use. Add 「な」 and put it before a noun: 「ずいぶんな言い方|ずいぶんないいかた」 means that's a harsh way to put it. Here it suggests something is excessive or unkind. Said alone, 「ずいぶんですね」 can mean that's a bit much. Most of the time, though, you'll use it as a plain adverb with adjectives and verbs. Remember that it happily modifies verbs of waiting and walking, and you'll find many chances to use it." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several words for very or quite, and choosing well makes you sound natural. 「とても」 is the textbook standard, neutral and polite. 「すごく」 is the casual, energetic version you hear among friends. 「かなり」 means fairly or considerably, and sounds a bit more objective, like you're measuring. 「だいぶ」 is about change, how far something has moved, as in 「だいぶ慣れました|だいぶなれました」, I've gotten pretty used to it. 「けっこう」 means more than you'd think, often with mild, pleasant surprise: 「けっこうおいしい」, it's surprisingly good. And our word adds a feeling of noticing how much something has changed or exceeded expectations. A useful pair to compare is 「だいぶ」 and 「ずいぶん」. Both describe change, but ours carries more emotion and surprise. With 「とても」 for safety and the others for color, you can fine tune exactly how impressed you sound." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word is usually written in hiragana, but its kanji form is 「随分|ずいぶん」. The second kanji is the familiar 「分|ぶん」 from words like 「自分|じぶん」, oneself, and 「半分|はんぶん」, half, where it means a portion or share. The first kanji, 「随|ずい」, means to follow or go along with. Dictionaries explain that the word originally meant something like in keeping with one's share or ability, doing as much as your portion allowed. Over time the meaning drifted toward quite a lot, a considerable amount, which is how it's used today. You won't need the kanji for reading most modern texts, since kana is the usual spelling. Still, knowing the pieces helps. You can see the idea of an amount hiding inside the word, in that 「分|ぶん」. So a word that once meant matching your share now means more than you expected." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Unlike 「とても」, it carries surprise that something is more than expected or than before, as in 「ずいぶん上手になりましたね|ずいぶんじょうずになりましたね」; it's warm with praise but can sound pointed in criticism.",
      "It happily modifies verbs of time and amount, as in 「ずいぶん待ちました|ずいぶんまちました」 or 「ずいぶん久しぶりですね|ずいぶんひさしぶりですね」, and with 「な」 before a noun it means harsh or excessive.",
      "「とても」 is neutral, 「すごく」 casual, 「かなり」 objective, 「けっこう」 mildly surprised, and 「だいぶ」 measures change, while 「ずいぶん」 adds more emotion and surprise.",
      "Its kanji form is 「随分|ずいぶん」, follow plus share, once meaning in keeping with one's share, which drifted to quite a lot, more than you expected."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}は[ずいぶん]{寒い|さむい}ですね。",
    en: "It's quite cold today, isn't it?" },
  "たとえば|例えば": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "One kanji, 「例|たと」, with its native reading, plus 「えば」. The kanji means example. On the left is the slim person part, and you'll see it read with its Chinese-origin reading, 「れい」, in words like 「例文|れいぶん」, example sentence. Where does the word come from? The native verb 「たとえる」 means to compare one thing to another, to liken it, and 「たとえば」 is built from an old if form of that verb. So it is literally if I compare it, if I give a likeness. That's exactly what you're doing when you say for example. You're offering something to compare with. Now usage. It works just like English for example. Put it before the examples you're giving: 「日本の食べ物、たとえばすしが好きです|にほんのたべもの、たとえばすしがすきです」, I like Japanese food, for example sushi. You can also start a sentence with it to offer a suggestion, like for instance, how about Saturday? It often pairs with 「とか」 or 「や」 to list a few examples together. It's neutral in politeness, fine in conversation and in writing. Recap: 「例|たと」, example, from an old if form of 「たとえる」, to liken, gives for example." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The example kanji, read 「れい」, gives you a handful of useful words. On its own, 「例|れい」 means an example. Teachers say 「例を見てください|れいをみてください」, please look at the example. 「例外|れいがい」 means exception, literally outside the examples. Grammar books are full of them. There's also an interesting everyday use: 「例の|れいの」, the usual or that one we talked about. 「例の店に行こう|れいのみせにいこう」 means let's go to that place, you know the one. It's a bit like a wink between people who share knowledge. And 「例年|れいねん」 means an ordinary year, used in weather news, like warmer than usual for this time of year. So the native reading 「たと」 shows up in our word and in 「例える|たとえる」, to liken, while 「れい」 shows up in these nouns. Two readings, one idea of an example or model. Collect them together and this kanji will feel familiar everywhere." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In conversation this word does more than introduce examples. One of its most common uses is as a one-word question. If someone says they like sports, you can ask 「たとえば」? with a rising tone, meaning like what? It's a natural way to keep someone talking. It also softens suggestions. Instead of saying let's meet Saturday, you can say 「たとえば、土曜日はどうですか|たとえば、どようびはどうですか」, how about Saturday, for instance? That leaves room for the other person to propose something else, which feels polite. The related verb gives another phrase, 「例えると|たとえると」, if I put it in a comparison. People use it before a metaphor: 「例えると、人生はマラソンです|たとえると、じんせいはマラソンです」, to put it another way, life is a marathon. A mini dialogue: 「趣味は何ですか|しゅみはなんですか」? 「スポーツです」. 「たとえば」? 「テニスとか、水泳とか|テニスとか、すいえいとか」. Use this little question and your conversations will flow more easily." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on an English sound-alike. Picture a tattoo parlor. You sit down, nervous, and the artist slaps a thick sample book on the table. Tattoo? Eh, ba? The artist starts flipping pages, pointing. For example, a dragon. For example, a cherry blossom. For example, a slightly confused beaver. Every page is another for example. Tattoo, eh, ba, for example. Now add the kanji. The left side of 「例|たと」 is the slim person part, so imagine that person is you, leaning in toward the sample book, choosing from the examples lined up in front of you. Whenever you hear 「たとえば」, picture that tattoo artist holding up page after page of examples. And whenever you want to give an example in Japanese, remember the book. It's a silly scene, but it makes the word stick." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from an old if form of 「たとえる」, to liken, so if I give a likeness; put it before examples, as in 「日本の食べ物、たとえばすしが好きです|にほんのたべもの、たとえばすしがすきです」.",
      "Read 「れい」, the kanji gives 「例|れい」, an example, 「例外|れいがい」, an exception, 「例年|れいねん」, an ordinary year, and 「例の店|れいのみせ」, you know, that place.",
      "Asked alone with a rising tone, 「たとえば」 means like what, it softens suggestions like 「たとえば、土曜日はどうですか|たとえば、どようびはどうですか」, and 「例えると|たとえると」 introduces a metaphor.",
      "Picture a tattoo parlor, tattoo, eh, ba, where the artist flips through a sample book, for example a dragon, for example a confused beaver."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の{食べ物|たべもの}、[例えば|たとえば]すしが{好き|すき}です。",
    en: "I like Japanese food, for example sushi." },
  "〜にくらべて|〜に比べて": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This expression is the particle 「に」 plus 「比べて|くらべて」, the te-form of the verb 「比べる|くらべる」, to compare. The kanji 「比|くら」 is read with its native reading. Its shape is real and easy to see. It's two identical figures standing side by side, usually explained as two people lined up next to each other. When you put two things side by side, you compare them. So 「に比べて|にくらべて」 is literally side by side with, compared to. In use, the thing you're comparing against comes before 「に比べて|にくらべて」: 「東京に比べて、この町はしずかです|とうきょうにくらべて、このまちはしずかです」, compared with Tokyo, this town is quiet. Notice it's a little different from 「より」, which you learned for comparisons in lesson ten. 「より」 is short and simple, A is bigger than B. 「に比べて|にくらべて」 sounds a bit more formal and thoughtful, like you're carefully weighing two things. It's common in essays, the news, and reports. The plain verb 「比べる|くらべる」 also works alone: 「二つを比べる|ふたつをくらべる」, to compare two things. Recap: the two figures side by side mean compare, and 「に比べて|にくらべて」 means compared with." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This pattern has several close variants. 「と比べて|とくらべて」 is also correct and means nearly the same thing, with 「と」 suggesting the two items are compared on more equal footing. 「に比べると|にくらべると」 uses the conditional and means when compared with. It's very common in speech. In writing, especially news and reports, you'll see the shortened 「に比べ|にくらべ」, with the te-form ending dropped. A favorite use is comparing with the past. 「去年に比べて、今年はずいぶん暑いです|きょねんにくらべて、ことしはずいぶんあついです」, compared with last year, this year is quite hot. That pairs nicely with 「ずいぶん」 from this lesson. You can also combine it with the comparison style from lesson ten: 「東京に比べて、大阪のほうが安いです|とうきょうにくらべて、おおさかのほうがやすいです」, compared with Tokyo, Osaka is cheaper. So the pattern stretches easily. Remember these variants and you'll recognize comparisons wherever you see them." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「比べる|くらべる」 shows up in some lively expressions. One very common one is 「比べ物にならない|くらべものにならない」, there's no comparison. Literally, it's not even something you can compare. People say it when one thing is far better: 「このラーメンは、ほかの店とは比べ物にならない|このラーメンは、ほかのみせとはくらべものにならない」, this ramen is in a different league. The stem 「比べ|くらべ」 also sticks onto nouns to make contests. 「背比べ|せいくらべ」 means comparing heights, standing back to back. There's a well known children's song about two brothers comparing heights. 「力比べ|ちからくらべ」 is a contest of strength. With the Chinese-origin reading 「ひ」, the kanji appears in 「比較|ひかく」, comparison, a formal word for essays and science. So you have casual contests, a strong compliment, and a formal academic word. All come from putting two things side by side." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick built on sound. The word 「くらべる」 starts like the English word club. Picture two club members at a fancy social club, standing side by side in front of a giant mirror. They're comparing everything. Whose suit is nicer, whose hair is shinier, whose shoes are taller. Club, eh, ruh. The club is all about comparing. Now bring in the kanji 「比|くら」. Its shape looks like two figures standing side by side, and that's exactly what our two club members are doing in front of the mirror. One more detail to lock in the particle: the club rules say you always compare to someone, so you need 「に」. When you hear 「に比べて|にくらべて」, picture those vain club members side by side at the mirror, checking who comes out ahead. The sillier you make them, the better it will stick." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「比|くら」 shows two figures side by side, so compare, and the thing you compare against comes before it; it sounds more formal and thoughtful than 「より」.",
      "Variants include 「と比べて|とくらべて」, 「に比べると|にくらべると」 in speech, and 「に比べ|にくらべ」 in news, and it pairs nicely with 「ずいぶん」 for comparing with the past.",
      "「比べ物にならない|くらべものにならない」 means there's no comparison, 「背比べ|せいくらべ」 is comparing heights, and read 「ひ」 it gives the formal 「比較|ひかく」.",
      "Two vain club members stand side by side at a mirror comparing everything, club, eh, ruh, and the club rules say you always compare to someone, with 「に」."
    ],
    ja: "{東京|とうきょう}[に比べて|にくらべて]、この{町|まち}はしずかです。",
    en: "Compared with Tokyo, this town is quiet." },
  "〜によると|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This expression means according to, and it's used to name your source of information. It's built from the particle 「に」 plus 「よる」, a verb meaning to depend on or be based on, plus 「と」, which here works like when or if. So, roughly, if you go by this source. Now the key grammar point that this lesson teaches. Sentences with 「によると」 almost always end with 「そうです」, meaning I hear that or they say. That's the hearsay ending. You're reporting what you learned, not claiming it yourself. 「天気予報によると、明日は雨だそうです|てんきよほうによると、あしたはあめだそうです」, according to the weather forecast, it will rain tomorrow. You'll use it constantly with sources like 「ニュース」, the news, 「新聞|しんぶん」, the newspaper, or a person. 「先生によると|せんせいによると」, according to the teacher. A common mistake English speakers make is forgetting 「そうです」 at the end, which makes it sound like you're stating the fact yourself. Always close the loop. Recap: source plus 「によると」, then the news, then 「そうです」, according to, I hear that." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This expression has a slightly more formal twin, 「によれば」. It uses the other conditional ending, and the meaning is the same, according to. You'll see it more in writing. The same verb 「よる」 also gives a very useful related pattern, 「によって」, which means depending on. 「人によって違います|ひとによってちがいます」, it differs from person to person. Or simply 「場合によります|ばあいによります」, it depends on the situation, a handy answer to tricky questions. Back to reporting. Besides 「そうです」, the sentence can end with 「ということです」, which sounds a bit more formal, or 「らしいです」, which adds a feeling that it seems to be so. All of them show the information comes from somewhere else. One more tip: for something you read yourself, you can also say 「新聞で読みました|しんぶんでよみました」, I read it in the paper, with 「で」 for the source. So one little verb gives you according to and depending on." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Turn on the Japanese news and you'll hear this expression in its polite form, 「によりますと」. Newscasters use the polite masu form even in the middle of the sentence. 「警察によりますと|けいさつによりますと」, according to the police. 「気象庁によりますと|きしょうちょうによりますと」, according to the Meteorological Agency. Learning to catch that phrase is a great listening skill, because what comes next is the news itself. In daily life, people use the plain form when passing on information. A coworker might say 「部長によると、会議は三時からだそうです|ぶちょうによると、かいぎはさんじからだそうです」, according to the manager, the meeting starts at three. Friends share gossip the same way: 「うわさによると|うわさによると」, rumor has it. Notice that the source can be a person, an organization, a forecast, or even a rumor. The pattern stays the same. Listen for 「によりますと」 on the news, and you'll know a report is coming." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch out, because Japanese has two different 「そうです」, and they connect differently. The hearsay one, which you use with 「によると」, comes after a full plain form. 「雨が降るそうです|あめがふるそうです」, I hear it will rain. 「おいしいそうです」, I hear it's delicious. The other one means looks like or seems about to, and it attaches to a stem. 「雨が降りそうです|あめがふりそうです」, it looks like it's going to rain. 「おいしそうです」, it looks delicious. Listen to the difference: 「おいしいそうです」 keeps the full 「い」, and means someone told you. 「おいしそうです」 drops it, and means you can see for yourself. With 「によると」 you always want the hearsay version, because you're quoting a source. For nouns, the hearsay version needs 「だ」: 「明日は休みだそうです|あしたはやすみだそうです」. Remember, full plain form for what you heard, stem for what you see, and you won't mix them up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「に」 plus 「よる」, be based on, plus 「と」, so if you go by this source, and the sentence almost always ends with hearsay 「そうです」; don't forget it.",
      "The formal twin is 「によれば」, the same verb gives 「によって」, depending on, as in 「場合によります|ばあいによります」, and endings like 「らしいです」 also mark secondhand information.",
      "News anchors say 「によりますと」, as in 「警察によりますと|けいさつによりますと」, so catching that phrase tells you the news itself is next; the source can even be 「うわさ」, a rumor.",
      "Hearsay 「そうです」 follows a full plain form, 「おいしいそうです」, while looks-like attaches to a stem, 「おいしそうです」, so with 「によると」 always use the full form."
    ],
    ja: "{天気予報|てんきよほう}[によると]、{明日|あした}は{雨|あめ}だそうです。",
    en: "According to the weather forecast, it will rain tomorrow." },
  "まえ|前": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You've known 「前|まえ」, front, for a long time, with its native reading. In front of the station, in front of the house. In this lesson it gets a time meaning, before. That makes sense if you think of time as a line. The front of an event is the part before it. The kanji also shows up in words you know with its Chinese-origin reading, 「ぜん」. 「午前|ごぜん」, morning, from lesson one, literally before noon. And 「名前|なまえ」, name, with the native reading. The grammar point is the big one here. To say before doing something, use the verb's dictionary form plus 「前に|まえに」: 「寝る前に|ねるまえに」, before sleeping. Here's the trap. Even when you're talking about the past, the verb before 「前|まえ」 stays in dictionary form. Last night, before I slept, I brushed my teeth. You still say 「寝る前に|ねるまえに」, never the past form. That's because the action hadn't happened yet at that point. With nouns, use 「の」: 「食事の前に|しょくじのまえに」, before the meal. With time, just add it: 「三年前|さんねんまえ」, three years ago. Recap: front becomes before, and verbs stay in dictionary form before 「前に|まえに」, even in the past." },
      { title: "Before and after", text: "The best way to lock in 「前に|まえに」 is to put it next to its mirror image, 「後で|あとで」, after. The two look like twins, but they treat verbs differently. Before takes the dictionary form, because the action hasn't happened yet. After takes the past form, because the action is already done. So 「食べる前に|たべるまえに」 is before eating, and 「食べた後で|たべたあとで」 is after eating. Same verb, two different shapes. Picture a timeline with a meal in the middle. On the left, the meal is still in the future, so the verb looks forward. On the right, the meal is finished, so the verb looks back. There is also a third option you know, the te-form plus 「から」, as in 「食べてから|たべてから」, after eating, which feels like and then. When you mix these up, the timeline picture fixes it. Dictionary form on the left, past form on the right." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Listen for this word all over Japanese towns. Bus stops and shops are often named after what they sit in front of, so you see 「駅前|えきまえ」, in front of the station, on signs everywhere. A shopping street by the station is a classic 「駅前|えきまえ」 street. On a train, the announcement may tell you the stop is the one before, 「一つ前|ひとつまえ」. When you talk about how long ago something was, add it straight after a time amount: 「一週間前|いっしゅうかんまえ」, a week ago, 「十分前|じゅっぷんまえ」, ten minutes ago. Notice there is no particle in between. One more word to recognize but not to use. 「お前|おまえ」 is a rough, very casual way to say you, heard in anime and among close male friends. It once was respectful, but today it can sound rude, so stick with the person's name. A small word with a big everyday job." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Imagine you are standing in a long line at a ramen shop. The person directly in front of you turns around, holds up a hand, and says, ma, eh, wait, I was here first. They are in front of you, so they come before you. In this one scene you have both meanings at once, the place in front and the time before. Now add the kanji. Picture the person in front holding up a big sign written with 「前|まえ」, and the shop clock above it showing it is still morning, 「午前|ごぜん」, the time before noon. The same kanji sits in your own 「名前|なまえ」, your name, the thing you say first, before anything else, when you meet someone. Front, before, morning, and name, all in one ramen line." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Front turns into before in time, and the verb before 「前に|まえに」 stays in dictionary form even in the past, as in 「寝る前に|ねるまえに」, because the action hadn't happened yet.",
      "Pair it with its mirror 「後で|あとで」: before takes the dictionary form, after takes the past form, so 「食べる前に|たべるまえに」 versus 「食べた後で|たべたあとで」.",
      "You'll see 「駅前|えきまえ」, in front of the station, on signs everywhere, and it goes straight after time amounts with no particle, as in 「一週間前|いっしゅうかんまえ」, a week ago.",
      "Picture the person in front of you in a ramen line saying ma, eh, wait, I was here first, holding a sign with 「前|まえ」, so they're both in front and before you."
    ],
    ja: "{寝る|ねる}[前|まえ]に{歯|は}をみがきます。",
    en: "I brush my teeth before I go to bed." },
  "やっぱり|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A very common, very natural conversation word, written in hiragana. It's the emphatic, spoken version of 「やはり」, a more formal word. In speech, Japanese often adds a little pause, the small 「っ」, to give a word more punch. 「やはり」 becomes 「やっぱり」. You'll hear both, but 「やっぱり」 is the everyday one. It has two main uses. First, as expected, just as I thought. You suspected something and it turned out true. 「やっぱり雨だ|やっぱりあめだ」, it's raining, just as I thought. Second, after all, when you change your mind and go back to an earlier choice. You're ordering coffee, you think about tea, then you say, 「やっぱりコーヒーにします」, actually, I'll have coffee after all. Both uses share one idea, coming back to what you expected or where you started. It's also used for strong opinions that feel natural, like 「やっぱりすしがいちばんです」, sushi really is the best. You'll hear it constantly in casual and polite speech alike. A memory hook, and it is just a hook: imagine a yacht, yah, that went out, then pulled back to shore. Recap: as expected or after all, coming back to what you thought." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The small 「っ」 that turns 「やはり」 into 「やっぱり」 is part of a whole family of punchier spoken words. Once you notice it, you will hear it everywhere. 「とても」, very, becomes 「とっても」, very very. 「すごく」, really, becomes 「すっごく」 when someone is excited. 「ばかり」, only, becomes 「ばっかり」, nothing but. 「あまり」 does something similar with a 「ん」 instead and becomes 「あんまり」. In each case, the meaning stays the same but the feeling gets stronger and more casual. Young people go even further and shorten our word to just 「やっぱ」, which you will hear in casual chat and dramas. So you have a little ladder. 「やはり」 sounds careful and a bit formal, good for writing and speeches. 「やっぱり」 is everyday speech. 「やっぱ」 is very casual. Choose the step that fits the situation." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Two tiny phrases built on this word are worth stealing. The first is 「やっぱりね」, said with a knowing smile, meaning I knew it. A friend admits they forgot their homework, and you say 「やっぱりね」. The second is very handy in shops. When a clerk offers you something and you start to say yes, then change your mind, you can say 「やっぱりいいです」, actually, never mind, I'm fine. The 「いいです」 here means no thanks, not good. You can also use it on its own as a full answer. Someone asks which one you chose in the end, and you sigh and say 「やっぱり、これ」, this one after all. A common mistake is to use it for simple surprise. It is not wow. It always points back to an expectation or an earlier choice. So keep it for I knew it and I'll go back to my first idea." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the punchy spoken form of 「やはり」, and it means either as expected or after all, both about coming back to what you thought or chose first.",
      "The small 「っ」 makes spoken words punchier, like 「とっても」 and 「すっごく」, giving a ladder from formal 「やはり」 to everyday 「やっぱり」 to very casual 「やっぱ」.",
      "Steal two phrases: 「やっぱりね」 for I knew it, and 「やっぱりいいです」 for actually, never mind, but don't use it for simple surprise."
    ],
    ja: "[やっぱり]すしがいちばんおいしいです。",
    en: "Sushi really is the tastiest, after all." },
  "〜かな|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A sentence-ending expression made of two particles you know. 「か」 is the question marker. 「な」 is a reflective, talking-to-yourself particle. Together, 「かな」 is like asking yourself a question out loud, I wonder. 「明日は雨かな|あしたはあめかな」, I wonder if it will rain tomorrow. It's casual. You use it in plain speech, talking with friends or murmuring to yourself, not with a teacher or boss. In polite situations, use 「でしょうか」 instead. How do you attach it? Put it after the plain form of verbs and i-adjectives: 「来るかな|くるかな」, I wonder if they'll come, 「おいしいかな」, I wonder if it's tasty. After nouns and na-adjectives, attach it directly: 「雨かな|あめかな」. Don't put 「だ」 before it. Even when you say it to someone, it sounds like thinking aloud, which actually makes it a gentle way to ask. It invites the other person to answer without pressure. Some people say 「かしら」 instead, which has a softer, more traditionally feminine feel. Recap: question 「か」 plus reflective 「な」 means I wonder, casual, after plain forms, with 「でしょうか」 as its polite partner." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Once you have the basic I wonder, a few small patterns open up. Put a negative in front and it becomes a wish. 「早く来ないかな|はやくこないかな」 literally means I wonder if they won't come soon, but it really means I wish they'd hurry up and come. Kids waiting for summer say 「夏休みにならないかな|なつやすみにならないかな」. Add the volitional form and you are making up your mind. 「何を食べようかな|なにをたべようかな」, what shall I eat, I wonder. It is the sound of someone staring at a menu. Add 「と思う|とおもう」 after it to soften your own idea. 「行こうかなと思います|いこうかなとおもいます」, I'm kind of thinking of going. That last one is polite enough to use with almost anyone, because the 「思います|おもいます」 carries the politeness. Three patterns, one gentle little ending." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Don't mix this ending up with another word that sounds exactly the same. 「仮名|かな」 is the name of the Japanese phonetic scripts, the ones you learned first. 「ひらがな」 and 「カタカナ」 both end with it, with the sound changing to 「がな」 inside the compound. So if a teacher says please write this in 「かな」, they mean the script, not I wonder. Context sorts them out easily. The ending comes at the very end of a sentence, usually with a thoughtful, slightly rising or trailing tone. The script name is a normal noun in the middle of a sentence, often followed by a particle like 「で」, in kana. There is also 「かなあ」 with a stretched vowel, which just sounds more dreamy and drawn out, someone really pondering. Same meaning, longer sigh." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Question 「か」 plus reflective 「な」 gives a casual I wonder, attached to plain forms or straight to nouns without 「だ」, with 「でしょうか」 as the polite partner.",
      "A negative before it makes a wish, like 「早く来ないかな|はやくこないかな」, and the volitional makes a decision, like 「何を食べようかな|なにをたべようかな」.",
      "Don't confuse it with 「仮名|かな」, the name of the scripts, which sits mid-sentence as a noun, while the ending comes last with a thoughtful tone."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}は{来る|くる}[かな]。",
    en: "I wonder if the teacher will come." },
  "そうか|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "A casual reply you'll hear constantly in conversation. It's made of 「そう」, so or like that, plus 「か」, the question particle. But here 「か」 isn't asking anything. Said with a falling tone, it's realizing something. Oh, I see. So that's how it is. The polite version is 「そうですか」, which you already know well. 「そうか」 just drops the 「です」 for casual speech, so use it with friends and family, not with a teacher or boss. A common mistake is the intonation. If your voice goes up at the end, it sounds like you doubt it, really? Let it fall gently for I see. You'll also hear a softer version, 「そっか」, with a little pause, which sounds friendly and relaxed. Some speakers, often men, say 「そうか」 with a slow, thoughtful tone when they're taking in news. It often comes right before another reaction, like 「そうか、わかった」, I see, got it. A memory hook, and it is just a hook: so, ka, so, now I know. It's the casual cousin of 「なるほど」, which also means I see. Recap: 「そう」 plus 「か」 with a falling tone, the casual 「そうですか」, meaning I see." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese conversation is full of small reaction words, and this one has lots of relatives. Here is how they differ. 「そうですか」 is the polite I see. 「そうか」 and 「そっか」 are the casual versions. 「そうなんだ」 is also casual, but it adds a sense of oh, so that's how it is, I didn't know. Its polite form is 「そうなんですか」, which sounds more interested than plain 「そうですか」. Then there is 「なるほど」, which means I see in the sense of that makes sense, I understand the logic. Use it when someone explains a reason. Finally 「そうですね」 is different. It doesn't mean I see. It means I agree, or that's right, or it's a filler while you think. A common mistake is answering new information with 「そうですね」. Save it for agreeing. For brand new facts, reach for 「そうですか」 or 「そうか」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese listeners keep up a steady stream of little noises while someone else talks. These are called 「相づち|あいづち」. The word comes from two blacksmiths hammering the same piece of metal in turn, so each strike answers the other. Words like 「そうか」, 「うん」, and 「へえ」 are those answering strikes. They don't always mean I agree. They mean I'm with you, keep going. An English speaker who listens in silence can seem cold or confused to a Japanese speaker, and a Japanese speaker on the phone who hears nothing may ask if you are still there. So try sprinkling 「そうか」 or 「そっか」 into casual chats with friends, and 「そうですか」 in polite ones. It makes your Japanese feel warm and natural, even when your vocabulary is small. Good listening, in Japanese, is something you can hear." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「そう」 plus 「か」 with a falling tone for oh, I see, the casual form of 「そうですか」, and a rising tone would sound doubtful instead.",
      "Among its relatives, 「そうなんだ」 adds I didn't know, 「なるほど」 means that makes sense, and 「そうですね」 means I agree, so don't use it for brand new facts.",
      "It's one of the 「相づち|あいづち」, little listening noises like hammer strikes answering each other, which mean I'm with you, keep going, rather than I agree."
    ],
    ja: "[そうか]、{明日|あした}は{休み|やすみ}なんだ。",
    en: "I see, so tomorrow is a day off." },
  "おめでとうございます|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a set phrase, but it has a real history that helps it make sense. It comes from the old adjective 「めでたい」, meaning happy, auspicious, worth celebrating. When polite 「ございます」 attaches to an adjective like this, the ending changes sound over time and becomes long 「とう」. Add polite 「お」 at the front, and you get 「おめでとうございます」. Here's the best part. You already know a word built in exactly the same way, 「ありがとうございます」. It comes from 「ありがたい」, which means rare, precious, something you're grateful for. Same sound change, same ending. Once you see the pattern, both phrases feel less random. In casual speech you can drop the polite part: 「おめでとう」. Put the occasion in front. 「お誕生日おめでとうございます|おたんじょうびおめでとうございます」, happy birthday. 「ご結婚おめでとうございます|ごけっこんおめでとうございます」, congratulations on your wedding. And at New Year, 「お正月|おしょうがつ」, everyone says 「あけましておめでとうございます」, happy New Year, literally congratulations on the year opening. Recap: from 「めでたい」, worth celebrating, built just like 「ありがとうございます」, said for birthdays, weddings, and New Year." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "The New Year version has some careful timing. You only say 「あけましておめでとうございます」 once the new year has actually begun, usually during the first week or so of January. Before New Year, in late December, the parting greeting is 「よいお年を|よいおとしを」, have a good year. People also send New Year cards, 「年賀状|ねんがじょう」, timed by the post office to arrive on January first. There is an important exception. If someone's close family member died during the year, that family doesn't celebrate. They send a short card in early winter saying they are in mourning, and friends skip the congratulations to them. For weddings, guests give money in a special decorated envelope rather than a gift from a list. So this happy phrase comes with real manners around it. Say it at the right moment, and people notice." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase is a little machine. You put almost any happy event in front, and it works. 「合格おめでとう|ごうかくおめでとう」, congratulations on passing the exam. 「卒業おめでとうございます|そつぎょうおめでとうございます」, congratulations on graduating. 「就職おめでとう|しゅうしょくおめでとう」, congratulations on the new job. And for a new baby, 「ご出産おめでとうございます|ごしゅっさんおめでとうございます」. Use the short 「おめでとう」 with friends and family, and the full form with teachers, colleagues, and anyone older. The usual reply is simply 「ありがとうございます」, often with a little bow. You can also reply 「ありがとう」 to a friend. One thing it never does is soften bad news. There is no congratulations-on-surviving-the-flu use. Keep it for genuinely happy events, put the event in front, and you are set." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Imagine a party where a very proud goat has just won a prize at the county fair. Everyone crowds around and shouts, oh, meh, de toe! The goat bleats meh, then taps a ribbon on its toe. Ridiculous, but it holds the sound in place. Now make the scene do grammar work. The goat's owner, being polite, bows deeply and adds the long ending, 「ございます」, just as people do when they say 「ありがとうございます」. Two goats at the same fair, one getting congratulated and one saying thank you, both with the same polite tail. Whenever you hear one of these phrases, let the other pop into your head too. They really are built the same way." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「めでたい」, worth celebrating, with the same sound change and polite tail as 「ありがとうございます」, which comes from 「ありがたい」.",
      "Say 「あけましておめでとうございます」 only once the new year has begun, use 「よいお年を|よいおとしを」 before it, and skip it for families in mourning.",
      "Put almost any happy event in front, like 「合格おめでとう|ごうかくおめでとう」, use the short form with friends and the full form with elders, and reply with 「ありがとうございます」.",
      "Picture a prize goat at the county fair as the crowd shouts oh, meh, de toe, while its owner bows and adds 「ございます」 just like 「ありがとうございます」."
    ],
    ja: "ご{結婚|けっこん}[おめでとうございます]！",
    en: "Congratulations on your marriage!" },
  "ソファ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one is borrowed straight from English sofa. The English word has a long journey behind it. It came through French and Turkish from the Arabic word for a raised bench or platform with cushions. Japanese has no true f sound of its own. The closest native sound is 「ふ」, made by blowing air lightly through the lips. To spell the fa in sofa, Japanese takes 「フ」 and adds a small 「ァ」, giving 「ファ」. You will also see and hear 「ソファー」 with a long vowel at the end. Both spellings are common and mean the same thing. It lives in the same family as other furniture and household loanwords you know, like 「カーテン」, 「ドア」, and 「テレビ」. Notice that a lot of Western-style furniture in Japan simply kept its English name, while the general word for furniture, 「家具|かぐ」, is a kanji word. Many Japanese apartments are small, so a two-seat sofa facing the TV is the classic setup, and people often sit on the floor and lean back against it. To sit on the sofa, say 「ソファにすわる」. To sum up, it is English sofa, with the f squeezed into 「ファ」." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The 「ファ」 in this word belongs to a set of newer katakana combinations made for foreign sounds. Japanese takes 「フ」 and adds a small vowel to get a full row: 「ファ」, 「フィ」, 「フェ」, 「フォ」. You meet them all the time. 「ファン」, a fan of a band or team. 「ファイル」, a file. 「カフェ」, a café. 「フォーク」, a fork. In older loanwords you sometimes see a simpler spelling with just 「フ」 or even 「ホ」, but modern words keep the f sound with these pairs. A good habit is to read the small vowel as one quick beat with the 「フ」, not as two separate sounds. So it is so, fa, two beats in total, not so, fu, a. Once this row feels natural, a whole shelf of borrowed words becomes easy to read." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is a nice chance to practice choosing particles. When you sit down on the sofa, the sofa is where you land, so use 「に」. 「ソファに座る|ソファにすわる」, to sit on the sofa. When you do an activity there, the sofa is the place of action, so use 「で」. 「ソファで寝る|ソファでねる」, to sleep on the sofa, or 「ソファで本を読む|ソファでほんをよむ」, to read a book on the sofa. And when you just say where it is, use 「に」 with 「あります」. 「リビングにソファがあります」, there's a sofa in the living room. Here 「リビング」 is the usual word for a living room. In many Japanese homes, people end up sitting on the floor, on cushions, and leaning against the front of the sofa. Land with 「に」, act with 「で」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's English sofa, and since Japanese has no true f sound, it takes 「フ」 plus a small 「ァ」 to make 「ファ」, with 「ソファー」 as a common long spelling.",
      "Its 「ファ」 belongs to a row made for foreign f sounds, 「ファ」, 「フィ」, 「フェ」, 「フォ」, and each pair is read as one quick beat.",
      "Use 「に」 to sit on it, as in 「ソファに座る|ソファにすわる」, and 「で」 to do something there, as in 「ソファで寝る|ソファでねる」, so land with 「に」, act with 「で」."
    ],
    ja: "[ソファ]でテレビをみます。",
    en: "I watch TV on the sofa." },
  "れいぞうこ|冷蔵庫": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Break it into three kanji and it describes itself. The first, 「冷|れい」, means cold or to cool. Here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. You already know it with its native reading in 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch. Look at its left side: those two little strokes are the ice radical, a hint that the kanji is about coldness. The second kanji, 「蔵|ぞう」, means a storehouse, a place where things are kept and stored. Old sake breweries and merchant houses in Japan had thick-walled storehouses, and this is that kanji. The third, 「庫|こ」, also means a storeroom or warehouse. It has the roof-like shape on top that shows up in building words. Put them together and you get cold storage room. A fridge is literally a little cold warehouse in your kitchen. A related appliance you know is 「電子レンジ|でんしレンジ」, the microwave, which shows how Japanese mixes kanji and loanwords for appliances. To put something in the fridge, say 「冷蔵庫に入れる|れいぞうこにいれる」, using 「入れる|いれる」. To recap: cold, plus storehouse, plus storeroom, makes the cold box that keeps your food." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each kanji in this word brings friends. The first, 「冷|れい」, gives you 「冷凍庫|れいとうこ」, the freezer, a frozen storeroom, and 「冷房|れいぼう」, air conditioning, literally cooling a room. With native readings you meet 「冷たい|つめたい」, cold to the touch, and 「冷める|さめる」, to cool down, like tea left on the table. The last kanji, 「庫|こ」, means a storehouse, and it closes lots of words for places that keep things. 「車庫|しゃこ」 is a garage, a car storehouse. 「金庫|きんこ」 is a safe, a money storehouse. 「倉庫|そうこ」 is a warehouse. So the fridge and the freezer sit side by side, as they do in the kitchen, and differ only in the middle kanji. Cold storage or frozen storage. Learn them as a pair and you get two appliances for the price of one." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A few phrases cover most fridge talk. To put something in, say 「冷蔵庫に入れる|れいぞうこにいれる」. To take it out, use the partner verb 「出す|だす」: 「冷蔵庫から出す|れいぞうこからだす」. To chill a drink on purpose, use 「冷やす|ひやす」, as in 「ビールを冷やす|ビールをひやす」, to chill the beer. When food is left out, a Japanese parent might say 「冷蔵庫にしまって|れいぞうこにしまって」, put it away in the fridge. Japanese fridges often have several separate doors and drawers, including a drawer just for vegetables, so you might hear a family member say which drawer something is in. A common mix-up is thinking 「冷たい|つめたい」 and 「寒い|さむい」 are the same cold. Drinks and fridges are 「冷たい|つめたい」. Weather is 「寒い|さむい」. Cold things live in the fridge." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Its kanji are cold 「冷|れい」, storehouse 「蔵|ぞう」, and storeroom 「庫|こ」, so a fridge is literally a little cold warehouse in your kitchen.",
      "Swap the middle and you get 「冷凍庫|れいとうこ」, the freezer, while 「庫|こ」 also closes words like 「車庫|しゃこ」, garage, and 「金庫|きんこ」, safe.",
      "Put things in with 「冷蔵庫に入れる|れいぞうこにいれる」 and take them out with 「冷蔵庫から出す|れいぞうこからだす」, and remember drinks are 「冷たい|つめたい」 while weather is 「寒い|さむい」."
    ],
    ja: "ジュースは[冷蔵庫|れいぞうこ]にあります。",
    en: "The juice is in the refrigerator." },
  "スイッチ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English switch. Watch how the sounds were reshaped. Japanese doesn't allow a consonant cluster like sw, so the s gets its own vowel, becoming 「ス」. The w sound blends into 「イ」, so the start becomes 「スイ」. The tch at the end is a sharp, clipped stop, and Japanese shows that with a small 「ッ」 before 「チ」, a tiny pause that doubles the sound. So you get 「スイッチ」. This word fits perfectly into lesson eighteen, because so many of the new verbs are about things turning on and off. You switch on a light with 「つける」 and off with 「けす」. The light itself turns on with 「つく」 and goes off with 「きえる」. A natural phrase with the switch itself is 「スイッチをいれる」, literally putting the switch in, meaning switching it on, and 「スイッチをきる」, cutting it, meaning switching it off. You can also simply 「スイッチをおす」, push the switch, using the new verb 「押す|おす」. In Japanese, a switch is just the physical button or lever. To sum up, English switch, with an added vowel and a small 「ッ」 for the snap at the end." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "That small 「ッ」 plus 「チ」 at the end is a pattern you see in many loanwords from English words ending in tch or ch. 「マッチ」 is a match, the kind you strike, and also a match in sports. 「サンドイッチ」 is a sandwich. 「ストレッチ」 is stretching. And 「キッチン」, the kitchen, uses the same sound inside the word. In each case, the short, sharp vowel before the ch in English gets a little pause, written with the small 「ッ」. If you say these words without the pause, they sound off to Japanese ears, a bit like saying swi, chi instead of switch. So when you read a loanword, look for the small 「ッ」 and give it a full beat of silence. 「スイッチ」 has four beats, not three. Feel the tiny gap before the 「チ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Besides the physical switch on the wall, this word has a fun figurative life in Japanese. People talk about a 「やる気スイッチ|やるきスイッチ」, a motivation switch. Parents joke about trying to find where their kid's motivation switch is. You might hear someone say 「スイッチが入った|スイッチがはいった」, the switch went on, meaning they suddenly got focused and fired up. When it comes to lights, though, people usually don't mention the switch at all. They just say 「電気をつける|でんきをつける」 and 「電気を消す|でんきをけす」, turn the light on and off, with 「電気|でんき」 standing for the light itself. Save 「スイッチ」 for when the button really matters, like when you can't find it in a dark room. The switch is there in the wall and in your head." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English switch gets a vowel to break up the sw, and a small 「ッ」 before 「チ」 for the clipped snap at the end, and you switch it on with 「スイッチをいれる」.",
      "Loanwords ending in tch or ch, like 「マッチ」 and 「サンドイッチ」, get the same small 「ッ」, so give it a full beat, making 「スイッチ」 four beats.",
      "People joke about a 「やる気スイッチ|やるきスイッチ」, a motivation switch, but for lights they usually just say 「電気をつける|でんきをつける」 without mentioning the switch."
    ],
    ja: "[スイッチ]をおしてください。",
    en: "Please press the switch." },
  "しょうゆ|しょう油": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The second kanji, 「油|ゆ」, means oil. Here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. On its own with the native reading, oil is 「あぶら」. Its left side is the water radical, three drops, because oil is a liquid. The first part, 「しょう」, is a rare kanji meaning a fermented, salted paste or sauce. It is hard to write, so Genki and many labels just use kana for it. So the word is roughly fermented sauce liquid. It is not oily at all, so think of oil here as a dark liquid seasoning. Here is a fun fact. The English word soy actually comes from this Japanese word. Dutch traders took it to Europe centuries ago, and soy sauce really means soy sauce sauce. 「しょうゆ」 is the backbone of Japanese cooking, along with miso and dashi. A good manners tip: when eating sushi, dip the fish side lightly into the 「しょうゆ」, not the rice, or the rice falls apart and soaks up too much. A common request at the table is 「しょうゆをとってください」, please pass the soy sauce. To recap: a fermented sauce, plus the kanji for oil or liquid, makes the dark seasoning that gave English the word soy." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「油|ゆ」 links soy sauce to a whole shelf of other liquids. On its own with the native reading, 「油|あぶら」 is cooking oil. In 「ごま油|ごまあぶら」 you get sesame oil, a fragrant favorite in Japanese and Chinese cooking. With the Chinese-origin reading 「ゆ」, as in our word, you find 「石油|せきゆ」, petroleum, literally rock oil, and 「灯油|とうゆ」, kerosene, lamp oil, which many Japanese homes still use for winter heaters. Watch out for a sound-alike. Fatty meat is also 「あぶら」, but written with a different kanji, 「脂|あぶら」, solid fat. Liquid oil versus solid fat, same sound. In our word, the oil kanji simply marks a dark liquid. Soy sauce isn't oily, but it sits on the shelf with the other bottles." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Japanese shoppers choose between several kinds of soy sauce. The everyday standard is dark soy sauce, 「濃口|こいくち」, the one you probably know. Kansai cooking, around Osaka and Kyoto, often uses light colored soy sauce, 「薄口|うすくち」. Here is the surprise. Light colored doesn't mean mild. It is actually saltier, but it keeps soups and simmered dishes pale and pretty. There is also low sodium soy sauce for people watching their salt. At the table, a little dish is set out for dipping. Pour only a small amount. Filling the dish to the brim and leaving most of it can look wasteful. And remember that it pairs with lots of verbs: you 「かける」 it, pour it over, on tofu, and you 「つける」 it, dip into it, with sushi. Pour or dip, but just a little." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. Imagine a theater stage. The curtain rises, and the star of the show is a giant bottle of soy sauce in a sparkly costume. The announcer shouts, it's show time! Then the bottle takes a bow, and a little dark puddle drips out, you, you, you, onto the stage. Show, you. Silly, but now the sound and the meaning are stuck together. Add the kanji to the scene. The puddle on the stage is shiny and liquid, so the kanji for oil, with its water drops on the left, lights up above the stage like a sign. That sign says you are looking at a dark liquid, not real oil. Every time you see that bottle on a Japanese table, hear the announcer." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's a rare kanji for fermented sauce plus 「油|ゆ」, oil or liquid, and the English word soy actually comes from this Japanese word.",
      "The oil kanji links it to 「ごま油|ごまあぶら」, sesame oil, and 「石油|せきゆ」, petroleum, while solid fat is a different 「脂|あぶら」 with the same sound.",
      "Light colored 「薄口|うすくち」 is actually saltier than standard 「濃口|こいくち」, and at the table pour only a little, then 「かける」 it on tofu or 「つける」 sushi into it.",
      "Picture a soy sauce bottle starring on stage as the announcer shouts show time, then dark drips go you, you, you, under a glowing oil kanji sign."
    ],
    ja: "[しょう油|しょうゆ]をとってください。",
    en: "Please pass the soy sauce." },
  "スープ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is from English soup, which itself came from French. The long oo sound is shown with the long vowel mark, so you get 「スー」 and then 「プ」 with a little u vowel added after the p, because Japanese words can't end in a bare p sound. In Japan, 「スープ」 usually means Western-style soup, like corn soup or consomme, or the broth of ramen. Traditional miso soup has its own word, 「みそしる」, so you wouldn't usually call that a 「スープ」. Corn potage soup is hugely popular, and you can even buy it hot in cans from vending machines in winter. Here is a common mistake for English speakers. In English we eat soup, but in Japanese you drink it, so you say 「スープをのむ」 with 「飲む|のむ」, not 「食べる|たべる」. If it's a thick stew with big pieces, people might switch to 「食べる|たべる」, but for normal soup, think drinking. A way to remember it: you sip soup, so treat it like a drink. To sum up, English soup with a long vowel, used for Western soups and broth, and you drink it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "How you eat soup changes with the kind of soup. Western style soup in Japan is eaten the Western way, with a spoon, quietly, the bowl staying on the table. But Japanese soups are a different story. With 「みそしる」 and clear soup, 「お吸い物|おすいもの」, you lift the bowl in one hand and drink straight from it, using your chopsticks to hold back or pick up the tofu, seaweed, and other pieces. No spoon at all. And ramen is famous for slurping. Slurping the noodles is completely normal and even shows you're enjoying them. Many people also drink some of the ramen broth from the bowl at the end. So in Japan, one table might hold three ways to eat soup. A spoon for the Western style one, a lifted bowl for miso, and noisy slurps for ramen." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several soup words, and each one points to a different style. 「スープ」 covers Western soups and broths, like corn soup, onion soup, and the soup of ramen. 「みそしる」 is miso soup, where 「しる」 means juice or soup. A heartier cousin is 「豚汁|とんじる」, pork and vegetable miso soup, a winter favorite. Some people read it 「ぶたじる」 instead. 「お吸い物|おすいもの」 is a clear, delicate broth, often served at formal meals. Its name comes from 「吸う|すう」, to sip or to suck in. And 「だし」 is the stock that Japanese soups start from, usually made from dried fish flakes and kelp. So when a Japanese friend says 「スープ」, picture something Western or ramen. When they say 「しる」, picture a lacquered bowl and chopsticks." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's English soup, used for Western soups and ramen broth rather than miso soup, and you drink it with 「飲む|のむ」 rather than eat it.",
      "Western soup gets a spoon with the bowl on the table, miso soup is drunk from the lifted bowl with chopsticks, and ramen is slurped noisily.",
      "Each soup word points to a style: 「スープ」 for Western or ramen, 「みそしる」 and 「豚汁|とんじる」 for miso soups, and 「お吸い物|おすいもの」 for clear broth."
    ],
    ja: "{毎朝|まいあさ}[スープ]をのみます。",
    en: "I have soup every morning." },
  "バナナ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This one is easy, because it sounds almost exactly like English banana. Every syllable is already a clean consonant plus vowel, which is exactly the shape Japanese likes, so nothing needed to be squeezed. The word came into European languages through Portuguese and Spanish traders, most likely from a West African language. Japan doesn't grow many bananas itself. Most of them are imported, a large share from the Philippines. Bananas are a standard cheap and healthy breakfast or snack in Japan, and you'll often see single bananas sold in convenience stores. Here is a useful grammar point. When you count bananas, use the counter 「〜本|〜ほん」, the counter for long, thin things like pens, bottles, and umbrellas. One banana is 「一本|いっぽん」, two is 「二本|にほん」, three is 「三本|さんぼん」. Notice how the sound of the counter changes with the number. A related food word you know is 「くだもの」, fruit. To recap: English banana, unchanged in sound, and counted with 「〜本|〜ほん」 because it's long and thin." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Today bananas are one of the cheapest fruits in a Japanese supermarket, but they weren't always. For years after the war, imported bananas were expensive, a special treat for when someone was sick or for a celebration. Imports were opened up in the early nineteen sixties, and prices dropped until bananas became an ordinary everyday fruit. Older Japanese people sometimes remember them as a luxury. There's also a famous school joke. Before a school trip, teachers set a small budget for snacks, and some student always asks, 「バナナはおやつに入りますか|バナナはおやつにはいりますか」, do bananas count as snacks? It's become a classic line that everyone in Japan recognizes. So this simple fruit carries a bit of history and a bit of playground humor." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You already know to count single bananas with 「〜本|〜ほん」. When you buy a whole bunch, though, you need another word, 「房|ふさ」, a bunch or cluster. 「バナナ一房|バナナひとふさ」 is one bunch of bananas. Grapes come in bunches too, and use the same word. At the shop you might say 「バナナを一房ください|バナナをひとふさください」. If you just want a few, single bananas sold at convenience stores are counted with 「本|ほん」. Japanese doesn't add an s for plurals, so 「バナナ」 is one banana or a hundred, and the counter does the work. One more natural phrase is 「バナナの皮|バナナのかわ」, banana peel, the thing cartoon characters slip on. Count with 「本|ほん」, buy by the 「房|ふさ」, and watch out for the 「皮|かわ」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It sounds almost exactly like English banana, and since it's long and thin, you count it with 「〜本|〜ほん」, as in 「一本|いっぽん」 and 「三本|さんぼん」.",
      "Bananas were an expensive treat after the war until imports opened, and every student knows the joke 「バナナはおやつに入りますか|バナナはおやつにはいりますか」.",
      "Count single ones with 「本|ほん」 but buy a bunch with 「房|ふさ」, as in 「バナナ一房|バナナひとふさ」, and watch out for 「バナナの皮|バナナのかわ」."
    ],
    ja: "[バナナ]を{一本|いっぽん}たべました。",
    en: "I ate one banana." },
  "ポップコーン|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is English popcorn, made of two parts. Pop becomes 「ポップ」. The small 「ッ」 shows the quick, clipped stop of the p, and an extra u vowel is added at the end, since Japanese can't end a syllable with p. Corn becomes 「コーン」. The r sound disappears and turns into a long vowel, which is how Japanese usually handles English r after a vowel. That long mark is important, because 「コーン」 on its own means corn. You'll see it on menus in 「コーンスープ」, corn soup. Popcorn is the classic snack at the movie theater, 「えいがかん」, just like in English-speaking countries. Japan has fun flavors though. Theme parks such as Tokyo Disneyland are famous for flavors like curry, soy sauce butter, and caramel, and people line up just for them. The verb is the usual 「食べる|たべる」. To sum up: pop plus corn, with a small 「ッ」 for the pop and a long vowel where the English r used to be." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The 「コーン」 half of this word has a twin. English corn and English cone both become 「コーン」 in Japanese, because Japanese drops the r and turns both vowels into the same long o. So 「コーン」 can be corn, as in 「コーンスープ」 and 「コーンフレーク」, cornflakes. But it can also be the cone you put ice cream in, and the orange traffic cone on the road. Context nearly always makes it clear. You won't eat a traffic cone, and you won't put ice cream in popcorn, probably. This happens a lot with loanwords, because English has more vowel and consonant contrasts than Japanese. Light and right both come out as 「ライト」. So when a katakana word seems to have two meanings, think back to which English words could have collapsed into it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a Japanese movie theater, the snack counter works much like at home. You might hear 「ポップコーンとコーラのセット」, a popcorn and cola set, since set meals are popular. Flavors are named with 「味|あじ」, taste or flavor. 「しお味|しおあじ」 is salted, 「キャラメル味|キャラメルあじ」 is caramel, and 「バター醤油味|バターしょうゆあじ」, butter and soy sauce, is a very Japanese twist. You can order with the simple pattern 「〜をください」. Sizes are usually called S, M, and L, said in English letters. If you want both sweet and salty in one tub, many theaters offer a half and half option. To say you want to eat popcorn while watching the movie, use the 「〜ながら」 pattern: 「見ながら食べる|みながらたべる」. A big tub and two flavors, and the movie can start." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Pop becomes 「ポップ」 with a small 「ッ」 for the clipped p, and corn becomes 「コーン」, with a long vowel where the English r used to be.",
      "English corn and cone both collapse into 「コーン」, so it can mean corn in 「コーンスープ」 or an ice cream cone, and context sorts it out.",
      "Flavors are named with 「味|あじ」, like 「キャラメル味|キャラメルあじ」 or very Japanese 「バター醤油味|バターしょうゆあじ」, and you order with 「〜をください」."
    ],
    ja: "えいがを{見ながら|みながら}[ポップコーン]をたべます。",
    en: "I eat popcorn while watching a movie." },
  "シャンプー|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English shampoo, and English took it from Hindi, where the original word meant to press or massage, as in massaging the head. The sh sound becomes 「シャ」. The m before p turns into 「ン」, because Japanese uses 「ン」 for any nasal sound at the end of a syllable. Then 「プー」 with a long vowel for the long oo. In Japan, the partner word is 「リンス」, from English rinse, which is what Japanese people call hair conditioner. That's a nice example of a loanword whose meaning shifted. A typical Japanese bathroom has a washing area outside the tub, so you wash and rinse your hair with 「シャンプー」 there first, then get into the bath clean. Hotel and onsen bathrooms usually have pump bottles marked in katakana, so being able to read 「シャンプー」 and 「リンス」 is actually practical. Related words you know are 「シャワー」, shower, and 「かみ」, hair. To wash your hair, say 「かみをあらう」. To recap: Hindi via English shampoo, with the m turned into 「ン」 and a long vowel at the end." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The 「ン」 in this word hides a neat sound rule. Japanese writes one letter for its nasal sound, but how you actually say it depends on what comes next. Before p, b, and m sounds, your lips close, so it comes out as an m. That's why 「シャンプー」 sounds so close to the English. The same thing happens in words you know. 「さんぽ」, a walk, sounds like sampo. 「しんぶん」, newspaper, sounds like shimbun, and that is exactly how older English spellings of Japanese newspaper names wrote it. 「てんぷら」 sounds like tempura. Before k and g sounds, the same letter slides toward an ng sound, as in 「ぎんこう」, bank. You don't need to think about it. Your mouth will do it naturally if you just move toward the next sound. One letter, several shapes." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Next time you are in a Japanese bathroom, run your fingers along the side of the bottles. On most shampoo bottles in Japan you'll feel a row of small raised ridges. Conditioner bottles have none. This was started by a Japanese company and later made into an industry standard, so that people with poor eyesight, or anyone with their eyes closed and soap in their hair, can tell the bottles apart by touch. It's a lovely small piece of universal design. At a Japanese hair salon, having your hair washed is a big part of the visit, often with a gentle head massage, which fits the word's old meaning of massaging the head. You'll also see 「シャンプーする」 used as a verb, to shampoo. Feel for the ridges, and you'll never mix the bottles up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English shampoo came from a Hindi word for massaging the head, with the m turned into 「ン」, and its partner 「リンス」 is what Japanese call conditioner.",
      "The 「ン」 sounds like m before p, b, and m, which is why 「シャンプー」 sounds so English, just like 「さんぽ」 sounds like sampo.",
      "Japanese shampoo bottles have small raised ridges on the side while conditioner bottles don't, so you can tell them apart by touch with your eyes closed."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[シャンプー]でかみをあらいます。",
    en: "I wash my hair with shampoo every night." },
  "ろうそく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word does have kanji, but they are hard and rare, so Genki writes it in kana. The first part, 「ろう」, means wax. The second part, 「そく」, comes from a kanji that means a lamp or a light. So the word is literally wax light, which is exactly what a candle is. Candles are a perfect fit for this lesson's on and off verbs. To light a candle, use 「つける」, to light it, or put it out with 「けす」. When the candle goes out by itself, use 「きえる」. Birthday cakes in Japan have candles just like in the West, and you blow them out after the birthday song. Candles also matter in Japanese homes for the family altar, and many households keep some ready for earthquakes and power cuts. A way to remember it: picture a row of candles all saying so, so softly in the dark, row so ku. That's just a hook, not the origin. To recap, wax plus light, and it pairs with 「つける」, 「けす」, and 「きえる」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Candles come with their own little set of phrases. To light one, people often say 「ろうそくに火をつける|ろうそくにひをつける」, put fire to the candle. Here the candle takes 「に」, because the fire goes onto it. To blow it out, there is a handy compound verb, 「ふき消す|ふきけす」, made of 「ふく」, to blow, and 「消す|けす」, to put out. At a birthday party you'll hear 「ろうそくをふき消して|ろうそくをふきけして」, blow out the candles. Like bananas and pens, candles are long and thin, so you count them with 「〜本|〜ほん」. A cake for a ten year old might have 「十本|じゅっぽん」 candles. When the power goes out and a candle is your only light, its flame flickering is described with 「ゆらゆら」, a sound word for gently swaying. Light it with 「に」, blow it out with 「ふき消す|ふきけす」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In many Japanese homes, candles belong to the family Buddhist altar, 「仏壇|ぶつだん」, a cabinet holding memorial tablets for ancestors. When people pay their respects, they light a small candle, then use its flame to light a stick of incense, 「線香|せんこう」. They put the candle out by waving a hand or using a small snuffer, not by blowing on it. Blowing on the altar flame is traditionally considered impolite, since breath is seen as unclean. That's very different from a birthday cake, where blowing is the whole point. Candles also show up in Japanese emergency kits. Because earthquakes can cut the power, many families keep candles and flashlights ready. So the same word moves between a happy party, a quiet moment for the ancestors, and a dark night after a quake." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「ろう」, wax, plus 「そく」, light, so literally wax light, and it pairs with 「つける」 to light it, 「けす」 to put it out, and 「きえる」 when it goes out.",
      "Light it with 「ろうそくに火をつける|ろうそくにひをつける」, blow it out with 「ふき消す|ふきけす」, and count candles with 「〜本|〜ほん」 since they're long and thin.",
      "At the family altar, 「仏壇|ぶつだん」, the candle lights the incense and is put out with a wave of the hand, because blowing on it is considered impolite."
    ],
    ja: "ケーキの[ろうそく]をけしました。",
    en: "I blew out the candles on the cake." },
  "タオル|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is from English towel. Watch the sound changes. The t becomes 「タ」. The ow sound is simplified to 「オ」. The final l becomes 「ル」. Japanese has no separate l sound, so English l moves into the r row, and since a word can't end in a consonant, it gets a u vowel too. So towel becomes 「タオル」. Towels are a big part of daily life in Japan. Many people carry a small hand towel or handkerchief, because public restrooms often have no paper towels. Restaurants give you a wet towel called 「おしぼり」 to wipe your hands before eating. At hot springs, people bring a small towel into the bathing area and often fold it on their head, but it should never go into the bath water. The big one for drying off is a bath towel, 「バスタオル」. A related word is 「ハンカチ」, from handkerchief. To recap: English towel, with the l becoming 「ル」, a small everyday item that Japanese people really do carry around." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The 「ル」 at the end of this word comes from an English l, and you'll find the same ending all over katakana. 「ホテル」, hotel. 「ボール」, ball. 「メール」, email. 「ビル」, a building, short for English building, which surprises a lot of learners. In each of these, the English l at the end of the word becomes 「ル」, because Japanese needs a vowel after the consonant and uses its r sound for l. That's also why English speakers sometimes hear Japanese mixing up l and r. To Japanese ears they are one sound. A tip for reading: when you see 「ル」 at the end of a katakana word, try an English l first. A tip for speaking: keep the 「ル」 light and short. Say it like a quick tap of the tongue." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese splits towels into several sizes, and the names are easy. 「バスタオル」, a bath towel. 「フェイスタオル」, a face towel, the long narrow one. 「ハンドタオル」, a small square hand towel that people carry in a bag. Then there is a traditional cousin, 「手ぬぐい|てぬぐい」, a thin cotton cloth printed with patterns. Its name means hand wiper. It dries fast and is used as a towel, a headband, or a wrapping cloth. The verb for wiping or drying with a towel is 「ふく」: 「タオルで手をふく|タオルでてをふく」, to dry your hands with a towel. Use 「で」 for the towel, since it's the tool. You'll also see laborers and festival dancers with a towel tied around their head or hung around the neck, a very Japanese look. Big, medium, small, and the old cotton one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English towel becomes 「タオル」, with the final l turned into 「ル」, and people really carry small towels because restrooms often have no paper towels.",
      "Many English words ending in l get 「ル」, like 「ホテル」, 「ボール」, and 「ビル」, so when you see a final 「ル」, try an English l first.",
      "Towels come in sizes like 「バスタオル」 and 「ハンドタオル」, plus the traditional cotton 「手ぬぐい|てぬぐい」, and you dry with 「タオルで手をふく|タオルでてをふく」."
    ],
    ja: "おふろのあとで[タオル]をつかいます。",
    en: "I use a towel after my bath." },
  "スカート|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from English skirt. The sk cluster gets a vowel added after the s, giving 「ス」 then 「カ」. The ir sound, like in bird, becomes a long a sound, 「カー」. That is because Japanese loanwords are mostly modeled on British-style pronunciation without a strong r, so the vowel just stretches. Then the final t gets an o vowel, 「ト」, since 「ト」 is how Japanese often handles a word-final t. Here is the key grammar point. In lesson seven you learned different verbs for wearing different things. For clothes on your top half, like a shirt or jacket, use 「着る|きる」. For things on your lower half, like pants, socks, shoes, and skirts, use 「はく」. So you say 「スカートをはく」, not 「きる」. That's a classic mistake. A way to remember it: 「はく」 is for anything you step into. School uniforms in Japan usually include a pleated skirt for girls, which is a strong image in anime and drama. To recap: English skirt with a long vowel where the r was, and you wear it with 「はく」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Japanese chooses the wearing verb by body part, so here is the whole set in one place. Hats go on with 「かぶる」. Glasses with 「かける」. Things on your upper body, like shirts and coats, with 「着る|きる」. Things you step into, like skirts, pants, socks, and shoes, with 「はく」. Small accessories, like a watch, a tie, or a necklace, with 「する」. Taking things off is simpler. For clothes, use 「ぬぐ」, whether it's a skirt or a coat. For hats and glasses, people usually say 「とる」. To say someone is wearing something right now, use the te-form plus 「いる」: 「スカートをはいている」. Notice that this describes a state, she is wearing it, not that she is putting it on at this moment. That's a classic mistake. Step in with 「はく」, step out with 「ぬぐ」." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "The long 「カー」 in this word comes from the r sound after a vowel in English. Japanese loanwords drop that r and stretch the vowel instead, and the result is the long mark. You'll find this in many words you know. 「カード」, card. 「ハート」, heart. 「スタート」, start. 「パーティー」, party. 「デパート」, department store. In each one, wherever English has a vowel plus r, Japanese gives a long vowel. Holding the long vowel matters. It counts as an extra beat, and cutting it short can change the word or make it hard to understand. 「スカート」 has four beats, su, ka, a, to. Try clapping them. So whenever you see an English word with ar, er, ir, or or, expect a long vowel in the katakana version." }
    ],
    short: [
      "English skirt gets a long 「カー」 where the r was, and since you step into it, you wear it with 「はく」, not 「着る|きる」.",
      "The wearing verb depends on the body part, 「かぶる」 for hats, 「着る|きる」 for tops, 「はく」 for things you step into, and you take clothes off with 「ぬぐ」.",
      "Wherever English has a vowel plus r, katakana gives a long vowel, like 「カード」 and 「スタート」, and that long vowel counts as a full extra beat."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は{赤い|あかい}[スカート]をはいています。",
    en: "My older sister is wearing a red skirt." },
  "にっき|日記": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, read with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「日|にち」, is the sun and the day. You know it in lots of words, like 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, 「日本|にほん」, Japan, and 「日曜日|にちようび」, Sunday. In this word, 「にち」 shrinks to 「にっ」 before 「き」. That kind of sound squeeze, where the ending of a reading becomes a small 「ッ」 before a k sound, is very common in Japanese compounds. The second kanji, 「記|き」, means to write down or record. Look at its left side: that is the speech or words radical, the same one you see in 「話|わ」 in 「電話|でんわ」, and in 「語|ご」 in 「日本語|にほんご」. It tells you the kanji has to do with words. So the whole thing is a day record, a written record of each day. To keep a diary, say 「日記を書く|にっきをかく」, using 「書く|かく」. A cultural note: Japanese elementary school kids often have to keep a picture diary over summer vacation, drawing a picture and writing a few lines each day. To recap: day plus record, with 「にち」 squeezed into 「にっ」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「日|ひ」 has more readings than almost any other, so this word is a good place to tour them. Here it's 「にち」, squeezed to 「にっ」. On its own, the sun or a day is 「日|ひ」, and inside compounds that can become 「び」, as in 「日曜日|にちようび」. In 「毎日|まいにち」 it's 「にち」. In 「日本|にほん」 it's 「に」. For counting days it switches to 「か」, as in 「二日|ふつか」 and 「三日|みっか」. And 「休日|きゅうじつ」, a day off, uses 「じつ」. The second kanji, 「記|き」, shows up in 「暗記|あんき」, memorizing, built with the kanji for dark, as if recording something without looking, inside your head. Every vocab student knows that word. You'll also meet 「記事|きじ」, a newspaper article, the record of an event. Write down your day, write down the news, or write it into memory." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Diaries have a special place in Japanese literature. About a thousand years ago, in the Heian period, court writers produced famous diaries that are still read in schools today. One early example was written by a male courtier who pretended to be a woman, so he could write in the flowing kana script that women used, instead of formal Chinese. Diaries by court women became some of the great works of Japanese writing. The tradition carries on in small ways. Many children keep picture diaries over summer vacation, and teenage friends sometimes keep an exchange diary, 「交換日記|こうかんにっき」, passing one notebook back and forth and writing to each other. Today, a blog can even be called an online diary. From court ladies to school kids, writing down the day is a very Japanese habit." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The verb you naturally pair with this word is not only 「書く|かく」. Japanese people very often say 「日記をつける|にっきをつける」, to keep a diary. Here 「つける」 means to record or to enter, the same verb used for keeping a household account book. So 「毎日日記をつけています|まいにちにっきをつけています」 means I keep a diary every day, with the ongoing 「〜ている」 form for a habit. You might also say 「日記に書く|にっきにかく」, to write something in the diary, with 「に」 because the diary is where the words land. If you've ever given up on a diary, you can confess 「三日坊主|みっかぼうず」, a three day monk, someone who quits a new habit after only three days. It's a popular way to admit you didn't keep it up. Keep it with 「つける」, and try not to be a three day monk." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's day 「日|にち」 plus record 「記|き」, with the words radical on its left, and 「にち」 squeezes into 「にっ」 before 「き」, so a diary is a day record.",
      "Its first kanji has many readings, like 「ひ」, 「に」, 「か」 in 「三日|みっか」, and 「じつ」, while 「記|き」 also appears in 「暗記|あんき」, memorizing.",
      "Court writers in the Heian period produced famous diaries still read today, and the habit lives on in summer picture diaries and the 「交換日記|こうかんにっき」.",
      "People often say 「日記をつける|にっきをつける」 to keep a diary, and someone who gives up quickly is a 「三日坊主|みっかぼうず」, a three day monk."
    ],
    ja: "{毎晩|まいばん}[日記|にっき]を{書きます|かきます}。",
    en: "I write in my diary every night." },
  "けいたい|携帯": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is actually a shortened form. The full word is 「携帯電話|けいたいでんわ」, a carried telephone. In daily speech people drop the 「電話|でんわ」 and just say 「携帯|けいたい」. Now the kanji. The first, 「携|けい」, means to carry something in your hand, to take along. The second, 「帯|たい」, means a belt or sash, and by extension to wear something on your body. Both are Chinese-origin readings. Together they mean something you carry on you. That's a pretty good description of a phone that never leaves your pocket. You may also hear 「スマホ」, short for smartphone, which is now even more common. Both are fine. A cultural note: on Japanese trains, people are expected not to talk on the phone, and you'll see signs asking you to switch to silent mode, which is called manner mode, 「マナーモード」. Related words you know include 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, and 「電話する|でんわする」, to call. To recap: carry-in-hand plus worn-on-the-body, a shortened name for a phone you take everywhere." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll often see this word written in katakana, 「ケータイ」, with a long mark instead of 「い」. That spelling became popular in casual writing and ads, and it feels modern and friendly. Some useful phrases. 「携帯の番号|けいたいのばんごう」, a mobile number. 「携帯を見る|けいたいをみる」, to check your phone. 「携帯を忘れた|けいたいをわすれた」, I forgot my phone, said with a little panic. Older flip phones got a nickname, 「ガラケー」, short for Galapagos phone. The joke is that Japanese phones developed their own unique features in isolation, like the animals on the Galapagos islands, before smartphones took over. The word also works as a prefix meaning portable. A pocket ashtray, for example, is called a carrying ashtray. Phone or not, if you carry it, this word fits." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「帯|たい」, is a belt or sash, and on its own with the native reading 「帯|おび」, it's the wide sash tied around a kimono. That image of something wrapped around your body helps the whole family. With 「たい」, it also means a zone or band. 「時間帯|じかんたい」 is a time slot, a band of time, like a busy period at a restaurant. 「熱帯|ねったい」 is the tropics, the hot band around the earth. The first kanji, 「携|けい」, carry by hand, is rarer, but you'll see it in signs and instructions. Put them together and you get carrying something wrapped on you, like a phone in a pocket. So when you see 「帯|たい」 in a word, think belt, band, or zone. It's a kimono sash stretched across time, space, and your pocket." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's short for 「携帯電話|けいたいでんわ」, built from carry in hand 「携|けい」 and belt or worn on the body 「帯|たい」, so a phone you carry on you.",
      "It's often written 「ケータイ」 in casual writing, old flip phones are 「ガラケー」, Galapagos phones, and the word also works as a prefix for portable.",
      "The second kanji is the kimono sash 「帯|おび」, which stretches into bands and zones like 「時間帯|じかんたい」, time slot, and 「熱帯|ねったい」, the tropics."
    ],
    ja: "[携帯|けいたい]を{家|いえ}にわすれました。",
    en: "I left my cell phone at home." },
  "さくら|桜": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native Japanese reading here. Look at the left side: that is 「木|き」, the tree radical, which you'll see in kanji for many kinds of trees and wooden things. That tells you 「桜|さくら」 is first of all a tree, the cherry tree, and also its famous pale pink blossoms. Cherry blossoms are the symbol of spring in Japan. They bloom around late March and early April, when the school year and the work year begin. So the blossoms are tied to fresh starts, entrance ceremonies, and new jobs. They also fall within about a week, and that quick fading has long been linked to the idea that beautiful things don't last. People gather under the trees for picnics called 「花見|はなみ」, flower viewing, which uses 「花|はな」, flower. Each spring the news even reports a cherry blossom front moving north up the country. The new verb 「咲く|さく」, to bloom, pairs naturally with this word: 「桜が咲く|さくらがさく」. A way to remember it: picture sakura petals drifting past, and you saying sock, ra-ra, to the falling flowers. To recap: a tree kanji, the symbol of spring and new beginnings." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Cherry blossoms have a whole vocabulary of their own. When the trees are in full bloom, the news says they are 「満開|まんかい」, fully open. When the petals fall, the verb is 「散る|ちる」, to scatter, and falling petals drifting like snow are called a petal blizzard, 「桜吹雪|さくらふぶき」. Viewing the blossoms at night under lanterns is 「夜桜|よざくら」, night cherry, and notice how 「さくら」 becomes 「ざくら」 inside the compound. There's even a color, 「桜色|さくらいろ」, a very pale pink. And a sweet, 「桜もち|さくらもち」, a pink rice cake wrapped in a salted cherry leaf, eaten in spring. Most of the trees you see in parks are one variety, which spread across Japan from Tokyo. They are clones grown from cuttings, which is why a whole park blooms at nearly the same moment. One tree, a whole season of words." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Because cherry blossoms come at the start of the Japanese school year, they became a symbol of passing entrance exams. In the past, when students couldn't travel to see the results board, they could ask for a telegram. A pass was famously announced with a short poetic message saying the cherry blossoms have bloomed. A fail said the cherry blossoms have scattered. Today exams are checked online, but people still use the phrase for passing, and you'll see cherry blossom designs on good luck charms for students. There's another side to the blossoms too. Their short life made them a symbol of things that are beautiful because they don't last, a feeling deep in Japanese poetry. So the same tree means hope for a student and a gentle sadness for a poet. Spring in one flower." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Flower viewing parties are a big event, and they come with their own customs. In popular parks, someone from an office group is often sent early in the morning to save a spot by spreading out a blue plastic sheet. That job usually falls to the newest employee. People bring food, drinks, and sometimes a portable speaker, and the parties can get lively. Each spring, the weather service and the news track when the trees will bloom across the country, and people plan around it. A natural thing to say is 「桜がきれいですね|さくらがきれいですね」, the cherry blossoms are pretty, aren't they. If you want to invite someone, try 「お花見に行きませんか|おはなみにいきませんか」, won't you go cherry blossom viewing with me. Bring a sheet, check the forecast, and get there early." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The tree radical 「木|き」 on its left marks it as a tree, and its blossoms, blooming as the school and work year begin, symbolize spring and fresh starts.",
      "Blossoms have their own vocabulary, like 「満開|まんかい」, full bloom, 「散る|ちる」, to scatter, and 「夜桜|よざくら」, where 「さくら」 becomes 「ざくら」 inside the compound.",
      "Exam results were once sent by telegram saying the cherry blossoms have bloomed for a pass, and their short life also makes them a symbol of fleeting beauty.",
      "At flower viewing parties the newest employee often saves a spot early with a blue sheet, and you can invite someone with 「お花見に行きませんか|おはなみにいきませんか」."
    ],
    ja: "{四月|しがつ}に[桜|さくら]がさきます。",
    en: "The cherry blossoms bloom in April." },
  "むし|虫": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading here. It started as a picture of a crawling creature, often explained as a snake. Today it is the general word for bugs: insects, but also spiders, worms, and other creepy crawlies. English speakers often use insect for a narrower group, but 「虫|むし」 is wider, closer to bug. The kanji also works as a radical. You'll find it on the left side of many kanji for small creatures, so once you know it, you'll start spotting it everywhere. There is a fun related word you may hear: 「虫歯|むしば」, a cavity, literally a bug tooth, from the old idea that bugs ate holes in teeth. Japan has a strong bug culture. In summer, children catch beetles and keep them as pets, and the buzzing of cicadas is the classic sound of a Japanese summer. In autumn, people enjoy the singing of crickets in the evening. A way to remember it: when you see a bug, you might mutter moo, she is there, a little hook for the sound. To recap: a picture of a crawling creature, the all-purpose word for bugs." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word turns up in some funny compounds about people. A crybaby is 「泣き虫|なきむし」, a crying bug. A coward is 「弱虫|よわむし」, a weak bug. A bookworm in Japanese is 「本の虫|ほんのむし」, a book bug, very close to English. And when you suddenly have a bad feeling that something is wrong, people call it 「虫の知らせ|むしのしらせ」, a message from the bug, a premonition. There's an old belief behind expressions like these, the idea that little creatures living inside you affect your moods. That's why an irritated mood is described as the bug being in a bad state. You don't need to believe it to enjoy the words. When you meet a compound ending in 「虫|むし」 about a person, it usually means someone who has that habit a bit too much." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a long love of insect sounds. In late summer and autumn, people listen for the bell cricket, 「鈴虫|すずむし」, whose song sounds like a tiny ringing bell. For centuries people have kept singing insects in small cages just to hear them in the evening, and some pet shops still sell them. Children have their own bug culture in summer. They head out with nets and cages to catch cicadas, dragonflies, and especially the big horned rhinoceros beetle, 「かぶとむし」, a superstar of summer vacation. Pet shops and even department stores sell beetles as summer pets. Grammar tip: since bugs are living creatures, use 「いる」 for them, never 「ある」, and count them with 「〜匹|〜ひき」. So 「虫が三匹いる|むしがさんびきいる」 means there are three bugs. Listen for the evening chorus." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji began as a picture of a crawling creature, and the word is wider than insect, closer to bug, covering spiders and worms too.",
      "Compounds about people describe someone with a habit a bit too much, like 「泣き虫|なきむし」, crybaby, and 「弱虫|よわむし」, coward.",
      "Japan loves insect sounds like the 「鈴虫|すずむし」, and since bugs are alive, use 「いる」 and count them with 「〜匹|〜ひき」."
    ],
    ja: "{部屋|へや}に[虫|むし]がいます！",
    en: "There's a bug in the room!" },
  "やちん|家賃": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji. The first is 「家|や」, house. You know it as 「家|いえ」, home, and with its Chinese-origin reading in 「家族|かぞく」, family, and 「家具|かぐ」, furniture. Here it uses another native reading, 「や」. The best sibling is 「大家さん|おおやさん」, your landlord, which uses the very same 「や」. The landlord is the big person of the house, and you pay them the house money. The second kanji, 「賃|ちん」, means a fee or a charge, money paid for using something or for work. Its bottom part is 「貝|かい」, a shell. Long ago in China, shells were used as money, so the shell part shows up in many kanji about money and value. So the word is house fee. Notice it mixes a native reading with a Chinese-origin one, which is less common but happens. A cultural note: when renting an apartment in Japan, besides monthly rent you often pay a deposit, and sometimes key money, a non-refundable thank-you gift to the landlord. To pay rent, say 「家賃をはらう|やちんをはらう」. To recap: house plus fee, paid to the 「大家さん|おおやさん」, who shares the same 「や」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「賃|ちん」, a fee or charge, shows up wherever you pay to use something. 「運賃|うんちん」 is a fare, the fee for being carried, the price on a bus or train ticket. More casually, people say 「電車賃|でんしゃちん」, train fare, or 「バス賃|バスちん」. 「賃金|ちんぎん」 means wages, the fee paid for work. So 「家賃|やちん」 sits in this group as the fee for using a house. As for the first kanji, 「家|や」 also appears in 「家主|やぬし」, the owner of a rented house. Together with 「大家さん|おおやさん」, that gives you two words for a landlord with the same 「や」. The neat thing about 「賃|ちん」 is that it's almost always about money for a service, not money for buying something outright. You rent it, you ride it, or you work for it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Renting in Japan comes with a lot of vocabulary. People look for apartments through a real estate agent, a 「不動産屋|ふどうさんや」. A typical listing shows the monthly rent plus a small maintenance fee. Then there are move-in costs. A deposit, 「敷金|しききん」, is held and partly returned when you leave. Key money, 「礼金|れいきん」, literally thank-you money, is paid to the landlord and not returned. Many places now advertise zero key money to attract tenants. Floor plans are written with letters, like 1K or 2LDK. The number is rooms, and the letters mean living, dining, and kitchen. To complain about expensive rent, say 「家賃が高い|やちんがたかい」. When you're lucky, 「家賃が安い|やちんがやすい」. Count the rent, the fees, and the thank-you money too." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's house 「家|や」 plus fee 「賃|ちん」, with a shell for money at its bottom, and the 「や」 is the same as in 「大家さん|おおやさん」, the landlord you pay.",
      "The fee kanji 「賃|ちん」 appears wherever you pay to use something, like 「運賃|うんちん」, fare, and 「賃金|ちんぎん」, wages, rather than buying outright.",
      "Besides rent, renting means a deposit, 「敷金|しききん」, and non-refundable key money, 「礼金|れいきん」, and you complain with 「家賃が高い|やちんがたかい」."
    ],
    ja: "{毎月|まいつき}[家賃|やちん]をはらいます。",
    en: "I pay rent every month." },
  "そと|外": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji uses its native reading, 「そと」, when it stands alone. You already know its Chinese-origin reading, 「がい」, from 「外国|がいこく」, foreign country, 「外国人|がいこくじん」, foreigner, and 「外国語|がいこくご」, foreign language. A foreign country is literally an outside country. Its opposite is 「中|なか」, inside. To say outside of something, use the same pattern as inside: 「家の外|いえのそと」, outside the house. To do something outside, use the particle 「で」, as in 「外であそぶ|そとであそぶ」, to play outside. There is also a famous cultural idea built on this word. Japanese often divides the world into 「うち」, inside, meaning your own home, family, or company group, and 「そと」, outside, meaning everyone else. How politely you speak often depends on whether someone is inside or outside your group. That's why you use humble words about your own family when talking to outsiders. A way to remember the sound: you go out to the soft toe of the lawn, so-to. That's only a hook. To recap: 「そと」 alone, 「がい」 in compounds, and the opposite of 「なか」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has several readings, and each one opens a different set of words. Besides 「そと」 and 「がい」, there's 「はず」, used in two verbs. 「外す|はずす」 means to take off or remove, like taking off glasses or a watch. 「外れる|はずれる」 means to come off, or to miss, like a guess or a lottery ticket that misses. When a fortune paper or a scratch card loses, it says 「はずれ」. There's also a rare reading, 「げ」, in 「外科|げか」, surgery, the medical department that treats the outside of the body. And in common compounds with 「がい」, you'll meet 「外食|がいしょく」, eating out, 「外出|がいしゅつ」, going out, and 「海外|かいがい」, overseas, beyond the sea. Outside, foreign, remove, miss, all one kanji." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Some handy phrases. 「外は寒い|そとはさむい」, it's cold outside. 「外に出る|そとにでる」, to go out. When you want to suggest eating out instead of cooking, say 「外で食べよう|そとでたべよう」, let's eat out. A useful related word is 「以外|いがい」, other than, except. 「水以外|みずいがい」 means anything except water. A word to handle with care is 「外人|がいじん」, a shortened form of 「外国人|がいこくじん」. Many people use it casually without meaning harm, but some foreign residents find it a bit excluding, as if it means outsider. The full 「外国人|がいこくじん」, or even more politely 「外国の方|がいこくのかた」, is the safer choice. So the outside word can be cozy, like eating out, or a little sensitive. Choose the longer form when talking about people." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the kanji. Look at its shape. On the left is 「夕|ゆう」, the kanji for evening. On the right is a shape that looks like a single stick with a little branch. That part is used in fortune telling words, and some teachers connect it to fortune telling in the evening, though the real history is debated, so treat this purely as a picture. Imagine yourself stepping outside on a summer evening, the sky turning orange, holding a single stick, ready to poke around in the dark garden. Evening on the left, stick on the right, and you, outside the house. As you step out the door, you whisper so, toe, as your bare toe touches the cool grass. Evening, stick, toe on the grass. That's outside." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Alone it's read 「そと」 and in compounds 「がい」, as in 「外国|がいこく」, an outside country, and it underlies the 「うち」 and 「そと」 split between your group and everyone else.",
      "Its 「はず」 reading gives 「外す|はずす」, to remove, and 「外れる|はずれる」, to miss, so one kanji covers outside, foreign, remove, and miss.",
      "Say 「外で食べよう|そとでたべよう」 to suggest eating out, but use the full 「外国人|がいこくじん」 rather than 「外人|がいじん」, which some people find excluding.",
      "Picture stepping outside on an evening, with 「夕|ゆう」 on the left of the kanji and a stick on the right, whispering so, toe as your toe touches the grass."
    ],
    ja: "{子供|こども}たちは[外|そと]であそんでいます。",
    en: "The children are playing outside." },
  "ゆうがた|夕方": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings. The first, 「夕|ゆう」, means evening. Its shape began as a picture of a crescent moon, the moon you see as the sun goes down. The second, 「方|かた」, means direction, side, or way, and also an approximate time, around a certain time. In this word 「かた」 becomes 「がた」. That is a common sound change in Japanese compounds, where the second part's first consonant gets voiced, like 「ひと」 becoming 「びと」. So the word is the moon side of the day, the time around dusk. It means the late afternoon into early evening, roughly from four to six o'clock, as the light fades. After that comes 「夜|よる」 or 「晩|ばん」, night. Compare the other time words you know: 「朝|あさ」, morning, and 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight. You may also hear 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」, the evening meal, with the same first kanji. A cultural note: in many Japanese towns, a loudspeaker plays a gentle melody around five o'clock in the 「夕方|ゆうがた」, telling children it's time to go home. To recap: crescent moon plus the time around, giving the period around dusk." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The evening kanji 「夕|ゆう」 shows up in several food and sky words. 「夕日|ゆうひ」 is the setting sun. When the whole sky glows red, that's 「夕焼け|ゆうやけ」, an evening burn. For the evening meal you'll hear 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」 at home, 「夕食|ゆうしょく」 in a more formal or written style, and 「夕飯|ゆうはん」 in between. Then there's a special one, 「七夕|たなばた」, the star festival on the seventh of July, written seven evening but read with a completely special reading you just have to learn. As for 「方|かた」, it also means way of doing, as in 「読み方|よみかた」, how to read, and it's a polite word for a person, as in 「あの方|あのかた」, that person. So this word joins evening with a sense of around or toward." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Where does this time of day end and night begin? Japanese people use it fairly loosely, from late afternoon until it gets dark, so it moves a little with the seasons. In winter it can be dark by five. Typical phrases include 「夕方から雨|ゆうがたからあめ」, rain from the evening, a common line in weather forecasts. 「夕方に会いましょう|ゆうがたにあいましょう」, let's meet in the late afternoon. Notice the 「に」, since it's a specific part of the day, though people often drop it in casual speech. A friendly greeting at this time is still 「こんにちは」, and 「こんばんは」 starts once it's properly dark. Also notice the contrast with 「晩|ばん」, which is the evening to night time when dinner happens. Late afternoon light, a weather forecast, then dinner." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's crescent moon evening 「夕|ゆう」 plus 「方|かた」, around a time, voiced into 「がた」, meaning the stretch around dusk, roughly four to six.",
      "The evening kanji gives 「夕日|ゆうひ」, setting sun, 「夕焼け|ゆうやけ」, a red sky, and 「夕ご飯|ゆうごはん」, while 「七夕|たなばた」 has a special reading.",
      "It runs loosely from late afternoon until dark, so it shifts with the seasons, and you'll hear 「夕方から雨|ゆうがたからあめ」 in weather forecasts."
    ],
    ja: "[夕方|ゆうがた]{雨|あめ}がふりました。",
    en: "It rained in the evening." },
  "あかるい|明るい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「明|あか」 is a lovely one to break apart. On the left is 「日|ひ」, the sun. On the right is 「月|つき」, the moon. The two brightest things in the sky, side by side, give you bright. Here it uses its native reading, with the kana 「るい」 added to make it an 「い」-adjective. You know this kanji in other words too. In 「明日|あした」, tomorrow, it has a special reading. In 「説明する|せつめいする」, to explain, it uses the Chinese-origin reading 「めい」, and explaining makes something clear, or bright. Like in English, 「明るい|あかるい」 isn't only about light. It also describes a cheerful, upbeat person. 「明るい人|あかるいひと」 means a bright, outgoing, sunny person, and it's a common compliment. It can also describe a bright color. Its opposite is the new word 「暗い|くらい」, dark, and that also works for personality, meaning gloomy. A grammar tip: to say it got bright, use 「明るくなる|あかるくなる」. To recap: sun plus moon equals bright, for rooms, colors, and sunny personalities." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji links several words you already know, with surprising readings. 「明ける|あける」 means for the night or a year to end and the light to come. That's the 「あけまして」 in 「あけましておめでとうございます」. The year has dawned, congratulations. 「明かり|あかり」 is a light, like a lamp in a window. With the Chinese-origin reading 「めい」 you have 「説明|せつめい」, explanation, making something clear. And there's 「明日|あした」, with its special reading, the next bright day. In formal speech, tomorrow can also be 「あす」 or 「みょうにち」, where the kanji is read 「みょう」. So one kanji gives you the dawn of the new year, a lamp, an explanation, and tomorrow. Every one of them has to do with light breaking through, either in the sky or in your mind." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "As an 「い」-adjective, this word follows all the regular patterns, and it's a good one to practice with. Past: 「明るかった|あかるかった」, it was bright. Negative: 「明るくない|あかるくない」, it isn't bright. To make something brighter on purpose, use 「〜くする」: 「部屋を明るくする|へやをあかるくする」, to brighten the room, maybe by opening the curtains. To say it became bright on its own, use 「〜くなる」, as with the sky in the morning. To turn it into a noun, swap 「い」 for 「さ」: 「明るさ|あかるさ」, brightness. You'll see that word on phone screens and settings menus. And for personality, people often say 「明るい性格|あかるいせいかく」, a cheerful personality. Bright, brighter, brightness, it's all just the regular pattern at work." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji puts sun 「日|ひ」 and moon 「月|つき」 side by side for bright, and like English it also describes a cheerful person, 「明るい人|あかるいひと」.",
      "The same kanji gives 「明ける|あける」, the dawn in 「あけましておめでとうございます」, plus 「明かり|あかり」, a light, and 「説明|せつめい」, explanation, all about light breaking through.",
      "It follows regular 「い」-adjective patterns, so 「明るくする|あかるくする」 is to brighten something, and swapping 「い」 for 「さ」 gives 「明るさ|あかるさ」, brightness."
    ],
    ja: "この{部屋|へや}は[明るい|あかるい]です。",
    en: "This room is bright." },
  "くらい|暗い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the opposite of 「明るい|あかるい」, bright, and the kanji are worth comparing. Both have 「日|ひ」, the sun, on the left, because both are about light. In 「暗|くら」, the right side is 「音|おと」, which means sound. In this kanji it is mainly there to hint at the original Chinese pronunciation, not to add meaning. So don't look for a story about sound. Just remember the sun on the left, as in all light-related kanji. A way to remember it: the sun is on the left, but something else is drowning it out, so it's dark. That's only a hook. The word uses its native reading with 「い」 at the end, so it's an 「い」-adjective. Like in English, it can describe a person: 「暗い人|くらいひと」 is a gloomy, quiet, negative person, the opposite of 「明るい人|あかるいひと」. Watch out for a sound-alike. 「くらい」 is also a word meaning about or approximately, as in thirty minutes or so. Context tells them apart. When it gets dark in the evening, say 「暗くなる|くらくなる」. To recap: sun on the left, opposite of 「明るい|あかるい」, for dark rooms and gloomy moods." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「暗|あん」 has a Chinese-origin reading, and it turns up in some useful words. 「暗記|あんき」 means memorizing something by heart, so that you could recite it with your eyes closed, as if in the dark. Students say they have to 「暗記する|あんきする」 vocabulary before a test. 「暗号|あんごう」 is a secret code, a message kept in the dark. With the native reading, you get 「真っ暗|まっくら」, pitch dark, with a small pause that makes it feel sharp and total. And 「薄暗い|うすぐらい」 means dim or gloomy, literally thinly dark, where the 「く」 softens into 「ぐ」. So the native reading 「くら」 is for everyday darkness, while 「あん」 shows up in harder words about hidden things. Pair them in your head: 「暗い|くらい」 for a dark room, 「暗記|あんき」 for learning in your head. One kanji, two doors into it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll use this word most for rooms, streets, and the evening sky. Someone walks into a room and says 「暗いですね|くらいですね」, and that's a gentle hint to turn on the light: 「電気をつけましょうか|でんきをつけましょうか」. Parents tell kids to come home 「暗くなる前に|くらくなるまえに」, before it gets dark. It also works for faces and moods. 「顔が暗い|かおがくらい」 means someone looks down or troubled, and 「暗いニュース|くらいニュース」 is depressing news. Here's a trap for English speakers. Dark as in a deep color is usually not this word. Dark blue is more naturally 「濃い青|こいあお」, using the word for thick or strong. This word is about a lack of light, or a lack of cheer. And calling someone 「暗い|くらい」 is fairly harsh, so be careful saying it about a person. Keep it for light and mood, not for paint colors." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Since this is a regular 「い」-adjective, every form comes from dropping the final 「い」. Not dark is 「暗くない|くらくない」. Was dark is 「暗かった|くらかった」. Wasn't dark is 「暗くなかった|くらくなかった」. To join it to another adjective, use the te-form 「暗くて|くらくて」, as in 「暗くて静か|くらくてしずか」, dark and quiet. The te-form can also give a reason: 「暗くて見えません|くらくてみえません」, it's dark, so I can't see. With 「なる」, it changes into the adverb shape: 「暗くなりました|くらくなりました」, it got dark. And with 「する」, you make it dark on purpose, as in 「部屋を暗くする|へやをくらくする」, to darken the room before a movie. Notice the difference: 「なる」 for it happening, 「する」 for you doing it. Master these forms once and every 「い」-adjective follows." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the opposite of 「明るい|あかるい」, with the sun on the left and 「音|おと」 on the right only as a sound hint, and it also means gloomy for people.",
      "The 「あん」 reading appears in harder words like 「暗記|あんき」, memorizing, while the native 「くら」 gives everyday 「真っ暗|まっくら」, pitch dark.",
      "It's about a lack of light or cheer, so dark blue is 「濃い青|こいあお」 instead, and calling a person 「暗い|くらい」 is fairly harsh.",
      "Use 「暗くなる|くらくなる」 for it getting dark and 「暗くする|くらくする」 for darkening on purpose, while 「暗くて|くらくて」 can give a reason."
    ],
    ja: "{外|そと}はもう[暗い|くらい]です。",
    en: "It's already dark outside." },
  "はずかしい|恥ずかしい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「恥|は」 is built from 「耳|みみ」, ear, on the left, and 「心|こころ」, heart or mind, on the right. A way to remember it: when your heart feels ashamed, your ears turn red. That's a popular memory hook rather than proven history, but it works well. The kanji means shame, and the kana that follow make the word an 「い」-adjective. This one word covers what English splits into several. It can mean embarrassing, as in a situation. It can mean embarrassed or ashamed, as in your own feeling. And it often means shy. If a Japanese person says 「恥ずかしい|はずかしい」 when asked to sing karaoke or give a speech, they mean I'm shy, it's embarrassing, I'd rather not. When the feeling is yours, you just say 「恥ずかしいです|はずかしいです」. You don't need a word for I. If you're talking about someone else, add 「そう」 to show how they seem: 「恥ずかしそう|はずかしそう」, they look embarrassed. A common compliment response is to say this when praised, just to be modest. To recap: ear plus heart as a hook, one word for embarrassing, embarrassed, and shy." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「恥|はじ」 on its own is a noun, shame. A common phrase is 「恥をかく|はじをかく」, to be embarrassed or lose face in front of people, literally to sweat out shame. From the adjective you get 「恥ずかしがる|はずかしがる」, to act shy or be bashful, used about other people. Add 「屋|や」, a person who tends to be a certain way, and you get 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」, a shy person. You can say 「私は恥ずかしがり屋です|わたしははずかしがりやです」 to describe yourself. A close cousin is 「照れる|てれる」, to feel bashful, especially when praised or teased about a crush. 「照れる|てれる」 is a warm, slightly happy embarrassment, while 「恥ずかしい|はずかしい」 can range from shy all the way to truly ashamed. One shared root, from a single blush to a lifelong shy streak." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, modesty is a social skill, and this word is one of its tools. When someone praises your cooking or your Japanese, a natural reply is to wave a hand and say 「いえいえ、恥ずかしいです|いえいえ、はずかしいです」. It doesn't mean you are truly ashamed. It signals that you're not taking the praise too proudly. In polite speech you'll also hear 「お恥ずかしい話ですが|おはずかしいはなしですが」, which means this is embarrassing to admit, but. People use it to open a small confession, like admitting they can't cook or forgot something. It softens the statement and sounds humble. Adults also use it about their own family's behavior, for example a child acting up in public. So when a Japanese person says this word, listen for whether it's a real blush or just polite humility. Very often, it's humility." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Feeling words like this one are normally about the speaker. So 「恥ずかしいです|はずかしいです」 on its own means I'm embarrassed. To describe what someone else feels, Japanese adds 「がる」 to the stem: 「恥ずかしがる|はずかしがる」. In the te-form with 「いる」, 「弟は恥ずかしがっています|おとうとははずかしがっています」 means my little brother is being shy. This is the same pattern as 「ほしがる」, to want, used for other people. Another handy form is the te-form as a reason. 「恥ずかしくて、言えませんでした|はずかしくて、いえませんでした」 means I was too embarrassed to say it. And to make a negative request, 「恥ずかしがらないで|はずかしがらないで」 means don't be shy, a classic line at karaoke when someone won't take the microphone. Speaker feels it, others show it with 「がる」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has ear plus heart, as if ashamed ears turn red, and the one word covers embarrassing, embarrassed, and shy.",
      "The noun 「恥|はじ」 gives 「恥をかく|はじをかく」, to lose face, and a shy person is 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」, while 「照れる|てれる」 is a warmer bashfulness.",
      "It's often polite humility, as in 「いえいえ、恥ずかしいです|いえいえ、はずかしいです」 when praised, or 「お恥ずかしい話ですが|おはずかしいはなしですが」 to open a small confession.",
      "On its own it's about the speaker, so for someone else add 「がる」, as in 「恥ずかしがっています|はずかしがっています」, and 「恥ずかしがらないで|はずかしがらないで」 means don't be shy."
    ],
    ja: "{人|ひと}のまえでうたうのは[恥ずかしい|はずかしい]です。",
    en: "Singing in front of people is embarrassing." },
  "たいせつ|大切": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「大|たい」, means big. It uses the 「たい」 reading here, the same as in 「大変|たいへん」, tough. You also know it as 「だい」 in 「大学|だいがく」 and 「大好き|だいすき」. The second, 「切|せつ」, normally means to cut. You know it from 「切る|きる」, to cut, 「切符|きっぷ」, ticket, and with the same 「せつ」 reading in 「親切|しんせつ」, kind. In these words it doesn't mean slicing. It adds a sense of something keen, deep, or heartfelt. So the word is greatly deep, or very heartfelt, meaning precious or important. A way to remember it: something so precious that losing it would cut you deeply. That's a hook, not the history. It is a 「な」-adjective. Say 「大切な人|たいせつなひと」 for a precious, important person in your life. A very useful pattern is 「大切にする|たいせつにする」, to treasure, to take good care of. For example, you take good care of a gift. You'll also hear 「お体を大切に|おからだをたいせつに」, take care of yourself, at the end of letters. To recap: big plus deep feeling, the word for things and people you treasure." },
      { title: "Near synonyms", text: "Japanese has three words that all translate as important, and they feel different. 「大切|たいせつ」 is the warmest. It's for things and people you care about, like family, a promise, or a keepsake. 「大事|だいじ」 is very close and often interchangeable, a little more everyday. You know it from 「お大事に|おだいじに」, take care, said to someone who is sick. 「重要|じゅうよう」 is the cool, formal one. It's for importance by logic or rank, like an important meeting or an important point in a report. So 「大切な人|たいせつなひと」 is someone dear to you, but 「重要な人|じゅうようなひと」 sounds like a VIP, a key person in a business sense. If you tell someone they are 「重要|じゅうよう」 to you, it sounds strangely like a job evaluation. Use the warm word for the heart, the formal word for the office." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Listen for this word in everyday care and announcements. When giving a present, people may say 「大切にしてね|たいせつにしてね」, take good care of it. Teachers tell children 「ものを大切にしましょう|ものをたいせつにしましょう」, let's treat our things with care, a value tied to not wasting. Adults talk about 「時間を大切にする|じかんをたいせつにする」, making good use of time, and 「家族を大切にする|かぞくをたいせつにする」, cherishing your family. In emails and on station signs you'll see 「大切なお知らせ|たいせつなおしらせ」, an important notice. A mini dialogue: someone asks 「それ、何|それ、なに」, what's that, and you answer 「祖母の指輪です。とても大切なものです|そぼのゆびわです。とてもたいせつなものです」, it's my grandmother's ring, something very precious. Whether it's a ring, your health, or your time, this word says handle with care." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Look closely at 「切|せつ」. On the right is 「刀|かたな」, a sword or blade, which is why it's about cutting. On the left is a shape that looks like the number seven, 「七|なな」. It is commonly explained that this shape originally pictured a cut itself, and the blade was added later to make the meaning clear, though old character histories are not always certain. The kanji has several readings you'll meet. With 「き」, it's 「切る|きる」, to cut, and 「切手|きって」, a postage stamp, from the way stamps were cut from sheets. With a short pause after the 「き」, there's 「切符|きっぷ」, a ticket. With 「せつ」, there's 「親切|しんせつ」, kind, and this word. Later you'll meet 「切ない|せつない」, heartrending, with the same 「せつ」 sound. Notice how the cutting idea grows into feelings that cut deep. A blade on the right, and a meaning that reaches the heart." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's big 「大|たい」 plus 「切|せつ」 in its heartfelt sense, so precious or important, and 「大切にする|たいせつにする」 means to treasure or take good care of.",
      "It's the warmest of the important words, while 「大事|だいじ」 is close and everyday and 「重要|じゅうよう」 is cool and formal, so use the warm one for the heart.",
      "You'll hear 「大切にしてね|たいせつにしてね」 when someone gives a present, and 「大切なお知らせ|たいせつなおしらせ」 on important notices, so it says handle with care.",
      "The kanji 「切|せつ」 has a blade 「刀|かたな」 on the right, and its cutting idea grows into feelings that cut deep, as in 「親切|しんせつ」 and 「切ない|せつない」."
    ],
    ja: "これは{母|はは}の[大切な|たいせつな|たいせつ]しゃしんです。",
    en: "This is my mother's precious photo." },
  "ふあん|不安": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「不|ふ」, is a negative prefix meaning not or un-. It shows up in many words, the way un- does in English. The second, 「安|あん」, means peace, calm, safety. You know this kanji with its native reading in 「安い|やすい」, cheap. Its shape is a roof on top with 「女|おんな」, woman, underneath. A way to remember it: a woman safe at home under her roof feels calm. That's a traditional memory hook, not certain history. So the whole word is not calm, not at ease, which means anxious or uneasy. It is a 「な」-adjective, and it can be used for your feelings and for situations. To say you're worried about something, say that thing, then 「が不安です|がふあんです」. To say you started to feel anxious, say 「不安になる|ふあんになる」. It feels a bit more serious and lasting than a little worry, like a vague unease about the future or a test. Its relaxed opposite is 「安心|あんしん」, feeling relieved, where the same 「安|あん」 appears with heart. To recap: not plus calm, the uneasy feeling of not being at peace." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The prefix 「不|ふ」 flips a word into its opposite, and you can collect a whole set. 「便利|べんり」 is convenient, and 「不便|ふべん」 is inconvenient. 「親切|しんせつ」 is kind, and 「不親切|ふしんせつ」 is unkind. 「満|まん」 means full, and 「不満|ふまん」 is dissatisfaction. Then there's this word, not at peace. Now follow the other half, 「安|あん」. 「安心|あんしん」, relief, adds heart, so a peaceful heart. 「安全|あんぜん」, safety, adds the kanji for whole or complete. And with the native reading, 「安い|やすい」 means cheap, a price that puts you at ease. So 「安心|あんしん」 and this word are a natural pair: one is a calm heart, the other is calm taken away. When you see 「不|ふ」 at the start of a word, try reading it as un-, and the meaning often falls right out." },
      { title: "Two kinds of worry", text: "English speakers often mix this up with 「心配|しんぱい」, so let's separate them. 「心配|しんぱい」 is worry about something or someone specific, and it works as a 「する」 verb. 「母は私を心配しています|はははわたしをしんぱいしています」 means my mother is worried about me. You can worry about other people with it. This word is more an inner state, a feeling of unease, often vague and about what might happen. It's a 「な」-adjective and a noun, not a 「する」 verb. You say 「将来が不安です|しょうらいがふあんです」, I feel uneasy about the future, or 「不安を感じる|ふあんをかんじる」, to feel anxiety. When comforting someone, either works: 「心配しないで|しんぱいしないで」, don't worry, is the everyday phrase. So 「心配|しんぱい」 points at a target, while this word describes the fog you're standing in." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word often comes up before something new or uncertain: a first trip abroad, a new job, a big exam, living alone. A typical sentence is 「一人で旅行するのは、ちょっと不安です|ひとりでりょこうするのは、ちょっとふあんです」, traveling alone makes me a little nervous. The 「ちょっと」 keeps it light. A mini dialogue: one friend says 「明日の面接、不安だな|あしたのめんせつ、ふあんだな」, I'm nervous about tomorrow's interview. The other answers 「大丈夫だよ|だいじょうぶだよ」, you'll be fine. You'll also hear 「不安な気持ち|ふあんなきもち」, an anxious feeling, and 「不安そうな顔|ふあんそうなかお」, a worried-looking face. On the news, it can describe society, like economic unease. Notice that in casual speech it ends in 「だ」 or 「だな」, like any 「な」-adjective. Say it when the future feels shaky, and expect someone to reassure you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the negative 「不|ふ」 plus calm 「安|あん」, so not at ease, a lasting vague unease, and its relaxed opposite is 「安心|あんしん」, relief.",
      "The prefix 「不|ふ」 works like un-, flipping 「便利|べんり」 into 「不便|ふべん」, and 「安|あん」 gives 「安心|あんしん」 and 「安全|あんぜん」.",
      "Unlike 「心配|しんぱい」, which targets someone specific and works as a 「する」 verb, this word is a vague inner fog, a 「な」-adjective as in 「将来が不安です|しょうらいがふあんです」.",
      "It comes up before something new or uncertain, softened as in 「ちょっと不安です|ちょっとふあんです」, and friends usually answer with 「大丈夫だよ|だいじょうぶだよ」."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}のしけんが[不安|ふあん]です。",
    en: "I'm worried about tomorrow's exam." },
  "むり|無理": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「無|む」, means nothing, without, or there isn't. The second, 「理|り」, means reason, logic, or the natural order of things. You know it from 「料理|りょうり」, cooking, where it means arranging or handling things properly. Put them together and you get without reason. Something that goes against reason is unreasonable, too much, or impossible. It's a 「な」-adjective. The most useful phrase is simply 「無理です|むりです」, that's impossible, or I can't. It's a common, fairly direct way to turn something down. A softer way is 「ちょっと無理です|ちょっとむりです」, which is a classic polite refusal. Among friends, just 「無理|むり」 or 「むりむり」 means no way. Another phrase you'll hear all the time is 「無理しないでください|むりしないでください」, please don't overdo it, said when someone is working too hard or sick. Here 「無理する|むりする」 means pushing yourself beyond reason. A way to remember it: moo, ree, the cow says this is impossible. That's just a hook. To recap: without plus reason, for impossible tasks and for not pushing yourself too hard." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this word lead to useful relatives. 「無|む」 means without. 「無料|むりょう」 is free of charge, literally without a fee, and you'll see it on signs and websites everywhere. 「無駄|むだ」 means wasteful or useless. The same kanji has another reading, 「ぶ」, in 「無事|ぶじ」, safe and sound, literally without incident. And its native reading gives 「無い|ない」, there isn't, though that's usually written in kana. Now 「理|り」, reason or logic. You know 「料理|りょうり」, cooking. There's also 「理由|りゆう」, reason, as in the reason why, and 「地理|ちり」, geography, the logic of the land. So the word you're learning combines without and reason, and its cousins all lean on one half or the other. Next time you see free on a sign, look for that same 「無|む」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Beyond the basic sentence, this word shifts shape in handy ways. As a 「な」-adjective before a noun, 「無理なお願い|むりなおねがい」 is an unreasonable request, and people sometimes start with 「無理なお願いですが|むりなおねがいですが」, I know this is a lot to ask, but. With 「に」 it becomes an adverb, forcibly or against one's will. 「無理に食べなくてもいいですよ|むりにたべなくてもいいですよ」 means you don't have to force yourself to eat it. A stronger version is 「無理やり|むりやり」, by force. And one phrase surprises learners: 「無理もない|むりもない」. It looks like no impossibility, but it means it's only natural, no wonder. If a friend is tired after a long shift, you can say 「疲れるのも無理もない|つかれるのもむりもない」, no wonder you're tired. One word, several useful tools." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese generally avoids a flat no, and how this word is used shows that. Saying it straight is clear, but it can feel blunt to a boss or a customer. In business, people often say 「それはちょっと難しいですね|それはちょっとむずかしいですね」, that's a bit difficult, and everyone understands it as a polite no. A learner might hear difficult and offer to try harder, when the answer was already given. Among friends, though, the word is used freely, even playfully. Young people say it with feeling about things they can't stand, like a bug or a scary movie, meaning no way, I can't. So the word sits at the casual end of refusing. With strangers and superiors, reach for softer phrases. With friends, it's perfectly natural. Knowing the level is half of using it well." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's without 「無|む」 plus reason 「理|り」, so impossible, with 「ちょっと無理です|ちょっとむりです」 as a polite refusal and 「無理しないでください|むりしないでください」 meaning don't overdo it.",
      "Its kanji lead to relatives like 「無料|むりょう」, free of charge, 「無事|ぶじ」, safe and sound, and 「理由|りゆう」, reason.",
      "With 「に」 it means forcibly, as in 「無理に食べなくてもいい|むりにたべなくてもいい」, and surprisingly 「無理もない|むりもない」 means no wonder.",
      "It's the casual end of refusing, fine with friends, but with bosses or customers people say 「ちょっと難しいですね|ちょっとむずかしいですね」 as a polite no."
    ],
    ja: "{一日|いちにち}でぜんぶよむのは[無理|むり]です。",
    en: "It's impossible to read it all in one day." },
  "あく|開く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「開ける|あける」, to open, from lesson six. They share the same kanji, 「開|あ」. Look at its outside: that is 「門|もん」, a gate. The part inside is traditionally explained as hands lifting the bar, so picture a gate being opened. Here's the key idea of lesson eighteen. Japanese has pairs of verbs. One describes someone doing something to an object. That's the transitive verb, and the object takes 「を」. The other describes something happening by itself. That's the intransitive verb, and the thing takes 「が」. So 「ドアを開ける|ドアをあける」 means someone opens the door. And 「ドアが開く|ドアがあく」 means the door opens, with no one mentioned. Japanese often prefers the 「が」 version when describing what happened, as in the door opened, or the store opens at ten. Notice the shape here: 「あける」 ends in 「える」, 「あく」 ends in 「う」. With 「〜ている」, 「開いている|あいている」 means it is open right now. One more note: the same kanji can also be read 「ひらく」, to open up, like a book or flower. To recap: same gate kanji as 「開ける|あける」, but with 「が」, the door opens by itself." },
      { title: "Verb pairs", text: "This verb belongs to a club of partner verbs, and knowing the club helps a lot. 「閉まる|しまる」, to close by itself, pairs with 「閉める|しめる」, to close something. 「つく」, a light comes on, pairs with 「つける」, to turn it on. 「始まる|はじまる」, to begin, pairs with 「始める|はじめる」, to start something. 「消える|きえる」, to go out, pairs with 「消す|けす」, to turn off. Notice a common pattern. Many verbs ending in 「ある」 are the happens-by-itself kind, and many ending in 「える」 are the someone-does-it kind. But it's only a tendency. In the 「消える|きえる」 pair, the 「える」 one is the intransitive side. So learn pairs together, as sets. When you see a store sign, say both: 「店が開く|みせがあく」, 「店を開ける|みせをあける」. Pairs learned together stay together." },
      { title: "One spelling, two readings", text: "The same written verb can be read 「ひらく」, and the two readings feel different. 「あく」 is about something being open versus shut, like a door or a box. 「ひらく」 is about opening up or spreading out, like a book, an umbrella, a hand, or a flower in bloom. 「花が開く|はながひらく」 means a flower opens. 「ひらく」 can also be used with 「を」, and it's the verb for holding events: 「パーティーを開く|パーティーをひらく」, to throw a party, and 「店を開く|みせをひらく」, to open a new business. There's also a Chinese-origin reading, 「かい」, as in 「開店|かいてん」, a store opening, and 「開始|かいし」, the start of something formal. Since both native readings are written the same way, the context tells you which to say. Opening shut things is 「あく」, opening out and launching is 「ひらく」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Here's a word you'll mix up if you're not careful. There is another 「あく」, written with the kanji for sky or empty, 「空く|あく」. It means to become empty or free. The famous question 「この席、空いていますか|このせき、あいていますか」 means is this seat free? Not open, but vacant. When you hear 「あいている」, ask yourself: is something open, or is something empty? A store door that is open is 「開いている|あいている」. A seat, a room, or your schedule that is free is 「空いている|あいている」. 「時間が空いている|じかんがあいている」 means I have free time. A memory trick: picture a door that opens, ah, on its own, and behind it an empty room, ah again, nobody there. Two sounds, one surprise. Gate kanji for opening, sky kanji for empty." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the gate kanji from 「開ける|あける」, but intransitive, so 「ドアが開く|ドアがあく」 means the door opens by itself with 「が」, while 「を開ける|をあける」 is someone opening it.",
      "It belongs to a club of verb pairs like 「閉まる|しまる」 and 「閉める|しめる」, and since endings are only a tendency, learn each pair together as a set.",
      "Read as 「ひらく」, it means opening out or spreading, like a book or flower, and launching, as in 「パーティーを開く|パーティーをひらく」, while 「あく」 is open versus shut.",
      "Don't confuse it with 「空く|あく」, to become empty, so a free seat is 「空いている|あいている」 while an open door is 「開いている|あいている」."
    ],
    ja: "みせは{十時|じゅうじ}に[開きます|あきます|あく]。",
    en: "The store opens at ten o'clock." },
  "しまる|閉まる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「閉める|しめる」, to close something, from lesson six. Both use the kanji 「閉|し」. Like 「開|あ」, open, it has the gate shape, 「門|もん」, around the outside. Picture something blocking the gateway, a gate shut tight. That picture is a hook. Now the pair. 「閉める|しめる」 is transitive: someone closes something, and that thing takes 「を」, as in 「まどを閉める|まどをしめる」, I close the window. 「閉まる|しまる」 is intransitive: the thing closes on its own, and it takes 「が」, as in 「まどが閉まる|まどがしまる」, the window closes. This pair shows a very common pattern. The intransitive verb ends in 「まる」, and the transitive one ends in 「める」. You'll see the same pattern in 「助かる|たすかる」 and 「助ける|たすける」. Use 「閉まる|しまる」 for store hours too: the bank closes at three. With 「〜ている」, 「閉まっている|しまっている」 means it is closed right now, a common sign of bad luck when you arrive late. To recap: gate kanji, 「を閉める|をしめる」 when you close it, 「が閉まる|がしまる」 when it closes." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「閉|へい」 has a second life in shop signs, where it uses its Chinese-origin reading. On a closed shop door you may see 「閉店|へいてん」, shop closed, or closing for good. Its mirror image is 「開店|かいてん」, shop open, built with the open kanji 「開|かい」. Both kanji sit inside the same gate frame, so the pair is easy to spot side by side on a sign. Inside the gate of 「閉|へい」 is a small shape that looks like a bar or a peg wedged in place. Japanese school books describe it as something blocking the gate, though the deeper history is debated, so treat that as a handy picture. When you read the verb out loud, use the native reading, 「しまる」. When you read the sign, it's 「へい」. Two readings, one locked gate." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Ride any train in Japan and you'll hear this verb dozens of times a day. Just before departure, a recorded voice says 「ドアが閉まります|ドアがしまります」, the doors are closing, often followed by 「ご注意ください|ごちゅういください」, please be careful. Notice it's the doors closing by themselves, so it uses 「が」 and the intransitive verb. Nobody is named as the closer. Shops use it too. You might ask a clerk 「何時に閉まりますか|なんじにしまりますか」, what time do you close? And when you arrive too late, a friend might sigh 「もう閉まっている|もうしまっている」, it's already closed. Listen for it next time you watch a video of a Tokyo train. Once you hear it, you'll never miss it." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Three words start with the same 「しま」 or 「しめ」 sounds, and learners mix them up. 「閉まる|しまる」 is the door closing by itself. 「閉める|しめる」 is you closing it. And 「しまう」 means to put something away, like putting dishes back into a cupboard. That one looks close, but it ends in 「う」, not 「る」. It's also the 「しまう」 hiding inside 「〜てしまう」, the pattern for doing something completely or regrettably. Here's a memory trick to keep them apart. 「しまる」 has an 「る」 like a door rolling shut on its rail. 「しまう」 has an 「う」, like the sigh you make putting the laundry away. Silly, but it works. Listen for the last sound, and you'll know which one you heard." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the intransitive partner of 「閉める|しめる」, so 「まどが閉まる|まどがしまる」 means the window closes by itself, following the 「まる」 and 「める」 pattern.",
      "On shop signs the kanji reads 「へい」, as in 「閉店|へいてん」, shop closed, the mirror of 「開店|かいてん」, both inside the same gate frame.",
      "Every train announces 「ドアが閉まります|ドアがしまります」, the doors closing by themselves, and you can ask a shop 「何時に閉まりますか|なんじにしまりますか」.",
      "Keep it apart from 「閉める|しめる」, you closing it, and 「しまう」, to put away, by listening for the last sound, 「る」 like a door rolling shut."
    ],
    ja: "ぎんこうは{三時|さんじ}に[閉まります|しまります|しまる]。",
    en: "The bank closes at three." },
  "あやまる|謝る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「謝|あやま」 uses its native reading here. Look at its left side: that's the speech or words radical, the same one you see in 「話|はなし」 and 「語|ご」. Apologizing is something done with words, so it makes sense. The kanji also carries the idea of thanks. In the word 「感謝|かんしゃ」, gratitude, the same kanji appears. In Japanese culture, apologizing and thanking are closely tied. That's why 「すみません」 can mean both I'm sorry and thank you for your trouble. Grammar point: you apologize to someone using 「に」. So 「ともだちに謝る|ともだちにあやまる」 means to apologize to a friend. To say what you apologize for, you can use the 「て」-form or 「こと」. The actual words you say when you 「謝る|あやまる」 are 「ごめんなさい」, casual 「ごめん」, or 「すみません」, and very politely 「もうしわけありません」. Japan has a strong culture of apologizing, even for small things, to keep relationships smooth. A way to remember it: ah, yama, the mountain of things I must apologize for. That's a hook. To recap: words radical, apologize to someone with 「に」, and closely linked to thanks." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "There's a homophone hiding here. 「誤る|あやまる」, written with a different kanji, means to make a mistake, to err. Its noun form, 「誤り|あやまり」, means an error, and you'll see it in formal writing and on test instructions. The two words sound identical, and many Japanese people enjoy the link: you make a mistake, then you apologize for it. Linguists generally treat them as related, but don't lean on that history too hard. In everyday speech, though, the apologize meaning is the one you'll hear, because ordinary conversation says 「まちがえる」 for making a mistake. So if someone says 「謝ってください|あやまってください」, they want an apology, not an error. The kanji tells them apart in writing, and context does it in speech. One sound, two linked ideas: err, then apologize." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a regular 「う」-verb ending in 「る」, so don't treat it like a 「る」-verb. The polite form is 「謝ります|あやまります」, the 「て」-form is 「謝って|あやまって」, and the negative is 「謝らない|あやまらない」. That 「ら」 in the negative is your proof it's a 「う」-verb. It works with patterns from your lessons. 「謝ったほうがいい|あやまったほうがいい」 means you'd better apologize. 「謝らなくてもいい|あやまらなくてもいい」 means you don't have to apologize, a kind thing to say to someone who's over-apologizing. And with the volitional, 「謝ろう|あやまろう」, I'll go apologize, you can show resolve. To name the reason, you usually say it in the apology itself, like 「おくれてすみません」, sorry for being late. Remember: 「ら」 in the negative, 「って」 in the 「て」-form." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The word you use for an apology changes with how serious things are. Between friends, 「ごめん」 is plenty. At work you'd say 「すみません」 or 「もうしわけありません」. When a company makes a big mistake, the news uses a stiff Chinese-origin word, 「謝罪|しゃざい」, a formal apology, and you'll see executives bowing deeply at a press conference. In business letters, an apology is often 「おわび」, as in a notice that begins 「おわびします」, we apologize. So the verb 「謝る|あやまる」 is the neutral, everyday word for the act itself. Here's a tiny dialogue. A child breaks a cup. The parent says 「ちゃんと謝りなさい|ちゃんとあやまりなさい」, apologize properly. The child bows and says 「ごめんなさい」. Same act, many levels of words." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The words radical on its left fits apologizing, the same kanji means thanks in 「感謝|かんしゃ」, and you apologize to someone with 「に」.",
      "A homophone, 「誤る|あやまる」, means to err, a handy link of making a mistake and then apologizing, though everyday speech uses 「まちがえる」 for mistakes.",
      "It's a 「う」-verb, so the negative is 「謝らない|あやまらない」 and the 「て」-form is 「謝って|あやまって」, as in 「謝ったほうがいい|あやまったほうがいい」.",
      "It's the neutral verb for the act, while the words themselves range from 「ごめん」 to 「もうしわけありません」, and the news uses formal 「謝罪|しゃざい」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}に[謝りました|あやまりました|あやまる]。",
    en: "I apologized to my mother." },
  "おす|押す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「押|お」 uses its native reading. Its left side is the hand radical, a squeezed form of 「手|て」. Lots of action verbs have it, because they're things you do with your hands. So this is a hand action: pressing or pushing. It covers both pushing something big like a door or a cart, and pressing something small like a button. So 「ボタンを押す|ボタンをおす」 is to press a button, and 「スイッチを押す|スイッチをおす」 is to push the switch. It's a regular transitive verb, so what you push takes 「を」. Here is a very practical tip. On doors in Japan you'll often see the kanji 「押|おす」, meaning push, and its opposite 「引|ひく」, meaning pull. Knowing these two saves you from walking into doors. Another place you'll hear it is on crowded trains, where staff sometimes gently push people in, and people say 「押さないで|おさないで」, don't push. In conjugation it's a normal 「う」-verb: 「押します|おします」, 「押して|おして」. To recap: hand radical, push or press, and look for it written on doors." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese school kids learn a fire drill slogan built partly on this verb. It's 「おかしも」, which also sounds like the word for snacks, so it sticks. Each syllable starts a rule. 「お」 is 「おさない」, don't push. 「か」 is 「かけない」, don't run. 「し」 is 「しゃべらない」, don't talk. And 「も」 is 「もどらない」, don't go back. The very first rule is the negative of 「押す|おす」, because pushing in a crowd is what makes evacuations dangerous. You'll also see signs at busy stations or events saying 「押さないでください|おさないでください」, please don't push. So this little verb carries real safety weight in Japan. Remember 「おかしも」, and you've got a negative of this verb for free." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Two other words share this sound, and one of them is huge in pop culture. 「推す|おす」, with a different kanji, means to recommend or to back someone. From it comes 「推し|おし」, your favorite member of an idol group, or the character you root for. Fans say 「私の推し|わたしのおし」, my fave. Many people feel the image of pushing someone forward fits that meaning, which is why the slang feels natural, but treat that as a helpful picture. There's also 「雄|おす」, a male animal, with its partner 「雌|めす」, female. Its accent is different from the verb, and context almost always makes the meaning clear. So when you hear 「おす」, ask yourself: is someone pressing a button, cheering for a favorite, or talking about a male cat? Three words, one sound." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. Imagine a huge, grumpy ox standing in front of an elevator. He can't use his hooves to tap the tiny button, so he leans in and pushes it with his giant nose. Ox, 「おす」, push. The elevator dings, the doors open, and the ox squeezes in, pushing everyone to the back. Now add the kanji. The ox needs a hand to press the button properly, and the left side of 「押|お」 is the hand radical. Picture the ox wearing a single white glove on one hoof, finally pressing the button with style. Every time you press an elevator button, think of that ox. Pushy ox, pressed button." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The hand radical marks it as a hand action, pushing or pressing, as in 「ボタンを押す|ボタンをおす」, and doors in Japan show 「押|おす」 for push and 「引|ひく」 for pull.",
      "The school fire drill slogan 「おかしも」 starts with 「おさない」, don't push, because pushing in a crowd makes evacuations dangerous.",
      "It shares its sound with 「推す|おす」, to back someone, source of 「推し|おし」, your fave, and with 「雄|おす」, a male animal.",
      "Picture a grumpy ox wearing a single white glove for the hand radical, pushing an elevator button with style."
    ],
    ja: "このボタンを[押して|おして|おす]ください。",
    en: "Please press this button." },
  "おとす|落とす": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the transitive partner of 「落ちる|おちる」, which is also in this lesson. Both use the kanji 「落|お」, which means to fall. It has the grass radical on top and the water radical on the left. A way to picture it: leaves and drops falling down. That's a nice image, but take it as a hook. Now the pair. 「落とす|おとす」 means to drop something, and the thing takes 「を」: 「さいふを落とす|さいふをおとす」, to drop your wallet. 「落ちる|おちる」 means something falls by itself, and it takes 「が」: 「さいふが落ちる|さいふがおちる」, the wallet falls. Notice the pattern. Many transitive verbs end in 「す」, like 「落とす|おとす」, 「こわす」, 「よごす」, and 「けす」. Very often, that 「す」 is your clue that someone is doing it. An important meaning shift: 「落とす|おとす」 is also how Japanese says to lose something you dropped somewhere. If you say you dropped your wallet, people understand you lost it. Lost wallets in Japan are often returned to a police box, which is a famous point of pride. You also know 「落ち込む|おちこむ」, to get depressed, where your mood falls. To recap: 「を落とす|をおとす」, you drop it, versus 「が落ちる|がおちる」, it falls." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Lost something in Japan? The word you need is 「落とし物|おとしもの」, literally a dropped thing, a lost item. You'd go to the 「交番|こうばん」, the little police box, and say 「さいふを落としました|さいふをおとしました」, I dropped my wallet. Japan is famous for returning lost wallets with the cash still inside, and train stations have lost-and-found offices full of umbrellas. The verb has other everyday uses too. 「スピードを落とす|スピードをおとす」 means to slow down. 「よごれを落とす|よごれをおとす」 means to get a stain out. And 「気を落とす|きをおとす」 means to lose heart, as in 「気を落とさないで|きをおとさないで」, don't be discouraged. Dropping objects, speed, stains, and spirits: one handy verb." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「落|らく」 has a fun Chinese-origin reading in 「落語|らくご」, Japan's traditional comic storytelling, where one performer kneels on a cushion and plays every character. Every story builds to a final punchline, and that punchline is called the 「落ち|おち」, literally the drop, from the same kanji. Today people use 「おち」 for the punchline of any joke or story. If a joke falls flat, someone might say there's no 「おち」. You'll also hear 「落ちる|おちる」, the intransitive partner, and 「落ち葉|おちば」, fallen leaves. So one kanji covers dropping your wallet, leaves falling in autumn, and the moment a story lands. Next time a joke gets a laugh, think: that was the drop." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Dropping things is almost always an accident, so this verb pairs perfectly with 「〜てしまう」, the pattern for regret. 「さいふを落としてしまった|さいふをおとしてしまった」 means I went and dropped my wallet, ugh. In casual speech it shrinks to 「落としちゃった|おとしちゃった」. The verb itself is a regular 「う」-verb: 「落とします|おとします」, 「落として|おとして」, 「落とさない|おとさない」. With 「〜ないように」 you can give a warning, like 「落とさないように|おとさないように」, careful not to drop it, which you'll hear when someone hands you a fragile cup. And remember that the dropped thing takes 「を」, while the place it falls from takes 「から」 or 「に」. Wrap it up with this: dropped it, 「しまった」, and 「ちゃった」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the transitive partner of 「落ちる|おちる」, so you drop something with 「を」, and the 「す」 ending is a clue that someone is doing it.",
      "A lost item is 「落とし物|おとしもの」, reported at a 「交番|こうばん」, and the verb also means slowing down, removing stains, or losing heart, as in 「気を落とす|きをおとす」.",
      "From the same kanji, the punchline of 「落語|らくご」 is the 「落ち|おち」, the drop, now used for the punchline of any story.",
      "Since dropping is usually an accident, it pairs with 「〜てしまう」, as in 「落としちゃった|おとしちゃった」, and 「落とさないように|おとさないように」 is a warning."
    ],
    ja: "さいふを[落としました|おとしました|おとす]。",
    en: "I dropped my wallet." },
  "おゆがわく|お湯が沸く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a whole phrase. The first part, 「お湯|おゆ」, is hot water. Japanese uses different words for cold and hot water. Cold or normal water is 「水|みず」, and hot water is 「お湯|おゆ」, usually with the polite 「お」. The kanji 「湯|ゆ」 has the water radical on the left, three drops. The verb 「沸く|わく」 means to boil, and its kanji also has the water radical. So it's literally hot water boils. Japanese describes boiling with hot water even before it's hot, which surprises English speakers. This verb comes in a pair. In lesson seventeen you learned 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」, to boil water, where you do it and the water takes 「を」. Here, 「お湯が沸く|おゆがわく」, the water boils by itself, and it takes 「が」. The transitive one ends in 「かす」, and the intransitive one ends in plain 「く」. In daily life, you'll hear 「お湯が沸いたよ|おゆがわいたよ」, the water has boiled, when the kettle whistles or beeps. Also, Japanese baths are often heated by a machine that announces in a voice that the bath is ready. To recap: hot water plus boil, the 「が」 partner of 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Many Japanese homes have an automatic bath. You press a button in the kitchen, the tub fills itself and heats up, and then a cheerful recorded voice announces 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」, the bath is ready. It's the same verb, used for the bath water reaching the right heat. Some machines even play a little tune. People say 「お風呂が沸いた|おふろがわいた」 at home to tell the family the bath is ready, even though the water is nowhere near boiling. That shows the verb really means heated up to the target temperature. Electric kettles beep when the water boils, and you might say 「お湯が沸いたよ|おゆがわいたよ」, the water's ready. So this verb is part of daily household rhythm. Kettles and baths both get to 「沸く|わく」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Both kanji have Chinese-origin readings you'll meet later. 「湯|ゆ」 is read 「とう」 in 「銭湯|せんとう」, a public bath house. Outside many bath houses hangs a cloth curtain with just one big kana on it, 「ゆ」, hot water, so you know you can bathe there. Hot spring signs show the same idea with a little steaming symbol. The verb kanji 「沸|わ」 is read 「ふつ」 in the science word 「沸騰|ふっとう」, boiling, as in a full rolling boil. Notice the small 「っ」 there. Both kanji carry the three-drop water radical on the left, which makes sense for things about water. So: native readings in your phrase, Chinese readings in bath houses and science class. Water on the left, heat in the meaning." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. Picture a pot of water on the stove. As it heats up, the little bubbles start jumping and dancing, totally excited. They're shouting 「わくわく」, which is the Japanese sound word for excited, thrilled anticipation. Some people link 「わくわく」 to 「沸く|わく」, bubbles rising like excitement rising in your chest, though that link is best used as a hook. Now add the kettle whistle and a cat jumping off the counter in surprise. The water is 「わく」-ing, the bubbles are 「わくわく」-ing, and you are excited for your cup of tea. So when the water boils, think of happy bubbles, thrilled and rising. 「わく」, it bubbles up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Japanese says hot water boils, using 「お湯|おゆ」, and this is the 「が」 partner of 「お湯を沸かす|おゆをわかす」, where you boil it.",
      "Automatic baths announce 「お風呂が沸きました|おふろがわきました」, showing the verb really means heated up to the target temperature, not boiling.",
      "The kanji read 「とう」 in 「銭湯|せんとう」, a public bath, and 「ふつ」 in 「沸騰|ふっとう」, boiling, both with the water radical on the left.",
      "Picture bubbles in a pot dancing and shouting 「わくわく」, excited, as the water 「わく」, bubbling up."
    ],
    ja: "[お湯が沸きました|おゆがわきました|おゆがわく]よ。",
    en: "The water has boiled." },
  "ころぶ|転ぶ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「転|ころ」 means to turn or roll. Look at its left side: it's 「車|くるま」, a wheel or vehicle, something that rolls. You already know this kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「てん」 in two words. 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, bicycle, is literally a self-turning vehicle, the wheels turn by your own power. And 「運転する|うんてんする」, to drive, is moving and turning the wheels. Here it uses a native reading, 「ころ」. That sound connects to a fun Japanese sound word, 「ころころ」, the sound of something small rolling along. A way to remember it: when you fall down, you roll, 「ころころ」. The sound word link is a hook, but a handy one. The meaning is to trip and fall over, the kind of fall where you lose your footing, slip on ice, or trip on a step. It's intransitive, so there's no object. You simply say the person 「転ぶ|ころぶ」. Use 「で」 for where it happened. A common follow-up is 「転んでけがをした|ころんでけがをした」, I fell and got hurt. To someone who might slip on ice, say 「転ばないでね|ころばないでね」, don't fall. To recap: the rolling wheel kanji from 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, used for tripping and falling over." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「う」-verb ending in 「ぶ」, which means its 「て」-form and past form use the 「ん」 sound. So it's 「転んで|ころんで」 and 「転んだ|ころんだ」, just like 「あそぶ」 becomes 「あそんで」 and 「よぶ」 becomes 「よんで」. Learners sometimes say 「ころって」 by mistake, so watch for that. It's intransitive. You are the one who falls, and you take 「が」 or 「は」. The place you fall uses 「で」, as in 「えきで転んだ|えきでころんだ」, I fell at the station. Pair it with 「〜てしまう」 for regret, or warn someone with 「転ばないように|ころばないように」, careful you don't fall. If you hurt yourself, add 「けがをした」. Remember: 「ぶ」 verbs go 「んで」 and 「んだ」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Two well-known sayings use this verb. The first is 「七転び八起き|ななころびやおき」, fall down seven times, get up eight. It's the Japanese version of never give up. It's closely tied to the 「だるま」 doll, a round, weighted red figure that pops back upright every time you knock it over. People buy one for a new goal, paint in one eye, and paint the other when the goal is achieved. The second saying is 「転ばぬ先の杖|ころばぬさきのつえ」, a cane before you fall. That one means it's better to prepare ahead, like better safe than sorry. The 「ぬ」 is an old negative, so it's literally before not falling. Both sayings turn a simple trip into wisdom. Fall, then rise." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful here, because one wrong sound turns a small accident into a crime. 「ころぶ」 means to fall over. But 「ころす」, with 「す」 at the end, means to kill. Mix them up and 「ゆきの日に転んだ|ゆきのひにころんだ」, I fell on a snowy day, could become something very alarming. Also compare 「たおれる」. That word is for collapsing or toppling over, like a tree falling in a storm, or a person fainting from heat. 「転ぶ|ころぶ」 is specifically tripping and falling while walking or running. A bike can 「たおれる」 when it tips over, but you 「転ぶ|ころぶ」 when you lose your footing. So: 「ぶ」 for a tumble, never 「す」. Listen carefully to that final sound." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has a wheel 「車|くるま」 on its left, the same one in 「自転車|じてんしゃ」, and it means tripping and falling over, rolling 「ころころ」 as a hook.",
      "As a 「ぶ」 verb, its 「て」-form and past are 「転んで|ころんで」 and 「転んだ|ころんだ」, and the place you fall takes 「で」.",
      "The saying 「七転び八起き|ななころびやおき」, fall seven times, get up eight, is tied to the 「だるま」 doll that always pops back up.",
      "Don't swap the last sound, since 「ころす」 means to kill, and 「たおれる」 is for toppling or collapsing rather than tripping."
    ],
    ja: "{雪|ゆき}の{日|ひ}に[転びました|ころびました|ころぶ]。",
    en: "I fell down on a snowy day." },
  "こわす|壊す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「壊|こわ」 means to break down or destroy. Its left side is the earth radical, 「土|つち」, which appears in many kanji about ground, walls, and buildings, a reminder of walls crumbling. This is the transitive member of a pair. 「壊す|こわす」 means someone breaks something, and the thing takes 「を」. Its partner, also in this lesson, is 「壊れる|こわれる」, something breaks, and that takes 「が」. Notice the endings: 「す」 for the doing verb, 「れる」 for the happening verb. You'll see the same pattern with 「汚す|よごす」 and 「汚れる|よごれる」. The 「す」 ending is a useful clue that someone is doing the action. Use 「壊す|こわす」 for breaking objects like a camera, a toy, or a chair, and for machines you make stop working. A very common expression is 「おなかを壊す|おなかをこわす」, to break your stomach, meaning to get an upset stomach. Note that for snapping thin things like bones or sticks, Japanese uses a different verb, so 「壊す|こわす」 is mainly for objects and machines. To recap: the earth radical, someone breaks something with 「を」, and its partner 「壊れる|こわれる」 takes 「が」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb stretches far beyond cameras and toys. Japanese people say 「からだを壊す|からだをこわす」, to ruin your health, usually from overwork. 「おなかを壊す|おなかをこわす」 means to get an upset stomach, often after eating something bad. And 「ふんいきを壊す|ふんいきをこわす」 means to ruin the mood, like telling a sad story at a birthday party. Here's a common mistake. Breaking a large bill into smaller change is not 「壊す|こわす」. That uses a different verb, 「くずす」, as in asking a shop clerk to break a thousand-yen note. If you say 「こわす」 there, it sounds like you want to tear the money up. So health, stomachs, moods and machines can all be broken with this verb, but money gets broken with 「くずす」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「壊|かい」 has a Chinese-origin reading you'll see in dramatic words. 「破壊|はかい」 means destruction, the kind a monster does to a city in a movie. 「崩壊|ほうかい」 means collapse, as in a building or even a society falling apart. These sound much heavier than the everyday verb. In daily life, stick with 「壊す|こわす」 and its partner 「壊れる|こわれる」. Also compare verbs for specific kinds of breaking. 「わる」 is to smash or crack, like a plate or an egg. 「おる」 is to snap something long, like a stick or a bone. 「やぶる」 is to tear, like paper. 「壊す|こわす」 is the general word for making something stop working or fall apart. Pick the verb that matches how it breaks." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a sound-alike memory trick. You already know 「こわい」, scary. Now picture a scary little monster, three inches tall, with tiny horns. It sneaks into your room at night and goes straight for your favorite things. It sits on your headphones, chews your phone charger, and kicks your camera off the desk. It's a 「こわい」 monster that 「こわす」 everything. Now give it a wall to knock down, because the left side of 「壊|こわ」 is the earth radical, ground and walls. The monster stomps on a little clay wall, and it crumbles into dust. The real words aren't related, it's only a trick. But next time something breaks, blame the scary little monster." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The earth radical suggests crumbling walls, and it's the transitive partner of 「壊れる|こわれる」, so someone breaks something with 「を」, as in 「おなかを壊す|おなかをこわす」.",
      "It stretches to ruining your health or the mood, but breaking a large bill into change uses 「くずす」, not this verb.",
      "The 「かい」 reading appears in heavy words like 「破壊|はかい」, destruction, while 「わる」, 「おる」, and 「やぶる」 cover specific kinds of breaking.",
      "Picture a tiny 「こわい」 monster that 「こわす」 everything, even stomping a clay wall to dust for the earth radical."
    ],
    ja: "おとうとがカメラを[壊しました|こわしました|こわす]。",
    en: "My little brother broke the camera." },
  "さく|咲く": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「咲|さ」 has 「口|くち」, mouth, on the left. Here's an interesting real fact. In Chinese, this character was originally a form of the kanji for laughing or smiling. Japanese borrowed it for blooming. So a flower opening its petals is a little like a mouth breaking into a smile. That's a lovely image to remember it by. The verb uses its native reading, 「さ」, and conjugates as a normal 「う」-verb: 「咲きます|さきます」, 「咲いて|さいて」. It's intransitive, so the flower is the subject with 「が」: 「花が咲く|はながさく」, flowers bloom. You don't need someone to make it bloom. To say flowers are in bloom right now, use 「咲いている|さいている」. This verb comes up constantly with 「桜|さくら」 in spring. People ask, have the cherry blossoms bloomed yet, and news reports track when the first blossoms open, and when they are in full bloom. You'll also hear it used for things like conversations flourishing, as in a conversation blooming, a poetic but common phrase. To recap: mouth radical, a flower smiling open, and the flower takes 「が」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Every spring, Japan tracks blooming like a weather report. The news shows the 「さくら前線|さくらぜんせん」, the cherry blossom front, a line that moves north across the map as the trees bloom, from Okinawa up to Hokkaido. When a special sample tree in a city opens enough flowers, the weather agency makes an official announcement that the blossoms have started. About a week later, they reach 「満開|まんかい」, full bloom. Then people rush out for 「花見|はなみ」, picnics under the trees with friends and coworkers. The blooming only lasts about a week, which makes it feel precious. So this simple verb carries a whole season of excitement. When people ask 「もう咲いた|もうさいた」, has it bloomed yet, the whole country is listening." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Blooming has a life cycle, and Japanese has a word for each stage. First come buds, 「つぼみ」. Then the flowers 「咲く|さく」, bloom. When they're at their peak, you say 「満開|まんかい」, full bloom. Finally the petals 「ちる」, scatter and fall, especially used for cherry blossoms drifting in the wind. That falling image is very famous in Japanese poetry, a picture of beauty that doesn't last. You might wonder about 「開く|ひらく」, to open, for flowers. It works, especially for one flower opening up, but 「咲く|さく」 is the standard verb for blooming. There's also a causative form, 「咲かせる|さかせる」, to make bloom, used in sayings like making a conversation flower. Buds, bloom, full bloom, scatter: a whole season in four words." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Listen to 「さくら」, cherry blossom. Its first two sounds are 「さく」, exactly like this verb. One popular explanation says the name comes from 「咲く|さく」 plus a little ending, so cherry blossom would mean something like the blooming one. But that's only one theory among several, and scholars don't agree, so use it as a memory trick rather than a fact. Still, it's a great trick. Every time you see 「さくら」, you can hear the verb inside it. And there's a real sound-alike to keep apart: 「さく」 written with a different kanji means to tear or to split, as in tearing paper. The bloom verb uses the mouth-on-the-left kanji. When you hear 「さくら」, think: the flower that 「さく」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has mouth 「口|くち」 on the left and was once a form of laughing in Chinese, so a flower blooms like a smile, with 「が」 as in 「花が咲く|はながさく」.",
      "Japan tracks blooming like weather, with the 「さくら前線|さくらぜんせん」 moving north, official announcements, then 「満開|まんかい」 and 「花見|はなみ」.",
      "Blooming has stages, buds 「つぼみ」, bloom 「咲く|さく」, full bloom 「満開|まんかい」, and scattering 「ちる」, a whole season in four words.",
      "You can hear 「さく」 inside 「さくら」, which one theory says means the blooming one, but treat that as a memory trick rather than fact."
    ],
    ja: "にわに{花|はな}が[咲いています|さいています|さく]。",
    en: "Flowers are blooming in the garden." },
  "たすかる|助かる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「助|たす」 means help. Its right side is 「力|ちから」, power or strength, so think of lending someone your strength. It uses its native reading here. This is the intransitive member of a pair. Its partner is 「助ける|たすける」, to help or save someone, which takes 「を」. 「助かる|たすかる」 is what happens to you: you are saved, you are helped, things get easier. Notice the pattern, same as 「閉まる|しまる」 and 「閉める|しめる」: 「かる」 for the happening verb, 「ける」 for the doing verb. In daily life, the phrase you'll hear constantly is 「助かりました|たすかりました」. It means that really helped me, and it's said as a warm thank you when someone does you a favor, lends you something, or just makes things easier. Among friends, 「助かる|たすかる」 or 「助かった|たすかった」 alone means oh, that's a big help, phew. It can also be serious, as in being rescued in an accident. Using it as thanks is a little more natural than a plain thank you, because it tells the person exactly how much they helped. To recap: power for help, and 「助かりました|たすかりました」 is a thank you for help received." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「助|じょ」 has a Chinese-origin reading that turns up in a word you use every day. 「助詞|じょし」 means particle, as in 「は」, 「が」, 「を」 and 「に」. It's literally helper words, the little words that help a sentence work. Grammar class suddenly feels friendlier. You'll also meet 「助手|じょしゅ」, an assistant, literally a helping hand, and 「手助け|てだすけ」, a helping hand, with a native reading and the 「た」 changing to 「だ」 in the middle. Then there's the famous cry for help: 「助けて|たすけて」, help me, from the transitive partner. So this kanji helps sentences, scientists, and people in trouble. Next time you study particles, remember they're the helpers, 「じょし」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb has a serious side and a light side. The serious side is about survival. 「いのちが助かった|いのちがたすかった」 means a life was saved, the kind of thing you hear in news about an accident. The light side is everyday relief. If the train you thought you'd missed is still at the platform, you might breathe out 「助かった|たすかった」, phew, saved. When a coworker covers your shift, you'd say 「本当に助かります|ほんとうにたすかります」, that really helps me. It's warmer than a plain thanks, because it tells the other person exactly how much their help mattered. Here's a mini dialogue. Your friend says 「かさ、かそうか」, want to borrow my umbrella? You say 「助かる|たすかる」, lifesaver. Saved lives, saved afternoons." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here's a useful combination from your lessons. Put 「〜てくれて」 before this verb, and you get a perfect thank-you that names the favor. 「てつだってくれて、助かった|てつだってくれて、たすかった」 means thanks for helping me, it was a big help. The first part says what they did for you, and the second part says how it affected you. This verb is a 「う」-verb ending in 「る」, so the negative is 「助からない|たすからない」, and the 「て」-form is 「助かって|たすかって」. Since it's about how something affects you, you rarely use it with 「を」. The thing that helped you comes before it as a reason, often with 「から」 or the 「て」-form. Remember the formula: what they did, 「てくれて」, then 「助かった|たすかった」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has power 「力|ちから」 on its right, and as the 「かる」 partner of 「助ける|たすける」, it's what happens to you, so 「助かりました|たすかりました」 is a warm thank you.",
      "The 「じょ」 reading gives 「助詞|じょし」, particles, literally helper words, and 「助手|じょしゅ」, an assistant.",
      "It can mean a life was saved, or everyday relief, like 「助かった|たすかった」, phew, saved, and it's warmer than a plain thanks.",
      "Name the favor with 「〜てくれて」 first, as in 「てつだってくれて、助かった|てつだってくれて、たすかった」, thanks for helping, it was a big help."
    ],
    ja: "てつだってくれて、[助かりました|たすかりました|たすかる]。",
    en: "Thanks for helping me, it was a big help." },
  "たのむ|頼む": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「頼|たの」 means to rely on or request. Its right side is a radical that represents a head, and it shows up in many kanji. The kanji also appears in 「信頼|しんらい」, trust, so the core idea is leaning on someone. This verb is for asking someone to do a favor for you, which is relying on them. The pattern is: person 「に」, thing 「を」, 「頼む|たのむ」. So you ask a friend for something with 「ともだちに頼む|ともだちにたのむ」. Here's a common mistake. English ask covers both asking a question and asking a favor. In Japanese, asking a question is 「聞く|きく」, but asking for a favor is 「頼む|たのむ」. The polite request phrase 「お願いします|おねがいします」 is closely related in meaning. Casually, 「頼む|たのむ」 or 「頼むよ|たのむよ」 alone means please, I'm counting on you. It's also used for ordering food or drinks at a restaurant, as in I ordered ramen, which is a casual alternative to 「注文する|ちゅうもんする」. A way to remember it: tano mu, I'll tell you the menu I want, a small hook. To recap: relying on someone, person 「に」 thing 「を」, for favors and food orders." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In a restaurant, you can order food with this verb. Friends at a table might say 「何を頼む|なにをたのむ」, what are you getting? It's more casual than 「注文する|ちゅうもんする」, which sounds a bit more formal. You'll also hear 「頼んでおいた|たのんでおいた」, I ordered it in advance, or I asked someone ahead of time. Another very common phrase is 「頼んだよ|たのんだよ」, said when handing a job to someone. It means I'm counting on you, and it shows trust as much as instruction. A boss might say it, or a teammate before a big game. And when someone keeps begging, they might put their hands together and say 「頼む、頼む|たのむ、たのむ」, please, please. Ordering, entrusting, begging: all one verb." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「頼|たの」 has a sister verb with a different native reading: 「頼る|たよる」, to rely on or depend on. You 「頼む|たのむ」 when you ask for one favor, but you 「頼る|たよる」 on someone over time, like relying on your parents. From the same kanji comes the adjective 「頼もしい|たのもしい」, reliable or dependable, the kind of person you'd want next to you in a crisis. And with a Chinese-origin reading, 「信頼|しんらい」 means trust. So the kanji covers a whole family: asking for help, leaning on someone, being dependable, and deep trust. Notice how it all comes back to putting your weight on another person. Request, rely, reliable, trust." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a sound-based memory trick. You know 「楽しむ|たのしむ」, to enjoy. Now take away the 「し」, and you get 「たのむ」. Picture a friend at a theme park who's busy enjoying a ride. You're holding three bags, a drink, and a balloon, and you yell, please hold this for me! Your friend has to stop enjoying to help you. You took the 「し」, the fun part, right out of their day. 「楽しむ|たのしむ」 becomes 「頼む|たのむ」. The two words aren't actually related in history, so this is only a hook. But it reminds you that asking a favor means borrowing someone's time. Fun minus one sound equals a favor." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji means relying on someone, as in 「信頼|しんらい」, and you ask a favor with person 「に」, thing 「を」, while asking a question is 「聞く|きく」.",
      "It's a casual way to order food, as in 「何を頼む|なにをたのむ」, and 「頼んだよ|たのんだよ」 means I'm counting on you.",
      "Its sister verb 「頼る|たよる」 means to depend on someone over time, and 「頼もしい|たのもしい」 means reliable.",
      "Take the 「し」, the fun, out of 「楽しむ|たのしむ」 and you get 「頼む|たのむ」, since asking a favor borrows someone's time."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}にりょうりを[頼みました|たのみました|たのむ]。",
    en: "I asked my mother to cook." },
  "つく|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「つける」, to turn something on, from lesson six. It is usually written in kana, because the kanji is not common in daily writing. Here's the pair. 「つける」 is transitive. Someone turns on the light, and the light takes 「を」: 「電気をつける|でんきをつける」. 「つく」 is intransitive. The light comes on by itself, and it takes 「が」: 「電気がつく|でんきがつく」. Notice the shape: 「つける」 ends in 「ける」, and 「つく」 ends in plain 「く」. A similar pairing: 「あける」 and 「あく」, open. Japanese often uses the intransitive version when the person who did it doesn't matter or is unknown. If lights in a building suddenly come on, you'd say 「電気がついた|でんきがついた」. To say it's on right now, use 「ついている」, which you'll hear in reminders like the light is still on. The same verb also means to arrive, as in arriving at a station, but that's written with a different kanji. Context makes it clear. Its opposite partner in this lesson is 「きえる」, something goes off, paired with 「けす」. To recap: 「をつける」 when you switch it on, 「がつく」 when it comes on." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "The sound 「つく」 is one of the busiest in Japanese, which is a good reason to write it in kana when you mean the light coming on. With different kanji, 「着く|つく」 means to arrive, as in arriving at the station. 「付く|つく」 means to stick or be attached, like a stain getting on your shirt. And 「つく」 for lights, as in this lesson, uses yet another kanji that's rarely written. There's even 「うそをつく」, to tell a lie, where it's a different verb again. You can almost always tell which one you heard from the particles and the subject. A train 「につく」 means arriving somewhere. A light 「がつく」 means it comes on. So listen for what's doing the 「つく」, and the meaning snaps into place." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Some very common phrases are built on this verb. 「気がつく|きがつく」 means to notice, literally the mind switches on. 「つけっぱなし」, from its partner 「つける」, means left on, like a light left on all night. And a very fun slang use: 「ついている」, often squeezed to 「ついてる」, means to be lucky. If you win a prize, you might say 「今日はついてる|きょうはついてる」, I'm on a roll today. The opposite, 「ついてない」, means you're having bad luck. When something is broken, you'll hear 「テレビがつかない」, the TV won't turn on, using the negative. So this verb covers lights, noticing, and luck. When things go well, remember: you're switched on." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the partner of 「つける」, so 「電気をつける|でんきをつける」 is you switching it on, while 「電気がつく|でんきがつく」 is the light coming on by itself.",
      "The sound 「つく」 is very busy, like 「着く|つく」, to arrive, and 「付く|つく」, to stick, so the particles and subject tell you which one you heard.",
      "It builds phrases like 「気がつく|きがつく」, to notice, and slang 「ついてる」, to be lucky, while 「テレビがつかない」 means the TV won't turn on."
    ],
    ja: "{電気|でんき}が[つきました|つきました|つく]。",
    en: "The light came on." },
  "よごす|汚す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「汚|よご」 means dirty. Its left side is the water radical, three drops, and dirty water is a good picture for it. You already know this kanji in 「汚い|きたない」, dirty, an adjective with a different native reading. Here it's read 「よご」. This is the transitive member of a pair. 「汚す|よごす」 means someone makes something dirty, and the thing takes 「を」. Its partner, also in this lesson, is 「汚れる|よごれる」, to become dirty, which takes 「が」. It's the same ending pattern as 「壊す|こわす」 and 「壊れる|こわれる」: 「す」 for doing it, 「れる」 for it happening. So you stain your shirt with 「を汚す|をよごす」, but the shirt got dirty with 「が汚れる|がよごれる」. In Japanese homes, people take off shoes at the entrance, partly so they don't dirty the floor. Children are often told 「服を汚さないで|ふくをよごさないで」, don't get your clothes dirty, a very common phrase for parents. Japanese often uses this verb with 「〜てしまう」 to show regret: I went and got it dirty. To recap: water radical for dirty, someone dirties something with 「を」, and 「汚れる|よごれる」 is its 「が」 partner." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「汚|よご」 has at least three readings you'll run into. In the adjective 「汚い|きたない」, dirty, it's read 「きたな」. In this verb and its partner it's 「よご」. And in serious words it's the Chinese-origin reading 「お」, as in 「汚染|おせん」, pollution, the kind of word you'll see in news about rivers or air quality. There's also the noun 「汚れ|よごれ」, a stain or dirt, which you see on laundry soap ads. Three readings, one kanji, and the water radical holding them together. Which reading do you use? Look at the kana after the kanji. 「い」 tells you 「きたない」, 「す」 or 「れる」 tells you 「よご」, and no kana usually points to 「お」. Let the trailing kana guide you." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Parents say this one a lot. 「服を汚さないでね|ふくをよごさないでね」, don't get your clothes dirty, is a classic warning before a child goes out to play. When it happens anyway, you'll hear 「汚しちゃった|よごしちゃった」, I got it dirty, a casual form of 「〜てしまった」. Restaurants that serve messy food, like curry udon or crab, often hand out paper bibs so you won't stain your shirt. There's also a figurative use: 「手を汚す|てをよごす」, to dirty your hands, meaning to do something shady or unpleasant yourself, just like in English. And in the kitchen, 「汚さないように|よごさないように」, so as not to make a mess, is a polite note on shared office kitchens. Clean clothes, clean hands, clean kitchen." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The water radical suggests dirty water, and it's the transitive partner of 「汚れる|よごれる」, so parents say 「服を汚さないで|ふくをよごさないで」.",
      "The kanji reads 「きたな」 in 「汚い|きたない」, 「よご」 in this verb, and 「お」 in 「汚染|おせん」, so let the trailing kana guide which reading you use.",
      "When it happens anyway you'll hear 「汚しちゃった|よごしちゃった」, and 「手を汚す|てをよごす」 means dirtying your hands with something shady, just like in English."
    ],
    ja: "シャツを[汚しました|よごしました|よごす]。",
    en: "I got my shirt dirty." },
  "おちる|落ちる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the intransitive partner of 「落とす|おとす」, to drop something, also in this lesson. Both use the kanji 「落|お」, to fall, with the grass radical on top and the water radical on the left. Think of leaves and drops falling. Take that picture as a hook. Here's the pair. 「落とす|おとす」 means someone drops something, with 「を」. 「落ちる|おちる」 means something falls by itself, with 「が」. So 「りんごが落ちる|りんごがおちる」 is an apple falls. It's a 「る」-verb: 「落ちます|おちます」, 「落ちて|おちて」. A very important extra meaning: to fail an exam or not get selected. The phrase is 「しけんに落ちる|しけんにおちる」, to fail the test, with 「に」 marking the exam. That's why Japanese students avoid saying this word around exam time, and lucky charms for exams are sometimes things that won't fall. You also know 「落ち込む|おちこむ」, to get depressed, your feelings falling into a hole. Other things that fall: 「さいふが落ちている|さいふがおちている」, there's a wallet lying on the ground. To recap: the 「が」 partner of 「落とす|おとす」, falling, and also failing an exam." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb hides inside several everyday words. The most useful is 「落ち着く|おちつく」, to calm down or settle. Picture something that was bouncing around finally dropping into place. Someone panicking might hear 「落ち着いて|おちついて」, calm down. Autumn gives you 「落ち葉|おちば」, fallen leaves, crunchy underfoot in the park. And thunder uses it too: 「かみなりが落ちる|かみなりがおちる」, lightning strikes, literally lightning falls. Families even say a parent's thunder fell when someone gets yelled at. Compare these with 「落とす|おとす」, the doing partner, where somebody drops something. In every word here, nobody is doing the dropping. Things simply settle, fall, or strike. Fall, settle, strike: all by themselves." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Besides apples and exams, lots of things can fall in everyday Japanese. 「せいせきが落ちる|せいせきがおちる」 means your grades drop. 「人気が落ちる|にんきがおちる」 means popularity goes down. 「よごれが落ちる|よごれがおちる」 means a stain comes out in the wash, which you'll hear in every detergent ad. And in offices, 「電源が落ちる|でんげんがおちる」 means the power goes off, or a computer shuts down. So the verb covers anything that drops in level or simply comes off. A mini dialogue: your friend looks at a shirt and asks 「このよごれ、落ちる|このよごれ、おちる」, will this stain come out? You say 「たぶん落ちるよ|たぶんおちるよ」, it'll probably come out. Grades, stains, power, popularity: all of them can fall." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the 「が」 partner of 「落とす|おとす」, so things fall by themselves, and 「しけんに落ちる|しけんにおちる」 also means to fail an exam.",
      "It hides in 「落ち着く|おちつく」, to calm down, like something settling into place, and in 「落ち葉|おちば」, fallen leaves, with nobody doing the dropping.",
      "Grades, popularity, and power can all fall, and 「よごれが落ちる|よごれがおちる」 means a stain comes out, which you'll hear in detergent ads."
    ],
    ja: "{木|き}からりんごが[落ちました|おちました|おちる]。",
    en: "An apple fell from the tree." },
  "かたづける|片付ける": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings. The first, 「片|かた」, means one side, a piece, or one of a pair. The second, 「付|つ」, means to attach or put something on. You know it from 「付き合う|つきあう」, to date or keep someone company, which is two people sticking together. In this word, 「つける」 changes to 「づける」. That's the usual sound change when a second part joins a compound. That's why it's spelled with 「づ」, not 「ず」, a detail that trips up learners. So the word is roughly putting the pieces in their place. When you tidy up, you take all the scattered pieces and put each one back where it belongs. It's a 「る」-verb that takes 「を」: 「部屋を片付ける|へやをかたづける」, to tidy up the room. It also works for putting away dishes, clearing the table, or finishing off work. A cultural note: Japan has a strong tidying culture. Marie Kondo, whose tidying method became famous worldwide, is Japanese, and schools have students clean their own classrooms every day. Parents often say 「片付けなさい|かたづけなさい」, tidy up, to children. To recap: piece plus attach, put each piece back in its place, spelled with 「づ」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Tidying became a global topic thanks to a Japanese organizing expert, Marie Kondo. Her bestselling book's Japanese title includes 「片づけ|かたづけ」, tidying, and the phrase she made famous comes from 「ときめく」, to make your heart flutter, usually translated as spark joy. Her method asks you to hold each item and keep it only if it sparks joy. In Japan, tidying also has a seasonal ritual: 「大そうじ|おおそうじ」, the big end-of-year cleaning, done before the New Year so you start fresh. Schools have students clean their own classrooms every day, too, so tidying is a habit taught early. So this verb isn't just a chore. For many people it's a small way of resetting life. Tidy room, fresh start." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Look again at the first kanji, 「片|かた」, one side or one of a pair. It shows up in useful words. 「片道|かたみち」 is a one-way trip, literally one side of the road, the opposite of a round trip. 「片手|かたて」 is one hand, as in carrying something with one hand. And the verb itself has an intransitive partner: 「片付く|かたづく」, to get tidied, or to get settled. 「部屋が片付いた|へやがかたづいた」 means the room is all tidy now. Notice the pair follows the 「つく」 and 「つける」 pattern you know from lights. The verb also stretches beyond rooms. 「仕事を片付ける|しごとをかたづける」 means to finish off your work, clearing it away. One side, one way, one hand, and everything in its place." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll hear this verb most from parents. 「早く片付けなさい|はやくかたづけなさい」, tidy up right now, is a classic. The 「なさい」 ending makes it a firm instruction from an adult to a child. In restaurants, staff ask 「お皿をお片付けしてもよろしいですか|おさらをおかたづけしてもよろしいですか」, may I clear your plate? That's the same verb in very polite form. At a party, friends might say 「みんなで片付けよう|みんなでかたづけよう」, let's all clean up together. A useful tip: after a picnic or a festival in Japan, people take their trash home and leave the space spotless, so clearing up is part of the event. From toy boxes to dinner tables, this verb is about putting the world back in order." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's piece 「片|かた」 plus attach 「付|つ」, with 「つける」 voiced into 「づける」, so tidying means putting each piece back in its place, spelled with 「づ」.",
      "Tidying is a big part of Japanese life, from Marie Kondo's spark joy method to 「大そうじ|おおそうじ」, the big end-of-year cleaning.",
      "The kanji 「片|かた」 gives 「片道|かたみち」, one way, and the verb has an intransitive partner, 「片付く|かたづく」, following the 「つく」 and 「つける」 pattern.",
      "Parents say 「早く片付けなさい|はやくかたづけなさい」, restaurant staff ask to clear your plate with it, and after picnics people take their trash home."
    ],
    ja: "{部屋|へや}を[片付けて|かたづけて|かたづける]ください。",
    en: "Please tidy up your room." },
  "かんがえる|考える": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「考|かんが」 uses a native reading. Its top part also appears in 「老|ろう」, the kanji for old, as in an elderly person. A way to remember it: picture a wise old person deep in thought. That's a memory hook, not a proven origin story. The meaning is to think something through, to consider, to give it real thought. Here's the most useful point for English speakers. You already know 「思う|おもう」, also to think. They're different. 「思う|おもう」 is for opinions, feelings, and impressions, I think it'll rain, I think it's good. 「考える|かんがえる」 is for actively thinking: working out a problem, considering a plan, thinking about your future. You 「考える|かんがえる」 about what to do. The pattern is 「〜について考える|〜についてかんがえる」, to think about something, or 「〜を考える|〜をかんがえる」. A very common polite phrase is 「考えておきます|かんがえておきます」, I'll think about it, which is often a gentle way to say no. It's a 「る」-verb: 「考えます|かんがえます」, 「考えて|かんがえて」. To recap: an old wise thinker as a hook, actively thinking things through, unlike 「思う|おもう」 for opinions." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here's a phrase every business traveler should know: 「考えておきます|かんがえておきます」, I'll think about it. On the surface it's a promise to consider your idea. But very often it's a gentle way of saying no without hurting anyone's feelings. Tone and context tell you which it is. You'll also use the noun form, 「考え|かんがえ」, a thought or an idea. 「いい考えですね|いいかんがえですね」 means that's a good idea. And 「考えすぎ|かんがえすぎ」 means overthinking, something a friend might tell you when you worry too much. A mini dialogue: your friend asks, shall we go to Kyoto? You say 「ちょっと考えさせて|ちょっとかんがえさせて」, let me think about it a bit. Listen for whether it means maybe, or politely no." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「る」-verb, so the 「て」-form is simply 「考えて|かんがえて」, and the negative is 「考えない|かんがえない」. It combines well with patterns from your lessons. 「考えてみる|かんがえてみる」 means to try thinking about it, to give it a go. 「考えている|かんがえている」 means you're thinking about it right now, or you're considering it seriously, like changing jobs. The volitional 「考えよう|かんがえよう」 means let's think about it, a nice way to start a group discussion. And with 「〜たほうがいい」, you get 「もう少し考えたほうがいい|もうすこしかんがえたほうがいい」, you'd better think it over a bit more. To name what you think about, use 「を」 or 「について」. A regular 「る」-verb, ready for every pattern." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「考|こう」 has a Chinese-origin reading that shows up in study words. 「参考|さんこう」 means reference, something you consult while thinking. You'll see 「参考書|さんこうしょ」, a study guide or reference book, in every Japanese bookstore. 「思考|しこう」 is a formal word for thought or thinking, as in critical thinking. Notice that it pairs with 「思|し」 from 「思う|おもう」, the other thinking verb, so the two kanji you've been comparing actually team up in one word. In daily speech, though, you'll use the verb and its noun 「考え|かんがえ」 far more. Remember the Chinese reading as 「こう」, and the native reading as 「かんが」. Two kinds of thinking, sometimes working together." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Unlike 「思う|おもう」 for opinions and feelings, it means actively thinking something through, and 「考えておきます|かんがえておきます」 is often a gentle no.",
      "「考えておきます|かんがえておきます」 can be a polite no depending on tone, and the noun 「考え|かんがえ」 gives 「いい考えですね|いいかんがえですね」, that's a good idea.",
      "As a regular 「る」-verb it fits every pattern, like 「考えてみる|かんがえてみる」, to try thinking about it, and 「考えよう|かんがえよう」, let's think about it.",
      "Its 「こう」 reading appears in 「参考書|さんこうしょ」, a study guide, and in 「思考|しこう」, thought, where it teams up with the kanji from 「思う|おもう」."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}のことを[考えています|かんがえています|かんがえる]。",
    en: "I'm thinking about tomorrow." },
  "きえる|消える": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「消す|けす」, to turn off or erase, from lesson six. They share the kanji 「消|け」, which means to put out, to vanish. Its left side is the water radical. A way to remember it: water puts out a fire. That's a hook rather than the true history, but it fits nicely. You also know this kanji with its Chinese-origin reading 「しょう」 in 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, firefighter, someone who puts out fires. Here's the pair. 「消す|けす」 is transitive: someone turns off the light, and it takes 「を」. 「消える|きえる」 is intransitive: the light goes off by itself, and it takes 「が」. So 「電気が消える|でんきがきえる」 is the light goes out. Notice the readings change between them, 「け」 and 「き」, even with the same kanji. Its on-partner is 「つく」, to come on. But 「消える|きえる」 is broader than lights going off. It means anything disappearing: a candle going out, a fire dying down, a person vanishing from sight, or a word erased from a board. In a power cut, everyone says 「電気が消えた|でんきがきえた」. To recap: water puts out fire, the 「が」 partner of 「消す|けす」, for going off and disappearing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb is about vanishing in all kinds of ways. Lights go out, but so does fire: 「ろうそくの火が消えた|ろうそくのひがきえた」, the candle went out. Files disappear too, which is a modern nightmare: 「データが消えた|データがきえた」, my data's gone. People can vanish from sight, like a cat that slips away. Sounds can fade, and so can memories. A rough command form, 「消えろ|きえろ」, means get lost, and it's very rude, so it's one to recognize, not to use. Here's a little scene. You finish a long essay on your computer. The screen flashes, and you scream 「消えた|きえた」! That's one word you'll feel deeply. Lights, flames, files and people can all 「消える|きえる」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "You use the doing partner, 「消す|けす」, inside a word you probably own: 「消しゴム|けしゴム」, an eraser, literally an erasing rubber, half Japanese verb and half Dutch-origin loanword. Then there's the Chinese-origin reading, 「しょう」. Every time you buy something in Japan, you pay 「消費税|しょうひぜい」, consumption tax, from 「消費|しょうひ」, consumption, using things up until they vanish. And firefighters, 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, prevent fires. So the kanji stretches from candles to cash registers. The core idea is always making something disappear. 「け」 and 「き」 in the native verbs, 「しょう」 in the Chinese words. Erasers, taxes, firefighters: all about things vanishing." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Three verbs start with 「き」 and are easy to blur together. 「消える|きえる」 is to go out or disappear. 「聞こえる|きこえる」 is to be audible, to be heard, as in I can hear music. And 「着る|きる」 is to wear, or with another kanji, 「切る|きる」, to cut. Notice that 「きえる」 and 「きこえる」 differ by just the 「こ」 in the middle. A memory trick: 「きこえる」 has an extra sound because you can hear it, and 「きえる」 has lost one, because something disappeared. That's only a hook, but it helps. So 「おとがきこえる」 is a sound you can hear, and 「おとがきえる」 is a sound fading away. One missing syllable, and the sound vanishes." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the 「が」 partner of 「消す|けす」, with the reading shifting from 「け」 to 「き」, and it covers anything disappearing, not just lights going out.",
      "Candle flames, files, and people can all vanish, as in 「データが消えた|データがきえた」, but the rough command 「消えろ|きえろ」 is very rude.",
      "The doing partner hides in 「消しゴム|けしゴム」, an eraser, and the 「しょう」 reading gives 「消費税|しょうひぜい」 and 「消防士|しょうぼうし」, all about making things vanish.",
      "Don't blur it with 「聞こえる|きこえる」, which has an extra 「こ」 because you can hear it, while 「きえる」 has lost one because something disappeared."
    ],
    ja: "{電気|でんき}が[消えました|きえました|きえる]。",
    en: "The light went off." },
  "こわれる|壊れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the intransitive partner of 「壊す|こわす」, to break something, also in this lesson. Both share the kanji 「壊|こわ」, with the earth radical on the left, picture a wall crumbling. Here's the pair. 「壊す|こわす」 means someone breaks it, with 「を」. 「壊れる|こわれる」 means it breaks, with 「が」, without saying who did it. Notice the endings: 「す」 for doing, 「れる」 for happening. 「汚す|よごす」 and 「汚れる|よごれる」 follow the exact same pattern. This verb is the natural way to say something is broken or stopped working. To describe a machine that's broken right now, use 「壊れている|こわれている」. For example, my computer is broken, or this elevator is broken. Japanese often prefers 「壊れる|こわれる」 even when you did break it, because it sounds less like blaming someone. It just broke. That's a real difference in how English and Japanese describe accidents. If you want to own it, though, use 「壊す|こわす」 with 「てしまう」 for regret. To recap: the 「が」 partner of 「壊す|こわす」, for things breaking down or being broken." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has several verbs for things breaking, and choosing the right one sounds very natural. 「壊れる|こわれる」 is the general word, best for machines and objects that stop working, like a phone, a TV, or a chair. 「われる」 is for things that crack or shatter, like a glass, a plate, or an egg. 「おれる」 is for long things that snap, like a pencil, a branch, or a bone. And 「やぶれる」 is for things that tear, like paper or a bag. So a dropped cup 「われる」, a snapped pencil 「おれる」, and a broken laptop 「こわれる」. All of these are intransitive, the happening kind. Pick the verb by how the thing breaks, and you'll sound like a native." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a 「る」-verb: 「壊れます|こわれます」, 「壊れて|こわれて」, 「壊れない|こわれない」. In the past plain form, 「壊れた|こわれた」 can sit in front of a noun like an adjective. 「壊れたかさ|こわれたかさ」 is a broken umbrella, and 「壊れたいす|こわれたいす」 is a broken chair. Add 「やすい」 to the stem and you get 「壊れやすい|こわれやすい」, fragile, easy to break, which you'll see on delivery boxes. To show it happened unexpectedly, use 「〜てしまう」: 「パソコンが壊れてしまった|パソコンがこわれてしまった」, my computer went and broke. And the state right now is 「壊れている|こわれている」. A short summary: 「た」 for broken things, 「やすい」 for fragile ones." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the 「が」 partner of 「壊す|こわす」, so things just break, and Japanese often prefers it even when you broke something, because it sounds less like blame.",
      "It's the general word for machines and objects that stop working, while 「われる」 is for shattering, 「おれる」 for snapping, and 「やぶれる」 for tearing.",
      "The past form works like an adjective, as in 「壊れたかさ|こわれたかさ」, a broken umbrella, and 「壊れやすい|こわれやすい」 means fragile."
    ],
    ja: "パソコンが[壊れました|こわれました|こわれる]。",
    en: "My computer broke." },
  "よごれる|汚れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the intransitive partner of 「汚す|よごす」, to make dirty, also in this lesson. Both use the kanji 「汚|よご」, which has the water radical, think of muddy water. You also know it in 「汚い|きたない」, dirty, with a different native reading. Here's the pair. 「汚す|よごす」 means someone gets something dirty, with 「を」. 「汚れる|よごれる」 means something becomes dirty, with 「が」. It follows the same pattern as 「壊す|こわす」 and 「壊れる|こわれる」. When you just want to describe a state, use 「汚れている|よごれている」, it is dirty. For example, a dirty shirt or a dirty window. How is it different from 「汚い|きたない」? 「汚い|きたない」 is an adjective that just describes dirtiness, and can sound strong, even gross. 「汚れている|よごれている」 focuses on something that became soiled, like a stain or dirt on it. It's a softer, factual way to describe it. After playing outside or gardening, your hands become dirty, so you say 「手が汚れた|てがよごれた」 and go wash them. To recap: the 「が」 partner of 「汚す|よごす」, for something becoming dirty." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The noun form, 「汚れ|よごれ」, a stain or grime, is everywhere in Japanese ads. Detergent commercials promise that 「汚れが落ちる|よごれがおちる」, the stains come right out. Rain on a white shirt, curry on a tie, mud on sneakers: these are classic 「汚れ|よごれ」. When something is stained, Japanese often describes the state with 「汚れている|よごれている」. And to warn someone, you might say 「手が汚れるよ|てがよごれるよ」, you'll get your hands dirty, when they reach into a messy box. There's also a figurative use: a polluted river, or even a corrupted heart, can be described with this verb. So it covers stains, grime, pollution, and moral dirt. A short dialogue: Mom asks why your jeans are muddy. You say 「サッカーで汚れた|サッカーでよごれた」, they got dirty playing soccer." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japanese daily life has many habits for keeping dirt out. At home, you take off your shoes at the 「げんかん」, the entrance area, so the floors won't get dirty. Many homes have separate slippers just for the toilet room. Schools have indoor shoes called 「うわばき」, and students change into them every morning. Then the students clean their own classrooms, so they learn to notice when things get dirty. Even construction workers often rinse truck tires before leaving a site so the roads won't be dirtied. Behind all this is a strong value of cleanliness. When you visit Japan, follow the shoe rules, and nothing will get dirty because of you. Shoes off, slippers on, floors clean." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the 「が」 partner of 「汚す|よごす」, and 「汚れている|よごれている」 is a softer, factual way to say something is soiled than the strong 「汚い|きたない」.",
      "The noun 「汚れ|よごれ」 fills detergent ads promising 「汚れが落ちる|よごれがおちる」, and the verb stretches to polluted rivers and even corrupted hearts.",
      "Japan keeps dirt out with habits like removing shoes at the 「げんかん」 and wearing 「うわばき」 at school, all so floors won't get dirty."
    ],
    ja: "{手|て}が[汚れました|よごれました|よごれる]。",
    en: "My hands got dirty." },
  "ちゅうもんする|注文する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「注|ちゅう」, means to pour or to focus attention. It has the water radical, as in pouring liquid. You know it from 「注意する|ちゅういする」, to watch out, which is pouring your attention onto something. The second, 「文|もん」, means writing or text. Usually it's read 「ぶん」, as in 「文化|ぶんか」, culture, and 「文法|ぶんぽう」, grammar. In this word it uses another reading, 「もん」. So a way to picture the word is focused writing, a written request full of specific details. Take that as a hook. That's what an order is. Add 「する」 and it becomes a verb: to place an order. It takes 「を」 for what you order. Use it in restaurants, ordering items online, or ordering from a shop. A waiter may ask 「ご注文は|ごちゅうもんは」, what's your order, or may you take your order. Compare it with 「頼む|たのむ」, which is also used for ordering food, but is more casual. 「注文する|ちゅうもんする」 is the standard, slightly more formal word, and the only natural choice for online orders. To recap: focused attention plus writing, a detailed request, the word for placing an order." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "At a Japanese restaurant, the ordering script is very predictable. When you're ready, you call out 「すみません」 to get the staff's attention, or press the little call button many tables have. The server comes over and asks 「ご注文はお決まりですか|ごちゅうもんはおきまりですか」, have you decided on your order? After you order, they often check 「以上でよろしいですか|いじょうでよろしいですか」, will that be all? Notice the polite 「ご」 in front, because it's the customer's order. In many ramen and noodle shops, though, you don't talk at all. You buy a ticket from a machine at the door, a 「食券|しょっけん」, and hand it over. So be ready for both: speaking your order, or pressing a button for it." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "One of Japan's most famous children's stories plays with this word. It's 「注文の多い料理店|ちゅうもんのおおいりょうりてん」, The Restaurant of Many Orders, by the writer 「宮沢賢治|みやざわけんじ」. Two hunters, lost in the mountains, find a fancy restaurant. As they walk through, door after door has a sign giving them an order: brush your hair, take off your guns, rub cream on your body, sprinkle on salt. They think the restaurant is very particular. Slowly they realize the orders aren't for the customers' comfort. They're preparing the hunters to be eaten. The twist works because 「注文|ちゅうもん」 means both an order you place and a demand. A spooky lesson in reading signs carefully." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The pouring kanji, 「注|ちゅう」, shows up in a word you'd rather not experience: 「注射|ちゅうしゃ」, an injection, literally pouring in with a shot. You know it from 「注意|ちゅうい」, caution. And in books, 「注|ちゅう」 also marks notes and footnotes, where extra information is poured into the margin. Now look at 「文|もん」. You know its usual reading, 「ぶん」. The reading 「もん」 is rarer, but it shows up in a few words, like 「文句|もんく」, a complaint. Interesting connection: in a restaurant you place a 「注文|ちゅうもん」, and if the food is bad, you might make a 「文句|もんく」. Both use 「もん」. So remember this pair as the two things you say to a waiter: an order, and hopefully not a complaint." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's pour or focus 「注|ちゅう」 plus writing 「文|もん」, a detailed request, and it's the standard word for ordering, more formal than 「頼む|たのむ」 and the only natural one online.",
      "Servers ask 「ご注文はお決まりですか|ごちゅうもんはおきまりですか」 with a polite 「ご」, though in many ramen shops you just buy a 「食券|しょっけん」 from a machine.",
      "In the famous story 「注文の多い料理店|ちゅうもんのおおいりょうりてん」, the restaurant's orders turn out to be preparing the hunters to be eaten.",
      "The 「もん」 reading also appears in 「文句|もんく」, a complaint, so it's the pair of things you say to a waiter, an order and hopefully not a complaint."
    ],
    ja: "コーヒーを[注文しました|ちゅうもんしました|ちゅうもんする]。",
    en: "I ordered a coffee." },
  "いますぐ|今すぐ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is just two words you already know, put together. 「今|いま」 is now. The kanji uses its native reading here. You've seen it with other readings in 「今日|きょう」, today, 「今晩|こんばん」, tonight, and 「今週|こんしゅう」, this week. 「すぐ」, from lesson six, means right away or soon. So the phrase is now, right away, and it means immediately, this very moment. It's stronger than 「すぐ」 alone. 「すぐ」 can mean soon or in a minute, but 「今すぐ|いますぐ」 means right now, not later. Use it when something is urgent: come right now, or do it right away. It often goes with a request, as in please come right away, or with a command. Be careful though, because it can sound pushy or urgent if you use it for small things, so it fits emergencies or excitement. You'll also hear it in ads, like buy now or call right now. Compare it with 「もうすぐ」, very soon, which looks similar but means something will happen soon, not that you should act now. To recap: now plus right away, the urgent version of 「すぐ」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You'll see this phrase constantly online. Shopping sites put it on buttons, like 「今すぐ購入|いますぐこうにゅう」, buy now. Ads shout 「今すぐ登録|いますぐとうろく」, register now. It creates a sense of urgency, exactly like English marketing. In speech, it's for real urgency. A parent calls a child, 「今すぐ来なさい|いますぐきなさい」, come here this instant. A friend in trouble texts 「今すぐ来て|いますぐきて」, come right now. You can soften it with 「できれば」, if possible, so 「できれば今すぐ|できればいますぐ」 means right away if you can. Also notice how it differs from 「今から|いまから」, from now. 「今から|いまから」 is starting now, but 「今すぐ|いますぐ」 means instantly. Use it when there's no time to lose." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Two words that start the same way trip up beginners: 「すぐ」, right away, and 「すごく」, very or incredibly. They differ by just one sound, a 「ご」 in the middle. 「すぐ来て|すぐきて」 means come right away, while 「すごく来て|すごくきて」 is just strange. Another trap is 「すぎる」, too much, which also starts with 「す」. So keep 「すぐ」 short and sharp, two quick syllables, just like its meaning. Here's a memory trick. Picture a speed racer whose helmet says 「すぐ」. He's so fast that he doesn't have time for an extra syllable. Put 「今|いま」 in front, and he's off the starting line before you finish speaking. Short word, short wait." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「今|いま」 plus 「すぐ」, the urgent version meaning right now, not later, so save it for emergencies, and don't confuse it with 「もうすぐ」, very soon.",
      "Shopping sites use it for urgency, like 「今すぐ購入|いますぐこうにゅう」, buy now, and unlike 「今から|いまから」, starting now, it means instantly.",
      "Don't mix up 「すぐ」 with 「すごく」, very, which has an extra 「ご」, so keep 「すぐ」 short and sharp like a speed racer."
    ],
    ja: "[今すぐ|いますぐ]きてください。",
    en: "Please come right away." },
  "〜までに|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is built from two pieces. 「まで」 means until. 「に」 marks a point in time, like at three o'clock. Together, 「までに」 means by a certain time, a deadline. The difference between 「まで」 and 「までに」 is the key lesson here, and it's a classic mistake for English speakers. 「まで」 alone means something continues all the way until that time. I'll study until five means you keep studying the whole time. 「までに」 means something happens once, at some point before that time. I'll be home by five means you arrive before five, any time before. So: 「五時まで勉強する|ごじまでべんきょうする」, study until five. And 「五時までにかえる|ごじまでにかえる」, go home by five. A way to remember it: the extra 「に」 is a little pin marking the deadline. That's a hook. You already know a related phrase, 「今日中に|きょうじゅうに」, by the end of today, which also uses 「に」 for a deadline. Use 「までに」 for homework due dates, appointments, and anything with a deadline. To recap: 「まで」 is until, continuing, and 「までに」 is by, a deadline." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "You can put a verb in front of this pattern, not just a time. Use the dictionary form. 「かえるまでに」 means by the time I get home, or before I get home. 「日本に行くまでに|にほんにいくまでに」 means before I go to Japan, as a deadline: I want to learn kanji by then. Compare the plain version: 「かえるまで」 means until I get home, the whole time. So with 「まで」 the action fills the whole stretch, and with 「までに」 it's done somewhere inside it. You'll also hear 「までには」, with an added 「は」, which softens or stresses it: by then, at least. 「五時までには|ごじまでには」 means by five at the latest. Times and verbs both work. Deadlines, met." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This is the language of school and office deadlines. A teacher says 「金曜日までにしゅくだいを出してください|きんようびまでにしゅくだいをだしてください」, please hand in your homework by Friday. A boss asks 「いつまでにできますか」, by when can you have it done? And the word for a deadline itself is 「締め切り|しめきり」, which you'll hear a lot near the end of the month. Here's a tiny dialogue. Your coworker asks 「このレポート、いつまで」, when is this report due? You answer 「あしたまでにおねがいします」, by tomorrow, please. One mistake to avoid: don't say 「まで」 with a one-time action like submit or arrive. Those need 「に」 for the deadline. Hand it in, on time." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here's a memory trick. Picture 「まで」 as a long red carpet rolled out from now until five o'clock. With plain 「まで」, you walk along the whole carpet, studying every step of the way, until the very end. Now picture 「までに」. Somebody has stuck a giant pushpin into the end of the carpet, labelled 「に」. You don't have to walk the whole carpet. You just have to reach your destination, any time before the pin. Arrive at three, arrive at four, it doesn't matter, as long as you're there before the pin. So: no pin, walk the whole way. Pin, just beat the deadline. A carpet and a pin." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Plain 「まで」 means something continues until a time, while 「までに」 means it happens once before a deadline, as in 「五時までにかえる|ごじまでにかえる」, go home by five.",
      "A dictionary form verb can go in front, as in 「日本に行くまでに|にほんにいくまでに」, before I go to Japan, and 「までには」 means at the latest.",
      "It's the language of deadlines, like 「いつまでにできますか」, by when can you do it, and one-time actions like submitting need the 「に」.",
      "Picture 「まで」 as a red carpet you walk the whole way, and 「までに」 as a pushpin labelled 「に」 at the end that you just need to reach before."
    ],
    ja: "{五時|ごじ}[までに]かえります。",
    en: "I'll be home by five." },
  "ほんとうに|本当に": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, then 「に」. The first, 「本|ほん」, you know as book, and in 「日本|にほん」, Japan. Its shape is a tree with a mark near the bottom, the root. So its deeper meaning is origin, basis, or the real thing. The second, 「当|とう」, means to hit the mark, to be right or correct. Both use Chinese-origin readings. Put them together and 「本当|ほんとう」 means the truth, the real thing. Add 「に」 and it becomes an adverb, really or truly. You know the family: 「本当ですか|ほんとうですか」, really? and 「本当は|ほんとうは」, in fact, the truth is. Use 「本当に|ほんとうに」 to make your feelings stronger, as in thank you very much, I'm really sorry, or it was truly delicious. It's warmer and more heartfelt than 「とても」, which just means very. In casual speech, people shorten it to 「ほんとに」, and 「ほんと？」 alone means really? A way to remember it: the root of the matter hits the mark, so it's really true. To recap: root plus hitting the mark makes truth, and 「に」 turns it into really." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In casual speech, this word often shrinks to 「ほんとに」, dropping the long vowel. You'll hear it constantly between friends. On its own with rising intonation, 「ほんと」 means really? seriously? And young people often use the slang word 「マジで」 in the same spot, as in seriously? Here's a mini dialogue. Your friend says 「あした、休みだって|あした、やすみだって」, I heard tomorrow's a day off. You say 「ほんと」? They nod: 「ほんとだよ」, it's true. Another classic reaction is 「うそ」, literally a lie. When a friend shares surprising news, saying 「うそ」 doesn't mean you think they're lying. It means no way! So: 「ほんと」 and 「うそ」 are both ways to say really? Use whichever matches your excitement." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The second kanji, 「当|とう」, has a native reading you'll meet in a very useful verb: 「当たる|あたる」, to hit, to be correct, or to win. If you win the lottery, your ticket 「当たる|あたる」. So 「本当|ほんとう」 is roughly the root that hits the mark, the true thing. Compare it with 「本物|ほんもの」, the real thing, as opposed to a fake. A genuine designer bag is 「本物|ほんもの」, while 「本当|ほんとう」 is about truth in what's said. And 「本|ほん」 also turns up in 「本気|ほんき」, serious, real intentions, as in I'm serious about this. So 「本|ほん」 keeps pointing at what's real underneath. Truth, the real thing, real intentions: all rooted in the same kanji." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Watch the long vowel in the middle. It's 「ほんとう」, with a stretched 「とう」, so it has four beats: 「ほ」, 「ん」, 「と」, 「う」. In kana it's written with 「う」, but you pronounce it as a long o sound, the same as in 「先生|せんせい」 written with 「い」 but heard as a long e. Learners sometimes write 「ほんと」 in a test, which is fine in casual texting but wrong in standard spelling. Also notice the 「ん」 counts as a full beat, so don't rush past it. Saying it with a nice even rhythm, four steady beats, then 「に」, makes you sound more natural. Count it on your fingers once: 「ほ」, 「ん」, 「と」, 「う」, 「に」. Five beats of sincerity." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's root or origin 「本|ほん」 plus hit the mark 「当|とう」, making the truth, and 「に」 turns it into really, warmer and more heartfelt than 「とても」.",
      "Casually it shrinks to 「ほんとに」, and 「ほんと」 with a rise means really, while 「うそ」 with surprising news means no way, not that they're lying.",
      "The second kanji gives 「当たる|あたる」, to hit or win, while 「本物|ほんもの」 is the real thing versus a fake and 「本気|ほんき」 means real intentions.",
      "Hold the long vowel, since 「ほんとう」 has four even beats with the 「ん」 counting fully, and 「ほんと」 is fine in texting but not standard spelling."
    ],
    ja: "[本当に|ほんとうに]ありがとうございました。",
    en: "Thank you very much, really." },
  "まず|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is usually written in kana. It means first of all, to start with, before anything else. Use it to begin a list of steps or to say what should be done first. A common sequence is 「まず」, then 「それから」, then 「つぎに」, and finally 「さいごに」. That's very useful for explaining instructions or recipes. For example, first wash your hands, then cut the vegetables. It can also mean first and foremost, as in the most important thing to do first. Let's eat first and talk later. Here's a common mix-up. 「はじめて」 means for the first time, as in a first experience, like I went to Japan for the first time. 「まず」 is about the order of steps, not experiences. And 「はじめに」, at the beginning, is close to 「まず」 but slightly more formal. 「まず」 comes at the start of the sentence, often followed by a pause, so it's easy to use. A way to remember it: picture a chef saying, must do this first, ma-zu. That's just a sound hook. To recap: 「まず」 starts a sequence of steps, followed by 「それから」 or 「つぎに」." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Be careful, because 「まず」 is just one sound away from 「まずい」, which means bad-tasting, or bad as in uh-oh, this is a bad situation. The only difference you see in writing is that extra 「い」 at the end of 「まずい」. So 「まず、食べましょう|まず、たべましょう」, first, let's eat, is cheerful. 「まずい」 after tasting the food is an insult. Here's a memory trick: picture a chef proudly saying 「まず」, first, the soup! You take a sip and gasp 「まずい」! The extra 「い」 is the sound of disgust. In a restaurant in Japan, avoid 「まずい」, and if you want to praise food, say 「おいしい」. First comes 「まず」, and hopefully never 「まずい」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word often comes with 「は」 attached: 「まずは」, first of all, to start. It's common in speeches and meetings. A host might start a party with 「まずは、かんぱい」, first, let's toast. At an izakaya, a famous habit is to order beer before even looking at the menu. People often say 「とりあえずビール」 for that, where 「とりあえず」 means for now, to start with. 「まず」 is a little more about the proper order, while 「とりあえず」 is more like for the moment, let's just do this. 「まず」 also has another meaning: almost certainly. 「まず大丈夫|まずだいじょうぶ」 means it's almost certainly fine. Context tells you which meaning is meant. First, a toast." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It starts a sequence of steps, followed by 「それから」 or 「つぎに」, and unlike 「はじめて」, for the first time, it's about order, not experiences.",
      "It's one sound away from 「まずい」, bad-tasting, so first, let's eat is cheerful while 「まずい」 after tasting is an insult.",
      "It often becomes 「まずは」, as in 「まずは、かんぱい」, and it can also mean almost certainly, as in 「まず大丈夫|まずだいじょうぶ」."
    ],
    ja: "[まず]{手|て}をあらってください。",
    en: "First of all, please wash your hands." },
  "おかげで|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from 「お陰|おかげ」, an old word based on 「陰|かげ」, shade or shadow. The idea is resting in the shade of someone's protection, receiving their blessing or help. Add 「で」, meaning because of, and you get thanks to. The pattern is noun 「のおかげで」, as in thanks to my teacher. With a verb, use the past short form: 「てつだってくれたおかげで」, thanks to your help. It's used for good results. If the result is bad, Japanese uses a different word, 「せいで」, because of, with a blaming feeling. That's an important difference. You also know a famous phrase from this word: 「おかげさまで」. When someone asks how you are, Japanese people often answer 「おかげさまで、元気です|おかげさまで、げんきです」, thanks to you, I'm fine. It's polite and humble, even if the person didn't actually do anything. It's a way of saying everyone's support helped. A way to remember it: picture standing in the cool shadow of someone kind, they're the reason you're okay. To recap: from shade or protection, it means thanks to, for good results, and gives the polite 「おかげさまで」." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji in 「お陰|おかげ」 is 「陰|かげ」, shade. Its left side is a narrow radical that looks a bit like a tall ear. When it's on the left side of a kanji, it signals hills or slopes. Shade behind a hill, out of the sun, makes a nice picture. You'll meet 「陰|かげ」 in 「日陰|ひかげ」, the shady spot, where you'd want to sit on a hot day. Don't confuse it with another kanji read the same way, 「影|かげ」, a shadow, as in the shadow your body casts. In everyday writing, 「おかげ」 is usually written in kana, so you won't need the kanji often. But knowing it explains the idea: under someone's shade and protection, things went well. Shade, kindly given." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word has a surprising sarcastic side. Normally, 「おかげで」 is for good results, and its opposite, 「せいで」, is for blaming. But people sometimes use 「おかげで」 ironically. If a friend makes you miss the train, you might grumble 「あなたのおかげで、おくれたよ」, thanks to you, I was late. Just like sarcastic thanks a lot in English. Tone makes it clear. In sincere use, it's lovely in speeches and thank-you notes: 「みなさんのおかげで」, thanks to all of you, is how people start accepting an award. And the set phrase 「おかげさまで」, from the main lesson, is the polite humble response to how are you. Sincere or sarcastic, the tone tells you which." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from shade 「陰|かげ」, resting under someone's protection, so it means thanks to for good results, unlike blaming 「せいで」, and gives polite 「おかげさまで」.",
      "The kanji 「陰|かげ」 has the hill radical on the left, shade behind a hill, as in 「日陰|ひかげ」, and differs from 「影|かげ」, a cast shadow.",
      "It can be sarcastic, like 「あなたのおかげで、おくれたよ」, thanks to you, I was late, while sincerely it opens award speeches with 「みなさんのおかげで」."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}の[おかげで]{日本語|にほんご}がじょうずになりました。",
    en: "Thanks to my teacher, my Japanese got better." },
  "どうしよう|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is two pieces you know. 「どう」 means how, or what way. 「しよう」 is the volitional form of 「する」, to do, which you learned in lesson fifteen. It means let's do, or I'll do. So 「どうしよう」 is literally how shall I do it. In practice it means oh no, what should I do? It's something you say when you're in a bit of trouble and unsure what to do next. You'll often hear it said to yourself, out loud, in a panic: I lost my wallet, what do I do! It's casual, and it's usually about yourself or a group you're in. When asking someone else politely what to do, you'd use 「どうしましょうか」, shall we, what should we do? That's the polite version, often used to offer or ask for suggestions. There's also 「どうしようかな」, a softer, thinking-aloud version, like hmm, what should I do. Anime and drama characters say 「どうしよう」 a lot when they panic. A way to remember it: dough, she, yo, my bread is burning, what do I do! That's only a sound hook. To recap: how plus let's do, a panicked what should I do." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The heart of this phrase is the volitional form, the let's or I'll form. It is worth knowing how to make it for any verb, because the same trick works everywhere. For 「る」 verbs, drop 「る」 and add 「よう」, so 「食べる|たべる」 becomes 「食べよう|たべよう」. For 「う」 verbs, change the final sound to its O version and add 「う」, so 「行く|いく」 becomes 「行こう|いこう」 and 「飲む|のむ」 becomes 「飲もう|のもう」. The two irregulars are 「しよう」 and 「来よう|こよう」. Now put a question word in front. 「何を食べよう|なにをたべよう」 means what shall I eat. 「どこに行こう|どこにいこう」 means where shall I go. Add 「か」 and it becomes gentle wondering, 「何を食べようか|なにをたべようか」. So 「どうしよう」 is just this pattern with the most general verb of all, do, and the most general question word, how. Once you see it, you can build your own panics and plans. Question word plus volitional is a whole family of thinking-out-loud sentences." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Picture two friends at a train station. One checks her phone and says 「やばい、電車に遅れる。どうしよう|やばい、でんしゃにおくれる。どうしよう」, oh no, I'll miss the train, what do I do. Her friend asks 「どうしたの」, what happened? That pairing is very natural. One person panics with 「どうしよう」, the other asks what's wrong. People often double it for extra drama, 「どうしよう、どうしよう」. It isn't only for bad news. Someone who just won a prize or got a surprise confession might say it too, overwhelmed and happy. A related phrase you will hear is 「どうしようもない」, literally there is no way to do anything. It means it can't be helped, or hopeless, and it is often said with a sigh about a situation, or even about a person who is a lost cause. One thing to avoid: don't use the bare phrase to ask your teacher or boss for advice. It sounds like you are falling apart in front of them. Ask 「どうしたらいいですか」 instead. Panic to yourself, ask politely to others." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "You have collected a whole set of words built on 「どう」, how, and seeing them side by side makes each one clearer. 「どうですか」 asks how is it, for opinions or offers. 「どうして」 asks why, literally how did it come to this. 「どうやって」 asks by what method, how do you do it, like how do you get to the station. 「どうしたらいい」 asks what should I do, politely seeking advice, using the conditional 「たら」. And 「どうしよう」 is the volitional one, what shall I do, said mostly to yourself. Notice how the second half changes the job. With 「ですか」 you ask for a judgment. With 「して」 you ask for a reason. With 「やって」 you ask for steps. With 「たら」 you ask for advice. With the volitional you are making, or failing to make, your own plan. One small word, how, does all of the heavy lifting, and the ending tells you which kind of how you mean." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's how 「どう」 plus 「しよう」, the volitional of 「する」, so a panicked what should I do, with 「どうしましょうか」 as the polite version for asking others.",
      "Any verb's volitional form plus a question word makes thinking-aloud sentences, like 「何を食べよう|なにをたべよう」, and this phrase uses the most general verb and question word.",
      "It's for panicking to yourself, often doubled, while 「どうしようもない」 means it can't be helped, and with a teacher or boss you should ask 「どうしたらいいですか」.",
      "Words built on 「どう」 change job by ending, 「どうして」 for why, 「どうやって」 for method, 「どうしたらいい」 for advice, and the volitional for your own plan."
    ],
    ja: "さいふがない！[どうしよう]。",
    en: "My wallet's gone! What should I do?" },
  "〜だろう|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the short, casual form of 「でしょう」, which you know means probably or I suppose. The two have the same meaning. 「でしょう」 is polite, and 「だろう」 is plain. It goes after short forms: a verb like 「行く|いく」 plus 「だろう」, an 「い」-adjective plus 「だろう」, and a noun or 「な」-adjective directly plus 「だろう」, as in rain, probably. It shows a guess you're fairly confident about. You'll often find it in writing, like newspapers, diaries, and essays, and in thinking-aloud phrases like 「なぜだろう」, I wonder why. With a question mark, 「だろう？」 asks for agreement: right? you know, don't you? In speech, it sounds fairly masculine and casual. Women often use 「でしょう」 or the shorter 「でしょ」 instead in casual speech. Japanese weather forecasts in written form often use this, for example tomorrow will probably be rainy. A way to remember it: since 「だ」 is the plain version of 「です」, 「だろう」 is just the plain version of 「でしょう」. To recap: same meaning as 「でしょう」, probably, but plain and casual." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's go beyond the basic connections and look at tense and negatives, because 「だろう」 itself never changes. Everything happens in front of it. For a past guess, put the past short form first. 「行っただろう|いっただろう」 means he probably went. 「高かっただろう|たかかっただろう」 means it was probably expensive. 「雨だっただろう|あめだっただろう」 means it was probably rainy. For a negative guess, use the negative short form, 「来ないだろう|こないだろう」, he probably won't come. Watch the noun rule. Before 「だろう」 you never say 「だ」, so it is 「雨だろう|あめだろう」, not rain-da-darou. It is very common with 「たぶん」, probably, at the start, which doubles the sense of guessing. It also sits happily inside 「と思う|とおもう」, as in 「明日は晴れるだろうと思う|あしたははれるだろうとおもう」, I think it will probably be sunny tomorrow. That combination softens an opinion nicely in writing. So the front piece carries the tense and the yes or no, and 「だろう」 just adds probably." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "Listen to the ending. 「だろう」 and 「でしょう」 both end with that long O sound, just like the volitional forms 「しよう」 and 「行こう|いこう」. That is not a coincidence. Historically, 「だろう」 comes from 「であろう」, the volitional form of 「である」, a formal way of saying is. Over time 「であろう」 squeezed down to 「だろう」. You can still see 「であろう」 in serious writing and speeches today. The polite 「でしょう」 grew out of the volitional of 「です」 in a similar way. Why would a let's form mean probably? In older Japanese, that ending covered both intention and conjecture, what someone means to do and what is likely to happen. Modern Japanese split them up. With most verbs it now means let's or I'll, but attached to is, it kept the old guessing meaning. So when you hear that long O at the end, think of something not yet certain, either a plan or a guess. One ending, two kinds of looking ahead." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In conversation you'll hear this word most in two ways. First, as self-talk. You hear the doorbell and mutter 「だれだろう」, who could that be? You see a crowd and wonder 「何だろう|なんだろう」, what's going on? Here it is a private question, and it is used by everyone, men and women alike. Second, with rising intonation to push for agreement. 「おいしいだろう」, said with a rise, means it's good, right? This one sounds firm and fairly masculine, a bit like you expect the other person to agree. Many speakers soften it to 「でしょ」. Compare it with 「〜かな」, which you also know. 「だれかな」 and 「だれだろう」 both mean I wonder who, but 「かな」 is lighter and more casual, while 「だろう」 sounds a little more serious and thoughtful. A short exchange: 「あの人、だれだろう|あのひと、だれだろう」. 「さあ、新しい先生かな|さあ、あたらしいせんせいかな」. Who could that be? Hmm, maybe a new teacher. Wondering to yourself, or nudging someone to agree, are the two everyday jobs." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the plain form of 「でしょう」, probably, attached to short forms or directly to nouns, and with a rise, 「だろう？」 asks for agreement.",
      "The tense and negative go in front, as in 「行っただろう|いっただろう」 or 「来ないだろう|こないだろう」, while 「だろう」 itself never changes and never follows 「だ」.",
      "It squeezed down from 「であろう」, the volitional of 「である」, because that long O ending once covered both intention and conjecture.",
      "It's used as self-talk, like 「だれだろう」, who could that be, slightly more serious than 「かな」, or with a rise to push for agreement, which sounds firm."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は{雨|あめ}[だろう]。",
    en: "It'll probably rain tomorrow." },
  "おさきにしつれいします|お先に失礼します": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a set phrase built from two parts. First, 「お先に|おさきに」. 「先|さき」 means ahead or before, and you know it with its Chinese-origin reading in 「先生|せんせい」, teacher, the one born ahead, and 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week. The 「お」 makes it polite. So 「お先に|おさきに」 means ahead of you, before you. Second, 「失礼します|しつれいします」, which you learned as excuse me. 「失|しつ」 means to lose, and 「礼|れい」 means manners or courtesy. So it's literally I commit a loss of manners, I'm being rude. Put together, it means I'm being rude by going before you. It's what you say when you leave work while your coworkers are still there. In Japanese offices, leaving before others can feel awkward, so this phrase politely acknowledges it. The standard reply from those staying is 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」, thank you for your hard work. You can also use it when going first in other situations, like leaving a party early, or getting on an elevator first. Among close coworkers, just 「お先に|おさきに」 is enough. To recap: ahead plus excuse my rudeness, the polite way to leave before others." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Imagine a Japanese office at six in the evening. Officially the day is over, but the boss is still typing, and so is everyone else. For a long time, many workers felt they shouldn't leave before their manager, and staying late, 「残業|ざんぎょう」, was almost expected. This phrase is the polite key that lets you out of that room. You stand up, gather your things, say it clearly to the room, often with a small bow, and the people staying answer 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」. Nobody is offended, because you have acknowledged that you are leaving them behind. It works in other groups too, like a part-time job, a school club, or a volunteer team. Japan has been pushing to reduce long working hours in recent years, so leaving on time is becoming more normal, but the phrase is as alive as ever. Even in a relaxed office, walking out silently would feel cold. A few polite words turn leaving early into a small act of respect." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closely at 「先|さき」. Here it uses its native reading, 「さき」, which means the point ahead, or what comes first. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「せん」, and you already know it in 「先生|せんせい」 and 「先週|せんしゅう」, last week, the week before this one. You will also meet 「先月|せんげつ」, last month, and 「先輩|せんぱい」, your senior at school or work, the one who started ahead of you. The native reading shows up in everyday phrases. 「先に食べてください|さきにたべてください」 means please go ahead and eat first. And the perfect partner to our phrase is 「お先にどうぞ|おさきにどうぞ」, please, after you, which you say when holding a door or letting someone go first in line. So 「お先に|おさきに」 can point two ways. With 「どうぞ」 you offer someone else the first turn, and with 「失礼します|しつれいします」 you apologize for taking it yourself. One kanji, ahead, and a lot of good manners built around it." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second half, 「失礼します|しつれいします」, is one of the hardest-working phrases in polite Japanese, and this set phrase is just one of its uses. You say 「失礼します|しつれいします」 when you knock and enter a teacher's office. You say 「失礼しました|しつれいしました」, in the past tense, when you leave it. You say it at the end of a business phone call, right before hanging up. And 「失礼ですが|しつれいですが」, excuse me, but, softens a personal question, like asking someone's name. The kanji 「礼|れい」, manners or thanks, also appears in 「お礼|おれい」, a thank-you or a thank-you gift. So 「失礼|しつれい」 is losing that courtesy, and saying it out loud is a way of keeping it. With 「お先に|おさきに」 in front, it names the exact rudeness you are sorry for, going first. Whenever you cross a line, into a room, out of a room, or out the door early, this phrase is the polite way across." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's 「お先に|おさきに」, ahead of you, plus 「失礼します|しつれいします」, I'm being rude, said when leaving work before others, who reply 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」.",
      "In offices where staying late, 「残業|ざんぎょう」, was almost expected, this phrase is the polite key that lets you leave without walking out coldly.",
      "The kanji 「先|さき」 gives 「先輩|せんぱい」 and the partner phrase 「お先にどうぞ|おさきにどうぞ」, after you, offering the first turn instead of apologizing for taking it.",
      "「失礼します|しつれいします」 is said entering a teacher's office, ending phone calls, and in 「失礼ですが|しつれいですが」, always a polite way across a line."
    ],
    ja: "じゃあ、[お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします]。",
    en: "Well, I'll be leaving ahead of you." },
  "おつかれさま|お疲れ様": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This comes from the verb 「疲れる|つかれる」, to get tired. The kanji 「疲|つか」 has the sickness radical around it, the same shape you see in many kanji for illness. Being tired is a kind of weakness. The 「お」 at the front and 「様|さま」 at the end are both politeness markers. You know 「様|さま」 from 「奥様|おくさま」, someone's wife, said politely. So literally it's your honorable tiredness, meaning you must be tired, thank you for your hard work. It's one of the most-used phrases in Japanese work life. Coworkers say 「お疲れ様です|おつかれさまです」 as a greeting in the hallway, at the start of emails, and when someone finishes a task. When someone leaves for the day, others say 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」 in the past tense, thanks for your work today. It's the natural reply to 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」. Among friends, after a club activity or a long day, just 「おつかれ」 works. A similar phrase, 「ご苦労様|ごくろうさま」, is traditionally said by a boss to staff, so 「お疲れ様|おつかれさま」 is the safe one to say to anyone, including your boss. To recap: tired plus two politeness markers, the everyday way to thank someone for their hard work." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb inside this greeting, 「疲れる|つかれる」, causes one of the most common mistakes for English speakers. It is a 「る」 verb, and it describes a change, becoming tired. So when you want to say I'm tired right now, you don't use the present form. 「疲れます|つかれます」 sounds like I get tired, as a habit, or I will get tired. The natural way to say I'm tired is the past, 「疲れました|つかれました」, or casually 「疲れた|つかれた」, I have become tired. You can also say 「疲れています|つかれています」, I'm in a tired state. The same logic explains our greeting. You can't feel someone else's tiredness, so instead of saying you're tired, you honor their tiredness as a thing, with 「お」 and 「様|さま」, which turns it into a thank-you. If you want to ask whether someone is worn out, try 「疲れましたか|つかれましたか」, are you tired? Become tired, then be tired, so use the past or the 「ている」 form." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Let's follow the phrase through a working day. In the morning, a coworker passes you in the hallway. 「お疲れ様です|おつかれさまです」. Yes, even at nine a.m., when nobody is tired yet. In offices it works as an all-purpose hello between colleagues. At lunch, an email to another department starts with it too, like a friendly greeting before the business. In the afternoon, a teammate finishes a big presentation, and you say it again, this time really meaning good job. At the end of the day, people leaving hear 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」. Then, at the after-work dinner, glasses go up and someone shouts 「おつかれ」 before the first sip, almost like cheers. One limit: it is for people on your side, such as coworkers, teammates, and club members. You wouldn't greet a customer this way, because they haven't been working with you. From hello to good job to cheers, one phrase covers the whole day." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The 「様|さま」 at the end of this phrase is the same 「様|さま」 that honors people, like in 「お客様|おきゃくさま」, customer, or 「奥様|おくさま」. But in a handful of set phrases, it attaches to a situation instead of a person, and the result is thanks or sympathy. You know 「ごちそうさまでした」, which you say after a meal, honoring the feast someone gave you. 「おかげさまで」 means thanks to you, and it is the polite answer when someone asks how you are doing. 「ご苦労様|ごくろうさま」 honors someone's trouble or hard labor. And a restaurant worker bringing your food might say 「おまちどおさま」, sorry to keep you waiting. In each one, 「お」 or 「ご」 sits in front and 「さま」 sits behind, wrapping a feeling in politeness. Once you notice the frame, you'll start hearing it everywhere. Polite prefix, a feeling or situation, and 「さま」, the standard shape for Japanese thank-you phrases." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's tired 「疲れる|つかれる」 wrapped in polite 「お」 and 「様|さま」, thanking someone for their hard work, and it's safe for anyone, unlike 「ご苦労様|ごくろうさま」.",
      "Since 「疲れる|つかれる」 means becoming tired, say I'm tired with 「疲れました|つかれました」 or 「疲れています|つかれています」, not the present form.",
      "It works all day among coworkers, as a hello, a good job, a goodbye with 「お疲れ様でした|おつかれさまでした」, and even a cheers, but not toward customers.",
      "It follows the frame of polite prefix, a feeling or situation, then 「さま」, just like 「ごちそうさまでした」 and 「おまちどおさま」."
    ],
    ja: "{今日|きょう}も[お疲れ様|おつかれさま]でした。",
    en: "Thanks for all your hard work today too." },
  "おくさま|奥様": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the extra-respectful cousin of 「奥さん|おくさん」, which you met in lesson fourteen. Break it apart. The first kanji, 「奥|おく」, uses its native reading here, and it means the deep inside, the back part of a place, like the inner rooms of a house. In older, upper-class households, the lady of the house lived and ran things in those inner rooms, so people politely referred to her by the place itself. The second piece, 「様|さま」, is a much more respectful version of 「さん」. You have already seen it in 「お疲れ様|おつかれさま」, and you will hear it in shops in 「お客様|おきゃくさま」. So 「奥さん|おくさん」 is everyday polite, and 「奥様|おくさま」 is the honorific level that fits this lesson's 「敬語|けいご」. Who uses it? You, talking to or about someone you respect, about that person's wife. A host greeting a guest, a salesperson, or an employee asking a boss about his family would say it. The big rule: never use it for your own wife. Talking about your own family, you lower them, not raise them. A common mistake for English speakers is to treat it like a neutral word for wife. It is not neutral. It always points at someone else. Recap: the respected lady of the inner house, plus the extra-polite 「様|さま」, means someone else's wife, said with real respect." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has different words for a wife depending on whose wife it is, and this is where learners often slip. For someone else's wife, use 「奥さん|おくさん」, or the more respectful 「奥様|おくさま」. For your own wife, there are a few choices. 「妻|つま」 is the neutral, modern word, and the safest one today. 「家内|かない」, literally inside the house, is an older, humbler word. Notice it shares the same idea as 「奥|おく」, the woman of the inner home. Husbands work the same way. Someone else's husband is 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」, while your own is 「夫|おっと」, or traditionally 「主人|しゅじん」, master. Many younger Japanese people now feel 「主人|しゅじん」 and 「家内|かない」 sound old-fashioned, because they picture the wife at home and the husband in charge, so 「夫|おっと」 and 「妻|つま」 are becoming the norm. Your own family gets plain or humble words, and other people's families get the decorated ones." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「奥|おく」 is worth knowing on its own, because it shows up wherever something is deep inside. On its own, 「奥|おく」 means the back or the depths. 「店の奥|みせのおく」 is the back of the shop. 「奥の部屋|おくのへや」 is the back room. 「山奥|やまおく」 is deep in the mountains, far from any town. 「奥歯|おくば」, back teeth, are your molars. And 「引き出しの奥|ひきだしのおく」 is the very back of a drawer, where lost things hide. In all of these, the native reading 「おく」 is used, the same one you hear in 「奥様|おくさま」. The shape is useful to notice. The big character 「大|だい」 sits at the bottom, with a roof-like frame on top holding something inside, a nice way to picture a deep, enclosed space. Treat that as a memory hook, not a history lesson. So when you hear 「おく」, think of the deep inner part, whether it is a shop, a mountain, a mouth, or a home." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is where you'll actually hear this word. A guest visits a coworker's home and says 「奥様によろしくお伝えください|おくさまによろしくおつたえください」, please give my regards to your wife, a phrase that matches nicely with what you learned in this lesson. A business partner on the phone asks 「奥様はお元気ですか|おくさまはおげんきですか」. In a department store, staff sometimes address a woman customer as 「奥様|おくさま」, as a way of saying madam. That use is common but a little risky, since it assumes she is married, and some customers find it presumptuous, so many shops now prefer 「お客様|おきゃくさま」. You will also see the word in advertising, magazines and TV, where it labels the stylish, well-off housewife image. For you as a learner, the clear rule is: say it about the wife of someone you respect, in polite situations. Among friends, 「奥さん|おくさん」 is perfectly fine and less stiff. Respect for others' wives, never for your own." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It's the inner part of the house 「奥|おく」, where the lady of the house lived, plus very respectful 「様|さま」, so someone else's wife, never your own.",
      "For your own wife say 「妻|つま」 or the older 「家内|かない」, and for husbands 「ご主人|ごしゅじん」 versus 「夫|おっと」, so others' families get the decorated words.",
      "On its own 「奥|おく」 means the deep inner part, as in 「店の奥|みせのおく」, the back of the shop, and 「山奥|やまおく」, deep in the mountains.",
      "You'll hear 「奥様によろしくお伝えください|おくさまによろしくおつたえください」, and shops calling customers 「奥様|おくさま」 is risky, so many prefer 「お客様|おきゃくさま」."
    ],
    ja: "[奥様|おくさま]はおげんきですか。",
    en: "Is your wife doing well?" },
  "おこさん|お子さん": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Start with the kanji 「子|こ」, child, read here with its native reading. It is one of the oldest picture kanji. You can see a baby wrapped up tight, with a round head on top and two little arms stretched out to the sides. You already know it from 「子供|こども」, child, where it has the same reading. Now look at the wrapping. In front sits 「お」, the polite prefix, and behind it sits 「さん」, the same 「さん」 you put on people's names. Put both on, and the plain word for child becomes a respectful word for someone else's child. This is how Japanese politeness often works. You decorate the other person's things with 「お」 or 「ご」, and you leave your own things plain. So a coworker might ask you, 「お子さんはいますか|おこさんはいますか」, do you have children? And you would answer about your own kids with the plain 「子供|こども」, never 「お子さん|おこさん」. Using it for your own child sounds like you are praising your own family, which feels odd in Japanese. It pairs with the other family words in this lesson, like 「奥様|おくさま」 for someone's wife. Recap: the picture of a wrapped baby, dressed up with 「お」 in front and 「さん」 behind, is a polite way to talk about other people's children." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「子|こ」 has three readings you will meet, and each one tells you something. The native reading 「こ」 is used for real children. You know it in 「子供|こども」, and in 「男の子|おとこのこ」 and 「女の子|おんなのこ」, boy and girl. It also appears in 「息子|むすこ」, son. The Chinese-origin reading 「し」 shows up in words where 「子|し」 has become a little suffix, meaning a small thing or a piece. You know 「帽子|ぼうし」, hat, and 「電子レンジ|でんしレンジ」, microwave oven, where 「電子|でんし」 means electron, a tiny particle of electricity. There is even a third reading, 「す」, in 「椅子|いす」, chair, though that one is special. So if 「子|こ」 means a real child, expect 「こ」. If it sits at the end of a technical or everyday object word, expect 「し」. In 「お子さん|おこさん」, it is a real child, so you hear 「こ」. One small kanji, from children all the way to electrons." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Asking about family is a common small-talk topic, and this word is how you begin. A coworker might ask 「お子さんはいらっしゃいますか|おこさんはいらっしゃいますか」, do you have children, using the honorific verb for being, since the children are someone else's. You answer with plain words, 「はい、子供が二人います|はい、こどもがふたりいます」, yes, I have two kids. To ask a child's age politely, say 「お子さんはおいくつですか|おこさんはおいくつですか」. For a specific child, Japanese uses 「息子さん|むすこさん」, your son, and 「娘さん|むすめさん」, your daughter, and when talking about your own, simply 「息子|むすこ」 and 「娘|むすめ」. There is also a step above this word, 「お子様|おこさま」, used by shops, hotels and restaurants toward customers. A common mistake is answering a question with 「私のお子さん|わたしのおこさん」. It sounds like you are honoring your own kids. Their kids get the polite wrapping, and yours get the plain word." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Walk into a family restaurant in Japan and look at the menu for 「お子様ランチ|おこさまランチ」, the kids' lunch, using the extra-polite version of this word. It is a famous little plate. Typically there is a small dome of rice with a tiny flag stuck on top, plus a mini hamburger steak, fried shrimp, spaghetti, maybe jelly or pudding, and often a small toy. The dish is usually credited to a department store restaurant in Tokyo around 1930, and the little flag became its trademark. Many Japanese adults remember it fondly, and some restaurants even offer grown-up versions for nostalgia. You will see the same polite word on signs like 「お子様メニュー|おこさまメニュー」, children's menu. Children are also celebrated nationally on 「子供の日|こどものひ」, Children's Day, on May fifth, when families fly carp-shaped streamers. So when shops and restaurants speak to families, children become honored little guests." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The picture kanji of a wrapped baby gets polite 「お」 in front and 「さん」 behind for someone else's child, while your own are plain 「子供|こども」.",
      "The kanji reads 「こ」 for real children, as in 「息子|むすこ」, but 「し」 as a suffix in object words like 「帽子|ぼうし」 and 「電子レンジ|でんしレンジ」.",
      "Ask 「お子さんはいらっしゃいますか|おこさんはいらっしゃいますか」, answer with plain 「子供が二人います|こどもがふたりいます」, and never say 「私のお子さん|わたしのおこさん」.",
      "Restaurants offer 「お子様ランチ|おこさまランチ」, a kids' plate famous for its rice dome with a little flag, using the extra-polite version of this word."
    ],
    ja: "[お子さん|おこさん]は{何歳|なんさい}ですか。",
    en: "How old is your child?" },
  "ちゅうがくせい|中学生": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Three kanji, all with their Chinese-origin readings, and you know every one of them. The first is 「中|ちゅう」, middle. Its shape is a box with a line straight through the center, which makes it easy to remember. You have it in 「中国|ちゅうごく」, China, the middle kingdom, and in 「一日中|いちにちじゅう」, all day long, where the sound changes. Then comes 「学|がく」, learning, from 「大学|だいがく」 and 「学生|がくせい」. Last is 「生|せい」, which here means student, the same ending as in 「学生|がくせい」 and 「留学生|りゅうがくせい」. So 「中学|ちゅうがく」 is middle learning, a shortened form of middle school, and adding 「生|せい」 makes a middle school student. Line it up with a word you know from lesson one, 「高校生|こうこうせい」, high school student. Same pattern, different first kanji. A cultural note: Japanese schools run six years of elementary school, then three years of junior high, then three years of high school. Junior high covers roughly ages twelve to fifteen, it is the last part of compulsory education, and most students wear a uniform and join a club. Recap: middle, learning, student, put together, gives a junior high school student." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word sits in a neat ladder of student words, and the learner who knows one rung can climb the whole thing. Down at the bottom is 「小学生|しょうがくせい」, an elementary school student, where 「小|しょう」 means small. Small learning, middle learning. Then comes our word. Next is 「高校生|こうこうせい」, a high school student, and at the top is 「大学生|だいがくせい」, a college student, big learning. After that, some people go on to graduate school and become 「大学院生|だいがくいんせい」. Each rung follows the same recipe. Name the school, then add 「生|せい」 for the student. The schools themselves follow the same pattern with 「校|こう」 for school: 「小学校|しょうがっこう」, 「中学校|ちゅうがっこう」, and 「高校|こうこう」, which is a shortened form of the longer official name for high school. In everyday speech, people also shorten the school name to just 「中学|ちゅうがく」, as in I went to that junior high. So when you hear any of these words, look at the first kanji. Small, middle, high, or big tells you the stage of school. Small, middle, high, big: four steps, one pattern." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In Japan, these three years are a big chapter of life, and the vocabulary around them comes up all the time. Students say which year they are in by counting from the start of junior high. A first-year is 「中学一年生|ちゅうがくいちねんせい」, and in casual talk that shrinks all the way down to 「中一|ちゅういち」. Second-years are 「中二|ちゅうに」, and third-years are 「中三|ちゅうさん」. You will hear these in anime and dramas constantly. Most students join a club, called 「部活|ぶかつ」, and many practice almost every day, even on weekends. The third year is famous for stress, because students take entrance exams to get into high school. That exam season is called 「受験|じゅけん」. If someone asks about your family, a natural answer is my younger sister is a junior high student, or my son is in his second year of junior high. Use 「です」 with it like any other noun. One small tip: a teen in Japan would rarely call themselves a teenager. They name their school stage instead. Next time you hear a quick 「中二|ちゅうに」, you will know exactly who is talking." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Listen closely to the sounds here, because this word has a long vowel and its cousins have a squeeze. The first syllable is a long 「ちゅう」, held for two beats. If you cut it short, it sounds wrong, so count it out: 「ちゅ」, 「う」, 「が」, 「く」, 「せ」, 「い」. Six beats in total, because the final 「せい」 is also a long vowel. Now compare the word for the school itself, 「中学校|ちゅうがっこう」. The 「く」 of 「がく」 has vanished and turned into a small pause. That happens because the next sound starts with a k. When 「がく」 meets a k sound, the 「く」 often shrinks into that little stop. You already know this from the plain word 「学校|がっこう」, school, and you will meet it again in 「学期|がっき」, a school term. But before 「せい」, there is no k, so 「がくせい」 stays whole. Here is a quick check: say 「ちゅうがくせい」 and then 「ちゅうがっこう」 back to back, and feel the difference in your mouth. Long vowels at both ends for the student, a little hiccup in the middle for the school." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「中|ちゅう」 is middle, 「学|がく」 is learning, and 「生|せい」 is student, so middle learning plus student gives a junior high student, built just like 「高校生|こうこうせい」.",
      "It sits on a ladder: 「小学生|しょうがくせい」, then this word, then 「高校生|こうこうせい」 and 「大学生|だいがくせい」. Small, middle, high, big tells you the stage of school.",
      "Students name their year by counting from the start of junior high, so a first-year is 「中一|ちゅういち」, then 「中二|ちゅうに」 and 「中三|ちゅうさん」, and the third year means entrance exam stress.",
      "Hold both long vowels, six beats in all. In 「中学校|ちゅうがっこう」 the 「く」 shrinks to a small pause before the k sound, but before 「せい」 it stays whole."
    ],
    ja: "{妹|いもうと}は[中学生|ちゅうがくせい]です。",
    en: "My younger sister is a junior high school student." },
  "ぶちょう|部長": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「部|ぶ」, means a section or division of a larger whole. In a company it is a department. In a school it is a club, which is why school clubs are called club activities using this same kanji. You have seen it in a disguised reading in 「部屋|へや」, room, a section of a house. The second kanji is 「長|ちょう」, head or chief. Its native reading is the one you know from 「長い|ながい」, long. Here it means the person at the top. You met the same piece in 「社長|しゃちょう」, the company president, where 「社|しゃ」 is company. So the pattern is easy. Whatever unit comes first, add 「長|ちょう」 and you get its boss. Department plus head gives department manager. A cultural note that ties into this lesson's 「敬語|けいご」. In Japanese offices, people usually call a manager by the title instead of by name, or by family name plus title. You would say the title directly, and you would not add 「さん」 on top, because the title is already respectful. When you talk about your 「部長|ぶちょう」, you also switch to honorific verbs like 「いらっしゃる」. Recap: department plus chief, built just like 「社長|しゃちょう」, gives the department manager." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese companies have a ladder of titles, and almost every rung ends in 「長|ちょう」, chief. Learn the ladder and you can read any business card. At the very top is 「会長|かいちょう」, the chairman. Then comes 「社長|しゃちょう」, the company president. Below the president is our word, the head of a whole department. Under that is 「課長|かちょう」, the head of a smaller section, where 「課|か」 means section. Below that you may find 「係長|かかりちょう」, a team leader. The same ending works outside the office too. The head of a school is 「校長|こうちょう」, the principal, usually called 「校長先生|こうちょうせんせい」. A store manager is 「店長|てんちょう」, where 「店|てん」 is the kanji from 「店|みせ」, shop. And in a school club, the student who leads it is also called 「部長|ぶちょう」, because a club uses the same kanji 「部|ぶ」. So a sixteen-year-old can be a department head of the tennis club. The first kanji always names the unit. The 「長|ちょう」 always makes it the boss. Unit plus chief, every time." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a twist that surprises almost every learner. Inside the company, you use your manager's title with respect, as in 「田中部長|たなかぶちょう」, Manager Tanaka. But when you talk to someone from outside the company, like a client on the phone, the rules flip. Your own manager becomes part of your group, and you do not lift your own group up in front of outsiders. So you drop the title and even drop 「さん」. You might say 「田中は今おりません|たなかはいまおりません」, Tanaka is not here right now, using a humble verb. To a Western ear, it sounds almost rude to the boss, but in Japan it is the correct, polite thing to do. It shows respect to the client instead. If you need to say the role, you put it first, 「部長の田中|ぶちょうのたなか」, our manager Tanaka. This in-group and out-group idea is a big part of the honorific system you are learning now, and you will see much more of the humble side next lesson. Remember: inside, the title lifts the boss up. Outside, the boss steps back with you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「部|ぶ」 is a department or club and 「長|ちょう」 is chief, built just like 「社長|しゃちょう」. Use the title itself to address your manager, without adding 「さん」.",
      "Almost every company title ends in 「長|ちょう」: 「会長|かいちょう」, 「社長|しゃちょう」, this word, then 「課長|かちょう」. A student who leads a school club is also called 「部長|ぶちょう」.",
      "Inside the company you say 「田中部長|たなかぶちょう」, but to an outside client your boss joins your group, so you drop the title and say 「部長の田中|ぶちょうのたなか」."
    ],
    ja: "[部長|ぶちょう]はいま{会社|かいしゃ}にいません。",
    en: "The department manager isn't at the office right now." },
  "しゅっちょう|出張": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings, and one sound gets squeezed. The first kanji, 「出|しゅつ」, means to go out or come out. Its shape stacks two mountain-like shapes, and you know it well from 「出かける|でかける」, to go out, 「出す|だす」, to take out, and 「出身|しゅっしん」, where you come from. Notice that 「出身|しゅっしん」 has the same squeezed sound as this word. The full sound is 「しゅつ」, and before another consonant it shrinks to a small pause. The second kanji, 「張|ちょう」, means to stretch or extend. Its left side is the bow kanji, like stretching a bowstring. A way to remember the pair: you go out from your home base, stretching your reach to another city. That is a business trip. It is used only for work travel. A vacation is 「旅行|りょこう」, not this. You say you go on a business trip with 「出張|しゅっちょう」 followed by 「に行く|にいく」, or you use 「出張する|しゅっちょうする」. A cultural note: when Japanese office workers come back from a business trip, they usually bring a box of individually wrapped snacks, 「お土産|おみやげ」, to share with the whole office. Recap: going out and stretching to another place for your job gives a business trip." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look closer at the second kanji, 「張|ちょう」, because you have been using it for a long time without knowing. Its native reading is 「は」, and the verb 「張る|はる」 means to stretch out or spread something tight, like putting up a tent, sticking up a poster, or filling a tub with water. Now here is the surprise. The famous word 「頑張る|がんばる」, to do your best, uses this very kanji. The first kanji of that word means stubborn, and the second is our stretching kanji. So doing your best is, in its writing, stubbornly stretching yourself. The first kanji, 「出|しゅつ」, also appears in other trip words. 「出発|しゅっぱつ」 is departure, with the same squeezed sound. 「出口|でぐち」 is an exit, using the native reading. So in this one word you get going out plus stretching, and the stretching kanji is the same one you cheer with before a test. Next time you say 「頑張って|がんばって」 to a coworker leaving on a trip, you are using the same kanji twice over." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Office workers in Japan use this word constantly, and it comes in a handful of handy combinations. If your coworker is away, someone might tell you they are 「出張中|しゅっちょうちゅう」, on a business trip right now. The place they went is their 「出張先|しゅっちょうさき」, the trip destination. A trip abroad is 「海外出張|かいがいしゅっちょう」, where 「海外|かいがい」 means overseas. A quick there-and-back trip with no overnight stay is a 「日帰り出張|ひがえりしゅっちょう」. The basic sentence patterns are simple. You can say you are going to Osaka on business with 「出張で大阪に行きます|しゅっちょうでおおさかにいきます」, using 「で」 for the reason. One more use catches learners off guard. On flyers and websites, the word also means that a service comes to you. A business might offer to come to your home to buy your old books, or to repair something on site, and they use this word for that visit. So the core idea is really a worker going out from their base to somewhere else. A short wrap-up: business trip in the office, house call on the flyer." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a silly memory trick that ties the sound to the meaning. Picture a tired salaryman boarding the bullet train at dawn with his briefcase. As the doors close, the train goes 「しゅっ」, that quick hissing sound of air brakes and sliding doors. Then he slumps down in his seat, opens a boxed lunch, and says a long, sleepy 「ちょう」, as if he is groaning, this trip is so long. Hiss, then groan. 「しゅっ」, 「ちょう」. Now add the kanji. The first one, going out, is the train leaving his home city. The second one, stretching, is his arms stretching up as he yawns on the way to a meeting three hundred miles away. When he gets back at night, he is carrying a big paper bag of snack boxes for the office. Replay the scene once: hiss of the doors, groan of the long ride, stretch in the seat. That is a business trip." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「出|しゅつ」 is going out and 「張|ちょう」 is stretching, so you go out from your home base and stretch to another city for work. A vacation is 「旅行|りょこう」 instead.",
      "The stretching kanji 「張|ちょう」 is the same one in 「頑張る|がんばる」, to do your best, which in writing means stubbornly stretching yourself.",
      "A coworker who is away is 「出張中|しゅっちょうちゅう」, and you go somewhere on business with 「出張で|しゅっちょうで」. On flyers, the word also means a service that comes to your home.",
      "Picture a salaryman on the bullet train: the doors hiss 「しゅっ」, then he groans a long, sleepy 「ちょう」 about the long ride, stretching in his seat."
    ],
    ja: "{父|ちち}は{来週|らいしゅう}[出張|しゅっちょう]にいきます。",
    en: "My father is going on a business trip next week." },
  "けいご|敬語": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the name of the whole topic of this lesson. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「敬|けい」, means respect, the feeling of looking up to someone. The second, 「語|ご」, means language or words, and you know it from 「日本語|にほんご」, Japanese, 「外国語|がいこくご」, foreign language, and the very first lesson's 「英語|えいご」. So respect plus language gives respect language, or honorific language. It is not just a list of polite words. It is a whole system with three branches. The first branch is the polite style you already use, ending in 「です」 and 「ます」. The second branch, which you learn in this lesson, raises up the other person. Special verbs like 「いらっしゃる」, 「召し上がる|めしあがる」, and 「おっしゃる」 replace plain verbs when the subject is someone you respect. The third branch, coming next lesson, lowers yourself instead. Who uses it? Everyone, in the right setting. Shop staff to customers, employees to bosses and clients, students to professors, and anyone to an older stranger. Even Japanese people find it hard, and companies train new hires in it. Recap: respect plus words, the system of speaking that lifts the people you talk to and about." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The three branches of this system each have their own name, and every name ends in the same 「語|ご」, language. Polite language, the 「です」 and 「ます」 style you already use, is called 「丁寧語|ていねいご」. The kanji 「丁寧|ていねい」 means careful and courteous. Respectful language, which lifts up the other person with verbs like 「いらっしゃる」, is 「尊敬語|そんけいご」. Humble language, which lowers yourself, coming next lesson, is 「謙譲語|けんじょうご」. You do not need to memorize the last name yet, but the middle one is worth knowing, because 「尊敬|そんけい」 is also an everyday word. 「尊敬する|そんけいする」 means to respect or look up to someone, as in I respect my father. It shares the same respect kanji, 「敬|けい」, with our word. That kanji also shows up in a national holiday, 「敬老の日|けいろうのひ」, Respect for the Aged Day, in September, where 「老|ろう」 means old. So the respect kanji is not just about grammar. It is a real feeling in Japanese culture. Polite, respectful, humble: three branches, one family name." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Native speakers worry about getting this right, and there are some classic slip-ups worth knowing. The biggest one for learners is lifting yourself up. Honorific verbs are for the other person. If you say that you yourself will go using 「いらっしゃる」, it sounds like you are bowing to yourself. Another trap is stacking honorifics on top of each other. Japanese people call this 「二重敬語|にじゅうけいご」, double honorifics. For example, 「おっしゃる」 is already the honorific for say, so adding another honorific ending on top of it is considered too much, even though some people do it trying to sound extra polite. A few doubled forms, like 「お召し上がりになる|おめしあがりになる」, have become so common that they are widely accepted. A third trap is overdoing it with friends. Honorifics with a close friend sound cold or sarcastic, as if you are putting up a wall. The best approach for now is simple. Use polite 「です」 and 「ます」 by default, and add special honorific verbs when the subject is a teacher, a boss, or a customer. Respect goes outward, never back to yourself." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Why does Japanese have such a complex system? A big reason is the idea of inside and outside groups, called 「うち」 and 「そと」. You speak one way to people in your circle and another way to people outside it, and the line moves depending on the situation. To a client, even your own boss counts as inside, so you do not lift your boss up in front of them. Age and rank also matter. Japanese schools and clubs strongly mark seniors, called 「先輩|せんぱい」, and juniors, called 「後輩|こうはい」. Even a one-year gap can mean the younger student speaks politely to the older one. This system shows up in everyday places. Train announcements, convenience stores, and department stores are full of honorific phrases. Many companies hold training for new employees just on phone manners and business speech. And plenty of Japanese adults admit they find it tricky, so you are in good company. The good news for learners is that people are forgiving. A foreigner who tries is usually appreciated, even with mistakes. In short, this system maps who is inside, who is outside, and who is above." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「敬|けい」 is respect and 「語|ご」 is language, so this is honorific language, a whole system with polite, respectful, and humble branches.",
      "The three branches all end in 「語|ご」: 「丁寧語|ていねいご」 is polite, 「尊敬語|そんけいご」 is respectful, and 「謙譲語|けんじょうご」 is humble. 「尊敬する|そんけいする」 means to respect someone.",
      "Never use honorific verbs like 「いらっしゃる」 about yourself, avoid stacking double honorifics, and skip them with close friends. Respect goes outward, never back to yourself.",
      "The system maps 「うち」 and 「そと」, inside and outside groups, plus rank, like 「先輩|せんぱい」 and 「後輩|こうはい」. Japanese people find it tricky too, so learners who try are appreciated."
    ],
    ja: "{日本|にほん}の[敬語|けいご]はむずかしいです。",
    en: "Japanese honorific language is difficult." },
  "どちら|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You already met this word in lesson ten as the polite form of which one, the partner of 「どっち」. In this lesson it gets a second job. It is also the polite way to say where, stepping in for 「どこ」. It belongs to a whole family of polite place words that end in 「ちら」. 「こちら」 is the polite version of 「ここ」, here. 「そちら」 replaces 「そこ」, there. 「あちら」 replaces 「あそこ」, over there. And 「どちら」 replaces 「どこ」, where. Literally these point in a direction, this way or that way, rather than at a spot, and pointing vaguely at a direction feels softer and more polite than pointing straight at a place. Who uses it? Anyone speaking politely, especially to customers, guests, and people you respect. A host might ask a guest where they are from with 「お国はどちらですか|おくにはどちらですか」. On the phone, 「どちら様|どちらさま」 politely asks who is calling. A shop clerk will say 「こちらです」, this way, please. A common mistake is to think it can only mean which. Context tells you. If the question is about a place, it means where. A way to remember it: a polite person does not jab a finger at a spot, they gesture in a direction. Recap: the polite direction word, used for which and, in honorific speech, for where." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Think of the question words as a ladder from casual to polite. When there are three or more choices, you use 「どれ」, which one. When there are just two, friends say 「どっち」. In a polite setting, 「どっち」 becomes 「どちら」. For places, the plain question is 「どこ」, where, and the polite version, as you learn now, is again 「どちら」. There is also a polite way to ask who. The plain word is 「だれ」, and the polite word is 「どなた」. Then on top of that sits 「どちら様|どちらさま」, an even more polite who, used for guests and callers. Notice how the polite side borrows the direction word for so many jobs. Which way, where, and even who. That is because pointing at a direction sounds softer than pointing at a person or a thing. So a receptionist uses this word for almost everything, while a teenager at home uses 「どっち」 and 「どこ」. The ladder is: 「どれ」 and 「どっち」 and 「どこ」 for friends, this word for polite company." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word plays a starring role in comparisons, a pattern from lesson ten. To ask which of two things is more something, you say A and B, which is more, like 「コーヒーと紅茶とどちらが好きですか|コーヒーとこうちゃとどちらがすきですか」, which do you like more, coffee or tea? The answer uses 「ほう」, side, as in 「紅茶のほうが好きです|こうちゃのほうがすきです」, I like tea more. Now add 「も」 and the word changes its meaning to both. 「どちらも好きです|どちらもすきです」 means I like both. With a negative verb, it flips to neither. 「どちらも好きじゃないです|どちらもすきじゃないです」 means I like neither. Add 「でも」 and you get either one is fine, 「どちらでもいいです」, a very handy phrase when a host asks what you would like to drink. One small note: in all these patterns, 「どっち」 can take its place in casual speech, with exactly the same grammar. So once you know the polite version, you get the casual one for free. Which one, both, neither, either: four meanings from one word plus little particles." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "In polite conversation, this word often appears with an honorific verb, and you will hear it at offices, hotels, and introductions. A new acquaintance might politely ask where you work with 「どちらにお勤めですか|どちらにおつとめですか」. A receptionist might ask where you are coming from, meaning which company, with 「どちらからいらっしゃいましたか」, using this lesson's honorific verb for come. A neighbor might ask where you are off to today with 「どちらへ」, short and polite. Your answer does not need this word. You just name the place plainly, like to the station. One more classic exchange. When someone thanks you, you can say 「こちらこそ」, the same to you, literally this side, if anything. It is the polite direction word for this, from the same family. Picture a business card exchange. One person bows and says, where are you from? The other answers, from a company in Tokyo. Then both say thank you and 「こちらこそ」. Polite direction words carry the whole conversation." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Besides polite which, this word is the polite where, replacing 「どこ」. Pointing at a direction, like 「こちら」 and 「そちら」, sounds softer than pointing at a spot.",
      "Friends say 「どれ」, 「どっち」, and 「どこ」, while polite speech borrows this one word for which and where, and even 「どちら様|どちらさま」 for who.",
      "In comparisons it asks which of two, with 「ほう」 in the answer. Add 「も」 for both or neither, and 「でも」 for either is fine, as in 「どちらでもいいです」.",
      "It pairs with honorific verbs, as in 「どちらからいらっしゃいましたか」. You answer with a plain place name, and its cousin shows up in 「こちらこそ」."
    ],
    ja: "おくには[どちら]ですか。",
    en: "Where is your home country?" },
  "おれい|お礼": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji 「礼|れい」 uses its Chinese-origin reading and means courtesy, manners, and the gestures that show them, including a bow and a thank-you. In school, Japanese students stand up and bow at the start of class when someone calls out this kanji's sound. You have already used it without noticing in 「失礼します|しつれいします」, excuse me, from lesson sixteen. There, 「失|しつ」 means to lose, so you are literally saying I am losing my manners, as an apology for interrupting. You heard it again in 「お先に失礼します|おさきにしつれいします」 when leaving work. Put the polite prefix 「お」 in front, and you get thanks as a thing you give. That can be words, as in 「お礼を言う|おれいをいう」, to say thank you, or it can be an actual present, as in 「お礼にケーキをあげる|おれいにケーキをあげる」, to give cake as a thank-you. In Japan, returning a favor with a small gift is very common, so this word comes up often. A common mistake is to use it as a greeting. You do not say this word to thank someone. You say 「ありがとうございます」, and you call that act 「お礼|おれい」. Recap: courtesy and bowing, made polite with 「お」, becomes an expression of gratitude, in words or in a gift." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, gratitude often comes with a physical thank-you and a second round of words. When someone gives you a present for a big occasion like a wedding or a new baby, it is customary to send a return gift, called 「お返し|おかえし」. Traditionally, the return gift for weddings and similar events is worth about half of what you received, a custom called 「半返し|はんがえし」, half return. On a smaller scale, if a neighbor helps you, you might bring over a box of sweets the next day. Another habit surprises English speakers: thanking someone again later. If a friend treated you to dinner, the next time you meet, you say 「この間はありがとうございました|このあいだはありがとうございました」, thank you for the other day. In business, people say 「先日は|せんじつは」 and the same thanks. Forgetting this second thank-you can seem a little ungrateful. So a single thank-you is often just the beginning. The thanks continues as a gift, a card, and a reminder at the next meeting." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「礼|れい」 is all about proper form and showing respect, and it builds a small family of useful words. 「礼儀|れいぎ」 means manners or etiquette, and someone with good manners is described as 「礼儀正しい|れいぎただしい」, literally manners correct. At many Japanese schools and companies, the day begins with a morning meeting called 「朝礼|ちょうれい」, morning courtesy, where people gather for announcements. When a class starts, a student calls out 「起立|きりつ」, stand up, and then 「礼|れい」, bow, and everyone bows to the teacher. On the opposite side, 「無礼|ぶれい」 means rude, with the kanji for without. It shows up in period dramas, when a samurai shouts at someone for being insolent. And you already know 「失礼|しつれい」 from excuse me. So the family covers bowing, manners, morning assemblies, and rudeness. In each, this kanji means respect shown through proper form. The thank-you in our word is that same respect, turned toward someone who helped you." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a puzzle about the little 「お」 at the front. In general, Japanese adds 「お」 to native words, like 「お金|おかね」, and 「ご」 to Chinese-origin words, like 「ご家族|ごかぞく」, your family, or 「ご両親|ごりょうしん」, your parents. But this word uses a Chinese reading, 「れい」, and still takes 「お」. It is one of a group of very everyday words that broke the rule because people say them so often. You already know others: 「お茶|おちゃ」, tea, 「お電話|おでんわ」, a phone call, and 「お弁当|おべんとう」, a boxed lunch. All Chinese readings, all with 「お」. Here, the 「お」 is not optional decoration. It is fixed, and you would not say the word without it in this meaning. Now for patterns. 「お礼を言う|おれいをいう」 is to say thanks, 「お礼をする|おれいをする」 is to do something to thank someone, and 「お礼の手紙|おれいのてがみ」 is a thank-you letter. The 「の」 links it to the thing it describes. The rule of thumb, then: 「お」 for native words, 「ご」 for Chinese ones, and a few beloved exceptions like this one." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「礼|れい」 is courtesy and bowing, the same kanji as 「失礼します|しつれいします」. With 「お」, it is thanks you give, in words or a gift, but you never say it to thank someone.",
      "Gratitude in Japan often means a return gift, 「お返し|おかえし」, sometimes worth half, and a second thank-you at the next meeting, like 「この間はありがとうございました|このあいだはありがとうございました」.",
      "The kanji 「礼|れい」 means respect shown through proper form, as in 「礼儀|れいぎ」, manners, 「朝礼|ちょうれい」, a morning assembly, and 「無礼|ぶれい」, rude.",
      "Though 「れい」 is a Chinese reading, it takes a fixed 「お」, like 「お茶|おちゃ」 and 「お電話|おでんわ」. Use 「お礼を言う|おれいをいう」 to say thanks."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}に[お礼|おれい]をいいました。",
    en: "I thanked my teacher." },
  "しゅるい|種類": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「種|しゅ」, means seed. Its native reading is 「たね」, the everyday word for a seed you plant. The left side is the grain plant shape, a stalk of rice with a drooping head. From seed, the meaning grew into kind or species, because plants from the same seed are the same kind. The second kanji, 「類|るい」, means a group of similar things, a category. It shows up in big words like the word for mankind. So seed plus category makes a kind or a sort. The word works in two ways. As a noun, you can ask what kind of something it is. As a counter, you put a number in front to count kinds: 「一種類|いっしゅるい」, one kind, 「三種類|さんしゅるい」, three kinds. That counter use is where English speakers slip. In English we say three kinds of cake. In Japanese the counting goes after the thing: cake, three kinds, there are. It pairs naturally with 「いろいろ」, various, from lesson thirteen, as in many different kinds. A way to remember it: every kind of plant starts from its own seed. Recap: seed plus category gives a kind or a sort, and it doubles as a counter for kinds." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The second kanji, 「類|るい」, means a group of things that belong together, and it builds a handful of words you will meet soon. 「書類|しょるい」 means documents or paperwork. It is the writing kanji plus category, the kind of things that are written. 「人類|じんるい」 is humankind, the people category. 「親類|しんるい」 means relatives, the parent category, your extended family. 「衣類|いるい」 means clothing, as a category on a store sign. And 「分類|ぶんるい」, with the divide kanji, is classification, sorting things into groups. The first kanji, 「種|しゅ」, has relatives too. 「人種|じんしゅ」 means race, a kind of people. 「一種|いっしゅ」 means a sort of, as in a sort of art. In every one of these words, 「類|るい」 is a group of the same kind and 「種|しゅ」 is a type. So when you meet a new word ending in 「類|るい」, try asking, what is the category here? Usually you can guess the meaning from the first kanji. Seed and category, two kanji that sort the world into groups." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "When you use this word as a counter, some numbers change their sound, and it is worth practicing them out loud. One kind is 「一種類|いっしゅるい」, with a little pause. Two kinds is a plain 「二種類|にしゅるい」, and three is 「三種類|さんしゅるい」. Four is 「四種類|よんしゅるい」, and five is 「五種類|ごしゅるい」. Six stays 「六種類|ろくしゅるい」. Eight usually squeezes to 「八種類|はっしゅるい」, and ten becomes 「十種類|じゅっしゅるい」. These are the same squeezes you know from counting with 「回|かい」 or with 「本|ほん」, so the pattern should feel familiar. To ask how many kinds, you say 「何種類|なんしゅるい」. One more sound tip. The 「しゅ」 is a single beat, short and quick, not 「しゅう」. If you stretch it, it starts to sound like the word for week, 「週|しゅう」. Say the whole word in three even beats: 「しゅ」, 「る」, 「い」. So, three short beats, and watch for the little pause on one, eight, and ten." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this word a lot when shopping and eating out, because Japanese shops love to advertise variety. A bakery sign might boast twenty kinds of bread. A clerk might tell you there are three kinds of sauce to choose from. A useful way to praise a shop is to say the selection is big, 「種類が多い|しゅるいがおおい」, literally the kinds are many. The opposite, not much variety, is 「種類が少ない|しゅるいがすくない」. Here is a quick shop dialogue. You ask how many kinds of ice cream they have, 「何種類ありますか|なんしゅるいありますか」. The clerk answers, there are eight kinds. You can also link it with 「の」 to describe something: 「いろいろな種類の本|いろいろなしゅるいのほん」, many different kinds of books. One mistake to avoid: English speakers sometimes use this word to mean kind as in kind-hearted. That is a totally different meaning, and Japanese uses 「優しい|やさしい」 for that. This word is only for types and varieties. So next time you face a long menu, you can say there are so many kinds." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「種|しゅ」 is seed and 「類|るい」 is category, since plants from the same seed are the same kind. It also counts kinds, as in 「三種類|さんしゅるい」, placed after the thing.",
      "「類|るい」 is a group of the same kind, as in 「書類|しょるい」, documents, and 「人類|じんるい」, humankind, while 「種|しゅ」 is a type, as in 「一種|いっしゅ」, a sort of.",
      "As a counter, one, eight, and ten squeeze into 「いっしゅるい」, 「はっしゅるい」, and 「じゅっしゅるい」. Keep 「しゅ」 short, or it sounds like 「週|しゅう」.",
      "Praise a big selection with 「種類が多い|しゅるいがおおい」 and ask 「何種類ありますか|なんしゅるいありますか」. It means types, never kind-hearted, which is 「優しい|やさしい」."
    ],
    ja: "この{店|みせ}にはケーキが{三|さん}[種類|しゅるい]あります。",
    en: "This shop has three kinds of cake." },
  "はなし|話": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the noun form of 「話す|はなす」, to speak, from lesson three, and it uses the same native reading. The verb is talking. The noun is the talk itself, a conversation, a story, or the thing someone wants to discuss. Look at the kanji. The left side is the speech radical, 「言|げん」, which you know from 「言う|いう」, to say. It shows up on the left of many kanji about words. A way to remember the right side: it looks like the kanji for tongue. Words plus tongue, talking. You already know the Chinese-origin reading of this kanji from 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, which is literally electric talk. The noun is everywhere. 「おもしろい話|おもしろいはなし」 is an interesting story. 「話がある|はなしがある」 means I have something to talk to you about, which can sound serious, so be ready when a boss says it. Old folk tales are called old stories using this same word. In this lesson you also meet 「話をする|はなしをする」, to have a talk, which is just this noun plus 「する」. A common mix-up for English speakers: this word means both story and talk, so the context decides. Recap: words plus a tongue, the noun form of 「話す|はなす」, means a talk or a story." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "When this word is joined to another word in front of it, its first sound often softens from 「は」 to 「ば」. Japanese does this to many words when they become the second half of a compound. Watch it happen. 「昔話|むかしばなし」 is an old tale, a folk story, from 「昔|むかし」, long ago. 「作り話|つくりばなし」 is a made-up story, from the verb to make. 「笑い話|わらいばなし」 is a funny story, from the verb to laugh. 「立ち話|たちばなし」 is a chat you have while standing, like running into a neighbor on the street. And 「世間話|せけんばなし」 is small talk about the weather and everyday news. In every one, the softened 「ばなし」 still means story or talk, and the first word tells you what kind. If you hear 「ばなし」 at the end of a word, think of this word in disguise. Learning to spot this softening will help you recognize many new words by ear. A soft 「ば」 at the end of a compound is just this word wearing a costume." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This little noun is the heart of many everyday expressions. 「話を聞く|はなしをきく」 means to listen to someone, often to hear someone out when they are upset. A good friend is someone who will listen to your story. 「話が合う|はなしがあう」, the talk fits, means two people click in conversation and have lots in common. 「話が長い|はなしがながい」 describes someone long-winded, like a principal at a school assembly. A sharp one is 「話が違う|はなしがちがう」, the story is different, which means that is not what you told me, said when a deal changes unfairly. And when you pass on news you heard, you can add 「という話です|というはなしです」 at the end, meaning so I hear. Here is a tiny scene. A friend says, I have something to talk to you about. You answer, sure, and they say, actually, the rent is going up. You frown and say, that is not what we agreed. Learn these chunks as whole phrases, and you will sound much more natural." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan has a famous traditional art built entirely on storytelling, called 「落語|らくご」. A single performer kneels on a cushion on a bare stage, wearing a kimono, and tells a long comic story. They play every character alone, just by turning their head left and right and changing their voice. Their only props are a folding fan and a small cloth towel. The fan can become chopsticks, a pipe, or a brush, and the towel can become a wallet or a letter. Many of these stories are hundreds of years old, and fans enjoy hearing the same ones told by different performers, the way people enjoy different singers covering the same song. Children's folk tales are another big part of storytelling culture. Nearly every Japanese child knows the story of 「桃太郎|ももたろう」, the boy born from a peach who fights ogres. These are called old stories, and grandparents traditionally tell them at bedtime. So whether it is a comedy on stage or a tale at bedtime, a good story has a special place in Japan." }
    ],
    short: [
      "This is the noun form of 「話す|はなす」, to speak, meaning a talk or a story. The kanji is the speech radical plus a tongue shape, the same kanji as in 「電話|でんわ」.",
      "As the second half of a compound it softens to 「ばなし」, as in 「昔話|むかしばなし」, an old tale, and 「立ち話|たちばなし」, a chat while standing.",
      "Learn it in chunks: 「話を聞く|はなしをきく」 is to hear someone out, 「話が合う|はなしがあう」 is to click, and 「話が違う|はなしがちがう」 means that is not what you told me.",
      "Japan's storytelling art 「落語|らくご」 has one kneeling performer playing every character, with only a fan and a towel, and children grow up on tales like 「桃太郎|ももたろう」."
    ],
    ja: "おじいさんの[話|はなし]はおもしろいです。",
    en: "My grandfather's stories are interesting." },
  "なやみ|悩み": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word uses the native reading, and it is the noun form of the verb for to be troubled or to agonize over something. It is a worry you carry around and keep thinking about, a personal problem. Look at the kanji. The left side is the heart radical, the narrow standing form of 「心|しん」, heart. You have seen the same left side in 「忙しい|いそがしい」, busy. Many kanji about feelings carry this heart mark, so when you see it, think emotions. The okurigana 「み」 at the end turns the verb into a noun, the same way other verbs become nouns. Compare it with a word you know from lesson twelve, 「心配|しんぱい」. That one is anxiety about something that might happen, like worrying whether your friend got home safely. This word is deeper and more personal. It is a trouble that weighs on you, like problems with love, work, or family. Typical patterns: 「悩みがある|なやみがある」, I have a worry, and asking a friend if something is bothering them with 「何か悩みがありますか|なにかなやみがありますか」. Magazines in Japan often have advice columns built around readers' worries. A way to remember it: a weight on the heart side of the kanji. Recap: a heart-marked kanji, a worry or trouble you keep turning over in your mind." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb behind this noun is 「悩む|なやむ」, to be troubled or to agonize, and it is a regular u-verb. So the polite form is 「悩みます|なやみます」, and the 「て」 form is 「悩んで|なやんで」, just like 「読む|よむ」 becomes 「読んで|よんで」. To say someone is troubled right now, use the 「ている」 form: 「悩んでいる|なやんでいる」. The thing you are troubled about usually takes 「で」 or 「について」, as in worried about work or troubled about the future. Now look at how the noun is made. Take the polite stem, drop 「ます」, and you have a noun. You already know several nouns made this way. 「休み|やすみ」, a holiday, comes from 「休む|やすむ」. 「楽しみ|たのしみ」, something to look forward to, comes from 「楽しむ|たのしむ」. 「頼み|たのみ」, a request or a favor, comes from 「頼む|たのむ」. Our word follows the exact same recipe. This trick lets you guess many nouns from verbs you know. Verb stem, no 「ます」, and the action becomes a thing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Talking about worries is a big part of friendship in Japan, and this word shows up in a few set ways. When you share a worry with someone to get advice, you use 「相談|そうだん」, consultation, from lesson fourteen. You say 「悩みを相談する|なやみをそうだんする」, to talk over a worry with someone. Advice columns and radio shows are often called worry consultations. A gentle opener is 「実は|じつは」, actually, before you explain your problem. Here is a small dialogue. One friend asks, you look down, is something wrong? The other says, actually, I have a little worry. Then the first friend says, I will listen, and the talk begins. There is also a fun, slightly teasing phrase, 「贅沢な悩み|ぜいたくななやみ」, a luxurious worry. You say it when someone complains about a problem that is really a nice one to have, like having too many job offers. You might also hear 「悩みがなさそう|なやみがなさそう」, you look like you have no worries at all, said to a cheerful person. So the word lives in heart-to-heart talks and in friendly teasing alike." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the sound. Picture your friend sitting at a famous ramen shop, the kind people line up for hours to get into. A steaming bowl arrives. Everyone around them is slurping happily. But your friend just stares at the noodles, sighs, and mutters, nah, yummy? Not today. They push the bowl away. That is what a real worry does. It takes the taste out of even the best food. Nah, yummy, said with a sigh, gives you the sound 「なやみ」. Now add the kanji. Its left side is the heart mark, so picture your friend with one hand pressed to their chest as they sigh over the bowl. When you hear the word, replay the scene: the beautiful ramen, the sigh, the hand on the heart. And to finish the scene, you lean over and ask, what is on your mind? A worry so heavy that even ramen loses its flavor." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the heart mark on the left, so think emotions. It is a deep, personal worry that weighs on you, unlike 「心配|しんぱい」, anxiety about what might happen.",
      "It comes from the u-verb 「悩む|なやむ」, to be troubled. Drop 「ます」 from the stem and you get a noun, just like 「休み|やすみ」 and 「楽しみ|たのしみ」.",
      "You talk over a worry with 「悩みを相談する|なやみをそうだんする」, often opening with 「実は|じつは」. A nice problem to have is teasingly called 「贅沢な悩み|ぜいたくななやみ」.",
      "Picture your friend at a famous ramen shop, hand on their heart, sighing nah, yummy, not today, because a worry so heavy that even ramen loses its flavor gives 「なやみ」."
    ],
    ja: "なにか[悩み|なやみ]がありますか。",
    en: "Do you have something worrying you?" },
  "まちがい|間違い": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings, and you know both. The first, 「間|ま」, shows a sun peeking through the gap in a double door, and it means the space or interval between things. You know it with other readings in 「時間|じかん」, time, and 「この間|このあいだ」, the other day, literally this interval. The second kanji, 「違|ちが」, means to differ, and you met it in 「違い|ちがい」, difference, in lesson seventeen. Put them together. A way to think of it: the gap, or the timing, comes out different from what it should be. That gives a mistake or an error. The verb version is to make a mistake, and this noun is the result. You use it for wrong answers on a test, a typo, getting on the wrong train, or a misunderstanding. A handy pattern is to say something has mistakes in it, using 「間違いがある|まちがいがある」. One useful phrase: 「間違いない|まちがいない」, no mistake, means definitely or for sure. A common mix-up for learners: 「違う|ちがう」 by itself means different or wrong in conversation, while this noun names the error itself. Recap: the interval comes out different, and you get a mistake." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This noun has two verb relatives, and the difference matters. The first is 「間違える|まちがえる」, a ru-verb that means to make a mistake about something, with the thing taking 「を」. You can mix up the road, 「道を間違える|みちをまちがえる」, or get an answer wrong, 「答えを間違えました|こたえをまちがえました」. The second is 「間違う|まちがう」, a u-verb, which you mostly hear in the 「ている」 form, 「間違っている|まちがっている」, meaning something is wrong. For example, this answer is wrong. So one verb is about what a person does, and the other describes the state of the thing. There is also a very handy 「て」 form use. 「間違って|まちがって」 at the start of a sentence means by mistake. You might say you took your friend's umbrella home by mistake. Finally, the negative of the noun phrase, 「間違いない|まちがいない」, means there is no mistake, so definitely. Its adverb, 「間違いなく|まちがいなく」, means without fail. Make a mistake, be wrong, by mistake, and definitely: four uses from one family." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Mistakes happen everywhere, so this word has lots of handy combinations. A wrong number on the phone is 「間違い電話|まちがいでんわ」, mistake phone call. If you dial wrong, you just say 「すみません、間違えました|すみません、まちがえました」, sorry, I got it wrong, and hang up. When you mishear someone, that is 「聞き間違い|ききまちがい」, a listening mistake, and a typo or slip of the pen is 「書き間違い|かきまちがい」. Notice the pattern: a verb stem in front tells you what kind of mistake it was. To fix mistakes, you use 「直す|なおす」, as in please correct my mistakes, a perfect phrase for a language exchange partner. Teachers mark mistakes on tests in red, and in Japan a right answer gets a circle, while a wrong answer often gets a check mark or a slash. That surprises many English speakers, who expect a check mark to mean correct. So when you get a test back in Japan, look for circles, not checks. Mistakes on the phone, in your ears, on paper, all with this one word." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The first kanji, 「間|ま」, has more readings and relatives than almost any other kanji you know. With the reading 「ま」, it means a space or a gap in time. 「間に合う|まにあう」 means to be in time, literally to fit into the gap, as in making the last train. 「居間|いま」 is a living room, the space where you stay. 「仲間|なかま」 means companions or teammates, the people in your circle. With the reading 「あいだ」, it means between, as in between the station and the bank. With the reading 「かん」, it shows up in 「時間|じかん」, time, and 「一週間|いっしゅうかん」, one week. And with 「げん」, you get 「人間|にんげん」, a human being, the person in the space between others. In every case, the kanji keeps its core idea of a space or interval, whether it is a room, a stretch of time, or the gap between people. That makes it a great kanji to collect words for. Space, time, and people, all inside a sun peeking through a gate." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「間|ま」 is a gap or interval and 「違|ちが」 is to differ, so the gap comes out different and you get a mistake. 「間違いない|まちがいない」 means definitely.",
      "「間違える|まちがえる」 is making a mistake about something with 「を」, while 「間違っている|まちがっている」 says something is wrong. 「間違って|まちがって」 means by mistake.",
      "A verb stem in front names the mistake, as in 「聞き間違い|ききまちがい」, mishearing, and 「間違い電話|まちがいでんわ」 is a wrong number. On Japanese tests, circles mean correct.",
      "The kanji 「間|ま」 always keeps its core idea of a space or interval, whether a room in 「居間|いま」, time in 「時間|じかん」, or people in 「人間|にんげん」."
    ],
    ja: "テストに[間違い|まちがい]がみっつありました。",
    en: "There were three mistakes on the test." },
  "せいかく|性格": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「性|せい」, is built from two parts you know. On the left is the heart mark, the narrow form of 「心|しん」. On the right is 「生|せい」, life or birth, from 「学生|がくせい」 and 「生まれる|うまれる」, to be born. Heart plus birth: the nature you were born with. That is the core meaning, nature or inborn character. The second kanji, 「格|かく」, means a standard, a framework, or a rank. Its left side is the tree mark. Together, the two describe the built-in shape of your nature, which is personality. The usual pattern is someone's personality is good, 「性格がいい|せいかくがいい」, meaning a nice person, or is bad, 「性格が悪い|せいかくがわるい」, meaning not a nice person. A common mistake English speakers make is to use this for looks. Japanese splits it clearly. Looks and attractiveness go with words like 「かっこいい」 or 「かわいい」, while personality is about how someone acts and treats others. This lesson gives you some personality words to use with it, like 「まじめ」, serious, 「怠け者|なまけもの」, lazy person, and 「恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや」, shy person. Recap: the heart you were born with, in a fixed frame, is your personality." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Each of these two kanji opens up its own family. The first, 「性|せい」, nature, appears in the words for man and woman, 「男性|だんせい」 and 「女性|じょせい」, a man's nature and a woman's nature. These are the polite words you see on forms and restroom signs. It also works like the English ending -ity or -ness. 「可能性|かのうせい」 means possibility, and 「安全性|あんぜんせい」 means safety, as a quality something has. The second kanji, 「格|かく」, frame or standard, appears in 「合格|ごうかく」, passing an exam, meeting the standard. It also shows up in 「価格|かかく」, price, on store tags. And here is a fun one. The word 「かっこいい」, cool, comes from 「格好|かっこう」, appearance or style, plus 「いい」. Over time it shrank into the word every teen uses. So the 「格|かく」 in personality is a cousin of the 「かっこ」 in cool. Nature on one side, frame on the other, and both kanji have big families." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "To talk about personality, you need adjectives to put with this word. The most common ones you know already. 「明るい|あかるい」, bright, describes a cheerful, outgoing person. Its opposite, 「暗い|くらい」, dark, means gloomy or withdrawn, and it can sting, so be careful. 「優しい|やさしい」 means kind and gentle, maybe the most common compliment of all. The pattern is simple. Put the person, then 「は」, then this word, then 「が」, then the adjective. My sister's personality is bright, meaning she is cheerful. You can also say two people's personalities fit, 「性格が合う|せいかくがあう」, meaning they are compatible, or that they do not fit. A common question in dating and roommate talk is, what kind of personality do you have? People often answer with a little humility, like, I am a bit shy. Here is a tiny exchange. One person asks, what is your new boss like? The other says, strict, but her personality is kind. Practice it with people you know, and the adjectives will stick." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, a popular way to guess someone's personality is by their blood type. The question 「何型ですか|なにがたですか」, what type are you, comes up in casual chats, on TV, and even in magazine horoscopes. According to the popular belief, type A people are careful and organized, type B people are free spirits and a bit selfish, type O people are confident leaders, and type AB people are a mysterious mix. Blood type itself is 「血液型|けつえきがた」. There is no scientific evidence that blood type shapes personality, and many Japanese people treat it as a fun game, much like star signs in English-speaking countries. Still, it is common enough that you should be ready for the question. If you do not know your type, that alone can surprise people, since many Japanese know theirs. A good answer if you want to play along: I am type A, so I am serious, ha ha. Knowing this custom gives you an easy small-talk topic that almost always gets a laugh." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「性|せい」 is heart plus birth, the nature you were born with, and 「格|かく」 is a frame or standard, so together they are your personality, never your looks.",
      "「性|せい」 appears in 「男性|だんせい」 and 「可能性|かのうせい」, and 「格|かく」 in 「合格|ごうかく」. Even 「かっこいい」 comes from 「格好|かっこう」, a cousin of this frame kanji.",
      "Pair it with adjectives like 「明るい|あかるい」 for cheerful or 「優しい|やさしい」 for kind, as in her personality is kind, and say people are compatible with 「性格が合う|せいかくがあう」.",
      "Many Japanese people playfully guess personality from blood type, 「血液型|けつえきがた」, so be ready for 「何型ですか|なにがたですか」, even though there is no scientific evidence behind it."
    ],
    ja: "{姉|あね}は[性格|せいかく]がいいです。",
    en: "My older sister has a nice personality." },
  "なまけもの|怠け者": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is the verb to be lazy or to slack off, plus a word for person. The first kanji, 「怠|なま」, uses its native reading. Look at the bottom: it is 「心|しん」, heart, one more feelings kanji, a heart that does not want to move. The okurigana 「け」 follows, from the verb for slacking off. The last kanji, 「者|もの」, means person, and here it uses its native reading. You know its Chinese-origin reading from 「医者|いしゃ」, doctor, and 「研究者|けんきゅうしゃ」, researcher. Those are people defined by their job. This word is a person defined by a habit, so it is a label, and not a nice one. Calling someone this is a criticism, so use it about yourself as a joke, or about a little brother, but not to your boss. A fun memory hook: the animal called a sloth in English is called by this same word in Japanese, usually written in katakana, 「ナマケモノ」. Picture a sloth hanging from a tree, too lazy to move, and you have the word. A common learner error is to treat it like an adjective. It is a noun, so you say someone is a lazy person with 「です」, not that they are lazy. Recap: a person with a heart that slacks off, a lazy person, and also the sloth." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The ending 「者|もの」, person, makes a whole set of words that label people by a trait. The best one to learn alongside this word is its opposite, 「働き者|はたらきもの」, a hard worker, from 「働く|はたらく」, to work. Lazy person versus working person: a perfect pair. 「人気者|にんきもの」 is someone popular, a person with 「人気|にんき」, popularity, the star of the class. 「若者|わかもの」 means young people, from 「若い|わかい」, young. You will see it in news stories about youth trends. It can also sound humble. In polite speech, 「家の者|いえのもの」 means someone in my household. Notice the pattern in most of these: a verb stem or an adjective stem, then 「者|もの」. Compare 「屋|や」 in shy person, which is softer and more playful. 「者|もの」 is more of a straight label. So you have lazy and hardworking, popular and young. Pick one, add 「者|もの」, and you have described a person." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb inside this word is 「怠ける|なまける」, to slack off or to be lazy, and it is a ru-verb, so it conjugates like 「食べる|たべる」. The polite form is 「怠けます|なまけます」, and the 「て」 form is 「怠けて|なまけて」. What you slack off on takes 「を」, as in slacking off on work or on homework. To say someone is goofing off right now, use the 「ている」 form, they are slacking off. A teacher might say 「怠けないでください|なまけないでください」, please do not slack off, using the negative request from lesson eight. The noun, then, is just the stem 「怠け|なまけ」 plus person. Here is a contrast worth knowing. 「休む|やすむ」 is to rest or take a break, which is neutral and often good. This verb always carries blame. Resting is fine, slacking is not. So if you take a nap on your day off, you are resting. If you nap when you should be working, someone might call it slacking. Same nap, different verb, depending on whether you should be doing something else." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Calling someone this word is pretty direct, so Japanese speakers often reach for softer ways to describe a relaxed person. 「のんびりしている」 means laid-back or easygoing, and it can even be a compliment. A person who takes life slowly in the countryside is described this way. 「マイペース」, a made-in-Japan phrase from English words meaning my pace, describes someone who does things at their own speed, ignoring the rush. And for a lazy day, Japanese uses sound words. 「ごろごろする」 is lying around at home, rolling about on the floor. 「だらだらする」 is dragging along, doing things in a sluggish way. So you could say, on Sunday I just lay around at home all day, and nobody would think you were insulting yourself. Our word, by contrast, is a judgment about who you are. A playful way to use it is about yourself, with a laugh: I am such a lazy person. So choose your word by how kind you want to be. Laid-back is gentle, lying around is a description, and this word is a verdict." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The verb for slacking off has a heart at the bottom of its kanji, a heart that will not move, plus 「者|もの」, person. It is a critical label, and it also means sloth.",
      "Its opposite is 「働き者|はたらきもの」, a hard worker. The same ending gives 「人気者|にんきもの」, a popular person, and 「若者|わかもの」, young people.",
      "The verb 「怠ける|なまける」 is a ru-verb that always carries blame, unlike neutral 「休む|やすむ」, to rest. Same nap, different verb, depending on whether you should be working.",
      "Softer options are 「のんびりしている」, laid-back, 「マイペース」, at your own pace, and 「ごろごろする」 for lying around. This word is a verdict, so joke about yourself with it."
    ],
    ja: "{弟|おとうと}は[怠け者|なまけもの]です。",
    en: "My younger brother is a lazy person." },
  "はずかしがりや|恥ずかしがり屋": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This word is built from three pieces, and you know two of them. The first is 「恥ずかしい|はずかしい」, embarrassed or embarrassing, from lesson eighteen. Its kanji has 「耳|みみ」, ear, on the left and 「心|しん」, heart, on the right. A way to remember it: when your heart feels embarrassed, your ears turn red. Next comes 「がる」, a suffix that means to show signs of a feeling. Japanese often describes other people's feelings with it, because you can see the signs but not the feeling itself. Someone who acts embarrassed easily is described with it. Last is 「屋|や」. You know it from 「本屋|ほんや」, bookstore, and 「部屋|へや」, room. It means shop or building, and the person who runs the shop. From that, it turned into a suffix for a person with a certain strong habit or nature. So this word is a person who easily shows embarrassment, which is a shy person. The same 「屋|や」 pattern gives other personality words, like someone who tries hard or someone who gets lonely easily. Unlike 「怠け者|なまけもの」, this label is gentle, even affectionate. It is fine to say about yourself or a friend. Recap: feeling embarrassed, showing it, and being that kind of person, gives a shy person." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The middle piece, 「がる」, is a little suffix with a big job. Japanese is careful about feelings. You can say I am cold, 「寒い|さむい」, or I want it, 「ほしい」, about yourself, because you feel it. But you cannot see inside another person. So for someone else, you add 「がる」 to the adjective stem, and it means they show signs of that feeling. 「寒がる|さむがる」 is to act cold. 「ほしがる」 is to act like you want something, as in my little brother wants a new game. 「いやがる」 is to act reluctant. And 「恥ずかしがる|はずかしがる」 is to act embarrassed. These are regular u-verbs, and for a current state you use the 「ている」 form, as in the child is acting shy. Then take the stem, 「恥ずかしがり|はずかしがり」, and you have a noun for that habit. Add 「屋|や」, and it becomes a person. So the whole word is built in layers: feeling, showing the feeling, the habit, the person. Once you see the layers, the long word stops looking long." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Here is the fun set of 「屋|や」 personality words this one belongs to. 「頑張り屋|がんばりや」, from 「頑張る|がんばる」, is a hard worker, the person who never gives up. Teachers say it with real praise. 「さびしがり屋|さびしがりや」 is someone who gets lonely easily, from 「さびしい」, lonely, plus the same 「がる」 you see in our word. 「寒がり|さむがり」 is someone who always feels cold, the friend who wears a sweater in June, and its opposite is 「暑がり|あつがり」. These two often drop 「屋|や」, though you can add it. 「照れ屋|てれや」 comes from 「照れる|てれる」, to feel bashful, especially when praised. That one is very close to our word. A nice difference: a shy person in our sense is shy in many situations, while 「照れ屋|てれや」 is someone who blushes at compliments. Notice how most of these are affectionate, the kind of thing you say with a smile. That warmth is part of what 「屋|や」 brings." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When Japanese people describe shyness, they often reach for a second word too, 「人見知り|ひとみしり」. It is built from 「人|ひと」, person, 「見る|みる」, to see, and 「知る|しる」, to know. It means being shy with strangers, uneasy around people you do not know yet. Babies who cry when a stranger holds them are described this way, and so are adults who go quiet at parties. A very common self-introduction line is I am a bit shy with new people, but please be patient with me. Our word is broader. It is a general shy nature, including blushing when speaking in front of the class. Another useful word is 「内気|うちき」, introverted or timid, which is a little more serious. In conversation, people soften it. You might hear a mother say about her child, she is shy, so she will not say hello, and then laugh. If someone calls you this, do not take offense. It is usually said kindly, almost as a cute trait. So, in Japan shyness is often treated gently, as a charm rather than a flaw." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「恥ずかしい|はずかしい」 is embarrassed, 「がる」 means showing signs of a feeling, and 「屋|や」 marks a person with that nature, so it is someone who easily shows embarrassment, a gentle label.",
      "For other people's feelings you add 「がる」, as in 「寒がる|さむがる」, to act cold. The stem 「恥ずかしがり|はずかしがり」 names the habit, and 「屋|や」 turns it into a person.",
      "It belongs to a warm set of 「屋|や」 words: 「頑張り屋|がんばりや」, a hard worker, 「さびしがり屋|さびしがりや」, someone easily lonely, and 「照れ屋|てれや」, someone who blushes at praise.",
      "Japanese also says 「人見知り|ひとみしり」 for shyness with strangers, while this word is a broader shy nature. It is usually said kindly, as a charm rather than a flaw."
    ],
    ja: "{妹|いもうと}は[恥ずかしがり屋|はずかしがりや]です。",
    en: "My younger sister is shy." },
  "なかがいい|仲がいい": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a whole little phrase: the relationship is good. The key kanji, 「仲|なか」, uses its native reading. It is built from two parts. On the left is the person mark, a narrow standing form of 「人|ひと」. On the right is 「中|なか」, middle or inside. Person plus middle: what goes on in the middle, between people. That is the relationship. Notice the sound is the same as 「中|なか」, inside, which makes it easy to remember. Then comes 「が」 and 「いい」, good. So the relationship is good, which means two people get along well or are close. The usual pattern puts both people first, joined with 「と」: A and B have a good relationship. To say they do not get along, swap in 「悪い|わるい」, bad. A common mistake English speakers make is to try to use it like a verb meaning to get along with someone, with an object. It is really an adjective phrase about the relationship, so it conjugates like 「いい」. In the past, it becomes 「仲がよかった|なかがよかった」, not 「いかった」. You will also hear 「仲良く|なかよく」, getting along, as in parents telling kids to play nicely together. Recap: the space between people is good, and that means close friends." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「仲|なか」 shows up in several everyday words about people being together. 「仲良し|なかよし」 means good friends, or a close pair. Children say my best buddy with it, and it is also used about couples or siblings. 「仲良く|なかよく」 means in a friendly way, and 「仲良くなる|なかよくなる」 means to become friends, which is what you hope for with a new classmate. 「仲間|なかま」 means companions or teammates, a group on the same side, like your band, your club, or your fellow workers. The 「間|ま」 here is the kanji for space or between. And 「仲直り|なかなおり」 means making up after a fight. It uses 「直る|なおる」, to be fixed. So after 「けんか」, a quarrel, comes this, the relationship gets repaired. You can see the story of a friendship in this one family. Meet, become close, have a fight, make up, and become close again." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This phrase has a hidden structure worth knowing. It is really a small sentence: the relationship, 「が」, is good. When you use it to describe a noun, the 「が」 often changes to 「の」. So a close friend can be 「仲のいい友だち|なかのいいともだち」. Both versions are correct, but 「の」 sounds very natural here. To name the other person, use 「と」. You get along well with your sister, 「妹と仲がいい|いもうととなかがいい」. To ask a question, you can say are you two close? Because 「いい」 is irregular, its forms come from 「よい」. The negative is 「仲がよくない|なかがよくない」, they are not on good terms. To become close, use the adverb form with 「なる」, which gives 「仲良くなる|なかよくなる」. Notice the kanji 「良|よ」 shows up there. Many people also write the adjective part as 「仲が良い|なかがよい」 in more formal writing. So, keep 「いい」 for speaking, and remember that its other forms all come from 「よい」." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick built on the kanji. 「仲|なか」 is the person mark beside 「中|なか」, middle. Picture two old friends sitting on a park bench. Between them, right in the middle, sits a big bag of snacks. That snack bag is the relationship. When the two friends are close, they both reach into the middle and share happily. The middle is good. That is the phrase. Now picture two people who are not getting along. They sit at opposite ends of the bench, and the snack bag in the middle sits there untouched. The middle is bad, 「仲が悪い|なかがわるい」. Every time you see the kanji, find the little person standing next to the middle, guarding what is between people. And remember the sound: the relationship is literally the middle, read the same way. So when you hear this phrase, imagine that shared snack bag sitting happily between two friends." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「仲|なか」 is the person mark beside 「中|なか」, middle, so it is what lies between people, the relationship. The relationship is good means two people are close.",
      "The kanji tells a friendship story: 「仲良くなる|なかよくなる」, become friends, 「仲間|なかま」, companions, then 「仲直り|なかなおり」, making up after a fight.",
      "Before a noun, 「が」 often becomes 「の」, as in 「仲のいい友だち|なかのいいともだち」. Name the other person with 「と」, and remember the other forms come from 「よい」.",
      "Picture two friends on a bench sharing a snack bag in the middle. When the middle is good, they are close, and when it sits untouched, it is 「仲が悪い|なかがわるい」."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}と{私|わたし}は[仲がいい|なかがいい]です。",
    en: "My older brother and I get along well." },
  "まじめ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a native Japanese word, usually written in kana at this level. When it is written with kanji, it uses three characters meaning true, face, and eye, 「真面目|まじめ」. A way to remember it: a true, straight face, the face of someone who is not joking around. It is a 「な」 adjective, so you say a serious student with 「まじめな学生|まじめながくせい」, and someone is serious with 「です」. The meaning is wider than the English word serious. It covers being earnest, hard-working, honest, and reliable. The kind of person who does all the homework, shows up on time, and follows the rules. In Japan, calling someone this is mostly a compliment. A boss would be happy to hear a new employee described this way. A common mistake for English speakers is to think it means gloomy or grim. It does not. It is about attitude, not mood. Sometimes, though, friends tease someone for being too serious, a bit stiff and no fun. You can also say 「まじめに」 as an adverb, meaning to do something seriously, not half-heartedly. It fits right in with this lesson's personality words. It is the opposite of 「怠け者|なまけもの」, the lazy person. Recap: a true straight face, the earnest, diligent person who takes things seriously." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When this word is written in kanji, it uses a special reading. Each character takes a sound that fits only in this word. The first is 「真|ま」, true or genuine. You will also meet it in 「真ん中|まんなか」, the very middle, and 「真夜中|まよなか」, the dead of night, where it means exactly or right in. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「しん」, as in 「写真|しゃしん」, photo, literally copying the truth. The second is 「面|めん」, face or surface, also a mask. Here is a surprise: you already know it from 「面白い|おもしろい」, interesting. The third is 「目|め」, eye, one of the first kanji you learned. Where does the word come from? The origin is not certain. One idea links it to staring with fixed eyes, but scholars debate it, so treat that as a guess. What is clear is that the kanji were chosen to fit the meaning: a true face, with eyes that mean it. Simple kana is completely fine for now." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word turns up in a lot of everyday phrases. 「まじめに」 means seriously, and a friend might say please answer seriously when you keep joking. 「まじめな話|まじめなはなし」 means a serious talk, and people use it to switch the mood: okay, serious talk now. To call someone too earnest, add 「すぎる」, as in he is too serious. The opposite is 「ふまじめ」, not serious or irresponsible, with the negative prefix in front. And in casual speech, young people shorten it to 「マジ」, written in katakana. 「マジで」 means really? or seriously?, used for surprise, like the English no way. It comes from the same word, just clipped. So the full word describes a person, and the short form is a reaction. A teacher praises a student with the full word. Two friends hearing gossip say the short one. Remember both, and you will understand a big slice of daily conversation." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japan, this quality is one of the most valued traits in school and at work. A report card comment, a job reference, or a matchmaker's description may all praise someone for it. It suggests a person who keeps promises, shows up on time, follows rules, and finishes tasks without being told. Think of the word in a job interview. Saying you are this kind of person is a safe, strong thing to say about yourself. On the other hand, there is a gentle teasing side. A person who is very earnest may be called stiff, the one who never relaxes at a party. There is even a slang phrase, 「くそまじめ」, a rude but common way to say ridiculously serious. So the trait is respected, but people also like it balanced with some fun. In this lesson's list of personality words, it is the one most likely to get you hired, though maybe not the one that makes you the life of the party." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Written 「真面目|まじめ」, true face eye, it is a straight, earnest face. It means diligent, honest, and reliable, a compliment about attitude, not a gloomy mood.",
      "Each kanji takes a special reading here: 「真|ま」 is true, as in 「真ん中|まんなか」, 「面|めん」 is face, as in 「面白い|おもしろい」, and 「目|め」 is eye.",
      "「まじめに」 means seriously and 「まじめな話|まじめなはなし」 is a serious talk, while young people clip it to 「マジ」, so 「マジで」 means really or no way.",
      "In Japan it is a top trait in school and work, safe to claim in a job interview, though someone too earnest may be teased as 「くそまじめ」."
    ],
    ja: "{田中|たなか}さんはとても[まじめ]です。",
    en: "Mr. Tanaka is very diligent." },
  "いらっしゃる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is one of the busiest verbs in 「敬語|けいご」, because it replaces three plain verbs at once: 「行く|いく」, to go, 「来る|くる」, to come, and 「いる」, to be somewhere. It is usually written in kana. Who uses it? You use it when the subject of the sentence is someone you respect, like a teacher, a boss, a customer, or an older stranger. It raises them up. You never use it about yourself or your own family. So you would say your professor is in the office, or the president is coming tomorrow, with this verb. Context tells you which of the three meanings is meant. You already know a piece of it. Every shop in Japan greets you with 「いらっしゃいませ」, welcome, which comes from this very verb. The shop is politely saying you have come. Now the tricky part. Its polite form is irregular. It is 「いらっしゃいます」, with 「い」 in the middle, not the 「り」 you would expect. The same twist happens to a few other honorific verbs in this lesson, like 「なさる」 and 「おっしゃる」. A way to remember it: the welcome you hear at every shop door is your built-in model. Recap: the honorific for go, come, and be, used only to lift up someone else." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This verb has a long history. It is usually traced back to an old respectful form of the verb 「入る|いる」, to enter, the same kanji you know from 「入る|はいる」. In older Japanese, adding respectful endings to verbs was very common, and over the centuries one of those long forms got worn down and squeezed into the shape we use today. That is why it looks so different from the plain verbs it replaces. It is not a polite version of 「行く|いく」 or 「来る|くる」 at all. It started as a respectful way of saying someone enters or is present, and later spread to cover go and come as well. You can still feel the enter meaning in the greeting at every shop, welcome, please come in. Knowing the history also explains the odd polite form. The squeezing left a few irregular corners behind. So the verb looks strange because it is old and well used, worn smooth like a stone in a river." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Let's go beyond the polite form. This is a u-verb, so most of its forms are regular. The 「て」 form is 「いらっしゃって」, and the past is 「いらっしゃった」. To invite someone politely to come, you say 「いらっしゃってください」. In everyday speech you will also hear a shorter version, 「いらしてください」, which comes from a short form of the verb. Both are common. Now the command form. It is 「いらっしゃい」, ending in 「い」, just like 「ください」 from 「くださる」. That is the casual welcome a small shop owner calls out, and also what a parent says when calling a child over, come here. Add 「ませ」 and it becomes the very polite store greeting. So the shop greeting is literally the command form, made extra polite. For the three meanings, context decides. If a place is marked with 「に」 and the person is moving, it is go or come. If the person is just there, it is be. So the forms are mostly regular, and the irregular corners are ones you hear every day." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how this verb sounds in a real exchange. A student knocks on a professor's office door and asks a secretary whether the professor is in. The polite question is, is the professor here?, using this verb in place of 「いる」. The secretary might answer, no, she is not in today. Notice that the secretary, talking about her own colleague to an outsider, would often switch to humble speech instead. That is a rule you will meet more in lesson twenty: inside and outside groups. Another common use is welcoming someone who came a long way. Hosts say 「ようこそいらっしゃいました」, welcome, we are so glad you came. Hotels, ryokan, and homes all use it. You will also hear it on the phone: will Mr. Tanaka be coming tomorrow? A common mistake is saying it about yourself, like I will come tomorrow. That sounds like you are honoring yourself. For your own movement, just use the normal verb in polite form. Put simply, this verb is for other people's comings, goings, and being there." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for 「行く|いく」, 「来る|くる」, and 「いる」, used only to lift someone else. Its polite form is irregular, 「いらっしゃいます」, as in the shop greeting 「いらっしゃいませ」.",
      "It traces back to an old respectful form of 「入る|いる」, to enter, worn down over centuries. That history explains its odd shape and its irregular polite form.",
      "It is a u-verb with regular forms like 「いらっしゃって」, but its command is 「いらっしゃい」, and adding 「ませ」 gives the polite store greeting. Context decides go, come, or be.",
      "Use it for respected people, as in 「ようこそいらっしゃいました」 to welcome a guest. Saying it about your own coming or going sounds like you are honoring yourself."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}は{明日|あした}{大学|だいがく}に[いらっしゃいます|いらっしゃいます|いらっしゃる]。",
    en: "The professor will come to the university tomorrow." },
  "めしあがる|召し上がる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific for two plain verbs, 「食べる|たべる」, to eat, and 「飲む|のむ」, to drink. Both kanji use native readings. The first, 「召|め」, has a mouth 「口|くち」 at the bottom. Its basic meaning is to summon or call, and in old court language it was a respectful verb for things noble people did, like eating, wearing, or calling for things. The second part is 「上がる|あがる」, to rise, with 「上|うえ」, up, the kanji you know from 「上手|じょうず」 and 「年上|としうえ」. Together they make a very elegant verb. Who uses it? You use it when someone you respect is the one eating or drinking. A host to a guest, a waiter to a customer, or a student to a teacher. The classic phrase is 「どうぞ召し上がってください|どうぞめしあがってください」, please go ahead and eat, said when you serve food to a guest. A common mistake is mixing it up with 「いただきます」. That phrase is the humble side. You say it about your own eating. You never say this verb about yourself. A way to remember it: picture food being called up and lifted to an honored guest. Recap: the honorific for eat and drink, used to offer food to people you respect." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this verb a lot, even as a beginner, especially at fast food places and cafes. The cashier often asks, will you eat it in the shop?, which is 「店内でお召し上がりですか|てんないでおめしあがりですか」. 「店内|てんない」 means inside the shop. The other choice is takeout, 「お持ち帰り|おもちかえり」. So if you hear this verb at the counter, the question is for here or to go. On food packaging, you will see 「お早めにお召し上がりください|おはやめにおめしあがりください」, please eat it soon, which is a polite best before note. At a friend's house, the host says please eat, and you answer 「いただきます」. A subtle point: 「どうぞ召し上がってください|どうぞめしあがってください」 is perfect and common. Shops tend to prefer the 「お」 plus stem plus 「ください」 version, which sounds extra polished. So this verb lives at the counter, on the label, and at the dinner table." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Let's look at the pieces that make up this verb. The second part, 「上がる|あがる」, to go up, is the happening partner of 「上げる|あげる」, to raise. You already know 「あげる」 from giving gifts, which is giving upward to someone. The kanji 「上|うえ」 has many readings. 「上|うえ」 is above. 「上手|じょうず」 is skillful. 「上る|のぼる」 is to climb up. And in this verb, 「上|あ」 begins rise. The first part, 「召|め」, comes from 「召す|めす」, an elegant old verb meaning to summon, or for an honored person to eat, wear, or catch a cold. You still hear it in very polite phrases like a person catching a cold, or in a kimono shop, wearing a kimono. Now the triangle you need for eating. Plain is 「食べる|たべる」. Honorific, for the other person, is this verb. Humble, for yourself, is 「いただく」, which also means to receive. So there are three ways to talk about eating, depending on whose mouth the food is going into." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Good news first: unlike 「いらっしゃる」 and 「なさる」, this verb is completely regular. It is a u-verb ending in 「る」, so its polite form is 「召し上がります|めしあがります」, with 「り」, exactly as you would expect. The 「て」 form is 「召し上がって|めしあがって」, and the past is 「召し上がった|めしあがった」. You can use it with patterns you know. With 「たことがある」, a host might ask, have you ever eaten sushi?, honoring the guest. With the volitional, though, be careful. Volitional is about what you yourself will do, so it does not fit with an honorific verb. To offer, use 「ませんか」 instead: would you like to have some? And to ask what someone will drink, ask what will you have, using this verb in the polite present. It covers drinks too. Many learners forget that and use the honorific only for food. So this verb is regular in form, and it covers drinks as well as food." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for eating and drinking: 「召|め」 is summoning, as for nobles, plus 「上がる|あがる」, rise. Offer food with 「どうぞ召し上がってください|どうぞめしあがってください」, but never say it about yourself.",
      "At a counter, 「店内でお召し上がりですか|てんないでおめしあがりですか」 asks for here or to go, and packages say 「お早めにお召し上がりください|おはやめにおめしあがりください」, eat soon.",
      "Eating has three faces: plain 「食べる|たべる」, honorific 「召し上がる|めしあがる」 for the other person, and humble 「いただく」 for yourself.",
      "Unlike 「いらっしゃる」, it is fully regular, 「召し上がります|めしあがります」 with 「り」. Offer with 「ませんか」, not the volitional, and remember it covers drinks too."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}はなにを[召し上がりますか|めしあがりますか|めしあがる]。",
    en: "What will you have, Professor?" },
  "くださる|下さる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific for 「くれる」, to give to me or to my group. The kanji is 「下|くだ」, down, with its native reading, the same kanji as in 「下|した」, below. The image fits perfectly. A gift from someone above you comes down to you. Here is the fun part: you have been saying a form of this verb since your first weeks. 「ください」, please give me, is its polite command form. So every time you asked for something with 「ください」, you were already using honorific language. Who uses it? You use it when a person you respect gives something to you, or does something for you. For an object, the teacher gave me a book. For a favor, you add it after a 「て」 form, like the teacher explained it for me. That is the honorific version of the 「てくれる」 pattern. Never use it for yourself giving something. Its polite form is irregular, like the other verbs in this group. It is 「くださいます」, not 「くださります」. A common mistake: since 「くれる」 is only for gifts coming toward you, this verb is too. For gifts going out to others, you need a different verb. Recap: the gift that comes down from above, the honorific of 「くれる」, and the root of the 「ください」 you use every day." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The most useful thing to build with this verb is a very polite request. You know 「てください」, please do something. If you turn it into a negative question, 「てくださいませんか」, it becomes would you kindly do it for me? That is the form you use with a teacher, a boss, or a stranger. For example, would you kindly write a recommendation letter for me? This is one step softer than 「てくれませんか」, which is for friends. Another use is thanking. To thank someone for doing something, Japanese often puts the favor first. Thank you for coming, said to a guest, is built with this verb in the 「て」 form, then thank you. And the command form, 「ください」, has an irregular 「い」 ending, the same as 「いらっしゃい」 and 「なさい」. This small group of special verbs shares that quirk in both the polite form and the command. So one verb gives you the polite request, the polite thanks, and the everyday please." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb is one corner of the giving triangle, and each corner has a plain and a respectful form. When you give to someone, the plain verb is 「あげる」, and the humble version, for giving to someone above you, is 「さしあげる」. You will meet that in lesson twenty. When someone gives to you, the plain verb is 「くれる」, and the honorific is this verb. When you receive, the plain verb is 「もらう」, and the humble version is 「いただく」. So, the teacher gave me a book can be said two ways. With this verb, the teacher is the subject and is lifted up. With 「いただく」, I am the subject, receiving humbly from the teacher. Both are polite, and Japanese speakers switch between them freely. Notice the direction built into the kanji: 「下|くだ」, down, for things coming down to you. In the giving triangle, every arrow has a polite version, and this verb is the arrow pointing at you." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「下|した」 is one of the simplest pointing characters, a mark placed under a line to show below, the mirror image of 「上|うえ」, above. But it has a big set of readings. You know 「下|した」, below. In 「下手|へた」, unskillful, the reading is special and irregular. In 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, subway, it is 「か」, the Chinese-origin reading. In 「下りる|おりる」 it means to come down. In 「下ろす|おろす」, it means to take down or withdraw money. And 「下さる|くださる」 uses the native verb stem 「くだ」, which you also see in 「下る|くだる」, to go down, like going downhill or downstream. A tip for reading: in everyday writing, this verb and its request form are often written in kana. When you add 「ください」 after another verb as a request, it is normally written in kana. When it means give me a thing, you may see the kanji. So the same kanji covers subways, bad skills, and the polite please." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for 「くれる」, a gift coming down from above, with 「下|くだ」, down. The everyday 「ください」 is its command form, and the polite form is 「くださいます」.",
      "Turn 「てください」 into 「てくださいませんか」 for a very polite request, one step softer than 「てくれませんか」, perfect for teachers, bosses, and strangers.",
      "In the giving triangle, 「あげる」 has humble 「さしあげる」, 「もらう」 has humble 「いただく」, and 「くれる」 has this honorific, the arrow pointing at you.",
      "The kanji 「下|した」 has many readings, as in 「下手|へた」, 「地下鉄|ちかてつ」, and 「下りる|おりる」. After another verb, 「ください」 is normally written in kana."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}が{本|ほん}を[くださいました|くださいました|くださる]。",
    en: "My teacher gave me a book." },
  "なさる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific for 「する」, to do. Because 「する」 is in so many verbs, this one is just as flexible. Any 「する」 verb can take it instead, so 「勉強する|べんきょうする」 becomes study, said respectfully, and so on. Who uses it? Someone talking to or about a respected person who is the one doing the action. Shop staff, waiters, office workers about their boss, students about a teacher. A very common restaurant question is 「何になさいますか|なにになさいますか」, what will you have? It is the honorific version of using 「する」 to decide on an item, which you learned in lesson fifteen. Its polite form is irregular, just like 「いらっしゃる」. It is 「なさいます」, not 「なさります」. Here is a sound connection you already know. 「おやすみなさい」, good night, ends with a short command form of this very verb. It literally means please rest. Parents also use the same ending to give gentle orders to children, like eat or study. So you have been hearing it for a long time. A common mistake: never use it for your own actions. Saying you yourself do something with this verb sounds like you are bowing to yourself. Recap: the honorific of 「する」, from restaurant orders to the good night you already say." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This verb comes from an old verb, 「なす」, meaning to do, to make, or to accomplish, combined with a respectful ending from classical Japanese. Over time the two fused into the single verb we use today. You can still meet the old verb in proverbs and written Japanese. A famous saying, often quoted to students, means if you try, you can do it, and if you do not try, nothing gets done. It uses this older verb for doing. So even though this honorific looks nothing like 「する」, it has its own respectable ancestor with the very same meaning. That is a pattern with honorific verbs in general. They are often not polite twists on the plain verb, but entirely different old words that were reserved for important people. The irregular polite form and the command 「なさい」 are leftovers from that history. So when you use this verb, you are borrowing a respectful word for doing that has been around for centuries." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Two phrases with this verb are worth memorizing as whole chunks. The first is at a restaurant or shop: 「何になさいますか|なにになさいますか」, what will you have? You can answer with your choice plus 「にします」, using the plain humble side, not the honorific. The second is at a clinic or when someone looks unwell: 「どうなさいましたか」, what seems to be the matter? A doctor asks this as you sit down. It is the honorific version of what happened? Another common pattern adds 「ご」 to a Chinese-origin noun and then this verb. 「ご心配なさらないでください|ごしんぱいなさらないでください」 means please do not worry. And with names of actions, like a respected person getting married, you can say 「ご結婚なさる|ごけっこんなさる」. Finally, the short command 「なさい」 is what parents and teachers use for gentle orders. Go to bed, finish your homework. It is firm but not rude. So one verb covers ordering food, seeing a doctor, and being told to brush your teeth." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The verb 「する」 has three faces in polite Japanese. The plain one is 「する」 itself. The honorific, which lifts up the other person, is this verb. The humble one, which lowers yourself, is 「いたす」, and you will meet it in lesson twenty, as in 「いたします」, I will do it. Shop staff use both in the same breath. What will you have? uses this verb, and then I will bring it right away uses the humble one. Now compare this verb with the general honorific recipe of 「お」 plus verb stem plus 「になる」. That recipe works for most verbs, like waiting or reading. But for 「する」 verbs, the usual method is this verb, with 「ご」 in front of the noun. So waiting becomes 「お待ちになる|おまちになる」, while explaining becomes 「ご説明なさる|ごせつめいなさる」. Remember 「する」 as a family of three: plain for friends, this verb for respected people, and 「いたす」 for yourself." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for 「する」, so it works with any 「する」 verb, as in 「何になさいますか|なにになさいますか」. Its polite form is 「なさいます」, and 「おやすみなさい」 ends with its command form.",
      "It comes from the old verb 「なす」, to do or accomplish, fused with a classical respectful ending, so it is a separate old word rather than a twist on 「する」.",
      "Memorize 「何になさいますか|なにになさいますか」 for ordering and 「どうなさいましたか」 at a clinic. Parents use 「なさい」 for firm but gentle orders.",
      "「する」 has three faces: plain 「する」, honorific 「なさる」, and humble 「いたす」. For 「する」 nouns, the honorific is 「ご」 plus this verb, as in 「ご説明なさる|ごせつめいなさる」."
    ],
    ja: "{部長|ぶちょう}はゴルフを[なさいます|なさいます|なさる]。",
    en: "The department manager plays golf." },
  "おやすみになる|お休みになる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific for 「寝る|ねる」, to go to bed or sleep. Look at the kanji in the middle, 「休|やす」, rest, with its native reading. It is one of the friendliest kanji to remember: a person, the narrow mark on the left, leaning against a tree, 「木|き」, on the right. A person resting by a tree. You know it from 「休む|やすむ」, to rest, and 「休み|やすみ」, holiday. So instead of saying a respected person sleeps, Japanese says they rest, which is gentler. This verb also shows you the general honorific recipe. Take 「お」, add the verb stem, and finish with 「になる」, to become. Many verbs can be made honorific this way, like waiting or reading. For sleeping, the recipe uses the stem of the verb for resting instead. Who uses it? A host to a guest, a hotel worker to a customer, or a student about a teacher. A host might ask a guest when they usually go to bed, or tell them to sleep well. A common mistake: it does not mean taking a day off here. In this honorific frame it means sleeping. You already know its cousin, 「おやすみなさい」, good night. Recap: a person leaning on a tree, wrapped in 「お」 and 「になる」, is the polite way to say someone you respect goes to sleep." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is your model for the most useful honorific recipe in Japanese. Take 「お」, add a verb's stem, which is the masu form without 「ます」, then add 「になる」. Waiting becomes 「お待ちになる|おまちになる」. Going home becomes 「お帰りになる|おかえりになる」. Reading becomes 「お読みになる|およみになる」. Writing becomes 「お書きになる|おかきになる」. The 「になる」 part is then conjugated normally, so the past polite ending is 「になりました」. But there is a catch. The recipe does not work well when the stem is very short, like the one syllable stems of 「寝る|ねる」 or 「見る|みる」. Those verbs borrow a replacement instead. For sleeping, Japanese uses the stem of resting. For seeing, it uses 「ご覧|ごらん」. And some verbs, like going and coming, have their own special honorific, 「いらっしゃる」. So this verb is a perfect example of both the rule and the exception." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will hear this verb most in hospitality. At a traditional inn, staff may ask when you would like your futon laid out, or wish you a good night's rest. Their parting phrase is often 「ごゆっくりお休みください|ごゆっくりおやすみください」, please rest well. That is the request form of the recipe, 「お」 plus stem plus 「ください」, and it sounds warm and polished. A host might ask a guest, did you sleep well?, with the past form of this verb. The same phrase is also kind to say to someone who is sick or very tired. One more point: because the verb is literally the honorific of resting, it can also mean that a respected person is taking time off, depending on context. If a secretary says the manager is resting today, it can mean the manager is off work. Listen to the setting. At night in a hotel it is sleep. In an office, it may be a day off. Either way, it is the gentle way to talk about someone else's rest." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「休|やす」, the person leaning on a tree, appears in a whole family of words about breaks. 「休み|やすみ」 is a holiday, a day off, or a break. Add a season and you get 「夏休み|なつやすみ」, summer vacation. 「昼休み|ひるやすみ」 is the lunch break. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「きゅう」. 「休日|きゅうじつ」 means a day off, a holiday, a word you will see on shop signs and calendars. 「休講|きゅうこう」 is a cancelled class, the happiest word on a university notice board. And 「休憩|きゅうけい」 is a short break, like during a long meeting or at a highway rest stop. All of these share the idea of stopping for a while. Our honorific verb takes that idea and uses it as a soft, polite way to talk about sleep. Rather than saying a respected person lies down and sleeps, you say they take their rest. So every member of this family is about pausing, and the honorific just makes the pause elegant." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for 「寝る|ねる」, using 「休|やす」, a person resting against a tree, so a respected person rests rather than sleeps. It follows the 「お」 plus stem plus 「になる」 recipe.",
      "The recipe 「お」 plus stem plus 「になる」 gives 「お待ちになる|おまちになる」, but short stems like 「寝る|ねる」 and 「見る|みる」 borrow replacements, resting and 「ご覧|ごらん」.",
      "Inns say 「ごゆっくりお休みください|ごゆっくりおやすみください」, please rest well. In an office, though, the same verb can mean a respected person is taking the day off.",
      "The kanji 「休|やす」 is about pausing, as in 「夏休み|なつやすみ」, 「休日|きゅうじつ」, and 「休憩|きゅうけい」, and this verb makes that pause an elegant word for sleep."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}は{何時|なんじ}に[お休みになりますか|おやすみになりますか|おやすみになる]。",
    en: "What time do you go to bed, Professor?" },
  "ごらんになる|ご覧になる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific for 「見る|みる」, to see, look at, or watch. The kanji 「覧|らん」 uses its Chinese-origin reading, and it means to look over or view. Here is the anchor. Look at the bottom of the kanji. It contains 「見|み」, the see kanji itself, the eye on legs. So this is a fancy, formal kind of seeing. Since it is a Chinese-origin word, it takes the polite prefix 「ご」 in front, and then the honorific ending 「になる」, the same ending as in 「お休みになる|おやすみになる」. Who uses it? You use it when a person you respect is the one looking or watching. You might ask a teacher if they have seen a movie, or tell a customer to look at something. A tour guide or a shop clerk will say 「ご覧ください|ごらんください」, please look, and you will hear it often on Japanese TV and in announcements. A common mistake: do not use it for your own looking. For yourself, you will learn a humble verb next lesson. Also, do not mix it with 「見せる|みせる」, to show. That is a different action. Recap: a kanji with see hidden inside it, plus 「ご」 and 「になる」, is the honorific way to say someone you respect sees or watches something." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「覧|らん」 is not common on its own, but it appears in some delightful words. The best one is 「観覧車|かんらんしゃ」, a Ferris wheel. 「観|かん」 is viewing, 「覧|らん」 is looking over, and 「車|しゃ」 is a wheel or car. A wheel for looking at the view: perfect. 「展覧会|てんらんかい」 is an exhibition, like an art show at a museum. 「一覧|いちらん」 means a list or overview, literally one look, the kind of table that shows everything at a glance. You may see a page titled with this word on Japanese websites. So the kanji is all about looking over a wide scene. Now, the humble partner for your own looking is 「拝見する|はいけんする」, which you will meet soon. When a teacher shows you a photo, you say you will humbly look at it. The teacher, looking at your photo, uses this verb. So for any act of seeing, there is a lift-up verb for them and a bow-down verb for you." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb has a casual cousin that is very useful. Take just 「ご覧|ごらん」 by itself, and it works as a soft command, look. Parents say it to children, pointing at something: look, a rainbow. Even more useful is 「てご覧|てごらん」, added to the 「て」 form of a verb. It means try doing it and see, the gentle version of the 「てみる」 pattern you learned in lesson thirteen. A parent might say try eating it, or a teacher might say try reading this. That makes sense, because 「てみる」 is built on 「見る|みる」, to see, and this is its honorific twin. In the polite world, the request is 「ご覧ください|ごらんください」, please look, heard in museums, on TV, and from tour guides. And the regular honorific question, have you seen it?, uses the past of 「になる」. So remember the three steps: the full honorific for respected people, the polite request for customers, and the short form for kids." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick for the sound. The kanji is read 「らん」. Picture a very fancy guest of honor arriving at a museum. The museum director bows deeply and leads her to a giant Ferris wheel inside the building. As they climb into the car, the director says, please, look at the view. She leans out the window, and the wheel starts to turn so fast that she has to run around inside the car to keep looking. Run, run, run, look, look, look. Run is the sound, and looking is the meaning. Then remember the kanji, with the eye on legs, 「見|み」, hiding at the bottom, the eye that keeps running around to look. And of course 「観覧車|かんらんしゃ」, the Ferris wheel, has this same kanji in it. So whenever you hear 「ご覧|ごらん」, picture the honored guest running around a Ferris wheel car, admiring everything." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for 「見る|みる」, and the kanji 「覧|らん」 hides 「見|み」 at the bottom. As a Chinese-origin word it takes 「ご」 plus 「になる」, and guides say 「ご覧ください|ごらんください」.",
      "「覧|らん」 is looking over a wide scene, as in 「観覧車|かんらんしゃ」, a Ferris wheel, and 「展覧会|てんらんかい」, an exhibition. Your own humble looking is 「拝見する|はいけんする」.",
      "Alone, 「ご覧|ごらん」 is a soft look for kids, and 「てご覧|てごらん」 means try and see, the gentle twin of 「てみる」. Customers hear 「ご覧ください|ごらんください」.",
      "Picture an honored guest in a Ferris wheel car, running around inside to keep looking at the view. Run gives the sound 「らん」, and looking gives the meaning."
    ],
    ja: "この{写真|しゃしん}を[ご覧になりましたか|ごらんになりましたか|ごらんになる]。",
    en: "Have you seen this photo?" },
  "おっしゃる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific for 「言う|いう」, to say. It is written in kana. Who uses it? You use it when someone you respect is the one saying something, like a teacher, a boss, or a customer. In reports, students often say the professor said something, using this verb. In conversation, you might politely ask what the person said. Like 「いらっしゃる」 and 「なさる」, its polite form is irregular. It is 「おっしゃいます」, not 「おっしゃります」. One very useful phrase is the polite way to ask someone's name. Instead of asking what their name is, you ask what they are called, 「お名前は何とおっしゃいますか|おなまえはなんとおっしゃいますか」. That literally means what do you say your name is. You will hear it at hotels, clinics, and offices. Another common one is 「おっしゃる通りです|おっしゃるとおりです」, it is exactly as you say, a polite way to agree with a superior. A common mistake for learners: do not use it about yourself, and do not use it for your own family members when talking to outsiders. A way to remember it: it starts with a little explosive pause, like someone clearing their throat before an important statement. Recap: the honorific for say, used only when someone you respect does the talking." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This verb comes from an old respectful phrase built on 「仰せ|おおせ」, which means the words or orders of a high-ranking person. Over centuries, that phrase got shortened and squeezed, and the long vowel turned into the small pause you hear today. That history also explains why the verb is sometimes written with the kanji 「仰る|おっしゃる」. The kanji 「仰|あお」 means to look up, as in 「仰ぐ|あおぐ」, to look up at the sky, or to look up to someone with respect. So when you look at the written form, you can see the idea: words that come from someone you look up to. In daily writing, though, the kana spelling is more common, and that is what you will see in your textbook. Like 「いらっしゃる」 and 「なさる」, this verb is not a polite twist on the plain verb for saying. It is a separate old word that was always reserved for people of rank. So this verb literally carries the words of someone you look up to." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Watch the small pause in this word. If you drop it, you get something close to 「おしゃれ」, fashionable, a word you learned in lesson fourteen. Imagine telling your boss, the president said, and accidentally saying the president is fashionable. Funny, but confusing. So hold the beat. The rhythm is four beats: 「お」, the small pause, 「しゃ」, 「る」. Clap it out. The pause should feel like a tiny held breath. The same goes for the polite form, 「おっしゃいます」, which has six beats. Another trap is to mix up the honorific and the humble verbs for saying. The humble one is 「申す|もうす」, which you know from the self-introduction phrase 「と申します|ともうします」, my name is. So when you introduce yourself, you use the humble verb. When you ask the other person's name, you use this one. Keep the beat, and remember that the humble verb is for your name and this one is for theirs." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb loves quotations. Like 「言う|いう」, it takes 「と」 to mark what was said. The teacher said there will be a test tomorrow becomes the sentence, then 「と」, then this verb. In casual speech among students talking about a teacher, you often hear 「って」 instead of 「と」: the teacher said there is no class. Note that you can be casual with your friends and still honor the teacher. The verb itself is honorific, so the respect is there even in a casual sentence. Another useful pattern is the polite question, as in what did you say? You would ask what did you say, with this verb, when you did not catch a superior's words. A softer way is to ask, excuse me, what did you just say? Also useful is 「とおっしゃいました」 in reports, as in the doctor said to rest. And remember the polite form is 「おっしゃいます」, with 「い」. So the verb quotes like 「言う|いう」, but it puts the speaker on a pedestal." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific for 「言う|いう」, with the irregular polite form 「おっしゃいます」. Ask a name with 「お名前は何とおっしゃいますか|おなまえはなんとおっしゃいますか」, never about yourself.",
      "It comes from 「仰せ|おおせ」, the words of a high-ranking person, squeezed over centuries. The kanji 「仰|あお」 means to look up, words from someone you look up to.",
      "Hold the small pause, four beats, or it sounds like 「おしゃれ」, fashionable. The humble partner is 「申す|もうす」, so use that for your name and this one for theirs.",
      "Like 「言う|いう」, it quotes with 「と」, or 「って」 casually, so students can chat with friends and still honor the teacher, since the verb itself carries the respect."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}がそう[おっしゃいました|おっしゃいました|おっしゃる]。",
    en: "That's what the professor said." },
  "〜ていらっしゃる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the honorific version of the 「ている」 pattern you learned in lesson seven. Remember that 「ている」 describes an action in progress, like is reading, or an ongoing state, like is married or lives somewhere. It works because 「いる」, the helper verb at the end, is one of the three verbs that 「いらっしゃる」 replaces. So you simply swap the helper. Keep the 「て」 form of the main verb, and put 「いらっしゃる」 after it instead of 「いる」. The professor is reading a book becomes respectful just by changing that last part. Who uses it? You use it when the person doing the action is someone you respect, like a teacher, a boss, a customer, or an older person. It is very common in business, for example asking whether someone knows something, or saying that the manager is waiting. The polite form ends in 「いらっしゃいます」, with the same irregular 「い」 as before. A common mistake is to make the main verb honorific too. You only need to change the helper at the end. Never use it for your own actions. Recap: swap the helper 「いる」 for 「いらっしゃる」, and any is doing something becomes respectful." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Remember that 「ている」 has two jobs, and this honorific version keeps both. The first is an action in progress. The professor is talking on the phone right now. The second is a lasting state that began in the past. With verbs like living, getting married, or working somewhere, it describes the current situation. So you can say the professor is married, or the manager lives in Osaka, just by swapping the helper. In fast speech, the first 「い」 often drops out, and you hear 「てらっしゃる」, like 「読んでらっしゃる|よんでらっしゃる」. That is fine in conversation. The past is 「ていらっしゃった」, and a softer version, 「ていらした」, is common too. One special case to learn: for knowing, Japanese usually does not use this pattern. Instead it uses 「ご存じ|ごぞんじ」, as in do you know? 「ご存じですか|ごぞんじですか」. So use the swap for most verbs, but remember the special word for knowing." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is a short office scene. Inside a company, coworkers talking to each other about their boss use this pattern. One might tell a colleague that the manager is waiting in the meeting room. But when an outside caller asks for the manager, staff usually switch to humble speech about their own boss. That inside versus outside rule is something you will see more in lesson twenty. Another common use is with clients or customers. A shop clerk might say, are you looking for something?, with this pattern. Or a host might say, I hear your son is studying in America, honoring the guest's family. Common verbs include waiting, working, living, and studying. In daily life, you will hear it most in polite questions: where do you live?, what kind of work do you do?, are you married? So this pattern is how you ask about someone's life without sounding nosy." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the honorific 「ている」: keep the 「て」 form and swap the helper 「いる」 for 「いらっしゃる」. Only the helper changes, and never use it for yourself.",
      "It keeps both jobs of 「ている」, action in progress and lasting state, and often shrinks to 「てらっしゃる」. For knowing, Japanese uses 「ご存じ|ごぞんじ」 instead.",
      "Coworkers use it about their boss among themselves, and hosts use it for a guest's life, as in where do you live, so you can ask politely without sounding nosy."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}は{本|ほん}を[読んでいらっしゃいます|よんでいらっしゃいます|〜ていらっしゃる]。",
    en: "The professor is reading a book." },
  "おこる|怒る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading. Look at the shape. The bottom is 「心|しん」, heart, the same feelings mark you saw in 「悩み|なやみ」 and 「怠け者|なまけもの」. So this kanji, too, is about an emotion. The top part, by itself, can mean a servant or a guy. A way to remember it: a heart pushed around like a servant finally boils over, and gets angry. The verb means to get angry. Like many Japanese verbs of change, the 「ている」 form describes the state. So to say someone is angry right now, you say they have gotten angry, 「怒っている|おこっている」. Saying just the dictionary form talks about getting angry in general or in the future. That is a common mistake for English speakers. A second big use: this verb also means to scold, to get angry at someone. You say you got angry at someone with the particle 「に」. And in the passive form, which you will learn later, it means to get scolded, like by a teacher or a parent. One more helpful note on sound: it sounds just like 「起こす|おこす」, to wake someone up, from lesson sixteen, but the kanji and meaning are different. A hook: waking someone up early can make them angry. Recap: a heart that boils over, getting angry or scolding someone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Japanese has many ways to talk about anger, and they differ in strength and tone. Our verb is the standard one, to get angry, and also to scold. A close neighbor is 「叱る|しかる」, to scold. The difference: 「叱る|しかる」 is scolding for someone's own good, the way a coach or parent corrects you. Our verb can include pure anger. A child might say I got yelled at with either one. Another common phrase is 「腹が立つ|はらがたつ」, literally the belly stands up, meaning to be annoyed or offended. It describes the feeling inside, not the outburst. In casual speech, young people say 「むかつく」, something is irritating, or it makes me mad. 「いらいらする」 means to feel irritated, a sound word for impatience, like waiting for a late bus. And 「キレる」, in katakana, means to snap, to lose your temper suddenly. So from a slow burn to an explosion, Japanese has a word for each stage. Our verb sits in the middle, the everyday way to say someone got angry." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Let's look at the kanji's other readings. The native reading 「おこ」 is what you use for the everyday verb. But the same kanji can also be read 「いか」, in the verb 「怒る|いかる」, which also means to be angry. That reading is more formal and literary, and you will meet it in writing, news, and phrases like a furious face. So one kanji, two native verbs, and the everyday one is ours. The noun from that formal verb, 「怒り|いかり」, means anger or rage. The Chinese-origin reading is 「ど」, as in 「怒号|どごう」, an angry roar, a word from news reports. A related kanji to compare is 「努|ど」, as in 「努力|どりょく」, effort. It has the same top part, 「奴|ど」, but with power at the bottom instead of heart. That is why they share the sound 「ど」: the top part gives the sound. The bottom gives the meaning. Heart for an emotion, power for effort. So this kanji is a nice example of how one side gives sound and the other gives meaning." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a silly sound-alike trick. Picture a small boy at home playing with his mother's favorite vase, the one she carried all the way back from Kyoto. He tosses it in the air, it falls, and it shatters. He stares at the pieces, sparkly all over the floor, and he shouts, oh, cool! In walks his mother. She sees the vase, she hears oh, cool, and her face turns red. Steam comes out of her ears. She is furious. Oh, cool, and then she gets angry. Every time you need this verb, picture that little boy's happy face and his mother's red one. And to remember the 「ている」 form, picture her still standing there, arms folded, still angry an hour later. Now when you say my mother is angry, the scene is ready. So remember: oh, cool, a broken vase, and a very angry mother." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has 「心|しん」, heart, at the bottom, a heart that boils over. Use 「怒っている|おこっている」 for is angry now, and 「に」 for getting angry at or scolding someone.",
      "It is the everyday word for getting angry or scolding, while 「叱る|しかる」 is scolding for someone's good, 「腹が立つ|はらがたつ」 is the feeling inside, and 「キレる」 is snapping.",
      "The same kanji is read 「いか」 in formal 「怒る|いかる」 and 「怒り|いかり」. Its top gives the sound 「ど」, shared with 「努力|どりょく」, while the heart at the bottom gives the meaning.",
      "Picture a boy who shatters his mother's vase and shouts oh, cool, just as she walks in, red and steaming. Oh, cool, then a furious mother gives 「おこる」."
    ],
    ja: "{母|はは}は[怒って|おこって|おこる]います。",
    en: "My mother is angry." },
  "きまる|決まる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the partner of 「決める|きめる」, to decide, from lesson ten, and it uses the same kanji with the same native reading. The kanji has the water mark on its left, three drops. Its meaning is to decide or settle something. The two verbs form a pair that Japanese uses a lot. 「決める|きめる」 is the doing verb. Someone decides something, and the thing decided takes 「を」. This verb is the happening verb. Something becomes decided, and the thing takes 「が」. You do not have to say who decided. That makes it very useful. Japanese often prefers to say the date has been decided, rather than naming the person who did it. You already know half of a similar pair from lesson eight, 「始める|はじめる」, to begin something. Its happening partner is 「始まる|はじまる」, something begins. Notice the sound pattern. Many happening verbs end in 「まる」, and their doing partners end in 「める」. A common use is 「決まりました|きまりました」, it has been decided, like a trip date, a job, or a menu order. A waiter may ask if you have decided, using this verb. A common mistake English speakers make is using 「を」 with this verb. Use 「が」. Recap: the same kanji as 「決める|きめる」, but in its happening form, something is decided." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "Why does a kanji for deciding have water on the left? Dictionaries commonly explain that its original meaning was water breaking through a dike, bursting out all at once. From that sudden, unstoppable break, the meaning moved to making a firm decision, cutting through hesitation. You can still see the old sense in 「決壊|けっかい」, a dam or riverbank bursting, a word you hear in typhoon news. The Chinese-origin reading is 「けつ」, and it often shrinks to 「けっ」 with a small pause. 「決心|けっしん」 is making up your mind. 「決定|けってい」 is an official decision, often seen on notices. 「解決|かいけつ」 is solving a problem. And sports fans know 「決勝|けっしょう」, the final match, where the winner is decided. So in kana verbs like ours, it uses the native reading 「き」, while in compounds it is 「けつ」. Picture the water breaking through, and you will feel how final this kanji is." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb belongs to one of the tidiest patterns in Japanese verb pairs. The happening verb ends in 「まる」, and the doing verb ends in 「める」. You already have several of them. 「閉まる|しまる」 and 「閉める|しめる」: the door closes, and I close the door. 「始まる|はじまる」 and 「始める|はじめる」: class begins, and the teacher begins class. 「集まる|あつまる」 and 「集める|あつめる」: people gather, and I collect stamps. 「止まる|とまる」 and 「止める|とめる」: the car stops, and I stop the car. And our pair, decided and decide. The happening verb always takes 「が」 for the thing that changes. The doing verb takes 「を」 for the thing someone acts on. Here is a quick test. Ask yourself, is there a person doing it in my sentence? If yes, use the 「める」 verb. If the thing just happens, or you do not want to say who did it, use the 「まる」 verb. So learn these as pairs, and each new one is half learned already." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This verb has a few everyday expressions you will hear constantly. A waiter, coming back to your table, asks 「お決まりですか|おきまりですか」, have you decided?, which is a polite way to ask for your order. The noun form, 「決まり|きまり」, means a rule. School rules, house rules, the rules of a dormitory. The ending 「に決まっている|にきまっている」 means it is certain, of course, no doubt. Someone might say, she will pass, of course she will. It is a very confident, sometimes cheeky phrase. You will also hear 「決まった|きまった」 used before a noun, meaning regular or fixed, as in at a set time every day. And when you get a job offer, you say your job has been decided, a happy announcement. One cultural note: Japanese often uses this happening verb in group settings, saying it was decided, rather than naming who decided. That keeps the focus on the group. So this verb works in restaurants, schools, and offices, wherever things get settled." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the happening partner of 「決める|きめる」: something becomes decided, with 「が」, not 「を」, and you need not say who decided, as in 「決まりました|きまりました」.",
      "The water mark on the left reflects an old meaning, water bursting through a dike, which became a firm decision. In compounds it reads 「けつ」, as in 「決心|けっしん」 and 「決勝|けっしょう」.",
      "It follows the tidy pattern of happening verbs in 「まる」 and doing verbs in 「める」, like 「始まる|はじまる」 and 「始める|はじめる」. If someone does it, use 「める」.",
      "Waiters ask 「お決まりですか|おきまりですか」, the noun 「決まり|きまり」 is a rule, and 「に決まっている|にきまっている」 means of course, no doubt."
    ],
    ja: "{旅行|りょこう}の{日|ひ}が[決まりました|きまりました|きまる]。",
    en: "The date of the trip has been decided." },
  "しりあう|知り合う": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This verb is built from two verbs you know, and both use native readings. The first is 「知る|しる」, to get to know, from lesson seven. The kanji has 「矢|や」, arrow, on the left and 「口|くち」, mouth, on the right. A way to remember it: knowledge that comes out of your mouth as quick and straight as an arrow. The second part is 「合う|あう」, which means to fit together or to do something to each other. You have seen that mutual sense before, in 「似合う|にあう」, to look good on someone, and in 「付き合う|つきあう」, to date someone, from lesson fifteen. So this verb is to know each other, which means to get acquainted, to meet someone for the first time and become people who know each other. The usual pattern is two people get acquainted with 「と」, or you say where with 「で」. A very common conversation question is where two people met, using this verb. That is the kind of thing people ask a couple. The noun form, 「知り合い|しりあい」, means an acquaintance, someone you know but who is not a close friend. A common mistake: 「会う|あう」 is just to meet or see someone. This verb is about becoming acquainted. Recap: know plus each other, getting acquainted." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The ending 「合う|あう」 can be attached to many verb stems to mean doing something to each other. 「話し合う|はなしあう」 means to talk it over, to discuss together, like a family deciding on vacation plans. 「助け合う|たすけあう」 is to help each other. 「見つめ合う|みつめあう」 is to gaze into each other's eyes, a classic romance scene. And our verb, know plus each other, is to become acquainted. Because it is about two people, the partner takes 「と」. You got acquainted with your partner at work. You will mostly hear it in the past, since meeting someone is a single event. And the state is shown with the noun, as in he is an acquaintance of mine. Compare 「知っている|しっている」, which means you know someone. You can know a famous actor without ever having met them. But you cannot become acquainted with them unless you have both met. So this verb is always two-sided, and that is the gift of 「合う|あう」." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The question how did you two meet? is a classic in Japanese small talk, and it uses this verb. People ask it at weddings, parties, and dinners: where did you get to know each other? Common answers are through work, at university, or through a friend's introduction, 「友だちの紹介で|ともだちのしょうかいで」. Another phrase you will hear is 「知り合いの紹介|しりあいのしょうかい」, an introduction through an acquaintance, which is a very common way to find a job, a doctor, or an apartment in Japan. Here is a cultural point. Japanese tends to separate 「友だち|ともだち」, a friend, from 「知り合い|しりあい」, an acquaintance, more sharply than English does. In English, people sometimes call almost anyone a friend. In Japanese, calling a person you have only met twice a friend can sound too strong. So it is natural to say he is someone I know. Remember that the first step is getting acquainted, and friendship comes later." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「知る|しる」, to know, plus 「合う|あう」, each other, so it means getting acquainted. The noun 「知り合い|しりあい」 is an acquaintance, unlike plain 「会う|あう」, to meet.",
      "The ending 「合う|あう」 means doing something to each other, as in 「話し合う|はなしあう」 and 「助け合う|たすけあう」, so this verb is always two-sided, with the partner marked by 「と」.",
      "How did you two meet is classic small talk using this verb. Japanese separates 「友だち|ともだち」 from 「知り合い|しりあい」 more sharply than English, so friendship comes later."
    ],
    ja: "{友|とも}だちとは{大学|だいがく}で[知り合いました|しりあいました|しりあう]。",
    en: "I got to know my friend at college." },
  "よぶ|呼ぶ": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This kanji uses its native reading. Look at the left side. It is 「口|くち」, mouth, which makes sense for a verb about using your voice. A way to remember it: an open mouth calling out across the room. The verb has two main jobs. First, to call someone, as in to call out their name or call them over, and also to call something by a name, like what do you call this in Japanese. Second, to invite someone, especially to a party or to your home, and to send for someone, like calling a taxi or a doctor. The pattern is to call someone with 「を」. A common mistake English speakers make: in English, call also means to phone. This verb does not. Phoning someone is 「電話する|でんわする」, from lesson six. This verb is about calling with your voice, or summoning someone to come. In this same lesson you also have 「招待する|しょうたいする」, to invite, which is more formal, like a wedding invitation or a business event. This verb is the everyday, casual way to invite friends over. And in 「敬語|けいご」, the honorific 「召し上がる|めしあがる」 starts with a kanji that also means to call or summon. Recap: a mouth calling out, to call someone or invite them over." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "This kanji has a second reading you will meet in an important word. The Chinese-origin reading is 「こ」, and it appears in 「呼吸|こきゅう」, breathing. The first kanji is ours, and in that word it means breathing out, the way a call goes out of your mouth. The second kanji is 「吸|きゅう」, breathing in, which you know from 「たばこを吸う|たばこをすう」, to smoke. So breathing in Japanese is literally out plus in. That is a lovely way to remember both kanji. Notice they both have the mouth on the left. Breath goes out of the mouth when you call, and in through the mouth when you inhale. The right side of our kanji, 「乎|こ」, mainly gives the sound. In classical Chinese it was used as an exclamation or a question word, which fits a kanji about calling out, but treat that as a bonus rather than the main story. So remember the mouth that breathes out, and the call that goes with it." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A very common use of this verb is naming. You say what people call someone with 「と」. For example, everyone calls me T, or what do you call this in Japanese? A friend might ask, what should I call you? That is a big question in Japan, because names come with titles. Calling someone by their family name with 「さん」 is the safe default. Dropping the title and using just the name is called 「呼び捨て|よびすて」, literally calling and throwing away. Among close friends, it is warm. With a teacher or a new coworker, it is rude. Nicknames are 「あだ名|あだな」, and kids love them. Another daily use is summoning help. Call a taxi, call an ambulance, call the doctor. At a restaurant, you might call the waiter with excuse me. And you can be called by a name at a clinic or bank when your turn comes. So this verb covers names, nicknames, taxis, and parties, always using your voice or a summons, never the phone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This verb combines with others to make useful compounds. 「呼び出す|よびだす」, call plus put out, means to summon someone or to page them, like being called to the teacher's office. The noun form, being summoned, is something no student wants to hear. 「呼び方|よびかた」 is a way of calling someone, a form of address, combining the stem with 「方|かた」, way of doing. 「呼びかける|よびかける」 means to call out to someone, or to appeal to the public, like a campaign asking people to save water. Now compare it with two neighbors. 「招待する|しょうたいする」 is a formal invitation, like to a wedding. 「誘う|さそう」, from lesson fifteen, is to invite someone to join an activity, like going to a movie together. Our verb is about bringing someone to where you are, like inviting friends to your home or calling a doctor to the house. Ask yourself, are we going somewhere together, or are they coming to me? Coming to me is our verb." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has 「口|くち」, mouth, on the left, a mouth calling out. It means calling someone, calling something by a name, or inviting, but never phoning, which is 「電話する|でんわする」.",
      "Its Chinese-origin reading 「こ」 appears in 「呼吸|こきゅう」, breathing, literally breathing out plus 「吸|きゅう」, breathing in, both with the mouth on the left.",
      "You name someone with 「と」 plus this verb. Dropping the title is 「呼び捨て|よびすて」, warm among close friends but rude with a teacher or a new coworker.",
      "It brings someone to where you are, as in 「呼び出す|よびだす」, to summon, unlike 「誘う|さそう」, going somewhere together, or formal 「招待する|しょうたいする」."
    ],
    ja: "パーティーに{友|とも}だちを[呼びました|よびました|よぶ]。",
    en: "I invited my friends to the party." },
  "ひっこす|引っ越す": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with native readings, joined with a small pause. The first, 「引|ひ」, means to pull. Its left side is the bow kanji, and the right side is a straight line, like pulling back a bowstring. You have used this verb already, in 「風邪をひく|かぜをひく」, to catch a cold, which is literally to pull in a cold. The second kanji, 「越|こ」, means to cross over, like crossing a mountain pass or a border. It has the running mark on its left. When the two verbs join, the end of the first one shrinks into a small pause. So the picture is pulling all your stuff along and crossing over to a new place. That means to move house. The pattern is to move to a place with 「に」, and the noun form, 「引っ越し|ひっこし」, means the move itself. A common mistake: this verb is only for moving where you live. For moving an object or for a car moving, you need different words. A cultural note: in Japan, when you move into a new place, it is a custom to visit your neighbors with a small gift to introduce yourself. Traditionally it was soba noodles. Recap: pull your things and cross over, and you have moved to a new home." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Moving in Japan has its own customs. The busiest moving season is March and April, because the school year and many jobs start in April. Moving companies get booked up and prices go up. When people move in, they often visit neighbors with a small gift, and the traditional gift is 「引っ越しそば|ひっこしそば」, moving buckwheat noodles. One common explanation is a pun. 「そば」 means buckwheat noodles, and it also means near or beside. So the gift says I am now living near you. Long, thin noodles are also said to wish for a long relationship. Today people often give towels, snacks, or detergent instead, but the custom of greeting the neighbors is still common. Another custom is with renting. Many apartments require extra money up front, such as a deposit and a gift payment to the landlord. And when you leave, you must clean and return the keys. So moving in Japan is not just packing boxes. It is about saying hello to your new neighbors." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "Both kanji in this verb have useful relatives. 「引|ひ」, to pull, gives 「引き出し|ひきだし」, a drawer, the thing you pull out. You know it from 「割引券|わりびきけん」, a discount coupon, where 「割引|わりびき」 is pulling some off the price. And 「引っ張る|ひっぱる」 means to pull hard, with the same small pause you see in our verb. The second kanji, 「越|こ」, appears in 「越える|こえる」, to cross over, like crossing a mountain or a border. Our verb is really pull plus cross over, joined with a small pause that makes it sound quick and lively. The noun 「引っ越し|ひっこし」 is the move, and 「引っ越し屋|ひっこしや」 is a moving company, using the same 「屋|や」 as a shop. Compare 「移る|うつる」, to shift to another place, which can mean moving seats, moving to a new department, or a cold spreading. Our verb is just for changing homes. So pull and cross, and you have changed your address." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This verb is a regular u-verb ending in 「す」, so the polite form is 「引っ越します|ひっこします」 and the 「て」 form is 「引っ越して|ひっこして」. Destination takes 「に」, and the place you left takes 「から」. I moved from Osaka to Tokyo. A nice combination is with 「てくる」 and 「ていく」, which you may already have met. 「引っ越してきた|ひっこしてきた」 means someone moved here, toward the speaker, like a new neighbor who just arrived. 「引っ越していった|ひっこしていった」 means someone moved away, leaving the speaker behind, like a friend who moved to another city. So the direction is shown by the helper verb. A new family moved in next door uses the first. My best friend moved away uses the second. You can also use 「たばかり」, just did, as in we have just moved here, which is a great excuse for not knowing where the supermarket is. So this one verb, with a helper, tells you who arrived and who left." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「引|ひ」 is to pull and 「越|こ」 is to cross over, joined with a small pause, so you pull your things and cross to a new home. It is only for moving house.",
      "Spring is the busy moving season in Japan, and new arrivals traditionally give neighbors 「引っ越しそば|ひっこしそば」, a pun on 「そば」, which also means near.",
      "「引|ひ」 gives 「引き出し|ひきだし」, a drawer, and 「越|こ」 gives 「越える|こえる」, to cross over. Unlike 「移る|うつる」, which shifts anything, this verb just changes homes.",
      "Mark the destination with 「に」 and the old place with 「から」. 「引っ越してきた|ひっこしてきた」 means someone moved here, and 「引っ越していった|ひっこしていった」 means they moved away."
    ],
    ja: "{来月|らいげつ}{東京|とうきょう}に[引っ越します|ひっこします|ひっこす]。",
    en: "I'm moving to Tokyo next month." },
  "よる|寄る": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading. On top is the roof mark, the same roof you see on top of 「家|いえ」, house. A way to remember it: you stop under someone's roof for a moment. The verb means to drop by or stop by somewhere on the way to somewhere else, and also to come close to something. The pattern is to stop by a place with 「に」. A very common use is on the way home, like stopping by a convenience store or a bookstore after school. You say on the way home with 「帰りに|かえりに」, and then the place, and then this verb. It is also used when inviting someone, like please drop by sometime. A useful sound note: it sounds exactly like 「夜|よる」, night, from lesson five, but it is a different word with a different kanji. A hook to link them: on your way home at night, you stop by a shop. A common mistake for English speakers is to use 「行く|いく」 for these quick visits. That is fine, but this verb adds the idea that it is a short stop on the way. Recap: stepping under a roof for a moment, stopping by on the way." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This kanji appears in some very handy words. 「最寄り|もより」 means nearest, and 「最寄り駅|もよりえき」, the nearest station, is something every Japanese apartment ad lists. The first kanji is 「最|さい」, most, so it is literally the most nearby. 「年寄り|としより」 means an elderly person. 「年|とし」 is years, and our verb's stem shows the years coming close and piling up. The polite form is 「お年寄り|おとしより」, which you see on priority seats on trains. 「近寄る|ちかよる」 means to approach, to come near, as in a sign saying do not come near. And 「立ち寄る|たちよる」 is a slightly more formal way to say stop by, as in a tour bus stopping at a souvenir shop. The Chinese-origin reading is 「き」, as in 「寄付|きふ」, a donation, money brought close to someone who needs it. So this family is all about coming close, whether it is a station, a person, or years." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "A very common invitation uses this verb with 「ていく」: why not drop in on your way?, as in come in for a cup of tea. Friends say, want to stop by somewhere on the way home?, which usually means a cafe, a bar, or a convenience store. Japanese daily life is full of these little stops. After work, people stop by the supermarket. After school, students stop by a fast food place. Another polite phrase is please stop by if you are in the neighborhood, often written on new year cards or said when someone moves. A second meaning is to move closer to one side. On a crowded train or in a photo, someone might say, please move in a little, using the request form of this verb. So you may hear it when a photographer is squeezing everyone into the picture. The transitive partner, 「寄せる|よせる」, means to bring something closer, like pulling a car over to the side. So whether you are dropping in or squeezing in, this verb is about coming close." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This verb shares its sound with 「夜|よる」, night, but in standard Tokyo speech the pitch is different. For night, the voice starts high on the first beat and falls on the second. For to stop by, the voice starts low and rises, and it stays up when endings are added. Try saying them side by side: night falls, stopping by rises. Even if your pitch is not perfect yet, grammar will tell them apart instantly. Night is a noun, so it comes with particles like 「に」, 「は」, or 「の」. The verb changes its ending, so you hear 「よります」, 「よって」, or 「よりたい」, none of which can be night. A sentence that ends with stopped by is never about the night. You can even use both in one sentence: at night, on the way home, I stopped by a shop. That is a handy practice line for the pitch. So listen for the rise and for the verb endings, and the two words will never trip you up." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji has the roof mark on top, so you step under someone's roof for a moment. It means stopping by on the way, as in 「帰りに|かえりに」 plus a place with 「に」.",
      "The kanji means coming close, as in 「最寄り駅|もよりえき」, the nearest station, 「お年寄り|おとしより」, elderly people, and 「近寄る|ちかよる」, to approach.",
      "Friends invite each other to stop by somewhere on the way home, and the verb also means scooting closer, as when a photographer squeezes everyone into the picture.",
      "It sounds like 「夜|よる」, but night falls in pitch while this verb rises. Verb endings like 「よって」 and 「よりたい」 can never be night."
    ],
    ja: "{帰|かえ}りに{本屋|ほんや}に[寄りました|よりました|よる]。",
    en: "I stopped by a bookstore on the way home." },
  "おくれる|遅れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The kanji uses its native reading here, and you already know it from 「遅い|おそい」, slow or late, from lesson ten. Same kanji, different reading. Look at the shape. The bottom left is the movement mark, the road radical that shows up in kanji about going and traveling. So this kanji is about moving slowly. Then add the verb ending, and you get to fall behind or to become late. The pattern is to be late for something with 「に」. You can be late for class, a meeting, or a train. You have seen this verb inside 「乗り遅れる|のりおくれる」, to miss a train or bus, from lesson sixteen, literally to be late for getting on. Compare it with two other words. 「遅刻する|ちこくする」, from lesson eleven, also means to be late, but mostly for a set time like school or work, and it has a strong feeling of breaking a rule. 「遅くなる|おそくなる」 means it gets late, like coming home late. This verb is the most general. It is also used for things, like when a train is delayed. Japanese trains are famous for being on time, so a train running late is announced with apologies. Recap: the slow kanji on the move, to fall behind or become late." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This verb shares its exact sound with another one, and the two are easy to confuse. 「送る|おくる」, to send, from lesson fourteen, has a potential form, can send, which is 「送れる|おくれる」. Same sound as our verb! So if someone says, I could not send the email, and someone else says, the email was late, the words can sound almost identical. The kanji tell them apart in writing. Our verb has the movement mark with the kanji for slow. The other has the movement mark with a different right side. In speech, context saves you. Late things are trains, people, and deadlines, with 「に」 for what you are late for. Can send is about letters and packages. Another close sound is 「遅い|おそい」, the same kanji with a different reading. So one kanji gives you slow and late, and one sound gives you late and can send. Keep the subject in mind, and you will hear which one is meant." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Japan's trains are famous for punctuality, and that gives this verb a special role in daily life. When a train is even a few minutes behind schedule, the station staff announce it and apologize. If a train is seriously delayed, stations hand out 「遅延証明書|ちえんしょうめいしょ」, a delay certificate. 「遅延|ちえん」 is the formal word for delay, using the Chinese-origin reading 「ち」. Passengers show this slip to their school or boss to prove the lateness was not their fault. Being late for work or a meeting is taken seriously in Japan. People often arrive early, and a common rule of thumb is to be five or ten minutes ahead. If you are going to be late, the polite thing is to contact the other person in advance and apologize. A useful phrase when you finally arrive is 「遅れてすみません|おくれてすみません」, sorry I am late. So in a country of on-time trains, this verb usually comes with an apology attached." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a ru-verb, so it conjugates like 「食べる|たべる」. The polite form is 「遅れます|おくれます」, and the 「て」 form is 「遅れて|おくれて」. The thing you are late for takes 「に」, as in late for class or late for the deadline. The 「て」 form plus an apology is very common, as in sorry for being late. You can also use it with 「そう」, to say it looks like you will be late, which is useful for a quick message: I think I will be late. The negative request, please do not be late, is a common reminder from teachers. With 「ないように」, you can say leave early so as not to be late. There is a related verb, 「遅らせる|おくらせる」, to delay something on purpose, like pushing back a meeting. And the word for being behind in work or school uses this same verb, like falling behind in class. So the pattern is simple: 「に」 for what you are late for, and an apology right after." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It uses the kanji of 「遅い|おそい」, slow, with the movement mark, so it means falling behind or becoming late, with 「に」. It is more general than 「遅刻する|ちこくする」 and works for trains too.",
      "It sounds just like 「送れる|おくれる」, can send. Late is for trains, people, and deadlines, while can send is for letters and packages, so context tells them apart.",
      "Japanese trains are so punctual that delays come with apologies and a 「遅延証明書|ちえんしょうめいしょ」, a delay certificate. When you arrive late, say 「遅れてすみません|おくれてすみません」.",
      "It is a ru-verb, with 「に」 for what you are late for and often an apology right after. Its partner 「遅らせる|おくらせる」 means to delay something on purpose."
    ],
    ja: "{電車|でんしゃ}が[遅れました|おくれました|おくれる]。",
    en: "The train was late." },
  "かける|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You already know this verb in other jobs. In lesson seven you put on glasses with it, 「めがねをかける」. Later you locked a door with it, 「鍵をかける|かぎをかける」, ironed clothes, 「アイロンをかける」, and you can make a phone call with it too. The core idea behind all of them is to hang or hook one thing onto another. Glasses hook over your ears, a lock hooks onto a door. Sitting down fits the same picture. The full old phrase is to hang your hips onto a seat, and the short form is just this verb. So it means to sit down, specifically on a chair, a bench, or a sofa, something raised. Compare it with 「座る|すわる」 from lesson six. That one is the general word for sitting, and it is the one you use for sitting on the floor or on a cushion. This verb is a bit softer and more polite, and it is only for seats. Its most common use is in 「敬語|けいご」. When a host, a doctor, or an office worker wants you to sit, they say 「おかけください」, please have a seat. That is the honorific request pattern, 「お」 plus the verb stem plus 「ください」. A common mistake is to think it is a different word each time. It is one verb with one picture. Recap: hang yourself onto a chair, and please have a seat is 「おかけください」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a group two verb, the kind that ends in 「える」 and simply drops 「る」 to conjugate. So the polite form is 「かけます」, the te-form is 「かけて」, and the negative is 「かけない」. It has an intransitive partner, 「かかる」, which you know from talking about time and money, as in it takes ten minutes. The pair shares the same hanging picture. You hang something with this verb, and something hangs or catches with its partner. That is why a phone call you make uses this verb, but a call that comes in to you uses 「かかる」. For sitting, the honorific version is 「おかけになる」, so a very polite host may say 「おかけになってください」. The humble side does not exist in the same way, because you do not need to raise up your own sitting. When you are the one sitting down after being invited, you simply sit and say 「失礼します|しつれいします」. So remember: one regular group two verb, with a quiet partner that ends in 「かる」." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese offices and job interviews, sitting down is a small ceremony. You enter the room, bow, and then you stand beside the chair and wait. You do not sit until the interviewer says 「どうぞ、おかけください」. Then you answer 「失礼します|しつれいします」, excuse me, and sit. Sitting before being invited can look a bit pushy, so many job-hunting guides in Japan drill this exact sequence. Where you sit matters too. The best seat, called 「上座|かみざ」, is usually the one farthest from the door, and it goes to the guest or the most senior person. The seat nearest the door is for the host or the most junior person, who can get up to fetch things. So when a host waves you toward a seat with this phrase, it is often the honored seat, and it is polite to accept it. The short version: wait for the invitation, thank them, then sit." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a memory trick. Picture a coat rack in a waiting room. A tired businessman walks in, takes off his coat, and hangs it on a hook. Then, still in a daze, he tries to hang himself on the next hook too, legs dangling, briefcase swinging. The receptionist sighs, points to a chair, and says 「おかけください」, as if to say: please, hang yourself on this instead. He lowers himself onto the chair, and his body folds over the seat just like the coat folds over the hook. Every time you see someone sit on a chair, picture them being hung up neatly, like a coat. The verb that hangs your glasses on your ears and your coat on a hook also hangs you on a seat. One picture, many jobs." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The core picture is hanging one thing onto another, like glasses or a lock, and sitting means hanging yourself on a chair. Hosts say 「おかけください」, please have a seat.",
      "It is a regular group two verb with the partner 「かかる」. The honorific is 「おかけになる」, and when you sit after being invited you just say 「失礼します|しつれいします」.",
      "In interviews you wait beside the chair until told 「どうぞ、おかけください」, reply 「失礼します|しつれいします」, then sit. The honored seat farthest from the door is 「上座|かみざ」.",
      "Picture a dazed businessman hanging his coat on a hook, then trying to hang himself too, until the receptionist points to a chair and says 「おかけください」."
    ],
    ja: "どうぞ、いすに[かけて|かけて|かける]ください。",
    en: "Please have a seat on the chair." },
  "もてる|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This casual verb is written in kana, and it means to be popular, almost always with the opposite sex or in a romantic way. It looks exactly like the potential form of 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, from lesson six, which would mean can be held. A way to remember it: everyone wants to hold on to the popular person. The pattern is to be popular with a group, using 「に」, like popular with women or popular with men. Like many verbs that describe a state, you often use the 「ている」 form to say someone is popular right now. The plain form can talk about being popular in general. A common mistake English speakers make: this is not the word for popular in the sense of a popular song, a popular shop, or a famous singer. For that, Japanese uses a different word that means popularity. This one is about people attracting admirers. It is also very casual, the kind of word friends use when teasing each other, or in magazines and TV shows about dating. You would not use it in a business meeting. The negative, 「もてない」, means never having luck in love, and people often joke about themselves with it. Recap: the person everyone wants to hold on to, popular in the romantic sense." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This verb is generally connected to 「持つ|もつ」, to hold, through its old potential sense of being able to hold, or to keep up. Japanese also has the related verb 「もてはやす」, to make a big fuss over someone, to praise them to the skies. Many dictionaries link the popularity meaning to that idea of being treated well and made much of. The details of the drift are not completely settled, so treat this as the broad picture rather than a precise history. What is clear is that the word separated from its parent. Today, when people mean popular with admirers, they often write it in katakana as 「モテる」, which makes it look like slang and keeps it apart from the ordinary potential form of to hold. So the short version: a word that grew out of holding and being made much of, now living its own life." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This word is everywhere in Japanese pop culture. Magazines run features on how to become popular, often written 「モテる」 in katakana. Doubling it gives 「モテモテ」, which means wildly popular, swarmed with admirers. Friends tease each other with it: wow, you are so popular. There is also 「モテ期|もてき」, a playful idea that everyone gets a short period in life when they are suddenly popular, and people joke about whether theirs has come yet or already passed. On the other side, someone who never has luck in love may call themselves 「非モテ|ひもて」, unpopular, usually as self-mockery. In a mini dialogue, one friend says 「田中さん、モテますね|たなかさん、もてますね」, and the other laughs and says 「いえいえ、ぜんぜん」, not at all. Denying it is the expected modest reply. Remember this word for teasing, magazines, and dating talk." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "Because this word looks like the potential form of 「持つ|もつ」, context has to do all the work. 「この荷物は重くて持てません|このにもつはおもくてもてません」 means this luggage is too heavy and I cannot carry it. Nothing to do with romance. The popularity verb almost always comes with a group and 「に」, like popular with women, or stands alone in a teasing comment about a person. So ask yourself: is there something being carried? Then it is to be able to hold. Is a person being admired? Then it is to be popular. Also watch the te-form. 「もって」, with a small 「っ」, is from to hold, as in please hold this. 「もてて」, no small 「っ」, comes from this verb. Listen carefully for that little pause. The wrap-up: same sound, two meanings, and the context of a person being admired is your clue." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It means being popular, almost always romantically, with 「に」 for the admirers. It looks like the potential of 「持つ|もつ」, as if everyone wants to hold on to that person.",
      "It is generally linked to 「持つ|もつ」 and to 「もてはやす」, making a fuss over someone, though the details are unsettled. Today it is often written 「モテる」 in katakana.",
      "Pop culture loves it: 「モテモテ」 is wildly popular and 「モテ期|もてき」 is a sudden popular phase. When teased with it, the expected modest reply is 「いえいえ、ぜんぜん」.",
      "If something is being carried, it is can hold, but if a person is admired, it is popular. Also listen for 「もって」 with a small pause versus 「もてて」 without."
    ],
    ja: "{兄|あに}は{女|おんな}の{人|ひと}に[もてます|もてます|もてる]。",
    en: "My older brother is popular with women." },
  "はれる|晴れる": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is the verb version of 「晴れ|はれ」, sunny, from lesson eight, and it uses the same native reading. Look at the kanji. It is one of the easy ones to break apart. On the left is 「日|ひ」, the sun. On the right is 「青|あお」, blue. Sun plus blue gives you a clear blue sky with the sun shining. The noun names the weather. This verb describes the change, the sky clearing up and becoming sunny. It works just like 「雨|あめ」 and 「くもり」 in weather talk, and it is often used with the forecast. A typical pattern is it will probably be sunny tomorrow, using 「でしょう」, the way weather forecasters talk. Another common one is wishing for good weather with 「といいですね」, I hope it is sunny. Since the verb is a change, the past tense means it cleared up, and the 「ている」 form means it is clear now. A small extra: the verb also has a figurative use. Feelings or doubts can clear up too, like a heavy mood lifting. A cultural note: before school trips or sports days, Japanese children hang a little cloth doll in the window to wish for sunny weather. Recap: sun plus blue, the sky becoming clear and sunny." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The sun and blue kanji shows up in more places than the weather report. Its Chinese-origin reading is 「せい」, and forecasters use it in 「快晴|かいせい」, perfectly clear weather with almost no clouds. 「晴天|せいてん」 means a clear sky. With the native reading you get 「晴れ着|はれぎ」, your best clothes for a special day, like the kimono young women wear to the coming of age ceremony. Then there is 「素晴らしい|すばらしい」, wonderful, which is written with this same kanji, a nice reminder that clear and bright feel good. The opposite of this verb is 「曇る|くもる」, to cloud over, the verb behind 「くもり」, cloudy. And there is a fun pair of nicknames: 「晴れ男|はれおとこ」 and 「晴れ女|はれおんな」, a man or woman who seems to bring sunshine wherever they go. To sum up: one bright kanji, from forecasts to fine clothes to wonderful." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "In Japanese folklore studies there is a famous pair of ideas, 「ハレ」 and 「ケ」. The folklorist Yanagita Kunio described them early in the twentieth century. 「ケ」 is ordinary everyday life, the regular days of work and plain meals. 「ハレ」, from the same root as this verb, is the special, festive time: festivals, weddings, New Year, the days when you wear your best clothes and eat special food. That is why a big special occasion is called 「晴れの日|はれのひ」, and a grand stage or a big moment in the spotlight is 「晴れ舞台|はればぶたい」. So clearing skies and festive days share a word. The idea is that something bright breaks through the ordinary. Next time you hear this verb in a forecast, remember that it also carries the feeling of a special day." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a group two verb and it is intransitive. The sky clears up by itself. Because it describes a change, the tenses work in a special way. 「晴れる|はれる」 talks about the future or a general truth. 「晴れました|はれました」 means it cleared up, the change happened. 「晴れています|はれています」 means it is clear right now, the result of that change is still in place. Do not use the plain present to describe the sky outside your window right now; use the 「ている」 form. There is also a transitive partner, 「晴らす|はらす」, to clear something away. You will meet it in phrases like clearing up a suspicion, or 「気を晴らす|きをはらす」, cheering yourself up by clearing your mood. Its intransitive twin appears in 「気が晴れる|きがはれる」, to feel refreshed. The key point: use the 「ている」 form for sunny now." }
    ],
    short: [
      "The kanji is 「日|ひ」, sun, plus 「青|あお」, blue, a clear sunny sky. As the verb of 「晴れ|はれ」 it describes the sky clearing, and feelings can clear up too.",
      "The kanji reads 「せい」 in 「快晴|かいせい」, perfectly clear weather, and appears in 「晴れ着|はれぎ」, best clothes, and even 「素晴らしい|すばらしい」, wonderful.",
      "Folklore pairs 「ハレ」, festive special days, with 「ケ」, ordinary life, which is why a big occasion is 「晴れの日|はれのひ」 and a moment in the spotlight is 「晴れ舞台|はればぶたい」.",
      "It is a change verb, so use 「晴れています|はれています」 for sunny right now and 「晴れました|はれました」 for it cleared up. Its transitive partner is 「晴らす|はらす」."
    ],
    ja: "{明日|あした}は[晴れる|はれる]でしょう。",
    en: "It will probably be sunny tomorrow." },
  "ごちそうする|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You already know the end of a meal phrase, 「ごちそうさまでした」, thank you for the meal. This verb comes from the same word, 「ごちそう」, which means a feast or a nice meal. It can be written with two kanji that both mean to run around. The traditional explanation is that a host would run around gathering the best things to serve a guest, so the word came to mean the hospitality and the food itself. Add 「する」, and you get to treat someone to a meal. In practice it usually means paying for someone's food or drink. The pattern is to treat a person with 「に」, and the food with 「を」. The other side of the treat is 「ごちそうになる」, to be treated. That is very common too, like when a boss pays for dinner. A cultural note: in Japan, the senior person, like a boss or an older friend, often pays for the younger one. The younger person shows thanks by saying the end-of-meal phrase directly to the person who paid, and again the next day. A common mistake English speakers make is thinking 「ごちそうさま」 is just for the cook. It is also how you thank the person who paid. Recap: the host running around to prepare a feast, now meaning to treat someone to a meal." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "When written in full, this word is 「ご馳走|ごちそう」. The first piece is just the polite prefix 「ご」, the same one in 「ご飯|ごはん」 and 「ご家族|ごかぞく」. The next two kanji both involve running. The second one, 「走|そう」, is the kanji you know from 「走る|はしる」, to run. Here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. The first one, 「馳|ち」, has the horse radical on its left, and it means to gallop or rush about. A horse galloping plus running: the whole idea is dashing around. The usual explanation is that a host once rushed about, maybe on horseback, to gather the best food for a guest. That busy effort became the word for the feast itself. You rarely need to write these kanji, and most people write the word in kana. But knowing them makes the thanks at the end of a meal much richer. In short: a polite prefix plus galloping and running." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "There are two common ways to say I will treat you. This verb is the polite, warm one. The other is 「おごる」, which is casual and used among friends and coworkers. A senior coworker might say 「今日はおごるよ|きょうはおごるよ」, it is on me today. To someone you respect, you might say 「今日はわたしにごちそうさせてください|きょうはわたしにごちそうさせてください」, please let me treat you today. And when nobody is treating, the group splits the bill, which is 「割り勘|わりかん」. A typical exchange after dinner: the boss pays, and the junior says 「ごちそうになりました」, thank you for treating me. The next morning, they say it again: 「昨日はごちそうさまでした|きのうはごちそうさまでした」. Forgetting that second thanks is a small but noticed slip. The main idea: polite treating with this verb, casual treating with 「おごる」." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Notice the direction of giving here, because it trips up learners. With this verb, the subject is the one who pays. 「わたしは友だちにごちそうしました|わたしはともだちにごちそうしました」, I treated my friend. To say you were the one treated, switch to 「ごちそうになる」, literally to become someone's treat. The giver now takes 「に」: 「部長にごちそうになりました|ぶちょうにごちそうになりました」, I was treated by the department manager. You can also combine the verb with giving and receiving patterns. 「ごちそうしてくれました」 means someone kindly treated me. And there is a fun joking use of the noun. When a friend gushes on and on about their sweet romance, you can say 「ごちそうさま」, as if to say thanks, I am full now. The wrap-up: this verb when you pay, 「ごちそうになる」 when you are paid for." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from 「ごちそう」, a feast, from kanji for running around, as a host gathers the best food. It means treating someone, and 「ごちそうさま」 also thanks whoever paid.",
      "Written 「ご馳走|ごちそう」, it is the polite 「ご」 plus 「馳|ち」, a horse galloping, and 「走|そう」, running, a host dashing around to gather food for a guest.",
      "This verb is the polite way to treat, while 「おごる」 is casual. After being treated, say 「ごちそうになりました」, and thank them again the next day.",
      "The subject of this verb pays, while 「ごちそうになる」 means you were treated, with the payer marked by 「に」. Jokingly, 「ごちそうさま」 also answers gushing romance talk."
    ],
    ja: "{友|とも}だちに{晩|ばん}ごはんを[ごちそうしました|ごちそうしました|ごちそうする]。",
    en: "I treated my friend to dinner." },
  "えんりょする|遠慮する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「遠|えん」, means far. Its native form is the word for far away. It has the road mark, the movement radical on the bottom left, just like its opposite, 「近い|ちかい」, near, from lesson thirteen. The second kanji, 「慮|りょ」, means careful thought or consideration. Look at its lower half. It holds 「思|おも」, the kanji for to think, with a heart at the bottom. So the word is far-reaching thought. Thinking ahead about others leads you to hold back, to not take too much, not speak up, not impose. That is the meaning: to hold back out of consideration, or to refrain. This is a central idea in Japanese manners. A guest may hold back from taking the last piece of food. That is why hosts often say 「遠慮しないでください|えんりょしないでください」, please do not hold back, or 「遠慮なく|えんりょなく」, help yourself. You also see it on signs, in the very polite form please refrain, for things like smoking or phone calls. And it is a soft way to decline an offer, saying I will hold back this time. A common mistake is to read it as shy. It is about consideration for others. Recap: thinking far ahead about others, and so holding back." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This word has a surprisingly old and serious history. It goes back to the Analects of Confucius, the collection of his sayings from ancient China. One line says, roughly, a person without far-reaching thought will surely have troubles close at hand. The phrase for far-reaching thought there is written with these same two kanji. So originally the word meant thinking far ahead, planning with foresight. Over time in Japanese, the focus shifted from planning ahead to thinking ahead about how your actions affect others. And from there it came to mean holding yourself back, being reserved, not imposing. So the drift went from long-range planning, to consideration, to restraint. The two kanji still hold the old meaning clearly: far plus careful thought. A good summary: a word that started as foresight in Chinese philosophy and became good manners in Japan." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "Picture a shared plate of fried chicken at a Japanese dinner party. Everyone eats happily until one piece is left. Then nobody touches it. Everyone is holding back, and the lonely piece sits there. In the Kansai region around Osaka, people even have a name for it: 「遠慮のかたまり|えんりょのかたまり」, the lump of reserve. Someone usually breaks the spell with a laugh, or the host pushes it onto a guest's plate. That is why hosts so often say 「遠慮なく|えんりょなく」, without holding back, as they offer food. And guests often decline once before accepting, so the dance of offering and refusing can go back and forth. It is not fake politeness. It is a way of showing that you are not greedy and that you are thinking about the others at the table. Remember the lonely last piece; it shows the whole idea." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "You will meet this word in three everyday situations. First, on signs and announcements, it appears as 「ご遠慮ください|ごえんりょください」, please refrain. On a train you may hear that phone calls are to be refrained from, and a shop door might say please refrain from bringing food inside. It is a soft way to say do not. Second, it is a gentle way to say no to an offer. If someone invites you for a second drink, you can say 「今日は遠慮しておきます|きょうはえんりょしておきます」, I will pass this time. That sounds much softer than a flat no. Third, people say 「遠慮します|えんりょします」 to turn down something politely, like an extra helping. In a mini dialogue, a host says 「もう少しどうですか|もうすこしどうですか」, and the guest answers 「いえ、もう十分です|いえ、もうじゅうぶんです」. The key point: on signs it means do not, and in conversation it means no, thank you." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「遠|えん」 is far and 「慮|りょ」 is careful thought, so far-reaching thought about others leads you to hold back. Hosts say 「遠慮なく|えんりょなく」, help yourself.",
      "It goes back to a Confucian saying about far-reaching thought, and in Japanese it drifted from foresight to consideration of others to holding back.",
      "The last piece on a shared plate that nobody takes is called 「遠慮のかたまり|えんりょのかたまり」 in Kansai, a sign of not being greedy and thinking of others.",
      "On signs, 「ご遠慮ください|ごえんりょください」 means please refrain, a soft do not, and 「今日は遠慮しておきます|きょうはえんりょしておきます」 is a gentle no, thank you."
    ],
    ja: "どうぞ[遠慮しないで|えんりょしないで|えんりょする]ください。",
    en: "Please don't hold back." },
  "しょうたいする|招待する": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji, both with Chinese-origin readings, and both have pieces you know. The first, 「招|しょう」, means to beckon or invite. Look at it. On the left is the hand mark. On the right is 「召|め」, the same part that begins 「召し上がる|めしあがる」 in this lesson, and it means to summon. A hand beckoning someone to come. The second kanji, 「待|たい」, is the wait kanji from 「待つ|まつ」, to wait, in lesson four. Here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. So the word is to beckon someone and wait for them to arrive. That is a formal invitation. The pattern is to invite a person with 「を」, to an event or a place with 「に」. Use it for weddings, parties, dinners, events, and anything with an official invitation. The written invitation card uses this same word plus a kanji for letter. Compare it with 「呼ぶ|よぶ」, also in this lesson. That one is the everyday, casual way to invite friends over, or to call someone. This one is more formal and is the word you see in writing. A common mistake is to use it for a quick casual invite to hang out. That would sound oddly stiff. Recap: a hand beckoning plus waiting for the guest, a formal invitation." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "You have probably seen the beckoning cat, 「招き猫|まねきねこ」, in Japanese and Chinese restaurants, with one paw raised. That first kanji, 「招|まね」, is the same one that starts this word, here with its native reading, from the verb 「招く|まねく」, to beckon. The cat is waving customers and good fortune in. People commonly say a raised right paw invites money and a raised left paw invites customers, though shops interpret it in different ways. There is a twist for English speakers. The cat's paw is curled downward, because the Japanese gesture for come here is done with the palm facing down, fingers waving toward you. To many Westerners that looks like shooing someone away. So if a Japanese person waves at you palm down, they are inviting you over, not sending you off. Remember: the beckoning cat, palm down, is calling you in." },
      { title: "Weddings and invitations", text: "The most famous use of this word is the wedding invitation, 「招待状|しょうたいじょう」. It usually comes with a reply card, and there is a charming rule about filling it out. The card is printed with polite prefixes meant for you, the honored guest, like 「ご出席|ごしゅっせき」, your attendance. When you reply, you draw neat lines through those honorific bits, because you should not honor yourself. Then you circle attend and add a short congratulation. If you go, you bring a gift of money in a special envelope called 「ご祝儀|ごしゅうぎ」, with fresh bills to show you prepared for the day. Being invited is formal, so the reply is formal too. One more note: the guest list is carefully decided, and seats are assigned by name. To sum up: this word means a formal invitation, and Japanese weddings show its full formality." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This is a noun plus 「する」 verb, so it conjugates like 「する」: 「招待します|しょうたいします」, 「招待した|しょうたいした」, 「招待して|しょうたいして」. The noun can also stand alone. 「ご招待|ごしょうたい」 with the polite prefix means your kind invitation, and you will hear 「ご招待ありがとうございます|ごしょうたいありがとうございます」, thank you for the invitation. You can also use it in the 「ている」 form for an invitation that is set up already. Later in Genki you will learn the passive, which lets you say I was invited, 「招待されました|しょうたいされました」. Japanese people often prefer that version when talking about themselves, because the focus is on the honor of being invited. Combined with this lesson's patterns, you can ask very politely whether someone would like to come. In short: a regular 「する」 verb, with a polite noun form for thanking the host." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「招|しょう」 is a hand beckoning, with the summon part from 「召し上がる|めしあがる」, and 「待|たい」 is waiting, so you beckon a guest and wait for them, a formal invitation.",
      "The beckoning cat 「招き猫|まねきねこ」 uses the same first kanji. Its paw curls down because the Japanese gesture for come here is palm down, not a shooing motion.",
      "A wedding 「招待状|しょうたいじょう」 comes with a reply card, and you cross out honorifics like 「ご出席|ごしゅっせき」 meant for you, since you should not honor yourself.",
      "It is a regular 「する」 verb, and the noun gives 「ご招待ありがとうございます|ごしょうたいありがとうございます」. People often prefer the passive 「招待されました|しょうたいされました」 about themselves."
    ],
    ja: "{友|とも}だちを{結婚式|けっこんしき}に[招待しました|しょうたいしました|しょうたいする]。",
    en: "I invited my friend to my wedding." },
  "はなしをする|話をする": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is just the noun 「話|はなし」, a talk or a story, also in this lesson, plus 「をする」, to do. So it means to do a talk, or to have a talk. The kanji 「話|はなし」 has the speech radical on its left, the same as 「言う|いう」, and it uses its native reading. So how is it different from the verb 「話す|はなす」, to speak, from lesson three? The verb is about the act of speaking. You use it for languages, like speaking Japanese, and for speaking in general. This phrase is about having a conversation or discussion with someone, and especially about a topic. You can put the topic in front with 「の」. For example, to talk about the future. You talk with a person using 「と」. You will hear it in serious moments, like wanting to have a talk with someone, which can sound a little ominous, the same way it does in English. You can also put other words in front of the noun, like an interesting talk or a long talk, which you cannot do as easily with the verb. A common mistake English speakers make is to use this phrase for languages. You do not have a talk Japanese. You speak Japanese with 「話す|はなす」. Recap: the noun for talk plus 「する」, to have a talk or conversation about something." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The talk kanji, with the speech radical on its left, appears in many words you know or will meet soon. With its Chinese-origin reading 「わ」, it gives 「電話|でんわ」, telephone, literally electric talk. 「会話|かいわ」 is conversation, meeting plus talk, the word on your textbook's dialogue pages. 「世話|せわ」 means care or looking after someone, as in the set phrase 「お世話になります|おせわになります」, thank you for taking care of me. With the native reading you have 「話|はなし」, a talk or story, the verb 「話す|はなす」, and the compound verb 「話し合う|はなしあう」, to discuss something together, talk it over. Notice the small difference in writing. The noun usually has no extra kana after the kanji, while the verb adds 「す」. The wrap-up: one speech kanji, from phones to conversations to looking after someone." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "When the noun 「はなし」 is attached to the end of another word, its first sound often softens from 「は」 to 「ば」. This is a regular Japanese sound change called 「連濁|れんだく」. So an old tale, a folk story like Momotaro, is 「昔話|むかしばなし」, an old-days story. A chat while standing in the street is 「立ち話|たちばなし」, a standing talk. A ghost story is 「怖い話|こわいはなし」, but notice that here there is no softening, because it is an adjective plus a noun, not a single compound word. A funny story is 「笑い話|わらいばなし」. When you hear 「ばなし」 at the end of a word, you can now guess it means some kind of story or talk. It is the same word, just with softer sound at the joint. In short: 「はなし」 alone, and often 「ばなし」 at the end of a compound." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "The noun 「話|はなし」 does a lot of work in set phrases. 「話がある|はなしがある」 means I have something to talk to you about. When someone says 「ちょっと話があるんだけど|ちょっとはなしがあるんだけど」, you might feel a little nervous, just like with we need to talk in English. 「話を聞く|はなしをきく」 means to listen to someone's story or to hear them out, a kind thing to offer a friend who is upset. 「話が違う|はなしがちがう」, literally the talk is different, means that is not what we agreed. And 「話が合う|はなしがあう」 means two people get along well and can talk easily, they are on the same wavelength. Each of these uses the noun with a particle, not the verb 「話す|はなす」. So hold onto the noun as a thing you can have, hear, and match. The key point: having a talk is just one of many phrases built on this handy noun." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is the noun 「話|はなし」 plus 「をする」, having a talk about a topic with someone, unlike 「話す|はなす」, the act of speaking, which you use for languages.",
      "The speech kanji reads 「わ」 in 「電話|でんわ」, 「会話|かいわ」, and 「世話|せわ」, and reads 「はなし」 in the noun, which usually has no extra kana, while the verb adds 「す」.",
      "At the end of a compound, 「はなし」 often softens to 「ばなし」, as in 「昔話|むかしばなし」 and 「立ち話|たちばなし」, but not after an adjective, as in 「怖い話|こわいはなし」.",
      "The noun builds set phrases: 「話がある|はなしがある」, I need to talk to you, 「話を聞く|はなしをきく」, to hear someone out, and 「話が合う|はなしがあう」, to click."
    ],
    ja: "{先生|せんせい}と{将来|しょうらい}の[話をしました|はなしをしました|はなしをする]。",
    en: "I had a talk with my teacher about my future." },
  "おととい|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This native word is usually written in kana at this level. In kanji it is 「一昨日|おととい」, a special reading where the kanji mean one, previous, and day. Look at the middle kanji. It is the same one that starts 「昨日|きのう」, yesterday, from lesson four. So the kanji spell out one day before yesterday. The word itself comes from an older phrase meaning the distant day. It fits into a neat set of day words you know. Today is 「今日|きょう」. Yesterday is 「昨日|きのう」. Tomorrow is 「明日|あした」. The day after tomorrow is 「あさって」, from lesson eight. And this word fills the last slot, the day before yesterday. There is also a matching word for years, 「おととし」, the year before last. Notice the shared start. A way to remember it: both point two steps back in time. Like other time words such as yesterday and tomorrow, it does not take the particle 「に」. You just put it at the start of the sentence. A common mistake English speakers make is to say yesterday's yesterday, but Japanese has its own word, and using it sounds much more natural. Recap: the day before yesterday, partner of 「あさって」, written with the same kanji that begins 「昨日|きのう」." },
      { title: "Formal twins", text: "Most of the everyday day words have stiff, formal twins that use Chinese-origin readings. You will hear them in business, on the news, and in polite announcements. Yesterday, 「昨日|きのう」, can be read 「さくじつ」 in formal speech. This word, written 「一昨日|おととい」, has the formal reading 「いっさくじつ」, one before yesterday. On the future side, tomorrow becomes 「明日|みょうにち」, and the day after tomorrow becomes 「明後日|みょうごにち」. A hotel clerk or a company email might use these. In daily conversation, though, stick with the native words. There is also a fun extension: three days ago is 「さきおととい」, literally the day before the day before yesterday, and Japanese people do use it, a bit playfully. So the day words come in two styles, soft native ones for chatting and crisp formal ones for business. The wrap-up: say this word with friends and 「いっさくじつ」 at work." },
      { title: "Sound alike", text: "This word lives in a crowded neighborhood of similar sounds, so give it extra care. 「おととい」, the day before yesterday, ends in 「い」. 「おととし」, the year before last, ends in 「し」. And 「おとうと」, younger brother, has a long 「とう」 in the middle and ends in 「と」. Say them slowly side by side: 「おととい」, 「おととし」, 「おとうと」. The first two have short, quick 「と」 sounds, three beats each after the 「お」. The brother word stretches the middle. A good anchor is the ending. The year word ends with 「とし」, exactly like 「とし」, year, so it is easy to tie to years. That leaves the 「い」 ending for the day. And the brother has that long vowel. The key point: listen for the ending and for the long vowel, and these three will stop blurring together." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a sound-alike memory trick. The word sounds a bit like oh, toe, toy. Picture this. The day before yesterday, you got up in the dark and stepped barefoot on a sharp plastic toy. Oh, toe, toy! You hopped around the room clutching your foot. Today your toe is still a little purple, a souvenir from two days ago. Yesterday it throbbed, and the day before yesterday was the day of the disaster. So whenever you need the day before yesterday, picture that purple toe and the toy that did it: oh, toe, toy, two days back. Just remember that the word itself ends in a crisp 「い」 sound, not toy with a full English oy. The wrap-up: the toy you stepped on two days ago reminds you of the day before yesterday." }
    ],
    short: [
      "In kanji it is 「一昨日|おととい」, one day before 「昨日|きのう」, completing the set with 「あさって」. Like other time words, it takes no 「に」.",
      "Formal speech uses Chinese-origin twins, so this word becomes 「いっさくじつ」 at work, alongside 「さくじつ」 and 「みょうにち」. Stick with the native word among friends.",
      "Watch the endings: 「おととい」 ends in 「い」 for the day, 「おととし」 ends in 「とし」 like year, and 「おとうと」, younger brother, has a long middle vowel.",
      "Picture stepping barefoot on a sharp toy two days ago and yelling oh, toe, toy, with your toe still purple today, but end the word on a crisp 「い」."
    ],
    ja: "[おととい]{友|とも}だちと{映画|えいが}をみました。",
    en: "I saw a movie with a friend the day before yesterday." },
  "まいあさ|毎朝": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "Two kanji you already know, but with a mix of readings. The first, 「毎|まい」, means every, and it uses its Chinese-origin reading. You have it in 「毎日|まいにち」, every day, 「毎晩|まいばん」, every night, and 「毎週|まいしゅう」, every week. The second, 「朝|あさ」, morning, uses its native reading, just like in 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, breakfast. Its shape has the sun on the left with a little grass above and below it, and the moon on the right. A way to remember it: the sun rising while the moon is still in the sky. So every plus morning gives every morning. It is a little unusual to mix a Chinese-origin reading with a native one in one word, so be careful not to say it with the other reading of the morning kanji. Watch out for a similar-looking word, 「今朝|けさ」, this morning. That one has a special reading, and it is not built the same way. Like 「毎日|まいにち」, this word does not take 「に」. You just put it at the start of the sentence, and then say what you do. Many Japanese people's every morning includes a quick breakfast and a crowded train. Recap: every plus morning, built the same way as 「毎日|まいにち」 and 「毎晩|まいばん」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The every kanji, 「毎|まい」, builds a whole set of routine words with the same Chinese-origin reading. 「毎日|まいにち」 every day, 「毎晩|まいばん」 every night, 「毎週|まいしゅう」 every week, 「毎月|まいつき」 every month, and 「毎年|まいとし」 every year, which can also be read 「まいねん」. Notice that some of these mix readings like this word does, with the second kanji in its native form. The morning kanji also has a family. With its native reading you have 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」 and 「朝日|あさひ」, the morning sun. With its Chinese-origin reading 「ちょう」, you get 「朝食|ちょうしょく」, a more formal word for breakfast that you see on hotel signs. And 「朝寝坊|あさねぼう」 means oversleeping, a word you will want on bad mornings. So two kanji, two families, and this word sits right where they meet. In short: every plus a time word makes a routine." },
      { title: "Culture note", text: "What does every morning look like in Japan? For some, it begins with a traditional breakfast: rice, miso soup, grilled fish, pickles, and maybe natto, fermented soybeans. Many others just grab toast and coffee, or a rice ball from a convenience store on the way to the station. Morning trains in big cities are famously crowded. Many people also start the day with television. The national broadcaster airs a short drama every weekday morning, known as 「朝ドラ|あさどら」, and millions follow each series for months. And there is 「ラジオ体操|らじおたいそう」, a set of radio exercises broadcast since the early Showa era. Children in summer vacation gather in parks early in the morning to do it together, collecting stamps on a card. So this simple word can call up rice and miso, packed trains, a morning drama, and kids stretching in the park. The wrap-up: every morning in Japan has its own rituals." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Frequency words like this one behave in a special way. They do not take the particle 「に」, so you never say every morning plus 「に」. They usually come near the start of the sentence, either before or after the topic. To describe an ongoing habit, Japanese often uses the 「ている」 form, which you learned in lesson seven. 「毎朝走っています|まいあさはしっています」 means I run every morning, as a regular habit these days. The plain 「ます」 form works too, but 「ている」 gives a sense of a current routine. You can also add 「は」 to make a contrast: 「毎朝はいそがしいです|まいあさはいそがしいです」, mornings are busy, at least, unlike evenings. And it pairs nicely with 「ようにする」, which you will meet later, to say you make a point of doing something every morning. To sum up: no 「に」, and use 「ている」 for a habit." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「毎|まい」 is every, with its Chinese-origin reading, and 「朝|あさ」 is morning, with its native reading, built like 「毎日|まいにち」. Like 「毎日|まいにち」, it takes no 「に」.",
      "「毎|まい」 builds routines like 「毎晩|まいばん」 and 「毎週|まいしゅう」, while the morning kanji gives 「朝ご飯|あさごはん」, formal 「朝食|ちょうしょく」, and 「朝寝坊|あさねぼう」.",
      "Japanese mornings range from rice and miso soup to a convenience store rice ball, plus the morning TV drama 「朝ドラ|あさどら」 and kids doing 「ラジオ体操|らじおたいそう」 in the park.",
      "Frequency words take no 「に」, and the 「ている」 form shows a current habit, as in 「毎朝走っています|まいあさはしっています」, I run every morning."
    ],
    ja: "[毎朝|まいあさ]コーヒーをのみます。",
    en: "I drink coffee every morning." },
  "それで|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This connecting word is built from two simple parts. 「それ」 is that, and 「で」 is the particle that can mean by means of or because of. So literally it is because of that, or with that. It links two sentences, where the first one explains how the second came about. It translates as so, and so, or then. For example: I overslept. And so, I was late for school. It has two close cousins you know. 「だから」, from lesson four, also means so, but it sounds stronger, more like a firm conclusion, and it can sound pushy, like I told you so. This word is softer and more like telling a story, step by step, explaining what led to what. And 「それから」, from lesson four, means and then, just the next thing in time, without saying the first caused the second. This word, by contrast, hints that one thing led to the other. A very common use in conversation is to say it with a rising tone as a question, meaning and then what happened? It shows you are listening and want the rest of the story. A common mistake English speakers make is using it for every and. If there is no cause, use 「それから」 instead. Recap: because of that, the gentle so that links a reason to what happened next." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word belongs to a family of connectors that all start with 「それ」, that. Each adds a different little particle, and that changes the meaning. 「それから」, that plus from, means and then, the next event in time. 「それに」, that plus to, means on top of that, adding another reason. 「それでも」, that plus even, means even so, or still, showing a result you would not expect. 「それでは」 means well then, a way to move on or wrap up, and its casual short form is 「じゃ」 or 「では」. And this one, with 「で」, means because of that, and so. Think of the particles as little arrows. 「から」 points onward in time. 「に」 piles things on. 「でも」 pushes against. 「で」 shows the cause. Once you see the pattern, you can build these connectors yourself. The key point: one 「それ」, many little arrows that give each connector its job." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "Here is a rule that separates this word from 「だから」. After this word, the second sentence normally describes a fact or result that has already happened, or a natural outcome. It does not usually introduce a command, a request, an invitation, or your own decision. So it is natural to say: I overslept, and so I was late. But to say it is raining, so let's take a taxi, you would use 「だから」 or 「から」, not this word, because let's take a taxi is a suggestion. In the same way, you would not use it before please be quiet. Think of this word as a storyteller explaining how things turned out, not someone telling others what to do. In conversation, when you hear it, you can expect a result. And when you use it, check that what follows is a fact. The wrap-up: this word leads to results, not to requests or decisions." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Listen to Japanese conversations, and you will hear this word doing two jobs. The first is the listener's prompt. A friend is telling you about a terrible date, pauses, and you say 「それで？」, so then what? It shows you are hooked. Asked with a rising tone, it pushes the story forward. The second job is the speaker picking the thread back up after a side comment, like saying anyway, so in English. Someone says, I went to Kyoto, oh, the weather was awful, and 「それで」, so, I visited a temple. There is also a handy set phrase, 「それでいいです」, that will be fine, or that works. A clerk asks if a smaller size is okay, and you say that is fine. Notice how gentle it sounds. To sum up: a soft so, a curious and then, and a quick that works." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「それ」 is that and 「で」 shows cause, so it is a soft so that links a reason to what happened. It is gentler than 「だから」, and unlike 「それから」 it implies cause.",
      "It belongs to the 「それ」 connectors, where each particle is an arrow: 「それから」, and then, 「それに」, on top of that, 「それでも」, even so, and this one for cause.",
      "What follows it should be a fact or result, not a command, request, or suggestion. For let's take a taxi, use 「だから」 or 「から」 instead.",
      "Listeners say 「それで？」 to ask what happened next, speakers use it to pick the story back up, and 「それでいいです」 means that will be fine."
    ],
    ja: "{朝|あさ}ねぼうしました。[それで]{学校|がっこう}に{遅|おく}れました。",
    en: "I overslept. So I was late for school." },
  "なぜ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is another word for why, next to the one you learned in lesson four, 「どうして」. It can be written with two kanji, 「何故|なぜ」, which mean what and reason. You know the first one from 「何|なに」, what. So it is literally for what reason, which matches the meaning. The difference between the why words is mostly about tone. 「なんで」 is casual, the word friends use. 「どうして」 is the everyday neutral one, and it can sound gentle, even a little emotional. This word is more formal and logical. It shows up in writing, in news, in essays, in school questions, and in serious or polite talk. A teacher might ask it, and a textbook would use it in a question asking why something happened. When you answer, you usually explain with 「から」 or with 「んです」, just as you would with 「どうして」. A common mistake for English speakers: asking why directly can sound like a challenge in any language. In Japanese, softening it with 「んですか」 at the end makes it gentler, so the person does not feel accused. A way to remember the word: think of a curious child asking the formal question, now why is that? Recap: what plus reason, the more formal, logical way to ask why." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "In kanji, this word is written 「何故|なぜ」, and the reading belongs to the word as a whole, not to each kanji separately. The first kanji is 「何|なに」, what. The second, 「故|こ」, means reason, cause, or sometimes old or deceased. It has the strike or action mark on its right side, and 「古|ふる」, old, on its left, which also gives its sound. Its reading here would normally be 「こ」, and you will meet it in 「故障|こしょう」, a breakdown, as when a machine stops working, and 「故郷|こきょう」, your old hometown. Japanese borrowed the Chinese way of writing what reason and simply laid its own native word on top. Most people write this word in kana in everyday writing, but you will see the kanji in novels and essays. In short: what plus reason, with a native word laid over the top." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "This word has some handy relatives. 「なぜなら」 means the reason is, or because, and it starts an answer. It usually ends with 「から」 or 「からです」: 「なぜなら、時間がないからです|なぜなら、じかんがないからです」, because there is no time. It sounds formal and logical, perfect for essays and speeches. 「なぜか」, with the question particle added, means for some reason, or somehow. 「なぜか眠いです|なぜかねむいです」, for some reason, I am sleepy. The same trick works with 「どうしてか」 and 「なんでか」 too. Speaking of those, here is the full ladder of why. 「なんで」 is casual, 「どうして」 is everyday, and this word is the formal top rung. A news anchor asks this word, a friend asks 「なんで」. So choose by situation. The wrap-up: this word asks, 「なぜなら」 answers, and 「なぜか」 shrugs." },
      { title: "Memory scene", text: "Here is a sound-alike memory trick. This word sounds a bit like nah, zay. Imagine a very serious professor in a tweed jacket, standing at a chalkboard. A student says, the answer is obvious. The professor slowly removes his glasses, shakes his head, and says nah, then points at the student and says, say why. Nah, say why. That is his whole teaching method: he refuses every answer until you explain the reason. Logical, formal, demanding, exactly the tone of this word. Whenever you need a formal why, picture the professor saying nah, say why, chalk dust floating in the air. Keep the Japanese vowels short and clean, two beats only, with the stress fairly even. The wrap-up: the stern professor who always asks you to say why." }
    ],
    short: [
      "Written 「何故|なぜ」, what plus reason, it is the formal, logical why, used in writing, news, and serious talk. Soften a direct question with 「んですか」.",
      "In 「何故|なぜ」 the reading belongs to the whole word. 「故|こ」 means reason, as in 「故障|こしょう」 and 「故郷|こきょう」, with a native word laid on top.",
      "「なぜなら」 starts a formal answer ending in 「から」, and 「なぜか」 means for some reason. On the why ladder, 「なんで」 is casual, 「どうして」 everyday, and this one formal.",
      "Picture a stern professor who refuses every answer, saying nah, then pointing and demanding, say why. Nah, say why gives the formal 「なぜ」, two short beats."
    ],
    ja: "[なぜ]{日本語|にほんご}をべんきょうしていますか。",
    en: "Why are you studying Japanese?" },
  "ほんとうは|本当は": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "You know the core of this word well. 「本当|ほんとう」 means true or real, from 「本当ですか|ほんとうですか」, really, and 「本当に|ほんとうに」, really, in lesson eighteen. Both kanji use their Chinese-origin readings. The first, 「本|ほん」, is the book kanji, but its original meaning is a root or origin. It is a tree with a line marking the root at the bottom. The second, 「当|とう」, means to hit the mark, to be right. So root plus hitting the mark gives the real truth. Now add the topic particle 「は」. In Japanese, 「は」 often sets up a contrast. So this word means as for the truth, in contrast to what it seemed or what was planned. It translates as actually, in fact, or the truth is. It is used to confess something or to reveal what is really going on. For example, you said you were busy, but actually you just did not want to go. Or you planned to be a doctor, but originally you wanted to be a singer. That is where the meaning originally comes from. A common mistake English speakers make is to use it like really for emphasis. For emphasis, use 「本当に|ほんとうに」. This word sets up a contrast. Recap: the real truth, set apart with 「は」, means actually or in fact." },
      { title: "Near twin", text: "This word has a close cousin, 「実は|じつは」, and learners often wonder how they differ. Both can be translated as actually. 「実は|じつは」 means to tell you the truth, and it introduces new information the listener did not know, often before a request or a confession. 「実は、来月結婚します|じつは、らいげつけっこんします」, actually, I am getting married next month. This word, on the other hand, sets the truth against something that seems to be true, or against a plan or pretense. 「本当は行きたかったです|ほんとうはいきたかったです」, the truth is I wanted to go, implying I did not. So 「実は|じつは」 opens a reveal, while this word corrects an appearance. A handy test: if you can add but it seemed otherwise, use this word. In short: one reveals, the other contrasts reality with appearance." },
      { title: "Sound and rhythm", text: "Notice the long vowel in the middle. It is 「ほんとう」, with 「と」 stretched, so the word has five beats before 「は」. In casual speech, Japanese people shorten it constantly to 「ほんと」, dropping the long vowel: 「ほんとは」, actually. You will hear 「ほんと？」 as a quick really? and 「ほんとに」 as really, truly. In the Kansai region around Osaka, the same idea is 「ほんま」, so 「ほんまに？」 means really? in Osaka style. In writing, though, and in polite speech, keep the full long vowel. Also remember the particle at the end is written 「は」 but pronounced like 「わ」, as usual for the topic marker. So the polite version flows as five beats plus 「わ」. The wrap-up: full and long in polite speech, short and snappy among friends." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "This word often pairs with patterns that show something did not go as planned or as it looks. A common one is with 「つもり」, intention, and 「けど」: 「本当は行くつもりだったけど|ほんとうはいくつもりだったけど」, I was actually planning to go, but. Another is with 「たい」 in the past: 「本当は医者になりたかったです|ほんとうはいしゃになりたかったです」, I actually wanted to become a doctor. It also fits beautifully with 「んです」, the explaining form: 「本当は、さびしいんです|ほんとうは、さびしいんです」, the truth is, I am lonely. That combination sounds like a heartfelt confession. Because the word implies a contrast, the sentence often trails off with 「けど」 or 「が」, leaving the other side unsaid. The listener fills in the gap. To sum up: pair it with past wishes, plans, and 「んです」 to confess what is really true." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「本当|ほんとう」 is root plus hitting the mark, the real truth, and 「は」 sets up a contrast, so it means actually, in contrast to how things seemed. For emphasis, use 「本当に|ほんとうに」.",
      "「実は|じつは」 opens a reveal of new information, while this word corrects an appearance or plan, as in 「本当は行きたかったです|ほんとうはいきたかったです」, implying you did not go.",
      "Polite speech keeps the long vowel, five beats plus 「は」 said as 「わ」, while friends shorten it to 「ほんとは」, and Osaka says 「ほんま」.",
      "It pairs with past plans, past wishes, and 「んです」 for confessions, as in 「本当は医者になりたかったです|ほんとうはいしゃになりたかったです」, often trailing off with 「けど」."
    ],
    ja: "[本当は|ほんとうは]{行|い}きたくないです。",
    en: "Actually, I don't want to go." },
  "しょうしょう|少々": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "The first kanji, 「少|しょう」, means a little or few, and here it uses its Chinese-origin reading. You know its native readings from 「少ない|すくない」, few, in lesson seventeen, and the everyday word for a little bit. Its shape is 「小|ちい」, small, from 「小さい|ちいさい」, with one extra stroke sweeping down. The second character is not really a kanji. It is a repeat mark that means say the previous kanji again. You also see it in words like people or sometimes. So the word is a little, a little, which means just a bit or just a moment. It is the formal, polite version of 「ちょっと」. That is why it belongs in a lesson about 「敬語|けいご」. You hear it constantly in shops, restaurants, hotels, and on the phone, in the phrase 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, please wait a moment. A clerk says it while checking something, and a receptionist says it when transferring your call. A common mistake learners make is to use it with friends. It sounds stiff in casual talk, so with friends, use 「ちょっと」. Another note: it is about a small amount in general, so you can use it for time and for quantity, like a little salt in a recipe. Recap: a little, said twice, the polite way to ask someone to wait a moment." },
      { title: "The repeat mark", text: "Look closely at the second character. It is not a kanji at all, but a repeat mark meaning write the previous kanji again. Its formal name is 「踊り字|おどりじ」, a dancing character, and many people casually call it 「ノマ」 because it looks like the katakana 「ノ」 squeezed onto 「マ」. You know it from 「時々|ときどき」, sometimes, and you will see it in 「人々|ひとびと」, people, 「色々|いろいろ」, various, and 「我々|われわれ」, we. Notice something in some of these words. When the kanji repeats, the second sound may soften, so 「ひと」 becomes 「ひとびと」 and 「とき」 becomes 「ときどき」. In this word there is no change, just 「しょう」 twice. Because it is not a real kanji, you cannot look the mark up by its own reading, and on a phone you type the whole word to get it. In short: a little dancing mark that saves you writing the same kanji twice." },
      { title: "Politeness ladder", text: "Think of asking someone to wait as a ladder with several rungs. At the bottom, with close friends, you say 「ちょっと待って|ちょっとまって」, wait a sec. One step up, with classmates or coworkers, 「ちょっと待ってください|ちょっとまってください」. Next comes the customer service level: 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, using this word and the honorific request pattern. At the very top, in department stores and hotels, staff add an extra elegant ending: 「少々お待ちくださいませ|しょうしょうおまちくださいませ」. Each rung swaps in a more formal piece. This word replaces 「ちょっと」, and 「お」 plus the stem plus 「ください」 replaces the plain te-form request. You can also ask for a bit of someone's time with 「少々お時間よろしいですか|しょうしょうおじかんよろしいですか」, do you have a moment? The wrap-up: climbing from 「ちょっと」 to this word is how you climb into 「敬語|けいご」." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「少|しょう」 shows up in several words with both kinds of reading. With native readings, you know 「少し|すこし」, a little, and 「少ない|すくない」, few. With the Chinese-origin reading 「しょう」, you get 「少年|しょうねん」, a boy, literally few years, and 「少女|しょうじょ」, a girl. Those two are famous from manga categories, where boys' comics and girls' comics are labeled with these words. Then there is 「多少|たしょう」, more or less, built from many plus few, a neat pair of opposites. Recipes use this word too: 「塩少々|しおしょうしょう」, a little salt, which tells you to add just a pinch to taste. Do not confuse it with 「小|しょう」, small, which shares the same reading and looks like it minus a stroke. One is about size, the other is about amount. To sum up: few years make a boy, and a little salt is just a pinch." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「少|しょう」 means a little and the second mark repeats it, a little a little. It is the polite form of 「ちょっと」, as in 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, but stiff with friends.",
      "The second character is a repeat mark called 「踊り字|おどりじ」, also in 「時々|ときどき」 and 「人々|ひとびと」. Some repeats soften the sound, but here it is just 「しょう」 twice.",
      "Asking someone to wait climbs from 「ちょっと待って|ちょっとまって」 up to 「少々お待ちください|しょうしょうおまちください」, with this word replacing 「ちょっと」, and department stores add 「ませ」.",
      "「少|しょう」 gives 「少年|しょうねん」, a boy, 「少女|しょうじょ」, a girl, and 「多少|たしょう」, more or less. Recipes say 「塩少々|しおしょうしょう」, a pinch of salt."
    ],
    ja: "[少々|しょうしょう]おまちください。",
    en: "Please wait a moment." },
  "〜めいさま|〜名様": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a counter for people, used in honorific speech. It goes after a number. The first kanji, 「名|めい」, uses its Chinese-origin reading and means name. You know its native reading from the word for name in lesson one, and you know this exact reading from 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, from lesson eight, which is literally having a name. Here it counts people by their names, as in how many names are in your party. The second part, 「様|さま」, is the extra-respectful version of 「さん」, the same one as in 「奥様|おくさま」 and 「お客様|おきゃくさま」. So the counter raises up the people being counted. Who uses it? Restaurant and shop staff talking to customers. The moment you walk into a restaurant in Japan, a server will ask how many people are in your party, using this counter with the word for what. It is like party of how many? Here is the important part. You do not use it to answer. Since it is honorific, you would be honoring yourself. You just answer with the ordinary people counter, like one person or two people. The numbers are regular, so a party of one, two, or three is easy. Recap: name plus extra respect, the polite way staff count customers in a party." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "Here is how the scene really plays out at a Japanese restaurant. You walk in, and staff call out 「いらっしゃいませ」. A server approaches and asks 「何名様ですか|なんめいさまですか」, how many in your party? Many people simply hold up fingers and say 「二人です|ふたりです」, two. The server confirms 「二名様ですね|にめいさまですね」 and might ask whether you prefer smoking or non-smoking, or the counter or a table. For one or two people, staff often switch to another polite form, 「お一人様|おひとりさま」 or 「お二人様|おふたりさま」. In fact, 「おひとりさま」 has become a popular word for someone who happily eats, travels, or goes out alone, and some restaurants even advertise that solo diners are welcome. So the honorific counter has spilled into everyday culture. The wrap-up: the server asks with this counter, you answer with plain people words." },
      { title: "Counter family", text: "Japanese has three main ways to count people, and they form a neat politeness scale. The everyday one is 「〜人|にん」, with the special forms 「一人|ひとり」 and 「二人|ふたり」 at the start. Then there is 「〜名|めい」, which is more formal and businesslike. You see it on forms, signs, and notices, like a class limited to twenty people, or the number of staff in a company. It has no special forms, so one person is 「一名|いちめい」, and four is 「四名|よんめい」. At the top is this counter, with 「様|さま」 added to honor customers. So the rule is simple. Use 「〜人|にん」 when you talk about yourself or in daily conversation, 「〜名|めい」 in formal writing or announcements, and this one only when staff are honoring customers. The key point: the same headcount, dressed up at three levels." },
      { title: "Kanji up close", text: "The kanji 「名|な」 is made of two parts. On top is 「夕|ゆう」, evening, the kanji from 「夕方|ゆうがた」. Below is 「口|くち」, mouth. An old Chinese dictionary explains it this way: in the evening dark, you cannot see each other, so you call out your name with your mouth. Scholars debate whether that is the true origin, but it is a lovely picture and easy to remember. The kanji has three readings you will meet. The native 「な」 in 「名前|なまえ」, name. The Chinese-origin 「めい」 in 「有名|ゆうめい」, famous, and in this counter. And a second Chinese-origin reading, 「みょう」, in 「名字|みょうじ」, family name. So in this counter, each person is counted as a name, the way a reservation list is a list of names. In short: evening plus mouth, calling out your name, now counting heads politely." }
    ],
    short: [
      "「名|めい」 is name, as in 「有名|ゆうめい」, and 「様|さま」 is extra respect, so staff count customers by name. Answer with plain 「二人です|ふたりです」, never this counter.",
      "A server asks 「何名様ですか|なんめいさまですか」, you hold up fingers and say 「二人です|ふたりです」. For one person they say 「お一人様|おひとりさま」, now also a word for happily doing things alone.",
      "People are counted at three levels: everyday 「〜人|にん」, formal 「〜名|めい」 on forms and notices, and this honorific counter only when staff honor customers.",
      "The kanji 「名|な」 is 「夕|ゆう」, evening, over 「口|くち」, mouth, calling out your name in the dark, though scholars debate that origin. Here each person counts as a name."
    ],
    ja: "[二名様|にめいさま|〜めいさま]ですか。",
    en: "A party of two?" },
  "ようこそ|": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This is a warm, set greeting meaning welcome, and it is written in kana. It comes from an old phrase meaning you have come so well, roughly how good of you to come. You say it to someone arriving at a place, like a home, a city, a country, or an event. The usual pattern puts the place first with the particle 「へ」, as in welcome to Japan, 「ようこそ日本へ|ようこそにほんへ」. You will see it on signs at airports, stations, and tourist spots. You can also make it more respectful with this lesson's honorific verb, by adding the past polite form of 「いらっしゃる」, which means welcome, how kind of you to come. A host might greet an honored guest that way. Here is a common mix-up for English speakers. Shop staff greet you with 「いらっしゃいませ」, also translated as welcome, but that is different. That is used for customers entering a shop or restaurant, and you do not reply to it. This word is for welcoming a visitor or guest to a place, often with real feeling. Another note: it is not used to answer thank you. English uses you are welcome for that, but in Japanese you would say something else. Recap: you have come so well, the warm welcome to a place, often followed by the place and 「へ」." },
      { title: "Origin story", text: "This greeting comes from 「よく」, well, plus 「こそ」, a particle that adds strong emphasis, something like indeed or truly. Over time, 「よくこそ」 softened into 「ようこそ」, a regular sound change in Japanese where a 「く」 in the middle of a word becomes a 「う」. So it once meant something like how very well you have come. The full greeting used to continue with a verb of coming, and eventually the opening part alone carried the meaning. Here is the fun part: you already know 「こそ」. It is the same particle in 「こちらこそ」, the polite reply meaning the pleasure is mine, or likewise. In that phrase it emphasizes this side, me, indeed. So both phrases are built with the same little emphasis word. To sum up: well plus indeed, softened over time into a warm welcome." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "When you visit someone's home, the welcome usually goes back and forth in a small set pattern. The host opens the door and says 「ようこそいらっしゃいました」, welcome, how kind of you to come. The guest answers 「おじゃまします」, literally I am going to disturb you, as they step inside and take off their shoes. The guest might hand over a small gift and add 「つまらないものですが」, it is just a little something. This word also greets you on airport banners, at ski resorts, and on the first page of websites. A city might put up a sign saying welcome to the town, using this word with the place name and 「へ」. A common mistake is replying to it with thank you very much in a stiff way. With a host, 「おじゃまします」 is the natural answer. The wrap-up: the host says this word, and the guest says 「おじゃまします」." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It comes from an old phrase meaning you have come so well, a warm welcome to a place, often as place plus 「へ」, as in 「ようこそ日本へ|ようこそにほんへ」. It differs from 「いらっしゃいませ」.",
      "It comes from 「よく」 plus the emphasis particle 「こそ」, softened into 「ようこそ」. The same 「こそ」 appears in 「こちらこそ」, likewise.",
      "At a home, the host says 「ようこそいらっしゃいました」 and the guest answers 「おじゃまします」 while stepping inside, not a stiff thank you."
    ],
    ja: "[ようこそ]{日本|にほん}へ。",
    en: "Welcome to Japan." },
  "よろしくおつたえください|よろしくお伝えください": {
    explain: [
      { title: "Why it's that word", text: "This phrase is built from parts you know. It starts with 「よろしく」, the same word from your very first introduction, 「よろしくお願いします|よろしくおねがいします」. It means something like favorably, or with kind regards. Then comes the honorific request pattern, 「お」 plus a verb stem plus 「ください」, which you also saw in 「おかけください」. The verb here is to convey or pass along a message. Its kanji, 「伝|つた」, uses its native reading. It has the person mark on the left. You know it from 「手伝う|てつだう」, to help, from lesson six, which is literally to pass along a hand. So the phrase means please pass on my kind regards. You put the person who should receive the message in front, with 「に」. For example, please say hello to your wife for me. Who uses it? It is a polite closing, said when ending a conversation, a visit, or a letter, to send good wishes to someone's family, boss, or colleagues. Because the request is honorific, it is perfect for people you respect. With friends, you might shorten it to just send regards to your family. A common mistake is to forget the person with 「に」. Recap: kind regards plus a polite please pass it on, sending your best wishes to someone." },
      { title: "Word family", text: "The kanji 「伝|でん」, with the person mark on its left, carries the idea of passing something along from person to person. With its native reading, you have 「伝える|つたえる」, to pass on or convey, and its partner 「伝わる|つたわる」, to be passed on or to get across. 「手伝う|てつだう」, to help, is literally to pass along your hands. With the Chinese-origin reading 「でん」, you get 「伝言|でんごん」, a message left for someone, the word you hear on phones and answering machines. And 「伝統|でんとう」 means tradition, the customs passed down through generations. So whether it is a message, a helping hand, or centuries of culture, the kanji always means something moving from one person to the next. The wrap-up: this phrase asks someone to be the person who passes your warm wishes along." },
      { title: "In real life", text: "This phrase changes shape depending on who you are talking to. With a respected person, like a teacher or a client, use the full polite version, with the person in front plus 「に」. With friends, you can shorten it a lot. 「お母さんによろしく|おかあさんによろしく」, say hi to your mom, is perfectly natural. Or 「よろしく言っといて|よろしくいっといて」, tell them I said hi. And how should you answer when someone says it to you? A simple reply is 「はい、伝えます|はい、つたえます」, yes, I will pass it on. Business people may say something even humbler. In a mini dialogue at the end of a visit, a teacher says please give my regards to your parents, and the student replies 「はい、伝えます|はい、つたえます」, then bows. It is a small, kind ritual. In short: full form for respect, short form for friends, and a simple promise to pass it on." },
      { title: "Grammar corner", text: "The verb inside this phrase is 「伝える|つたえる」, a group two verb, and it is transitive. You convey something. Its intransitive partner is 「伝わる|つたわる」, to get across, to be conveyed. You will hear that partner in phrases like 「気持ちが伝わる|きもちがつたわる」, your feelings get across, a very common compliment on a heartfelt speech or letter. Now look at how the request is built. The plain request is 「伝えてください|つたえてください」, please tell them. To make it honorific, take the stem 「伝え|つたえ」, put 「お」 in front, and 「ください」 after. The same machine turns 「待つ|まつ」 into 「お待ちください|おまちください」 and 「入る|はいる」 into 「お入りください|おはいりください」. Once you see it, you can build many polite requests. To sum up: a transitive verb with an intransitive partner, wrapped in the honorific request pattern." }
    ],
    short: [
      "It is 「よろしく」, kind regards, plus the honorific request 「お」 plus stem plus 「ください」 on 「伝える|つたえる」, to pass along, so please pass on my regards, with the person marked by 「に」.",
      "The kanji 「伝|でん」 means passing something from person to person, as in 「手伝う|てつだう」, 「伝言|でんごん」, a message, and 「伝統|でんとう」, tradition.",
      "Use the full form with respected people and 「お母さんによろしく|おかあさんによろしく」 with friends. When someone says it to you, reply 「はい、伝えます|はい、つたえます」.",
      "「伝える|つたえる」 is transitive, with the partner 「伝わる|つたわる」, as in 「気持ちが伝わる|きもちがつたわる」. The stem plus 「お」 and 「ください」 makes the polite request, just like 「お待ちください|おまちください」."
    ],
    ja: "{奥様|おくさま}に[よろしくお伝えください|よろしくおつたえください]。",
    en: "Please give my best regards to your wife." }
});
